10.40.0	data_table/Common Bean Varieties and Their Best Uses	<div class="stack"><p class="muted">A quick field guide, <regex>user_name</regex> — dry or canned, most of these swap fine at a pinch.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Bean</th><th>Texture</th><th>Best for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Black</td><td>Firm, earthy</td><td>Tacos, black bean soup, rice bowls</td></tr><tr><td>Kidney</td><td>Meaty, holds shape</td><td>Chili, stews, three-bean salad</td></tr><tr><td>Pinto</td><td>Creamy when cooked</td><td>Refried beans, burritos, cowboy beans</td></tr><tr><td>Navy</td><td>Small, soft</td><td>Baked beans, soups, purees</td></tr><tr><td>Chickpea</td><td>Nutty, dense</td><td>Hummus, curries, roasted snacks</td></tr><tr><td>Cannellini</td><td>Buttery, delicate</td><td>White bean dip, minestrone, salads</td></tr><tr><td>Lentil</td><td>Cook fast, no soak</td><td>Dal, soup, salad — green ones hold best</td></tr><tr><td>Edamame</td><td>Firm, grassy</td><td>Snacking, grain bowls, fried rice</td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class="acc"><summary>Soaking &amp; salt notes</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Most dry beans want 6–8 hours cold soak (or a quick boil-and-rest), then 45–90 min simmer. Except lentils and split peas — toss them straight in. Salt the water early; it seasons them through and keeps skins intact.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="stack"><p class="muted">A quick field guide,  — dry or canned, most of these swap fine at a pinch.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Bean</th><th>Texture</th><th>Best for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Black</td><td>Firm, earthy</td><td>Tacos, black bean soup, rice bowls</td></tr><tr><td>Kidney</td><td>Meaty, holds shape</td><td>Chili, stews, three-bean salad</td></tr><tr><td>Pinto</td><td>Creamy when cooked</td><td>Refried beans, burritos, cowboy beans</td></tr><tr><td>Navy</td><td>Small, soft</td><td>Baked beans, soups, purees</td></tr><tr><td>Chickpea</td><td>Nutty, dense</td><td>Hummus, curries, roasted snacks</td></tr><tr><td>Cannellini</td><td>Buttery, delicate</td><td>White bean dip, minestrone, salads</td></tr><tr><td>Lentil</td><td>Cook fast, no soak</td><td>Dal, soup, salad — green ones hold best</td></tr><tr><td>Edamame</td><td>Firm, grassy</td><td>Snacking, grain bowls, fried rice</td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class="acc"><summary>Soaking &amp; salt notes</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Most dry beans want 6–8 hours cold soak (or a quick boil-and-rest), then 45–90 min simmer. Except lentils and split peas — toss them straight in. Salt the water early; it seasons them through and keeps skins intact.</p></div></details></div>	-
10.41.0	data_table/Common Household Batteries and What They Power	<div class="stack"><p class="muted">Grab-and-go reference, <regex>user_name</regex>. Voltage is the thing people trip on.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Size</th><th class="num">Voltage</th><th>Typical devices</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>AAA</td><td class="num">1.5 V</td><td>TV remotes, small toys, pen lights, earbuds cases</td></tr><tr><td>AA</td><td class="num">1.5 V</td><td>Game controllers, cameras, clocks, smoke detectors</td></tr><tr><td>C</td><td class="num">1.5 V</td><td>Flashlights, portable radios, big toys</td></tr><tr><td>D</td><td class="num">1.5 V</td><td>Lanterns, camping fans, large flashlights</td></tr><tr><td>9V</td><td class="num">9 V</td><td>Smoke alarms, guitar pedals, multimeters</td></tr><tr><td>CR2032</td><td class="num">3 V</td><td>Key fobs, watches, kitchen scales, motherboard clocks</td></tr><tr><td>LR44</td><td class="num">1.5 V</td><td>Calculators, thermometers, tiny toys</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small"><strong>Rechargeable swap:</strong> NiMH AAs and AAAs work in most devices, but run ~1.2 V instead of 1.5 V. Fine for remotes and controllers; some smoke detectors prefer fresh alkalines.</p></div></div>	<div class="stack"><p class="muted">Grab-and-go reference, . Voltage is the thing people trip on.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Size</th><th class="num">Voltage</th><th>Typical devices</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>AAA</td><td class="num">1.5 V</td><td>TV remotes, small toys, pen lights, earbuds cases</td></tr><tr><td>AA</td><td class="num">1.5 V</td><td>Game controllers, cameras, clocks, smoke detectors</td></tr><tr><td>C</td><td class="num">1.5 V</td><td>Flashlights, portable radios, big toys</td></tr><tr><td>D</td><td class="num">1.5 V</td><td>Lanterns, camping fans, large flashlights</td></tr><tr><td>9V</td><td class="num">9 V</td><td>Smoke alarms, guitar pedals, multimeters</td></tr><tr><td>CR2032</td><td class="num">3 V</td><td>Key fobs, watches, kitchen scales, motherboard clocks</td></tr><tr><td>LR44</td><td class="num">1.5 V</td><td>Calculators, thermometers, tiny toys</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small"><strong>Rechargeable swap:</strong> NiMH AAs and AAAs work in most devices, but run ~1.2 V instead of 1.5 V. Fine for remotes and controllers; some smoke detectors prefer fresh alkalines.</p></div></div>	-
10.42.0	data_table/Common Bird Feeder Types and Who Shows Up	<div class="stack"><p class="muted">Pick by the guests you want, <regex>user_name</regex> — the feeder is really just a guest list.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Feeder</th><th>Fills with</th><th>Likely visitors</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Hopper / house</td><td>Mixed seed, sunflower</td><td>Cardinals, chickadees, house finches, sparrows</td></tr><tr><td>Tube</td><td>Sunflower, nyjer</td><td>Goldfinches, titmice, nuthatches, siskins</td></tr><tr><td>Platform</td><td>Anything</td><td>Jays, doves, grackles, juncos — plus squirrels</td></tr><tr><td>Suet / cage</td><td>Beef suet cakes</td><td>Woodpeckers, chickadees, wrens</td></tr><tr><td>Nectar</td><td>Sugar water 1:4</td><td>Hummingbirds, orioles (occasionally)</td></tr><tr><td>Window-mount</td><td>Sunflower, mealworms</td><td>Chickadees, titmice — close-up viewing</td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class="acc"><summary>Keep everyone healthy</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Clean feeders every 1–2 weeks with hot soapy water and let them dry fully. Skip the nectar in summer heat for more than 2 days at a time, and don't put seed on wet ground — that's where the trouble starts.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="stack"><p class="muted">Pick by the guests you want,  — the feeder is really just a guest list.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Feeder</th><th>Fills with</th><th>Likely visitors</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Hopper / house</td><td>Mixed seed, sunflower</td><td>Cardinals, chickadees, house finches, sparrows</td></tr><tr><td>Tube</td><td>Sunflower, nyjer</td><td>Goldfinches, titmice, nuthatches, siskins</td></tr><tr><td>Platform</td><td>Anything</td><td>Jays, doves, grackles, juncos — plus squirrels</td></tr><tr><td>Suet / cage</td><td>Beef suet cakes</td><td>Woodpeckers, chickadees, wrens</td></tr><tr><td>Nectar</td><td>Sugar water 1:4</td><td>Hummingbirds, orioles (occasionally)</td></tr><tr><td>Window-mount</td><td>Sunflower, mealworms</td><td>Chickadees, titmice — close-up viewing</td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class="acc"><summary>Keep everyone healthy</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Clean feeders every 1–2 weeks with hot soapy water and let them dry fully. Skip the nectar in summer heat for more than 2 days at a time, and don't put seed on wet ground — that's where the trouble starts.</p></div></details></div>	-
10.43.0	data_table/Common Cutting Techniques and When to Use Them	<div class="stack"><p class="muted">The cut sets the cook time, <regex>user_name</regex>. Same carrot, three different dinners.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Cut</th><th>Size</th><th>Good for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Chop</td><td>Rough, ½–1 in</td><td>Soups, stews, roasting — no skill required</td></tr><tr><td>Dice</td><td>¼ in cubes</td><td>Salsas, sauces, even cooking in sautés</td></tr><tr><td>Julienne</td><td>Matchsticks, ⅛×2 in</td><td>Stir-fries, slaws, garnish</td></tr><tr><td>Brunoise</td><td>⅛ in dice</td><td>Fine sauces, consommé, stuffing</td></tr><tr><td>Chiffonade</td><td>Thin ribbons</td><td>Basil, mint, leafy greens on top of things</td></tr><tr><td>Mince</td><td>Very fine, uneven</td><td>Garlic, ginger, shallots — flavor base</td></tr><tr><td>Bias / roll cut</td><td>Angled slices</td><td>Carrots, asparagus — looks fancy, cooks fast</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout good"><p class="t-small">Rule of thumb: cut everything to the size of the fastest-cooking thing in the pan, or the slow stuff won't be done and the quick stuff will be mush.</p></div></div>	<div class="stack"><p class="muted">The cut sets the cook time, . Same carrot, three different dinners.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Cut</th><th>Size</th><th>Good for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Chop</td><td>Rough, ½–1 in</td><td>Soups, stews, roasting — no skill required</td></tr><tr><td>Dice</td><td>¼ in cubes</td><td>Salsas, sauces, even cooking in sautés</td></tr><tr><td>Julienne</td><td>Matchsticks, ⅛×2 in</td><td>Stir-fries, slaws, garnish</td></tr><tr><td>Brunoise</td><td>⅛ in dice</td><td>Fine sauces, consommé, stuffing</td></tr><tr><td>Chiffonade</td><td>Thin ribbons</td><td>Basil, mint, leafy greens on top of things</td></tr><tr><td>Mince</td><td>Very fine, uneven</td><td>Garlic, ginger, shallots — flavor base</td></tr><tr><td>Bias 