10.44.0	data_table/Common Drill Bits and What They're For	<div class="stack"><p class="muted">Quick swap guide, <regex>user_name</regex> — wrong bit is how a project becomes a story.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Bit</th><th>Material</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Twist (HSS)</td><td>Wood, metal, plastic</td><td>The all-rounder; fine for thin metal</td></tr><tr><td>Brad point</td><td>Wood</td><td>Sharp center point, no wandering</td></tr><tr><td>Spade</td><td>Wood</td><td>Fast, big holes (½–1½ in) in softwood</td></tr><tr><td>Auger</td><td>Wood</td><td>Deep, clean holes for framing and pipes</td></tr><tr><td>Forstner</td><td>Wood</td><td>Flat-bottomed holes for hinges and dowels</td></tr><tr><td>Masonry</td><td>Brick, concrete, tile</td><td>Hammer-drill only; use a pilot hole and go slow</td></tr><tr><td>Hole saw</td><td>Wood, drywall, metal</td><td>Door locks, vents, cable pass-throughs</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout warn"><p class="t-small"><strong>Heads up:</strong> masonry bits don't belong in wood, and wood bits have no business in concrete. Match the bit tip to the material, and let the drill do the work.</p></div></div>	<div class="stack"><p class="muted">Quick swap guide,  — wrong bit is how a project becomes a story.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Bit</th><th>Material</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Twist (HSS)</td><td>Wood, metal, plastic</td><td>The all-rounder; fine for thin metal</td></tr><tr><td>Brad point</td><td>Wood</td><td>Sharp center point, no wandering</td></tr><tr><td>Spade</td><td>Wood</td><td>Fast, big holes (½–1½ in) in softwood</td></tr><tr><td>Auger</td><td>Wood</td><td>Deep, clean holes for framing and pipes</td></tr><tr><td>Forstner</td><td>Wood</td><td>Flat-bottomed holes for hinges and dowels</td></tr><tr><td>Masonry</td><td>Brick, concrete, tile</td><td>Hammer-drill only; use a pilot hole and go slow</td></tr><tr><td>Hole saw</td><td>Wood, drywall, metal</td><td>Door locks, vents, cable pass-throughs</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout warn"><p class="t-small"><strong>Heads up:</strong> masonry bits don't belong in wood, and wood bits have no business in concrete. Match the bit tip to the material, and let the drill do the work.</p></div></div>	-
10.45.0	data_table/Common Sleep Positions and Their Trade-offs	<div class="stack"><p class="muted">None of these is perfect, <regex>user_name</regex> — the best one is the one you fall asleep in.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Position</th><th>Helps</th><th>Worth knowing</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Side</td><td>Snoring, reflux, pregnancy</td><td>Keep a pillow between knees to keep hips level</td></tr><tr><td>Back</td><td>Neck and spine alignment</td><td>Can worsen snoring and reflux; pillow under knees helps</td></tr><tr><td>Stomach</td><td>Less snoring for some</td><td>Tough on the neck — twist stresses the spine</td></tr><tr><td>Fetal</td><td>Low-back comfort</td><td>Too curled can strain the neck and ribs</td></tr><tr><td>Starfish</td><td>Free shoulders, no face pressure</td><td>Space-hungry; light on spine support</td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class="acc"><summary>Small tweaks that actually help</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list t-small"><li>Match pillow height to your shoulder width, especially side-sleepers.</li><li>Try one change a week, not five in a night.</li><li>If you wake with pain most mornings, it might be the mattress, not the pose.</li></ul></div></details></div>	<div class="stack"><p class="muted">None of these is perfect,  — the best one is the one you fall asleep in.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Position</th><th>Helps</th><th>Worth knowing</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Side</td><td>Snoring, reflux, pregnancy</td><td>Keep a pillow between knees to keep hips level</td></tr><tr><td>Back</td><td>Neck and spine alignment</td><td>Can worsen snoring and reflux; pillow under knees helps</td></tr><tr><td>Stomach</td><td>Less snoring for some</td><td>Tough on the neck — twist stresses the spine</td></tr><tr><td>Fetal</td><td>Low-back comfort</td><td>Too curled can strain the neck and ribs</td></tr><tr><td>Starfish</td><td>Free shoulders, no face pressure</td><td>Space-hungry; light on spine support</td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class="acc"><summary>Small tweaks that actually help</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list t-small"><li>Match pillow height to your shoulder width, especially side-sleepers.</li><li>Try one change a week, not five in a night.</li><li>If you wake with pain most mornings, it might be the mattress, not the pose.</li></ul></div></details></div>	-
10.46.0	data_table/Common Oven Temperatures for Baking	<div class="stack"><h3 class="card-title">Common Oven Temperatures for Baking</h3><p class="muted">Here's a quick reference for <regex>user_name</regex>—remember, every oven lies a little.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Baked good</th><th class="num">Temp (°F)</th><th class="num">Temp (°C)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cookies</td><td class="num">350–375</td><td class="num">175–190</td></tr><tr><td>Cakes</td><td class="num">325–350</td><td class="num">160–175</td></tr><tr><td>Bread (lean dough)</td><td class="num">425–450</td><td class="num">220–230</td></tr><tr><td>Pies (fruit)</td><td class="num">375–425</td><td class="num">190–220</td></tr><tr><td>Muffins</td><td class="num">375–400</td><td class="num">190–205</td></tr><tr><td>Roasted vegetables</td><td class="num">425–450</td><td class="num">220–230</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="t-small dim">When in doubt, start lower. You can always bake longer, but you can't un-burn.</p></div>	<div class="stack"><h3 class="card-title">Common Oven Temperatures for Baking</h3><p class="muted">Here's a quick reference for —remember, every oven lies a little.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Baked good</th><th class="num">Temp (°F)</th><th class="num">Temp (°C)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cookies</td><td class="num">350–375</td><td class="num">175–190</td></tr><tr><td>Cakes</td><td class="num">325–350</td><td class="num">160–175</td></tr><tr><td>Bread (lean dough)</td><td class="num">425–450</td><td class="num">220–230</td></tr><tr><td>Pies (fruit)</td><td class="num">375–425</td><td class="num">190–220</td></tr><tr><td>Muffins</td><td class="num">375–400</td><td class="num">190–205</td></tr><tr><td>Roasted vegetables</td><td class="num">425–450</td><td class="num">220–230</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="t-small dim">When in doubt, start lower. You can always bake longer, but you can't un-burn.</p></div>	-
