10.58.0	data_table/Common Espresso Drinks and Their Milk Ratios	<div class="stack"><p class="muted">Here you go, <regex>user_name</regex> — ratios are by volume, so a 2:1 drink is more milk than coffee.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Drink</th><th>Espresso</th><th>Milk</th><th class="num">Foam</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Espresso</td><td>1 shot (30 ml)</td><td>none</td><td class="num">—</td></tr><tr><td>Macchiato</td><td>1 shot</td><td>1 splash</td><td class="num">dot</td></tr><tr><td>Cortado</td><td>1 shot</td><td>1 part (1:1)</td><td class="num">thin</td></tr><tr><td>Flat white</td><td>1–2 shots</td><td>2 parts (1:2)</td><td class="num">micro</td></tr><tr><td>Cappuccino</td><td>1 shot</td><td>1 part (1:1)</td><td class="num">thick</td></tr><tr><td>Latte</td><td>1 shot</td><td>4–6 parts</td><td class="num">1 cm</td></tr><tr><td>Americano</td><td>1–2 shots</td><td>hot water</td><td class="num">—</td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class="acc"><summary>The one-line rule</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Cappuccino and flat white use the same 1:1 base — the cappuccino just piles on dry, airy foam while the flat white gets velvety microfoam. A latte is basically the same idea, drowned in milk.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="stack"><p class="muted">Here you go,  — ratios are by volume, so a 2:1 drink is more milk than coffee.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Drink</th><th>Espresso</th><th>Milk</th><th class="num">Foam</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Espresso</td><td>1 shot (30 ml)</td><td>none</td><td class="num">—</td></tr><tr><td>Macchiato</td><td>1 shot</td><td>1 splash</td><td class="num">dot</td></tr><tr><td>Cortado</td><td>1 shot</td><td>1 part (1:1)</td><td class="num">thin</td></tr><tr><td>Flat white</td><td>1–2 shots</td><td>2 parts (1:2)</td><td class="num">micro</td></tr><tr><td>Cappuccino</td><td>1 shot</td><td>1 part (1:1)</td><td class="num">thick</td></tr><tr><td>Latt