10.12.1	data_table/Common Ice Cream Bases and Their Fat Levels	<p class="muted t-small">Fat is the knob that controls richness and how cold the scoop tastes. Here's the ladder, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Base</th><th class="num">Butterfat</th><th>Churn &amp; feel</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sorbet</td><td class="num">0%</td><td>No dairy; icy, fruit-forward</td></tr><tr><td>Sherbet</td><td class="num">1&ndash;2%</td><td>Milk plus fruit; light and tart</td></tr><tr><td>Gelato</td><td class="num">4&ndash;8%</td><td>Slow churn, low air; dense and silky</td></tr><tr><td>Philadelphia style</td><td class="num">10&ndash;12%</td><td>No eggs; clean dairy flavour</td></tr><tr><td>Standard American</td><td class="num">14&ndash;16%</td><td>Egg yolks optional; the supermarket norm</td></tr><tr><td>Frozen custard</td><td class="num">10%+</td><td>At least 1.4% yolk; soft, poured cold</td></tr><tr><td>Premium / super-premium</td><td class="num">16&ndash;20%</td><td>Low overrun; slow to melt, coats the spoon</td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class="acc"><summary>Why more fat feels colder-resistant</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted">Fat slows melt and mutes sweetness, so heavy bases taste less saccharine at the same sugar level. Lighter bases need a touch more sugar or a stabiliser to stay scoopable.</p></div></details><p class="muted t-small">Quick rule: 10% for weekday scoops, 16%+ when you want one spoonful to count.</p>	<p class="muted t-small">Fat is the knob that controls richness and how cold the scoop tastes. Here's the ladder, .</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Base</th><th class="num">Butterfat</th><th>Churn &amp; feel</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sorbet</td><td class="num">0%</td><td>No dairy; icy, fruit-forward</td></tr><tr><td>Sherbet</td><td class="num">1&ndash;2%</td><td>Milk plus fruit; light and tart</td></tr><tr><td>Gelato</td><td class="num">4&ndash;8%</td><td>Slow churn, low air; dense and silky</td></tr><tr><td>Philadelphia style</td><td class="num">10&ndash;12%</td><td>No eggs; clean dairy flavour</td></tr><tr><td>Standard American</td><td class="num">14&ndash;16%</td><td>Egg yolks optional; the supermarket norm</td></tr><tr><td>Frozen custard</td><td class="num">10%+</td><td>At least 1.4% yolk; soft, poured cold</td></tr><tr><td>Premium / super-premium</td><td class="num">16&ndash;20%</td><td>Low overrun; slow to melt, coats the spoon</td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class="acc"><summary>Why more fat feels colder-resistant</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted">Fat slows melt and mutes sweetness, so heavy bases taste less saccharine at the same sugar level. Lighter bases need a touch more sugar or a stabiliser to stay scoopable.</p></div></details><p class="muted t-small">Quick rule: 10% for weekday scoops, 16%+ when you want one spoonful to count.</p>	-
10.13.1	data_table/Common Desk Heights and Who They Suit	<p class="muted t-small">Desk height is elbow height, not preference. Rough guide for <regex>user_name</regex>:</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Your height</th><th class="num">Seated desk</th><th class="num">Standing desk</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Under 5'0" (152 cm)</td><td class="num">24&ndash;25 in</td><td class="num">36&ndash;38 in</td></tr><tr><td>5'2"&ndash;5'5" (157&ndash;165)</td><td class="num">25&ndash;26 in</td><td class="num">38&ndash;40 in</td></tr><tr><td>5'6"&ndash;5'9" (168&ndash;175)</td><td class="num">26&ndash;28 in</td><td class="num">40&ndash;42 in</td></tr><tr><td>5'10"&ndash;6'1" (178&ndash;185)</td><td class="num">28&ndash;30 in</td><td class="num">42&ndash;45 in</td></tr><tr><td>6'2"&ndash;6'5" (188&ndash;196)</td><td class="num">30&ndash;31 in</td><td class="num">45&ndash;48 in</td></tr><tr><td>Over 6'6" (198 cm)</td><td class="num">31&ndash;32 in</td><td class="num">48&ndash;50 in</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="muted t-small">Sit or stand, aim for forearms level and elbows near 90&deg;. Chair height matters more than the desk.</p><div class="callout info"><p class="muted">Typing all day? Drop the keyboard tray half an inch below elbow height. Your wrists will send a thank-you note.</p></div>	<p class="muted t-small">Desk height is elbow height, not preference. Rough guide for :</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Your height</th><th class="num">Seated desk</th><th class="num">Standing desk</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Under 5'0" (152 cm)</td><td class="num">24&ndash;25 in</td><td class="num">36&ndash;38 in</td></tr><tr><td>5'2"&ndash;5'5" (157&ndash;165)</td><td class="num">25&ndash;26 in</td><td class="num">38&ndash;40 in</td></tr><tr><td>5'6"&ndash;5'9" (168&ndash;175)</td><td class="num">26&ndash;28 in</td><td class="num">40&ndash;42 in</td></tr><tr><td>5'10"&ndash;6'1" (178&ndash;185)</td><td class="num">28&ndash;30 in</td><td class="num">42&ndash;45 in</td></tr><tr><td>6'2"&ndash;6'5" (188&ndash;196)</td><td class="num">30&ndash;31 in</td><td class="num">45&ndash;48 in</td></tr><tr><td>Over 6'6" (198 cm)</td><td class="num">31&ndash;32 in</td><td class="num">48&ndash;50 in</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="muted t-small">Sit or stand, aim for forearms level and elbows near 90&deg;. Chair height matters more than the desk.</p><div class="callout info"><p class="muted">Typing all day? Drop the keyboard tray half an inch below elbow height. Your wrists will send a thank-you note.</p></div>	-
10.14.1	data_table/Common Mustard Styles and Their Heat	<p class="muted t-small">All mustard is the same seed, ground differently and cut with different liquids. Here's the heat ladder, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Style</th><th>Base liquid</th><th>Heat</th><th>Best for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Yellow</td><td>Vinegar + turmeric</td><td>Mild</td><td>Hot dogs, burgers, ballpark nostalgia</td></tr><tr><td>Dijon</td><td>White wine, verjuice</td><td>Medium</td><td>Vinaigrettes, ham, pan sauces</td></tr><tr><td>Whole grain</td><td>Vinegar, coarse seed</td><td>Mild&ndash;medium</td><td>Sausages, charcuterie boards</td></tr><tr><td>English</td><td>Water + flour</td><td>Very hot</td><td>Roast beef, a little goes far</td></tr><tr><td>Chinese hot</td><td>Water, spices</td><td>Sharp, sinusy</td><td>Egg rolls, dipping</td></tr><tr><td>Beer / honey</td><td>Ale, honey</td><td>Sweet-mild</td><td>Pretzels, grilled cheese</td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class="acc"><summary>Why water makes it hotter</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted">The pungent compound forms in cold water. Acid stops that reaction, which is why vinegar-based mustards bite gentler. Hot water kills it entirely.</p></div></details>	<p class="muted t-small">All mustard is the same seed, ground differently and cut with different liquids. Here's the heat ladder, .</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Style</th><th>Base liquid</th><th>Heat</th><th>Best for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Yellow</td><td>Vinegar + turmeric</td><td>Mild</td><td>Hot dogs, burgers, ballpark nostalgia</td></tr><tr><td>Dijon</td><td>White wine, verjuice</td><td>Medium</td><td>Vinaigrettes, ham, pan sauces</td></tr><tr><td>Whole grain</td><td>Vinegar, coarse seed</td><td>Mild&ndash;medium</td><td>Sausages, charcuterie boards</td></tr><tr><td>English</td><td>Water + flour</td><td>Very hot</td><td>Roast beef, a little goes far</td></tr><tr><td>C