13.0.0	timeline_view/Sourdough Starter	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Sourdough starter, day by day</h3><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 0</span><p>Mix 50g whole wheat flour with 50g warm water. Scrape down. Cover loosely.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Bubbles? Good. Do nothing but peek. A little hooch is normal.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>First feed: discard half, add 50g flour + 50g water. Stir well.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3–4</span><p>Feed once a day. It may smell like acetone or gym socks. That's the awkward phase.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 5–7</span><p>Feed twice a day if it's bubbly. Look for a dome and a slightly tangy, yeasty smell.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 8+</span><p>Ready when it doubles in 4–6 hours after feeding. Bake, <regex>user_name</regex>. You did it.</p></li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Sourdough starter, day by day</h3><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 0</span><p>Mix 50g whole wheat flour with 50g warm water. Scrape down. Cover loosely.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Bubbles? Good. Do nothing but peek. A little hooch is normal.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>First feed: discard half, add 50g flour + 50g water. Stir well.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3–4</span><p>Feed once a day. It may smell like acetone or gym socks. That's the awkward phase.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 5–7</span><p>Feed twice a day if it's bubbly. Look for a dome and a slightly tangy, yeasty smell.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 8+</span><p>Ready when it doubles in 4–6 hours after feeding. Bake, . You did it.</p></li></ul></div>	-
13.1.0	timeline_view/Late-Night Cat Zoomies	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Typical cat night shift</h3><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">9:00 pm</span><p>Last nap ends. Eyes dilate. A tail twitches.</p></li><li><span class="when">10:30 pm</span><p>You go to bed. Cat follows, sits on your chest, purrs, then leaves.</p></li><li><span class="when">11:15 pm</span><p>First zoom: hallway laps. A rug gets bunched.</p></li><li><span class="when">1:00 am</span><p>Second zoom, with sound effects. A pen falls off the desk.</p></li><li><span class="when">3:00 am</span><p>Stares at you. Knocks something small off a shelf. Maintains eye contact.</p></li><li><span class="when">5:30 am</span><p>Sleeps like an angel on your pillow. You wake up tired.</p></li></ul><p class="muted t-small">Vet says: more play before bed helps. Try a feather wand, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Typical cat night shift</h3><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">9:00 pm</span><p>Last nap ends. Eyes dilate. A tail twitches.</p></li><li><span class="when">10:30 pm</span><p>You go to bed. Cat follows, sits on your chest, purrs, then leaves.</p></li><li><span class="when">11:15 pm</span><p>First zoom: hallway laps. A rug gets bunched.</p></li><li><span class="when">1:00 am</span><p>Second zoom, with sound effects. A pen falls off the desk.</p></li><li><span class="when">3:00 am</span><p>Stares at you. Knocks something small off a shelf. Maintains eye contact.</p></li><li><span class="when">5:30 am</span><p>Sleeps like an angel on your pillow. You wake up tired.</p></li></ul><p class="muted t-small">Vet says: more play before bed helps. Try a feather wand, .</p></div>	-
13.2.0	timeline_view/Tomato Plant Milestones	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">From seed to sauce</h3><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Week 0</span><p>Sow seeds 6–8 weeks before last frost. Keep soil warm and damp.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 1–2</span><p>Seedlings pop up. Give them light—a sunny window or a shop light.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 6</span><p>Transplant into bigger pots or the garden after frost passes. Bury stems deep.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 8–10</span><p>Yellow flowers appear. Tap the stems to help pollination.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 12–14</span><p>Tiny green tomatoes. Water consistently, feed every two weeks.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 16+</span><p>Colour shifts. Pick when fully red (or whatever colour you planted). Enjoy, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">From seed to sauce</h3><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Week 0</span><p>Sow seeds 6–8 weeks before last frost. Keep soil warm and damp.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 1–2</span><p>Seedlings pop up. Give them light—a sunny window or a shop light.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 6</span><p>Transplant into bigger pots or the garden after frost passes. Bury stems deep.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 8–10</span><p>Yellow flowers appear. Tap the stems to help pollination.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 12–14</span><p>Tiny green tomatoes. Water consistently, feed every two weeks.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 16+</span><p>Colour shifts. Pick when fully red (or whatever colour you planted). Enjoy, .</p></li></ul></div>	-
13.3.0	timeline_view/The First Week of Couch to 5K	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Couch to 5K: Week 1</h3><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Five-minute warm-up walk, then 60 seconds jogging, 90 seconds walking. Repeat 8 times. Cool down.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>Rest. Your legs are thinking about it.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>Same intervals. Jog slower than you think you should.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 4</span><p>Rest or a gentle walk. Stretch if you like.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 5</span><p>Intervals again. Notice anything? You're getting better at this.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 6–7</span><p>Rest. Then repeat Week 1 or move on. You set the pace, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></li></ul><p class="muted t-small">Week 1 done. That's the whole trick.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Couch to 5K: Week 1</h3><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Five-minute warm-up walk, then 60 seconds jogging, 90 seconds walking. Repeat 8 times. Cool down.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>Rest. Your legs are thinking about it.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>Same intervals. Jog slower than you think you should.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 4</span><p>Rest or a gentle walk. Stretch if you like.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 5</span><p>Intervals again. Notice anything? You're getting better at this.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 6–7</span><p>Rest. Then repeat Week 1 or move on. You set the pace, .</p></li></ul><p class="muted t-small">Week 1 done. That's the whole trick.</p></div>	-
13.4.0	timeline_view/A Sourdough Loaf, Start to Finish	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Bake day, hour by hour</h3><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">9:00 am</span><p>Feed starter. Wait until it peaks—domed, bubbly, smells like yogurt.</p></li><li><span class="when">1:00 pm</span><p>Mix dough: flour, water, salt, starter. Rest 30 minutes.</p></li><li><span class="when">1:30–3:30 pm</span><p>Stretch and fold every 30 minutes. Four sets. Dough gets smoother.</p></li><li><span class="when">3:30–5:30 pm</span><p>Bulk ferment. It should rise by about half. Room temp matters.</p></li><li><span class="when">5:30 pm</span><p>Shape. Put in a floured basket. Cold proof overnight in the fridge.</p></li><li><span class="when">Next morning</span><p>Bake in a hot Dutch oven: 20 min lid on, 20 min lid off. Cool before slicing, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Bake day, hour by hour</h3><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">9:00 am</span><p>Feed starter. Wait until it peaks—domed, bubbly, smells like yogurt.</p></li><li><span class="when">1:00 pm</span><p>Mix dough: flour, water, salt, starter. Rest 30 minutes.</p></li><li><span class="when">1:30–3:30 pm</span><p>Stretch and fold every 30 minutes. Four sets. Dough gets smoother.</p></li><li><span class="when">3:30–5:30 pm</span><p>Bulk ferment. It should rise by about half. Room temp matters.</p></li><li><span class="when">5:30 pm</span><p>Shape. Put in a floured basket. Cold proof overnight in the fridge.</p></li><li><span class="when">Next morning</span><p>Bake in a hot Dutch oven: 20 min lid on, 20 min lid off. Cool before slicing, .</p></li></ul></div>	-
13.5.0	timeline_view/Moving Out: The Last Two Weeks	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Moving out, two weeks out</h3><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">14 days</span><p>Book a van or movers. Confirm date and time. Tell your landlord.</p></li><li><span class="when">12 days</span><p>Start packing things you don't use: books, off-season clothes, décor.</p></li><li><span class="when">10 days</span><p>Change your address: post, bank, subscriptions. Cancel local services.</p></li><li><span class="when">7 days</span><p>Pack kitchen except one pot, one pan, one plate per person.</p></li><li><span class="when">3 days</span><p>Pack a suitcase with clothes and toiletries for the first night.</p></li><li><span class="when">Moving day</span><p>Label boxes by room. Take a photo of the meter readings. Breathe, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Moving out, two weeks out</h3><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">14 days</span><p>Book a van or movers. Confirm date and time. Tell your landlord.</p></li><li><span class="when">12 days</span><p>Start packing things you don't use: books, off-season clothes, décor.</p></li><li><span class="when">10 days</span><p>Change your address: post, bank, subscriptions. Cancel local services.</p></li><li><span class="when">7 days</span><p>Pack kitchen except one pot, one pan, one plate per person.</p></li><li><span class="when">3 days</span><p>Pack a suitcase with clothes and toiletries for the first night.</p></li><li><span class="when">Moving day</span><p>Label boxes by room. Take a photo of the meter readings. Breathe, .</p></li></ul></div>	-
13.6.0	timeline_view/A Kettle of Boiling Water, Minute by Minute	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Kettle Watch: the full boil, minute by minute</h3><p class="muted t-small">For <regex>user_name</regex>. One litre, lid on, no salt trickery.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">0:00</span><p>Cold tap water in, lid down. Switch on. Nothing happens. Suspicious.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:45</span><p>Faint hiss from the base. The element is warming and tiny bubbles cling to the walls.</p></li><li><span class="when">1:30</span><p>A low roar builds and the bubbles detach. This is dissolved air escaping — not a boil yet.</p></li><li><span class="when">2:40</span><p>Roar fades to a murmur, then near-silence. Confusing calm before the action.</p></li><li><span class="when">3:20</span><p>Big rumble, bubbles streaming steadily, water gone slightly cloudy.</p></li><li><span class="when">3:50</span><p>Rolling boil, 100&nbsp;°C at sea level, lid dancing. Cut it now.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Why does it roar before it boils?</summary><div class="body"><p>Dissolved air bubbles collapse as they rise, and that imploding roar is loudest at around 80&nbsp;°C. Once that air is gone, the kettle quiets — the water still needs another few minutes.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Kettle Watch: the full boil, minute by minute</h3><p class="muted t-small">For . One litre, lid on, no salt trickery.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">0:00</span><p>Cold tap water in, lid down. Switch on. Nothing happens. Suspicious.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:45</span><p>Faint hiss from the base. The element is warming and tiny bubbles cling to the walls.</p></li><li><span class="when">1:30</span><p>A low roar builds and the bubbles detach. This is dissolved air escaping — not a boil yet.</p></li><li><span class="when">2:40</span><p>Roar fades to a murmur, then near-silence. Confusing calm before the action.</p></li><li><span class="when">3:20</span><p>Big rumble, bubbles streaming steadily, water gone slightly cloudy.</p></li><li><span class="when">3:50</span><p>Rolling boil, 100&nbsp;°C at sea level, lid dancing. Cut it now.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Why does it roar before it boils?</summary><div class="body"><p>Dissolved air bubbles collapse as they rise, and that imploding roar is loudest at around 80&nbsp;°C. Once that air is gone, the kettle quiets — the water still needs another few minutes.</p></div></details></div>	-
13.7.0	timeline_view/Learning to Play the Wooden Flute: First Three Weeks	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Wooden flute: the honest first three weeks</h3><p class="muted t-small">For <regex>user_name</regex>, based on a typical C-pitch Irish flute.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>You mostly make whistles and hiss. Goal: a clean note, any note. Embouchure is a tiny air-channel over the hole, not a puff.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>Low D rings out on maybe one try in five. The flutters are an air-flow problem, not a talent problem.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>Low G to A to B slide smoothly. You can find your embouchure from cold in under a minute.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>A five-minute drill that actually works</summary><div class="body"><p>Play one long note. Aim the airstream so the sound blooms — roll the flute out or in a hair. Do this for each of the six lowest notes once. Then stop. Better than an hour of noodling.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>First tunes to try</summary><div class="body"><p><em>Star of the County Down</em> and <em>The Foggy Dew</em> sit nicely in the low octave and build breath control.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Wooden flute: the honest first three weeks</h3><p class="muted t-small">For , based on a typical C-pitch Irish flute.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>You mostly make whistles and hiss. Goal: a clean note, any note. Embouchure is a tiny air-channel over the hole, not a puff.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>Low D rings out on maybe one try in five. The flutters are an air-flow problem, not a talent problem.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>Low G to A to B slide smoothly. You can find your embouchure from cold in under a minute.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>A five-minute drill that actually works</summary><div class="body"><p>Play one long note. Aim the airstream so the sound blooms — roll the flute out or in a hair. Do this for each of the six lowest notes once. Then stop. Better than an hour of noodling.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>First tunes to try</summary><div class="body"><p><em>Star of the County Down</em> and <em>The Foggy Dew</em> sit nicely in the low octave and build breath control.</p></div></details></div>	-
13.8.0	timeline_view/Retiling a Shower: Two Weekend Job Log	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Retiling a shower: two weekends, no panic</h3><p class="muted t-small">For <regex>user_name</regex>. Nine square metres of porcelain, one walk-in shower.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Sat 8am</span><p>Strip the old tiles, scrape adhesive, and vacuum. A cheap oscillating tool with a scraper blade earns its keep.</p></li><li><span class="when">Sat 11am</span><p>Fill gouges with rapid-set filler and let it cure. Grouting later is easier if the substrate is true.</p></li><li><span class="when">Sat 2pm</span><p>Tanking kit on the walls and floor. Two coats, corner tape, no bare gypsum. Let it dry overnight.</p></li><li><span class="when">Sun 9am</span><p>Set out from the floor line and work up. Thinset, notch trowel, spacers. Stop halfway for the night.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2 Sat</span><p>Finish tiling, wait 24 hours, then grout. Squeegee the walls, sponge the joints, buff the haze.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2 Sun</span><p>Full cure, silicone the corners and tray edges, then take the shower you have dreamed about for four days.</p></li></ul><div class="callout warn"><p><strong>Near miss:</strong> tiling yourself is fine, but a leaking shower tray is not a weekend fix. If the tray is soft underfoot, pause and call someone.</p></div></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Retiling a shower: two weekends, no panic</h3><p class="muted t-small">For . Nine square metres of porcelain, one walk-in shower.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Sat 8am</span><p>Strip the old tiles, scrape adhesive, and vacuum. A cheap oscillating tool with a scraper blade earns its keep.</p></li><li><span class="when">Sat 11am</span><p>Fill gouges with rapid-set filler and let it cure. Grouting later is easier if the substrate is true.</p></li><li><span class="when">Sat 2pm</span><p>Tanking kit on the walls and floor. Two coats, corner tape, no bare gypsum. Let it dry overnight.</p></li><li><span class="when">Sun 9am</span><p>Set out from the floor line and work up. Thinset, notch trowel, spacers. Stop halfway for the night.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2 Sat</span><p>Finish tiling, wait 24 hours, then grout. Squeegee the walls, sponge the joints, buff the haze.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2 Sun</span><p>Full cure, silicone the corners and tray edges, then take the shower you have dreamed about for four days.</p></li></ul><div class="callout warn"><p><strong>Near miss:</strong> tiling yourself is fine, but a leaking shower tray is not a weekend fix. If the tray is soft underfoot, pause and call someone.</p></div></div>	-
13.9.0	timeline_view/Blueberry Bush Care Across the Seasons	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Blueberry bushes: what to do, and when</h3><p class="muted t-small">For <regex>user_name</regex>. Acid soil is the whole ballgame.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Late winter</span><p>Prune hard: cut oldest stems to the base, thin crossing branches. You are building new fruiting wood.</p></li><li><span class="when">Early spring</span><p>Top up with pine bark mulch and a rhododendron feed. Never lime, never general garden compost near the roots.</p></li><li><span class="when">Late spring</span><p>Pollination overdrive. Two different varieties within a few metres doubles the crop.</p></li><li><span class="when">Summer</span><p>Water deeply twice a week if it is dry. Shallow roots hate drought, and so do the berries you are waiting for.</p></li><li><span class="when">Autumn</span><p>Leaves go red and orange, then drop. Pick up and bin fruit mummies to break pest cycles.</p></li><li><span class="when">Again</span><p>Expect the best harvest from the cuts you made two summers ago. Berries follow pruning with a lag.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Small berries? Three likely causes</summary><div class="body"><p>Soil above pH&nbsp;5.5, no pollinator variety nearby, or a dry June during the sizing stage. Fix in that order — it usually is the soil.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Blueberry bushes: what to do, and when</h3><p class="muted t-small">For . Acid soil is the whole ballgame.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Late winter</span><p>Prune hard: cut oldest stems to the base, thin crossing branches. You are building new fruiting wood.</p></li><li><span class="when">Early spring</span><p>Top up with pine bark mulch and a rhododendron feed. Never lime, never general garden compost near the roots.</p></li><li><span class="when">Late spring</span><p>Pollination overdrive. Two different varieties within a few metres doubles the crop.</p></li><li><span class="when">Summer</span><p>Water deeply twice a week if it is dry. Shallow roots hate drought, and so do the berries you are waiting for.</p></li><li><span class="when">Autumn</span><p>Leaves go red and orange, then drop. Pick up and bin fruit mummies to break pest cycles.</p></li><li><span class="when">Again</span><p>Expect the best harvest from the cuts you made two summers ago. Berries follow pruning with a lag.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Small berries? Three likely causes</summary><div class="body"><p>Soil above pH&nbsp;5.5, no pollinator variety nearby, or a dry June during the sizing stage. Fix in that order — it usually is the soil.</p></div></details></div>	-
13.10.0	timeline_view/Sourdough Pie Crust, From Starter to Slice	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Sourdough pie crust: starter to slice</h3><p class="muted t-small">For <regex>user_name</regex>. One single-crust double batch.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Step 1</span><p>Mix 250&nbsp;g flour, 150&nbsp;g cold butter cubes, 2&nbsp;g salt, 100&nbsp;g discard. Use a pie lifter to keep flat butter shards.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 2</span><p>Bring together, then chill 30 minutes. Cold dough, hot oven. This is the rule you keep indiscretely true.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 3</span><p>Roll and fold once or twice to build rough laminations. Chill again 20 minutes. Patience is the flake.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 4</span><p>Roll to a round, line the tin, trim, and dock the base with a fork all over.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 5</span><p>Blind-bake at 200&nbsp;°C for 20 minutes with parchment and beans, then 8 minutes bare to dry the base.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 6</span><p>Cool on a rack, fill and serve. The uncooked shell also freezes for a month.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>What starter version should I use?</summary><div class="body"><p>Any unfed discard works, but 100&nbsp;% hydration is easiest to measure. It brings tang without the wait — the rest is all technique.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Sourdough pie crust: starter to slice</h3><p class="muted t-small">For . One single-crust double batch.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Step 1</span><p>Mix 250&nbsp;g flour, 150&nbsp;g cold butter cubes, 2&nbsp;g salt, 100&nbsp;g discard. Use a pie lifter to keep flat butter shards.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 2</span><p>Bring together, then chill 30 minutes. Cold dough, hot oven. This is the rule you keep indiscretely true.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 3</span><p>Roll and fold once or twice to build rough laminations. Chill again 20 minutes. Patience is the flake.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 4</span><p>Roll to a round, line the tin, trim, and dock the base with a fork all over.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 5</span><p>Blind-bake at 200&nbsp;°C for 20 minutes with parchment and beans, then 8 minutes bare to dry the base.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 6</span><p>Cool on a rack, fill and serve. The uncooked shell also freezes for a month.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>What starter version should I use?</summary><div class="body"><p>Any unfed discard works, but 100&nbsp;% hydration is easiest to measure. It brings tang without the wait — the rest is all technique.</p></div></details></div>	-
13.11.0	timeline_view/Training a Rescue Greyhound, Week One Home	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Rescue greyhound: the first week home</h3><p class="muted t-small">For <regex>user_name</regex>. Slow is fast with ex-racers.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Crate or quiet bed, daylight, minimal visitors. Offer water and a light meal. No baths, no big welcomes.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>Leash walks only, on the same route. Count on a lot of standing and staring. That is progress, not failure.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>Start a routine: out at 7am, meet, feed, nap. Greyhounds are creatures of pattern, like little striped statues.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 4</span><p>Introduce stairs gently with a treat trail, one step at a time. Some take weeks. Stairs are the first real hurdle.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 5</span><p>Begin short alone-time in another room, two minutes, then back. No long absences yet.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 7</span><p>First settled nighttime. Most sleep through the night by now. If yours does not, slow down the day instead.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info"><p><strong>Near miss:</strong> calm might look like a happy dog who then explodes on a walk. Give 24&ndash;48 hours after new things. Greyhounds decompress quietly, then all at once.</p></div></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Rescue greyhound: the first week home</h3><p class="muted t-small">For . Slow is fast with ex-racers.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Crate or quiet bed, daylight, minimal visitors. Offer water and a light meal. No baths, no big welcomes.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>Leash walks only, on the same route. Count on a lot of standing and staring. That is progress, not failure.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>Start a routine: out at 7am, meet, feed, nap. Greyhounds are creatures of pattern, like little striped statues.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 4</span><p>Introduce stairs gently with a treat trail, one step at a time. Some take weeks. Stairs are the first real hurdle.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 5</span><p>Begin short alone-time in another room, two minutes, then back. No long absences yet.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 7</span><p>First settled nighttime. Most sleep through the night by now. If yours does not, slow down the day instead.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info"><p><strong>Near miss:</strong> calm might look like a happy dog who then explodes on a walk. Give 24&ndash;48 hours after new things. Greyhounds decompress quietly, then all at once.</p></div></div>	-
13.12.0	timeline_view/The Beekeeper's Season: A Year in the Hive	<div class="card"><p class="card-title">A Year in the Hive</p><p class="muted t-small">Northern-temperate apiary, roughly <regex>user_name</regex>'s latitude. Every hive is its own small country with its own opinions.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Late winter</span><p>Brood rearing restarts while snow is still on the ground. First cleansing flights on any day above 10&nbsp;°C.</p></li><li><span class="when">March</span><p>Willow and hazel pollen. Feed thin syrup <em>inside</em> the hive or you'll wake the neighbours' hives too.</p></li><li><span class="when">April</span><p>Dandelion flow begins. First full inspection. Look for queen cells before they look for you.</p></li><li><span class="when">May–June</span><p>Main flow. Supers on. Checkerboard frames to give the queen room. Swarms possible any warm afternoon.</p></li><li><span class="when">July</span><p>Linden and bramble. Peak population, peak grumpiness.</p></li><li><span class="when">August</span><p>Harvest honey, leave 15&nbsp;kg for winter. Treat for varroa once the supers are off.</p></li><li><span class="when">September</span><p>Ivory and heather top-ups. Feed thick syrup if stores are light.</p></li><li><span class="when">October</span><p>Cluster forms. Mouse guards on, entrances reduced, brood box closed until spring.</p></li><li><span class="when">Winter</span><p>Nothing. Resist the urge to open the lid. Heft the hive from behind instead.</p></li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><p class="card-title">A Year in the Hive</p><p class="muted t-small">Northern-temperate apiary, roughly 's latitude. Every hive is its own small country with its own opinions.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Late winter</span><p>Brood rearing restarts while snow is still on the ground. First cleansing flights on any day above 10&nbsp;°C.</p></li><li><span class="when">March</span><p>Willow and hazel pollen. Feed thin syrup <em>inside</em> the hive or you'll wake the neighbours' hives too.</p></li><li><span class="when">April</span><p>Dandelion flow begins. First full inspection. Look for queen cells before they look for you.</p></li><li><span class="when">May–June</span><p>Main flow. Supers on. Checkerboard frames to give the queen room. Swarms possible any warm afternoon.</p></li><li><span class="when">July</span><p>Linden and bramble. Peak population, peak grumpiness.</p></li><li><span class="when">August</span><p>Harvest honey, leave 15&nbsp;kg for winter. Treat for varroa once the supers are off.</p></li><li><span class="when">September</span><p>Ivory and heather top-ups. Feed thick syrup if stores are light.</p></li><li><span class="when">October</span><p>Cluster forms. Mouse guards on, entrances reduced, brood box closed until spring.</p></li><li><span class="when">Winter</span><p>Nothing. Resist the urge to open the lid. Heft the hive from behind instead.</p></li></ul></div>	-
13.13.0	timeline_view/A Baby's First Night Home, Hour by Hour	<div class="card"><p class="card-title">Night One: What Actually Happens</p><p class="muted t-small">Roughly. Yours will differ and that's normal. <regex>user_name</regex>, you will not sleep properly and it will still be fine.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">19:00</span><p>You come home from the hospital. Baby falls asleep in the car seat. Every adult has a moment of "wait, are we allowed to just take it home?"</p></li><li><span class="when">20:00</span><p>First feed attempt on your own sofa. Cluster feeding is common. Expect 8–12 feeds in 24&nbsp;hours from here.</p></li><li><span class="when">21:30</span><p>First solo nappy change. Have a spare nappy and spare onesie within arm's reach. Trust this one.</p></li><li><span class="when">22:00</span><p>Baby is wide awake and inspecting the ceiling. Newborns don't know day from night yet. That comes around 6–8 weeks.</p></li><li><span class="when">01:00</span><p>The fussy stretch. White noise, swaddle, dark room, on your chest upright after feeds if there's reflux.</p></li><li><span class="when">03:00</span><p>Partner's shift. Whoever is awake does nappy + resettle; the other one sleeps properly, no half-sleeps.</p></li><li><span class="when">05:00</span><p>Nappy count check: by day 5 you want at least 6 wet nappies a day. That's the only real dashboard.</p></li><li><span class="when">07:00</span><p>First light, both of you still in yesterday's clothes. Congratulations, you've done night one.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3–5</span><p>Milk comes in for many people. Baby feeds and feeds and feeds. It gets easier.</p></li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><p class="card-title">Night One: What Actually Happens</p><p class="muted t-small">Roughly. Yours will differ and that's normal. , you will not sleep properly and it will still be fine.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">19:00</span><p>You come home from the hospital. Baby falls asleep in the car seat. Every adult has a moment of "wait, are we allowed to just take it home?"</p></li><li><span class="when">20:00</span><p>First feed attempt on your own sofa. Cluster feeding is common. Expect 8–12 feeds in 24&nbsp;hours from here.</p></li><li><span class="when">21:30</span><p>First solo nappy change. Have a spare nappy and spare onesie within arm's reach. Trust this one.</p></li><li><span class="when">22:00</span><p>Baby is wide awake and inspecting the ceiling. Newborns don't know day from night yet. That comes around 6–8 weeks.</p></li><li><span class="when">01:00</span><p>The fussy stretch. White noise, swaddle, dark room, on your chest upright after feeds if there's reflux.</p></li><li><span class="when">03:00</span><p>Partner's shift. Whoever is awake does nappy + resettle; the other one sleeps properly, no half-sleeps.</p></li><li><span class="when">05:00</span><p>Nappy count check: by day 5 you want at least 6 wet nappies a day. That's the only real dashboard.</p></li><li><span class="when">07:00</span><p>First light, both of you still in yesterday's clothes. Congratulations, you've done night one.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3–5</span><p>Milk comes in for many people. Baby feeds and feeds and feeds. It gets easier.</p></li></ul></div>	-
13.14.0	timeline_view/Fermenting Hot Sauce: Three Weeks, Jar to Bottle	<div class="card"><p class="card-title">Hot Sauce Ferment Log</p><p class="muted t-small">3% brine by total weight (chilis + water). Using <regex>user_name</regex>'s kitchen towel as the lid is optional but traditional.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 0</span><p>Chilis, garlic, 3% salt by weight, enough water to keep everything under the brine. Weight it down.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>First bubbles at the shoulder of the jar. That's the good fizz. No need to burp it if you used an airlock.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 5</span><p>Sharp, raw smell eases into something rounder. Brine goes slightly cloudy. Normal. White film is kahm yeast — skim it, don't panic.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 8–10</span><p>Taste it. It's the actual test. Fruity, tangy, still hot. If it's pleasant, keep going.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 14</span><p>Active bubbling slows. pH should be 3.5 or lower — a cheap meter earns its keep here.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 18</span><p>Blend the solids with some brine. Vinegar is optional now; the ferment already has acid.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 21</span><p>Strain, bottle, fridge. It keeps for months and gets better for the first few weeks.</p></li><li><span class="when">Any day</span><p>Orange or pink fuzzy mould? Bin it. White film, pink or grey fuzz, no.</p></li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><p class="card-title">Hot Sauce Ferment Log</p><p class="muted t-small">3% brine by total weight (chilis + water). Using 's kitchen towel as the lid is optional but traditional.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 0</span><p>Chilis, garlic, 3% salt by weight, enough water to keep everything under the brine. Weight it down.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>First bubbles at the shoulder of the jar. That's the good fizz. No need to burp it if you used an airlock.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 5</span><p>Sharp, raw smell eases into something rounder. Brine goes slightly cloudy. Normal. White film is kahm yeast — skim it, don't panic.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 8–10</span><p>Taste it. It's the actual test. Fruity, tangy, still hot. If it's pleasant, keep going.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 14</span><p>Active bubbling slows. pH should be 3.5 or lower — a cheap meter earns its keep here.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 18</span><p>Blend the solids with some brine. Vinegar is optional now; the ferment already has acid.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 21</span><p>Strain, bottle, fridge. It keeps for months and gets better for the first few weeks.</p></li><li><span class="when">Any day</span><p>Orange or pink fuzzy mould? Bin it. White film, pink or grey fuzz, no.</p></li></ul></div>	-
13.15.0	timeline_view/Six Weeks of Guitar Calluses	<div class="card"><p class="card-title">Callus Diary</p><p class="muted t-small">The unglamorous part of learning guitar. Small print: thin strings hurt more than thick ones, so an acoustic is a character-building choice, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Fingertips go red and stingy after 15 minutes. Stop there. Sore is fine; bleeding is not.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>A pale dent appears on each fingertip. This is the beginning. You are doing it right.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Fingers are tender but the initial bite has gone. Play 10-ish minutes twice a day; shorter is better than one long session.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>Hard shiny patches. Barre chords are still unplayable and will be for a while. That's not a talent problem.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>Real calluses. Chords ring cleanly for the first time without you watching each finger.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 4</span><p>You overdid it and a callus peeled. Rough edge snagging the string? File it smooth, keep playing gently.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 6</span><p>You stop noticing your fingers at all. That's the milestone. Your sound, however, still needs work — welcome to the long part.</p></li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><p class="card-title">Callus Diary</p><p class="muted t-small">The unglamorous part of learning guitar. Small print: thin strings hurt more than thick ones, so an acoustic is a character-building choice, .</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Fingertips go red and stingy after 15 minutes. Stop there. Sore is fine; bleeding is not.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>A pale dent appears on each fingertip. This is the beginning. You are doing it right.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Fingers are tender but the initial bite has gone. Play 10-ish minutes twice a day; shorter is better than one long session.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>Hard shiny patches. Barre chords are still unplayable and will be for a while. That's not a talent problem.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>Real calluses. Chords ring cleanly for the first time without you watching each finger.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 4</span><p>You overdid it and a callus peeled. Rough edge snagging the string? File it smooth, keep playing gently.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 6</span><p>You stop noticing your fingers at all. That's the milestone. Your sound, however, still needs work — welcome to the long part.</p></li></ul></div>	-
13.16.0	timeline_view/Weeks of Planning a 250-Person Wedding	<div class="card"><p class="card-title">Wedding Countdown</p><p class="muted t-small">Names on the frock coat, not the guest list — <regex>user_name</regex>, this is the calm version. Everything crowds the last two weeks; spend the early months on venue and vendors.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">12 months</span><p>Guest number first, then venue. At 250 people the venue picks your date, not the other way round.</p></li><li><span class="when">11 months</span><p>Photographer, caterer, band. These three go first for big weddings. Deposit by now.</p></li><li><span class="when">9 months</span><p>Dress, suit, cake tastings. Save-the-dates out.</p></li><li><span class="when">6 months</span><p>Invitations designed. Block hotel rooms. Book hair and makeup trials.</p></li><li><span class="when">3 months</span><p>Invitations sent. Seating chart begins, and it will be revised about nine times.</p></li><li><span class="when">1 month</span><p>Final numbers to the caterer. Timeline of the day written out. Officiant gets the script.</p></li><li><span class="when">1 week</span><p>Any late RSVPs. Confirm vendors. Pack an emergency box: safety pins, plasters, spare tights, painkillers.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day before</span><p>Rehearsal, then eat properly and go to bed. The morning of is not the time to start the seating chart.</p></li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><p class="card-title">Wedding Countdown</p><p class="muted t-small">Names on the frock coat, not the guest list — , this is the calm version. Everything crowds the last two weeks; spend the early months on venue and vendors.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">12 months</span><p>Guest number first, then venue. At 250 people the venue picks your date, not the other way round.</p></li><li><span class="when">11 months</span><p>Photographer, caterer, band. These three go first for big weddings. Deposit by now.</p></li><li><span class="when">9 months</span><p>Dress, suit, cake tastings. Save-the-dates out.</p></li><li><span class="when">6 months</span><p>Invitations designed. Block hotel rooms. Book hair and makeup trials.</p></li><li><span class="when">3 months</span><p>Invitations sent. Seating chart begins, and it will be revised about nine times.</p></li><li><span class="when">1 month</span><p>Final numbers to the caterer. Timeline of the day written out. Officiant gets the script.</p></li><li><span class="when">1 week</span><p>Any late RSVPs. Confirm vendors. Pack an emergency box: safety pins, plasters, spare tights, painkillers.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day before</span><p>Rehearsal, then eat properly and go to bed. The morning of is not the time to start the seating chart.</p></li></ul></div>	-
13.17.0	timeline_view/Training a Puppy to Sleep Through the Night	<div class="card"><p class="card-title">Nights, Week by Week</p><p class="muted t-small">A rule of thumb: puppies can hold their bladder roughly one hour per month of age, plus one. So a two-month-old gets about three hours max, <regex>user_name</regex>. Set an alarm before they do.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Night 1–3</span><p>Crate or pen right next to your bed. They can smell you and it genuinely helps. Expect crying. Be boring and calm.</p></li><li><span class="when">Night 4–7</span><p>Take them out once or twice a night — quiet, no chat, straight back in. It is not playtime, however charming they are at 3am.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>Whining settles faster. If it starts at a fixed time, that's your cue to pre-empt it by ten minutes.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 3–4</span><p>Down to one night trip for many pups this age. Last water at about two hours before bed.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 6–8</span><p>Six or seven solid hours becomes possible as they approach four months. Wait for them at the crate, don't let them bolt out.</p></li><li><span class="when">Month 5–6</span><p>Sleeping through, most nights. Any regression usually means a growth spurt, a new tooth, or a very exciting day.</p></li><li><span class="when">Setback days</span><p>Go back one step, don't shout it. Puppies remember tone far longer than they remember rules.</p></li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><p class="card-title">Nights, Week by Week</p><p class="muted t-small">A rule of thumb: puppies can hold their bladder roughly one hour per month of age, plus one. So a two-month-old gets about three hours max, . Set an alarm before they do.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Night 1–3</span><p>Crate or pen right next to your bed. They can smell you and it genuinely helps. Expect crying. Be boring and calm.</p></li><li><span class="when">Night 4–7</span><p>Take them out once or twice a night — quiet, no chat, straight back in. It is not playtime, however charming they are at 3am.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>Whining settles faster. If it starts at a fixed time, that's your cue to pre-empt it by ten minutes.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 3–4</span><p>Down to one night trip for many pups this age. Last water at about two hours before bed.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 6–8</span><p>Six or seven solid hours becomes possible as they approach four months. Wait for them at the crate, don't let them bolt out.</p></li><li><span class="when">Month 5–6</span><p>Sleeping through, most nights. Any regression usually means a growth spurt, a new tooth, or a very exciting day.</p></li><li><span class="when">Setback days</span><p>Go back one step, don't shout it. Puppies remember tone far longer than they remember rules.</p></li></ul></div>	-
