li><span class="when">8:11 am</span><p>Shut-off valve under the sink turns with a satisfying squeak. Water stops.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">8:20 am</span><p>Neighbour lends a wet/dry vac. You owe them baked goods now.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">8:47 am</span><p>Floor dry. Legs a bit damp. Dignity slightly dented but recoverable.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Next day</span><p>Drain hose replaced. Disaster becomes a story.</p></li>    </ul>  </div></div>	-
13.19.0	timeline_view/Waiting for a Doctor's Appointment (from booking to waiting 	<div class="umi-page">  <div class="card">    <div class="card-title">Waiting for a Doctor's Appointment (from booking to waiting room)</div>    <p class="t-small dim">For <regex>user_name</regex> — the whole arc, so you know what's coming.</p>    <ul class="timeline">      <li><span class="when">Day 0</span><p>You call. The receptionist says "we can fit you in three weeks from Tuesday."</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>Confirmation text arrives. You screenshot it and file it under 'important for exactly one day.'</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Day 19</span><p>Reminder text: "Reply C to confirm." You type C like it's a sacred ritual.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Day 21, 8:00 am</span><p>You check in online. Print the form. Forget the form at home.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">8:30 am</span><p>Arrive. Waiting room chairs, 12 minutes of a home renovation show on mute.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">8:52 am</span><p>Name called. You stand up slightly too fast. Everyone watches.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">9:13 am</span><p>In the room. The paper crinkles under you. The doctor knocks and enters.</p></li>    </ul>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <div class="card">    <div class="card-title">Waiting for a Doctor's Appointment (from booking to waiting room)</div>    <p class="t-small dim">For  — the whole arc, so you know what's coming.</p>    <ul class="timeline">      <li><span class="when">Day 0</span><p>You call. The receptionist says "we can fit you in three weeks from Tuesday."</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>Confirmation text arrives. You screenshot it and file it under 'important for exactly one day.'</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Day 19</span><p>Reminder text: "Reply C to confirm." You type C like it's a sacred ritual.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Day 21, 8:00 am</span><p>You check in online. Print the form. Forget the form at home.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">8:30 am</span><p>Arrive. Waiting room chairs, 12 minutes of a home renovation show on mute.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">8:52 am</span><p>Name called. You stand up slightly too fast. Everyone watches.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">9:13 am</span><p>In the room. The paper crinkles under you. The doctor knocks and enters.</p></li>    </ul>  </div></div>	-
13.20.0	timeline_view/A Sourdough Starter, Day by Day (First 5 Days)	<div class="umi-page">  <div class="card">    <div class="card-title">A Sourdough Starter, Day by Day (First 5 Days)</div>    <p class="t-small dim">For <regex>user_name</regex> — feeding schedule and what to expect.</p>    <ul class="timeline">      <li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Mix 50g whole wheat flour + 50g water. Cover. Walk away.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>No bubbles. Looks like paste. This is normal. Do not panic.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>Feed: discard half, add 50g flour + 50g water. A few tiny bubbles appear. Hope.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Day 4</span><p>It smells like yogurt and regret. That's actually good. Feed again.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Day 5</span><p>It doubles in 6 hours. It smells like fresh bread. You have created life.</p></li>    </ul>    <div class="callout info">      <p>Feed every 12–24 hours once it's active. If you forget, it forgives you.</p>    </div>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <div class="card">    <div class="card-title">A Sourdough Starter, Day by Day (First 5 Days)</div>    <p class="t-small dim">For  — feeding schedule and what to expect.</p>    <ul class="timeline">      <li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Mix 50g whole wheat flour + 50g water. Cover. Walk away.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>No bubbles. Looks like paste. This is normal. Do not panic.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>Feed: discard half, add 50g flour + 50g water. A few tiny bubbles appear. Hope.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Day 4</span><p>It smells like yogurt and regret. That's actually good. Feed again.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Day 5</span><p>It doubles in 6 hours. It smells like fresh bread. You have created life.</p></li>    </ul>    <div class="callout info">      <p>Feed every 12–24 hours once it's active. If you forget, it forgives you.</p>    </div>  </div></div>	-
13.21.0	timeline_view/Repotting a Houseplant That Outgrew Its Pot	<div class="umi-page">  <div class="card">    <div class="card-title">Repotting a Houseplant That Outgrew Its Pot</div>    <p class="t-small dim">For <regex>user_name</regex> — the sequence, start to finish.</p>    <ul class="timeline">      <li><span class="when">The signs</span><p>Roots snaking out the drainage hole. Water runs straight through. A slight lean.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Prep</span><p>New pot 2 inches wider. Fresh potting mix. Old towel on the floor for the mess.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Water</span><p>Water the plant a day before. Dry roots snap; damp roots bend.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Unpot</span><p>Lay it on its side. Tap the pot. Slide it out gently. Roots might be a tight ball.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Loosen</span><p>Break up the root ball with your fingers. Don't be shy.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Replant</span><p>Fresh soil, same depth. Press down. Water until it drains out the bottom.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Aftercare</span><p>Keep it out of direct sun for a week. Expect a sulk. It'll bounce back.</p></li>    </ul>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <div class="card">    <div class="card-title">Repotting a Houseplant That Outgrew Its Pot</div>    <p class="t-small dim">For  — the sequence, start to finish.</p>    <ul class="timeline">      <li><span class="when">The signs</span><p>Roots snaking out the drainage hole. Water runs straight through. A slight lean.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Prep</span><p>New pot 2 inches wider. Fresh potting mix. Old towel on the floor for the mess.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Water</span><p>Water the plant a day before. Dry roots snap; damp roots bend.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Unpot</span><p>Lay it on its side. Tap the pot. Slide it out gently. Roots might be a tight ball.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Loosen</span><p>Break up the root ball with your fingers. Don't be shy.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Replant</span><p>Fresh soil, same depth. Press down. Water until it drains out the bottom.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Aftercare</span><p>Keep it out of direct sun for a week. Expect a sulk. It'll bounce back.</p></li>    </ul>  </div></div>	-
13.22.0	timeline_view/Learning to Ride a Bike as an Adult (First Session)	<div class="umi-page">  <div class="card">    <div class="card-title">Learning to Ride a Bike as an Adult (First Session)</div>    <p class="t-small dim">For <regex>user_name</regex> — a friendly sequence of small wins.</p>    <ul class="timeline">      <li><span class="when">Step 1</span><p>Lower the seat so both feet touch the ground. No shame.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Step 2</span><p>Walk the bike like a scooter. Push, glide, push. Feel the balance point.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Step 3</span><p>Once gliding feels easy, lift your feet for two seconds. Then four.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Step 4</span><p>Add the other pedal. Push once, then coast. This is the hard part.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Step 5</span><p>Pedal twice. You wobble. You laugh. You try again.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Step 6</span><p>Five pedal strokes in a row. The bike stays under you. That's it.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Step 7</span><p>End on a good one. Tired legs learn nothing.</p></li>    </ul>    <div class="callout good">      <p>Most adults need two or three sessions. Your body knows how; your brain just needs to trust it.</p>    </div>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <div class="card">    <div class="card-title">Learning to Ride a Bike as an Adult (First Session)</div>    <p class="t-small dim">For  — a friendly sequence of small wins.</p>    <ul class="timeline">      <li><span class="when">Step 1</span><p>Lower the seat so both feet touch the ground. No shame.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Step 2</span><p>Walk the bike like a scooter. Push, glide, push. Feel the balance point.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Step 3</span><p>Once gliding feels easy, lift your feet for two seconds. Then four.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Step 4</span><p>Add the other pedal. Push once, then coast. This is the hard part.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Step 5</span><p>Pedal twice. You wobble. You laugh. You try again.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Step 6</span><p>Five pedal strokes in a row. The bike stays under you. That's it.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Step 7</span><p>End on a good one. Tired legs learn nothing.</p></li>    </ul>    <div class="callout good">      <p>Most adults need two or three sessions. Your body knows how; your brain just needs to trust it.</p>    </div>  </div></div>	-
13.23.0	timeline_view/The Last 24 Hours Before a Big Move	<div class="umi-page">  <div class="card">    <div class="card-title">The Last 24 Hours Before a Big Move</div>    <p class="t-small dim">For <regex>user_name</regex> — when it's down to the wire.</p>    <ul class="timeline">      <li><span class="when">T-24h</span><p>Boxes are labeled. The kitchen is packed except for one mug and a spoon.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">T-18h</span><p>Friends arrive with pizza. You eat standing up because chairs are gone.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">T-12h</span><p>Last walk-through. You find a charger, two socks, and a note from your past self.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">T-8h</span><p>Decide to sleep. Toss and turn on a mattress with no sheets.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">T-2h</span><p>Moving truck idles outside. You do one final sweep.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">T-0</span><p>Keys on the counter. Door closes. You don't look back—except once.</p></li>    </ul>    <p class="t-small muted">Take a photo of the empty room. You'll want it someday.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <div class="card">    <div class="card-title">The Last 24 Hours Before a Big Move</div>    <p class="t-small dim">For  — when it's down to the wire.</p>    <ul class="timeline">      <li><span class="when">T-24h</span><p>Boxes are labeled. The kitchen is packed except for one mug and a spoon.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">T-18h</span><p>Friends arrive with pizza. You eat standing up because chairs are gone.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">T-12h</span><p>Last walk-through. You find a charger, two socks, and a note from your past self.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">T-8h</span><p>Decide to sleep. Toss and turn on a mattress with no sheets.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">T-2h</span><p>Moving truck idles outside. You do one final sweep.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">T-0</span><p>Keys on the counter. Door closes. You don't look back—except once.</p></li>    </ul>    <p class="t-small muted">Take a photo of the empty room. You'll want it someday.</p>  </div></div>	-
13.24.0	timeline_view/The Balcony Herb Box, Month by Month	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">The Balcony Herb Box, Month by Month</h2><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Week 0</span><p>Fill a 40 cm box with potting mix, leave 2 cm headroom. Basil, mint, and thyme go in as seedlings, not seeds — you want a head start.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>First real leaves. Pinch the basil tips above a leaf pair. The plant answers with two stems instead of one.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 5</span><p>Mint tries to escape the box. It always does. Sink a 15 cm pot rim-deep to jail the runners.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 9</span><p>Thyme starts to flop and go woody in the middle. Shear it back by a third — never into the bare brown wood.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 14</span><p>Basil bolts. Let one flower for the bees, cut the rest. The leaves turn peppery after flowering.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 20</span><p>Water every second evening in heat, mornings not noon. Feed half-strength every fortnight. That's the whole job.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info"><p><regex>user_name</regex>, your box is north-facing — swap basil for parsley and it'll be much happier.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">The Balcony Herb Box, Month by Month</h2><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Week 0</span><p>Fill a 40 cm box with potting mix, leave 2 cm headroom. Basil, mint, and thyme go in as seedlings, not seeds — you want a head start.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>First real leaves. Pinch the basil tips above a leaf pair. The plant answers with two stems instead of one.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 5</span><p>Mint tries to escape the box. It always does. Sink a 15 cm pot rim-deep to jail the runners.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 9</span><p>Thyme starts to flop and go woody in the middle. Shear it back by a third — never into the bare brown wood.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 14</span><p>Basil bolts. Let one flower for the bees, cut the rest. The leaves turn peppery after flowering.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 20</span><p>Water every second evening in heat, mornings not noon. Feed half-strength every fortnight. That's the whole job.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info"><p>, your box is north-facing — swap basil for parsley and it'll be much happier.</p></div></div>	-
13.25.0	timeline_view/Your First Month Back at the Gym	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Your First Month Back at the Gym</h2><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Go, but do half of what you think you can. Your ego remembers the old you; your tendons don't.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>Worst soreness. Walk, stretch, drink water. Do not "work through it" — that's how week one becomes month three off.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 7</span><p>Second session. Same weights as day one. Add one set, not one plate.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 14</span><p>Sleep improves first. Strength second. Mirror last. Be patient with the mirror.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 21</span><p>Habit forms around the time, not the workout. Protect the slot: same days, same hour.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 30</span><p>Now add weight — about 5% per lift per week. Log it, or you'll drift back to day one weights by accident.</p></li></ul><div class="callout warn"><p>If something hurts sharp and one-sided, stop. Dull and even is fine. Sharp and specific is a message.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Your First Month Back at the Gym</h2><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Go, but do half of what you think you can. Your ego remembers the old you; your tendons don't.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>Worst soreness. Walk, stretch, drink water. Do not "work through it" — that's how week one becomes month three off.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 7</span><p>Second session. Same weights as day one. Add one set, not one plate.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 14</span><p>Sleep improves first. Strength second. Mirror last. Be patient with the mirror.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 21</span><p>Habit forms around the time, not the workout. Protect the slot: same days, same hour.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 30</span><p>Now add weight — about 5% per lift per week. Log it, or you'll drift back to day one weights by accident.</p></li></ul><div class="callout warn"><p>If something hurts sharp and one-sided, stop. Dull and even is fine. Sharp and specific is a message.</p></div></div>	-
13.26.0	timeline_view/Composting in a Small Kitchen	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Composting in a Small Kitchen</h2><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 0</span><p>Get a 5 L countertop caddy with a lid and a carbon filter. Line the bottom with a sheet of newspaper. No filter, no peace.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>Empty it. Daily-ish in summer, every three days in winter. Fruit flies are a scheduling problem, not a moral one.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Balance rule: for every bowl of scraps, add a bowl of dry — shredded paper, torn cardboard, egg cartons.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>Wet and sour-smelling? Too much green. Add browns, turn it, and stop adding citrus peel in bulk.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 6</span><p>It warms in the middle when you dig in. That heat is the whole point — it means the pile is working.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 10</span><p>Crumbly, dark, smells like forest floor. That's finished compost. Screen out the avocado pits and use it.</p></li></ul><div class="callout good"><p>No garden? Give it to a neighbour with a balcony, or a community plot. Compost is a social good.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Composting in a Small Kitchen</h2><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 0</span><p>Get a 5 L countertop caddy with a lid and a carbon filter. Line the bottom with a sheet of newspaper. No filter, no peace.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>Empty it. Daily-ish in summer, every three days in winter. Fruit flies are a scheduling problem, not a moral one.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Balance rule: for every bowl of scraps, add a bowl of dry — shredded paper, torn cardboard, egg cartons.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>Wet and sour-smelling? Too much green. Add browns, turn it, and stop adding citrus peel in bulk.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 6</span><p>It warms in the middle when you dig in. That heat is the whole point — it means the pile is working.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 10</span><p>Crumbly, dark, smells like forest floor. That's finished compost. Screen out the avocado pits and use it.</p></li></ul><div class="callout good"><p>No garden? Give it to a neighbour with a balcony, or a community plot. Compost is a social good.</p></div></div>	-
13.27.0	timeline_view/The Cold That Won't Quit: Day by Day	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">The Cold That Won't Quit: Day by Day</h2><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 0</span><p>Scratchy throat. You'll swear it's allergies. It isn't. You're most contagious right now and don't feel it yet.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>Peak misery: blocked nose, sneezing, low fever. Rest, fluids, paracetamol if you need it. Antibiotics do nothing for a cold.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 4</span><p>Snot turns thick and yellow. This is normal, not "it's gone to my chest". Colour is not a verdict.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 7</span><p>You feel human-ish. You are still shedding virus. Stay out of the office for one more day if you can.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 10</span><p>Most colds are done here. If yours isn't, keep reading.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 14</span><p>Still coughing? A lingering dry cough can last three weeks. But fever returning, breathlessness, or pain in one ear means see a doctor.</p></li></ul><div class="callout warn"><p>That's the rule: symptoms worsening after day 10, or fever after day 5 — get it looked at.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">The Cold That Won't Quit: Day by Day</h2><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 0</span><p>Scratchy throat. You'll swear it's allergies. It isn't. You're most contagious right now and don't feel it yet.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>Peak misery: blocked nose, sneezing, low fever. Rest, fluids, paracetamol if you need it. Antibiotics do nothing for a cold.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 4</span><p>Snot turns thick and yellow. This is normal, not "it's gone to my chest". Colour is not a verdict.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 7</span><p>You feel human-ish. You are still shedding virus. Stay out of the office for one more day if you can.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 10</span><p>Most colds are done here. If yours isn't, keep reading.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 14</span><p>Still coughing? A lingering dry cough can last three weeks. But fever returning, breathlessness, or pain in one ear means see a doctor.</p></li></ul><div class="callout warn"><p>That's the rule: symptoms worsening after day 10, or fever after day 5 — get it looked at.</p></div></div>	-
13.28.0	timeline_view/Learning to Swim Front Crawl as an Adult	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Learning to Swim Front Crawl as an Adult</h2><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Session 1</span><p>Face in the water, blow bubbles, float on your front holding a wall. Sounds silly. It's the whole foundation.</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 3</span><p>Kick with a board, face down, turning to breathe every three kicks. You'll drink some water. That's the tuition.</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 6</span><p>Add one arm at a time with a pull buoy between your legs. Legs out of the equation means you can think about arms.</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 10</span><p>Full stroke for 25 m. It'll feel frantic. Count your strokes — anything over 30 per length means you're fighting the water.</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 15</span><p>Bilateral breathing: every third stroke. Awkward for a fortnight, then suddenly not.</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 20</span><p>100 m without stopping. Slow is fine. Slow and continuous beats fast and gasping, every time.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info"><p><regex>user_name</regex>, front crawl is mostly exhale. Breathe out underwater, and the inhale takes care of itself.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Learning to Swim Front Crawl as an Adult</h2><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Session 1</span><p>Face in the water, blow bubbles, float on your front holding a wall. Sounds silly. It's the whole foundation.</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 3</span><p>Kick with a board, face down, turning to breathe every three kicks. You'll drink some water. That's the tuition.</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 6</span><p>Add one arm at a time with a pull buoy between your legs. Legs out of the equation means you can think about arms.</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 10</span><p>Full stroke for 25 m. It'll feel frantic. Count your strokes — anything over 30 per length means you're fighting the water.</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 15</span><p>Bilateral breathing: every third stroke. Awkward for a fortnight, then suddenly not.</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 20</span><p>100 m without stopping. Slow is fine. Slow and continuous beats fast and gasping, every time.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info"><p>, front crawl is mostly exhale. Breathe out underwater, and the inhale takes care of itself.</p></div></div>	-
13.29.0	timeline_view/Reupholstering a Second-Hand Armchair	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Reupholstering a Second-Hand Armchair</h2><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Hour 0</span><p>Photograph every seam before you touch a staple. Future-you will be very grateful to past-you.</p></li><li><span class="when">Hour 1</span><p>Strip it. Keep the old fabric pieces — they're your pattern. Label them with tape: left arm, right arm, back.</p></li><li><span class="when">Hour 3</span><p>Inspect the frame and webbing. Reglue loose joints now, not after it's covered.</p></li><li><span class="when">Hour 5</span><p>Replace or top up the foam. This is what makes it feel new; skip it and you've just changed the colour.</p></li><li><span class="when">Hour 8</span><p>Cut new fabric with 5 cm extra all round. Staple the seat first, working from the centre outward to avoid ripples.</p></li><li><span class="when">Hour 12</span><p>Arms, then back, then the trim and dust cover underneath. Stand back. Admire. Sit down carefully, just in case.</p></li></ul><div class="callout good"><p>Budget roughly double the hours you first guessed. Everyone does.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Reupholstering a Second-Hand Armchair</h2><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Hour 0</span><p>Photograph every seam before you touch a staple. Future-you will be very grateful to past-you.</p></li><li><span class="when">Hour 1</span><p>Strip it. Keep the old fabric pieces — they're your pattern. Label them with tape: left arm, right arm, back.</p></li><li><span class="when">Hour 3</span><p>Inspect the frame and webbing. Reglue loose joints now, not after it's covered.</p></li><li><span class="when">Hour 5</span><p>Replace or top up the foam. This is what makes it feel new; skip it and you've just changed the colour.</p></li><li><span class="when">Hour 8</span><p>Cut new fabric with 5 cm extra all round. Staple the seat first, working from the centre outward to avoid ripples.</p></li><li><span class="when">Hour 12</span><p>Arms, then back, then the trim and dust cover underneath. Stand back. Admire. Sit down carefully, just in case.</p></li></ul><div class="callout good"><p>Budget roughly double the hours you first guessed. Everyone does.</p></div></div>	-
13.30.0	timeline_view/Three Weeks of Learning to Whistle	<div class="stack"><h3 class="card-title">Three Weeks of Learning to Whistle</h3><p class="muted t-small">Puckered lips, dry mouth, zero dignity. Here's how it went, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Made a sound. It was air. That counts as a start.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>First real note, high and squeaky, lasted half a second.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 7</span><p>Could hold a steady tone for three seconds. Mouth went numb.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 12</span><p>Found the sweet spot: tongue back, lips relaxed, tiny hole. Whole scale-ish thing appeared.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 18</span><p>First tune. Badly. Something recognisable anyway.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 21</span><p>Whistled along to the radio without thinking about it. That's the milestone, honestly.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Dry lips whistle better. Also: practice somewhere people can't hear you fail.</p></div></div>	<div class="stack"><h3 class="card-title">Three Weeks of Learning to Whistle</h3><p class="muted t-small">Puckered lips, dry mouth, zero dignity. Here's how it went, .</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Made a sound. It was air. That counts as a start.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>First real note, high and squeaky, lasted half a second.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 7</span><p>Could hold a steady tone for three seconds. Mouth went numb.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 12</span><p>Found the sweet spot: tongue back, lips relaxed, tiny hole. Whole scale-ish thing appeared.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 18</span><p>First tune. Badly. Something recognisable anyway.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 21</span><p>Whistled along to the radio without thinking about it. That's the milestone, honestly.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Dry lips whistle better. Also: practice somewhere people can't hear you fail.</p></div></div>	-
13.31.0	timeline_view/A Week of Relearning How to Cook Rice	<div class="stack"><h3 class="card-title">A Week of Relearning How to Cook Rice</h3><p class="muted t-small">Seven days, seven pots. Here's the honest log, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Mon</span><p>Rinsed it. Once. Glue, basically. Ate it anyway.</p></li><li><span class="when">Tue</span><p>Rinsed until the water ran clear, about five changes. Massive improvement.</p></li><li><span class="when">Wed</span><p>Tried the finger-knuckle water method. Knuckle sizes vary. Mine lied.</p></li><li><span class="when">Thu</span><p>Weighed it: 1 part rice, 1.5 parts water. Boring, correct.</p></li><li><span class="when">Fri</span><p>Lid on, lowest heat, 12 minutes, then 10 minutes off the heat. No peeking.</p></li><li><span class="when">Sat</span><p>Fork fluff, not spoon stir. Stopped the mush.</p></li><li><span class="when">Sun</span><p>Perfect grains. Same pot. Only the method changed.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>The one rule that fixed everything</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Never lift the lid while it steams. Every peek costs you steam, and steam is the whole trick.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="stack"><h3 class="card-title">A Week of Relearning How to Cook Rice</h3><p class="muted t-small">Seven days, seven pots. Here's the honest log, .</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Mon</span><p>Rinsed it. Once. Glue, basically. Ate it anyway.</p></li><li><span class="when">Tue</span><p>Rinsed until the water ran clear, about five changes. Massive improvement.</p></li><li><span class="when">Wed</span><p>Tried the finger-knuckle water method. Knuckle sizes vary. Mine lied.</p></li><li><span class="when">Thu</span><p>Weighed it: 1 part rice, 1.5 parts water. Boring, correct.</p></li><li><span class="when">Fri</span><p>Lid on, lowest heat, 12 minutes, then 10 minutes off the heat. No peeking.</p></li><li><span class="when">Sat</span><p>Fork fluff, not spoon stir. Stopped the mush.</p></li><li><span class="when">Sun</span><p>Perfect grains. Same pot. Only the method changed.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>The one rule that fixed everything</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Never lift the lid while it steams. Every peek costs you steam, and steam is the whole trick.</p></div></details></div>	-
13.32.0	timeline_view/The Month I Learned to Drive Stick	<div class="stack"><h3 class="card-title">The Month I Learned to Drive Stick</h3><p class="muted t-small">Four weeks of stalling at junctions, <regex>user_name</regex>. It does click.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Stalled eleven times in a car park. Learned the clutch bites about two-thirds of the way up.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>Found the trick: hold the bite point, add a little gas, count to two, then release. Fewer stalls.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>Hill starts. Rolled back once, into nothing, but the shame was real.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 4</span><p>Shifted up through gears without watching the stick. My hands just knew.</p></li><li><span class="when">End of month</span><p>Drove a full journey in traffic. Forgot I was doing anything unusual.</p></li></ul><div class="callout good"><p class="t-small">Stalling is not failure. It's your foot learning where the bite point lives.</p></div></div>	<div class="stack"><h3 class="card-title">The Month I Learned to Drive Stick</h3><p class="muted t-small">Four weeks of stalling at junctions, . It does click.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Stalled eleven times in a car park. Learned the clutch bites about two-thirds of the way up.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>Found the trick: hold the bite point, add a little gas, count to two, then release. Fewer stalls.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>Hill starts. Rolled back once, into nothing, but the shame was real.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 4</span><p>Shifted up through gears without watching the stick. My hands just knew.</p></li><li><span class="when">End of month</span><p>Drove a full journey in traffic. Forgot I was doing anything unusual.</p></li></ul><div class="callout good"><p class="t-small">Stalling is not failure. It's your foot learning where the bite point 