13.35.0	timeline_view/Nine Months of Learning Basic Plumbing	<div class="stack"><h3 class="card-title">Nine Months of Learning Basic Plumbing</h3><p class="muted t-small">Slow, damp, satisfying work, <regex>user_name</regex>. Here's the honest arc.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Month 1</span><p>Learned where the stopcock is. That alone was worth it.</p></li><li><span class="when">Month 2</span><p>Changed a washer in a dripping tap. Took an hour. No more drip.</p></li><li><span class="when">Month 3</span><p>Learned to use PTFE tape properly. Tighten, don't over-tighten.</p></li><li><span class="when">Month 5</span><p>Replaced a flexible tap connector. Only minor panic.</p></li><li><span class="when">Month 7</span><p>Unblocked a slow trap with a bucket and patience.</p></li><li><span class="when">Month 9</span><p>Fixed a running toilet cistern. Confidence is now real.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Water off first, bucket always, and know when a job belongs to a professional.</p></div></div>	<div class="stack"><h3 class="card-title">Nine Months of Learning Basic Plumbing</h3><p class="muted t-small">Slow, damp, satisfying work, . Here's the honest arc.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Month 1</span><p>Learned where the stopcock is. That alone was worth it.</p></li><li><span class="when">Month 2</span><p>Changed a washer in a dripping tap. Took an hour. No more drip.</p></li><li><span class="when">Month 3</span><p>Learned to use PTFE tape properly. Tighten, don't over-tighten.</p></li><li><span class="when">Month 5</span><p>Replaced a flexible tap connector. Only minor panic.</p></li><li><span class="when">Month 7</span><p>Unblocked a slow trap with a bucket and patience.</p></li><li><span class="when">Month 9</span><p>Fixed a running toilet cistern. Confidence is now real.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Water off first, bucket always, and know when a job belongs to a professional.</p></div></div>	-
13.36.0	timeline_view/Learning to Juggle Three Balls: First Week	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Juggle Log: Week One</h3><p class="t-small muted">Throws, drops, and small victories. Tossed in order.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>One ball, hand to hand. Twenty perfect tosses before you drop it. <span class="badge">start</span></p></li><li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>Two balls. The second throw always goes sideways. That is normal. Aim for ten catches in a row.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>Two balls, eyes closed for one beat. You feel the rhythm. Still dropping every fifth throw.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 4</span><p>First three-ball attempt. You throw all three, catch zero. Laugh. Try again.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 5</span><p>Three balls, one full cycle. Throw, throw, throw, catch, catch, drop. Progress.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 6</span><p>Two cycles. Six throws. The third ball no longer feels like a stranger.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 7</span><p>Ten catches without moving your feet. You are officially juggling. Badly, but officially.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small"><strong>Umi's note:</strong> Practice over a bed or couch. Saves your back and your balls.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Juggle Log: Week One</h3><p class="t-small muted">Throws, drops, and small victories. Tossed in order.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>One ball, hand to hand. Twenty perfect tosses before you drop it. <span class="badge">start</span></p></li><li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>Two balls. The second throw always goes sideways. That is normal. Aim for ten catches in a row.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>Two balls, eyes closed for one beat. You feel the rhythm. Still dropping every fifth throw.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 4</span><p>First three-ball attempt. You throw all three, catch zero. Laugh. Try again.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 5</span><p>Three balls, one full cycle. Throw, throw, throw, catch, catch, drop. Progress.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 6</span><p>Two cycles. Six throws. The third ball no longer feels like a stranger.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 7</span><p>Ten catches without moving your feet. You are officially juggling. Badly, but officially.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small"><strong>Umi's note:</strong> Practice over a bed or couch. Saves your back and your balls.</p></div></div></div>	-
13.37.0	timeline_view/A Week of Making Your Own Yoghurt	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Yoghurt: Days 1–7</h3><p class="muted t-small">Milk, culture, warmth, patience. Mostly patience.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Heat 1L milk to 82°C. Cool to 43°C. Whisk in 2 tbsp live yoghurt. Pour into jars.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>Incubate 8 hours in a warm spot (oven with light on). Check at hour 6. Still liquid. Wait.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>It set! Tangy, thick. Refrigerate. Save 2 tbsp as starter for next batch.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 4</span><p>Batch two. Same method, but you forgot to cool the milk enough. Killed the culture. Try again.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 5</span><p>Batch two, take two. Perfect set. You are now a person with a yoghurt routine.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 6</span><p>Strain half through muslin for Greek style. Thick, creamy, gone by lunch.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 7</span><p>Flavour experiments: honey, vanilla, a swirl of jam. You will never buy tubs again.</p></li></ul><div class="callout good"><p class="t-small">Your starter is alive. Feed it milk every week or freeze it for a month off.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Yoghurt: Days 1–7</h3><p class="muted t-small">Milk, culture, warmth, patience. Mostly patience.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Heat 1L milk to 82°C. Cool to 43°C. Whisk in 2 tbsp live yoghurt. Pour into jars.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>Incubate 8 hours in a warm spot (oven with light on). Check at hour 6. Still liquid. Wait.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>It set! Tangy, thick. Refrigerate. Save 2 tbsp as starter for next batch.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 4</span><p>Batch two. Same method, but you forgot to cool the milk enough. Killed the culture. Try again.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 5</span><p>Batch two, take two. Perfect set. You are now a person with a yoghurt routine.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 6</span><p>Strain half through muslin for Greek style. Thick, creamy, gone by lunch.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 7</span><p>Flavour experiments: honey, vanilla, a swirl of jam. You will never buy tubs again.</p></li></ul><div class="callout good"><p class="t-small">Your starter is alive. Feed it milk every week or freeze it for a month off.</p></div></div></div>	-
13.38.0	timeline_view/Training a Parrot to Step Up: First Month	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Step Up: Month One</h3><p class="t-small muted">Trust falls, feathers, and millet. Mostly millet.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Just sit near the cage. Talk softly. Offer millet through the bars. No touching.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>Hand inside the cage, holding millet. Let the parrot come to you. It will, eventually.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>First touch. Beak nudges your finger. Do not flinch. Praise quietly.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 4</span><p>Step up onto a perch held in your hand. Treat immediately. Repeat ten times.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 5</span><p>Step up onto bare finger. It feels like a tiny warm velcro grip. Do not pull away.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 6</span><p>Step down on command. Two-way traffic now. You are a bird taxi.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 7</span><p>Step up from the cage door, no treat needed. The trust is the treat.</p></li></ul><div class="callout warn"><p class="t-small">Never grab. One bad grab sets you back two weeks. Ask <regex>user_name</regex> to be patient.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Step Up: Month One</h3><p class="t-small muted">Trust falls, feathers, and millet. Mostly millet.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Just sit near the cage. Talk softly. Offer millet through the bars. No touching.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>Hand inside the cage, holding millet. Let the parrot come to you. It will, eventually.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>First touch. Beak nudges your finger. Do not flinch. Praise quietly.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 4</span><p>Step up onto a perch held in your hand. Treat immediately. Repeat ten times.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 5</span><p>Step up onto bare finger. It feels like a tiny warm velcro grip. Do not pull away.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 6</span><p>Step down on command. Two-way traffic now. You are a bird taxi.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 7</span><p>Step up from the cage door, no treat needed. The trust is the treat.</p></li></ul><div class="callout warn"><p class="t-small">Never grab. One bad grab sets you back two weeks. Ask  to be patient.</p></div></div></div>	-
13.39.0	timeline_view/The Month I Ate Only What I Grew	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Garden-to-Table: 30 Days</h3><p class="muted t-small">An honest log of dirt, hunger, and surprising joy.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Salad, radishes, herbs. You feel virtuous. Also hungry by 3pm.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>Zucchini arrives. You eat zucchini every meal. You dream of zucchini. You resent zucchini.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>Tomatoes ripen. A turning point. Caprese salad becomes a food group.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 4</span><p>Beans, kale, and a single perfect cucumber. You trade zucchini with a neighbour for eggs.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 25</span><p>You realise you have not bought vegetables in three weeks. The grocery bill is tiny.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 28</span><p>You make a meal entirely from the garden. It tastes like pride and soil.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 30</span><p>Reflection: you will never take a supermarket tomato for granted again. Also, never plant six zucchini.</p></li></ul><div class="callout good"><p class="t-small">The biggest lesson: grow what you actually eat, not what looks good in the seed catalogue.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Garden-to-Table: 30 Days</h3><p class="muted t-small">An honest log of dirt, hunger, and surprising joy.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Salad, radishes, herbs. You feel virtuous. Also hungry by 3pm.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>Zucchini arrives. You eat zucchini every meal. You dream of zucchini. You resent zucchini.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>Tomatoes ripen. A turning point. Caprese salad becomes a food group.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 4</span><p>Beans, kale, and a single perfect cucumber. You trade zucchini with a neighbour for eggs.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 25</span><p>You realise you have not bought vegetables in three weeks. The grocery bill is tiny.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 28</span><p>You make a meal entirely from the garden. It tastes like pride and soil.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 30</span><p>Reflection: you will never take a supermarket tomato for granted again. Also, never plant six zucchini.</p></li></ul><div class="callout good"><p class="t-small">The biggest lesson: grow what you actually eat, not what looks good in the seed catalogue.</p></div></div></div>	-
13.40.0	timeline_view/Learning to Solder: First Five Sessions	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Soldering: Five Sessions</h3><p class="t-small muted">Heat, flux, and the occasional burnt finger. Progress, not perfection.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Session 1</span><p>Learn to tin the iron. Watch solder melt like mercury. Burn your thumb on the barrel. Ouch.</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 2</span><p>First joints on a scrap board. They look like grey blobs. But they conduct.</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 3</span><p>Cold joint diagnosis. You learn that a shiny cone is good, a dull ball is bad.</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 4</span><p>Wire-to-wire splice. It holds. You tug it. It still holds. Small victory dance.</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 5</span><p>You repair a broken headphone cable. It works. You feel like an engineer.</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 6</span><p>Desoldering practice. Sucking up old joints with a pump. Surprisingly satisfying.</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 7</span><p>You solder a simple LED circuit. It lights up. You have made light from metal and heat.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Good ventilation is not optional. Fumes are the one thing you cannot practice away.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Soldering: Five Sessions</h3><p class="t-small muted">Heat, flux, and the occasional burnt finger. Progress, not perfection.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Session 1</span><p>Learn to tin the iron. Watch solder melt like mercury. Burn your thumb on the barrel. Ouch.</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 2</span><p>First joints on a scrap board. They look like grey blobs. But they conduct.</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 3</span><p>Cold joint diagnosis. You learn that a shiny cone is good, a dull ball is bad.</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 4</span><p>Wire-to-wire splice. It holds. You tug it. It still holds. Small victory dance.</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 5</span><p>You repair a broken headphone cable. It works. You feel like an engineer.</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 6</span><p>Desoldering practice. Sucking up old joints with a pump. Surprisingly satisfying.</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 7</span><p>You solder a simple LED circuit. It lights up. You have made light from metal and heat.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Good ventilation is not optional. Fumes are the one thing you cannot practice away.</p></div></div></div>	-
13.41.0	timeline_view/A Week of Cold Showers: Morning by Morning	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Cold Shower: Seven Mornings</h3><p class="muted t-small">A log of gasps, goosebumps, and a strange new alertness.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Monday</span><p>Thirty seconds. It feels like a thousand needles. You yell. You survive.</p></li><li><span class="when">Tuesday</span><p>Forty-five seconds. Still awful. But you remember to breathe out slowly.</p></li><li><span class="when">Wednesday</span><p>One minute. The gasp is shorter. Your skin feels electric afterwards.</p></li><li><span class="when">Thursday</span><p>One minute, thirty. You start to notice the warmth after you step out. It is glorious.</p></li><li><span class="when">Friday</span><p>Two minutes. You no longer dread it. You still do not enjoy it. But you do it.</p></li><li><span class="when">Saturday</span><p>Two minutes, twenty. You sing a song in your head. It helps. Slightly.</p></li><li><span class="when">Sunday</span><p>Three minutes. For the first time, you feel calm in the cold. Then it is over. You are awake.</p></li></ul><div class="callout good"><p class="t-small">The hardest part is the first ten seconds. After that, it is just weather.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Cold Shower: Seven Mornings</h3><p class="muted t-small">A log of gasps, goosebumps, and a strange new alertness.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Monday</span><p>Thirty seconds. It feels like a thousand needles. You yell. You survive.</p></li><li><span class="when">Tuesday</span><p>Forty-five seconds. Still awful. But you remember to breathe out slowly.</p></li><li><span class="when">Wednesday</span><p>One minute. The gasp is shorter. Your skin feels electric afterwards.</p></li><li><span class="when">Thursday</span><p>One minute, thirty. You start to notice the warmth after you step out. It is glorious.</p></li><li><span class="when">Friday</span><p>Two minutes. You no longer dread it. You still do not enjoy it. But you do it.</p></li><li><span class="when">Saturday</span><p>Two minutes, twenty. You sing a song in your head. It helps. Slightly.</p></li><li><span class="when">Sunday</span><p>Three minutes. For the first time, you feel calm in the cold. Then it is over. You are awake.</p></li></ul><div class="callout good"><p class="t-small">The hardest part is the first ten seconds. After that, it is just weather.</p></div></div></div>	-
13.42.0	timeline_view/Learning to Braid Hair: My First Four Tries	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Braiding: first four attempts</h3><p class="muted t-small">Each try got a little less lopsided. Here's the honest log, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Attempt 1</span><p>Simple three-strand. Ended up with a lumpy rope and a sore scalp. <span class="badge warn"><i class="dot"></i>tangled</span></p></li><li><span class="when">Attempt 2</span><p>Watched a video first. Held sections between fingers instead of fists. Tension still uneven.</p></li><li><span class="when">Attempt 3</span><p>Started lower, at the nape. Much easier to see. Braid held all afternoon.</p></li><li><span class="when">Attempt 4</span><p>Added a fourth strand. Slow, but recognisably a braid. Wore it out. <span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>progress</span></p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>What actually helped</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Practise on damp hair, not wet.</li><li>Look in a mirror set slightly to the side.</li><li>Stop when your arms ache, not when it looks perfect.</li></ul></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Braiding: first four attempts</h3><p class="muted t-small">Each try got a little less lopsided. Here's the honest log, .</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Attempt 1</span><p>Simple three-strand. Ended up with a lumpy rope and a sore scalp. <span class="badge warn"><i class="dot"></i>tangled</span></p></li><li><span class="when">Attempt 2</span><p>Watched a video first. Held sections between fingers instead of fists. Tension still uneven.</p></li><li><span class="when">Attempt 3</span><p>Started lower, at the nape. Much easier to see. Braid held all afternoon.</p></li><li><span class="when">Attempt 4</span><p>Added a fourth strand. Slow, but recognisably a braid. Wore it out. <span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>progress</span></p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>What actually helped</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Practise on damp hair, not wet.</li><li>Look in a mirror set slightly to the side.</li><li>Stop when your arms ache, not when it looks perfect.</li></ul></div></details></div>	-
13.43.0	timeline_view/Breaking In New Hiking Boots: A Four-Week Log	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Boot break-in, week by week</h3><p class="muted t-small">Leather boots, 4 weeks in. Blister count trending down.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>20 min walks only. Heels rubbed raw. <span class="badge bad"><i class="dot"></i>2 blisters</span> Wore thick socks and taped hot spots.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>45 min walks. Left boot creasing nicely, right still stiff. No new blisters.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>First proper hill. Felt solid. One small rub on the right heel after 90 min.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 4</span><p>4-hour hike, zero issues. Leather softened and moulded. <span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>broken in</span></p></li></ul><div class="callout info">Boots that still hurt after 40+ miles might just be the wrong shape for your feet. Not every boot can be broken in.</div></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Boot break-in, week by week</h3><p class="muted t-small">Leather boots, 4 weeks in. Blister count trending down.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>20 min walks only. Heels rubbed raw. <span class="badge bad"><i class="dot"></i>2 blisters</span> Wore thick socks and taped hot spots.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>45 min walks. Left boot creasing nicely, right still stiff. No new blisters.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>First proper hill. Felt solid. One small rub on the right heel after 90 min.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 4</span><p>4-hour hike, zero issues. Leather softened and moulded. <span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>broken in</span></p></li></ul><div class="callout info">Boots that still hurt after 40+ miles might just be the wrong shape for your feet. Not every boot can be broken in.</div></div>	-
13.44.0	timeline_view/Learning to Make Proper Espresso at Home	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Dialling in espresso: first week</h3><p class="muted t-small">Same beans, same machine, one variable at a time.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Shot gushed through in 12s. Sour and thin. Grind too coarse. <span class="badge warn"><i class="dot"></i>sour</span></p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>Went finer. 20s, still sour but thicker. Dosing 18g, yield 40g.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 5</span><p>Found the sweet spot: 27s, 36g out. Sweet, balanced, no channeling. <span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>on target</span></p></li><li><span class="when">Day 7</span><p>Switched beans and started over. At least now I know the drill.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>What I'd tell a beginner</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Change one thing at a time.</li><li>Weigh your output, every time.</li><li>Taste, don't just time.</li></ul></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Dialling in espresso: first week</h3><p class="muted t-small">Same beans, same machine, one variable at a time.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Shot gushed through in 12s. Sour and thin. Grind too coarse. <span class="badge warn"><i class="dot"></i>sour</span></p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>Went finer. 20s, still sour but thicker. Dosing 18g, yield 40g.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 5</span><p>Found the sweet spot: 27s, 36g out. Sweet, balanced, no channeling. <span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>on target</span></p></li><li><span class="when">Day 7</span><p>Switched beans and started over. At least now I know the drill.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>What I'd tell a beginner</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Change one thing at a time.</li><li>Weigh your output, every time.</li><li>Taste, don't just time.</li></ul></div></details></div>	-
13.45.0	timeline_view/Decluttering the Kitchen Drawer: An Afternoon	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">The junk drawer, sorted</h3><p class="muted t-small">One drawer, 90 minutes, four small victories.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">1:00</span><p>Emptied everything onto the counter. More batteries than I expected.</p></li><li><span class="when">1:20</span><p>Sorted into keep, relocate, and bin. Rubber bands everywhere.</p></li><li><span class="when">1:45</span><p>Bought nothing. Found an old takeaway menu from a place that closed. <span class="badge info"><i class="dot"></i>nostalgia</span></p></li><li><span class="when">2:30</span><p>Put it all back with dividers. Still one mystery key. <span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>done</span></p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>What to keep</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>One tape measure, one pen that works.</li><li>Spare keys, labelled.</li><li>Not 14 half-empty lighters.</li></ul></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">The junk drawer, sorted</h3><p class="muted t-small">One drawer, 90 minutes, four small victories.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">1:00</span><p>Emptied everything onto the counter. More batteries than I expected.</p></li><li><span class="when">1:20</span><p>Sorted into keep, relocate, and bin. Rubber bands everywhere.</p></li><li><span class="when">1:45</span><p>Bought nothing. Found an old takeaway menu from a place that closed. <span class="badge info"><i class="dot"></i>nostalgia</span></p></li><li><span class="when">2:30</span><p>Put it all back with dividers. Still one mystery key. <span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>done</span></p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>What to keep</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>One tape measure, one pen that works.</li><li>Spare keys, labelled.</li><li>Not 14 half-empty lighters.</li></ul></div></details></div>	-
13.46.0	timeline_view/Training a Kitten to Use a Scratching Post	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Kitten vs scratching post</h3><p class="muted t-small"><regex>user_name</regex>, the sofa was winning until week three.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Post ignored. Sofa corner shredded. <span class="badge bad"><i class="dot"></i>sofa 1, post 0</span></p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>Moved post next to sofa. Rubbed catnip on it. Curiosity piqued. One claw, then two.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>Caught her using it unprompted. Rewarded with a treat immediately. <span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>breakthrough</span></p></li><li><span class="when">Week 4</span><p>Sofa mostly spared. Post is now her morning stretch spot.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info">Never punish scratching. Redirect to the post and reward. They learn what works.</div></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Kitten vs scratching post</h3><p class="muted t-small">, the sofa was winning until week three.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Post ignored. Sofa corner shredded. <span class="badge bad"><i class="dot"></i>sofa 1, post 0</span></p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>Moved post next to sofa. Rubbed catnip on it. Curiosity piqued. One claw, then two.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>Caught her using it unprompted. Rewarded with a treat immediately. <span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>breakthrough</span></p></li><li><span class="when">Week 4</span><p>Sofa mostly spared. Post is now her morning stretch spot.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info">Never punish scratching. Redirect to the post and reward. They learn what works.</div></div>	-
13.47.0	timeline_view/Learning to Change a Bike Tyre: First Solo Attempt	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">First solo tyre change</h3><p class="muted t-small">Rear wheel, one puncture, 40 minutes, minimal swearing.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Step 1</span><p>Removed wheel. Forgot the chain was in the way. Wiped hands. <span class="badge warn"><i class="dot"></i>learning</span></p></li><li><span class="when">Step 2</span><p>Used tyre levers to pop the bead. Found the thorn still in the tyre. Kept it as evidence.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 3</span><p>Fitted new tube, seated the tyre by hand. Inflated slowly to avoid pinching.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 4</span><p>Wheel back on, chain on, spun freely. <span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>fixed</span> Rode home.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Tools I actually used</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Tyre levers x2</li><li>Spare inner tube</li><li>Mini pump</li><li>Clean rag (essential)</li></ul></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">First solo tyre change</h3><p class="muted t-small">Rear wheel, one puncture, 40 minutes, minimal swearing.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Step 1</span><p>Removed wheel. Forgot the chain was in the way. Wiped hands. <span class="badge warn"><i class="dot"></i>learning</span></p></li><li><span class="when">Step 2</span><p>Used tyre levers to pop the bead. Found the thorn still in the tyre. Kept it as evidence.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 3</span><p>Fitted new tube, seated the tyre by hand. Inflated slowly to avoid pinching.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 4</span><p>Wheel back on, chain on, spun freely. <span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>fixed</span> Rode home.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Tools I actually used</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Tyre levers x2</li><li>Spare inner tube</li><li>Mini pump</li><li>Clean rag (essential)</li></ul></div></details></div>	-
13.48.0	timeline_view/Building a Blanket Fort That Actually Stays Up	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Building a Blanket Fort That Actually Stays Up</h2><p class="muted">Good forts are mostly engineering, a little romance. Here's the order that works, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Step 1</span><p>Pick the corner. Two walls do half the work before you lift a single blanket.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 2</span><p>Anchor the frame. Chairs, a sofa back, or a clothes horse. Weight beats tension.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 3</span><p>Drape the big sheet first, then tuck the edges under cushions. Loose edges are the enemy.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 4</span><p>Add the roof last. A flat sheet pulled taut between two high points sags less than you'd think.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 5</span><p>Test by crawling in. If the ceiling grazes your hair, lower the entry or raise the roof.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Quick fixes when it caves in</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Pillows at the corners as ballast.</li><li>A broom handle as a ridge pole across the top.</li><li>Clips instead of knots, so you can adjust without unmaking the whole thing.</li></ul></div></details><div class="callout info"><p>Lights inside matter more than size outside. A string of fairy lights or a lamp with a scarf over it turns a wobbly fort into a room.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Building a Blanket Fort That Actually Stays Up</h2><p class="muted">Good forts are mostly engineering, a little romance. Here's the order that works, .</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Step 1</span><p>Pick the corner. Two walls do half the work before you lift a single blanket.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 2</span><p>Anchor the frame. Chairs, a sofa back, or a clothes horse. Weight beats tension.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 3</span><p>Drape the big sheet first, then tuck the edges under cushions. Loose edges are the enemy.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 4</span><p>Add the roof last. A flat sheet pulled taut between two high points sags less than you'd think.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 5</span><p>Test by crawling in. If the ceiling grazes your hair, lower the entry or raise the roof.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Quick fixes when it caves in</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Pillows at the corners as ballast.</li><li>A broom handle as a ridge pole across the top.</li><li>Clips instead of knots, so you can adjust without unmaking the whole thing.</li></ul></div></details><div class="callout info"><p>Lights inside matter more than size outside. A string of fairy lights or a lamp with a scarf over it turns a wobbly fort into a room.</p></div></div></div>	-
13.49.0	timeline_view/Making Cold Brew Coffee Overnight	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Making Cold Brew Coffee Overnight</h2><p class="muted">Cold brew is just patience and a jar. Start tonight, drink tomorrow, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Step 1</span><p>Coarse grind. Roughly 1 part coffee to 4 parts water for concentrate, 1 to 8 for ready-to-drink.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 2</span><p>Stir once so all grounds get wet. No need to stir again.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 3</span><p>Cover and leave on the counter 12 to 18 hours. Fridge works too, just slower.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 4</span><p>Strain through a fine sieve or cloth. Don't squeeze the grounds unless you like bitterness.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 5</span><p>Keep the concentrate in the fridge up to a week. Dilute with water or milk as you go.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>What coffee works best</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted">Medium or dark roasts hold up. Light roasts can taste grassy over long steeps. Any cheap pre-ground is fine if it's coarse enough.</p></div></details><div class="callout good"><p>Cold brew has less acid than hot coffee, so it's gentler on your stomach and easier to make in bulk for the week.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Making Cold Brew Coffee Overnight</h2><p class="muted">Cold brew is just patience and a jar. Start tonight, drink tomorrow, .</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Step 1</span><p>Coarse grind. Roughly 1 part coffee to 4 parts water for concentrate, 1 to 8 for ready-to-drink.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 2</span><p>Stir once so all grounds get wet. No need to stir again.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 3</span><p>Cover and leave on the counter 12 to 18 hours. Fridge works too, just slower.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 4</span><p>Strain through a fine sieve or cloth. Don't squeeze the grounds unless you like bitterness.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 5</span><p>Keep the concentrate in the fridge up to a week. Dilute with water or milk as you go.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>What coffee works best</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted">Medium or dark roasts hold up. Light roasts can taste grassy over long steeps. Any cheap pre-ground is fine if it's coarse enough.</p></div></details><div class="callout good"><p>Cold brew has less acid than hot coffee, so it's gentler on your stomach and easier to make in bulk for the week.</p></div></div></div>	-
13.50.0	timeline_view/Learning to Juggle Three Balls in a Weekend	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Learning to Juggle Three Balls in a Weekend</h2><p class="muted">Juggling is 20% throwing and 80% not caring where the balls land. Here's the weekend plan, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Session 1</span><p>One ball. Throw from waist to eye level and catch. Ten minutes, then stop.</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 2</span><p>Two balls. Throw right, throw left, catch both. Don't chase them.</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 3</span><p>Three balls. One extra throw, let the third drop. Count your catches, not your throws.</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 4</span><p>Add a fourth throw. Your hands should move in a small circle, not a big scramble.</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 5</span><p>Practice facing a wall or bed. Balls bounce back and you look less foolish.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>What to practice on day two</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted">Throw-catch rhythm without looking at your hands. Look at the peak of the throws. Your hands will find the balls.</p></div></details><div class="callout warn"><p>Don't practice until your arms ache. Ten focused minutes beats an hour of flailing.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Learning to Juggle Three Balls in a Weekend</h2><p class="muted">Juggling is 20% throwing and 80% not caring where the balls land. Here's the weekend plan, .</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Session 1</span><p>One ball. Throw from waist to eye level and catch. Ten minutes, then stop.</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 2</span><p>Two balls. Throw right, throw left, catch both. Don't chase them.</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 3</span><p>Three balls. One extra throw, let the third drop. Count your catches, not your throws.</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 4</span><p>Add a fourth throw. Your hands should move in a small circle, not a big scramble.</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 5</span><p>Practice facing a wall or bed. Balls bounce back and you look less foolish.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>What to practice on day two</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted">Throw-catch rhythm without looking at your hands. Look at the peak of the throws. Your hands will find the balls.</p></div></details><div class="callout warn"><p>Don't practice until your arms ache. Ten focused minutes beats an hour of flailing.</p></div></div></div>	-
13.51.0	timeline_view/Patching a Bicycle Inner Tube	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Patching a Bicycle Inner Tube</h2><p class="muted">A patch kit costs less than a coffee. Here's the order that gets you rolling, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Step 1</span><p>Find the hole. Inflate the tube and listen, or dunk it in water and watch for bubbles.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 2</span><p>Roughen the area around the hole with sandpaper. Glue sticks to texture, not shine.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 3</span><p>Apply a thin layer of rubber cement. Let it dry until it's tacky, not wet.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 4</span><p>Press the patch on hard for a minute. Peel the backing off slowly.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 5</span><p>Inflate and check. If it holds overnight, put it back in the tyre and ride.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>If the hole is near the valve</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted">Patches don't like curves. Use two smaller patches side by side, or replace the tube if it's a cheap one.</p></div></details><div class="callout info"><p>Carry a spare tube and levers on every ride. Patching at home is calm; patching on a dark roadside is not.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Patching a Bicycle Inner Tube</h2><p class="muted">A patch kit costs less than a coffee. Here's the order that gets you rolling, .</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Step 1</span><p>Find the hole. Inflate the tube and listen, or dunk it in water and watch for bubbles.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 2</span><p>Roughen the area around the hole with sandpaper. Glue sticks to texture, not shine.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 3</span><p>Apply a thin layer of rubber cement. Let it dry until it's tacky, not wet.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 4</span><p>Press the patch on hard for a minute. Peel the backing off slowly.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 5</span><p>Inflate and check. If it holds overnight, put it back in the tyre and ride.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>If the hole is near the valve</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted">Patches don't like curves. Use two smaller patches side by side, or replace the tube if it's a cheap one.</p></div></details><div class="callout info"><p>Carry a spare tube and levers on every ride. Patching at home is calm; patching on a dark roadside is not.</p></div></div></div>	-
13.52.0	timeline_view/Keeping a Sourdough Starter Alive While You're Away	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Keeping a Sourdough Starter Alive While You're Away</h2><p class="muted">Starters are hardier than they look. Here's the minimum-effort plan, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Before you go</span><p>Feed it one last time and let it sit out for an hour. Then it goes in the fridge.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 1-7</span><p>Nothing. The cold slows everything down. A little liquid on top is normal, not a crisis.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 8-14</span><p>If you're gone longer, take it out, pour off the dark liquid, feed, and put it back.</p></li><li><span class="when">Return day</span><p>Let it come to room temperature. Feed it and wait for bubbles before baking.</p></li><li><span class="when">If it smells odd</span><p>Pour off the top layer, feed with fresh flour and water. It usually comes back within two feeds.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Freezing as a backup</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted">Spread a bit on parchment, freeze, and store the flakes in a bag. Thaw and feed to revive. It's not identical, but it's alive.</p></div></details><div class="callout good"><p>Neglect is not death. Starters survive weeks in the fridge. The worst that happens is a slower rise next time.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Keeping a Sourdough Starter Alive While You're Away</h2><p class="muted">Starters are hardier than they look. Here's the minimum-effort plan, .</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Before you go</span><p>Feed it one last time and let it sit out for an hour. Then it goes in the fridge.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 1-7</span><p>Nothing. The cold slows everything down. A little liquid on top is normal, not a crisis.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 8-14</span><p>If you're gone longer, take it out, pour off the dark liquid, feed, and put it back.</p></li><li><span class="when">Return day</span><p>Let it come to room temperature. Feed it and wait for bubbles before baking.</p></li><li><span class="when">If it smells odd</span><p>Pour off the top layer, feed with fresh flour and water. It usually comes back within two feeds.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Freezing as a backup</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted">Spread a bit on parchment, freeze, and store the flakes in a bag. Thaw and feed to revive. It's not identical, but it's alive.</p></div></details><div class="callout good"><p>Neglect is not death. Starters survive weeks in the fridge. The worst that happens is a slower rise next time.</p></div></div></div>	-
13.53.0	timeline_view/Making a Simple Bookshelf from Scaffold Boards	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Making a Simple Bookshelf from Scaffold Boards</h2><p class="muted">Scaffold boards are cheap, sturdy, and already look like shelves. Here's the order, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Step 1</span><p>Choose boards with straight edges and few knots. Two boards for the frame, one for each shelf.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 2</span><p>Cut to size. A hand saw works; just clamp the board and go slow.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 3</span><p>Sand the faces and edges. 120 grit, then 180 if you want it smoother.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 4</span><p>Drill pilot holes and screw the shelves in. Countersink so the screw heads sit below the wood.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 5</span><p>Finish with oil or wax. Let it dry a day before loading books.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>How many shelves to add</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted">Three is a good default for paperback-sized gaps. Measure your tallest book and add a thumb's width.</p></div></details><div class="callout info"><p>Scaffold boards are usually 225mm wide. That's deep enough for most hardbacks, so you don't need to fuss over depth.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Making a Simple Bookshelf from Scaffold Boards</h2><p class="muted">Scaffold boards are cheap, sturdy, and already look like shelves. Here's the order, .</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Step 1</span><p>Choose boards with straight edges and few knots. Two boards for the frame, one for each shelf.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 2</span><p>Cut to size. A hand saw works; just clamp the board and go slow.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 3</span><p>Sand the faces and edges. 120 grit, then 180 if you want it smoother.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 4</span><p>Drill pilot holes and screw the shelves in. Countersink so the screw heads sit below the wood.</p></li><li><span class="when">Step 5</span><p>Finish with oil or wax. Let it dry a day before loading books.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>How many shelves to add</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted">Three is a good default for paperback-sized gaps. Measure your tallest book and add a thumb's width.</p></div></details><div class="callout info"><p>Scaffold boards are usually 225mm wide. That's deep enough for most hardbacks, so you don't need to fuss over depth.</p></div></div></div>	-
13.54.0	timeline_view/Seven Days of Homemade Hummus	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">First week, no filter</p>  <p class="t-small muted">The rules are simple: ten minutes, one jar, one bean. It gets loud around day four.</p>  <ul class="timeline">    <li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Soaked 200&nbsp;g of dried chickpeas overnight. They swelled to nearly double. No bubbles yet, just wet legumes and hope.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>Whizzed them with garlic, lemon, tahini, ice water. The ice water trick keeps it pale and fluffy. Still tastes like a good shop version.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>Forgot the jar on the counter. Came home to a faint sour tang. That tang is the point, but it was too young to be pleasant.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Day 4</span><p>First real bubbles. The smell turned from raw to bright. Ten minutes a day is doing its job.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Day 5</span><p>Salt adjustment. Half a teaspoon more and the sourness snapped into focus. This is the batch you want to repeat.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Day 6</span><p>Fed it a spoon of the liquid from the last jar. Yes, this is fermentation snobbery. Yes, it worked.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Day 7</span><p>Tasted side by side with a supermarket tub. No contest. Yours has a finish; theirs has a shrug.</p></li>  </ul>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Why ice water and not tap?</summary>    <div class="body"><p>Cold keeps the mixture from warming up and turning gluey while the blades run. If you must use tap, chill it in the fridge first. Same idea, slower.</p></div>  </details></div>	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">First week, no filter</p>  <p class="t-small muted">The rules are simple: ten minutes, one jar, one bean. It gets loud around day four.</p>  <ul class="timeline">    <li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Soaked 200&nbsp;g of dried chickpeas overnight. They swelled to nearly double. No bubbles yet, just wet legumes and hope.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>Whizzed them with garlic, lemon, tahini, ice water. The ice water trick keeps it pale and fluffy. Still tastes like a good shop version.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>Forgot the jar on the counter. Came home to a faint sour tang. That tang is the point, but it was too young to be pleasant.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Day 4</span><p>First real bubbles. The smell turned from raw to bright. Ten minutes a day is doing its job.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Day 5</span><p>Salt adjustment. Half a teaspoon more and the sourness snapped into focus. This is the batch you want to repeat.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Day 6</span><p>Fed it a spoon of the liquid from the last jar. Yes, this is fermentation snobbery. Yes, it worked.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Day 7</span><p>Tasted side by side with a supermarket tub. No contest. Yours has a finish; theirs has a shrug.</p></li>  </ul>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Why ice water and not tap?</summary>    <div class="body"><p>Cold keeps the mixture from warming up and turning gluey while the blades run. If you must use tap, chill it in the fridge first. Same idea, slower.</p></div>  </details></div>	-
13.55.0	timeline_view/Replacing a Dripping Tap Washer	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Fixing the drip</p>  <p class="t-small muted">One washer, one wrench, one towel you don't love. Fifteen minutes if nothing is seized.</p>  <ul class="timeline">    <li><span class="when">Min 0</span><p>Shut the valve under the sink. Turn it clockwise until it stops. If it won't stop, shut the whole house valve and call it a day.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Min 2</span><p>Opened the tap to drain the line. Water stops. Good sign.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Min 4</span><p>Plugged the drain with the stopper so a dropped screw doesn't vanish forever. Cheap insurance.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Min 6</span><p>Pried off the little cap on the handle. Under it, a screw with a decade of soap scum. Undid it.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Min 8</span><p>Lifted the handle off and exposed the cartridge. Noted which way it faced before pulling it. Took a photo just in case.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Min 11</span><p>Swapped the worn rubber washer for a fresh one. It cost less than a coffee. The old one was flat as a coin.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Min 14</span><p>Reassembled, opened the valve, ran the tap. No drip. Dry as a biscuit.</p></li>  </ul>  <details class="acc">    <summary>It still drips a little. Now what?</summary>    <div class="body"><p>You likely overtightened the packing nut or the cartridge seat is pitted. Back the nut off a quarter turn first. If that fails, the seat needs a reseat or a new cartridge. Not a crisis.</p></div>  </details></div>	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Fixing the drip</p>  <p class="t-small muted">One washer, one wrench, one towel you don't love. Fifteen minutes if nothing is seized.</p>  <ul class="timeline">    <li><span class="when">Min 0</span><p>Shut the valve under the sink. Turn it clockwise until it stops. If it won't stop, shut the whole house valve and call it a day.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Min 2</span><p>Opened the tap to drain the line. Water stops. Good sign.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Min 4</span><p>Plugged the drain with the stopper so a dropped screw doesn't vanish forever. Cheap insurance.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Min 6</span><p>Pried off the little cap on the handle. Under it, a screw with a decade of soap scum. Undid it.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Min 8</span><p>Lifted the handle off and exposed the cartridge. Noted which way it faced before pulling it. Took a photo just in case.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Min 11</span><p>Swapped the worn rubber washer for a fresh one. It cost less than a coffee. The old one was flat as a coin.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Min 14</span><p>Reassembled, opened the valve, ran the tap. No drip. Dry as a biscuit.</p></li>  </ul>  <details class="acc">    <summary>It still drips a little. Now what?</summary>    <div class="body"><p>You likely overtightened the packing nut or the cartridge seat is pitted. Back the nut off a quarter turn first. If that fails, the seat needs a reseat or a new cartridge. Not a crisis.</p></div>  </details></div>	-
13.56.0	timeline_view/A Week of Baking Bread Without a Recipe	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Week one of the loaf</p>  <p class="t-small muted">Bread gets better when you stop watching it. Here's how the first week went.</p>  <ul class="timeline">    <li><span class="when">Mon</span><p>Baked the first loaf. Crumb was tight, crust was pale. Ate it anyway with too much butter.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Tue</span><p>Read that hydration matters. Added 50&nbsp;ml more water. Dough turned from clay to something alive.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Wed</span><p>Learned to fold instead of knead. Four folds, thirty minutes apart. Less mess, better structure.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Thu</span><p>Oven too hot. Burnt the bottom. The crumb was still good, so I called it a win with a crunchy footnote.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Fri</span><p>Cold proofed overnight in the fridge. Flavour deepened. This is the trick nobody tells you soon enough.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Sat</span><p>Scored the top with a knife. The ear opened. First time it looked like bread instead of a rock.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Sun</span><p>Shared half with a neighbour. She asked for the recipe. I pretended it was nothing.</p></li>  </ul>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What actually changed the bread most?</summary>    <div class="body"><p>The cold overnight proof. It slows everything down so the yeast has time to make flavour instead of just gas. Everything else was decoration.</p></div>  </details></div>	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Week one of the loaf</p>  <p class="t-small muted">Bread gets better when you stop watching it. Here's how the first week went.</p>  <ul class="timeline">    <li><span class="when">Mon</span><p>Baked the first loaf. Crumb was tight, crust was pale. Ate it anyway with too much butter.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Tue</span><p>Read that hydration matters. Added 50&nbsp;ml more water. Dough turned from clay to something alive.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Wed</span><p>Learned to fold instead of knead. Four folds, thirty minutes apart. Less mess, better structure.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Thu</span><p>Oven too hot. Burnt the bottom. The crumb was still good, so I called it a win with a crunchy footnote.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Fri</span><p>Cold proofed overnight in the fridge. Flavour deepened. This is the trick nobody tells you soon enough.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Sat</span><p>Scored the top with a knife. The ear opened. First time it looked like bread instead of a rock.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Sun</span><p>Shared half with a neighbour. She asked for the recipe. I pretended it was nothing.</p></li>  </ul>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What actually changed the bread most?</summary>    <div class="body"><p>The cold overnight proof. It slows everything down so the yeast has time to make flavour instead of just gas. Everything else was decoration.</p></div>  </details></div>	-
13.57.0	timeline_view/First Week Back on a Bicycle	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Raising the saddle, one notch at a time</p>  <p class="t-small muted">Sit bones need a week to stop complaining. After that, it's mostly your legs.</p>  <ul class="timeline">    <li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Twenty minutes on the flat. Legs felt fine. Everything else did not. Saddle height was two fingers too low.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>Raised the saddle. Knees stopped aching. The bike felt longer and stranger, in a good way.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>First hill. Discovered gears are not optional. Dropped to the small ring and survived.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Day 4</span><p>Bought padded shorts. Dignity: mildly bruised. Comfort: dramatically improved.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Day 5</span><p>Rode to the shop and back without thinking about it. That's the milestone, not the distance.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Day 6</span><p>Learned to look over the shoulder without veering. Took four tries and one apology to a hedge.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Day 7</span><p>Eight miles. Walked up one hill. Counted it as a ride anyway.</p></li>  </ul>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Why does my neck hurt?</summary>    <div class="body"><p>Usually the bars are too low or too far. Try a shorter stem or flip it up. Your neck is not the problem; your reach is.</p></div>  </details></div>	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Raising the saddle, one notch at a time</p>  <p class="t-small muted">Sit bones need a week to stop complaining. After that, it's mostly your legs.</p>  <ul class="timeline">    <li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Twenty minutes on the flat. Legs felt fine. Everything else did not. Saddle height was two fingers too low.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>Raised the saddle. Knees stopped aching. The bike felt longer and stranger, in a good way.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>First hill. Discovered gears are not optional. Dropped to the small ring and survived.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Day 4</span><p>Bought padded shorts. Dignity: mildly bruised. Comfort: dramatically improved.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Day 5</span><p>Rode to the shop and back without thinking about it. That's the milestone, not the distance.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Day 6</span><p>Learned to look over the shoulder without veering. Took four tries and one apology to a hedge.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Day 7</span><p>Eight miles. Walked up one hill. Counted it as a ride anyway.</p></li>  </ul>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Why does my neck hurt?</summary>    <div class="body"><p>Usually the bars are too low or too far. Try a shorter stem or flip it up. Your neck is not the problem; your reach is.</p></div>  </details></div>	-
13.58.0	timeline_view/Fermenting Sauerkraut in Ten Days	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Ten days, one jar, no vinegar</p>  <p class="t-small muted">Cabbage, salt, time. That's the whole ingredient list. The rest is patience and clean hands.</p>  <ul class="timeline">    <li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Sliced a green cabbage thin, massaged in 2% salt by weight until it wept. Packed it tight under its own brine.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>Bubbles appeared at the edge of the jar. Smell was still cabbage. Nothing alarming.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>Brine turned cloudy. That's lactic acid bacteria doing their quiet work. Cloudy is good.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Day 4</span><p>Tasted a strand. Salty, crunchy, a little fizzy. Not sour yet, but heading there.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Day 5</span><p>Skimmed a bit of white film off the top. Kahm yeast, harmless. The pink stuff would not be.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Day 6</span><p>First real sour note. Tucked the jar in the fridge to slow it down.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Day 10</span><p>Fridge-cold and properly tangy. Made a sandwich. Considered making another immediately.</p></li>  </ul>  <details class="acc">    <summary>How do I know if it's spoiled?</summary>    <div class="body"><p>Trust your nose and eyes. Sour and salty is fine. Slimy, pink, fuzzy, or rotten-smelling is not. When in doubt, throw it out. Salt is cheaper than regret.</p></div>  </details></div>	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Ten days, one jar, no vinegar</p>  <p class="t-small muted">Cabbage, salt, time. That's the whole ingredient list. The rest is patience and clean hands.</p>  <ul class="timeline">    <li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Sliced a green cabbage thin, massaged in 2% salt by weight until it wept. Packed it tight under its own brine.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>Bubbles appeared at the edge of the jar. Smell was still cabbage. Nothing alarming.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>Brine turned cloudy. That's lactic acid bacteria doing their quiet work. Cloudy is good.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Day 4</span><p>Tasted a strand. Salty, crunchy, a little fizzy. Not sour yet, but heading there.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Day 5</span><p>Skimmed a bit of white film off the top. Kahm yeast, harmless. The pink stuff would not be.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Day 6</span><p>First real sour note. Tucked the jar in the fridge to slow it down.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Day 10</span><p>Fridge-cold and properly tangy. Made a sandwich. Considered making another immediately.</p></li>  </ul>  <details class="acc">    <summary>How do I know if it's spoiled?</summary>    <div class="body"><p>Trust your nose and eyes. Sour and salty is fine. Slimy, pink, fuzzy, or rotten-smelling is not. When in doubt, throw it out. Salt is cheaper than regret.</p></div>  </details></div>	-
13.59.0	timeline_view/Knitting a Scarf: Four Weeks, One Rectangle	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Four weeks of knitting one scarf</p>  <p class="t-small muted">A scarf is a long rectangle. The hard part is not the rectangle.</p>  <ul class="timeline">    <li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Cast on forty stitches. Dropped three in the first hour. Learned to pick them up with a crochet hook.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Tension was all over the place. One edge looked like a mountain range. Frogged the whole thing and started again.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>Switched to a chunkier yarn. Fewer stitches, faster progress, less despair. Should have started here.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>First purl row. Hated it. Did twenty more. Now it's fine.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>Counted stitches mid-row and found two extra. No idea where they came from. Kept going anyway.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Week 4</span><p>Bound off loosely, wove in the ends, blocked it with a damp towel. The edges relaxed. It looked like a scarf.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Week 4</span><p>Wore it. Someone asked if I bought it. Best compliment of the month.</p></li>  </ul>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Why does my knitting curl at the edges?</summary>    <div class="body"><p>Stockinette stitch curls. That's just what it does. A garter or seed stitch border on each edge stops it, or blocking with a little steam if the yarn allows.</p></div>  </details></div>	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Four weeks of knitting one scarf</p>  <p class="t-small muted">A scarf is a long rectangle. The hard part is not the rectangle.</p>  <ul class="timeline">    <li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Cast on forty stitches. Dropped three in the first hour. Learned to pick them up with a crochet hook.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Tension was all over the place. One edge looked like a mountain range. Frogged the whole thing and started again.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>Switched to a chunkier yarn. Fewer stitches, faster progress, less despair. Should have started here.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>First purl row. Hated it. Did twenty more. Now it's fine.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>Counted stitches mid-row and found two extra. No idea where they came from. Kept going anyway.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Week 4</span><p>Bound off loosely, wove in the ends, blocked it with a damp towel. The edges relaxed. It looked like a scarf.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Week 4</span><p>Wore it. Someone asked if I bought it. Best compliment of the month.</p></li>  </ul>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Why does my knitting curl at the edges?</summary>    <div class="body"><p>Stockinette stitch curls. That's just what it does. A garter or seed stitch border on each edge stops it, or blocking with a little steam if the yarn allows.</p></div>  </details></div>	-
13.60.0	timeline_view/Couch to 5K: Week One	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Couch to 5K: Week One</h3><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>20 minutes: brisk 5-min warm-up walk, then alternate 60 sec jog / 90 sec walk for 20 min.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>Rest or gentle walk. Soreness is normal; sharp pain is not.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>Repeat Day 1. Same intervals. Notice your breathing settling.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 4</span><p>Rest.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 5</span><p>Repeat Day 1. You might find the jogging bits feel less frantic.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 6</span><p>Rest or easy walk.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 7</span><p>Optional: a 20-min walk to keep the habit ticking.</p></li></ul><p class="muted t-small">Speed doesn't matter yet. Showing up does.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Couch to 5K: Week One</h3><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>20 minutes: brisk 5-min warm-up walk, then alternate 60 sec jog / 90 sec walk for 20 min.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>Rest or gentle walk. Soreness is normal; sharp pain is not.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>Repeat Day 1. Same intervals. Notice your breathing settling.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 4</span><p>Rest.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 5</span><p>Repeat Day 1. You might find the jogging bits feel less frantic.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 6</span><p>Rest or easy walk.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 7</span><p>Optional: a 20-min walk to keep the habit ticking.</p></li></ul><p class="muted t-small">Speed doesn't matter yet. Showing up does.</p></div>	-
13.61.0	timeline_view/Keeping Sourdough Starter Alive While You're Away	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Keeping Sourdough Starter Alive While You're Away</h3><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">1 week before</span><p>Give it a couple of normal feeds to make sure it's strong and active.</p></li><li><span class="when">2 days before</span><p>Feed it a stiff feed: more flour, less water. A thick paste slows things down.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day of leaving</span><p>Transfer to a clean jar, seal loosely, and pop it in the fridge.</p></li><li><span class="when">While away</span><p>Ignore it. Seriously. The cold and the stiff feed buy you time.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day you return</span><p>Pour off any dark liquid (hooch), then feed as normal. It may smell sharp.</p></li><li><span class="when">24 hours later</span><p>One or two feeds should bring it back to bubbling life.</p></li></ul><p class="muted t-small">If you're gone longer than three weeks, ask a friend to feed it once a week.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Keeping Sourdough Starter Alive While You're Away</h3><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">1 week before</span><p>Give it a couple of normal feeds to make sure it's strong and active.</p></li><li><span class="when">2 days before</span><p>Feed it a stiff feed: more flour, less water. A thick paste slows things down.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day of leaving</span><p>Transfer to a clean jar, seal loosely, and pop it in the fridge.</p></li><li><span class="when">While away</span><p>Ignore it. Seriously. The cold and the stiff feed buy you time.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day you return</span><p>Pour off any dark liquid (hooch), then feed as normal. It may smell sharp.</p></li><li><span class="when">24 hours later</span><p>One or two feeds should bring it back to bubbling life.</p></li></ul><p class="muted t-small">If you're gone longer than three weeks, ask a friend to feed it once a week.</p></div>	-
13.62.0	timeline_view/A Month of Learning to Shuffle Cards	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">A Month of Learning to Shuffle Cards</h2><p class="muted">A quiet, patient log for <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Deck sticks together like a paperback with jam on it. Drop half the cards on the floor twice.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>First riffle happens by accident. Nobody sees it. Celebrate anyway.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 7</span><p>Thumbs figure out the corner flick. Cards land in two neat piles instead of a fan.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 12</span><p>Bridge attempt. Deck springs into the air like a startled pigeon. Laugh, restart.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 18</span><p>Overhand shuffle is now the safe move when guests are watching.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 24</span><p>Riffle bridge works slowly, quietly, almost politely.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 30</span><p>Ten clean shuffles in a row. Hands stop thinking. Good.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Slow thumbs beat fast thumbs. Every time.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">A Month of Learning to Shuffle Cards</h2><p class="muted">A quiet, patient log for .</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Deck sticks together like a paperback with jam on it. Drop half the cards on the floor twice.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>First riffle happens by accident. Nobody sees it. Celebrate anyway.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 7</span><p>Thumbs figure out the corner flick. Cards land in two neat piles instead of a fan.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 12</span><p>Bridge attempt. Deck springs into the air like a startled pigeon. Laugh, restart.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 18</span><p>Overhand shuffle is now the safe move when guests are watching.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 24</span><p>Riffle bridge works slowly, quietly, almost politely.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 30</span><p>Ten clean shuffles in a row. Hands stop thinking. Good.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Slow thumbs beat fast thumbs. Every time.</p></div></div>	-
13.63.0	timeline_view/Raising a Seedling: A Six-Week Window Log	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Raising a Seedling: A Six-Week Window Log</h2><p class="muted">Half hope, half soil. For <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Radio silence. Soil looks identical. You check it four times a day, which changes nothing.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>Tiny green hooks push up. Two fuzzy leaves unfold. Real leaves come later.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>Stems stretch toward the window. Rotate the tray so nobody leans into the glass.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 4</span><p>True leaves appear with jagged edges. Now they actually need sunlight, not just warmth.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 5</span><p>Roots peek out of the drainage holes. Time to think about bigger pots.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 6</span><p>Transplant day. Gentle hands, damp soil, and a small pep talk.</p></li></ul><div class="callout good"><p class="t-small">Leggy and pale? More light, less water. Almost always the answer.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Raising a Seedling: A Six-Week Window Log</h2><p class="muted">Half hope, half soil. For .</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Radio silence. Soil looks identical. You check it four times a day, which changes nothing.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>Tiny green hooks push up. Two fuzzy leaves unfold. Real leaves come later.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>Stems stretch toward the window. Rotate the tray so nobody leans into the glass.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 4</span><p>True leaves appear with jagged edges. Now they actually need sunlight, not just warmth.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 5</span><p>Roots peek out of the drainage holes. Time to think about bigger pots.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 6</span><p>Transplant day. Gentle hands, damp soil, and a small pep talk.</p></li></ul><div class="callout good"><p class="t-small">Leggy and pale? More light, less water. Almost always the answer.</p></div></div>	-
13.0.1	timeline_view/Learning Morse Code: First Two Weeks	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Learning Morse Code: First Two Weeks</h2><p class="muted">A slow rhythm for <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Learn E, T, and I. Three letters. Feels enormous.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>Dots blur into dashes. Write the alphabet on a sticky note. It helps.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 6</span><p>Start hearing letters as sounds, not as counting. This is the real shift.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 9</span><p>Copy your name at 15 words per minute. Miss half of it. Try again.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 11</span><p>Numbers arrive. Five is the easiest. Zero is a long, lonely dash.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 14</span><p>Short phrases instead of single letters. Slow, but real.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Never count the dots. Let your ear do the work.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Learning Morse Code: First Two Weeks</h2><p class="muted">A slow rhythm for .</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Learn E, T, and I. Three letters. Feels enormous.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>Dots blur into dashes. Write the alphabet on a sticky note. It helps.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 6</span><p>Start hearing letters as sounds, not as counting. This is the real shift.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 9</span><p>Copy your name at 15 words per minute. Miss half of it. Try again.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 11</span><p>Numbers arrive. Five is the easiest. Zero is a long, lonely dash.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 14</span><p>Short phrases instead of single letters. Slow, but real.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Never count the dots. Let your ear do the work.</p></div></div>	-
13.1.1	timeline_view/A Ten-Day Log of Pressing Tofu	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">A Ten-Day Log of Pressing Tofu</h2><p class="muted">Squeaky, patient work. For <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Wrap block in a tea towel, stack three cookbooks. Wait thirty minutes. Wonder if this is a prank.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>Skip the towel, use paper towels instead. Water wicks out faster.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 5</span><p>Try a cast-iron pan as the weight. Do not drop it on your foot.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 6</span><p>Press for an hour. Tofu turns dense and quiet. This is the sweet spot.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 8</span><p>Cut into cubes, salt, cornstarch. First pan-fry gets actual browning.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 10</span><p>Freeze the pressed block overnight. Texture turns spongy and holds sauce like a dream.</p></li></ul><div class="callout good"><p class="t-small">Press longer than feels reasonable. Water is the enemy.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">A Ten-Day Log of Pressing Tofu</h2><p class="muted">Squeaky, patient work. For .</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Wrap block in a tea towel, stack three cookbooks. Wait thirty minutes. Wonder if this is a prank.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>Skip the towel, use paper towels instead. Water wicks out faster.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 5</span><p>Try a cast-iron pan as the weight. Do not drop it on your foot.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 6</span><p>Press for an hour. Tofu turns dense and quiet. This is the sweet spot.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 8</span><p>Cut into cubes, salt, cornstarch. First pan-fry gets actual browning.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 10</span><p>Freeze the pressed block overnight. Texture turns spongy and holds sauce like a dream.</p></li></ul><div class="callout good"><p class="t-small">Press longer than feels reasonable. Water is the enemy.</p></div></div>	-
