13.3.1	timeline_view/Training a Cat to Use a Cat Flap: First Month	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Training a Cat to Use a Cat Flap: First Month</h2><p class="muted">Bribery, patience, and a very dignified cat. For <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Prop the flap open with tape. Let the cat sniff the hole without pressure.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>Treats placed just inside the flap. Cat stretches one paw through. Progress.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>Flap lowered halfway. Cat pushes it with the top of the head. Pauses. Retreats. Returns.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 4</span><p>Cat goes out and comes back in thirty seconds. Learns the flap is a door, not a wall.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 26</span><p>Neighbour cat watches through the glass. Yours sits like a smug king.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 30</span><p>Flap is just furniture now. Nothing to celebrate, which is the best sign.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Never push the cat through. Let it choose the next step.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Training a Cat to Use a Cat Flap: First Month</h2><p class="muted">Bribery, patience, and a very dignified cat. For .</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Prop the flap open with tape. Let the cat sniff the hole without pressure.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>Treats placed just inside the flap. Cat stretches one paw through. Progress.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>Flap lowered halfway. Cat pushes it with the top of the head. Pauses. Retreats. Returns.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 4</span><p>Cat goes out and comes back in thirty seconds. Learns the flap is a door, not a wall.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 26</span><p>Neighbour cat watches through the glass. Yours sits like a smug king.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 30</span><p>Flap is just furniture now. Nothing to celebrate, which is the best sign.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Never push the cat through. Let it choose the next step.</p></div></div>	-
13.4.1	timeline_view/Ten Days of Pressing Tofu	<div class="umi-page">  <div class="card">    <h2 class="card-title">Ten Days of Pressing Tofu</h2>    <p class="muted">A log of texture, water, and patience, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p>    <ul class="timeline">      <li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>No press. Crumbled in the pan. Wet, but fine for scrambles.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>15 minutes, light weight. Still spongy. Better browning.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>30 minutes, heavy pan. Noticeably firmer. Holds a cube shape.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Day 4</span><p>1 hour. Very firm. Almost chicken-like. Good for skewers.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Day 5</span><p>2 hours. Dense. Marinade sits on top rather than soaking in. Interesting.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Day 6</span><p>Overnight in fridge, weighted. Dry edges, chewy center. A bit much.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Day 7</span><p>Back to 30 minutes. This is the sweet spot for a stir-fry.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Day 8</span><p>30 minutes plus freeze-thaw. The texture is now entirely different. Porous, sauce-hungry.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Day 9</span><p>Compare 30 min vs 2 hr side by side. The short press wins for flavor, the long press for bite.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Day 10</span><p>Final verdict: press 30 minutes for most things. Longer is a tool, not a rule.</p></li>    </ul>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <div class="card">    <h2 class="card-title">Ten Days of Pressing Tofu</h2>    <p class="muted">A log of texture, water, and patience, .</p>    <ul class="timeline">      <li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>No press. Crumbled in the pan. Wet, but fine for scrambles.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>15 minutes, light weight. Still spongy. Better browning.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>30 minutes, heavy pan. Noticeably firmer. Holds a cube shape.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Day 4</span><p>1 hour. Very firm. Almost chicken-like. Good for skewers.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Day 5</span><p>2 hours. Dense. Marinade sits on top rather than soaking in. Interesting.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Day 6</span><p>Overnight in fridge, weighted. Dry edges, chewy center. A bit much.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Day 7</span><p>Back to 30 minutes. This is the sweet spot for a stir-fry.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Day 8</span><p>30 minutes plus freeze-thaw. The texture is now entirely different. Porous, sauce-hungry.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Day 9</span><p>Compare 30 min vs 2 hr side by side. The short press wins for flavor, the long press for bite.</p></li>      <li><span class="when">Day 10</span><p>Final verdict: press 30 minutes for most things. Longer is a tool, not a rule.</p></li>    </ul>  </div></div>	-
13.5.1	timeline_view/Learning to Iron a Shirt Properly: Five Laundry Sessions	<ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Session 1</span><p><strong>The collar first, always.</strong> Iron it from the points inward before anything else touches the board. It's the one part everyone sees and the one part you can't fix later.</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 2</span><p><strong>Cuffs and sleeves.</strong> Flatten the sleeve seam, press cuff, then run the point of the iron up toward the shoulder. No stretching — you're pressing, not wrestling.</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 3</span><p><strong>The body, in halves.</strong> Front panel, over the board, then the back, then the other front. Realise the shirt is 80% done and you only spent four minutes.</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 4</span><p><strong>Steam and slight heat.</strong> You stop fighting creases and start letting steam do it, <regex>user_name</regex>. Fewer burns, better results.</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 5</span><p><strong>Hang it immediately.</strong> Straight to the hanger while it's still warm. The shirt cools into the shape you left it in — so leave it in a good one.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">One honest near-miss: the first two shirts will have a faint shine on the collar from too much heat. It fades with washing. Mostly.</p></div>	<ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Session 1</span><p><strong>The collar first, always.</strong> Iron it from the points inward before anything else touches the board. It's the one part everyone sees and the one part you can't fix later.</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 2</span><p><strong>Cuffs and sleeves.</strong> Flatten the sleeve seam, press cuff, then run the point of the iron up toward the shoulder. No stretching — you're pressing, not wrestling.</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 3</span><p><strong>The body, in halves.</strong> Front panel, over the board, then the back, then the other front. Realise the shirt is 80% done and you only spent four minutes.</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 4</span><p><strong>Steam and slight heat.</strong> You stop fighting creases and start letting steam do it, . Fewer burns, better results.</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 5</span><p><strong>Hang it immediately.</strong> Straight to the hanger while it's still warm. The shirt cools into the shape you left it in — so leave it in a good one.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">One honest near-miss: the first two shirts will have a faint shine on the collar from too much heat. It fades with washing. Mostly.</p></div>	-
13.6.1	timeline_view/A Month of Learning to Whistle With Two Fingers	<ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Just air and spit. You're folding your tongue back and pressing two fingers under it — finding the spot, not making sound yet.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>First squeak. Terrible, brief, gloriously yours. The angle of the fingers matters more than the force of the breath.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>A real note, held for a second. You tighten the mouth shape and stop puffing — the whistle wants a narrow stream, not a storm.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 4</span><p>Loud enough that the neighbour's dog objects. You can now summon a taxi in 1994.</p></li></ul><div class="callout good"><p>Best early win for <regex>user_name</regex>: practise in a stairwell. The echo teaches you which adjustments actually changed the sound.</p></div>	<ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Just air and spit. You're folding your tongue back and pressing two fingers under it — finding the spot, not making sound yet.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>First squeak. Terrible, brief, gloriously yours. The angle of the fingers matters more than the force of the breath.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>A real note, held for a second. You tighten the mouth shape and stop puffing — the whistle wants a narrow stream, not a storm.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 4</span><p>Loud enough that the neighbour's dog objects. You can now summon a taxi in 1994.</p></li></ul><div class="callout good"><p>Best early win for : practise in a stairwell. The echo teaches you which adjustments actually changed the sound.</p></div>	-
13.7.1	timeline_view/Seven Days of Making Oat Milk at Home	<ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Oats, cold water, blend thirty seconds — no longer. Over-blending is what makes it gluey. Strain through a cloth. Tastes like the ghost of a biscuit. That's normal.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>Add a pinch of salt and a drop of vanilla. Suddenly it's a drink rather than a punishment.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>You measure properly: 1 part oats to 4 parts water. Consistency arrives, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 4</span><p>First cup in coffee. Verdict: fine, faintly oaty, better than expected.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 5</span><p>Batch two made in four minutes flat. This is now just a thing you do.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 6</span><p>It separates in the fridge overnight. Shake before pouring. Every time.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 7</span><p>Cost tally: pennies per litre. Keeps about four days, so make it twice a week and stop hoarding.</p></li></ul>	<ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Oats, cold water, blend thirty seconds — no longer. Over-blending is what makes it gluey. Strain through a cloth. Tastes like the ghost of a biscuit. That's normal.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>Add a pinch of salt and a drop of vanilla. Suddenly it's a drink rather than a punishment.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>You measure properly: 1 part oats to 4 parts water. Consistency arrives, .</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 4</span><p>First cup in coffee. Verdict: fine, faintly oaty, better than expected.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 5</span><p>Batch two made in four minutes flat. This is now just a thing you do.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 6</span><p>It separates in the fridge overnight. Shake before pouring. Every time.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 7</span><p>Cost tally: pennies per litre. Keeps about four days, so make it twice a week and stop hoarding.</p></li></ul>	-
13.8.1	timeline_view/Training a Dog to Wait at the Door: Three Weeks	<ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Days 1–3</span><p>Door stays closed the whole time. Hand on the handle, dog sits, treat, hand off the handle. You're building one rule: handle moves, bottom stays down.</p></li><li><span class="when">Days 4–8</span><p>Handle turns. Door opens two centimetres, then shuts. Most dogs lunge at this stage. No scolding — just close the door and wait for the sit again.</p></li><li><span class="when">Days 9–15</span><p>Door opens fully, you step through first, dog waits. Release word comes only when you're ready, never when they are.</p></li><li><span class="when">Days 16–21</span><p>Real world: driveway, car door, the vet's waiting room. Same rule everywhere, at <regex>user_name</regex>'s pace.</p></li></ul><div class="callout warn"><p>Expect one spectacular regression around day ten. It's not failure, it's the dog testing whether the rule is genuinely a rule.</p></div>	<ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Days 1–3</span><p>Door stays closed the whole time. Hand on the handle, dog sits, treat, hand off the handle. You're building one rule: handle moves, bottom stays down.</p></li><li><span class="when">Days 4–8</span><p>Handle turns. Door opens two centimetres, then shuts. Most dogs lunge at this stage. No scolding — just close the door and wait for the sit again.</p></li><li><span class="when">Days 9–15</span><p>Door opens fully, you step through first, dog waits. Release word comes only when you're ready, never when they are.</p></li><li><span class="when">Days 16–21</span><p>Real world: driveway, car door, the vet's waiting room. Same rule everywhere, at 's pace.</p></li></ul><div class="callout warn"><p>Expect one spectacular regression around day ten. It's not failure, it's the dog testing whether the rule is genuinely a rule.</p></div>	-
13.9.1	timeline_view/A Fortnight of Fixing a Squeaky Door Hinge	<ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Diagnosis: the squeak is the pin, not the hinge. You can hear it if you swing the door slowly and listen near the top.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>Tap the pin up with a nail and a hammer, thin end first. It comes out more easily than you feared.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>Wipe the pin clean with a cloth. Grit is the actual villain here, not dryness.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 5</span><p>A drop of proper lubricant — not cooking oil, please, <regex>user_name</regex> — rolled along the pin. Back in it goes.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 10</span><p>Silence. You keep opening and closing the door just to enjoy it.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 14</span><p>Still quiet. Repeat the whole job in about six months, or when the mood strikes.</p></li></ul>	<ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Diagnosis: the squeak is the pin, not the hinge. You can hear it if you swing the door slowly and listen near the top.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>Tap the pin up with a nail and a hammer, thin end first. It comes out more easily than you feared.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>Wipe the pin clean with a cloth. Grit is the actual villain here, not dryness.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 5</span><p>A drop of proper lubricant — not cooking oil, please,  — rolled along the pin. Back in it goes.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 10</span><p>Silence. You keep opening and closing the door just to enjoy it.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 14</span><p>Still quiet. Repeat the whole job in about six months, or when the mood strikes.</p></li></ul>	-
13.10.1	timeline_view/First Two Weeks With a Rainwater Barrel	<ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Downpipe diverted into the barrel, overflow pointing away from the house. If the overflow points at the wall you've built a damp problem, not a water supply.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>First rain. You stand at the window watching a plastic tub fill like it's a nature documentary. Ten minutes of genuine joy.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 5</span><p>A lid goes on. Open water plus sunlight equals algae, and algae equals a green barrel and a faint smell.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 9</span><p>First watering can drawn off. Plants seem thrilled. Rainwater is soft and slightly acidic — good for most garden things, less good for seedlings you're fussing over.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 14</span><p>Routine, <regex>user_name</regex>: check the mesh filter, skim any leaf litter, and let it refill. Free water, minimal admin.</p></li></ul>	<ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Downpipe diverted into the barrel, overflow pointing away from the house. If the overflow points at the wall you've built a damp problem, not a water supply.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>First rain. You stand at the window watching a plastic tub fill like it's a nature documentary. Ten minutes of genuine joy.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 5</span><p>A lid goes on. Open water plus sunlight equals algae, and algae equals a green barrel and a faint smell.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 9</span><p>First watering can drawn off. Plants seem thrilled. Rainwater is soft and slightly acidic — good for most garden things, less good for seedlings you're fussing over.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 14</span><p>Routine, : check the mesh filter, skim any leaf litter, and let it refill. Free water, minimal admin.</p></li></ul>	-
13.11.1	timeline_view/Learning to Whistle With Two Fingers: Three Weeks	<ul class="timeline">  <li><span class="when">Week 1, day 1</span><p>Watched a tutorial. Tried the two-finger method. Got a faint, breathy wheeze. <regex>user_name</regex>, that counts as a start.</p></li>  <li><span class="when">Week 1, day 4</span><p>Discovered I was tucking my fingers too far in. Moved them to just past the first knuckle. Still nothing loud, but the air felt different.</p></li>  <li><span class="when">Week 2, day 2</span><p>First real squeak! Neighbour's dog looked concerned. I apologised. I also practised more quietly after that.</p></li>  <li><span class="when">Week 2, day 6</span><p>Started wetting my lips first. Game changer. The sound got sharper, almost a note.</p></li>  <li><span class="when">Week 3, day 1</span><p>Loud enough to stop a conversation. Not melodic, but unmistakably a whistle. Fingers still ache a bit.</p></li>  <li><span class="when">Week 3, day 5</span><p>Can now do it on demand, though the pitch wanders. Called it: learned. Next up, one-handed whistle?</p></li></ul>	<ul class="timeline">  <li><span class="when">Week 1, day 1</span><p>Watched a tutorial. Tried the two-finger method. Got a faint, breathy wheeze. , that counts as a start.</p></li>  <li><span class="when">Week 1, day 4</span><p>Discovered I was tucking my fingers too far in. Moved them to just past the first knuckle. Still nothing loud, but the air felt different.</p></li>  <li><span class="when">Week 2, day 2</span><p>First real squeak! Neighbour's dog looked concerned. I apologised. I also practised more quietly after that.</p></li>  <li><span class="when">Week 2, day 6</span><p>Started wetting my lips first. Game changer. The sound got sharper, almost a note.</p></li>  <li><span class="when">Week 3, day 1</span><p>Loud enough to stop a conversation. Not melodic, but unmistakably a whistle. Fingers still ache a bit.</p></li>  <li><span class="when">Week 3, day 5</span><p>Can now do it on demand, though the pitch wanders. Called it: learned. Next up, one-handed whistle?</p></li></ul>	-
13.12.1	timeline_view/Learning to Whistle With Two Fingers: Ten Days	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Ten days, four fingers, one whistle</h3><p class="muted">For <regex>user_name</regex>. Day one is mostly spit. Day ten is a noise that scares the cat.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Watched a video. Wet fingers. Got a sad puff of air.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>Tried two fingers instead of four. Nearly fainted. No sound.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 5</span><p>A squeak. A real squeak. Neighbours looked up.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 7</span><p>Fingers too far back. Gagged. Adjusted. Still just air.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 10</span><p>Loud, ugly, glorious whistle. Cat left the room.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info"><strong>What finally worked:</strong> fingers in just past the first knuckle, tongue pulled back, blow like you mean it.</div></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Ten days, four fingers, one whistle</h3><p class="muted">For . Day one is mostly spit. Day ten is a noise that scares the cat.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Watched a video. Wet fingers. Got a sad puff of air.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>Tried two fingers instead of four. Nearly fainted. No sound.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 5</span><p>A squeak. A real squeak. Neighbours looked up.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 7</span><p>Fingers too far back. Gagged. Adjusted. Still just air.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 10</span><p>Loud, ugly, glorious whistle. Cat left the room.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info"><strong>What finally worked:</strong> fingers in just past the first knuckle, tongue pulled back, blow like you mean it.</div></div>	-
13.13.1	timeline_view/Learning to Sail a Small Dinghy: First Six Sessions	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Six sessions on the water</h3><p class="muted">For <regex>user_name</regex>. Capsizing is part of the curriculum, not a failure.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Session 1</span><p>Rigging on land. Confused by ropes. Every rope has a name and none of them are "rope".</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 2</span><p>Tiller in hand. Boat went in circles. Instructor said "that's the point".</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 3</span><p>Learned to tack. Almost hit a buoy. Buoy was fine.</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 4</span><p>Wind picked up. Capsized. Water was cold. Righted the boat and kept going.</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 6</span><p>Sailed a full triangle without help. Felt like a pirate. Arms like noodles.</p></li></ul><div class="callout warn"><strong>Bring:</strong> a change of clothes in a dry bag. You will get wet. That's the deal.</div></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Six sessions on the water</h3><p class="muted">For . Capsizing is part of the curriculum, not a failure.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Session 1</span><p>Rigging on land. Confused by ropes. Every rope has a name and none of them are "rope".</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 2</span><p>Tiller in hand. Boat went in circles. Instructor said "that's the point".</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 3</span><p>Learned to tack. Almost hit a buoy. Buoy was fine.</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 4</span><p>Wind picked up. Capsized. Water was cold. Righted the boat and kept going.</p></li><li><span class="when">Session 6</span><p>Sailed a full triangle without help. Felt like a pirate. Arms like noodles.</p></li></ul><div class="callout warn"><strong>Bring:</strong> a change of clothes in a dry bag. You will get wet. That's the deal.</div></div>	-
13.14.1	timeline_view/A Weekend of Fixing a Doorbell	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Two days, one doorbell</h3><p class="muted">For <regex>user_name</regex>. Old houses have old wiring. Old wiring has opinions.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Sat 09:00</span><p>Removed the button. Wires looked like they'd been chewed by history.</p></li><li><span class="when">Sat 10:30</span><p>Tested the transformer with a multimeter. 16V, so it works. Good.</p></li><li><span class="when">Sat 13:00</span><p>New button in. Still nothing. The chime is the weak link.</p></li><li><span class="when">Sun 10:00</span><p>Swapped the chime unit. Wires back in. Ding-dong.</p></li><li><span class="when">Sun 11:00</span><p>Replaced the cover. Tested twice. Wife pressed it for fun.</p></li></ul><div class="callout good"><strong>Total cost:</strong> about the price of two coffees and a sore back from the ladder.</div></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Two days, one doorbell</h3><p class="muted">For . Old houses have old wiring. Old wiring has opinions.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Sat 09:00</span><p>Removed the button. Wires looked like they'd been chewed by history.</p></li><li><span class="when">Sat 10:30</span><p>Tested the transformer with a multimeter. 16V, so it works. Good.</p></li><li><span class="when">Sat 13:00</span><p>New button in. Still nothing. The chime is the weak link.</p></li><li><span class="when">Sun 10:00</span><p>Swapped the chime unit. Wires back in. Ding-dong.</p></li><li><span class="when">Sun 11:00</span><p>Replaced the cover. Tested twice. Wife pressed it for fun.</p></li></ul><div class="callout good"><strong>Total cost:</strong> about the price of two coffees and a sore back from the ladder.</div></div>	-
13.15.1	timeline_view/Learning to Make a Proper Omelette: Five Breakfasts	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Five mornings, one pan</h3><p class="muted">For <regex>user_name</regex>. The trick is heat and timing, not ingredients.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Mon</span><p>Too hot. Brown spots. Tasted like a sponge left in the sun.</p></li><li><span class="when">Tue</span><p>Lower heat. Better colour. Folded it too early and got scrambled eggs with ambition.</p></li><li><span class="when">Wed</span><p>Added a splash of water. Fluffier. Stuck to the pan anyway.</p></li><li><span class="when">Thu</span><p>More butter. Shook the pan. Folded with a fork. Almost restaurant-looking.</p></li><li><span class="when">Fri</span><p>Pale, soft, rolled. Ate it standing up. Felt smug.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info"><strong>The secret:</strong> take it off the heat before it looks done. It keeps cooking.</div></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Five mornings, one pan</h3><p class="muted">For . The trick is heat and timing, not ingredients.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Mon</span><p>Too hot. Brown spots. Tasted like a sponge left in the sun.</p></li><li><span class="when">Tue</span><p>Lower heat. Better colour. Folded it too early and got scrambled eggs with ambition.</p></li><li><span class="when">Wed</span><p>Added a splash of water. Fluffier. Stuck to the pan anyway.</p></li><li><span class="when">Thu</span><p>More butter. Shook the pan. Folded with a fork. Almost restaurant-looking.</p></li><li><span class="when">Fri</span><p>Pale, soft, rolled. Ate it standing up. Felt smug.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info"><strong>The secret:</strong> take it off the heat before it looks done. It keeps cooking.</div></div>	-
13.16.1	timeline_view/A Month of Learning to Use Chopsticks	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Thirty days of noodles and frustration</h3><p class="muted">For <regex>user_name</regex>. Day one: cereal. Day thirty: edamame.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Held them like drumsticks. Ate rice with a fork. Dignity intact.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 5</span><p>Watched a video. Bottom stick stays still, top one moves. It's a lever, not scissors.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 12</span><p>Could pick up a grape. Dropped it twice. Third time: success.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 20</span><p>Noodles. Slippery, but doable. Broth splattered shirt.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 30</span><p>Edamame, one by one. Felt like a local. Still not confident with peas.</p></li></ul><div class="callout good"><strong>Practice food:</strong> marshmallows. They're forgiving and you get a snack.</div></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Thirty days of noodles and frustration</h3><p class="muted">For . Day one: cereal. Day thirty: edamame.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Held them like drumsticks. Ate rice with a fork. Dignity intact.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 5</span><p>Watched a video. Bottom stick stays still, top one moves. It's a lever, not scissors.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 12</span><p>Could pick up a grape. Dropped it twice. Third time: success.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 20</span><p>Noodles. Slippery, but doable. Broth splattered shirt.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 30</span><p>Edamame, one by one. Felt like a local. Still not confident with peas.</p></li></ul><div class="callout good"><strong>Practice food:</strong> marshmallows. They're forgiving and you get a snack.</div></div>	-
13.17.1	timeline_view/Learning to Use a Sewing Machine: Six Projects	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Six things I made badly, then better</h3><p class="muted">For <regex>user_name</regex>. The machine is not broken. The tension just needs love.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Project 1</span><p>Threaded the bobbin wrong. Sewed a bird's nest. Unpicked it all.</p></li><li><span class="when">Project 2</span><p>A simple tote bag. Straps are wonky. It holds books. That counts.</p></li><li><span class="when">Project 3</span><p>Learned to backstitch. Seams stopped unravelling. Progress.</p></li><li><span class="when">Project 5</span><p>A zip pouch. First zipper. Took three tries. Looks store-bought-ish.</p></li><li><span class="when">Project 6</span><p>A basic skirt. Wore it out. Nobody pointed and laughed.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info"><strong>Rule one:</strong> change your needle. Often. Dull needles cause more problems than anything else.</div></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Six things I made badly, then better</h3><p class="muted">For . The machine is not broken. The tension just needs love.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Project 1</span><p>Threaded the bobbin wrong. Sewed a bird's nest. Unpicked it all.</p></li><li><span class="when">Project 2</span><p>A simple tote bag. Straps are wonky. It holds books. That counts.</p></li><li><span class="when">Project 3</span><p>Learned to backstitch. Seams stopped unravelling. Progress.</p></li><li><span class="when">Project 5</span><p>A zip pouch. First zipper. Took three tries. Looks store-bought-ish.</p></li><li><span class="when">Project 6</span><p>A basic skirt. Wore it out. Nobody pointed and laughed.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info"><strong>Rule one:</strong> change your needle. Often. Dull needles cause more problems than anything else.</div></div>	-
13.18.1	timeline_view/Training a Puppy to Sleep Through the Night: First Two Weeks	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Puppy Night Log: Weeks 1–2</h3><p class="muted">A simple timeline of how we got from hourly wake-ups to a full night's sleep. <regex>user_name</regex>, consistency is everything.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Night 1</span><p>Whined every 2 hours. Took out on leash, no play, straight back to crate.</p></li><li><span class="when">Night 3</span><p>Stretched to 3 hours. Small success: only one 4am wake-up.</p></li><li><span class="when">Night 5</span><p>Slept 10pm–3am. Cried for 10 min at bedtime, then settled.</p></li><li><span class="when">Night 7</span><p>Made it to 5am without a peep. Huge win.</p></li><li><span class="when">Night 9</span><p>Two steps back: tummy upset from new treat. Back to 3am wake-up.</p></li><li><span class="when">Night 11</span><p>6 hours straight. We're getting there.</p></li><li><span class="when">Night 13</span><p>Slept 10:30pm–6am. No crying at bedtime.</p></li><li><span class="when">Night 14</span><p>Full night. Woke up to an alarm, not a whimper.</p></li></ul><div class="callout good"><p><strong>What worked:</strong> Crate next to bed, no water after 7pm, potty break before bed, and a worn t-shirt in the crate.</p></div></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Puppy Night Log: Weeks 1–2</h3><p class="muted">A simple timeline of how we got from hourly wake-ups to a full night's sleep. , consistency is everything.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Night 1</span><p>Whined every 2 hours. Took out on leash, no play, straight back to crate.</p></li><li><span class="when">Night 3</span><p>Stretched to 3 hours. Small success: only one 4am wake-up.</p></li><li><span class="when">Night 5</span><p>Slept 10pm–3am. Cried for 10 min at bedtime, then settled.</p></li><li><span class="when">Night 7</span><p>Made it to 5am without a peep. Huge win.</p></li><li><span class="when">Night 9</span><p>Two steps back: tummy upset from new treat. Back to 3am wake-up.</p></li><li><span class="when">Night 11</span><p>6 hours straight. We're getting there.</p></li><li><span class="when">Night 13</span><p>Slept 10:30pm–6am. No crying at bedtime.</p></li><li><span class="when">Night 14</span><p>Full night. Woke up to an alarm, not a whimper.</p></li></ul><div class="callout good"><p><strong>What worked:</strong> Crate next to bed, no water after 7pm, potty break before bed, and a worn t-shirt in the crate.</p></div></div>	-
13.19.1	timeline_view/A Month of Learning to Poach an Egg	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Poached Egg Progress: 30 Mornings</h3><p class="muted">From egg-drop soup to café-worthy. <regex>user_name</regex>, here's the honest month-long log.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Vinegar? Swirl? Got a mess of egg whites. Ate scrambled instead.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>Tried a fine-mesh sieve to drain watery whites. Better shape.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 7</span><p>Learned to use a shallow pan and gentle simmer. No more tornado.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 10</span><p>First egg that stayed together. Yolk runny. Celebrated with toast.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 13</span><p>Overcooked two in a row. Timer is non-negotiable: 3 minutes.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 18</span><p>Cracked egg into a cup first. Game changer.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 22</span><p>Perfect shape three days straight. Started adding a splash of vinegar.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 30</span><p>Can poach two eggs at once without stress. Breakfast is sorted.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info"><p><strong>Key lesson:</strong> Fresher eggs hold together better. And patience beats swirling.</p></div></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Poached Egg Progress: 30 Mornings</h3><p class="muted">From egg-drop soup to café-worthy. , here's the honest month-long log.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Vinegar? Swirl? Got a mess of egg whites. Ate scrambled instead.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>Tried a fine-mesh sieve to drain watery whites. Better shape.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 7</span><p>Learned to use a shallow pan and gentle simmer. No more tornado.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 10</span><p>First egg that stayed together. Yolk runny. Celebrated with toast.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 13</span><p>Overcooked two in a row. Timer is non-negotiable: 3 minutes.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 18</span><p>Cracked egg into a cup first. Game changer.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 22</span><p>Perfect shape three days straight. Started adding a splash of vinegar.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 30</span><p>Can poach two eggs at once without stress. Breakfast is sorted.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info"><p><strong>Key lesson:</strong> Fresher eggs hold together better. And patience beats swirling.</p></div></div>	-
13.20.1	timeline_view/Learning to Pogo on a Skateboard: First Month	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Pogo Log: Four Weeks</h3><p class="muted">A pogo is the trick where you bounce on the tail. <regex>user_name</regex>, here's how it went.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Couldn't get the board off the ground. Slammed the tail too hard and slipped out.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Watched a slow-mo video. Realized it's a push down with the back foot while pulling up with the front.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>First tiny hop. Maybe an inch. Felt like a foot.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>Learned to keep weight centered. No more slipping.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>Got three consecutive bounces. Then ate pavement. Wear a helmet.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>Started practicing on grass to build confidence.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 4</span><p>Can pogo five times in a row on flat ground. Still wobbly but fun.</p></li></ul><div class="callout warn"><p><strong>Safety note:</strong> Pogo puts stress on the tail. Check for cracks regularly.</p></div></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Pogo Log: Four Weeks</h3><p class="muted">A pogo is the trick where you bounce on the tail. , here's how it went.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Couldn't get the board off the ground. Slammed the tail too hard and slipped out.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Watched a slow-mo video. Realized it's a push down with the back foot while pulling up with the front.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>First tiny hop. Maybe an inch. Felt like a foot.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>Learned to keep weight centered. No more slipping.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>Got three consecutive bounces. Then ate pavement. Wear a helmet.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>Started practicing on grass to build confidence.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 4</span><p>Can pogo five times in a row on flat ground. Still wobbly but fun.</p></li></ul><div class="callout warn"><p><strong>Safety note:</strong> Pogo puts stress on the tail. Check for cracks regularly.</p></div></div>	-
13.21.1	timeline_view/A Week of Meal Prepping Without a Microwave	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Microwave-Free Meal Prep: 7 Days</h3><p class="muted">No microwave? No problem. <regex>user_name</regex>, here's how I prepped and reheated for a week.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Cooked grains and roasted veggies. Stored in glass containers. Reheated on stovetop with a splash of water.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>Learned that stir-fries reheat beautifully in a pan. Added fresh herbs after heating.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>Made a big pot of soup. Reheated in a saucepan. Even better the second day.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 4</span><p>Tried reheating pasta in a skillet with a little olive oil. Crispy edges. Delicious.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 5</span><p>Discovered that steamed veggies can be refreshed in a steamer basket. No sogginess.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 6</span><p>Prepped burrito bowls. Reheated the components separately to keep textures right.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 7</span><p>Used the oven to reheat a whole tray of roasted chicken and potatoes. Worked great.</p></li></ul><div class="callout good"><p><strong>Best tip:</strong> A cast-iron skillet is your microwave. It reheats and adds flavor.</p></div></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Microwave-Free Meal Prep: 7 Days</h3><p class="muted">No microwave? No problem. , here's how I prepped and reheated for a week.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Cooked grains and roasted veggies. Stored in glass containers. Reheated on stovetop with a splash of water.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>Learned that stir-fries reheat beautifully in a pan. Added fresh herbs after heating.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>Made a big pot of soup. Reheated in a saucepan. Even better the second day.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 4</span><p>Tried reheating pasta in a skillet with a little olive oil. Crispy edges. Delicious.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 5</span><p>Discovered that steamed veggies can be refreshed in a steamer basket. No sogginess.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 6</span><p>Prepped burrito bowls. Reheated the components separately to keep textures right.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 7</span><p>Used the oven to reheat a whole tray of roasted chicken and potatoes. Worked great.</p></li></ul><div class="callout good"><p><strong>Best tip:</strong> A cast-iron skillet is your microwave. It reheats and adds flavor.</p></div></div>	-
13.22.1	timeline_view/Learning to Use a Chef's Knife: First Month	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Chef's Knife: 4 Weeks</h3><p class="muted">From awkward chops to confident cuts. <regex>user_name</regex>, here's the journey.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Held the knife like a hammer. Learned the pinch grip—thumb and forefinger on the blade.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>First onion: hacked it to pieces. Took 10 minutes.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>Practiced the claw—curled fingers to guide the blade. No cuts yet.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>Onion diced in 5 minutes. Still uneven but usable.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>Learned to sharpen on a whetstone. Sharp knife changed everything.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>Chopped carrots, celery, and garlic in one session. Felt like a pro.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 4</span><p>Onion in 2 minutes. Consistent dice. No tears (thanks, sharp blade).</p></li></ul><div class="callout good"><p><strong>Biggest lesson:</strong> A dull knife is more dangerous than a sharp one. Keep it honed.</p></div></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Chef's Knife: 4 Weeks</h3><p class="muted">From awkward chops to confident cuts. , here's the journey.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Held the knife like a hammer. Learned the pinch grip—thumb and forefinger on the blade.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>First onion: hacked it to pieces. Took 10 minutes.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>Practiced the claw—curled fingers to guide the blade. No cuts yet.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>Onion diced in 5 minutes. Still uneven but usable.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>Learned to sharpen on a whetstone. Sharp knife changed everything.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>Chopped carrots, celery, and garlic in one session. Felt like a pro.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 4</span><p>Onion in 2 minutes. Consistent dice. No tears (thanks, sharp blade).</p></li></ul><div class="callout good"><p><strong>Biggest lesson:</strong> A dull knife is more dangerous than a sharp one. Keep it honed.</p></div></div>	-
13.23.1	timeline_view/Learning to Riffle Shuffle: One Month, Less Dropping	<div class="umi-page"><article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>Nice pick, <regex>user_name</regex>. Shuffling looks like magic until your thumbs learn the grip. Here's the log.</p></article><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Practiced the grip on a worn deck. Cards sprayed everywhere twice. <span class="badge warn">messy</span></p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>Riffle without a bridge. Half the deck goes, half stays. Progress is progress.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>Added the bridge. Dropped six cards on the floor. Cat judged me.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 4</span><p>Full riffle-bridge, no drops. Slow, but it works. <span class="badge good">nailed it</span></p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Tips that actually helped</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Loosen the grip, don't squeeze.</li><li>Practice over a soft surface.</li><li>Worn cards beat new ones for learning.</li></ul></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>Nice pick, . Shuffling looks like magic until your thumbs learn the grip. Here's the log.</p></article><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Practiced the grip on a worn deck. Cards sprayed everywhere twice. <span class="badge warn">messy</span></p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>Riffle without a bridge. Half the deck goes, half stays. Progress is progress.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>Added the bridge. Dropped six cards on the floor. Cat judged me.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 4</span><p>Full riffle-bridge, no drops. Slow, but it works. <span class="badge good">nailed it</span></p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Tips that actually helped</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Loosen the grip, don't squeeze.</li><li>Practice over a soft surface.</li><li>Worn cards beat new ones for learning.</li></ul></div></details></div>	-
13.24.1	timeline_view/Raising a Seedling: A Six-Week Windowsill Log	<div class="umi-page"><article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>Seeds want light, water, and patience, <regex>user_name</regex>. Mostly patience. Here's how it went.</p></article><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Planted basil and tomato seeds in egg cartons. Kept soil damp, not soggy.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>First green hooks appeared. Moved tray to the sunniest sill.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>Tomatoes got leggy, so I rotated the tray daily. Stems thickened.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 4</span><p>Thinned the basil to one per cell. Felt brutal. Necessary.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 6</span><p>Transplanted the strongest into bigger pots. Roots looked happy.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>What I'd do differently</summary><div class="body"><p>Start tomatoes later. They outgrew the windowsill by week four.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>Seeds want light, water, and patience, . Mostly patience. Here's how it went.</p></article><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Planted basil and tomato seeds in egg cartons. Kept soil damp, not soggy.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>First green hooks appeared. Moved tray to the sunniest sill.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>Tomatoes got leggy, so I rotated the tray daily. Stems thickened.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 4</span><p>Thinned the basil to one per cell. Felt brutal. Necessary.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 6</span><p>Transplanted the strongest into bigger pots. Roots looked happy.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>What I'd do differently</summary><div class="body"><p>Start tomatoes later. They outgrew the windowsill by week four.</p></div></details></div>	-
13.25.1	timeline_view/Learning to Change a Bike Chain: First Month	<div class="umi-page"><h2>Learning to Change a Bike Chain: First Month</h2><p class="muted">Hey <regex>user_name</regex> — four weeks, one greasy rag, and a chain that finally shifts clean.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Measured chain wear with a ruler. 12 links over 12 inches means it's stretched — time to retire it.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>Bought the right chain (8, 9, 10, 11 speed all differ). Counted links against the old one.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>Fitted a quick-link, threaded the derailleur cage the right way round, checked tension by hand.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 4</span><p>Rode 20 miles. Silent drivetrain. Small win, big grin.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Common mistakes I made</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Threaded the chain outside the cage tab — it rattled for a day.</li><li>Forgot to route through the jockey wheels before closing the quick-link.</li><li>Overtightened the quick-link pliers and bent a pin.</li></ul></div></details><div class="callout info">Tools that paid for themselves: chain tool, quick-link pliers, wear indicator.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h2>Learning to Change a Bike Chain: First Month</h2><p class="muted">Hey  — four weeks, one greasy rag, and a chain that finally shifts clean.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Measured chain wear with a ruler. 12 links over 12 inches means it's stretched — time to retire it.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>Bought the right chain (8, 9, 10, 11 speed all differ). Counted links against the old one.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>Fitted a quick-link, threaded the derailleur cage the right way round, checked tension by hand.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 4</span><p>Rode 20 miles. Silent drivetrain. Small win, big grin.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Common mistakes I made</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Threaded the chain outside the cage tab — it rattled for a day.</li><li>Forgot to route through the jockey wheels before closing the quick-link.</li><li>Overtightened the quick-link pliers and bent a pin.</li></ul></div></details><div class="callout info">Tools that paid for themselves: chain tool, quick-link pliers, wear indicator.</div></div>	-
13.26.1	timeline_view/A Fortnight of Cleaning Windows Without Streaks	<div class="umi-page"><h2>A Fortnight of Cleaning Windows Without Streaks</h2><p class="muted">Two weeks, <regex>user_name</regex>, and the secret was less soap, more squeegee.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Washed with a drop of dish soap in warm water. Streaks everywhere — too much soap.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 4</span><p>Switched to a 1:10 vinegar-water mix. Better. Still a haze on sunny panes.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 8</span><p>Learned to work top-down with a squeegee, wiping the blade between passes.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 14</span><p>Sunny afternoon test: zero streaks. Framed it in my head as a small trophy.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>What actually mattered</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Clean water every pane — dirty water equals streaks.</li><li>Shade, not sunshine. Sun dries the glass before you finish.</li><li>Squeegee angled slightly, one smooth pull.</li><li>Buff edges with a dry microfibre cloth.</li></ol></div></details><div class="callout good">Cheap vinegar beat the fancy spray every time.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h2>A Fortnight of Cleaning Windows Without Streaks</h2><p class="muted">Two weeks, , and the secret was less soap, more squeegee.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Washed with a drop of dish soap in warm water. Streaks everywhere — too much soap.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 4</span><p>Switched to a 1:10 vinegar-water mix. Better. Still a haze on sunny panes.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 8</span><p>Learned to work top-down with a squeegee, wiping the blade between passes.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 14</span><p>Sunny afternoon test: zero streaks. Framed it in my head as a small trophy.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>What actually mattered</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Clean water every pane — dirty water equals streaks.</li><li>Shade, not sunshine. Sun dries the glass before you finish.</li><li>Squeegee angled slightly, one smooth pull.</li><li>Buff edges with a dry microfibre cloth.</li></ol></div></details><div class="callout good">Cheap vinegar beat the fancy spray every time.</div></div>	-
13.27.1	timeline_view/Learning to Do a Handstand Against a Wall: Six Weeks	<div class="umi-page"><h2>Learning to Do a Handstand Against a Wall: Six Weeks</h2><p class="muted">Six weeks, <regex>user_name</regex>, one wall, and a lot of falling over (safely).</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Wrist warm-ups and plank holds. Boring but essential — wrists complained loudly.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>Kicked up to wall. Held 5 seconds. Elbows bent like a frog.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>Focused on straight arms and shrugged shoulders. Hold jumped to 20 seconds.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 5</span><p>Toe pulls off the wall. Two seconds free. Felt like flying.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 6</span><p>45-second wall hold, controlled descent. Next stop: middle of the room.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Safety basics</summary><div class="body"><p>Learn to bail: tuck chin, roll to one side. Practise the roll before you ever kick up. Pad the area. Stop if wrists ache.</p></div></details><div class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i> Progress beats perfection</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h2>Learning to Do a Handstand Against a Wall: Six Weeks</h2><p class="muted">Six weeks, , one wall, and a lot of falling over (safely).</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Wrist warm-ups and plank holds. Boring but essential — wrists complained loudly.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>Kicked up to wall. Held 5 seconds. Elbows bent like a frog.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>Focused on straight arms and shrugged shoulders. Hold jumped to 20 seconds.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 5</span><p>Toe pulls off the wall. Two seconds free. Felt like flying.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 6</span><p>45-second wall hold, controlled descent. Next stop: middle of the room.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Safety basics</summary><div class="body"><p>Learn to bail: tuck chin, roll to one side. Practise the roll before you ever kick up. Pad the area. Stop if wrists ache.</p></div></details><div class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i> Progress beats perfection</div></div>	-
13.28.1	timeline_view/Two Weeks of Learning Basic Car Maintenance Checks	<div class="umi-page"><h2>Two Weeks of Learning Basic Car Maintenance Checks</h2><p class="muted">Hey <regex>user_name</regex> — no mechanic training required. Ten minutes a week keeps the scary lights off.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Learned where the dipstick lives. Checked oil cold, on flat ground. Level looked fine.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>Tyre pressures — read the sticker inside the driver's door, not the tyre wall.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 6</span><p>Topped up coolant (engine cold!). Learned the hard way that hot systems bite.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 10</span><p>Checked wiper blades, washer fluid, and all lights with a wall reflection.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 14</span><p>Full ten-minute loop. Confident enough to spot a problem before it becomes one.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>My ten-minute weekly loop</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Oil level (cold engine)</li><li>Tyre pressures + visual tread check</li><li>Coolant and washer fluid levels</li><li>All lights: head, brake, indicators, reverse</li><li>Wipers and horn</li></ol></div></details><div class="callout warn">Never open the coolant cap while the engine is warm.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h2>Two Weeks of Learning Basic Car Maintenance Checks</h2><p class="muted">Hey  — no mechanic training required. Ten minutes a week keeps the scary lights off.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Learned where the dipstick lives. Checked oil cold, on flat ground. Level looked fine.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>Tyre pressures — read the sticker inside the driver's door, not the tyre wall.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 6</span><p>Topped up coolant (engine cold!). Learned the hard way that hot systems bite.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 10</span><p>Checked wiper blades, washer fluid, and all lights with a wall reflection.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 14</span><p>Full ten-minute loop. Confident enough to spot a problem before it becomes one.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>My ten-minute weekly loop</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Oil level (cold engine)</li><li>Tyre pressures + visual tread check</li><li>Coolant and washer fluid levels</li><li>All lights: head, brake, indicators, reverse</li><li>Wipers and horn</li></ol></div></details><div class="callout warn">Never open the coolant cap while the engine is warm.</div></div>	-
13.29.1	timeline_view/Learning to Sew a Button Back On: Five Evenings	<div class="umi-page"><h2>Learning to Sew a Button Back On: Five Evenings</h2><p class="muted">Five evenings, <regex>user_name</regex>, and a shirt rescued from the donate pile.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Evening 1</span><p>Threaded a needle on the third try. Tied a knot. Felt absurdly proud.</p></li><li><span class="when">Evening 2</span><p>Practised on a scrap of fabric. Learned to anchor the thread with a tiny backstitch.</p></li><li><span class="when">Evening 3</span><p>First real button — two-hole, matched the thread colour. Crooked but attached.</p></li><li><span class="when">Evening 4</span><p>Four-hole button with a cross pattern. Added a toothpick under it for a shank.</p></li><li><span class="when">Evening 5</span><p>Shirt is wearable again. Nobody will ever notice the wobbly one.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>The trick I wish I'd known sooner</summary><div class="body"><p>Slide a toothpick between button and fabric while sewing. It creates slack so the button doesn't strangle the buttonhole. Remove it before the final knot.</p></div></details><div class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i> Repair before replace</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h2>Learning to Sew a Button Back On: Five Evenings</h2><p class="muted">Five evenings, , and a shirt rescued from the donate pile.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Evening 1</span><p>Threaded a needle on the third try. Tied a knot. Felt absurdly proud.</p></li><li><span class="when">Evening 2</span><p>Practised on a scrap of fabric. Learned to anchor the thread with a tiny backstitch.</p></li><li><span class="when">Evening 3</span><p>First real button — two-hole, matched the thread colour. Crooked but attached.</p></li><li><span class="when">Evening 4</span><p>Four-hole button with a cross pattern. Added a toothpick under it for a shank.</p></li><li><span class="when">Evening 5</span><p>Shirt is wearable again. Nobody will ever notice the wobbly one.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>The trick I wish I'd known sooner</summary><div class="body"><p>Slide a toothpick between button and fabric while sewing. It creates slack so the button doesn't strangle the buttonhole. Remove it before the final knot.</p></div></details><div class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i> Repair before replace</div></div>	-
13.30.1	timeline_view/A Month of Learning to Make Proper Pizza Dough	<div class="umi-page"><h2>A Month of Learning to Make Proper Pizza Dough</h2><p class="muted">Four weeks, <regex>user_name</regex>, and one very patient fridge.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Basic flour, water, yeast, salt. Dough fought back. Pizza came out like a cracker.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>Learned to knead until the windowpane test passes. Texture transformed.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>Cold ferment for 48 hours. Flavour went from flat to bakery-level.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 4</span><p>Stretched by hand, not rolled. Puffy edges, blistered base, dinner applause.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Ratios that finally worked</summary><div class="body"><p>100% flour, 62% water, 2% salt, 0.5% instant yeast. Mix, rest 20 minutes, knead, then fridge for 2–3 days. Take out an hour before shaping.</p></div></details><div class="callout good">The fridge does the hard work. You just wait.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h2>A Month of Learning to Make Proper Pizza Dough</h2><p class="muted">Four weeks, , and one very patient fridge.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Basic flour, water, yeast, salt. Dough fought back. Pizza came out like a cracker.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>Learned to knead until the windowpane test passes. Texture transformed.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>Cold ferment for 48 hours. Flavour went from flat to bakery-level.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 4</span><p>Stretched by hand, not rolled. Puffy edges, blistered base, dinner applause.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Ratios that finally worked</summary><div class="body"><p>100% flour, 62% water, 2% salt, 0.5% instant yeast. Mix, rest 20 minutes, knead, then fridge for 2–3 days. Take out an hour before shaping.</p></div></details><div class="callout good">The fridge does the hard work. You just wait.</div></div>	-
13.31.1	timeline_view/Replacing Windshield Wiper Blades: One Week, Three Checks	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Fitting a New Wiper Blade — Five Minutes, Three Cars</h3><p class="muted">Rain is coming. So is that noise you've been ignoring since March.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Bought blades online. Wrote the year, make and model on my hand <em>before</em> opening the site. This is the step everyone skips and then repeats.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>Watched the old arm with the wiper sitting <strong>up</strong> — never on the glass. One drop and the arm chips the windshield. Mine stayed up.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>Unclipped the old blade. Heard the click, lifted the tab, slid it off. Ten seconds. Felt like a mechanic. Was not a mechanic.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 4</span><p>New blade clicked in. Peeked under to check the little latch had caught. It hadn't. Second click. Now it had.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 5</span><p>Lowered it slowly onto the glass, ran it wet, then dry. No streaks, no judder. Considered telling everyone at work. Did.</p></li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Fitting a New Wiper Blade — Five Minutes, Three Cars</h3><p class="muted">Rain is coming. So is that noise you've been ignoring since March.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Bought blades online. Wrote the year, make and model on my hand <em>before</em> opening the site. This is the step everyone skips and then repeats.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>Watched the old arm with the wiper sitting <strong>up</strong> — never on the glass. One drop and the arm chips the windshield. Mine stayed up.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>Unclipped the old blade. Heard the click, lifted the tab, slid it off. Ten seconds. Felt like a mechanic. Was not a mechanic.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 4</span><p>New blade clicked in. Peeked under to check the little latch had caught. It hadn't. Second click. Now it had.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 5</span><p>Lowered it slowly onto the glass, ran it wet, then dry. No streaks, no judder. Considered telling everyone at work. Did.</p></li></ul></div>	-
13.32.1	timeline_view/Jump-Starting a Car: Four Attempts and One Close Call	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Learning to Jump-Start a Car: First Four Attempts</h3><div class="callout warn"><p>Do the red-red, black-bare-metal order. Reverse it taking them off. Sparks are not part of the plan.</p></div><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Attempt 1</span><p>Read the order out loud twice: red to dead, red to live, black to live, black to unpainted metal on the dead car. Not to the dead battery's negative. Felt fussy. Was right.</p></li><li><span class="when">Attempt 2</span><p>Clamps on, nothing happened. Checked the clamps, not the cables. Loose jaw. Snugged it. Dash lights came up.</p></li><li><span class="when">Attempt 3</span><p>Let the donor car idle five minutes before trying to start. Cranked. Caught on the second try. Left it running twenty minutes after.</p></li><li><span class="when">Attempt 4</span><p>Removed in reverse, black first, keeping them apart. No drama. Drove twenty minutes to charge it up instead of turning it off at once.</p></li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Learning to Jump-Start a Car: First Four Attempts</h3><div class="callout warn"><p>Do the red-red, black-bare-metal order. Reverse it taking them off. Sparks are not part of the plan.</p></div><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Attempt 1</span><p>Read the order out loud twice: red to dead, red to live, black to live, black to unpainted metal on the dead car. Not to the dead battery's negative. Felt fussy. Was right.</p></li><li><span class="when">Attempt 2</span><p>Clamps on, nothing happened. Checked the clamps, not the cables. Loose jaw. Snugged it. Dash lights came up.</p></li><li><span class="when">Attempt 3</span><p>Let the donor car idle five minutes before trying to start. Cranked. Caught on the second try. Left it running twenty minutes after.</p></li><li><span class="when">Attempt 4</span><p>Removed in reverse, black first, keeping them apart. No drama. Drove twenty minutes to charge it up instead of turning it off at once.</p></li></ul></div>	-
13.33.1	timeline_view/Tyre Pressure Checks: A Fortnight of Small Measurements	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Checking Tyre Pressure: Two Weeks, One Petrol Station</h3><p class="muted">The sticker lives on the driver's door jamb. Not the tyre. Not your memory.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Found the door-jamb sticker. Two numbers, front and rear. Wrote them in my phone notes. The tyre sidewall says something different — that's the maximum, not the target.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 4</span><p>Checked them cold, first thing, before driving. Warm tyres read high and lie to you.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 7</span><p>One tyre was 6 psi low. Not flat. Just quietly costing me fuel for months. Topped it up at the station, took the cap off, pressed, listened for the hiss.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 10</span><p>Twisted the cap back on properly. Bought a £6 gauge for the glovebox so I stop guessing at the station's clunky machine.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 14</span><p>All four held. Same pressure as day seven. Small, boring, and exactly what I wanted.</p></li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Checking Tyre Pressure: Two Weeks, One Petrol Station</h3><p class="muted">The sticker lives on the driver's door jamb. Not the tyre. Not your memory.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Found the door-jamb sticker. Two numbers, front and rear. Wrote them in my phone notes. The tyre sidewall says something different — that's the maximum, not the target.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 4</span><p>Checked them cold, first thing, before driving. Warm tyres read high and lie to you.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 7</span><p>One tyre was 6 psi low. Not flat. Just quietly costing me fuel for months. Topped it up at the station, took the cap off, pressed, listened for the hiss.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 10</span><p>Twisted the cap back on properly. Bought a £6 gauge for the glovebox so I stop guessing at the station's clunky machine.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 14</span><p>All four held. Same pressure as day seven. Small, boring, and exactly what I wanted.</p></li></ul></div>	-
13.34.1	timeline_view/Engine Oil: Seven Days of Dipstick Diplomacy	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Topping Up Engine Oil: One Week, Zero Overfills</h3><div class="callout info"><p>A small funnel costs a pound and saves an engine bay.</p></div><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Parked flat. Engine off. Waited ten minutes so the oil drained back down. Pulled the dipstick, wiped it, dipped again. That second dip is the real reading.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>Reading sat halfway between the two marks. Fine, not urgent. Told myself I'd check again in a week.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 4</span><p>Checked again — it hadn't moved. Halfway is a perfectly good place to be.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 6</span><p>Decided to top it up anyway. Half a litre, a splash at a time, re-dipping between each. Overfilling is worse than being a bit low.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 7</span><p>Back to just under the top mark. Cap screwed on tight. Old rag kept in the boot because this never gets cleaner.</p></li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Topping Up Engine Oil: One Week, Zero Overfills</h3><div class="callout info"><p>A small funnel costs a pound and saves an engine bay.</p></div><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Parked flat. Engine off. Waited ten minutes so the oil drained back down. Pulled the dipstick, wiped it, dipped again. That second dip is the real reading.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>Reading sat halfway between the two marks. Fine, not urgent. Told myself I'd check again in a week.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 4</span><p>Checked again — it hadn't moved. Halfway is a perfectly good place to be.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 6</span><p>Decided to top it up anyway. Half a litre, a splash at a time, re-dipping between each. Overfilling is worse than being a bit low.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 7</span><p>Back to just under the top mark. Cap screwed on tight. Old rag kept in the boot because this never gets cleaner.</p></li></ul></div>	-
13.35.1	timeline_view/Changing a Headlight Bulb: Five Evenings and Some Swearing	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Changing a Car Bulb: Five Evenings, Two Fingers, One Headlight</h3><p class="muted">Halfords will look up the exact bulb. Take the old one in if you can.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Evening 1</span><p>Googled the bulb type for my model year. Ordered two — if one went, the other is not far behind.</p></li><li><span class="when">Evening 2</span><p>Popped the bonnet. Found the back of the headlight housing behind the battery. Space: limited. Patience: also limited.</p></li><li><span class="when">Evening 3</span><p>Watched a video for my exact car. Learned the clip was a wire spring, not a screw. Pushed, released, and the old bulb came free.</p></li><li><span class="when">Evening 4</span><p>New bulb in. Held it by the plastic base only — fingers on the glass shorten its life. Spring clipped back. Connector pressed on.</p></li><li><span class="when">Evening 5</span><p>Lights on, engine off, stood in front of the wall. Both sides matched. Two bulbs done, one spare in the glovebox.</p></li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Changing a Car Bulb: Five Evenings, Two Fingers, One Headlight</h3><p class="muted">Halfords will look up the exact bulb. Take the old one in if you can.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Evening 1</span><p>Googled the bulb type for my model year. Ordered two — if one went, the other is not far behind.</p></li><li><span class="when">Evening 2</span><p>Popped the bonnet. Found the back of the headlight housing behind the battery. Space: limited. Patience: also limited.</p></li><li><span class="when">Evening 3</span><p>Watched a video for my exact car. Learned the clip was a wire spring, not a screw. Pushed, released, and the old bulb came free.</p></li><li><span class="when">Evening 4</span><p>New bulb in. Held it by the plastic base only — fingers on the glass shorten its life. Spring clipped back. Connector pressed on.</p></li><li><span class="when">Evening 5</span><p>Lights on, engine off, stood in front of the wall. Both sides matched. Two bulbs done, one spare in the glovebox.</p></li></ul></div>	-
13.36.1	timeline_view/Coolant Checks: A Fortnight of Cold-Engine Inspections	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Checking Coolant: Two Weeks of Watching a Little Tank</h3><div class="callout bad"><p>Never open the cap when the engine is warm. It is pressurised and it is angry.</p></div><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Found the translucent tank with MIN and MAX lines. Level sat just above MIN. Engine stone cold. Cap stayed on.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 5</span><p>Checked again cold. Same level. Nothing is leaking. Small mercies.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 9</span><p>Bought premixed coolant — no diluting, no guessing. Topped up to between the lines with a funnel and a steady hand.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 12</span><p>Watched for drips on the driveway. A small green puddle would mean a hose or a radiator job. Nothing there.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 14</span><p>Level holding. Cap on. If it drops again quickly, that's a garage visit, not a top-up.</p></li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Checking Coolant: Two Weeks of Watching a Little Tank</h3><div class="callout bad"><p>Never open the cap when the engine is warm. It is pressurised and it is angry.</p></div><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Found the translucent tank with MIN and MAX lines. Level sat just above MIN. Engine stone cold. Cap stayed on.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 5</span><p>Checked again cold. Same level. Nothing is leaking. Small mercies.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 9</span><p>Bought premixed coolant — no diluting, no guessing. Topped up to between the lines with a funnel and a steady hand.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 12</span><p>Watched for drips on the driveway. A small green puddle would mean a hose or a radiator job. Nothing there.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 14</span><p>Level holding. Cap on. If it drops again quickly, that's a garage visit, not a top-up.</p></li></ul></div>	-
