14.18.0	comparison/Pan vs. Pot: Which for Your Kitchen?	<div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Pan</h4><p>Shallow sides. Great for searing, sautéing, frying eggs. Quick evaporation, browning. Usually oven-safe.</p></div><div class="card"><h4>Pot</h4><p>Deep sides. Ideal for soups, stews, pasta, boiling. Holds liquid, reduces splatter. Often comes with a lid.</p></div></div><div class="callout info"><strong>Quick rule:</strong> If you're cooking something you stir and evaporate, use a pan. If you're cooking something you simmer and cover, use a pot.</div>	<div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Pan</h4><p>Shallow sides. Great for searing, sautéing, frying eggs. Quick evaporation, browning. Usually oven-safe.</p></div><div class="card"><h4>Pot</h4><p>Deep sides. Ideal for soups, stews, pasta, boiling. Holds liquid, reduces splatter. Often comes with a lid.</p></div></div><div class="callout info"><strong>Quick rule:</strong> If you're cooking something you stir and evaporate, use a pan. If you're cooking something you simmer and cover, use a pot.</div>	-
14.19.0	comparison/Washing Dishes by Hand vs. Dishwasher	<div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Hand Washing</h4><p>Uses about 20 gallons per load if you leave the tap running. Can be less if you use a basin. Good for delicate items.</p></div><div class="card"><h4>Dishwasher</h4><p>Uses 3–4 gallons per cycle. More water- and energy-efficient for full loads. Not for cast iron or fragile china.</p></div></div><div class="callout good"><strong>Water-wise:</strong> A full dishwasher wins most of the time. But if you only have a few dishes, hand washing with a basin is fine.</div>	<div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Hand Washing</h4><p>Uses about 20 gallons per load if you leave the tap running. Can be less if you use a basin. Good for delicate items.</p></div><div class="card"><h4>Dishwasher</h4><p>Uses 3–4 gallons per cycle. More water- and energy-efficient for full loads. Not for cast iron or fragile china.</p></div></div><div class="callout good"><strong>Water-wise:</strong> A full dishwasher wins most of the time. But if you only have a few dishes, hand washing with a basin is fine.</div>	-
14.20.0	comparison/Solar Panels vs. Wind Turbines for Home	<div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Solar Panels</h4><p>Work in most locations. Low maintenance, no moving parts. Output depends on sunlight and roof orientation.</p></div><div class="card"><h4>Wind Turbines</h4><p>Need consistent wind (at least 10 mph). More maintenance, noise, and zoning hurdles. Better for rural areas.</p></div></div><div class="callout info"><strong>Consider:</strong> Solar is usually the easier start for suburban homes. Wind can complement if you have space and steady breezes.</div>	<div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Solar Panels</h4><p>Work in most locations. Low maintenance, no moving parts. Output depends on sunlight and roof orientation.</p></div><div class="card"><h4>Wind Turbines</h4><p>Need consistent wind (at least 10 mph). More maintenance, noise, and zoning hurdles. Better for rural areas.</p></div></div><div class="callout info"><strong>Consider:</strong> Solar is usually the easier start for suburban homes. Wind can complement if you have space and steady breezes.</div>	-
14.21.0	comparison/Streaming vs. Cable: Which for Your TV?	<div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Streaming</h4><p>On-demand, often cheaper. Watch anywhere. Can add up if you stack services. Needs good internet.</p></div><div class="card"><h4>Cable</h4><p>Live channels, local sports, bundled internet. Higher monthly cost, contracts, set-top boxes. Reliable for live events.</p></div></div><div class="callout info"><strong>Depends on:</strong> If you love live sports and local news, cable might still be worth it. If you mostly watch series and movies, streaming is more flexible.</div>	<div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Streaming</h4><p>On-demand, often cheaper. Watch anywhere. Can add up if you stack services. Needs good internet.</p></div><div class="card"><h4>Cable</h4><p>Live channels, local sports, bundled internet. Higher monthly cost, contracts, set-top boxes. Reliable for live events.</p></div></div><div class="callout info"><strong>Depends on:</strong> If you love live sports and local news, cable might still be worth it. If you mostly watch series and movies, streaming is more flexible.</div>	-
14.22.0	comparison/Running vs. Swimming: Which Workout?	<div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Running</h4><p>High-impact, builds bone density. Needs only shoes. Can be hard on joints. Great for cardio and mood.</p></div><div class="card"><h4>Swimming</h4><p>Low-impact, full-body. Easy on joints. Requires pool access. Excellent for recovery and endurance.</p></div></div><div class="callout good"><strong>Best of both:</strong> Mix them if you can—run for bone health, swim for recovery. Your knees will thank you.</div>	<div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Running</h4><p>High-impact, builds bone density. Needs only shoes. Can be hard on joints. Great for cardio and mood.</p></div><div class="card"><h4>Swimming</h4><p>Low-impact, full-body. Easy on joints. Requires pool access. Excellent for recovery and endurance.</p></div></div><div class="callout good"><strong>Best of both:</strong> Mix them if you can—run for bone health, swim for recovery. Your knees will thank you.</div>	-
14.23.0	comparison/Smartphone Camera vs. DSLR: Which for Photos?	<div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Smartphone</h4><p>Always with you. Great in good light. Computational tricks for low light. Instant sharing.</p></div><div class="card"><h4>DSLR</h4><p>Larger sensor, interchangeable lenses. Better in low light and for action. Manual control. Heavier and pricier.</p></div></div><div class="callout info"><strong>Honest take:</strong> The best camera is the one you have. For casual shots, your phone is plenty. For serious photography, a DSLR still wins.</div>	<div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Smartphone</h4><p>Always with you. Great in good light. Computational tricks for low light. Instant sharing.</p></div><div class="card"><h4>DSLR</h4><p>Larger sensor, interchangeable lenses. Better in low light and for action. Manual control. Heavier and pricier.</p></div></div><div class="callout info"><strong>Honest take:</strong> The best camera is the one you have. For casual shots, your phone is plenty. For serious photography, a DSLR still wins.</div>	-
14.24.0	comparison/Image Stabilization: Lens vs. Camera Body	<div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Lens Stabilization</h4><p>Tuned to the specific lens. Works on any compatible body, even old ones. Great for telephoto. Slightly bulkier and pricier glass.</p><ul class="list"><li>Independent of body</li><li>Often better for long focal lengths</li><li>Adds size and cost to the lens</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>In-Body Stabilization (IBIS)</h4><p>Sensor moves to counteract shake. Works with any lens you mount, even vintage glass. Modern IBIS can hit 5–8 stops.</p><ul class="list"><li>Benefits all lenses</li><li>Usually smaller lenses</li><li>Best on newer mirrorless bodies</li></ul></div></div><div class="callout info"><p>Many systems combine both. If you shoot handheld in low light, IBIS plus a stabilised lens is the smoothest ride.</p></div>	<div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Lens Stabilization</h4><p>Tuned to the specific lens. Works on any compatible body, even old ones. Great for telephoto. Slightly bulkier and pricier glass.</p><ul class="list"><li>Independent of body</li><li>Often better for long focal lengths</li><li>Adds size and cost to the lens</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>In-Body Stabilization (IBIS)</h4><p>Sensor moves to counteract shake. Works with any lens you mount, even vintage glass. Modern IBIS can hit 5–8 stops.</p><ul class="list"><li>Benefits all lenses</li><li>Usually smaller lenses</li><li>Best on newer mirrorless bodies</li></ul></div></div><div class="callout info"><p>Many systems combine both. If you shoot handheld in low light, IBIS plus a stabilised lens is the smoothest ride.</p></div>	-
14.25.0	comparison/Sourdough Starter: Rye vs. All-Purpose Flour	<div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Rye Flour</h4><p>Ferments fast and vigorously. Great for getting a sluggish starter back on its feet. Imparts a tangy, earthy flavor. Can be sticky and rise like a beast.</p><ul class="list"><li>Quick to bubble</li><li>Robust against neglect</li><li>Strong flavor in bread</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>All-Purpose Flour</h4><p>Milder, more predictable. The classic choice for a starter you plan to keep for years. Slower to ferment, but easier to manage.</p><ul class="list"><li>Neutral flavor</li><li>Less fussy</li><li>Works in any recipe</li></ul></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Which should you pick?</summary><div class="body"><p>Start with all-purpose for consistency. Keep a rye side starter for oomph. <regex>user_name</regex>, you can also blend—a spoonful of rye in a white starter gives the best of both.</p></div></details>	<div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Rye Flour</h4><p>Ferments fast and vigorously. Great for getting a sluggish starter back on its feet. Imparts a tangy, earthy flavor. Can be sticky and rise like a beast.</p><ul class="list"><li>Quick to bubble</li><li>Robust against neglect</li><li>Strong flavor in bread</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>All-Purpose Flour</h4><p>Milder, more predictable. The classic choice for a starter you plan to keep for years. Slower to ferment, but easier to manage.</p><ul class="list"><li>Neutral flavor</li><li>Less fussy</li><li>Works in any recipe</li></ul></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Which should you pick?</summary><div class="body"><p>Start with all-purpose for consistency. Keep a rye side starter for oomph. , you can also blend—a spoonful of rye in a white starter gives the best of both.</p></div></details>	-
14.26.0	comparison/Rain Jacket: 2-Layer vs. 3-Layer	<div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>2-Layer</h4><p>Waterproof shell with a separate mesh or taffeta lining inside. Lighter on the wallet, softer feel. The lining can snag and may wear out.</p><ul class="list"><li>More affordable</li><li>Comfortable against skin</li><li>Bulkier and heavier</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>3-Layer</h4><p>Face fabric, membrane, and backer all bonded. Durable, packable, and breathable. Often found in serious alpine gear.</p><ul class="list"><li>More packable</li><li>Better breathability</li><li>Higher price tag</li></ul></div></div><div class="callout good"><p>For daily commuting, 2-layer is plenty. For backpacking or skiing, <regex>user_name</regex> will appreciate 3-layer's durability.</p></div>	<div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>2-Layer</h4><p>Waterproof shell with a separate mesh or taffeta lining inside. Lighter on the wallet, softer feel. The lining can snag and may wear out.</p><ul class="list"><li>More affordable</li><li>Comfortable against skin</li><li>Bulkier and heavier</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>3-Layer</h4><p>Face fabric, membrane, and backer all bonded. Durable, packable, and breathable. Often found in serious alpine gear.</p><ul class="list"><li>More packable</li><li>Better breathability</li><li>Higher price tag</li></ul></div></div><div class="callout good"><p>For daily commuting, 2-layer is plenty. For backpacking or skiing,  will appreciate 3-layer's durability.</p></div>	-
14.27.0	comparison/Natural Deodorant: Cream vs. Stick	<div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Cream</h4><p>Often in a jar, applied with fingers. Usually baking-soda-free options. Can feel moist at first but absorbs quickly. Great for very sensitive skin.</p><ul class="list"><li>Gentle on pits</li><li>Easy to adjust amount</li><li>Some find it messy</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Stick</h4><p>Twist-up tube, glides on like conventional deodorant. Less mess, more portable. May contain baking soda for extra odor control.</p><ul class="list"><li>Convenient</li><li>Travel-friendly</li><li>Can cause irritation for some</li></ul></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Which works better?</summary><div class="body"><p>It depends on your skin and sweat. Try cream if you're sensitive, stick if you want speed. <regex>user_name</regex>, both need a week or two of adjustment.</p></div></details>	<div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Cream</h4><p>Often in a jar, applied with fingers. Usually baking-soda-free options. Can feel moist at first but absorbs quickly. Great for very sensitive skin.</p><ul class="list"><li>Gentle on pits</li><li>Easy to adjust amount</li><li>Some find it messy</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Stick</h4><p>Twist-up tube, glides on like conventional deodorant. Less mess, more portable. May contain baking soda for extra odor control.</p><ul class="list"><li>Convenient</li><li>Travel-friendly</li><li>Can cause irritation for some</li></ul></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Which works better?</summary><div class="body"><p>It depends on your skin and sweat. Try cream if you're sensitive, stick if you want speed. , both need a week or two of adjustment.</p></div></details>	-
14.28.0	comparison/Butter vs. Shortening in Pie Crust	<div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Butter</h4><p>Unbeatable flavor. Creates flaky layers but melts at lower temp, so dough can be finicky. Keep everything cold.</p><ul class="list"><li>Rich taste</li><li>Flaky if handled right</li><li>Needs refrigeration</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Shortening</h4><p>Higher melting point, so it's forgiving and easy to work with. Makes a tender, sturdy crust. But it lacks butter's flavor.</p><ul class="list"><li>Foolproof texture</li><li>Holds shape well</li><li>Bland taste</li></ul></div></div><div class="callout info"><p>Many bakers do half butter, half shortening. Flavor from butter, flakiness from shortening. A win-win.</p></div>	<div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Butter</h4><p>Unbeatable flavor. Creates flaky layers but melts at lower temp, so dough can be finicky. Keep everything cold.</p><ul class="list"><li>Rich taste</li><li>Flaky if handled right</li><li>Needs refrigeration</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Shortening</h4><p>Higher melting point, so it's forgiving and easy to work with. Makes a tender, sturdy crust. But it lacks butter's flavor.</p><ul class="list"><li>Foolproof texture</li><li>Holds shape well</li><li>Bland taste</li></ul></div></div><div class="callout info"><p>Many bakers do half butter, half shortening. Flavor from butter, flakiness from shortening. A win-win.</p></div>	-
14.29.0	comparison/Note-Taking Apps: Notion vs. Obsidian	<div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Notion</h4><p>All-in-one workspace: notes, databases, wikis, project management. Cloud-based, collaborative, and polished. Slower for huge databases.</p><ul class="list"><li>Team-friendly</li><li>Web clipper and API</li><li>Subscription for heavy use</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Obsidian</h4><p>Local-first markdown files, fast, private. Powerful linking and graph view. Steeper learning curve, but endless customization.</p><ul class="list"><li>Offline and fast</li><li>Own your data</li><li>Free for personal use</li></ul></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Which should you choose?</summary><div class="body"><p>If you need collaboration and databases, Notion. If you value speed and privacy, Obsidian. <regex>user_name</regex>, many folks use both—Notion for work, Obsidian for personal.</p></div></details>	<div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Notion</h4><p>All-in-one workspace: notes, databases, wikis, project management. Cloud-based, collaborative, and polished. Slower for huge databases.</p><ul class="list"><li>Team-friendly</li><li>Web clipper and API</li><li>Subscription for heavy use</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Obsidian</h4><p>Local-first markdown files, fast, private. Powerful linking and graph view. Steeper learning curve, but endless customization.</p><ul class="list"><li>Offline and fast</li><li>Own your data</li><li>Free for personal use</li></ul></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Which should you choose?</summary><div class="body"><p>If you need collaboration and databases, Notion. If you value speed and privacy, Obsidian. , many folks use both—Notion for work, Obsidian for personal.</p></div></details>	-
