14.36.0	comparison/Umbrella vs. Rain Jacket: Which for a Commute?	<div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Umbrella</h4><p><strong>Pros:</strong> Keeps head and shoulders dry without trapping heat. Easy to shake off and store. <strong>Cons:</strong> Hands full, flips in wind, drips everywhere.</p><p class="t-small dim">Best for: short walks, warm rain, no wind.</p></div><div class="card"><h4>Rain Jacket</h4><p><strong>Pros:</strong> Hands-free, works in wind, packs small. <strong>Cons:</strong> Can get clammy, needs reproofing, legs still get wet.</p><p class="t-small dim">Best for: biking, long walks, cold rain.</p></div></div><p class="muted">For most commutes under 20 minutes, a compact umbrella wins. Over that, or on a bike, jacket up.</p>	<div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Umbrella</h4><p><strong>Pros:</strong> Keeps head and shoulders dry without trapping heat. Easy to shake off and store. <strong>Cons:</strong> Hands full, flips in wind, drips everywhere.</p><p class="t-small dim">Best for: short walks, warm rain, no wind.</p></div><div class="card"><h4>Rain Jacket</h4><p><strong>Pros:</strong> Hands-free, works in wind, packs small. <strong>Cons:</strong> Can get clammy, needs reproofing, legs still get wet.</p><p class="t-small dim">Best for: biking, long walks, cold rain.</p></div></div><p class="muted">For most commutes under 20 minutes, a compact umbrella wins. Over that, or on a bike, jacket up.</p>	-
14.37.0	comparison/Dryer vs. Clothesline: Which Saves More?	<div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Dryer</h4><p><strong>Pros:</strong> Fast, works in any weather, softens clothes. <strong>Cons:</strong> Uses electricity, can shrink delicates, wears elastic over time.</p><p class="t-small dim">Best for: busy weeks, towels, rainy climates.</p></div><div class="card"><h4>Clothesline</h4><p><strong>Pros:</strong> Free drying, gentle on fabric, fresh smell. <strong>Cons:</strong> Slow, weather-dependent, stiff towels, pollen.</p><p class="t-small dim">Best for: sunny days, delicates, saving money.</p></div></div><p class="muted">A dryer costs roughly $0.50–$1.50 per load. Line drying saves that but takes hours. Many people line dry in summer and dryer in winter.</p>	<div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Dryer</h4><p><strong>Pros:</strong> Fast, works in any weather, softens clothes. <strong>Cons:</strong> Uses electricity, can shrink delicates, wears elastic over time.</p><p class="t-small dim">Best for: busy weeks, towels, rainy climates.</p></div><div class="card"><h4>Clothesline</h4><p><strong>Pros:</strong> Free drying, gentle on fabric, fresh smell. <strong>Cons:</strong> Slow, weather-dependent, stiff towels, pollen.</p><p class="t-small dim">Best for: sunny days, delicates, saving money.</p></div></div><p class="muted">A dryer costs roughly $0.50–$1.50 per load. Line drying saves that but takes hours. Many people line dry in summer and dryer in winter.</p>	-
