14.5.1	comparison/Water Flosser vs. String Floss: Which for Daily Cleaning?	<div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>String Floss</h4><p>Cheap, precise, travels flat. Scrapes plaque off tooth walls where a brush can't. Takes about a minute if you're honest about it. Tight contacts and back molars are the tricky part.</p><span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>Ruthless on plaque</span></div><div class="card"><h4>Water Flosser</h4><p>Gentle on gums, kind to braces and bridges. One tank lasts a full pass. Needs a counter outlet and a refill habit. Loosens debris, but doesn't scrape like floss does.</p><span class="badge info"><i class="dot"></i>Gum-friendly</span></div></div><blockquote class="quote">Floss reaches what a brush can't. A water flosser reaches what floss won't.<cite>- every hygienist, paraphrased</cite></blockquote>	<div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>String Floss</h4><p>Cheap, precise, travels flat. Scrapes plaque off tooth walls where a brush can't. Takes about a minute if you're honest about it. Tight contacts and back molars are the tricky part.</p><span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>Ruthless on plaque</span></div><div class="card"><h4>Water Flosser</h4><p>Gentle on gums, kind to braces and bridges. One tank lasts a full pass. Needs a counter outlet and a refill habit. Loosens debris, but doesn't scrape like floss does.</p><span class="badge info"><i class="dot"></i>Gum-friendly</span></div></div><blockquote class="quote">Floss reaches what a brush can't. A water flosser reaches what floss won't.<cite>- every hygienist, paraphrased</cite></blockquote>	-
14.6.1	comparison/Tap Water vs. Filtered Water: Which for Everyday Drinking?	<div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Tap</h4><p>Free, instant, and regulated. In most cities it meets safety standards straight from the faucet. Taste varies with pipes and chlorine. Zero waste, zero refills.</p><span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>Cheapest by far</span></div><div class="card"><h4>Filtered</h4><p>Pitcher or faucet mount smooths out chlorine and sediment. Costs a few cents a litre. You have to change the cartridge, which is the real test. Cold, clean taste is the reward.</p><span class="badge info"><i class="dot"></i>Taste upgrade</span></div></div><p class="muted t-small">If your tap tastes fine, save the money. If you keep reaching for bottled, a filter pays for itself in a month.</p>	<div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Tap</h4><p>Free, instant, and regulated. In most cities it meets safety standards straight from the faucet. Taste varies with pipes and chlorine. Zero waste, zero refills.</p><span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>Cheapest by far</span></div><div class="card"><h4>Filtered</h4><p>Pitcher or faucet mount smooths out chlorine and sediment. Costs a few cents a litre. You have to change the cartridge, which is the real test. Cold, clean taste is the reward.</p><span class="badge info"><i class="dot"></i>Taste upgrade</span></div></div><p class="muted t-small">If your tap tastes fine, save the money. If you keep reaching for bottled, a filter pays for itself in a month.</p>	-
14.7.1	comparison/Bar Soap vs. Body Wash: Which for the Shower?	<div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Bar Soap</h4><p>One bar lasts weeks. Minimal packaging, easy to travel with. Can feel tight on dry skin if you pick the wrong one. Keep it on a draining dish or it turns to paste.</p><span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>Low waste</span></div><div class="card"><h4>Body Wash</h4><p>Lathers fast, rinses clean, and often has moisturizers built in. Great for sensitive or dry skin. Plastic bottle is the trade-off. A loofah makes a little go a long way.</p><span class="badge info"><i class="dot"></i>Skin-friendly</span></div></div><p class="muted t-small">Both clean you. Pick by skin type and how much plastic you want to carry.</p>	<div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Bar Soap</h4><p>One bar lasts weeks. Minimal packaging, easy to travel with. Can feel tight on dry skin if you pick the wrong one. Keep it on a draining dish or it turns to paste.</p><span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>Low waste</span></div><div class="card"><h4>Body Wash</h4><p>Lathers fast, rinses clean, and often has moisturizers built in. Great for sensitive or dry skin. Plastic bottle is the trade-off. A loofah makes a little go a long way.</p><span class="badge info"><i class="dot"></i>Skin-friendly</span></div></div><p class="muted t-small">Both clean you. Pick by skin type and how much plastic you want to carry.</p>	-
14.8.1	comparison/Ceiling Fan vs. Standing Fan: Which Cools Your Room?	<div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Ceiling Fan</h4><p>Moves air across the whole room. Out of the way, silent on low, and reversible for winter. Needs wiring and a ladder to install. Best if you'll stay put for years.</p><span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>Whole-room breeze</span></div><div class="card"><h4>Standing Fan</h4><p>Plug in and point. Rolls from room to room, no tools needed. Takes floor space and you feel it most when it's aimed right at you. Easy to store in winter.</p><span class="badge info"><i class="dot"></i>No install</span></div></div><p class="muted t-small">Fans cool people, not rooms. Aim for airflow where you sit, not at the thermostat.</p>	<div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Ceiling Fan</h4><p>Moves air across the whole room. Out of the way, silent on low, and reversible for winter. Needs wiring and a ladder to install. Best if you'll stay put for years.</p><span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>Whole-room breeze</span></div><div class="card"><h4>Standing Fan</h4><p>Plug in and point. Rolls from room to room, no tools needed. Takes floor space and you feel it most when it's aimed right at you. Easy to store in winter.</p><span class="badge info"><i class="dot"></i>No install</span></div></div><p class="muted t-small">Fans cool people, not rooms. Aim for airflow where you sit, not at the thermostat.</p>	-
14.9.1	comparison/Reusable Water Bottle vs. Disposable: Which to Carry?	<div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Reusable</h4><p>One bottle, years of use. Pays for itself in about two weeks of bottled water. You control the water source and the temperature. Needs washing, and you have to remember it.</p><span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>Cheaper long-term</span></div><div class="card"><h4>Disposable</h4><p>Grab and go, no washing, no planning. Convenient at events and on road trips. Costs add up fast, and the plastic pile does too. Recycling helps but isn't a full fix.</p><span class="badge warn"><i class="dot"></i>Convenience tax</span></div></div><p class="muted t-small">Keep a reusable bottle by the door and you'll stop buying plastic out of habit, not virtue.</p>	<div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Reusable</h4><p>One bottle, years of use. Pays for itself in about two weeks of bottled water. You control the water source and the temperature. Needs washing, and you have to remember it.</p><span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>Cheaper long-term</span></div><div class="card"><h4>Disposable</h4><p>Grab and go, no washing, no planning. Convenient at events and on road trips. Costs add up fast, and the plastic pile does too. Recycling helps but isn't a full fix.</p><span class="badge warn"><i class="dot"></i>Convenience tax</span></div></div><p class="muted t-small">Keep a reusable bottle by the door and you'll stop buying plastic out of habit, not virtue.</p>	-
14.10.1	comparison/LED vs. Incandescent Bulbs: Which for Your Home?	<div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>LED</h4><p>Uses a fraction of the power and lasts years. Cool to the touch, dimmable with the right switch, and comes in warm or cool tones. Costs more up front, less every month after.</p><span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>Energy sipper</span></div><div class="card"><h4>Incandescent</h4><p>Cheap to buy, warm glow, instantly bright. Most of the energy leaves as heat, not light. Burns out fast and adds to the cooling bill in summer. Still handy in the garage or oven hood.</p><span class="badge warn"><i class="dot"></i>Heat-heavy</span></div></div><p class="muted t-small">Swap the five bulbs you leave on longest. That's where the savings actually live.</p>	<div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>LED</h4><p>Uses a fraction of the power and lasts years. Cool to the touch, dimmable with the right switch, and comes in warm or cool tones. Costs more up front, less every month after.</p><span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>Energy sipper</span></div><div class="card"><h4>Incandescent</h4><p>Cheap to buy, warm glow, instantly bright. Most of the energy leaves as heat, not light. Burns out fast and adds to the cooling bill in summer. Still handy in the garage or oven hood.</p><span class="badge warn"><i class="dot"></i>Heat-heavy</span></div></div><p class="muted t-small">Swap the five bulbs you leave on longest. That's where the savings actually live.</p>	-
