21.5.0	illustration/Choosing a Book to Read	<article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>What's the last book you couldn't put down, <regex>user_name</regex>? That's your clue.</p><ul class="list"><li>If you liked <em>Gone Girl</em>, try <em>The Silent Patient</em>.</li><li>If you liked <em>Educated</em>, try <em>Know My Name</em>.</li><li>If you liked <em>The Martian</em>, try <em>Project Hail Mary</em>.</li></ul><div class="callout info"><p>Short on time? Aim for under 300 pages. No shame in a slim volume.</p></div></article>	<article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>What's the last book you couldn't put down, ? That's your clue.</p><ul class="list"><li>If you liked <em>Gone Girl</em>, try <em>The Silent Patient</em>.</li><li>If you liked <em>Educated</em>, try <em>Know My Name</em>.</li><li>If you liked <em>The Martian</em>, try <em>Project Hail Mary</em>.</li></ul><div class="callout info"><p>Short on time? Aim for under 300 pages. No shame in a slim volume.</p></div></article>	-
21.6.0	illustration/Making Pour-Over Coffee	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Pour-Over Coffee, Simplified</h3>  <div class="row between">    <span class="badge accent">cached match</span>    <span class="t-small muted">4 min read</span>  </div>  <p>Hey <regex>user_name</regex>, here's a no-fuss pour-over that tastes like you tried harder than you did.</p>  <div class="chart">    <svg viewBox="0 0 200 120">      <circle cx="60" cy="30" r="15" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"></circle>      <rect x="55" y="45" width="10" height="50" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"></rect>      <path d="M45 95 L75 95 L65 115 L55 115 Z" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"></path>      <path d="M85 60 Q100 70 85 80" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2" class="line"></path>      <text x="130" y="70" class="lbl">~3 min</text>    </svg>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Heat 350g water to just off boil (200°F).</li>    <li>Rinse filter, add 22g medium-ground coffee.</li>    <li>Bloom with 50g water, wait 30s.</li>    <li>Pour slowly in circles, finishing by 2:30.</li>  </ol>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Pro tip: the swirl</summary>    <div class="body">After the final pour, give the brewer a gentle swirl to level the bed. It makes extraction even.</div>  </details>  <div class="callout info">    <p class="t-small">No scale? 22g is about 2.5 tablespoons. Water: a mug and a half.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Pour-Over Coffee, Simplified</h3>  <div class="row between">    <span class="badge accent">cached match</span>    <span class="t-small muted">4 min read</span>  </div>  <p>Hey , here's a no-fuss pour-over that tastes like you tried harder than you did.</p>  <div class="chart">    <svg viewBox="0 0 200 120">      <circle cx="60" cy="30" r="15" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"></circle>      <rect x="55" y="45" width="10" height="50" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"></rect>      <path d="M45 95 L75 95 L65 115 L55 115 Z" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"></path>      <path d="M85 60 Q100 70 85 80" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2" class="line"></path>      <text x="130" y="70" class="lbl">~3 min</text>    </svg>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Heat 350g water to just off boil (200°F).</li>    <li>Rinse filter, add 22g medium-ground coffee.</li>    <li>Bloom with 50g water, wait 30s.</li>    <li>Pour slowly in circles, finishing by 2:30.</li>  </ol>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Pro tip: the swirl</summary>    <div class="body">After the final pour, give the brewer a gentle swirl to level the bed. It makes extraction even.</div>  </details>  <div class="callout info">    <p class="t-small">No scale? 22g is about 2.5 tablespoons. Water: a mug and a half.</p>  </div></div>	-
21.7.0	illustration/Tying a Tie	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">The Four-in-Hand Knot</h3>  <p><regex>user_name</regex>, this is the knot that looks sharp and forgives a little mess.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat">      <span class="label">Difficulty</span>      <span class="value">Easy</span>    </div>    <div class="stat">      <span class="label">Time</span>      <span class="value">30s</span>    </div>  </div>  <div class="chart">    <svg viewBox="0 0 120 120">      <path d="M40 20 L60 50 L80 20 Z" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"></path>      <path d="M60 50 L60 100" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="4"></path>      <circle cx="60" cy="50" r="8" fill="var(--panel)" stroke="var(--accent)"></circle>      <text x="90" y="60" class="lbl">knot</text>    </svg>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Drape tie around neck, seam side down. Wide end on your dominant side, about 12 inches lower.</li>    <li>Cross wide end over narrow end.</li>    <li>Bring wide end up through the loop around your neck.</li>    <li>Wrap wide end across the front of the knot.</li>    <li>Pull wide end up through the loop again, then down through the front knot.</li>    <li>Tighten and adjust.</li>  </ol>  <details class="acc">    <summary>If it looks lumpy</summary>    <div class="body">Undo and start over with a smoother crossing. The knot should be snug but not choking.</div>  </details></div>	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">The Four-in-Hand Knot</h3>  <p>, this is the knot that looks sharp and forgives a little mess.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat">      <span class="label">Difficulty</span>      <span class="value">Easy</span>    </div>    <div class="stat">      <span class="label">Time</span>      <span class="value">30s</span>    </div>  </div>  <div class="chart">    <svg viewBox="0 0 120 120">      <path d="M40 20 L60 50 L80 20 Z" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"></path>      <path d="M60 50 L60 100" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="4"></path>      <circle cx="60" cy="50" r="8" fill="var(--panel)" stroke="var(--accent)"></circle>      <text x="90" y="60" class="lbl">knot</text>    </svg>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Drape tie around neck, seam side down. Wide end on your dominant side, about 12 inches lower.</li>    <li>Cross wide end over narrow end.</li>    <li>Bring wide end up through the loop around your neck.</li>    <li>Wrap wide end across the front of the knot.</li>    <li>Pull wide end up through the loop again, then down through the front knot.</li>    <li>Tighten and adjust.</li>  </ol>  <details class="acc">    <summary>If it looks lumpy</summary>    <div class="body">Undo and start over with a smoother crossing. The knot should be snug but not choking.</div>  </details></div>	-
21.8.0	illustration/Boiling an Egg	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Boiled Eggs, Your Way</h3>  <div class="row between">    <span class="badge good">cached match</span>    <span class="t-small muted">timing chart</span>  </div>  <p><regex>user_name</regex>, the egg is a tiny, patient teacher. Here's the cheat sheet.</p>  <div class="table-wrap">    <table>      <thead>        <tr><th>Doneness</th><th class="num">Time (min)</th></tr>      </thead>      <tbody>        <tr><td>Soft-boiled (runny yolk)</td><td class="num">6</td></tr>        <tr><td>Medium (jammy yolk)</td><td class="num">8</td></tr>        <tr><td>Hard-boiled</td><td class="num">10</td></tr>      </tbody>    </table>  </div>  <div class="chart">    <svg viewBox="0 0 150 100">      <ellipse cx="50" cy="50" rx="30" ry="40" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"></ellipse>      <circle cx="50" cy="50" r="15" fill="var(--accent)"></circle>      <text x="100" y="55" class="lbl">yolk</text>    </svg>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Place eggs in a pot, cover with cold water by an inch.</li>    <li>Bring to a rolling boil over high heat.</li>    <li>Cover, remove from heat, and set a timer.</li>    <li>Transfer to ice water to stop cooking.</li>  </ol>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Peeling tip</summary>    <div class="body">Tap the egg on the counter, roll gently, and peel under running water. Older eggs peel easier.</div>  </details></div>	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Boiled Eggs, Your Way</h3>  <div class="row between">    <span class="badge good">cached match</span>    <span class="t-small muted">timing chart</span>  </div>  <p>, the egg is a tiny, patient teacher. Here's the cheat sheet.</p>  <div class="table-wrap">    <table>      <thead>        <tr><th>Doneness</th><th class="num">Time (min)</th></tr>      </thead>      <tbody>        <tr><td>Soft-boiled (runny yolk)</td><td class="num">6</td></tr>        <tr><td>Medium (jammy yolk)</td><td class="num">8</td></tr>        <tr><td>Hard-boiled</td><td class="num">10</td></tr>      </tbody>    </table>  </div>  <div class="chart">    <svg viewBox="0 0 150 100">      <ellipse cx="50" cy="50" rx="30" ry="40" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"></ellipse>      <circle cx="50" cy="50" r="15" fill="var(--accent)"></circle>      <text x="100" y="55" class="lbl">yolk</text>    </svg>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Place eggs in a pot, cover with cold water by an inch.</li>    <li>Bring to a rolling boil over high heat.</li>    <li>Cover, remove from heat, and set a timer.</li>    <li>Transfer to ice water to stop cooking.</li>  </ol>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Peeling tip</summary>    <div class="body">Tap the egg on the counter, roll gently, and peel under running water. Older eggs peel easier.</div>  </details></div>	-
21.9.0	illustration/Removing Red Wine Stains	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Red Wine Stain Rescue</h3>  <p><regex>user_name</regex>, act fast. Blot, don't rub. You've got this.</p>  <div class="callout warn">    <p class="t-small">Never rub — it pushes the stain deeper. Blot from the outside in.</p>  </div>  <div class="chart">    <svg viewBox="0 0 200 100">      <path d="M50 30 Q50 70 70 70 Q90 70 90 30 Z" fill="var(--bad-soft)" stroke="var(--bad)"></path>      <path d="M70 70 L70 90" stroke="var(--bad)" stroke-width="4"></path>      <text x="110" y="60" class="lbl">blot!</text>    </svg>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Blot up as much wine as possible with a clean cloth.</li>    <li>Cover the stain with salt or baking soda to absorb remaining liquid.</li>    <li>For fabric: stretch over a bowl and pour boiling water from a height.</li>    <li>For carpet: mix 1 tbsp dish soap, 1 tbsp white vinegar, 2 cups warm water. Blot, then rinse.</li>    <li>Repeat until stain lifts.</li>  </ol>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What about old stains?</summary>    <div class="body">Try a paste of baking soda and water. Let it sit for 30 minutes, then blot and rinse.</div>  </details></div>	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Red Wine Stain Rescue</h3>  <p>, act fast. Blot, don't rub. You've got this.</p>  <div class="callout warn">    <p class="t-small">Never rub — it pushes the stain deeper. Blot from the outside in.</p>  </div>  <div class="chart">    <svg viewBox="0 0 200 100">      <path d="M50 30 Q50 70 70 70 Q90 70 90 30 Z" fill="var(--bad-soft)" stroke="var(--bad)"></path>      <path d="M70 70 L70 90" stroke="var(--bad)" stroke-width="4"></path>      <text x="110" y="60" class="lbl">blot!</text>    </svg>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Blot up as much wine as possible with a clean cloth.</li>    <li>Cover the stain with salt or baking soda to absorb remaining liquid.</li>    <li>For fabric: stretch over a bowl and pour boiling water from a height.</li>    <li>For carpet: mix 1 tbsp dish soap, 1 tbsp white vinegar, 2 cups warm water. Blot, then rinse.</li>    <li>Repeat until stain lifts.</li>  </ol>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What about old stains?</summary>    <div class="body">Try a paste of baking soda and water. Let it sit for 30 minutes, then blot and rinse.</div>  </details></div>	-
21.10.0	illustration/Packing a Suitcase	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Pack a Suitcase Like a Pro</h3>  <div class="row between">    <span class="badge accent">cached match</span>    <span class="t-small muted">save space</span>  </div>  <p><regex>user_name</regex>, here's how to fit a week into a carry-on without sitting on it.</p>  <div class="chart">    <svg viewBox="0 0 200 120">      <rect x="40" y="20" width="120" height="80" rx="10" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"></rect>      <line x1="40" y1="40" x2="160" y2="40" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"></line>      <line x1="40" y1="80" x2="160" y2="80" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"></line>      <text x="100" y="60" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">clothes</text>    </svg>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Lay clothes flat and roll them tightly — rolls fill gaps better than folds.</li>    <li>Put heavy items at the bottom, near the wheels.</li>    <li>Stuff socks and underwear into shoes.</li>    <li>Use packing cubes or zip bags to compress and organize.</li>    <li>Keep essentials (meds, charger, one outfit) in your carry-on.</li>  </ol>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Wrinkle-free trick</summary>    <div class="body">Roll with tissue paper between layers, or use the bundle method: wrap clothes around a core item.</div>  </details></div>	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Pack a Suitcase Like a Pro</h3>  <div class="row between">    <span class="badge accent">cached match</span>    <span class="t-small muted">save space</span>  </div>  <p>, here's how to fit a week into a carry-on without sitting on it.</p>  <div class="chart">    <svg viewBox="0 0 200 120">      <rect x="40" y="20" width="120" height="80" rx="10" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"></rect>      <line x1="40" y1="40" x2="160" y2="40" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"></line>      <line x1="40" y1="80" x2="160" y2="80" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"></line>      <text x="100" y="60" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">clothes</text>    </svg>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Lay clothes flat and roll them tightly — rolls fill gaps better than folds.</li>    <li>Put heavy items at the bottom, near the wheels.</li>    <li>Stuff socks and underwear into shoes.</li>    <li>Use packing cubes or zip bags to compress and organize.</li>    <li>Keep essentials (meds, charger, one outfit) in your carry-on.</li>  </ol>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Wrinkle-free trick</summary>    <div class="body">Roll with tissue paper between layers, or use the bundle method: wrap clothes around a core item.</div>  </details></div>	-
21.11.0	illustration/Jump-Starting a Car	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Jump-Start a Car Safely</h3>  <div class="callout bad">    <p><strong>Safety first:</strong> Never let the red and black clamps touch. Wear eye protection if you have it.</p>  </div>  <p><regex>user_name</regex>, dead battery? Here's the order that keeps sparks away.</p>  <div class="chart">    <svg viewBox="0 0 240 100">      <rect x="20" y="30" width="60" height="40" rx="5" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"></rect>      <rect x="160" y="30" width="60" height="40" rx="5" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"></rect>      <line x1="80" y1="40" x2="160" y2="40" stroke="var(--good)" stroke-width="4" stroke-dasharray="4"></line>      <line x1="80" y1="60" x2="160" y2="60" stroke="var(--bad)" stroke-width="4" stroke-dasharray="4"></line>      <text x="120" y="20" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">red to red</text>      <text x="120" y="90" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">black to ground</text>    </svg>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Park the good car close, engines off. Set both parking brakes.</li>    <li>Connect red clamp to positive (+) of dead battery.</li>    <li>Connect other red clamp to positive (+) of good battery.</li>    <li>Connect black clamp to negative (-) of good battery.</li>    <li>Connect other black clamp to a bare metal bolt on the dead car's engine block (not the battery).</li>    <li>Start the good car, let it run a few minutes, then start the dead car.</li>    <li>Once running, remove clamps in reverse order.</li>  </ol>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Why not connect black to dead battery?</summary>    <div class="body">The last connection can spark. Connecting to the engine block moves the spark away from battery gases.</div>  </details></div>	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Jump-Start a Car Safely</h3>  <div class="callout bad">    <p><strong>Safety first:</strong> Never let the red and black clamps touch. Wear eye protection if you have it.</p>  </div>  <p>, dead battery? Here's the order that keeps sparks away.</p>  <div class="chart">    <svg viewBox="0 0 240 100">      <rect x="20" y="30" width="60" height="40" rx="5" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"></rect>      <rect x="160" y="30" width="60" height="40" rx="5" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"></rect>      <line x1="80" y1="40" x2="160" y2="40" stroke="var(--good)" stroke-width="4" stroke-dasharray="4"></line>      <line x1="80" y1="60" x2="160" y2="60" stroke="var(--bad)" stroke-width="4" stroke-dasharray="4"></line>      <text x="120" y="20" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">red to red</text>      <text x="120" y="90" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">black to ground</text>    </svg>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Park the good car close, engines off. Set both parking brakes.</li>    <li>Connect red clamp to positive (+) of dead battery.</li>    <li>Connect other red clamp to positive (+) of good battery.</li>    <li>Connect black clamp to negative (-) of good battery.</li>    <li>Connect other black clamp to a bare metal bolt on the dead car's engine block (not the battery).</li>    <li>Start the good car, let it run a few minutes, then start the dead car.</li>    <li>Once running, remove clamps in reverse order.</li>  </ol>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Why not connect black to dead battery?</summary>    <div class="body">The last connection can spark. Connecting to the engine block moves the spark away from battery gases.</div>  </details></div>	-
21.12.0	illustration/Writing a Thank-You Note	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Writing a Thank-You Note</p>  <p class="muted t-small">Warm, short, and specific. <regex>user_name</regex>, five sentences is plenty.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Greet.</strong> "Dear Sam," is fine. Match how you actually talk.</li>    <li><strong>Name the gift or act.</strong> "Thank you for the coffee grinder."</li>    <li><strong>Say what it means.</strong> "My mornings just got louder and better."</li>    <li><strong>Add one detail that's only true for you.</strong> That's the part people keep.</li>    <li><strong>Close.</strong> "Love, <regex>user_name</regex>" or "Thanks again, <regex>user_name</regex>."</li>  </ol>  <details class="acc">    <summary>A sentence you can borrow</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>"Thank you for the <em>thing</em>. I used it <em>this specific way</em> and thought of you."</p>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout info">Send within two weeks. Late beats never — the note still lands.</div>  <div class="chart">    <svg viewBox="0 0 120 60">      <rect x="14" y="12" width="80" height="36" rx="4" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line-2)"/>      <path d="M14 14 L54 38 L94 14" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"/>      <path d="M94 12 L108 30 L94 48" fill="none" stroke="var(--line-2)" stroke-width="2"/>      <text x="54" y="57" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">say it plainly</text>    </svg>  </div></div>	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Writing a Thank-You Note</p>  <p class="muted t-small">Warm, short, and specific. , five sentences is plenty.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Greet.</strong> "Dear Sam," is fine. Match how you actually talk.</li>    <li><strong>Name the gift or act.</strong> "Thank you for the coffee grinder."</li>    <li><strong>Say what it means.</strong> "My mornings just got louder and better."</li>    <li><strong>Add one detail that's only true 