21.13.0	illustration/Fixing a Stripped Screw	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Fixing a Stripped Screw</p>  <p class="muted t-small">Don't dig. <regex>user_name</regex>, digging widens the hole and makes it worse.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Rubber band.</strong> Lay a wide rubber band over the head, press hard, turn slowly. Grips the stripped edges.</li>    <li><strong>Bigger bit.</strong> Step up one size so the driver bites fresh metal.</li>    <li><strong>Left-hand drill bit.</strong> Spins counter-clockwise, so it often backs the screw out on its own.</li>    <li><strong>Screw extractor.</strong> Drill a small pilot hole, then twist the extractor in with a wrench.</li>    <li><strong>Last resort.</strong> Cut a slot with a rotary tool and use a flathead.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout warn">Safety glasses. Tiny metal shavings have terrible aim.</div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Why screws strip in the first place</summary>    <div class="body"><p>Usually three things: the wrong bit size, too little downward pressure, or a bitator bit that's rounded off at the tip. Replace worn bits early.</p></div>  </details>  <div class="chart">    <svg viewBox="0 0 120 60">      <circle cx="40" cy="30" r="16" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line-2)"/>      <path d="M40 14 L40 46 M24 30 L56 30" stroke="var(--bad)" stroke-width="2"/>      <path d="M52 20 L64 30 L52 40" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"/>      <text x="90" y="34" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">counter-rotate</text>    </svg>  </div></div>	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Fixing a Stripped Screw</p>  <p class="muted t-small">Don't dig. , digging widens the hole and makes it worse.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Rubber band.</strong> Lay a wide rubber band over the head, press hard, turn slowly. Grips the stripped edges.</li>    <li><strong>Bigger bit.</strong> Step up one size so the driver bites fresh metal.</li>    <li><strong>Left-hand drill bit.</strong> Spins counter-clockwise, so it often backs the screw out on its own.</li>    <li><strong>Screw extractor.</strong> Drill a small pilot hole, then twist the extractor in with a wrench.</li>    <li><strong>Last resort.</strong> Cut a slot with a rotary tool and use a flathead.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout warn">Safety glasses. Tiny metal shavings have terrible aim.</div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Why screws strip in the first place</summary>    <div class="body"><p>Usually three things: the wrong bit size, too little downward pressure, or a bitator bit that's rounded off at the tip. Replace worn bits early.</p></div>  </details>  <div class="chart">    <svg viewBox="0 0 120 60">      <circle cx="40" cy="30" r="16" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line-2)"/>      <path d="M40 14 L40 46 M24 30 L56 30" stroke="var(--bad)" stroke-width="2"/>      <path d="M52 20 L64 30 L52 40" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"/>      <text x="90" y="34" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">counter-rotate</text>    </svg>  </div></div>	-
21.14.0	illustration/Reading a Nutrition Label	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Reading a Nutrition Label</p>  <p class="muted t-small">Top-down is the trick, <regex>user_name</regex>. Every number depends on the one above it.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Serving size.</strong> Read it first. A "150-calorie" bag often holds 2.5 servings.</li>    <li><strong>Calories per serving</strong>, not per package.</li>    <li><strong>Added sugars.</strong> Naturally occurring sugar and spooned-in sugar are listed separately now.</li>    <li><strong>Fiber and protein.</strong> Higher here usually means it keeps you full longer.</li>    <li><strong>Sodium.</strong> Soups, sauces, and breads hide most of it.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout good">Short ingredient list you can picture? Good sign. Fifty unpronounceable things? Ask why.</div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>"% Daily Value" in one line</summary>    <div class="body"><p>It's based on a 2,000-calorie day. 5% is low, 20% is high. Use it for comparison, not judgment.</p></div>  </details>  <div class="chart">    <svg viewBox="0 0 120 60">      <rect x="20" y="10" width="80" height="42" rx="4" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line-2)"/>      <path d="M26 20 H94 M26 30 H74 M26 40 H60" stroke="var(--ink-3)" stroke-width="2"/>      <circle cx="26" cy="20" r="3" fill="var(--accent)"/>      <text x="104" y="55" text-anchor="end" class="lbl">start here</text>    </svg>  </div></div>	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Reading a Nutrition Label</p>  <p class="muted t-small">Top-down is the trick, . Every number depends on the one above it.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Serving size.</strong> Read it first. A "150-calorie" bag often holds 2.5 servings.</li>    <li><strong>Calories per serving</strong>, not per package.</li>    <li><strong>Added sugars.</strong> Naturally occurring sugar and spooned-in sugar are listed separately now.</li>    <li><strong>Fiber and protein.</strong> Higher here usually means it keeps you full longer.</li>    <li><strong>Sodium.</strong> Soups, sauces, and breads hide most of it.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout good">Short ingredient list you can picture? Good sign. Fifty unpronounceable things? Ask why.</div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>"% Daily Value" in one line</summary>    <div class="body"><p>It's based on a 2,000-calorie day. 5% is low, 20% is high. Use it for comparison, not judgment.</p></div>  </details>  <div class="chart">    <svg viewBox="0 0 120 60">      <rect x="20" y="10" width="80" height="42" rx="4" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line-2)"/>      <path d="M26 20 H94 M26 30 H74 M26 40 H60" stroke="var(--ink-3)" stroke-width="2"/>      <circle cx="26" cy="20" r="3" fill="var(--accent)"/>      <text x="104" y="55" text-anchor="end" class="lbl">start here</text>    </svg>  </div></div>	-
21.15.0	illustration/Cleaning a Cast-Iron Skillet	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Cleaning a Cast-Iron Skillet</p>  <p class="muted t-small">Short version, <regex>user_name</regex>: wash, dry fast, oil thin, done.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Wash while warm.</strong> Hot water, stiff brush. A little mild soap is fine on modern seasoning.</li>    <li><strong>Scrub stuck bits</strong> with coarse salt and a splash of oil — no steel wool.</li>    <li><strong>Dry on the stove.</strong> Low heat for a couple minutes until every drop is gone. Rust starts in moisture.</li>    <li><strong>Oil thin.</strong> A few drops of neutral oil, wiped with a paper towel until it looks almost dry.</li>    <li><strong>Store dry.</strong> A folded paper towel between stacked pans keeps air moving.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout warn">Don't soak it. Don't air-dry it. Cast iron holds grudges.</div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What if it's already rusty?</summary>    <div class="body"><p>Scrub the rust off with steel wool or fine sandpaper, wash, dry, then re-season: thin oil, 45 minutes in a 450°F oven, upside down.</p></div>  </details>  <div class="chart">    <svg viewBox="0 0 120 60">      <ellipse cx="50" cy="32" rx="28" ry="14" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line-2)"/>      <path d="M78 32 H96" stroke="var(--ink-3)" stroke-width="4" stroke-linecap="round"/>      <circle cx="44" cy="28" r="2" fill="var(--accent)"/>      <circle cx="56" cy="36" r="2" fill="var(--accent)"/>      <text x="60" y="57" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">thin coat wins</text>    </svg>  </div></div>	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Cleaning a Cast-Iron Skillet</p>  <p class="muted t-small">Short version, : wash, dry fast, oil thin, done.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Wash while warm.</strong> Hot water, stiff brush. A little mild soap is fine on modern seasoning.</li>    <li><strong>Scrub stuck bits</strong> with coarse salt and a splash of oil — no steel wool.</li>    <li><strong>Dry on the stove.</strong> Low heat for a couple minutes until every drop is gone. Rust starts in moisture.</li>    <li><strong>Oil thin.</strong> A few drops of neutral oil, wiped with a paper towel until it looks almost dry.</li>    <li><strong>Store dry.</strong> A folded paper towel between stacked pans keeps air moving.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout warn">Don't soak it. Don't air-dry it. Cast iron holds grudges.</div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What if it's already rusty?</summary>    <div class="body"><p>Scrub the rust off with steel wool or fine sandpaper, wash, dry, then re-season: thin oil, 45 minutes in a 450°F oven, upside down.</p></div>  </details>  <div class="chart">    <svg viewBox="0 0 120 60">      <ellipse cx="50" cy="32" rx="28" ry="14" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line-2)"/>      <path d="M78 32 H96" stroke="var(--ink-3)" stroke-width="4" stroke-linecap="round"/>      <circle cx="44" cy="28" r="2" fill="var(--accent)"/>      <circle cx="56" cy="36" r="2" fill="var(--accent)"/>      <text x="60" y="57" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">thin coat wins</text>    </svg>  </div></div>	-
21.16.0	illustration/Taking a Passport Photo at Home	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Taking a Passport Photo at Home</p>  <p class="muted t-small">Rules first, camera second. <regex>user_name</regex>, one wrong detail means a do-over.</p>  <ul class="list">    <li><strong>Plain white background.</strong> A light wall or a sheet, no shadows behind you.</li>    <li><strong>Face the light.</strong> Soft, even, no flash glare and no side shadows.</li>    <li><strong>Neutral face.</strong> Eyes open, mouth closed, both eyes visible, no glasses glare.</li>    <li><strong>Head size matters.</strong> The head should fill roughly 50–69% of the frame height.</li>    <li><strong>No filters, no sunglasses,</strong> no hats unless worn for religious reasons.</li>  </ul>  <div class="callout info">Most countries want 2×2 in (51×51 mm). Check your country's exact size before printing.</div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Getting the shot without another person</summary>    <div class="body"><p>Phone on a tripod or stack of books at eye level, timer on, stand back and lean in slightly. Take twenty, pick the calm one.</p></div>  </details>  <div class="chart">    <svg viewBox="0 0 120 60">      <rect x="34" y="6" width="52" height="48" rx="3" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line-2)"/>      <circle cx="60" cy="26" r="11" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"/>      <path d="M46 52 Q60 40 74 52" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)"/>      <text x="60" y="62" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">head fills frame</text>    </svg>  </div></div>	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Taking a Passport Photo at Home</p>  <p class="muted t-small">Rules first, camera second. , one wrong detail means a do-over.</p>  <ul class="list">    <li><strong>Plain white background.</strong> A light wall or a sheet, no shadows behind you.</li>    <li><strong>Face the light.</strong> Soft, even, no flash glare and no side shadows.</li>    <li><strong>Neutral face.</strong> Eyes open, mouth closed, both eyes visible, no glasses glare.</li>    <li><strong>Head size matters.</strong> The head should fill roughly 50–69% of the frame height.</li>    <li><strong>No filters, no sunglasses,</strong> no hats unless worn for religious reasons.</li>  </ul>  <div class="callout info">Most countries want 2×2 in (51×51 mm). Check your country's exact size before printing.</div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Getting the shot without another person</summary>    <div class="body"><p>Phone on a tripod or stack of books at eye level, timer on, stand back and lean in slightly. Take twenty, pick the calm one.</p></div>  </details>  <div class="chart">    <svg viewBox="0 0 120 60">      <rect x="34" y="6" width="52" height="48" rx="3" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line-2)"/>      <circle cx="60" cy="26" r="11" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"/>      <path d="M46 52 Q60 40 74 52" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)"/>      <text x="60" y="62" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">head fills frame</text>    </svg>  </div></div>	-
21.17.0	illustration/Getting Rid of Fruit Flies	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Getting Rid of Fruit Flies</p>  <p class="muted t-small">Lure them, then cut off the nursery. <regex>user_name</regex>, both halves matter.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Apple cider vinegar trap.</strong> A small jar, splash of vinegar, drop of dish soap. They land and sink.</li>    <li><strong>Cover the trap with plastic wrap</strong> and poke a few holes if you want to see results, not flies escaping.</li>    <li><strong>Hunt the source.</strong> Overripe bananas, the compost pail, a forgotten potato in the back of the drawer.</li>    <li><strong>Clean drains.</strong> Pour boiling water, then scrub the sides. They breed in gunk.</li>    <li><strong>Rinse recyclables.</strong> Bottles and cans with residue are fruit-fly nurseries.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout good">A trap catches adults. Removing the source stops the next generation.</div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>One fly or a swarm?</summary>    <div class="body"><p>A swarm means an active breeding site within a few feet. Check under the fridge, behind the bin, and inside the dishwasher filter.</p></div>  </details>  <div class="chart">    <svg viewBox="0 0 120 60">      <path d="M40 10 L80 10 L70 50 L50 50 Z" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line-2)"/>      <circle cx="60" cy="48" r="8" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"/>      <circle cx="56" cy="24" r="2" fill="var(--ink-3)"/>      <circle cx="66" cy="30" r="2" fill="var(--ink-3)"/>      <text x="60" y="60" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">they just walk in</text>    </svg>  </div></div>	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Getting Rid of Fruit Flies</p>  <p class="muted t-small">Lure them, then cut off the nursery. , both halves matter.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Apple cider vinegar trap.</strong> A small jar, splash of vinegar, drop of dish soap. They land and sink.</li>    <li><strong>Cover the trap with plastic wrap</strong> and poke a few holes if you want to see results, not flies escaping.</li>    <li><strong>Hunt the source.</strong> Overripe bananas, the compost pail, a forgotten potato in the back of the drawer.</li>    <li><strong>Clean drains.</strong> Pour boiling water, then scrub the sides. They breed in gunk.</li>    <li><strong>Rinse recyclables.</strong> Bottles and cans with residue are fruit-fly nurseries.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout good">A trap catches adults. Removing the source stops the next generation.</div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>One fly or a swarm?</summary>    <div class="body"><p>A swarm means an active breeding site within a few feet. Check under the fridge, behind the bin, and inside the dishwasher filter.</p></div>  </details>  <div class="chart">    <svg viewBox="0 0 120 60">      <path d="M40 10 L80 10 L70 50 L50 50 Z" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line-2)"/>      <circle cx="60" cy="48" r="8" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"/>      <circle cx="56" cy="24" r="2" fill="var(--ink-3)"/>      <circle cx="66" cy="30" r="2" fill="var(--ink-3)"/>      <text x="60" y="60" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">they just walk in</text>    </svg>  </div></div>	-
21.18.0	illustration/Folding a Fitted Sheet	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Folding a Fitted Sheet</div><p class="muted t-small">Hey <regex>user_name</regex> — the trick is to stop fighting the elastic and let the corners nest.</p><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 110" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="18" y="16" width="76" height="76" rx="10" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink-3)" stroke-width="2"></rect><path d="M18 16 Q56 34 94 16" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"></path><path d="M18 92 Q56 74 94 92" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"></path><path d="M52 42 L104 42 L104 94 L52 94 Z" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"></path><path d="M104 42 L138 30 L138 82 L104 94 Z" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"></path><text x="150" y="60" class="lbl">nest 4 → 1</text></svg></div><ol class="list"><li>Lay it flat, elastic up. Tuck one corner inside its neighbour so all four stack in a pocket.</li><li>Fold in thirds lengthwise, then in thirds again. Smooth with your palm as you go.</li><li>Store standing up, not stacked — you'll actually see what you own.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Still lumpy? Nobody inspects your linen closet. Neat beats perfect.</p></div></div>	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Folding a Fitted Sheet</div><p class="muted t-small">Hey  — the trick is to stop fighting the elastic and let the corners nest.</p><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 110" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="18" y="16" width="76" height="76" rx="10" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink-3)" stroke-width="2"></rect><path d="M18 16 Q56 34 94 16" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"></path><path d="M18 92 Q56 74 94 92" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"></path><path d="M52 42 L104 42 L104 94 L52 94 Z" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"></path><path d="M104 42 L138 30 L138 82 L104 94 Z" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"></path><text x="150" y="60" class="lbl">nest 4 → 1</text></svg></div><ol class="list"><li>Lay it flat, elastic up. Tuck one corner inside its neighbour so all four stack in a pocket.</li><li>Fold in thirds lengthwise, then in thirds again. Smooth with your palm as you go.</li><li>Store standing up, not stacked — you'll actually see what you own.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Still lumpy? Nobody inspects your linen closet. Neat beats perfect.</p></div></div>	-
21.19.0	illustration/Learning Basic Bike Maintenance	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Learning Basic Bike Maintenance</div><p class="muted t-small">Ten minutes a week keeps <regex>user_name</regex> off the bus. Here's the whole ritual.</p><div class="row" style="gap:var(--s-3);flex-wrap:wrap"><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>weekly</span><span class="badge info">monthly</span><span class="badge">seasonal</span></div><details class="acc"><summary>Weekly five-point check</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li><strong>Tyres</strong> — squeeze. Firm means fine; spongy means pump.</li><li><strong>Chain</strong> — spin the pedals backwards, listen for crunch.</li><li><strong>Brakes</strong> — both should bite before the lever hits the bar.</li><li><strong>Bolts</strong> — quick touch on stem and seat post.</li><li><strong>Tyres again</strong> — seriously, they lose air quietly.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Chain cleaning, the lazy way</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Wipe with a rag, drip lube on the inside of the chain while turning the pedals, wipe the excess. Skip the fancy solvents unless it's truly grim.</p></div></details><div class="callout warn"><p class="t-small">Lube is not the same as grease. One drop too many just collects grit.</p></div></div>	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Learning Basic Bike Maintenance</div><p class="muted t-small">Ten minutes a week keeps  off the bus. Here's the whole ritual.</p><div class="row" style="gap:var(--s-3);flex-wrap:wrap"><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>weekly</span><span class="badge info">monthly</span><span class="badge">seasonal</span></div><details class="acc"><summary>Weekly five-point check</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li><strong>Tyres</strong> — squeeze. Firm means fine; spongy means pump.</li><li><strong>Chain</strong> — spin the pedals backwards, listen for crunch.</li><li><strong>Brakes</strong> — both should bite before the lever hits the bar.</li><li><strong>Bolts</strong> — quick touch on stem and seat post.</li><li><strong>Tyres again</strong> — seriously, they lose air quietly.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Chain cleaning, the lazy way</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Wipe with a rag, drip lube on the inside of the chain while turning the pedals, wipe the excess. Skip the fancy solvents unless it's truly grim.</p></div></details><div class="callout warn"><p class="t-small">Lube is not the same as grease. One drop too many just collects grit.</p></div></div>	-
21.20.0	illustration/Making Bread Without a Machine	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Making Bread Without a Machine</div><p class="muted t-small">Four things, <regex>user_name</regex>: flour, water, salt, yeast. Your hands do the rest.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Flour</span><span class="value">500<span class="unit">g</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Water</span><span class="value">375<span class="unit">g</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Salt</span><span class="value">10<span class="unit">g</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Yeast</span><span class="value">7<span class="unit">g</span></span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Stir everything into a shaggy mess. Cover. Wait 30 minutes.</li><li>Fold the dough over itself four times. Repeat twice more, 30 minutes apart.</li><li>Shape, rest, bake hot — 230°C in a covered pot, 40 minutes.</li></ol><blockquote class="quote"><p>Bread is just patience you can eat.</p><cite>— a note on your fridge</cite></blockquote><div class="callout good"><p class="t-small">Dense loaf? Probably under-proofed. Give it another half hour next time.</p></div></div>	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Making Bread Without a Machine</div><p class="muted t-small">Four things, : flour, water, salt, yeast. Your hands do the rest.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Flour</span><span class="value">500<span class="unit">g</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Water</span><span class="value">375<span class="unit">g</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Salt</span><span class="value">10<span class="unit">g</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Yeast</span><span class="value">7<span class="unit">g</span></span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Stir everything into a shaggy mess. Cover. Wait 30 minutes.</li><li>Fold the dough over itself four times. Repeat twice more, 30 minutes apart.</li><li>Shape, rest, bake hot — 230°C in a covered pot, 40 minutes.</li></ol><blockquote class="quote"><p>Bread is just patience you can eat.</p><cite>— a note on your fridge</cite></blockquote><div class="callout good"><p class="t-small">Dense loaf? Probably under-proofed. Give it another half hour next time.</p></div></div>	-
21.21.0	illustration/Starting Seeds Indoors	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Starting Seeds Indoors</div><p class="muted t-small">Cheap trays, a sunny sill, and a little restraint — that's the whole game, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 220 100" aria-hidden="true"><line x1="24" y1="70" x2="200" y2="70" class="axis"></line><rect x="40" y="40" width="26" height="30" class="bar"></rect><rect x="78" y="28" width="26" height="42" class="bar"></rect><rect x="116" y="18" width="26" height="52" class="bar alt"></rect><rect x="154" y="10" width="26" height="60" class="bar"></rect><text x="40" y="86" class="lbl">wk1</text><text x="116" y="86" class="lbl">wk3</text><text x="192" y="86" class="lbl">wk6</text></svg></div><ul class="list"><li>Damp mix, not soaked. Think wrung-out sponge.</li><li>Warmth to sprout, light to grow. They are different rooms.</li><li>Brush the leaves with your hand daily — it thickens the stems.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Why they get leggy</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Not enough light. Move closer to the window or add a cheap lamp a few inches above the tray.</p></div></details><div class="callout warn"><p class="t-small">More water kills more seedlings than drought. When in doubt, wait a day.</p></div></div>	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Starting Seeds Indoors</div><p class="muted t-small">Cheap trays, a sunny sill, and a little restraint — that's the whole game, .</p><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 220 100" aria-hidden="true"><line x1="24" y1="70" x2="200" y2="70" class="axis"></line><rect x="40" y="40" width="26" height="30" class="bar"></rect><rect x="78" y="28" width="26" height="42" class="bar"></rect><rect x="116" y="18" width="26" height="52" class="bar alt"></rect><rect x="154" y="10" width="26" height="60" class="bar"></rect><text x="40" y="86" class="lbl">wk1</text><text x="116" y="86" class="lbl">wk3</text><text x="192" y="86" class="lbl">wk6</text></s