21.23.0	illustration/Hanging a Picture Straight	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Hanging a Picture Straight</div><p class="muted t-small">Once you know the eye-line rule, <regex>user_name</regex>, crooked walls become someone else's problem.</p><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 220 120" aria-hidden="true"><line x1="30" y1="34" x2="30" y2="104" class="guide"></line><circle cx="30" cy="34" r="3" fill="var(--accent)"></circle><rect x="70" y="40" width="110" height="72" rx="4" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line-2)" stroke-width="2"></rect><rect x="82" y="52" width="86" height="48" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"></rect><line x1="70" y1="76" x2="180" y2="76" class="guide"></line><text x="40" y="80" class="lbl">centre 145cm</text></svg></div><ol class="list"><li>Centre of the art at eye level — roughly 145 cm from the floor.</li><li>Two hooks beats one nail. A picture can spin on a single point.</li><li>Over furniture, keep 15–25 cm between frame and the top of the sofa or table.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>No stud where you need one</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Use a proper wall plug and screw for anything heavier than a page. Small frames can hang on a pin hook, no drama.</p></div></details><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">A bit of blue tack on the bottom corners is a surprisingly effective anti-tilt device.</p></div></div>	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Hanging a Picture Straight</div><p class="muted t-small">Once you know the eye-line rule, , crooked walls become someone else's problem.</p><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 220 120" aria-hidden="true"><line x1="30" y1="34" x2="30" y2="104" class="guide"></line><circle cx="30" cy="34" r="3" fill="var(--accent)"></circle><rect x="70" y="40" width="110" height="72" rx="4" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line-2)" stroke-width="2"></rect><rect x="82" y="52" width="86" height="48" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="va