21.24.0	illustration/Changing a Bike Flat Tire	<div class="card"><p class="muted t-small">Hey <regex>user_name</regex> — flats happen to everyone. Here's the drill.</p><ol class="list"><li>Pop the wheel off. Flip the bike, open the brake, undo the quick-release.</li><li>Lever the tire bead off with two tire levers.</li><li>Pull the tube out and find the hole — pump it and listen, or dunk it in water.</li><li>Run a finger inside the tire for glass or thorns. This is the step people skip.</li><li>Patch it or swap in a new tube.</li><li>Seat one bead, tuck the tube in, then the second bead by hand.</li><li>Inflate slowly to the pressure printed on the sidewall.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><strong>Quick check:</strong> if the hole is on the rim side, it's a pinch flat — you ran it too soft.</div><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 90"><circle cx="40" cy="45" r="32" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink-3)"></circle><circle cx="40" cy="45" r="24" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)"></circle><text x="40" y="49" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">tube</text><line x1="90" y1="45" x2="170" y2="45" class="guide"></line><circle cx="90" cy="45" r="4" fill="var(--bad)"></circle><text x="130" y="38" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">puncture</text></svg></div></div>	<div class="card"><p class="muted t-small">Hey  — flats happen to everyone. Here's the drill.</p><ol class="list"><li>Pop the wheel off. Flip the bike, open the brake, undo the quick-release.</li><li>Lever the tire bead off with two tire levers.</li><li>Pull the tube out and find the hole — pump it and listen, or dunk it in water.</li><li>Run a finger inside the tire for glass or thorns. This is the step people skip.</li><li>Patch it or swap in a new tube.</li><li>Seat one bead, tuck the tube in, then the second bead by hand.</li><li>Inflate slowly to the pressure printed on the sidewall.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><strong>Quick check:</strong> if the hole is on the rim side, it's a pinch flat — you ran it too soft.</div><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 90"><circle cx="40" cy="45" r="32" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink-3)"></circle><circle cx="40" cy="45" r="24" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)"></circle><text x="40" y="49" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">tube</text><line x1="90" y1="45" x2="170" y2="45" class="guide"></line><circle cx="90" cy="45" r="4" fill="var(--bad)"></circle><text x="130" y="38" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">puncture</text></svg></div></div>	-
21.25.0	illustration/Unclogging a Slow Sink Drain	<div class="card"><p class="muted t-small">Before you reach for the harsh stuff, <regex>user_name</regex> — try this order.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Boiling water.</strong> Pour it down in thirds. Clears grease half the time.</li><li><strong>Plunger.</strong> Block the overflow hole with a wet rag so suction actually works.</li><li><strong>Baking soda + vinegar.</strong> Half cup each, wait 15 min, flush with hot water.</li><li><strong>Drain snake.</strong> Feed it in, twist, pull out the hair — yes, it's usually hair.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>When to call someone</summary><div class="body"><p>If multiple drains are slow at once, the clog is past the trap. That's a pro's job.</p></div></details><div class="callout warn"><strong>Never</strong> put chemical drain cleaner down <em>after</em> a plunger attempt. If it splashes, it burns.</div></div>	<div class="card"><p class="muted t-small">Before you reach for the harsh stuff,  — try this order.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Boiling water.</strong> Pour it down in thirds. Clears grease half the time.</li><li><strong>Plunger.</strong> Block the overflow hole with a wet rag so suction actually works.</li><li><strong>Baking soda + vinegar.</strong> Half cup each, wait 15 min, flush with hot water.</li><li><strong>Drain snake.</strong> Feed it in, twist, pull out the hair — yes, it's usually hair.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>When to call someone</summary><div class="body"><p>If multiple drains are slow at once, the clog is past the trap. That's a pro's job.</p></div></details><div class="callout warn"><strong>Never</strong> put chemical drain cleaner down <em>after</em> a plunger attempt. If it splashes, it burns.</div></div>	-
21.26.0	illustration/Parallel Parking in One Go	<div class="card"><p class="muted t-small">It's geometry, not talent, <regex>user_name</regex>. Three reference points and you're in.</p><ol class="list"><li>Signal, pull alongside the front car, mirror-to-mirror. About a metre apart.</li><li>Stop. Reverse slowly, full lock toward the curb.</li><li>When your back bumper lines up with their rear bumper — straighten the wheel.</li><li>At 45° to the curb, full lock the other way. Tuck in.</li></ol><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Spot length</th><th class="num">Difficulty</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Car + 2 m</td><td class="num">easy</td></tr><tr><td>Car + 1 m</td><td class="num">normal</td></tr><tr><td>Car + 30 cm</td><td class="num">park elsewhere</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout good"><strong>Free win:</strong> adjust your mirror down so you can see the rear wheel and the curb. That's the whole trick.</div></div>	<div class="card"><p class="muted t-small">It's geometry, not talent, . Three reference points and you're in.</p><ol class="list"><li>Signal, pull alongside the front car, mirror-to-mirror. About a metre apart.</li><li>Stop. Reverse slowly, full lock toward the curb.</li><li>When your back bumper lines up with their rear bumper — straighten the wheel.</li><li>At 45° to the curb, full lock the other way. Tuck in.</li></ol><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Spot length</th><th class="num">Difficulty</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Car + 2 m</td><td class="num">easy</td></tr><tr><td>Car + 1 m</td><td class="num">normal</td></tr><tr><td>Car + 30 cm</td><td class="num">park elsewhere</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout good"><strong>Free win:</strong> adjust your mirror down so you can see the rear wheel and the curb. That's the whole trick.</div></div>	-
21.27.0	illustration/Doing Laundry Without Ruining Clothes	<div class="card"><p class="muted t-small">Most laundry damage is temperature, <regex>user_name</regex>. Here's the short version.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Load</th><th>Temp</th><th class="num">Risk</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Underwear, socks, towels</td><td>40°C</td><td class="num">low</td></tr><tr><td>Colour shirts, jeans</td><td>30°C</td><td class="num">low</td></tr><tr><td>Wool, silk, delicates</td><td>cold / hand</td><td class="num">high</td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class="acc"><summary>The four symbols that matter</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Bucket with a hand = hand wash only</li><li>Circle with an X = do not tumble dry</li><li>Triangle crossed = no bleach</li><li>Iron with one dot = low heat</li></ul></div></details><div class="callout good"><strong>Two rules:</strong> zip zips, empty pockets, and turn prints inside out. Sorted.</div></div>	<div class="card"><p class="muted t-small">Most laundry damage is temperature, . Here's the short version.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Load</th><th>Temp</th><th class="num">Risk</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Underwear, socks, towels</td><td>40°C</td><td class="num">low</td></tr><tr><td>Colour shirts, jeans</td><td>30°C</td><td class="num">low</td></tr><tr><td>Wool, silk, delicates</td><td>cold / hand</td><td class="num">high</td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class="acc"><summary>The four symbols that matter</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Bucket with a hand = hand wash only</li><li>Circle with an X = do not tumble dry</li><li>Triangle crossed = no bleach</li><li>Iron with one dot = low heat</li></ul></div></details><div class="callout good"><strong>Two rules:</strong> zip zips, empty pockets, and turn prints inside out. Sorted.</div></div>	-
21.28.0	illustration/Killing a Phone Battery Slowly	<div class="card"><p class="muted t-small">Batteries age by <em>cycles</em> and heat, <regex>user_name</regex>. Not by being topped up.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Stay between 20–80%.</strong> The last 20% costs the most wear.</li><li><strong>Avoid heat.</strong> No charging on a hot dashboard, no gaming while plugged in.</li><li><strong>Slow charge overnight.</strong> Fast charge is for daytime top-ups.</li><li><strong>Dim the screen.</strong> It's usually the biggest drain, not the apps.</li></ol><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 240 80"><line x1="20" y1="60" x2="220" y2="60" class="axis"></line><rect x="40" y="35" width="28" height="25" class="bar"></rect><rect x="90" y="20" width="28" height="40" class="bar alt"></rect><rect x="140" y="45" width="28" height="15" class="bar"></rect><text x="54" y="74" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">0–20</text><text x="104" y="74" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">20–80</text><text x="154" y="74" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">80–100</text></svg></div><p class="dim t-small">Wear per charge, by band. The middle is kind.</p></div>	<div class="card"><p class="muted t-small">Batteries age by <em>cycles</em> and heat, . Not by being topped up.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Stay between 20–80%.</strong> The last 20% costs the most wear.</li><li><strong>Avoid heat.</strong> No charging on a hot dashboard, no gaming while plugged in.</li><li><strong>Slow charge overnight.</strong> Fast charge is for daytime top-ups.</li><li><strong>Dim the screen.</strong> It's usually the biggest drain, not the apps.</li></ol><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 240 80"><line x1="20" y1="60" x2="220" y2="60" class="axis"></line><rect x="40" y="35" width="28" height="25" class="bar"></rect><rect x="90" y="20" width="28" height="40" class="bar alt"></rect><rect x="140" y="45" width="28" height="15" class="bar"></rect><text x="54" y="74" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">0–20</text><text x="104" y="74" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">20–80</text><text x="154" y="74" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">80–100</text></svg></div><p class="dim t-small">Wear per charge, by band. The middle is kind.</p></div>	-
21.29.0	illustration/Jump-Starting a Conversation at a Party	<div class="card"><p class="muted t-small">Nobody remembers your opening line, <regex>user_name</regex>. They remember whether you seemed glad to be there.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Comment, don't interrogate.</strong> "That dip is dangerous" beats "so what do you do?"</li><li><strong>Give before you ask.</strong> A little detail about yourself makes the door easy to walk through.</li><li><strong>Follow the second sentence.</strong> Whatever they mention after the first — go there.</li><li><strong>Exit well.</strong> "I'm going to grab a drink, but I'm glad we talked."</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Three openers that never fail</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>"How do you know the host?"</li><li>"What's the best thing you've eaten this week?"</li><li>"I'm terrible at parties. You?" <span class="dim">— honesty buys a lot.</span></li></ul></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><p class="muted t-small">Nobody remembers your opening line, . They remember whether you seemed glad to be there.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Comment, don't interrogate.</strong> "That dip is dangerous" beats "so what do you do?"</li><li><strong>Give before you ask.</strong> A little detail about yourself makes the door easy to walk through.</li><li><strong>Follow the second sentence.</strong> Whatever they mention after the first — go there.</li><li><strong>Exit well.</strong> "I'm going to grab a drink, but I'm glad we talked."</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Three openers that never fail</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>"How do you know the host?"</li><li>"What's the best thing you've eaten this week?"</li><li>"I'm terrible at parties. You?" <span class="dim">— honesty buys a lot.</span></li></ul></div></details></div>	-
21.30.0	illustration/Making a Sourdough Starter	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Sourdough starter, day by day</h3>  <p class="muted">Flour and water, plus patience. That's it, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p>  <div class="chart">    <svg viewBox="0 0 200 90" role="img" aria-label="Jar with bubbles rising over five days">      <rect x="70" y="10" width="60" height="70" rx="10" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--line-2)"></rect>      <rect x="70" y="30" width="60" height="50" rx="8" fill="var(--accent-soft)"></rect>      <circle cx="88" cy="52" r="4" fill="var(--accent)" opacity="0.7"></circle>      <circle cx="110" cy="64" r="5" fill="var(--accent)" opacity="0.5"></circle>      <circle cx="98" cy="74" r="3" fill="var(--accent)" opacity="0.6"></circle>      <path d="M70 30 H130" stroke="var(--line-2)"></path>      <text x="100" y="88" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">day 5, bubbly</text>    </svg>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Mix 50 g flour + 50 g water in a jar. Stir well.</li>    <li>Cover loosely. Leave at room temp, 20–24°C.</li>    <li>Each day, toss half and feed the same amounts again.</li>    <li>By day 5–7 it doubles and smells like yogurt. It's alive.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout info">    <p class="t-small">Warm kitchen speeds it up; cold slows it down. Neither is a problem — just slower bread.</p>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>It smells like nail polish remover. Bad?</summary>    <div class="body"><p>Just hungry. Feed it, wait, and the smell settles. Brown liquid on top means the same — pour it off, feed, carry on.</p></div>  </details></div>	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Sourdough starter, day by day</h3>  <p class="muted">Flour and water, plus patience. That's it, .</p>  <div class="chart">    <svg viewBox="0 0 200 90" role="img" aria-label="Jar with bubbles rising over five days">      <rect x="70" y="10" width="60" height="70" rx="10" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--line-2)"></rect>      <rect x="70" y="30" width="60" height="50" rx="8" fill="var(--accent-soft)"></rect>      <circle cx="88" cy="52" r="4" fill="var(--accent)" opacity="0.7"></circle>      <circle cx="110" cy="64" r="5" fill="var(--accent)" opacity="0.5"></circle>      <circle cx="98" cy="74" r="3" fill="var(--accent)" opacity="0.6"></circle>      <path d="M70 30 H130" stroke="var(--line-2)"></path>      <text x="100" y="88" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">day 5, bubbly</text>    </svg>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Mix 50 g flour + 50 g water in a jar. Stir well.</li>    <li>Cover loosely. Leave at room temp, 20–24°C.</li>    <li>Each day, toss half and feed the same amounts again.</li>    <li>By day 5–7 it doubles and smells like yogurt. It's alive.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout info">    <p class="t-small">Warm kitchen speeds it up; cold slows it down. Neither is a problem — just slower bread.</p>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>It smells like nail polish remover. Bad?</summary>    <div class="body"><p>Just hungry. Feed it, wait, and the smell settles. Brown liquid on top means the same — pour it off, feed, carry on.</p></div>  </details></div>	-
21.31.0	illustration/Reading Your First Knitting Pattern	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Your first knitting pattern, translated</h3>  <p class="muted">Patterns are just shorthand, <regex>user_name</regex>. Once you crack the code it reads fast.</p>  <div class="table-wrap">    <table>      <thead><tr><th>Short</th><th>Means</th></tr></thead>      <tbody>        <tr><td><code>k</code></td><td>knit</td></tr>        <tr><td><code>p</code></td><td>purl</td></tr>        <tr><td><code>yo</code></td><td>wrap yarn over needle — makes a hole</td></tr>        <tr><td><code>k2tog</code></td><td>knit two together — one decrease</td></tr>        <tr><td><code>sl1</code></td><td>slip one stitch without working it</td></tr>      </tbody>    </table>  </div>  <div class="grid two stack">    <div class="stat"><span class="label">Brackets</span><span class="value">repeat</span></div>    <div class="stat"><span class="label">Asterisks</span><span class="value">repeat</span></div>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>"Rep from * to last 3 sts" — huh?</summary>    <div class="body"><p>Do the thing between the stars over and over, but stop when 3 stitches are left. Those 3 get their own instruction.</p></div>  </details>  <blockquote class="quote"><p>Count your stitches. Every single row.</p><cite>every knitting book, ever</cite></blockquote></div>	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Your first knitting pattern, translated</h3>  <p class="muted">Patterns are just shorthand, . Once you crack the code it reads fast.</p>  <div class="table-wrap">    <table>      <thead><tr><th>Short</th><th>Means</th></tr></thead>      <tbody>        <tr><td><code>k</code></td><td>knit</td></tr>        <tr><td><code>p</code></td><td>purl</td></tr>        <tr><td><code>yo</code></td><td>wrap yarn over needle — makes a hole</td></tr>        <tr><td><code>k2tog</code></td><td>knit two together — one decrease</td></tr>        <tr><td><code>sl1</code></td><td>slip one stitch without working it</td></tr>      </tbody>    </table>  </div>  <div class="grid two stack">    <div class="stat"><span class="label">Brackets</span><span class="value">repeat</span></div>    <div class="stat"><span class="label">Asterisks</span><span class="value">repeat</span></div>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>"Rep from * to last 3 sts" — huh?</summary>    <div class="body"><p>Do the thing between the stars over and over, but stop when 3 stitches are left. Those 3 get their own instruction.</p></div>  </details>  <blockquote class="quote"><p>Count your stitches. Every single row.</p><cite>every knitting book, ever</cite></blockquote></div>	-
21.32.0	illustration/Patching a Bicycle Tube	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Patching a tube, the slow good way</h3>  <p class="muted">A patch costs pennies and outlasts a spare. Thanks for fixing instead of tossing, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p>  <div class="chart">    <svg viewBox="0 0 220 80" role="img" aria-label="Tube with a patch applied">      <path d="M10 45 C60 15, 160 75, 210 45" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink-3)" stroke-width="6" stroke-linecap="round"></path>      <rect x="96" y="33" width="30" height="22" rx="4" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"></rect>      <text x="111" y="74" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">patch</text>    </svg>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Find the hole. Inflate a little, listen, or dunk in water and watch for bubbles.</li>    <li>Roughen the area around it with the sandpaper in the kit.</li>    <li>Smear a thin layer of glue. Wait until it's tacky, not wet.</li>    <li>Press the patch on, hold hard for 30 seconds.</li>    <li>Peel the backing, dust with chalk, reinstall, inflate.</li>  </ol>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Wait, which side faces the tyre?</summary>    <div class="body"><p>Glue on the tube, patch over it, plastic film comes off last. If the patch has a shiny side, that's the backing.</p></div>  </details></div>	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Patching a tube, the slow good way</h3>  <p class="muted">A patch costs pennies and outlasts a spare. Thanks for fixing instead of tossing, .</p>  <div class="chart">    <svg viewBox="0 0 220 80" role="img" aria-label="Tube with a patch applied">      <path d="M10 45 C60 15, 160 75, 210 45" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink-3)" stroke-width="6" stroke-linecap="round"></path>      <rect x="96" y="33" width="30" height="22" rx="4" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"></rect>      <text x="111" y="74" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">patch</text>    </svg>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Find the hole. Inflate a little, listen, or dunk in water and watch for bubbles.</li>    <li>Roughen the area around it with the sandpaper in the kit.</li>    <li>Smear a thin layer of glue. Wait until it's tacky, not wet.</li>    <li>Press the patch on, hold hard for 30 seconds.</li>    <li>Peel the backing, dust with chalk, reinstall, inflate.</li>  </ol>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Wait, which side faces the tyre?</summary>    <div class="body"><p>Glue on the tube, patch over it, plastic film comes off last. If the patch has a shiny side, that's the backing.</p></div>  </details></div>	-
21.33.0	illustration/Cleaning a Coffee Grinder	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Grinder cleaning, so your coffee tastes like coffee</h3>  <p class="muted">Old oils go stale and taint every cup. Ten minutes fixes it, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p>  <div class="row between">    <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>burr</span>    <span class="badge info"><i class="dot"></i>blade</span>  </div>  <div class="grid two stack">    <div class="stat"><span class="label">Quick brush</span><span class="value">weekly</span></div>    <div class="stat"><span class="label">Deep clean</span><span class="value">monthly</span></div>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Burr grinder</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Unplug it. Remove the hopper and upper burr.</li>        <li>Brush both burrs with a stiff dry brush.</li>        <li>Wipe the chute with a dry cloth — no water.</li>        <li>Reassemble, grind a few beans and toss them. Seasoned.</li>      </ol>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Blade grinder</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Wipe the bowl with a dry cloth or paper towel. Never wash it — water kills the motor. A grind of dry rice clears the rest.</p>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout warn"><p class="t-small">No water, ever. Beans and moisture don't mix inside a machine.</p></div></div>	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Grinder cleaning, so your coffee tastes like coffee</h3>  <p class="muted">Old oils go stale and taint every cup. Ten minutes fixes it, .</p>  <div class="row between">    <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>burr</span>    <span class="badge info"><i class="dot"></i>blade</span>  </div>  <div class="grid two stack">    <div class="stat"><span class="label">Quick brush</span><span class="value">weekly</span></div>    <div class="stat"><span class="label">Deep clean</span><span class="value">monthly</span></div>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Burr grinder</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Unplug it. Remove the hopper and upper burr.</li>        <li>Brush both burrs with a stiff dry brush.</li>        <li>Wipe the chute with a dry cloth — no water.</li>        <li>Reassemble, grind a few beans and toss them. Seasoned.</li>      </ol>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Blade grinder</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Wipe the bowl with a dry cloth or paper towel. Never wash it — water kills the motor. A grind of dry rice clears the rest.</p>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout warn"><p class="t-small">No water, ever. Beans and moisture don't mix inside a machine.</p></div></div>	-
21.34.0	illustration/Removing a Sticker From Glass	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Getting that sticker off clean</h3>  <p class="muted">Paper's easy. The glue is the boss fight, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p>  <div class="row between">    <span class="badge good">soap + water</span>    <span class="badge warn">oil</span>    <span class="badge bad">scraper</span>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Peel what you can with a fingernail. Go slow, warm it with your hand first.</li>    <li>Soak in warm soapy water 10–20 minutes.</li>    <li>Rub the residue with a drop of cooking oil on a cloth.</li>    <li>Wash with dish soap to get the oil off. Done.</li>  </ol>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What about a razor blade?</summary>    <div class="body"><p>Fine on flat glass at a shallow angle. Never on a mirror's edge or on plastic — it'll scratch. Plastic scrapers or an old credit card are safer.</p></div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Heat gun or hairdryer?</summary>    <div class="body"><p>A hairdryer softens the glue so the label lifts in one piece. Useful on big stubborn labels.</p></div>  </details></div>	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Getting that sticker off clean</h3>  <p class="muted">Paper's easy. The glue is the boss fight, .</p>  <div class="row between">    <span class="badge good">soap + water</span>    <span class="badge warn">oil</span>    <span class="badge bad">scraper</span>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Peel what you can with a fingernail. Go slow, warm it with your hand first.</li>    <li>Soak in warm soapy water 10–20 minutes.</li>    <li>Rub the residue with a drop of cooking oil on a cloth.</li>    <li>Wash with dish soap to get the oil off. Done.</li>  </ol>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What about a razor blade?</summary>    <div class="body"><p>Fine on flat glass at a shallow angle. Never on a mirror's edge or on plastic — it'll scratch. Plastic scrapers or an old credit card are safer.</p></div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Heat gun or hairdryer?</summary>    <div class="body"><p>A hairdryer softens the glue so the label lifts in one piece. Useful on big stubborn labels.</p></div>  </details></div>	-
21.35.0	illustration/Watering a Christmas Cactus	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Christmas cactus, watered right</h3>  <p class="muted">It's a cactus that lives in trees, not a desert, <regex>user_name</regex>. Water like a houseplant, not a rock.</p>  <div class="chart">    <svg viewBox="0 0 200 100" role="img" aria-label="Segmented cactus with a bud">      <path d="M100 92 C100 70, 100 50, 100 30" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="3" fill="none"></path>      <ellipse cx="100" cy="72" rx="14" ry="9" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--line-2)"></ellipse>      <ellipse cx="86" cy="55" rx="12" ry="8" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--line-2)"></ellipse>      <ellipse cx="114" cy="52" rx="12" ry="8" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--line-2)"></ellipse>      <circle cx="100" cy="26" r="6" fill="var(--accent)"></circle>      <text x="100" y="98" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">happy segments</text>    </svg>  </div>  <div class="grid two stack">    <div class="stat"><span class="label">Growing</span><span class="value">when top 2 cm dry</span></div>    <div class="stat"><span class="label">After flowering</span><span class="value">less water</span></div>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>The segments look wrinkled</summary>    <div class="body"><p>Thirsty — water it. If they're mushy and yellow, you've gone the other way. Pull back and let it dry.</p></div>  </details>  <div class="callout good"><p class="t-small">Drainage matters. A pot that sits in water will rot the roots quietly.</p></div></div>	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Christmas cactus, watered right</h3>  <p class="muted">It's a cactus that lives in trees, not a desert, . Water like a houseplant, not a rock.</p>  <div class="chart">    <svg viewBox="0 0 200 100" role="img" aria-label="Segmented cactus with a bud">      <path d="M100 92 C100 70, 100 50, 100 30" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="3" fill="none"></path>      <ellipse cx="100" cy="72" rx="14" ry="9" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--line-2)"></ellipse>      <ellipse cx="86" cy="55" rx="12" ry="8" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--line-2)"></ellipse>      <ellipse cx="114" cy="52" rx="12" ry="8" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--line-2)"></ellipse>      <circle cx="100" cy="26" r="6" fill="var(--accent)"></circle>      <text x="100" y="98" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">happy segments</text>    </svg>  </div>  <div class="grid two stack">    <div class="stat"><span class="label">Growing</span><span class="value">when top 2 cm dry</span></div>    <div class="stat"><span class="label">After flowering</span><span class="value">less water</span></div>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>The segments look wrinkled</summary>    <div class="body"><p>Thirsty — water it. If they're mushy and yellow, you've gone the other way. Pull back and let it dry.</p></div>  </details>  <div class="callout good"><p class="t-small">Drainage matters. A pot that sits in water will rot the roots quietly.</p></div></div>	-
21.36.0	illustration/Ironing a Shirt Collar Properly	<div class="card stack"><p class="t-small muted">Hi <regex>user_name</regex> — the collar is the one part of a shirt everyone reads first. Press it from the points inward, never the other way.</p><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 220 120" role="img" aria-label="Collar pressed from the points toward the centre"><path d="M40 30 L110 95 L180 30" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink-3)" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round"></path><circle cx="40" cy="30" r="6" fill="var(--accent)"></circle><circle cx="180" cy="30" r="6" fill="var(--accent-2)"></circle><path d="M62 34 L104 72" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="5 4"></path><path d="M158 34 L116 72" stroke="var(--accent-2)" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="5 4"></path><text x="110" y="115" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">points to centre</text></svg></div><ol class="list"><li>Button the top button. It holds the collar's shape while you work.</li><li>Flip the collar up and iron the inside band first — that's the hidden half.</li><li>Flip it down and press each point <em>toward</em> the middle, so the crease never lands on the fold.</li><li>Let it cool flat for a minute before hanging. Warm fabric forgets nothing.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p class="t-small">Steam loves collars. If your iron spits water, press dry with a cloth between.</p></div></div>	<div class="card stack"><p class="t-small muted">Hi  — the collar is the one part of a shirt everyone reads first. Press it from the points inward, never the other way.</p><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 220 120" role="img" aria-label="Collar pressed from the points toward the centre"><path d="M40 30 L110 95 L180 30" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink-3)" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round"></path><circle cx="40" cy="30" r="6" fill="var(--accent)"></circle><circle cx="180" cy="30" r="6" fill="var(--accent-2)"></circle><path d="M62 34 L104 72" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="5 4"></path><path d="M158 34 L116 72" stroke="var(--accent-2)" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="5 4"></path><text x="110" y="115" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">points to centre</text></svg></div><ol class="list"><li>Button the top button. It holds the collar's shape while you work.</li><li>Flip the collar up and iron the inside band first — that's the hidden half.</li><li>Flip it down and press each point <em>toward</em> the middle, so the crease never lands on the fold.</li><li>Let it cool flat for a minute before hanging. Warm fabric forgets nothing.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p class="t-small">Steam loves collars. If your iron spits water, press dry with a cloth between.</p></div></div>	-
21.37.0	illustration/Sharpening Kitchen Knives at Home	<div class="card stack"><p class="t-small muted">A dull knife is the dangerous one, <regex>user_name</regex>. It slips where a sharp one bites.</p><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 220 120" role="img" aria-label="Blade held at fifteen degrees on a stone"><rect x="20" y="88" width="180" height="18" rx="4" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line)"></rect><line x1="30" y1="88" x2="190" y2="30" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="4" stroke-linecap="round"></line><path d="M60 76 A40 40 0 0 0 66 66" fill="none" stroke="var(--line-2)" stroke-width="2"></path><text x="78" y="72" class="lbl">15°</text><text x="110" y="118" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">stone</text></svg></div><details class="acc"><summary>Rough grit, then fine</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Soak a water stone five minutes. Splash-and-go stones just need a wipe.</li><li>Hold the blade at 15° — about two stacked coins under the spine.</li><li>Push five strokes on one side, feel for a burr on the far edge, then repeat on the other.</li><li>Finish on the fine side with light pressure, then strop on newspaper.</li></ul></div></details><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Slice paper to test. If it tears, ten more gentle strokes, fine side only.</p></div></div>	<div class="card stack"><p class="t-small muted">A dull knife is the dangerous one, . It slips where a sharp one bites.</p><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 220 120" role="img" aria-label="Blade held at fifteen degrees on a stone"><rect x="20" y="88" width="180" height="18" rx="4" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line)"></rect><line x1="30" y1="88" x2="190" y2="30" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="4" stroke-linecap="round"></line><path d="M60 76 A40 40 0 0 0 66 66" fill="none" stroke="var(--line-2)" stroke-width="2"></path><text x="78" y="72" class="lbl">15°</text><text x="110" y="118" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">stone</text></svg></div><details class="acc"><summary>Rough grit, then fine</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Soak a water stone five minutes. Splash-and-go stones just need a wipe.</li><li>Hold the blade at 15° — about two stacked coins under the spine.</li><li>Push five strokes on one side, feel for a burr on the far edge, then repeat on the other.</li><li>Finish on the fine side with light pressure, then strop on newspaper.</li></ul></div></details><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Slice paper to test. If it tears, ten more gentle strokes, fine side only.</p></div></div>	-
21.38.0	illustration/Getting a Cat to Take a Pill	<div class="card stack"><p class="t-small muted">Cats have opinions, <regex>user_name</regex>. Your job is to be faster than the opinion.</p><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 220 110" role="img" aria-label="Pill placed at the back of a cat's tongue"><circle cx="110" cy="55" r="34" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line)"></circle><path d="M86 34 L74 18 L102 30 Z" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line)"></path><path d="M134 34 L146 18 L118 30 Z" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line)"></path><circle cx="98" cy="50" r="3" fill="var(--ink-3)"></circle><circle cx="122" cy="50" r="3" fill="var(--ink-3)"></circle><ellipse cx="110" cy="70" rx="12" ry="5" fill="var(--accent)"></ellipse><text x="110" y="108" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">drop it deep, close the mouth</text></svg></div><ol class="list"><li>Wrap the cat in a towel like a burrito. Only the head stays out.</li><li>Hold the pill between two fingers, tilt the head back gently.</li><li>Drop it past the hump of the tongue — there's a spot where swallowing is automatic.</li><li>Close the mouth, stroke the throat, wait for the lick.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p class="t-small">Follow with a syringe of water. Pills that stick in the throat come back up.</p></div></div>	<div class="card stack"><p class="t-small muted">Cats have opinions, . Your job is to be faster than the opinion.</p><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 220 110" role="img" aria-label="Pill placed at the back of a cat's tongue"><circle cx="110" cy="55" r="34" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line)"></circle><path d="M86 34 L74 18 L102 30 Z" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line)"></path><path d="M134 34 L146 18 L118 30 Z" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line)"></path><circle cx="98" cy="50" r="3" fill="var(--ink-3)"></circle><circle cx="122" cy="50" r="3" fill="var(--ink-3)"></circle><ellipse cx="110" cy="70" rx="12" ry="5" fill="var(--accent)"></ellipse><text x="110" y="108" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">drop it deep, close the mouth</text></svg></div><ol class="list"><li>Wrap the cat in a towel like a burrito. Only the head stays out.</li><li>Hold the pill between two fingers, tilt the head back gently.</li><li>Drop it past the hump of the tongue — there's a spot where swallowing is automatic.</li><li>Close the mouth, stroke the throat, wait for the lick.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p class="t-small">Follow with a syringe of water. Pills that stick in the throat come back up.</p></div></div>	-
21.39.0	illustration/Making Pizza Dough the Night Before	<div class="card stack"><p class="t-small muted">The fridge does the work while you sleep, <regex>user_name</regex>. All you do is stir and forget.</p><div class="recipe"><div class="head"><strong>No-knead overnight dough</strong></div><p class="meta">Makes 2 rounds · 12 hours mostly unattended</p><ul class="ing two-column"><li>500 g bread flour</li><li>375 g cool water</li><li>10 g salt</li><li>2 g instant yeast</li></ul></div><ol class="list"><li>Stir everything in a bowl until no dry flour hides at the bottom.</li><li>Cover and leave on the counter 2 hours. It should look puffy and alive.</li><li>Move to the fridge overnight — up to three days, honestly.</li><li>Pull it out an hour before baking. Divide, shape, top, bake hot.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Cold dough is easier to stretch. Don't fight it warm.</p></div></div>	<div class="card stack"><p class="t-small muted">The fridge does the work while you sleep, . All you do is stir and forget.</p><div class="recipe"><div class="head"><strong>No-knead overnight dough</strong></div><p class="meta">Makes 2 rounds · 12 hours mostly unattended</p><ul class="ing two-column"><li>500 g bread flour</li><li>375 g cool water</li><li>10 g salt</li><li>2 g instant yeast</li></ul></div><ol class="list"><li>Stir everything in a bowl until no dry flour hides at the bottom.</li><li>Cover and leave on the counter 2 hours. It should look puffy and alive.</li><li>Move to the fridge overnight — up to three days, honestly.</li><li>Pull it out an hour before baking. Divide, shape, top, bake hot.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Cold dough is easier to stretch. Don't fight it warm.</p></div></div>	-
21.40.0	illustration/Replacing a Watch Battery	<div class="card stack"><p class="t-small muted">Most quartz watches take a fresh cell every two or three years, <regex>user_name</regex>. The trick is not scratching the case back.</p><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 220 110" role="img" aria-label="Watch case back with a small notch for a tool"><circle cx="110" cy="55" r="38" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line-2)"></circle><circle cx="110" cy="55" r="28" fill="none" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-dasharray="3 4"></circle><line x1="110" y1="17" x2="110" y2="27" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="6"></line><text x="110" y="105" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">notch at 12 o'clock</text></svg></div><ol class="list"><li>Note the number on the old cell — usually a 4-digit code.</li><li>Use a case-back tool or a small rubber ball, not a kitchen knife.</li><li>Lift the back straight up. Keep the gasket with the lid, not the watch.</li><li>Pry the old cell out, press the new one in with plastic tweezers.</li><li>Reset the time, then close the back with firm even pressure.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p class="t-small">If the back won't sit flush, stop. Bent case backs leak water.</p></div></div>	<div class="card stack"><p class="t-small muted">Most quartz watches take a fresh cell every two or three years, . The trick is not scratching the case back.</p><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 220 110" role="img" aria-label="Watch case back with a small notch for a tool"><circle cx="110" cy="55" r="38" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line-2)"></circle><circle cx="110" cy="55" r="28" fill="none" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-dasharray="3 4"></circle><line x1="110" y1="17" x2="110" y2="27" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="6"></line><text x="110" y="105" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">notch at 12 o'clock</text></svg></div><ol class="list"><li>Note the number on the old cell — usually a 4-digit code.</li><li>Use a case-back tool or a small rubber ball, not a kitchen knife.</li><li>Lift the back straight up. Keep the gasket with the lid, not the watch.</li><li>Pry the old cell out, press the new one in with plastic tweezers.</li><li>Reset the time, then close the back with firm even pressure.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p class="t-small">If the back won't sit flush, stop. Bent case backs leak water.</p></div></div>	-
21.41.0	illustration/Removing Chewing Gum From Fabric	<div class="card stack"><p class="t-small muted">Cold first, <regex>user_name</regex>. Gum is stubborn warm and brittle cold.</p><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 220 110" role="img" aria-label="Ice cube hardening gum before peeling"><rect x="30" y="30" width="70" height="50" rx="8" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"></rect><ellipse cx="150" cy="65" rx="34" ry="18" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line)"></ellipse><text x="65" y="60" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">ice</text><text x="150" y="70" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">gum</text><path d="M100 55 L116 55" stroke="var(--ink-3)" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="3 3"></path></svg></div><ol class="list"><li>Freeze it. Ice cube, freezer, or a can of fizzy from the fridge.</li><li>Once hard, lift an edge with a butter knife or your thumbnail.</li><li>Peel slowly. Rushing snaps it and spreads the mess.</li><li>For any ghost residue, dab a little rubbing alcohol on a cloth.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>If it's already been through the dryer</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Warm gum soaks into fibres. Try the freeze-peel first, then a small amount of oil on a hidden hem to test for staining.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="card stack"><p class="t-small muted">Cold first, . Gum is stubborn warm and brittle cold.</p><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 220 110" role="img" aria-label="Ice cube hardening gum before peeling"><rect x="30" y="30" width="70" height="50" rx="8" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"></rect><ellipse cx="150" cy="65" rx="34" ry="18" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line)"></ellipse><text x="65" y="60" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">ice</text><text x="150" y="70" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">gum</text><path d="M100 55 L116 55" stroke="var(--ink-3)" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="3 3"></path></svg></div><ol class="list"><li>Freeze it. Ice cube, freezer, or a can of fizzy from the fridge.</li><li>Once hard, lift an edge with a butter knife or your thumbnail.</li><li>Peel slowly. Rushing snaps it and spreads the mess.</li><li>For any ghost residue, dab a little rubbing alcohol on a cloth.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>If it's already been through the dryer</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Warm gum soaks into fibres. Try the freeze-peel first, then a small amount of oil on a hidden hem to test for staining.</p></div></details></div>	-
21.42.0	illustration/Descaling a Kettle	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Descaling a Kettle</h3><p class="muted t-small">That chalky crust is limescale — harmless, but it makes tea taste flat.</p><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 90"><path d="M60 20 h50 a10 10 0 0 1 10 10 v40 a10 10 0 0 1 -10 10 h-50 a10 10 0 0 1 -10 -10 v-40 a10 10 0 0 1 10 -10 z" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line-2)"/><path d="M60 72 q25 -12 50 0" fill="var(--warn-soft)" stroke="none"/><path d="M50 26 q-12 20 0 44" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink-3)"/><path d="M118 30 l14 -6" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink-3)" stroke-width="3"/><text x="100" y="88" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">the crust settles at the bottom</text></svg></div><ol class="list"><li>Fill halfway with equal parts water and white vinegar.</li><li>Boil, switch off, and let it sit 20 minutes.</li><li>Rinse twice with fresh water and boil once more, discarding that water.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Faster option</summary><div class="body"><p>Citric acid powder, one tablespoon, same method. No vinegar smell in the kitchen.</p></div></details><p class="dim t-small">Do it monthly if your water is hard — <regex>user_name</regex>, your kettle will thank you quietly.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Descaling a Kettle</h3><p class="muted t-small">That chalky crust is limescale — harmless, but it makes tea taste flat.</p><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 90"><path d="M60 20 h50 a10 10 0 0 1 10 10 v40 a10 10 0 0 1 -10 10 h-50 a10 10 0 0 1 -10 -10 v-40 a10 10 0 0 1 10 -10 z" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line-2)"/><path d="M60 72 q25 -12 50 0" fill="var(--warn-soft)" stroke="none"/><path d="M50 26 q-12 20 0 44" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink-3)"/><path d="M118 30 l14 -6" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink-3)" stroke-width="3"/><text x="100" y="88" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">the crust settles at the bottom</text></svg></div><ol class="list"><li>Fill halfway with equal parts water and white vinegar.</li><li>Boil, switch off, and let it sit 20 minutes.</li><li>Rinse twice with fresh water and boil once more, discarding that water.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Faster option</summary><div class="body"><p>Citric acid powder, one tablespoon, same method. No vinegar smell in the kitchen.</p></div></details><p class="dim t-small">Do it monthly if your water is hard — , your kettle will thank you quietly.</p></div>	-
21.43.0	illustration/Removing a Key Stuck in a Lock	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Removing a Key Stuck in a Lock</h3><div class="callout warn">Don't twist hard. The key snaps, and now it's a locksmith call.</div><ul class="list"><li><b>Wiggle, don't wrench.</b> Light back-and-forth while pushing in and pulling out.</li><li><b>Lift the door slightly</b> by the handle — a sagging hinge misaligns the barrel.</li><li><b>Lube it.</b> Graphite powder or a dry PTFE spray. Never cooking oil — it gums up.</li><li><b>Still stuck?</b> Tap the key head gently with a screwdriver handle while pulling.</li></ul><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 80"><rect x="30" y="30" width="70" height="8" rx="4" fill="var(--ink-3)"/><circle cx="24" cy="34" r="10" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="3"/><circle cx="118" cy="34" r="14" fill="none" stroke="var(--line-2)" stroke-width="4"/><path d="M132 34 h30" stroke="var(--bad)" stroke-width="3"/><text x="150" y="58" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">no force here</text></svg></div><p class="dim t-small">If it's cold out, <regex>user_name</regex>, warm the key with your hands first. Metal shrinks.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Removing a Key Stuck in a Lock</h3><div class="callout warn">Don't twist hard. The key snaps, and now it's a locksmith call.</div><ul class="list"><li><b>Wiggle, don't wrench.</b> Light back-and-forth while pushing in and pulling out.</li><li><b>Lift the door slightly</b> by the handle — a sagging hinge misaligns the barrel.</li><li><b>Lube it.</b> Graphite powder or a dry PTFE spray. Never cooking oil — it gums up.</li><li><b>Still stuck?</b> Tap the key head gently with a screwdriver handle while pulling.</li></ul><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 80"><rect x="30" y="30" width="70" height="8" rx="4" fill="var(--ink-3)"/><circle cx="24" cy="34" r="10" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="3"/><circle cx="118" cy="34" r="14" fill="none" stroke="var(--line-2)" stroke-width="4"/><path d="M132 34 h30" stroke="var(--bad)" stroke-width="3"/><text x="150" y="58" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">no force here</text></svg></div><p class="dim t-small">If it's cold out, , warm the key with your hands first. Metal shrinks.</p></div>	-
21.44.0	illustration/Getting Wax Out of Candle Holders	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Getting Wax Out of Candle Holders</h3><p class="muted t-small">Cold makes wax brittle. Brittle wax pops out in one piece. That's the whole trick.</p><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 90"><path d="M70 25 h60 l-8 50 h-44 z" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line-2)"/><path d="M74 45 h52 l-5 28 h-42 z" fill="var(--info-soft)"/><text x="100" y="86" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">freezer, 30 minutes</text></svg></div><ol class="list"><li>Freeze the holder for 30 minutes.</li><li>Press the wax with your thumb — it should crack free.</li><li>Wipe residue with a paper towel and hot water.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>If it's glued to the glass</summary><div class="body"><p>Pour boiling water in, let it float up, then fish the disc out with a spoon. Dry fully before reusing.</p></div></details><p class="dim t-small">Works on candle jars too — <regex>user_name</regex>, keep the jar, lose the wax.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Getting Wax Out of Candle Holders</h3><p class="muted t-small">Cold makes wax brittle. Brittle wax pops out in one piece. That's the whole trick.</p><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 90"><path d="M70 25 h60 l-8 50 h-44 z" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line-2)"/><path d="M74 45 h52 l-5 28 h-42 z" fill="var(--info-soft)"/><text x="100" y="86" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">freezer, 30 minutes</text></svg></div><ol class="list"><li>Freeze the holder for 30 minutes.</li><li>Press the wax with your thumb — it should crack free.</li><li>Wipe residue with a paper towel and hot water.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>If it's glued to the glass</summary><div class="body"><p>Pour boiling water in, let it float up, then fish the disc out with a spoon. Dry fully before reusing.</p></div></details><p class="dim t-small">Works on candle jars too — , keep the jar, lose the wax.</p></div>	-
21.45.0	illustration/Cleaning a Humidifier	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Cleaning a Humidifier</h3><div class="callout bad">Pink slime is a bacterial film. Not a decoration.</div><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><div class="label">Water tank</div><div class="value">Daily</div></div><div class="stat"><div class="label">Deep clean</div><div class="value">Weekly</div></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Unplug it. Always.</li><li>Empty the tank and rinse with warm water.</li><li>Fill with 1 part white vinegar to 3 parts water; soak 20 minutes.</li><li>Scrub corners with a soft brush — a bottle brush reaches the shoulder.</li><li>Rinse until the vinegar smell is gone.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>White dust on furniture?</summary><div class="body"><p>That's minerals from hard water. Use distilled water, or switch to an evaporative (wick) model.</p></div></details><p class="dim t-small">Never leave standing water in it, <regex>user_name</regex>. That's how the slime moves in.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Cleaning a Humidifier</h3><div class="callout bad">Pink slime is a bacterial film. Not a decoration.</div><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><div class="label">Water tank</div><div class="value">Daily</div></div><div class="stat"><div class="label">Deep clean</div><div class="value">Weekly</div></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Unplug it. Always.</li><li>Empty the tank and rinse with warm water.</li><li>Fill with 1 part white vinegar to 3 parts water; soak 20 minutes.</li><li>Scrub corners with a soft brush — a bottle brush reaches the shoulder.</li><li>Rinse until the vinegar smell is gone.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>White dust on furniture?</summary><div class="body"><p>That's minerals from hard water. Use distilled water, or switch to an evaporative (wick) model.</p></div></details><p class="dim t-small">Never leave standing water in it, . That's how the slime moves in.</p></div>	-
21.46.0	illustration/Keeping Headphones From Tangling	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Keeping Headphones From Tangling</h3><p class="muted t-small">Cables tangle when they can move freely. Give them a shape and they behave.</p><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 90"><circle cx="45" cy="45" r="22" fill="none" stroke="var(--bad)" stroke-width="3"/><text x="45" y="80" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">loop-and-pull</text><circle cx="150" cy="45" r="22" fill="none" stroke="var(--good)" stroke-width="3"/><circle cx="150" cy="45" r="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--good)" stroke-width="3"/><text x="150" y="80" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">over-under</text></svg></div><ol class="list"><li><b>Over-under:</b> make a loop, then a reverse loop, alternating. The cable leaves flat every time.</li><li><b>Figure-eight</b> the slack around your fingers instead of circling.</li><li><b>Tie it off</b> with the cable itself — no zip ties squeezing the wire.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Cheat code</summary><div class="body"><p>A small pouch or a hair tie keeps the coil tight in a bag. Bags are where the chaos lives.</p></div></details><p class="dim t-small">Learn it once, <regex>user_name</regex>, and you'll never untangle in public again.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Keeping Headphones From Tangling</h3><p class="muted t-small">Cables tangle when they can move freely. Give them a shape and they behave.</p><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 90"><circle cx="45" cy="45" r="22" fill="none" stroke="var(--bad)" stroke-width="3"/><text x="45" y="80" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">loop-and-pull</text><circle cx="150" cy="45" r="22" fill="none" stroke="var(--good)" stroke-width="3"/><circle cx="150" cy="45" r="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--good)" stroke-width="3"/><text x="150" y="80" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">over-under</text></svg></div><ol class="list"><li><b>Over-under:</b> make a loop, then a reverse loop, alternating. The cable leaves flat every time.</li><li><b>Figure-eight</b> the slack around your fingers instead of circling.</li><li><b>Tie it off</b> with the cable itself — no zip ties squeezing the wire.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Cheat code</summary><div class="body"><p>A small pouch or a hair tie keeps the coil tight in a bag. Bags are where the chaos lives.</p></div></details><p class="dim t-small">Learn it once, , and you'll never untangle in public again.</p></div>	-
21.47.0	illustration/Getting a Smoke Smell Out of a Room	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Getting Smoke Smell Out of a Room</h3><div class="callout info">Air freshener hides it. You want to remove the particles and the residue.</div><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 90"><path d="M20 60 q40 -30 80 -10 q40 20 80 -20" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="3"/><path d="M20 75 q50 -8 80 0 q30 8 80 -4" fill="none" stroke="var(--line-2)" stroke-width="2"/><text x="100" y="86" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">open two windows, opposite sides</text></svg></div><ol class="list"><li><b>Ventilate crosswise</b> — two open windows, fan pointing out of one.</li><li><b>Wash soft surfaces:</b> curtains, bedding, cushion covers. Smoke settles in fabric.</li><li><b>Wipe hard surfaces</b> with warm water and a drop of dish soap.</li><li><b>Set out</b> a bowl of baking soda or activated charcoal overnight.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Didn't work?</summary><div class="body"><p>Check the AC filter and any carpet. Those hold the smell long after the air clears.</p></div></details><p class="dim t-small">Give it 48 hours, <regex>user_name</regex>. Smells forgive slowly.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Getting Smoke Smell Out of a Room</h3><div class="callout info">Air freshener hides it. You want to remove the particles and the residue.</div><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 90"><path d="M20 60 q40 -30 80 -10 q40 20 80 -20" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="3"/><path d="M20 75 q50 -8 80 0 q30 8 80 -4" fill="none" stroke="var(--line-2)" stroke-width="2"/><text x="100" y="86" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">open two windows, opposite sides</text></svg></div><ol class="list"><li><b>Ventilate crosswise</b> — two open windows, fan pointing out of one.</li><li><b>Wash soft surfaces:</b> curtains, bedding, cushion covers. Smoke settles in fabric.</li><li><b>Wipe hard surfaces</b> with warm water and a drop of dish soap.</li><li><b>Set out</b> a bowl of baking soda or activated charcoal overnight.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Didn't work?</summary><div class="body"><p>Check the AC filter and any carpet. Those hold the smell long after the air clears.</p></div></details><p class="dim t-small">Give it 48 hours, . Smells forgive slowly.</p></div>	-
21.48.0	illustration/Keeping a Cast-Iron Pan From Rusting	<div class="card"><p class="muted t-small">Why it happens, in one picture:</p><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 260 110" role="img" aria-label="Water on bare iron leads to rust; oil seals it out"><rect x="10" y="14" width="100" height="82" rx="8" fill="var(--bad-soft)" stroke="var(--line)"></rect><circle cx="38" cy="45" r="6" fill="var(--info)"></circle><circle cx="70" cy="66" r="6" fill="var(--info)"></circle><circle cx="52" cy="82" r="6" fill="var(--info)"></circle><text x="60" y="106" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">bare iron + water</text><text x="128" y="58" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">→</text><rect x="150" y="14" width="100" height="82" rx="8" fill="var(--good-soft)" stroke="var(--line)"></rect><path d="M150 44h100M150 66h100" stroke="var(--good)" stroke-width="8" fill="none"></path><text x="200" y="106" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">thin oil coat</text></svg></div><details class="acc"><summary>After every wash, <regex>user_name</regex></summary><div class="body"><p>Dry it on a low flame for a minute, then rub a drop of oil over the whole surface while it's still warm. Warm iron drinks the oil; cold iron leaves it greasy.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><p class="muted t-small">Why it happens, in one picture:</p><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 260 110" role="img" aria-label="Water on bare iron leads to rust; oil seals it out"><rect x="10" y="14" width="100" height="82" rx="8" fill="var(--bad-soft)" stroke="var(--line)"></rect><circle cx="38" cy="45" r="6" fill="var(--info)"></circle><circle cx="70" cy="66" r="6" fill="var(--info)"></circle><circle cx="52" cy="82" r="6" fill="var(--info)"></circle><text x="60" y="106" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">bare iron + water</text><text x="128" y="58" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">→</text><rect x="150" y="14" width="100" height="82" rx="8" fill="var(--good-soft)" stroke="var(--line)"></rect><path d="M150 44h100M150 66h100" stroke="var(--good)" stroke-width="8" fill="none"></path><text x="200" y="106" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">thin oil coat</text></svg></div><details class="acc"><summary>After every wash, </summary><div class="body"><p>Dry it on a low flame for a minute, then rub a drop of oil over the whole surface while it's still warm. Warm iron drinks the oil; cold iron leaves it greasy.</p></div></details></div>	-
