21.54.0	illustration/Removing a Stripped Screw the Second Time	<div class="card"><p class="muted">A rubber band, a wider bit, and patience. That's the whole kit, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><svg viewBox="0 0 240 90" class="chart"><rect x="14" y="38" width="130" height="14" rx="3" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line-2)"></rect><circle cx="156" cy="45" r="13" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"></circle><path d="M150 39 L162 51 M162 39 L150 51" stroke="var(--bad)" stroke-width="2"></path><rect x="176" y="36" width="50" height="18" rx="4" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent-2)" stroke-dasharray="4 3"></rect><text x="201" y="22" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">wider bit</text></svg><ul class="list"><li>Press down hard before turning. Most slips are lift, not torque.</li><li>Lay a wide rubber band over the head and push the driver through it.</li><li>Still spinning? Step up one bit size, or try a flathead that wedges.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>When it's truly gone</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Drill the head off with a bit the width of the shank, lift the part, then grip the stub with pliers. Slow speed, steady hand.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><p class="muted">A rubber band, a wider bit, and patience. That's the whole kit, .</p><svg viewBox="0 0 240 90" class="chart"><rect x="14" y="38" width="130" height="14" rx="3" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line-2)"></rect><circle cx="156" cy="45" r="13" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"></circle><path d="M150 39 L162 51 M162 39 L150 51" stroke="var(--bad)" stroke-width="2"></path><rect x="176" y="36" width="50" height="18" rx="4" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent-2)" stroke-dasharray="4 3"></rect><text x="201" y="22" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">wider bit</text></svg><ul class="list"><li>Press down hard before turning. Most slips are lift, not torque.</li><li>Lay a wide rubber band over the head and push the driver through it.</li><li>Still spinning? Step up one bit size, or try a flathead that wedges.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>When it's truly gone</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Drill the head off with a bit the width of the shank, lift the part, then grip the stub with pliers. Slow speed, steady hand.</p></div></details></div>	-
21.55.0	illustration/Getting Gum Off Fabric	<div class="card"><p class="muted">Cold first, then oil. Gum lets go when it's brittle, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="row"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Step 1</span><span class="value">Ice</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Step 2</span><span class="value">Scrape</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Step 3</span><span class="value">Oil</span></div></div><div class="meter"><div class="progress"><span style="width:80%"></span></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>The method</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Freeze it with an ice cube or a bag of frozen peas until it cracks.</li><li>Peel or scrape off the bulk with a butter knife. No picking at the smear.</li><li>Work a drop of cooking oil or peanut butter into what's left, then wash as usual.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p class="t-small">Test oil on a hidden seam first if the fabric is silk or dry-clean only.</p></div></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>If it already went through the dryer</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Re-freeze, then repeat. Heat spreads gum into the weave, so you may need two rounds.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><p class="muted">Cold first, then oil. Gum lets go when it's brittle, .</p><div class="row"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Step 1</span><span class="value">Ice</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Step 2</span><span class="value">Scrape</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Step 3</span><span class="value">Oil</span></div></div><div class="meter"><div class="progress"><span style="width:80%"></span></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>The method</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Freeze it with an ice cube or a bag of frozen peas until it cracks.</li><li>Peel or scrape off the bulk with a butter knife. No picking at the smear.</li><li>Work a drop of cooking oil or peanut butter into what's left, then wash as usual.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p class="t-small">Test oil on a hidden seam first if the fabric is silk or dry-clean only.</p></div></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>If it already went through the dryer</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Re-freeze, then repeat. Heat spreads gum into the weave, so you may need two rounds.</p></div></details></div>	-
21.56.0	illustration/Getting a Key Out of a Lock	<div class="card"><p class="muted">Don't push it deeper. That's the one move that turns a nuisance into a locksmith bill, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 220 80"><rect x="20" y="28" width="120" height="24" rx="6" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line-2)"></rect><circle cx="150" cy="40" r="12" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"></circle><rect x="162" y="34" width="34" height="12" rx="2" fill="var(--warn-soft)" stroke="var(--warn)"></rect><text x="179" y="22" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">stuck bit</text></svg></div><ul class="list"><li>Spray a little graphite or dry lubricant into the keyway. Not cooking oil.</li><li>Slide a thin hacksaw blade or a spare key in beside it, teeth toward the tip, and tease it out.</li><li>No luck? Needle-nose pliers if any part still shows.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Prevention, since you're here</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Worn keys snap. Get a fresh copy cut from the original, not from the tired spare.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><p class="muted">Don't push it deeper. That's the one move that turns a nuisance into a locksmith bill, .</p><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 220 80"><rect x="20" y="28" width="120" height="24" rx="6" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line-2)"></rect><circle cx="150" cy="40" r="12" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"></circle><rect x="162" y="34" width="34" height="12" rx="2" fill="var(--warn-soft)" stroke="var(--warn)"></rect><text x="179" y="22" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">stuck bit</text></svg></div><ul class="list"><li>Spray a little graphite or dry lubricant into the keyway. Not cooking oil.</li><li>Slide a thin hacksaw blade or a spare key in beside it, teeth toward the tip, and tease it out.</li><li>No luck? Needle-nose pliers if any part still shows.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Prevention, since you're here</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Worn keys snap. Get a fresh copy cut from the original, not from the tired spare.</p></div></details></div>	-
21.57.0	illustration/Keeping Cast Iron From Rusting	<div class="card"><p class="muted">Iron rusts the moment it's bare and wet. The fix is a thin coat of oil, every single time, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="row"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Enemy</span><span class="value">Water</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Ally</span><span class="value">Oil</span></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>After every wash</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Dry it on the stove over low heat for a minute. Heat drives off moisture.</li><li>Rub a few drops of flaxseed or grapeseed oil over the whole pan while warm.</li><li>Buff off the excess. It should look dark and dry, not greasy.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Already rusty?</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Scrub with steel wool and a bit of oil, wash, dry, then re-season. It comes back.</p></div></div></details><div class="callout bad"><p class="t-small">Never soak it, never dishwasher it. Overnight in the sink is how rust starts.</p></div></div>	<div class="card"><p class="muted">Iron rusts the moment it's bare and wet. The fix is a thin coat of oil, every single time, .</p><div class="row"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Enemy</span><span class="value">Water</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Ally</span><span class="value">Oil</span></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>After every wash</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Dry it on the stove over low heat for a minute. Heat drives off moisture.</li><li>Rub a few drops of flaxseed or grapeseed oil over the whole pan while warm.</li><li>Buff off the excess. It should look dark and dry, not greasy.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Already rusty?</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Scrub with steel wool and a bit of oil, wash, dry, then re-season. It comes back.</p></div></div></details><div class="callout bad"><p class="t-small">Never soak it, never dishwasher it. Overnight in the sink is how rust starts.</p></div></div>	-
21.58.0	illustration/Choosing a Ripe Watermelon	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Picking a Ripe Watermelon</div><p class="muted"><regex>user_name</regex>, thumping is theater. Use your hands and eyes instead.</p><ul class="list"><li><strong>Field spot.</strong> Look for a creamy yellow patch where it sat on the ground. White or absent means picked too soon.</li><li><strong>Weight.</strong> Heavy for its size — it's mostly water. Light ones are sad inside.</li><li><strong>Webbing.</strong> Brown honeycomb scars on the rind usually mean more pollination, so sweeter fruit.</li><li><strong>Shape.</strong> Symmetrical, no soft dents or splits. Both ends should feel firm.</li></ul><div class="callout info">Skip the fridge test — a paler, duller rind is normal on some varieties.</div><details class="acc"><summary>Small watermelon or big?</summary><div class="body">Both work. Smaller ones ripen more evenly; bigger ones give you more to share.</div></details></div>	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Picking a Ripe Watermelon</div><p class="muted">, thumping is theater. Use your hands and eyes instead.</p><ul class="list"><li><strong>Field spot.</strong> Look for a creamy yellow patch where it sat on the ground. White or absent means picked too soon.</li><li><strong>Weight.</strong> Heavy for its size — it's mostly water. Light ones are sad inside.</li><li><strong>Webbing.</strong> Brown honeycomb scars on the rind usually mean more pollination, so sweeter fruit.</li><li><strong>Shape.</strong> Symmetrical, no soft dents or splits. Both ends should feel firm.</li></ul><div class="callout info">Skip the fridge test — a paler, duller rind is normal on some varieties.</div><details class="acc"><summary>Small watermelon or big?</summary><div class="body">Both work. Smaller ones ripen more evenly; bigger ones give you more to share.</div></details></div>	-
21.59.0	illustration/Washing Dishes Without Hating It	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Washing Dishes Without Losing Your Mind</div><div class="row between"><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</span></div><ol class="list"><li>Scrape, don't rinse. Food goes in the bin, not down the drain.</li><li>Fill the sink with hot water and a squirt of soap. Soak the greasy stuff first.</li><li>Clean in order: glasses, plates, utensils, then the gross pans.</li><li>Rinse in a second basin or a slow stream — not a full blast.</li><li>Air-dry on a rack. Towels just move grease around.</li></ol><div class="callout good">A little soak does most of the scrubbing for you. Let chemistry earn its keep.</div><details class="acc"><summary>Cast iron and wood?</summary><div class="body">Hand-wash, dry immediately, and never soak them. A little oil on the pan after.</div></details></div>	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Washing Dishes Without Losing Your Mind</div><div class="row between"><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</span></div><ol class="list"><li>Scrape, don't rinse. Food goes in the bin, not down the drain.</li><li>Fill the sink with hot water and a squirt of soap. Soak the greasy stuff first.</li><li>Clean in order: glasses, plates, utensils, then the gross pans.</li><li>Rinse in a second basin or a slow stream — not a full blast.</li><li>Air-dry on a rack. Towels just move grease around.</li></ol><div class="callout good">A little soak does most of the scrubbing for you. Let chemistry earn its keep.</div><details class="acc"><summary>Cast iron and wood?</summary><div class="body">Hand-wash, dry immediately, and never soak them. A little oil on the pan after.</div></details></div>	-
21.60.0	illustration/Getting Better Sleep on a Hot Night	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Sleeping Through a Hot Night</div><p class="muted">The trick isn't cooling the room. It's cooling <em>you</em>.</p><ul class="list"><li><strong>Warm shower before bed.</strong> Sounds wrong, works anyway — it dumps heat from your skin.</li><li><strong>Damp sheet.</strong> Cold water wrung out, laid over you, fan aimed at it. Poor-person air conditioning.</li><li><strong>Feet and wrists out.</strong> Those are your radiators. Let them breathe.</li><li><strong>Frozen water bottle.</strong> Wrap it in a sock, tuck it behind your knees.</li><li><strong>Block the day.</strong> Close curtains during sunlight hours, open windows at night.</li></ul><div class="callout warn">Fan directly on you, not into an empty room. Moving air only cools skin.</div></div>	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Sleeping Through a Hot Night</div><p class="muted">The trick isn't cooling the room. It's cooling <em>you</em>.</p><ul class="list"><li><strong>Warm shower before bed.</strong> Sounds wrong, works anyway — it dumps heat from your skin.</li><li><strong>Damp sheet.</strong> Cold water wrung out, laid over you, fan aimed at it. Poor-person air conditioning.</li><li><strong>Feet and wrists out.</strong> Those are your radiators. Let them breathe.</li><li><strong>Frozen water bottle.</strong> Wrap it in a sock, tuck it behind your knees.</li><li><strong>Block the day.</strong> Close curtains during sunlight hours, open windows at night.</li></ul><div class="callout warn">Fan directly on you, not into an empty room. Moving air only cools skin.</div></div>	-
21.61.0	illustration/Packing a Weekender Bag	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Packing a Weekender in Ten Minutes</div><p class="muted">Aim for one bag you can carry with one hand.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><div class="label">Bottoms</div><div class="value">2</div></div><div class="stat"><div class="label">Tops</div><div class="value">3</div></div><div class="stat"><div class="label">Socks</div><div class="value">2<span class="unit">pr</span></div></div><div class="stat"><div class="label">Underwear</div><div class="value">2<span class="unit">pr</span></div></div></div><ul class="list"><li>Roll, don't fold. Less creasing, more room.</li><li>Toiletries in a zip bag, and decant into tiny bottles.</li><li>One pair of shoes <em>worn</em>, one packed.</li><li>Charger, meds, and a book. The non-negotiable trio.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>The just-in-case layer</summary><div class="body">A thin sweater and one collapsible tote. That covers most surprises.</div></details></div>	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Packing a Weekender in Ten Minutes</div><p class="muted">Aim for one bag you can carry with one hand.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><div class="label">Bottoms</div><div class="value">2</div></div><div class="stat"><div class="label">Tops</div><div class="value">3</div></div><div class="stat"><div class="label">Socks</div><div class="value">2<span class="unit">pr</span></div></div><div class="stat"><div class="label">Underwear</div><div class="value">2<span class="unit">pr</span></div></div></div><ul class="list"><li>Roll, don't fold. Less creasing, more room.</li><li>Toiletries in a zip bag, and decant into tiny bottles.</li><li>One pair of shoes <em>worn</em>, one packed.</li><li>Charger, meds, and a book. The non-negotiable trio.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>The just-in-case layer</summary><div class="body">A thin sweater and one collapsible tote. That covers most surprises.</div></details></div>	-
21.62.0	illustration/Fixing a Squeaky Door Hinge	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Silencing a Squeaky Hinge</div><p class="muted">Squeaks are just metal complaining. Give it a reason to relax.</p><ol class="list"><li>Pry off the hinge pin cap if there is one. Tap the pin upward with a screwdriver and hammer.</li><li>Wipe the pin clean with a rag. Old grit causes most of the noise.</li><li>Rub a small amount of petroleum jelly or white lithium grease on the pin.</li><li>Slide the pin back down and work the door open and closed a few times.</li></ol><div class="callout info">Cooking spray works in a pinch, but it dries out and attracts dust. Grease lasts longer.</div><details class="acc"><summary>Still squeaking?</summary><div class="body">Check if the hinge plate is loose. A quarter turn on the screws often does it.</div></details></div>	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Silencing a Squeaky Hinge</div><p class="muted">Squeaks are just metal complaining. Give it a reason to relax.</p><ol class="list"><li>Pry off the hinge pin cap if there is one. Tap the pin upward with a screwdriver and hammer.</li><li>Wipe the pin clean with a rag. Old grit causes most of the noise.</li><li>Rub a small amount of petroleum jelly or white lithium grease on the pin.</li><li>Slide the pin back down and work the door open and closed a few times.</li></ol><div class="callout info">Cooking spray works in a pinch, but it dries out and attracts dust. Grease lasts longer.</div><details class="acc"><summary>Still squeaking?</summary><div class="body">Check if the hinge plate is loose. A quarter turn on the screws often does it.</div></details></div>	-
21.63.0	illustration/Reading a Train Timetable	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Reading a Train Timetable</div><p class="muted">One habit saves you a missed connection: read <em>left to right</em>.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Pick your direction.</strong> Columns are stations, rows are trains, or the reverse. Check the header.</li><li><strong>Find your starting row.</strong> Follow it across, not down.</li><li><strong>Watch for letters and symbols.</strong> Little footnotes mean things like "weekdays only" or "stops on request".</li><li><strong>Reverse the trip.</strong> A return trip may sit on a different page or section entirely.</li></ol><div class="callout good">Apps are fine, but a printed timetable tells you why a train skipped your station.</div><details class="acc"><summary>Two times, same train?</summary><div class="body">Arrival and departure differ. That's the dwell time, not a mistake.</div></details></div>	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Reading a Train Timetable</div><p class="muted">One habit saves you a missed connection: read <em>left to right</em>.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Pick your direction.</strong> Columns are stations, rows are trains, or the reverse. Check the header.</li><li><strong>Find your starting row.</strong> Follow it across, not down.</li><li><strong>Watch for letters and symbols.</strong> Little footnotes mean things like "weekdays only" or "stops on request".</li><li><strong>Reverse the trip.</strong> A return trip may sit on a different page or section entirely.</li></ol><div class="callout good">Apps are fine, but a printed timetable tells you why a train skipped your station.</div><details class="acc"><summary>Two times, same train?</summary><div class="body">Arrival and departure differ. That's the dwell time, not a mistake.</div></details></div>	-
21.0.1	illustration/Packing a Carry-On for a Cold City	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Cold-city carry-on, in layers</h3><p class="t-small muted">Plan for <regex>user_name</regex>: three layers, two pairs of shoes, one bag.</p><ol class="list"><li>Wear the bulkiest coat and boots on the plane.</li><li>Roll base layers; leave the puffy jacket flat in the lid.</li><li>Pack 3 tops, 2 bottoms, 2 socks per day-ish. One knit is enough.</li><li>Squish air out with compression bags. It buys a whole outfit.</li></ol><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 120 60" role="img" aria-label="Layered clothing illustration"><rect x="10" y="34" width="100" height="8" rx="3" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"></rect><rect x="18" y="24" width="84" height="8" rx="3" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"></rect><rect x="26" y="14" width="68" height="8" rx="3" fill="var(--accent)" opacity="0.35"></rect></svg></div><div class="callout info"><p>Rule of thumb: if you can't lift the bag with one arm, it's a checked bag you haven't admitted to yet.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Cold-city carry-on, in layers</h3><p class="t-small muted">Plan for : three layers, two pairs of shoes, one bag.</p><ol class="list"><li>Wear the bulkiest coat and boots on the plane.</li><li>Roll base layers; leave the puffy jacket flat in the lid.</li><li>Pack 3 tops, 2 bottoms, 2 socks per day-ish. One knit is enough.</li><li>Squish air out with compression bags. It buys a whole outfit.</li></ol><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 120 60" role="img" aria-label="Layered clothing illustration"><rect x="10" y="34" width="100" height="8" rx="3" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"></rect><rect x="18" y="24" width="84" height="8" rx="3" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"></rect><rect x="26" y="14" width="68" height="8" rx="3" fill="var(--accent)" opacity="0.35"></rect></svg></div><div class="callout info"><p>Rule of thumb: if you can't lift the bag with one arm, it's a checked bag you haven't admitted to yet.</p></div></div></div>	-
21.1.1	illustration/Keeping a Basil Plant Alive	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Basil, the honest version</h3><p class="t-small dim">The supermarket pot is many plants crammed together. Separate them and everything changes.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Light</span><span class="value">6h<small class="unit"> sun</small></span><span class="delta down">south window</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Water</span><span class="value">top<small class="unit"> 2cm</small></span><span class="delta down">when dry</span></div></div><ul class="list"><li>Split the clump into 3-4 pots. Yes, really.</li><li>Pinch above a leaf pair to make it branch, not stretch.</li><li>Never let it flower unless you want seeds and no leaves.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Why did the leaves go black?</summary><div class="body t-small muted">Cold water on cold roots, usually. Water in the morning with room-temperature water. Basil hates a soggy bottom.</div></details></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Basil, the honest version</h3><p class="t-small dim">The supermarket pot is many plants crammed together. Separate them and everything changes.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Light</span><span class="value">6h<small class="unit"> sun</small></span><span class="delta down">south window</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Water</span><span class="value">top<small class="unit"> 2cm</small></span><span class="delta down">when dry</span></div></div><ul class="list"><li>Split the clump into 3-4 pots. Yes, really.</li><li>Pinch above a leaf pair to make it branch, not stretch.</li><li>Never let it flower unless you want seeds and no leaves.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Why did the leaves go black?</summary><div class="body t-small muted">Cold water on cold roots, usually. Water in the morning with room-temperature water. Basil hates a soggy bottom.</div></details></div></div>	-
21.2.1	illustration/Learning to Whistle With Your Fingers	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">The two-finger whistle</h3><p class="t-small muted">Volume comes from air, not force. <regex>user_name</regex>, keep it loose.</p><ol class="list"><li>Wet your lips. Fold your tongue back, tip resting behind the lower teeth.</li><li>Two fingers in the corners of your mouth, pressing the tongue back and up.</li><li>Blow <em>down</em> over the tongue, not out. Steady, not hard.</li></ol><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 120 50" role="img" aria-label="Airflow direction"><path d="M10 12 C40 12 60 34 110 34" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="3"></path><path d="M104 28 l8 6 -8 6" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="3"></path><text x="10" y="9" class="lbl">blow</text></svg></div><div class="callout warn"><p>You'll get a wet raspberry for a while. That's a tuning note, not a failure.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">The two-finger whistle</h3><p class="t-small muted">Volume comes from air, not force. , keep it loose.</p><ol class="list"><li>Wet your lips. Fold your tongue back, tip resting behind the lower teeth.</li><li>Two fingers in the corners of your mouth, pressing the tongue back and up.</li><li>Blow <em>down</em> over the tongue, not out. Steady, not hard.</li></ol><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 120 50" role="img" aria-label="Airflow direction"><path d="M10 12 C40 12 60 34 110 34" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="3"></path><path d="M104 28 l8 6 -8 6" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="3"></path><text x="10" y="9" class="lbl">blow</text></svg></div><div class="callout warn"><p>You'll get a wet raspberry for a while. That's a tuning note, not a failure.</p></div></div></div>	-
21.3.1	illustration/Storing Winter Boots Until Next Year	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Boots, off duty</h3><p class="t-small dim">Dust, moisture, and creased shafts. That's the battle, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ol class="list"><li>Brush off dried salt and mud before anything else.</li><li>Clean, then condition leather. Let it dry away from heat.</li><li>Stuff shafts with rolled magazines or a boot tree. Upright or they slump.</li><li>Store in cloth, not plastic. Plastic traps the damp.</li></ol><div class="row between"><span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>Cloth bag</span><span class="badge bad"><i class="dot"></i>Plastic bin</span></div><div class="callout good"><p>One cedar block per boot keeps them smelling like a cabin, not a gym bag.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Boots, off duty</h3><p class="t-small dim">Dust, moisture, and creased shafts. That's the battle, .</p><ol class="list"><li>Brush off dried salt and mud before anything else.</li><li>Clean, then condition leather. Let it dry away from heat.</li><li>Stuff shafts with rolled magazines or a boot tree. Upright or they slump.</li><li>Store in cloth, not plastic. Plastic traps the damp.</li></ol><div class="row between"><span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>Cloth bag</span><span class="badge bad"><i class="dot"></i>Plastic bin</span></div><div class="callout good"><p>One cedar block per boot keeps them smelling like a cabin, not a gym bag.</p></div></div></div>	-
21.4.1	illustration/Getting a Stuck Ring Off Without Panic	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Calm, then physics</h3><p class="t-small muted">First: <regex>user_name</regex>, don't yank. Swelling means the finger wins every tug-of-war.</p><ol class="list"><li>Raise the hand above your head for a few minutes.</li><li>Soap, oil, or lotion, generously. Work the ring in a twist, never straight off.</li><li>Advance past the knuckle, keep going. Back off if it pinches.</li><li>Still stuck? The dental floss trick: thread under the ring, wrap the finger from the ring outward, then unwrap the floss to slide it off.</li></ol><div class="callout bad"><p>Finger blue, numb, or angry? Stop and get it cut at a jeweler or clinic. Metal is replaceable.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Calm, then physics</h3><p class="t-small muted">First: , don't yank. Swelling means the finger wins every tug-of-war.</p><ol class="list"><li>Raise the hand above your head for a few minutes.</li><li>Soap, oil, or lotion, generously. Work the ring in a twist, never straight off.</li><li>Advance past the knuckle, keep going. Back off if it pinches.</li><li>Still stuck? The dental floss trick: thread under the ring, wrap the finger from the ring outward, then unwrap the floss to slide it off.</li></ol><div class="callout bad"><p>Finger blue, numb, or angry? Stop and get it cut at a jeweler or clinic. Metal is replaceable.</p></div></div></div>	-
21.5.1	illustration/Cleaning a Bagless Vacuum Filter	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Suction returns when the filter breathes</h3><p class="t-small dim">Most "broken" bagless vacuums are just clogged, <regex>user_name</regex>. Ten minutes, no parts.</p><ol class="list"><li>Unplug it. Always first.</li><li>Empty the bin, then tap the filter over a trash can.</li><li>Rinse under lukewarm water until it runs clear. No soap needed.</li><li>Air-dry 24 hours. Bone dry, or you'll smell mildew every sweep.</li></ol><div class="meter" role="img" aria-label="Suction after cleaning"><span style="width:92%"></span></div><p class="t-small muted">Suction after an honest cleaning.</p><details class="acc"><summary>How often?</summary><div class="body t-small muted">Tap it monthly, wash it every 3 months. More often with pets or a dusty remodel.</div></details></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Suction returns when the filter breathes</h3><p class="t-small dim">Most "broken" bagless vacuums are just clogged, . Ten minutes, no parts.</p><ol class="list"><li>Unplug it. Always first.</li><li>Empty the bin, then tap the filter over a trash can.</li><li>Rinse under lukewarm water until it runs clear. No soap needed.</li><li>Air-dry 24 hours. Bone dry, or you'll smell mildew every sweep.</li></ol><div class="meter" role="img" aria-label="Suction after cleaning"><span style="width:92%"></span></div><p class="t-small muted">Suction after an honest cleaning.</p><details class="acc"><summary>How often?</summary><div class="body t-small muted">Tap it monthly, wash it every 3 months. More often with pets or a dusty remodel.</div></details></div></div>	-
21.6.1	illustration/Reseasoning a Cast-Iron Pan After It Loses Its Finish	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Reseasoning a Cast-Iron Pan</div>  <p class="t-small muted">So the black came off. It happens. Here's the fix.</p>  <div class="chart">    <svg viewBox="0 0 120 90">      <ellipse cx="60" cy="45" rx="50" ry="30" fill="var(--bg-alt)" stroke="var(--line-2)"/>      <ellipse cx="60" cy="45" rx="35" ry="20" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line)"/>      <path d="M25 45 Q60 15 95 45" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/>      <circle cx="60" cy="45" r="6" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"/>      <text x="60" y="48" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl" font-size="6">season</text>    </svg>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Scrub off any rust with steel wool and hot water. Dry it completely.</li>    <li>Rub a thin layer of flaxseed or canola oil all over. Buff off the excess — it should look matte, not greasy.</li>    <li>Bake upside down at 230°C (450°F) for an hour. Let it cool in the oven.</li>    <li>Repeat the oil-and-bake cycle 2–3 times for a solid coat.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout info">    <span class="badge info">tip</span>    <p class="t-small">If it smokes a lot, your oil layer is too thick. Wipe more off next time.</p>  </div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Reseasoning a Cast-Iron Pan</div>  <p class="t-small muted">So the black came off. It happens. Here's the fix.</p>  <div class="chart">    <svg viewBox="0 0 120 90">      <ellipse cx="60" cy="45" rx="50" ry="30" fill="var(--bg-alt)" stroke="var(--line-2)"/>      <ellipse cx="60" cy="45" rx="35" ry="20" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line)"/>      <path d="M25 45 Q60 15 95 45" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/>      <circle cx="60" cy="45" r="6" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"/>      <text x="60" y="48" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl" font-size="6">season</text>    </svg>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Scrub off any rust with steel wool and hot water. Dry it completely.</li>    <li>Rub a thin layer of flaxseed or canola oil all over. Buff off the excess — it should look matte, not greasy.</li>    <li>Bake upside down at 230°C (450°F) for an hour. Let it cool in the oven.</li>    <li>Repeat the oil-and-bake cycle 2–3 times for a solid coat.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout info">    <span class="badge info">tip</span>    <p class="t-small">If it smokes a lot, your oil layer is too thick. Wipe more off next time.</p>  </div></div></div>	-
21.7.1	illustration/Getting Smoke Smell Out of a Room	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Getting Smoke Smell Out of a Room</div>  <p class="t-small muted">Smoke clings to fabric and walls. Here's how to evict it.</p>  <div class="chart">    <svg viewBox="0 0 120 90">      <rect x="20" y="20" width="80" height="50" rx="6" fill="var(--bg-alt)" stroke="var(--line-2)"/>      <path d="M35 40 Q45 30 55 40 T75 40" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink-3)" stroke-width="2"/>      <path d="M35 50 Q45 40 55 50 T75 50" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink-3)" stroke-width="2" opacity="0.6"/>      <circle cx="90" cy="30" r="8" fill="var(--good-soft)" stroke="var(--good)"/>      <text x="90" y="33" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl" font-size="6">✓</text>    </svg>  </div>  <ul class="list">    <li>Open windows and run a fan to push air out, not around.</li>    <li>Wash curtains, blankets, and anything porous. Smoke lives in fabric.</li>    <li>Wipe walls and hard surfaces with a vinegar-water mix.</li>    <li>Set out bowls of baking soda or activated charcoal. They absorb odor over a day or two.</li>    <li>If it lingers, an ozone generator works but you must leave the room.</li>  </ul>  <div class="callout warn">    <span class="badge warn">near miss</span>    <p class="t-small">Don't mask it with candles — that just adds another smell to the pile.</p>  </div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Getting Smoke Smell Out of a Room</div>  <p class="t-small muted">Smoke clings to fabric and walls. Here's how to evict it.</p>  <div class="chart">    <svg viewBox="0 0 120 90">      <rect x="20" y="20" width="80" height="50" rx="6" fill="var(--bg-alt)" stroke="var(--line-2)"/>      <path d="M35 40 Q45 30 55 40 T75 40" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink-3)" stroke-width="2"/>      <path d="M35 50 Q45 40 55 50 T75 50" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink-3)" stroke-width="2" opacity="0.6"/>      <circle cx="90" cy="30" r="8" fill="var(--good-soft)" stroke="var(--good)"/>      <text x="90" y="33" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl" font-size="6">✓</text>    </svg>  </div>  <ul class="list">    <li>Open windows and run a fan to push air out, not around.</li>    <li>Wash curtains, blankets, and anything porous. Smoke lives in fabric.</li>    <li>Wipe walls and hard surfaces with a vinegar-water mix.</li>    <li>Set out bowls of baking soda or activated charcoal. They absorb odor over a day or two.</li>    <li>If it lingers, an ozone generator works but you must leave the room.</li>  </ul>  <div class="callout warn">    <span class="badge warn">near miss</span>    <p class="t-small">Don't mask it with candles — that just adds another smell to the pile.</p>  </div></div></div>	-
21.8.1	illustration/Folding a Fitted Sheet Without Losing Your Mind	<div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Folding a Fitted Sheet Without Losing Your Mind</div>  <div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 240 120">    <rect x="20" y="30" width="60" height="60" rx="8" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"></rect>    <text x="50" y="65" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">1</text>    <line x1="88" y1="60" x2="108" y2="60" class="guide"></line>    <rect x="110" y="30" width="60" height="60" rx="8" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line-2)"></rect>    <text x="140" y="65" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">2</text>    <line x1="178" y1="60" x2="198" y2="60" class="guide"></line>    <rect x="200" y="42" width="30" height="36" rx="6" fill="var(--accent)"></rect>    <text x="215" y="65" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl in">3</text>  </svg></div>  <p>Corners first. That's the whole secret.</p>  <details class="acc"><summary>Steps</summary><div class="body">    <ol class="list">      <li>Hold two corners inside out, tuck one over the other.</li>      <li>Do the same with the other pair. Now you have a lumpy rectangle.</li>      <li>Lay flat, fold edges in until it's square-ish, then fold into thirds.</li>    </ol>  </div></details>  <p class="muted t-small">Honest note: it will never be as crisp as a flat sheet. That's fine, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Folding a Fitted Sheet Without Losing Your Mind</div>  <div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 240 120">    <rect x="20" y="30" width="60" height="60" rx="8" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"></rect>    <text x="50" y="65" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">1</text>    <line x1="88" y1="60" x2="108" y2="60" class="guide"></line>    <rect x="110" y="30" width="60" height="60" rx="8" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line-2)"></rect>    <text x="140" y="65" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">2</text>    <line x1="178" y1="60" x2="198" y2="60" class="guide"></line>    <rect x="200" y="42" width="30" height="36" rx="6" fill="var(--accent)"></rect>    <text x="215" y="65" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl in">3</text>  </svg></div>  <p>Corners first. That's the whole secret.</p>  <details class="acc"><summary>Steps</summary><div class="body">    <ol class="list">      <li>Hold two corners inside out, tuck one over the other.</li>      <li>Do the same with the other pair. Now you have a lumpy rectangle.</li>      <li>Lay flat, fold edges in until it's square-ish, then fold into thirds.</li>    </ol>  </div></details>  <p class="muted t-small">Honest note: it will never be as crisp as a flat sheet. That's fine, .</p></div>	-
21.9.1	illustration/Boiling an Egg So It Peels Cleanly	<div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Boiling an Egg So It Peels Cleanly</div>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat"><span class="label">Jammy</span><span class="value">6<span class="unit">min</span></span></div>    <div class="stat"><span class="label">Firm</span><span class="value">10<span class="unit">min</span></span></div>  </div>  <p>The trick isn't boiling — it's the ice bath and peeling under running water.</p>  <details class="acc"><summary>Full method</summary><div class="body">    <ol class="list">      <li>Lower eggs into already-boiling water.</li>      <li>Start the timer. 6 min jammy, 10 min firm.</li>      <li>Shock in ice water for 2 minutes.</li>      <li>Crack all over, peel under a thin stream of water.</li>    </ol>  </div></details>  <div class="callout warn">Very fresh eggs peel worse. Give them a week in the fridge first.</div></div>	<div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Boiling an Egg So It Peels Cleanly</div>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat"><span class="label">Jammy</span><span class="value">6<span class="unit">min</span></span></div>    <div class="stat"><span class="label">Firm</span><span class="value">10<span class="unit">min</span></span></div>  </div>  <p>The trick isn't boiling — it's the ice bath and peeling under running water.</p>  <details class="acc"><summary>Full method</summary><div class="body">    <ol class="list">      <li>Lower eggs into already-boiling water.</li>      <li>Start the timer. 6 min jammy, 10 min firm.</li>      <li>Shock in ice water for 2 minutes.</li>      <li>Crack all over, peel under a thin stream of water.</li>    </ol>  </div></details>  <div class="callout warn">Very fresh eggs peel worse. Give them a week in the fridge first.</div></div>	-
21.10.1	illustration/Getting a Splinter Out Gently	<div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Getting a Splinter Out Gently</div>  <div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 240 100">    <rect x="40" y="20" width="160" height="60" rx="30" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line-2)"></rect>    <line x1="90" y1="50" x2="150" y2="50" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round"></line>    <circle cx="90" cy="50" r="5" fill="var(--accent)"></circle>    <text x="120" y="90" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">grab the tip, pull with the entry angle</text>  </svg></div>  <p>Wash your hands, then pull in the same direction it went in. Pulling straight up snaps it.</p>  <details class="acc"><summary>If it won't budge</summary><div class="body">    <ul class="list">      <li>Soak the spot in warm water 10 minutes to soften skin.</li>      <li>Use clean tweezers, angled, not squeezing the skin.</li>      <li>Leave it overnight — skin pushes it out on its own, often.</li>    </ul>  </div></details>  <div class="callout bad">Deep, dark, or bleeding much? That's a clinic visit, not a kitchen-table project. <regex>user_name</regex>, don't play hero.</div></div>	<div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Getting a Splinter Out Gently</div>  <div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 240 100">    <rect x="40" y="20" width="160" height="60" rx="30" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line-2)"></rect>    <line x1="90" y1="50" x2="150" y2="50" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round"></line>    <circle cx="90" cy="50" r="5" fill="var(--accent)"></circle>    <text x="120" y="90" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">grab the tip, pull with the entry angle</text>  </svg></div>  <p>Wash your hands, then pull in the same direction it went in. Pulling straight up snaps it.</p>  <details class="acc"><summary>If it won't budge</summary><div class="body">    <ul class="list">      <li>Soak the spot in warm water 10 minutes to soften skin.</li>      <li>Use clean tweezers, angled, not squeezing the skin.</li>      <li>Leave it overnight — skin pushes it out on its own, often.</li>    </ul>  </div></details>  <div class="callout bad">Deep, dark, or bleeding much? That's a clinic visit, not a kitchen-table project. , don't play hero.</div></div>	-
21.11.1	illustration/Removing a Red Wine Stain From a Rug	<div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Removing a Red Wine Stain From a Rug</div>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat"><span class="label">Speed</span><span class="value">S<span class="unit">tier</span></span></div>    <div class="stat"><span class="label">Heat</span><span class="value">Never</span></div>  </div>  <p>Blot now, cold water only, no rubbing. Rubbing sets the dye into the fibers.</p>  <details class="acc"><summary>What to actually do</summary><div class="body">    <ol class="list">      <li>Blot with a white cloth until nothing new lifts.</li>      <li>Mix a drop of dish soap in cold water, dab, blot.</li>      <li>Repeat, then rinse with cold water and blot dry.</li>      <li>Still pink? Try a little white vinegar diluted 1:3.</li>    </ol>  </div></details>  <div class="callout info">Test any solution on a hidden corner first. Rugs differ wildly, <regex>user_name</regex>.</div></div>	<div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Removing a Red Wine Stain From a Rug</div>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat"><span class="label">Speed</span><span class="value">S<span class="unit">tier</span></span></div>    <div class="stat"><span class="label">Heat</span><span class="value">Never</span></div>  </div>  <p>Blot now, cold water only, no rubbing. Rubbing sets the dye into the fibers.</p>  <details class="acc"><summary>What to actually do</summary><div class="body">    <ol class="list">      <li>Blot with a white cloth until nothing new lifts.</li>      <li>Mix a drop of dish soap in cold water, dab, blot.</li>      <li>Repeat, then rinse with cold water and blot dry.</li>      <li>Still pink? Try a little white vinegar diluted 1:3.</li>    </ol>  </div></details>  <div class="callout info">Test any solution on a hidden corner first. Rugs differ wildly, .</div></div>	-
21.12.1	illustration/Keeping Bread From Going Stale Overnight	<div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Keeping Bread From Going Stale Overnight</div>  <table><thead><tr><th>Loaf</th><th>Where</th><th>How long</th></tr></thead><tbody>    <tr><td>Sourdough</td><td>Paper bag, cut side down</td><td>2–3 days</td></tr>    <tr><td>Sandwich loaf</td><td>Bag, room temp</td><td>3–5 days</td></tr>    <tr><td>Sliced, extra</td><td>Freezer</td><td>Months</td></tr>  </tbody></table>  <p>The fridge is the trap. Cold makes starch retrograde faster — stale in hours, not days.</p>  <details class="acc"><summary>Reviving a stale loaf</summary><div class="body">    <p>Run it under water, wrap in foil, bake at 180°C for 10 minutes. Sounds wrong, works every time.</p>  </div></details></div>	<div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Keeping Bread From Going Stale Overnight</div>  <table><thead><tr><th>Loaf</th><th>Where</th><th>How long</th></tr></thead><tbody>    <tr><td>Sourdough</td><td>Paper bag, cut side down</td><td>2–3 days</td></tr>    <tr><td>Sandwich loaf</td><td>Bag, room temp</td><td>3–5 days</td></tr>    <tr><td>Sliced, extra</td><td>Freezer</td><td>Months</td></tr>  </tbody></table>  <p>The fridge is the trap. Cold makes starch retrograde faster — stale in hours, not days.</p>  <details class="acc"><summary>Reviving a stale loaf</summary><div class="body">    <p>Run it under water, wrap in foil, bake at 180°C for 10 minutes. Sounds wrong, works every time.</p>  </div></details></div>	-
21.13.1	illustration/Silencing a Dripping Tap Before Bed	<div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Silencing a Dripping Tap Before Bed</div>  <div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 240 100">    <rect x="100" y="10" width="40" height="20" rx="4" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line-2)"></rect>    <circle cx="120" cy="52" r="5" fill="var(--accent)"></circle>    <circle cx="120" cy="78" r="3" fill="var(--accent-soft)"></circle>    <text x="120" y="96" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">turn the valve off at the wall</text>  </svg></div>  <p>For tonight: put a sponge under it. For tomorrow: it's almost always the washer.</p>  <details class="acc"><summary>The actual fix</summary><div class="body">    <ol class="list">      <li>Shut the water off under the sink.</li>      <li>Pop the cap off the handle, unscrew, lift the cartridge.</li>      <li>Swap the rubber washer — a few cents — and reassemble.</li>    </ol>  </div></details>  <div class="callout good">If the tap still drips after a new washer, the seat is worn. That's a plumber, <regex>user_name</regex>.</div></div>	<div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Silencing a Dripping Tap Before Bed</div>  <div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 240 100">    <rect x="100" y="10" width="40" height="20" rx="4" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line-2)"></rect>    <circle cx="120" cy="52" r="5" fill="var(--accent)"></circle>    <circle cx="120" cy="78" r="3" fill="var(--accent-soft)"></circle>    <text x="120" y="96" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">turn the valve off at the wall</text>  </svg></div>  <p>For tonight: put a sponge under it. For tomorrow: it's almost always the washer.</p>  <details class="acc"><summary>The actual fix</summary><div class="body">    <ol class="list">      <li>Shut the water off under the sink.</li>      <li>Pop the cap off the handle, unscrew, lift the cartridge.</li>      <li>Swap the rubber washer — a few cents — and reassemble.</li>    </ol>  </div></details>  <div class="callout good">If the tap still drips after a new washer, the seat is worn. That's a plumber, .</div></div>	-
21.14.1	illustration/Descaling a Shower Head	<div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Descaling a Shower Head</div>  <p class="muted t-small">Hard water leaves calcium crust that chokes the spray. A weekly rinse won't cut it—you need an acid soak.</p>  <div class="chart" aria-label="Scale buildup illustration">    <svg viewBox="0 0 200 80" role="img">      <rect x="10" y="20" width="180" height="40" rx="20" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line)"/>      <circle cx="30" cy="40" r="3" fill="var(--accent)"/>      <circle cx="45" cy="40" r="3" fill="var(--accent)"/>      <circle cx="60" cy="40" r="3" fill="var(--accent)"/>      <circle cx="75" cy="40" r="3" fill="var(--accent)"/>      <circle cx="90" cy="40" r="3" fill="var(--accent)"/>      <path d="M110 30 Q130 10 150 30 T190 30" stroke="var(--ink-3)" fill="none" stroke-width="1.5"/>      <text x="110" y="55" class="lbl" font-size="10">limescale</text>    </svg>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>How to descale (safe for most heads)</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Unscrew the shower head. If it's fixed, fill a plastic bag with vinegar and rubber-band it around the head.</li>        <li>Mix equal parts white vinegar and water. Submerge the head (or let the bag soak) for 1–2 hours.</li>        <li>Scrub the nozzles with an old toothbrush. For stubborn bits, use a pin to poke each hole.</li>        <li>Rinse thoroughly and reattach. Run hot water for a minute.</li>        <li>To prevent future buildup, dry the head after each shower or install a water softener.</li>      </ol>      <p class="callout info">Vinegar works for most finishes, but avoid it on brass or gold—use a commercial descaler instead.</p>    </div>  </details>  <div class="row between t-small muted">    <span>Time: 15 min + soak</span>    <span>Cost: ~$0.50</span>  </div></div>	<div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Descaling a Shower Head</div>  <p class="muted t-small">Hard water leaves calcium crust that chokes the spray. A weekly rinse won't cut it—you need an acid soak.</p>  <div class="chart" aria-label="Scale buildup illustration">    <svg viewBox="0 0 200 80" role="img">      <rect x="10" y="20" width="180" height="40" rx="20" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line)"/>      <circle cx="30" cy="40" r="3" fill="var(--accent)"/>      <circle cx="45" cy="40" r="3" fill="var(--accent)"/>      <circle cx="60" cy="40" r="3" fill="var(--accent)"/>      <circle cx="75" cy="40" r="3" fill="var(--accent)"/>      <circle cx="90" cy="40" r="3" fill="var(--accent)"/>      <path d="M110 30 Q130 10 150 30 T190 30" stroke="var(--ink-3)" fill="none" stroke-width="1.5"/>      <text x="110" y="55" class="lbl" font-size="10">limescale</text>    </svg>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>How to descale (safe for most heads)</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Unscrew the shower head. If it's fixed, fill a plastic bag with vinegar and rubber-band it around the head.</li>        <li>Mix equal parts white vinegar and water. Submerge the head (or let the bag soak) for 1–2 hours.</li>        <li>Scrub the nozzles with an old toothbrush. For stubborn bits, use a pin to poke each hole.</li>        <li>Rinse thoroughly and reattach. Run hot water for a minute.</li>        <li>To prevent future buildup, dry the head after each shower or install a water softener.</li>      </ol>      <p class="callout info">Vinegar works for most finishes, but avoid it on brass or gold—use a commercial descaler instead.</p>    </div>  </details>  <div class="row between t-small muted">    <span>Time: 15 min + soak</span>    <span>Cost: ~$0.50</span>  </div></div>	-
21.15.1	illustration/Getting Permanent Marker Off a Whiteboard	<div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Getting Permanent Marker Off a Whiteboard</div>  <p class="muted t-small">Permanent marker bonds with the board's surface, but a solvent can lift it without damage.</p>  <div class="chart">    <svg viewBox="0 0 200 80" role="img" aria-label="Whiteboard with marker stain">      <rect x="10" y="10" width="180" height="60" rx="8" fill="var(--panel)" stroke="var(--line)"/>      <path d="M40 30 Q60 20 80 30 T120 30" stroke="var(--bad)" fill="none" stroke-width="2"/>      <text x="100" y="50" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl" font-size="10">permanent marker</text>    </svg>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Methods that work</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li><strong>Dry-erase marker:</strong> Scribble over the permanent mark with a fresh dry-erase marker, then wipe. The solvent in the dry-erase ink reactivates the permanent ink.</li>        <li><strong>Rubbing alcohol:</strong> Apply 70% isopropyl alcohol to a cloth and rub. For tough spots, let it sit for 30 seconds.</li>        <li><strong>Hand sanitizer:</strong> Gel sanitizer clings to vertical boards. Squirt, wait, wipe.</li>        <li><strong>Hairspray:</strong> Spray, wait 10 seconds, wipe. Works in a pinch.</li>      </ul>      <p class="callout good">Always test in a corner first. Most whiteboards handle alcohol fine, but cheap laminates may cloud.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Getting Permanent Marker Off a Whiteboard</div>  <p class="muted t-small">Permanent marker bonds with the board's surface, but a solvent can lift it without damage.</p>  <div class="chart">    <svg viewBox="0 0 200 80" role="img" aria-label="Whiteboard with marker stain">      <rect x="10" y="10" width="180" height="60" rx="8" fill="var(--panel)" stroke="var(--line)"/>      <path d="M40 30 Q60 20 80 30 T120 30" stroke="var(--bad)" fill="none" stroke-width="2"/>      <text x="100" y="50" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl" font-size="10">permanent marker</text>    </svg>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Methods that work</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li><strong>Dry-erase marker:</strong> Scribble over the permanent mark with a fresh dry-erase marker, then wipe. The solvent in the dry-erase ink reactivates the permanent ink.</li>        <li><strong>Rubbing alcohol:</strong> Apply 70% isopropyl alcohol to a cloth and rub. For tough spots, let it sit for 30 seconds.</li>        <li><strong>Hand sanitizer:</strong> Gel sanitizer clings to vertical boards. Squirt, wait, wipe.</li>        <li><strong>Hairspray:</strong> Spray, wait 10 seconds, wipe. Works in a pinch.</li>      </ul>      <p class="callout good">Always test in a corner first. Most whiteboards handle alcohol fine, but cheap laminates may cloud.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
21.16.1	illustration/Organizing a Tangled Mess of Cables	<div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Organizing a Tangled Mess of Cables</div>  <p class="muted t-small">A drawer of mystery cables is a modern curse. Ten minutes now saves hours of untangling later.</p>  <div class="chart">    <svg viewBox="0 0 200 80" role="img" aria-label="Cable organization">      <path d="M20 40 Q40 10 60 40 T100 40 T140 40 T180 40" stroke="var(--accent)" fill="none" stroke-width="3"/>      <circle cx="20" cy="40" r="5" fill="var(--accent-2)"/>      <circle cx="180" cy="40" r="5" fill="var(--accent-2)"/>      <rect x="80" y="20" width="40" height="40" rx="8" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line)"/>      <text x="100" y="45" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl" font-size="8">tie</text>    </svg>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Step-by-step cable control</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li><strong>Declutter:</strong> Dump all cables in one spot. Keep only ones you use regularly. Recycle or donate the rest.</li>        <li><strong>Label:</strong> Use washi tape or a label maker to tag each cable with its device.</li>        <li><strong>Wrap:</strong> Use the over-under technique (like coiling a rope) to prevent kinks. Secure with a velcro tie or a bread bag clip.</li>        <li><strong>Store:</strong> Use a drawer organizer, a shoe box with dividers, or pegboard with hooks. Keep like with like (USB, power, audio).</li>        <li><strong>Desk setup:</strong> Route cables behind furniture with adhesive clips. Use a cable tray under the desk.</li>      </ol>      <p class="callout info">For chargers you use daily, leave them plugged in and use a cable management box to hide the mess.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Organizing a Tangled Mess of Cables</div>  <p class="muted t-small">A drawer of mystery cables is a modern curse. Ten minutes now saves hours of untangling later.</p>  <div class="chart">    <svg viewBox="0 0 200 80" role="img" aria-label="Cable organization">      <path d="M20 40 Q40 10 60 40 T100 40 T140 40 T180 40" stroke="var(--accent)" fill="none" stroke-width="3"/>      <circle cx="20" cy="40" r="5" fill="var(--accent-2)"/>      <circle cx="180" cy="40" r="5" fill="var(--accent-2)"/>      <rect x="80" y="20" width="40" height="40" rx="8" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line)"/>      <text x="100" y="45" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl" font-size="8">tie</text>    </svg>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Step-by-step cable control</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li><strong>Declutter:</strong> Dump all cables in one spot. Keep only ones you use regularly. Recycle or donate the rest.</li>        <li><strong>Label:</strong> Use washi tape or a label maker to tag each cable with its device.</li>        <li><strong>Wrap:</strong> Use the over-under technique (like coiling a rope) to prevent kinks. Secure with a velcro tie or a bread bag clip.</li>        <li><strong>Store:</strong> Use a drawer organizer, a shoe box with dividers, or pegboard with hooks. Keep like with like (USB, power, audio).</li>        <li><strong>Desk setup:</strong> Route cables behind furniture with adhesive clips. Use a cable tray under the desk.</li>      </ol>      <p class="callout info">For chargers you use daily, leave them plugged in and use a cable management box to hide the mess.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
21.17.1	illustration/Cleaning a Glass Stovetop Without Scratching	<div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Cleaning a Glass Stovetop Without Scratching</div>  <p class="muted t-small">Glass cooktops look great until a burnt-on spill fuses to the surface. Abrasives are the enemy—gentle and patient wins.</p>  <div class="chart">    <svg viewBox="0 0 200 80" role="img" aria-label="Glass stovetop cleaning">      <rect x="10" y="10" width="180" height="60" rx="10" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line)"/>      <circle cx="50" cy="40" r="20" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink-3)" stroke-width="2"/>      <circle cx="100" cy="40" r="20" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink-3)" stroke-width="2"/>      <circle cx="150" cy="40" r="20" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink-3)" stroke-width="2"/>      <path d="M130 30 Q150 20 170 30" stroke="var(--good)" fill="none" stroke-width="2"/>      <text x="150" y="25" class="lbl" font-size="8">clean</text>    </svg>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Safe cleaning routine</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Let the stovetop cool completely.</li>        <li>Apply a dedicated glass stovetop cleaner (or a paste of baking soda and water) to the stains.</li>        <li>Let it sit for 15–20 minutes to soften burnt-on food.</li>        <li>Wipe with a soft cloth or non-scratch sponge. For stubborn spots, use a plastic scraper held at a 30-degree angle.</li>        <li>Buff with a clean microfiber cloth and a dab of glass cleaner for shine.</li>      </ol>      <p class="callout warn">Never use steel wool, abrasive powders, or scouring pads—they'll scratch the glass permanently.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Cleaning a Glass Stovetop Without Scratching</div>  <p class="muted t-small">Glass cooktops look great until a burnt-on spill fuses to the surface. Abrasives are the enemy—gentle and patient wins.</p>  <div class="chart">    <svg viewBox="0 0 200 80" role="img" aria-label="Glass stovetop cleaning">      <rect x="10" y="10" width="180" height="60" rx="10" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line)"/>      <circle cx="50" cy="40" r="20" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink-3)" stroke-width="2"/>      <circle cx="100" cy="40" r="20" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink-3)" stroke-width="2"/>      <circle cx="150" cy="40" r="20" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink-3)" stroke-width="2"/>      <path d="M130 30 Q150 20 170 30" stroke="var(--good)" fill="none" stroke-width="2"/>      <text x="150" y="25" class="lbl" font-size="8">clean</text>    </svg>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Safe cleaning routine</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Let the stovetop cool completely.</li>        <li>Apply a dedicated glass stovetop cleaner (or a paste of baking soda and water) to the stains.</li>        <li>Let it sit for 15–20 minutes to soften burnt-on food.</li>        <li>Wipe with a soft cloth or non-scratch sponge. For stubborn spots, use a plastic scraper held at a 30-degree angle.</li>        <li>Buff with a clean microfiber cloth and a dab of glass cleaner for shine.</li>      </ol>      <p class="callout warn">Never use steel wool, abrasive powders, or scouring pads—they'll scratch the glass permanently.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
21.18.1	illustration/Quick Pickled Onions	<div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Quick Pickled Onions</div>  <p class="muted t-small">Tangy, crunchy, and ready in 30 minutes. They wake up tacos, sandwiches, and salads.</p>  <div class="chart">    <svg viewBox="0 0 200 80" role="img" aria-label="Pickled onions">      <circle cx="50" cy="40" r="15" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"/>      <circle cx="100" cy="40" r="15" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"/>      <circle cx="150" cy="40" r="15" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"/>      <text x="100" y="70" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl" font-size="10">vinegar + onion</text>    </svg>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Recipe (makes 1 jar)</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>1 red onion, thinly sliced</li>        <li>1/2 cup apple cider vinegar</li>        <li>1/2 cup water</li>        <li>1 tbsp sugar</li>        <li>1 tsp salt</li>      </ul>      <p><strong>Method:</strong> Pack onion slices in a clean jar. In a small saucepan, combine vinegar, water, sugar, and salt. Bring to a simmer, stirring until dissolved. Pour hot brine over onions. Let cool, then refrigerate. Ready in 30 minutes; best after a day. Keeps 2–3 weeks.</p>      <p class="callout good">Add a pinch of red pepper flakes or a few peppercorns for extra zing.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Quick Pickled Onions</div>  <p class="muted t-small">Tangy, crunchy, and ready in 30 minutes. They wake up tacos, sandwiches, and salads.</p>  <div class="chart">    <svg viewBox="0 0 200 80" role="img" aria-label="Pickled onions">      <circle cx="50" cy="40" r="15" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"/>      <circle cx="100" cy="40" r="15" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"/>      <circle cx="150" cy="40" r="15" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"/>      <text x="100" y="70" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl" font-size="10">vinegar + onion</text>    </svg>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Recipe (makes 1 jar)</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>1 red onion, thinly sliced</li>        <li>1/2 cup apple cider vinegar</li>        <li>1/2 cup water</li>        <li>1 tbsp sugar</li>        <li>1 tsp salt</li>      </ul>      <p><strong>Method:</strong> Pack onion slices in a clean jar. In a small saucepan, combine vinegar, water, sugar, and salt. Bring to a simmer, stirring until dissolved. Pour hot brine over onions. Let cool, then refrigerate. Ready in 30 minutes; best after a day. Keeps 2–3 weeks.</p>      <p class="callout good">Add a pinch of red pepper flakes or a few peppercorns for extra zing.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
21.19.1	illustration/Removing Glue Residue From a Label	<div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Removing Glue Residue From a Label</div>  <p class="muted t-small">That gummy ghost left by a price tag or jar label is no match for a little oil and patience.</p>  <div class="chart">    <svg viewBox="0 0 200 80" role="img" aria-label="Label residue removal">      <rect x="20" y="20" width="60" height="40" rx="5" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line)"/>      <path d="M80 30 Q90 20 100 30 T120 30" stroke="var(--warn)" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="4"/>      <circle cx="150" cy="40" r="15" fill="var(--good-soft)" stroke="var(--good)"/>      <text x="150" y="45" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl" font-size="10">gone</text>    </svg>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Best methods</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li><strong>Oil:</strong> Rub a little cooking oil (olive, vegetable) over the sticky area. Let sit 5 minutes, then wipe with a paper towel. Works on glass, plastic, and metal.</li>        <li><strong>Rubbing alcohol:</strong> For non-porous surfaces, saturate a cotton ball and rub. Great for removing residue from electronics.</li>        <li><strong>Vinegar:</strong> Soak a cloth in white vinegar and press onto the sticker for 10 minutes.</li>        <li><strong>Heat:</strong> Use a hair dryer to warm the adhesive, then peel. Good for stubborn labels on jars.</li>      </ul>      <p class="callout info">Avoid oily methods on wood or unsealed surfaces—they can stain. Test first.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Removing Glue Residue From a Label</div>  <p class="muted t-small">That gummy ghost left by a price tag or jar label is no match for a little oil and patience.</p>  <div class="chart">    <svg viewBox="0 0 200 80" role="img" aria-label="Label residue removal">      <rect x="20" y="20" width="60" height="40" rx="5" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line)"/>      <path d="M80 30 Q90 20 100 30 T120 30" stroke="var(--warn)" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="4"/>      <circle cx="150" cy="40" r="15" fill="var(--good-soft)" stroke="var(--good)"/>      <text x="150" y="45" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl" font-size="10">gone</text>    </svg>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Best methods</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li><strong>Oil:</strong> Rub a little cooking oil (olive, vegetable) over the sticky area. Let sit 5 minutes, then wipe with a paper towel. Works on glass, plastic, and metal.</li>        <li><strong>Rubbing alcohol:</strong> For non-porous surfaces, saturate a cotton ball and rub. Great for removing residue from electronics.</li>        <li><strong>Vinegar:</strong> Soak a cloth in white vinegar and press onto the sticker for 10 minutes.</li>        <li><strong>Heat:</strong> Use a hair dryer to warm the adhesive, then peel. Good for stubborn labels on jars.</li>      </ul>      <p class="callout info">Avoid oily methods on wood or unsealed surfaces—they can stain. Test first.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
21.20.1	illustration/Sharpening a Kitchen Knife Without a Stone	<div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">The Mug Trick</h2><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 100"><path d="M20 80 L180 80" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M40 20 L60 80 L140 80 L160 20 Z" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M90 30 L110 30" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="3"/><path d="M85 40 L115 40" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="3"/></svg></div><p>No whetstone? The unglazed ring on the bottom of a ceramic mug works in a pinch. Hold the knife at a 20-degree angle and draw it across the rough ring 10–15 times per side. <span class="muted">It's not a perfect edge, but it'll get you through dinner.</span></p><details class="acc"><summary>How to find the angle</summary><div class="body"><p>Stack two coins under the spine of the blade. That's about 20 degrees. Keep the spine off the mug and maintain that gap as you stroke.</p></div></details><p class="t-small dim">Then hone on the smooth side of the mug to realign the edge.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">The Mug Trick</h2><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 100"><path d="M20 80 L180 80" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M40 20 L60 80 L140 80 L160 20 Z" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M90 30 L110 30" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="3"/><path d="M85 40 L115 40" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="3"/></svg></div><p>No whetstone? The unglazed ring on the bottom of a ceramic mug works in a pinch. Hold the knife at a 20-degree angle and draw it across the rough ring 10–15 times per side. <span class="muted">It's not a perfect edge, but it'll get you through dinner.</span></p><details class="acc"><summary>How to find the angle</summary><div class="body"><p>Stack two coins under the spine of the blade. That's about 20 degrees. Keep the spine off the mug and maintain that gap as you stroke.</p></div></details><p class="t-small dim">Then hone on the smooth side of the mug to realign the edge.</p></div>	-
21.21.1	illustration/Cleaning a Coffee Grinder Without Taking It Apart	<div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">The Rice Method</h2><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 100"><circle cx="100" cy="50" r="30" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M80 40 Q100 20 120 40" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2" fill="none"/><circle cx="80" cy="60" r="4" fill="var(--accent)"/><circle cx="100" cy="65" r="4" fill="var(--accent)"/><circle cx="120" cy="60" r="4" fill="var(--accent)"/></svg></div><p>Grind a handful of uncooked white rice to a powder. The rice absorbs oils and pushes out old grounds. Dump it, then grind a second small batch and toss that too. <span class="muted">Your next cup won't taste like last week's beans.</span></p><details class="acc"><summary>For burr grinders</summary><div class="body"><p>Rice can jam some burr grinders. Instead, use a stiff brush and a vacuum with a crevice tool. Never wash with water.</p></div></details><p class="t-small dim">Do this monthly, or whenever the grinder smells stale.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">The Rice Method</h2><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 100"><circle cx="100" cy="50" r="30" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M80 40 Q100 20 120 40" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2" fill="none"/><circle cx="80" cy="60" r="4" fill="var(--accent)"/><circle cx="100" cy="65" r="4" fill="var(--accent)"/><circle cx="120" cy="60" r="4" fill="var(--accent)"/></svg></div><p>Grind a handful of uncooked white rice to a powder. The rice absorbs oils and pushes out old grounds. Dump it, then grind a second small batch and toss that too. <span class="muted">Your next cup won't taste like last week's beans.</span></p><details class="acc"><summary>For burr grinders</summary><div class="body"><p>Rice can jam some burr grinders. Instead, use a stiff brush and a vacuum with a crevice tool. Never wash with water.</p></div></details><p class="t-small dim">Do this monthly, or whenever the grinder smells stale.</p></div>	-
21.22.1	illustration/Getting Rid of Fruit Flies in the Kitchen	<div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">The Apple Cider Vinegar Trap</h2><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 100"><rect x="80" y="40" width="40" height="50" rx="5" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M85 40 L115 40 L110 30 L90 30 Z" fill="var(--accent)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"/><circle cx="100" cy="70" r="5" fill="var(--accent)"/><circle cx="90" cy="65" r="3" fill="var(--accent)"/><circle cx="110" cy="65" r="3" fill="var(--accent)"/></svg></div><p>Pour a little apple cider vinegar into a jar, add a drop of dish soap, and cover with plastic wrap. Poke a few holes with a toothpick. The flies crawl in and can't get out. <span class="muted">Set it near the fruit bowl.</span></p><details class="acc"><summary>Why it works</summary><div class="body"><p>The vinegar smells like rotting fruit, and the soap breaks the surface tension so they sink instead of floating.</p></div></details><p class="t-small dim">Empty and refresh every couple of days until they're gone.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">The Apple Cider Vinegar Trap</h2><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 100"><rect x="80" y="40" width="40" height="50" rx="5" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M85 40 L115 40 L110 30 L90 30 Z" fill="var(--accent)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"/><circle cx="100" cy="70" r="5" fill="var(--accent)"/><circle cx="90" cy="65" r="3" fill="var(--accent)"/><circle cx="110" cy="65" r="3" fill="var(--accent)"/></svg></div><p>Pour a little apple cider vinegar into a jar, add a drop of dish soap, and cover with plastic wrap. Poke a few holes with a toothpick. The flies crawl in and can't get out. <span class="muted">Set it near the fruit bowl.</span></p><details class="acc"><summary>Why it works</summary><div class="body"><p>The vinegar smells like rotting fruit, and the soap breaks the surface tension so they sink instead of floating.</p></div></details><p class="t-small dim">Empty and refresh every couple of days until they're gone.</p></div>	-
21.23.1	illustration/Silencing a Squeaky Door Hinge	<div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">The Toothpick Trick</h2><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 100"><rect x="40" y="30" width="30" height="40" rx="5" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"/><rect x="130" y="30" width="30" height="40" rx="5" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"/><line x1="70" y1="50" x2="130" y2="50" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="4"/><circle cx="100" cy="50" r="6" fill="var(--accent)"/></svg></div><p>If you don't have WD-40, rub a bar of soap or a candle stub on the hinge pin. Or pull the pin, wipe it with a little olive oil, and tap it back in. <span class="muted">The squeak is just metal grinding on metal.</span></p><details class="acc"><summary>If it still squeaks</summary><div class="body"><p>The hinge might be loose. Tighten the screws with a screwdriver. If the holes are stripped, poke a toothpick in with wood glue and snap it off, then re-screw.</p></div></details><p class="t-small dim">A drop of oil on each hinge twice a year keeps things quiet.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">The Toothpick Trick</h2><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 100"><rect x="40" y="30" width="30" height="40" rx="5" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"/><rect x="130" y="30" width="30" height="40" rx="5" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"/><line x1="70" y1="50" x2="130" y2="50" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="4"/><circle cx="100" cy="50" r="6" fill="var(--accent)"/></svg></div><p>If you don't have WD-40, rub a bar of soap or a candle stub on the hinge pin. Or pull the pin, wipe it with a little olive oil, and tap it back in. <span class="muted">The squeak is just metal grinding on metal.</span></p><details class="acc"><summary>If it still squeaks</summary><div class="body"><p>The hinge might be loose. Tighten the screws with a screwdriver. If the holes are stripped, poke a toothpick in with wood glue and snap it off, then re-screw.</p></div></details><p class="t-small dim">A drop of oil on each hinge twice a year keeps things quiet.</p></div>	-
21.24.1	illustration/Removing Hard Water Stains From Shower Glass	<div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">The Vinegar Soak</h2><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 100"><rect x="60" y="20" width="80" height="60" rx="8" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M70 30 L130 30" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M70 40 L130 40" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M70 50 L130 50" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M70 60 L130 60" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M80 70 L120 70" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"/></svg></div><p>Soak a cloth or paper towel in white vinegar and press it against the glass for 15–20 minutes. The acid breaks down the minerals. Wipe with a damp microfiber cloth and dry with a squeegee. <span class="muted">No scrubbing required.</span></p><details class="acc"><summary>For tough spots</summary><div class="body"><p>Make a paste of baking soda and vinegar. Apply to the spots, let it fizz, then wipe. Don't use abrasive cleaners on glass.</p></div></details><p class="t-small dim">Squeegee after every shower to prevent buildup.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">The Vinegar Soak</h2><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 100"><rect x="60" y="20" width="80" height="60" rx="8" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M70 30 L130 30" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M70 40 L130 40" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M70 50 L130 50" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M70 60 L130 60" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M80 70 L120 70" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"/></svg></div><p>Soak a cloth or paper towel in white vinegar and press it against the glass for 15–20 minutes. The acid breaks down the minerals. Wipe with a damp microfiber cloth and dry with a squeegee. <span class="muted">No scrubbing required.</span></p><details class="acc"><summary>For tough spots</summary><div class="body"><p>Make a paste of baking soda and vinegar. Apply to the spots, let it fizz, then wipe. Don't use abrasive cleaners on glass.</p></div></details><p class="t-small dim">Squeegee after every shower to prevent buildup.</p></div>	-
21.25.1	illustration/Keeping Brown Sugar Soft	<div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">The Marshmallow Trick</h2><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 100"><rect x="70" y="30" width="60" height="50" rx="10" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"/><circle cx="90" cy="55" r="8" fill="var(--accent)"/><circle cx="110" cy="55" r="8" fill="var(--accent)"/><rect x="85" y="20" width="30" height="10" rx="3" fill="var(--accent)"/></svg></div><p>Toss a marshmallow or a slice of apple into the container with your brown sugar. The sugar pulls moisture from it and stays soft for months. <span class="muted">Change the apple every few weeks to avoid mold.</span></p><details class="acc"><summary>If it's already hard</summary><div class="body"><p>Put the sugar in a bowl with a damp paper towel, cover, and microwave in 20-second bursts until it softens. Or leave it with an apple slice overnight.</p></div></details><p class="t-small dim">Store in an airtight container, not the original bag.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">The Marshmallow Trick</h2><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 100"><rect x="70" y="30" width="60" height="50" rx="10" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"/><circle cx="90" cy="55" r="8" fill="var(--accent)"/><circle cx="110" cy="55" r="8" fill="var(--accent)"/><rect x="85" y="20" width="30" height="10" rx="3" fill="var(--accent)"/></svg></div><p>Toss a marshmallow or a slice of apple into the container with your brown sugar. The sugar pulls moisture from it and stays soft for months. <span class="muted">Change the apple every few weeks to avoid mold.</span></p><details class="acc"><summary>If it's already hard</summary><div class="body"><p>Put the sugar in a bowl with a damp paper towel, cover, and microwave in 20-second bursts until it softens. Or leave it with an apple slice overnight.</p></div></details><p class="t-small dim">Store in an airtight container, not the original bag.</p></div>	-
21.26.1	illustration/Stopping a Toilet That Runs After Flushing	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Stopping a Toilet That Runs After Flushing</h2><p class="muted">A running toilet is almost always the flapper or the fill valve. Both are cheap, and the fix is beginner-friendly. Start by shutting off the water at the wall valve and flushing to empty the tank.</p><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 120"><rect x="10" y="10" width="180" height="60" rx="8" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line)"></rect><rect x="20" y="20" width="160" height="40" rx="4" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"></rect><circle cx="100" cy="40" r="6" fill="var(--accent)"></circle><text x="100" y="90" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">flapper valve</text><text x="100" y="110" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl dim">lift chain, replace if warped</text></svg></div><ol class="list"><li>Flush and watch the flapper. If water keeps trickling into the bowl after it drops, the flapper isn't sealing.</li><li>Turn off the water, empty the tank, and unhook the flapper chain. Feel the flapper's rim for stiffness or grit.</li><li>Take the old flapper to the store or match the brand. A new one snaps on in seconds.</li><li>If the flapper looks fine, the fill valve may be the culprit. Check the float height: water should stop about an inch below the overflow tube. Adjust the float or replace the valve.</li></ol><div class="callout info">A dye test helps: drop food coloring in the tank, wait 10 minutes. Color in the bowl means the flapper leaks.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Stopping a Toilet That Runs After Flushing</h2><p class="muted">A running toilet is almost always the flapper or the fill valve. Both are cheap, and the fix is beginner-friendly. Start by shutting off the water at the wall valve and flushing to empty the tank.</p><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 120"><rect x="10" y="10" width="180" height="60" rx="8" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line)"></rect><rect x="20" y="20" width="160" height="40" rx="4" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"></rect><circle cx="100" cy="40" r="6" fill="var(--accent)"></circle><text x="100" y="90" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">flapper valve</text><text x="100" y="110" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl dim">lift chain, replace if warped</text></svg></div><ol class="list"><li>Flush and watch the flapper. If water keeps trickling into the bowl after it drops, the flapper isn't sealing.</li><li>Turn off the water, empty the tank, and unhook the flapper chain. Feel the flapper's rim for stiffness or grit.</li><li>Take the old flapper to the store or match the brand. A new one snaps on in seconds.</li><li>If the flapper looks fine, the fill valve may be the culprit. Check the float height: water should stop about an inch below the overflow tube. Adjust the float or replace the valve.</li></ol><div class="callout info">A dye test helps: drop food coloring in the tank, wait 10 minutes. Color in the bowl means the flapper leaks.</div></div>	-
21.27.1	illustration/Removing Red Wine Stains From Carpet	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Removing Red Wine Stains From Carpet</h2><p class="muted">Blot, don't rub. Rubbing pushes wine deeper and fuzzes the fibers. Work from the outside in so you don't spread the stain.</p><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 100"><ellipse cx="100" cy="50" rx="60" ry="30" fill="var(--bad-soft)" stroke="var(--bad)"></ellipse><ellipse cx="100" cy="50" rx="30" ry="15" fill="var(--bad)" opacity="0.3"></ellipse><text x="100" y="90" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">stain spreading</text></svg></div><ol class="list"><li>Blot up as much wine as you can with a clean white towel or paper towels. Stand on it if needed.</li><li>Mix one tablespoon dish soap, one tablespoon white vinegar, and two cups warm water.</li><li>Sponge the solution onto the stain, let it sit 5 minutes, then blot. Repeat until no more color transfers.</li><li>Rinse with plain water and blot dry. If a faint stain remains, try a 3% hydrogen peroxide solution on a hidden spot first, then dab it on.</li></ol><div class="callout warn">Never use hot water or heat to dry—it sets the stain. Air dry only.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Removing Red Wine Stains From Carpet</h2><p class="muted">Blot, don't rub. Rubbing pushes wine deeper and fuzzes the fibers. Work from the outside in so you don't spread the stain.</p><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 100"><ellipse cx="100" cy="50" rx="60" ry="30" fill="var(--bad-soft)" stroke="var(--bad)"></ellipse><ellipse cx="100" cy="50" rx="30" ry="15" fill="var(--bad)" opacity="0.3"></ellipse><text x="100" y="90" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">stain spreading</text></svg></div><ol class="list"><li>Blot up as much wine as you can with a clean white towel or paper towels. Stand on it if needed.</li><li>Mix one tablespoon dish soap, one tablespoon white vinegar, and two cups warm water.</li><li>Sponge the solution onto the stain, let it sit 5 minutes, then blot. Repeat until no more color transfers.</li><li>Rinse with plain water and blot dry. If a faint stain remains, try a 3% hydrogen peroxide solution on a hidden spot first, then dab it on.</li></ol><div class="callout warn">Never use hot water or heat to dry—it sets the stain. Air dry only.</div></div>	-
21.28.1	illustration/Getting Mildew Smell Out of Towels	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Getting Mildew Smell Out of Towels</h2><p class="muted">That sour smell means bacteria or mildew are living in the fibers. Regular detergent won't kill them—you need to strip the towels.</p><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 100"><rect x="20" y="20" width="160" height="60" rx="8" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line)"></rect><text x="100" y="55" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">vinegar + baking soda</text><text x="100" y="75" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl dim">kills odor at the source</text></svg></div><ol class="list"><li>Wash towels in hot water with one cup of white vinegar. No detergent yet.</li><li>Run a second hot wash with half a cup of baking soda.</li><li>Dry on high heat until completely dry. Damp towels grow mildew fast.</li><li>Prevent it: hang towels to dry after each use, and never leave wet towels in a pile.</li></ol><div class="callout good">Once stripped, towels often feel fluffier too—detergent residue builds up over time.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Getting Mildew Smell Out of Towels</h2><p class="muted">That sour smell means bacteria or mildew are living in the fibers. Regular detergent won't kill them—you need to strip the towels.</p><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 100"><rect x="20" y="20" width="160" height="60" rx="8" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line)"></rect><text x="100" y="55" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">vinegar + baking soda</text><text x="100" y="75" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl dim">kills odor at the source</text></svg></div><ol class="list"><li>Wash towels in hot water with one cup of white vinegar. No detergent yet.</li><li>Run a second hot wash with half a cup of baking soda.</li><li>Dry on high heat until completely dry. Damp towels grow mildew fast.</li><li>Prevent it: hang towels to dry after each use, and never leave wet towels in a pile.</li></ol><div class="callout good">Once stripped, towels often feel fluffier too—detergent residue builds up over time.</div></div>	-
21.29.1	illustration/Unclogging a Shower Drain	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Unclogging a Shower Drain</h2><p class="muted">Hair and soap scum are the usual suspects. You can clear most clogs without calling a plumber or pouring harsh chemicals.</p><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 100"><circle cx="100" cy="50" r="30" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line)"></circle><path d="M80 50 Q100 30 120 50" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="4"></path><text x="100" y="90" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">drain snake path</text></svg></div><ol class="list"><li>Remove the drain cover. Shine a flashlight down—often you can see the hair wad.</li><li>Bend a wire coat hanger into a hook. Fish it down and pull out the gunk. Wear gloves.</li><li>If that fails, use a plunger. Block the overflow hole with a wet rag, then plunge firmly 10–15 times.</li><li>For stubborn clogs, try a drain snake (cheap at hardware stores). Feed it in until you hit resistance, then crank and pull.</li></ol><div class="callout info">Avoid chemical drain cleaners—they can damage pipes and aren't great for the environment. Mechanical removal is safer.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Unclogging a Shower Drain</h2><p class="muted">Hair and soap scum are the usual suspects. You can clear most clogs without calling a plumber or pouring harsh chemicals.</p><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 100"><circle cx="100" cy="50" r="30" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line)"></circle><path d="M80 50 Q100 30 120 50" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="4"></path><text x="100" y="90" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">drain snake path</text></svg></div><ol class="list"><li>Remove the drain cover. Shine a flashlight down—often you can see the hair wad.</li><li>Bend a wire coat hanger into a hook. Fish it down and pull out the gunk. Wear gloves.</li><li>If that fails, use a plunger. Block the overflow hole with a wet rag, then plunge firmly 10–15 times.</li><li>For stubborn clogs, try a drain snake (cheap at hardware stores). Feed it in until you hit resistance, then crank and pull.</li></ol><div class="callout info">Avoid chemical drain cleaners—they can damage pipes and aren't great for the environment. Mechanical removal is safer.</div></div>	-
21.30.1	illustration/Fixing a Zipper That Won't Stay Up	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Fixing a Zipper That Won't Stay Up</h2><p class="muted">If your zipper slides down on its own, the slider has loosened. You can often fix it in two minutes with pliers—no sewing required.</p><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 100"><rect x="60" y="20" width="20" height="60" rx="3" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"></rect><rect x="120" y="20" width="20" height="60" rx="3" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"></rect><text x="100" y="90" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">slider loosens over time</text></svg></div><ol class="list"><li>Zip the zipper all the way up.</li><li>Use needle-nose pliers to gently squeeze the top and bottom of the slider (the metal part that moves). Do this on both sides.</li><li>Test the zipper. If it still slides, squeeze a little more—but don't overdo it or the zipper will jam.</li><li>If the slider is plastic, try a tiny dab of clear nail polish on the teeth near the top to add friction.</li></ol><div class="callout warn">If the zipper teeth are broken or missing, no amount of squeezing will help. The zipper needs replacing.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Fixing a Zipper That Won't Stay Up</h2><p class="muted">If your zipper slides down on its own, the slider has loosened. You can often fix it in two minutes with pliers—no sewing required.</p><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 100"><rect x="60" y="20" width="20" height="60" rx="3" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"></rect><rect x="120" y="20" width="20" height="60" rx="3" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"></rect><text x="100" y="90" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">slider loosens over time</text></svg></div><ol class="list"><li>Zip the zipper all the way up.</li><li>Use needle-nose pliers to gently squeeze the top and bottom of the slider (the metal part that moves). Do this on both sides.</li><li>Test the zipper. If it still slides, squeeze a little more—but don't overdo it or the zipper will jam.</li><li>If the slider is plastic, try a tiny dab of clear nail polish on the teeth near the top to add friction.</li></ol><div class="callout warn">If the zipper teeth are broken or missing, no amount of squeezing will help. The zipper needs replacing.</div></div>	-
21.31.1	illustration/Cleaning a Dishwasher Filter	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Cleaning a Dishwasher Filter</h2><p class="muted">A clogged filter makes dishes gritty and the dishwasher smelly. Most manufacturers recommend cleaning it monthly. It's a five-minute job.</p><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 100"><circle cx="100" cy="50" r="30" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line)"></circle><circle cx="100" cy="50" r="15" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"></circle><text x="100" y="90" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">filter basket</text></svg></div><ol class="list"><li>Remove the bottom rack. The filter is usually a cylindrical mesh screen at the base.</li><li>Twist the filter counterclockwise and lift it out. Some models have two parts: a coarse and a fine filter.</li><li>Rinse under warm running water. Use a soft brush (an old toothbrush works) to scrub away grease and food bits.</li><li>For stubborn buildup, soak in warm soapy water for 10 minutes, then rinse.</li><li>Reinstall and run a hot cycle with a cup of white vinegar in the top rack to freshen the whole machine.</li></ol><div class="callout good">Clean filter = cleaner dishes and no more funky smell. Add it to your monthly routine.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Cleaning a Dishwasher Filter</h2><p class="muted">A clogged filter makes dishes gritty and the dishwasher smelly. Most manufacturers recommend cleaning it monthly. It's a five-minute job.</p><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 100"><circle cx="100" cy="50" r="30" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line)"></circle><circle cx="100" cy="50" r="15" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"></circle><text x="100" y="90" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">filter basket</text></svg></div><ol class="list"><li>Remove the bottom rack. The filter is usually a cylindrical mesh screen at the base.</li><li>Twist the filter counterclockwise and lift it out. Some models have two parts: a coarse and a fine filter.</li><li>Rinse under warm running water. Use a soft brush (an old toothbrush works) to scrub away grease and food bits.</li><li>For stubborn buildup, soak in warm soapy water for 10 minutes, then rinse.</li><li>Reinstall and run a hot cycle with a cup of white vinegar in the top rack to freshen the whole machine.</li></ol><div class="callout good">Clean filter = cleaner dishes and no more funky smell. Add it to your monthly routine.</div></div>	-
21.32.1	illustration/Keeping Charging Cables From Tangling in Your Bag	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Tame the cable octopus</div><p class="muted">A few loops and a twist tie beat any fancy gadget.</p><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 240 80"><path d="M20 40 C 40 10, 60 70, 80 40 S 120 10, 140 40 S 180 70, 200 40" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="3"/><circle cx="220" cy="40" r="6" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"/></svg></div><ol class="list"><li>Loop the cable loosely around your hand—about four fingers wide.</li><li>Slide the loop off and wrap a twist tie or Velcro strap around the middle.</li><li>Don’t coil it tight around the plug; that’s where breaks start.</li></ol><p class="t-small dim">Bonus: label each one with a bit of masking tape.</p></div>	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Tame the cable octopus</div><p class="muted">A few loops and a twist tie beat any fancy gadget.</p><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 240 80"><path d="M20 40 C 40 10, 60 70, 80 40 S 120 10, 140 40 S 180 70, 200 40" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="3"/><circle cx="220" cy="40" r="6" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"/></svg></div><ol class="list"><li>Loop the cable loosely around your hand—about four fingers wide.</li><li>Slide the loop off and wrap a twist tie or Velcro strap around the middle.</li><li>Don’t coil it tight around the plug; that’s where breaks start.</li></ol><p class="t-small dim">Bonus: label each one with a bit of masking tape.</p></div>	-
21.33.1	illustration/Roasting Chickpeas Until They Actually Crunch	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Crunch time</div><p class="muted">The secret is dry, dry, dry—and a hot oven.</p><div class="row between"><span class="badge info"><i class="dot"></i> 400°F / 200°C</span><span class="badge good">25–35 min</span></div><ol class="list"><li>Drain and rinse a can of chickpeas. Pat them very dry with a towel.</li><li>Pick off any loose skins—they burn before the insides crisp.</li><li>Toss with a tablespoon of oil and salt. Spread on a sheet pan in one layer.</li><li>Roast, shaking the pan every 10 minutes, until they rattle and taste crunchy.</li></ol><p class="t-small dim">They soften as they cool, so eat them soon.</p></div>	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Crunch time</div><p class="muted">The secret is dry, dry, dry—and a hot oven.</p><div class="row between"><span class="badge info"><i class="dot"></i> 400°F / 200°C</span><span class="badge good">25–35 min</span></div><ol class="list"><li>Drain and rinse a can of chickpeas. Pat them very dry with a towel.</li><li>Pick off any loose skins—they burn before the insides crisp.</li><li>Toss with a tablespoon of oil and salt. Spread on a sheet pan in one layer.</li><li>Roast, shaking the pan every 10 minutes, until they rattle and taste crunchy.</li></ol><p class="t-small dim">They soften as they cool, so eat them soon.</p></div>	-
21.34.1	illustration/Sanitizing a Kitchen Sponge Without Bleach	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Sponge spa day</div><p class="muted">A wet sponge is a petri dish. Dry it out and nuke it.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Microwave</span><span class="value">1 min<small> on high</small></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Dishwasher</span><span class="value">Full cycle<small> with dry</small></span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Rinse the sponge and squeeze out excess water.</li><li>Microwave it wet for one minute. It will steam—that’s the point.</li><li>Let it cool before touching. Or run it through a dishwasher cycle with a heated dry.</li></ol><p class="callout warn">Never microwave a dry sponge. It can catch fire.</p></div>	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Sponge spa day</div><p class="muted">A wet sponge is a petri dish. Dry it out and nuke it.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Microwave</span><span class="value">1 min<small> on high</small></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Dishwasher</span><span class="value">Full cycle<small> with dry</small></span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Rinse the sponge and squeeze out excess water.</li><li>Microwave it wet for one minute. It will steam—that’s the point.</li><li>Let it cool before touching. Or run it through a dishwasher cycle with a heated dry.</li></ol><p class="callout warn">Never microwave a dry sponge. It can catch fire.</p></div>	-
21.35.1	illustration/Tightening a Wobbly Chair Leg	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">No more rocking</div><p class="muted">Most wobbles are just a loose screw or a tired hole.</p><details class="acc"><summary>If the screw is loose</summary><div class="body"><p>Turn the chair over. Tighten the screw or bolt with a screwdriver. Don’t overtighten—wood can crack.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>If the screw spins in place</summary><div class="body"><p>Dip a toothpick in wood glue, push it into the hole, snap it off flush. Then reinsert the screw. The wood fibers give it something to bite.</p></div></details><p class="t-small dim">For metal chairs, a drop of thread-locking fluid works.</p></div>	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">No more rocking</div><p class="muted">Most wobbles are just a loose screw or a tired hole.</p><details class="acc"><summary>If the screw is loose</summary><div class="body"><p>Turn the chair over. Tighten the screw or bolt with a screwdriver. Don’t overtighten—wood can crack.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>If the screw spins in place</summary><div class="body"><p>Dip a toothpick in wood glue, push it into the hole, snap it off flush. Then reinsert the screw. The wood fibers give it something to bite.</p></div></details><p class="t-small dim">For metal chairs, a drop of thread-locking fluid works.</p></div>	-
21.36.1	illustration/Keeping White Laundry From Going Gray	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">The white stuff</div><p class="muted">Gray happens when soil redeposits. Separate and boost.</p><div class="row"><span class="badge accent">Wash whites separately</span><span class="badge">Warm water</span></div><ul class="list"><li>Wash whites only with other whites—no light grays or creams.</li><li>Use a detergent with enzymes and a scoop of oxygen bleach (not chlorine).</li><li>Add a half-cup of white vinegar to the rinse to strip residue.</li><li>Dry in the sun if you can. UV is a gentle brightener.</li></ul><p class="t-small dim">Skip fabric softener—it leaves a waxy film that traps dirt.</p></div>	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">The white stuff</div><p class="muted">Gray happens when soil redeposits. Separate and boost.</p><div class="row"><span class="badge accent">Wash whites separately</span><span class="badge">Warm water</span></div><ul class="list"><li>Wash whites only with other whites—no light grays or creams.</li><li>Use a detergent with enzymes and a scoop of oxygen bleach (not chlorine).</li><li>Add a half-cup of white vinegar to the rinse to strip residue.</li><li>Dry in the sun if you can. UV is a gentle brightener.</li></ul><p class="t-small dim">Skip fabric softener—it leaves a waxy film that traps dirt.</p></div>	-
21.37.1	illustration/Fixing a Zipper That Splits Open	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Zip it back</div><p class="muted">Usually the slider has spread. A squeeze can save it.</p><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 60"><path d="M20 30 L 80 30" stroke="var(--line-2)" stroke-width="4"/><path d="M120 30 L 180 30" stroke="var(--line-2)" stroke-width="4"/><rect x="80" y="15" width="40" height="30" rx="6" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"/><text x="100" y="35" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">squeeze</text></svg></div><ol class="list"><li>Move the slider to the bottom of the zipper.</li><li>With pliers, gently squeeze the top and bottom of the slider—just a hair.</li><li>Test the zipper. Repeat if it still splits.</li></ol><p class="callout warn">Go slow. One too-hard squeeze and the slider won’t move at all.</p></div>	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Zip it back</div><p class="muted">Usually the slider has spread. A squeeze can save it.</p><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 60"><path d="M20 30 L 80 30" stroke="var(--line-2)" stroke-width="4"/><path d="M120 30 L 180 30" stroke="var(--line-2)" stroke-width="4"/><rect x="80" y="15" width="40" height="30" rx="6" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)"/><text x="100" y="35" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">squeeze</text></svg></div><ol class="list"><li>Move the slider to the bottom of the zipper.</li><li>With pliers, gently squeeze the top and bottom of the slider—just a hair.</li><li>Test the zipper. Repeat if it still splits.</li></ol><p class="callout warn">Go slow. One too-hard squeeze and the slider won’t move at all.</p></div>	-
22.0.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Photosynthesis basics	<div class="flip"><div class="front">What are the two main stages of photosynthesis?</div><div class="back"><strong>Light-dependent reactions</strong> (thylakoid membranes) split water, release O₂, and make ATP + NADPH.<br><strong>Calvin cycle</strong> (stroma) uses that ATP/NADPH to fix CO₂ into glucose.<br><span class="muted t-small">Overall: 6CO₂ + 6H₂O + light → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂</span></div></div>	<div class="flip"><div class="front">What are the two main stages of photosynthesis?</div><div class="back"><strong>Light-dependent reactions</strong> (thylakoid membranes) split water, release O₂, and make ATP + NADPH.<br><strong>Calvin cycle</strong> (stroma) uses that ATP/NADPH to fix CO₂ into glucose.<br><span class="muted t-small">Overall: 6CO₂ + 6H₂O + light → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂</span></div></div>	-
22.1.0	flashcard/Flashcard: ISO date format	<div class="flip"><div class="front">How does ISO 8601 write a date and timestamp?</div><div class="back"><strong>Date:</strong> YYYY-MM-DD → 2024-03-07<br><strong>Time:</strong> Thh:mm:ss±hh:mm → 2024-03-07T14:30:00+01:00<br><span class="muted t-small">Biggest unit first, always zero-padded. Sorts alphabetically = chronologically.</span></div></div>	<div class="flip"><div class="front">How does ISO 8601 write a date and timestamp?</div><div class="back"><strong>Date:</strong> YYYY-MM-DD → 2024-03-07<br><strong>Time:</strong> Thh:mm:ss±hh:mm → 2024-03-07T14:30:00+01:00<br><span class="muted t-small">Biggest unit first, always zero-padded. Sorts alphabetically = chronologically.</span></div></div>	-
22.2.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Knife grip for chopping	<div class="flip"><div class="front">What is the "pinch grip" and why use it?</div><div class="back">Pinch the blade just above the handle between thumb and index finger; wrap the remaining fingers around the handle.<br><span class="muted t-small">Gives control and leverage, so the tip stays down and the blade rocks instead of slipping. Curl your guiding hand into a claw.</span></div></div>	<div class="flip"><div class="front">What is the "pinch grip" and why use it?</div><div class="back">Pinch the blade just above the handle between thumb and index finger; wrap the remaining fingers around the handle.<br><span class="muted t-small">Gives control and leverage, so the tip stays down and the blade rocks instead of slipping. Curl your guiding hand into a claw.</span></div></div>	-
22.3.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Compound interest rule of 72	<div class="flip"><div class="front">Rule of 72 — what does it estimate?</div><div class="back">Years to double your money ≈ 72 ÷ annual return %.<br><span class="muted t-small">At 6%: 72 ÷ 6 = ~12 years. At 9%: ~8 years. Rough, but handy in your head.</span></div></div>	<div class="flip"><div class="front">Rule of 72 — what does it estimate?</div><div class="back">Years to double your money ≈ 72 ÷ annual return %.<br><span class="muted t-small">At 6%: 72 ÷ 6 = ~12 years. At 9%: ~8 years. Rough, but handy in your head.</span></div></div>	-
22.4.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Difference between affect and effect	<div class="flip"><div class="front">Affect vs effect — which is which?</div><div class="back"><strong>Affect</strong> = verb, to influence: <em>Rain affects the game.</em><br><strong>Effect</strong> = noun, the result: <em>The effect was a soggy field.</em><br><span class="muted t-small">Memory hook: <em>a</em>ffect = <em>a</em>ction. <em>e</em>ffect = <em>e</em>nd result.</span></div></div>	<div class="flip"><div class="front">Affect vs effect — which is which?</div><div class="back"><strong>Affect</strong> = verb, to influence: <em>Rain affects the game.</em><br><strong>Effect</strong> = noun, the result: <em>The effect was a soggy field.</em><br><span class="muted t-small">Memory hook: <em>a</em>ffect = <em>a</em>ction. <em>e</em>ffect = <em>e</em>nd result.</span></div></div>	-
22.5.0	flashcard/Flashcard: CPR compression rate	<div class="flip"><div class="front">How fast and how deep are CPR chest compressions?</div><div class="back"><strong>Rate:</strong> 100–120 per minute (think "Stayin' Alive").<br><strong>Depth:</strong> about 5–6 cm (2–2.4 in) in adults.<br><span class="muted t-small">Push hard, push fast, let the chest come all the way back up. This is a study aid — take a certified course.</span></div></div>	<div class="flip"><div class="front">How fast and how deep are CPR chest compressions?</div><div class="back"><strong>Rate:</strong> 100–120 per minute (think "Stayin' Alive").<br><strong>Depth:</strong> about 5–6 cm (2–2.4 in) in adults.<br><span class="muted t-small">Push hard, push fast, let the chest come all the way back up. This is a study aid — take a certified course.</span></div></div>	-
22.6.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Tying a bowline knot	<div class="flip"><div class="front">What is a bowline knot and when do you use it?</div><div class="back"><p>A bowline creates a fixed loop at the end of a rope that won't slip or tighten under load. Use it for rescue, mooring, or anytime you need a reliable loop.</p><p><strong>Steps:</strong> Make a small loop, pass the working end up through it, around the standing part, and back down. Tighten.</p><p class="muted">Remember: the rabbit comes out of the hole, around the tree, and back down the hole.</p></div></div>	<div class="flip"><div class="front">What is a bowline knot and when do you use it?</div><div class="back"><p>A bowline creates a fixed loop at the end of a rope that won't slip or tighten under load. Use it for rescue, mooring, or anytime you need a reliable loop.</p><p><strong>Steps:</strong> Make a small loop, pass the working end up through it, around the standing part, and back down. Tighten.</p><p class="muted">Remember: the rabbit comes out of the hole, around the tree, and back down the hole.</p></div></div>	-
22.7.0	flashcard/Flashcard: How a bill becomes a law	<div class="flip"><div class="front">What are the main steps for a bill to become a law in the U.S.?</div><div class="back"><p><strong>1.</strong> Introduction by a legislator.<br><strong>2.</strong> Committee review and hearings.<br><strong>3.</strong> Floor debate and vote in both chambers.<br><strong>4.</strong> Conference committee if versions differ.<br><strong>5.</strong> Presidential approval or veto override.</p><p class="muted">Most bills die in committee. Persistence matters.</p></div></div>	<div class="flip"><div class="front">What are the main steps for a bill to become a law in the U.S.?</div><div class="back"><p><strong>1.</strong> Introduction by a legislator.<br><strong>2.</strong> Committee review and hearings.<br><strong>3.</strong> Floor debate and vote in both chambers.<br><strong>4.</strong> Conference committee if versions differ.<br><strong>5.</strong> Presidential approval or veto override.</p><p class="muted">Most bills die in committee. Persistence matters.</p></div></div>	-
22.8.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Folding a paper airplane	<div class="flip"><div class="front">How do you fold a classic dart paper airplane?</div><div class="back"><p><strong>1.</strong> Fold paper in half lengthwise, unfold.<br><strong>2.</strong> Fold top corners to center crease.<br><strong>3.</strong> Fold the new top edges to center again.<br><strong>4.</strong> Fold in half along the center crease.<br><strong>5.</strong> Fold wings down to meet the bottom edge.</p><p class="muted">Throw with a gentle push, not a hard fling.</p></div></div>	<div class="flip"><div class="front">How do you fold a classic dart paper airplane?</div><div class="back"><p><strong>1.</strong> Fold paper in half lengthwise, unfold.<br><strong>2.</strong> Fold top corners to center crease.<br><strong>3.</strong> Fold the new top edges to center again.<br><strong>4.</strong> Fold in half along the center crease.<br><strong>5.</strong> Fold wings down to meet the bottom edge.</p><p class="muted">Throw with a gentle push, not a hard fling.</p></div></div>	-
22.9.0	flashcard/Flashcard: The 50/30/20 budget rule	<div class="flip"><div class="front">What is the 50/30/20 budgeting rule?</div><div class="back"><p>Split your after-tax income:</p><ul><li><strong>50%</strong> for needs (rent, groceries, utilities)</li><li><strong>30%</strong> for wants (dining out, hobbies)</li><li><strong>20%</strong> for savings and debt repayment</li></ul><p class="muted">Adjust percentages to fit your reality. The point is intentional spending.</p></div></div>	<div class="flip"><div class="front">What is the 50/30/20 budgeting rule?</div><div class="back"><p>Split your after-tax income:</p><ul><li><strong>50%</strong> for needs (rent, groceries, utilities)</li><li><strong>30%</strong> for wants (dining out, hobbies)</li><li><strong>20%</strong> for savings and debt repayment</li></ul><p class="muted">Adjust percentages to fit your reality. The point is intentional spending.</p></div></div>	-
22.10.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Folding a fitted sheet	<div class="flip"><div class="front">What's the trick to folding a fitted sheet?</div><div class="back"><p><strong>1.</strong> Hold two corners, tuck one into the other.<br><strong>2.</strong> Do the same with the other two corners.<br><strong>3.</strong> Lay flat, fold in half, then in quarters.<br><strong>4.</strong> Smooth and stack.</p><p class="muted">It won't be perfect, but it'll be tidy enough.</p></div></div>	<div class="flip"><div class="front">What's the trick to folding a fitted sheet?</div><div class="back"><p><strong>1.</strong> Hold two corners, tuck one into the other.<br><strong>2.</strong> Do the same with the other two corners.<br><strong>3.</strong> Lay flat, fold in half, then in quarters.<br><strong>4.</strong> Smooth and stack.</p><p class="muted">It won't be perfect, but it'll be tidy enough.</p></div></div>	-
22.11.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Parallel parking	<div class="flip"><div class="front">What are the steps for parallel parking?</div><div class="back"><p><strong>1.</strong> Pull alongside the front car, mirror-to-mirror.<br><strong>2.</strong> Reverse until your rear bumper aligns with theirs.<br><strong>3.</strong> Turn wheel sharply toward the curb, reverse to 45 degrees.<br><strong>4.</strong> Straighten wheel, reverse until close.<br><strong>5.</strong> Turn wheel away from curb to swing front in.</p><p class="muted">Practice in an empty lot first.</p></div></div>	<div class="flip"><div class="front">What are the steps for parallel parking?</div><div class="back"><p><strong>1.</strong> Pull alongside the front car, mirror-to-mirror.<br><strong>2.</strong> Reverse until your rear bumper aligns with theirs.<br><strong>3.</strong> Turn wheel sharply toward the curb, reverse to 45 degrees.<br><strong>4.</strong> Straighten wheel, reverse until close.<br><strong>5.</strong> Turn wheel away from curb to swing front in.</p><p class="muted">Practice in an empty lot first.</p></div></div>	-
22.12.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Reading a nutrition label	<div class="flip"><details><summary><span class="front">How do you read a nutrition label?</span></summary><div class="back"><p>Start with <strong>serving size</strong> at the top—everything below is for that amount, not the whole package. Then check <strong>calories</strong> and <strong>added sugars</strong>. The <strong>% Daily Value</strong> column tells you how much one serving contributes to a 2,000‑calorie diet: 5% is low, 20% is high. Aim for more fiber, protein, and vitamins; less saturated fat, sodium, and added sugar.</p><p class="t-small muted">Tip: if a bag has 2.5 servings, double the numbers for the whole thing.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="flip"><details><summary><span class="front">How do you read a nutrition label?</span></summary><div class="back"><p>Start with <strong>serving size</strong> at the top—everything below is for that amount, not the whole package. Then check <strong>calories</strong> and <strong>added sugars</strong>. The <strong>% Daily Value</strong> column tells you how much one serving contributes to a 2,000‑calorie diet: 5% is low, 20% is high. Aim for more fiber, protein, and vitamins; less saturated fat, sodium, and added sugar.</p><p class="t-small muted">Tip: if a bag has 2.5 servings, double the numbers for the whole thing.</p></div></details></div>	-
22.13.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Roth vs traditional IRA	<div class="flip"><details><summary><span class="front">Roth vs. traditional IRA—what's the difference?</span></summary><div class="back"><p><strong>Traditional IRA:</strong> you may deduct contributions now, pay income tax when you withdraw in retirement.<br><strong>Roth IRA:</strong> you contribute after‑tax money, it grows tax‑free, and withdrawals in retirement are tax‑free.</p><p>Pick traditional if you expect a lower tax rate later. Pick Roth if you're early in your career or expect higher taxes later.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="flip"><details><summary><span class="front">Roth vs. traditional IRA—what's the difference?</span></summary><div class="back"><p><strong>Traditional IRA:</strong> you may deduct contributions now, pay income tax when you withdraw in retirement.<br><strong>Roth IRA:</strong> you contribute after‑tax money, it grows tax‑free, and withdrawals in retirement are tax‑free.</p><p>Pick traditional if you expect a lower tax rate later. Pick Roth if you're early in your career or expect higher taxes later.</p></div></details></div>	-
22.14.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Tying a necktie (Windsor knot)	<div class="flip"><details><summary><span class="front">How do you tie a necktie (Windsor knot)?</span></summary><div class="back"><ol class="list"><li>Drape the tie around your neck, seam side down, wide end on your right, about a foot lower.</li><li>Cross the wide end over the narrow end, then bring it up through the neck loop.</li><li>Bring the wide end down and around the narrow end, then up through the loop again.</li><li>Cross the wide end in front, then up through the loop one more time.</li><li>Slip the wide end down through the front knot, tighten, and adjust.</li></ol></div></details></div>	<div class="flip"><details><summary><span class="front">How do you tie a necktie (Windsor knot)?</span></summary><div class="back"><ol class="list"><li>Drape the tie around your neck, seam side down, wide end on your right, about a foot lower.</li><li>Cross the wide end over the narrow end, then bring it up through the neck loop.</li><li>Bring the wide end down and around the narrow end, then up through the loop again.</li><li>Cross the wide end in front, then up through the loop one more time.</li><li>Slip the wide end down through the front knot, tighten, and adjust.</li></ol></div></details></div>	-
22.15.0	flashcard/Flashcard: HTTP status codes	<div class="flip"><details><summary><span class="front">What do common HTTP status codes mean?</span></summary><div class="back"><ul class="list"><li><strong>200 OK</strong> — request succeeded.</li><li><strong>301 Moved Permanently</strong> — page has a new permanent URL.</li><li><strong>404 Not Found</strong> — the server can't find that resource.</li><li><strong>500 Internal Server Error</strong> — something broke on the server side.</li></ul><p class="t-small muted">First digit tells the story: 2xx success, 3xx redirect, 4xx client error, 5xx server error.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="flip"><details><summary><span class="front">What do common HTTP status codes mean?</span></summary><div class="back"><ul class="list"><li><strong>200 OK</strong> — request succeeded.</li><li><strong>301 Moved Permanently</strong> — page has a new permanent URL.</li><li><strong>404 Not Found</strong> — the server can't find that resource.</li><li><strong>500 Internal Server Error</strong> — something broke on the server side.</li></ul><p class="t-small muted">First digit tells the story: 2xx success, 3xx redirect, 4xx client error, 5xx server error.</p></div></details></div>	-
22.16.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Jump-starting a car	<div class="flip"><details><summary><span class="front">How do you safely jump‑start a car?</span></summary><div class="back"><ol class="list"><li>Park both cars close but not touching. Turn both off.</li><li>Connect <strong>red</strong> clamp to dead battery's positive (+).</li><li>Connect other <strong>red</strong> clamp to good battery's positive (+).</li><li>Connect <strong>black</strong> clamp to good battery's negative (–).</li><li>Connect last <strong>black</strong> clamp to an unpainted metal bolt on the dead car's engine block—not the battery.</li><li>Start the good car, let it run a minute, then start the dead car.</li></ol><p class="t-small muted">Remove in reverse order. If it won't start, call for help.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="flip"><details><summary><span class="front">How do you safely jump‑start a car?</span></summary><div class="back"><ol class="list"><li>Park both cars close but not touching. Turn both off.</li><li>Connect <strong>red</strong> clamp to dead battery's positive (+).</li><li>Connect other <strong>red</strong> clamp to good battery's positive (+).</li><li>Connect <strong>black</strong> clamp to good battery's negative (–).</li><li>Connect last <strong>black</strong> clamp to an unpainted metal bolt on the dead car's engine block—not the battery.</li><li>Start the good car, let it run a minute, then start the dead car.</li></ol><p class="t-small muted">Remove in reverse order. If it won't start, call for help.</p></div></details></div>	-
22.17.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Taking a screenshot on iPhone	<div class="flip"><details><summary><span class="front">How do you take a screenshot on an iPhone?</span></summary><div class="back"><p>On iPhones with Face ID: press the <strong>Side button</strong> and the <strong>Volume Up button</strong> at the same time, then quickly release. On iPhones with a Home button: press the <strong>Side button</strong> (or Top button) and the <strong>Home button</strong> together.</p><p>The screen will flash, and the screenshot appears in Photos &gt; Albums &gt; Screenshots.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="flip"><details><summary><span class="front">How do you take a screenshot on an iPhone?</span></summary><div class="back"><p>On iPhones with Face ID: press the <strong>Side button</strong> and the <strong>Volume Up button</strong> at the same time, then quickly release. On iPhones with a Home button: press the <strong>Side button</strong> (or Top button) and the <strong>Home button</strong> together.</p><p>The screen will flash, and the screenshot appears in Photos &gt; Albums &gt; Screenshots.</p></div></details></div>	-
22.18.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Fixing a bike flat	<div class="flip"><details class="acc"><summary>How do you fix a flat bike tire?</summary><div class="body"><p>You'll need tire levers, a new tube (or patch kit), and a pump. Here's the drill:</p><ol class="list"><li>Shift to the smallest cog, loosen the brake, and remove the wheel.</li><li>Deflate fully, then use levers to pry one bead off the rim.</li><li>Pull out the tube, find the puncture (inflate and listen/feel).</li><li>Run a finger inside the tire to find the culprit—thorn, glass, etc.</li><li>Install the new tube (or patch), tuck the bead back on by hand.</li><li>Inflate slowly to seat the bead, then pump to recommended pressure.</li><li>Reinstall wheel, reconnect brake, and test ride.</li></ol><p class="muted">Pro tip: line up the tire label with the valve stem so you can find punctures faster next time.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="flip"><details class="acc"><summary>How do you fix a flat bike tire?</summary><div class="body"><p>You'll need tire levers, a new tube (or patch kit), and a pump. Here's the drill:</p><ol class="list"><li>Shift to the smallest cog, loosen the brake, and remove the wheel.</li><li>Deflate fully, then use levers to pry one bead off the rim.</li><li>Pull out the tube, find the puncture (inflate and listen/feel).</li><li>Run a finger inside the tire to find the culprit—thorn, glass, etc.</li><li>Install the new tube (or patch), tuck the bead back on by hand.</li><li>Inflate slowly to seat the bead, then pump to recommended pressure.</li><li>Reinstall wheel, reconnect brake, and test ride.</li></ol><p class="muted">Pro tip: line up the tire label with the valve stem so you can find punctures faster next time.</p></div></details></div>	-
22.19.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Storing coffee beans	<div class="flip"><details class="acc"><summary>How should you store coffee beans?</summary><div class="body"><p>Keep them away from air, light, heat, and moisture. The best spot is an opaque, airtight container in a cool, dark cupboard—not the fridge (condensation) and definitely not the freezer unless you're storing long-term and thawing completely before use.</p><p>Buy whole beans and grind just before brewing. A week's worth in a sealed bag with a one-way valve is fine; for longer, squeeze out extra air. <span class="muted">Avoid the supermarket bulk bins if you can—they're a flavor graveyard.</span></p></div></details></div>	<div class="flip"><details class="acc"><summary>How should you store coffee beans?</summary><div class="body"><p>Keep them away from air, light, heat, and moisture. The best spot is an opaque, airtight container in a cool, dark cupboard—not the fridge (condensation) and definitely not the freezer unless you're storing long-term and thawing completely before use.</p><p>Buy whole beans and grind just before brewing. A week's worth in a sealed bag with a one-way valve is fine; for longer, squeeze out extra air. <span class="muted">Avoid the supermarket bulk bins if you can—they're a flavor graveyard.</span></p></div></details></div>	-
22.20.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Removing red wine stains	<div class="flip"><details class="acc"><summary>How do you get red wine out of fabric?</summary><div class="body"><p>Act fast: blot (don't rub) with a clean cloth to lift as much as possible. Then try one of these:</p><ul class="list"><li><strong>Salt:</strong> Cover the stain with salt to absorb, then vacuum or brush off.</li><li><strong>Club soda:</strong> Pour on, blot, repeat.</li><li><strong>Dish soap + hydrogen peroxide:</strong> Mix a little, dab on, let sit 5 minutes, then rinse.</li><li><strong>Commercial stain remover:</strong> Follow the label.</li></ul><p>For carpets, a mix of dish soap and white vinegar works well. Always test on a hidden spot first.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="flip"><details class="acc"><summary>How do you get red wine out of fabric?</summary><div class="body"><p>Act fast: blot (don't rub) with a clean cloth to lift as much as possible. Then try one of these:</p><ul class="list"><li><strong>Salt:</strong> Cover the stain with salt to absorb, then vacuum or brush off.</li><li><strong>Club soda:</strong> Pour on, blot, repeat.</li><li><strong>Dish soap + hydrogen peroxide:</strong> Mix a little, dab on, let sit 5 minutes, then rinse.</li><li><strong>Commercial stain remover:</strong> Follow the label.</li></ul><p>For carpets, a mix of dish soap and white vinegar works well. Always test on a hidden spot first.</p></div></details></div>	-
22.21.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Tying a necktie (four-in-hand)	<div class="flip"><details class="acc"><summary>How do you tie a four-in-hand knot?</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Drape the tie around your neck, seam side down, with the wide end on your right, hanging about 12 inches lower than the narrow end.</li><li>Cross the wide end over the narrow end, then bring it back underneath.</li><li>Cross the wide end over the front again, forming a loop around the narrow end.</li><li>Bring the wide end up through the neck loop from underneath.</li><li>Feed the wide end down through the front loop you just made.</li><li>Pull down gently and slide the knot up to your collar. Tighten and adjust.</li></ol><p class="muted">The four-in-hand is slightly asymmetrical and works with most collars.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="flip"><details class="acc"><summary>How do you tie a four-in-hand knot?</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Drape the tie around your neck, seam side down, with the wide end on your right, hanging about 12 inches lower than the narrow end.</li><li>Cross the wide end over the narrow end, then bring it back underneath.</li><li>Cross the wide end over the front again, forming a loop around the narrow end.</li><li>Bring the wide end up through the neck loop from underneath.</li><li>Feed the wide end down through the front loop you just made.</li><li>Pull down gently and slide the knot up to your collar. Tighten and adjust.</li></ol><p class="muted">The four-in-hand is slightly asymmetrical and works with most collars.</p></div></details></div>	-
22.22.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Using a fire extinguisher (PASS)	<div class="flip"><details class="acc"><summary>What does PASS stand for?</summary><div class="body"><p><strong>P</strong>ull the pin. <strong>A</strong>im at the base of the fire. <strong>S</strong>queeze the handle. <strong>S</strong>weep side to side.</p><p>Remember: only fight small, contained fires, and always keep your back to an exit. If in doubt, get out and call for help.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="flip"><details class="acc"><summary>What does PASS stand for?</summary><div class="body"><p><strong>P</strong>ull the pin. <strong>A</strong>im at the base of the fire. <strong>S</strong>queeze the handle. <strong>S</strong>weep side to side.</p><p>Remember: only fight small, contained fires, and always keep your back to an exit. If in doubt, get out and call for help.</p></div></details></div>	-
22.23.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Folding a paper crane	<div class="flip"><details class="acc"><summary>How do you fold a paper crane?</summary><div class="body"><p>Start with a square piece of paper. Here's the condensed version:</p><ol class="list"><li>Fold diagonally both ways and horizontally/vertically both ways, then collapse into a square base.</li><li>Fold the top layers' bottom corners to the center crease, unfold, then petal fold up.</li><li>Flip and repeat on the other side.</li><li>Fold the top flaps inward to the center on both sides.</li><li>Inside reverse fold the bottom points up to form the head and tail.</li><li>Fold down the head and wings.</li></ol><p class="muted">It's fiddly the first time—go slow and crease sharply.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="flip"><details class="acc"><summary>How do you fold a paper crane?</summary><div class="body"><p>Start with a square piece of paper. Here's the condensed version:</p><ol class="list"><li>Fold diagonally both ways and horizontally/vertically both ways, then collapse into a square base.</li><li>Fold the top layers' bottom corners to the center crease, unfold, then petal fold up.</li><li>Flip and repeat on the other side.</li><li>Fold the top flaps inward to the center on both sides.</li><li>Inside reverse fold the bottom points up to form the head and tail.</li><li>Fold down the head and wings.</li></ol><p class="muted">It's fiddly the first time—go slow and crease sharply.</p></div></details></div>	-
22.24.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Changing a car tire	<div class="flip">  <details>    <summary>      <span class="front">You're on the shoulder with a flat. What's the safe order for swapping on the spare?</span>    </summary>    <div class="back">      <p class="t-small dim">Pull off the road, hazards on. Then:</p>      <ol class="list">        <li>Park on flat ground, set the parking brake. If you have a manual, leave it in gear; auto, shift to Park.</li>        <li>Loosen the lug nuts <strong>a quarter turn</strong> while the tire is still on the ground. If they won't budge, stand on the wrench&mdash;carefully.</li>        <li>Place the jack under the car's designated lift point (check the manual; usually a notch or arrow on the rocker panel). Raise until the flat tire is just off the ground.</li>        <li>Remove the lug nuts and pull the tire straight off. It may need a firm tug.</li>        <li>Lift the spare on, line up the holes, and hand-tighten the lug nuts in a star pattern. Snug them, don't crank yet.</li>        <li>Lower the car until the tire touches the ground, then tighten the nuts firmly in a star pattern.</li>      </ol>      <p class="callout info">Spare tires are often temporary: keep under 50&nbsp;mph and get the real tire fixed or replaced soon. Re-torque the lug nuts after about 50 miles.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="flip">  <details>    <summary>      <span class="front">You're on the shoulder with a flat. What's the safe order for swapping on the spare?</span>    </summary>    <div class="back">      <p class="t-small dim">Pull off the road, hazards on. Then:</p>      <ol class="list">        <li>Park on flat ground, set the parking brake. If you have a manual, leave it in gear; auto, shift to Park.</li>        <li>Loosen the lug nuts <strong>a quarter turn</strong> while the tire is still on the ground. If they won't budge, stand on the wrench&mdash;carefully.</li>        <li>Place the jack under the car's designated lift point (check the manual; usually a notch or arrow on the rocker panel). Raise until the flat tire is just off the ground.</li>        <li>Remove the lug nuts and pull the tire straight off. It may need a firm tug.</li>        <li>Lift the spare on, line up the holes, and hand-tighten the lug nuts in a star pattern. Snug them, don't crank yet.</li>        <li>Lower the car until the tire touches the ground, then tighten the nuts firmly in a star pattern.</li>      </ol>      <p class="callout info">Spare tires are often temporary: keep under 50&nbsp;mph and get the real tire fixed or replaced soon. Re-torque the lug nuts after about 50 miles.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
22.25.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Fixing a running toilet	<div class="flip">  <details>    <summary>      <span class="front">The toilet runs and runs. What's the usual culprit and the cheap fix?</span>    </summary>    <div class="back">      <p>Most likely: the flapper (rubber seal at the tank bottom) isn't sealing, so water leaks into the bowl and the fill valve keeps topping up.</p>      <ol class="list">        <li>Turn off the water at the shut-off valve behind the toilet. Flush to empty the tank.</li>        <li>Lift the tank lid and set it somewhere safe.</li>        <li>Check the flapper: if it's warped, cracked, or covered in slime, replace it. A new flapper costs a couple of dollars.</li>        <li>Also check the chain: too tight holds the flapper open; too loose lets it slip under. Adjust for a little slack.</li>        <li>If the water level is above the overflow tube, adjust the fill valve float so the tank stops filling about an inch below the top of the tube.</li>      </ol>      <p>Turn the water back on and watch. If it still runs, the fill valve itself may need replacing&mdash;also inexpensive and usually a screw-on job.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="flip">  <details>    <summary>      <span class="front">The toilet runs and runs. What's the usual culprit and the cheap fix?</span>    </summary>    <div class="back">      <p>Most likely: the flapper (rubber seal at the tank bottom) isn't sealing, so water leaks into the bowl and the fill valve keeps topping up.</p>      <ol class="list">        <li>Turn off the water at the shut-off valve behind the toilet. Flush to empty the tank.</li>        <li>Lift the tank lid and set it somewhere safe.</li>        <li>Check the flapper: if it's warped, cracked, or covered in slime, replace it. A new flapper costs a couple of dollars.</li>        <li>Also check the chain: too tight holds the flapper open; too loose lets it slip under. Adjust for a little slack.</li>        <li>If the water level is above the overflow tube, adjust the fill valve float so the tank stops filling about an inch below the top of the tube.</li>      </ol>      <p>Turn the water back on and watch. If it still runs, the fill valve itself may need replacing&mdash;also inexpensive and usually a screw-on job.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
22.26.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Removing a stripped screw	<div class="flip">  <details>    <summary>      <span class="front">The screw head is stripped and won't turn. What now?</span>    </summary>    <div class="back">      <p>Try these in order, easiest first:</p>      <ol class="list">        <li><strong>Rubber band trick:</strong> Place a wide rubber band over the screw head, press the screwdriver into it, and turn slowly. The rubber fills the gaps and adds grip.</li>        <li><strong>Bigger screwdriver:</strong> If the head is Phillips, try the next size up. Sometimes the original was too small and rounded the edges.</li>        <li><strong>Pliers:</strong> If the head sticks out, grab it with locking pliers and turn.</li>        <li><strong>Screw extractor:</strong> A dedicated bit (sold in kits) bites into the stripped head and backs it out. You'll need a drill to use it.</li>        <li><strong>Cut a new slot:</strong> With a rotary tool or hacksaw, cut a slot across the head and use a flathead screwdriver.</li>      </ol>      <p class="callout warn">If the screw is in wood and you've tried everything, you may need to drill out the head and use a plug cutter or a larger screw nearby. Last resort.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="flip">  <details>    <summary>      <span class="front">The screw head is stripped and won't turn. What now?</span>    </summary>    <div class="back">      <p>Try these in order, easiest first:</p>      <ol class="list">        <li><strong>Rubber band trick:</strong> Place a wide rubber band over the screw head, press the screwdriver into it, and turn slowly. The rubber fills the gaps and adds grip.</li>        <li><strong>Bigger screwdriver:</strong> If the head is Phillips, try the next size up. Sometimes the original was too small and rounded the edges.</li>        <li><strong>Pliers:</strong> If the head sticks out, grab it with locking pliers and turn.</li>        <li><strong>Screw extractor:</strong> A dedicated bit (sold in kits) bites into the stripped head and backs it out. You'll need a drill to use it.</li>        <li><strong>Cut a new slot:</strong> With a rotary tool or hacksaw, cut a slot across the head and use a flathead screwdriver.</li>      </ol>      <p class="callout warn">If the screw is in wood and you've tried everything, you may need to drill out the head and use a plug cutter or a larger screw nearby. Last resort.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
22.27.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Making scrambled eggs	<div class="flip">  <details>    <summary>      <span class="front">What's the key to soft, creamy scrambled eggs?</span>    </summary>    <div class="back">      <p><strong>Low heat and constant stirring.</strong> That's the secret.</p>      <ol class="list">        <li>Crack 2&ndash;3 eggs into a bowl, add a pinch of salt, and whisk until just combined. Don't overbeat.</li>        <li>Melt a knob of butter in a nonstick pan over <em>medium-low</em> heat.</li>        <li>Pour in the eggs. Let them set for a few seconds, then push them gently with a spatula, folding rather than stirring fast.</li>        <li>When they're still slightly wet and glossy, take the pan off the heat. The residual heat finishes cooking them.</li>      </ol>      <p class="callout good">For extra creaminess, stir in a spoonful of cr&egrave;me fra&icirc;che or cream cheese at the end. Serve immediately&mdash;scrambled eggs wait for no one.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="flip">  <details>    <summary>      <span class="front">What's the key to soft, creamy scrambled eggs?</span>    </summary>    <div class="back">      <p><strong>Low heat and constant stirring.</strong> That's the secret.</p>      <ol class="list">        <li>Crack 2&ndash;3 eggs into a bowl, add a pinch of salt, and whisk until just combined. Don't overbeat.</li>        <li>Melt a knob of butter in a nonstick pan over <em>medium-low</em> heat.</li>        <li>Pour in the eggs. Let them set for a few seconds, then push them gently with a spatula, folding rather than stirring fast.</li>        <li>When they're still slightly wet and glossy, take the pan off the heat. The residual heat finishes cooking them.</li>      </ol>      <p class="callout good">For extra creaminess, stir in a spoonful of cr&egrave;me fra&icirc;che or cream cheese at the end. Serve immediately&mdash;scrambled eggs wait for no one.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
22.28.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Removing a red wine stain	<div class="flip">  <details>    <summary>      <span class="front">Red wine on the carpet. Quick&mdash;what do you do?</span>    </summary>    <div class="back">      <p><strong>Blot, don't rub.</strong> And act fast.</p>      <ol class="list">        <li>Blot up as much wine as you can with a clean cloth or paper towel. Press down; don't scrub, which pushes it deeper.</li>        <li>Mix one tablespoon of dish soap with two cups of cool water. Sponge the stain gently.</li>        <li>Blot again. Repeat until the stain stops transferring.</li>        <li>If it persists, try a mix of one part white vinegar to two parts water. Sponge, blot, repeat.</li>        <li>For stubborn stains on fabric, a commercial stain remover or a paste of baking soda and water can help.</li>      </ol>      <p class="callout info">Never use hot water&mdash;it can set the stain. And test any cleaner on a hidden spot first.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="flip">  <details>    <summary>      <span class="front">Red wine on the carpet. Quick&mdash;what do you do?</span>    </summary>    <div class="back">      <p><strong>Blot, don't rub.</strong> And act fast.</p>      <ol class="list">        <li>Blot up as much wine as you can with a clean cloth or paper towel. Press down; don't scrub, which pushes it deeper.</li>        <li>Mix one tablespoon of dish soap with two cups of cool water. Sponge the stain gently.</li>        <li>Blot again. Repeat until the stain stops transferring.</li>        <li>If it persists, try a mix of one part white vinegar to two parts water. Sponge, blot, repeat.</li>        <li>For stubborn stains on fabric, a commercial stain remover or a paste of baking soda and water can help.</li>      </ol>      <p class="callout info">Never use hot water&mdash;it can set the stain. And test any cleaner on a hidden spot first.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
22.29.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Choosing olive oil	<div class="flip"><details><summary><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>flashcard</span> Choosing olive oil</summary><div class="body"><p><strong>Front:</strong> What should you look for on an olive oil label?</p><p><strong>Back:</strong> "Extra virgin" means cold-pressed, unrefined, and low acidity (under 0.8%). Look for a harvest date, a single origin, and dark glass. "Light" and "pure" are refined blends with less flavour and fewer polyphenols.</p><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Fresh oil tastes peppery and slightly bitter. If it tastes greasy or flat, it's old.</p></div></div></details></div>	<div class="flip"><details><summary><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>flashcard</span> Choosing olive oil</summary><div class="body"><p><strong>Front:</strong> What should you look for on an olive oil label?</p><p><strong>Back:</strong> "Extra virgin" means cold-pressed, unrefined, and low acidity (under 0.8%). Look for a harvest date, a single origin, and dark glass. "Light" and "pure" are refined blends with less flavour and fewer polyphenols.</p><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Fresh oil tastes peppery and slightly bitter. If it tastes greasy or flat, it's old.</p></div></div></details></div>	-
22.30.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Checking tire tread	<div class="flip"><details><summary><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>flashcard</span> Checking tire tread</summary><div class="body"><p><strong>Front:</strong> How do you check tire tread depth at home?</p><p><strong>Back:</strong> Use a tread depth gauge or a penny. Insert a penny upside down into the groove. If Lincoln's head is fully visible, tread is 2/32" or less — replace the tire. Most new tires start at 10/32".</p><div class="callout warn"><p class="t-small">Check in three spots per tire: inner, center, outer. Uneven wear means alignment or pressure issues.</p></div></div></details></div>	<div class="flip"><details><summary><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>flashcard</span> Checking tire tread</summary><div class="body"><p><strong>Front:</strong> How do you check tire tread depth at home?</p><p><strong>Back:</strong> Use a tread depth gauge or a penny. Insert a penny upside down into the groove. If Lincoln's head is fully visible, tread is 2/32" or less — replace the tire. Most new tires start at 10/32".</p><div class="callout warn"><p class="t-small">Check in three spots per tire: inner, center, outer. Uneven wear means alignment or pressure issues.</p></div></div></details></div>	-
22.31.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Removing a tick	<div class="flip"><details><summary><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>flashcard</span> Removing a tick</summary><div class="body"><p><strong>Front:</strong> What's the correct way to remove a tick?</p><p><strong>Back:</strong> Use fine-tipped tweezers. Grasp the tick as close to the skin as possible and pull straight up with steady, even pressure. Don't twist or jerk. Clean the bite with rubbing alcohol.</p><div class="callout bad"><p class="t-small">Never use petroleum jelly, a hot match, or nail polish. They can make the tick regurgitate into the wound.</p></div></div></details></div>	<div class="flip"><details><summary><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>flashcard</span> Removing a tick</summary><div class="body"><p><strong>Front:</strong> What's the correct way to remove a tick?</p><p><strong>Back:</strong> Use fine-tipped tweezers. Grasp the tick as close to the skin as possible and pull straight up with steady, even pressure. Don't twist or jerk. Clean the bite with rubbing alcohol.</p><div class="callout bad"><p class="t-small">Never use petroleum jelly, a hot match, or nail polish. They can make the tick regurgitate into the wound.</p></div></div></details></div>	-
22.32.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Sharpening a knife	<div class="flip"><details><summary><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>flashcard</span> Sharpening a knife</summary><div class="body"><p><strong>Front:</strong> What's the difference between honing and sharpening?</p><p><strong>Back:</strong> Honing realigns the edge — do it often with a rod. Sharpening removes metal to create a new edge — do it rarely with a stone. A dull knife that won't cut paper needs sharpening, not honing.</p><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Hold the blade at about 15–20° to the stone. Maintain that angle with light pressure.</p></div></div></details></div>	<div class="flip"><details><summary><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>flashcard</span> Sharpening a knife</summary><div class="body"><p><strong>Front:</strong> What's the difference between honing and sharpening?</p><p><strong>Back:</strong> Honing realigns the edge — do it often with a rod. Sharpening removes metal to create a new edge — do it rarely with a stone. A dull knife that won't cut paper needs sharpening, not honing.</p><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Hold the blade at about 15–20° to the stone. Maintain that angle with light pressure.</p></div></div></details></div>	-
22.33.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Testing egg freshness	<div class="flip"><details><summary><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>flashcard</span> Testing egg freshness</summary><div class="body"><p><strong>Front:</strong> How can you tell if an egg is still fresh?</p><p><strong>Back:</strong> Place it in a bowl of water. Fresh eggs sink and lie flat. Older eggs stand up. If it floats, toss it. The air cell grows as the egg ages, making it buoyant.</p><div class="callout good"><p class="t-small">Fresh eggs also have a thick white and a firm yolk. Thin, runny whites mean it's older.</p></div></div></details></div>	<div class="flip"><details><summary><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>flashcard</span> Testing egg freshness</summary><div class="body"><p><strong>Front:</strong> How can you tell if an egg is still fresh?</p><p><strong>Back:</strong> Place it in a bowl of water. Fresh eggs sink and lie flat. Older eggs stand up. If it floats, toss it. The air cell grows as the egg ages, making it buoyant.</p><div class="callout good"><p class="t-small">Fresh eggs also have a thick white and a firm yolk. Thin, runny whites mean it's older.</p></div></div></details></div>	-
22.34.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Ironing a shirt	<div class="flip"><details><summary><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>flashcard</span> Ironing a shirt</summary><div class="body"><p><strong>Front:</strong> What's the correct order for ironing a dress shirt?</p><p><strong>Back:</strong> Start with the collar and cuffs, then the sleeves, then the body. Finish with the front panels. This way you don't wrinkle what you've already pressed.</p><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Use steam for stubborn wrinkles. Iron the back of the collar first to avoid shine.</p></div></div></details></div>	<div class="flip"><details><summary><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>flashcard</span> Ironing a shirt</summary><div class="body"><p><strong>Front:</strong> What's the correct order for ironing a dress shirt?</p><p><strong>Back:</strong> Start with the collar and cuffs, then the sleeves, then the body. Finish with the front panels. This way you don't wrinkle what you've already pressed.</p><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Use steam for stubborn wrinkles. Iron the back of the collar first to avoid shine.</p></div></div></details></div>	-
22.35.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Whistling with two fingers	<div class="flip"><details><summary><span class="badge accent">front</span> How do you whistle loudly using two fingers?</summary><div class="body"><p><strong>Back:</strong> Fold your lips over your teeth. Place your index and middle fingers (or thumb and index) under your tongue, pushing it back. Blow hard through the small gap. Adjust finger depth until the whistle is loud and clear. Keep trying; it takes a few tries.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="flip"><details><summary><span class="badge accent">front</span> How do you whistle loudly using two fingers?</summary><div class="body"><p><strong>Back:</strong> Fold your lips over your teeth. Place your index and middle fingers (or thumb and index) under your tongue, pushing it back. Blow hard through the small gap. Adjust finger depth until the whistle is loud and clear. Keep trying; it takes a few tries.</p></div></details></div>	-
22.36.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Folding a paper boat	<div class="flip"><details><summary><span class="badge accent">front</span> How do you fold a paper boat?</summary><div class="body"><p><strong>Back:</strong> Fold a rectangular paper in half top to bottom. Fold the top corners to the center. Fold up the bottom flaps on both sides. Open the hat and squash it flat. Fold the bottom points up to the top. Open again and pull the sides apart to form the boat. Done.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="flip"><details><summary><span class="badge accent">front</span> How do you fold a paper boat?</summary><div class="body"><p><strong>Back:</strong> Fold a rectangular paper in half top to bottom. Fold the top corners to the center. Fold up the bottom flaps on both sides. Open the hat and squash it flat. Fold the bottom points up to the top. Open again and pull the sides apart to form the boat. Done.</p></div></details></div>	-
22.37.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Tying shoelaces	<div class="flip"><details><summary><span class="badge accent">front</span> How do you tie shoelaces using the bunny ears method?</summary><div class="body"><p><strong>Back:</strong> Make a loop with each lace. Cross the loops, then tuck one loop under the other. Pull tight. Or use the 'around the tree' method: make a loop with one lace, wrap the other lace around it, then push it through the hole. Pull both loops to tighten.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="flip"><details><summary><span class="badge accent">front</span> How do you tie shoelaces using the bunny ears method?</summary><div class="body"><p><strong>Back:</strong> Make a loop with each lace. Cross the loops, then tuck one loop under the other. Pull tight. Or use the 'around the tree' method: make a loop with one lace, wrap the other lace around it, then push it through the hole. Pull both loops to tighten.</p></div></details></div>	-
22.38.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Hard-boiling an egg	<div class="flip"><details><summary><span class="badge accent">front</span> How do you hard-boil an egg?</summary><div class="body"><p><strong>Back:</strong> Place eggs in a pot and cover with cold water by an inch. Bring to a boil, then turn off heat and cover. Let sit for 10-12 minutes. Transfer to ice water to cool. Peel and enjoy.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="flip"><details><summary><span class="badge accent">front</span> How do you hard-boil an egg?</summary><div class="body"><p><strong>Back:</strong> Place eggs in a pot and cover with cold water by an inch. Bring to a boil, then turn off heat and cover. Let sit for 10-12 minutes. Transfer to ice water to cool. Peel and enjoy.</p></div></details></div>	-
22.39.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Folding a T-shirt	<div class="flip"><details><summary class="front">How do you fold a T-shirt in one quick flip?</summary><div class="back"><p>Lay flat. Pinch the shoulder seam at A, the matching hem point at B, and the center point C between them. Cross B over A, then C over both. Lift and fold the trailing sleeve behind. One flip, done.</p><p class="dim t-small">Works best on a table, but you can do it on your thigh in a pinch.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="flip"><details><summary class="front">How do you fold a T-shirt in one quick flip?</summary><div class="back"><p>Lay flat. Pinch the shoulder seam at A, the matching hem point at B, and the center point C between them. Cross B over A, then C over both. Lift and fold the trailing sleeve behind. One flip, done.</p><p class="dim t-small">Works best on a table, but you can do it on your thigh in a pinch.</p></div></details></div>	-
22.40.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Writing a Clear Email Subject Line	<div class="flip"><details><summary class="front">What makes an email subject line get opened?</summary><div class="back"><p>Be specific and short—aim for 5–7 words. Front-load the point. Avoid vague words like "Quick question" and ALL CAPS. If it's a request, say what and by when.</p><p class="dim t-small">Example: "Budget approval needed by Thu 3pm" beats "Following up."</p></div></details></div>	<div class="flip"><details><summary class="front">What makes an email subject line get opened?</summary><div class="back"><p>Be specific and short—aim for 5–7 words. Front-load the point. Avoid vague words like "Quick question" and ALL CAPS. If it's a request, say what and by when.</p><p class="dim t-small">Example: "Budget approval needed by Thu 3pm" beats "Following up."</p></div></details></div>	-
22.41.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Remembering Names	<div class="flip"><details><summary class="front">How do you actually remember someone's name?</summary><div class="back"><p>Repeat it back immediately—"Nice to meet you, Priya." Then link it to something vivid: Priya who loves priya (the snack), or Priya with the purple scarf. Say it once more before you part. That's three reps and a hook.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="flip"><details><summary class="front">How do you actually remember someone's name?</summary><div class="back"><p>Repeat it back immediately—"Nice to meet you, Priya." Then link it to something vivid: Priya who loves priya (the snack), or Priya with the purple scarf. Say it once more before you part. That's three reps and a hook.</p></div></details></div>	-
22.42.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Cleaning a Cast-Iron Skillet	<div class="flip"><details><summary class="front">Can you use soap on cast iron?</summary><div class="back"><p>Yes, a little mild soap won't hurt modern seasoning. Scrub with hot water and a stiff brush or chainmail. Dry on the stove over low heat, then rub a thin film of oil while it's warm. No soaking, ever.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="flip"><details><summary class="front">Can you use soap on cast iron?</summary><div class="back"><p>Yes, a little mild soap won't hurt modern seasoning. Scrub with hot water and a stiff brush or chainmail. Dry on the stove over low heat, then rub a thin film of oil while it's warm. No soaking, ever.</p></div></details></div>	-
22.43.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Tying a Necktie (Half-Windsor)	<div class="flip"><details><summary class="front">How do you tie a Half-Windsor knot?</summary><div class="back"><p>Start with the wide end on your right, about a foot lower. Cross wide over narrow, bring it up through the neck loop, then down and around the narrow end. Up through the loop again, then down through the front knot. Tighten and adjust.</p><p class="dim t-small">Half-Windsor: symmetrical, slightly smaller than full Windsor. Good for most collars.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="flip"><details><summary class="front">How do you tie a Half-Windsor knot?</summary><div class="back"><p>Start with the wide end on your right, about a foot lower. Cross wide over narrow, bring it up through the neck loop, then down and around the narrow end. Up through the loop again, then down through the front knot. Tighten and adjust.</p><p class="dim t-small">Half-Windsor: symmetrical, slightly smaller than full Windsor. Good for most collars.</p></div></details></div>	-
22.44.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Removing Coffee Stains from Carpet	<div class="flip"><details><summary class="front">What's the best way to lift a coffee stain from carpet?</summary><div class="back"><p>Blot, don't rub. Mix 1 tsp dish soap + 1 tbsp white vinegar + 2 cups warm water. Dab it on, let it sit 5 minutes, blot with a clean cloth. Rinse with plain water and blot dry. Repeat if needed.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="flip"><details><summary class="front">What's the best way to lift a coffee stain from carpet?</summary><div class="back"><p>Blot, don't rub. Mix 1 tsp dish soap + 1 tbsp white vinegar + 2 cups warm water. Dab it on, let it sit 5 minutes, blot with a clean cloth. Rinse with plain water and blot dry. Repeat if needed.</p></div></details></div>	-
22.45.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Jump-Starting a Car	<div class="flip"><div class="front">What's the correct order to connect jumper cables?</div><div class="back"><p>Dead first, then live, then ground.</p><ol class="list"><li>Red clamp to <strong>dead</strong> battery positive (+).</li><li>Red clamp to <strong>good</strong> battery positive (+).</li><li>Black clamp to <strong>good</strong> battery negative (&minus;).</li><li>Black clamp to bare metal on the <strong>dead</strong> car's engine block &mdash; not the battery.</li></ol><p class="muted t-small">The last step avoids a spark near battery fumes. Reverse the order to remove.</p></div></div>	<div class="flip"><div class="front">What's the correct order to connect jumper cables?</div><div class="back"><p>Dead first, then live, then ground.</p><ol class="list"><li>Red clamp to <strong>dead</strong> battery positive (+).</li><li>Red clamp to <strong>good</strong> battery positive (+).</li><li>Black clamp to <strong>good</strong> battery negative (&minus;).</li><li>Black clamp to bare metal on the <strong>dead</strong> car's engine block &mdash; not the battery.</li></ol><p class="muted t-small">The last step avoids a spark near battery fumes. Reverse the order to remove.</p></div></div>	-
22.46.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Fixing a Running Toilet	<div class="flip"><div class="front">Why does my toilet keep running after a flush?</div><div class="back"><p>Most likely the flapper isn't sealing. It's a $5 rubber valve that warps over time.</p><ul class="list"><li>Lift the tank lid and check if water is flowing into the bowl.</li><li>If yes, jiggle the flapper &mdash; if the sound stops, replace it.</li><li>Turn off the water supply, flush to empty, swap the flapper.</li></ul><p class="muted t-small">Still running? Check the fill valve float height.</p></div></div>	<div class="flip"><div class="front">Why does my toilet keep running after a flush?</div><div class="back"><p>Most likely the flapper isn't sealing. It's a $5 rubber valve that warps over time.</p><ul class="list"><li>Lift the tank lid and check if water is flowing into the bowl.</li><li>If yes, jiggle the flapper &mdash; if the sound stops, replace it.</li><li>Turn off the water supply, flush to empty, swap the flapper.</li></ul><p class="muted t-small">Still running? Check the fill valve float height.</p></div></div>	-
22.47.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Removing a Stripped Screw	<div class="flip"><div class="front">How do you get out a screw with a stripped head?</div><div class="back"><p>Try a rubber band first.</p><ul class="list"><li>Place a wide rubber band over the screw head.</li><li>Press the screwdriver in firmly and turn slowly.</li><li>Still stuck? Try a slightly larger screwdriver or a screw extractor bit.</li></ul><p class="muted t-small">If all else fails, drill off the head and grab the shaft with pliers.</p></div></div>	<div class="flip"><div class="front">How do you get out a screw with a stripped head?</div><div class="back"><p>Try a rubber band first.</p><ul class="list"><li>Place a wide rubber band over the screw head.</li><li>Press the screwdriver in firmly and turn slowly.</li><li>Still stuck? Try a slightly larger screwdriver or a screw extractor bit.</li></ul><p class="muted t-small">If all else fails, drill off the head and grab the shaft with pliers.</p></div></div>	-
22.48.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Testing Egg Freshness	<div class="flip"><div class="front">How can you tell if an egg is still fresh?</div><div class="back"><p>Float it in a bowl of water.</p><ul class="list"><li><strong>Sinks flat</strong> &rarr; fresh.</li><li><strong>Stands upright</strong> &rarr; older, still okay for baking.</li><li><strong>Floats</strong> &rarr; toss it. The air pocket inside has grown too big.</li></ul><p class="muted t-small">The shell is porous, so eggs slowly lose moisture and take on air over time.</p></div></div>	<div class="flip"><div class="front">How can you tell if an egg is still fresh?</div><div class="back"><p>Float it in a bowl of water.</p><ul class="list"><li><strong>Sinks flat</strong> &rarr; fresh.</li><li><strong>Stands upright</strong> &rarr; older, still okay for baking.</li><li><strong>Floats</strong> &rarr; toss it. The air pocket inside has grown too big.</li></ul><p class="muted t-small">The shell is porous, so eggs slowly lose moisture and take on air over time.</p></div></div>	-
22.49.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Whistling with Two Fingers	<div class="flip"><div class="front">How do you whistle loudly with two fingers?</div><div class="back"><ol class="list"><li>Make a <strong>V</strong> with your index and middle fingers.</li><li>Place them under your tongue, about a third of the way in.</li><li>Press down gently to create a small gap under your tongue.</li><li>Close your lips around your fingers and blow <strong>hard and steady</strong>.</li></ol><p class="muted t-small">It takes practice. Adjust finger depth until you get a clean tone.</p></div></div>	<div class="flip"><div class="front">How do you whistle loudly with two fingers?</div><div class="back"><ol class="list"><li>Make a <strong>V</strong> with your index and middle fingers.</li><li>Place them under your tongue, about a third of the way in.</li><li>Press down gently to create a small gap under your tongue.</li><li>Close your lips around your fingers and blow <strong>hard and steady</strong>.</li></ol><p class="muted t-small">It takes practice. Adjust finger depth until you get a clean tone.</p></div></div>	-
22.50.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Reading a Tape Measure	<div class="flip"><div class="front">Reading a tape measure</div><div class="back"><p>Big numbers = inches. The longest line between them is the half inch. The next shorter lines split each half into quarters, then eighths, then sixteenths. Count the lines from the whole inch to your mark to find the fraction. The tiny line before 1 is 15/16, not 1/16.</p><p class="dim t-small">Tip: The hook at the end slides on purpose. It's loose to account for its own thickness when measuring inside vs. outside.</p></div></div>	<div class="flip"><div class="front">Reading a tape measure</div><div class="back"><p>Big numbers = inches. The longest line between them is the half inch. The next shorter lines split each half into quarters, then eighths, then sixteenths. Count the lines from the whole inch to your mark to find the fraction. The tiny line before 1 is 15/16, not 1/16.</p><p class="dim t-small">Tip: The hook at the end slides on purpose. It's loose to account for its own thickness when measuring inside vs. outside.</p></div></div>	-
22.51.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Brewing a Proper Cup of Tea	<div class="flip"><div class="front">Brewing a proper cup of tea</div><div class="back"><p>Use fresh water, just off the boil for black tea (about 200–212°F / 93–100°C). One teaspoon of loose leaves or one bag per cup. Steep 3–5 minutes. Green and white teas prefer cooler water (160–180°F / 70–80°C) and shorter steeps (1–3 min) to avoid bitterness.</p><p class="dim t-small">Remove the leaves when done. Leaving them in makes it tannic and harsh.</p></div></div>	<div class="flip"><div class="front">Brewing a proper cup of tea</div><div class="back"><p>Use fresh water, just off the boil for black tea (about 200–212°F / 93–100°C). One teaspoon of loose leaves or one bag per cup. Steep 3–5 minutes. Green and white teas prefer cooler water (160–180°F / 70–80°C) and shorter steeps (1–3 min) to avoid bitterness.</p><p class="dim t-small">Remove the leaves when done. Leaving them in makes it tannic and harsh.</p></div></div>	-
22.52.0	flashcard/Flashcard: How Noise-Cancelling Headphones Work	<div class="flip"><div class="front">How noise-cancelling headphones work</div><div class="back"><p>Small microphones listen to the outside noise. The headphones then play a sound wave that is the exact opposite—an anti-noise wave. The two waves cancel each other out. It works best on steady low sounds like engine hum, less on sudden or high-pitched noises.</p><p class="dim t-small">That slight pressure feeling? That's your brain noticing the missing low frequencies.</p></div></div>	<div class="flip"><div class="front">How noise-cancelling headphones work</div><div class="back"><p>Small microphones listen to the outside noise. The headphones then play a sound wave that is the exact opposite—an anti-noise wave. The two waves cancel each other out. It works best on steady low sounds like engine hum, less on sudden or high-pitched noises.</p><p class="dim t-small">That slight pressure feeling? That's your brain noticing the missing low frequencies.</p></div></div>	-
22.53.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Tying a Clove Hitch	<div class="flip"><div class="front">Tying a clove hitch</div><div class="back"><p>Wrap the rope around the post. Cross over the standing part and wrap again. Tuck the end under the second wrap, then pull tight. It's two loops that cross over each other.</p><p class="dim t-small">Good for starting lashings or temporary tie-offs. It can slip if the load is not constant, so add a half hitch if unsure.</p></div></div>	<div class="flip"><div class="front">Tying a clove hitch</div><div class="back"><p>Wrap the rope around the post. Cross over the standing part and wrap again. Tuck the end under the second wrap, then pull tight. It's two loops that cross over each other.</p><p class="dim t-small">Good for starting lashings or temporary tie-offs. It can slip if the load is not constant, so add a half hitch if unsure.</p></div></div>	-
22.54.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Poaching an Egg	<div class="flip"><div class="front">Poaching an egg</div><div class="back"><p>Bring a pot of water to a bare simmer—small bubbles, not a rolling boil. Add a splash of vinegar (optional). Crack the egg into a small bowl, then slide it gently into the water. Cook 3–4 minutes for a runny yolk. Lift out with a slotted spoon and drain on paper towel.</p><p class="dim t-small">Fresher eggs hold their shape better. If your egg is old, the white will spread.</p></div></div>	<div class="flip"><div class="front">Poaching an egg</div><div class="back"><p>Bring a pot of water to a bare simmer—small bubbles, not a rolling boil. Add a splash of vinegar (optional). Crack the egg into a small bowl, then slide it gently into the water. Cook 3–4 minutes for a runny yolk. Lift out with a slotted spoon and drain on paper towel.</p><p class="dim t-small">Fresher eggs hold their shape better. If your egg is old, the white will spread.</p></div></div>	-
22.55.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Setting Up a Simple Budget	<div class="flip"><div class="front">Setting up a simple budget</div><div class="back"><p>Start with your take-home pay. List fixed costs (rent, utilities, loan payments). Then estimate variable costs (food, gas, fun). Subtract them from your income. If you're negative, trim the variable ones. If positive, move the extra to savings or debt.</p><p class="dim t-small">Review it once a month. A budget is a guess that gets better with practice.</p></div></div>	<div class="flip"><div class="front">Setting up a simple budget</div><div class="back"><p>Start with your take-home pay. List fixed costs (rent, utilities, loan payments). Then estimate variable costs (food, gas, fun). Subtract them from your income. If you're negative, trim the variable ones. If positive, move the extra to savings or debt.</p><p class="dim t-small">Review it once a month. A budget is a guess that gets better with practice.</p></div></div>	-
22.56.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Sharpening a kitchen knife	<div class="flip"><div class="front">How do you sharpen a kitchen knife with a whetstone?</div><div class="back"><p>Soak the stone 10 minutes. Hold the knife at ~15–20° (two stacked coins under the spine). Slide the blade across the stone in a sweeping arc, light pressure, 10 passes per side. Flip, repeat. Finish with a few light strokes on the other side, then hone on a steel or strop. Test on paper—it should slice cleanly.</p></div></div>	<div class="flip"><div class="front">How do you sharpen a kitchen knife with a whetstone?</div><div class="back"><p>Soak the stone 10 minutes. Hold the knife at ~15–20° (two stacked coins under the spine). Slide the blade across the stone in a sweeping arc, light pressure, 10 passes per side. Flip, repeat. Finish with a few light strokes on the other side, then hone on a steel or strop. Test on paper—it should slice cleanly.</p></div></div>	-
22.57.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Removing coffee stains from carpet	<div class="flip"><div class="front">How do you get a coffee stain out of carpet?</div><div class="back"><p>Blot—don't rub—with a clean cloth. Mix 1 tbsp dish soap, 1 tbsp white vinegar, and 2 cups warm water. Sponge onto the stain, let sit 5 minutes, blot again. Repeat until lifted. For dried stains, use a bit of hydrogen peroxide (test a hidden spot first). Rinse with water and blot dry.</p></div></div>	<div class="flip"><div class="front">How do you get a coffee stain out of carpet?</div><div class="back"><p>Blot—don't rub—with a clean cloth. Mix 1 tbsp dish soap, 1 tbsp white vinegar, and 2 cups warm water. Sponge onto the stain, let sit 5 minutes, blot again. Repeat until lifted. For dried stains, use a bit of hydrogen peroxide (test a hidden spot first). Rinse with water and blot dry.</p></div></div>	-
22.58.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Reading a tape measure	<div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Reading a tape measure</div>  <p class="muted">Inches break down by halves, not tenths. Halve your way down and you can name any line.</p>  <div class="flip">    <details>      <summary>Front: what does each line size mean?</summary>      <div class="body">        <ul class="list">          <li><strong>Longest line + number</strong> = whole inch.</li>          <li><strong>Second longest</strong> = half inch (1/2).</li>          <li><strong>Next</strong> = quarters (1/4, 3/4).</li>          <li><strong>Next</strong> = eighths (1/8, 3/8, 5/8, 7/8).</li>          <li><strong>Shortest</strong> = sixteenths.</li>        </ul>        <p class="t-small dim">Read left to right: count whole inches first, then add the fraction to the last big mark.</p>      </div>    </details>  </div>  <div class="flip">    <details>      <summary>Front: handy marks you may spot</summary>      <div class="body">        <p><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>16"</span> Black diamond or red box = stud spacing.</p>        <p><span class="badge info"><i class="dot"></i>19.2"</span> Marks for five studs per 8-foot sheet.</p>        <p><span class="badge warn"><i class="dot"></i>Hook</span> The metal end slides about 1/16" on purpose, so inside and outside measurements both stay true.</p>      </div>    </details>  </div>  <p class="t-small dim">Nice work, <regex>user_name</regex>. Go measure something.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Reading a tape measure</div>  <p class="muted">Inches break down by halves, not tenths. Halve your way down and you can name any line.</p>  <div class="flip">    <details>      <summary>Front: what does each line size mean?</summary>      <div class="body">        <ul class="list">          <li><strong>Longest line + number</strong> = whole inch.</li>          <li><strong>Second longest</strong> = half inch (1/2).</li>          <li><strong>Next</strong> = quarters (1/4, 3/4).</li>          <li><strong>Next</strong> = eighths (1/8, 3/8, 5/8, 7/8).</li>          <li><strong>Shortest</strong> = sixteenths.</li>        </ul>        <p class="t-small dim">Read left to right: count whole inches first, then add the fraction to the last big mark.</p>      </div>    </details>  </div>  <div class="flip">    <details>      <summary>Front: handy marks you may spot</summary>      <div class="body">        <p><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>16"</span> Black diamond or red box = stud spacing.</p>        <p><span class="badge info"><i class="dot"></i>19.2"</span> Marks for five studs per 8-foot sheet.</p>        <p><span class="badge warn"><i class="dot"></i>Hook</span> The metal end slides about 1/16" on purpose, so inside and outside measurements both stay true.</p>      </div>    </details>  </div>  <p class="t-small dim">Nice work, . Go measure something.</p></div>	-
22.59.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Tying a square knot	<div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Tying a square knot</div>  <p class="muted">Right over left, left over right. Two identical over-unders cancel out and lock flat.</p>  <div class="flip">    <details>      <summary>Front: the steps</summary>      <div class="body">        <ol class="list">          <li>Hold one end in each hand, say <em>right</em> over <em>left</em>.</li>          <li>Cross right over left, tuck it under, pull snug.</li>          <li>Now cross <em>left</em> over <em>right</em>, tuck under, pull snug.</li>          <li>Both working ends should exit parallel, on the same side.</li>        </ol>      </div>    </details>  </div>  <div class="flip">    <details>      <summary>Front: how do I know it's not a granny knot?</summary>      <div class="body">        <p>Look at the two ends. Square: both lie flat against the standing parts, tidy and parallel. Granny: they stick out at odd angles and the knot skews.</p>        <svg viewBox="0 0 220 70" role="img" aria-label="Two tidy parallel knot ends versus two skewed ends">          <path class="line" d="M20 35 h60 M80 35 q20 -18 40 0 M120 35 h60" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="3" />          <path class="line" d="M140 20 l60 30 M140 50 l60 -30" fill="none" stroke="var(--warn)" stroke-width="3" />        </svg>        <p class="t-small dim">Not for load-bearing. A square knot capsizes under heavy pull; use a sheet bend or bowline instead.</p>      </div>    </details>  </div>  <p class="t-small dim">Tidy ropes, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Tying a square knot</div>  <p class="muted">Right over left, left over right. Two identical over-unders cancel out and lock flat.</p>  <div class="flip">    <details>      <summary>Front: the steps</summary>      <div class="body">        <ol class="list">          <li>Hold one end in each hand, say <em>right</em> over <em>left</em>.</li>          <li>Cross right over left, tuck it under, pull snug.</li>          <li>Now cross <em>left</em> over <em>right</em>, tuck under, pull snug.</li>          <li>Both working ends should exit parallel, on the same side.</li>        </ol>      </div>    </details>  </div>  <div class="flip">    <details>      <summary>Front: how do I know it's not a granny knot?</summary>      <div class="body">        <p>Look at the two ends. Square: both lie flat against the standing parts, tidy and parallel. Granny: they stick out at odd angles and the knot skews.</p>        <svg viewBox="0 0 220 70" role="img" aria-label="Two tidy parallel knot ends versus two skewed ends">          <path class="line" d="M20 35 h60 M80 35 q20 -18 40 0 M120 35 h60" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="3" />          <path class="line" d="M140 20 l60 30 M140 50 l60 -30" fill="none" stroke="var(--warn)" stroke-width="3" />        </svg>        <p class="t-small dim">Not for load-bearing. A square knot capsizes under heavy pull; use a sheet bend or bowline instead.</p>      </div>    </details>  </div>  <p class="t-small dim">Tidy ropes, .</p></div>	-
22.60.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Removing a screw with a rubber band	<div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Removing a screw with a rubber band</div>  <p class="muted">The rubber band fills the gap your stripped head left behind. Free grip, no trip to the store.</p>  <div class="flip">    <details>      <summary>Front: how to do it</summary>      <div class="body">        <ol class="list">          <li>Lay a wide rubber band flat over the screw head.</li>          <li>Press the driver in hard so the band squishes into the slots.</li>          <li>Turn slowly, keeping heavy downward pressure. Fast spinning just re-strips it.</li>          <li>Once it budges, remove the band and back it out normally.</li>        </ol>      </div>    </details>  </div>  <div class="flip">    <details>      <summary>Front: if that fails</summary>      <div class="body">        <p><span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>Step up</span> Try a slightly larger Phillips or a square-drive bit that bites fresh metal.</p>        <p><span class="badge warn"><i class="dot"></i>Last resort</span> Cut a slot with a rotary tool and use a flathead, or drill the head off and grab the shaft with pliers.</p>      </div>    </details>  </div>  <p class="t-small dim">Patience beats torque, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Removing a screw with a rubber band</div>  <p class="muted">The rubber band fills the gap your stripped head left behind. Free grip, no trip to the store.</p>  <div class="flip">    <details>      <summary>Front: how to do it</summary>      <div class="body">        <ol class="list">          <li>Lay a wide rubber band flat over the screw head.</li>          <li>Press the driver in hard so the band squishes into the slots.</li>          <li>Turn slowly, keeping heavy downward pressure. Fast spinning just re-strips it.</li>          <li>Once it budges, remove the band and back it out normally.</li>        </ol>      </div>    </details>  </div>  <div class="flip">    <details>      <summary>Front: if that fails</summary>      <div class="body">        <p><span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>Step up</span> Try a slightly larger Phillips or a square-drive bit that bites fresh metal.</p>        <p><span class="badge warn"><i class="dot"></i>Last resort</span> Cut a slot with a rotary tool and use a flathead, or drill the head off and grab the shaft with pliers.</p>      </div>    </details>  </div>  <p class="t-small dim">Patience beats torque, .</p></div>	-
22.61.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Unclogging a drain with baking soda and vinegar	<div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Unclogging a drain with baking soda and vinegar</div>  <p class="muted">Fizz does the scrubbing, hot water does the flushing. Best for gunk and smells, not solid blockages.</p>  <div class="flip">    <details>      <summary>Front: the method</summary>      <div class="body">        <ol class="list">          <li>Pull the stopper and clear visible hair with a bent wire or zip tool.</li>          <li>Pour <strong>1/2 cup baking soda</strong> down the drain.</li>          <li>Follow with <strong>1/2 cup white vinegar</strong>. Cover with a plug to push fizz downward.</li>          <li>Wait 15 minutes.</li>          <li>Flush with a kettle of <strong>hot (not boiling)</strong> water.</li>        </ol>      </div>    </details>  </div>  <div class="callout warn">    <p><span class="badge bad"><i class="dot"></i>Never</span> mix vinegar or baking soda with store-bought drain cleaner. The reaction can splash and burn.</p>    <p class="t-small dim">If water still pools, the clog is past the trap: a snake or a plumber is the honest answer.</p>  </div>  <p class="t-small dim">Smells better already, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Unclogging a drain with baking soda and vinegar</div>  <p class="muted">Fizz does the scrubbing, hot water does the flushing. Best for gunk and smells, not solid blockages.</p>  <div class="flip">    <details>      <summary>Front: the method</summary>      <div class="body">        <ol class="list">          <li>Pull the stopper and clear visible hair with a bent wire or zip tool.</li>          <li>Pour <strong>1/2 cup baking soda</strong> down the drain.</li>          <li>Follow with <strong>1/2 cup white vinegar</strong>. Cover with a plug to push fizz downward.</li>          <li>Wait 15 minutes.</li>          <li>Flush with a kettle of <strong>hot (not boiling)</strong> water.</li>        </ol>      </div>    </details>  </div>  <div class="callout warn">    <p><span class="badge bad"><i class="dot"></i>Never</span> mix vinegar or baking soda with store-bought drain cleaner. The reaction can splash and burn.</p>    <p class="t-small dim">If water still pools, the clog is past the trap: a snake or a plumber is the honest answer.</p>  </div>  <p class="t-small dim">Smells better already, .</p></div>	-
22.62.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Washing a down jacket	<div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Washing a down jacket</div>  <p class="muted">Down hates detergent residue and heat. Gentle soap, low spin, and a long tumble with dryer balls bring the loft back.</p>  <div class="flip">    <details>      <summary>Front: wash steps</summary>      <div class="body">        <ol class="list">          <li>Zip all zippers, close flaps, check pockets.</li>          <li>Use a <strong>front-loading</strong> machine on gentle, cold water.</li>          <li>Add <strong>down-specific or mild soap</strong>. No fabric softener, no bleach, no regular detergent.</li>          <li>Run an extra rinse cycle to flush residue.</li>          <li>Spin on low. Down holds a lot of water.</li>        </ol>      </div>    </details>  </div>  <div class="flip">    <details>      <summary>Front: drying and re-lofting</summary>      <div class="body">        <p>Tumble on <strong>low</strong> with two or three tennis balls or dryer balls. It can take 2–3 hours.</p>        <p>Shake it out every 30 minutes to break up clumps. Keep going until it feels completely dry and puffy. Damp down clumps and smells.</p>      </div>    </details>  </div>  <p class="t-small dim">Fluffy again, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Washing a down jacket</div>  <p class="muted">Down hates detergent residue and heat. Gentle soap, low spin, and a long tumble with dryer balls bring the loft back.</p>  <div class="flip">    <details>      <summary>Front: wash steps</summary>      <div class="body">        <ol class="list">          <li>Zip all zippers, close flaps, check pockets.</li>          <li>Use a <strong>front-loading</strong> machine on gentle, cold water.</li>          <li>Add <strong>down-specific or mild soap</strong>. No fabric softener, no bleach, no regular detergent.</li>          <li>Run an extra rinse cycle to flush residue.</li>          <li>Spin on low. Down holds a lot of water.</li>        </ol>      </div>    </details>  </div>  <div class="flip">    <details>      <summary>Front: drying and re-lofting</summary>      <div class="body">        <p>Tumble on <strong>low</strong> with two or three tennis balls or dryer balls. It can take 2–3 hours.</p>        <p>Shake it out every 30 minutes to break up clumps. Keep going until it feels completely dry and puffy. Damp down clumps and smells.</p>      </div>    </details>  </div>  <p class="t-small dim">Fluffy again, .</p></div>	-
22.63.0	flashcard/Flashcard: Jump-starting a car with a portable pack	<div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Jump-starting a car with a portable pack</div>  <p class="muted">No second car needed. Order matters less than not letting the clamps kiss.</p>  <div class="flip">    <details>      <summary>Front: the sequence</summary>      <div class="body">        <ol class="list">          <li>Turn the pack on and make sure it has charge.</li>          <li>Clamp <span class="badge bad"><i class="dot"></i>red</span> to the battery's positive (+) post.</li>          <li>Clamp <span class="badge info"><i class="dot"></i>black</span> to a bare metal bolt on the engine, not the negative post.</li>          <li>Wait a minute, then start the car.</li>          <li>Remove black first, then red, and keep the clamps apart.</li>        </ol>      </div>    </details>  </div>  <div class="flip">    <details>      <summary>Front: if it still won't start</summary>      <div class="body">        <p>Listen for a click or a slow crank. A click means the pack moved current but the battery is too far gone to hold it. Drive 20–30 minutes to recharge, or replace the battery.</p>        <p class="t-small dim">Don't crank longer than 5 seconds at a time; let the starter cool between tries.</p>      </div>    </details>  </div>  <p class="t-small dim">Back on the road, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Jump-starting a car with a portable pack</div>  <p class="muted">No second car needed. Order matters less than not letting the clamps kiss.</p>  <div class="flip">    <details>      <summary>Front: the sequence</summary>      <div class="body">        <ol class="list">          <li>Turn the pack on and make sure it has charge.</li>          <li>Clamp <span class="badge bad"><i class="dot"></i>red</span> to the battery's positive (+) post.</li>          <li>Clamp <span class="badge info"><i class="dot"></i>black</span> to a bare metal bolt on the engine, not the negative post.</li>          <li>Wait a minute, then start the car.</li>          <li>Remove black first, then red, and keep the clamps apart.</li>        </ol>      </div>    </details>  </div>  <div class="flip">    <details>      <summary>Front: if it still won't start</summary>      <div class="body">        <p>Listen for a click or a slow crank. A click means the pack moved current but the battery is too far gone to hold it. Drive 20–30 minutes to recharge, or replace the battery.</p>        <p class="t-small dim">Don't crank longer than 5 seconds at a time; let the starter cool between tries.</p>      </div>    </details>  </div>  <p class="t-small dim">Back on the road, .</p></div>	-
22.0.1	flashcard/Flashcard: Making mayonnaise	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Making mayonnaise</p>  <p class="t-small muted">Yellow brick road to a broken sauce — slow down at the start.</p>  <details class="flip">    <summary>Front: Why does mayo break?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Oil gets added faster than the lecithin in egg yolk can coat it. Invert that ratio and the emulsion snaps back into puddles.</p>      <div class="callout warn">        <p><strong>Rescue:</strong> start a fresh yolk in a clean bowl, whisk, and drizzle the broken mayo back in — it will re-emulsify.</p>      </div>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary>Front: Base ratio?</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>1 egg yolk (pasteurised if you like)</li>        <li>1 tsp lemon juice or vinegar</li>        <li>1 tsp Dijon mustard</li>        <li>150–200 ml neutral oil</li>        <li>Salt to finish</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary>Front: The method, in order</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Whisk yolk, acid, mustard, salt — pale and thick.</li>        <li>Add oil drop by drop at first, whisking hard.</li>        <li>Once it looks creamy, switch to a thin steady stream.</li>        <li>Taste, adjust salt and lemon, then chill.</li>      </ol>      <p class="t-small dim">Immersion blender works: keep the blade buried and tilt slowly from the bottom up.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Making mayonnaise</p>  <p class="t-small muted">Yellow brick road to a broken sauce — slow down at the start.</p>  <details class="flip">    <summary>Front: Why does mayo break?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Oil gets added faster than the lecithin in egg yolk can coat it. Invert that ratio and the emulsion snaps back into puddles.</p>      <div class="callout warn">        <p><strong>Rescue:</strong> start a fresh yolk in a clean bowl, whisk, and drizzle the broken mayo back in — it will re-emulsify.</p>      </div>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary>Front: Base ratio?</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>1 egg yolk (pasteurised if you like)</li>        <li>1 tsp lemon juice or vinegar</li>        <li>1 tsp Dijon mustard</li>        <li>150–200 ml neutral oil</li>        <li>Salt to finish</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary>Front: The method, in order</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Whisk yolk, acid, mustard, salt — pale and thick.</li>        <li>Add oil drop by drop at first, whisking hard.</li>        <li>Once it looks creamy, switch to a thin steady stream.</li>        <li>Taste, adjust salt and lemon, then chill.</li>      </ol>      <p class="t-small dim">Immersion blender works: keep the blade buried and tilt slowly from the bottom up.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
22.1.1	flashcard/Flashcard: Reading a resistor	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Reading a resistor</p>  <p class="t-small muted">Four bands, six colours, one very small stripe.</p>  <details class="flip">    <summary>Front: What do the bands mean?</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Digit 1</li>        <li>Digit 2</li>        <li>Multiplier (number of zeros)</li>        <li>Tolerance — gold ±5%, silver ±10%</li>      </ol>      <p class="t-small dim">Read the tightest-spaced end first.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary>Front: The colour numbers</summary>    <div class="body">      <p><strong>0</strong> black · <strong>1</strong> brown · <strong>2</strong> red · <strong>3</strong> orange · <strong>4</strong> yellow · <strong>5</strong> green · <strong>6</strong> blue · <strong>7</strong> violet · <strong>8</strong> grey · <strong>9</strong> white</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary>Front: Red red brown gold?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>2, 2, ×10, ±5% = <strong>220 Ω</strong>. Red red red would be 2.2 kΩ.</p>      <div class="callout info">        <p>Measure with a meter when you can — colour reading is for speed, not certainty.</p>      </div>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Reading a resistor</p>  <p class="t-small muted">Four bands, six colours, one very small stripe.</p>  <details class="flip">    <summary>Front: What do the bands mean?</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Digit 1</li>        <li>Digit 2</li>        <li>Multiplier (number of zeros)</li>        <li>Tolerance — gold ±5%, silver ±10%</li>      </ol>      <p class="t-small dim">Read the tightest-spaced end first.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary>Front: The colour numbers</summary>    <div class="body">      <p><strong>0</strong> black · <strong>1</strong> brown · <strong>2</strong> red · <strong>3</strong> orange · <strong>4</strong> yellow · <strong>5</strong> green · <strong>6</strong> blue · <strong>7</strong> violet · <strong>8</strong> grey · <strong>9</strong> white</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary>Front: Red red brown gold?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>2, 2, ×10, ±5% = <strong>220 Ω</strong>. Red red red would be 2.2 kΩ.</p>      <div class="callout info">        <p>Measure with a meter when you can — colour reading is for speed, not certainty.</p>      </div>    </div>  </details></div>	-
22.2.1	flashcard/Flashcard: Backing up your phone photos	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Backing up your phone photos</p>  <p class="t-small muted">A photo you own twice is a photo you keep.</p>  <details class="flip">    <summary>Front: The 3-2-1 rule?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p><strong>3</strong> copies, on <strong>2</strong> kinds of media, with <strong>1</strong> somewhere off-site.</p>      <p class="t-small dim">Phone + laptop + cloud is enough for most people.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary>Front: Easiest working setup</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Turn on automatic cloud sync.</li>        <li>Once a month, plug into a computer and copy the camera roll.</li>        <li>Keep the copied folder on an external drive too.</li>      </ol>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary>Front: What "backed up" is not</summary>    <div class="body">      <div class="callout warn">        <p>Freeing up space by deleting from the phone is not a backup. If the only copy lives in the cloud and you lose access, it is gone.</p>      </div>      <p class="t-small dim">Test a restore once. A backup you have never opened is a rumour.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Backing up your phone photos</p>  <p class="t-small muted">A photo you own twice is a photo you keep.</p>  <details class="flip">    <summary>Front: The 3-2-1 rule?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p><strong>3</strong> copies, on <strong>2</strong> kinds of media, with <strong>1</strong> somewhere off-site.</p>      <p class="t-small dim">Phone + laptop + cloud is enough for most people.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary>Front: Easiest working setup</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Turn on automatic cloud sync.</li>        <li>Once a month, plug into a computer and copy the camera roll.</li>        <li>Keep the copied folder on an external drive too.</li>      </ol>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary>Front: What "backed up" is not</summary>    <div class="body">      <div class="callout warn">        <p>Freeing up space by deleting from the phone is not a backup. If the only copy lives in the cloud and you lose access, it is gone.</p>      </div>      <p class="t-small dim">Test a restore once. A backup you have never opened is a rumour.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
22.3.1	flashcard/Flashcard: Reading a food label's serving size	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Reading a food label's serving size</p>  <p class="t-small muted">The number that undoes every other number on the box.</p>  <details class="flip">    <summary>Front: Why does it matter so much?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Every figure — calories, sugar, sodium — is per serving, not per package. A "230 kcal" snack with 2.5 servings is really 575 kcal if you finish the bag.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary>Front: Quick maths</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Find servings per container.</li>        <li>Estimate what you actually ate, as a fraction of one serving.</li>        <li>Multiply.</li>      </ol>      <p class="t-small dim">Half a serving = halve every number.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary>Front: Per 100 g trick</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Use it to compare two products fairly, since serving sizes differ wildly between brands.</p>      <div class="callout info"><p>Lower sugar per 100 g is a cleaner comparison than "low sugar" on the front.</p></div>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Reading a food label's serving size</p>  <p class="t-small muted">The number that undoes every other number on the box.</p>  <details class="flip">    <summary>Front: Why does it matter so much?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Every figure — calories, sugar, sodium — is per serving, not per package. A "230 kcal" snack with 2.5 servings is really 575 kcal if you finish the bag.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary>Front: Quick maths</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Find servings per container.</li>        <li>Estimate what you actually ate, as a fraction of one serving.</li>        <li>Multiply.</li>      </ol>      <p class="t-small dim">Half a serving = halve every number.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary>Front: Per 100 g trick</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Use it to compare two products fairly, since serving sizes differ wildly between brands.</p>      <div class="callout info"><p>Lower sugar per 100 g is a cleaner comparison than "low sugar" on the front.</p></div>    </div>  </details></div>	-
22.4.1	flashcard/Flashcard: Using a torque wrench	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Using a torque wrench</p>  <p class="t-small muted">Enough is a measurement, not a feeling.</p>  <details class="flip">    <summary>Front: What does it do?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>It clicks or slips once you hit a set twisting force, measured in Nm or lb-ft. The click means <em>stop</em> — not "keep going a bit"</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary>Front: The order for lug nuts</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Finger-tight all of them first.</li>        <li>Then snug in a star pattern, skipping across.</li>        <li>Finish to spec, still in the star pattern.</li>      </ol>      <p class="t-small dim">Never a circle — that warps the wheel.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary>Front: Aftercare</summary>    <div class="body">      <div class="callout warn"><p>Wind the setting back to its lowest value before storing. A spring left compressed drifts out of calibration.</p></div>      <p class="t-small dim">Also: never use it as a breaker bar.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Using a torque wrench</p>  <p class="t-small muted">Enough is a measurement, not a feeling.</p>  <details class="flip">    <summary>Front: What does it do?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>It clicks or slips once you hit a set twisting force, measured in Nm or lb-ft. The click means <em>stop</em> — not "keep going a bit"</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary>Front: The order for lug nuts</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Finger-tight all of them first.</li>        <li>Then snug in a star pattern, skipping across.</li>        <li>Finish to spec, still in the star pattern.</li>      </ol>      <p class="t-small dim">Never a circle — that warps the wheel.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary>Front: Aftercare</summary>    <div class="body">      <div class="callout warn"><p>Wind the setting back to its lowest value before storing. A spring left compressed drifts out of calibration.</p></div>      <p class="t-small dim">Also: never use it as a breaker bar.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
22.5.1	flashcard/Flashcard: Making pour-over coffee	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Making pour-over coffee</p>  <p class="t-small muted">Three minutes of standing still for a much better cup.</p>  <details class="flip">    <summary>Front: The ratio</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Start at <strong>1:16</strong> — 15 g coffee to 240 g water. Adjust to taste, not to the internet.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary>Front: Step by step</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Rinse the filter with hot water, then dump it.</li>        <li>Add medium-fine grounds, level the bed.</li>        <li>Pour 45 g water, wait 30–45 s for the bloom.</li>        <li>Pour in slow circles to 240 g, total 2:30–3:00.</li>      </ol>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary>Front: Sour or bitter?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p><strong>Sour / thin</strong> = under-extracted: grind finer or pour slower.</p>      <p><strong>Bitter / dry</strong> = over-extracted: grind coarser or use less time.</p>      <div class="callout good"><p>Change one thing at a time.</p></div>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Making pour-over coffee</p>  <p class="t-small muted">Three minutes of standing still for a much better cup.</p>  <details class="flip">    <summary>Front: The ratio</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Start at <strong>1:16</strong> — 15 g coffee to 240 g water. Adjust to taste, not to the internet.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary>Front: Step by step</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Rinse the filter with hot water, then dump it.</li>        <li>Add medium-fine grounds, level the bed.</li>        <li>Pour 45 g water, wait 30–45 s for the bloom.</li>        <li>Pour in slow circles to 240 g, total 2:30–3:00.</li>      </ol>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary>Front: Sour or bitter?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p><strong>Sour / thin</strong> = under-extracted: grind finer or pour slower.</p>      <p><strong>Bitter / dry</strong> = over-extracted: grind coarser or use less time.</p>      <div class="callout good"><p>Change one thing at a time.</p></div>    </div>  </details></div>	-
22.6.1	flashcard/Flashcard: Descalеing a kettle with lemon	<div class="card">  <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</span>  <div class="card-title">Flashcard: Descaling a kettle with lemon</div>  <p class="t-small muted">Fresh angle for you, <regex>user_name</regex> — no vinegar smell in your kitchen.</p>  <details class="flip">    <summary><strong>Front</strong> — Why does lemon juice work better than vinegar here?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Both are acids. Lemon is citric acid, weaker and friendlier-smelling, so you can fill the kettle to the max and let it soak without the whole room smelling like chips.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary><strong>Front</strong> — What are the steps?</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Cut 2 lemons, squeeze into the kettle, toss the rinds in too.</li>        <li>Fill with water to the max mark.</li>        <li>Boil, then switch off and let it sit <strong>15–20 min</strong>.</li>        <li>Pour out, rinse twice, and boil one fresh kettle of water to discard.</li>      </ol>      <p class="callout warn">Scale that has caked on for years may need a repeat — two rounds beat one rough scrub.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary><strong>Front</strong> — What should you never do?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Don't scrub the element with anything metal. Scratches are where the next layer of scale grabs hold first.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="card">  <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</span>  <div class="card-title">Flashcard: Descaling a kettle with lemon</div>  <p class="t-small muted">Fresh angle for you,  — no vinegar smell in your kitchen.</p>  <details class="flip">    <summary><strong>Front</strong> — Why does lemon juice work better than vinegar here?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Both are acids. Lemon is citric acid, weaker and friendlier-smelling, so you can fill the kettle to the max and let it soak without the whole room smelling like chips.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary><strong>Front</strong> — What are the steps?</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Cut 2 lemons, squeeze into the kettle, toss the rinds in too.</li>        <li>Fill with water to the max mark.</li>        <li>Boil, then switch off and let it sit <strong>15–20 min</strong>.</li>        <li>Pour out, rinse twice, and boil one fresh kettle of water to discard.</li>      </ol>      <p class="callout warn">Scale that has caked on for years may need a repeat — two rounds beat one rough scrub.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary><strong>Front</strong> — What should you never do?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Don't scrub the element with anything metal. Scratches are where the next layer of scale grabs hold first.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
22.7.1	flashcard/Flashcard: Reading a bus timetable	<div class="card">  <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</span>  <div class="card-title">Flashcard: Reading a bus timetable</div>  <p class="t-small muted">For you, <regex>user_name</regex> — those tiny letters aren't decoration.</p>  <details class="flip">    <summary><strong>Front</strong> — What do letters beside a time mean?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>They're footnotes. The time only applies under the condition the letter names — like <em>runs only on school days</em> or <em>stops here on request</em>. Find the legend at the bottom of the sheet or on the back of the sign.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary><strong>Front</strong> — The 10:15 has a footnote "S". Do I celebrate?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Only if the legend says S = Saturday. Otherwise the column header above it — Monday–Friday, Sat, Sun — is the real authority.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary><strong>Front</strong> — Timetables lie. What's the field check?</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>Scan the printed code on the stop pole and read the live arrival.</li>        <li>Check direction: buses on opposite sides can share a route name.</li>        <li>Arrive 3–5 min early — drivers leave on time, not on you.</li>      </ul>      <p class="callout info">A timetable is a promise. Live data is a rumor with better odds.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="card">  <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</span>  <div class="card-title">Flashcard: Reading a bus timetable</div>  <p class="t-small muted">For you,  — those tiny letters aren't decoration.</p>  <details class="flip">    <summary><strong>Front</strong> — What do letters beside a time mean?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>They're footnotes. The time only applies under the condition the letter names — like <em>runs only on school days</em> or <em>stops here on request</em>. Find the legend at the bottom of the sheet or on the back of the sign.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary><strong>Front</strong> — The 10:15 has a footnote "S". Do I celebrate?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Only if the legend says S = Saturday. Otherwise the column header above it — Monday–Friday, Sat, Sun — is the real authority.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary><strong>Front</strong> — Timetables lie. What's the field check?</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>Scan the printed code on the stop pole and read the live arrival.</li>        <li>Check direction: buses on opposite sides can share a route name.</li>        <li>Arrive 3–5 min early — drivers leave on time, not on you.</li>      </ul>      <p class="callout info">A timetable is a promise. Live data is a rumor with better odds.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
22.8.1	flashcard/Flashcard: Hanging a picture level	<div class="card">  <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</span>  <div class="card-title">Flashcard: Hanging a picture level</div>  <p class="t-small muted">Here's the trick, <regex>user_name</regex> — no spirit level required.</p>  <details class="flip">    <summary><strong>Front</strong> — What's the marker-tape method?</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Run a strip of painter's tape across the wall where the top edge will sit.</li>        <li>Hold the frame against the tape, hover a small bubble level on its top rail, and slide until the bubble centres — mark the tape at both corners.</li>        <li>Measure down from each mark to the hanging point and transfer the same distance onto the wall.</li>        <li>Peel the tape. No pencil ghosts, no second holes.</li>      </ol>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary><strong>Front</strong> — No level at all?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Open a phone level app, or suspend a heavy object from a string to make a plumb line and measure in from it. Gravity has no opinion about your decor; use it anyway.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary><strong>Front</strong> — Why does a wire-hung frame drift crooked?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>The wire slides through the hook. Pinch two small loops of wire either side of the hook, or swap to two fixed hangers — long-term, that frame stops wandering.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="card">  <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</span>  <div class="card-title">Flashcard: Hanging a picture level</div>  <p class="t-small muted">Here's the trick,  — no spirit level required.</p>  <details class="flip">    <summary><strong>Front</strong> — What's the marker-tape method?</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Run a strip of painter's tape across the wall where the top edge will sit.</li>        <li>Hold the frame against the tape, hover a small bubble level on its top rail, and slide until the bubble centres — mark the tape at both corners.</li>        <li>Measure down from each mark to the hanging point and transfer the same distance onto the wall.</li>        <li>Peel the tape. No pencil ghosts, no second holes.</li>      </ol>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary><strong>Front</strong> — No level at all?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Open a phone level app, or suspend a heavy object from a string to make a plumb line and measure in from it. Gravity has no opinion about your decor; use it anyway.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary><strong>Front</strong> — Why does a wire-hung frame drift crooked?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>The wire slides through the hook. Pinch two small loops of wire either side of the hook, or swap to two fixed hangers — long-term, that frame stops wandering.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
22.9.1	flashcard/Flashcard: Cutting an onion without tears	<div class="card">  <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</span>  <div class="card-title">Flashcard: Cutting an onion without tears</div>  <p class="t-small muted">You, <regex>user_name</regex>, versus an onion. You can win.</p>  <details class="flip">    <summary><strong>Front</strong> — What is actually making you cry?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Cut cells release a sulfur compound. It wafts up, hits the water film on your eyes, and turns into a mild acid. Tearing up is your eyes rinsing themselves. Nothing personal.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary><strong>Front</strong> — What actually works?</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li><strong>Chill it.</strong> 10–15 min in the fridge slows the reaction.</li>        <li><strong>Cut the root last.</strong> The root end holds the most of the irritant — leave it intact while you slice.</li>        <li><strong>Fan the vapour away.</strong> Run a low kitchen extractor or a small fan off to one side, not straight at your face.</li>        <li><strong>Work fast and sharp.</strong> A sharp knife crushes fewer cells.</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary><strong>Front</strong> — And the myths?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Bread in your mouth, a candle, a wet paper towel on the board, breathing through your mouth: folklore. Skip them. Chill, root last, ventilate — that's the whole set.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="card">  <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</span>  <div class="card-title">Flashcard: Cutting an onion without tears</div>  <p class="t-small muted">You, , versus an onion. You can win.</p>  <details class="flip">    <summary><strong>Front</strong> — What is actually making you cry?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Cut cells release a sulfur compound. It wafts up, hits the water film on your eyes, and turns into a mild acid. Tearing up is your eyes rinsing themselves. Nothing personal.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary><strong>Front</strong> — What actually works?</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li><strong>Chill it.</strong> 10–15 min in the fridge slows the reaction.</li>        <li><strong>Cut the root last.</strong> The root end holds the most of the irritant — leave it intact while you slice.</li>        <li><strong>Fan the vapour away.</strong> Run a low kitchen extractor or a small fan off to one side, not straight at your face.</li>        <li><strong>Work fast and sharp.</strong> A sharp knife crushes fewer cells.</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary><strong>Front</strong> — And the myths?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Bread in your mouth, a candle, a wet paper towel on the board, breathing through your mouth: folklore. Skip them. Chill, root last, ventilate — that's the whole set.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
22.10.1	flashcard/Flashcard: Punctuating dialogue	<div class="card">  <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</span>  <div class="card-title">Flashcard: Punctuating dialogue</div>  <p class="t-small muted">For your draft, <regex>user_name</regex>. Two rules cover most of it.</p>  <details class="flip">    <summary><strong>Front</strong> — Where does the comma go?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Inside the closing quote mark, always. "I'll be there," she said. In US style, commas and periods live inside the quotation marks even when they logically belong to the outer sentence.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary><strong>Front</strong> — Speech tag or new sentence?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>If the words after the quote are a tag (said, asked, whispered), use a comma and keep the tag lowercase:<br><em>"Wait," he said.</em></p>      <p>If it's a separate action, use a period and capitalise:<br><em>"Wait." He checked his watch.</em></p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary><strong>Front</strong> — The bit writers get wrong most often</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Dialogue interrupted by an action: use em dashes with commas after.<br><em>"I was going to say" — she paused — "that it doesn't matter."</em></p>      <p class="callout info">One speaker per paragraph. A new person talking means a new line, even if their line is two words long.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="card">  <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</span>  <div class="card-title">Flashcard: Punctuating dialogue</div>  <p class="t-small muted">For your draft, . Two rules cover most of it.</p>  <details class="flip">    <summary><strong>Front</strong> — Where does the comma go?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Inside the closing quote mark, always. "I'll be there," she said. In US style, commas and periods live inside the quotation marks even when they logically belong to the outer sentence.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary><strong>Front</strong> — Speech tag or new sentence?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>If the words after the quote are a tag (said, asked, whispered), use a comma and keep the tag lowercase:<br><em>"Wait," he said.</em></p>      <p>If it's a separate action, use a period and capitalise:<br><em>"Wait." He checked his watch.</em></p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary><strong>Front</strong> — The bit writers get wrong most often</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Dialogue interrupted by an action: use em dashes with commas after.<br><em>"I was going to say" — she paused — "that it doesn't matter."</em></p>      <p class="callout info">One speaker per paragraph. A new person talking means a new line, even if their line is two words long.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
22.11.1	flashcard/Flashcard: Reading a screw thread	<div class="card">  <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</span>  <div class="card-title">Flashcard: Reading a screw thread</div>  <p class="t-small muted">Handy if you're holding three anonymous screws, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p>  <details class="flip">    <summary><strong>Front</strong> — What does <code>8 × 1.25 × 40</code> mean?</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li><strong>8</strong> — thread diameter in millimetres (the shaft, not the head).</li>        <li><strong>1.25</strong> — pitch: the gap between thread crests, in mm. Coarser thread, bigger number.</li>        <li><strong>40</strong> — length in mm, measured under the head.</li>      </ul>      <p>Imperial screws work in gauge numbers: <code>#8 × 1"</code>.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary><strong>Front</strong> — How do I check pitch without a gauge?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Press the screw against a matching bolt or nut. If the threads mesh smoothly and rattle-free, they match. Forcing a mismatched pair chews both.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary><strong>Front</strong> — Length: what gets measured?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Flat-head (countersunk) screws are measured <strong>including the head</strong>, because the head sinks flush. Pan-head and hex screws are measured <strong>below the head</strong>.</p>      <p class="callout good">In doubt: take a photo next to a coin and show a hardware shop. They've seen worse.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="card">  <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</span>  <div class="card-title">Flashcard: Reading a screw thread</div>  <p class="t-small muted">Handy if you're holding three anonymous screws, .</p>  <details class="flip">    <summary><strong>Front</strong> — What does <code>8 × 1.25 × 40</code> mean?</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li><strong>8</strong> — thread diameter in millimetres (the shaft, not the head).</li>        <li><strong>1.25</strong> — pitch: the gap between thread crests, in mm. Coarser thread, bigger number.</li>        <li><strong>40</strong> — length in mm, measured under the head.</li>      </ul>      <p>Imperial screws work in gauge numbers: <code>#8 × 1"</code>.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary><strong>Front</strong> — How do I check pitch without a gauge?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Press the screw against a matching bolt or nut. If the threads mesh smoothly and rattle-free, they match. Forcing a mismatched pair chews both.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="flip">    <summary><strong>Front</strong> — Length: what gets measured?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Flat-head (countersunk) screws are measured <strong>including the head</strong>, because the head sinks flush. Pan-head and hex screws are measured <strong>below the head</strong>.</p>      <p class="callout good">In doubt: take a photo next to a coin and show a hardware shop. They've seen worse.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
22.12.1	flashcard/Flashcard: Measuring a Room for Flooring	<details class="flip"><summary class="front">How do I measure a room for flooring?</summary><div class="back"><p>Measure the <strong>length</strong> and <strong>width</strong> at the floor's widest points, then multiply to get square feet.</p><ol class="list"><li>Clear the floor.</li><li>Measure from wall to wall in feet.</li><li>Multiply length × width.</li><li>Add 5–10% for cuts and waste.</li></ol><p class="t-small muted">For odd shapes, split into rectangles and add them up.</p></div></details>	<details class="flip"><summary class="front">How do I measure a room for flooring?</summary><div class="back"><p>Measure the <strong>length</strong> and <strong>width</strong> at the floor's widest points, then multiply to get square feet.</p><ol class="list"><li>Clear the floor.</li><li>Measure from wall to wall in feet.</li><li>Multiply length × width.</li><li>Add 5–10% for cuts and waste.</li></ol><p class="t-small muted">For odd shapes, split into rectangles and add them up.</p></div></details>	-
22.13.1	flashcard/Flashcard: Fixing a Squeaky Door Hinge	<details class="flip"><summary class="front">How do I fix a squeaky door hinge?</summary><div class="back"><p>A squeak usually means dry metal. Lubricate the hinge pins.</p><ol class="list"><li>Tap the hinge pin up with a hammer and nail set.</li><li>Wipe the pin clean.</li><li>Apply a dab of white lithium grease or petroleum jelly.</li><li>Tap the pin back in and swing the door.</li></ol><p class="t-small muted">No grease? A graphite pencil rubbed on the pin works in a pinch.</p></div></details>	<details class="flip"><summary class="front">How do I fix a squeaky door hinge?</summary><div class="back"><p>A squeak usually means dry metal. Lubricate the hinge pins.</p><ol class="list"><li>Tap the hinge pin up with a hammer and nail set.</li><li>Wipe the pin clean.</li><li>Apply a dab of white lithium grease or petroleum jelly.</li><li>Tap the pin back in and swing the door.</li></ol><p class="t-small muted">No grease? A graphite pencil rubbed on the pin works in a pinch.</p></div></details>	-
22.14.1	flashcard/Flashcard: Unclogging a Drain with Baking Soda and Vinegar	<details class="flip"><summary class="front">How do I unclog a drain with baking soda and vinegar?</summary><div class="back"><p>This fizzing combo can break up light clogs.</p><ol class="list"><li>Pour 1/2 cup baking soda down the drain.</li><li>Follow with 1/2 cup white vinegar.</li><li>Cover with a plug and wait 15 minutes.</li><li>Flush with hot water.</li></ol><p class="t-small muted">For tough clogs, repeat or use a plunger.</p></div></details>	<details class="flip"><summary class="front">How do I unclog a drain with baking soda and vinegar?</summary><div class="back"><p>This fizzing combo can break up light clogs.</p><ol class="list"><li>Pour 1/2 cup baking soda down the drain.</li><li>Follow with 1/2 cup white vinegar.</li><li>Cover with a plug and wait 15 minutes.</li><li>Flush with hot water.</li></ol><p class="t-small muted">For tough clogs, repeat or use a plunger.</p></div></details>	-
22.15.1	flashcard/Flashcard: Making a Paper Airplane That Flies Far	<details class="flip"><summary class="front">How do I make a paper airplane that flies far?</summary><div class="back"><p>The classic dart design flies far when folded sharply.</p><ol class="list"><li>Fold a sheet in half lengthwise, then unfold.</li><li>Fold the top corners to the center crease.</li><li>Fold the angled edges to the center again.</li><li>Fold in half along the original crease.</li><li>Fold wings down to meet the bottom edge.</li></ol><p class="t-small muted">Throw with a smooth, level release.</p></div></details>	<details class="flip"><summary class="front">How do I make a paper airplane that flies far?</summary><div class="back"><p>The classic dart design flies far when folded sharply.</p><ol class="list"><li>Fold a sheet in half lengthwise, then unfold.</li><li>Fold the top corners to the center crease.</li><li>Fold the angled edges to the center again.</li><li>Fold in half along the original crease.</li><li>Fold wings down to meet the bottom edge.</li></ol><p class="t-small muted">Throw with a smooth, level release.</p></div></details>	-
22.16.1	flashcard/Flashcard: Changing a Bicycle Tire	<details class="flip"><summary class="front">How do I change a bicycle tire?</summary><div class="back"><p>You'll need tire levers, a new tube, and a pump.</p><ol class="list"><li>Shift to the smallest cog and remove the wheel.</li><li>Use levers to pry the tire off the rim.</li><li>Remove the old tube and check for sharp objects.</li><li>Insert new tube, seating one side of the tire first.</li><li>Push the tire back on, inflate to recommended pressure.</li></ol><p class="t-small muted">Listen for the tube not pinching as you inflate.</p></div></details>	<details class="flip"><summary class="front">How do I change a bicycle tire?</summary><div class="back"><p>You'll need tire levers, a new tube, and a pump.</p><ol class="list"><li>Shift to the smallest cog and remove the wheel.</li><li>Use levers to pry the tire off the rim.</li><li>Remove the old tube and check for sharp objects.</li><li>Insert new tube, seating one side of the tire first.</li><li>Push the tire back on, inflate to recommended pressure.</li></ol><p class="t-small muted">Listen for the tube not pinching as you inflate.</p></div></details>	-
22.17.1	flashcard/Flashcard: Tying a Bowline Knot	<div class="flip">  <details class="acc">    <summary>How do you tie a bowline knot?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>The bowline makes a fixed loop that won't slip or jam — the classic "rescue loop" that holds firm under load and unties easily after.</p>      <ol class="list">        <li>Make a small loop in the standing part (the long, non-working end). The working end should point up through it.</li>        <li>Bring the working end up through that small loop.</li>        <li>Pass it around <em>behind</em> the standing part.</li>        <li>Feed it back down through the small loop.</li>        <li>Pull the standing part and the loop to set it. Tighten.</li>      </ol>      <p class="muted"><b>The rhyme:</b> the rabbit comes out of the hole, around the tree, and back down the hole.</p>      <p class="muted">The loop stays the same size no matter how hard you pull. Good for towing, hoisting, or a quick arm loop on a line.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="flip">  <details class="acc">    <summary>How do you tie a bowline knot?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>The bowline makes a fixed loop that won't slip or jam — the classic "rescue loop" that holds firm under load and unties easily after.</p>      <ol class="list">        <li>Make a small loop in the standing part (the long, non-working end). The working end should point up through it.</li>        <li>Bring the working end up through that small loop.</li>        <li>Pass it around <em>behind</em> the standing part.</li>        <li>Feed it back down through the small loop.</li>        <li>Pull the standing part and the loop to set it. Tighten.</li>      </ol>      <p class="muted"><b>The rhyme:</b> the rabbit comes out of the hole, around the tree, and back down the hole.</p>      <p class="muted">The loop stays the same size no matter how hard you pull. Good for towing, hoisting, or a quick arm loop on a line.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
22.18.1	flashcard/Flashcard: Fixing a Scratched Phone Screen	<div class="flip">  <details class="acc">    <summary>Can you remove scratches from a phone screen?</summary>    <div class="body">      <svg viewBox="0 0 240 70" role="img" aria-label="Diagram: fine scratch in glass with polish">        <rect x="6" y="14" width="228" height="42" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--line-2)" stroke-width="1.5"/>        <line x1="40" y1="30" x2="120" y2="38" stroke="var(--bad)" stroke-width="2"/>        <line x1="60" y1="48" x2="150" y2="44" stroke="var(--bad)" stroke-width="2"/>        <circle cx="180" cy="35" r="12" fill="var(--accent-soft)"/>        <text x="180" y="39" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" fill="var(--accent)">polish</text>        <text x="12" y="64" font-size="9" fill="var(--ink-3)">fine scratches near surface</text>      </svg>      <p class="callout info"><b>First:</b> check if it's a scratch in the glass or just residue. Clean the screen and look again.</p>      <p><b>Light surface scratches</b> (you can barely feel them with a fingernail): a tiny bit of polishing paste — cerium oxide or a screen-safe polish — can soften them. Rub gently with a soft cloth, not your finger.</p>      <p><b>Deep scratches</b> (fingernail catches): no polish will fix these. A screen protector can fill and hide them temporarily.</p>      <p class="muted"><b>Avoid:</b> toothpaste. It's abrasive, gritty, and often leaves a worse haze than the scratch.</p>      <p class="callout warn">If the crack affects touch or spreads, replace the screen or protector instead of polishing.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="flip">  <details class="acc">    <summary>Can you remove scratches from a phone screen?</summary>    <div class="body">      <svg viewBox="0 0 240 70" role="img" aria-label="Diagram: fine scratch in glass with polish">        <rect x="6" y="14" width="228" height="42" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--line-2)" stroke-width="1.5"/>        <line x1="40" y1="30" x2="120" y2="38" stroke="var(--bad)" stroke-width="2"/>        <line x1="60" y1="48" x2="150" y2="44" stroke="var(--bad)" stroke-width="2"/>        <circle cx="180" cy="35" r="12" fill="var(--accent-soft)"/>        <text x="180" y="39" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" fill="var(--accent)">polish</text>        <text x="12" y="64" font-size="9" fill="var(--ink-3)">fine scratches near surface</text>      </svg>      <p class="callout info"><b>First:</b> check if it's a scratch in the glass or just residue. Clean the screen and look again.</p>      <p><b>Light surface scratches</b> (you can barely feel them with a fingernail): a tiny bit of polishing paste — cerium oxide or a screen-safe polish — can soften them. Rub gently with a soft cloth, not your finger.</p>      <p><b>Deep scratches</b> (fingernail catches): no polish will fix these. A screen protector can fill and hide them temporarily.</p>      <p class="muted"><b>Avoid:</b> toothpaste. It's abrasive, gritty, and often leaves a worse haze than the scratch.</p>      <p class="callout warn">If the crack affects touch or spreads, replace the screen or protector instead of polishing.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
22.19.1	flashcard/Flashcard: Reading a Tire Sidewall	<div class="flip">  <details class="acc">    <summary>What do the numbers on a tire sidewall mean?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Take the code <b>205/55 R16 91V</b>. Each part tells you something:</p>      <ul class="list">        <li><b>205</b> — width in millimetres.</li>        <li><b>55</b> — aspect ratio: sidewall height is 55% of the width.</li>        <li><b>R</b> — radial construction (most cars).</li>        <li><b>16</b> — wheel diameter in inches.</li>        <li><b>91</b> — load index. 91 ≈ 615 kg per tire.</li>        <li><b>V</b> — speed rating. V ≈ 240 km/h (149 mph).</li>      </ul>      <p>Also look for <b>DOT</b> and the last four digits — that's the manufacture date: <b>2321</b> means week 23 of 2021.</p>      <p class="muted">Buy the same size and rating as the sticker in your door jamb. Mixing sizes is what makes ABS and speedometers grumpy.</p>      <p class="callout good">Tread depth check: stick a coin in the groove. If you see the whole head, you're near the legal limit (1.6 mm).</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="flip">  <details class="acc">    <summary>What do the numbers on a tire sidewall mean?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Take the code <b>205/55 R16 91V</b>. Each part tells you something:</p>      <ul class="list">        <li><b>205</b> — width in millimetres.</li>        <li><b>55</b> — aspect ratio: sidewall height is 55% of the width.</li>        <li><b>R</b> — radial construction (most cars).</li>        <li><b>16</b> — wheel diameter in inches.</li>        <li><b>91</b> — load index. 91 ≈ 615 kg per tire.</li>        <li><b>V</b> — speed rating. V ≈ 240 km/h (149 mph).</li>      </ul>      <p>Also look for <b>DOT</b> and the last four digits — that's the manufacture date: <b>2321</b> means week 23 of 2021.</p>      <p class="muted">Buy the same size and rating as the sticker in your door jamb. Mixing sizes is what makes ABS and speedometers grumpy.</p>      <p class="callout good">Tread depth check: stick a coin in the groove. If you see the whole head, you're near the legal limit (1.6 mm).</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
22.20.1	flashcard/Flashcard: Choosing a Watermelon	<div class="flip">  <details class="acc">    <summary>How do you pick a ripe watermelon?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Four quick checks, in order of usefulness:</p>      <ul class="list">        <li><b>Field spot.</b> Look for a creamy yellow patch where it sat on the ground. White or green = picked too early.</li>        <li><b>Thump it.</b> Knock with your knuckles. A ripe one sounds hollow and deep — like a drum, not a thud.</li>        <li><b>Weight.</b> It should feel heavy for its size. Heavy = juicy.</li>        <li><b>Shape.</b> Symmetrical and slightly oval. Odd bumps mean uneven watering.</li>      </ul>      <p class="muted"><b>Skip:</b> the stem test. A dried stem just means it's been off the vine a while, not that it's sweet.</p>      <p class="callout good">At the store, pick the one that feels densest in your hand. Your wrist usually knows before your eyes do.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="flip">  <details class="acc">    <summary>How do you pick a ripe watermelon?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Four quick checks, in order of usefulness:</p>      <ul class="list">        <li><b>Field spot.</b> Look for a creamy yellow patch where it sat on the ground. White or green = picked too early.</li>        <li><b>Thump it.</b> Knock with your knuckles. A ripe one sounds hollow and deep — like a drum, not a thud.</li>        <li><b>Weight.</b> It should feel heavy for its size. Heavy = juicy.</li>        <li><b>Shape.</b> Symmetrical and slightly oval. Odd bumps mean uneven watering.</li>      </ul>      <p class="muted"><b>Skip:</b> the stem test. A dried stem just means it's been off the vine a while, not that it's sweet.</p>      <p class="callout good">At the store, pick the one that feels densest in your hand. Your wrist usually knows before your eyes do.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
22.21.1	flashcard/Flashcard: Removing Red Wine from Fabric	<div class="flip">  <details class="acc">    <summary>How do you remove a red wine stain?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p><b>Act in the first minute.</b> Fresh stains come out; set stains are a fight.</p>      <ol class="list">        <li><b>Blot, don't rub.</b> Press with a clean cloth or paper towel to lift the wine. Rubbing pushes it deeper.</li>        <li><b>Dilute.</b> Pour a little cold water through the fabric from the back, or dab with cold water.</li>        <li><b>Apply.</b> A drop of dish soap or a spoon of baking soda mixed with water. Let it sit 5–10 minutes.</li>        <li><b>Rinse and repeat</b> until the water runs clear.</li>      </ol>      <p class="muted"><b>On carpet:</b> blot, then sprinkle baking soda to absorb what's left. Vacuum once dry.</p>      <p class="callout warn"><b>Don't:</b> use hot water or the dryer until the stain is fully gone. Heat sets it permanently.</p>      <p class="muted">Salt works only as an absorbent on a fresh spill — it won't lift a stain on its own.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="flip">  <details class="acc">    <summary>How do you remove a red wine stain?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p><b>Act in the first minute.</b> Fresh stains come out; set stains are a fight.</p>      <ol class="list">        <li><b>Blot, don't rub.</b> Press with a clean cloth or paper towel to lift the wine. Rubbing pushes it deeper.</li>        <li><b>Dilute.</b> Pour a little cold water through the fabric from the back, or dab with cold water.</li>        <li><b>Apply.</b> A drop of dish soap or a spoon of baking soda mixed with water. Let it sit 5–10 minutes.</li>        <li><b>Rinse and repeat</b> until the water runs clear.</li>      </ol>      <p class="muted"><b>On carpet:</b> blot, then sprinkle baking soda to absorb what's left. Vacuum once dry.</p>      <p class="callout warn"><b>Don't:</b> use hot water or the dryer until the stain is fully gone. Heat sets it permanently.</p>      <p class="muted">Salt works only as an absorbent on a fresh spill — it won't lift a stain on its own.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
22.22.1	flashcard/Flashcard: Understanding HTTP Status Codes	<div class="flip">  <details class="acc">    <summary>What do HTTP status codes mean?</summary>    <div class="body">      <svg viewBox="0 0 260 70" role="img" aria-label="Four status code families by colour">        <rect x="6" y="22" width="58" height="26" rx="6" fill="var(--good-soft)" stroke="var(--good)"/>        <rect x="70" y="22" width="58" height="26" rx="6" fill="var(--info-soft)" stroke="var(--info)"/>        <rect x="134" y="22" width="58" height="26" rx="6" fill="var(--warn-soft)" stroke="var(--warn)"/>        <rect x="198" y="22" width="58" height="26" rx="6" fill="var(--bad-soft)" stroke="var(--bad)"/>        <text x="35" y="39" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="var(--ink)">2xx</text>        <text x="99" y="39" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="var(--ink)">3xx</text>        <text x="163" y="39" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="var(--ink)">4xx</text>        <text x="227" y="39" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="var(--ink)">5xx</text>        <text x="6" y="62" font-size="9" fill="var(--ink-3)">ok · redirect · client · server</text>      </svg>      <ul class="list">        <li><b>2xx — success.</b> <code>200 OK</code>, <code>201 Created</code>.</li>        <li><b>3xx — redirect.</b> <code>301</code> permanent, <code>302</code> temporary.</li>        <li><b>4xx — your request.</b> <code>404</code> not found, <code>403</code> forbidden, <code>429</code> too many requests.</li>        <li><b>5xx — their server.</b> <code>500</code> generic error, <code>503</code> unavailable.</li>      </ul>      <p class="muted"><b>Rule of thumb:</b> 4xx = you did something wrong; 5xx = they did. Retrying a 4xx rarely helps.</p>      <p class="callout info"><b>Fun one:</b> <code>418 I'm a teapot</code> was an April Fools' RFC. Servers still return it as an easter egg.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="flip">  <details class="acc">    <summary>What do HTTP status codes mean?</summary>    <div class="body">      <svg viewBox="0 0 260 70" role="img" aria-label="Four status code families by colour">        <rect x="6" y="22" width="58" height="26" rx="6" fill="var(--good-soft)" stroke="var(--good)"/>        <rect x="70" y="22" width="58" height="26" rx="6" fill="var(--info-soft)" stroke="var(--info)"/>        <rect x="134" y="22" width="58" height="26" rx="6" fill="var(--warn-soft)" stroke="var(--warn)"/>        <rect x="198" y="22" width="58" height="26" rx="6" fill="var(--bad-soft)" stroke="var(--bad)"/>        <text x="35" y="39" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="var(--ink)">2xx</text>        <text x="99" y="39" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="var(--ink)">3xx</text>        <text x="163" y="39" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="var(--ink)">4xx</text>        <text x="227" y="39" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="var(--ink)">5xx</text>        <text x="6" y="62" font-size="9" fill="var(--ink-3)">ok · redirect · client · server</text>      </svg>      <ul class="list">        <li><b>2xx — success.</b> <code>200 OK</code>, <code>201 Created</code>.</li>        <li><b>3xx — redirect.</b> <code>301</code> permanent, <code>302</code> temporary.</li>        <li><b>4xx — your request.</b> <code>404</code> not found, <code>403</code> forbidden, <code>429</code> too many requests.</li>        <li><b>5xx — their server.</b> <code>500</code> generic error, <code>503</code> unavailable.</li>      </ul>      <p class="muted"><b>Rule of thumb:</b> 4xx = you did something wrong; 5xx = they did. Retrying a 4xx rarely helps.</p>      <p class="callout info"><b>Fun one:</b> <code>418 I'm a teapot</code> was an April Fools' RFC. Servers still return it as an easter egg.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
22.23.1	flashcard/Flashcard: Reading a Food Label	<div class="flip"><div class="front">How do you read a nutrition facts label?</div><div class="back"><p>Start with the <strong>serving size</strong>—everything else depends on it. Then check <strong>calories per serving</strong>. Next, scan the nutrients to limit: saturated fat, sodium, added sugars. Finally, look for nutrients to get enough of: fiber, protein, vitamins.</p><p class="t-small muted">% Daily Value (DV) tells you how much one serving contributes to a 2,000‑calorie diet. 5% is low, 20% is high.</p></div></div>	<div class="flip"><div class="front">How do you read a nutrition facts label?</div><div class="back"><p>Start with the <strong>serving size</strong>—everything else depends on it. Then check <strong>calories per serving</strong>. Next, scan the nutrients to limit: saturated fat, sodium, added sugars. Finally, look for nutrients to get enough of: fiber, protein, vitamins.</p><p class="t-small muted">% Daily Value (DV) tells you how much one serving contributes to a 2,000‑calorie diet. 5% is low, 20% is high.</p></div></div>	-
22.24.1	flashcard/Flashcard: Sewing On a Button	<div class="flip"><details><summary class="front">How do you sew a button back on?</summary><div class="back"><p>Thread a needle, double the thread, knot the end. Come up through the fabric where the button goes. Place the button on top. Sew through one hole and down through another, repeating 3–5 times per pair of holes. For a shank, place a toothpick under the button while sewing. Wrap thread around the threads under the button, then knot on the underside. <regex>user_name</regex>, you've got this.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="flip"><details><summary class="front">How do you sew a button back on?</summary><div class="back"><p>Thread a needle, double the thread, knot the end. Come up through the fabric where the button goes. Place the button on top. Sew through one hole and down through another, repeating 3–5 times per pair of holes. For a shank, place a toothpick under the button while sewing. Wrap thread around the threads under the button, then knot on the underside. , you've got this.</p></div></details></div>	-
22.25.1	flashcard/Flashcard: Removing Permanent Marker from a Whiteboard	<div class="flip"><details><summary class="front">How do you remove permanent marker from a whiteboard?</summary><div class="back"><p>Trace over the permanent marks with a fresh dry-erase marker. The solvent in the dry-erase ink lifts the permanent ink. Wipe immediately with a soft cloth. For stubborn spots, use a little isopropyl alcohol on a cloth. <regex>user_name</regex>, no scrubbing required.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="flip"><details><summary class="front">How do you remove permanent marker from a whiteboard?</summary><div class="back"><p>Trace over the permanent marks with a fresh dry-erase marker. The solvent in the dry-erase ink lifts the permanent ink. Wipe immediately with a soft cloth. For stubborn spots, use a little isopropyl alcohol on a cloth. , no scrubbing required.</p></div></details></div>	-
22.26.1	flashcard/Flashcard: Folding a Paper Crane	<div class="flip"><details><summary class="front">How do you fold a paper crane?</summary><div class="back"><p>Start with a square. Fold diagonally both ways, then unfold. Fold in half horizontally and vertically, unfold. Collapse into a square base. Fold the top flaps to the center, unfold, then petal fold up. Repeat on the back. Fold the sides to the center. Inside reverse fold the bottom points up to form the head and tail. Fold down the wings. <regex>user_name</regex>, take your time.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="flip"><details><summary class="front">How do you fold a paper crane?</summary><div class="back"><p>Start with a square. Fold diagonally both ways, then unfold. Fold in half horizontally and vertically, unfold. Collapse into a square base. Fold the top flaps to the center, unfold, then petal fold up. Repeat on the back. Fold the sides to the center. Inside reverse fold the bottom points up to form the head and tail. Fold down the wings. , take your time.</p></div></details></div>	-
22.27.1	flashcard/Flashcard: Tying a Necktie (Four-in-Hand)	<div class="flip"><details><summary class="front">How do you tie a necktie with a Four-in-Hand knot?</summary><div class="back"><p>Drape the tie around your neck, seam side down. Cross the wide end over the narrow end. Bring the wide end under and back up through the loop. Wrap the wide end across the front. Pull it up through the loop and down through the knot. Tighten and adjust. <regex>user_name</regex>, looking sharp.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="flip"><details><summary class="front">How do you tie a necktie with a Four-in-Hand knot?</summary><div class="back"><p>Drape the tie around your neck, seam side down. Cross the wide end over the narrow end. Bring the wide end under and back up through the loop. Wrap the wide end across the front. Pull it up through the loop and down through the knot. Tighten and adjust. , looking sharp.</p></div></details></div>	-
22.28.1	flashcard/Flashcard: Boiling an Egg	<div class="flip"><details><summary class="front">How do you boil an egg perfectly?</summary><div class="back"><p>Place eggs in a saucepan and cover with cold water by an inch. Bring to a rolling boil, then turn off heat and cover. Let sit: 4 minutes for soft-boiled, 9–12 for hard-boiled. Transfer to an ice bath for easy peeling. <regex>user_name</regex>, no green yolks.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="flip"><details><summary class="front">How do you boil an egg perfectly?</summary><div class="back"><p>Place eggs in a saucepan and cover with cold water by an inch. Bring to a rolling boil, then turn off heat and cover. Let sit: 4 minutes for soft-boiled, 9–12 for hard-boiled. Transfer to an ice bath for easy peeling. , no green yolks.</p></div></details></div>	-
22.29.1	flashcard/Flashcard: Changing a Flat Tire	<div class="flip"><details><summary class="front">How do you change a flat tire?</summary><div class="back"><p>Pull over safely and turn on hazard lights. Apply parking brake. Loosen lug nuts slightly before jacking. Place jack under the frame and raise until the flat clears the ground. Remove lug nuts, swap the tire, and hand-tighten the nuts. Lower the jack and tighten nuts in a star pattern. <regex>user_name</regex>, you're almost there.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="flip"><details><summary class="front">How do you change a flat tire?</summary><div class="back"><p>Pull over safely and turn on hazard lights. Apply parking brake. Loosen lug nuts slightly before jacking. Place jack under the frame and raise until the flat clears the ground. Remove lug nuts, swap the tire, and hand-tighten the nuts. Lower the jack and tighten nuts in a star pattern. , you're almost there.</p></div></details></div>	-
22.30.1	flashcard/Flashcard: How to Read a Tape Measure	<div class="umi-page"><div class="flip"><div class="front">Reading a tape measure</div><div class="back"><strong>Count from the big mark to your line.</strong> Longest lines are inches, half and quarter lines are shorter, sixteenths shortest.</div></div><div class="card"><div class="card-title">Finding the line</div><svg viewBox="0 0 240 60" width="100%" role="img" aria-label="Tape measure markings"><rect x="8" y="18" width="224" height="24" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line)"/><line x1="20" y1="14" x2="20" y2="46" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/><line x1="55" y1="20" x2="55" y2="46" stroke="currentColor"/><line x1="90" y1="24" x2="90" y2="46" stroke="currentColor"/><line x1="125" y1="28" x2="125" y2="46" stroke="currentColor"/><text x="20" y="12" font-size="9" class="lbl" text-anchor="middle" fill="currentColor">1"</text></svg><ul class="list"><li>Numbered line = whole inch.</li><li>Halfway line = half inch.</li><li>Next shorter = quarter, then eighth, then sixteenth.</li><li>Always add the whole inch to the fraction.</li></ul><p class="muted t-small">Hook the metal end on the edge or a mark, then read the distance to your pencil.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="flip"><div class="front">Reading a tape measure</div><div class="back"><strong>Count from the big mark to your line.</strong> Longest lines are inches, half and quarter lines are shorter, sixteenths shortest.</div></div><div class="card"><div class="card-title">Finding the line</div><svg viewBox="0 0 240 60" width="100%" role="img" aria-label="Tape measure markings"><rect x="8" y="18" width="224" height="24" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--line)"/><line x1="20" y1="14" x2="20" y2="46" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/><line x1="55" y1="20" x2="55" y2="46" stroke="currentColor"/><line x1="90" y1="24" x2="90" y2="46" stroke="currentColor"/><line x1="125" y1="28" x2="125" y2="46" stroke="currentColor"/><text x="20" y="12" font-size="9" class="lbl" text-anchor="middle" fill="currentColor">1"</text></svg><ul class="list"><li>Numbered line = whole inch.</li><li>Halfway line = half inch.</li><li>Next shorter = quarter, then eighth, then sixteenth.</li><li>Always add the whole inch to the fraction.</li></ul><p class="muted t-small">Hook the metal end on the edge or a mark, then read the distance to your pencil.</p></div></div>	-
22.31.1	flashcard/Flashcard: How to Use a Fire Extinguisher	<div class="umi-page">  <div class="stack">    <article class="msg umi">      <span class="who">Umi</span>      <p>Here's a flashcard on using a fire extinguisher. Stay safe, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p>    </article>    <div class="card">      <div class="card-title">How to Use a Fire Extinguisher</div>      <details class="flip">        <summary>What does the PASS technique stand for?</summary>        <div class="back">          <p><strong>P</strong>ull the pin. <strong>A</strong>im at the base of the fire. <strong>S</strong>queeze the handle. <strong>S</strong>weep side to side.</p>          <p>Remember: only fight small, contained fires. Always keep your back to an exit.</p>        </div>      </details>      <details class="flip">        <summary>When should you NOT use a fire extinguisher?</summary>        <div class="back">          <p>If the fire is larger than a wastebasket, spreading quickly, or producing thick smoke—evacuate and call emergency services.</p>        </div>      </details>    </div>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <div class="stack">    <article class="msg umi">      <span class="who">Umi</span>      <p>Here's a flashcard on using a fire extinguisher. Stay safe, .</p>    </article>    <div class="card">      <div class="card-title">How to Use a Fire Extinguisher</div>      <details class="flip">        <summary>What does the PASS technique stand for?</summary>        <div class="back">          <p><strong>P</strong>ull the pin. <strong>A</strong>im at the base of the fire. <strong>S</strong>queeze the handle. <strong>S</strong>weep side to side.</p>          <p>Remember: only fight small, contained fires. Always keep your back to an exit.</p>        </div>      </details>      <details class="flip">        <summary>When should you NOT use a fire extinguisher?</summary>        <div class="back">          <p>If the fire is larger than a wastebasket, spreading quickly, or producing thick smoke—evacuate and call emergency services.</p>        </div>      </details>    </div>  </div></div>	-
22.32.1	flashcard/Flashcard: Staying Safe in a Crowd	<div class="umi-page">  <div class="stack">    <article class="msg umi">      <span class="who">Umi</span>      <p>Here's a flashcard on staying safe in crowds. Keep it in mind, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p>    </article>    <div class="card">      <div class="card-title">Staying Safe in a Crowd</div>      <details class="flip">        <summary>What should you do if you feel the crowd surging?</summary>        <div class="back">          <p>Move diagonally to the edge, not against the flow. Keep your arms in front like a boxer to protect your chest. Stay on your feet.</p>        </div>      </details>      <details class="flip">        <summary>If you fall down in a crowd, what’s the safest position?</summary>        <div class="back">          <p>Roll into a fetal position, cover your head with your arms, and try to get up as soon as possible. Shout for help.</p>        </div>      </details>    </div>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <div class="stack">    <article class="msg umi">      <span class="who">Umi</span>      <p>Here's a flashcard on staying safe in crowds. Keep it in mind, .</p>    </article>    <div class="card">      <div class="card-title">Staying Safe in a Crowd</div>      <details class="flip">        <summary>What should you do if you feel the crowd surging?</summary>        <div class="back">          <p>Move diagonally to the edge, not against the flow. Keep your arms in front like a boxer to protect your chest. Stay on your feet.</p>        </div>      </details>      <details class="flip">        <summary>If you fall down in a crowd, what’s the safest position?</summary>        <div class="back">          <p>Roll into a fetal position, cover your head with your arms, and try to get up as soon as possible. Shout for help.</p>        </div>      </details>    </div>  </div></div>	-
22.33.1	flashcard/Flashcard: Performing CPR	<div class="umi-page">  <div class="stack">    <article class="msg umi">      <span class="who">Umi</span>      <p>Here's a flashcard on CPR. It could save a life, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p>    </article>    <div class="card">      <div class="card-title">Performing CPR</div>      <details class="flip">        <summary>What are the steps for adult CPR?</summary>        <div class="back">          <p>1. Check responsiveness and breathing. 2. Call emergency services. 3. Place heel of hand on center of chest, other hand on top. 4. Push hard and fast at least 2 inches deep, 100–120 compressions per minute. 5. Continue until help arrives.</p>        </div>      </details>      <details class="flip">        <summary>How deep should compressions be for an adult?</summary>        <div class="back">          <p>At least 2 inches (5 cm), but not more than 2.4 inches (6 cm). Allow full recoil between compressions.</p>        </div>      </details>    </div>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <div class="stack">    <article class="msg umi">      <span class="who">Umi</span>      <p>Here's a flashcard on CPR. It could save a life, .</p>    </article>    <div class="card">      <div class="card-title">Performing CPR</div>      <details class="flip">        <summary>What are the steps for adult CPR?</summary>        <div class="back">          <p>1. Check responsiveness and breathing. 2. Call emergency services. 3. Place heel of hand on center of chest, other hand on top. 4. Push hard and fast at least 2 inches deep, 100–120 compressions per minute. 5. Continue until help arrives.</p>        </div>      </details>      <details class="flip">        <summary>How deep should compressions be for an adult?</summary>        <div class="back">          <p>At least 2 inches (5 cm), but not more than 2.4 inches (6 cm). Allow full recoil between compressions.</p>        </div>      </details>    </div>  </div></div>	-
22.34.1	flashcard/Flashcard: Using a Defibrillator (AED)	<div class="umi-page">  <div class="stack">    <article class="msg umi">      <span class="who">Umi</span>      <p>Here's a flashcard on using an AED. It talks you through it, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p>    </article>    <div class="card">      <div class="card-title">Using a Defibrillator (AED)</div>      <details class="flip">        <summary>What should you do first when using an AED?</summary>        <div class="back">          <p>Turn it on and follow the voice prompts. Expose the chest, dry it if wet, and attach the pads as shown (upper right chest, lower left side).</p>        </div>      </details>      <details class="flip">        <summary>Can you touch the person during defibrillation?</summary>        <div class="back">          <p>No. Ensure no one is touching the person when the shock is delivered. Then immediately resume CPR.</p>        </div>      </details>    </div>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <div class="stack">    <article class="msg umi">      <span class="who">Umi</span>      <p>Here's a flashcard on using an AED. It talks you through it, .</p>    </article>    <div class="card">      <div class="card-title">Using a Defibrillator (AED)</div>      <details class="flip">        <summary>What should you do first when using an AED?</summary>        <div class="back">          <p>Turn it on and follow the voice prompts. Expose the chest, dry it if wet, and attach the pads as shown (upper right chest, lower left side).</p>        </div>      </details>      <details class="flip">        <summary>Can you touch the person during defibrillation?</summary>        <div class="back">          <p>No. Ensure no one is touching the person when the shock is delivered. Then immediately resume CPR.</p>        </div>      </details>    </div>  </div></div>	-
22.35.1	flashcard/Flashcard: What to Do in a House Fire	<div class="umi-page">  <div class="stack">    <article class="msg umi">      <span class="who">Umi</span>      <p>Here's a flashcard on house fire safety. Plan ahead, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p>    </article>    <div class="card">      <div class="card-title">What to Do in a House Fire</div>      <details class="flip">        <summary>If you wake up to a fire, what’s the first thing to do?</summary>        <div class="back">          <p>Get out immediately. Stay low to avoid smoke, check doors with the back of your hand before opening, and once out, stay out.</p>        </div>      </details>      <details class="flip">        <summary>How can you prepare for a house fire?</summary>        <div class="back">          <p>Install smoke alarms on every level, test monthly, practice an escape plan twice a year, and designate a meeting spot outside.</p>        </div>      </details>    </div>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <div class="stack">    <article class="msg umi">      <span class="who">Umi</span>      <p>Here's a flashcard on house fire safety. Plan ahead, .</p>    </article>    <div class="card">      <div class="card-title">What to Do in a House Fire</div>      <details class="flip">        <summary>If you wake up to a fire, what’s the first thing to do?</summary>        <div class="back">          <p>Get out immediately. Stay low to avoid smoke, check doors with the back of your hand before opening, and once out, stay out.</p>        </div>      </details>      <details class="flip">        <summary>How can you prepare for a house fire?</summary>        <div class="back">          <p>Install smoke alarms on every level, test monthly, practice an escape plan twice a year, and designate a meeting spot outside.</p>        </div>      </details>    </div>  </div></div>	-
22.36.1	flashcard/Flashcard: Treating a Burn	<div class="umi-page">  <div class="stack">    <article class="msg umi">      <span class="who">Umi</span>      <p>Here's a flashcard on treating burns. Quick action helps, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p>    </article>    <div class="card">      <div class="card-title">Treating a Burn</div>      <details class="flip">        <summary>What should you do immediately for a minor burn?</summary>        <div class="back">          <p>Cool the burn under cool (not cold) running water for 10–20 minutes. Remove rings or tight items before swelling. Cover loosely with sterile gauze.</p>        </div>      </details>      <details class="flip">        <summary>What should you avoid putting on a burn?</summary>        <div class="back">          <p>Don’t use ice, butter, ointments, or fluffy cotton. These can worsen the injury or cause infection.</p>        </div>      </details>    </div>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <div class="stack">    <article class="msg umi">      <span class="who">Umi</span>      <p>Here's a flashcard on treating burns. Quick action helps, .</p>    </article>    <div class="card">      <div class="card-title">Treating a Burn</div>      <details class="flip">        <summary>What should you do immediately for a minor burn?</summary>        <div class="back">          <p>Cool the burn under cool (not cold) running water for 10–20 minutes. Remove rings or tight items before swelling. Cover loosely with sterile gauze.</p>        </div>      </details>      <details class="flip">        <summary>What should you avoid putting on a burn?</summary>        <div class="back">          <p>Don’t use ice, butter, ointments, or fluffy cotton. These can worsen the injury or cause infection.</p>        </div>      </details>    </div>  </div></div>	-
23.0.0	quiz_question/Kitchen knife skills quiz	<div class="quiz"><div class="q"><regex>user_name</regex>, what's the safest way to hold a knife when walking across the kitchen?</div><details><summary>A) Point it outward like a sword</summary></details><details><summary>B) Blade down, close to your side</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Blade down, close to your leg, no dramatic flourishes.</div></details><details><summary>C) Hold it by the blade</summary></details><details><summary>D) Toss it to the counter</summary></details></div>	<div class="quiz"><div class="q">, what's the safest way to hold a knife when walking across the kitchen?</div><details><summary>A) Point it outward like a sword</summary></details><details><summary>B) Blade down, close to your side</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Blade down, close to your leg, no dramatic flourishes.</div></details><details><summary>C) Hold it by the blade</summary></details><details><summary>D) Toss it to the counter</summary></details></div>	-
23.1.0	quiz_question/Weather basics quiz	<div class="quiz"><div class="q"><regex>user_name</regex>, what causes thunder?</div><details><summary>A) Clouds bumping together</summary></details><details><summary>B) Rapid expansion of air heated by lightning</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Lightning heats the air, it expands fast, and that shockwave is thunder.</div></details><details><summary>C) The sun reflecting off rain</summary></details><details><summary>D) Wind whistling through trees</summary></details></div>	<div class="quiz"><div class="q">, what causes thunder?</div><details><summary>A) Clouds bumping together</summary></details><details><summary>B) Rapid expansion of air heated by lightning</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Lightning heats the air, it expands fast, and that shockwave is thunder.</div></details><details><summary>C) The sun reflecting off rain</summary></details><details><summary>D) Wind whistling through trees</summary></details></div>	-
