23.10.0	quiz_question/Houseplant care quiz	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Houseplant care quiz</div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">How do you know when to water most houseplants?</div><details><summary>A) Every Sunday</summary></details><details><summary>B) When the top inch of soil is dry</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Stick a finger in. Most plants hate soggy roots more than they hate a dry spell.</div></details><details><summary>C) When leaves droop</summary></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">A plant leaning toward the window means…</div><details><summary>A) It's dying</summary></details><details><summary>B) It needs more light</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Rotate it a quarter turn each week so it grows evenly.</div></details><details><summary>C) It's thirsty</summary></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">Yellow leaves on a pothos usually signal…</div><details><summary>A) Too much water</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Overwatering is the usual suspect. Let the soil dry out between drinks.</div></details><details><summary>B) Too little water</summary></details><details><summary>C) Old age</summary></details></div><p class="t-small muted"><regex>user_name</regex>, plants are forgiving once you learn their rhythm. Less fuss, more watching.</p></div>	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Houseplant care quiz</div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">How do you know when to water most houseplants?</div><details><summary>A) Every Sunday</summary></details><details><summary>B) When the top inch of soil is dry</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Stick a finger in. Most plants hate soggy roots more than they hate a dry spell.</div></details><details><summary>C) When leaves droop</summary></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">A plant leaning toward the window means…</div><details><summary>A) It's dying</summary></details><details><summary>B) It needs more light</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Rotate it a quarter turn each week so it grows evenly.</div></details><details><summary>C) It's thirsty</summary></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">Yellow leaves on a pothos usually signal…</div><details><summary>A) Too much water</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Overwatering is the usual suspect. Let the soil dry out between drinks.</div></details><details><summary>B) Too little water</summary></details><details><summary>C) Old age</summary></details></div><p class="t-small muted">, plants are forgiving once you learn their rhythm. Less fuss, more watching.</p></div>	-
23.11.0	quiz_question/Internet safety quiz	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Internet safety quiz</div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">An email says your account is locked and asks you to click a link. What do you do?</div><details><summary>A) Click and log in quickly</summary></details><details><summary>B) Ignore the link and go to the site directly</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Type the address yourself or use a bookmark. Legit companies don't mind.</div></details><details><summary>C) Reply asking if it's real</summary></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">The strongest password is…</div><details><summary>A) Your pet's name plus 1</summary></details><details><summary>B) A long passphrase like "blue-canoe-sings-loud"</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Length beats complexity. Even better: use a password manager and unique passwords everywhere.</div></details><details><summary>C) Your birthday</summary></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">What does HTTPS in the address bar mean?</div><details><summary>A) The site is trustworthy</summary></details><details><summary>B) The connection is encrypted</summary><div class="answer">Correct. It encrypts your traffic, but a scam site can still have HTTPS. Check the domain, not just the lock.</div></details><details><summary>C) The site is government-approved</summary></details></div><p class="t-small muted"><regex>user_name</regex>, a little suspicion goes a long way online.</p></div>	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Internet safety quiz</div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">An email says your account is locked and asks you to click a link. What do you do?</div><details><summary>A) Click and log in quickly</summary></details><details><summary>B) Ignore the link and go to the site directly</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Type the address yourself or use a bookmark. Legit companies don't mind.</div></details><details><summary>C) Reply asking if it's real</summary></detail