23.13.0	quiz_question/Photography exposure quiz	<div class="card flat"><div class="card-title">Photography exposure quiz</div><p class="muted">Three quick checks on the exposure triangle.</p><div class="quiz"><div class="q">1. What does a lower f-stop number do?</div><details><summary>A) Lets in more light and blurs the background</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Lower f-stop, wider aperture, more light, shallower depth of field.</div></details><details><summary>B) Lets in less light</summary></details><details><summary>C) Freezes motion</summary></details></div><div class="divider"></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">2. Which setting freezes fast action?</div><details><summary>A) A slow shutter speed</summary></details><details><summary>B) A fast shutter speed</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Fast shutter stops motion.</div></details><details><summary>C) A low ISO</summary></details></div><div class="divider"></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">3. When would you raise ISO?</div><details><summary>A) In bright sunlight</summary></details><details><summary>B) In low light when you can't use a slower shutter</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Raising ISO brightens the image but adds noise.</div></details><details><summary>C) To blur the background</summary></details></div></div>	<div class="card flat"><div class="card-title">Photography exposure quiz</div><p class="muted">Three quick checks on the exposure triangle.</p><div class="quiz"><div class="q">1. What does a lower f-stop number do?</div><details><summary>A) Lets in more light and blurs the background</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Lower f-stop, wider aperture, more light, shallower depth of field.</div></details><details><summary>B) Lets in less light</summary></details><details><summary>C) Freezes motion</summary></details></div><div class="divider"></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">2. Which setting freezes fast action?</div><details><summary>A) A slow shutter speed</summary></details><details><summary>B) A fast shutter speed</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Fast shutter stops motion.</div></details><details><summary>C) A low ISO</summary></details></div><div class="divider"></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">3. When would you raise ISO?</div><details><summary>A) In bright sunlight</summary></details><details><summary>B) In low light when you can't use a slower shutter</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Raising ISO brightens the image but adds noise.</div></details><details><summary>C) To blur the background</summary></details></div></div>	-
23.14.0	quiz_question/Public speaking quiz	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Public speaking quiz</div><p class="muted">See if your talk is ready for the room.</p><div class="quiz"><div class="q">1. What's the best way to start a speech?</div><details><summary>A) A long introduction of yourself</summary></details><details><summary>B) A clear hook that connects with the audience</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Grab attention early.</div></details><details><summary>C) An apology for being nervous</summary></details></div><div class="divider"></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">2. How should you handle a mistake while speaking?</div><details><summary>A) Stop and apologize</summary></details><details><summary>B) Keep going smoothly</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Most people won't notice.</div></details><details><summary>C) Start over</summary></details></div><div class="divider"></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">3. What keeps an audience engaged?</div><details><summary>A) Eye contact and vocal variety</summary><div class="answer">Correct. It's about connection, not perfection.</div></details><details><summary>B) Reading directly from notes</summary></details><details><summary>C) Speaking very fast</summary></details></div></div>	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Public speaking quiz</div><p class="muted">See if your talk is ready for the room.</p><div class="quiz"><div class="q">1. What's the best way to start a speech?</div><details><summary>A) A long introduction of yourself</summary></details><details><summary>B) A clear hook that connects with the audience</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Grab attention early.</div></details><details><summary>C) An apology for being nervous</summary></details></div><div class="divider"></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">2. How should you handle a mistake while speaking?</div><details><summary>A) Stop and apologize</summary></details><details><summary>B) Keep going smoothly</summary><div 