23.45.0	quiz_question/Basic pasta cooking quiz	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Pasta cooking quiz for <regex>user_name</regex></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">How much salt should you add to pasta water?</div><details><summary>A) None</summary><div class="answer">Unsalted water makes bland pasta. Salt is your friend.</div></details><details><summary>B) A generous handful</summary><div class="answer">Correct. It should taste like seasoned soup. That's the only chance to flavor the pasta itself.</div></details><details><summary>C) A pinch</summary><div class="answer">A pinch is too little for a big pot. Be bold.</div></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">Should you add oil to the pasta water?</div><details><summary>A) Yes, to prevent sticking</summary><div class="answer">Oil just floats and makes the pasta slippery, so sauce won't cling.</div></details><summary></summary><details><summary>B) No</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Stir the pasta for the first minute; that's all you need.</div></details><details><summary>C) Only for long pasta</summary><div class="answer">Still no. Stirring is the real fix.</div></details></div><p class="muted">Now go make a perfect bowl.</p></div>	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Pasta cooking quiz for </div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">How much salt should you add to pasta water?</div><details><summary>A) None</summary><div class="answer">Unsalted water makes bland pasta. Salt is your friend.</div></details><details><summary>B) A generous handful</summary><div class="answer">Correct. It should taste like seasoned soup. That's the only chance to flavor the pasta itself.</div></details><details><summary>C) A pinch</summary><div class="answer">A pinch is too little for a big pot. Be bold.</div></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">Should you add oil to the pasta water?</div><details><summary>A) Yes, to prevent sticking</summary><div class="answer">Oil just floats and makes the pasta slippery, so sauce won't cling.</div></details><summary></summary><details><summary>B) No</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Stir the pasta for the first minute; that's all you need.</div></details><details><summary>C) Only for long pasta</summary><div class="answer">Still no. Stirring is the real fix.</div></details></div><p class="muted">Now go make a perfect bowl.</p></div>	-
23.46.0	quiz_question/Basic bike brake check quiz	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Bike brake check quiz for <regex>user_name</regex></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">How much gap should there be between brake pads and the rim?</div><details><summary>A) 1–2 mm</summary><div class="answer">Correct. That's about the thickness of a nickel. Enough to spin freely but close enough to grab.</div></details><details><summary>B) 5 mm</summary><div class="answer">Too wide. You'll have to pull the lever too far before braking.</div></details><details><summary>C) No gap, pads touching</summary><div class="answer">That creates drag and wears pads fast. You need a small gap.</div></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">What should you check on brake cables?</div><details><summary>A) For fraying and tension</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Frayed cables can snap; loose ones mean weak braking.</div></details><details><summary>B) Only the color</summary><div class="answer">Color won't stop you. Check for wear and proper tension.</div></details><details><summary>C) Nothing, they're maintenance-free</summary><div class="answer">No cable is truly maintenance-free. A quick squeeze tells you a lot.</div></details></div><p class="muted">Safe brakes make every ride better.</p></div>	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Bike brake check quiz for </div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">How much gap should there be between brake pads and the rim?</div><details><summary>A) 1–2 mm</summary><div class="answer">Correct. That's about the thickness of a nickel. Enough to spin freely but close enough to grab.</div></details><details><summary>B) 5 mm</summary><div class="answer">Too wide. You'll have to pull the lever too far before braking.</div></details><details><summary>C) No gap, pads touching</summary><div class="answer">That creates drag and wears pads fast. You need a small gap.</div></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">What should you check on brake cables?</div><details><summary>A) For fraying and tension</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Frayed cables can snap; loose ones mean weak braking.</div></details><details><summary>B) Only the color</summary><div class="answer">Color won't stop you. Check for wear and proper tension.</div></details><details><summary>C) Nothing, they're maintenance-free</summary><div class="answer">No cable is truly maintenance-free. A quick squeeze tells you a lot.</div></details></div><p class="muted">Safe brakes make every ride better.</p></div>	-
23.47.0	quiz_question/Basic knot tying quiz	<div class="quiz umi-page"><div class="q">Which knot is best for securing a line to a post and is easy to untie?</div><details><summary>A) Figure-eight knot</summary></details><details><summary>B) Clove hitch</summary><div class="answer">Yep. A clove hitch grips a post or rail, and it comes apart quickly when you're done. Just don't trust it for heavy loads that shift.</div></details><details><summary>C) Bowline</summary></details><details><summary>D) Square knot</summary></details></div>	<div class="quiz umi-page"><div class="q">Which knot is best for securing a line to a post and is easy to untie?</div><details><summary>A) Figure-eight knot</summary></details><details><summary>B) Clove hitch</summary><div class="answer">Yep. A clove hitch grips a post or rail, and it comes apart quickly when you're done. Just don't trust it for heavy loads that shift.</div></details><details><summary>C) Bowline</summary></details><details><summary>D) Square knot</summary></details></div>	-
