23.55.0	quiz_question/Basic food safety quiz	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><div class="card-title">Food safety basics</div><p class="muted">Let's check your kitchen instincts, <regex>user_name</regex>. Tap to reveal.</p><div class="quiz"><div class="q">How long can cooked leftovers sit out at room temperature?</div><details><summary>A) 2 hours</summary><div class="answer">Correct. The "danger zone" is 40–140°F (4–60°C), and bacteria multiply fast. Two hours is the max — one hour if it's above 90°F.</div></details><details><summary>B) 4 hours</summary></details><details><summary>C) Until they smell bad</summary></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">What's the best way to thaw frozen meat?</div><details><summary>A) On the counter</summary></details><details><summary>B) In the fridge</summary><div class="answer">Right. Fridge thawing is slow but safe. For speed, use cold water or the microwave — then cook immediately.</div></details><details><summary>C) In hot water</summary></details></div><div class="callout good">Handy rule: keep hot food hot (above 140°F) and cold food cold (below 40°F).</div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><div class="card-title">Food safety basics</div><p class="muted">Let's check your kitchen instincts, . Tap to reveal.</p><div class="quiz"><div class="q">How long can cooked leftovers sit out at room temperature?</div><details><summary>A) 2 hours</summary><div class="answer">Correct. The "danger zone" is 40–140°F (4–60°C), and bacteria multiply fast. Two hours is the max — one hour if it's above 90°F.</div></details><details><summary>B) 4 hours</summary></details><details><summary>C) Until they smell bad</summary></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">What's the best way to thaw frozen meat?</div><details><summary>A) On the counter</summary></details><details><summary>B) In the fridge</summary><div class="answer">Right. Fridge thawing is slow but safe. For speed, use cold water or the microwave — then cook immediately.</div></details><details><summary>C) In hot water</summary></details></div><div class="callout good">Handy rule: keep hot food hot (above 140°F) and cold food cold (below 40°F).</div></div></div>	-
