23.0.1	quiz_question/Coffee roasting basics quiz	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Coffee roasting basics</div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">1. What happens during first crack?</div><details><summary>A) Beans turn green</summary></details><details><summary>B) Moisture escapes and beans expand</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Steam pressure builds and the bean structure fractures, making a popping sound.</div></details><details><summary>C) Oils appear on the surface</summary></details><details><summary>D) Caffeine is removed</summary></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">2. Which roast level is typically lightest?</div><details><summary>A) City</summary><div class="answer">Correct. City roast is stopped right after first crack, before second crack.</div></details><details><summary>B) Full City</summary></details><details><summary>C) Vienna</summary></details><details><summary>D) French</summary></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">3. What is a common sign of a dark roast?</div><details><summary>A) Dry, matte surface</summary></details><details><summary>B) Oily, shiny surface</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Oils migrate to the surface as roasting continues.</div></details><details><summary>C) Green color</summary></details><details><summary>D) No aroma</summary></details></div><p class="muted t-small">Roast levels are a spectrum. Taste as you go, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></div>	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Coffee roasting basics</div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">1. What happens during first crack?</div><details><summary>A) Beans turn green</summary></details><details><summary>B) Moisture escapes and beans expand</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Steam pressure builds and the bean structure fractures, making a popping sound.</div></details><details><summary>C) Oils appear on the surface</summary></details><details><summary>D) Caffeine is removed</summary></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">2. Which roast level is typically lightest?</div><details><summary>A) City</summary><div class="answer">Correct. City roast is stopped right after first crack, before second crack.</div></details><details><summary>B) Full City</summary></details><details><summary>C) Vienna</summary></details><details><summary>D) French</summary></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">3. What is a common sign of a dark roast?</div><details><summary>A) Dry, matte surface</summary></details><details><summary>B) Oily, shiny surface</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Oils migrate to the surface as roasting continues.</div></details><details><summary>C) Green color</summary></details><details><summary>D) No aroma</summary></details></div><p class="muted t-small">Roast levels are a spectrum. Taste as you go, .</p></div>	-
23.1.1	quiz_question/Bread baking basics quiz	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Bread baking basics</div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">1. What does kneading develop?</div><details><summary>A) Flavor only</summary></details><details><summary>B) Gluten structure</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Kneading aligns gluten proteins for elasticity.</div></details><details><summary>C) Color</summary></details><details><summary>D) Yeast count</summary></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">2. What is a poolish?</div><details><summary>A) A type of flour</summary></details><details><summary>B) A pre-ferment</summary><div class="answer">Correct. A wet pre-ferment that adds flavor and extensibility.</div></details><details><summary>C) A baking stone</summary></details><details><summary>D) A scoring tool</summary></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">3. Why score the top of a loaf?</div><details><summary>A) To make it pretty</summary></details><details><summary>B) To control expansion</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Scoring gives the dough a place to open during oven spring.</div></details><details><summary>C) To add seeds</summary></details><details><summary>D) To cool faster</summary></details></div><p class="muted t-small">Baking is forgiving, <regex>user_name</regex>. Keep notes.</p></div>	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Bread baking basics</div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">1. What does kneading develop?</div><details><summary>A) Flavor only</summary></details><details><summary>B) Gluten structure</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Kneading aligns gluten proteins for elasticity.</div></details><details><summary>C) Color</summary></details><details><summary>D) Yeast count</summary></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">2. What is a poolish?</div><details><summary>A) A type of flour</summary></details><details><summary>B) A pre-ferment</summary><div class="answer">Correct. A wet pre-ferment that adds flavor and extensibility.</div></details><details><summary>C) A baking stone</summary></details><details><summary>D) A scoring tool</summary></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">3. Why score the top of a loaf?</div><details><summary>A) To make it pretty</summary></details><details><summary>B) To control expansion</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Scoring gives the dough a place to open during oven spring.</div></details><details><summary>C) To add seeds</summary></details><details><summary>D) To cool faster</summary></details></div><p class="muted t-small">Baking is forgiving, . Keep notes.</p></div>	-
23.2.1	quiz_question/Camera exposure quiz	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Camera exposure</div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">1. What does a lower f-number mean?</div><details><summary>A) Smaller aperture</summary></details><details><summary>B) Wider aperture</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Smaller f-number = larger opening = more light.</div></details><details><summary>C) Faster shutter</summary></details><details><summary>D) Lower ISO</summary></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">2. What does ISO control?</div><details><summary>A) Focus distance</summary></details><details><summary>B) Sensitivity to light</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Higher ISO brightens but adds noise.</div></details><details><summary>C) Color temperature</summary></details><details><summary>D) Depth of field</summary></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">3. Which shutter speed freezes motion best?</div><details><summary>A) 1/30s</summary></details><details><summary>B) 1/1000s</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Faster shutter stops action.</div></details><details><summary>C) 1/4s</summary></details><details><summary>D) 1s</summary></details></div><p class="muted t-small">Exposure is a balance, <regex>user_name</regex>. Adjust one, compensate another.</p></div>	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Camera exposure</div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">1. What does a lower f-number mean?</div><details><summary>A) Smaller aperture</summary></details><details><summary>B) Wider aperture</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Smaller f-number = larger opening = more light.</div></details><details><summary>C) Faster shutter</summary></details><details><summary>D) Lower ISO</summary></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">2. What does ISO control?</div><details><summary>A) Focus distance</summary></details><details><summary>B) Sensitivity to light</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Higher ISO brightens but adds noise.</div></details><details><summary>C) Color temperature</summary></details><details><summary>D) Depth of field</summary></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">3. Which shutter speed freezes motion best?</div><details><summary>A) 1/30s</summary></details><details><summary>B) 1/1000s</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Faster shutter stops action.</div></details><details><summary>C) 1/4s</summary></details><details><summary>D) 1s</summary></details></div><p class="muted t-small">Exposure is a balance, . Adjust one, compensate another.</p></div>	-
23.3.1	quiz_question/Essential knots quiz	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Essential knots</div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">1. Which knot is best for tying two ropes together?</div><details><summary>A) Bowline</summary></details><details><summary>B) Sheet bend</summary><div class="answer">Correct. The sheet bend joins two ropes securely.</div></details><details><summary>C) Clove hitch</summary></details><details><summary>D) Figure eight</summary></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">2. What is the bowline used for?</div><details><summary>A) A fixed loop</summary><div class="answer">Correct. It creates a loop that won't slip under load.</div></details><details><summary>B) Joining two ropes</summary></details><details><summary>C) Shortening a rope</summary></details><details><summary>D) Decorating</summary></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">3. Which knot tightens under load but is easy to untie?</div><details><summary>A) Clove hitch</summary><div class="answer">Correct. It grips and releases easily.</div></details><details><summary>B) Reef knot</summary></details><details><summary>C) Overhand</summary></details><details><summary>D) Alpine butterfly</summary></details></div><p class="muted t-small">Practice a few, <regex>user_name</regex>. Your hands will remember.</p></div>	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Essential knots</div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">1. Which knot is best for tying two ropes together?</div><details><summary>A) Bowline</summary></details><details><summary>B) Sheet bend</summary><div class="answer">Correct. The sheet bend joins two ropes securely.</div></details><details><summary>C) Clove hitch</summary></details><details><summary>D) Figure eight</summary></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">2. What is the bowline used for?</div><details><summary>A) A fixed loop</summary><div class="answer">Correct. It creates a loop that won't slip under load.</div></details><details><summary>B) Joining two ropes</summary></details><details><summary>C) Shortening a rope</summary></details><details><summary>D) Decorating</summary></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">3. Which knot tightens under load but is easy to untie?</div><details><summary>A) Clove hitch</summary><div class="answer">Correct. It grips and releases easily.</div></details><details><summary>B) Reef knot</summary></details><details><summary>C) Overhand</summary></details><details><summary>D) Alpine butterfly</summary></details></div><p class="muted t-small">Practice a few, . Your hands will remember.</p></div>	-
