23.22.1	quiz_question/Basic time blocking quiz	<div class="umi-page"><div class="stack"><p class="muted t-small">Alright <regex>user_name</regex>. Four on making the calendar do the work.</p><div class="quiz"><div class="q">1. Start with what on the day's list?</div><details><summary>A) The easiest thing</summary></details><details><summary>B) The one thing that matters most if nothing else gets done</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Block it first, protect it hardest, schedule around it.</div></details><details><summary>C) Whatever email is asking for</summary></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">2. How long should a focus block usually be?</div><details><summary>A) 30 minutes max</summary></details><details><summary>B) 60-90 minutes, with a real break after</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Deep work has a rhythm. Ninety minutes then a walk beats four hours of drift.</div></details><details><summary>C) As long as the day allows</summary></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">3. What goes in a buffer block?</div><details><summary>A) Nothing, that is the point</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Twenty minutes of white space catches the overruns and the surprise call.</div></details><details><summary>B) Low-priority emails</summary></details><details><summary>C) Meetings that got cancelled</summary></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">4. Someone asks for a slot in your focus block. Best reply?</div><details><summary>A) Yes, always say yes</summary></details><details><summary>B) Offer two other slots instead</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Protect the block, be generous with the alternatives.</div></details><details><summary>C) Ignore it and hope</summary></details></div><p class="muted t-small">Good. Your calendar is a promise to yourself. Keep it.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="stack"><p class="muted t-small">Alright . Four on making the calendar do the work.</p><div class="quiz"><div class="q">1. Start with what on the day's list?</div><details><summary>A) The easiest thing</summary></details><details><summary>B) The one thing that matters most if nothing else gets done</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Block it first, protect it hardest, schedule around it.</div></details><details><summary>C) Whatever email is asking for</summary></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">2. How long should a focus block usually be?</div><details><summary>A) 30 minutes max</summary></details><details><summary>B) 60-90 minutes, with a real break after</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Deep work has a rhythm. Ninety minutes then a walk beats four hours of drift.</div></details><details><summary>C) As long as the day allows</summary></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">3. What goes in a buffer block?</div><details><summary>A) Nothing, that is the point</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Twenty minutes of white space catches the overruns and the surprise call.</div></details><details><summary>B) Low-priority emails</summary></details><details><summary>C) Meetings that got cancelled</summary></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">4. Someone asks for a slot in your focus block. Best reply?</div><details><summary>A) Yes, always say yes</summary></details><details><summary>B) Offer two other slots instead</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Protect the block, be generous with the alternatives.</div></details><details><summary>C) Ignore it and hope</summary></details></div><p class="muted t-small">Good. Your calendar is a promise to yourself. Keep it.</p></div></div>	-
23.23.1	quiz_question/Basic watercolor wash quiz	<div class="quiz"><div class="q">What's the purpose of a light watercolor wash as a base layer?</div><details><summary>A) To create texture and detail</summary></details><details><summary>B) To block out large areas of light colour and guide later layers</summary><div class="answer">Correct. A flat wash sets the tone and covers big shapes; detail comes later with dry brush or glazing.</div></details><details><summary>C) To mix all pigments at once</summary></details><details><summary>D) To seal the paper so it won't buckle</summary></details></div>	<div class="quiz"><div class="q">What's the purpose of a light watercolor wash as a base layer?</div><details><summary>A) To create texture and detail</summary></details><details><summary>B) To block out large areas of light colour and guide later layers</summary><div class="answer">Correct. A flat wash sets the tone and covers big shapes; detail comes later with dry brush or glazing.</div></details><details><summary>C) To mix all pigments at once</summary></details><details><summary>D) To seal the paper so it won't buckle</summary></details></div>	-
23.24.1	quiz_question/Basic houseplant light needs quiz	<div class="quiz"><div class="q">Your ZZ plant gets bright, direct afternoon sun and its leaves are curling. What does it likely need?</div><details><summary>A) More water</summary></details><details><summary>B) Less intense, indirect light</summary><div class="answer">Correct. ZZ plants prefer medium to low indirect light; direct sun can scorch and curl leaves.</div></details><details><summary>C) A bigger pot</summary></details><details><summary>D) Fertilizer</summary></details></div>	<div class="quiz"><div class="q">Your ZZ plant gets bright, direct afternoon sun and its leaves are curling. What does it likely need?</div><details><summary>A) More water</summary></details><details><summary>B) Less intense, indirect light</summary><div class="answer">Correct. ZZ plants prefer medium to low indirect light; direct sun can scorch and curl leaves.</div></details><details><summary>C) A bigger pot</summary></details><details><summary>D) Fertilizer</summary></details></div>	-
23.25.1	quiz_question/Basic fabric stain removal quiz	<div class="quiz"><div class="q">You spilled red wine on a white cotton shirt. What should you blot with first?</div><details><summary>A) Hot water and soap</summary></details><details><summary>B) A clean, dry cloth to absorb as much as possible</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Blotting lifts the liquid; rubbing or hot water can set the stain and spread it.</div></details><details><summary>C) Hairspray</summary></details><details><summary>D) Bleach directly on the stain</summary></details></div>	<div class="quiz"><div class="q">You spilled red wine on a white cotton shirt. What should you blot with first?</div><details><summary>A) Hot water and soap</summary></details><details><summary>B) A clean, dry cloth to absorb as much as possible</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Blotting lifts the liquid; rubbing or hot water can set the stain and spread it.</div></details><details><summary>C) Hairspray</summary></details><details><summary>D) Bleach directly on the stain</summary></details></div>	-
23.26.1	quiz_question/Basic fire extinguisher use quiz	<div class="quiz"><div class="q">What does the acronym PASS stand for when using a fire extinguisher?</div><details><summary>A) Pull, Aim, Squeeze, Sweep</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Pull the pin, aim at the base, squeeze the handle, sweep side to side.</div></details><details><summary>B) Push, Aim, Spray, Stop</summary></details><details><summary>C) Point, Alert, Spray, Secure</summary></details><details><summary>D) Prepare, Act, Spray, Stay</summary></details></div>	<div class="quiz"><div class="q">What does the acronym PASS stand for when using a fire extinguisher?</div><details><summary>A) Pull, Aim, Squeeze, Sweep</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Pull the pin, aim at the base, squeeze the handle, sweep side to side.</div></details><details><summary>B) Push, Aim, Spray, Stop</summary></details><details><summary>C) Point, Alert, Spray, Secure</summary></details><details><summary>D) Prepare, Act, Spray, Stay</summary></details></div>	-
23.27.1	quiz_question/Basic kitchen knife sharpening quiz	<div class="quiz"><div class="q">When sharpening a Western-style kitchen knife on a whetstone, what angle should you aim for?</div><details><summary>A) 5 degrees</summary></details><details><summary>B) 15 to 20 degrees per side</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Most Western knives are ground at about 15–20° per side; thinner angles for Japanese knives.</div></details><details><summary>C) 45 degrees</summary></details><details><summary>D) 90 degrees</summary></details></div>	<div class="quiz"><div class="q">When sharpening a Western-style kitchen knife on a whetstone, what angle should you aim for?</div><details><summary>A) 5 degrees</summary></details><details><summary>B) 15 to 20 degrees per side</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Most Western knives are ground at about 15–20° per side; thinner angles for Japanese knives.</div></details><details><summary>C) 45 degrees</summary></details><details><summary>D) 90 degrees</summary></details></div>	-
23.28.1	quiz_question/Basic vitamin D quiz	<div class="quiz"><div class="q">What is the main way most people get vitamin D?</div><details><summary>A) Eating leafy greens</summary></details><details><summary>B) Sun exposure on skin</summary><div class="answer">Correct. UVB rays trigger vitamin D synthesis in skin; diet and supplements fill the gaps.</div></details><details><summary>C) Drinking milk</summary></details><details><summary>D) Breathing fresh air</summary></details></div>	<div class="quiz"><div class="q">What is the main way most people get vitamin D?</div><details><summary>A) Eating leafy greens</summary></details><details><summary>B) Sun exposure on skin</summary><div class="answer">Correct. UVB rays trigger vitamin D synthesis in skin; diet and supplements fill the gaps.</div></details><details><summary>C) Drinking milk</summary></details><details><summary>D) Breathing fresh air</summary></details></div>	-
23.29.1	quiz_question/Basic tea steeping quiz	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Tea steeping basics</div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">What water temperature is best for green tea?</div><details><summary>A) Rolling boil, 100&deg;C</summary></details><details><summary>B) 70&ndash;80&deg;C</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Boiling water scalds green leaves and brings out bitterness. Let the kettle rest a minute or two after boiling.</div></details><details><summary>C) 40&deg;C</summary></details><details><summary>D) It doesn't matter</summary></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">How much loose tea for a standard 250 ml cup?</div><details><summary>A) 1 teaspoon</summary><div class="answer">Correct. About 2&ndash;3 grams. Adjust to taste, but start there.</div></details><details><summary>B) 1 tablespoon</summary></details><details><summary>C) A pinch</summary></details><details><summary>D) Fill the cup halfway</summary></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">Black tea usually steeps for how long?</div><details><summary>A) 30 seconds</summary></details><details><summary>B) 3&ndash;5 minutes</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Over-steeping makes it tannic. Set a timer if you tend to forget.</div></details><details><summary>C) 10 minutes</summary></details><details><summary>D) Until it looks dark enough</summary></details></div></div>	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Tea steeping basics</div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">What water temperature is best for green tea?</div><details><summary>A) Rolling boil, 100&deg;C</summary></details><details><summary>B) 70&ndash;80&deg;C</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Boiling water scalds green leaves and brings out bitterness. Let the kettle rest a minute or two after boiling.</div></details><details><summary>C) 40&deg;C</summary></details><details><summary>D) It doesn't matter</summary></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">How much loose tea for a standard 250 ml cup?</div><details><summary>A) 1 teaspoon</summary><div class="answer">Correct. About 2&ndash;3 grams. Adjust to taste, but start there.</div></details><details><summary>B) 1 tablespoon</summary></details><details><summary>C) A pinch</summary></details><details><summary>D) Fill the cup halfway</summary></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">Black tea usually steeps for how long?</div><details><summary>A) 30 seconds</summary></details><details><summary>B) 3&ndash;5 minutes</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Over-steeping makes it tannic. Set a timer if you tend to forget.</div></details><details><summary>C) 10 minutes</summary></details><details><summary>D) Until it looks dark enough</summary></details></div></div>	-
23.30.1	quiz_question/Basic first aid quiz	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Basic first aid quiz</div><p class="muted t-small">Three questions, <regex>user_name</regex>. Tap an option to see the why.</p><div class="quiz"><div class="q">1. A minor cut is bleeding. First move?</div><details><summary>A) Press clean gauze on it and elevate</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Steady direct pressure with a clean cloth for 5–10 minutes stops most small bleeds. Raise the area above heart level if you can.</div></details><details><summary>B) Rinse it under warm running water</summary><div class="answer">Not first. That's for cleaning the wound once bleeding slows — pressure comes before rinsing.</div></details><details><summary>C) Blow on it</summary><div class="answer">Nope. That just adds bacteria. Keep it clean.</div></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">2. Someone small swallows a burn on their hand from a hot pan. Do you pop the blister?</div><details><summary>A) Yes, then cover it</summary><div class="answer">No. Popping opens the door to infection. Leave it be.</div></details><details><summary>B) No — cool with room-temp water, loosely cover</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Run cool (not icy) water for 10–20 minutes, then wrap loosely. Skip the butter.</div></details><details><summary>C) Butter. Definitely butter.</summary><div class="answer">Kitchen legend. Butter traps heat and adds bacteria — skip it.</div></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">3. An adult collapses and isn't breathing. Chest compressions start at what rate?</div><details><summary>A) Around 100–120 per minute</summary><div class="answer">Correct. That's the speed of "Stayin' Alive" if it helps you find the beat.</div></details><details><summary>B) As fast as possible</summary><div class="answer">Slower than you'd think. Fast and shallow won't move blood. Aim for the steady 100–120.</div></details><details><summary>C) One per second</summary><div class="answer">Close — a hair fast, but decent. The 100–120 range is the target.</div></details></div><div class="callout info t-small"><strong>Bonus honesty:</strong> this is a refresher, not a certification. Take the real course when you can.</div></div>	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Basic first aid quiz</div><p class="muted t-small">Three questions, . Tap an option to see the why.</p><div class="quiz"><div class="q">1. A minor cut is bleeding. First move?</div><details><summary>A) Press clean gauze on it and elevate</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Steady direct pressure with a clean cloth for 5–10 minutes stops most small bleeds. Raise the area above heart level if you can.</div></details><details><summary>B) Rinse it under warm running water</summary><div class="answer">Not first. That's for cleaning the wound once bleeding slows — pressure comes before rinsing.</div></details><details><summary>C) Blow on it</summary><div class="answer">Nope. That just adds bacteria. Keep it clean.</div></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">2. Someone small swallows a burn on their hand from a hot pan. Do you pop the blister?</div><details><summary>A) Yes, then cover it</summary><div class="answer">No. Popping opens the door to infection. Leave it be.</div></details><details><summary>B) No — cool with room-temp water, loosely cover</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Run cool (not icy) water for 10–20 minutes, then wrap loosely. Skip the butter.</div></details><details><summary>C) Butter. Definitely butter.</summary><div class="answer">Kitchen legend. Butter traps heat and adds bacteria — skip it.</div></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">3. An adult collapses and isn't breathing. Chest compressions start at what rate?</div><details><summary>A) Around 100–120 per minute</summary><div class="answer">Correct. That's the speed of "Stayin' Alive" if it helps you find the beat.</div></details><details><summary>B) As fast as possible</summary><div class="answer">Slower than you'd think. Fast and shallow won't move blood. Aim for the steady 100–120.</div></details><details><summary>C) One per second</summary><div class="answer">Close — a hair fast, but decent. The 100–120 range is the target.</div></details></div><div class="callout info t-small"><strong>Bonus honesty:</strong> this is a refresher, not a certification. Take the real course when you can.</div></div>	-
23.31.1	quiz_question/Basic pet care quiz	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Basic pet care quiz</div><p class="muted t-small">Four questions. Nice work showing up for the animals, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="quiz"><div class="q">1. Chocolate and dogs — safe?</div><details><summary>A) A little is fine, it's a treat</summary><div class="answer">No. Even small amounts can bother a dog. Keep chocolate, grapes, onions, and xylitol well out of reach.</div></details><details><summary>B) Never; several common foods are toxic</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Chocolate, grapes/raisins, onion, garlic, and the sweetener xylitol all cause trouble. Know your list.</div></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">2. Cats and water bowls — how often should you freshen it?</div><details><summary>A) Every day, ideally twice</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Cats are picky about stale water and many drink too little. Clean bowls and daily refills help prevent urinary issues.</div></details><details><summary>B) Whenever they seem thirsty</summary><div class="answer">Too late by the time they act thirsty. Keep it fresh daily.</div></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">3. Best spot for a new dog to sleep on night one?</div><details><summary>A) Your bed</summary><div class="answer">Your call, but it sets a precedent fast. Fine if you're committed to it forever.</div></details><details><summary>B) A quiet crate or bed near your room</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Close enough to smell and hear you, but a place of their own. Cuts first-night stress both ways.</div></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">4. A cat stops eating for two days. Wait it out?</div><details><summary>A) Yes, cats fast naturally</summary><div class="answer">No — this is the one to take seriously. Cats can develop serious liver problems after just a couple of days without food. Call your vet.</div></details><details><summary>B) No, call the vet</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Two days without eating in a cat is a real red flag, not a phase.</div></details></div></div>	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Basic pet care quiz</div><p class="muted t-small">Four questions. Nice work showing up for the animals, .</p><div class="quiz"><div class="q">1. Chocolate and dogs — safe?</div><details><summary>A) A little is fine, it's a treat</summary><div class="answer">No. Even small amounts can bother a dog. Keep chocolate, grapes, onions, and xylitol well out of reach.</div></details><details><summary>B) Never; several common foods are toxic</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Chocolate, grapes/raisins, onion, garlic, and the sweetener xylitol all cause trouble. Know your list.</div></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">2. Cats and water bowls — how often should you freshen it?</div><details><summary>A) Every day, ideally twice</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Cats are picky about stale water and many drink too little. Clean bowls and daily refills help prevent urinary issues.</div></details><details><summary>B) Whenever they seem thirsty</summary><div class="answer">Too late by the time they act thirsty. Keep it fresh daily.</div></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">3. Best spot for a new dog to sleep on night one?</div><details><summary>A) Your bed</summary><div class="answer">Your call, but it sets a precedent fast. Fine if you're committed to it forever.</div></details><details><summary>B) A quiet crate or bed near your room</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Close enough to smell and hear you, but a place of their own. Cuts first-night stress both ways.</div></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">4. A cat stops eating for two days. Wait it out?</div><details><summary>A) Yes, cats fast naturally</summary><div class="answer">No — this is the one to take seriously. Cats can develop serious liver problems after just a couple of days without food. Call your vet.</div></details><details><summary>B) No, call the vet</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Two days without eating in a cat is a real red flag, not a phase.</div></details></div></div>	-
23.32.1	quiz_question/Basic reading nutrition labels quiz	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Basic reading nutrition labels quiz</div><p class="muted t-small">The label's a tiny cheat sheet once you know where to look, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="quiz"><div class="q">1. A bottle says 2 servings. You drink the whole thing. How many calories did you get?</div><details><summary>A) Whatever the label says</summary><div class="answer">Careful — that number is per serving, not per bottle. Double it.</div></details><details><summary>B) Double the labeled calories</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Serving sizes trip almost everyone up. Check how many servings are in the container first.</div></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">2. Ingredient list — what's the order mean?</div><details><summary>A) Alphabetical</summary><div class="answer">Nope. It's by weight, always.</div></details><details><summary>B) Heaviest ingredient first</summary><div class="answer">Correct. The earlier an ingredient sits, the more of it there is by weight. Sugar hiding in slot three? That's a lot of sugar.</div></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">3. "Added sugars" vs "total sugars" — difference?</div><details><summary>A) None, same thing</summary><div class="answer">There's a real gap. Total includes what's naturally there — like the lactose in milk — while added is what got stirred in during processing.</div></details><details><summary>B) Added is the sneaky part; total includes natural</summary><div class="answer">Correct. For a juice, total sugars include the fruit's own; added sugars tell you what the factory put in.</div></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">4. Healthiest move on the % Daily Value column?</div><details><summary>A) Low for sodium, high for fiber</summary><div class="answer">Correct. 5% or less is low, 20% or more is high. You want the fiber, calcium, and iron numbers up; sodium, saturated fat, and added sugar down.</div></details><details><summary>B) High for everything</summary><div class="answer">Not quite. For sodium, saturated fat, and added sugar you actually want those percentages low.</div></details></div></div>	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Basic reading nutrition labels quiz</div><p class="muted t-small">The label's a tiny cheat sheet once you know where to look, .</p><div class="quiz"><div class="q">1. A bottle says 2 servings. You drink the whole thing. How many calories did you get?</div><details><summary>A) Whatever the label says</summary><div class="answer">Careful — that number is per serving, not per bottle. Double it.</div></details><details><summary>B) Double the labeled calories</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Serving sizes trip almost everyone up. Check how many servings are in the container first.</div></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">2. Ingredient list — what's the order mean?</div><details><summary>A) Alphabetical</summary><div class="answer">Nope. It's by weight, always.</div></details><details><summary>B) Heaviest ingredient first</summary><div class="answer">Correct. The earlier an ingredient sits, the more of it there is by weight. Sugar hiding in slot three? That's a lot of sugar.</div></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">3. "Added sugars" vs "total sugars" — difference?</div><details><summary>A) None, same thing</summary><div class="answer">There's a real gap. Total includes what's naturally there — like the lactose in milk — while added is what got stirred in during processing.</div></details><details><summary>B) Added is the sneaky part; total includes natural</summary><div class="answer">Correct. For a juice, total sugars include the fruit's own; added sugars tell you what the factory put in.</div></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">4. Healthiest move on the % Daily Value column?</div><details><summary>A) Low for sodium, high for fiber</summary><div class="answer">Correct. 5% or less is low, 20% or more is high. You want the fiber, calcium, and iron numbers up; sodium, saturated fat, and added sugar down.</div></details><details><summary>B) High for everything</summary><div class="answer">Not quite. For sodium, saturated fat, and added sugar you actually want those percentages low.</div></details></div></div>	-
23.33.1	quiz_question/Basic budgeting quiz	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Basic budgeting quiz</div><p class="muted t-small">No spreadsheet required to pass, <regex>user_name</regex>. Just the ideas.</p><div class="quiz"><div class="q">1. First step when you sit down to make a budget?</div><details><summary>A) Pick a spending goal</summary><div class="answer">Tempting, but you're guessing. Find out what's actually happening first.</div></details><details><summary>B) Track what you actually spend for a month</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Can't plan a route without knowing where you stand. A month of real numbers beats any estimate.</div></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">2. The 50/30/20 rule splits take-home pay into…</div><details><summary>A) Needs, wants, savings</summary><div class="answer">Correct. 50% needs, 30% wants, 20% savings and debt payoff. A starting frame, not a law.</div></details><details><summary>B) Rent, food, fun</summary><div class="answer">That's more granular than the rule intends. The 50/30/20 buckets are needs, wants, savings.</div></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">3. Your emergency fund goal is usually…</div><details><summary>A) One month of rent</summary><div class="answer">That's a start, but thin. Most guides suggest more.</div></details><details><summary>B) Three to six months of expenses</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Start with $500–$1,000 if six months feels far off. Something beats nothing.</div></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">4. An automated transfer to savings on payday is…</div><details><summary>A) A great way to save without willpower</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Pay yourself first. What you don't see, you don't spend.</div></details><details><summary>B) Pointless, you'll move it back</summary><div class="answer">Only if you set it too high. An amount you won't miss works fine — even $20.</div></details></div><div class="callout info t-small"><strong>Honesty label:</strong> this is a 101 refresher, not personalized financial advice.</div></div>	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Basic budgeting quiz</div><p class="muted t-small">No spreadsheet required to pass, . Just the ideas.</p><div class="quiz"><div class="q">1. First step when you sit down to make a budget?</div><details><summary>A) Pick a spending goal</summary><div class="answer">Tempting, but you're guessing. Find out what's actually happening first.</div></details><details><summary>B) Track what you actually spend for a month</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Can't plan a route without knowing where you stand. A month of real numbers beats any estimate.</div></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">2. The 50/30/20 rule splits take-home pay into…</div><details><summary>A) Needs, wants, savings</summary><div class="answer">Correct. 50% needs, 30% wants, 20% savings and debt payoff. A starting frame, not a law.</div></details><details><summary>B) Rent, food, fun</summary><div class="answer">That's more granular than the rule intends. The 50/30/20 buckets are needs, wants, savings.</div></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">3. Your emergency fund goal is usually…</div><details><summary>A) One month of rent</summary><div class="answer">That's a start, but thin. Most guides suggest more.</div></details><details><summary>B) Three to six months of expenses</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Start with $500–$1,000 if six months feels far off. Something beats nothing.</div></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">4. An automated transfer to savings on payday is…</div><details><summary>A) A great way to save without willpower</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Pay yourself first. What you don't see, you don't spend.</div></details><details><summary>B) Pointless, you'll move it back</summary><div class="answer">Only if you set it too high. An amount you won't miss works fine — even $20.</div></details></div><div class="callout info t-small"><strong>Honesty label:</strong> this is a 101 refresher, not personalized financial advice.</div></div>	-
23.34.1	quiz_question/Basic safe driving quiz	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Basic safe driving quiz</div><p class="muted t-small">Short and useful, <regex>user_name</regex>. Tap a choice to see why.</p><div class="quiz"><div class="q">1. Tailgating. The three-second rule is…</div><details><summary>A) The minimum following distance in good conditions</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Pick a fixed point the car ahead passes, count three seconds. In rain or at night, double it.</div></details><details><summary>B) Only for highway driving</summary><div class="answer">It applies everywhere. Dense city traffic is exactly when it matters most.</div></details><details><summary>C) Three car lengths exactly</summary><div class="answer">Eyeballing car lengths fails at speed. Time, not distance, is the reliable measure.</div></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">2. Your phone buzzes. Safest handling?</div><details><summary>A) Glance at it quickly at the light</summary><div class="answer">Even a two-second glance at speed is a long, blind stretch of road. Not worth it.</div></details><details><summary>B) Let it wait, or pull over to check</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Nothing on a screen outranks the road. If it's urgent, pull into a lot.</div></details><details><summary>C) Keep it in your lap to peek</summary><div class="answer">Research keeps showing lap peeks are as bad as full-on texting. Put it out of reach.</div></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">3. The first sign tires need air?</div><details><summary>A) They look low</summary><div class="answer">Looks lie. A tire can lose a lot of pressure and look fine.</div></details><details><summary>B) Your dashboard pressure light, or a monthly gauge check</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Check cold once a month with a gauge — under-inflated tires hurt mileage and safety.</div></details></div><div class="callout good t-small"><strong>Small habit, big payoff:</strong> adjust your mirrors before you shift into drive, every time.</div></div>	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Basic safe driving quiz</div><p class="muted t-small">Short and useful, . Tap a choice to see why.</p><div class="quiz"><div class="q">1. Tailgating. The three-second rule is…</div><details><summary>A) The minimum following distance in good conditions</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Pick a fixed point the car ahead passes, count three seconds. In rain or at night, double it.</div></details><details><summary>B) Only for highway driving</summary><div class="answer">It applies everywhere. Dense city traffic is exactly when it matters most.</div></details><details><summary>C) Three car lengths exactly</summary><div class="answer">Eyeballing car lengths fails at speed. Time, not distance, is the reliable measure.</div></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">2. Your phone buzzes. Safest handling?</div><details><summary>A) Glance at it quickly at the light</summary><div class="answer">Even a two-second glance at speed is a long, blind stretch of road. Not worth it.</div></details><details><summary>B) Let it wait, or pull over to check</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Nothing on a screen outranks the road. If it's urgent, pull into a lot.</div></details><details><summary>C) Keep it in your lap to peek</summary><div class="answer">Research keeps showing lap peeks are as bad as full-on texting. Put it out of reach.</div></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">3. The first sign tires need air?</div><details><summary>A) They look low</summary><div class="answer">Looks lie. A tire can lose a lot of pressure and look fine.</div></details><details><summary>B) Your dashboard pressure light, or a monthly gauge check</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Check cold once a month with a gauge — under-inflated tires hurt mileage and safety.</div></details></div><div class="callout good t-small"><strong>Small habit, big payoff:</strong> adjust your mirrors before you shift into drive, every time.</div></div>	-
23.35.1	quiz_question/Basic composting quiz	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Basic composting quiz</div><p class="muted t-small">Good microorganisms, bad smell. Let's sort it out, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="quiz"><div class="q">1. The two ingredients a pile needs are usually called…</div><details><summary>A) Wet and dry</summary><div class="answer">Close on vibes, off on names. The pair is greens and browns.</div></details><details><summary>B) Greens and browns</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Greens (kitchen scraps, grass) are nitrogen; browns (leaves, cardboard) are carbon. Aim for roughly one part greens to two or three parts browns.</div></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">2. Which of these belongs in the pile?</div><details><summary>A) Coffee grounds and banana peels</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Both are classic greens — coffee grounds, veggie scraps, eggshells (although shells are more neutral, they're fine).</div></details><details><summary>B) Meat, bones, and dairy</summary><div class="answer">Skip these in a home pile. They smell, attract animals, and slow things down. Great for industrial compost, not your backyard.</div></details><details><summary>C) Weeds, always</summary><div class="answer">Risky. Weed seeds often survive a cool home pile and reseed your garden. Best left out.</div></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">3. The pile smells like rot. Likely fix?</div><details><summary>A) Add more greens and water</summary><div class="answer">Wrong direction. Too much green or wet is usually the cause.</div></details><details><summary>B) Add browns and turn it</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Sour smell means too wet or too much nitrogen. Brown material soaks up extra moisture, and turning adds air.</div></details></div><div class="callout warn t-small"><strong>Heads up:</strong> a pile should smell earthy, not terrible. Bad smell is a signal, not a sentence.</div></div>	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">Basic composting quiz</div><p class="muted t-small">Good microorganisms, bad smell. Let's sort it out, .</p><div class="quiz"><div class="q">1. The two ingredients a pile needs are usually called…</div><details><summary>A) Wet and dry</summary><div class="answer">Close on vibes, off on names. The pair is greens and browns.</div></details><details><summary>B) Greens and browns</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Greens (kitchen scraps, grass) are nitrogen; browns (leaves, cardboard) are carbon. Aim for roughly one part greens to two or three parts browns.</div></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">2. Which of these belongs in the pile?</div><details><summary>A) Coffee grounds and banana peels</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Both are classic greens — coffee grounds, veggie scraps, eggshells (although shells are more neutral, they're fine).</div></details><details><summary>B) Meat, bones, and dairy</summary><div class="answer">Skip these in a home pile. They smell, attract animals, and slow things down. Great for industrial compost, not your backyard.</div></details><details><summary>C) Weeds, always</summary><div class="answer">Risky. Weed seeds often survive a cool home pile and reseed your garden. Best left out.</div></details></div><div class="quiz"><div class="q">3. The pile smells like rot. Likely fix?</div><details><summary>A) Add more greens and water</summary><div class="answer">Wrong direction. Too much green or wet is usually the cause.</div></details><details><summary>B) Add browns and turn it</summary><div class="answer">Correct. Sour smell means too wet or too much nitrogen. Brown material soaks up extra moisture, and turning adds air.</div></details></div><div class="callout warn t-small"><strong>Heads up:</strong> a pile should smell earthy, not terrible. Bad smell is a signal, not a sentence.</div></div>	-
23.36.1	quiz_question/Basic camping knots quiz	<div class="quiz">  <div class="q">Which knot is best for tying two ropes of different thickness together?</div>  <details><summary>A) Square knot</summary><div class="answer">Not ideal—it can slip with different diameters.</div></details>  <details><summary>B) Sheet bend</summary><div class="answer">Correct. The sheet bend (or becket bend) holds securely even when rope sizes differ.</div></details>  <details><summary>C) Bowline</summary><div class="answer">The bowline makes a fixed loop, not a join.</div></details>  <details><summary>D) Clove hitch</summary><div class="answer">A clove hitch attaches a rope to a post, not to another rope.</div></details></div>	<div class="quiz">  <div class="q">Which knot is best for tying two ropes of different thickness together?</div>  <details><summary>A) Square knot</summary><div class="answer">Not ideal—it can slip with different diameters.</div></details>  <details><summary>B) Sheet bend</summary><div class="answer">Correct. The sheet bend (or becket bend) holds securely even when rope sizes differ.</div></details>  <details><summary>C) Bowline</summary><div class="answer">The bowline makes a fixed loop, not a join.</div></details>  <details><summary>D) Clove hitch</summary><div class="answer">A clove hitch attaches a rope to a post, not to another rope.</div></details></div>	-
24.0.0	glossary/Espresso Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Espresso Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">Starter kit for the coffee-curious.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Crema</b> — the golden foam on top of a fresh shot. Mostly CO₂ and oils. Tastes like victory.</li><li><b>Ristretto</b> — a short, concentrated shot. Less water, more punch. Italian for “restricted,” which tracks.</li><li><b>Lungo</b> — the opposite: a long pull. More water, gentler flavour, slightly bitter edge.</li><li><b>Portafilter</b> — the handle-thing that holds the coffee puck. If yours is sticky, you're doing it right.</li><li><b>Puck</b> — the spent coffee disc after brewing. Knock it out with dignity.</li><li><b>Channeling</b> — when water finds a shortcut through the puck. Leads to sour, uneven shots. Fix your distribution.</li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Espresso Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">Starter kit for the coffee-curious.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Crema</b> — the golden foam on top of a fresh shot. Mostly CO₂ and oils. Tastes like victory.</li><li><b>Ristretto</b> — a short, concentrated shot. Less water, more punch. Italian for “restricted,” which tracks.</li><li><b>Lungo</b> — the opposite: a long pull. More water, gentler flavour, slightly bitter edge.</li><li><b>Portafilter</b> — the handle-thing that holds the coffee puck. If yours is sticky, you're doing it right.</li><li><b>Puck</b> — the spent coffee disc after brewing. Knock it out with dignity.</li><li><b>Channeling</b> — when water finds a shortcut through the puck. Leads to sour, uneven shots. Fix your distribution.</li></ul></div>	-
24.1.0	glossary/Git Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Git Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">The words that make version control sound scarier than it is.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Repository (repo)</b> — your project's folder, but with a memory.</li><li><b>Commit</b> — a saved snapshot. A tiny time capsule with a message.</li><li><b>Branch</b> — a parallel line of work. Cheap, so make lots.</li><li><b>Merge</b> — combining branches. Sometimes tidy, sometimes a negotiation.</li><li><b>Rebase</b> — replaying commits onto a new base. Rewrites history; use with care.</li><li><b>HEAD</b> — a pointer to where you are right now. Like a bookmark that moves.</li><li><b>Stash</b> — a pocket for half-done work. <code>git stash</code>, then breathe.</li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Git Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">The words that make version control sound scarier than it is.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Repository (repo)</b> — your project's folder, but with a memory.</li><li><b>Commit</b> — a saved snapshot. A tiny time capsule with a message.</li><li><b>Branch</b> — a parallel line of work. Cheap, so make lots.</li><li><b>Merge</b> — combining branches. Sometimes tidy, sometimes a negotiation.</li><li><b>Rebase</b> — replaying commits onto a new base. Rewrites history; use with care.</li><li><b>HEAD</b> — a pointer to where you are right now. Like a bookmark that moves.</li><li><b>Stash</b> — a pocket for half-done work. <code>git stash</code>, then breathe.</li></ul></div>	-
24.2.0	glossary/CSS Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>CSS Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">Because “it's just styling” is a lie we tell ourselves.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Selector</b> — what you're aiming at. <code>.class</code>, <code>#id</code>, or a tag.</li><li><b>Cascade</b> — the rules for which style wins when several apply. Order matters.</li><li><b>Specificity</b> — a score for selectors. IDs beat classes beat tags. Usually.</li><li><b>Box model</b> — content, padding, border, margin. The rectangle that rules all.</li><li><b>Flexbox</b> — one-dimensional layout. Great for rows or columns.</li><li><b>Grid</b> — two-dimensional layout. Rows and columns, like a spreadsheet with taste.</li><li><b>Media query</b> — <code>@media (min-width: 600px)</code> — how you make things responsive.</li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>CSS Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">Because “it's just styling” is a lie we tell ourselves.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Selector</b> — what you're aiming at. <code>.class</code>, <code>#id</code>, or a tag.</li><li><b>Cascade</b> — the rules for which style wins when several apply. Order matters.</li><li><b>Specificity</b> — a score for selectors. IDs beat classes beat tags. Usually.</li><li><b>Box model</b> — content, padding, border, margin. The rectangle that rules all.</li><li><b>Flexbox</b> — one-dimensional layout. Great for rows or columns.</li><li><b>Grid</b> — two-dimensional layout. Rows and columns, like a spreadsheet with taste.</li><li><b>Media query</b> — <code>@media (min-width: 600px)</code> — how you make things responsive.</li></ul></div>	-
24.3.0	glossary/Astronomy Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Astronomy Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">Small words for very large things.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Light-year</b> — the distance light travels in a year. Not a time. About 9.5 trillion km.</li><li><b>Parsec</b> — 3.26 light-years. Astronomers use it because the numbers get silly otherwise.</li><li><b>Nebula</b> — a cloud of gas and dust. Some are stellar nurseries, some are leftovers.</li><li><b>Black hole</b> — gravity so strong light can't escape. Not a vacuum; a one-way door.</li><li><b>Event horizon</b> — the point of no return around a black hole. Cross it and you're not coming back.</li><li><b>Redshift</b> — light stretched as things move away. The universe's way of saying “I'm out.”</li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Astronomy Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">Small words for very large things.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Light-year</b> — the distance light travels in a year. Not a time. About 9.5 trillion km.</li><li><b>Parsec</b> — 3.26 light-years. Astronomers use it because the numbers get silly otherwise.</li><li><b>Nebula</b> — a cloud of gas and dust. Some are stellar nurseries, some are leftovers.</li><li><b>Black hole</b> — gravity so strong light can't escape. Not a vacuum; a one-way door.</li><li><b>Event horizon</b> — the point of no return around a black hole. Cross it and you're not coming back.</li><li><b>Redshift</b> — light stretched as things move away. The universe's way of saying “I'm out.”</li></ul></div>	-
24.4.0	glossary/Cooking Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Cooking Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">So you can read a recipe without Googling every third word.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Mise en place</b> — everything in its place. Chop first, cook calm.</li><li><b>Fold</b> — gently combine without deflating. Think of a spatula hug.</li><li><b>Julienne</b> — matchsticks. Thin, even, impressive.</li><li><b>Roux</b> — flour and fat cooked together. The thickener behind many a sauce.</li><li><b>Deglaze</b> — splash liquid into a hot pan to lift the tasty brown bits. Free flavour.</li><li><b>Emulsion</b> — two liquids that shouldn't mix, mixed. Like vinaigrette or hollandaise.</li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Cooking Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">So you can read a recipe without Googling every third word.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Mise en place</b> — everything in its place. Chop first, cook calm.</li><li><b>Fold</b> — gently combine without deflating. Think of a spatula hug.</li><li><b>Julienne</b> — matchsticks. Thin, even, impressive.</li><li><b>Roux</b> — flour and fat cooked together. The thickener behind many a sauce.</li><li><b>Deglaze</b> — splash liquid into a hot pan to lift the tasty brown bits. Free flavour.</li><li><b>Emulsion</b> — two liquids that shouldn't mix, mixed. Like vinaigrette or hollandaise.</li></ul></div>	-
24.5.0	glossary/Music Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Music Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">Theory words, minus the headache.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Key</b> — the home base of a song. The note that feels like “done.”</li><li><b>Scale</b> — a set of notes in order. Major, minor, and friends.</li><li><b>Chord</b> — three or more notes at once. Instant mood.</li><li><b>Syncopation</b> — accents where you don't expect them. Makes you nod off-beat.</li><li><b>Pentatonic</b> — five-note scale. Hard to sound bad on. Try it.</li><li><b>Tempo</b> — speed, in BPM. The heartbeat of the track.</li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Music Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">Theory words, minus the headache.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Key</b> — the home base of a song. The note that feels like “done.”</li><li><b>Scale</b> — a set of notes in order. Major, minor, and friends.</li><li><b>Chord</b> — three or more notes at once. Instant mood.</li><li><b>Syncopation</b> — accents where you don't expect them. Makes you nod off-beat.</li><li><b>Pentatonic</b> — five-note scale. Hard to sound bad on. Try it.</li><li><b>Tempo</b> — speed, in BPM. The heartbeat of the track.</li></ul></div>	-
24.6.0	glossary/Baking Glossary	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">Baking Glossary</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">Hey <regex>user_name</regex> — the words that make dough sound fancy, decoded.</p>  <ul class="list">    <li><b>Autolyse</b> — Resting flour and water before adding yeast or salt. Gluten starts forming on its own.</li>    <li><b>Proof</b> — Letting dough rise so yeast can make gas. Also the final rise before baking.</li>    <li><b>Fold</b> — Gentle stretch-and-turn instead of kneading. Builds strength without flattening the bubbles.</li>    <li><b>Crumb</b> — The inside texture. Open crumb means big airy holes; tight crumb means dense.</li>    <li><b>Bulk ferment</b> — The first long rise, before you shape anything.</li>    <li><b>Scoring</b> — Slashing the top so the loaf expands where you want it to.</li>    <li><b>Bakers' percentage</b> — Flour is always 100%. Everything else is measured against it.</li>    <li><b>Dead dough</b> — Dough that won't rise. Usually old yeast or too-hot water.</li>  </ul></div>}, {	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">Baking Glossary</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">Hey  — the words that make dough sound fancy, decoded.</p>  <ul class="list">    <li><b>Autolyse</b> — Resting flour and water before adding yeast or salt. Gluten starts forming on its own.</li>    <li><b>Proof</b> — Letting dough rise so yeast can make gas. Also the final rise before baking.</li>    <li><b>Fold</b> — Gentle stretch-and-turn instead of kneading. Builds strength without flattening the bubbles.</li>    <li><b>Crumb</b> — The inside texture. Open crumb means big airy holes; tight crumb means dense.</li>    <li><b>Bulk ferment</b> — The first long rise, before you shape anything.</li>    <li><b>Scoring</b> — Slashing the top so the loaf expands where you want it to.</li>    <li><b>Bakers' percentage</b> — Flour is always 100%. Everything else is measured against it.</li>    <li><b>Dead dough</b> — Dough that won'