24.7.0	glossary/Sleep Glossary	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">Sleep Glossary</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">For <regex>user_name</regex> — because everyone says "get more sleep" and nobody says how.</p>  <ul class="list">    <li><b>Sleep latency</b> — How long it takes you to actually fall asleep. Under 20 minutes is normal.</li>    <li><b>REM</b> — Rapid eye movement sleep. Where most dreaming happens. Comes in longer stretches toward morning.</li>    <li><b>Deep sleep</b> — Slow-wave sleep. Body repair, memory sorting. Hard to wake from.</li>    <li><b>Sleep debt</b> — Hours you owe. It accumulates and doesn't vanish with one long lie-in.</li>    <li><b>Circadian rhythm</b> — Your internal 24-hour clock, set mostly by morning light.</li>    <li><b>Sleep inertia</b> — That groggy fog right after waking. Usually fades in 15–30 minutes.</li>    <li><b>Microsleep</b> — Seconds-long blackouts your brain takes when you're badly under-slept.</li>  </ul>  <div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">One honest note: consistent wake times help more than perfect bedtimes. Annoying, but true.</p></div></div>	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">Sleep Glossary</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">For  — because everyone says "get more sleep" and nobody says how.</p>  <ul class="list">    <li><b>Sleep latency</b> — How long it takes you to actually fall asleep. Under 20 minutes is normal.</li>    <li><b>REM</b> — Rapid eye movement sleep. Where most dreaming happens. Comes in longer stretches toward morning.</li>    <li><b>Deep sleep</b> — Slow-wave sleep. Body repair, memory sorting. Hard to wake from.</li>    <li><b>Sleep debt</b> — Hours you owe. It accumulates and doesn't vanish with one long lie-in.</li>    <li><b>Circadian rhythm</b> — Your internal 24-hour clock, set mostly by morning light.</li>    <li><b>Sleep inertia</b> — That groggy fog right after waking. Usually fades in 15–30 minutes.</li>    <li><b>Microsleep</b> — Seconds-long blackouts your brain takes when you're badly under-slept.</li>  </ul>  <div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">One honest note: consistent wake times help more than perfect bedtimes. Annoying, but true.</p></div></div>	-
24.8.0	glossary/Gardening Glossary	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">Gardening Glossary</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">For <regex>user_name</regex> — plant talk, minus the Latin lecture.</p>  <ul class="list">    <li><b>Annual</b> — Lives one season, then quits. Marigolds, basil, zinnias.</li>    <li><b>Perennial</b> — Comes back year after year from the same roots. Peonies, lavender.</li>    <li><b>Biennial</b> — Two-year cycle: leaves the first year, flowers the second. Foxglove, parsley.</li>    <li><b>Deadheading</b> — Snapping off spent flowers so the plant makes more instead of seeds.</li>    <li><b>Full sun</b> — Six or more hours of direct light. Not "it's bright outside."</li>    <li><b>Companion planting</b> — Putting plants together because they help each other. Some pairings are folklore; some, like basil and tomatoes, are just convenient.</li>    <li><b>Bolting</b> — A leafy crop shooting up to flower early, usually from heat. Lettuce goes bitter.</li>    <li><b>Hardening off</b> — Slowly introducing indoor seedlings to outdoor life so they don't sulk.</li>  </ul></div>	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">Gardening Glossary</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">For  — plant talk, minus the Latin lecture.</p>  <ul class="list">    <li><b>Annual</b> — Lives one season, then quits. Marigolds, basil, zinnias.</li>    <li><b>Perennial</b> — Comes back year after year from the same roots. Peonies, lavender.</li>    <li><b>Biennial</b> — Two-year cycle: leaves the first year, flowers the second. Foxglove, parsley.</li>    <li><b>Deadheading</b> — Snapping off spent flowers so the plant makes more instead of seeds.</li>    <li><b>Full sun</b> — Six or more hours of direct light. Not "it's bright outside."</li>    <li><b>Companion planting</b> — Putting plants together because they help each other. Some pairings are folklore; some, like basil and tomatoes, are just convenient.</li>    <li><b>Bolting</b> — A leafy crop shooting up to flower early, usually from heat. Lettuce goes bitter.</li>    <li><b>Hardening off</b> — Slowly introducing indoor seedlings to outdoor life so they don't sulk.</li>  </ul></div>	-
24.9.0	glossary/Photography Glossary	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">Photography Glossary</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">Hi <regex>user_name</regex> — the exposure triangle, in plain words.</p>  <ul class="list">    <li><b>Aperture</b> — The hole in the lens. Lower f-number, bigger hole, more light, blurrier background.</li>    <li><b>Shutter speed</b> — How long the sensor sees. Fast freezes motion; slow smears it.</li>    <li><b>ISO</b> — How sensitive the sensor is. Higher means brighter but grainier.</li>    <li><b>Bokeh</b> — The quality of the out-of-focus blur. Not just "blurry" — pleasant blurry.</li>    <li><b>Stops</b> — Doubling or halving light. The unit everyone argues in.</li>    <li><b>Golden hour</b> — The soft warm light just after sunrise or before sunset.</li>    <li><b>Focal length</b> — The number in millimetres. Small = wide scene, large = zoomed in.</li>    <li><b>Histogram</b> — A graph of brightness. More reliable than the picture on your screen.</li>  </ul></div>	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">Photography Glossary</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">Hi  — the exposure triangle, in plain words.</p>  <ul class="list">    <li><b>Aperture</b> — The hole in the lens. Lower f-number, bigger hole, more light, blurrier background.</li>    <li><b>Shutter speed</b> — How long the sensor sees. Fast freezes motion; slow smears it.</li>    <li><b>ISO</b> — How sensitive the sensor is. Higher means brighter but grainier.</li>    <li><b>Bokeh</b> — The quality of the out-of-focus blur. Not just "blurry" — pleasant blurry.</li>    <li><b>Stops</b> — Doubling or halving light. The unit everyone argues in.</li>    <li><b>Golden hour</b> — The soft warm light just after sunrise or before sunset.</li>    <li><b>Focal length</b> — The number in millimetres. Small = wide scene, large = zoomed in.</li>    <li><b>Histogram</b> — A graph of brightness. More reliable than the picture on your screen.</li>  </ul></div>	-
24.10.0	glossary/Knitting Glossary	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">Knitting Glossary</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">For <regex>user_name</regex> — yes, "frogging" is real and you will do it.</p>  <ul class="list">    <li><b>Knit / Purl</b> — The two basic stitches. Everything else is a remix.</li>    <li><b>Gauge</b> — Stitches and rows per inch. Skip it and your sweater fits a different human.</li>    <li><b>Frogging</b> — Ripping out rows. Named for "rip it, rip it."</li>    <li><b>Tinking</b> — Un-knitting stitch by stitch, when you only need to go back a little.</li>    <li><b>Lifeline</b> — A scrap thread run through a row so you can safely unravel to that point.</li>    <li><b>Yarn over</b> — Wrapping the yarn to make an extra stitch, usually a deliberate hole.</li>    <li><b>Blocking</b> — Wetting and pinning finished work so it settles into its real shape.</li>    <li><b>WIP</b> — Work in progress. You have several. That's allowed.</li>  </ul></div>	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">Knitting Glossary</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">For  — yes, "frogging" is real and you will do it.</p>  <ul class="list">    <li><b>Knit / Purl</b> — The two basic stitches. Everything else is a remix.</li>    <li><b>Gauge</b> — Stitches and rows per inch. Skip it and your sweater fits a different human.</li>    <li><b>Frogging</b> — Ripping out rows. Named for "rip it, rip it."</li>    <li><b>Tinking</b> — Un-knitting stitch by stitch, when you only need to go back a little.</li>    <li><b>Lifeline</b> — A scrap thread run through a row so you can safely unravel to that point.</li>    <li><b>Yarn over</b> — Wrapping the yarn to make an extra stitch, usually a deliberate hole.</li>    <li><b>Blocking</b> — Wetting and pinning finished work so it settles into its real shape.</li>    <li><b>WIP</b> — Work in progress. You have several. That's allowed.</li>  </ul></div>	-
24.11.0	glossary/Sailing Glossary	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">Sailing Glossary</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">For <regex>user_name</regex> — enough to sound competent at the dock.</p>  <ul class="list">    <li><b>Port / Starboard</b> — Left and right, facing forward. Port is the four-letter word for left.</li>    <li><b>Bow / Stern</b> — Front and back of the boat.</li>    <li><b>Tack</b> — Turning the bow through the wind. Also the front corner of a sail.</li>    <li><b>Jibe</b> — Turning the stern through the wind. More sudden; watch your head.</li>    <li><b>Main / Jib</b> — The big sail behind the mast, and the smaller one in front.</li>    <li><b>Heeling</b> — Tipping sideways from wind pressure. Normal, not sinking.</li>    <li><b>In irons</b> — Stuck pointing straight into the wind with no power. Everyone has done it.</li>    <li><b>Knot</b> — One nautical mile per hour. About 1.15 land miles per hour.</li>  </ul></div>	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">Sailing Glossary</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">For  — enough to sound competent at the dock.</p>  <ul class="list">    <li><b>Port / Starboard</b> — Left and right, facing forward. Port is the four-letter word for left.</li>    <li><b>Bow / Stern</b> — Front and back of the boat.</li>    <li><b>Tack</b> — Turning the bow through the wind. Also the front corner of a sail.</li>    <li><b>Jibe</b> — Turning the stern through the wind. More sudden; watch your head.</li>    <li><b>Main / Jib</b> — The big sail behind the mast, and the smaller one in front.</li>    <li><b>Heeling</b> — Tipping sideways from wind pressure. Normal, not sinking.</li>    <li><b>In irons</b> — Stuck pointing straight into the wind with no power. Everyone has done it.</li>    <li><b>Knot</b> — One nautical mile per hour. About 1.15 land miles per hour.</li>  </ul></div>	-
24.12.0	glossary/Sourdough Glossary	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Sourdough Glossary</h3>  <p class="muted t-small">The words that show up in every recipe, decoded for you, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p>  <ul class="list">    <li><b>Starter</b> — Flour and water colonised by wild yeast. Your pet, basically. Feed it or it sulks.</li>    <li><b>Levain</b> — A fresh offshoot of the starter, built for one bake. The starter's understudy.</li>    <li><b>Autolyse</b> — Resting flour and water before adding starter. Gluten starts forming while you do nothing.</li>    <li><b>Hydration</b> — Water as a percentage of flour weight. 75% means 750 g water per 1000 g flour.</li>    <li><b>Bulk ferment</b> — The first long rise, dough in one mass, before shaping.</li>    <li><b>Banneton</b> — The woven basket that holds the shaped loaf and gives it those spiral lines.</li>    <li><b>Crumb</b> — The inside texture. Open crumb = big airy holes.</li>    <li><b>Scoring</b> — Slashing the top so steam escapes where you want it.</li>  </ul>  <div class="callout info">    <b>Handy rule:</b> if the dough feels like a warm earlobe, it's probably proofed.  </div></div>	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Sourdough Glossary</h3>  <p class="muted t-small">The words that show up in every recipe, decoded for you, .</p>  <ul class="list">    <li><b>Starter</b> — Flour and water colonised by wild yeast. Your pet, basically. Feed it or it sulks.</li>    <li><b>Levain</b> — A fresh offshoot of the starter, built for one bake. The starter's understudy.</li>    <li><b>Autolyse</b> — Resting flour and water before adding starter. Gluten starts forming while you do nothing.</li>    <li><b>Hydration</b> — Water as a percentage of flour weight. 75% means 750 g water per 1000 g flour.</li>    <li><b>Bulk ferment</b> — The first long rise, dough in one mass, before shaping.</li>    <li><b>Banneton</b> — The woven basket that holds the shaped loaf and gives it those spiral lines.</li>    <li><b>Crumb</b> — The inside texture. Open crumb = big airy holes.</li>    <li><b>Scoring</b> — Slashing the top so steam escapes where you want it.</li>  </ul>  <div class="callout info">    <b>Handy rule:</b> if the dough feels like a warm earlobe, it's probably proofed.  </div></div>	-
24.13.0	glossary/Running Glossary	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Running Glossary</h3>  <p class="muted t-small">Lace up, <regex>user_name</regex>. Here's the vocabulary of the pavement.</p>  <ul class="list">    <li><b>Split</b> — The time for one segment, usually a mile or kilometre.</li>    <li><b>Negative split</b> — Second half faster than the first. The classy way to race.</li>    <li><b>Cadence</b> — Steps per minute. Most runners land somewhere near 170–180.</li>    <li><b>Tempo run</b> — Comfortably hard, held steadily. You can speak, but not chat.</li>    <li><b>Fartlek</b> — Swedish for speed play: fast bursts scattered through an easy run.</li>    <li><b>Easy pace</b> — Genuinely easy. If it feels sluggish, it's working.</li>    <li><b>Taper</b> — Cutting volume before race day so your legs arrive fresh.</li>    <li><b>PR / PB</b> — Personal record, personal best. Same thing, different continents.</li>  </ul>  <div class="callout good">    <b>Note:</b> sore is normal, sharp is a message. Listen to the message.  </div></div>	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Running Glossary</h3>  <p class="muted t-small">Lace up, . Here's the vocabulary of the pavement.</p>  <ul class="list">    <li><b>Split</b> — The time for one segment, usually a mile or kilometre.</li>    <li><b>Negative split</b> — Second half faster than the first. The classy way to race.</li>    <li><b>Cadence</b> — Steps per minute. Most runners land somewhere near 170–180.</li>    <li><b>Tempo run</b> — Comfortably hard, held steadily. You can speak, but not chat.</li>    <li><b>Fartlek</b> — Swedish for speed play: fast bursts scattered through an easy run.</li>    <li><b>Easy pace</b> — Genuinely easy. If it feels sluggish, it's working.</li>    <li><b>Taper</b> — Cutting volume before race day so your legs arrive fresh.</li>    <li><b>PR / PB</b> — Personal record, personal best. Same thing, different continents.</li>  </ul>  <div class="callout good">    <b>Note:</b> sore is normal, sharp is a message. Listen to the message.  </div></div>	-
24.14.0	glossary/Wine Glossary	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Wine Glossary</h3>  <p class="muted t-small">No sommelier required, <regex>user_name</regex>. Just the words people actually use.</p>  <ul class="list">    <li><b>Dry</b> — Not sweet. Almost all table wine is dry, even the fruity ones.</li>    <li><b>Tannin</b> — The grippy, drying feeling from grape skins. Strong in reds, absent in most whites.</li>    <li><b>Body</b> — Weight on your tongue. Skim milk versus cream.</li>    <li><b>Acidity</b> — The fresh, mouth-watering zip. High acidity makes food taste better.</li>    <li><b>Bouquet</b> — Aromas that develop with age, not from the grape itself.</li>    <li><b>Legs</b> — The streaks running down the glass. Mostly about alcohol, not quality.</li>    <li><b>Terroir</b> — The place: soil, slope, climate. The wine's accent.</li>  </ul>  <div class="callout info">    <b>Shortcut:</b> if you like it, it's good. That's the whole exam.  </div></div>	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Wine Glossary</h3>  <p class="muted t-small">No sommelier required, . Just the words people actually use.</p>  <ul class="list">    <li><b>Dry</b> — Not sweet. Almost all table wine is dry, even the fruity ones.</li>    <li><b>Tannin</b> — The grippy, drying feeling from grape skins. Strong in reds, absent in most whites.</li>    <li><b>Body</b> — Weight on your tongue. Skim milk versus cream.</li>    <li><b>Acidity</b> — The fresh, mouth-watering zip. High acidity makes food taste better.</li>    <li><b>Bouquet</b> — Aromas that develop with age, not from the grape itself.</li>    <li><b>Legs</b> — The streaks running down the glass. Mostly about alcohol, not quality.</li>    <li><b>Terroir</b> — The place: soil, slope, climate. The wine's accent.</li>  </ul>  <div class="callout info">    <b>Shortcut:</b> if you like it, it's good. That's the whole exam.  </div></div>	-
24.15.0	glossary/Coding Interview Glossary	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Interview Glossary</h3>  <p class="muted t-small">The words interviewers say like everyone already knows them, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p>  <ul class="list">    <li><b>Big O</b> — How runtime grows as input grows. O(n) scales with size, O(1) doesn't care.</li>    <li><b>Time complexity</b> — Cost in steps. <b>Space complexity</b> — cost in memory.</li>    <li><b>Hash map</b> — Key-to-value lookup, usually O(1). Your first move for "have I seen this?"</li>    <li><b>Brute force</b> — The obvious slow answer. Say it out loud, then improve it.</li>    <li><b>Edge case</b> — Empty input, one item, duplicates, huge numbers. Where solutions break.</li>    <li><b>Two pointers</b> — Walking two indexes toward each other. Sneaky fast.</li>    <li><b>Trade-off</b> — Faster usually costs memory. Say which you chose, and why.</li>  </ul>  <div class="callout warn">    <b>Tip:</b> narrate your thinking. Silence reads as stuck, even when you're not.  </div></div>	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Interview Glossary</h3>  <p class="muted t-small">The words interviewers say like everyone already knows them, .</p>  <ul class="list">    <li><b>Big O</b> — How runtime grows as input grows. O(n) scales with size, O(1) doesn't care.</li>    <li><b>Time complexity</b> — Cost in steps. <b>Space complexity</b> — cost in memory.</li>    <li><b>Hash map</b> — Key-to-value lookup, usually O(1). Your first move for "have I seen this?"</li>    <li><b>Brute force</b> — The obvious slow answer. Say it out loud, then improve it.</li>    <li><b>Edge case</b> — Empty input, one item, duplicates, huge numbers. Where solutions break.</li>    <li><b>Two pointers</b> — Walking two indexes toward each other. Sneaky fast.</li>    <li><b>Trade-off</b> — Faster usually costs memory. Say which you chose, and why.</li>  </ul>  <div class="callout warn">    <b>Tip:</b> narrate your thinking. Silence reads as stuck, even when you're not.  </div></div>	-
