24.17.0	glossary/Investing Glossary	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Investing Glossary</h3>  <p class="muted t-small">Plain English, no suits, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p>  <ul class="list">    <li><b>Index fund</b> — A basket that copies a whole market, so you don't pick winners.</li>    <li><b>Expense ratio</b> — The yearly fee, as a percent. 0.05% is cheap; 1% eats a lot over decades.</li>    <li><b>Diversification</b> — Spreading money so one bad bet can't sink everything.</li>    <li><b>Compound interest</b> — Growth on your growth. Boring early, startling later.</li>    <li><b>Bull market</b> — Prices rising. <b>Bear market</b> — down about 20% or more.</li>    <li><b>Volatility</b> — How jumpy prices are. Not the same as risk, but friends with it.</li>    <li><b>Dollar-cost averaging</b> — Investing a fixed amount on a schedule, ignoring the noise.</li>  </ul>  <div class="callout warn">    <b>Not advice:</b> I explain words. Your money, your call.  </div></div>	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Investing Glossary</h3>  <p class="muted t-small">Plain English, no suits, .</p>  <ul class="list">    <li><b>Index fund</b> — A basket that copies a whole market, so you don't pick winners.</li>    <li><b>Expense ratio</b> — The yearly fee, as a percent. 0.05% is cheap; 1% eats a lot over decades.</li>    <li><b>Diversification</b> — Spreading money so one bad bet can't sink everything.</li>    <li><b>Compound interest</b> — Growth on your growth. Boring early, startling later.</li>    <li><b>Bull market</b> — Prices rising. <b>Bear market</b> — down about 20% or more.</li>    <li><b>Volatility</b> — How jumpy prices are. Not the same as risk, but friends with it.</li>    <li><b>Dollar-cost averaging</b> — Investing a fixed amount on a schedule, ignoring the noise.</li>  </ul>  <div class="callout warn">    <b>Not advice:</b> I explain words. Your money, your call.  </div></div>	-
24.18.0	glossary/Chess Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Chess Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">Hey <regex>user_name</regex> — the words that make chess clubs sound secretive.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Fork</b> — one piece attacks two at once. Rude, effective.</li><li><b>Pin</b> — a piece can't move without exposing something better behind it.</li><li><b>Skewer</b> — like a pin, but the valuable piece is in front.</li><li><b>En passant</b> — a pawn's one-shot revenge on a neighbour's two-square dash.</li><li><b>Castling</b> — king and rook swap seats, once per game, king first.</li><li><b>Zugzwang</b> — German for "your move, sadly." Every option hurts.</li><li><b>Tempo</b> — one move of time. Lose a few and you'll feel it.</li><li><b>Fianchetto</b> — bishop tucked on the long diagonal, biding its time.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Notation, decoded</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small"><code>Nf3</code> = knight to f3. <code>O-O</code> = castle kingside. <code>+</code> check, <code>#</code> mate, <code>x</code> takes.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Chess Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">Hey  — the words that make chess clubs sound secretive.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Fork</b> — one piece attacks two at once. Rude, effective.</li><li><b>Pin</b> — a piece can't move without exposing something better behind it.</li><li><b>Skewer</b> — like a pin, but the valuable piece is in front.</li><li><b>En passant</b> — a pawn's one-shot revenge on a neighbour's two-square dash.</li><li><b>Castling</b> — king and rook swap seats, once per game, king first.</li><li><b>Zugzwang</b> — German for "your move, sadly." Every option hurts.</li><li><b>Tempo</b> — one move of time. Lose a few and you'll feel it.</li><li><b>Fianchetto</b> — bishop tucked on the long diagonal, biding its time.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Notation, decoded</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small"><code>Nf3</code> = knight to f3. <code>O-O</code> = castle kingside. <code>+</code> check, <code>#</code> mate, <code>x</code> takes.</p></div></details></div>	-
24.19.0	glossary/Brewing Beer Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Brewing Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small"><regex>user_name</regex>, here's the vocabulary before your first boil-over.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Wort</b> — sugary liquid before yeast gets involved. Pronounced "wert."</li><li><b>Mash</b> — steeping malt in warm water to convert starch to sugar.</li><li><b>Sparge</b> — rinsing the grains with hot water to grab the last sugars.</li><li><b>OG / FG</b> — original and final gravity; the gap becomes alcohol.</li><li><b>IBU</b> — bitterness units. Higher means hoppier, not stronger.</li><li><b>Dry hopping</b> — adding hops after fermentation for aroma without bite.</li><li><b>Krausen</b> — the foamy cap on fermenting beer. Healthy, not scary.</li><li><b>Flocculation</b> — how eagerly yeast clumps and drops out. Clear beer, happy brewer.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>ABV, quickly</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Roughly <code>(OG − FG) × 131</code>. A 1.050 to 1.010 beer lands near 5.2%.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Brewing Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">, here's the vocabulary before your first boil-over.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Wort</b> — sugary liquid before yeast gets involved. Pronounced "wert."</li><li><b>Mash</b> — steeping malt in warm water to convert starch to sugar.</li><li><b>Sparge</b> — rinsing the grains with hot water to grab the last sugars.</li><li><b>OG / FG</b> — original and final gravity; the gap becomes alcohol.</li><li><b>IBU</b> — bitterness units. Higher means hoppier, not stronger.</li><li><b>Dry hopping</b> — adding hops after fermentation for aroma without bite.</li><li><b>Krausen</b> — the foamy cap on fermenting beer. Healthy, not scary.</li><li><b>Flocculation</b> — how eagerly yeast clumps and drops out. Clear beer, happy brewer.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>ABV, quickly</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Roughly <code>(OG − FG) × 131</code>. A 1.050 to 1.010 beer lands near 5.2%.</p></div></details></div>	-
24.20.0	glossary/Weather Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Weather Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">The forecast, translated, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Dew point</b> — the temperature air must cool to for fog or sweat. Above 65°F feels muggy.</li><li><b>Relative humidity</b> — how full the air is, relative to its own temperature. Sneaky number.</li><li><b>Wind chill</b> — what the wind makes it feel like on skin.</li><li><b>Trough</b> — a long dip in pressure. Often brings unsettled weather.</li><li><b>Ridge</b> — the opposite: a pressure hump, usually calmer and warmer.</li><li><b>Heat dome</b> — a stubborn ridge parked overhead, trapping hot air.</li><li><b>Virga</b> — rain that evaporates before it reaches you. All tease, no soak.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Why dew point beats humidity</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Humidity changes with temperature; dew point doesn't. 55°F feels nice, 70°F feels like soup.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Weather Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">The forecast, translated, .</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Dew point</b> — the temperature air must cool to for fog or sweat. Above 65°F feels muggy.</li><li><b>Relative humidity</b> — how full the air is, relative to its own temperature. Sneaky number.</li><li><b>Wind chill</b> — what the wind makes it feel like on skin.</li><li><b>Trough</b> — a long dip in pressure. Often brings unsettled weather.</li><li><b>Ridge</b> — the opposite: a pressure hump, usually calmer and warmer.</li><li><b>Heat dome</b> — a stubborn ridge parked overhead, trapping hot air.</li><li><b>Virga</b> — rain that evaporates before it reaches you. All tease, no soak.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Why dew point beats humidity</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Humidity changes with temperature; dew point doesn't. 55°F feels nice, 70°F feels like soup.</p></div></details></div>	-
24.21.0	glossary/Sewing Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Sewing Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">Pattern envelopes talk fast, <regex>user_name</regex>. Here's the crib sheet.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Seam allowance</b> — the extra fabric beyond the stitch line. Usually 5/8".</li><li><b>Grainline</b> — the arrow showing how a piece aligns with the fabric's threads.</li><li><b>Selvage</b> — the tightly woven finished edge. Don't cut it off.</li><li><b>Basting</b> — long, loose stitches to hold things temporarily.</li><li><b>Notch</b> — a small snip marking where two pieces should meet.</li><li><b>Clipping</b> — cutting into a curve's allowance so it lies flat.</li><li><b>Nap</b> — fabric's one-way texture. Velvet will show if you cut it wrong.</li><li><b>Ease</b> — fabric's tiny stretch, or extra room in a garment.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Bias, briefly</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Cut at 45° to the grain and fabric drapes beautifully — and stretches. Great for binding, tricky for structure.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Sewing Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">Pattern envelopes talk fast, . Here's the crib sheet.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Seam allowance</b> — the extra fabric beyond the stitch line. Usually 5/8".</li><li><b>Grainline</b> — the arrow showing how a piece aligns with the fabric's threads.</li><li><b>Selvage</b> — the tightly woven finished edge. Don't cut it off.</li><li><b>Basting</b> — long, loose stitches to hold things temporarily.</li><li><b>Notch</b> — a small snip marking where two pieces should meet.</li><li><b>Clipping</b> — cutting into a curve's allowance so it lies flat.</li><li><b>Nap</b> — fabric's one-way texture. Velvet will show if you cut it wrong.</li><li><b>Ease</b> — fabric's tiny stretch, or extra room in a garment.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Bias, briefly</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Cut at 45° to the grain and fabric drapes beautifully — and stretches. Great for binding, tricky for structure.</p></div></details></div>	-
24.22.0	glossary/Cocktail Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Cocktail Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">For when the menu is all confidence and no footnotes, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Neat</b> — room temperature, no ice, straight in the glass.</li><li><b>Up</b> — chilled and strained into a stemmed glass. Still no ice.</li><li><b>On the rocks</b> — poured over ice. Yes, it dilutes. That's the point.</li><li><b>Muddle</b> — pressing fruit or herbs to release flavour. Gentle, not a mash.</li><li><b>Jigger</b> — the two-sided measuring cup. Trust it.</li><li><b>Dry</b> — less vermouth (martinis), or less sweetness generally.</li><li><b>Rinse</b> — swirling a spirit around the glass, then tipping it out.</li><li><b>Double strain</b> — a fine sieve catches shards and pulp.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Shaken vs stirred</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Shake citrus, dairy, and egg. Stir spirit-only drinks so they stay silky and clear.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Cocktail Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">For when the menu is all confidence and no footnotes, .</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Neat</b> — room temperature, no ice, straight in the glass.</li><li><b>Up</b> — chilled and strained into a stemmed glass. Still no ice.</li><li><b>On the rocks</b> — poured over ice. Yes, it dilutes. That's the point.</li><li><b>Muddle</b> — pressing fruit or herbs to release flavour. Gentle, not a mash.</li><li><b>Jigger</b> — the two-sided measuring cup. Trust it.</li><li><b>Dry</b> — less vermouth (martinis), or less sweetness generally.</li><li><b>Rinse</b> — swirling a spirit around the glass, then tipping it out.</li><li><b>Double strain</b> — a fine sieve catches shards and pulp.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Shaken vs stirred</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Shake citrus, dairy, and egg. Stir spirit-only drinks so they stay silky and clear.</p></div></details></div>	-
24.23.0	glossary/Birdwatching Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Birdwatching Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">Birders speak in code. Here's the key, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Lifer</b> — a species you've never seen before. The good stuff.</li><li><b>Patch</b> — your local, regularly-walked birding spot.</li><li><b>Twitch</b> — dashing off to see a rare bird someone else found.</li><li><b>Mega</b> — a rarity so unusual it empties offices.</li><li><b>Peep</b> — a small sandpiper. Not a sound, a bird.</li><li><b>Jizz</b> — the overall vibe of a bird: shape, movement, posture. Genuinely a real term.</li><li><b>Pishing</b> — a soft shushing noise that draws curious songbirds in.</li><li><b>Fallout</b> — migrants grounded en masse by weather. Chaos, delightfully.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Good binoculars, fast</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Look for 8×42: steady, bright, forgiving. 10× zooms sound tempting and shake a lot.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Birdwatching Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">Birders speak in code. Here's the key, .</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Lifer</b> — a species you've never seen before. The good stuff.</li><li><b>Patch</b> — your local, regularly-walked birding spot.</li><li><b>Twitch</b> — dashing off to see a rare bird someone else found.</li><li><b>Mega</b> — a rarity so unusual it empties offices.</li><li><b>Peep</b> — a small sandpiper. Not a sound, a bird.</li><li><b>Jizz</b> — the overall vibe of a bird: shape, movement, posture. Genuinely a real term.</li><li><b>Pishing</b> — a soft shushing noise that draws curious songbirds in.</li><li><b>Fallout</b> — migrants grounded en masse by weather. Chaos, delightfully.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Good binoculars, fast</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Look for 8×42: steady, bright, forgiving. 10× zooms sound tempting and shake a lot.</p></div></details></div>	-
24.24.0	glossary/Tea Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Tea, decoded</h4><p class="muted t-small">Six words that cover most of the tin. <regex>user_name</regex>, tap any row for more.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Steeping</b> — soaking leaves in hot water to pull out flavour. Time and temperature do the heavy lifting.</li><li><b>First flush</b> — the first spring picking. Lighter, greener, often pricier.</li><li><b>Liquor</b> — the brewed liquid itself, not the leaves. A tasting word.</li><li><b>Oxidation</b> — how much the leaf browned before drying. Green is barely, black is fully.</li><li><b>Orange pekoe</b> — a leaf grade, not a flavour. It says nothing about citrus.</li><li><b>Gongfu</b> — short, repeated infusions in a small vessel. Lots of tea, little water.</li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Tea, decoded</h4><p class="muted t-small">Six words that cover most of the tin. , tap any row for more.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Steeping</b> — soaking leaves in hot water to pull out flavour. Time and temperature do the heavy lifting.</li><li><b>First flush</b> — the first spring picking. Lighter, greener, often pricier.</li><li><b>Liquor</b> — the brewed liquid itself, not the leaves. A tasting word.</li><li><b>Oxidation</b> — how much the leaf browned before drying. Green is barely, black is fully.</li><li><b>Orange pekoe</b> — a leaf grade, not a flavour. It says nothing about citrus.</li><li><b>Gongfu</b> — short, repeated infusions in a small vessel. Lots of tea, little water.</li></ul></div>	-
24.25.0	glossary/Jazz Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Jazz, in plain words</h4><p class="muted t-small">For <regex>user_name</regex>, before the next jam.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Words</span><span class="value">6</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Reading time</span><span class="value">40<span class="unit">s</span></span></div></div><ul class="list"><li><b>Standard</b> — a tune everyone knows by heart, like a shared poem.</li><li><b>Comping</b> — chords played under a soloist. Support, not spotlight.</li><li><b>Head</b> — the written melody, played first and last.</li><li><b>Changes</b> — the chord progression. "Follow the changes."</li><li><b>In the pocket</b> — locked into the groove so tightly it feels easy.</li><li><b>Trading fours</b> — soloists swapping four-bar phrases like a chat.</li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Jazz, in plain words</h4><p class="muted t-small">For , before the next jam.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Words</span><span class="value">6</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Reading time</span><span class="value">40<span class="unit">s</span></span></div></div><ul class="list"><li><b>Standard</b> — a tune everyone knows by heart, like a shared poem.</li><li><b>Comping</b> — chords played under a soloist. Support, not spotlight.</li><li><b>Head</b> — the written melody, played first and last.</li><li><b>Changes</b> — the chord progression. "Follow the changes."</li><li><b>In the pocket</b> — locked into the groove so tightly it feels easy.</li><li><b>Trading fours</b> — soloists swapping four-bar phrases like a chat.</li></ul></div>	-
24.26.0	glossary/Chess Openings Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Chess openings, explained</h4><p class="muted t-small">The first ten moves have names. Here are six, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Gambit</b> — offering a pawn for faster development. A bribe with pieces.</li><li><b>Fianchetto</b> — bishop tucked on the long diagonal, eyeing the centre from afar.</li><li><b>Tempo</b> — one move's worth of time. Losing one is like being late to your own party.</li><li><b>Castling</b> — king and rook swap spots in one move. Safety plus activation.</li><li><b>Open file</b> — a column with no pawns. Rooks love it there.</li><li><b>Zugzwang</b> — every move you have makes things worse, but you must move.</li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Chess openings, explained</h4><p class="muted t-small">The first ten moves have names. Here are six, .</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Gambit</b> — offering a pawn for faster development. A bribe with pieces.</li><li><b>Fianchetto</b> — bishop tucked on the long diagonal, eyeing the centre from afar.</li><li><b>Tempo</b> — one move's worth of time. Losing one is like being late to your own party.</li><li><b>Castling</b> — king and rook swap spots in one move. Safety plus activation.</li><li><b>Open file</b> — a column with no pawns. Rooks love it there.</li><li><b>Zugzwang</b> — every move you have makes things worse, but you must move.</li></ul></div>	-
