24.28.0	glossary/Bike Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>On the bike, decoded</h4><p class="muted t-small">Six words to survive the group ride, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Cadence</b> — how fast you pedal, in revolutions per minute. Spin, don't grind.</li><li><b>Drafting</b> — tucking behind another rider to save effort. Legal, sociable theft of wind.</li><li><b>Bonk</b> — running out of fuel mid-ride. Sudden, humbling, fixable with a snack.</li><li><b>Granny gear</b> — the easiest combo for steep hills. No shame in it.</li><li><b>Saddle sore</b> — exactly what it sounds like. Chamois cream is your friend.</li><li><b>Paceline</b> — riders rotating in a chain, each taking a turn in the wind.</li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>On the bike, decoded</h4><p class="muted t-small">Six words to survive the group ride, .</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Cadence</b> — how fast you pedal, in revolutions per minute. Spin, don't grind.</li><li><b>Drafting</b> — tucking behind another rider to save effort. Legal, sociable theft of wind.</li><li><b>Bonk</b> — running out of fuel mid-ride. Sudden, humbling, fixable with a snack.</li><li><b>Granny gear</b> — the easiest combo for steep hills. No shame in it.</li><li><b>Saddle sore</b> — exactly what it sounds like. Chamois cream is your friend.</li><li><b>Paceline</b> — riders rotating in a chain, each taking a turn in the wind.</li></ul></div>	-
24.29.0	glossary/Meditation Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Meditation, minus the incense</h4><p class="muted t-small">Plain definitions for <regex>user_name</regex>. No cushion required.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Mindfulness</b> — noticing what's happening right now, without arguing with it.</li><li><b>Monkey mind</b> — thoughts swinging from branch to branch. Normal, not a failure.</li><li><b>Mantra</b> — a word or phrase you repeat to give the mind a rail to hold.</li><li><b>Body scan</b> — moving attention slowly from head to toes, checking in as you go.</li><li><b>Anchor</b> — whatever you return to when you drift: breath, sound, feet.</li><li><b>Sitting</b> — the practice itself. Boring on purpose, useful in practice.</li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Meditation, minus the incense</h4><p class="muted t-small">Plain definitions for . No cushion required.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Mindfulness</b> — noticing what's happening right now, without arguing with it.</li><li><b>Monkey mind</b> — thoughts swinging from branch to branch. Normal, not a failure.</li><li><b>Mantra</b> — a word or phrase you repeat to give the mind a rail to hold.</li><li><b>Body scan</b> — moving attention slowly from head to toes, checking in as you go.</li><li><b>Anchor</b> — whatever you return to when you drift: breath, sound, feet.</li><li><b>Sitting</b> — the practice itself. Boring on purpose, useful in practice.</li></ul></div>	-
24.30.0	glossary/Bread Glossary	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">Bread Glossary</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">Fresh from the oven, <regex>user_name</regex>. Tap a term for the details.</p>  <div class="stack">    <details class="acc"><summary>Hydration</summary>      <div class="body"><p>Water as a percentage of flour weight. 75% hydration means 750g water per 1kg flour. Higher hydration means a more open, holey crumb.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Autolyse</summary>      <div class="body"><p>Resting flour and water before adding salt or yeast, letting gluten form on its own. Twenty minutes does a lot of quiet work.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Bulk Ferment</summary>      <div class="body"><p>The first rise, from mixing to shaping. This is where flavour is built. Rushing it is the classic beginner's mistake.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Proof</summary>      <div class="body"><p>The second rise, usually in the shaped loaf. Under-proofed dough springs back fast when poked; over-proofed dough leaves a dent.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Levain / Starter</summary>      <div class="body"><p>A flour-and-water culture of wild yeast and bacteria. Feed it, and it rewards you with lift and tang.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Crumb</summary>      <div class="body"><p>The inside structure of the loaf. Not the same as crumbs on your counter, though those happen too.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Baker's Percentage</summary>      <div class="body"><p>A system where flour is always 100% and everything else is measured against it. Makes scaling a recipe painless.</p></div>    </details>  </div></div>	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">Bread Glossary</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">Fresh from the oven, . Tap a term for the details.</p>  <div class="stack">    <details class="acc"><summary>Hydration</summary>      <div class="body"><p>Water as a percentage of flour weight. 75% hydration means 750g water per 1kg flour. Higher hydration means a more open, holey crumb.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Autolyse</summary>      <div class="body"><p>Resting flour and water before adding salt or yeast, letting gluten form on its own. Twenty minutes does a lot of quiet work.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Bulk Ferment</summary>      <div class="body"><p>The first rise, from mixing to shaping. This is where flavour is built. Rushing it is the classic beginner's mistake.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Proof</summary>      <div class="body"><p>The second rise, usually in the shaped loaf. Under-proofed dough springs back fast when poked; over-proofed dough leaves a dent.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Levain / Starter</summary>      <div class="body"><p>A flour-and-water culture of wild yeast and bacteria. Feed it, and it rewards you with lift and tang.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Crumb</summary>      <div class="body"><p>The inside structure of the loaf. Not the same as crumbs on your counter, though those happen too.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Baker's Percentage</summary>      <div class="body"><p>A system where flour is always 100% and everything else is measured against it. Makes scaling a recipe painless.</p></div>    </details>  </div></div>	-
24.31.0	glossary/Chess Endgames Glossary	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">Chess Endgame Glossary</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">Fewer pieces, sharper ideas, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p>  <div class="stack">    <details class="acc"><summary>Zugzwang</summary>      <div class="body"><p>German for "compulsion to move." Every move you make worsens your position, but pass is not allowed. Cruel and elegant.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Opposition</summary>      <div class="body"><p>When kings face each other with one square between them. Whoever must move loses the standoff—a king-and-pawn endgame essential.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Promotion</summary>      <div class="body"><p>A pawn reaching the last rank becomes any piece, almost always a queen. "Underpromotion" to a knight is rare and glorious.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Lucena Position</summary>      <div class="body"><p>A classic rook-and-pawn winning setup. Learn it once and you'll spot it for life.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Fortress</summary>      <div class="body"><p>A drawn setup where the weaker side walls up so completely that the extra material can't break through.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Fifty-Move Rule</summary>      <div class="body"><p>If fifty moves pass with no capture and no pawn move, either player may claim a draw. Memory aid for long grinds.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Triangulation</summary>      <div class="body"><p>A king manoeuvre that wastes a tempo to hand the opponent the zugzwang. Sneaky, satisfying, worth practising.</p></div>    </details>  </div></div>	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">Chess Endgame Glossary</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">Fewer pieces, sharper ideas, .</p>  <div class="stack">    <details class="acc"><summary>Zugzwang</summary>      <div class="body"><p>German for "compulsion to move." Every move you make worsens your position, but pass is not allowed. Cruel and elegant.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Opposition</summary>      <div class="body"><p>When kings face each other with one square between them. Whoever must move loses the standoff—a king-and-pawn endgame essential.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Promotion</summary>      <div class="body"><p>A pawn reaching the last rank becomes any piece, almost always a queen. "Underpromotion" to a knight is rare and glorious.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Lucena Position</summary>      <div class="body"><p>A classic rook-and-pawn winning setup. Learn it once and you'll spot it for life.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Fortress</summary>      <div class="body"><p>A drawn setup where the weaker side walls up so completely that the extra material can't break through.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Fifty-Move Rule</summary>      <div class="body"><p>If fifty moves pass with no capture and no pawn move, either player may claim a draw. Memory aid for long grinds.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Triangulation</summary>      <div class="body"><p>A king manoeuvre that wastes a tempo to hand the opponent the zugzwang. Sneaky, satisfying, worth practising.</p></div>    </details>  </div></div>	-
24.32.0	glossary/Pottery Glossary	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">Pottery Glossary</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">Clay under your nails, words in your head, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p>  <div class="stack">    <details class="acc"><summary>Greenware</summary>      <div class="body"><p>Unfired clay that's bone-dry and ready for the kiln. Handle it like it owes you money—because it cracks easily.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Bisque Firing</summary>      <div class="body"><p>The first kiln run, which hardens the piece but leaves it porous so glaze can cling. Usually around cone 04.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Slip</summary>      <div class="body"><p>Clay mixed with water into a cream. Used to join pieces (score and slip) or cast shapes in plaster moulds.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Throwing</summary>      <div class="body"><p>Shaping clay on a spinning wheel. The centring step is where most beginners discover patience.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Wedging</summary>      <div class="body"><p>Kneading clay to remove air pockets and even out moisture. Skip it and your kiln will find the bubbles.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Glaze</summary>      <div class="body"><p>A glassy coating applied before the glaze firing. Runs the colour range from matte oatmeal to glossy cobalt.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Cone</summary>      <div class="body"><p>A small pyrometric stick that bends at a set temperature, telling you when the kiln has reached the right heat.</p></div>    </details>  </div></div>	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">Pottery Glossary</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">Clay under your nails, words in your head, .</p>  <div class="stack">    <details class="acc"><summary>Greenware</summary>      <div class="body"><p>Unfired clay that's bone-dry and ready f