24.33.0	glossary/Fishing Glossary	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">Fishing Glossary</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">Cast, wait, learn a word, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p>  <div class="stack">    <details class="acc"><summary>Bait vs Lure</summary>      <div class="body"><p>Bait is something a fish eats (worms, chunk bait). A lure is a fake that triggers a strike reaction. Different psychology, same rod.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Leader</summary>      <div class="body"><p>A short length of clear or heavier line between your main line and the hook. Adds stealth and abrasion resistance.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Drag</summary>      <div class="body"><p>The reel's tension setting. Too loose and a big fish runs; too tight and the line snaps. Set it by feel, not by hope.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Noodle Rod</summary>      <div class="body"><p>A very flexible rod, often used for light line and big fish. Bends almost double so the line doesn't break.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Backing</summary>      <div class="body"><p>Extra line behind the fly line on a reel. Buys you room when a fish decides to head for the far bank.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Strike Zone</summary>      <div class="body"><p>The depth band where fish are actively feeding. Find it and your catch rate triples; ignore it and you're just casting.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Snag / Hang-Up</summary>      <div class="body"><p>When your hook grabs underwater structure. Gentle jiggling beats yanking, every time.</p></div>    </details>  </div></div>	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">Fishing Glossary</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">Cast, wait, learn a word, .</p>  <div class="stack">    <details class="acc"><summary>Bait vs Lure</summary>      <div class="body"><p>Bait is something a fish eats (worms, chunk bait). A lure is a fake that triggers a strike reaction. Different psychology, same rod.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Leader</summary>      <div class="body"><p>A short length of clear or heavier line between your main line and the hook. Adds stealth and abrasion resistance.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Drag</summary>      <div class="body"><p>The reel's tension setting. Too loose and a big fish runs; too tight and the line snaps. Set it by feel, not by hope.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Noodle Rod</summary>      <div class="body"><p>A very flexible rod, often used for light line and big fish. Bends almost double so the line doesn't break.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Backing</summary>      <div class="body"><p>Extra line behind the fly line on a reel. Buys you room when a fish decides to head for the far bank.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Strike Zone</summary>      <div class="body"><p>The depth band where fish are actively feeding. Find it and your catch rate triples; ignore it and you're just casting.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Snag / Hang-Up</summary>      <div class="body"><p>When your hook grabs underwater structure. Gentle jiggling beats yanking, every time.</p></div>    </details>  </div></div>	-
24.34.0	glossary/Crochet Glossary	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">Crochet Glossary</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">Hooks, loops, and a few words that save you ripping out rows, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p>  <div class="stack">    <details class="acc"><summary>Chain (ch)</summary>      <div class="body"><p>The foundation stitch—a series of loops. Most patterns start with a chain, then build from there.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Single Crochet (sc)</summary>      <div class="body"><p>Insert hook, yarn over, pull through, yarn over, pull through both loops. Short and sturdy. Also called double crochet in UK patterns—yes, really.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Slip Stitch (sl st)</summary>      <div class="body"><p>The flattest stitch, used to join rounds or travel across without adding height. Nearly invisible.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Turning Chain</summary>      <div class="body"><p>Extra chains at the end of a row that replace the first stitch of the next row. Count them or your edges will drift.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Gauge</summary>      <div class="body"><p>Stitches and rows per inch. Match it or your sweater fits someone else. Swatch first, sigh later.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Magic Ring</summary>      <div class="body"><p>An adjustable loop for starting in-the-round projects with no hole in the middle. Amigurumi's best friend.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Frogging</summary>      <div class="body"><p>Ripping out stitches (rip-it, rip-it). Not a failure, just part of the process.</p></div>    </details>  </div></div>	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">Crochet Glossary</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">Hooks, loops, and a few words that save you ripping out rows, .</p>  <div class="stack">    <details class="acc"><summary>Chain (ch)</summary>      <div class="body"><p>The foundation stitch—a series of loops. Most patterns start with a chain, then build from there.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Single Crochet (sc)</summary>      <div class="body"><p>Insert hook, yarn over, pull through, yarn over, pull through both loops. Short and sturdy. Also called double crochet in UK patterns—yes, really.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Slip Stitch (sl st)</summary>      <div class="body"><p>The flattest stitch, used to join rounds or travel across without adding height. Nearly invisible.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Turning Chain</summary>      <div class="body"><p>Extra chains at the end of a row that replace the first stitch of the next row. Count them or your edges will drift.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Gauge</summary>      <div class="body"><p>Stitches and rows per inch. Match it or your sweater fits someone else. Swatch first, sigh later.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Magic Ring</summary>      <div class="body"><p>An adjustable loop for starting in-the-round projects with no hole in the middle. Amigurumi's best friend.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Frogging</summary>      <div class="body"><p>Ripping out stitches (rip-it, rip-it). Not a failure, just part of the process.</p></div>    </details>  </div></div>	-
24.35.0	glossary/Home Networking Glossary	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">Home Networking Glossary</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">For when the internet "just stops," <regex>user_name</regex>.</p>  <div class="stack">    <details class="acc"><summary>Router vs Modem</summary>      <div class="body"><p>The modem translates your provider's signal. The router shares that connection across your devices. Sometimes one box does both.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>DHCP</summary>      <div class="body"><p>The service that hands out IP addresses automatically. If it fails, devices show a 169.254 address and nothing works.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>DNS</summary>      <div class="body"><p>The phonebook that turns names like example.com into numeric addresses. Slow DNS feels like slow internet.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>NAT</summary>      <div class="body"><p>Network Address Translation lets many home devices share one public IP. The reason your laptop and phone don't fight over addresses.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Band</summary>      <div class="body"><p>Wi-Fi broadcasts on 2.4GHz (longer range, slower) and 5GHz (shorter range, faster). Pick by distance, not by pride.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Mesh</summary>      <div class="body"><p>Multiple access points working as one network. Great for big houses; useless if you only have one hallway.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>SSID</summary>      <div class="body"><p>The network's name. Don't name it something embarrassing, because your neighbours can see it.</p></div>    </details>  </div></div>	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">Home Networking Glossary</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">For when the internet "just stops," .</p>  <div class="stack">    <details class="acc"><summary>Router vs Modem</summary>      <div class="body"><p>The modem translates your provider's signal. The router shares that connection across your devices. Sometimes one box does both.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>DHCP</summary>      <div class="body"><p>The service that hands out IP addresses automatically. If it fails, devices show a 169.254 address and nothing works.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>DNS</summary>      <div class="body"><p>The phonebook that turns names like example.com into numeric addresses. Slow DNS feels like slow internet.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>NAT</summary>      <div class="body"><p>Network Address Translation lets many home devices share one public IP. The reason your laptop and phone don't fight over addresses.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Band</summary>      <div class="body"><p>Wi-Fi broadcasts on 2.4GHz (longer range, slower) and 5GHz (shorter range, faster). Pick by distance, not by pride.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>Mesh</summary>      <div class="body"><p>Multiple access points working as one network. Great for big houses; useless if you only have one hallway.</p></div>    </details>    <details class="acc"><summary>SSID</summary>      <div class="body"><p>The network's name. Don't name it something embarrassing, because your neighbours can see it.</p></div>    </details>  </div></div>	-
24.36.0	glossary/Woodworking Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Woodworking Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">Handy before you make your first pile of sawdust, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Grain</b> — the direction wood fibres run; cutting <i>with</i> it gives clean edges, against it gives splinters.</li><li><b>Kerf</b> — the slot a saw blade removes. A 3&nbsp;mm kerf means your board is 3&nbsp;mm narrower than you planned.</li><li><b>Rip vs crosscut</b> — rip cuts run along the grain, crosscuts go across it. Different blades, different feel.</li><li><b>Tearout</b> — ragged fibres left when a blade exits. Score the line or use tape to tame it.</li><li><b>Square</b> — a true 90° corner. <span class="dim">"Is it square?" is the woodworker's version of "are we there yet?"</span></li><li><b>MDF</b> — medium-density fibreboard; glue and sawdust pressed flat. Stable, dusty, hates water.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Quick safety aside</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Sharp tools need less force than dull ones, and less force means fewer surprises. Sharpen first, cut second.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Woodworking Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">Handy before you make your first pile of sawdust, .</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Grain</b> — the direction wood fibres run; cutting <i>with</i> it gives clean edges, against it gives splinters.</li><li><b>Kerf</b> — the slot a saw blade removes. A 3&nbsp;mm kerf means your board is 3&nbsp;mm narrower than you planned.</li><li><b>Rip vs crosscut</b> — rip cuts run along the grain, crosscuts go across it. Different blades, different feel.</li><li><b>Tearout</b> — ragged fibres left when a blade exits. Score the line or use tape to tame it.</li><li><b>Square</b> — a true 90° corner. <span class="dim">"Is it square?" is the woodworker's version of "are we there yet?"</span></li><li><b>MDF</b> — medium-density fibreboard; glue and sawdust pressed flat. Stable, dusty, hates water.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Quick safety aside</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Sharp tools need less force than dull ones, and less force means fewer surprises. Sharpen first, cut second.</p></div></details></div>	-
24.37.0	glossary/Anatomy Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Anatomy Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">The body's own map legend, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Anatomical position</b> — standing, palms forward, feet flat. Every direction word assumes this pose.</li><li><b>Superior / inferior</b> — toward the head / toward the feet.</li><li><b>Anterior / posterior</b> — front / back. In animals, often ventral / dorsal instead.</li><li><b>Medial / lateral</b> — closer to the midline / further from it.</li><li><b>Proximal / distal</b> — nearer the trunk / further out along a limb. Your elbow is proximal to your wrist.</li><li><b>Flexion / extension</b> — bending a joint / straightening it.</li><li><b>Abduction / adduction</b> — moving a limb away from the body / bringing it back in.</li></ul><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Learn these in pairs. One word alone is a coin with one side.</p></div></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Anatomy Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">The body's own map legend, .</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Anatomical position</b> — standing, palms forward, feet flat. Every direction word assumes this pose.</li><li><b>Superior / inferior</b> — toward the head / toward the feet.</li><li><b>Anterior / posterior</b> — front / back. In animals, often ventral / dorsal instead.</li><li><b>Medial / lateral</b> — closer to the midline / further from it.</li><li><b>Proximal / distal</b> — nearer the trunk / further out along a limb. Your elbow is proximal to your wrist.</li><li><b>Flexion / extension</b> — bending a joint / straightening it.</li><li><b>Abduction / adduction</b> — moving a limb away from the body / bringing it back in.</li></ul><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Learn these in pairs. One word alone is a coin with one side.</p></div></div>	-
24.38.0	glossary/Personal Finance Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Personal Finance Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">The words on the statements, in plain English, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>APR</b> — the yearly cost of borrowing, interest plus fees. The honest headline number.</li><li><b>Compound interest</b> — interest that earns interest. Small now, strange and mighty later.</li><li><b>Principal</b> — the amount you actually borrowed, before interest joins the party.</li><li><b>Deductible</b> — what you pay before insurance kicks in.</li><li><b>Premium</b> — the regular fee you pay to keep insurance alive.</li><li><b>Liquidity</b> — how fast an asset turns into cash without losing value. Your savings account: very. Your house: not at all.</li><li><b>Net worth</b> — everything you own minus everything you owe.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Order of operations</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Tiny emergency fund, then employer match, then high-interest debt, then everything else. Boring order, good results.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Personal Finance Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">The words on the statements, in plain English, .</p><ul class="list"><li><b>APR</b> — the yearly cost of borrowing, interest plus fees. The honest headline number.</li><li><b>Compound interest</b> — interest that earns interest. Small now, strange and mighty later.</li><li><b>Principal</b> — the amount you actually borrowed, before interest joins the party.</li><li><b>Deductible</b> — what you pay before insurance kicks in.</li><li><b>Premium</b> — the regular fee you pay to keep insurance alive.</li><li><b>Liquidity</b> — how fast an asset turns into cash without losing value. Your savings account: very. Your house: not at all.</li><li><b>Net worth</b> — everything you own minus everything you owe.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Order of operations</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Tiny eme