24.40.0	glossary/Chess Tactics Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Chess Tactics Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">The little traps, named at last, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Fork</b> — one piece attacks two at once. Knights are the classic troublemakers.</li><li><b>Pin</b> — a piece can't move without exposing something better behind it.</li><li><b>Skewer</b> — a pin backwards: the valuable piece is in front and must move.</li><li><b>Discovered attack</b> — you move one piece and unveil another's attack behind it.</li><li><b>Zwischenzug</b> — an "in-between move" that delays the expected reply. Slightly smug, very effective.</li><li><b>Back rank mate</b> — mate on the row behind the pawns, usually because the king is boxed in by its own friends.</li><li><b>Overloading</b> — giving a defender two jobs it cannot both do.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Practice tip</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Before every move, ask what your opponent just left undefended. Tactics live in that question.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Chess Tactics Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">The little traps, named at last, .</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Fork</b> — one piece attacks two at once. Knights are the classic troublemakers.</li><li><b>Pin</b> — a piece can't move without exposing something better behind it.</li><li><b>Skewer</b> — a pin backwards: the valuable piece is in front and must move.</li><li><b>Discovered attack</b> — you move one piece and unveil another's attack behind it.</li><li><b>Zwischenzug</b> — an "in-between move" that delays the expected reply. Slightly smug, very effective.</li><li><b>Back rank mate</b> — mate on the row behind the pawns, usually because the king is boxed in by its own friends.</li><li><b>Overloading</b> — giving a defender two jobs it cannot both do.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Practice tip</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Before every move, ask what your 