24.41.0	glossary/Canoeing Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Canoeing Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">Paddle in hand, words in pocket, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Bow / stern</b> — front / back of the boat. The bow paddler sets the rhythm, the stern steers.</li><li><b>J-stroke</b> — a stern stroke with a little hook at the end that keeps you going straight.</li><li><b>Eddy</b> — water swirling back upstream behind a rock or bend. Calm, and a great place to stop.</li><li><b>Ferry</b> — angling across a current without drifting downstream.</li><li><b>Portage</b> — carrying the canoe over land between waterways. Character building.</li><li><b>Gunwale</b> — the top edge of the hull, pronounced "gunnel".</li><li><b>Sweep stroke</b> — a wide arc that turns the boat.</li></ul><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Read the current before you read your phone. Rivers move faster than maps.</p></div></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Canoeing Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">Paddle in hand, words in pocket, .</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Bow / stern</b> — front / back of the boat. The bow paddler sets the rhythm, the stern steers.</li><li><b>J-stroke</b> — a stern stroke with a little hook at the end that keeps you going straight.</li><li><b>Eddy</b> — water swirling back upstream behind a rock or bend. Calm, and a great place to stop.</li><li><b>Ferry</b> — angling across a current without drifting downstream.</li><li><b>Portage</b> — carrying the canoe over land between waterways. Character building.</li><li><b>Gunwale</b> — the top edge of the hull, pronounced "gunnel".</li><li><b>Sweep stroke</b> — a wide arc that turns the boat.</li></ul><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Read the current before you read your phone. Rivers move faster than maps.</p></div></div>	-
24.42.0	glossary/Chess Middlegame Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Chess Middlegame Glossary</h4><p class="t-small muted">For <regex>user_name</regex> — the messy, fun middle part. ♟️</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Outpost</b> — a square on the 4th–6th rank that no enemy pawn can attack. A knight parked there is a tenant you can't evict cheaply.</li><li><b>Zugzwang</b> — German for "compulsion to move." Every legal move makes your position worse, but you must play one anyway.</li><li><b>Prophylaxis</b> — quietly preventing your opponent's idea before doing your own. Chess's version of locking the door first.</li><li><b>Fianchetto</b> — bishop tucked onto the long diagonal behind a knight-pawn (b2/g2/b7/g7). Slow, then suddenly not.</li><li><b>Doubled pawns</b> — two pawns stacked on one file. Weak, but they open the file next to them.</li><li><b>Loose piece</b> — a piece defending nothing and defended by nothing. Free-lunch energy.</li><li><b>Initiative</b> — the right to keep making threats rather than answering them.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Quick check</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">An enemy pawn can attack your outpost? Then it isn't an outpost — it's a square. Pawn attacks are the rule that matters.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Chess Middlegame Glossary</h4><p class="t-small muted">For  — the messy, fun middle part. ♟️</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Outpost</b> — a square on the 4th–6th rank that no enemy pawn can attack. A knight parked there is a tenant you can't evict cheaply.</li><li><b>Zugzwang</b> — German for "compulsion to move." Every legal move makes your position worse, but you must play one anyway.</li><li><b>Prophylaxis</b> — quietly preventing your opponent's idea before doing your own. Chess's version of locking the door first.</li><li><b>Fianchetto</b> — bishop tucked onto the long diagonal behind a knight-pawn (b2/g2/b7/g7). Slow, then suddenly not.</li><li><b>Doubled pawns</b> — two pawns stacked on one file. Weak, but they open the file next to them.</li><li><b>Loose piece</b> — a piece defending nothing and defended by nothing. Free-lunch energy.</li><li><b>Initiative</b> — the right to keep making threats rather than answering them.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Quick check</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">An enemy pawn can attack your outpost? Then it isn't an outpost — it's a square. Pawn attacks are the rule that matters.</p></div></details></div>	-
24.43.0	glossary/Basketball Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Basketball Glossary</h4><p class="t-small muted">For <regex>user_name</regex> — hoops words, in plain talk. 🏀</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Screen (pick)</b> — standing still so a defender has to go around you, freeing a teammate. Moving while screening is a foul.</li><li><b>Pick and roll</b> — set the screen, then roll toward the basket. Two players, one simple two-choice puzzle for the defense.</li><li><b>Box out</b> — put your body between your opponent and the rim before a shot lands. Rebounding is a positioning job, not a jumping contest.</li><li><b>Alley-oop</b> — a pass thrown near the rim, finished with a dunk or layup mid-air.</li><li><b>And-one</b> — you got fouled and the shot still went in. One free throw coming.</li><li><b>Double dribble</b> — picking up your dribble, then dribbling again. Rule: once you stop, you're a passer or shooter.</li><li><b>Travel</b> — taking too many steps without dribbling. Two is the number.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Why pick and roll works</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">The defender guarding the screener must choose: help on the ball handler, or stay with the roll. Either choice leaves somebody open.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Basketball Glossary</h4><p class="t-small muted">For  — hoops words, in plain talk. 🏀</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Screen (pick)</b> — standing still so a defender has to go around you, freeing a teammate. Moving while screening is a foul.</li><li><b>Pick and roll</b> — set the screen, then roll toward the basket. Two players, one simple two-choice puzzle for the defense.</li><li><b>Box out</b> — put your body between your opponent and the rim before a shot lands. Rebounding is a positioning job, not a jumping contest.</li><li><b>Alley-oop</b> — a pass thrown near the rim, finished with a dunk or layup mid-air.</li><li><b>And-one</b> — you got fouled and the shot still went in. One free throw coming.</li><li><b>Double dribble</b> — picking up your dribble, then dribbling again. Rule: once you stop, you're a passer or shooter.</li><li><b>Travel</b> — taking too many steps without dribbling. Two is the number.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Why pick and roll works</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">The defender guarding the screener must choose: help on the ball handler, or stay with the roll. Either choice leaves somebody open.</p></div></details></div>	-
24.44.0	glossary/Accounting Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Accounting Glossary</h4><p class="t-small muted">For <regex>user_name</regex> — the words your accountant says very fast. 🧾</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Accrual</b> — recording money when the work happens, not when cash moves.</li><li><b>Debit / credit</b> — left side / right side of an entry. Not "good / bad" — just directions.</li><li><b>Assets</b> — what you own. <b>Liabilities</b> — what you owe. <b>Equity</b> — the difference.</li><li><b>Revenue</b> — earned this period. <b>Profit</b> — what's left after costs. <b>Cash flow</b> — money actually moving. A profitable month can still be a broke week.</li><li><b>Depreciation</b> — spreading a big purchase's cost over the years it helps you.</li><li><b>Working capital</b> — current assets minus current liabilities. Your short-term breathing room.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>The one rule</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Every entry balances: debits equal credits. If it doesn't balance, something is missing, not "close enough."</p></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Accounting Glossary</h4><p class="t-small muted">For  — the words your accountant says very fast. 🧾</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Accrual</b> — recording money when the work happens, not when cash moves.</li><li><b>Debit / credit</b> — left side / right side of an entry. Not "good / bad" — just directions.</li><li><b>Assets</b> — what you own. <b>Liabilities</b> — what you owe. <b>Equity</b> — the difference.</li><li><b>Revenue</b> — earned this period. <b>Profit</b> — what's left after costs. <b>Cash flow</b> — money actually moving. A profitable month can still be a broke week.</li><li><b>Depreciation</b> — spreading a big purchase's cost over the years it helps you.</li><li><b>Working capital</b> — current assets minus current liabilities. Your short-term breathing room.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>The one rule</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Every entry balances: debits equal credits. If it doesn't balance, something is missing, not "close enough."</p></div></details></div>	-
24.45.0	glossary/Geology Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Geology Glossary</h4><p class="t-small muted">For <regex>user_name</regex> — the planet's vocabulary. 🪨</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Igneous</b> — cooled from melt. <b>Sedimentary</b> — glued from bits and pieces. <b>Metamorphic</b> — an existing rock squeezed or cooked into something new.</li><li><b>Magma vs. lava</b> — same stuff; magma is underground, lava is what you see erupt.</li><li><b>Weathering vs. erosion</b> — weathering breaks rock in place; erosion carries the pieces away.</li><li><b>Fault</b> — a crack where rock blocks slide past each other. <b>Strike-slip</b> means they slide sideways — think of two hands rubbing.</li><li><b>Moraine</b> — the pile of debris a glacier leaves behind when it melts back.</li><li><b>Lithification</b> — loose sediment getting compacted and cemented into rock.</li><li><b>Bedrock</b> — the solid stuff under all the loose dirt.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Handy shortcut</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Ask how it formed: melted, layered, or squeezed. That's the rock cycle in three questions.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Geology Glossary</h4><p class="t-small muted">For  — the planet's vocabulary. 🪨</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Igneous</b> — cooled from melt. <b>Sedimentary</b> — glued from bits and pieces. <b>Metamorphic</b> — an existing rock squeezed or cooked into something new.</li><li><b>Magma vs. lava</b> — same stuff; magma is underground, lava is what you see erupt.</li><li><b>Weathering vs. erosion</b> — weathering breaks rock in place; erosion carries the pieces away.</li><li><b>Fault</b> — a crack where rock blocks slide past each other. <b>Strike-slip</b> means they slide sideways — think of two hands rubbing.</li><li><b>Moraine</b> — the pile of debris a glacier leaves behind when it melts back.</li><li><b>Lithification</b> — loose sediment getting compacted and cemented into rock.</li><li><b>Bedrock</b> — the solid stuff under all the loose dirt.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Handy shortcut</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Ask how it formed: melted, layered, or squeezed. That's the rock cycle in three questions.</p></div></details></div>	-
24.46.0	glossary/Hiking Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Hiking Glossary</h4><p class="t-small muted">For <regex>user_name</regex> — trail words, so you know what you signed up for. 🥾</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Switchback</b> — a zigzag trail that trades distance for a gentler climb.</li><li><b>Scramble</b> — easy climbing with hands, no rope needed.</li><li><b>Cairn</b> — a stacked-rock marker showing the route, often where it isn't obvious.</li><li><b>Exposure</b> — how much a slip would hurt. High exposure means big drop-offs, not just steep ground.</li><li><b>Col / saddle</b> — the low point between two peaks. Usually the route over.</li><li><b>False summit</b> — the top that isn't. The real one is behind it, grinning.</li><li><b>Thru-hike</b> — one long trail end to end, like the AT or PCT.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Trail courtesy</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Uphill hikers have right of way. Step aside, say hi, and let them keep their rhythm.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Hiking Glossary</h4><p class="t-small muted">For  — trail words, so you know what you signed up for. 🥾</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Switchback</b> — a zigzag trail that trades distance for a gentler climb.</li><li><b>Scramble</b> — easy climbing with hands, no rope needed.</li><li><b>Cairn</b> — a stacked-rock marker showing the route, often where it isn't obvious.</li><li><b>Exposure</b> — how much a slip would hurt. High exposure means big drop-offs, not just steep ground.</li><li><b>Col / saddle</b> — the low point between two peaks. Usually the route over.</li><li><b>False summit</b> — the top that isn't. The real one is behind it, grinning.</li><li><b>Thru-hike</b> — one long trail end to end, like the AT or PCT.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Trail courtesy</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Uphill hikers have right of way. Step aside, say hi, and let them keep their rhythm.</p></div></details></div>	-
24.47.0	glossary/Tailoring & Alterations Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Tailoring & Alterations Glossary</h4><p class="t-small muted">For <regex>user_name</regex> — words that make clothes fit. 🪡</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Seam allowance</b> — the extra fabric past the stitch line, kept for future adjustments.</li><li><b>Basting</b> — long, loose stitches to hold things temporarily. Temporary on purpose, not sloppy.</li><li><b>Dart</b> — a wedge of fabric stitched to shape a curve, usually around the bust or waist.</li><li><b>Hem</b> — the finished bottom edge. <b>Cuff</b> — the finished sleeve edge.</li><li><b>Tack</b> — a few tiny stitches to hold a fold or lining in place invisibly.</li><li><b>Taking in</b> — sewing a new, smaller seam line inside the old one. Reversible until you trim.</li><li><b>Letting out</b> — releasing a seam to gain fabric. Works best when the allowance is still there.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Rule of thumb</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Don't cut until you've tried it on. Alterations you can undo are cheap; fabric you've trimmed isn't.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Tailoring & Alterations Glossary</h4><p class="t-small muted">For  — words that make clothes fit. 🪡</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Seam allowance</b> — the extra fabric past the stitch line, kept for future adjustments.</li><li><b>Basting</b> — long, loose stitches to hold things temporarily. Temporary on purpose, not sloppy.</li><li><b>Dart</b> — a wedge of fabric stitched to shape a curve, usually around the bust or waist.</li><li><b>Hem</b> — the finished bottom edge. <b>Cuff</b> — the finished sleeve edge.</li><li><b>Tack</b> — a few tiny stitches to hold a fold or lining in place invisibly.</li><li><b>Taking in</b> — sewing a new, smaller seam line inside the old one. Reversible until you trim.</li><li><b>Letting out</b> — releasing a seam to gain fabric. Works best when the allowance is still there.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Rule of thumb</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Don't cut until you've tried it on. Alterations you can undo are cheap; fabric you've trimmed isn't.</p></div></details></div>	-
24.48.0	glossary/Coffee Roasting Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Coffee Roasting Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">The words roasters throw around, decoded for you, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Green coffee</b> — raw, unroasted beans. Smell like hay, taste like nothing yet.</li><li><b>Charge</b> — dropping beans into a hot drum. The roast clock starts here.</li><li><b>Drying phase</b> — first stretch, beans go from grassy to yellow. Mostly water leaving.</li><li><b>Maillard reaction</b> — browning sugars and amino acids. Where toast and caramel flavors are born.</li><li><b>First crack</b> — beans pop like tiny popcorn as steam escapes. Light roast territory begins.</li><li><b>Second crack</b> — quieter, faster snapping. Dark roast, oils rising.</li><li><b>Development time</b> — everything after first crack. Short means bright, long means deeper.</li><li><b>Roast profile</b> — the temperature-vs-time curve. Your recipe, basically.</li><li><b>Resting</b> — letting roasted beans degas for a day or two before brewing.</li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Coffee Roasting Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">The words roasters throw around, decoded for you, .</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Green coffee</b> — raw, unroasted beans. Smell like hay, taste like nothing yet.</li><li><b>Charge</b> — dropping beans into a hot drum. The roast clock starts here.</li><li><b>Drying phase</b> — first stretch, beans go from grassy to yellow. Mostly water leaving.</li><li><b>Maillard reaction</b> — browning sugars and amino acids. Where toast and caramel flavors are born.</li><li><b>First crack</b> — beans pop like tiny popcorn as steam escapes. Light roast territory begins.</li><li><b>Second crack</b> — quieter, faster snapping. Dark roast, oils rising.</li><li><b>Development time</b> — everything after first crack. Short means bright, long means deeper.</li><li><b>Roast profile</b> — the temperature-vs-time curve. Your recipe, basically.</li><li><b>Resting</b> — letting roasted beans degas for a day or two before brewing.</li></ul></div>	-
24.49.0	glossary/Chess Puzzles Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Chess Puzzles Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">Tiny words, big checkmates. Here's the toolbox, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Fork</b> — one piece attacks two at once. Knights are the classic fork gremlins.</li><li><b>Pin</b> — a piece can't move without exposing something better behind it.</li><li><b>Skewer</b> — a reverse pin: the valuable piece is in front, forced to move.</li><li><b>Discovered attack</b> — you move one piece, revealing an attack from another.</li><li><b>Double check</b> — two pieces check the king at once. Only the king can respond.</li><li><b>Zwischenzug</b> — an in-between move that flips the expected order.</li><li><b>Back-rank mate</b> — the king trapped behind its own pawns. Ouch.</li><li><b>Mate in two</b> — a forced checkmate in exactly two of your moves.</li><li><b>Stalemate</b> — no legal moves, but not in check. A draw, and a puzzle trap.</li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Chess Puzzles Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">Tiny words, big checkmates. Here's the toolbox, .</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Fork</b> — one piece attacks two at once. Knights are the classic fork gremlins.</li><li><b>Pin</b> — a piece can't move without exposing something better behind it.</li><li><b>Skewer</b> — a reverse pin: the valuable piece is in front, forced to move.</li><li><b>Discovered attack</b> — you move one piece, revealing an attack from another.</li><li><b>Double check</b> — two pieces check the king at once. Only the king can respond.</li><li><b>Zwischenzug</b> — an in-between move that flips the expected order.</li><li><b>Back-rank mate</b> — the king trapped behind its own pawns. Ouch.</li><li><b>Mate in two</b> — a forced checkmate in exactly two of your moves.</li><li><b>Stalemate</b> — no legal moves, but not in check. A draw, and a puzzle trap.</li></ul></div>	-
24.50.0	glossary/Knitting Abbreviations Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Knitting Abbreviations Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">Patterns read like code. Here's the decoder ring, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>k / p</b> — knit and purl, the two basic stitches.</li><li><b>k2tog</b> — knit two stitches together. A right-leaning decrease.</li><li><b>ssk</b> — slip, slip, knit. A left-leaning decrease.</li><li><b>yo</b> — yarn over. Makes a new stitch and a little eyelet hole.</li><li><b>psso</b> — pass the slipped stitch over. Tidy decrease move.</li><li><b>CO / BO</b> — cast on and bind off. The start and the finish line.</li><li><b>rep</b> — repeat. Usually across the row or until a marker.</li><li><b>RS / WS</b> — right side (public) and wrong side (hidden).</li><li><b>tbl</b> — through the back loop. Twists the stitch slightly.</li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Knitting Abbreviations Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">Patterns read like code. Here's the decoder ring, .</p><ul class="list"><li><b>k / p</b> — knit and purl, the two basic stitches.</li><li><b>k2tog</b> — knit two stitches together. A right-leaning decrease.</li><li><b>ssk</b> — slip, slip, knit. A left-leaning decrease.</li><li><b>yo</b> — yarn over. Makes a new stitch and a little eyelet hole.</li><li><b>psso</b> — pass the slipped stitch over. Tidy decrease move.</li><li><b>CO / BO</b> — cast on and bind off. The start and the finish line.</li><li><b>rep</b> — repeat. Usually across the row or until a marker.</li><li><b>RS / WS</b> — right side (public) and wrong side (hidden).</li><li><b>tbl</b> — through the back loop. Twists the stitch slightly.</li></ul></div>	-
24.51.0	glossary/Cycling Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Cycling Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">Ride like you know the words, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Peloton</b> — the main pack. Riding in it saves a huge chunk of energy.</li><li><b>Drafting</b> — tucking in behind another rider to cut wind resistance.</li><li><b>Breakaway</b> — riders who escape up the road ahead of the pack.</li><li><b>Cadence</b> — pedal revolutions per minute. Most folks sit around 80–90.</li><li><b>Domestique</b> — a teammate who works for the star rider, fetching bottles and setting pace.</li><li><b>Bonk</b> — running out of fuel mid-ride. Legs turn to sand.</li><li><b>Paceline</b> — riders rotating turns at the front to share the effort.</li><li><b>Chainring / cassette</b> — the gears up front and the stack at the back.</li><li><b>Attack</b> — a sudden hard acceleration to drop everyone else.</li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Cycling Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">Ride like you know the words, .</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Peloton</b> — the main pack. Riding in it saves a huge chunk of energy.</li><li><b>Drafting</b> — tucking in behind another rider to cut wind resistance.</li><li><b>Breakaway</b> — riders who escape up the road ahead of the pack.</li><li><b>Cadence</b> — pedal revolutions per minute. Most folks sit around 80–90.</li><li><b>Domestique</b> — a teammate who works for the star rider, fetching bottles and setting pace.</li><li><b>Bonk</b> — running out of fuel mid-ride. Legs turn to sand.</li><li><b>Paceline</b> — riders rotating turns at the front to share the effort.</li><li><b>Chainring / cassette</b> — the gears up front and the stack at the back.</li><li><b>Attack</b> — a sudden hard acceleration to drop everyone else.</li></ul></div>	-
24.52.0	glossary/Fishing Knots Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Fishing Knots Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">A knot is the only thing between you and the fish, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Palomar</b> — quick and brutally strong. Loop through the eye, overhand, done.</li><li><b>Improved clinch</b> — the old reliable for mono and fluoro.</li><li><b>Uni knot</b> — versatile; works for hooks, swivels, and line-to-line joins.</li><li><b>Blood knot</b> — joins two lines of similar diameter. Neat and slim.</li><li><b>Double uni</b> — a line-to-line join when diameters differ.</li><li><b>Loop knot</b> — ties on a lure with a free-swinging loop, more action.</li><li><b>Tag end</b> — the short leftover after tightening. Trim it close.</li><li><b>Seat the knot</b> — pull steadily so it tightens uniformly without burning the line.</li><li><b>Wet the knot</b> — spit on it before cinching. Reduces friction damage.</li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Fishing Knots Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">A knot is the only thing between you and the fish, .</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Palomar</b> — quick and brutally strong. Loop through the eye, overhand, done.</li><li><b>Improved clinch</b> — the old reliable for mono and fluoro.</li><li><b>Uni knot</b> — versatile; works for hooks, swivels, and line-to-line joins.</li><li><b>Blood knot</b> — joins two lines of similar diameter. Neat and slim.</li><li><b>Double uni</b> — a line-to-line join when diameters differ.</li><li><b>Loop knot</b> — ties on a lure with a free-swinging loop, more action.</li><li><b>Tag end</b> — the short leftover after tightening. Trim it close.</li><li><b>Seat the knot</b> — pull steadily so it tightens uniformly without burning the line.</li><li><b>Wet the knot</b> — spit on it before cinching. Reduces friction damage.</li></ul></div>	-
24.53.0	glossary/Frisbee / Disc Golf Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Frisbee / Disc Golf Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">Learn the flight language, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Hyzer</b> — released with the outer edge tilted down. Curves right for right-handers.</li><li><b>Anhyzer</b> — opposite tilt; the disc turns left, then may fade back.</li><li><b>Fade</b> — the reliable end-of-flight curve as a disc slows.</li><li><b>Turn</b> — high-speed drift in the opposite direction of fade.</li><li><b>Putts &amp; Approaches</b> — close-range shots, usually with a stable putter.</li><li><b>Distance driver</b> — fast, wide rim, made for max distance.</li><li><b>Midrange</b> — the workhorse for controlled, mid-length throws.</li><li><b>Thumber / Tomahawk</b> — overhand throws that flip the flight upside down.</li><li><b>Basket</b> — the chained target. Chain hits are the sweet sound of success.</li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Frisbee / Disc Golf Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">Learn the flight language, .</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Hyzer</b> — released with the outer edge tilted down. Curves right for right-handers.</li><li><b>Anhyzer</b> — opposite tilt; the disc turns left, then may fade back.</li><li><b>Fade</b> — the reliable end-of-flight curve as a disc slows.</li><li><b>Turn</b> — high-speed drift in the opposite direction of fade.</li><li><b>Putts &amp; Approaches</b> — close-range shots, usually with a stable putter.</li><li><b>Distance driver</b> — fast, wide rim, made for max distance.</li><li><b>Midrange</b> — the workhorse for controlled, mid-length throws.</li><li><b>Thumber / Tomahawk</b> — overhand throws that flip the flight upside down.</li><li><b>Basket</b> — the chained target. Chain hits are the sweet sound of success.</li></ul></div>	-
