24.60.0	glossary/Chess Traps & Gambits Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Chess Traps &amp; Gambits</h4><p class="muted">The daring half of opening theory, <regex>user_name</regex>. Names you'll hear at the board.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Gambit</b> — sacrifice material, usually a pawn, for fast development or open lines. Latin for "to trip up."</li><li><b>Trap</b> — a quiet move that looks harmless and dares your opponent to grab something.</li><li><b>Blunder</b> — a real mistake. Not the same as a trap; traps only work if they take the bait.</li><li><b>Poisoned pawn</b> — a pawn that's free in theory and painful in practice.</li><li><b>Zwischenzug</b> — an in-between move you sneak in before the obvious recapture.</li><li><b>Fork</b> — one piece, two targets. The knight is the usual troublemaker.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Example: the Fried Liver</summary><div class="body"><p>1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bc4 Nf6 4.Ng5 — Black's f7 is under pressure. If Black plays 4...d5 carelessly, 5.exd5 Nxd5 6.Nxf7! rips the king open. Sharp, famous, and still catching people off guard.</p></div></details><div class="callout info">Traps reward preparation, not luck. Learn the idea, not just the move order.</div></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Chess Traps &amp; Gambits</h4><p class="muted">The daring half of opening theory, . Names you'll hear at the board.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Gambit</b> — sacrifice material, usually a pawn, for fast development or open lines. Latin for "to trip up."</li><li><b>Trap</b> — a quiet move that looks harmless and dares your opponent to grab something.</li><li><b>Blunder</b> — a real mistake. Not the same as a trap; traps only work if they take the bait.</li><li><b>Poisoned pawn</b> — a pawn that's free in theory and painful in practice.</li><li><b>Zwischenzug</b> — an in-between move you sneak in before the obvious recapture.</li><li><b>Fork</b> — one piece, two targets. The knight is the usual troublemaker.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Example: the Fried Liver</summary><div class="body"><p>1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bc4 Nf6 4.Ng5 — Black's f7 is under pressure. If Black plays 4...d5 carelessly, 5.exd5 Nxd5 6.Nxf7! rips the king open. Sharp, famous, and still catching people off guard.</p></div></details><div class="callout info">Traps reward preparation, not luck. Learn the idea, not just the move order.</div></div>	-
24.61.0	glossary/Knife Skills Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Knife Skills</h4><p class="muted">Cuts, grips, and the words chefs yell across the line — explained for <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Julienne</b> — matchsticks, roughly 3 mm thick.</li><li><b>Brunoise</b> — julienne cut crosswise into tiny cubes. Fancy dice, basically.</li><li><b>Chiffonade</b> — ribboned leaves or herbs, rolled and sliced thin.</li><li><b>Paysanne</b> — flat, thin slices in squares, rounds, or triangles.</li><li><b>Claw grip</b> — knuckles forward, fingertips curled back. The safe way to hold what you're cutting.</li><li><b>Pinch grip</b> — thumb and forefinger pinching the blade's heel for control.</li><li><b>Honing</b> — realigning the edge on a steel. Not sharpening, just a tune-up.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Why size matters</summary><div class="body"><p>Even pieces cook evenly. Uneven ones give you burnt corners and raw middles in the same pan.</p></div></details><div class="callout warn">A dull knife slips. Sharpen, then cut.</div></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Knife Skills</h4><p class="muted">Cuts, grips, and the words chefs yell across the line — explained for .</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Julienne</b> — matchsticks, roughly 3 mm thick.</li><li><b>Brunoise</b> — julienne cut crosswise into tiny cubes. Fancy dice, basically.</li><li><b>Chiffonade</b> — ribboned leaves or herbs, rolled and sliced thin.</li><li><b>Paysanne</b> — flat, thin slices in squares, rounds, or triangles.</li><li><b>Claw grip</b> — knuckles forward, fingertips curled back. The safe way to hold what you're cutting.</li><li><b>Pinch grip</b> — thumb and forefinger pinching the blade's heel for control.</li><li><b>Honing</b> — realigning the edge on a steel. Not sharpening, just a tune-up.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Why size matters</summary><div class="body"><p>Even pieces cook evenly. Uneven ones give you burnt corners and raw middles in the same pan.</p></div></details><div class="callout warn">A dull knife slips. Sharpen, then cut.</div></div>	-
24.62.0	glossary/First Aid Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>First Aid Basics</h4><p class="muted">Plain-language terms for the moments that matter, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>RICE</b> — Rest, Ice, Compression, Elevation. The classic soft-tissue recipe.</li><li><b>Shock</b> — poor blood flow to vital organs. Pale, clammy, fast pulse: lie them down and call for help.</li><li><b>Sprain</b> — stretched or torn ligament, the tissue linking bone to bone.</li><li><b>Strain</b> — the same trouble, but in a muscle or tendon.</li><li><b>ABC</b> — Airway, Breathing, Circulation. The order of checks.</li><li><b>Defibrillator (AED)</b> — a device that talks you through restarting a stopped heart.</li><li><b>Recovery position</b> — on the side, chin up, airway clear, for someone unconscious but breathing.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>When to just call emergency services</summary><div class="body"><p>Unresponsive, not breathing normally, bleeding you can't stop, chest pain, or a head injury with confusion. Call first, fuss later.</p></div></details><div class="callout bad">This is not medical training. Take a real course — it's a few hours and worth every minute.</div></div>	<div class="card"><h4>First Aid Basics</h4><p class="muted">Plain-language terms for the moments that matter, .</p><ul class="list"><li><b>RICE</b> — Rest, Ice, Compression, Elevation. The classic soft-tissue recipe.</li><li><b>Shock</b> — poor blood flow to vital organs. Pale, clammy, fast pulse: lie them down and call for help.</li><li><b>Sprain</b> — stretched or torn ligament, the tissue linking bone to bone.</li><li><b>Strain</b> — the same trouble, but in a muscle or tendon.</li><li><b>ABC</b> — Airway, Breathing, Circulation. The order of checks.</li><li><b>Defibrillator (AED)</b> — a device that talks you through restarting a stopped heart.</li><li><b>Recovery position</b> — on the side, chin up, airway clear, for someone unconscious but breathing.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>When to just call emergency services</summary><div class="body"><p>Unresponsive, not breathing normally, bleeding you can't stop, chest pain, or a head injury with confusion. Call first, fuss later.</p></div></details><div class="callout bad">This is not medical training. Take a real course — it's a few hours and worth every minute.</div></div>	-
24.63.0	glossary/Chess Time Management Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Chess Time Management</h4><p class="muted">Clocks, increments, and the art of not flagging, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Time control</b> — how much time each player gets, and how it's added back.</li><li><b>Increment</b> — seconds added after each move you make. Fischer's gift to the world.</li><li><b>Delay</b> — a short buffer before your clock actually starts ticking.</li><li><b>Flag fall</b> — time runs out. Done, even with a winning position.</li><li><b>Zeitnot</b> — German for "time trouble." The sweaty, frantic endgame scramble.</li><li><b>Berserk</b> — online move: halve your time for bonus points. Bold, or reckless.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Rough bands</summary><div class="body"><p>Bullet: under 3 min. Blitz: 3–10. Rapid: 10–60. Classical: anything with a proper think in it.</p></div></details><div class="callout info">A saved minute at move 20 beats a brilliant plan you never get to play.</div></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Chess Time Management</h4><p class="muted">Clocks, increments, and the art of not flagging, .</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Time control</b> — how much time each player gets, and how it's added back.</li><li><b>Increment</b> — seconds added after each move you make. Fischer's gift to the world.</li><li><b>Delay</b> — a short buffer before your clock actually starts ticking.</li><li><b>Flag fall</b> — time runs out. Done, even with a winning position.</li><li><b>Zeitnot</b> — German for "time trouble." The sweaty, frantic endgame scramble.</li><li><b>Berserk</b> — online move: halve your time for bonus points. Bold, or reckless.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Rough bands</summary><div class="body"><p>Bullet: under 3 min. Blitz: 3–10. Rapid: 10–60. Classical: anything with a proper think in it.</p></div></details><div class="callout info">A saved minute at move 20 beats a brilliant plan you never get to play.</div></div>	-
24.0.1	glossary/Camping Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Camping</h4><p class="muted">Pitching, sleeping, and leaving it better than you found it, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Bivy</b> — a one-person shelter, barely more than a bag with a roof.</li><li><b>R-value</b> — a sleeping pad's insulation score. Higher means warmer back.</li><li><b>Dispersed camping</b> — camping outside designated sites, usually free, usually quiet.</li><li><b>Cat hole</b> — a small, dug hole for waste, 15–20 cm deep and well away from water.</li><li><b>Bear bag / canister</b> — your food, hung high or locked up, so it's nobody's midnight snack.</li><li><b>Leave No Trace</b> — pack it in, pack it out. The campsite should forget you were there.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Layering, in three words</summary><div class="body"><p>Base layer wicks. Mid layer traps warmth. Shell blocks wind and rain. Add or shed as the day warms.</p></div></details><div class="callout good">Cold ground steals more heat than cold air. Pad first, then bag.</div></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Camping</h4><p class="muted">Pitching, sleeping, and leaving it better than you found it, .</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Bivy</b> — a one-person shelter, barely more than a bag with a roof.</li><li><b>R-value</b> — a sleeping pad's insulation score. Higher means warmer back.</li><li><b>Dispersed camping</b> — camping outside designated sites, usually free, usually quiet.</li><li><b>Cat hole</b> — a small, dug hole for waste, 15–20 cm deep and well away from water.</li><li><b>Bear bag / canister</b> — your food, hung high or locked up, so it's nobody's midnight snack.</li><li><b>Leave No Trace</b> — pack it in, pack it out. The campsite should forget you were there.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Layering, in three words</summary><div class="body"><p>Base layer wicks. Mid layer traps warmth. Shell blocks wind and rain. Add or shed as the day warms.</p></div></details><div class="callout good">