24.2.1	glossary/Fitness Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Fitness Glossary</h4><ul class="list"><li><b>Rep</b> — one complete movement, like a single push-up.</li><li><b>Set</b> — a group of reps done without resting.</li><li><b>Hypertrophy</b> — muscle growth from resistance training.</li><li><b>Aerobic</b> — steady activity where you can still talk; builds endurance.</li><li><b>Anaerobic</b> — short, intense effort where talking isn't happening.</li><li><b>Progressive overload</b> — gradually asking more of your muscles over time.</li><li><b>DOMS</b> — the soreness that shows up a day or two after a workout.</li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Fitness Glossary</h4><ul class="list"><li><b>Rep</b> — one complete movement, like a single push-up.</li><li><b>Set</b> — a group of reps done without resting.</li><li><b>Hypertrophy</b> — muscle growth from resistance training.</li><li><b>Aerobic</b> — steady activity where you can still talk; builds endurance.</li><li><b>Anaerobic</b> — short, intense effort where talking isn't happening.</li><li><b>Progressive overload</b> — gradually asking more of your muscles over time.</li><li><b>DOMS</b> — the soreness that shows up a day or two after a workout.</li></ul></div>	-
24.3.1	glossary/Spanish Basics Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Spanish Basics Glossary</h4><ul class="list"><li><b>Hola</b> — hello.</li><li><b>Buenos días</b> — good morning.</li><li><b>Buenas noches</b> — good evening / good night.</li><li><b>Por favor</b> — please.</li><li><b>Gracias</b> — thank you.</li><li><b>De nada</b> — you're welcome.</li><li><b>¿Cómo estás?</b> — how are you? (informal)</li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Spanish Basics Glossary</h4><ul class="list"><li><b>Hola</b> — hello.</li><li><b>Buenos días</b> — good morning.</li><li><b>Buenas noches</b> — good evening / good night.</li><li><b>Por favor</b> — please.</li><li><b>Gracias</b> — thank you.</li><li><b>De nada</b> — you're welcome.</li><li><b>¿Cómo estás?</b> — how are you? (informal)</li></ul></div>	-
24.4.1	glossary/Music Production Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Music Production Glossary</h4><ul class="list"><li><b>DAW</b> — digital audio workstation; the software you record and arrange in.</li><li><b>Hook</b> — the catchy bit that sticks in your head.</li><li><b>Reverb</b> — the sound of a space, from small room to cathedral.</li><li><b>Lo-fi</b> — intentionally rough, warm, imperfect sound.</li><li><b>Sample</b> — a snippet of audio reused in a new track.</li><li><b>Mixing</b> — balancing levels, panning, and effects so everything sits right.</li><li><b>Mastering</b> — the final polish that makes a track sound consistent everywhere.</li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Music Production Glossary</h4><ul class="list"><li><b>DAW</b> — digital audio workstation; the software you record and arrange in.</li><li><b>Hook</b> — the catchy bit that sticks in your head.</li><li><b>Reverb</b> — the sound of a space, from small room to cathedral.</li><li><b>Lo-fi</b> — intentionally rough, warm, imperfect sound.</li><li><b>Sample</b> — a snippet of audio reused in a new track.</li><li><b>Mixing</b> — balancing levels, panning, and effects so everything sits right.</li><li><b>Mastering</b> — the final polish that makes a track sound consistent everywhere.</li></ul></div>	-
24.5.1	glossary/Automotive Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Automotive Glossary</h4><ul class="list"><li><b>Torque</b> — twisting force; what pushes you back in the seat.</li><li><b>Horsepower</b> — a measure of how quickly work gets done.</li><li><b>OBD-II</b> — the standard port mechanics plug into to read engine codes.</li><li><b>All-wheel drive</b> — all four wheels can receive power, automatically.</li><li><b>Oversteer</b> — the rear slides out more than the front.</li><li><b>Understeer</b> — the front slides wide before the rear does.</li><li><b>Wheelbase</b> — distance between front and rear axles.</li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Automotive Glossary</h4><ul class="list"><li><b>Torque</b> — twisting force; what pushes you back in the seat.</li><li><b>Horsepower</b> — a measure of how quickly work gets done.</li><li><b>OBD-II</b> — the standard port mechanics plug into to read engine codes.</li><li><b>All-wheel drive</b> — all four wheels can receive power, automatically.</li><li><b>Oversteer</b> — the rear slides out more than the front.</li><li><b>Understeer</b> — the front slides wide before the rear does.</li><li><b>Wheelbase</b> — distance between front and rear axles.</li></ul></div>	-
24.6.1	glossary/Internet Basics Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Internet Basics Glossary</h4><ul class="list"><li><b>IP address</b> — a number that identifies a device on a network.</li><li><b>DNS</b> — the system that turns names like example.com into IP addresses.</li><li><b>HTTP/HTTPS</b> — the rules browsers and servers use to talk; the S means encrypted.</li><li><b>Server</b> — a computer that waits to hand out web pages or data.</li><li><b>Client</b> — the device asking for those pages; your phone or laptop.</li><li><b>Bandwidth</b> — how much data can flow at once.</li><li><b>Latency</b> — the delay between asking and receiving.</li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Internet Basics Glossary</h4><ul class="list"><li><b>IP address</b> — a number that identifies a device on a network.</li><li><b>DNS</b> — the system that turns names like example.com into IP addresses.</li><li><b>HTTP/HTTPS</b> — the rules browsers and servers use to talk; the S means encrypted.</li><li><b>Server</b> — a computer that waits to hand out web pages or data.</li><li><b>Client</b> — the device asking for those pages; your phone or laptop.</li><li><b>Bandwidth</b> — how much data can flow at once.</li><li><b>Latency</b> — the delay between asking and receiving.</li></ul></div>	-
24.7.1	glossary/Aquarium Keeping Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Glossary: Aquarium Keeping</h4><p class="t-small muted">Hey <regex>user_name</regex>, the words that matter before you buy a fish.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Cycle</b> — the bacteria colony that turns fish waste into something harmless. Takes weeks. Sorry.</li><li><b>Ammonia</b> — the first toxic stop on that journey. Test daily in a new tank.</li><li><b>Nitrite</b> — second toxic stop. Also bad.</li><li><b>Nitrate</b> — the end product, removed by water changes.</li><li><b>Water change</b> — swapping part of the water for fresh. The main chore.</li><li><b>pH</b> — acidity. Most fish want steady more than perfect.</li><li><b>Hardness (GH/KH)</b> — minerals in the water. KH steadies pH.</li><li><b>Substrate</b> — the gravel or sand on the bottom.</li><li><b>Sump</b> — a separate tank holding filter gear and hiding it from view.</li><li><b>Quarantine</b> — a small tank where new fish wait before joining the crew.</li><li><b>Bioload</b> — how much waste your fish produce versus how much filtration you have.</li><li><b>Nano tank</b> — small, under about 20 litres. Charming, unforgiving.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Quick sanity check</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Ammonia and nitrite should read zero. If not, water change, then wait.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Glossary: Aquarium Keeping</h4><p class="t-small muted">Hey , the words that matter before you buy a fish.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Cycle</b> — the bacteria colony that turns fish waste into something harmless. Takes weeks. Sorry.</li><li><b>Ammonia</b> — the first toxic stop on that journey. Test daily in a new tank.</li><li><b>Nitrite</b> — second toxic stop. Also bad.</li><li><b>Nitrate</b> — the end product, removed by water changes.</li><li><b>Water change</b> — swapping part of the water for fresh. The main chore.</li><li><b>pH</b> — acidity. Most fish want steady more than perfect.</li><li><b>Hardness (GH/KH)</b> — minerals in the water. KH steadies pH.</li><li><b>Substrate</b> — the gravel or sand on the bottom.</li><li><b>Sump</b> — a separate tank holding filter gear and hiding it from view.</li><li><b>Quarantine</b> — a small tank where new fish wait before joining the crew.</li><li><b>Bioload</b> — how much waste your fish produce versus how much filtration you have.</li><li><b>Nano tank</b> — small, under about 20 litres. Charming, unforgiving.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Quick sanity check</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Ammonia and nitrite should read zero. If not, water change, then wait.</p></div></details></div>	-
24.8.1	glossary/Baking Measurements Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Glossary: Baking Measurements</h4><p class="t-small muted">Because "one cup" means three different things, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Gram (g)</b> — weight. The honest unit. A kitchen scale beats every cup.</li><li><b>Cup</b> — volume. In the US, 240 ml; in Australia, 250 ml; in metric recipes, often 200 ml. Yes, really.</li><li><b>Tablespoon (tbsp)</b> — 15 ml. Three teaspoons.</li><li><b>Teaspoon (tsp)</b> — 5 ml.</li><li><b>Fluid ounce</b> — volume, ~30 ml. Not the same as an ounce of flour.</li><li><b>Ounce (oz)</b> — weight, ~28 g. Weighs differently for honey and for flour.</li><li><b>Stick of butter</b> — US: 113 g, half a cup. Elsewhere: just cut from the block.</li><li><b>Sifted</b> — fluffed with air, so a cup holds less flour.</li><li><b>Packed</b> — pressed in, for brown sugar.</li><li><b>Level vs heaped</b> — flat across the top, or a mound. Big difference with baking powder.</li><li><b>Scant</b> — slightly less than the stated amount.</li></ul><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">If it matters, it's weighed. If it's soup, cups are fine.</p></div></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Glossary: Baking Measurements</h4><p class="t-small muted">Because "one cup" means three different things, .</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Gram (g)</b> — weight. The honest unit. A kitchen scale beats every cup.</li><li><b>Cup</b> — volume. In the US, 240 ml; in Australia, 250 ml; in metric recipes, often 200 ml. Yes, really.</li><li><b>Tablespoon (tbsp)</b> — 15 ml. Three teaspoons.</li><li><b>Teaspoon (tsp)</b> — 5 ml.</li><li><b>Fluid ounce</b> — volume, ~30 ml. Not the same as an ounce of flour.</li><li><b>Ounce (oz)</b> — weight, ~28 g. Weighs differently for honey and for flour.</li><li><b>Stick of butter</b> — US: 113 g, half a cup. Elsewhere: just cut from the block.</li><li><b>Sifted</b> — fluffed with air, so a cup holds less flour.</li><li><b>Packed</b> — pressed in, for brown sugar.</li><li><b>Level vs heaped</b> — flat across the top, or a mound. Big difference with baking powder.</li><li><b>Scant</b> — slightly less than the stated amount.</li></ul><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">If it matters, it's weighed. If it's soup, cups are fine.</p></div></div>	-
24.9.1	glossary/Car Maintenance Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Glossary: Car Maintenance</h4><p class="t-small muted">The words the mechanic says, translated for <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Oil change</b> — draining old engine oil and replacing the filter. Every ~8,000–15,000 km.</li><li><b>Dipstick</b> — the stick you pull to check oil level. Wipe, reinsert, read.</li><li><b>Coolant</b> — the coloured liquid that keeps the engine from cooking itself.</li><li><b>Brake pads</b> — the friction blocks that squeeze the disc. They wear down. They squeal first.</li><li><b>Rotors / discs</b> — the spinning metal the pads grab.</li><li><b>Tread depth</b> — how much rubber is left on a tyre. Below 1.6 mm is illegal in most places.</li><li><b>Alignment</b> — making all four wheels point the same way.</li><li><b>Balance</b> — spreading wheel weight evenly so it doesn't shake at speed.</li><li><b>Serpentine belt</b> — one long belt driving several accessories. Snaps loudly.</li><li><b>Timing belt/chain</b> — keeps the engine's internals in step. Expensive if ignored.</li><li><b>Check engine light</b> — a hint, not a diagnosis. A scanner reads the actual code.</li><li><b>OBD-II</b> — the standard port under the dash where that scanner plugs in.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Which ones can wait?</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Squealing brakes and a flashing check engine light: no. A steady light: book it soon, drive gently.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Glossary: Car Maintenance</h4><p class="t-small muted">The words the mechanic says, translated for .</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Oil change</b> — draining old engine oil and replacing the filter. Every ~8,000–15,000 km.</li><li><b>Dipstick</b> — the stick you pull to check oil level. Wipe, reinsert, read.</li><li><b>Coolant</b> — the coloured liquid that keeps the engine from cooking itself.</li><li><b>Brake pads</b> — the friction blocks that squeeze the disc. They wear down. They squeal first.</li><li><b>Rotors / discs</b> — the spinning metal the pads grab.</li><li><b>Tread depth</b> — how much rubber is left on a tyre. Below 1.6 mm is illegal in most places.</li><li><b>Alignment</b> — making all four wheels point the same way.</li><li><b>Balance</b> — spreading wheel weight evenly so it doesn't shake at speed.</li><li><b>Serpentine belt</b> — one long belt driving several accessories. Snaps loudly.</li><li><b>Timing belt/chain</b> — keeps the engine's internals in step. Expensive if ignored.</li><li><b>Check engine light</b> — a hint, not a diagnosis. A scanner reads the actual code.</li><li><b>OBD-II</b> — the standard port under the dash where that scanner plugs in.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Which ones can wait?</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Squealing brakes and a flashing check engine light: no. A steady light: book it soon, drive gently.</p></div></details></div>	-
24.10.1	glossary/Print & Typography Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Glossary: Print & Typography</h4><p class="t-small muted">Small words about small letters, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Point (pt)</b> — type size. 72 pt to an inch, roughly.</li><li><b>Leading</b> — the space between lines, measured baseline to baseline. Rhymes with "sledding".</li><li><b>Tracking</b> — even spacing across a whole word.</li><li><b>Kerning</b> — nudging one specific pair closer, like "AV".</li><li><b>Serif / sans-serif</b> — little feet on the letters, or none.</li><li><b>X-height</b> — how tall a lowercase x is. Bigger x-height reads better small.</li><li><b>Ascender / descender</b> — the parts sticking up (b, d) and hanging down (g, p).</li><li><b>Widow</b> — a lonely last line stranded at the top of a page.</li><li><b>Orphan</b> — a lone word stranded on a line by itself.</li><li><b>Bleed</b> — artwork running past the trim so no white edge shows.</li><li><b>Gutter</b> — the inner margin where the pages meet the spine.</li><li><b>Dpi</b> — dots per inch. 300 for print, 72 is screen territory.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>The classic mix-up</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Tracking adjusts everything. Kerning adjusts one pair. That's the whole argument.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Glossary: Print & Typography</h4><p class="t-small muted">Small words about small letters, .</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Point (pt)</b> — type size. 72 pt to an inch, roughly.</li><li><b>Leading</b> — the space between lines, measured baseline to baseline. Rhymes with "sledding".</li><li><b>Tracking</b> — even spacing across a whole word.</li><li><b>Kerning</b> — nudging one specific pair closer, like "AV".</li><li><b>Serif / sans-serif</b> — little feet on the letters, or none.</li><li><b>X-height</b> — how tall a lowercase x is. Bigger x-height reads better small.</li><li><b>Ascender / descender</b> — the parts sticking up (b, d) and hanging down (g, p).</li><li><b>Widow</b> — a lonely last line stranded at the top of a page.</li><li><b>Orphan</b> — a lone word stranded on a line by itself.</li><li><b>Bleed</b> — artwork running past the trim so no white edge shows.</li><li><b>Gutter</b> — the inner margin where the pages meet the spine.</li><li><b>Dpi</b> — dots per inch. 300 for print, 72 is screen territory.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>The classic mix-up</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Tracking adjusts everything. Kerning adjusts one pair. That's the whole argument.</p></div></details></div>	-
24.11.1	glossary/Sleep & Dreams Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Glossary: Sleep & Dreams</h4><p class="t-small muted">For the 3am crowd, including you, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Circadian rhythm</b> — your internal 24-hour clock, set mostly by morning light.</li><li><b>Melatonin</b> — the hormone that says "wind down". Dim light helps it along.</li><li><b>Sleep pressure</b> — the drowsiness that builds the longer you're awake.</li><li><b>REM</b> — rapid eye movement sleep. Vivid dreams live here.</li><li><b>NREM</b> — the other stages, including deep sleep, where the body repairs.</li><li><b>Sleep cycle</b> — roughly 90 minutes, REM included. Four or five a night is typical.</li><li><b>Sleep latency</b> — how long it takes you to drop off. Ten to twenty minutes is normal.</li><li><b>Sleep debt</b> — accumulated shortfall. Repayable, but slowly.</li><li><b>Sleep inertia</b> — the fog right after waking. It fades.</li><li><b>Apnea</b> — breathing pauses during sleep. Worth getting checked.</li><li><b>Hypnagogic</b> — the drifting state just before sleep, where odd images appear.</li><li><b>Lucid dream</b> — knowing you're dreaming while still inside it.</li></ul><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Consistent wake time beats a perfect bedtime. Boring, but true.</p></div></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Glossary: Sleep & Dreams</h4><p class="t-small muted">For the 3am crowd, including you, .</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Circadian rhythm</b> — your internal 24-hour clock, set mostly by morning light.</li><li><b>Melatonin</b> — the hormone that says "wind down". Dim light helps it along.</li><li><b>Sleep pressure</b> — the drowsiness that builds the longer you're awake.</li><li><b>REM</b> — rapid eye movement sleep. Vivid dreams live here.</li><li><b>NREM</b> — the other stages, including deep sleep, where the body repairs.</li><li><b>Sleep cycle</b> — roughly 90 minutes, REM included. Four or five a night is typical.</li><li><b>Sleep latency<