24.12.1	glossary/Beekeeping Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Glossary: Beekeeping</h4><p class="t-small muted">The vocabulary of tiny buzzing colleagues, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Colony</b> — the whole hive: one queen, thousands of workers, some drones.</li><li><b>Queen</b> — the only bee laying fertilised eggs. Lives years, not weeks.</li><li><b>Worker</b> — a female bee doing every other job. Lives about six weeks in summer.</li><li><b>Drone</b> — a male bee. His contribution is genetic, then he's evicted in autumn.</li><li><b>Brood</b> — eggs, larvae and pupae. The nursery.</li><li><b>Comb</b> — the wax honeycomb. Cells are hexagonal for good reason.</li><li><b>Nectar</b> — sugary flower liquid. Becomes honey once the bees fan and dry it.</li><li><b>Propolis</b> — plant resin the bees use as glue and disinfectant.</li><li><b>Super</b> — the box you add on top for honey you intend to take.</li><li><b>Brood box</b> — the lower box where the queen works. You leave that alone.</li><li><b>Smoker</b> — puffs cool smoke to calm the hive. It masks alarm pheromones.</li><li><b>Swarm</b> — half the colony leaving with the old queen. Not aggression; reproduction.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Why hexagons?</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Same wall material, most storage, least wax. The bees did the maths.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Glossary: Beekeeping</h4><p class="t-small muted">The vocabulary of tiny buzzing colleagues, .</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Colony</b> — the whole hive: one queen, thousands of workers, some drones.</li><li><b>Queen</b> — the only bee laying fertilised eggs. Lives years, not weeks.</li><li><b>Worker</b> — a female bee doing every other job. Lives about six weeks in summer.</li><li><b>Drone</b> — a male bee. His contribution is genetic, then he's evicted in autumn.</li><li><b>Brood</b> — eggs, larvae and pupae. The nursery.</li><li><b>Comb</b> — the wax honeycomb. Cells are hexagonal for good reason.</li><li><b>Nectar</b> — sugary flower liquid. Becomes honey once the bees fan and dry it.</li><li><b>Propolis</b> — plant resin the bees use as glue and disinfectant.</li><li><b>Super</b> — the box you add on top for honey you intend to take.</li><li><b>Brood box</b> — the lower box where the queen works. You leave that alone.</li><li><b>Smoker</b> — puffs cool smoke to calm the hive. It masks alarm pheromones.</li><li><b>Swarm</b> — half the colony leaving with the old queen. Not aggression; reproduction.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Why hexagons?</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Same wall material, most storage, least wax. The bees did the maths.</p></div></details></div>	-
24.13.1	glossary/Cocktail Glossaries: Gin & Rum	<div class="card"><h4>Gin &amp; Rum Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">For <regex>user_name</regex> — two spirits, one shelf, plenty of opinions.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Botanicals</b> — the flavorings steeped or vapor-infused into gin: juniper, coriander, citrus peel, the occasional odd duck.</li><li><b>London Dry</b> — a style rule, not a place: juniper forward, nothing sweetened after distillation.</li><li><b>New Western</b> — gin where juniper takes a back seat. Sometimes a whole different car.</li><li><b>Navy strength</b> — gin or rum at roughly 57% ABV, historically so spilled barrels still lit.</li><li><b>Overproof</b> — anything notably above standard strength. Rum in the 60–75% range. Respect it.</li><li><b>Cask strength</b> — bottled straight from the barrel, undiluted. Hot, loud, honest.</li><li><b>Pot still</b> — batch distillation; heavier, more character.</li><li><b>Column still</b> — continuous distillation; lighter and cleaner. Most white rum.</li><li><b>Agricole</b> — rum from fresh pressed cane juice, grassy and funky. Martinique's signature.</li><li><b>Funky</b> — the high-ester, overripe-fruit punch of Jamaican rum. It grows on you.</li><li><b>Aged rum</b> — rested in oak; tropical heat ages it faster than a Scottish warehouse.</li><li><b>Solera</b> — blending across barrels of different ages, so no bottle is truly one year.</li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Gin &amp; Rum Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">For  — two spirits, one shelf, plenty of opinions.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Botanicals</b> — the flavorings steeped or vapor-infused into gin: juniper, coriander, citrus peel, the occasional odd duck.</li><li><b>London Dry</b> — a style rule, not a place: juniper forward, nothing sweetened after distillation.</li><li><b>New Western</b> — gin where juniper takes a back seat. Sometimes a whole different car.</li><li><b>Navy strength</b> — gin or rum at roughly 57% ABV, historically so spilled barrels still lit.</li><li><b>Overproof</b> — anything notably above standard strength. Rum in the 60–75% range. Respect it.</li><li><b>Cask strength</b> — bottled straight from the barrel, undiluted. Hot, loud, honest.</li><li><b>Pot still</b> — batch distillation; heavier, more character.</li><li><b>Column still</b> — continuous distillation; lighter and cleaner. Most white rum.</li><li><b>Agricole</b> — rum from fresh pressed cane juice, grassy and funky. Martinique's signature.</li><li><b>Funky</b> — the high-ester, overripe-fruit punch of Jamaican rum. It grows on you.</li><li><b>Aged rum</b> — rested in oak; tropical heat ages it faster than a Scottish warehouse.</li><li><b>Solera</b> — blending across barrels of different ages, so no bottle is truly one year.</li></ul></div>	-
24.14.1	glossary/Chess Blunder Recovery Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Chess Blunder Recovery Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">For <regex>user_name</regex> — words for the moment after your hand leaves the piece.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Blunder</b> — a move that flips the evaluation. We've all fed the queen to a pawn.</li><li><b>Oversight</b> — the same thing, said more gently. Missed a knight fork, say.</li><li><b>Swindle</b> — a trap set in a lost position. Cheap? Yes. Legal? Also yes.</li><li><b>Fortress</b> — a locked setup that holds despite the material deficit. Draw it out.</li><li><b>Zugzwang</b> — any move makes you worse, but you must move. German for "sitting in traffic."</li><li><b>Stalemate</b> — no legal move, not in check. Draw. The great escape hatch.</li><li><b>Perpetual check</b> — checking forever with no progress. Also a draw. Also a rescue.</li><li><b>Insufficient material</b> — not enough pieces left to ever mate. Bring the king, call it a day.</li><li><b>Fifty-move rule</b> — 50 moves with no pawn move or capture ends it in a draw.</li><li><b>Threefold repetition</b> — same position three times. Draw on request.</li><li><b>Desperado</b> — a doomed piece that takes something on the way out.</li><li><b>Resource</b> — a hidden defense your opponent missed. That's why we don't resign instantly.</li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Chess Blunder Recovery Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">For  — words for the moment after your hand leaves the piece.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Blunder</b> — a move that flips the evaluation. We've all fed the queen to a pawn.</li><li><b>Oversight</b> — the same thing, said more gently. Missed a knight fork, say.</li><li><b>Swindle</b> — a trap set in a lost position. Cheap? Yes. Legal? Also yes.</li><li><b>Fortress</b> — a locked setup that holds despite the material deficit. Draw it out.</li><li><b>Zugzwang</b> — any move makes you worse, but you must move. German for "sitting in traffic."</li><li><b>Stalemate</b> — no legal move, not in check. Draw. The great escape hatch.</li><li><b>Perpetual check</b> — checking forever with no progress. Also a draw. Also a rescue.</li><li><b>Insufficient material</b> — not enough pieces left to ever mate. Bring the king, call it a day.</li><li><b>Fifty-move rule</b> — 50 moves with no pawn move or capture ends it in a draw.</li><li><b>Threefold repetition</b> — same position three times. Draw on request.</li><li><b>Desperado</b> — a doomed piece that takes something on the way out.</li><li><b>Resource</b> — a hidden defense your opponent missed. That's why we don't resign instantly.</li></ul></div>	-
24.15.1	glossary/Chess Pawn Structure Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Chess Pawn Structure Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">For <regex>user_name</regex> — pawns can't go back, so choose their shape carefully.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Doubled pawns</b> — two on the same file. Half as mobile, twice as stubborn.</li><li><b>Isolated pawn</b> — no friendly pawn on either adjacent file. Weak, but a fine attacking excuse.</li><li><b>Backward pawn</b> — behind its neighbors, and the square in front is a magnet for enemy pieces.</li><li><b>Passed pawn</b> — no enemy pawn can stop it or capture on its path. Push it.</li><li><b>Protected passer</b> — a passed pawn guarded by another pawn. Genuinely scary.</li><li><b>Hanging pawns</b> — a duo on the half-open files with no neighbors. Strong until they're not.</li><li><b>Pawn chain</b> — diagonal pawns bracing each other. Attack it at the base.</li><li><b>Pawn break</b> — a pawn advance that opens lines and stakes a claim.</li><li><b>Pawn island</b> — a group of connected pawns. Fewer islands is usually healthier.</li><li><b>En passant</b> — the one-move-only capture of a pawn that just ran past you.</li><li><b>Promotion</b> — a pawn reaching the last rank, usually returning as a queen.</li><li><b>Minority attack</b> — fewer pawns, advancing anyway, to carve a weakness. Very positional, very smug.</li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Chess Pawn Structure Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">For  — pawns can't go back, so choose their shape carefully.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Doubled pawns</b> — two on the same file. Half as mobile, twice as stubborn.</li><li><b>Isolated pawn</b> — no friendly pawn on either adjacent file. Weak, but a fine attacking excuse.</li><li><b>Backward pawn</b> — behind its neighbors, and the square in front is a magnet for enemy pieces.</li><li><b>Passed pawn</b> — no enemy pawn can stop it or capture on its path. Push it.</li><li><b>Protected passer</b> — a passed pawn guarded by another pawn. Genuinely scary.</li><li><b>Hanging pawns</b> — a duo on the half-open files with no neighbors. Strong until they're not.</li><li><b>Pawn chain</b> — diagonal pawns bracing each other. Attack it at the base.</li><li><b>Pawn break</b> — a pawn advance that opens lines and stakes a claim.</li><li><b>Pawn island</b> — a group of connected pawns. Fewer islands is usually healthier.</li><li><b>En passant</b> — the one-move-only capture of a pawn that just ran past you.</li><li><b>Promotion</b> — a pawn reaching the last rank, usually returning as a queen.</li><li><b>Minority attack</b> — fewer pawns, advancing anyway, to carve a weakness. Very positional, very smug.</li></ul></div>	-
24.16.1	glossary/Chess Rook Endgame Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Rook Endgame Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">For <regex>user_name</regex> — rook endings are drawn, until they aren't.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Lucena position</b> — rook in front of the king, pawn on the seventh. Win it with the bridge.</li><li><b>Building a bridge</b> — walking your king into the checks by hiding behind your rook.</li><li><b>Philidor position</b> — rook on the third rank, king in front of the pawn. A clean, permanent draw.</li><li><b>Short side vs long side</b> — the defending king hides from checks on the shorter half. Bar stool for the attacker.</li><li><b>Cutting off the king</b> — placing your rook so the enemy king can't walk to the pawns.</li><li><b>Opposition</b> — kings facing on the same file with one square between. Whoever must move, loses ground.</li><li><b>Active rook</b> — a rook behind passed pawns or raiding the pawns. Passivity loses these endings.</li><li><b>Rook behind the pawn</b> — generally the right square, yours or theirs.</li><li><b>Vancura position</b> — a rook pawn draw so elegant it gets a name.</li><li><b>Seventh rank</b> — a rook on the 7th eats pawns and makes threats.</li><li><b>Back rank</b> — unmoved rook territory, where mate arrives out of nowhere.</li><li><b>Zugzwang</b> — every move makes it worse, but the clock says move anyway.</li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Rook Endgame Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">For  — rook endings are drawn, until they aren't.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Lucena position</b> — rook in front of the king, pawn on the seventh. Win it with the bridge.</li><li><b>Building a bridge</b> — walking your king into the checks by hiding behind your rook.</li><li><b>Philidor position</b> — rook on the third rank, king in front of the pawn. A clean, permanent draw.</li><li><b>Short side vs long side</b> — the defending king hides from checks on the shorter half. Bar stool for the attacker.</li><li><b>Cutting off 