24.17.1	glossary/Sourdough Starter Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Sourdough Starter Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">The little jar colony, translated. Hi <regex>user_name</regex> — poke around.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Starter</b> — flour + water colonised by wild yeast and bacteria. Your pet, basically.</li><li><b>Ripe / peaked</b> — the moment the starter is most active: domed, bubbly, smells pleasantly sour. Best time to bake.</li><li><b>Hooch</b> — gray liquid on top; alcohol from hungry yeast. Not spoiled, just hangry. Pour off or stir in.</li><li><b>Hydration</b> — water as a % of flour weight. 100% = equal grams of each.</li><li><b>Discard</b> — the portion you remove before feeding. Keep it in the fridge for pancakes.</li><li><b>Float test</b> — a dab of starter dropped in water. Floats = gassy = ready.</li><li><b>Feed</b> — adding fresh flour and water so the yeast has new food.</li><li><b>Doubling</b> — volume roughly doubling after a feed; the classic readiness cue.</li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Sourdough Starter Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">The little jar colony, translated. Hi  — poke around.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Starter</b> — flour + water colonised by wild yeast and bacteria. Your pet, basically.</li><li><b>Ripe / peaked</b> — the moment the starter is most active: domed, bubbly, smells pleasantly sour. Best time to bake.</li><li><b>Hooch</b> — gray liquid on top; alcohol from hungry yeast. Not spoiled, just hangry. Pour off or stir in.</li><li><b>Hydration</b> — water as a % of flour weight. 100% = equal grams of each.</li><li><b>Discard</b> — the portion you remove before feeding. Keep it in the fridge for pancakes.</li><li><b>Float test</b> — a dab of starter dropped in water. Floats = gassy = ready.</li><li><b>Feed</b> — adding fresh flour and water so the yeast has new food.</li><li><b>Doubling</b> — volume roughly doubling after a feed; the classic readiness cue.</li></ul></div>	-
24.18.1	glossary/Sewing Machine Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Sewing Machine Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">Know the parts before you thread them, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Core parts</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li><b>Bobbin</b> — small spool of thread that sits under the needle and forms the stitch's underside.</li><li><b>Presser foot</b> — the metal shoe holding fabric flat against the feed dogs.</li><li><b>Feed dogs</b> — toothed bars that pull fabric forward.</li><li><b>Take-up lever</b> — arm that feeds thread with each stitch; must be up when threading.</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Adjustments</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li><b>Tension</b> — how tightly thread is pulled. Too loose = loops below; too tight = puckers.</li><li><b>Stitch length</b> — mm between needle punctures. Longer for basting, shorter for fine fabric.</li><li><b>Seam allowance</b> — gap between fabric edge and stitching line, often 1.5 cm (5/8 in).</li><li><b>Nesting</b> — thread tangling underneath, usually from poor threading or a bad bobbin.</li><li><b>Backstitch</b> — reverse a few stitches to lock a seam.</li></ul></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Sewing Machine Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">Know the parts before you thread them, .</p><details class="acc"><summary>Core parts</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li><b>Bobbin</b> — small spool of thread that sits under the needle and forms the stitch's underside.</li><li><b>Presser foot</b> — the metal shoe holding fabric flat against the feed dogs.</li><li><b>Feed dogs</b> — toothed bars that pull fabric forward.</li><li><b>Take-up lever</b> — arm that feeds thread with each stitch; must be up when threading.</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Adjustments</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li><b>Tension</b> — how tightly thread is pulled. Too loose = loops below; too tight = puckers.</li><li><b>Stitch length</b> — mm between needle punctures. Longer for basting, shorter for fine fabric.</li><li><b>Seam allowance</b> — gap between fabric edge and stitching line, often 1.5 cm (5/8 in).</li><li><b>Nesting</b> — thread tangling underneath, usually from poor threading or a bad bobbin.</li><li><b>Backstitch</b> — reverse a few stitches to lock a seam.</li></ul></div></details></div>	-
24.19.1	glossary/Dungeons & Dragons Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Dungeons &amp; Dragons Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">Starting a campaign, <regex>user_name</regex>? Here's the table talk.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>d20</b> — the twenty-sided die that decides most things.</li><li><b>Check</b> — roll d20 + modifier vs a target number to attempt something.</li><li><b>AC (Armour Class)</b> — how hard you are to hit. Attack rolls must meet or beat it.</li><li><b>Saving throw</b> — roll to resist a bad effect, like a spell or a trap.</li><li><b>Initiative</b> — the order turns go in during combat; everyone rolls once.</li><li><b>HP</b> — hit points. Zero means trouble.</li><li><b>Nat 20 / nat 1</b> — a natural (unmodified) 20 or 1 on the die: the great and the terrible.</li><li><b>DM</b> — Dungeon Master; runs the world and every monster in it.</li><li><b>Session zero</b> — the setup chat before play: tone, rules, characters.</li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Dungeons &amp; Dragons Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">Starting a campaign, ? Here's the table talk.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>d20</b> — the twenty-sided die that decides most things.</li><li><b>Check</b> — roll d20 + modifier vs a target number to attempt something.</li><li><b>AC (Armour Class)</b> — how hard you are to hit. Attack rolls must meet or beat it.</li><li><b>Saving throw</b> — roll to resist a bad effect, like a spell or a trap.</li><li><b>Initiative</b> — the order turns go in during combat; everyone rolls once.</li><li><b>HP</b> — hit points. Zero means trouble.</li><li><b>Nat 20 / nat 1</b> — a natural (unmodified) 20 or 1 on the die: the great and the terrible.</li><li><b>DM</b> — Dungeon Master; runs the world and every monster in it.</li><li><b>Session zero</b> — the setup chat before play: tone, rules, characters.</li></ul></div>	-
24.20.1	glossary/Bread Baking Troubleshooting Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Bread Baking Troubleshooting Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">For when the loaf has opinions. Chin up, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Crumb</b> — the inside texture: open (big holes) or tight (fine, dense).</li><li><b>Dense / gummy</b> — underproofed or underbaked. Give it more time, not more flour.</li><li><b>Overproofed</b> — dough rose too long; it collapses and bakes flat and sour.</li><li><b>Oven spring</b> — the rapid rise in the first minutes of baking. Weak spring = weak proof.</li><li><b>Ear</b> — the lifted flap of crust from a good scoring cut. Bragging rights.</li><li><b>Dough strength</b> — how well gluten holds gas. Built by kneading or folding.</li><li><b>Autolyse</b> — resting flour and water before adding salt or yeast.</li><li><b>Bulk ferment</b> — the first, whole-dough rise before shaping.</li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Bread Baking Troubleshooting Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">For when the loaf has opinions. Chin up, .</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Crumb</b> — the inside texture: open (big holes) or tight (fine, dense).</li><li><b>Dense / gummy</b> — underproofed or underbaked. Give it more time, not more flour.</li><li><b>Overproofed</b> — dough rose too long; it collapses and bakes flat and sour.</li><li><b>Oven spring</b> — the rapid rise in the first minutes of baking. Weak spring = weak proof.</li><li><b>Ear</b> — the lifted flap of crust from a good scoring cut. Bragging rights.</li><li><b>Dough strength</b> — how well gluten holds gas. Built by kneading or folding.</li><li><b>Autolyse</b> — resting flour and water before adding salt or yeast.</li><li><b>Bulk ferment</b> — the first, whole-dough rise before shaping.</li></ul></div>	-
24.21.1	glossary/Video Editing Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Video Editing Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">Words your editing app assumes you know, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Cutting &amp; timing</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li><b>Timeline</b> — the horizontal workspace where clips sit in order.</li><li><b>Cut</b> — an instant switch between clips.</li><li><b>Jump cut</b> — a cut in the same shot that skips time; jarring, sometimes intentional.</li><li><b>J-cut / L-cut</b> — audio from the next or previous clip leads or lags the picture.</li><li><b>Frame rate</b> — frames per second (fps). 24 cinematic, 30 standard, 60 smooth.</li><li><b>B-roll</b> — supporting footage layered over the main shot.</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Look &amp; sound</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li><b>Colour grading</b> — shaping the mood with colour, after basic correction.</li><li><b>LUT</b> — a saved colour preset you drop onto footage.</li><li><b>Keyframe</b> — a marked point an effect animates between.</li><li><b>Aspect ratio</b> — width:height, like 16:9 or 9:16.</li><li><b>Export / render</b> — turning the timeline into a finished file.</li></ul></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Video Editing Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">Words your editing app assumes you know, .</p><details class="acc"><summary>Cutting &amp; timing</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li><b>Timeline</b> — the horizontal workspace where clips sit in order.</li><li><b>Cut</b> — an instant switch between clips.</li><li><b>Jump cut</b> — a cut in the same shot that skips time; jarring, sometimes intentional.</li><li><b>J-cut / L-cut</b> — audio from the next or previous clip leads or lags the picture.</li><li><b>Frame rate</b> — frames per second (fps). 24 cinematic, 30 standard, 60 smooth.</li><li><b>B-roll</b> — supporting footage layered over the main shot.</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Look &amp; sound</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li><b>Colour grading</b> — shaping the mood with colour, after basic correction.</li><li><b>LUT</b> — a saved colour preset you drop onto footage.</li><li><b>Keyframe</b> — a marked point an effect animates between.</li><li><b>Aspect ratio</b> — width:height, like 16:9 or 9:16.</li><li><b>Export / render</b> — turning the timeline into a finished file.</li></ul></div></details></div>	-
24.22.1	glossary/Yoga Pose Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Yoga Pose Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">A quick reference for common yoga terms. Tap each for more.</p><ul class="list"><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Asana</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Any physical yoga posture. From Sanskrit, meaning "seat" or "pose."</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Pranayama</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Breath control. <i>Prana</i> = life force, <i>ayama</i> = extension. The practice of guiding your breath.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Vinyasa</b></summary><div class="body"><p>A flowing sequence linking breath to movement. Often used to describe a style of class.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Sun Salutation</b></summary><div class="body"><p>A series of poses (Surya Namaskar) performed in a flowing sequence, usually at the start of class to warm up.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Prone</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Lying face down. Think "prostrate."</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Supine</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Lying face up. Think "supine" = "on your spine."</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Namaste</b></summary><div class="body"><p>A greeting of respect, often said at the end of class with hands in prayer position at the heart.</p></div></details></li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Yoga Pose Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">A quick reference for common yoga terms. Tap each for more.</p><ul class="list"><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Asana</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Any physical yoga posture. From Sanskrit, meaning "seat" or "pose."</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Pranayama</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Breath control. <i>Prana</i> = life force, <i>ayama</i> = extension. The practice of guiding your breath.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Vinyasa</b></summary><div class="body"><p>A flowing sequence linking breath to movement. Often used to describe a style of class.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Sun Salutation</b></summary><div class="body"><p>A series of poses (Surya Namaskar) performed in a flowing sequence, usually at the start of class to warm up.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Prone</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Lying face down. Think "prostrate."</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Supine</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Lying face up. Think "supine" = "on your spine."</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Namaste</b></summary><div class="body"><p>A greeting of respect, often said at the end of class with hands in prayer position at the heart.</p></div></details></li></ul></div>	-
24.23.1	glossary/Electric Vehicle Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Electric Vehicle Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">Key terms for EVs and hybrids.</p><ul class="list"><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>EV</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Electric Vehicle. Runs purely on battery power, no gas engine.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Hybrid</b></summary><div class="body"><p>A car with both a gas engine and an electric motor. The battery charges via the engine and regenerative braking, not a plug.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Plug-in Hybrid (PHEV)</b></summary><div class="body"><p>A hybrid you can plug in to charge. Runs on electric for a limited range, then switches to gas.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Regenerative Braking</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Using the motor as a generator to slow the car, capturing energy and sending it back to the battery. Also called "regen."</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Range</b></summary><div class="body"><p>How far an EV can go on a full charge. Varies with speed, temperature, and driving style.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Level 1 / Level 2 / DC Fast Charging</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Charging speeds. Level 1 is a standard outlet (slow). Level 2 is a home or public station (faster). DC Fast is high-speed public charging.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>kWh</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Kilowatt-hour. A unit of energy, like a gallon of gas. Battery capacity is measured in kWh.</p></div></details></li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Electric Vehicle Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">Key terms for EVs and hybrids.</p><ul class="list"><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>EV</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Electric Vehicle. Runs purely on battery power, no gas engine.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Hybrid</b></summary><div class="body"><p>A car with both a gas engine and an electric motor. The battery charges via the engine and regenerative braking, not a plug.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Plug-in Hybrid (PHEV)</b></summary><div class="body"><p>A hybrid you can plug in to charge. Runs on electric for a limited range, then switches to gas.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Regenerative Braking</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Using the motor as a generator to slow the car, capturing energy and sending it back to the battery. Also called "regen."</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Range</b></summary><div class="body"><p>How far an EV can go on a full charge. Varies with speed, temperature, and driving style.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Level 1 / Level 2 / DC Fast Charging</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Charging speeds. Level 1 is a standard outlet (slow). Level 2 is a home or public station (faster). DC Fast is high-speed public charging.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>kWh</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Kilowatt-hour. A unit of energy, like a gallon of gas. Battery capacity is measured in kWh.</p></div></details></li></ul></div>	-
24.24.1	glossary/Camera Settings Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Camera Settings Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">The exposure triangle and beyond.</p><ul class="list"><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Aperture</b></summary><div class="body"><p>The size of the lens opening. Measured in f-stops (e.g., f/2.8, f/16). Lower f-number = wider opening = more light and shallower depth of field.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Shutter Speed</b></summary><div class="body"><p>How long the sensor is exposed to light. Measured in seconds or fractions (e.g., 1/500, 2s). Faster freezes motion; slower blurs it.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>ISO</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Sensitivity to light. Higher ISO = brighter image but more noise. Start low (100-200) and raise only when needed.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Exposure Triangle</b></summary><div class="body"><p>The three settings—aperture, shutter speed, ISO—that together control image brightness. Adjust one, compensate with another.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Depth of Field</b></summary><div class="body"><p>The range of distance that appears sharp. Shallow DOF (blurry background) comes from wide apertures; deep DOF from narrow ones.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>White Balance</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Adjusts colors so whites look white under different lighting. Auto usually works; presets (daylight, cloudy, tungsten) give more control.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>RAW vs JPEG</b></summary><div class="body"><p>RAW files contain all sensor data, great for editing. JPEGs are compressed and ready to share. Many cameras can shoot both.</p></div></details></li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Camera Settings Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">The exposure triangle and beyond.</p><ul class="list"><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Aperture</b></summary><div class="body"><p>The size of the lens opening. Measured in f-stops (e.g., f/2.8, f/16). Lower f-number = wider opening = more light and shallower depth of field.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Shutter Speed</b></summary><div class="body"><p>How long the sensor is exposed to light. Measured in seconds or fractions (e.g., 1/500, 2s). Faster freezes motion; slower blurs it.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>ISO</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Sensitivity to light. Higher ISO = brighter image but more noise. Start low (100-200) and raise only when needed.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Exposure Triangle</b></summary><div class="body"><p>The three settings—aperture, shutter speed, ISO—that together control image brightness. Adjust one, compensate with another.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Depth of Field</b></summary><div class="body"><p>The range of distance that appears sharp. Shallow DOF (blurry background) comes from wide apertures; deep DOF from narrow ones.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>White Balance</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Adjusts colors so whites look white under different lighting. Auto usually works; presets (daylight, cloudy, tungsten) give more control.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>RAW vs JPEG</b></summary><div class="body"><p>RAW files contain all sensor data, great for editing. JPEGs are compressed and ready to share. Many cameras can shoot both.</p></div></details></li></ul></div>	-
24.25.1	glossary/Investing Terms Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Investing Terms Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">Plain-language definitions for common investing words.</p><ul class="list"><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Stock</b></summary><div class="body"><p>A share of ownership in a company. If the company does well, your share may increase in value; if not, it may decrease.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Bond</b></summary><div class="body"><p>A loan you make to a government or corporation. They pay you interest and return your principal at maturity.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Mutual Fund</b></summary><div class="body"><p>A pool of money from many investors, managed professionally to buy a mix of stocks, bonds, or other assets.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Index Fund</b></summary><div class="body"><p>A type of mutual fund or ETF that tracks a market index (like the S&P 500). Low cost and passively managed.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>ETF</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Exchange-Traded Fund. Like a mutual fund but trades on an exchange throughout the day, like a stock.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Dividend</b></summary><div class="body"><p>A portion of a company's profits paid to shareholders, usually quarterly. Not all stocks pay dividends.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Diversification</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Spreading investments across different assets to reduce risk. Don't put all your eggs in one basket.</p></div></details></li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Investing Terms Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">Plain-language definitions for common investing words.</p><ul class="list"><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Stock</b></summary><div class="body"><p>A share of ownership in a company. If the company does well, your share may increase in value; if not, it may decrease.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Bond</b></summary><div class="body"><p>A loan you make to a government or corporation. They pay you interest and return your principal at maturity.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Mutual Fund</b></summary><div class="body"><p>A pool of money from many investors, managed professionally to buy a mix of stocks, bonds, or other assets.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Index Fund</b></summary><div class="body"><p>A type of mutual fund or ETF that tracks a market index (like the S&P 500). Low cost and passively managed.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>ETF</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Exchange-Traded Fund. Like a mutual fund but trades on an exchange throughout the day, like a stock.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Dividend</b></summary><div class="body"><p>A portion of a company's profits paid to shareholders, usually quarterly. Not all stocks pay dividends.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Diversification</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Spreading investments across different assets to reduce risk. Don't put all your eggs in one basket.</p></div></details></li></ul></div>	-
24.26.1	glossary/Food Label Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Food Label Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">Decode the buzzwords on packaging.</p><ul class="list"><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Organic</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Produced without synthetic pesticides, fertilizers, or GMOs. Certified by the USDA. Doesn't automatically mean healthier, but has environmental benefits.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Free Range</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Animals have some access to the outdoors. For poultry, it means they can go outside, but duration and space aren't strictly defined.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Pasture Raised</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Animals spent most of their lives on pasture, eating grass. A higher standard than free range, but not legally defined for all products.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Cage Free</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Hens aren't in cages but may still be crowded indoors. Often confused with free range.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>GMO</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Genetically Modified Organism. Crops engineered for traits like pest resistance. The USDA now requires disclosure via QR code or text.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Natural</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Minimally processed, no artificial ingredients. Largely unregulated except for meat and poultry. Not the same as organic.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Fair Trade</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Certifies that farmers and workers received fair prices and safe conditions. Common on coffee, chocolate, and bananas.</p></div></details></li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Food Label Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">Decode the buzzwords on packaging.</p><ul class="list"><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Organic</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Produced without synthetic pesticides, fertilizers, or GMOs. Certified by the USDA. Doesn't automatically mean healthier, but has environmental benefits.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Free Range</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Animals have some access to the outdoors. For poultry, it means they can go outside, but duration and space aren't strictly defined.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Pasture Raised</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Animals spent most of their lives on pasture, eating grass. A higher standard than free range, but not legally defined for all products.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Cage Free</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Hens aren't in cages but may still be crowded indoors. Often confused with free range.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>GMO</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Genetically Modified Organism. Crops engineered for traits like pest resistance. The USDA now requires disclosure via QR code or text.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Natural</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Minimally processed, no artificial ingredients. Largely unregulated except for meat and poultry. Not the same as organic.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Fair Trade</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Certifies that farmers and workers received fair prices and safe conditions. Common on coffee, chocolate, and bananas.</p></div></details></li></ul></div>	-
24.27.1	glossary/Electronic Music Genre Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Electronic Music Genre Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">A field guide to the dance floor and beyond.</p><ul class="list"><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>House</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Four-on-the-floor beats, soulful vocals, tempo around 120-130 BPM. Born in Chicago. Think Frankie Knuckles.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Techno</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Hypnotic, repetitive, often instrumental. Detroit origins. Tempo 120-140 BPM. More mechanical than house.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Dubstep</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Heavy bass, syncopated drums, around 140 BPM. The drop is king. Originated in South London.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Lo-fi</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Short for low fidelity. Intentionally imperfect sound: vinyl crackle, soft beats, jazzy chords. Great for studying.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Drop</b></summary><div class="body"><p>The moment in a track when the bass and beat hit full force after a build-up. The payoff.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>BPM</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Beats Per Minute. The tempo. House ~120, dubstep ~140, drum & bass ~174.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Sampling</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Taking a piece of an existing recording and reusing it in a new track. Foundational to hip-hop and electronic music.</p></div></details></li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Electronic Music Genre Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">A field guide to the dance floor and beyond.</p><ul class="list"><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>House</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Four-on-the-floor beats, soulful vocals, tempo around 120-130 BPM. Born in Chicago. Think Frankie Knuckles.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Techno</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Hypnotic, repetitive, often instrumental. Detroit origins. Tempo 120-140 BPM. More mechanical than house.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Dubstep</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Heavy bass, syncopated drums, around 140 BPM. The drop is king. Originated in South London.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Lo-fi</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Short for low fidelity. Intentionally imperfect sound: vinyl crackle, soft beats, jazzy chords. Great for studying.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Drop</b></summary><div class="body"><p>The moment in a track when the bass and beat hit full force after a build-up. The payoff.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>BPM</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Beats Per Minute. The tempo. House ~120, dubstep ~140, drum & bass ~174.</p></div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary><b>Sampling</b></summary><div class="body"><p>Taking a piece of an existing recording and reusing it in a new track. Foundational to hip-hop and electronic music.</p></div></details></li></ul></div>	-
24.28.1	glossary/Chess Endgame: King & Pawn Glossary	<div class="card"><div class="row between"><h4 class="card-title">King &amp; Pawn Endgames</h4><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</span></div><p class="muted t-small">Where most games are actually decided, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><details class="acc" open><summary>Core terms</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li><b>Opposition</b> — kings face off with one square between them; whoever must move loses ground.</li><li><b>Zugzwang</b> — you'd love to pass, but passing isn't allowed. Every move hurts.</li><li><b>Key square</b> — the square a king must reach to force the pawn through.</li><li><b>Square of the pawn</b> — mental box: if the defending king can step inside it, the pawn never promotes.</li><li><b>Passed pawn</b> — no enemy pawn can stop or capture it. Treasure it.</li><li><b>Outside passer</b> — a passed pawn far from the action; it lures the defender away.</li><li><b>Breakthrough</b> — a pawn sacrifice that clears a path for a remaining pawn.</li><li><b>Stalemate</b> — no legal move, and no check. Draw, sometimes from a winning position.</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Quick shape check</summary><div class="body"><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 160 40" role="img" aria-label="Diagram: white king, two squares, black king"><rect x="10" y="5" width="30" height="30" class="guide" fill="none"></rect><rect x="40" y="5" width="30" height="30" class="guide" fill="none"></rect><rect x="70" y="5" width="30" height="30" class="guide" fill="none"></rect><circle cx="25" cy="20" r="9" fill="currentColor"></circle><circle cx="85" cy="20" r="9" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.45"></circle><text x="55" y="26" class="lbl" text-anchor="middle">1</text></svg></div><p class="dim t-small">One empty square between kings = opposition.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><div class="row between"><h4 class="card-title">King &amp; Pawn Endgames</h4><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</span></div><p class="muted t-small">Where most games are actually decided, .</p><details class="acc" open><summary>Core terms</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li><b>Opposition</b> — kings face off with one square between them; whoever must move loses ground.</li><li><b>Zugzwang</b> — you'd love to pass, but passing isn't allowed. Every move hurts.</li><li><b>Key square</b> — the square a king must reach to force the pawn through.</li><li><b>Square of the pawn</b> — mental box: if the defending king can step inside it, the pawn never promotes.</li><li><b>Passed pawn</b> — no enemy pawn can stop or capture it. Treasure it.</li><li><b>Outside passer</b> — a passed pawn far from the action; it lures the defender away.</li><li><b>Breakthrough</b> — a pawn sacrifice that clears a path for a remaining pawn.</li><li><b>Stalemate</b> — no legal move, and no check. Draw, sometimes from a winning position.</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Quick shape check</summary><div class="body"><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 160 40" role="img" aria-label="Diagram: white king, two squares, black king"><rect x="10" y="5" width="30" height="30" class="guide" fill="none"></rect><rect x="40" y="5" width="30" height="30" class="guide" fill="none"></rect><rect x="70" y="5" width="30" height="30" class="guide" fill="none"></rect><circle cx="25" cy="20" r="9" fill="currentColor"></circle><circle cx="85" cy="20" r="9" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.45"></circle><text x="55" y="26" class="lbl" text-anchor="middle">1</text></svg></div><p class="dim t-small">One empty square between kings = opposition.</p></div></details></div>	-
24.29.1	glossary/Chess Rook & Bishop Endgame Glossary	<div class="card"><div class="row between"><h4 class="card-title">Rook Ending Vocabulary</h4><span class="badge info">study aid</span></div><ul class="list"><li><b>Lucena position</b> — the classic winning setup. Build a bridge, promote the pawn, take the applause.</li><li><b>Philidor position</b> — the classic drawing setup. Keep the rook on the third rank, then check from behind.</li><li><b>Bridge</b> — a rook maneuver that shelters your king from checks during promotion.</li><li><b>Seventh rank rook</b> — a rook on the seventh cuts off the enemy king. Painful for them.</li><li><b>Passive rook</b> — defending in front of the pawn. Usually a slow loss.</li><li><b>Short side / long side</b> — which side your king hangs out on determines whether you get checked forever.</li><li><b>Checking distance</b> — checking from three ranks away so the rook can't be grabbed.</li><li><b>Back-rank mate</b> — the reason you play <i>h3</i> sometimes. Just do it, <regex>user_name</regex>.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Priority order when defending</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Activate the rook.</li><li>Keep the king in front of the pawn when possible.</li><li>Check from behind, never in front.</li><li>Trade pawns, not your sanity.</li></ol></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><div class="row between"><h4 class="card-title">Rook Ending Vocabulary</h4><span class="badge info">study aid</span></div><ul class="list"><li><b>Lucena position</b> — the classic winning setup. Build a bridge, promote the pawn, take the applause.</li><li><b>Philidor position</b> — the classic drawing setup. Keep the rook on the third rank, then check from behind.</li><li><b>Bridge</b> — a rook maneuver that shelters your king from checks during promotion.</li><li><b>Seventh rank rook</b> — a rook on the seventh cuts off the enemy king. Painful for them.</li><li><b>Passive rook</b> — defending in front of the pawn. Usually a slow loss.</li><li><b>Short side / long side</b> — which side your king hangs out on determines whether you get checked forever.</li><li><b>Checking distance</b> — checking from three ranks away so the rook can't be grabbed.</li><li><b>Back-rank mate</b> — the reason you play <i>h3</i> sometimes. Just do it, .</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Priority order when defending</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Activate the rook.</li><li>Keep the king in front of the pawn when possible.</li><li>Check from behind, never in front.</li><li>Trade pawns, not your sanity.</li></ol></div></details></div>	-
24.30.1	glossary/Cocktail Glossaries: Whiskey & Agave	<div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Whiskey &amp; Agave, Word by Word</h4><p class="muted t-small">Order with confidence, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><details class="acc" open><summary>Whiskey</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li><b>Neat</b> — nothing added. Just you and your choices.</li><li><b>Up</b> — chilled, strained, served in a stemmed glass.</li><li><b>On the rocks</b> — over ice, deliberately diluted.</li><li><b>Proof</b> — in the US, twice the ABV. 100 proof is 50% alcohol.</li><li><b>Mash bill</b> — the grain recipe.</li><li><b>Single barrel</b> — one cask, one personality.</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Agave</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li><b>Blanco</b> — unaged, bright, peppery.</li><li><b>Reposado</b> — rested a couple months in wood.</li><li><b>Añejo</b> — a year-plus in barrels; rounder, sweeter.</li><li><b>Mezcal</b> — any agave spirit. Smoke is a style, not a rule.</li><li><b>Espadín</b> — the workhorse agave. Also the reliable one.</li></ul></div></details><blockquote class="quote">Salt, spirit, lime. In that order, and no one yells.<cite>— every good bartender</cite></blockquote></div>	<div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Whiskey &amp; Agave, Word by Word</h4><p class="muted t-small">Order with confidence, .</p><details class="acc" open><summary>Whiskey</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li><b>Neat</b> — nothing added. Just you and your choices.</li><li><b>Up</b> — chilled, strained, served in a stemmed glass.</li><li><b>On the rocks</b> — over ice, deliberately diluted.</li><li><b>Proof</b> — in the US, twice the ABV. 100 proof is 50% alcohol.</li><li><b>Mash bill</b> — the grain recipe.</li><li><b>Single barrel</b> — one cask, one personality.</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Agave</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li><b>Blanco</b> — unaged, bright, peppery.</li><li><b>Reposado</b> — rested a couple months in wood.</li><li><b>Añejo</b> — a year-plus in barrels; rounder, sweeter.</li><li><b>Mezcal</b> — any agave spirit. Smoke is a style, not a rule.</li><li><b>Espadín</b> — the workhorse agave. Also the reliable one.</li></ul></div></details><blockquote class="quote">Salt, spirit, lime. In that order, and no one yells.<cite>— every good bartender</cite></blockquote></div>	-
24.31.1	glossary/Chess Opening: Scandinavian & Pirc Glossary	<div class="card"><div class="row between"><h4 class="card-title">Offbeat Openings</h4><span class="badge accent">cached</span></div><p class="muted t-small">For when <regex>user_name</regex> wants a game nobody prepped for.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Scandinavian Defense</b> — 1.e4 d5. Grab the pawn, accept the tempo loss, get a solid position.</li><li><b>Queen's Gambit-style centre grab</b> — if White plays exd5, Black recaptures with the queen and gets nudged around a bit.</li><li><b>Pirc Defense</b> — Black lets White build a big centre, then attacks it. Fist vs. wall.</li><li><b>Modern Defense</b> — same spirit, king's bishop stays home a little longer.</li><li><b>Hypermodern</b> — control the centre from a distance instead of occupying it.</li><li><b>Fianchetto</b> — bishop tucked on b2/g2. Patient and rude in equal measure.</li><li><b>Center fork trick</b> — a knight sacrifice that regains the pawn with a fork. Sneaky, legal, effective.</li><li><b>Transposition</b> — arriving at a known position by a strange route. Feels like cheating. Isn't.</li></ul><div class="callout warn"><b>Careful</b> — offbeat isn't the same as unsound. Respect the opponent's centre.</div></div>	<div class="card"><div class="row between"><h4 class="card-title">Offbeat Openings</h4><span class="badge accent">cached</span></div><p class="muted t-small">For when  wants a game nobody prepped for.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Scandinavian Defense</b> — 1.e4 d5. Grab the pawn, accept the tempo loss, get a solid position.</li><li><b>Queen's Gambit-style centre grab</b> — if White plays exd5, Black recaptures with the queen and gets nudged around a bit.</li><li><b>Pirc Defense</b> — Black lets White build a big centre, then attacks it. Fist vs. wall.</li><li><b>Modern Defense</b> — same spirit, king's bishop stays home a little longer.</li><li><b>Hypermodern</b> — control the centre from a distance instead of occupying it.</li><li><b>Fianchetto</b> — bishop tucked on b2/g2. Patient and rude in equal measure.</li><li><b>Center fork trick</b> — a knight sacrifice that regains the pawn with a fork. Sneaky, legal, effective.</li><li><b>Transposition</b> — arriving at a known position by a strange route. Feels like cheating. Isn't.</li></ul><div class="callout warn"><b>Careful</b> — offbeat isn't the same as unsound. Respect the opponent's centre.</div></div>	-
24.32.1	glossary/Bread Scoring & Crumb Glossary	<div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Crumb, Crust, and Everything Between</h4><p class="muted t-small">Reading your loaf like a note from past <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Oven spring</span><span class="value">rise<small class="unit">in first 10 min</small></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Ear</span><span class="value">lift<small class="unit">from the score</small></span></div></div><ul class="list"><li><b>Crumb</b> — the inside structure. Open means big irregular holes; tight means dense and even.</li><li><b>Alveoli</b> — the holes themselves. Yes, bread has anatomy.</li><li><b>Oven spring</b> — the final rise once heat hits. Weak spring usually means weak shaping.</li><li><b>Scoring</b> — cutting the surface so the loaf expands where you allow it.</li><li><b>Ear</b> — the raised lip a good angled score produces. Purely cosmetic. Deeply satisfying.</li><li><b>Gummy</b> — underbaked or underfermented crumb. The most common sad outcome.</li><li><b>Fool's crumb</b> — big holes that look impressive but mean the dough was underproofed.</li><li><b>Bloom</b> — the flour dusting pattern; also how a loaf opens in the oven.</li><li><b>Honeycomb</b> — evenly distributed small holes. The sign of a well-behaved bulk ferment.</li></ul><div class="callout good"><b>Rule of thumb</b> — if it's gummy, add ten minutes and check the internal temperature next time.</div></div>	<div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Crumb, Crust, and Everything Between</h4><p class="muted t-small">Reading your loaf like a note from past .</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Oven spring</span><span class="value">rise<small class="unit">in first 10 min</small></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Ear</span><span class="value">lift<small class="unit">from the score</small></span></div></div><ul class="list"><li><b>Crumb</b> — the inside structure. Open means big irregular holes; tight means dense and even.</li><li><b>Alveoli</b> — the holes themselves. Yes, bread has anatomy.</li><li><b>Oven spring</b> — the final rise once heat hits. Weak spring usually means weak shaping.</li><li><b>Scoring</b> — cutting the surface so the loaf expands where you allow it.</li><li><b>Ear</b> — the raised lip a good angled score produces. Purely cosmetic. Deeply satisfying.</li><li><b>Gummy</b> — underbaked or underfermented crumb. The most common sad outcome.</li><li><b>Fool's crumb</b> — big holes that look impressive but mean the dough was underproofed.</li><li><b>Bloom</b> — the flour dusting pattern; also how a loaf opens in the oven.</li><li><b>Honeycomb</b> — evenly distributed small holes. The sign of a well-behaved bulk ferment.</li></ul><div class="callout good"><b>Rule of thumb</b> — if it's gummy, add ten minutes and check the internal temperature next time.</div></div>	-
24.33.1	glossary/Guitar Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Guitar Glossary</h4><p class="muted">Quick terms for the fretboard curious.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Fret</b> — the metal strip; also a numbered space on the neck.</li><li><b>Nut</b> — slotted bone or plastic at the headstock end.</li><li><b>Action</b> — string height above the frets.</li><li><b>Capo</b> — clamp that raises the pitch.</li><li><b>Chromatic</b> — moving in half steps.</li><li><b>Palm mute</b> — resting the palm on strings near the bridge for a chug.</li><li><b>Hammer-on</b> — sounding a note by fretting sharply, no pick.</li><li><b>Pull-off</b> — flicking a finger off to sound a lower note.</li></ul><p class="t-small dim">Pro tip: if it buzzes, check action and neck relief before blaming the cat.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Guitar Glossary</h4><p class="muted">Quick terms for the fretboard curious.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Fret</b> — the metal strip; also a numbered space on the neck.</li><li><b>Nut</b> — slotted bone or plastic at the headstock end.</li><li><b>Action</b> — string height above the frets.</li><li><b>Capo</b> — clamp that raises the pitch.</li><li><b>Chromatic</b> — moving in half steps.</li><li><b>Palm mute</b> — resting the palm on strings near the bridge for a chug.</li><li><b>Hammer-on</b> — sounding a note by fretting sharply, no pick.</li><li><b>Pull-off</b> — flicking a finger off to sound a lower note.</li></ul><p class="t-small dim">Pro tip: if it buzzes, check action and neck relief before blaming the cat.</p></div>	-
24.34.1	glossary/Cooking Terms Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Cooking Terms</h4><p class="muted">A pocket guide for recipe readers.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Mise en place</b> — everything prepped and in place before heat.</li><li><b>Julienne</b> — thin matchsticks.</li><li><b>Dice</b> — small cubes.</li><li><b>Fold</b> — gentle lift-and-turn to keep air in.</li><li><b>Deglaze</b> — splash liquid into a hot pan to lift the tasty bits.</li><li><b>Emulsion</b> — two liquids forced into friendship (vinaigrette, mayo).</li><li><b>Blanch</b> — quick boil, then ice bath to lock colour.</li><li><b>Reduce</b> — simmer to concentrate flavour.</li></ul><p class="t-small dim">Short on time? Mise en place is the one that saves dinner.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Cooking Terms</h4><p class="muted">A pocket guide for recipe readers.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Mise en place</b> — everything prepped and in place before heat.</li><li><b>Julienne</b> — thin matchsticks.</li><li><b>Dice</b> — small cubes.</li><li><b>Fold</b> — gentle lift-and-turn to keep air in.</li><li><b>Deglaze</b> — splash liquid into a hot pan to lift the tasty bits.</li><li><b>Emulsion</b> — two liquids forced into friendship (vinaigrette, mayo).</li><li><b>Blanch</b> — quick boil, then ice bath to lock colour.</li><li><b>Reduce</b> — simmer to concentrate flavour.</li></ul><p class="t-small dim">Short on time? Mise en place is the one that saves dinner.</p></div>	-
24.35.1	glossary/Photography Basics Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Photography Basics</h4><p class="muted">The exposure triangle, minus the headache.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Aperture</b> — lens opening; f/1.8 is wide, f/16 is a squint.</li><li><b>Shutter speed</b> — how long the sensor sees light; 1/1000 freezes, 1/30 blurs.</li><li><b>ISO</b> — sensitivity boost; higher = brighter but noisier.</li><li><b>Exposure</b> — overall brightness of the shot.</li><li><b>Depth of field</b> — how much is sharp front to back.</li><li><b>Bokeh</b> — the dreamy blur of out-of-focus highlights.</li><li><b>White balance</b> — colour temperature correction.</li><li><b>Raw</b> — unprocessed file with editing headroom.</li></ul><p class="t-small dim">Start in auto, peek at the settings, then take the wheel.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Photography Basics</h4><p class="muted">The exposure triangle, minus the headache.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Aperture</b> — lens opening; f/1.8 is wide, f/16 is a squint.</li><li><b>Shutter speed</b> — how long the sensor sees light; 1/1000 freezes, 1/30 blurs.</li><li><b>ISO</b> — sensitivity boost; higher = brighter but noisier.</li><li><b>Exposure</b> — overall brightness of the shot.</li><li><b>Depth of field</b> — how much is sharp front to back.</li><li><b>Bokeh</b> — the dreamy blur of out-of-focus highlights.</li><li><b>White balance</b> — colour temperature correction.</li><li><b>Raw</b> — unprocessed file with editing headroom.</li></ul><p class="t-small dim">Start in auto, peek at the settings, then take the wheel.</p></div>	-
24.36.1	glossary/Everyday Computer Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Everyday Computer Terms</h4><p class="muted">No jargon, promise.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Browser</b> — the app you open to visit websites (Chrome, Safari, Firefox).</li><li><b>Tab</b> — one page inside the browser window.</li><li><b>Bookmark</b> — a saved link for later.</li><li><b>Download</b> — pulling a file from the internet to your device.</li><li><b>Upload</b> — sending a file from your device to the internet.</li><li><b>Wi‑Fi</b> — wireless internet.</li><li><b>Cloud</b> — someone else's computer where your files live.</li><li><b>Spam</b> — junk mail; don't click the links.</li></ul><p class="t-small dim">If a pop‑up says you've won a prize, you haven't. Close the tab.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Everyday Computer Terms</h4><p class="muted">No jargon, promise.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Browser</b> — the app you open to visit websites (Chrome, Safari, Firefox).</li><li><b>Tab</b> — one page inside the browser window.</li><li><b>Bookmark</b> — a saved link for later.</li><li><b>Download</b> — pulling a file from the internet to your device.</li><li><b>Upload</b> — sending a file from your device to the internet.</li><li><b>Wi‑Fi</b> — wireless internet.</li><li><b>Cloud</b> — someone else's computer where your files live.</li><li><b>Spam</b> — junk mail; don't click the links.</li></ul><p class="t-small dim">If a pop‑up says you've won a prize, you haven't. Close the tab.</p></div>	-
24.37.1	glossary/Coffee Cupping Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Coffee Cupping Glossary</h4><ul class="list"><li><b>Cupping</b> — the standard method for tasting coffee: steep grounds in hot water, break the crust, slurp.</li><li><b>Slurp</b> — to noisily aspirate coffee so it sprays across your palate.</li><li><b>Crust</b> — the floating layer of grounds on top before you break it.</li><li><b>Break</b> — stirring the crust to release aromatics. Listen for the crack.</li><li><b>Bloom</b> — the initial swell when hot water hits fresh grounds. Fresh coffee puffs up.</li><li><b>Body</b> — the tactile weight: thin like tea or heavy like cream.</li><li><b>Acidity</b> — the bright, juicy snap. Not the same as sour.</li><li><b>Sweetness</b> — the sugary impression, from caramel to fruit.</li><li><b>Aftertaste</b> — the flavour that lingers after you swallow.</li><li><b>Score</b> — cuppers rate 6–10; specialty coffee usually scores 80+.</li></ul><p class="muted t-small">Pro tip: slurp like you mean it. It’s not rude, it’s science.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Coffee Cupping Glossary</h4><ul class="list"><li><b>Cupping</b> — the standard method for tasting coffee: steep grounds in hot water, break the crust, slurp.</li><li><b>Slurp</b> — to noisily aspirate coffee so it sprays across your palate.</li><li><b>Crust</b> — the floating layer of grounds on top before you break it.</li><li><b>Break</b> — stirring the crust to release aromatics. Listen for the crack.</li><li><b>Bloom</b> — the initial swell when hot water hits fresh grounds. Fresh coffee puffs up.</li><li><b>Body</b> — the tactile weight: thin like tea or heavy like cream.</li><li><b>Acidity</b> — the bright, juicy snap. Not the same as sour.</li><li><b>Sweetness</b> — the sugary impression, from caramel to fruit.</li><li><b>Aftertaste</b> — the flavour that lingers after you swallow.</li><li><b>Score</b> — cuppers rate 6–10; specialty coffee usually scores 80+.</li></ul><p class="muted t-small">Pro tip: slurp like you mean it. It’s not rude, it’s science.</p></div>	-
24.38.1	glossary/Web Development Jargon Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Web Development Jargon Glossary</h4><ul class="list"><li><b>HTML</b> — the skeleton: tags that give structure and meaning.</li><li><b>CSS</b> — the wardrobe: colours, spacing, layout.</li><li><b>JavaScript</b> — the muscle: behaviour and interactivity.</li><li><b>DOM</b> — the browser’s live model of your page; JS pokes it to change things.</li><li><b>API</b> — a menu of requests a service will answer.</li><li><b>Endpoint</b> — a specific URL where an API listens.</li><li><b>Framework</b> — pre-built scaffolding. React, Vue, Svelte… pick your poison.</li><li><b>Responsive</b> — design that adapts from phone to desktop without breaking.</li><li><b>Bug</b> — a feature you didn’t plan.</li><li><b>Deploy</b> — shipping your code to a server where real people can use it.</li></ul><p class="muted t-small">One does not simply “fix it in post” for websites.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Web Development Jargon Glossary</h4><ul class="list"><li><b>HTML</b> — the skeleton: tags that give structure and meaning.</li><li><b>CSS</b> — the wardrobe: colours, spacing, layout.</li><li><b>JavaScript</b> — the muscle: behaviour and interactivity.</li><li><b>DOM</b> — the browser’s live model of your page; JS pokes it to change things.</li><li><b>API</b> — a menu of requests a service will answer.</li><li><b>Endpoint</b> — a specific URL where an API listens.</li><li><b>Framework</b> — pre-built scaffolding. React, Vue, Svelte… pick your poison.</li><li><b>Responsive</b> — design that adapts from phone to desktop without breaking.</li><li><b>Bug</b> — a feature you didn’t plan.</li><li><b>Deploy</b> — shipping your code to a server where real people can use it.</li></ul><p class="muted t-small">One does not simply “fix it in post” for websites.</p></div>	-
24.39.1	glossary/Cooking Techniques Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Cooking Techniques Glossary</h4><ul class="list"><li><b>Sauté</b> — quick, high-heat toss in a little fat. From the French for “jump”.</li><li><b>Sear</b> — hard, fast browning to build flavour. Don’t crowd the pan.</li><li><b>Braise</b> — sear then simmer low and slow with liquid, lid on. Tough cuts go tender.</li><li><b>Roast</b> — dry, circulating oven heat. Think crispy edges.</li><li><b>Poach</b> — gentle simmer in liquid. Eggs, fish, fruit.</li><li><b>Blanch</b> — boil briefly, then shock in ice water to set colour and texture.</li><li><b>Deglaze</b> — splash liquid into a hot pan to lift the browned bits. Free flavour.</li><li><b>Emulsify</b> — whisk two liquids that don’t normally mix into a creamy suspension.</li><li><b>Reduce</b> — simmer a liquid to concentrate flavour and thicken.</li><li><b>Fold</b> — gentle lift-and-turn to keep air in. Don’t stir the life out of it.</li></ul><p class="muted t-small">If it’s burning, your pan’s too hot. If it’s steaming, it’s not searing.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Cooking Techniques Glossary</h4><ul class="list"><li><b>Sauté</b> — quick, high-heat toss in a little fat. From the French for “jump”.</li><li><b>Sear</b> — hard, fast browning to build flavour. Don’t crowd the pan.</li><li><b>Braise</b> — sear then simmer low and slow with liquid, lid on. Tough cuts go tender.</li><li><b>Roast</b> — dry, circulating oven heat. Think crispy edges.</li><li><b>Poach</b> — gentle simmer in liquid. Eggs, fish, fruit.</li><li><b>Blanch</b> — boil briefly, then shock in ice water to set colour and texture.</li><li><b>Deglaze</b> — splash liquid into a hot pan to lift the browned bits. Free flavour.</li><li><b>Emulsify</b> — whisk two liquids that don’t normally mix into a creamy suspension.</li><li><b>Reduce</b> — simmer a liquid to concentrate flavour and thicken.</li><li><b>Fold</b> — gentle lift-and-turn to keep air in. Don’t stir the life out of it.</li></ul><p class="muted t-small">If it’s burning, your pan’s too hot. If it’s steaming, it’s not searing.</p></div>	-
24.40.1	glossary/Music Theory Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Music Theory Glossary</h4><ul class="list"><li><b>Pitch</b> — how high or low a note sounds.</li><li><b>Scale</b> — a ladder of notes; major sounds happy, minor sounds moody.</li><li><b>Key</b> — the home base your song revolves around. It has a gravity.</li><li><b>Chord</b> — three or more notes stacked. The backbone of harmony.</li><li><b>Interval</b> — the distance between two notes. A third, a fifth, an octave.</li><li><b>Octave</b> — the same note, higher or lower. Double the frequency.</li><li><b>Mode</b> — a scale with a different starting point. Same notes, new flavour.</li><li><b>Time signature</b> — how many beats per bar and which note gets the beat.</li><li><b>Tempo</b> — speed, measured in BPM.</li><li><b>Dynamics</b> — how loud or soft: piano, forte, and everything between.</li></ul><p class="muted t-small">Theory is just names for things your ears already know. Now you can talk about them.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Music Theory Glossary</h4><ul class="list"><li><b>Pitch</b> — how high or low a note sounds.</li><li><b>Scale</b> — a ladder of notes; major sounds happy, minor sounds moody.</li><li><b>Key</b> — the home base your song revolves around. It has a gravity.</li><li><b>Chord</b> — three or more notes stacked. The backbone of harmony.</li><li><b>Interval</b> — the distance between two notes. A third, a fifth, an octave.</li><li><b>Octave</b> — the same note, higher or lower. Double the frequency.</li><li><b>Mode</b> — a scale with a different starting point. Same notes, new flavour.</li><li><b>Time signature</b> — how many beats per bar and which note gets the beat.</li><li><b>Tempo</b> — speed, measured in BPM.</li><li><b>Dynamics</b> — how loud or soft: piano, forte, and everything between.</li></ul><p class="muted t-small">Theory is just names for things your ears already know. Now you can talk about them.</p></div>	-
24.41.1	glossary/Basic Finance Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Basic Finance Glossary</h4><ul class="list"><li><b>Interest</b> — the price of borrowing money, paid to a lender.</li><li><b>Compound interest</b> — interest that earns interest. Einstein called it the eighth wonder.</li><li><b>Asset</b> — something you own that has value.</li><li><b>Liability</b> — something you owe. A debt.</li><li><b>Stock</b> — a slice of ownership in a company.</li><li><b>Bond</b> — a loan you make to a government or company. They pay you back with interest.</li><li><b>Portfolio</b> — your collection of investments.</li><li><b>Diversification</b> — not putting all your eggs in one basket.</li><li><b>Bull market</b> — prices are rising, optimism high.</li><li><b>Bear market</b> — prices are falling, caution advised.</li></ul><p class="muted t-small">Money talks. Compound interest talks louder, given time.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Basic Finance Glossary</h4><ul class="list"><li><b>Interest</b> — the price of borrowing money, paid to a lender.</li><li><b>Compound interest</b> — interest that earns interest. Einstein called it the eighth wonder.</li><li><b>Asset</b> — something you own that has value.</li><li><b>Liability</b> — something you owe. A debt.</li><li><b>Stock</b> — a slice of ownership in a company.</li><li><b>Bond</b> — a loan you make to a government or company. They pay you back with interest.</li><li><b>Portfolio</b> — your collection of investments.</li><li><b>Diversification</b> — not putting all your eggs in one basket.</li><li><b>Bull market</b> — prices are rising, optimism high.</li><li><b>Bear market</b> — prices are falling, caution advised.</li></ul><p class="muted t-small">Money talks. Compound interest talks louder, given time.</p></div>	-
24.42.1	glossary/Knife Sharpening Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Knife Sharpening Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">Sharp is a feeling, <regex>user_name</regex>. Here's the vocabulary.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Core terms</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li><b>Burr</b> — a tiny curled lip of metal. Feel it, then remove it.</li><li><b>Grit</b> — stone coarseness. Low (400) resets, high (6000) polishes.</li><li><b>Bevel</b> — the angled strip that forms your edge.</li><li><b>Honing</b> — realigning an edge. Doesn't remove metal.</li><li><b>Stropping</b> — leather + compound to finish and deburr.</li><li><b>Apex</b> — where the bevels actually meet. That's your edge.</li><li><b>Sharpie trick</b> — marker the bevel to see what you're grinding.</li></ul></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Knife Sharpening Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">Sharp is a feeling, . Here's the vocabulary.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Core terms</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li><b>Burr</b> — a tiny curled lip of metal. Feel it, then remove it.</li><li><b>Grit</b> — stone coarseness. Low (400) resets, high (6000) polishes.</li><li><b>Bevel</b> — the angled strip that forms your edge.</li><li><b>Honing</b> — realigning an edge. Doesn't remove metal.</li><li><b>Stropping</b> — leather + compound to finish and deburr.</li><li><b>Apex</b> — where the bevels actually meet. That's your edge.</li><li><b>Sharpie trick</b> — marker the bevel to see what you're grinding.</li></ul></div></details></div>	-
24.43.1	glossary/Chess Opening: 1.d4 Systems Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Chess Opening: 1.d4 Systems Glossary</h4><ul class="list"><li><b>Queen's Gambit</b> — 1.d4 d5 2.c4. Not really a gambit; you usually get it back.</li><li><b>QGD</b> — declined, with ...e6. Solid, patient, quietly grindy.</li><li><b>Slav</b> — ...c6 defends d5 while keeping the bishop's diagonal open.</li><li><b>Fianchetto</b> — bishop to b2 or g2, eyeing the long diagonal.</li><li><b>Nimzo-Indian</b> — ...Bb4 pins the knight and fights for e4.</li><li><b>King's Indian</b> — ...Nf6, ...g6, then ...e5 or ...c5. Sharp either way.</li><li><b>Catalan</b> — g3 + c4: slow squeeze with a fianchetto bishop.</li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Chess Opening: 1.d4 Systems Glossary</h4><ul class="list"><li><b>Queen's Gambit</b> — 1.d4 d5 2.c4. Not really a gambit; you usually get it back.</li><li><b>QGD</b> — declined, with ...e6. Solid, patient, quietly grindy.</li><li><b>Slav</b> — ...c6 defends d5 while keeping the bishop's diagonal open.</li><li><b>Fianchetto</b> — bishop to b2 or g2, eyeing the long diagonal.</li><li><b>Nimzo-Indian</b> — ...Bb4 pins the knight and fights for e4.</li><li><b>King's Indian</b> — ...Nf6, ...g6, then ...e5 or ...c5. Sharp either way.</li><li><b>Catalan</b> — g3 + c4: slow squeeze with a fianchetto bishop.</li></ul></div>	-
24.44.1	glossary/Running & Race Training Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Running &amp; Race Training Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">Your legs know more than your watch, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Easy pace</b> — conversational. If you can't chat, slow down.</li><li><b>Tempo run</b> — comfortably hard, held for 20–40 minutes.</li><li><b>Fartlek</b> — Swedish for "speed play." Unstructured surges.</li><li><b>Negative split</b> — second half faster than the first. Chef's kiss.</li><li><b>Cadence</b> — steps per minute. ~170–180 is a common target.</li><li><b>VO₂ max</b> — how much oxygen you can actually use. Trainable, slowly.</li><li><b>Taper</b> — cutting volume before a race so you arrive fresh.</li><li><b>Bonk</b> — glycogen's empty and the wall is real.</li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Running &amp; Race Training Glossary</h4><p class="muted t-small">Your legs know more than your watch, .</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Easy pace</b> — conversational. If you can't chat, slow down.</li><li><b>Tempo run</b> — comfortably hard, held for 20–40 minutes.</li><li><b>Fartlek</b> — Swedish for "speed play." Unstructured surges.</li><li><b>Negative split</b> — second half faster than the first. Chef's kiss.</li><li><b>Cadence</b> — steps per minute. ~170–180 is a common target.</li><li><b>VO₂ max</b> — how much oxygen you can actually use. Trainable, slowly.</li><li><b>Taper</b> — cutting volume before a race so you arrive fresh.</li><li><b>Bonk</b> — glycogen's empty and the wall is real.</li></ul></div>	-
24.45.1	glossary/Chess Knight & Bishop Endgame Glossary	<div class="card"><h4>Chess Knight &amp; Bishop Endgame Glossary</h4><ul class="list"><li><b>Wrong bishop</b> — it doesn't control your pawn's promotion square. Draw.</li><li><b>Opposite-coloured bishops</b> — drawish, even a pawn down.</li><li><b>Good bishop</b> — outside your own pawn chain, free to roam.</li><li><b>Knight on the rim</b> — dim. Centralised knights dominate.</li><li><b>Fortress</b> — a setup that holds forever despite being down material.</li><li><b>Zugzwang</b> — every move makes it worse. You must move anyway.</li><li><b>Domination</b> — the enemy piece has squares, none of them safe.</li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Chess Knight &amp; Bishop Endgame Glossary</h4><ul class="list"><li><b>Wrong bishop</b> — it doesn't control your pawn's promotion square. Draw.</li><li><b>Opposite-coloured bishops</b> — drawish, even a pawn down.</li><li><b>Good bishop</b> — outside your own pawn chain, free to roam.</li><li><b>Knight on the rim</b> — dim. Centralised knights dominate.</li><li><b>Fortress</b> — a setup that holds forever despite being down material.</li><li><b>Zugzwang</b> — every move makes it worse. You must move anyway.</li><li><b>Domination</b> — the enemy piece has squares, none of them safe.</li></ul></div>	-
25.0.0	recipe_card/Homemade Pizza Dough	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Homemade Pizza Dough</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 10 min</span><span>Rise 1 hr</span><span>Makes 2 crusts</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>3 cups all-purpose flour</li><li>1 packet active dry yeast</li><li>1 tsp sugar</li><li>1 tsp salt</li><li>1 cup warm water</li><li>2 tbsp olive oil</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>In a small bowl, mix yeast, sugar, and warm water. Let sit until foamy, about 5 minutes.</li><li>In a large bowl, combine flour and salt. Add yeast mixture and olive oil; stir until a shaggy dough forms.</li><li>Knead on a floured surface for 5–7 minutes until smooth and elastic.</li><li>Place in an oiled bowl, cover, and let rise until doubled, about 1 hour.</li><li>Punch down, divide in half, and roll out for your favorite toppings.</li></ol></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Homemade Pizza Dough</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 10 min</span><span>Rise 1 hr</span><span>Makes 2 crusts</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>3 cups all-purpose flour</li><li>1 packet active dry yeast</li><li>1 tsp sugar</li><li>1 tsp salt</li><li>1 cup warm water</li><li>2 tbsp olive oil</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>In a small bowl, mix yeast, sugar, and warm water. Let sit until foamy, about 5 minutes.</li><li>In a large bowl, combine flour and salt. Add yeast mixture and olive oil; stir until a shaggy dough forms.</li><li>Knead on a floured surface for 5–7 minutes until smooth and elastic.</li><li>Place in an oiled bowl, cover, and let rise until doubled, about 1 hour.</li><li>Punch down, divide in half, and roll out for your favorite toppings.</li></ol></div></div>	-
25.1.0	recipe_card/Cold Brew Coffee at Home	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Cold Brew Coffee</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 5 min</span><span>Steep 12–18 hrs</span><span>Makes 4 cups concentrate</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>1 cup coarse-ground coffee</li><li>4 cups cold filtered water</li><li>Optional: 1 tsp vanilla or a dash of cinnamon</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Combine coffee and water in a large jar or pitcher. Stir gently to ensure all grounds are wet.</li><li>Cover and let steep at room temperature for 12–18 hours.</li><li>Strain through a fine-mesh sieve lined with a coffee filter or cheesecloth. Discard grounds.</li><li>Store concentrate in the fridge for up to 2 weeks. Dilute with water or milk to taste.</li></ol></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Cold Brew Coffee</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 5 min</span><span>Steep 12–18 hrs</span><span>Makes 4 cups concentrate</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>1 cup coarse-ground coffee</li><li>4 cups cold filtered water</li><li>Optional: 1 tsp vanilla or a dash of cinnamon</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Combine coffee and water in a large jar or pitcher. Stir gently to ensure all grounds are wet.</li><li>Cover and let steep at room temperature for 12–18 hours.</li><li>Strain through a fine-mesh sieve lined with a coffee filter or cheesecloth. Discard grounds.</li><li>Store concentrate in the fridge for up to 2 weeks. Dilute with water or milk to taste.</li></ol></div></div>	-
25.2.0	recipe_card/Fix a Running Toilet	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Fix a Running Toilet</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 15–30 min</span><span>Difficulty Easy</span><span>Cost $5–$15</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>New flapper (most common fix)</li><li>Adjustable wrench</li><li>Sponge or towel</li><li>Optional: new fill valve</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Turn off the water supply and flush to empty the tank.</li><li>Inspect the flapper: if warped or dirty, clean it or replace it (cheap and easy).</li><li>Check the chain length—it should have a little slack but not be tangled.</li><li>If water still runs, adjust or replace the fill valve (follow the manufacturer's instructions).</li><li>Turn water back on, let the tank fill, and watch for a steady water level with no trickling into the bowl.</li></ol></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Fix a Running Toilet</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 15–30 min</span><span>Difficulty Easy</span><span>Cost $5–$15</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>New flapper (most common fix)</li><li>Adjustable wrench</li><li>Sponge or towel</li><li>Optional: new fill valve</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Turn off the water supply and flush to empty the tank.</li><li>Inspect the flapper: if warped or dirty, clean it or replace it (cheap and easy).</li><li>Check the chain length—it should have a little slack but not be tangled.</li><li>If water still runs, adjust or replace the fill valve (follow the manufacturer's instructions).</li><li>Turn water back on, let the tank fill, and watch for a steady water level with no trickling into the bowl.</li></ol></div></div>	-
25.3.0	recipe_card/Basic HTML Page	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Basic HTML Page</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 2 min</span><span>Level Beginner</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>Text editor (VS Code, Notepad)</li><li>Web browser</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Create a new file and save it as <code>index.html</code>.</li><li>Type the following structure:<pre><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;&lt;html&gt;  &lt;head&gt;    &lt;title&gt;My Page&lt;/title&gt;  &lt;/head&gt;  &lt;body&gt;    &lt;h1&gt;Hello, world!&lt;/h1&gt;  &lt;/body&gt;&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre></li><li>Save the file and open it in your browser. You should see “Hello, world!”.</li></ol></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Basic HTML Page</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 2 min</span><span>Level Beginner</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>Text editor (VS Code, Notepad)</li><li>Web browser</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Create a new file and save it as <code>index.html</code>.</li><li>Type the following structure:<pre><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;&lt;html&gt;  &lt;head&gt;    &lt;title&gt;My Page&lt;/title&gt;  &lt;/head&gt;  &lt;body&gt;    &lt;h1&gt;Hello, world!&lt;/h1&gt;  &lt;/body&gt;&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre></li><li>Save the file and open it in your browser. You should see “Hello, world!”.</li></ol></div></div>	-
25.4.0	recipe_card/Sunday Meal Prep: Grains & Veggies	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Sunday Meal Prep: Grains & Veggies</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 30 min</span><span>Cook 30 min</span><span>Makes 4–5 lunches</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>2 cups grains (quinoa, rice, or farro)</li><li>Assorted vegetables (broccoli, bell peppers, carrots, etc.)</li><li>Olive oil, salt, pepper</li><li>Protein of choice (chicken, tofu, chickpeas)</li><li>Lemon-tahini dressing (optional)</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Cook grains according to package instructions.</li><li>Roast chopped vegetables at 425°F (220°C) with olive oil, salt, and pepper for 20–25 minutes.</li><li>Cook protein: grill chicken, bake tofu, or rinse canned chickpeas.</li><li>Divide grains, veggies, and protein into containers. Add dressing if using.</li><li>Refrigerate for up to 4 days.</li></ol></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Sunday Meal Prep: Grains & Veggies</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 30 min</span><span>Cook 30 min</span><span>Makes 4–5 lunches</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>2 cups grains (quinoa, rice, or farro)</li><li>Assorted vegetables (broccoli, bell peppers, carrots, etc.)</li><li>Olive oil, salt, pepper</li><li>Protein of choice (chicken, tofu, chickpeas)</li><li>Lemon-tahini dressing (optional)</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Cook grains according to package instructions.</li><li>Roast chopped vegetables at 425°F (220°C) with olive oil, salt, and pepper for 20–25 minutes.</li><li>Cook protein: grill chicken, bake tofu, or rinse canned chickpeas.</li><li>Divide grains, veggies, and protein into containers. Add dressing if using.</li><li>Refrigerate for up to 4 days.</li></ol></div></div>	-
25.5.0	recipe_card/Basic CSS Grid	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Basic CSS Grid</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 5 min</span><span>Level Beginner</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>HTML container</li><li>CSS file or style tag</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Create a container element: <code>&lt;div class="grid"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</code>.</li><li>In CSS, set <code>.grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap: 1rem; }</code>.</li><li>Add child elements. They will automatically flow into three equal columns.</li><li>Adjust <code>grid-template-columns</code> for different layouts (e.g., <code>1fr 2fr</code>).</li><li>Use <code>grid-column</code> and <code>grid-row</code> on children for more control.</li></ol></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Basic CSS Grid</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 5 min</span><span>Level Beginner</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>HTML container</li><li>CSS file or style tag</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Create a container element: <code>&lt;div class="grid"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</code>.</li><li>In CSS, set <code>.grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap: 1rem; }</code>.</li><li>Add child elements. They will automatically flow into three equal columns.</li><li>Adjust <code>grid-template-columns</code> for different layouts (e.g., <code>1fr 2fr</code>).</li><li>Use <code>grid-column</code> and <code>grid-row</code> on children for more control.</li></ol></div></div>	-
25.6.0	recipe_card/No-Knead Bread	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>No-Knead Bread</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 10 min</span><span>Rise 12–18 h</span><span>Makes 1 loaf</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted">Hey <regex>user_name</regex> — if you can stir, you can make this. The fridge does the work.</p><ul class="ing"><li>400 g bread flour</li><li>300 g cool water</li><li>8 g fine salt</li><li>1 g instant yeast (a scant ¼ tsp)</li><li>Cornmeal or flour, for dusting</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Stir flour, salt and yeast. Add water; mix to a shaggy ball, 30 seconds.</li><li>Cover and leave 12–18 h at room temp.</li><li>Scrape onto a floured counter, fold four times, rest 15 min.</li><li>Shape into a round, proof 1 h on parchment.</li><li>Bake in a covered pot at 230 °C / 450 °F for 30 min, then uncovered 15 min.</li><li>Cool 1 h before slicing — I know. Worth it.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>No dutch oven?</summary><div class="body"><p>Any heavy pot with a lid works. Or bake on a tray with a pan of hot water below.</p></div></details></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>No-Knead Bread</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 10 min</span><span>Rise 12–18 h</span><span>Makes 1 loaf</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted">Hey  — if you can stir, you can make this. The fridge does the work.</p><ul class="ing"><li>400 g bread flour</li><li>300 g cool water</li><li>8 g fine salt</li><li>1 g instant yeast (a scant ¼ tsp)</li><li>Cornmeal or flour, for dusting</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Stir flour, salt and yeast. Add water; mix to a shaggy ball, 30 seconds.</li><li>Cover and leave 12–18 h at room temp.</li><li>Scrape onto a floured counter, fold four times, rest 15 min.</li><li>Shape into a round, proof 1 h on parchment.</li><li>Bake in a covered pot at 230 °C / 450 °F for 30 min, then uncovered 15 min.</li><li>Cool 1 h before slicing — I know. Worth it.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>No dutch oven?</summary><div class="body"><p>Any heavy pot with a lid works. Or bake on a tray with a pan of hot water below.</p></div></details></div></div>	-
25.7.0	recipe_card/Sourdough Starter From Scratch	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Sourdough Starter From Scratch</h3><div class="meta"><span>Day 1 to ~Day 10</span><span>Keeps forever</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted">Patience, <regex>user_name</regex>. You're just farming yeast in a jar.</p><ul class="ing"><li>50 g whole wheat or rye flour</li><li>50 g warm water (filtered if yours is chlorinated)</li><li>A clean jar, loosely covered</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Day 1: mix flour and water. Cover loosely. Wait 24 h.</li><li>Days 2–6: discard all but 50 g, feed 50 g flour + 50 g water, once daily.</li><li>Days 6–10: feed every 12 h until it doubles in 4–6 h.</li><li>Ready when it's domed, bubbly and smells like yogurt.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Odd smells, decoded</summary><div class="body"><p><strong>Nail polish:</strong> hungry. Feed it. <strong>Liquid on top:</strong> also hungry, pour it off. <strong>Pink or fuzzy:</strong> toss it and start again.</p></div></details></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Sourdough Starter From Scratch</h3><div class="meta"><span>Day 1 to ~Day 10</span><span>Keeps forever</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted">Patience, . You're just farming yeast in a jar.</p><ul class="ing"><li>50 g whole wheat or rye flour</li><li>50 g warm water (filtered if yours is chlorinated)</li><li>A clean jar, loosely covered</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Day 1: mix flour and water. Cover loosely. Wait 24 h.</li><li>Days 2–6: discard all but 50 g, feed 50 g flour + 50 g water, once daily.</li><li>Days 6–10: feed every 12 h until it doubles in 4–6 h.</li><li>Ready when it's domed, bubbly and smells like yogurt.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Odd smells, decoded</summary><div class="body"><p><strong>Nail polish:</strong> hungry. Feed it. <strong>Liquid on top:</strong> also hungry, pour it off. <strong>Pink or fuzzy:</strong> toss it and start again.</p></div></details></div></div>	-
25.8.0	recipe_card/Perfect Steamed Rice	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Perfect Steamed Rice</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 2 min</span><span>Cook 18 min</span><span>Serves 4</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted">No cooker needed, <regex>user_name</regex> — just a pot with a tight lid.</p><ul class="ing"><li>1 cup long-grain rice</li><li>1½ cups water</li><li>¼ tsp salt</li><li>1 tsp butter or oil (optional)</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Rinse the rice until the water runs clear. This is the anti-mush step.</li><li>Add water and salt. Bring to a boil uncovered.</li><li>Drop to the lowest heat, lid on, 15 min. Don't peek.</li><li>Off the heat, lid still on, 10 min.</li><li>Fluff with a fork, never a spoon.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>Doubling the batch? Use 1¾ cups water per 2 cups rice — less than you'd think.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Perfect Steamed Rice</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 2 min</span><span>Cook 18 min</span><span>Serves 4</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted">No cooker needed,  — just a pot with a tight lid.</p><ul class="ing"><li>1 cup long-grain rice</li><li>1½ cups water</li><li>¼ tsp salt</li><li>1 tsp butter or oil (optional)</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Rinse the rice until the water runs clear. This is the anti-mush step.</li><li>Add water and salt. Bring to a boil uncovered.</li><li>Drop to the lowest heat, lid on, 15 min. Don't peek.</li><li>Off the heat, lid still on, 10 min.</li><li>Fluff with a fork, never a spoon.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>Doubling the batch? Use 1¾ cups water per 2 cups rice — less than you'd think.</p></div></div></div>	-
25.9.0	recipe_card/Crispy Roast Chicken Thighs	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Crispy Roast Chicken Thighs</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 5 min</span><span>Roast 35 min</span><span>Serves 4</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted">Dry skin is the whole trick, <regex>user_name</regex>. Everything else is seasoning.</p><ul class="ing"><li>8 bone-in, skin-on thighs</li><li>2 tsp kosher salt</li><li>1 tsp smoked paprika</li><li>1 tsp garlic powder</li><li>1 tbsp olive oil</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Pat the skin very dry with paper towel.</li><li>Rub with oil, then salt and spices. Salt the skin most.</li><li>Arrange skin-up on a sheet pan, not touching.</li><li>Roast at 220 °C / 425 °F for 35–40 min.</li><li>Rest 5 min. They're done at 74 °C / 165 °F.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Soggy skin rescue</summary><div class="body"><p>Finish under the broiler for 3 min, watching closely. Ovens lie about their temps.</p></div></details></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Crispy Roast Chicken Thighs</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 5 min</span><span>Roast 35 min</span><span>Serves 4</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted">Dry skin is the whole trick, . Everything else is seasoning.</p><ul class="ing"><li>8 bone-in, skin-on thighs</li><li>2 tsp kosher salt</li><li>1 tsp smoked paprika</li><li>1 tsp garlic powder</li><li>1 tbsp olive oil</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Pat the skin very dry with paper towel.</li><li>Rub with oil, then salt and spices. Salt the skin most.</li><li>Arrange skin-up on a sheet pan, not touching.</li><li>Roast at 220 °C / 425 °F for 35–40 min.</li><li>Rest 5 min. They're done at 74 °C / 165 °F.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Soggy skin rescue</summary><div class="body"><p>Finish under the broiler for 3 min, watching closely. Ovens lie about their temps.</p></div></details></div></div>	-
25.10.0	recipe_card/Fluffy Scrambled Eggs	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Fluffy Scrambled Eggs</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 1 min</span><span>Cook 5 min</span><span>Serves 2</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted">Low heat, <regex>user_name</regex>. Rubber eggs are just eggs that were rushed.</p><ul class="ing"><li>4 large eggs</li><li>1 tbsp butter</li><li>1 tbsp milk or cream (optional)</li><li>Salt, added at the end</li><li>Chives, if you're feeling fancy</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Beat eggs until fully uniform — no streaks.</li><li>Melt butter in a nonstick pan over low heat.</li><li>Pour in eggs. Wait 20 seconds.</li><li>Push gently with a spatula; don't stir hard.</li><li>Pull off the heat while still glossy. They keep cooking.</li><li>Salt now, not before. Salt early and they weep.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p>Serve on toast with the pan still warm. Best five minutes of your morning.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Fluffy Scrambled Eggs</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 1 min</span><span>Cook 5 min</span><span>Serves 2</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted">Low heat, . Rubber eggs are just eggs that were rushed.</p><ul class="ing"><li>4 large eggs</li><li>1 tbsp butter</li><li>1 tbsp milk or cream (optional)</li><li>Salt, added at the end</li><li>Chives, if you're feeling fancy</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Beat eggs until fully uniform — no streaks.</li><li>Melt butter in a nonstick pan over low heat.</li><li>Pour in eggs. Wait 20 seconds.</li><li>Push gently with a spatula; don't stir hard.</li><li>Pull off the heat while still glossy. They keep cooking.</li><li>Salt now, not before. Salt early and they weep.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p>Serve on toast with the pan still warm. Best five minutes of your morning.</p></div></div></div>	-
25.11.0	recipe_card/Overnight Oats	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Overnight Oats</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 5 min</span><span>Chill overnight</span><span>Makes 1 jar</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted">Breakfast that makes itself while you sleep. Morning-you says thanks, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="ing"><li>½ cup rolled oats (not instant)</li><li>½ cup milk of choice</li><li>¼ cup Greek yogurt</li><li>1 tbsp chia seeds</li><li>1 tsp honey or maple</li><li>Pinch of salt</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Stir everything in a jar with a lid.</li><li>Refrigerate at least 4 h, ideally overnight.</li><li>Thin with a splash of milk before eating — they firm up.</li><li>Top with fruit, nuts or jam. Eat within 4 days.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Make a week at once</summary><div class="body"><p>Line up five jars on Sunday. Add wet ingredients only; keep crunchy toppings separate until morning.</p></div></details></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Overnight Oats</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 5 min</span><span>Chill overnight</span><span>Makes 1 jar</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted">Breakfast that makes itself while you sleep. Morning-you says thanks, .</p><ul class="ing"><li>½ cup rolled oats (not instant)</li><li>½ cup milk of choice</li><li>¼ cup Greek yogurt</li><li>1 tbsp chia seeds</li><li>1 tsp honey or maple</li><li>Pinch of salt</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Stir everything in a jar with a lid.</li><li>Refrigerate at least 4 h, ideally overnight.</li><li>Thin with a splash of milk before eating — they firm up.</li><li>Top with fruit, nuts or jam. Eat within 4 days.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Make a week at once</summary><div class="body"><p>Line up five jars on Sunday. Add wet ingredients only; keep crunchy toppings separate until morning.</p></div></details></div></div>	-
25.12.0	recipe_card/Classic Vinaigrette	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Classic Vinaigrette</h3>    <div class="meta">      <span>Prep 5 min</span>      <span>Makes ¾ cup</span>    </div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <ul class="ing">      <li>3 tbsp olive oil</li>      <li>1 tbsp red wine vinegar</li>      <li>1 tsp Dijon mustard</li>      <li>1 small shallot, minced</li>      <li>Salt and pepper to taste</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Combine vinegar, mustard, shallot, salt, and pepper in a jar.</li>      <li>Add olive oil. Shake vigorously until emulsified.</li>      <li>Taste and adjust. Serve over greens.</li>    </ol>    <p class="t-small muted">Keeps in the fridge for up to a week. Bring to room temp and shake before using.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Classic Vinaigrette</h3>    <div class="meta">      <span>Prep 5 min</span>      <span>Makes ¾ cup</span>    </div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <ul class="ing">      <li>3 tbsp olive oil</li>      <li>1 tbsp red wine vinegar</li>      <li>1 tsp Dijon mustard</li>      <li>1 small shallot, minced</li>      <li>Salt and pepper to taste</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Combine vinegar, mustard, shallot, salt, and pepper in a jar.</li>      <li>Add olive oil. Shake vigorously until emulsified.</li>      <li>Taste and adjust. Serve over greens.</li>    </ol>    <p class="t-small muted">Keeps in the fridge for up to a week. Bring to room temp and shake before using.</p>  </div></div>	-
25.13.0	recipe_card/Unclog a Drain Naturally	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Unclog a Drain Naturally</h3>    <div class="meta">      <span>Time 15 min</span>      <span>No harsh chemicals</span>    </div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <ul class="ing">      <li>½ cup baking soda</li>      <li>½ cup white vinegar</li>      <li>4 cups boiling water</li>      <li>Plunger or drain snake</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Pour baking soda down the drain.</li>      <li>Add vinegar. It will fizz. Let sit 10 minutes.</li>      <li>Flush with boiling water.</li>      <li>If still slow, use a plunger or snake.</li>    </ol>    <div class="callout warn">      <p class="t-small">Don't use this on a fully blocked drain—it can overflow. Use a snake first.</p>    </div>  </div></div>	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Unclog a Drain Naturally</h3>    <div class="meta">      <span>Time 15 min</span>      <span>No harsh chemicals</span>    </div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <ul class="ing">      <li>½ cup baking soda</li>      <li>½ cup white vinegar</li>      <li>4 cups boiling water</li>      <li>Plunger or drain snake</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Pour baking soda down the drain.</li>      <li>Add vinegar. It will fizz. Let sit 10 minutes.</li>      <li>Flush with boiling water.</li>      <li>If still slow, use a plunger or snake.</li>    </ol>    <div class="callout warn">      <p class="t-small">Don't use this on a fully blocked drain—it can overflow. Use a snake first.</p>    </div>  </div></div>	-
25.14.0	recipe_card/How to Tie a Tie (Four-in-Hand)	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>How to Tie a Tie (Four-in-Hand)</h3>    <div class="meta">      <span>Time 2 min</span>      <span>Beginner-friendly</span>    </div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <ul class="ing">      <li>One necktie</li>      <li>A mirror</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Drape tie around neck, wide end on your dominant side, about 12 inches lower.</li>      <li>Cross wide end over narrow end.</li>      <li>Bring wide end up through the neck loop.</li>      <li>Wrap wide end across the front of the knot.</li>      <li>Pull wide end up through the loop again.</li>      <li>Tuck wide end down through the front knot.</li>      <li>Tighten and adjust.</li>    </ol>    <p class="t-small muted">The dimple: pinch the tie just below the knot before tightening for a polished look.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>How to Tie a Tie (Four-in-Hand)</h3>    <div class="meta">      <span>Time 2 min</span>      <span>Beginner-friendly</span>    </div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <ul class="ing">      <li>One necktie</li>      <li>A mirror</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Drape tie around neck, wide end on your dominant side, about 12 inches lower.</li>      <li>Cross wide end over narrow end.</li>      <li>Bring wide end up through the neck loop.</li>      <li>Wrap wide end across the front of the knot.</li>      <li>Pull wide end up through the loop again.</li>      <li>Tuck wide end down through the front knot.</li>      <li>Tighten and adjust.</li>    </ol>    <p class="t-small muted">The dimple: pinch the tie just below the knot before tightening for a polished look.</p>  </div></div>	-
25.15.0	recipe_card/Remove a Red Wine Stain	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Remove a Red Wine Stain</h3>    <div class="meta">      <span>Time 15 min</span>      <span>For fabric and carpet</span>    </div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <ul class="ing">      <li>Clean cloths or paper towels</li>      <li>Cold water</li>      <li>Liquid dish soap</li>      <li>White vinegar or hydrogen peroxide (for tough stains)</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Blot—don't rub—the stain with a clean cloth to absorb as much wine as possible.</li>      <li>Mix 1 tsp dish soap with 1 cup cold water. Blot the stain with the solution.</li>      <li>Rinse with cold water. Repeat until stain fades.</li>      <li>If stain remains, apply a little vinegar or hydrogen peroxide. Let sit 5 minutes, then rinse.</li>      <li>Launder as usual (or shampoo carpet).</li>    </ol>    <div class="callout info">      <p class="t-small">Act fast—the sooner you treat it, the better. Never use hot water; it sets the stain.</p>    </div>  </div></div>	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Remove a Red Wine Stain</h3>    <div class="meta">      <span>Time 15 min</span>      <span>For fabric and carpet</span>    </div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <ul class="ing">      <li>Clean cloths or paper towels</li>      <li>Cold water</li>      <li>Liquid dish soap</li>      <li>White vinegar or hydrogen peroxide (for tough stains)</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Blot—don't rub—the stain with a clean cloth to absorb as much wine as possible.</li>      <li>Mix 1 tsp dish soap with 1 cup cold water. Blot the stain with the solution.</li>      <li>Rinse with cold water. Repeat until stain fades.</li>      <li>If stain remains, apply a little vinegar or hydrogen peroxide. Let sit 5 minutes, then rinse.</li>      <li>Launder as usual (or shampoo carpet).</li>    </ol>    <div class="callout info">      <p class="t-small">Act fast—the sooner you treat it, the better. Never use hot water; it sets the stain.</p>    </div>  </div></div>	-
25.16.0	recipe_card/Fix a Flat Bike Tire	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Fix a Flat Bike Tire</h3>    <div class="meta">      <span>Time 20 min</span>      <span>Difficulty: Easy</span>    </div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <ul class="ing">      <li>Tire levers</li>      <li>New inner tube (or patch kit)</li>      <li>Bike pump</li>      <li>Wrench (if needed)</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Shift gears to the smallest cog. Loosen the wheel's quick-release or bolts.</li>      <li>Remove the wheel. Let out any remaining air.</li>      <li>Use tire levers to pry one side of the tire off the rim.</li>      <li>Pull out the old inner tube. Check inside the tire for sharp objects.</li>      <li>Insert new tube, slightly inflated. Push the tire back onto the rim by hand.</li>      <li>Inflate to the pressure listed on the tire sidewall.</li>      <li>Reinstall the wheel and tighten.</li>    </ol>    <p class="t-small muted">Carry a spare tube and levers—flats happen when you least expect them.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Fix a Flat Bike Tire</h3>    <div class="meta">      <span>Time 20 min</span>      <span>Difficulty: Easy</span>    </div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <ul class="ing">      <li>Tire levers</li>      <li>New inner tube (or patch kit)</li>      <li>Bike pump</li>      <li>Wrench (if needed)</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Shift gears to the smallest cog. Loosen the wheel's quick-release or bolts.</li>      <li>Remove the wheel. Let out any remaining air.</li>      <li>Use tire levers to pry one side of the tire off the rim.</li>      <li>Pull out the old inner tube. Check inside the tire for sharp objects.</li>      <li>Insert new tube, slightly inflated. Push the tire back onto the rim by hand.</li>      <li>Inflate to the pressure listed on the tire sidewall.</li>      <li>Reinstall the wheel and tighten.</li>    </ol>    <p class="t-small muted">Carry a spare tube and levers—flats happen when you least expect them.</p>  </div></div>	-
25.17.0	recipe_card/Homemade Play Dough	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Homemade Play Dough</h3>    <div class="meta">      <span>Prep 10 min</span>      <span>Makes 1 ball</span>    </div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <ul class="ing">      <li>1 cup flour</li>      <li>½ cup salt</li>      <li>2 tsp cream of tartar</li>      <li>1 cup water</li>      <li>1 tbsp oil</li>      <li>Food coloring (optional)</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Mix dry ingredients in a saucepan.</li>      <li>Add water, oil, and food coloring. Stir until smooth.</li>      <li>Cook over medium heat, stirring constantly, until it forms a ball.</li>      <li>Remove from heat. Knead when cool enough to handle.</li>      <li>Store in an airtight container.</li>    </ol>    <div class="callout good">      <p class="t-small">Keeps for months. If it dries out, add a few drops of water and knead.</p>    </div>  </div></div>	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Homemade Play Dough</h3>    <div class="meta">      <span>Prep 10 min</span>      <span>Makes 1 ball</span>    </div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <ul class="ing">      <li>1 cup flour</li>      <li>½ cup salt</li>      <li>2 tsp cream of tartar</li>      <li>1 cup water</li>      <li>1 tbsp oil</li>      <li>Food coloring (optional)</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Mix dry ingredients in a saucepan.</li>      <li>Add water, oil, and food coloring. Stir until smooth.</li>      <li>Cook over medium heat, stirring constantly, until it forms a ball.</li>      <li>Remove from heat. Knead when cool enough to handle.</li>      <li>Store in an airtight container.</li>    </ol>    <div class="callout good">      <p class="t-small">Keeps for months. If it dries out, add a few drops of water and knead.</p>    </div>  </div></div>	-
25.18.0	recipe_card/Fluffy Pancakes From Scratch	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Fluffy Pancakes</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 5 min</span><span>Cook 15 min</span><span>Serves 3–4</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>1½ cups all-purpose flour</li><li>3½ tsp baking powder</li><li>1 tbsp sugar</li><li>½ tsp salt</li><li>1¼ cups milk</li><li>1 large egg</li><li>3 tbsp melted butter</li><li>1 tsp vanilla extract</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Whisk flour, baking powder, sugar, and salt in a large bowl.</li><li>In another bowl, whisk milk, egg, melted butter, and vanilla.</li><li>Pour wet into dry and stir gently until just combined—lumps are fine. Overmixing makes tough pancakes.</li><li>Let batter rest 5 minutes while you heat a nonstick pan over medium heat.</li><li>Lightly grease pan. Pour ¼ cup batter per pancake.</li><li>Cook until bubbles form and edges look set, about 2–3 minutes. Flip and cook 1–2 minutes more.</li><li>Keep warm on a plate under a towel. Serve with butter and syrup.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>No buttermilk? Add 1 tbsp lemon juice to the milk and let sit 5 minutes.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Fluffy Pancakes</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 5 min</span><span>Cook 15 min</span><span>Serves 3–4</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>1½ cups all-purpose flour</li><li>3½ tsp baking powder</li><li>1 tbsp sugar</li><li>½ tsp salt</li><li>1¼ cups milk</li><li>1 large egg</li><li>3 tbsp melted butter</li><li>1 tsp vanilla extract</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Whisk flour, baking powder, sugar, and salt in a large bowl.</li><li>In another bowl, whisk milk, egg, melted butter, and vanilla.</li><li>Pour wet into dry and stir gently until just combined—lumps are fine. Overmixing makes tough pancakes.</li><li>Let batter rest 5 minutes while you heat a nonstick pan over medium heat.</li><li>Lightly grease pan. Pour ¼ cup batter per pancake.</li><li>Cook until bubbles form and edges look set, about 2–3 minutes. Flip and cook 1–2 minutes more.</li><li>Keep warm on a plate under a towel. Serve with butter and syrup.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>No buttermilk? Add 1 tbsp lemon juice to the milk and let sit 5 minutes.</p></div></div></div>	-
25.19.0	recipe_card/Sew a Button Back On	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Sew a Button Back On</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 5 min</span><span>Skill: beginner</span><span>Tools: needle &amp; thread</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>Button</li><li>Needle</li><li>Thread (matching or contrasting)</li><li>Scissors</li><li>Toothpick (optional)</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Thread the needle and knot the end. Double the thread for strength.</li><li>Position the button where it belongs. Push needle up through fabric from the back.</li><li>Place a toothpick across the button's top to create a small gap—this makes room for fabric thickness.</li><li>Sew through one hole, then the opposite hole. Repeat 3–4 times.</li><li>If the button has four holes, sew an X or two parallel lines.</li><li>Remove toothpick. Bring needle to the back, wrap thread around the stitches under the button a few times to form a shank.</li><li>Knot securely on the back and trim.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p>Test the button—it should sit slightly raised and move freely.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Sew a Button Back On</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 5 min</span><span>Skill: beginner</span><span>Tools: needle &amp; thread</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>Button</li><li>Needle</li><li>Thread (matching or contrasting)</li><li>Scissors</li><li>Toothpick (optional)</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Thread the needle and knot the end. Double the thread for strength.</li><li>Position the button where it belongs. Push needle up through fabric from the back.</li><li>Place a toothpick across the button's top to create a small gap—this makes room for fabric thickness.</li><li>Sew through one hole, then the opposite hole. Repeat 3–4 times.</li><li>If the button has four holes, sew an X or two parallel lines.</li><li>Remove toothpick. Bring needle to the back, wrap thread around the stitches under the button a few times to form a shank.</li><li>Knot securely on the back and trim.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p>Test the button—it should sit slightly raised and move freely.</p></div></div></div>	-
25.20.0	recipe_card/Change a Car Tire	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Change a Car Tire</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 15–30 min</span><span>Tools: jack, lug wrench, spare</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>Spare tire (check pressure)</li><li>Jack</li><li>Lug wrench</li><li>Wheel chocks or bricks</li><li>Reflective triangles or flares</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Pull over to a safe, flat spot. Turn on hazard lights. Apply parking brake.</li><li>Place chocks on the wheel diagonally opposite the flat.</li><li>Loosen lug nuts one turn with the wrench while the tire is still on the ground.</li><li>Position jack under the car's lift point (see manual). Raise until the flat tire is off the ground.</li><li>Remove lug nuts and set aside. Pull the tire straight off.</li><li>Mount the spare, aligning holes. Hand-tighten lug nuts in a star pattern.</li><li>Lower the car, then tighten nuts firmly in a star pattern.</li><li>Stow the flat and tools. Check spare pressure soon—most spares are temporary.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p>Never go under a car supported only by a jack.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Change a Car Tire</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 15–30 min</span><span>Tools: jack, lug wrench, spare</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>Spare tire (check pressure)</li><li>Jack</li><li>Lug wrench</li><li>Wheel chocks or bricks</li><li>Reflective triangles or flares</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Pull over to a safe, flat spot. Turn on hazard lights. Apply parking brake.</li><li>Place chocks on the wheel diagonally opposite the flat.</li><li>Loosen lug nuts one turn with the wrench while the tire is still on the ground.</li><li>Position jack under the car's lift point (see manual). Raise until the flat tire is off the ground.</li><li>Remove lug nuts and set aside. Pull the tire straight off.</li><li>Mount the spare, aligning holes. Hand-tighten lug nuts in a star pattern.</li><li>Lower the car, then tighten nuts firmly in a star pattern.</li><li>Stow the flat and tools. Check spare pressure soon—most spares are temporary.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p>Never go under a car supported only by a jack.</p></div></div></div>	-
25.21.0	recipe_card/Paper Airplane That Flies	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Paper Airplane That Flies</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 2 min</span><span>Materials: one sheet of paper</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>1 sheet of A4 or letter paper</li><li>Flat surface</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Fold paper in half lengthwise. Crease well, then unfold.</li><li>Fold the top two corners to the center crease, forming a triangle.</li><li>Fold the new angled edges to the center again, making a narrower triangle.</li><li>Fold the whole thing in half along the original center crease.</li><li>Fold the wings down: one flap on each side, aligned with the bottom edge.</li><li>Open wings slightly so they angle up a bit.</li><li>Hold the body near the front, throw with a gentle push.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>Adjust wing angles—slightly up for lift, flat for speed.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Paper Airplane That Flies</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 2 min</span><span>Materials: one sheet of paper</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>1 sheet of A4 or letter paper</li><li>Flat surface</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Fold paper in half lengthwise. Crease well, then unfold.</li><li>Fold the top two corners to the center crease, forming a triangle.</li><li>Fold the new angled edges to the center again, making a narrower triangle.</li><li>Fold the whole thing in half along the original center crease.</li><li>Fold the wings down: one flap on each side, aligned with the bottom edge.</li><li>Open wings slightly so they angle up a bit.</li><li>Hold the body near the front, throw with a gentle push.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>Adjust wing angles—slightly up for lift, flat for speed.</p></div></div></div>	-
25.22.0	recipe_card/Tie a Bowline Knot	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Tie a Bowline Knot</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 1 min</span><span>Use: fixed loop</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>Rope</li><li>Your hands</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Make a small loop in the rope, leaving a long tail.</li><li>Bring the tail up through the loop from underneath.</li><li>Wrap the tail around behind the standing part.</li><li>Bring the tail back down through the loop.</li><li>Pull the tail while holding the standing part to tighten.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p>Remember: the rabbit comes out of the hole, around the tree, and back down the hole.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Tie a Bowline Knot</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 1 min</span><span>Use: fixed loop</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>Rope</li><li>Your hands</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Make a small loop in the rope, leaving a long tail.</li><li>Bring the tail up through the loop from underneath.</li><li>Wrap the tail around behind the standing part.</li><li>Bring the tail back down through the loop.</li><li>Pull the tail while holding the standing part to tighten.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p>Remember: the rabbit comes out of the hole, around the tree, and back down the hole.</p></div></div></div>	-
25.23.0	recipe_card/Jump Start a Car	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Jump Start a Car</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 10 min</span><span>Tools: jumper cables, another car</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>Jumper cables</li><li>Working car</li><li>Safety glasses (optional)</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Park the working car close, but not touching. Turn both cars off.</li><li>Connect red clamp to positive (+) terminal of dead battery.</li><li>Connect other red clamp to positive (+) of good battery.</li><li>Connect black clamp to negative (-) of good battery.</li><li>Connect other black clamp to a bare metal bolt on the dead car's engine block—not the battery.</li><li>Start the working car, let it run a minute, then start the dead car.</li><li>Once started, remove cables in reverse order: black from dead car, black from good, red from good, red from dead.</li><li>Keep the revived car running for at least 20 minutes.</li></ol><div class="callout bad"><p>Never let clamps touch each other. Don't lean over the battery.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Jump Start a Car</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 10 min</span><span>Tools: jumper cables, another car</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>Jumper cables</li><li>Working car</li><li>Safety glasses (optional)</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Park the working car close, but not touching. Turn both cars off.</li><li>Connect red clamp to positive (+) terminal of dead battery.</li><li>Connect other red clamp to positive (+) of good battery.</li><li>Connect black clamp to negative (-) of good battery.</li><li>Connect other black clamp to a bare metal bolt on the dead car's engine block—not the battery.</li><li>Start the working car, let it run a minute, then start the dead car.</li><li>Once started, remove cables in reverse order: black from dead car, black from good, red from good, red from dead.</li><li>Keep the revived car running for at least 20 minutes.</li></ol><div class="callout bad"><p>Never let clamps touch each other. Don't lean over the battery.</p></div></div></div>	-
25.24.0	recipe_card/Season a Cast Iron Skillet	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Season a Cast Iron Skillet</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 5 min</span><span>Bake 1 hr</span><span>Cool 1 hr</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted">Give your pan a glassy, nonstick coat that lasts. <regex>user_name</regex>, this is the reset button for rusty or sticky iron.</p><ul class="ing"><li>1 cast iron skillet</li><li>1 tbsp flaxseed or canola oil</li><li>Paper towels</li><li>Aluminum foil</li><li>Oven</li><li>Stiff brush</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Scrub off rust with steel wool and hot water. Dry thoroughly.</li><li>Preheat oven to 450&deg;F (230&deg;C).</li><li>Rub a thin layer of oil all over the pan, inside and out.</li><li>Wipe off excess with a paper towel. It should look dry, not greasy.</li><li>Place upside down on foil on the middle rack. Bake 1 hour.</li><li>Turn off oven and let the pan cool inside.</li><li>Repeat oil and bake 2&ndash;3 times for a dark, slick finish.</li></ol><p class="callout info">If it's sticky, you used too much oil. Scrub and start again.</p></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Season a Cast Iron Skillet</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 5 min</span><span>Bake 1 hr</span><span>Cool 1 hr</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted">Give your pan a glassy, nonstick coat that lasts. , this is the reset button for rusty or sticky iron.</p><ul class="ing"><li>1 cast iron skillet</li><li>1 tbsp flaxseed or canola oil</li><li>Paper towels</li><li>Aluminum foil</li><li>Oven</li><li>Stiff brush</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Scrub off rust with steel wool and hot water. Dry thoroughly.</li><li>Preheat oven to 450&deg;F (230&deg;C).</li><li>Rub a thin layer of oil all over the pan, inside and out.</li><li>Wipe off excess with a paper towel. It should look dry, not greasy.</li><li>Place upside down on foil on the middle rack. Bake 1 hour.</li><li>Turn off oven and let the pan cool inside.</li><li>Repeat oil and bake 2&ndash;3 times for a dark, slick finish.</li></ol><p class="callout info">If it's sticky, you used too much oil. Scrub and start again.</p></div></div>	-
25.25.0	recipe_card/Remove Gum From Hair	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Remove Gum From Hair</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 2 min</span><span>Work 10 min</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted">Don't reach for scissors yet, <regex>user_name</regex>. A little oil and patience will set you free.</p><ul class="ing"><li>Peanut butter or vegetable oil</li><li>Wide-tooth comb</li><li>Paper towels</li><li>Dish soap</li><li>Conditioner</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Apply a generous blob of peanut butter or oil directly on the gum.</li><li>Let it sit 2&ndash;3 minutes to break down the stickiness.</li><li>Gently work the gum out with your fingers, starting at the edges.</li><li>Comb through with a wide-tooth comb to loosen remaining bits.</li><li>Wash hair with dish soap to cut the oil, then condition.</li><li>Repeat if needed. No scissors required.</li></ol><p class="callout good">Works on clothes too.</p></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Remove Gum From Hair</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 2 min</span><span>Work 10 min</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted">Don't reach for scissors yet, . A little oil and patience will set you free.</p><ul class="ing"><li>Peanut butter or vegetable oil</li><li>Wide-tooth comb</li><li>Paper towels</li><li>Dish soap</li><li>Conditioner</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Apply a generous blob of peanut butter or oil directly on the gum.</li><li>Let it sit 2&ndash;3 minutes to break down the stickiness.</li><li>Gently work the gum out with your fingers, starting at the edges.</li><li>Comb through with a wide-tooth comb to loosen remaining bits.</li><li>Wash hair with dish soap to cut the oil, then condition.</li><li>Repeat if needed. No scissors required.</li></ol><p class="callout good">Works on clothes too.</p></div></div>	-
25.26.0	recipe_card/Fold a Fitted Sheet	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Fold a Fitted Sheet</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 1 min</span><span>Fold 2 min</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted">It's not magic, <regex>user_name</regex>&mdash;just corners into corners.</p><ul class="ing"><li>1 fitted sheet</li><li>Flat surface (bed or table)</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Lay the sheet flat, elastic up, with the long side toward you.</li><li>Find the two top corners. Tuck one corner inside the other.</li><li>Do the same with the bottom two corners, tucking them into the first pair.</li><li>Lay the sheet down. It should look like a loose rectangle.</li><li>Fold in half lengthwise, then in thirds crosswise.</li><li>Smooth and stack. Admire your handiwork.</li></ol><p class="callout info">Elastic corners are the enemy. Tame them by tucking inside.</p></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Fold a Fitted Sheet</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 1 min</span><span>Fold 2 min</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted">It's not magic, &mdash;just corners into corners.</p><ul class="ing"><li>1 fitted sheet</li><li>Flat surface (bed or table)</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Lay the sheet flat, elastic up, with the long side toward you.</li><li>Find the two top corners. Tuck one corner inside the other.</li><li>Do the same with the bottom two corners, tucking them into the first pair.</li><li>Lay the sheet down. It should look like a loose rectangle.</li><li>Fold in half lengthwise, then in thirds crosswise.</li><li>Smooth and stack. Admire your handiwork.</li></ol><p class="callout info">Elastic corners are the enemy. Tame them by tucking inside.</p></div></div>	-
25.27.0	recipe_card/Fold a Paper Crane	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Fold a Paper Crane</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 1 min</span><span>Fold 5 min</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted">A classic for a reason. <regex>user_name</regex>, take your time and crease sharply.</p><ul class="ing"><li>1 square sheet of paper</li><li>Flat surface</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Start with the coloured side up. Fold in half diagonally both ways, then unfold.</li><li>Turn over. Fold in half horizontally and vertically, then unfold.</li><li>Collapse into a square base using the creases.</li><li>Fold the side corners to the centre line. Flip and repeat.</li><li>Fold the top triangle down. Unfold both sides and reverse-fold the flaps up.</li><li>Fold the side corners to the centre again. Flip and repeat.</li><li>Fold the bottom points up to form the neck and tail. Shape the head.</li><li>Fold the wings down. Blow into the hole to puff the body.</li></ol><p class="callout good">You made a crane. Now make a thousand.</p></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Fold a Paper Crane</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 1 min</span><span>Fold 5 min</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted">A classic for a reason. , take your time and crease sharply.</p><ul class="ing"><li>1 square sheet of paper</li><li>Flat surface</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Start with the coloured side up. Fold in half diagonally both ways, then unfold.</li><li>Turn over. Fold in half horizontally and vertically, then unfold.</li><li>Collapse into a square base using the creases.</li><li>Fold the side corners to the centre line. Flip and repeat.</li><li>Fold the top triangle down. Unfold both sides and reverse-fold the flaps up.</li><li>Fold the side corners to the centre again. Flip and repeat.</li><li>Fold the bottom points up to form the neck and tail. Shape the head.</li><li>Fold the wings down. Blow into the hole to puff the body.</li></ol><p class="callout good">You made a crane. Now make a thousand.</p></div></div>	-
25.28.0	recipe_card/Clean a Coffee Grinder	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Clean a Coffee Grinder</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 5 min</span><span>Clean 10 min</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted">Old grounds turn rancid and ruin your cup. <regex>user_name</regex>, give it a spa day.</p><ul class="ing"><li>Uncooked white rice</li><li>Small brush or toothbrush</li><li>Damp cloth</li><li>Vacuum (optional)</li><li>Grinder</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Unplug the grinder.</li><li>Remove the hopper and burrs if possible.</li><li>Brush out loose grounds from all crevices.</li><li>Grind 1/4 cup of uncooked rice until powdery.</li><li>Dump the rice and brush again. Repeat if needed.</li><li>Wipe with a barely damp cloth. Let dry fully before reassembling.</li><li>Run a small batch of coffee through and discard to season.</li></ol><p class="callout warn">Never wash electric parts with water.</p></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Clean a Coffee Grinder</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 5 min</span><span>Clean 10 min</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted">Old grounds turn rancid and ruin your cup. , give it a spa day.</p><ul class="ing"><li>Uncooked white rice</li><li>Small brush or toothbrush</li><li>Damp cloth</li><li>Vacuum (optional)</li><li>Grinder</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Unplug the grinder.</li><li>Remove the hopper and burrs if possible.</li><li>Brush out loose grounds from all crevices.</li><li>Grind 1/4 cup of uncooked rice until powdery.</li><li>Dump the rice and brush again. Repeat if needed.</li><li>Wipe with a barely damp cloth. Let dry fully before reassembling.</li><li>Run a small batch of coffee through and discard to season.</li></ol><p class="callout warn">Never wash electric parts with water.</p></div></div>	-
25.29.0	recipe_card/Fix a Squeaky Door Hinge	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Fix a Squeaky Door Hinge</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 1 min</span><span>Fix 2 min</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted">That squeak is just metal grinding on metal. <regex>user_name</regex>, silence it in seconds.</p><ul class="ing"><li>White lithium grease or petroleum jelly</li><li>Cotton swab or small brush</li><li>Paper towel</li><li>Hammer (optional)</li><li>Nail or punch (optional)</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Open the door to expose the hinge pin.</li><li>Tap the hinge pin up from below with a hammer and nail. Catch it.</li><li>Wipe the pin clean with a paper towel.</li><li>Apply a thin coat of grease to the pin.</li><li>Slide the pin back in and tap gently until seated.</li><li>Open and close the door to work in the grease.</li></ol><p class="callout info">No grease? A pencil's graphite works in a pinch.</p></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Fix a Squeaky Door Hinge</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 1 min</span><span>Fix 2 min</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted">That squeak is just metal grinding on metal. , silence it in seconds.</p><ul class="ing"><li>White lithium grease or petroleum jelly</li><li>Cotton swab or small brush</li><li>Paper towel</li><li>Hammer (optional)</li><li>Nail or punch (optional)</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Open the door to expose the hinge pin.</li><li>Tap the hinge pin up from below with a hammer and nail. Catch it.</li><li>Wipe the pin clean with a paper towel.</li><li>Apply a thin coat of grease to the pin.</li><li>Slide the pin back in and tap gently until seated.</li><li>Open and close the door to work in the grease.</li></ol><p class="callout info">No grease? A pencil's graphite works in a pinch.</p></div></div>	-
25.30.0	recipe_card/Emergency Pasta From the Pantry	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Emergency Pasta From the Pantry</h3><div class="meta"><span>20 min</span><span>Serves 2</span><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>pantry raid</span></div></div><div class="body"><div class="callout info"><strong>Note for <regex>user_name</regex>:</strong> if you have olive oil, garlic and salt, you are already done.</div><ul class="ing"><li>200 g dry pasta</li><li>4 garlic cloves, sliced thin</li><li>4 tbsp olive oil</li><li>Chilli flakes, to taste</li><li>Salt for the water</li><li>Parmesan or pecorino, grated</li><li>Parsley if it exists</li><li>Reserved pasta water</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Boil salted water. Cook pasta one minute under the box time.</li><li>Meanwhile warm the oil on low. Add garlic. Let it go golden, never brown.</li><li>Add chilli flakes, then a splash of pasta water.</li><li>Drag the pasta straight in. Toss hard for a minute so the starch thickens the sauce.</li><li>Off heat, add cheese and a little more water. Toss again. Serve immediately.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Rescue moves</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li><strong>Garlic went dark?</strong> Start over. Bitter is forever.</li><li><strong>Sauce too thin?</strong> Keep tossing off the heat; it tightens fast.</li><li><strong>No cheese?</strong> Add a knob of butter at the end instead.</li></ul></div></details></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Emergency Pasta From the Pantry</h3><div class="meta"><span>20 min</span><span>Serves 2</span><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>pantry raid</span></div></div><div class="body"><div class="callout info"><strong>Note for :</strong> if you have olive oil, garlic and salt, you are already done.</div><ul class="ing"><li>200 g dry pasta</li><li>4 garlic cloves, sliced thin</li><li>4 tbsp olive oil</li><li>Chilli flakes, to taste</li><li>Salt for the water</li><li>Parmesan or pecorino, grated</li><li>Parsley if it exists</li><li>Reserved pasta water</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Boil salted water. Cook pasta one minute under the box time.</li><li>Meanwhile warm the oil on low. Add garlic. Let it go golden, never brown.</li><li>Add chilli flakes, then a splash of pasta water.</li><li>Drag the pasta straight in. Toss hard for a minute so the starch thickens the sauce.</li><li>Off heat, add cheese and a little more water. Toss again. Serve immediately.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Rescue moves</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li><strong>Garlic went dark?</strong> Start over. Bitter is forever.</li><li><strong>Sauce too thin?</strong> Keep tossing off the heat; it tightens fast.</li><li><strong>No cheese?</strong> Add a knob of butter at the end instead.</li></ul></div></details></div></div>	-
25.31.0	recipe_card/Defrost and Reheat Without Ruining Dinner	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Defrost and Reheat Without Ruining Dinner</h3><div class="meta"><span>Varies</span><span>Any portion</span></div></div><div class="body"><div class="callout warn"><strong>Rule one, <regex>user_name</regex>:</strong> thaw in the fridge or under cold water. Never on the counter.</div><ul class="ing"><li>Frozen item, bagged</li><li>Bowl of cold water</li><li>Fridge shelf space</li><li>Cover or lid for reheating</li><li>A splash of liquid</li><li>Thermometer, ideally</li><li>Patience or a microwave</li><li>Time, 20 min to overnight</li></ul><ol class="list"><li><strong>Last night:</strong> move it to the fridge. Slow and safe. About 24 hours per kilo.</li><li><strong>Right now:</strong> seal it, sink it in cold water, change the water every 30 minutes.</li><li><strong>Microwave:</strong> use the defrost setting, then cook straight away. It cannot sit.</li><li><strong>Reheating:</strong> cover it. Steam is your friend, dry heat is not.</li><li>Add a spoonful of water, stock or sauce. Reheat at 60 percent power, stirring midway.</li><li>Hit 74 C, or piping hot all the way through. Rice and shellfish do not get second chances.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Things that reheat badly</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted">Fried anything, rare steak, hard-boiled eggs, and anything you already reheated once. Eat those cold or not at all.</p></div></details></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Defrost and Reheat Without Ruining Dinner</h3><div class="meta"><span>Varies</span><span>Any portion</span></div></div><div class="body"><div class="callout warn"><strong>Rule one, :</strong> thaw in the fridge or under cold water. Never on the counter.</div><ul class="ing"><li>Frozen item, bagged</li><li>Bowl of cold water</li><li>Fridge shelf space</li><li>Cover or lid for reheating</li><li>A splash of liquid</li><li>Thermometer, ideally</li><li>Patience or a microwave</li><li>Time, 20 min to overnight</li></ul><ol class="list"><li><strong>Last night:</strong> move it to the fridge. Slow and safe. About 24 hours per kilo.</li><li><strong>Right now:</strong> seal it, sink it in cold water, change the water every 30 minutes.</li><li><strong>Microwave:</strong> use the defrost setting, then cook straight away. It cannot sit.</li><li><strong>Reheating:</strong> cover it. Steam is your friend, dry heat is not.</li><li>Add a spoonful of water, stock or sauce. Reheat at 60 percent power, stirring midway.</li><li>Hit 74 C, or piping hot all the way through. Rice and shellfish do not get second chances.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Things that reheat badly</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted">Fried anything, rare steak, hard-boiled eggs, and anything you already reheated once. Eat those cold or not at all.</p></div></details></div></div>	-
25.32.0	recipe_card/Fix a Slow Laptop in Ten Minutes	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Fix a Slow Laptop in Ten Minutes</h3><div class="meta"><span>10 min</span><span>No tools</span><span class="badge info"><i class="dot"></i>free</span></div></div><div class="body"><div class="callout good"><strong>Start here, <regex>user_name</regex>:</strong> restart it first. Genuinely. Half of these vanish.</div><ul class="ing"><li>Task Manager or Activity Monitor</li><li>Startup or Login Items list</li><li>At least 10 percent free disk space</li><li>Your browser's open tabs</li><li>A can of compressed air</li><li>A charger</li><li>One update queue</li><li>Ten quiet minutes</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Restart fully. Not sleep. Restart.</li><li>Open Task Manager (Cmd+Space, "Activity Monitor" on Mac). Sort by CPU, then by memory.</li><li>Close the top offender. Usually a browser with 40 tabs or a sync app nobody asked for.</li><li>Trim startup items to only what you use daily.</li><li>Free disk space until you have at least 10 percent free. A full drive crawls.</li><li>Plug it in. Many laptops throttle hard on battery.</li><li>Blow dust out of the vents. Heat is the quiet killer.</li><li>Install pending updates, then restart one more time.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>If it's still slow</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted">Check for a failing drive or a runaway backup. Both look like slowness and neither is fixed by cleanup.</p></div></details></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Fix a Slow Laptop in Ten Minutes</h3><div class="meta"><span>10 min</span><span>No tools</span><span class="badge info"><i class="dot"></i>free</span></div></div><div class="body"><div class="callout good"><strong>Start here, :</strong> restart it first. Genuinely. Half of these vanish.</div><ul class="ing"><li>Task Manager or Activity Monitor</li><li>Startup or Login Items list</li><li>At least 10 percent free disk space</li><li>Your browser's open tabs</li><li>A can of compressed air</li><li>A charger</li><li>One update queue</li><li>Ten quiet minutes</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Restart fully. Not sleep. Restart.</li><li>Open Task Manager (Cmd+Space, "Activity Monitor" on Mac). Sort by CPU, then by memory.</li><li>Close the top offender. Usually a browser with 40 tabs or a sync app nobody asked for.</li><li>Trim startup items to only what you use daily.</li><li>Free disk space until you have at least 10 percent free. A full drive crawls.</li><li>Plug it in. Many laptops throttle hard on battery.</li><li>Blow dust out of the vents. Heat is the quiet killer.</li><li>Install pending updates, then restart one more time.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>If it's still slow</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted">Check for a failing drive or a runaway backup. Both look like slowness and neither is fixed by cleanup.</p></div></details></div></div>	-
25.33.0	recipe_card/Water a Houseplant Without Drowning It	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Water a Houseplant Without Drowning It</h3><div class="meta"><span>5 min</span><span>Per plant</span></div></div><div class="body"><div class="callout warn"><strong>Most houseplants die of kindness, <regex>user_name</regex>.</strong> Overwatering kills more than neglect ever will.</div><ul class="ing"><li>Room-temperature water</li><li>A pot with a drainage hole</li><li>Your finger, or a wooden stick</li><li>A saucer or tray</li><li>Sink or bathtub access</li><li>Loose, well-draining soil</li><li>A cloth for spills</li><li>A calendar reminder, loose</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Push a finger two knuckles deep. If it's damp, walk away. That's the whole test.</li><li>When dry, water slowly until it runs out the bottom.</li><li>Let it drain fully. Empty the saucer after ten minutes.</li><li>Water in the morning so excess evaporates by evening.</li><li>Expect less in winter. Light drops, so thirst drops.</li><li>Yellow lower leaves and soggy soil mean too much. Crispy edges mean too little, or too dry air.</li><li>Repot into fresh soil if the pot stays heavy and wet for weeks.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Thirsty vs. drowning</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li><strong>Drooping but light pot:</strong> thirsty. Water it.</li><li><strong>Drooping and heavy pot:</strong> drowning. Stop, drain, wait.</li></ul></div></details></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Water a Houseplant Without Drowning It</h3><div class="meta"><span>5 min</span><span>Per plant</span></div></div><div class="body"><div class="callout warn"><strong>Most houseplants die of kindness, .</strong> Overwatering kills more than neglect ever will.</div><ul class="ing"><li>Room-temperature water</li><li>A pot with a drainage hole</li><li>Your finger, or a wooden stick</li><li>A saucer or tray</li><li>Sink or bathtub access</li><li>Loose, well-draining soil</li><li>A cloth for spills</li><li>A calendar reminder, loose</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Push a finger two knuckles deep. If it's damp, walk away. That's the whole test.</li><li>When dry, water slowly until it runs out the bottom.</li><li>Let it drain fully. Empty the saucer after ten minutes.</li><li>Water in the morning so excess evaporates by evening.</li><li>Expect less in winter. Light drops, so thirst drops.</li><li>Yellow lower leaves and soggy soil mean too much. Crispy edges mean too little, or too dry air.</li><li>Repot into fresh soil if the pot stays heavy and wet for weeks.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Thirsty vs. drowning</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li><strong>Drooping but light pot:</strong> thirsty. Water it.</li><li><strong>Drooping and heavy pot:</strong> drowning. Stop, drain, wait.</li></ul></div></details></div></div>	-
25.34.0	recipe_card/Hem a Pair of Trousers by Hand	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Hem a Pair of Trousers by Hand</h3><div class="meta"><span>45 min</span><span>One pair</span></div></div><div class="body"><div class="callout info"><strong>Measure twice, <regex>user_name</regex>.</strong> Pin the new length while wearing the shoes you'll wear with them.</div><ul class="ing"><li>Trousers</li><li>Pins or clips</li><li>Needle and matching thread</li><li>Small sharp scissors</li><li>Iron and ironing board</li><li>Tailor's chalk or a washable marker</li><li>Measuring tape</li><li>Thimble, if the fabric is thick</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Put them on, shoes on, and fold the cuff to where you want it. Pin at the side seam, front and back.</li><li>Take them off. Measure the fold height all the way around and even it out.</li><li>Trim the excess, leaving about 4 cm to fold under. Keep the original hem edge if you want the fades to match.</li><li>Fold the raw edge under, press it flat with a hot iron and a little steam.</li><li>Pin the hem in place, perpendicular to the edge, every few centimetres.</li><li>Stitch a slip stitch: tiny bite of the trouser, tiny bite of the fold, pull snug, repeat. Barely visible on both sides.</li><li>Knot off inside the fold, press once more, and try them on. Walk in them. Sit in them.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>If you hate sewing</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted">Fusible hem tape works for one or two washes and is a fine stopgap for a wedding this weekend. It is not a permanent hem.</p></div></details></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Hem a Pair of Trousers by Hand</h3><div class="meta"><span>45 min</span><span>One pair</span></div></div><div class="body"><div class="callout info"><strong>Measure twice, .</strong> Pin the new length while wearing the shoes you'll wear with them.</div><ul class="ing"><li>Trousers</li><li>Pins or clips</li><li>Needle and matching thread</li><li>Small sharp scissors</li><li>Iron and ironing board</li><li>Tailor's chalk or a washable marker</li><li>Measuring tape</li><li>Thimble, if the fabric is thick</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Put them on, shoes on, and fold the cuff to where you want it. Pin at the side seam, front and back.</li><li>Take them off. Measure the fold height all the way around and even it out.</li><li>Trim the excess, leaving about 4 cm to fold under. Keep the original hem edge if you want the fades to match.</li><li>Fold the raw edge under, press it flat with a hot iron and a little steam.</li><li>Pin the hem in place, perpendicular to the edge, every few centimetres.</li><li>Stitch a slip stitch: tiny bite of the trouser, tiny bite of the fold, pull snug, repeat. Barely visible on both sides.</li><li>Knot off inside the fold, press once more, and try them on. Walk in them. Sit in them.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>If you hate sewing</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted">Fusible hem tape works for one or two washes and is a fine stopgap for a wedding this weekend. It is not a permanent hem.</p></div></details></div></div>	-
25.35.0	recipe_card/Silence a Creaky Floorboard	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Silence a Creaky Floorboard</h3><div class="meta"><span>30 min</span><span>One board</span><span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>satisfying</span></div></div><div class="body"><div class="callout info"><strong>Find it first, <regex>user_name</regex>.</strong> Walk slowly, press down with one hand, and mark the exact spot with tape.</div><ul class="ing"><li>Talcum powder or graphite powder</li><li>Small finishing nails or trim screws</li><li>Hammer or driver</li><li>A scrap of wood for a shim</li><li>Wood glue</li><li>Stud finder</li><li>Nail punch</li><li>Matching wood filler</li></ul><ol class="list"><li><strong>From above, no mess:</strong> sprinkle talc into the gaps between boards and sweep it in. Quiet for months, and it's free.</li><li><strong>Better:</strong> mark a line on the joists. They usually run perpendicular to the boards.</li><li>Drive two finishing nails or trim screws straight down through the carpet and board into the joist. Sink the heads.</li><li>If the board is loose over a gap, tap a glued shim into the crevice and snap off the excess.</li><li>Fill and colour the nail holes if the floor is bare wood.</li><li>Walk the length of the room. Mark any remaining squeaks and repeat.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Before you drill anything</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Never drive into a spot where pipes or cables might run. Check from below if you can.</li><li>Under-floor access from a basement is easier and cleaner, if you have it.</li></ul></div></details></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Silence a Creaky Floorboard</h3><div class="meta"><span>30 min</span><span>One board</span><span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>satisfying</span></div></div><div class="body"><div class="callout info"><strong>Find it first, .</strong> Walk slowly, press down with one hand, and mark the exact spot with tape.</div><ul class="ing"><li>Talcum powder or graphite powder</li><li>Small finishing nails or trim screws</li><li>Hammer or driver</li><li>A scrap of wood for a shim</li><li>Wood glue</li><li>Stud finder</li><li>Nail punch</li><li>Matching wood filler</li></ul><ol class="list"><li><strong>From above, no mess:</strong> sprinkle talc into the gaps between boards and sweep it in. Quiet for months, and it's free.</li><li><strong>Better:</strong> mark a line on the joists. They usually run perpendicular to the boards.</li><li>Drive two finishing nails or trim screws straight down through the carpet and board into the joist. Sink the heads.</li><li>If the board is loose over a gap, tap a glued shim into the crevice and snap off the excess.</li><li>Fill and colour the nail holes if the floor is bare wood.</li><li>Walk the length of the room. Mark any remaining squeaks and repeat.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Before you drill anything</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Never drive into a spot where pipes or cables might run. Check from below if you can.</li><li>Under-floor access from a basement is easier and cleaner, if you have it.</li></ul></div></details></div></div>	-
25.36.0	recipe_card/Keep Bananas From Ripening Too Fast	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Keep Bananas From Ripening Too Fast</h3>    <div class="meta">      <span>Active 2 min</span>      <span>Makes 5&ndash;7 days</span>    </div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <p class="muted">Ripe bananas give off ethylene gas, and they're sensitive to it too. So the trick is separating them and slowing that gas down.</p>    <ul class="ing">      <li>1 bunch bananas, still green-tipped</li>      <li>1 roll plastic wrap or a beeswax wrap</li>      <li>1 banana hanger (optional)</li>      <li>1 cool, dark spot away from other fruit</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Wrap the crown (the top where they all join) tightly with plastic wrap. That's where most of the ethylene escapes.</li>      <li>Separate the bananas from each other. They ripen faster when touching.</li>      <li>Keep them at room temperature, out of direct sun. Fridge is fine once they're ripe, but the skin will go brown &mdash; the fruit inside stays good.</li>      <li>Don't store near apples, avocados, or tomatoes. They're ethylene factories.</li>    </ol>    <div class="callout info">      <p>Already too ripe? Peel, slice, and freeze for smoothies. No waste, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p>    </div>  </div></div>	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Keep Bananas From Ripening Too Fast</h3>    <div class="meta">      <span>Active 2 min</span>      <span>Makes 5&ndash;7 days</span>    </div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <p class="muted">Ripe bananas give off ethylene gas, and they're sensitive to it too. So the trick is separating them and slowing that gas down.</p>    <ul class="ing">      <li>1 bunch bananas, still green-tipped</li>      <li>1 roll plastic wrap or a beeswax wrap</li>      <li>1 banana hanger (optional)</li>      <li>1 cool, dark spot away from other fruit</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Wrap the crown (the top where they all join) tightly with plastic wrap. That's where most of the ethylene escapes.</li>      <li>Separate the bananas from each other. They ripen faster when touching.</li>      <li>Keep them at room temperature, out of direct sun. Fridge is fine once they're ripe, but the skin will go brown &mdash; the fruit inside stays good.</li>      <li>Don't store near apples, avocados, or tomatoes. They're ethylene factories.</li>    </ol>    <div class="callout info">      <p>Already too ripe? Peel, slice, and freeze for smoothies. No waste, .</p>    </div>  </div></div>	-
25.37.0	recipe_card/Get Rid of Fruit Flies (For Good)	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Get Rid of Fruit Flies (For Good)</h3>    <div class="meta">      <span>Active 5 min</span>      <span>Makes 2&ndash;3 days</span>    </div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <p class="muted">Fruit flies lay eggs in overripe produce and damp drains. Break the cycle and they vanish.</p>    <ul class="ing">      <li>1 small bowl or jar</li>      <li>Apple cider vinegar, about 1/4 cup</li>      <li>2 drops liquid dish soap</li>      <li>Plastic wrap and a rubber band (optional)</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Find and toss the source: overripe fruit, potatoes, onions, or a forgotten glass of wine. Check the compost bin.</li>      <li>Pour vinegar into the bowl. Add soap and swirl &mdash; it breaks the surface tension so flies sink.</li>      <li>Set the trap near the problem area. For a sealed version, cover with plastic wrap and poke a few holes with a toothpick.</li>      <li>Flush drains with boiling water or a vinegar-baking soda mix to kill eggs lurking in the gunk.</li>      <li>Repeat daily until you see none for two days.</li>    </ol>    <div class="callout good">      <p>Prevention: rinse produce when you bring it home, and keep fruit in the fridge. <regex>user_name</regex>, you'll be fly-free by the weekend.</p>    </div>  </div></div>	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Get Rid of Fruit Flies (For Good)</h3>    <div class="meta">      <span>Active 5 min</span>      <span>Makes 2&ndash;3 days</span>    </div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <p class="muted">Fruit flies lay eggs in overripe produce and damp drains. Break the cycle and they vanish.</p>    <ul class="ing">      <li>1 small bowl or jar</li>      <li>Apple cider vinegar, about 1/4 cup</li>      <li>2 drops liquid dish soap</li>      <li>Plastic wrap and a rubber band (optional)</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Find and toss the source: overripe fruit, potatoes, onions, or a forgotten glass of wine. Check the compost bin.</li>      <li>Pour vinegar into the bowl. Add soap and swirl &mdash; it breaks the surface tension so flies sink.</li>      <li>Set the trap near the problem area. For a sealed version, cover with plastic wrap and poke a few holes with a toothpick.</li>      <li>Flush drains with boiling water or a vinegar-baking soda mix to kill eggs lurking in the gunk.</li>      <li>Repeat daily until you see none for two days.</li>    </ol>    <div class="callout good">      <p>Prevention: rinse produce when you bring it home, and keep fruit in the fridge. , you'll be fly-free by the weekend.</p>    </div>  </div></div>	-
25.38.0	recipe_card/Fold a T-Shirt in Two Seconds	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Fold a T-Shirt in Two Seconds</h3>    <div class="meta">      <span>Active 10 sec</span>      <span>Makes 1 neat shirt</span>    </div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <p class="muted">The pinch-and-flip method. Once you get it, you'll never go back.</p>    <ul class="ing">      <li>1 clean t-shirt, laid flat, front up</li>      <li>A flat surface (table, bed, ironing board)</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Lay the shirt flat. Smooth out wrinkles with your hand.</li>      <li>Pinch the shirt at the shoulder seam (point A) with your left hand. With your right hand, pinch the middle of the shirt (point B) &mdash; roughly halfway down the side and halfway across.</li>      <li>Cross your right hand over your left, grabbing the bottom hem (point C) while still holding A and B.</li>      <li>Lift and pull. The shirt will fold itself. Lay it down, tuck the sleeves, and you're done.</li>    </ol>    <div class="callout info">      <p>Works for long sleeves too &mdash; just fold the sleeves in first. Great for packing, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p>    </div>  </div></div>	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Fold a T-Shirt in Two Seconds</h3>    <div class="meta">      <span>Active 10 sec</span>      <span>Makes 1 neat shirt</span>    </div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <p class="muted">The pinch-and-flip method. Once you get it, you'll never go back.</p>    <ul class="ing">      <li>1 clean t-shirt, laid flat, front up</li>      <li>A flat surface (table, bed, ironing board)</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Lay the shirt flat. Smooth out wrinkles with your hand.</li>      <li>Pinch the shirt at the shoulder seam (point A) with your left hand. With your right hand, pinch the middle of the shirt (point B) &mdash; roughly halfway down the side and halfway across.</li>      <li>Cross your right hand over your left, grabbing the bottom hem (point C) while still holding A and B.</li>      <li>Lift and pull. The shirt will fold itself. Lay it down, tuck the sleeves, and you're done.</li>    </ol>    <div class="callout info">      <p>Works for long sleeves too &mdash; just fold the sleeves in first. Great for packing, .</p>    </div>  </div></div>	-
25.39.0	recipe_card/Make a Bed Like a Hotel	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Make a Bed Like a Hotel</h3>    <div class="meta">      <span>Active 5 min</span>      <span>Makes 1 cozy bed</span>    </div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <p class="muted">Crisp sheets, a fluffy duvet, and a trick with the top sheet. No magic, just order.</p>    <ul class="ing">      <li>1 fitted sheet</li>      <li>1 flat sheet</li>      <li>1 duvet or comforter</li>      <li>2&ndash;4 pillows with cases</li>      <li>1 throw blanket (optional)</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Start with a clean mattress pad or protector. Then the fitted sheet &mdash; tuck corners tight.</li>      <li>Lay the flat sheet on top, right side down. Tuck the bottom and sides. At the top, fold the sheet back over the duvet about 12 inches to make a crisp cuff.</li>      <li>Put the duvet inside its cover (or use a comforter). Lay it over the bed, leaving the sheet cuff visible.</li>      <li>Fluff pillows and place them at the head. Fold a throw blanket at the foot for that hotel look.</li>    </ol>    <div class="callout good">      <p>Wash sheets weekly for fresh skin, <regex>user_name</regex>. Rotate two sets so there's always a clean one ready.</p>    </div>  </div></div>	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Make a Bed Like a Hotel</h3>    <div class="meta">      <span>Active 5 min</span>      <span>Makes 1 cozy bed</span>    </div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <p class="muted">Crisp sheets, a fluffy duvet, and a trick with the top sheet. No magic, just order.</p>    <ul class="ing">      <li>1 fitted sheet</li>      <li>1 flat sheet</li>      <li>1 duvet or comforter</li>      <li>2&ndash;4 pillows with cases</li>      <li>1 throw blanket (optional)</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Start with a clean mattress pad or protector. Then the fitted sheet &mdash; tuck corners tight.</li>      <li>Lay the flat sheet on top, right side down. Tuck the bottom and sides. At the top, fold the sheet back over the duvet about 12 inches to make a crisp cuff.</li>      <li>Put the duvet inside its cover (or use a comforter). Lay it over the bed, leaving the sheet cuff visible.</li>      <li>Fluff pillows and place them at the head. Fold a throw blanket at the foot for that hotel look.</li>    </ol>    <div class="callout good">      <p>Wash sheets weekly for fresh skin, . Rotate two sets so there's always a clean one ready.</p>    </div>  </div></div>	-
25.40.0	recipe_card/Perfect Boiled Eggs (Easy Peel)	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Perfect Boiled Eggs (Easy Peel)</h3>    <div class="meta">      <span>Active 2 min</span>      <span>Makes 6 eggs</span>    </div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <p class="muted">Forget the guesswork. Ice bath is the secret to peeling without a fight.</p>    <ul class="ing">      <li>6 large eggs</li>      <li>Water to cover by an inch</li>      <li>1 tbsp salt (optional, helps peeling)</li>      <li>Bowl of ice water</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Place eggs in a single layer in a pot. Add cold water to cover by 1 inch. Add salt if using.</li>      <li>Bring to a rolling boil over high heat. Once boiling, cover and turn off the heat. Let sit: 6 min for soft, 8 min for jammy, 10 min for hard-cooked.</li>      <li>Drain and immediately transfer eggs to the ice bath. Let cool 5 minutes.</li>      <li>Tap the egg gently all over, roll it on the counter, and peel under running water. The shell should slip right off.</li>    </ol>    <div class="callout info">      <p>Older eggs peel easier than fresh ones, <regex>user_name</regex>. If you're planning ahead, buy a week before boiling.</p>    </div>  </div></div>	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Perfect Boiled Eggs (Easy Peel)</h3>    <div class="meta">      <span>Active 2 min</span>      <span>Makes 6 eggs</span>    </div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <p class="muted">Forget the guesswork. Ice bath is the secret to peeling without a fight.</p>    <ul class="ing">      <li>6 large eggs</li>      <li>Water to cover by an inch</li>      <li>1 tbsp salt (optional, helps peeling)</li>      <li>Bowl of ice water</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Place eggs in a single layer in a pot. Add cold water to cover by 1 inch. Add salt if using.</li>      <li>Bring to a rolling boil over high heat. Once boiling, cover and turn off the heat. Let sit: 6 min for soft, 8 min for jammy, 10 min for hard-cooked.</li>      <li>Drain and immediately transfer eggs to the ice bath. Let cool 5 minutes.</li>      <li>Tap the egg gently all over, roll it on the counter, and peel under running water. The shell should slip right off.</li>    </ol>    <div class="callout info">      <p>Older eggs peel easier than fresh ones, . If you're planning ahead, buy a week before boiling.</p>    </div>  </div></div>	-
25.41.0	recipe_card/Soothe a Scratchy Throat	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Soothe a Scratchy Throat</h3>    <div class="meta">      <span>Active 5 min</span>      <span>Makes 1 cup</span>    </div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <p class="muted">Warm, sweet, and a little tart. This is the classic that actually works.</p>    <ul class="ing">      <li>1 cup hot water (not boiling)</li>      <li>1 tbsp honey</li>      <li>1 tbsp fresh lemon juice</li>      <li>1 slice fresh ginger (optional)</li>      <li>A pinch of cinnamon (optional)</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Steep ginger in hot water for 5 minutes if using.</li>      <li>Stir in honey and lemon until dissolved.</li>      <li>Sip slowly while warm. Repeat 2&ndash;3 times a day.</li>      <li>For extra relief, gargle with warm salt water (1/2 tsp salt in 8 oz water) before drinking.</li>    </ol>    <div class="callout warn">      <p>Don't give honey to babies under 1. If your throat is severely sore or you have a fever, check with a doctor. Feel better, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p>    </div>  </div></div>	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Soothe a Scratchy Throat</h3>    <div class="meta">      <span>Active 5 min</span>      <span>Makes 1 cup</span>    </div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <p class="muted">Warm, sweet, and a little tart. This is the classic that actually works.</p>    <ul class="ing">      <li>1 cup hot water (not boiling)</li>      <li>1 tbsp honey</li>      <li>1 tbsp fresh lemon juice</li>      <li>1 slice fresh ginger (optional)</li>      <li>A pinch of cinnamon (optional)</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Steep ginger in hot water for 5 minutes if using.</li>      <li>Stir in honey and lemon until dissolved.</li>      <li>Sip slowly while warm. Repeat 2&ndash;3 times a day.</li>      <li>For extra relief, gargle with warm salt water (1/2 tsp salt in 8 oz water) before drinking.</li>    </ol>    <div class="callout warn">      <p>Don't give honey to babies under 1. If your throat is severely sore or you have a fever, check with a doctor. Feel better, .</p>    </div>  </div></div>	-
25.42.0	recipe_card/Season a Stainless Steel Pan (Yes, Really)	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Season a Stainless Steel Pan</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 5 min</span><span>Total 15 min</span><span>Serves your future dinners</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted">Stainless isn't nonstick, but you can make it behave. The trick is heat and fat, not a coating.</p><ul class="ing"><li>1 stainless steel skillet, clean and dry</li><li>1 tbsp high-smoke-point oil (grapeseed, canola, or avocado)</li><li>1 paper towel</li><li>1 thin slice of ginger (optional test)</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Set the pan over medium heat for 2 minutes. Sprinkle a few drops of water: if they bead and skate, it's ready.</li><li>Add the oil and swirl to coat the bottom.</li><li>Tilt the pan and wipe the oil around with the paper towel, leaving a thin film. It will smoke a little.</li><li>Keep heating 2–3 more minutes until the oil darkens slightly and just begins to smoke.</li><li>Turn off the heat. Let the pan cool, then wipe out the excess oil. It's now seasoned enough for eggs.</li><li>Test with the ginger slice: if it slides, you're golden.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Why this works</summary><div class="body"><p>The heat opens the microscopic pores in the steel, and the oil fills them, creating a temporary slick surface. Re-season every few uses.</p></div></details><div class="callout info"><p>Don't use olive oil for this—it burns before the pan is ready. Save it for finishing.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Season a Stainless Steel Pan</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 5 min</span><span>Total 15 min</span><span>Serves your future dinners</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted">Stainless isn't nonstick, but you can make it behave. The trick is heat and fat, not a coating.</p><ul class="ing"><li>1 stainless steel skillet, clean and dry</li><li>1 tbsp high-smoke-point oil (grapeseed, canola, or avocado)</li><li>1 paper towel</li><li>1 thin slice of ginger (optional test)</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Set the pan over medium heat for 2 minutes. Sprinkle a few drops of water: if they bead and skate, it's ready.</li><li>Add the oil and swirl to coat the bottom.</li><li>Tilt the pan and wipe the oil around with the paper towel, leaving a thin film. It will smoke a little.</li><li>Keep heating 2–3 more minutes until the oil darkens slightly and just begins to smoke.</li><li>Turn off the heat. Let the pan cool, then wipe out the excess oil. It's now seasoned enough for eggs.</li><li>Test with the ginger slice: if it slides, you're golden.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Why this works</summary><div class="body"><p>The heat opens the microscopic pores in the steel, and the oil fills them, creating a temporary slick surface. Re-season every few uses.</p></div></details><div class="callout info"><p>Don't use olive oil for this—it burns before the pan is ready. Save it for finishing.</p></div></div></div>	-
25.43.0	recipe_card/Cold Brew Coffee, No Fancy Gear	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Cold Brew Coffee</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 5 min</span><span>Steep 12–18 hrs</span><span>Makes 4 cups</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>1 cup coarsely ground coffee (medium-dark roast)</li><li>4 cups cold filtered water</li><li>1 large jar or pitcher</li><li>Fine-mesh strainer or cheesecloth</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Combine coffee and water in the jar. Stir gently to wet all grounds.</li><li>Cover and let sit at room temperature for 12–18 hours. Longer means stronger, not bitter.</li><li>Strain through a fine-mesh sieve lined with cheesecloth or a clean dish towel.</li><li>Bottle and refrigerate. Keeps about a week.</li><li>To serve, dilute concentrate 1:1 with water or milk. Add ice.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Adjusting strength</summary><div class="body"><p>For a stronger concentrate, use 1½ cups coffee to 4 cups water. For a lighter brew, use ¾ cup.</p></div></details><div class="callout good"><p>No bitterness, no acid—just smooth caffeine. Your stomach will thank you.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Cold Brew Coffee</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 5 min</span><span>Steep 12–18 hrs</span><span>Makes 4 cups</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>1 cup coarsely ground coffee (medium-dark roast)</li><li>4 cups cold filtered water</li><li>1 large jar or pitcher</li><li>Fine-mesh strainer or cheesecloth</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Combine coffee and water in the jar. Stir gently to wet all grounds.</li><li>Cover and let sit at room temperature for 12–18 hours. Longer means stronger, not bitter.</li><li>Strain through a fine-mesh sieve lined with cheesecloth or a clean dish towel.</li><li>Bottle and refrigerate. Keeps about a week.</li><li>To serve, dilute concentrate 1:1 with water or milk. Add ice.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Adjusting strength</summary><div class="body"><p>For a stronger concentrate, use 1½ cups coffee to 4 cups water. For a lighter brew, use ¾ cup.</p></div></details><div class="callout good"><p>No bitterness, no acid—just smooth caffeine. Your stomach will thank you.</p></div></div></div>	-
25.44.0	recipe_card/Fix a Running Toilet in 15 Minutes	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Fix a Running Toilet</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 15 min</span><span>Difficulty Easy</span><span>Cost $5–$15</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>1 replacement flapper (bring the old one to the store)</li><li>1 adjustable wrench</li><li>1 sponge or towel</li><li>1 bucket (just in case)</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Turn off the water supply valve behind the toilet. Flush to empty the tank.</li><li>Sponge out remaining water. Unhook the old flapper from the overflow tube and chain.</li><li>Take the old flapper to the hardware store and match it exactly.</li><li>Install the new flapper: hook it onto the overflow tube, connect the chain with about ½ inch of slack.</li><li>Turn the water back on. Let the tank fill and watch. If it still runs, adjust the chain or the float height.</li><li>If water still runs, check the fill valve—sometimes a $10 part fixes it.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>When to call a plumber</summary><div class="body"><p>If the tank itself is cracked or the flange is broken, that's a bigger job. But 90% of running toilets are just the flapper.</p></div></details><div class="callout warn"><p>Don't overtighten the flapper chain—it can lift the flapper and cause a slow leak.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Fix a Running Toilet</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 15 min</span><span>Difficulty Easy</span><span>Cost $5–$15</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>1 replacement flapper (bring the old one to the store)</li><li>1 adjustable wrench</li><li>1 sponge or towel</li><li>1 bucket (just in case)</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Turn off the water supply valve behind the toilet. Flush to empty the tank.</li><li>Sponge out remaining water. Unhook the old flapper from the overflow tube and chain.</li><li>Take the old flapper to the hardware store and match it exactly.</li><li>Install the new flapper: hook it onto the overflow tube, connect the chain with about ½ inch of slack.</li><li>Turn the water back on. Let the tank fill and watch. If it still runs, adjust the chain or the float height.</li><li>If water still runs, check the fill valve—sometimes a $10 part fixes it.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>When to call a plumber</summary><div class="body"><p>If the tank itself is cracked or the flange is broken, that's a bigger job. But 90% of running toilets are just the flapper.</p></div></details><div class="callout warn"><p>Don't overtighten the flapper chain—it can lift the flapper and cause a slow leak.</p></div></div></div>	-
25.45.0	recipe_card/Your First HTML Page	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Basic HTML Page</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 10 min</span><span>Level Beginner</span><span>Output 1 web page</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>1 text editor (Notepad, TextEdit, VS Code)</li><li>1 web browser</li><li>1 folder for your project</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Open your text editor and create a new file. Save it as <code>index.html</code>.</li><li>Type this skeleton:<br><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;<br>&lt;html lang="en"&gt;<br>&lt;head&gt;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;meta charset="UTF-8"&gt;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;title&gt;My Page&lt;/title&gt;<br>&lt;/head&gt;<br>&lt;body&gt;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;h1&gt;Hello, world!&lt;/h1&gt;<br>&lt;/body&gt;<br>&lt;/html&gt;</code></li><li>Save the file. Double-click it to open in your browser.</li><li>Edit the text between <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;/h1&gt;</code>, save, and refresh to see changes.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>What each tag does</summary><div class="body"><p><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;</code> tells the browser it's modern HTML. <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> holds info about the page. <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> is what people see.</p></div></details><div class="callout info"><p>Indentation isn't required, but it makes your code readable. Future you will be grateful.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Basic HTML Page</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 10 min</span><span>Level Beginner</span><span>Output 1 web page</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>1 text editor (Notepad, TextEdit, VS Code)</li><li>1 web browser</li><li>1 folder for your project</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Open your text editor and create a new file. Save it as <code>index.html</code>.</li><li>Type this skeleton:<br><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;<br>&lt;html lang="en"&gt;<br>&lt;head&gt;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;meta charset="UTF-8"&gt;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;title&gt;My Page&lt;/title&gt;<br>&lt;/head&gt;<br>&lt;body&gt;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;h1&gt;Hello, world!&lt;/h1&gt;<br>&lt;/body&gt;<br>&lt;/html&gt;</code></li><li>Save the file. Double-click it to open in your browser.</li><li>Edit the text between <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;/h1&gt;</code>, save, and refresh to see changes.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>What each tag does</summary><div class="body"><p><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;</code> tells the browser it's modern HTML. <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> holds info about the page. <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> is what people see.</p></div></details><div class="callout info"><p>Indentation isn't required, but it makes your code readable. Future you will be grateful.</p></div></div></div>	-
25.46.0	recipe_card/Pour Over Coffee That Tastes Like a Coffee Shop	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Pour Over Coffee</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 2 min</span><span>Brew 4 min</span><span>Serves 1</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted t-small">Good beans, a decent grinder, and a steady hand matter more than the cone.</p><ul class="ing"><li>22 g coffee beans</li><li>350 g water, just off boil</li><li>Filter cone + paper filter</li><li>Kettle with a spout</li></ul><ol class="list"><li><strong>Rinse the filter.</strong> Put it in the cone, pour hot water through, dump the rinse. This warms the cup and kills papery taste.</li><li><strong>Grind medium.</strong> Like table salt, not powder. Weigh 22 g.</li><li><strong>Bloom.</strong> Add 50 g water, wait 30 seconds. The coffee will puff up and smell great.</li><li><strong>Pour in circles.</strong> Slow spirals from the center out. Keep the water level steady. Finish around 3:30.</li><li><strong>Wait.</strong> Let it drain completely. Total brew time 4:00 is the target.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><strong>Too weak?</strong> Grind finer or use less water. Too bitter? Grind coarser or cool the water a touch.</div></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Pour Over Coffee</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 2 min</span><span>Brew 4 min</span><span>Serves 1</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted t-small">Good beans, a decent grinder, and a steady hand matter more than the cone.</p><ul class="ing"><li>22 g coffee beans</li><li>350 g water, just off boil</li><li>Filter cone + paper filter</li><li>Kettle with a spout</li></ul><ol class="list"><li><strong>Rinse the filter.</strong> Put it in the cone, pour hot water through, dump the rinse. This warms the cup and kills papery taste.</li><li><strong>Grind medium.</strong> Like table salt, not powder. Weigh 22 g.</li><li><strong>Bloom.</strong> Add 50 g water, wait 30 seconds. The coffee will puff up and smell great.</li><li><strong>Pour in circles.</strong> Slow spirals from the center out. Keep the water level steady. Finish around 3:30.</li><li><strong>Wait.</strong> Let it drain completely. Total brew time 4:00 is the target.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><strong>Too weak?</strong> Grind finer or use less water. Too bitter? Grind coarser or cool the water a touch.</div></div></div>	-
25.47.0	recipe_card/Fresh Pasta Dough by Hand	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Fresh Pasta Dough</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 20 min</span><span>Rest 30 min</span><span>Serves 4</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted t-small">Flour, eggs, and a little patience. No machine needed.</p><ul class="ing"><li>300 g all-purpose flour (plus extra)</li><li>3 large eggs</li><li>1 tbsp olive oil</li><li>Pinch of salt</li><li>Semolina for dusting</li></ul><ol class="list"><li><strong>Make a well.</strong> Mound flour on a clean counter, hollow the center. Crack eggs into the well, add oil and salt.</li><li><strong>Whisk gently.</strong> Beat eggs with a fork, slowly pulling in flour from the walls. It'll look shaggy.</li><li><strong>Knead.</strong> Once it comes together, knead 8–10 minutes until smooth and springy. If sticky, add flour a tablespoon at a time.</li><li><strong>Rest.</strong> Wrap in plastic, rest 30 minutes at room temp. This relaxes the gluten so it rolls thin.</li><li><strong>Roll and cut.</strong> Roll as thin as you can, dust with semolina, cut into ribbons. Cook in salted boiling water 2–3 minutes.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><strong>Feel test:</strong> Dough should feel like soft Play-Doh, not sticky. A crumbly dough just needs a splash of water or another egg yolk.</div></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Fresh Pasta Dough</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 20 min</span><span>Rest 30 min</span><span>Serves 4</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted t-small">Flour, eggs, and a little patience. No machine needed.</p><ul class="ing"><li>300 g all-purpose flour (plus extra)</li><li>3 large eggs</li><li>1 tbsp olive oil</li><li>Pinch of salt</li><li>Semolina for dusting</li></ul><ol class="list"><li><strong>Make a well.</strong> Mound flour on a clean counter, hollow the center. Crack eggs into the well, add oil and salt.</li><li><strong>Whisk gently.</strong> Beat eggs with a fork, slowly pulling in flour from the walls. It'll look shaggy.</li><li><strong>Knead.</strong> Once it comes together, knead 8–10 minutes until smooth and springy. If sticky, add flour a tablespoon at a time.</li><li><strong>Rest.</strong> Wrap in plastic, rest 30 minutes at room temp. This relaxes the gluten so it rolls thin.</li><li><strong>Roll and cut.</strong> Roll as thin as you can, dust with semolina, cut into ribbons. Cook in salted boiling water 2–3 minutes.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><strong>Feel test:</strong> Dough should feel like soft Play-Doh, not sticky. A crumbly dough just needs a splash of water or another egg yolk.</div></div></div>	-
25.48.0	recipe_card/Deep Clean a Smelly Dishwasher	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Deep Clean a Dishwasher</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 30 min</span><span>Frequency Monthly</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted t-small">If your glasses smell like wet dog, it's not the soap. It's the filter and the door gasket.</p><ul class="ing"><li>White vinegar, 1 cup</li><li>Baking soda, 1/2 cup</li><li>Old toothbrush</li><li>Dish soap and warm water</li><li>Microfiber cloth</li></ul><ol class="list"><li><strong>Empty it.</strong> Remove all dishes and silverware.</li><li><strong>Pull the filter.</strong> Twist and lift the bottom filter. Rinse under hot water, scrub with dish soap and toothbrush. This is where the gunk lives.</li><li><strong>Wipe the gasket.</strong> The rubber seal around the door collects food bits. Wipe with warm soapy water, then dry.</li><li><strong>Vinegar cycle.</strong> Put a cup of vinegar in a bowl on the top rack. Run a hot water cycle. No detergent.</li><li><strong>Baking soda sprinkle.</strong> Sprinkle baking soda on the bottom, run a short hot rinse. It deodorizes and gently scours.</li><li><strong>Dry and done.</strong> Leave the door cracked to air out.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><strong>Don't skip the filter.</strong> A clogged filter is the #1 cause of bad dishwasher smells. Clean it monthly.</div></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Deep Clean a Dishwasher</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 30 min</span><span>Frequency Monthly</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted t-small">If your glasses smell like wet dog, it's not the soap. It's the filter and the door gasket.</p><ul class="ing"><li>White vinegar, 1 cup</li><li>Baking soda, 1/2 cup</li><li>Old toothbrush</li><li>Dish soap and warm water</li><li>Microfiber cloth</li></ul><ol class="list"><li><strong>Empty it.</strong> Remove all dishes and silverware.</li><li><strong>Pull the filter.</strong> Twist and lift the bottom filter. Rinse under hot water, scrub with dish soap and toothbrush. This is where the gunk lives.</li><li><strong>Wipe the gasket.</strong> The rubber seal around the door collects food bits. Wipe with warm soapy water, then dry.</li><li><strong>Vinegar cycle.</strong> Put a cup of vinegar in a bowl on the top rack. Run a hot water cycle. No detergent.</li><li><strong>Baking soda sprinkle.</strong> Sprinkle baking soda on the bottom, run a short hot rinse. It deodorizes and gently scours.</li><li><strong>Dry and done.</strong> Leave the door cracked to air out.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><strong>Don't skip the filter.</strong> A clogged filter is the #1 cause of bad dishwasher smells. Clean it monthly.</div></div></div>	-
25.49.0	recipe_card/Classic Vinaigrette (No Blender)	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Classic Vinaigrette</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 5 min</span><span>Makes 3/4 cup</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted t-small">Three parts oil, one part acid. Shake it in a jar. That's the whole trick.</p><ul class="ing"><li>3/4 cup olive oil</li><li>1/4 cup red wine vinegar (or lemon juice)</li><li>1 tsp Dijon mustard</li><li>1 small garlic clove, minced</li><li>Salt and pepper</li></ul><ol class="list"><li><strong>Combine.</strong> Put vinegar, mustard, garlic, salt, and pepper in a jar with a tight lid.</li><li><strong>Shake.</strong> Shake hard for 10 seconds until it looks cloudy.</li><li><strong>Add oil.</strong> Pour in the olive oil. Shake again for 20–30 seconds. It'll emulsify into a creamy dressing.</li><li><strong>Taste.</strong> Dip a lettuce leaf. Adjust: more oil if too sharp, more vinegar if too flat, salt to wake it up.</li><li><strong>Store.</strong> Keep in the fridge up to a week. Shake before each use; separation is normal.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><strong>No Dijon?</strong> A teaspoon of honey or mayo works as an emulsifier too. Just shake harder.</div></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Classic Vinaigrette</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 5 min</span><span>Makes 3/4 cup</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted t-small">Three parts oil, one part acid. Shake it in a jar. That's the whole trick.</p><ul class="ing"><li>3/4 cup olive oil</li><li>1/4 cup red wine vinegar (or lemon juice)</li><li>1 tsp Dijon mustard</li><li>1 small garlic clove, minced</li><li>Salt and pepper</li></ul><ol class="list"><li><strong>Combine.</strong> Put vinegar, mustard, garlic, salt, and pepper in a jar with a tight lid.</li><li><strong>Shake.</strong> Shake hard for 10 seconds until it looks cloudy.</li><li><strong>Add oil.</strong> Pour in the olive oil. Shake again for 20–30 seconds. It'll emulsify into a creamy dressing.</li><li><strong>Taste.</strong> Dip a lettuce leaf. Adjust: more oil if too sharp, more vinegar if too flat, salt to wake it up.</li><li><strong>Store.</strong> Keep in the fridge up to a week. Shake before each use; separation is normal.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><strong>No Dijon?</strong> A teaspoon of honey or mayo works as an emulsifier too. Just shake harder.</div></div></div>	-
25.50.0	recipe_card/Stovetop Popcorn, No Burning	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Stovetop Popcorn, Without the Burnt Bits</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 2 min</span><span>Cook 5 min</span><span>Serves 2–3</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted t-small">You only need a pot with a lid, oil, kernels, and a little faith.</p><ul class="ing"><li>3 tbsp coconut or canola oil</li><li>1/3 cup popcorn kernels</li><li>Salt, to taste</li><li>2 tbsp butter, melted (optional)</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Heat the oil in a large pot over medium-high with 3 kernels and the lid on.</li><li>When those test kernels pop, add the rest and cover. Shake gently.</li><li>Once popping slows to 2–3 seconds between pops, pull it off the heat.</li><li>Toss with salt and butter. Eat immediately, obviously.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>No burned popcorn?</summary><div class="body"><p>Two things: don't walk away, and take it off the heat the moment the popping slows. The kernels pop fast.</p></div></details></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Stovetop Popcorn, Without the Burnt Bits</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 2 min</span><span>Cook 5 min</span><span>Serves 2–3</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted t-small">You only need a pot with a lid, oil, kernels, and a little faith.</p><ul class="ing"><li>3 tbsp coconut or canola oil</li><li>1/3 cup popcorn kernels</li><li>Salt, to taste</li><li>2 tbsp butter, melted (optional)</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Heat the oil in a large pot over medium-high with 3 kernels and the lid on.</li><li>When those test kernels pop, add the rest and cover. Shake gently.</li><li>Once popping slows to 2–3 seconds between pops, pull it off the heat.</li><li>Toss with salt and butter. Eat immediately, obviously.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>No burned popcorn?</summary><div class="body"><p>Two things: don't walk away, and take it off the heat the moment the popping slows. The kernels pop fast.</p></div></details></div></div>	-
25.51.0	recipe_card/Silence a Squeaky Hardwood Floor	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Silence a Squeaky Floorboard</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 20 min</span><span>Tools minimal</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted t-small">If you can't get under the floor, you can still work from above.</p><ul class="ing"><li>Baby powder or graphite powder</li><li>Finish nails or trim screws</li><li>Hammer or driver</li><li>Nail set (optional)</li><li>Wood filler (optional)</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Find the squeak by walking slowly and marking the spot.</li><li>Dust powder into the seams around the board and walk it in.</li><li>If that fails, drive two finish nails at a slight angle into the joist below, near the squeak.</li><li>Set the nail heads below the surface and fill if you care about looks.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Which method first?</summary><div class="body"><p>Try powder first — it's clean and fast. Nails are for when the board is actually loose.</p></div></details></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Silence a Squeaky Floorboard</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 20 min</span><span>Tools minimal</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted t-small">If you can't get under the floor, you can still work from above.</p><ul class="ing"><li>Baby powder or graphite powder</li><li>Finish nails or trim screws</li><li>Hammer or driver</li><li>Nail set (optional)</li><li>Wood filler (optional)</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Find the squeak by walking slowly and marking the spot.</li><li>Dust powder into the seams around the board and walk it in.</li><li>If that fails, drive two finish nails at a slight angle into the joist below, near the squeak.</li><li>Set the nail heads below the surface and fill if you care about looks.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Which method first?</summary><div class="body"><p>Try powder first — it's clean and fast. Nails are for when the board is actually loose.</p></div></details></div></div>	-
25.52.0	recipe_card/Emergency Candle From a Orange	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Emergency Candle From an Orange</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 10 min</span><span>Burn ~2 hrs</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted t-small">A party trick that doubles as a real backup light. The peel's oil does the work.</p><ul class="ing"><li>1 orange</li><li>Knife</li><li>Olive or vegetable oil</li><li>Lighter or match</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Slice the orange in half and scoop out the fruit, keeping the peel intact.</li><li>Keep the white central pith in one half — that's your wick.</li><li>Pour a little oil into the hollow and let the pith soak for a minute.</li><li>Light the tip of the pith. It may take a few tries.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Safety note</summary><div class="body"><p>Set it on a heatproof plate. Never leave it unattended. It's a candle, after all.</p></div></details></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Emergency Candle From an Orange</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 10 min</span><span>Burn ~2 hrs</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted t-small">A party trick that doubles as a real backup light. The peel's oil does the work.</p><ul class="ing"><li>1 orange</li><li>Knife</li><li>Olive or vegetable oil</li><li>Lighter or match</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Slice the orange in half and scoop out the fruit, keeping the peel intact.</li><li>Keep the white central pith in one half — that's your wick.</li><li>Pour a little oil into the hollow and let the pith soak for a minute.</li><li>Light the tip of the pith. It may take a few tries.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Safety note</summary><div class="body"><p>Set it on a heatproof plate. Never leave it unattended. It's a candle, after all.</p></div></details></div></div>	-
25.53.0	recipe_card/Get Wrinkles Out Without an Iron	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Get Wrinkles Out Without an Iron</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 5–15 min</span><span>No iron needed</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted t-small">The dryer is your friend here, but the shower works too.</p><ul class="ing"><li>Wrinkled garment</li><li>Dryer or hot shower</li><li>Clean tennis ball (optional)</li><li>Spray bottle with water</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>For the dryer: toss the item in with a damp towel or ice cube for 10 minutes on medium.</li><li>For the shower: hang the garment in the bathroom while you take a hot shower. Steam does the rest.</li><li>For a quick fix: mist with water, tug gently, and hang to dry.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>What about delicate fabrics?</summary><div class="body"><p>Skip the dryer. Steam from a shower is gentler and won't shrink or damage.</p></div></details></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Get Wrinkles Out Without an Iron</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 5–15 min</span><span>No iron needed</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted t-small">The dryer is your friend here, but the shower works too.</p><ul class="ing"><li>Wrinkled garment</li><li>Dryer or hot shower</li><li>Clean tennis ball (optional)</li><li>Spray bottle with water</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>For the dryer: toss the item in with a damp towel or ice cube for 10 minutes on medium.</li><li>For the shower: hang the garment in the bathroom while you take a hot shower. Steam does the rest.</li><li>For a quick fix: mist with water, tug gently, and hang to dry.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>What about delicate fabrics?</summary><div class="body"><p>Skip the dryer. Steam from a shower is gentler and won't shrink or damage.</p></div></details></div></div>	-
25.54.0	recipe_card/Unstick a Zipper in Seconds	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Unstick a Zipper in Seconds</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 2 min</span><span>Tools: maybe none</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted t-small">Zippers rarely break. They just need a nudge and a little lube.</p><ul class="ing"><li>Graphite pencil or candle wax</li><li>Lip balm (in a pinch)</li><li>Small pliers (if the pull is bent)</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Hold the fabric flat and gently pull the slider down, not up.</li><li>Rub graphite or wax along the teeth, then work the slider up and down.</li><li>If the slider is loose, squeeze it gently with pliers — a tiny bit.</li><li>Test. If it still sticks, repeat the lube.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>What if the teeth are bent?</summary><div class="body"><p>Use needle-nose pliers to straighten individual teeth. Slow and gentle wins.</p></div></details></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Unstick a Zipper in Seconds</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 2 min</span><span>Tools: maybe none</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted t-small">Zippers rarely break. They just need a nudge and a little lube.</p><ul class="ing"><li>Graphite pencil or candle wax</li><li>Lip balm (in a pinch)</li><li>Small pliers (if the pull is bent)</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Hold the fabric flat and gently pull the slider down, not up.</li><li>Rub graphite or wax along the teeth, then work the slider up and down.</li><li>If the slider is loose, squeeze it gently with pliers — a tiny bit.</li><li>Test. If it still sticks, repeat the lube.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>What if the teeth are bent?</summary><div class="body"><p>Use needle-nose pliers to straighten individual teeth. Slow and gentle wins.</p></div></details></div></div>	-
25.55.0	recipe_card/Make a DIY Phone Stand From Cardboard	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>DIY Phone Stand From Cardboard</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 10 min</span><span>Tools: scissors</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted t-small">Sturdy enough for video calls and late-night scrolling.</p><ul class="ing"><li>Corrugated cardboard</li><li>Scissors or craft knife</li><li>Ruler</li><li>Pencil</li><li>Tape or glue</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Cut a rectangle about 4 inches wide and 6 inches tall.</li><li>Fold it in half to make a tent shape.</li><li>Cut a shallow notch in the folded edge for the phone to rest in.</li><li>Cut two small triangles from scrap and tape them to the sides for support.</li><li>Test with your phone, adjust the notch angle if needed.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Make it look nicer</summary><div class="body"><p>Wrap it in washi tape or paint it. Function first, form second.</p></div></details></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>DIY Phone Stand From Cardboard</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 10 min</span><span>Tools: scissors</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted t-small">Sturdy enough for video calls and late-night scrolling.</p><ul class="ing"><li>Corrugated cardboard</li><li>Scissors or craft knife</li><li>Ruler</li><li>Pencil</li><li>Tape or glue</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Cut a rectangle about 4 inches wide and 6 inches tall.</li><li>Fold it in half to make a tent shape.</li><li>Cut a shallow notch in the folded edge for the phone to rest in.</li><li>Cut two small triangles from scrap and tape them to the sides for support.</li><li>Test with your phone, adjust the notch angle if needed.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Make it look nicer</summary><div class="body"><p>Wrap it in washi tape or paint it. Function first, form second.</p></div></details></div></div>	-
25.56.0	recipe_card/Emergency Candle From an Orange	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Emergency Candle From an Orange</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 5 min</span><span>Burn 30 min</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>1 orange</li><li>Knife</li><li>Olive oil or any cooking oil</li><li>Lighter or match</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Cut the orange in half and scoop out the flesh.</li><li>Keep the white pith intact in one half.</li><li>Pour a little oil into the hollow.</li><li>Light the central pith strand.</li><li>Add oil as needed to keep burning.</li></ol><p class="muted t-small"><regex>user_name</regex>, the pith acts as a wick—it'll burn for a good while.</p></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Emergency Candle From an Orange</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 5 min</span><span>Burn 30 min</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>1 orange</li><li>Knife</li><li>Olive oil or any cooking oil</li><li>Lighter or match</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Cut the orange in half and scoop out the flesh.</li><li>Keep the white pith intact in one half.</li><li>Pour a little oil into the hollow.</li><li>Light the central pith strand.</li><li>Add oil as needed to keep burning.</li></ol><p class="muted t-small">, the pith acts as a wick—it'll burn for a good while.</p></div></div>	-
25.57.0	recipe_card/Chewy Chocolate Chip Cookies	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Chewy Chocolate Chip Cookies</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 15 min</span><span>Bake 11 min</span><span>Makes 20</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted">Hey <regex>user_name</regex> — the trick is melted butter and one egg plus one yolk. Cold dough, hot oven.</p><ul class="ing"><li>2¼ cups all-purpose flour</li><li>1 tsp baking soda</li><li>1 tsp fine salt</li><li>¾ cup unsalted butter, melted and cooled a bit</li><li>1 cup brown sugar, packed</li><li>½ cup white sugar</li><li>1 whole egg + 1 yolk</li><li>2 tsp vanilla extract</li><li>1½ cups chocolate chips</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Whisk flour, baking soda and salt in a bowl. Set aside.</li><li>Beat the melted butter with both sugars for about a minute — it'll look grainy, that's fine.</li><li>Add the egg, the extra yolk and the vanilla. Beat until glossy and a shade lighter.</li><li>Fold in the dry mix with a spatula, then the chips. Stop as soon as it comes together.</li><li>Chill the dough 30 minutes if you can wait. It makes a real difference.</li><li>Scoop 2-tablespoon balls onto a lined sheet, 2 inches apart.</li><li>Bake at 350°F / 175°C for 10–12 minutes, until the edges are set and the middles still look underdone.</li><li>Rest on the sheet 5 minutes before moving them. They finish cooking there.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>If they came out flat</summary><div class="body"><p>Warm dough, or butter you melted past "soft". Chill longer next time and skip the extra white sugar.</p></div></details></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Chewy Chocolate Chip Cookies</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 15 min</span><span>Bake 11 min</span><span>Makes 20</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted">Hey  — the trick is melted butter and one egg plus one yolk. Cold dough, hot oven.</p><ul class="ing"><li>2¼ cups all-purpose flour</li><li>1 tsp baking soda</li><li>1 tsp fine salt</li><li>¾ cup unsalted butter, melted and cooled a bit</li><li>1 cup brown sugar, packed</li><li>½ cup white sugar</li><li>1 whole egg + 1 yolk</li><li>2 tsp vanilla extract</li><li>1½ cups chocolate chips</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Whisk flour, baking soda and salt in a bowl. Set aside.</li><li>Beat the melted butter with both sugars for about a minute — it'll look grainy, that's fine.</li><li>Add the egg, the extra yolk and the vanilla. Beat until glossy and a shade lighter.</li><li>Fold in the dry mix with a spatula, then the chips. Stop as soon as it comes together.</li><li>Chill the dough 30 minutes if you can wait. It makes a real difference.</li><li>Scoop 2-tablespoon balls onto a lined sheet, 2 inches apart.</li><li>Bake at 350°F / 175°C for 10–12 minutes, until the edges are set and the middles still look underdone.</li><li>Rest on the sheet 5 minutes before moving them. They finish cooking there.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>If they came out flat</summary><div class="body"><p>Warm dough, or butter you melted past "soft". Chill longer next time and skip the extra white sugar.</p></div></details></div></div>	-
25.58.0	recipe_card/Build a Fire in a Fireplace (No Smoke in the Room)	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Build a Fire in a Fireplace (No Smoke in the Room)</h3><div class="meta"><span>Setup 10 min</span><span>Burns 2–3 hrs</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted"><regex>user_name</regex>, smoke in the room is almost always a cold flue or a pile of green wood. Fix both and it behaves.</p><ul class="ing"><li>Dry seasoned wood, split small-to-medium</li><li>Newspaper or a fire starter</li><li>Kindling, pencil-thick</li><li>A long lighter or matches</li><li>Ash bucket + metal shovel (for later)</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Open the damper fully and hold a lit match near the top of the firebox. If the flame pulls up and in, you have draft. If it sits there, warm the flue with a rolled newspaper torch for 30 seconds.</li><li>Crack the nearest window an inch. Sounds backwards; it stops the room from fighting the chimney for air.</li><li>Build a loose teepee of kindling over the starter. Air gaps matter more than arrangement.</li><li>Light it and leave the glass door open a hand-width until the kindling catches well.</li><li>Add two medium splits, not five. Overloading is the second cause of smoke.</li><li>Once it's burning cleanly, close the door and set the damper to about three-quarters open.</li><li>Burn hot for the first 20 minutes to establish the flue, then dial back.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p>Never close the damper until the ash is cold. Creosote plus a hot ember is a chimney fire.</p></div><details class="acc"><summary>Smoke still rolling in?</summary><div class="body"><p>Check the chimney cap for a bird nest or a blocked screen. Also: if the firebox is taller than the flue opening, a grate that raises the fire closer to the throat helps.</p></div></details></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Build a Fire in a Fireplace (No Smoke in the Room)</h3><div class="meta"><span>Setup 10 min</span><span>Burns 2–3 hrs</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted">, smoke in the room is almost always a cold flue or a pile of green wood. Fix both and it behaves.</p><ul class="ing"><li>Dry seasoned wood, split small-to-medium</li><li>Newspaper or a fire starter</li><li>Kindling, pencil-thick</li><li>A long lighter or matches</li><li>Ash bucket + metal shovel (for later)</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Open the damper fully and hold a lit match near the top of the firebox. If the flame pulls up and in, you have draft. If it sits there, warm the flue with a rolled newspaper torch for 30 seconds.</li><li>Crack the nearest window an inch. Sounds backwards; it stops the room from fighting the chimney for air.</li><li>Build a loose teepee of kindling over the starter. Air gaps matter more than arrangement.</li><li>Light it and leave the glass door open a hand-width until the kindling catches well.</li><li>Add two medium splits, not five. Overloading is the second cause of smoke.</li><li>Once it's burning cleanly, close the door and set the damper to about three-quarters open.</li><li>Burn hot for the first 20 minutes to establish the flue, then dial back.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p>Never close the damper until the ash is cold. Creosote plus a hot ember is a chimney fire.</p></div><details class="acc"><summary>Smoke still rolling in?</summary><div class="body"><p>Check the chimney cap for a bird nest or a blocked screen. Also: if the firebox is taller than the flue opening, a grate that raises the fire closer to the throat helps.</p></div></details></div></div>	-
25.59.0	recipe_card/Write a Cover Letter That Doesn't Sound Like Everyone Else's	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Write a Cover Letter That Doesn't Sound Like Everyone Else's</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 30 min</span><span>Length 250–300 words</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted">Short version, <regex>user_name</regex>: one specific reason you want <em>this</em> job, one proof you can do it, one line asking for the conversation.</p><ul class="ing"><li>The job posting, printed or on a second screen</li><li>The company's site, plus their last three announcements</li><li>Your résumé, so you don't repeat it word for word</li><li>One number that shows a result you caused</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Open with the hook: a specific thing about the company or role. "I've used your product since the 2019 beta" beats "I am writing to apply."</li><li>Name the role plainly in the first two sentences. Recruiters skim.</li><li>Spend the middle paragraph on one story, not a list of skills. Situation, what you did, what changed. Include the number.</li><li>Connect it forward: why that experience maps to what they said they need. Quote their own phrasing if it fits.</li><li>Address the gap. Changing industries or a career break? One calm sentence, no apology spiral.</li><li>Close with a direct ask: a conversation, a call, a next step. Thank them in the same sentence.</li><li>Read it out loud. Anything you'd never say to a person gets cut.</li><li>Match the format: PDF unless they ask otherwise, filename like Firstname-Lastname-Cover-Letter.pdf.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>Tailoring beats volume. Three tailored letters outperform thirty generic ones.</p></div><details class="acc"><summary>Sample opening line</summary><div class="body"><p>"Your posting mentions rebuilding the onboarding flow — I did exactly that at my last job and cut drop-off by 34%."</p></div></details></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Write a Cover Letter That Doesn't Sound Like Everyone Else's</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 30 min</span><span>Length 250–300 words</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted">Short version, : one specific reason you want <em>this</em> job, one proof you can do it, one line asking for the conversation.</p><ul class="ing"><li>The job posting, printed or on a second screen</li><li>The company's site, plus their last three announcements</li><li>Your résumé, so you don't repeat it word for word</li><li>One number that shows a result you caused</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Open with the hook: a specific thing about the company or role. "I've used your product since the 2019 beta" beats "I am writing to apply."</li><li>Name the role plainly in the first two sentences. Recruiters skim.</li><li>Spend the middle paragraph on one story, not a list of skills. Situation, what you did, what changed. Include the number.</li><li>Connect it forward: why that experience maps to what they said they need. Quote their own phrasing if it fits.</li><li>Address the gap. Changing industries or a career break? One calm sentence, no apology spiral.</li><li>Close with a direct ask: a conversation, a call, a next step. Thank them in the same sentence.</li><li>Read it out loud. Anything you'd never say to a person gets cut.</li><li>Match the format: PDF unless they ask otherwise, filename like Firstname-Lastname-Cover-Letter.pdf.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>Tailoring beats volume. Three tailored letters outperform thirty generic ones.</p></div><details class="acc"><summary>Sample opening line</summary><div class="body"><p>"Your posting mentions rebuilding the onboarding flow — I did exactly that at my last job and cut drop-off by 34%."</p></div></details></div></div>	-
25.60.0	recipe_card/Patch a Small Hole in Drywall	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Patch a Small Hole in Drywall</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 1–2 hrs + dry time</span><span>Difficulty easy</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted">Doorknob dents and picture-hook craters, <regex>user_name</regex> — all the same job. Small holes are the easy kind.</p><ul class="ing"><li>Spackle or lightweight joint compound</li><li>Flexible putty knife, 3–4 inch</li><li>Fine sanding sponge, 120–220 grit</li><li>Self-adhesive mesh patch (for holes over ½ inch)</li><li>Primer + matching paint</li><li>A damp rag</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Clean the area. Loose paint and dust make the patch fail.</li><li>For a nail hole: press spackle in with your finger, then drag the knife across flush in one smooth pass.</li><li>For a doorknob dent bigger than a dime: stick a mesh patch over it and press it flat.</li><li>Skim compound over the patch, feathering the edges 2 inches past the mesh. Thin coats beat thick ones.</li><li>Let it dry fully — usually 1–2 hours for spackle, longer for compound. Don't rush it with a hair dryer.</li><li>Sand lightly until your fingers can't feel a ridge. Wipe the dust with the damp rag.</li><li>Spot-prime. Skip this and the patch will flash through the paint as a dull spot.</li><li>Paint the patch, then feather the paint outward so it blends.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p>Test the texture with your fingertips, not your eyes. Fingers find what light hides.</p></div><details class="acc"><summary>Hole bigger than a fist?</summary><div class="body"><p>That's a drywall plug or a California patch, not spackle. Different job, still doable — say the word and I'll walk you through it.</p></div></details></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Patch a Small Hole in Drywall</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 1–2 hrs + dry time</span><span>Difficulty easy</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted">Doorknob dents and picture-hook craters,  — all the same job. Small holes are the easy kind.</p><ul class="ing"><li>Spackle or lightweight joint compound</li><li>Flexible putty knife, 3–4 inch</li><li>Fine sanding sponge, 120–220 grit</li><li>Self-adhesive mesh patch (for holes over ½ inch)</li><li>Primer + matching paint</li><li>A damp rag</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Clean the area. Loose paint and dust make the patch fail.</li><li>For a nail hole: press spackle in with your finger, then drag the knife across flush in one smooth pass.</li><li>For a doorknob dent bigger than a dime: stick a mesh patch over it and press it flat.</li><li>Skim compound over the patch, feathering the edges 2 inches past the mesh. Thin coats beat thick ones.</li><li>Let it dry fully — usually 1–2 hours for spackle, longer for compound. Don't rush it with a hair dryer.</li><li>Sand lightly until your fingers can't feel a ridge. Wipe the dust with the damp rag.</li><li>Spot-prime. Skip this and the patch will flash through the paint as a dull spot.</li><li>Paint the patch, then feather the paint outward so it blends.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p>Test the texture with your fingertips, not your eyes. Fingers find what light hides.</p></div><details class="acc"><summary>Hole bigger than a fist?</summary><div class="body"><p>That's a drywall plug or a California patch, not spackle. Different job, still doable — say the word and I'll walk you through it.</p></div></details></div></div>	-
25.61.0	recipe_card/Sharpen a Kitchen Knife on a Whetstone	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Sharpen a Kitchen Knife on a Whetstone</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 15 min</span><span>Angle 15–20°</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted">A dull knife is the dangerous one, <regex>user_name</regex>. It slips. Ten minutes on a stone fixes that for months.</p><ul class="ing"><li>Combination whetstone, 1000/6000 grit</li><li>Water (most stones are soak-and-go)</li><li>A towel or non-slip mat</li><li>Your knife</li><li>Optional: a leather strop or honing rod</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Soak the 1000-grit side in water for 10–15 minutes until bubbles stop. Keep it wet the whole time.</li><li>Set the stone on the towel, coarse side up, and hold it steady.</li><li>Find the angle: lay the blade flat, then lift the spine about the thickness of two stacked coins. That's roughly 18°.</li><li>Push the edge across the stone like you're slicing a thin layer off the top, heel to tip. Light pressure.</li><li>Do 10 passes on one side, then 10 on the other. Alternate. Keep the angle locked — wobbling rounds the edge.</li><li>Feel for a burr along the far side of the edge. A faint catch under your thumb means you're done on the coarse side.</li><li>Flip the stone to 6000 grit. Same motion, but barely any pressure — this is polishing, not grinding.</li><li>Rinse and dry the blade. Test on a tomato, not your thumb.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p>Don't run a good knife through the dishwasher. The heat and detergent wreck the edge you just built.</p></div><details class="acc"><summary>No stone yet?</summary><div class="body"><p>A ceramic mug's unglazed bottom ring works as a stopgap. Pull the edge across it at the same angle, 15 passes a side.</p></div></details></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Sharpen a Kitchen Knife on a Whetstone</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 15 min</span><span>Angle 15–20°</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted">A dull knife is the dangerous one, . It slips. Ten minutes on a stone fixes that for months.</p><ul class="ing"><li>Combination whetstone, 1000/6000 grit</li><li>Water (most stones are soak-and-go)</li><li>A towel or non-slip mat</li><li>Your knife</li><li>Optional: a leather strop or honing rod</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Soak the 1000-grit side in water for 10–15 minutes until bubbles stop. Keep it wet the whole time.</li><li>Set the stone on the towel, coarse side up, and hold it steady.</li><li>Find the angle: lay the blade flat, then lift the spine about the thickness of two stacked coins. That's roughly 18°.</li><li>Push the edge across the stone like you're slicing a thin layer off the top, heel to tip. Light pressure.</li><li>Do 10 passes on one side, then 10 on the other. Alternate. Keep the angle locked — wobbling rounds the edge.</li><li>Feel for a burr along the far side of the edge. A faint catch under your thumb means you're done on the coarse side.</li><li>Flip the stone to 6000 grit. Same motion, but barely any pressure — this is polishing, not grinding.</li><li>Rinse and dry the blade. Test on a tomato, not your thumb.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p>Don't run a good knife through the dishwasher. The heat and detergent wreck the edge you just built.</p></div><details class="acc"><summary>No stone yet?</summary><div class="body"><p>A ceramic mug's unglazed bottom ring works as a stopgap. Pull the edge across it at the same angle, 15 passes a side.</p></div></details></div></div>	-
25.62.0	recipe_card/Get a Good Night's Sleep When Your Brain Won't Shut Up	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Get a Good Night's Sleep When Your Brain Won't Shut Up</h3><div class="meta"><span>Wind-down 45 min</span><span>No pills required</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted">Can't sleep because you're thinking, or thinking because you can't sleep? Either way, <regex>user_name</regex>, the fix starts before you're in bed.</p><ul class="ing"><li>A consistent wake time, even on weekends</li><li>Dim light for the last hour</li><li>Cool room, roughly 65–68°F / 18–20°C</li><li>Paper and pen by the bed</li><li>Phone charging in another room, if you can stand it</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Set a wind-down alarm 45 minutes before bed. When it rings, screens go dim and work stops.</li><li>Write tomorrow's list on paper. Your brain loops on unfinished things; writing them down tells it the job is handled.</li><li>Keep the room cool and dark. Warm and bright both fight sleep.</li><li>Get in bed only when you're sleepy. Tired-but-not-sleepy in bed teaches your body that bed is for lying awake.</li><li>If you're still awake after 20 minutes, get up. Sit somewhere dim and boring until you feel drowsy, then go back.</li><li>Breathe slow: in for 4, out for 6, for two minutes. The long exhale does the work.</li><li>If a worry keeps surfacing, write it down with a one-line plan and close the notebook.</li><li>Same wake time every day. That's the anchor the whole thing hangs on.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>Two bad nights in a row is normal. It's the third night's panic about the first two that causes the trouble.</p></div><details class="acc"><summary>Coffee cutoff?</summary><div class="body"><p>Roughly 8–10 hours before bed. Caffeine's half-life is longer than most people assume.</p></div></details></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Get a Good Night's Sleep When Your Brain Won't Shut Up</h3><div class="meta"><span>Wind-down 45 min</span><span>No pills required</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted">Can't sleep because you're thinking, or thinking because you can't sleep? Either way, , the fix starts before you're in bed.</p><ul class="ing"><li>A consistent wake time, even on weekends</li><li>Dim light for the last hour</li><li>Cool room, roughly 65–68°F / 18–20°C</li><li>Paper and pen by the bed</li><li>Phone charging in another room, if you can stand it</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Set a wind-down alarm 45 minutes before bed. When it rings, screens go dim and work stops.</li><li>Write tomorrow's list on paper. Your brain loops on unfinished things; writing them down tells it the job is handled.</li><li>Keep the room cool and dark. Warm and bright both fight sleep.</li><li>Get in bed only when you're sleepy. Tired-but-not-sleepy in bed teaches your body that bed is for lying awake.</li><li>If you're still awake after 20 minutes, get up. Sit somewhere dim and boring until you feel drowsy, then go back.</li><li>Breathe slow: in for 4, out for 6, for two minutes. The long exhale does the work.</li><li>If a worry keeps surfacing, write it down with a one-line plan and close the notebook.</li><li>Same wake time every day. That's the anchor the whole thing hangs on.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>Two bad nights in a row is normal. It's the third night's panic about the first two that causes the trouble.</p></div><details class="acc"><summary>Coffee cutoff?</summary><div class="body"><p>Roughly 8–10 hours before bed. Caffeine's half-life is longer than most people assume.</p></div></details></div></div>	-
25.63.0	recipe_card/Make a Decent Espresso Without a Machine	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Make a Decent Espresso Without a Machine</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 4 min</span><span>Makes 1 shot</span><span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted t-small">No machine, no guilt. <regex>user_name</regex>, strong and syrupy is a technique, not a gadget.</p><ul class="ing"><li>2 tbsp fine-ground coffee</li><li>6 oz water, just off boil</li><li>A mug + a fine sieve</li><li>Optional: pinch of salt</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Grind as fine as your grinder allows. Finer = closer to espresso.</li><li>Heat water to ~200°F. Pour 1 tbsp onto the grounds, stir once, wait 30 seconds.</li><li>Add the rest of the water, stir, cover, steep 3 minutes.</li><li>Press through the sieve slowly. If it drips fast, grind finer next time.</li><li>Salt kills bitterness if it's harsh.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Why this works</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted t-small">Pressure isn't the magic, fine grind + short ratio is. Aim for about 2 oz of liquid from 2 tbsp of coffee.</p></div></details></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Make a Decent Espresso Without a Machine</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 4 min</span><span>Makes 1 shot</span><span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted t-small">No machine, no guilt. , strong and syrupy is a technique, not a gadget.</p><ul class="ing"><li>2 tbsp fine-ground coffee</li><li>6 oz water, just off boil</li><li>A mug + a fine sieve</li><li>Optional: pinch of salt</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Grind as fine as your grinder allows. Finer = closer to espresso.</li><li>Heat water to ~200°F. Pour 1 tbsp onto the grounds, stir once, wait 30 seconds.</li><li>Add the rest of the water, stir, cover, steep 3 minutes.</li><li>Press through the sieve slowly. If it drips fast, grind finer next time.</li><li>Salt kills bitterness if it's harsh.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Why this works</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted t-small">Pressure isn't the magic, fine grind + short ratio is. Aim for about 2 oz of liquid from 2 tbsp of coffee.</p></div></details></div></div>	-
25.0.1	recipe_card/Keep Basil Alive on a Windowsill	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Keep Basil Alive on a Windowsill</h3><div class="meta"><span>Setup 5 min</span><span>Daily 1 min</span></div></div><div class="body"><div class="callout info"><p><strong>Honest note:</strong> supermarket basil is grown in a crowded clump. Split it the day you bring it home and half your problems vanish.</p></div><ul class="ing"><li>1 basil plant, clumped</li><li>A 4–6" pot per stem</li><li>Loose potting mix</li><li>South-facing light</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Slide the whole clump out. Gently tease roots apart into 3–5 plants.</li><li>Replant each stem in its own pot. Bury up to the lowest leaves.</li><li>Water when the top inch is dry. Not on a schedule — on a poke test.</li><li>Pinch flower buds the second you see them. Flowering = bitter leaves = end of plant.</li><li>Harvest from the top down, always above a leaf pair so it branches.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>It's drooping — now what?</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted t-small">Yellow lower leaves = too wet. Crispy edges = too dry or too much sun. Tilt the pot; if water pours out, you've found your culprit.</p></div></details></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Keep Basil Alive on a Windowsill</h3><div class="meta"><span>Setup 5 min</span><span>Daily 1 min</span></div></div><div class="body"><div class="callout info"><p><strong>Honest note:</strong> supermarket basil is grown in a crowded clump. Split it the day you bring it home and half your problems vanish.</p></div><ul class="ing"><li>1 basil plant, clumped</li><li>A 4–6" pot per stem</li><li>Loose potting mix</li><li>South-facing light</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Slide the whole clump out. Gently tease roots apart into 3–5 plants.</li><li>Replant each stem in its own pot. Bury up to the lowest leaves.</li><li>Water when the top inch is dry. Not on a schedule — on a poke test.</li><li>Pinch flower buds the second you see them. Flowering = bitter leaves = end of plant.</li><li>Harvest from the top down, always above a leaf pair so it branches.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>It's drooping — now what?</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted t-small">Yellow lower leaves = too wet. Crispy edges = too dry or too much sun. Tilt the pot; if water pours out, you've found your culprit.</p></div></details></div></div>	-
25.1.1	recipe_card/Fix a Wobbly Chair Leg	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Fix a Wobbly Chair Leg</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 20 min</span><span>Plus dry time</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted t-small"><regex>user_name</regex>, a wobble is a loose joint, not a broken chair. Tighten the fit before you reach for glue.</p><ul class="ing"><li>Wood glue</li><li>A toothpick or two</li><li>Rubber mallet (or a shoe)</li><li>Clamp or a strong rubber band</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Flip the chair. Find the loose joint at the leg-to-seat or leg-to-rung.</li><li>Wiggle it to confirm. If the wood is cracked, glue won't hold — that's a clamp-and-drill job.</li><li>Work a little glue into the gap. Rotate the leg slightly to spread it.</li><li>If the socket is worn, jam in toothpick slivers coated in glue. That's the trick: wood fills wood.</li><li>Wipe squeeze-out, clamp, and let it sit 24 hours. Actually 24. Not 3.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Which joint is it?</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted t-small">Wobble side-to-side = seat joint. Wobble front-to-back = one rung is loose. Tap the rung back in and glue both ends.</p></div></details></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Fix a Wobbly Chair Leg</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 20 min</span><span>Plus dry time</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted t-small">, a wobble is a loose joint, not a broken chair. Tighten the fit before you reach for glue.</p><ul class="ing"><li>Wood glue</li><li>A toothpick or two</li><li>Rubber mallet (or a shoe)</li><li>Clamp or a strong rubber band</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Flip the chair. Find the loose joint at the leg-to-seat or leg-to-rung.</li><li>Wiggle it to confirm. If the wood is cracked, glue won't hold — that's a clamp-and-drill job.</li><li>Work a little glue into the gap. Rotate the leg slightly to spread it.</li><li>If the socket is worn, jam in toothpick slivers coated in glue. That's the trick: wood fills wood.</li><li>Wipe squeeze-out, clamp, and let it sit 24 hours. Actually 24. Not 3.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Which joint is it?</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted t-small">Wobble side-to-side = seat joint. Wobble front-to-back = one rung is loose. Tap the rung back in and glue both ends.</p></div></details></div></div>	-
25.2.1	recipe_card/Bake a Baked Potato That Isn't Sad	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Bake a Baked Potato That Isn't Sad</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 5 min</span><span>Bake 55 min</span><span>Oven 425°F</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted t-small">Foil steams it. Skip the foil, <regex>user_name</regex>, and you get skin worth eating.</p><ul class="ing"><li>1 russet potato per person</li><li>Olive oil or bacon fat</li><li>Kosher salt</li><li>A fork</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Heat oven to 425°F. Move a rack to the middle.</li><li>Scrub, dry completely, and prick each potato 6–8 times with a fork. Skip this and it can pop.</li><li>Rub with fat and roll in salt. That's your crispy skin.</li><li>Bake directly on the rack, 50–60 minutes for a medium potato. It should give when squeezed with a towel.</li><li>Rest 5 minutes before cutting. Steam inside finishes the job.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Speed mode</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted t-small">Microwave 8 minutes, then 15 minutes at 450°F to crisp the skin. Not identical, but 75 minutes faster.</p></div></details></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Bake a Baked Potato That Isn't Sad</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 5 min</span><span>Bake 55 min</span><span>Oven 425°F</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted t-small">Foil steams it. Skip the foil, , and you get skin worth eating.</p><ul class="ing"><li>1 russet potato per person</li><li>Olive oil or bacon fat</li><li>Kosher salt</li><li>A fork</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Heat oven to 425°F. Move a rack to the middle.</li><li>Scrub, dry completely, and prick each potato 6–8 times with a fork. Skip this and it can pop.</li><li>Rub with fat and roll in salt. That's your crispy skin.</li><li>Bake directly on the rack, 50–60 minutes for a medium potato. It should give when squeezed with a towel.</li><li>Rest 5 minutes before cutting. Steam inside finishes the job.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Speed mode</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted t-small">Microwave 8 minutes, then 15 minutes at 450°F to crisp the skin. Not identical, but 75 minutes faster.</p></div></details></div></div>	-
25.3.1	recipe_card/Iron a Shirt Collar Properly	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Iron a Shirt Collar Properly</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 6 min</span><span>No starch needed</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted t-small">The collar is ironed <em>first</em>, on the underside. <regex>user_name</regex>, order matters more than pressure.</p><ul class="ing"><li>An iron, steam on</li><li>Spray bottle of water</li><li>A folded towel</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Lay the collar flat, unbuttoned. Iron the <em>inside</em> first, from the points toward the center.</li><li>Flip, iron the outside. Use the towel as a curved pad so you press a shape, not a pancake.</li><li>Cuffs next, same inside-first rule.</li><li>Then body, then sleeves, then yoke. Always toward the hem.</li><li>Hang immediately. Sitting in a pile undoes all of it.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>No iron, big meeting</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted t-small">Hang it in the bathroom while the shower runs hot, 10 minutes. Then smooth the collar by hand while slightly damp and hang to dry.</p></div></details></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Iron a Shirt Collar Properly</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 6 min</span><span>No starch needed</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="muted t-small">The collar is ironed <em>first</em>, on the underside. , order matters more than pressure.</p><ul class="ing"><li>An iron, steam on</li><li>Spray bottle of water</li><li>A folded towel</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Lay the collar flat, unbuttoned. Iron the <em>inside</em> first, from the points toward the center.</li><li>Flip, iron the outside. Use the towel as a curved pad so you press a shape, not a pancake.</li><li>Cuffs next, same inside-first rule.</li><li>Then body, then sleeves, then yoke. Always toward the hem.</li><li>Hang immediately. Sitting in a pile undoes all of it.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>No iron, big meeting</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted t-small">Hang it in the bathroom while the shower runs hot, 10 minutes. Then smooth the collar by hand while slightly damp and hang to dry.</p></div></details></div></div>	-
25.4.1	recipe_card/Save an Overwatered Houseplant	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Save an Overwatered Houseplant</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 15 min</span><span>Recovery 2 weeks</span></div></div><div class="body"><div class="callout warn"><p>Yellow, soft leaves + moldy soil smell = classic overwater. Dry soil with brown crispy tips is a different problem entirely.</p></div><ul class="ing"><li>Fresh dry potting mix</li><li>A pot with a real drainage hole</li><li>Scissors</li><li>Newspaper</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Stop watering. Today. That's step one and it's the hardest.</li><li>Slide the plant out. If the roots are brown and mushy, cut them off with clean scissors until you hit firm white root.</li><li>Shake out the soggy soil. Don't rinse — just let it crumble.</li><li>Repot in dry mix, in a pot that drains. Water once, lightly, then leave it.</li><li>Bright indirect light, no direct sun, no fertilizer for two weeks.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>How do I know it's working?</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted t-small">New growth in 10–14 days. If the plant keeps dropping leaves but the stem is firm, it's recovering. Give it time, <regex>user_name</regex> — roots are slower than leaves.</p></div></details></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Save an Overwatered Houseplant</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 15 min</span><span>Recovery 2 weeks</span></div></div><div class="body"><div class="callout warn"><p>Yellow, soft leaves + moldy soil smell = classic overwater. Dry soil with brown crispy tips is a different problem entirely.</p></div><ul class="ing"><li>Fresh dry potting mix</li><li>A pot with a real drainage hole</li><li>Scissors</li><li>Newspaper</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Stop watering. Today. That's step one and it's the hardest.</li><li>Slide the plant out. If the roots are brown and mushy, cut them off with clean scissors until you hit firm white root.</li><li>Shake out the soggy soil. Don't rinse — just let it crumble.</li><li>Repot in dry mix, in a pot that drains. Water once, lightly, then leave it.</li><li>Bright indirect light, no direct sun, no fertilizer for two weeks.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>How do I know it's working?</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted t-small">New growth in 10–14 days. If the plant keeps dropping leaves but the stem is firm, it's recovering. Give it time,  — roots are slower than leaves.</p></div></details></div></div>	-
25.5.1	recipe_card/Clean a Burnt Pot Without Scrubbing Forever	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Clean a Burnt Pot</h3>    <div class="meta"><span>Hands-on 5 min</span><span>Waiting 20 min</span><span>No scrubbing</span></div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <div class="callout info"><p>Chemistry does the work, <regex>user_name</regex>. You just wait.</p></div>    <ul class="ing">      <li>1 burnt pot</li>      <li>Water, 1 inch deep</li>      <li>2 tbsp baking soda</li>      <li>Splash of vinegar</li>      <li>Wooden spoon</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Cover the burnt bottom with water.</li>      <li>Add baking soda, then vinegar. It fizzes. Enjoy that.</li>      <li>Simmer 10–15 min until the crust lifts.</li>      <li>Scrape gently with the spoon. Most of it slides right off.</li>      <li>Wash, dry, and pretend it never happened.</li>    </ol>    <details class="acc">      <summary>Enamel or nonstick?</summary>      <div class="body"><p>Skip the high simmer. Warm water, baking soda, 30 min soak, soft sponge only.</p></div>    </details>  </div></div>	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Clean a Burnt Pot</h3>    <div class="meta"><span>Hands-on 5 min</span><span>Waiting 20 min</span><span>No scrubbing</span></div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <div class="callout info"><p>Chemistry does the work, . You just wait.</p></div>    <ul class="ing">      <li>1 burnt pot</li>      <li>Water, 1 inch deep</li>      <li>2 tbsp baking soda</li>      <li>Splash of vinegar</li>      <li>Wooden spoon</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Cover the burnt bottom with water.</li>      <li>Add baking soda, then vinegar. It fizzes. Enjoy that.</li>      <li>Simmer 10–15 min until the crust lifts.</li>      <li>Scrape gently with the spoon. Most of it slides right off.</li>      <li>Wash, dry, and pretend it never happened.</li>    </ol>    <details class="acc">      <summary>Enamel or nonstick?</summary>      <div class="body"><p>Skip the high simmer. Warm water, baking soda, 30 min soak, soft sponge only.</p></div>    </details>  </div></div>	-
25.6.1	recipe_card/Kill the Smell in Your Gym Bag	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Kill the Smell in Your Gym Bag</h3>    <div class="meta"><span>Active 10 min</span><span>Overnight dwell</span></div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <p class="muted">The smell lives in the lining and the seams, <regex>user_name>—not the outside.</p>    <ul class="ing">      <li>1 cup baking soda</li>      <li>10 drops tea tree or lavender oil</li>      <li>Damp cloth</li>      <li>An open window</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Empty it completely. Check every pocket.</li>      <li>Wipe the lining with a damp cloth.</li>      <li>Mix soda and oil, sprinkle inside.</li>      <li>Zip it and leave overnight.</li>      <li>Shake out the powder in the morning. Air it a few hours.</li>    </ol>    <div class="callout warn"><p>Never machine-wash a bag with a rigid base or padding. It warps.</p></div>    <details class="acc">      <summary>Prevention, since we're here</summary>      <div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Keep dirty clothes in a separate dry bag.</li><li>Leave the bag open between uses.</li><li>One dryer sheet at the bottom.</li></ul></div>    </details>  </div></div>	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Kill the Smell in Your Gym Bag</h3>    <div class="meta"><span>Active 10 min</span><span>Overnight dwell</span></div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <p class="muted">The smell lives in the lining and the seams, <regex>user_name>—not the outside.</p>    <ul class="ing">      <li>1 cup baking soda</li>      <li>10 drops tea tree or lavender oil</li>      <li>Damp cloth</li>      <li>An open window</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Empty it completely. Check every pocket.</li>      <li>Wipe the lining with a damp cloth.</li>      <li>Mix soda and oil, sprinkle inside.</li>      <li>Zip it and leave overnight.</li>      <li>Shake out the powder in the morning. Air it a few hours.</li>    </ol>    <div class="callout warn"><p>Never machine-wash a bag with a rigid base or padding. It warps.</p></div>    <details class="acc">      <summary>Prevention, since we're here</summary>      <div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Keep dirty clothes in a separate dry bag.</li><li>Leave the bag open between uses.</li><li>One dryer sheet at the bottom.</li></ul></div>    </details>  </div></div>	-
25.7.1	recipe_card/Remove Red Wine From a Carpet	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Remove Red Wine From a Carpet</h3>    <div class="meta"><span>Act fast</span><span>Blot, never rub</span></div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <div class="callout warn"><p>Speed matters more than technique, <regex>user_name</regex>. First two minutes win the war.</p></div>    <ul class="ing">      <li>Clean white towels or paper towels</li>      <li>1 tbsp dish soap</li>      <li>1 tbsp white vinegar</li>      <li>2 cups cold water</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Blot from the outside in. Stand on the towel if you must.</li>      <li>Mix soap, vinegar, and water.</li>      <li>Dab the solution on, blot again. Repeat.</li>      <li>Rinse with cold water, blot dry.</li>      <li>For old stains: hydrogen peroxide on a hidden spot first, then dab.</li>    </ol>    <details class="acc">      <summary>Already dried in?</summary>      <div class="body"><p>Soak the spot with the vinegar mix for 10 minutes, then blot. It may need two rounds. Old stains are stubborn but rarely permanent.</p></div>    </details>  </div></div>	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Remove Red Wine From a Carpet</h3>    <div class="meta"><span>Act fast</span><span>Blot, never rub</span></div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <div class="callout warn"><p>Speed matters more than technique, . First two minutes win the war.</p></div>    <ul class="ing">      <li>Clean white towels or paper towels</li>      <li>1 tbsp dish soap</li>      <li>1 tbsp white vinegar</li>      <li>2 cups cold water</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Blot from the outside in. Stand on the towel if you must.</li>      <li>Mix soap, vinegar, and water.</li>      <li>Dab the solution on, blot again. Repeat.</li>      <li>Rinse with cold water, blot dry.</li>      <li>For old stains: hydrogen peroxide on a hidden spot first, then dab.</li>    </ol>    <details class="acc">      <summary>Already dried in?</summary>      <div class="body"><p>Soak the spot with the vinegar mix for 10 minutes, then blot. It may need two rounds. Old stains are stubborn but rarely permanent.</p></div>    </details>  </div></div>	-
25.8.1	recipe_card/Stop Silverware From Tarnishing	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Stop Silverware From Tarnishing</h3>    <div class="meta"><span>Setup 5 min</span><span>No polish needed</span></div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <p class="muted">Tarnish is a sulfur reaction. Starve it of air and sulfur, <regex>user_name</regex>, and it slows to a crawl.</p>    <ul class="ing">      <li>Anti-tarnish cloth or strips</li>      <li>Small zip bags</li>      <li>Aluminum foil (for rescue)</li>      <li>Baking soda</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Wash and dry silver fully. Moisture ruins the plan.</li>      <li>Wrap or store in anti-tarnish cloth inside bags.</li>      <li>Keep pieces from touching each other.</li>      <li>Rescue an already-dark piece: line a bowl with foil, add soda and hot water, soak for a minute.</li>      <li>Dry, buff, and breathe.</li>    </ol>    <details class="acc">      <summary>What about a pearl or gemstone?</summary>      <div class="body"><p>Skip the foil bath. It can harm them. Use polish and a soft cloth only.</p></div>    </details>  </div></div>	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Stop Silverware From Tarnishing</h3>    <div class="meta"><span>Setup 5 min</span><span>No polish needed</span></div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <p class="muted">Tarnish is a sulfur reaction. Starve it of air and sulfur, , and it slows to a crawl.</p>    <ul class="ing">      <li>Anti-tarnish cloth or strips</li>      <li>Small zip bags</li>      <li>Aluminum foil (for rescue)</li>      <li>Baking soda</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Wash and dry silver fully. Moisture ruins the plan.</li>      <li>Wrap or store in anti-tarnish cloth inside bags.</li>      <li>Keep pieces from touching each other.</li>      <li>Rescue an already-dark piece: line a bowl with foil, add soda and hot water, soak for a minute.</li>      <li>Dry, buff, and breathe.</li>    </ol>    <details class="acc">      <summary>What about a pearl or gemstone?</summary>      <div class="body"><p>Skip the foil bath. It can harm them. Use polish and a soft cloth only.</p></div>    </details>  </div></div>	-
25.9.1	recipe_card/Fix a Chipped Nail Without a Salon	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Fix a Chipped Nail Without a Salon</h3>    <div class="meta"><span>2 minutes</span><span>No filing anxiety</span></div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <p class="muted">Real talk, <regex>user_name</regex>: you can disguise this. Full regrowth is the long game.</p>    <ul class="ing">      <li>Nail file</li>      <li>Clear polish or a nail repair gel</li>      <li>Tea bag (yes, really) for a split</li>      <li>Toothpick</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>File the chip smooth. Don't chase the whole nail.</li>      <li>If it's a split, cut a small square of tea bag paper.</li>      <li>Seal the paper over the split with clear polish.</li>      <li>File flush once dry.</li>      <li>Top with a coat of polish to camouflage the edge.</li>    </ol>    <div class="callout good"><p>Files before polish. Never peel. Peeling takes layers of nail with it.</p></div>  </div></div>	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Fix a Chipped Nail Without a Salon</h3>    <div class="meta"><span>2 minutes</span><span>No filing anxiety</span></div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <p class="muted">Real talk, : you can disguise this. Full regrowth is the long game.</p>    <ul class="ing">      <li>Nail file</li>      <li>Clear polish or a nail repair gel</li>      <li>Tea bag (yes, really) for a split</li>      <li>Toothpick</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>File the chip smooth. Don't chase the whole nail.</li>      <li>If it's a split, cut a small square of tea bag paper.</li>      <li>Seal the paper over the split with clear polish.</li>      <li>File flush once dry.</li>      <li>Top with a coat of polish to camouflage the edge.</li>    </ol>    <div class="callout good"><p>Files before polish. Never peel. Peeling takes layers of nail with it.</p></div>  </div></div>	-
25.10.1	recipe_card/Get Sand Off Your Feet Before the Car	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Get Sand Off Your Feet Before the Car</h3>    <div class="meta"><span>1 minute</span><span>Works at any beach</span></div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <p class="muted">Water makes it worse. Dry powder breaks the dampness grip, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p>    <ul class="ing">      <li>Cornstarch or baby powder, small bottle</li>      <li>Clean towel</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Dust feet with powder before you brush.</li>      <li>Rub the towel over each foot in a downward sweep.</li>      <li>Powder between the toes too. That's where the real hoard lives.</li>      <li>Give shoes a shake and go.</li>    </ol>    <div class="callout info"><p>No powder? Sand as fine as flour can work. Dry, not wet. Real powder is still better.</p></div>  </div></div>	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Get Sand Off Your Feet Before the Car</h3>    <div class="meta"><span>1 minute</span><span>Works at any beach</span></div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <p class="muted">Water makes it worse. Dry powder breaks the dampness grip, .</p>    <ul class="ing">      <li>Cornstarch or baby powder, small bottle</li>      <li>Clean towel</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Dust feet with powder before you brush.</li>      <li>Rub the towel over each foot in a downward sweep.</li>      <li>Powder between the toes too. That's where the real hoard lives.</li>      <li>Give shoes a shake and go.</li>    </ol>    <div class="callout info"><p>No powder? Sand as fine as flour can work. Dry, not wet. Real powder is still better.</p></div>  </div></div>	-
25.11.1	recipe_card/Descale a Kettle With Vinegar	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Descale a Kettle With Vinegar</h3>    <div class="meta"><span>20 min</span><span>One kettle</span><span>Smells like pickles, briefly</span></div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <p class="muted">That chalky crust is limescale, and acid eats it for breakfast. <regex>user_name</regex>, white vinegar is enough.</p>    <ul class="ing">      <li>1 part white vinegar</li>      <li>2 parts cold water</li>      <li>Enough to cover the scale line</li>      <li>A lemon half (optional, for the smell)</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Fill the kettle so the vinegar mix sits above the crusty band.</li>      <li>Boil, then switch off and let it stand 15 minutes.</li>      <li>Pour it out and look inside. Still rough? Repeat once.</li>      <li>Boil plain water twice and tip it away to rinse the taste.</li>      <li>Wipe the inside dry. Dry kettle, slow scale.</li>    </ol>    <details class="acc">      <summary>If your kettle has a hidden element</summary>      <div class="body">        <p>Only fill just above the element and never boil it dry. The vinegar does the work at a simmer too, it just takes longer.</p>      </div>    </details>    <div class="callout warn"><p>Skip this for aluminium kettles. Vinegar pits the metal. Use citric acid instead.</p></div>  </div></div>	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Descale a Kettle With Vinegar</h3>    <div class="meta"><span>20 min</span><span>One kettle</span><span>Smells like pickles, briefly</span></div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <p class="muted">That chalky crust is limescale, and acid eats it for breakfast. , white vinegar is enough.</p>    <ul class="ing">      <li>1 part white vinegar</li>      <li>2 parts cold water</li>      <li>Enough to cover the scale line</li>      <li>A lemon half (optional, for the smell)</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Fill the kettle so the vinegar mix sits above the crusty band.</li>      <li>Boil, then switch off and let it stand 15 minutes.</li>      <li>Pour it out and look inside. Still rough? Repeat once.</li>      <li>Boil plain water twice and tip it away to rinse the taste.</li>      <li>Wipe the inside dry. Dry kettle, slow scale.</li>    </ol>    <details class="acc">      <summary>If your kettle has a hidden element</summary>      <div class="body">        <p>Only fill just above the element and never boil it dry. The vinegar does the work at a simmer too, it just takes longer.</p>      </div>    </details>    <div class="callout warn"><p>Skip this for aluminium kettles. Vinegar pits the metal. Use citric acid instead.</p></div>  </div></div>	-
25.12.1	recipe_card/Revive a Dull Chef's Knife With a Mug	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Revive a Dull Chef's Knife With a Mug</h3>    <div class="meta"><span>5 min</span><span>1 knife</span><span>Emergency-grade</span></div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <p class="muted">Not a true sharpening, but <regex>user_name</regex>, it will get you through dinner. The rough ring on the bottom of a ceramic mug is a decent hone.</p>    <ul class="ing">      <li>1 ceramic mug, unglazed ring underneath</li>      <li>1 dull knife</li>      <li>A towel to stop the mug sliding</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Flip the mug, ring side up, on the towel.</li>      <li>Hold the blade at about 20 degrees, edge trailing.</li>      <li>Draw it across the ring, heel to tip, 10 times per side.</li>      <li>Feel for a burr with your thumb. Light drag means it worked.</li>      <li>Wipe clean and test on a tomato. If it slides, keep your knife money handy.</li>    </ol>    <details class="acc">      <summary>Honing or sharpening?</summary>      <div class="body">        <p>Honing realigns the edge. Sharpening removes metal. This trick is 90% honing, which is why it fades in a week.</p>      </div>    </details>    <div class="callout bad"><p>Serrated knives stay out of this. You will just round off the teeth.</p></div>  </div></div>	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Revive a Dull Chef's Knife With a Mug</h3>    <div class="meta"><span>5 min</span><span>1 knife</span><span>Emergency-grade</span></div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <p class="muted">Not a true sharpening, but , it will get you through dinner. The rough ring on the bottom of a ceramic mug is a decent hone.</p>    <ul class="ing">      <li>1 ceramic mug, unglazed ring underneath</li>      <li>1 dull knife</li>      <li>A towel to stop the mug sliding</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Flip the mug, ring side up, on the towel.</li>      <li>Hold the blade at about 20 degrees, edge trailing.</li>      <li>Draw it across the ring, heel to tip, 10 times per side.</li>      <li>Feel for a burr with your thumb. Light drag means it worked.</li>      <li>Wipe clean and test on a tomato. If it slides, keep your knife money handy.</li>    </ol>    <details class="acc">      <summary>Honing or sharpening?</summary>      <div class="body">        <p>Honing realigns the edge. Sharpening removes metal. This trick is 90% honing, which is why it fades in a week.</p>      </div>    </details>    <div class="callout bad"><p>Serrated knives stay out of this. You will just round off the teeth.</p></div>  </div></div>	-
25.13.1	recipe_card/Get Sharpie Off a Whiteboard	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Get Sharpie Off a Whiteboard</h3>    <div class="meta"><span>2 min</span><span>One board</span><span>Chemistry, gently</span></div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <p class="muted">Permanent ink isn't permanent on a glossy surface. <regex>user_name</regex>, you just need a solvent that's safe for the coating.</p>    <ul class="ing">      <li>Dry-erase marker (yes, really)</li>      <li>Rubbing alcohol or hand sanitiser</li>      <li>Soft cloth or paper towel</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>First trick: scribble over the Sharpie with a dry-erase marker, then wipe. The fresh ink lifts the old.</li>      <li>Still there? Dampen the cloth with alcohol.</li>      <li>Press, hold a few seconds, then wipe in one direction.</li>      <li>Repeat on stubborn corners with a fresh corner of cloth.</li>      <li>Dry fully before writing again, or the next note smears.</li>    </ol>    <details class="acc">      <summary>Ghost lines that never leave</summary>      <div class="body">        <p>Those are ink soaked into the pores. A little whiteboard cleaner plus a microfiber cloth, worked in circles, usually clears them.</p>      </div>    </details>    <div class="callout info"><p>Test any solvent on a corner first. Some cheap boards have a coating that alcohol dulls.</p></div>  </div></div>	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Get Sharpie Off a Whiteboard</h3>    <div class="meta"><span>2 min</span><span>One board</span><span>Chemistry, gently</span></div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <p class="muted">Permanent ink isn't permanent on a glossy surface. , you just need a solvent that's safe for the coating.</p>    <ul class="ing">      <li>Dry-erase marker (yes, really)</li>      <li>Rubbing alcohol or hand sanitiser</li>      <li>Soft cloth or paper towel</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>First trick: scribble over the Sharpie with a dry-erase marker, then wipe. The fresh ink lifts the old.</li>      <li>Still there? Dampen the cloth with alcohol.</li>      <li>Press, hold a few seconds, then wipe in one direction.</li>      <li>Repeat on stubborn corners with a fresh corner of cloth.</li>      <li>Dry fully before writing again, or the next note smears.</li>    </ol>    <details class="acc">      <summary>Ghost lines that never leave</summary>      <div class="body">        <p>Those are ink soaked into the pores. A little whiteboard cleaner plus a microfiber cloth, worked in circles, usually clears them.</p>      </div>    </details>    <div class="callout info"><p>Test any solvent on a corner first. Some cheap boards have a coating that alcohol dulls.</p></div>  </div></div>	-
25.14.1	recipe_card/Stop Glasses Fogging Up	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Stop Glasses Fogging Up</h3>    <div class="meta"><span>1 min</span><span>One pair</span><span>Free fixes first</span></div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <p class="muted">Fog is warm damp air hitting cold glass. Break that meeting, <regex>user_name</regex>, and your lenses stay clear.</p>    <ul class="ing">      <li>Soap or shaving foam (a drop)</li>      <li>Soft lens cloth</li>      <li>Adjustable nose bridge on your mask</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Pinch the mask's top edge snug against your nose. Most fog is a gap problem.</li>      <li>Pull your glasses slightly forward so they sit over the mask edge, not under.</li>      <li>For real fogging: rub a tiny drop of plain soap on both sides of the lens.</li>      <li>Buff it off with the cloth. Don't rinse. The thin film is the point.</li>      <li>Redo every few hours or after cleaning.</li>    </ol>    <details class="acc">      <summary>Does it damage coatings?</summary>      <div class="body">        <p>Plain, unscented soap is fine on most anti-reflective coatings. Skip anything with grit, moisturiser or lemon.</p>      </div>    </details>    <div class="callout good"><p>Cooking? Breathe downward, out and away from the pan. Unglamorous, but it works.</p></div>  </div></div>	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Stop Glasses Fogging Up</h3>    <div class="meta"><span>1 min</span><span>One pair</span><span>Free fixes first</span></div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <p class="muted">Fog is warm damp air hitting cold glass. Break that meeting, , and your lenses stay clear.</p>    <ul class="ing">      <li>Soap or shaving foam (a drop)</li>      <li>Soft lens cloth</li>      <li>Adjustable nose bridge on your mask</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Pinch the mask's top edge snug against your nose. Most fog is a gap problem.</li>      <li>Pull your glasses slightly forward so they sit over the mask edge, not under.</li>      <li>For real fogging: rub a tiny drop of plain soap on both sides of the lens.</li>      <li>Buff it off with the cloth. Don't rinse. The thin film is the point.</li>      <li>Redo every few hours or after cleaning.</li>    </ol>    <details class="acc">      <summary>Does it damage coatings?</summary>      <div class="body">        <p>Plain, unscented soap is fine on most anti-reflective coatings. Skip anything with grit, moisturiser or lemon.</p>      </div>    </details>    <div class="callout good"><p>Cooking? Breathe downward, out and away from the pan. Unglamorous, but it works.</p></div>  </div></div>	-
25.15.1	recipe_card/Shine Patent Leather Without Polish	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Shine Patent Leather Without Polish</h3>    <div class="meta"><span>5 min</span><span>One pair</span><span>No wax, ever</span></div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <p class="muted">Patent leather is a plastic finish, so treat it like one. <regex>user_name</regex>, regular wax polish is the one thing that ruins it.</p>    <ul class="ing">      <li>Soft damp cloth</li>      <li>Mild soap</li>      <li>A dab of petroleum jelly or baby oil</li>      <li>Microfiber cloth</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Wipe off dust and grit with the damp cloth. Grit scratches the shine.</li>      <li>For scuffs, rub gently with a fingertip of petroleum jelly.</li>      <li>Let it sit 30 seconds, then buff with microfiber in small circles.</li>      <li>Cloudy whole shoe? One drop of baby oil on a cloth, worked evenly.</li>      <li>Store stuffed with tissue so it keeps its shape.</li>    </ol>    <details class="acc">      <summary>Is the shine gone for good?</summary>      <div class="body">        <p>If the finish has cracked into a web of fine lines, the plastic layer is failing. Nothing rubs that back. It's a refinish, not a wipe.</p>      </div>    </details>    <div class="callout bad"><p>Keep patent leather away from radiators and hot car boots. Heat makes it crack.</p></div>  </div></div>	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Shine Patent Leather Without Polish</h3>    <div class="meta"><span>5 min</span><span>One pair</span><span>No wax, ever</span></div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <p class="muted">Patent leather is a plastic finish, so treat it like one. , regular wax polish is the one thing that ruins it.</p>    <ul class="ing">      <li>Soft damp cloth</li>      <li>Mild soap</li>      <li>A dab of petroleum jelly or baby oil</li>      <li>Microfiber cloth</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Wipe off dust and grit with the damp cloth. Grit scratches the shine.</li>      <li>For scuffs, rub gently with a fingertip of petroleum jelly.</li>      <li>Let it sit 30 seconds, then buff with microfiber in small circles.</li>      <li>Cloudy whole shoe? One drop of baby oil on a cloth, worked evenly.</li>      <li>Store stuffed with tissue so it keeps its shape.</li>    </ol>    <details class="acc">      <summary>Is the shine gone for good?</summary>      <div class="body">        <p>If the finish has cracked into a web of fine lines, the plastic layer is failing. Nothing rubs that back. It's a refinish, not a wipe.</p>      </div>    </details>    <div class="callout bad"><p>Keep patent leather away from radiators and hot car boots. Heat makes it crack.</p></div>  </div></div>	-
25.16.1	recipe_card/Stop Ice Cubes Sticking Together	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Stop Ice Cubes Sticking Together</h3>    <div class="meta"><span>Prep 2 min</span><span>Per tray</span><span>Freezer sense</span></div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <p class="muted">Cubes fuse because the freezer thaws and refreezes them, fusing wet surfaces. <regex>user_name</regex>, the fix is drier, colder and calmer.</p>    <ul class="ing">      <li>Cold water (not warm)</li>      <li>Flexible silicone tray, or one with rounded wells</li>      <li>An airtight tub or bag</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Fill with cold water. Warm water holds more air and makes cloudy, brittle cubes.</li>      <li>Freeze flat and level, on a shelf not against the back wall.</li>      <li>Once solid, twist the tray to crack every cube loose.</li>      <li>Tip cubes straight into the airtight tub.</li>      <li>Keep the tub sealed. Less air moving means less fusing.</li>    </ol>    <details class="acc">      <summary>Already a solid block?</summary>      <div class="body">        <p>Warm the tray's underside for 20 seconds under the tap, or wrap a warm damp cloth around it. They'll surrender.</p>      </div>    </details>    <div class="callout info"><p>A full freezer runs steadier than an empty one. Pack the gaps with the ice you already made.</p></div>  </div></div>	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Stop Ice Cubes Sticking Together</h3>    <div class="meta"><span>Prep 2 min</span><span>Per tray</span><span>Freezer sense</span></div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <p class="muted">Cubes fuse because the freezer thaws and refreezes them, fusing wet surfaces. , the fix is drier, colder and calmer.</p>    <ul class="ing">      <li>Cold water (not warm)</li>      <li>Flexible silicone tray, or one with rounded wells</li>      <li>An airtight tub or bag</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Fill with cold water. Warm water holds more air and makes cloudy, brittle cubes.</li>      <li>Freeze flat and level, on a shelf not against the back wall.</li>      <li>Once solid, twist the tray to crack every cube loose.</li>      <li>Tip cubes straight into the airtight tub.</li>      <li>Keep the tub sealed. Less air moving means less fusing.</li>    </ol>    <details class="acc">      <summary>Already a solid block?</summary>      <div class="body">        <p>Warm the tray's underside for 20 seconds under the tap, or wrap a warm damp cloth around it. They'll surrender.</p>      </div>    </details>    <div class="callout info"><p>A full freezer runs steadier than an empty one. Pack the gaps with the ice you already made.</p></div>  </div></div>	-
25.17.1	recipe_card/Get Chewing Gum Off Fabric Without Losing Your Mind	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Get Chewing Gum Off Fabric</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 15 min</span><span>Difficulty easy</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="t-small muted">Cold first, solvent second. Heat is the enemy here, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="ing"><li>Ice cube or frozen peas</li><li>Plastic bag</li><li>Butter knife or old credit card</li><li>Rubbing alcohol or vinegar</li><li>Cotton ball</li><li>Dish soap</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Put ice in a bag and hold it on the gum 5 minutes. It will go hard and brittle.</li><li>Scrape with the knife at a shallow angle. Most of it pops off in one piece.</li><li>Dab the leftover smear with alcohol on a cotton ball. Work from the outside in so you don't spread the ring.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>If the gum is already melted in</summary><div class="body"><p>Scrape off what you can, then rub dish soap into the spot and wash on the hottest cycle the fabric tag allows. Check before drying, always.</p></div></details><div class="callout warn"><p>Never iron or tumble dry until you're sure it's gone. Heat welds gum into the weave.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Get Chewing Gum Off Fabric</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 15 min</span><span>Difficulty easy</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="t-small muted">Cold first, solvent second. Heat is the enemy here, .</p><ul class="ing"><li>Ice cube or frozen peas</li><li>Plastic bag</li><li>Butter knife or old credit card</li><li>Rubbing alcohol or vinegar</li><li>Cotton ball</li><li>Dish soap</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Put ice in a bag and hold it on the gum 5 minutes. It will go hard and brittle.</li><li>Scrape with the knife at a shallow angle. Most of it pops off in one piece.</li><li>Dab the leftover smear with alcohol on a cotton ball. Work from the outside in so you don't spread the ring.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>If the gum is already melted in</summary><div class="body"><p>Scrape off what you can, then rub dish soap into the spot and wash on the hottest cycle the fabric tag allows. Check before drying, always.</p></div></details><div class="callout warn"><p>Never iron or tumble dry until you're sure it's gone. Heat welds gum into the weave.</p></div></div></div>	-
25.18.1	recipe_card/Stop a Door From Slamming in the Wind	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Stop a Door From Slamming</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 10 min</span><span>Cost under $10</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="t-small muted">A slamming door is usually one of three things. Check in this order, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="ing"><li>Screwdriver</li><li>Felt pads or a foam wedge</li><li>Small bowl of water (for the hinge test)</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Open the door halfway. Does it drift on its own? The hinges are out of plumb and gravity is doing the slamming.</li><li>Tighten all three hinge screws. If they spin freely, poke a wooden matchstick into the hole and re-drive the screw.</li><li>Stick a felt pad on the door edge where it meets the frame. It cushions the last inch of travel.</li><li>For draft-driven slams, fit a foam wedge or an automatic door bottom so the door seats snugly.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Quick test for a draft</summary><div class="body"><p>Hold a lit candle an inch from the closed door's edges on a windy day. Flicker means air is getting through.</p></div></details><div class="callout good"><p class="t-small">Felt pads are the two-minute fix. Everything else is the real fix.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Stop a Door From Slamming</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 10 min</span><span>Cost under $10</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="t-small muted">A slamming door is usually one of three things. Check in this order, .</p><ul class="ing"><li>Screwdriver</li><li>Felt pads or a foam wedge</li><li>Small bowl of water (for the hinge test)</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Open the door halfway. Does it drift on its own? The hinges are out of plumb and gravity is doing the slamming.</li><li>Tighten all three hinge screws. If they spin freely, poke a wooden matchstick into the hole and re-drive the screw.</li><li>Stick a felt pad on the door edge where it meets the frame. It cushions the last inch of travel.</li><li>For draft-driven slams, fit a foam wedge or an automatic door bottom so the door seats snugly.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Quick test for a draft</summary><div class="body"><p>Hold a lit candle an inch from the closed door's edges on a windy day. Flicker means air is getting through.</p></div></details><div class="callout good"><p class="t-small">Felt pads are the two-minute fix. Everything else is the real fix.</p></div></div></div>	-
25.19.1	recipe_card/Make Crispy Roast Chickpeas That Stay Crispy	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Crispy Roast Chickpeas</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 5 min</span><span>Oven 40 min</span><span>Serves 4</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>1 can chickpeas (400g), drained</li><li>1 tbsp olive oil</li><li>1 tsp smoked paprika</li><li>1/2 tsp garlic powder</li><li>Salt, added after baking</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Heat oven to 200&deg;C / 400&deg;F.</li><li>Dry the chickpeas hard with a tea towel. Rub off the loose skins. Wet chickpeas steam, they don't crisp.</li><li>Toss with oil and spices. Spread in a single layer on a lined tray, no crowding.</li><li>Roast 35&ndash;40 minutes, shaking the tray at 20. They should rattle.</li><li>Salt the second they leave the oven, then cool on the tray. They crisp as they cool.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Why mine went chewy</summary><div class="body"><p>They were either still damp, packed too close, or sealed in a container while warm. All three let steam back in.</p></div></details></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Crispy Roast Chickpeas</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 5 min</span><span>Oven 40 min</span><span>Serves 4</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>1 can chickpeas (400g), drained</li><li>1 tbsp olive oil</li><li>1 tsp smoked paprika</li><li>1/2 tsp garlic powder</li><li>Salt, added after baking</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Heat oven to 200&deg;C / 400&deg;F.</li><li>Dry the chickpeas hard with a tea towel. Rub off the loose skins. Wet chickpeas steam, they don't crisp.</li><li>Toss with oil and spices. Spread in a single layer on a lined tray, no crowding.</li><li>Roast 35&ndash;40 minutes, shaking the tray at 20. They should rattle.</li><li>Salt the second they leave the oven, then cool on the tray. They crisp as they cool.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Why mine went chewy</summary><div class="body"><p>They were either still damp, packed too close, or sealed in a container while warm. All three let steam back in.</p></div></details></div></div>	-
25.20.1	recipe_card/Take a Passport Photo at Home That Actually Gets Accepted	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>A Passport Photo That Passes</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 20 min</span><span>Cost $0&ndash;$15</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="t-small muted">Officials reject photos for boring reasons. Beat them, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="ing"><li>Plain white or off-white wall</li><li>Phone on a tripod or stack of books</li><li>Tape measure</li><li>Daylight from a window, facing you</li><li>Dark, plain top</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Stand 1&ndash;2 m from the wall so you cast no shadow on it.</li><li>Face the window. No flash, no overhead lights, no filters.</li><li>Shoot at head height, phone level with your eyes, not tilted.</li><li>Neutral expression, mouth closed, eyes open, no glasses glare.</li><li>Measure: head should fill 70&ndash;80% of the frame, top of head to chin.</li><li>Print on matte photo paper at the size your country specifies.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Auto-reject list</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Shadows behind the head</li><li>Smiling or raised eyebrows</li><li>Patterned clothing or uniforms</li><li>Hair across the eyes</li><li>Any crop tighter than head-and-shoulders</li></ul></div></details><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Print two. Spares are worth more than a second trip.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>A Passport Photo That Passes</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 20 min</span><span>Cost $0&ndash;$15</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="t-small muted">Officials reject photos for boring reasons. Beat them, .</p><ul class="ing"><li>Plain white or off-white wall</li><li>Phone on a tripod or stack of books</li><li>Tape measure</li><li>Daylight from a window, facing you</li><li>Dark, plain top</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Stand 1&ndash;2 m from the wall so you cast no shadow on it.</li><li>Face the window. No flash, no overhead lights, no filters.</li><li>Shoot at head height, phone level with your eyes, not tilted.</li><li>Neutral expression, mouth closed, eyes open, no glasses glare.</li><li>Measure: head should fill 70&ndash;80% of the frame, top of head to chin.</li><li>Print on matte photo paper at the size your country specifies.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Auto-reject list</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Shadows behind the head</li><li>Smiling or raised eyebrows</li><li>Patterned clothing or uniforms</li><li>Hair across the eyes</li><li>Any crop tighter than head-and-shoulders</li></ul></div></details><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Print two. Spares are worth more than a second trip.</p></div></div></div>	-
25.21.1	recipe_card/Reset a Tripped Circuit Breaker Safely	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Reset a Tripped Breaker</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 10 min</span><span>Risk: medium</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>Torch or phone light</li><li>Dry hands, dry floor, rubber-soled shoes</li><li>Someone who knows where the panel is</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Unplug everything on the dead circuit. Note what's on it: kettle, heater, hair dryer, space heater all pull big loads.</li><li>Find the panel. Look for the switch sitting between ON and OFF, or clearly flipped.</li><li>Push it fully to OFF, then firmly to ON. One clean click.</li><li>If it holds, plug things back in one at a time. The guilty appliance will trip it again.</li><li>If it trips with nothing plugged in, stop and call an electrician. That's a wiring fault, not a load problem.</li></ol><div class="callout bad"><p>Never tape a breaker on, never swap in a bigger fuse. Both start fires.</p></div><details class="acc"><summary>Why it only trips at night</summary><div class="body"><p>Night is when heaters, dryers and kettles overlap. Count the wattage on one circuit before blaming the wiring.</p></div></details></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Reset a Tripped Breaker</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 10 min</span><span>Risk: medium</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>Torch or phone light</li><li>Dry hands, dry floor, rubber-soled shoes</li><li>Someone who knows where the panel is</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Unplug everything on the dead circuit. Note what's on it: kettle, heater, hair dryer, space heater all pull big loads.</li><li>Find the panel. Look for the switch sitting between ON and OFF, or clearly flipped.</li><li>Push it fully to OFF, then firmly to ON. One clean click.</li><li>If it holds, plug things back in one at a time. The guilty appliance will trip it again.</li><li>If it trips with nothing plugged in, stop and call an electrician. That's a wiring fault, not a load problem.</li></ol><div class="callout bad"><p>Never tape a breaker on, never swap in a bigger fuse. Both start fires.</p></div><details class="acc"><summary>Why it only trips at night</summary><div class="body"><p>Night is when heaters, dryers and kettles overlap. Count the wattage on one circuit before blaming the wiring.</p></div></details></div></div>	-
25.22.1	recipe_card/Get Pet Hair Off the Couch Without a Lint Roller	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Pet Hair Off the Couch</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 5 min</span><span>No lint roller needed</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="t-small muted">Fur clings by static and weave. Break both, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="ing"><li>Rubber gloves or a damp sponge</li><li>Squeegee, or the flat side of a spatula</li><li>Spray bottle with water</li><li>Dryer sheet (optional)</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Put on rubber gloves and wipe the couch in one direction. The rubber grabs fur in clumps.</li><li>For deep weave, drag the squeegee across the fabric in straight passes. Peel the roll off after each pass.</li><li>Mist lightly with water and wipe again. Damp fabric releases hair far easier than dry.</li><li>Finish with a dryer sheet, which kills the static that pulls new hair back on.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Fabric cheat sheet</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li><b>Microfibre:</b> rubber glove only, no water</li><li><b>Woven cloth:</b> squeegee works best</li><li><b>Velvet or chenille:</b> damp sponge, light pressure, one direction</li></ul></div></details><div class="callout good"><p class="t-small">Two minutes weekly beats a deep clean monthly. Your couch agrees.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Pet Hair Off the Couch</h3><div class="meta"><span>Time 5 min</span><span>No lint roller needed</span></div></div><div class="body"><p class="t-small muted">Fur clings by static and weave. Break both, .</p><ul class="ing"><li>Rubber gloves or a damp sponge</li><li>Squeegee, or the flat side of a spatula</li><li>Spray bottle with water</li><li>Dryer sheet (optional)</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Put on rubber gloves and wipe the couch in one direction. The rubber grabs fur in clumps.</li><li>For deep weave, drag the squeegee across the fabric in straight passes. Peel the roll off after each pass.</li><li>Mist lightly with water and wipe again. Damp fabric releases hair far easier than dry.</li><li>Finish with a dryer sheet, which kills the static that pulls new hair back on.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Fabric cheat sheet</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li><b>Microfibre:</b> rubber glove only, no water</li><li><b>Woven cloth:</b> squeegee works best</li><li><b>Velvet or chenille:</b> damp sponge, light pressure, one direction</li></ul></div></details><div class="callout good"><p class="t-small">Two minutes weekly beats a deep clean monthly. Your couch agrees.</p></div></div></div>	-
25.23.1	recipe_card/Remove a Stripped Screw Without a Drill	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Remove a Stripped Screw Without a Drill</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 5 min</span><span>You need: a rubber band, maybe a screwdriver</span></div></div><div class="body"><p>Stripped screw? Breathe. You can get it out with things you already own.</p><ul class="ing"><li>1 wide rubber band</li><li>1 screwdriver that fits the screw head</li><li>Optional: a tiny bit of valve grinding compound or toothpaste</li><li>Optional: a hammer and a nail (for tough ones)</li></ul><ol class="list"><li><strong>Try the rubber band trick.</strong> Place a wide rubber band flat over the screw head. Press the screwdriver into the rubber band and turn slowly. The rubber fills the stripped gaps and gives grip.</li><li><strong>Add grit.</strong> A dab of toothpaste or valve grinding compound on the screwdriver tip increases friction. Wipe clean after.</li><li><strong>Tap it.</strong> Give the screwdriver a few firm taps with a hammer while turning. Vibration can break the grip of rust or paint.</li><li><strong>If it still won't budge,</strong> use a rotary tool to cut a slot in the head and turn it with a flathead. Last resort: drill it out.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>Rubber band trick works for most mildly stripped screws. For really stuck ones, a screw extractor kit is worth the few dollars.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Remove a Stripped Screw Without a Drill</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 5 min</span><span>You need: a rubber band, maybe a screwdriver</span></div></div><div class="body"><p>Stripped screw? Breathe. You can get it out with things you already own.</p><ul class="ing"><li>1 wide rubber band</li><li>1 screwdriver that fits the screw head</li><li>Optional: a tiny bit of valve grinding compound or toothpaste</li><li>Optional: a hammer and a nail (for tough ones)</li></ul><ol class="list"><li><strong>Try the rubber band trick.</strong> Place a wide rubber band flat over the screw head. Press the screwdriver into the rubber band and turn slowly. The rubber fills the stripped gaps and gives grip.</li><li><strong>Add grit.</strong> A dab of toothpaste or valve grinding compound on the screwdriver tip increases friction. Wipe clean after.</li><li><strong>Tap it.</strong> Give the screwdriver a few firm taps with a hammer while turning. Vibration can break the grip of rust or paint.</li><li><strong>If it still won't budge,</strong> use a rotary tool to cut a slot in the head and turn it with a flathead. Last resort: drill it out.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>Rubber band trick works for most mildly stripped screws. For really stuck ones, a screw extractor kit is worth the few dollars.</p></div></div></div>	-
25.24.1	recipe_card/Get Rid of Ants in the Kitchen (Without Fogging the Whole Ho	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Get Rid of Ants in the Kitchen (Without Fogging the Whole House)</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 2 min</span><span>You need: vinegar, soap, patience</span></div></div><div class="body"><p>Ants follow invisible scent trails. Break the trail and they'll wander off.</p><ul class="ing"><li>1 cup white vinegar</li><li>1 cup water</li><li>A few drops of dish soap</li><li>Spray bottle</li><li>Optional: borax and sugar for a bait</li></ul><ol class="list"><li><strong>Wipe down surfaces.</strong> Mix vinegar and water in a spray bottle. Spray and wipe counters, floors, and any place you've seen ants. This destroys the pheromone trail.</li><li><strong>Find the entry point.</strong> Look for a crack or gap where they're coming in. Seal it with caulk or petroleum jelly.</li><li><strong>Set a bait if needed.</strong> Mix 1 part borax to 3 parts sugar, add a little water to make a paste. Put it in a shallow dish near the trail. Ants take it back to the nest. Keep pets and kids away.</li><li><strong>Wait a few days.</strong> The bait works slowly. Don't spray the ants you see feeding—they need to carry it home.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p>Don't kill the ants you see at the bait. They're the delivery service. Let them work.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Get Rid of Ants in the Kitchen (Without Fogging the Whole House)</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 2 min</span><span>You need: vinegar, soap, patience</span></div></div><div class="body"><p>Ants follow invisible scent trails. Break the trail and they'll wander off.</p><ul class="ing"><li>1 cup white vinegar</li><li>1 cup water</li><li>A few drops of dish soap</li><li>Spray bottle</li><li>Optional: borax and sugar for a bait</li></ul><ol class="list"><li><strong>Wipe down surfaces.</strong> Mix vinegar and water in a spray bottle. Spray and wipe counters, floors, and any place you've seen ants. This destroys the pheromone trail.</li><li><strong>Find the entry point.</strong> Look for a crack or gap where they're coming in. Seal it with caulk or petroleum jelly.</li><li><strong>Set a bait if needed.</strong> Mix 1 part borax to 3 parts sugar, add a little water to make a paste. Put it in a shallow dish near the trail. Ants take it back to the nest. Keep pets and kids away.</li><li><strong>Wait a few days.</strong> The bait works slowly. Don't spray the ants you see feeding—they need to carry it home.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p>Don't kill the ants you see at the bait. They're the delivery service. Let them work.</p></div></div></div>	-
25.25.1	recipe_card/Open a Stuck Jar Lid (When Your Hands Are Slippery)	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Open a Stuck Jar Lid (When Your Hands Are Slippery)</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 1 min</span><span>You need: grip and a little physics</span></div></div><div class="body"><p>That lid is not stronger than you. It just needs a different approach.</p><ul class="ing"><li>1 rubber band or rubber glove</li><li>Hot water (for metal lids)</li><li>A spoon or butter knife (for prying)</li><li>Optional: a firm tap on the lid edge</li></ul><ol class="list"><li><strong>Break the seal.</strong> Tap the edge of the lid firmly with the handle of a butter knife. You'll often hear a small pop as the vacuum breaks.</li><li><strong>Use heat.</strong> Run hot water over a metal lid for 30 seconds. Metal expands faster than glass, loosening the grip.</li><li><strong>Get a grip.</strong> Put on a rubber glove or wrap a rubber band around the lid. Twist with your whole hand, not just fingers.</li><li><strong>Try the spoon trick.</strong> Slide the tip of a spoon under the lid edge and gently pry upward. You'll hear the seal release. Then twist open.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p>If it's a vacuum-sealed lid, the tap trick almost always works. No hot water needed.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Open a Stuck Jar Lid (When Your Hands Are Slippery)</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 1 min</span><span>You need: grip and a little physics</span></div></div><div class="body"><p>That lid is not stronger than you. It just needs a different approach.</p><ul class="ing"><li>1 rubber band or rubber glove</li><li>Hot water (for metal lids)</li><li>A spoon or butter knife (for prying)</li><li>Optional: a firm tap on the lid edge</li></ul><ol class="list"><li><strong>Break the seal.</strong> Tap the edge of the lid firmly with the handle of a butter knife. You'll often hear a small pop as the vacuum breaks.</li><li><strong>Use heat.</strong> Run hot water over a metal lid for 30 seconds. Metal expands faster than glass, loosening the grip.</li><li><strong>Get a grip.</strong> Put on a rubber glove or wrap a rubber band around the lid. Twist with your whole hand, not just fingers.</li><li><strong>Try the spoon trick.</strong> Slide the tip of a spoon under the lid edge and gently pry upward. You'll hear the seal release. Then twist open.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p>If it's a vacuum-sealed lid, the tap trick almost always works. No hot water needed.</p></div></div></div>	-
25.26.1	recipe_card/Unclog a Slow Drain With Baking Soda and Vinegar	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Unclog a Slow Drain With Baking Soda and Vinegar</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 5 min</span><span>You need: baking soda, vinegar, boiling water</span></div></div><div class="body"><p>Before you reach for harsh chemicals, try this. It's cheap, safe, and often enough.</p><ul class="ing"><li>1/2 cup baking soda</li><li>1/2 cup white vinegar</li><li>4 cups boiling water</li><li>Optional: a plunger</li></ul><ol class="list"><li><strong>Remove standing water.</strong> If there's water in the sink, bail it out or wait for it to drain slowly.</li><li><strong>Pour in baking soda.</strong> Follow with vinegar. It will fizz and bubble. Let it sit for 15–30 minutes.</li><li><strong>Flush with boiling water.</strong> Pour slowly, in stages. The hot water helps melt grease and push debris through.</li><li><strong>Repeat if needed.</strong> For stubborn clogs, do it twice. If it's still slow, use a plunger or a drain snake.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>This works best on grease and soap scum. For hair clogs, a drain snake is more effective.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Unclog a Slow Drain With Baking Soda and Vinegar</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 5 min</span><span>You need: baking soda, vinegar, boiling water</span></div></div><div class="body"><p>Before you reach for harsh chemicals, try this. It's cheap, safe, and often enough.</p><ul class="ing"><li>1/2 cup baking soda</li><li>1/2 cup white vinegar</li><li>4 cups boiling water</li><li>Optional: a plunger</li></ul><ol class="list"><li><strong>Remove standing water.</strong> If there's water in the sink, bail it out or wait for it to drain slowly.</li><li><strong>Pour in baking soda.</strong> Follow with vinegar. It will fizz and bubble. Let it sit for 15–30 minutes.</li><li><strong>Flush with boiling water.</strong> Pour slowly, in stages. The hot water helps melt grease and push debris through.</li><li><strong>Repeat if needed.</strong> For stubborn clogs, do it twice. If it's still slow, use a plunger or a drain snake.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>This works best on grease and soap scum. For hair clogs, a drain snake is more effective.</p></div></div></div>	-
25.27.1	recipe_card/Remove Permanent Marker From a Dry Erase Board	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Remove Permanent Marker From a Dry Erase Board</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 2 min</span><span>You need: a dry erase marker, maybe alcohol</span></div></div><div class="body"><p>Permanent marker on a whiteboard is a classic mistake. Here's how to undo it.</p><ul class="ing"><li>1 dry erase marker (any color)</li><li>Isopropyl alcohol (rubbing alcohol) or hand sanitizer</li><li>A soft cloth or paper towel</li></ul><ol class="list"><li><strong>Trace over the permanent marker.</strong> Use a dry erase marker to draw directly over the permanent lines. Let it sit for a few seconds.</li><li><strong>Wipe it away.</strong> The dry erase ink contains a solvent that reactivates the permanent ink. One wipe and it's gone.</li><li><strong>If any remains,</strong> dab a little rubbing alcohol on a cloth and wipe. Hand sanitizer works in a pinch.</li><li><strong>For tough stains,</strong> use a magic eraser (melamine foam) with a bit of alcohol. Gentle circles.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p>The dry erase marker trick works on most non-porous surfaces, including glass and laminated schedules.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Remove Permanent Marker From a Dry Erase Board</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 2 min</span><span>You need: a dry erase marker, maybe alcohol</span></div></div><div class="body"><p>Permanent marker on a whiteboard is a classic mistake. Here's how to undo it.</p><ul class="ing"><li>1 dry erase marker (any color)</li><li>Isopropyl alcohol (rubbing alcohol) or hand sanitizer</li><li>A soft cloth or paper towel</li></ul><ol class="list"><li><strong>Trace over the permanent marker.</strong> Use a dry erase marker to draw directly over the permanent lines. Let it sit for a few seconds.</li><li><strong>Wipe it away.</strong> The dry erase ink contains a solvent that reactivates the permanent ink. One wipe and it's gone.</li><li><strong>If any remains,</strong> dab a little rubbing alcohol on a cloth and wipe. Hand sanitizer works in a pinch.</li><li><strong>For tough stains,</strong> use a magic eraser (melamine foam) with a bit of alcohol. Gentle circles.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p>The dry erase marker trick works on most non-porous surfaces, including glass and laminated schedules.</p></div></div></div>	-
25.28.1	recipe_card/Beeswax Food Wraps	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Beeswax Food Wraps</h3>    <div class="meta">      <span>Prep 15 min</span>      <span>Makes 3 wraps</span>    </div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <ul class="ing">      <li>3 squares cotton fabric (8x8 in)</li>      <li>1/2 cup beeswax pellets</li>      <li>2 tbsp jojoba oil (optional)</li>      <li>Parchment paper</li>      <li>Iron or oven</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Preheat oven to 200°F (93°C) or set iron to low.</li>      <li>Place fabric on a parchment-lined baking sheet.</li>      <li>Sprinkle beeswax evenly over fabric.</li>      <li>Bake 5–10 min until wax melts, or iron between parchment sheets.</li>      <li>Lift fabric with tongs; let cool 1 min.</li>      <li>Hang to dry completely before using.</li>    </ol>  </div></div>	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Beeswax Food Wraps</h3>    <div class="meta">      <span>Prep 15 min</span>      <span>Makes 3 wraps</span>    </div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <ul class="ing">      <li>3 squares cotton fabric (8x8 in)</li>      <li>1/2 cup beeswax pellets</li>      <li>2 tbsp jojoba oil (optional)</li>      <li>Parchment paper</li>      <li>Iron or oven</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Preheat oven to 200°F (93°C) or set iron to low.</li>      <li>Place fabric on a parchment-lined baking sheet.</li>      <li>Sprinkle beeswax evenly over fabric.</li>      <li>Bake 5–10 min until wax melts, or iron between parchment sheets.</li>      <li>Lift fabric with tongs; let cool 1 min.</li>      <li>Hang to dry completely before using.</li>    </ol>  </div></div>	-
25.29.1	recipe_card/Cardboard Phone Stand	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Cardboard Phone Stand</h3>    <div class="meta">      <span>Time 5 min</span>      <span>Upcycle</span>    </div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <ul class="ing">      <li>Sturdy cardboard (cereal box)</li>      <li>Scissors or craft knife</li>      <li>Ruler</li>      <li>Pen</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Cut a rectangle 4 in wide by 6 in tall.</li>      <li>Draw a U-shape notch at the top, 1/2 in deep, centered.</li>      <li>Fold the bottom 1 in forward to create a base.</li>      <li>Cut two triangular supports and tape to sides.</li>      <li>Slide phone into notch.</li>      <li>Adjust angle by bending base.</li>    </ol>  </div></div>	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Cardboard Phone Stand</h3>    <div class="meta">      <span>Time 5 min</span>      <span>Upcycle</span>    </div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <ul class="ing">      <li>Sturdy cardboard (cereal box)</li>      <li>Scissors or craft knife</li>      <li>Ruler</li>      <li>Pen</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Cut a rectangle 4 in wide by 6 in tall.</li>      <li>Draw a U-shape notch at the top, 1/2 in deep, centered.</li>      <li>Fold the bottom 1 in forward to create a base.</li>      <li>Cut two triangular supports and tape to sides.</li>      <li>Slide phone into notch.</li>      <li>Adjust angle by bending base.</li>    </ol>  </div></div>	-
25.30.1	recipe_card/Smoothie Without a Blender	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Smoothie Without a Blender</h3>    <div class="meta">      <span>Prep 10 min</span>      <span>Serves 1</span>    </div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <ul class="ing">      <li>1 ripe banana</li>      <li>1/2 cup frozen berries</li>      <li>1/2 cup yogurt or milk</li>      <li>1 tbsp honey (optional)</li>      <li>Fork and bowl</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Mash banana with fork until smooth.</li>      <li>Add berries; mash and stir vigorously.</li>      <li>Pour in yogurt or milk; mix well.</li>      <li>For smoother texture, push through a sieve.</li>      <li>Sweeten with honey if desired.</li>      <li>Serve cold.</li>    </ol>  </div></div>	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Smoothie Without a Blender</h3>    <div class="meta">      <span>Prep 10 min</span>      <span>Serves 1</span>    </div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <ul class="ing">      <li>1 ripe banana</li>      <li>1/2 cup frozen berries</li>      <li>1/2 cup yogurt or milk</li>      <li>1 tbsp honey (optional)</li>      <li>Fork and bowl</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Mash banana with fork until smooth.</li>      <li>Add berries; mash and stir vigorously.</li>      <li>Pour in yogurt or milk; mix well.</li>      <li>For smoother texture, push through a sieve.</li>      <li>Sweeten with honey if desired.</li>      <li>Serve cold.</li>    </ol>  </div></div>	-
25.31.1	recipe_card/Rope Rug	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Rope Rug</h3>    <div class="meta">      <span>Time 1 hr</span>      <span>Makes 1 rug</span>    </div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <ul class="ing">      <li>50 ft sisal rope (1/4 in)</li>      <li>Hot glue gun and sticks</li>      <li>Non-slip rug pad</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Cut rug pad to desired shape.</li>      <li>Coil rope tightly in a spiral, starting at center.</li>      <li>Glue each coil to the previous one as you go.</li>      <li>Continue until desired size.</li>      <li>Trim end and glue securely.</li>      <li>Press flat under a heavy book for 1 hour.</li>    </ol>  </div></div>	<div class="recipe">  <div class="head">    <h3>Rope Rug</h3>    <div class="meta">      <span>Time 1 hr</span>      <span>Makes 1 rug</span>    </div>  </div>  <div class="body">    <ul class="ing">      <li>50 ft sisal rope (1/4 in)</li>      <li>Hot glue gun and sticks</li>      <li>Non-slip rug pad</li>    </ul>    <ol class="list">      <li>Cut rug pad to desired shape.</li>      <li>Coil rope tightly in a spiral, starting at center.</li>      <li>Glue each coil to the previous one as you go.</li>      <li>Continue until desired size.</li>      <li>Trim end and glue securely.</li>      <li>Press flat under a heavy book for 1 hour.</li>    </ol>  </div></div>	-
25.32.1	recipe_card/Crispy Roast Chickpeas That Actually Stay Crispy	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Crispy Roast Chickpeas</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 10 min</span><span>Oven 40 min</span><span>Makes 2 cups</span></div></div><div class="body"><div class="callout info">The whole trick is dry beans and a hot oven. Wet beans steam. Steamed beans are sad.</div><ul class="ing"><li>2 cans chickpeas (15 oz each)</li><li>2 tbsp olive oil</li><li>1 tsp fine salt</li><li>1/2 tsp smoked paprika</li><li>1/4 tsp garlic powder</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Heat oven to <strong>400&deg;F / 205&deg;C</strong>. Rack in the middle.</li><li>Drain and rinse the chickpeas. Spread them on a clean towel and rub until they look matte. Loose skins can go in the bin.</li><li>Let them air-dry 10 minutes while the oven heats. This matters more than any spice.</li><li>Toss with oil and salt on a rimmed sheet pan. Spread into a single layer with space between them.</li><li>Roast 35&ndash;40 min, shaking the pan at 20 min. Pull them when they rattle and one cracks open easily.</li><li>Toss with paprika and garlic powder <em>after</em> roasting, while hot. Spices added early burn.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Keeping them crispy</summary><div class="body"><p>Cool completely on the pan, then store in a jar with the lid <strong>loosely</strong> on. A sealed jar traps steam and they go chewy by tomorrow. If they soften anyway, 5 minutes back in a 400&deg;F oven fixes them.</p></div></details></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Crispy Roast Chickpeas</h3><div class="meta"><span>Prep 10 min</span><span>Oven 40 min</span><span>Makes 2 cups</span></div></div><div class="body"><div class="callout info">The whole trick is dry beans and a hot oven. Wet beans steam. Steamed beans are sad.</div><ul class="ing"><li>2 cans chickpeas (15 oz each)</li><li>2 tbsp olive oil</li><li>1 tsp fine salt</li><li>1/2 tsp smoked paprika</li><li>1/4 tsp garlic powder</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Heat oven to <strong>400&deg;F / 205&deg;C</strong>. Rack in the middle.</li><li>Drain and rinse the chickpeas. Spread them on a clean towel and rub until they look matte. Loose skins can go in the bin.</li><li>Let them air-dry 10 minutes while the oven heats. This matters more than any spice.</li><li>Toss with oil and salt on a rimmed sheet pan. Spread into a single layer with space between them.</li><li>Roast 35&ndash;40 min, shaking the pan at 20 min. Pull them when they rattle and one cracks open easily.</li><li>Toss with paprika and garlic powder <em>after</em> roasting, while hot. Spices added early burn.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Keeping them crispy</summary><div class="body"><p>Cool completely on the pan, then store in a jar with the lid <strong>loosely</strong> on. A sealed jar traps steam and they go chewy by tomorrow. If they soften anyway, 5 minutes back in a 400&deg;F oven fixes them.</p></div></details></div></div>	-
25.33.1	recipe_card/Reset a Tripped Circuit Breaker Without Making It Worse	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Reset a Tripped Breaker</h3><div class="meta"><span>2 min</span><span>No tools needed</span><span>Read the safety note first</span></div></div><div class="body"><div class="callout bad">If you see scorch marks, smell burning, or the breaker is hot to the touch, stop and call an electrician. Do not reset it. Water plus electricity is also a call-someone situation.</div><ul class="ing"><li>A flashlight or your phone light</li><li>Dry hands and dry floor</li><li>One hand only, if you can manage it</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Find the panel. It is usually a grey metal box in a garage, hallway, or closet.</li><li>Open the door. Look for a switch that sits <strong>between</strong> ON and OFF, or one that is clearly flipped the other way from its neighbours.</li><li>Unplug whatever was running in the dead room. Space heaters and hair dryers are the usual suspects.</li><li>Push the breaker firmly to OFF first, then all the way to ON. You should feel a solid click.</li><li>If it holds, plug things back in one at a time. If it trips again on the same item, that item is the problem.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>It tripped again immediately</summary><div class="body"><p>That means the fault is still there. Leave the breaker OFF, unplug everything on that circuit, and try once more. Still tripping with nothing plugged in? The wiring itself needs a pro. Cheaper than a fire.</p></div></details></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Reset a Tripped Breaker</h3><div class="meta"><span>2 min</span><span>No tools needed</span><span>Read the safety note first</span></div></div><div class="body"><div class="callout bad">If you see scorch marks, smell burning, or the breaker is hot to the touch, stop and call an electrician. Do not reset it. Water plus electricity is also a call-someone situation.</div><ul class="ing"><li>A flashlight or your phone light</li><li>Dry hands and dry floor</li><li>One hand only, if you can manage it</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Find the panel. It is usually a grey metal box in a garage, hallway, or closet.</li><li>Open the door. Look for a switch that sits <strong>between</strong> ON and OFF, or one that is clearly flipped the other way from its neighbours.</li><li>Unplug whatever was running in the dead room. Space heaters and hair dryers are the usual suspects.</li><li>Push the breaker firmly to OFF first, then all the way to ON. You should feel a solid click.</li><li>If it holds, plug things back in one at a time. If it trips again on the same item, that item is the problem.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>It tripped again immediately</summary><div class="body"><p>That means the fault is still there. Leave the breaker OFF, unplug everything on that circuit, and try once more. Still tripping with nothing plugged in? The wiring itself needs a pro. Cheaper than a fire.</p></div></details></div></div>	-
25.34.1	recipe_card/Get Pet Hair Off the Couch (No Lint Roller Required)	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Pet Hair Off the Couch</h3><div class="meta"><span>5 min</span><span>Household items only</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>Rubber dish gloves (the bumpy kind)</li><li>Or a slightly damp sponge</li><li>Or a squeegee</li><li>Spray bottle with water</li><li>Dryer sheet (last resort)</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Mist the cushion <em>lightly</em>. Damp fabric releases hair; soaked fabric makes mud.</li><li>Put on the rubber glove and sweep your flat hand across the cushion in one direction. The hair rolls into a felt-like rope. Weirdly satisfying.</li><li>Collect the rope and bin it. Repeat in overlapping strips.</li><li>For corners and seams, push the glove edge in and drag outward.</li><li>Vacuum the loose bits last, not first. Vacuuming first just pushes hair deeper.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>If your couch is velvet or microfibre</summary><div class="body"><p>Skip the water. Use a dry squeegee in short strokes, then a dryer sheet rubbed lightly to cut static so the next batch doesn't cling. Test the sheet on a hidden spot first.</p></div></details></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Pet Hair Off the Couch</h3><div class="meta"><span>5 min</span><span>Household items only</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>Rubber dish gloves (the bumpy kind)</li><li>Or a slightly damp sponge</li><li>Or a squeegee</li><li>Spray bottle with water</li><li>Dryer sheet (last resort)</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Mist the cushion <em>lightly</em>. Damp fabric releases hair; soaked fabric makes mud.</li><li>Put on the rubber glove and sweep your flat hand across the cushion in one direction. The hair rolls into a felt-like rope. Weirdly satisfying.</li><li>Collect the rope and bin it. Repeat in overlapping strips.</li><li>For corners and seams, push the glove edge in and drag outward.</li><li>Vacuum the loose bits last, not first. Vacuuming first just pushes hair deeper.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>If your couch is velvet or microfibre</summary><div class="body"><p>Skip the water. Use a dry squeegee in short strokes, then a dryer sheet rubbed lightly to cut static so the next batch doesn't cling. Test the sheet on a hidden spot first.</p></div></details></div></div>	-
25.35.1	recipe_card/Take a Passport Photo at Home That Gets Accepted	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Passport Photo at Home</h3><div class="meta"><span>Setup 10 min</span><span>Free</span><span>Rules vary by country</span></div></div><div class="body"><div class="callout warn">Check your country's exact spec first. Sizes differ (US 2x2 in, UK 35x45 mm, and so on), and the photo is usually rejected for the little things, not the big ones.</div><ul class="ing"><li>Plain white or off-white wall</li><li>Bright, even light facing you</li><li>Phone on a steady surface</li><li>Plain dark shirt</li><li>A friend, or a timer</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Stand about 1 metre from the wall so you don't cast a shadow on it.</li><li>Face a window or lamp. No light behind you, no flash glare on your glasses.</li><li>Have the helper hold the phone at <strong>eye level</strong>, not chin level. Arm's length away.</li><li>Look straight at the lens. Neutral face, mouth closed, both eyes open and visible.</li><li>No glasses (many countries now ban them), no hats, no hair over your eyes or eyebrows.</li><li>Shoot several. Pick the one where your head fills most of the frame with a little headroom.</li><li>Crop to spec with a free passport-photo tool or your phone's editor, then print at a pharmacy or copy shop.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Common rejection reasons</summary><div class="body"><p>Shadows on the wall, a tilted head, smiling with teeth, a patterned background, glare on glasses, or a photo that's slightly too zoomed in. Fix the shadow and the tilt first. That's most of them.</p></div></details></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Passport Photo at Home</h3><div class="meta"><span>Setup 10 min</span><span>Free</span><span>Rules vary by country</span></div></div><div class="body"><div class="callout warn">Check your country's exact spec first. Sizes differ (US 2x2 in, UK 35x45 mm, and so on), and the photo is usually rejected for the little things, not the big ones.</div><ul class="ing"><li>Plain white or off-white wall</li><li>Bright, even light facing you</li><li>Phone on a steady surface</li><li>Plain dark shirt</li><li>A friend, or a timer</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Stand about 1 metre from the wall so you don't cast a shadow on it.</li><li>Face a window or lamp. No light behind you, no flash glare on your glasses.</li><li>Have the helper hold the phone at <strong>eye level</strong>, not chin level. Arm's length away.</li><li>Look straight at the lens. Neutral face, mouth closed, both eyes open and visible.</li><li>No glasses (many countries now ban them), no hats, no hair over your eyes or eyebrows.</li><li>Shoot several. Pick the one where your head fills most of the frame with a little headroom.</li><li>Crop to spec with a free passport-photo tool or your phone's editor, then print at a pharmacy or copy shop.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Common rejection reasons</summary><div class="body"><p>Shadows on the wall, a tilted head, smiling with teeth, a patterned background, glare on glasses, or a photo that's slightly too zoomed in. Fix the shadow and the tilt first. That's most of them.</p></div></details></div></div>	-
25.36.1	recipe_card/Get Rid of Ants in the Kitchen Without Fogging the House	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Ants in the Kitchen</h3><div class="meta"><span>15 min setup</span><span>Then wait 3&ndash;5 days</span></div></div><div class="body"><div class="callout good">Kill the trail and you kill the problem for a week. Kill the nest and you kill it for good. That's why bait beats spray.</div><ul class="ing"><li>Soapy water in a spray bottle</li><li>Ant bait gel or bait stations</li><li>Vinegar</li><li>Caulk or petroleum jelly</li><li>Sealed containers</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Follow the trail to find where they enter. It's usually a tiny gap at a window, baseboard, or pipe.</li><li>Wipe the trail with soapy water or vinegar to erase the scent line. Do this <em>after</em> placing bait, or you'll clean up the bait smell too.</li><li>Place bait stations right beside the trail, not in the middle of it. The ants need to find it and carry it home.</li><li>Resist squashing the ones you see. They're the delivery service. Let them work for 3&ndash;5 days.</li><li>Seal the entry gap with caulk. A smear of petroleum jelly works as a temporary barrier.</li><li>Move sugar, cereal, and pet food into sealed containers. Crumbs under the toaster count.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>It's been a week and they're back</summary><div class="body"><p>You probably baited one trail and missed a second entrance. Follow the new line. If ants keep appearing with no visible trail, check behind the dishwasher and under the sink, then re-bait and give it another few days.</p></div></details></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Ants in the Kitchen</h3><div class="meta"><span>15 min setup</span><span>Then wait 3&ndash;5 days</span></div></div><div class="body"><div class="callout good">Kill the trail and you kill the problem for a week. Kill the nest and you kill it for good. That's why bait beats spray.</div><ul class="ing"><li>Soapy water in a spray bottle</li><li>Ant bait gel or bait stations</li><li>Vinegar</li><li>Caulk or petroleum jelly</li><li>Sealed containers</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Follow the trail to find where they enter. It's usually a tiny gap at a window, baseboard, or pipe.</li><li>Wipe the trail with soapy water or vinegar to erase the scent line. Do this <em>after</em> placing bait, or you'll clean up the bait smell too.</li><li>Place bait stations right beside the trail, not in the middle of it. The ants need to find it and carry it home.</li><li>Resist squashing the ones you see. They're the delivery service. Let them work for 3&ndash;5 days.</li><li>Seal the entry gap with caulk. A smear of petroleum jelly works as a temporary barrier.</li><li>Move sugar, cereal, and pet food into sealed containers. Crumbs under the toaster count.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>It's been a week and they're back</summary><div class="body"><p>You probably baited one trail and missed a second entrance. Follow the new line. If ants keep appearing with no visible trail, check behind the dishwasher and under the sink, then re-bait and give it another few days.</p></div></details></div></div>	-
25.37.1	recipe_card/Clean a Coffee Grinder Without Taking It Apart	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Clean a Coffee Grinder Without Taking It Apart</h3><div class="meta"><span>5 min</span><span>No tools</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>1/4 cup uncooked white rice</li><li>A dry toothbrush or stiff brush</li><li>A damp cloth</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Unplug the grinder. Always.</li><li>Pour in the rice. Pulse 4–5 times until it's a coarse powder.</li><li>Dump the rice powder. It grabs old oils and fine dust on its way out.</li><li>Brush out the chute and the lid with the dry brush.</li><li>Wipe the exterior and the lid with the damp cloth. Let it dry fully before using.</li></ol><p class="muted t-small">Do this once a month. Your next cup will taste like coffee, not yesterday.</p></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Clean a Coffee Grinder Without Taking It Apart</h3><div class="meta"><span>5 min</span><span>No tools</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>1/4 cup uncooked white rice</li><li>A dry toothbrush or stiff brush</li><li>A damp cloth</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Unplug the grinder. Always.</li><li>Pour in the rice. Pulse 4–5 times until it's a coarse powder.</li><li>Dump the rice powder. It grabs old oils and fine dust on its way out.</li><li>Brush out the chute and the lid with the dry brush.</li><li>Wipe the exterior and the lid with the damp cloth. Let it dry fully before using.</li></ol><p class="muted t-small">Do this once a month. Your next cup will taste like coffee, not yesterday.</p></div></div>	-
25.38.1	recipe_card/Fold a Fitted Sheet Without Crying	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Fold a Fitted Sheet Without Crying</h3><div class="meta"><span>1 min</span><span>Makes a neat rectangle</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>One fitted sheet</li><li>A flat surface (bed or table)</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Lay the sheet flat, elastic side up.</li><li>Find all four corners. Tuck one corner into another, like a puppet mouth. Do the same with the other two.</li><li>You now have two corner bundles. Tuck one bundle into the other. You've made a quarter-folded shape.</li><li>Lay it flat on the bed. The elastic edges will try to curl. Smooth them.</li><li>Fold in half lengthwise, then in thirds. Done.</li></ol><p class="muted t-small">It won't be perfect. It will be folded. That's the win.</p></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Fold a Fitted Sheet Without Crying</h3><div class="meta"><span>1 min</span><span>Makes a neat rectangle</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>One fitted sheet</li><li>A flat surface (bed or table)</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Lay the sheet flat, elastic side up.</li><li>Find all four corners. Tuck one corner into another, like a puppet mouth. Do the same with the other two.</li><li>You now have two corner bundles. Tuck one bundle into the other. You've made a quarter-folded shape.</li><li>Lay it flat on the bed. The elastic edges will try to curl. Smooth them.</li><li>Fold in half lengthwise, then in thirds. Done.</li></ol><p class="muted t-small">It won't be perfect. It will be folded. That's the win.</p></div></div>	-
25.39.1	recipe_card/Make a Rope Rug That Actually Lies Flat	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Make a Rope Rug That Actually Lies Flat</h3><div class="meta"><span>2 hours</span><span>Makes a 2x3 ft rug</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>100 ft of 1/2-inch cotton rope</li><li>Hot glue gun and plenty of sticks</li><li>A non-stick mat or parchment paper</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Start at the center. Coil the rope into a tight spiral, gluing every inch as you go.</li><li>Keep the coils flat on the mat. Press each new ring against the last.</li><li>When you reach the size you want, cut the rope and glue the end under the last coil.</li><li>Flip it over and glue a few spots on the back to lock the shape.</li><li>Let it sit under a heavy book for an hour before you walk on it.</li></ol><p class="muted t-small">Cotton rope, not nylon. Nylon slides and refuses to stay put.</p></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Make a Rope Rug That Actually Lies Flat</h3><div class="meta"><span>2 hours</span><span>Makes a 2x3 ft rug</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>100 ft of 1/2-inch cotton rope</li><li>Hot glue gun and plenty of sticks</li><li>A non-stick mat or parchment paper</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Start at the center. Coil the rope into a tight spiral, gluing every inch as you go.</li><li>Keep the coils flat on the mat. Press each new ring against the last.</li><li>When you reach the size you want, cut the rope and glue the end under the last coil.</li><li>Flip it over and glue a few spots on the back to lock the shape.</li><li>Let it sit under a heavy book for an hour before you walk on it.</li></ol><p class="muted t-small">Cotton rope, not nylon. Nylon slides and refuses to stay put.</p></div></div>	-
25.40.1	recipe_card/Make Beeswax Food Wraps	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Make Beeswax Food Wraps</h3><div class="meta"><span>20 min</span><span>Makes 3 wraps</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>3 squares of cotton fabric (8x8, 10x10, 12x12 inches)</li><li>1/2 cup beeswax pellets</li><li>2 tbsp jojoba oil or coconut oil</li><li>Parchment paper and an iron</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Preheat iron to medium. Lay a fabric square on a sheet of parchment.</li><li>Sprinkle beeswax and a drizzle of oil evenly over the fabric.</li><li>Cover with another sheet of parchment. Iron until the wax melts and soaks through.</li><li>Peel the top parchment off. Lift the wrap by the corners and wave it for 30 seconds to set.</li><li>Repeat for the other squares. Store rolled in a drawer.</li></ol><p class="muted t-small">Wash in cool water. Hot water strips the wax.</p></div></div>	<div class="recipe"><div class="head"><h3>Make Beeswax Food Wraps</h3><div class="meta"><span>20 min</span><span>Makes 3 wraps</span></div></div><div class="body"><ul class="ing"><li>3 squares of cotton fabric (8x8, 10x10, 12x12 inches)</li><li>1/2 cup beeswax pellets</li><li>2 tbsp jojoba oil or coconut oil</li><li>Parchment paper and an iron</li></ul><ol class="list"><li>Preheat iron to medium. Lay a fabric square on a sheet of parchment.</li><li>Sprinkle beeswax and a drizzle of oil evenly over the fabric.</li><li>Cover with another sheet of parchment. Iron until the wax melts and soaks through.</li><li>Peel the top parchment off. Lift the wrap by the corners and wave it for 30 seconds to set.</li><li>Repeat for the other squares. Store rolled in a drawer.</li></ol><p class="muted t-small">Wash in cool water. Hot water strips the wax.</p></div></div>	-
