26.30.0	checklist/Balcony vegetable patch checklist	<div class="card"><h4>Balcony vegetable patch checklist</h4><p class="muted t-small">A patch the size of a doormat still counts. Start here.</p><ul class="list"><li>Measure the sun — 6+ hours means tomatoes, 3–4 means greens.</li><li>Check your rail or wall can take weight before pots go up.</li><li>Pick pots 30cm+ deep with drainage holes; saucers for the neighbours below.</li><li>Use fresh potting mix, not garden soil — it's lighter and sterile.</li><li>Plant easy starters: herbs, lettuce, radish, then cherry tomatoes.</li><li>Water in the morning; balcony pots dry twice as fast as beds.</li><li>Feed every two weeks once flowers show up.</li><li>Watch for whitefly and aphids — one squirt of soapy water, repeat weekly.</li></ul><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small"><b>Small tip:</b> group thirstier pots together so you're not guessing which one looks sad.</p></div></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Balcony vegetable patch checklist</h4><p class="muted t-small">A patch the size of a doormat still counts. Start here.</p><ul class="list"><li>Measure the sun — 6+ hours means tomatoes, 3–4 means greens.</li><li>Check your rail or wall can take weight before pots go up.</li><li>Pick pots 30cm+ deep with drainage holes; saucers for the neighbours below.</li><li>Use fresh potting mix, not garden soil — it's lighter and sterile.</li><li>Plant easy starters: herbs, lettuce, radish, then cherry tomatoes.</li><li>Water in the morning; balcony pots dry twice as fast as beds.</li><li>Feed every two weeks once flowers show up.</li><li>Watch for whitefly and aphids — one squirt of soapy water, repeat weekly.</li></ul><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small"><b>Small tip:</b> group thirstier pots together so you're not guessing which one looks sad.</p></div></div>	-
26.31.0	checklist/Before a tattoo appointment checklist	<div class="card"><h4>Before a tattoo appointment checklist</h4><p class="muted t-small">The art lasts forever. The prep lasts a day.</p><ul class="list"><li>Sleep properly the night before — tired skin hurts more.</li><li>Eat a real meal an hour before; low blood sugar is the enemy.</li><li>Hydrate, but not so much you're up mid-session.</li><li>Skip alcohol and painkillers that thin blood for 24 hours.</li><li>Wear loose clothing that leaves the area easy to reach.</li><li>Bring comfy layers, water, and a snack if it's a long session.</li><li>Shower, but no lotion or oils on the spot.</li><li>Confirm the design and placement with your artist once more.</li></ul><div class="callout warn"><p class="t-small"><b>Near miss:</b> don't shave the area yourself unless asked — artists usually do it to avoid nicks.</p></div></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Before a tattoo appointment checklist</h4><p class="muted t-small">The art lasts forever. The prep lasts a day.</p><ul class="list"><li>Sleep properly the night before — tired skin hurts more.</li><li>Eat a real meal an hour before; low blood sugar is the enemy.</li><li>Hydrate, but not so much you're up mid-session.</li><li>Skip alcohol and painkillers that thin blood for 24 hours.</li><li>Wear loose clothing that leaves the area easy to reach.</li><li>Bring comfy layers, water, and a snack if it's a long session.</li><li>Shower, but no lotion or oils on the spot.</li><li>Confirm the design and placement with your artist once more.</li></ul><div class="callout warn"><p class="t-small"><b>Near miss:</b> don't shave the area yourself unless asked — artists usually do it to avoid nicks.</p></div></div>	-
26.32.0	checklist/Sourdough starter first week checklist	<div class="card"><h4>Sourdough starter first week checklist</h4><p class="muted t-small">Flour, water, patience. Mostly patience.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Day 1:</b> mix 50g wholemeal flour with 50g warm water in a jar.</li><li><b>Day 2:</b> stir, look for tiny bubbles. Nothing? Normal.</li><li><b>Day 3–4:</b> it may smell like socks or nail polish. Also normal.</li><li><b>Day 3 onward:</b> discard all but 50g, feed 50g flour + 50g water daily.</li><li><b>Day 5–7:</b> watch for it doubling 4–8 hours after feeding.</li><li>Keep it somewhere 22–26°C, out of direct sun.</li><li>Use a loose lid — it needs to breathe, not explode.</li><li>Ready when it doubles reliably and smells like yogurt.</li></ul><div class="callout good"><p class="t-small"><b>Good sign:</b> a domed top and a web of bubbles underneath. Bake something.</p></div></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Sourdough starter first week checklist</h4><p class="muted t-small">Flour, water, patience. Mostly patience.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Day 1:</b> mix 50g wholemeal flour with 50g warm water in a jar.</li><li><b>Day 2:</b> stir, look for tiny bubbles. Nothing? Normal.</li><li><b>Day 3–4:</b> it may smell like socks or nail polish. Also normal.</li><li><b>Day 3 onward:</b> discard all but 50g, feed 50g flour + 50g water daily.</li><li><b>Day 5–7:</b> watch for it doubling 4–8 hours after feeding.</li><li>Keep it somewhere 22–26°C, out of direct sun.</li><li>Use a loose lid — it needs to breathe, not explode.</li><li>Ready when it doubles reliably and smells like yogurt.</li></ul><div class="callout good"><p class="t-small"><b>Good sign:</b> a domed top and a web of bubbles underneath. Bake something.</p></div></div>	-
26.33.0	checklist/Chess tournament day checklist	<div class="card"><h4>Chess tournament day checklist</h4><p class="muted t-small">Your brain plays. Your bag should have its back.</p><ul class="list"><li>Print or screenshot the schedule — phone batteries die at round four.</li><li>Bring a pen and a small scorebook or sheets if not provided.</li><li>Pack water, a light snack, and something with slow carbs.</li><li>Know the time control and whether increment applies.</li><li>Arrive 20–30 minutes early to find your board and settle.</li><li>Silence your phone; vibrations count as noise to some.</li><li>Review your openings once, not for an hour.</li><li>After each game, note one thing you'd do differently.</li></ul><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small"><b>Small tip:</b> between rounds, walk and breathe. Sitting still for six hours is its own opponent.</p></div></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Chess tournament day checklist</h4><p class="muted t-small">Your brain plays. Your bag should have its back.</p><ul class="list"><li>Print or screenshot the schedule — phone batteries die at round four.</li><li>Bring a pen and a small scorebook or sheets if not provided.</li><li>Pack water, a light snack, and something with slow carbs.</li><li>Know the time control and whether increment applies.</li><li>Arrive 20–30 minutes early to find your board and settle.</li><li>Silence your phone; vibrations count as noise to some.</li><li>Review your openings once, not for an hour.</li><li>After each game, note one thing you'd do differently.</li></ul><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small"><b>Small tip:</b> between rounds, walk and breathe. Sitting still for six hours is its own opponent.</p></div></div>	-
26.34.0	checklist/Hosting a board game night checklist	<div class="card"><h4>Hosting a board game night checklist</h4><p class="muted t-small">Rule one: nobody reads the manual out loud for 40 minutes.</p><ul class="list"><li>Pick 2–3 games: one warm-up, one main, one filler for late leavers.</li><li>Learn the rules beforehand. Yes, actually.</li><li>Set a table with room for cards, drinks, and elbows.</li><li>Provide coasters, napkins, and somewhere for phones to live.</li><li>Snacks that don't grease your fingers: pretzels, grapes, popcorn.</li><li>Water and something fizzy. Keep the strong stuff for after.</li><li>Decide the vibe: competitive, casual, or co-op.</li><li>Have a 'next game' ready so there's no dead air.</li></ul><div class="callout warn"><p class="t-small"><b>Near miss:</b> check your group can actually play the main game in under 2 hours. Nobody wants to start a 3-hour epic at 10pm.</p></div></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Hosting a board game night checklist</h4><p class="muted t-small">Rule one: nobody reads the manual out loud for 40 minutes.</p><ul class="list"><li>Pick 2–3 games: one warm-up, one main, one filler for late leavers.</li><li>Learn the rules beforehand. Yes, actually.</li><li>Set a table with room for cards, drinks, and elbows.</li><li>Provide coasters, napkins, and somewhere for phones to live.</li><li>Snacks that don't grease your fingers: pretzels, grapes, popcorn.</li><li>Water and something fizzy. Keep the strong stuff for after.</li><li>Decide the vibe: competitive, casual, or co-op.</li><li>Have a 'next game' ready so there's no dead air.</li></ul><div class="callout warn"><p class="t-small"><b>Near miss:</b> check your group can actually play the main game in under 2 hours. Nobody wants to start a 3-hour epic at 10pm.</p></div></div>	-
26.35.0	checklist/Preparing for a heavy rainstorm checklist	<div class="card"><h4>Preparing for a heavy rainstorm checklist</h4><p class="muted t-small">A little prep now beats a wet surprise later.</p><ul class="list"><li>Clear gutters and drains so water has somewhere to go.</li><li>Move anything loose from the balcony or garden inside.</li><li>Check windows close properly; fix that one sticky latch.</li><li>Charge power banks and phones; dig out a torch and batteries.</li><li>Fill a couple of bottles with drinking water.</li><li>Park the car away from trees and low spots if you can.</li><li>Bring pets in and set up a dry spot for them.</li><li>Save your power company's outage number offline.</li></ul><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small"><b>Small tip:</b> if you're in a flood-prone area, move valuables off the floor now, not when the water's rising.</p></div></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Preparing for a heavy rainstorm checklist</h4><p class="muted t-small">A little prep now beats a wet surprise later.</p><ul class="list"><li>Clear gutters and drains so water has somewhere to go.</li><li>Move anything loose from the balcony or garden inside.</li><li>Check windows close properly; fix that one sticky latch.</li><li>Charge power banks and phones; dig out a torch and batteries.</li><li>Fill a couple of bottles with drinking water.</li><li>Park the car away from trees and low spots if you can.</li><li>Bring pets in and set up a dry spot for them.</li><li>Save your power company's outage number offline.</li></ul><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small"><b>Small tip:</b> if you're in a flood-prone area, move valuables off the floor now, not when the water's rising.</p></div></div>	-
26.36.0	checklist/Balcony herb garden start checklist	<div class="umi-page"><div class="stack"><h2>Balcony herb garden start</h2><p class="muted">A small green corner, no big commitment. <regex>user_name</regex>, let's get you growing.</p><div class="card"><h4>Prep & plants</h4><ul class="list"><li>Check sunlight hours: most herbs want 4–6.</li><li>Pick pots with drainage holes; terracotta is forgiving.</li><li>Use potting mix, not garden soil—lighter and cleaner.</li><li>Start with hardy trio: basil, mint, chives.</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Ongoing care</h4><ul class="list"><li>Water when top inch of soil is dry.</li><li>Pinch basil tips to keep it bushy.</li><li>Rotate pots weekly for even growth.</li><li>Harvest little and often—it encourages more.</li></ul></div><div class="callout info"><p>Wind dries out pots fast. A simple screen helps.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="stack"><h2>Balcony herb garden start</h2><p class="muted">A small green corner, no big commitment. , let's get you growing.</p><div class="card"><h4>Prep & plants</h4><ul class="list"><li>Check sunlight hours: most herbs want 4–6.</li><li>Pick pots with drainage holes; terracotta is forgiving.</li><li>Use potting mix, not garden soil—lighter and cleaner.</li><li>Start with hardy trio: basil, mint, chives.</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Ongoing care</h4><ul class="list"><li>Water when top inch of soil is dry.</li><li>Pinch basil tips to keep it bushy.</li><li>Rotate pots weekly for even growth.</li><li>Harvest little and often—it encourages more.</li></ul></div><div class="callout info"><p>Wind dries out pots fast. A simple screen helps.</p></div></div></div>	-
26.37.0	checklist/Day hike essentials checklist	<div class="umi-page"><div class="stack"><h2>Day hike essentials</h2><p class="muted">Pack light, but pack smart. <regex>user_name</regex>, here's your day pack.</p><div class="card"><h4>Gear</h4><ul class="list"><li>Comfortable shoes with grip.</li><li>Water: 1–2 litres depending on heat.</li><li>Snacks: nuts, fruit, something salty.</li><li>Weather layer—even if it's sunny now.</li><li>Map or offline trail download.</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Just in case</h4><ul class="list"><li>Small first-aid kit with blister plasters.</li><li>Headlamp or phone light.</li><li>Whistle or emergency contact card.</li><li>Sunscreen and a hat.</li></ul></div><div class="callout good"><p>Tell someone your route and expected return time. Simple, and it matters.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="stack"><h2>Day hike essentials</h2><p class="muted">Pack light, but pack smart. , here's your day pack.</p><div class="card"><h4>Gear</h4><ul class="list"><li>Comfortable shoes with grip.</li><li>Water: 1–2 litres depending on heat.</li><li>Snacks: nuts, fruit, something salty.</li><li>Weather layer—even if it's sunny now.</li><li>Map or offline trail download.</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Just in case</h4><ul class="list"><li>Small first-aid kit with blister plasters.</li><li>Headlamp or phone light.</li><li>Whistle or emergency contact card.</li><li>Sunscreen and a hat.</li></ul></div><div class="callout good"><p>Tell someone your route and expected return time. Simple, and it matters.</p></div></div></div>	-
26.38.0	checklist/New laptop setup checklist	<div class="umi-page"><div class="stack"><h2>New laptop setup</h2><p class="muted">The unboxing high fades fast. <regex>user_name</regex>, let's make it a smooth start.</p><div class="card"><h4>First hour</h4><ul class="list"><li>Charge fully while you set up.</li><li>Run system updates—yes, all of them.</li><li>Sign in to your main account.</li><li>Enable automatic backups (cloud or external).</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Security & comfort</h4><ul class="list"><li>Turn on disk encryption if available.</li><li>Install a password manager.</li><li>Set up two-factor for key accounts.</li><li>Adjust display scaling and night light.</li><li>Uninstall bloatware you'll never touch.</li></ul></div><details class="acc"><summary>Nice extras</summary><div class="body"><p>A proper mouse, a laptop stand, and a cable organiser. Small joys.</p></div></details><div class="callout info"><p>Create a restore point or system image once it's clean and working. Future you will cheer.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="stack"><h2>New laptop setup</h2><p class="muted">The unboxing high fades fast. , let's make it a smooth start.</p><div class="card"><h4>First hour</h4><ul class="list"><li>Charge fully while you set up.</li><li>Run system updates—yes, all of them.</li><li>Sign in to your main account.</li><li>Enable automatic backups (cloud or external).</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Security & comfort</h4><ul class="list"><li>Turn on disk encryption if available.</li><li>Install a password manager.</li><li>Set up two-factor for key accounts.</li><li>Adjust display scaling and night light.</li><li>Uninstall bloatware you'll never touch.</li></ul></div><details class="acc"><summary>Nice extras</summary><div class="body"><p>A proper mouse, a laptop stand, and a cable organiser. Small joys.</p></div></details><div class="callout info"><p>Create a restore point or system image once it's clean and working. Future y