26.44.0	checklist/New bike helmet fit checklist	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h4>New bike helmet fit checklist</h4><p class="muted">A helmet only works if it sits right. Five minutes now saves your noggin later.</p><ul class="list"><li><strong>Level it</strong> — front rim just above eyebrows, back covers the base of your skull.</li><li><strong>Dial the fit</strong> — turn the rear adjuster until it's snug but not pinching.</li><li><strong>Check side straps</strong> — they should form a V just below your ears.</li><li><strong>Adjust chin strap</strong> — one finger should fit between strap and chin.</li><li><strong>Do the shake test</strong> — open your mouth wide; helmet should press down slightly.</li><li><strong>Wear it on every ride</strong> — even the quick ones. Especially the quick ones.</li><li><strong>Replace after a crash</strong> — or every 5 years, whichever comes first.</li></ul><div class="callout warn"><p class="t-small">If it slides around or feels loose, keep adjusting. A wobbly helmet is barely better than none.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h4>New bike helmet fit checklist</h4><p class="muted">A helmet only works if it sits right. Five minutes now saves your noggin later.</p><ul class="list"><li><strong>Level it</strong> — front rim just above eyebrows, back covers the base of your skull.</li><li><strong>Dial the fit</strong> — turn the rear adjuster until it's snug but not pinching.</li><li><strong>Check side straps</strong> — they should form a V just below your ears.</li><li><strong>Adjust chin strap</strong> — one finger should fit between strap and chin.</li><li><strong>Do the shake test</strong> — open your mouth wide; helmet should press down slightly.</li><li><strong>Wear it on every ride</strong> — even the quick ones. Especially the quick ones.</li><li><strong>Replace after a crash</strong> — or every 5 years, whichever comes first.</li></ul><div class="callout warn"><p class="t-small">If it slides around or feels loose, keep adjusting. A wobbly helmet is barely better than none.</p></div></div></div>	-
26.45.0	checklist/Emergency car kit checklist	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h4>Emergency car kit checklist</h4><p class="muted">Hopefully you'll never need it. But if you do, you'll be really glad it's there.</p><ul class="list"><li><strong>Jumper cables</strong> — or a portable jump starter. Know how to use them.</li><li><strong>Flashlight</strong> — with extra batteries. A headlamp is even better.</li><li><strong>First aid kit</strong> — bandages, antiseptic wipes, pain relievers.</li><li><strong>Blanket</strong> — thermal or wool. Also good for picnics.</li><li><strong>Water and non-perishable snacks</strong> — rotate every six months.</li><li><strong>Basic tools</strong> — screwdrivers, pliers, duct tape, multi-tool.</li><li><strong>Tire pressure gauge</strong> — and know your car's recommended PSI.</li><li><strong>Reflective triangles or flares</strong> — visibility if you're stuck on the road.</li><li><strong>Phone charger</strong> — car adapter or power bank.</li><li><strong>Rain poncho</strong> — changing a tire in the rain is no fun.</li></ul><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Check your kit twice a year. Batteries die, snacks expire, and seasons change.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h4>Emergency car kit checklist</h4><p class="muted">Hopefully you'll never need it. But if you do, you'll be really glad it's there.</p><ul class="list"><li><strong>Jumper cables</strong> — or a portable jump starter. Know how to use them.</li><li><strong>Flashlight</strong> — with extra batteries. A headlamp is even better.</li><li><strong>First aid kit</strong> — bandages, antiseptic wipes, pain relievers.</li><li><strong>Blanket</strong> — thermal or wool. Also good for picnics.</li><li><strong>Water and non-perishable snacks</strong> — rotate every six months.</li><li><strong>Basic tools</strong> — screwdrivers, pliers, duct tape, multi-tool.</li><li><strong>Tire pressure gauge</strong> — and know your car's recommended 