26.7.1	checklist/Volunteering at an animal shelter checklist	<div class="card">  <h4>Volunteering at an animal shelter checklist</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">Wear the clothes you don't love. Every shift finds a way to test that.</p>  <ul class="list">    <li>Confirm your shift time, entrance, and who to ask for</li>    <li>Wear closed shoes, long trousers, and nothing precious</li>    <li>Bring a spare t-shirt in a bag, just in case</li>    <li>Fill in any health forms, allergies, or tetanus status</li>    <li>Expect an induction: hygiene, handover, no wandering alone</li>    <li>Learn the hand-washing and sanitiser routine properly</li>    <li>Ask which animals you may handle and which you may not</li>    <li>Follow staff on behaviour calls, don't improvise</li>    <li>Tie long hair back, remove loose jewellery and dangling straps</li>    <li>Leave a bag of towels or old blankets if they're welcome</li>  </ul>  <div class="callout warn">    <p>Feeling emotional on day one is normal. Step away, breathe, then go back gently. The animals notice calm.</p>  </div>  <p class="muted t-small">Nice one, <regex>user_name</regex>. Shelters run on people who show up twice.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <h4>Volunteering at an animal shelter checklist</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">Wear the clothes you don't love. Every shift finds a way to test that.</p>  <ul class="list">    <li>Confirm your shift time, entrance, and who to ask for</li>    <li>Wear closed shoes, long trousers, and nothing precious</li>    <li>Bring a spare t-shirt in a bag, just in case</li>    <li>Fill in any health forms, allergies, or tetanus status</li>    <li>Expect an induction: hygiene, handover, no wandering alone</li>    <li>Learn the hand-washing and sanitiser routine properly</li>    <li>Ask which animals you may handle and which you may not</li>    <li>Follow staff on behaviour calls, don't improvise</li>    <li>Tie long hair back, remove loose jewellery and dangling straps</li>    <li>Leave a bag of towels or old blankets if they're welcome</li>  </ul>  <div class="callout warn">    <p>Feeling emotional on day one is normal. Step away, breathe, then go back gently. The animals notice calm.</p>  </div>  <p class="muted t-small">Nice one, . Shelters run on people who show up twice.</p></div>	-
26.8.1	checklist/First balcony rose bush checklist	<div class="card"><h4>First balcony rose bush checklist</h4><p class="muted">Roses are dramatic, but they’re not hard. They just want sun, water, and a little air around their roots.</p><ul class="list"><li>Choose a spot with at least 6 hours of direct sun.</li><li>Use a pot at least 45 cm wide with drainage holes.</li><li>Plant in quality potting mix, not garden soil.</li><li>Water deeply when the top 2 cm of soil feels dry.</li><li>Feed with rose fertiliser every 4–6 weeks during growing season.</li><li>Prune dead or crossing canes in late winter.</li><li>Check for aphids weekly; blast them off with water.</li><li>Move the pot slightly if leaves scorch in peak afternoon heat.</li></ul><p class="t-small dim">A rose in a pot is basically a thirsty houseplant with opinions. You’ve got this.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>First balcony rose bush checklist</h4><p class="muted">Roses are dramatic, but they’re not hard. They just want sun, water, and a little air around their roots.</p><ul class="list"><li>Choose a spot with at least 6 hours of direct sun.</li><li>Use a pot at least 45 cm wide with drainage holes.</li><li>Plant in quality potting mix, not garden soil.</li><li>Water deeply when the top 2 cm of soil feels dry.</li><li>Feed with rose fertiliser every 4–6 weeks during growing season.</li><li>Prune dead or crossing canes in late winter.</li><li>Check for aphids weekly; blast them off with water.</li><li>Move the pot slightly if leaves scorch in peak afternoon heat.</li></ul><p class="t-small dim">A rose in a pot is basically a thirsty houseplant with opinions. You’ve got this.</p></div>	-
26.9.1	checklist/Rescue dog adoption prep checklist	<div class="card"><h4>Rescue dog adoption prep checklist</h4><p class="muted">A rescue dog doesn’t need a perfect home. It needs a quiet one, at first.</p><ul class="list"><li>Buy a crate or bed and set it up before the dog arrives.</li><li>Stock the same food the shelter was using.</li><li>Get a martingale collar and a 2 m leash.</li><li>Pick a quiet room for the first few days, no visitors.</li><li>Dog-proof cables, shoes, and bin lids.</li><li>Find a vet and book a check-up for week one.</li><li>Keep a simple routine: same walk times, same feeding times.</li><li>Give space. Let the dog come to you.</li></ul><p class="t-small dim">The first 3 days are decompression, not a test. Go slow.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Rescue dog adoption prep checklist</h4><p class="muted">A rescue dog doesn’t need a perfect home. It needs a quiet one, at first.</p><ul class="list"><li>Buy a crate or bed and set it up before the dog arrives.</li><li>Stock the same food the shelter was using.</li><li>Get a martingale collar and a 2 m leash.</li><li>Pick a quiet room for the first few days, no visitors.</li><li>Dog-proof cables, shoes, and bin lids.</li><li>Find a vet and book a check-up for week one.</li><li>Keep a simple routine: same walk times, same feeding times.</li><li>Give space. Let the dog come to you.</li></ul><p class="t-small dim">The first 3 days are decompression, not a test. Go slow.</p></div>	-
26.10.1	checklist/Home weather station setup checklist	<div class="card"><h4>Home weather station setup checklist</h4><p class="muted">You’re about to become the person who checks the sky before the forecast does.</p><ul class="list"><li>Choose a spot away from buildings and trees, ideally 2 m above ground.</li><li>Mount the anemometer higher if you want accurate wind speed.</li><li>Keep the rain gauge level and clear of overhangs.</li><li>Place the thermometer in shade, not direct sun.</li><li>Connect the station to your Wi-Fi and check the signal.</li><li>Register with a weather network if you want to share data.</li><li>Log the first week’s readings to spot any oddities.</li><li>Clean the rain gauge monthly.</li></ul><p class="t-small dim">You’ll never look at a cloudy sky the same way again.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Home weather station setup checklist</h4><p class="muted">You’re about to become the person who checks the sky before the forecast does.</p><ul class="list"><li>Choose a spot away from buildings and trees, ideally 2 m above ground.</li><li>Mount the anemometer higher if you want accurate wind speed.</li><li>Keep the rain gauge level and clear of overhangs.</li><li>Place the thermometer in shade, not direct sun.</li><li>Connect the station to your Wi-Fi and check the signal.</li><li>Register with a weather network if you want to share data.</li><li>Log the first week’s readings to spot any oddities.</li><li>Clean the rain gauge monthly.</li></ul><p class="t-small dim">You’ll never look at a cloudy sky the same way again.</p></div>	-
26.11.1	checklist/First farmers market visit checklist	<div class="card"><h4>First farmers market visit checklist</h4><p class="muted">Go early for the best picks, bring cash, and don’t be shy about asking what’s good today.</p><ul class="list"><li>Bring a reusable tote and a small cooler bag.</li><li>Carry small bills and coins for easy transactions.</li><li>Walk the whole market once before buying.</li><li>Ask vendors what’s in season and what they’d buy.</li><li>Check for a market map or info stall if it’s a big one.</li><li>Buy delicate greens last so they don’t get squished.</li><li>Note stall names you liked for next time.</li><li>Don’t overbuy. You can always come back next week.</li></ul><p class="t-small dim">The tomatoes will tell you when they’re ready. Trust your nose.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>First farmers market visit checklist</h4><p class="muted">Go early for the best picks, bring cash, and don’t be shy about asking what’s good today.</p><ul class="list"><li>Bring a reusable tote and a small cooler bag.</li><li>Carry small bills and coins for easy transactions.</li><li>Walk the whole market once before buying.</li><li>Ask vendors what’s in season and what they’d buy.</li><li>Check for a market map or info stall if it’s a big one.</li><li>Buy delicate greens last so they don’t get squished.</li><li>Note stall names you liked for next time.</li><li>Don’t overbuy. You can always come back next week.</li></ul><p class="t-small dim">The tomatoes will tell you when they’re ready. Trust your nose.</p></div>	-
26.12.1	checklist/Bullet journal setup checklist	<div class="card"><h4>Bullet journal setup checklist</h4><p class="muted">A bullet journal is just a notebook with a plan. You can’t do it wrong.</p><ul class="list"><li>Pick a notebook you actually like holding.</li><li>Use a pen that doesn’t bleed through the pages.</li><li>Create an index page at the front.</li><li>Set up a future log for the next 6 months.</li><li>Add a monthly log with dates and key tasks.</li><li>Make a daily log for today only.</li><li>Use simple symbols: • task, ✗ done, → migrated.</li><li>Review and migrate unfinished tasks at month’s end.</li></ul><p class="t-small dim">Start ugly. You can make it pretty once it’s useful.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Bullet journal setup checklist</h4><p class="muted">A bullet journal is just a notebook with a plan. You can’t do it wrong.</p><ul class="list"><li>Pick a notebook you actually like holding.</li><li>Use a pen that doesn’t bleed through the pages.</li><li>Create an index page at the front.</li><li>Set up a future log for the next 6 months.</li><li>Add a monthly log with dates and key tasks.</li><li>Make a daily log for today only.</li><li>Use simple symbols: • task, ✗ done, → migrated.</li><li>Review and migrate unfinished tasks at month’s end.</li></ul><p class="t-small dim">Start ugly. You can make it pretty once it’s useful.</p></div>	-
26.13.1	checklist/Beginner roller skating checklist	<div class="card"><h4>Beginner roller skating checklist</h4><p class="muted">Everyone wobbles at first. The trick is to wobble forward.</p><ul class="list"><li>Wear a helmet and wrist guards, always.</li><li>Knee pads and elbow pads are worth the bulk.</li><li>Find a smooth, flat surface with a rail or wall nearby.</li><li>Start by standing still and shifting weight side to side.</li><li>Learn to fall forward onto your pads, not backward.</li><li>Practice gliding with feet in a V shape.</li><li>Keep sessions short and frequent rather than long and rare.</li><li>Check your skates’ toe stops and wheel tightness before each session.</li></ul><p class="t-small dim">Your first goal isn’t speed. It’s not falling. Then it’s falling well.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Beginner roller skating checklist</h4><p class="muted">Everyone wobbles at first. The trick is to wobble forward.</p><ul class="list"><li>Wear a helmet and wrist guards, always.</li><li>Knee pads and elbow pads are worth the bulk.</li><li>Find a smooth, flat surface with a rail or wall nearby.</li><li>Start by standing still and shifting weight side to side.</li><li>Learn to fall forward onto your pads, not backward.</li><li>Practice gliding with feet in a V shape.</li><li>Keep sessions short and frequent rather than long and rare.</li><li>Check your skates’ toe stops and wheel tightness before each session.</li></ul><p class="t-small dim">Your first goal isn’t speed. It’s not falling. Then it’s falling well.</p></div>	-
26.14.1	checklist/DMV visit checklist	<div class="card"><h4>DMV visit checklist</h4><p class="muted t-small">Paper beats waiting rooms. Bring more than you think you need.</p><ul class="list"><li>Appointment confirmation (printed or on phone)</li><li>Driver's license or state ID</li><li>Proof of identity: birth certificate or passport</li><li>Proof of residency: utility bill or lease (two if possible)</li><li>Social Security card or W-2</li><li>Filled-out form (if you can print it ahead)</li><li>Payment: card, check, or exact cash</li><li>Glasses or contacts if you need them for the eye test</li><li>Snack and water (wait times are real)</li><li>Book or podcast for the queue</li></ul><p class="muted t-small">Leave the expired documents at home. They won't help.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>DMV visit checklist</h4><p class="muted t-small">Paper beats waiting rooms. Bring more than you think you need.</p><ul class="list"><li>Appointment confirmation (printed or on phone)</li><li>Driver's license or state ID</li><li>Proof of identity: birth certificate or passport</li><li>Proof of residency: utility bill or lease (two if possible)</li><li>Social Security card or W-2</li><li>Filled-out form (if you can print it ahead)</li><li>Payment: card, check, or exact cash</li><li>Glasses or contacts if you need them for the eye test</li><li>Snack and water (wait times are real)</li><li>Book or podcast for the queue</li></ul><p class="muted t-small">Leave the expired documents at home. They won't help.</p></div>	-
26.15.1	checklist/First day of work checklist	<div class="card"><h4>First day of work checklist</h4><p class="muted t-small">Show up curious. The rest sorts itself out.</p><ul class="list"><li>ID and any onboarding paperwork</li><li>Notebook and pen (phones die, paper doesn't)</li><li>Laptop or bag if you're bringing your own</li><li>Water bottle and a snack</li><li>Layers—office AC is a mystery</li><li>Parking pass or transit card ready</li><li>Phone charger</li><li>A few questions written down</li><li>Emergency contact info filled out</li><li>Your best first-day smile</li></ul><p class="muted t-small">You don't need to know everything today. You just need to show up.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>First day of work checklist</h4><p class="muted t-small">Show up curious. The rest sorts itself out.</p><ul class="list"><li>ID and any onboarding paperwork</li><li>Notebook and pen (phones die, paper doesn't)</li><li>Laptop or bag if you're bringing your own</li><li>Water bottle and a snack</li><li>Layers—office AC is a mystery</li><li>Parking pass or transit card ready</li><li>Phone charger</li><li>A few questions written down</li><li>Emergency contact info filled out</li><li>Your best first-day smile</li></ul><p class="muted t-small">You don't need to know everything today. You just need to show up.</p></div>	-
26.16.1	checklist/Beach day with kids checklist	<div class="card"><h4>Beach day with kids checklist</h4><p class="muted t-small">Sand will get everywhere. Prepare for that, and you'll be fine.</p><ul class="list"><li>Sunscreen (SPF 30+, reapply every two hours)</li><li>Hats and sunglasses for everyone</li><li>Swimsuits + a dry change of clothes</li><li>Towels (one per kid, plus one for the car)</li><li>Water and juice boxes—lots</li><li>Snacks: fruit, crackers, anything not melty</li><li>Beach toys: buckets, shovels, ball</li><li>Umbrella or pop-up shade</li><li>Wet wipes and hand sanitizer</li><li>A plastic bag for wet stuff</li><li>Fully charged phone + portable charger</li></ul><p class="muted t-small">Set a meeting spot in case anyone wanders. Then relax—a little.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Beach day with kids checklist</h4><p class="muted t-small">Sand will get everywhere. Prepare for that, and you'll be fine.</p><ul class="list"><li>Sunscreen (SPF 30+, reapply every two hours)</li><li>Hats and sunglasses for everyone</li><li>Swimsuits + a dry change of clothes</li><li>Towels (one per kid, plus one for the car)</li><li>Water and juice boxes—lots</li><li>Snacks: fruit, crackers, anything not melty</li><li>Beach toys: buckets, shovels, ball</li><li>Umbrella or pop-up shade</li><li>Wet wipes and hand sanitizer</li><li>A plastic bag for wet stuff</li><li>Fully charged phone + portable charger</li></ul><p class="muted t-small">Set a meeting spot in case anyone wanders. Then relax—a little.</p></div>	-
26.17.1	checklist/Library visit checklist	<div class="card"><h4>Library visit checklist</h4><p class="muted t-small">Quiet, free, and full of answers. Bring a tote.</p><ul class="list"><li>Library card (or ID to get one)</li><li>Tote bag for books</li><li>List of books or topics you're after</li><li>Returns from last time (avoid fines)</li><li>Headphones if you'll use computers</li><li>Notebook for call numbers</li><li>Water bottle</li><li>Phone charger</li><li>Library app loaded (if you use one)</li><li>A little patience for the self-checkout</li></ul><p class="muted t-small">Ask a librarian. It's literally their job and they're good at it.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Library visit checklist</h4><p class="muted t-small">Quiet, free, and full of answers. Bring a tote.</p><ul class="list"><li>Library card (or ID to get one)</li><li>Tote bag for books</li><li>List of books or topics you're after</li><li>Returns from last time (avoid fines)</li><li>Headphones if you'll use computers</li><li>Notebook for call numbers</li><li>Water bottle</li><li>Phone charger</li><li>Library app loaded (if you use one)</li><li>A little patience for the self-checkout</li></ul><p class="muted t-small">Ask a librarian. It's literally their job and they're good at it.</p></div>	-
26.18.1	checklist/Concert night checklist	<div class="card"><h4>Concert night checklist</h4><p class="muted t-small">Loud, bright, and over too soon. Plan the small stuff.</p><ul class="list"><li>Tickets (digital or printed)</li><li>ID if venue requires it</li><li>Earplugs—your future self says thanks</li><li>Small bag within venue rules</li><li>Cash or card for merch and drinks</li><li>Phone charged + portable battery</li><li>Comfortable shoes for standing</li><li>Weather layer for the walk out</li><li>Ride plan: transit, parking, or pickup spot</li><li>Meet-up point for your group</li></ul><p class="muted t-small">Check the venue's bag policy before you leave. Some are strict.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Concert night checklist</h4><p class="muted t-small">Loud, bright, and over too soon. Plan the small stuff.</p><ul class="list"><li>Tickets (digital or printed)</li><li>ID if venue requires it</li><li>Earplugs—your future self says thanks</li><li>Small bag within venue rules</li><li>Cash or card for merch and drinks</li><li>Phone charged + portable battery</li><li>Comfortable shoes for standing</li><li>Weather layer for the walk out</li><li>Ride plan: transit, parking, or pickup spot</li><li>Meet-up point for your group</li></ul><p class="muted t-small">Check the venue's bag policy before you leave. Some are strict.</p></div>	-
26.19.1	checklist/Post office run checklist	<div class="card"><h4>Post office run checklist</h4><p class="muted t-small">The line is long. The satisfaction of sending it is worth it.</p><ul class="list"><li>Package(s) sealed and addressed</li><li>Return address written or label ready</li><li>Contents secured (tape, bubble wrap)</li><li>Addresses double-checked</li><li>Payment method</li><li>Tracking numbers if printing online</li><li>Any return slips or customs forms</li><li>Envelope for documents</li><li>Phone with the address you're sending to</li><li>Patience for the queue</li></ul><p class="muted t-small">Weigh it at home if you can. Saves a trip to the counter scale.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Post office run checklist</h4><p class="muted t-small">The line is long. The satisfaction of sending it is worth it.</p><ul class="list"><li>Package(s) sealed and addressed</li><li>Return address written or label ready</li><li>Contents secured (tape, bubble wrap)</li><li>Addresses double-checked</li><li>Payment method</li><li>Tracking numbers if printing online</li><li>Any return slips or customs forms</li><li>Envelope for documents</li><li>Phone with the address you're sending to</li><li>Patience for the queue</li></ul><p class="muted t-small">Weigh it at home if you can. Saves a trip to the counter scale.</p></div>	-
