26.31.1	checklist/First time at a farmers market checklist	<div class="card"><h4>First farmers market visit</h4><p class="muted t-small">Go early for the good stuff, <regex>user_name</regex>. Bring a tote and small bills.</p><ul class="list"><li>Cash in small bills, plus a card just in case</li><li>Reusable tote or two, plus a cooler bag for eggs and cheese</li><li>Walk the whole loop first, then buy</li><li>Ask the vendor what's peaking this week</li><li>Bring a list but stay open to what looks best</li><li>Note the stall names you liked for next time</li><li>Go early, before the crowd and the heat</li></ul><div class="callout info">Many stalls sell out by 11am. If you see one perfect tomato, buy it now, not on the way back.</div></div>	<div class="card"><h4>First farmers market visit</h4><p class="muted t-small">Go early for the good stuff, . Bring a tote and small bills.</p><ul class="list"><li>Cash in small bills, plus a card just in case</li><li>Reusable tote or two, plus a cooler bag for eggs and cheese</li><li>Walk the whole loop first, then buy</li><li>Ask the vendor what's peaking this week</li><li>Bring a list but stay open to what looks best</li><li>Note the stall names you liked for next time</li><li>Go early, before the crowd and the heat</li></ul><div class="callout info">Many stalls sell out by 11am. If you see one perfect tomato, buy it now, not on the way back.</div></div>	-
26.32.1	checklist/Before a root canal appointment checklist	<div class="card"><h4>Root canal prep</h4><p class="muted t-small">Deep breath, <regex>user_name</regex>. The buildup is worse than the thing itself.</p><ul class="list"><li>Eat a real meal beforehand unless told otherwise</li><li>Take any premedication exactly as instructed</li><li>Arrange a ride if you're getting sedation</li><li>Skip alcohol and caffeine the night before</li><li>Brush and floss, but go easy right around the tooth</li><li>Bring your insurance card and a list of meds</li><li>Charge your phone for the waiting room</li></ul><div class="callout good">Plan something soft for dinner. Soup, eggs, yogurt. Your jaw will thank you.</div></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Root canal prep</h4><p class="muted t-small">Deep breath, . The buildup is worse than the thing itself.</p><ul class="list"><li>Eat a real meal beforehand unless told otherwise</li><li>Take any premedication exactly as instructed</li><li>Arrange a ride if you're getting sedation</li><li>Skip alcohol and caffeine the night before</li><li>Brush and floss, but go easy right around the tooth</li><li>Bring your insurance card and a list of meds</li><li>Charge your phone for the waiting room</li></ul><div class="callout good">Plan something soft for dinner. Soup, eggs, yogurt. Your jaw will thank you.</div></div>	-
26.33.1	checklist/Changing a flat tire checklist	<div class="card"><h4>Flat tire, step by step</h4><p class="muted t-small">Pull as far off the road as you can, <regex>user_name</regex>. Hazards on first.</p><ol class="list"><li>Park on flat ground, hazards on, parking brake up</li><li>Get the spare, jack, and wrench out of the trunk</li><li>Loosen the lug nuts a quarter turn before lifting</li><li>Jack the car up until the flat clears the ground</li><li>Remove the lug nuts, then the wheel</li><li>Mount the spare, hand-tighten the nuts in a star pattern</li><li>Lower, then tighten firmly with the wrench</li></ol><div class="callout warn">Donut spares are usually good for about 50 miles at 50 mph. Get the real tire fixed soon.</div></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Flat tire, step by step</h4><p class="muted t-small">Pull as far off the road as you can, . Hazards on first.</p><ol class="list"><li>Park on flat ground, hazards on, parking brake up</li><li>Get the spare, jack, and wrench out of the trunk</li><li>Loosen the lug nuts a quarter turn before lifting</li><li>Jack the car up until the flat clears the ground</li><li>Remove the lug nuts, then the wheel</li><li>Mount the spare, hand-tighten the nuts in a star pattern</li><li>Lower, then tighten firmly with the wrench</li></ol><div class="callout warn">Donut spares are usually good for about 50 miles at 50 mph. Get the real tire fixed soon.</div></div>	-
26.34.1	checklist/First day of a new job checklist	<div class="card"><h4>First day at a new job</h4><p class="muted t-small">You already got the job, <regex>user_name</regex>. Today is just showing up.</p><ul class="list"><li>Confirm start time and where to go</li><li>Lay out your outfit the night before</li><li>Pack your ID, tax forms, and bank details if needed</li><li>Bring a notebook and pen (yes, the analog kind)</li><li>Plan your route with 15 minutes of buffer</li><li>Prep two questions to ask your manager</li><li>Save the front desk or HR number in your phone</li></ul><div class="callout info">Learn three names today. Coworkers remember the person who asked theirs first.</div></div>	<div class="card"><h4>First day at a new job</h4><p class="muted t-small">You already got the job, . Today is just showing up.</p><ul class="list"><li>Confirm start time and where to go</li><li>Lay out your outfit the night before</li><li>Pack your ID, tax forms, and bank details if needed</li><li>Bring a notebook and pen (yes, the analog kind)</li><li>Plan your route with 15 minutes of buffer</li><li>Prep two questions to ask your manager</li><li>Save the front desk or HR number in your phone</li></ul><div class="callout info">Learn three names today. Coworkers remember the person who asked theirs first.</div></div>	-
26.35.1	checklist/Hosting a dinner party checklist	<div class="card"><h4>Dinner party, the calm version</h4><p class="muted t-small">Pick one dish you've made before, <regex>user_name</regex>. Experiments are for Tuesdays.</p><ul class="list"><li>Confirm the guest count and any allergies</li><li>Choose a menu you can mostly make ahead</li><li>Shop two days before, not the day of</li><li>Set the table the night before if you can</li><li>Chill drinks early so you're not scrambling</li><li>Have a playlist ready and the volume low</li><li>Aim to be done cooking 20 minutes before they arrive</li></ul><div class="callout good">Nobody remembers the garnish. They remember whether you sat down with them.</div></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Dinner party, the calm version</h4><p class="muted t-small">Pick one dish you've made before, . Experiments are for Tuesdays.</p><ul class="list"><li>Confirm the guest count and any allergies</li><li>Choose a menu you can mostly make ahead</li><li>Shop two days before, not the day of</li><li>Set the table the night before if you can</li><li>Chill drinks early so you're not scrambling</li><li>Have a playlist ready and the volume low</li><li>Aim to be done cooking 20 minutes before they arrive</li></ul><div class="callout good">Nobody remembers the garnish. They remember whether you sat down with them.</div></div>	-
26.36.1	checklist/Buying a used car checklist	<div class="card"><h4>Used car walk-around</h4><p class="muted t-small">Trust the VIN more than the seller, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="list"><li>Run a VIN history report before you go</li><li>Check tire tread depth and manufacture date</li><li>Look for matching paint and panel gaps</li><li>Cold-start the engine, listen for rough idle</li><li>Test every button, window, and the AC</li><li>Check for rust in wheel wells and under the trunk mat</li><li>Ask when the timing belt, brakes, and fluids were last done</li></ul><div class="callout warn">"Runs great, no issues" from a seller is a rumor, not a fact. Get a mechanic to look it over before you pay.</div></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Used car walk-around</h4><p class="muted t-small">Trust the VIN more than the seller, .</p><ul class="list"><li>Run a VIN history report before you go</li><li>Check tire tread depth and manufacture date</li><li>Look for matching paint and panel gaps</li><li>Cold-start the engine, listen for rough idle</li><li>Test every button, window, and the AC</li><li>Check for rust in wheel wells and under the trunk mat</li><li>Ask when the timing belt, brakes, and fluids were last done</li></ul><div class="callout warn">"Runs great, no issues" from a seller is a rumor, not a fact. Get a mechanic to look it over before you pay.</div></div>	-
26.37.1	checklist/First aquarium setup checklist	<div class="card"><h4>First aquarium setup</h4><p class="muted t-small">Fish come last, <regex>user_name</regex>. The water needs a job first.</p><ul class="list"><li><details class="acc"><summary>Pick the spot</summary><div class="body">Away from windows and vents. Steady temperature beats a pretty view.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Wash substrate and decor</summary><div class="body">Rinse gravel in a bucket until the water runs clear. Hot water only, no soap, ever.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Fill and dechlorinate</summary><div class="body">Tap water is fine once treated. Dose for the full tank volume, not just what you pour.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Start the filter and heater</summary><div class="body">Let both run 24 hours before anything living goes in.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Test water daily</summary><div class="body">Ammonia and nitrite should climb, then fall to zero. That's the cycle doing its work.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Add fish slowly</summary><div class="body">Float the bag 15 minutes, then net the fish in. Skip the shop water.</div></details></li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>First aquarium setup</h4><p class="muted t-small">Fish come last, . The water needs a job first.</p><ul class="list"><li><details class="acc"><summary>Pick the spot</summary><div class="body">Away from windows and vents. Steady temperature beats a pretty view.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Wash substrate and decor</summary><div class="body">Rinse gravel in a bucket until the water runs clear. Hot water only, no soap, ever.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Fill and dechlorinate</summary><div class="body">Tap water is fine once treated. Dose for the full tank volume, not just what you pour.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Start the filter and heater</summary><div class="body">Let both run 24 hours before anything living goes in.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Test water daily</summary><div class="body">Ammonia and nitrite should climb, then fall to zero. That's the cycle doing its work.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Add fish slowly</summary><div class="body">Float the bag 15 minutes, then net the fish in. Skip the shop water.</div></details></li></ul></div>	-
26.38.1	checklist/First time at a pick-your-own farm checklist	<div class="card"><h4>Pick-your-own farm day</h4><p class="muted t-small">Dress like the field will win, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="list"><li><details class="acc"><summary>Call ahead or check the site</summary><div class="body">Fruit ripens on its own schedule. Weeds and weather close fields without notice.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Bring your own containers</summary><div class="body">Shallow buckets and berry baskets beat deep bags. Farm-supplied containers often cost extra.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Wear closed-toe shoes</summary><div class="body">Straw stubble and mud are the whole experience.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Pack water, hats, sunscreen</summary><div class="body">Fields have no shade and rarely a drinking fountain.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Ask where the ripe rows are</summary><div class="body">Staff know exactly which section was picked last. It saves twenty minutes of wandering.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Weigh and pay before you snack</summary><div class="body">Eating in the field is usually fine, but it still gets weighed.</div></details></li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Pick-your-own farm day</h4><p class="muted t-small">Dress like the field will win, .</p><ul class="list"><li><details class="acc"><summary>Call ahead or check the site</summary><div class="body">Fruit ripens on its own schedule. Weeds and weather close fields without notice.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Bring your own containers</summary><div class="body">Shallow buckets and berry baskets beat deep bags. Farm-supplied containers often cost extra.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Wear closed-toe shoes</summary><div class="body">Straw stubble and mud are the whole experience.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Pack water, hats, sunscreen</summary><div class="body">Fields have no shade and rarely a drinking fountain.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Ask where the ripe rows are</summary><div class="body">Staff know exactly which section was picked last. It saves twenty minutes of wandering.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Weigh and pay before you snack</summary><div class="body">Eating in the field is usually fine, but it still gets weighed.</div></details></li></ul></div>	-
26.39.1	checklist/First time using an instant pot checklist	<div class="card"><h4>Instant Pot first run</h4><p class="muted t-small">It's a pressure cooker, <regex>user_name</regex>. Respect the valve and it's just a very patient kettle.</p><ul class="list"><li><details class="acc"><summary>Read the valve, not the manual</summary><div class="body">Find Sealing and Venting on the lid. That one switch explains most scary stories.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Run the water test</summary><div class="body">Two cups of water, Manual 5 minutes, natural release. It proves the seal works and rinses the factory smell.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Never fill past two-thirds</summary><div class="body">Half full for anything that foams, like beans or pasta.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Use at least one cup of liquid</summary><div class="body">The pot needs steam to build pressure. Dry grains will scorch.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Plan the release</summary><div class="body">Quick release for veg, natural for meat. Ten minutes of natural release is a good default.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Hand wash the lid and ring</summary><div class="body">The silicone ring holds onto strong smells. Soak it in vinegar water now and then.</div></details></li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Instant Pot first run</h4><p class="muted t-small">It's a pressure cooker, . Respect the valve and it's just a very patient kettle.</p><ul class="list"><li><details class="acc"><summary>Read the valve, not the manual</summary><div class="body">Find Sealing and Venting on the lid. That one switch explains most scary stories.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Run the water test</summary><div class="body">Two cups of water, Manual 5 minutes, natural release. It proves the seal works and rinses the factory smell.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Never fill past two-thirds</summary><div class="body">Half full for anything that foams, like beans or pasta.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Use at least one cup of liquid</summary><div class="body">The pot needs steam to build pressure. Dry grains will scorch.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Plan the release</summary><div class="body">Quick release for veg, natural for meat. Ten minutes of natural release is a good default.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Hand wash the lid and ring</summary><div class="body">The silicone ring holds onto strong smells. Soak it in vinegar water now and then.</div></details></li></ul></div>	-
26.40.1	checklist/First time hosting a dinner party checklist	<div class="card"><h4>First dinner party</h4><p class="muted t-small">You're not cooking a menu, <regex>user_name</regex>. You're hosting a room.</p><ul class="list"><li><details class="acc"><summary>Pick one dish you've made before</summary><div class="body">New recipes are for Tuesdays. Guests are for showing off the thing you already nail.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Ask about diets when you invite</summary><div class="body">Not the night of. And keep one plain, safe dish on the table.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Write a backward timeline</summary><div class="body">Serve at 7 means oven at 5:40 and a shower at 4. Write it down.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Chill drinks and clear the fridge</summary><div class="body">You need a shelf for the food you just made. Do this the day before.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Set the table early</summary><div class="body">Plates, glasses, water pitcher. It's one thing you can't do while stirring.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Light, music, candles by the door</summary><div class="body">Two minutes of fuss that makes the whole night feel planned.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Accept every offer of help</summary><div class="body">Guests like a job. Say yes to ice, bread, and dish duty.</div></details></li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>First dinner party</h4><p class="muted t-small">You're not cooking a menu, . You're hosting a room.</p><ul class="list"><li><details class="acc"><summary>Pick one dish you've made before</summary><div class="body">New recipes are for Tuesdays. Guests are for showing off the thing you already nail.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Ask about diets when you invite</summary><div class="body">Not the night of. And keep one plain, safe dish on the table.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Write a backward timeline</summary><div class="body">Serve at 7 means oven at 5:40 and a shower at 4. Write it down.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Chill drinks and clear the fridge</summary><div class="body">You need a shelf for the food you just made. Do this the day before.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Set the table early</summary><div class="body">Plates, glasses, water pitcher. It's one thing you can't do while stirring.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Light, music, candles by the door</summary><div class="body">Two minutes of fuss that makes the whole night feel planned.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Accept every offer of help</summary><div class="body">Guests like a job. Say yes to ice, bread, and dish duty.</div></details></li></ul></div>	-
26.41.1	checklist/First time knitting a scarf checklist	<div class="card"><h4>First scarf, start to finish</h4><p class="muted t-small">One stitch until it stops being interesting, <regex>user_name</regex>. Then it gets good.</p><ul class="list"><li><details class="acc"><summary>Buy chunky yarn and size 8-10 needles</summary><div class="body">Thin yarn and tiny needles make everything harder. Save them for scarf two.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Knit a practice square first</summary><div class="body">Twenty rows you'll throw away teaches cast-on, knitting, and tension.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Learn cast-on and knit stitch only</summary><div class="body">Garter stitch is just knitting every row. That's a whole scarf. You don't need more yet.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Cast on 20-30 stitches</summary><div class="body">Chunky yarn, that's roughly a scarf. Count twice before row one.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Knit until it's as long as your person</summary><div class="body">Six feet is generous. Five is fine. Stop when the yarn runs low.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Cast off loosely and weave in the tails</summary><div class="body">Too tight and the edge puckers. Leave long tails, you'll thank yourself.</div></details></li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>First scarf, start to finish</h4><p class="muted t-small">One stitch until it stops being interesting, . Then it gets good.</p><ul class="list"><li><details class="acc"><summary>Buy chunky yarn and size 8-10 needles</summary><div class="body">Thin yarn and tiny needles make everything harder. Save them for scarf two.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Knit a practice square first</summary><div class="body">Twenty rows you'll throw away teaches cast-on, knitting, and tension.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Learn cast-on and knit stitch only</summary><div class="body">Garter stitch is just knitting every row. That's a whole scarf. You don't need more yet.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Cast on 20-30 stitches</summary><div class="body">Chunky yarn, that's roughly a scarf. Count twice before row one.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Knit until it's as long as your person</summary><div class="body">Six feet is generous. Five is fine. Stop when the yarn runs low.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Cast off loosely and weave in the tails</summary><div class="body">Too tight and the edge puckers. Leave long tails, you'll thank yourself.</div></details></li></ul></div>	-
26.42.1	checklist/First day with a new houseplant checklist	<div class="card"><h4>New houseplant, first week</h4><p class="muted t-small">Most new plants die of enthusiasm, <regex>user_name</regex>. Do less than you want to.</p><ul class="list"><li><details class="acc"><summary>Quarantine it for a week</summary><div class="body">Check the undersides of leaves for bugs before it meets your collection.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Wait on repotting</summary><div class="body">Let it settle. Repot only if roots are circling out of the drainage holes.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Find its light level</summary><div class="body">Bright indirect for most. Right up against a window is direct, and it burns leaves.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Water only when the top inch is dry</summary><div class="body">Stick a finger in. Schedules kill plants, fingertips don't.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Empty the saucer</summary><div class="body">Standing water rots roots. No saucer? Water in the sink and let it drip.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Keep the nursery tag</summary><div class="body">That little stick is your plant's name. You'll forget it by Friday.</div></details></li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>New houseplant, first week</h4><p class="muted t-small">Most new plants die of enthusiasm, . Do less than you want to.</p><ul class="list"><li><details class="acc"><summary>Quarantine it for a week</summary><div class="body">Check the undersides of leaves for bugs before it meets your collection.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Wait on repotting</summary><div class="body">Let it settle. Repot only if roots are circling out of the drainage holes.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Find its light level</summary><div class="body">Bright indirect for most. Right up against a window is direct, and it burns leaves.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Water only when the top inch is dry</summary><div class="body">Stick a finger in. Schedules kill plants, fingertips don't.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Empty the saucer</summary><div class="body">Standing water rots roots. No saucer? Water in the sink and let it drip.</div></details></li><li><details class="acc"><summary>Keep the nursery tag</summary><div class="body">That little stick is your plant's name. You'll forget it by Friday.</div></details></li></ul></div>	-
26.43.1	checklist/First drive-in movie checklist	<div class="card"><h4>First drive-in movie checklist</h4><p class="muted t-small">Roll in early, tune in, and don't let the battery die during the credits.</p><ul class="list"><li>Check showtime &amp; gate open time (arrive 45+ min early for a good spot)</li><li>Portable FM radio or car radio — sound comes through your stereo</li><li>Jumper cables or a portable jump starter (idling for 2 hours is a battery gamble)</li><li>Blankets, pillows, or camp chairs if you're sitting outside</li><li>Snacks and drinks — most drive-ins allow outside food</li><li>Bug spray (dusk = mosquitoes)</li><li>Trash bag for your spot — leave it cleaner than you found it</li><li>Cash or card for the snack bar, because nostalgia popcorn hits different</li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>First drive-in movie checklist</h4><p class="muted t-small">Roll in early, tune in, and don't let the battery die during the credits.</p><ul class="list"><li>Check showtime &amp; gate open time (arrive 45+ min early for a good spot)</li><li>Portable FM radio or car radio — sound comes through your stereo</li><li>Jumper cables or a portable jump starter (idling for 2 hours is a battery gamble)</li><li>Blankets, pillows, or camp chairs if you're sitting outside</li><li>Snacks and drinks — most drive-ins allow outside food</li><li>Bug spray (dusk = mosquitoes)</li><li>Trash bag for your spot — leave it cleaner than you found it</li><li>Cash or card for the snack bar, because nostalgia popcorn hits different</li></ul></div>	-
26.44.1	checklist/First therapy session checklist	<div class="card"><h4>First therapy session checklist</h4><p class="muted t-small">You don't need a speech. Just a few notes and an open mind.</p><ul class="list"><li>Jot down 2–3 things you want to talk about (bullet points are fine)</li><li>Note any major life events or stressors from the past year</li><li>List current medications and dosages</li><li>Insurance card and ID, if you're using coverage</li><li>Arrive 10–15 min early for paperwork</li><li>Bring water and a tissue — emotions sneak up</li><li>Decide what you're comfortable sharing, and what you're not (yet)</li><li>Give yourself permission to not click with the first therapist</li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>First therapy session checklist</h4><p class="muted t-small">You don't need a speech. Just a few notes and an open mind.</p><ul class="list"><li>Jot down 2–3 things you want to talk about (bullet points are fine)</li><li>Note any major life events or stressors from the past year</li><li>List current medications and dosages</li><li>Insurance card and ID, if you're using coverage</li><li>Arrive 10–15 min early for paperwork</li><li>Bring water and a tissue — emotions sneak up</li><li>Decide what you're comfortable sharing, and what you're not (yet)</li><li>Give yourself permission to not click with the first therapist</li></ul></div>	-
26.45.1	checklist/Job fair checklist	<div class="card"><h4>Job fair checklist</h4><p class="muted t-small">Print résumés, wear comfortable shoes, and have a 30-second intro ready.</p><ul class="list"><li>Update and print 10–15 copies of your résumé</li><li>Research the companies attending (pick your top 5)</li><li>Prepare a 30-second elevator pitch</li><li>Dress business casual — layers, because conference halls are unpredictable</li><li>Bring a padfolio or folder for business cards and notes</li><li>Write down 2–3 questions per target company</li><li>Charge your phone and bring a portable battery</li><li>Follow up with a thank-you email within 24 hours</li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Job fair checklist</h4><p class="muted t-small">Print résumés, wear comfortable shoes, and have a 30-second intro ready.</p><ul class="list"><li>Update and print 10–15 copies of your résumé</li><li>Research the companies attending (pick your top 5)</li><li>Prepare a 30-second elevator pitch</li><li>Dress business casual — layers, because conference halls are unpredictable</li><li>Bring a padfolio or folder for business cards and notes</li><li>Write down 2–3 questions per target company</li><li>Charge your phone and bring a portable battery</li><li>Follow up with a thank-you email within 24 hours</li></ul></div>	-
26.46.1	checklist/First casino visit checklist	<div class="card"><h4>First casino visit checklist</h4><p class="muted t-small">Set a budget, learn the table minimums, and tip the dealers.</p><ul class="list"><li>Set a strict cash budget before you walk in (and leave the ATM card at home if you're prone to "just one more")</li><li>Bring your ID — you'll need it to enter and to cash out</li><li>Wear comfortable shoes; casino floors are a hike</li><li>Know the table minimums before you sit down</li><li>Learn basic etiquette: tip dealers, don't touch the cards, place bets before the deal</li><li>Take breaks — free drinks are a trap, not a strategy</li><li>Set a time limit, not just a money limit</li><li>Remember: the house always has an edge. Play for fun, not for rent.</li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>First casino visit checklist</h4><p class="muted t-small">Set a budget, learn the table minimums, and tip the dealers.</p><ul class="list"><li>Set a strict cash budget before you walk in (and leave the ATM card at home if you're prone to "just one more")</li><li>Bring your ID — you'll need it to enter and to cash out</li><li>Wear comfortable shoes; casino floors are a hike</li><li>Know the table minimums before you sit down</li><li>Learn basic etiquette: tip dealers, don't touch the cards, place bets before the deal</li><li>Take breaks — free drinks are a trap, not a strategy</li><li>Set a time limit, not just a money limit</li><li>Remember: the house always has an edge. Play for fun, not for rent.</li></ul></div>	-
26.47.1	checklist/First Renaissance faire checklist	<div class="card"><h4>First Renaissance faire checklist</h4><p class="muted t-small">Comfortable shoes, cash for turkey legs, and a willingness to say "huzzah."</p><ul class="list"><li>Comfortable walking shoes (the grounds are usually grass or dirt)</li><li>Cash for food, drinks, and tipping performers — many vendors are cash-only</li><li>Sunscreen and a hat; shade is scarce</li><li>Refillable water bottle</li><li>Costume optional, but fun — check the faire's costume rules first</li><li>Small bag or belt pouch for your phone, wallet, and a turkey leg napkin</li><li>Check the schedule for jousting times and shows you don't want to miss</li><li>Bring a sense of humor for the improv actors — they will roast you lovingly</li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>First Renaissance faire checklist</h4><p class="muted t-small">Comfortable shoes, cash for turkey legs, and a willingness to say "huzzah."</p><ul class="list"><li>Comfortable walking shoes (the grounds are usually grass or dirt)</li><li>Cash for food, drinks, and tipping performers — many vendors are cash-only</li><li>Sunscreen and a hat; shade is scarce</li><li>Refillable water bottle</li><li>Costume optional, but fun — check the faire's costume rules first</li><li>Small bag or belt pouch for your phone, wallet, and a turkey leg napkin</li><li>Check the schedule for jousting times and shows you don't want to miss</li><li>Bring a sense of humor for the improv actors — they will roast you lovingly</li></ul></div>	-
26.48.1	checklist/First karaoke night checklist	<div class="card"><h4>First karaoke night checklist</h4><p class="muted t-small">Pick a song you know by heart, not one you wish you knew.</p><ul class="list"><li>Choose a song in your vocal comfort zone (no Mariah unless you can actually hit it)</li><li>Have a backup song ready</li><li>Practice the lyrics once or twice beforehand — the screen helps, but not that much</li><li>Hydrate and avoid too much liquid courage; slurred karaoke is only fun for you</li><li>Check the sign-up process (some places have apps, some have clipboards)</li><li>Tip the KJ (karaoke host) — they control your fate</li><li>Don't hog the mic; one song per rotation is the unwritten rule</li><li>Cheer for everyone else. It's a group activity, not a talent show.</li></ul></div>	<div class="card"><h4>First karaoke night checklist</h4><p class="muted t-small">Pick a song you know by heart, not one you wish you knew.</p><ul class="list"><li>Choose a song in your vocal comfort zone (no Mariah unless you can actually hit it)</li><li>Have a backup song ready</li><li>Practice the lyrics once or twice beforehand — the screen helps, but not that much</li><li>Hydrate and avoid too much liquid courage; slurred karaoke is only fun for you</li><li>Check the sign-up process (some places have apps, some have clipboards)</li><li>Tip the KJ (karaoke host) — they control your fate</li><li>Don't hog the mic; one song per rotation is the unwritten rule</li><li>Cheer for everyone else. It's a group activity, not a talent show.</li></ul></div>	-
26.49.1	checklist/Poker Night Host Checklist	<div class="card"><h4>Poker Night Host Checklist</h4><p class="muted t-small">For <regex>user_name</regex> — keep it friendly, keep the chips flying.</p><ul class="list"><li>Deck of cards (plus a spare)</li><li>Poker chips — at least 300 for 6 players</li><li>Felt table topper or a plain bedsheet</li><li>Snacks that don't leave grease on cards (pretzels, grapes, popcorn)</li><li>Drinks — water, soda, maybe a growler</li><li>Small notepad and pen for buy-ins</li><li>Blinds schedule taped to the wall</li><li>A clock or phone timer for levels</li><li>Trash bowl for wrappers</li><li>House rule sheet: no string bets, no slow-rolling</li><li>Music playlist ready — low key, no distractions</li><li>Enough chairs and elbow room</li></ul><div class="callout info"><p>New to dealing? Look up a 30-second YouTube on how to shuffle chips. It's oddly soothing.</p></div></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Poker Night Host Checklist</h4><p class="muted t-small">For  — keep it friendly, keep the chips flying.</p><ul class="list"><li>Deck of cards (plus a spare)</li><li>Poker chips — at least 300 for 6 players</li><li>Felt table topper or a plain bedsheet</li><li>Snacks that don't leave grease on cards (pretzels, grapes, popcorn)</li><li>Drinks — water, soda, maybe a growler</li><li>Small notepad and pen for buy-ins</li><li>Blinds schedule taped to the wall</li><li>A clock or phone timer for levels</li><li>Trash bowl for wrappers</li><li>House rule sheet: no string bets, no slow-rolling</li><li>Music playlist ready — low key, no distractions</li><li>Enough chairs and elbow room</li></ul><div class="callout info"><p>New to dealing? Look up a 30-second YouTube on how to shuffle chips. It's oddly soothing.</p></div></div>	-
26.50.1	checklist/Starting a Coin Collection Checklist	<div class="card"><h4>Starting a Coin Collection Checklist</h4><ul class="list"><li>Pick a focus: US pennies, world coins, silver, or a specific era</li><li>Get a folder or album with acid-free pages</li><li>Buy a magnifying glass (10x loupe is plenty)</li><li>Cotton gloves for handling uncirculated coins</li><li>Small plastic sleeves or 2x2 cardboard flips</li><li>Notebook to log finds — date, mint mark, condition</li><li>Join an online forum or local coin club for swaps</li><li>Learn the basics of grading (Good to Mint State)</li><li>Ask bank tellers for rolls of coins to search</li><li>Store away from humidity — silica packets help</li><li>Never clean old coins with polish; it kills value</li></ul><div class="callout good"><p>Start with what's in your pocket. That 1965 quarter? Probably not silver, but check the edge.</p></div></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Starting a Coin Collection Checklist</h4><ul class="list"><li>Pick a focus: US pennies, world coins, silver, or a specific era</li><li>Get a folder or album with acid-free pages</li><li>Buy a magnifying glass (10x loupe is plenty)</li><li>Cotton gloves for handling uncirculated coins</li><li>Small plastic sleeves or 2x2 cardboard flips</li><li>Notebook to log finds — date, mint mark, condition</li><li>Join an online forum or local coin club for swaps</li><li>Learn the basics of grading (Good to Mint State)</li><li>Ask bank tellers for rolls of coins to search</li><li>Store away from humidity — silica packets help</li><li>Never clean old coins with polish; it kills value</li></ul><div class="callout good"><p>Start with what's in your pocket. That 1965 quarter? Probably not silver, but check the edge.</p></div></div>	-
26.51.1	checklist/Blood Donation Day Checklist	<div class="card"><h4>Blood Donation Day Checklist</h4><p class="muted t-small">Thanks for rolling up a sleeve, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="list"><li>Eat a good meal 2–3 hours before — iron-rich if you can</li><li>Hydrate: 16 oz of water right before you go</li><li>Bring photo ID and a list of medications</li><li>Wear a short-sleeved or loose top</li><li>Avoid heavy exercise before and after</li><li>Skip alcohol for 24 hours prior</li><li>Get a good night's sleep</li><li>Bring a book or podcast for the wait</li><li>Plan a snack for after — cookies are tradition</li><li>No aspirin for 48 hours if you're on a platelet schedule</li></ul><div class="callout warn"><p>Feeling lightheaded? Tell the staff. They've seen it all and have juice boxes ready.</p></div></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Blood Donation Day Checklist</h4><p class="muted t-small">Thanks for rolling up a sleeve, .</p><ul class="list"><li>Eat a good meal 2–3 hours before — iron-rich if you can</li><li>Hydrate: 16 oz of water right before you go</li><li>Bring photo ID and a list of medications</li><li>Wear a short-sleeved or loose top</li><li>Avoid heavy exercise before and after</li><li>Skip alcohol for 24 hours prior</li><li>Get a good night's sleep</li><li>Bring a book or podcast for the wait</li><li>Plan a snack for after — cookies are tradition</li><li>No aspirin for 48 hours if you're on a platelet schedule</li></ul><div class="callout warn"><p>Feeling lightheaded? Tell the staff. They've seen it all and have juice boxes ready.</p></div></div>	-
26.52.1	checklist/Game Night Host Checklist	<div class="card"><h4>Game Night Host Checklist</h4><ul class="list"><li>Pick 2–3 games that fit the crowd (party, strategy, co-op)</li><li>Read the rules beforehand so you can teach fast</li><li>Set up a central table with enough chairs</li><li>Snacks in bowls — nothing too greasy</li><li>Drinks station away from the board</li><li>Phone basket by the door (optional but noble)</li><li>Timer or phone for turns if needed</li><li>Scorepad and pens</li><li>Background music low or off</li><li>Have a short filler game for late arrivals</li><li>Designate a rules lawyer — you, probably</li></ul><div class="callout info"><p>Pro move: teach the game in 3 minutes, then play a practice round open-handed.</p></div></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Game Night Host Checklist</h4><ul class="list"><li>Pick 2–3 games that fit the crowd (party, strategy, co-op)</li><li>Read the rules beforehand so you can teach fast</li><li>Set up a central table with enough chairs</li><li>Snacks in bowls — nothing too greasy</li><li>Drinks station away from the board</li><li>Phone basket by the door (optional but noble)</li><li>Timer or phone for turns if needed</li><li>Scorepad and pens</li><li>Background music low or off</li><li>Have a short filler game for late arrivals</li><li>Designate a rules lawyer — you, probably</li></ul><div class="callout info"><p>Pro move: teach the game in 3 minutes, then play a practice round open-handed.</p></div></div>	-
26.53.1	checklist/Beginning a Journal Checklist	<div class="card"><h4>Beginning a Journal Checklist</h4><ul class="list"><li>Pick a notebook you actually like touching</li><li>Choose a pen that flows — no skipping</li><li>Decide a time: morning pages or before bed</li><li>Set a tiny goal: 3 sentences, not 3 pages</li><li>Skip the pressure to be profound</li><li>Date every entry</li><li>Write about today's small win</li><li>Add one thing you're curious about</li><li>Don't edit — let it be messy</li><li>Keep it visible on your desk or nightstand</li><li>If you miss a day, just start again</li></ul><div class="callout good"><p>The blank page isn't a judge, <regex>user_name</regex>. It's just paper.</p></div></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Beginning a Journal Checklist</h4><ul class="list"><li>Pick a notebook you actually like touching</li><li>Choose a pen that flows — no skipping</li><li>Decide a time: morning pages or before bed</li><li>Set a tiny goal: 3 sentences, not 3 pages</li><li>Skip the pressure to be profound</li><li>Date every entry</li><li>Write about today's small win</li><li>Add one thing you're curious about</li><li>Don't edit — let it be messy</li><li>Keep it visible on your desk or nightstand</li><li>If you miss a day, just start again</li></ul><div class="callout good"><p>The blank page isn't a judge, . It's just paper.</p></div></div>	-
26.54.1	checklist/Picnic Planning Checklist	<div class="card"><h4>Picnic Planning Checklist</h4><ul class="list"><li>Pick a spot with shade and a flat surface</li><li>Check the weather — wind is the enemy</li><li>Blanket or a big towel</li><li>Cooler with ice packs</li><li>Reusable plates, cups, and cutlery</li><li>Napkins and a trash bag</li><li>Sandwiches, fruit, and something crunchy</li><li>Water bottle for each person</li><li>Sunscreen and bug spray</li><li>A board game or frisbee</li><li>Speaker for music, charged</li></ul><div class="callout info"><p>Bring a small cutting board — it doubles as a table for cups on grass.</p></div></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Picnic Planning Checklist</h4><ul class="list"><li>Pick a spot with shade and a flat surface</li><li>Check the weather — wind is the enemy</li><li>Blanket or a big towel</li><li>Cooler with ice packs</li><li>Reusable plates, cups, and cutlery</li><li>Napkins and a trash bag</li><li>Sandwiches, fruit, and something crunchy</li><li>Water bottle for each person</li><li>Sunscreen and bug spray</li><li>A board game or frisbee</li><li>Speaker for music, charged</li></ul><div class="callout info"><p>Bring a small cutting board — it doubles as a table for cups on grass.</p></div></div>	-
