32.6.0	css_snippet/CSS :has() for parent selection	<div class="card">  <h4>CSS snippet</h4>  <p class="muted">Style a parent when it contains a specific child using <code>:has()</code>.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>.card:has(img) {\n  background: var(--accent-soft);\n  border-color: var(--accent);\n}\n\n/* Real-world: highlight form group when input has a value */\n.field:has(input:not(:placeholder-shown)) .label {\n  color: var(--accent);\n  font-weight: 600;\n}</code></pre>  <div class="callout info">    <p><strong>Note:</strong> Support is good in all modern browsers (Chrome 105+, Safari 15.4+, Firefox 121+). For older browsers, a fallback class is needed.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="card">  <h4>CSS snippet</h4>  <p class="muted">Style a parent when it contains a specific child using <code>:has()</code>.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>.card:has(img) {\n  background: var(--accent-soft);\n  border-color: var(--accent);\n}\n\n/* Real-world: highlight form group when input has a value */\n.field:has(input:not(:placeholder-shown)) .label {\n  color: var(--accent);\n  font-weight: 600;\n}</code></pre>  <div class="callout info">    <p><strong>Note:</strong> Support is good in all modern browsers (Chrome 105+, Safari 15.4+, Firefox 121+). For older browsers, a fallback class is needed.</p>  </div></div>	-
32.7.0	css_snippet/CSS container queries for responsive components	<div class="card">  <h4>CSS snippet</h4>  <p class="muted">Make components adapt to their container width, not just viewport.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>.card-wrapper {\n  container-type: inline-size;\n  container-name: card;\n}\n\n@container card (min-width: 400px) {\n  .card-content {\n    display: flex;\n    gap: var(--s-4);\n  }\n}\n\n/* Fallback for older browsers */\n.card-content {\n  display: block;\n}</code></pre>  <div class="callout good">    <p><strong>Tip:</strong> Name your containers to avoid accidental matches. <code>container-name</code> keeps things tidy.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="card">  <h4>CSS snippet</h4>  <p class="muted">Make components adapt to their container width, not just viewport.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>.card-wrapper {\n  container-type: inline-size;\n  container-name: card;\n}\n\n@container card (min-width: 400px) {\n  .card-content {\n    display: flex;\n    gap: var(--s-4);\n  }\n}\n\n/* Fallback for older browsers */\n.card-content {\n  display: block;\n}</code></pre>  <div class="callout good">    <p><strong>Tip:</strong> Name your containers to avoid accidental matches. <code>container-name</code> keeps things tidy.</p>  </div></div>	-
32.8.0	css_snippet/CSS accent-color for form controls	<div class="card">  <h4>CSS snippet</h4>  <p class="muted">Tint checkboxes, radios, and range inputs with one line using <code>accent-color</code>.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>input[type="checkbox"],\ninput[type="radio"],\ninput[type="range"] {\n  accent-color: var(--accent);\n}\n\n/* Or set globally for all form controls */\n:root {\n  accent-color: var(--accent);\n}</code></pre>  <div class="callout info">    <p><strong>Support:</strong> Works in all modern browsers. For full custom styling, you'll still need <code>appearance: none</code> and pseudo-elements.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="card">  <h4>CSS snippet</h4>  <p class="muted">Tint checkboxes, radios, and range inputs with one line using <code>accent-color</code>.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>input[type="checkbox"],\ninput[type="radio"],\ninput[type="range"] {\n  accent-color: var(--accent);\n}\n\n/* Or set globally for all form controls */\n:root {\n  accent-color: var(--accent);\n}</code></pre>  <div class="callout info">    <p><strong>Support:</strong> Works in all modern browsers. For full custom styling, you'll still need <code>appearance: none</code> and pseudo-elements.</p>  </div></div>	-
32.9.0	css_snippet/CSS logical properties for RTL support	<div class="card">  <h4>CSS snippet</h4>  <p class="muted">Use logical properties to make your layout adapt automatically to different writing directions.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>.box {\n  /* Instead of margin-left / margin-right */\n  margin-inline: var(--s-4);\n\n  /* Instead of padding-top / padding-bottom */\n  padding-block: var(--s-3);\n\n  /* Instead of border-left */\n  border-inline-start: 2px solid var(--accent);\n\n  /* Instead of left / right for positioning */\n  inset-inline-start: 0;\n}</code></pre>  <div class="callout good">    <p><strong>Benefit:</strong> Works for both LTR and RTL without extra CSS. Set <code>dir="rtl"</code> on the container and it just works.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="card">  <h4>CSS snippet</h4>  <p class="muted">Use logical properties to make your layout adapt automatically to different writing directions.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>.box {\n  /* Instead of margin-left / margin-right */\n  margin-inline: var(--s-4);\n\n  /* Instead of padding-top / padding-bottom */\n  padding-block: var(--s-3);\n\n  /* Instead of border-left */\n  border-inline-start: 2px solid var(--accent);\n\n  /* Instead of left / right for positioning */\n  inset-inline-start: 0;\n}</code></pre>  <div class="callout good">    <p><strong>Benefit:</strong> Works for both LTR and RTL without extra CSS. Set <code>dir="rtl"</code> on the container and it just works.</p>  </div></div>	-
32.10.0	css_snippet/CSS @supports for feature detection	<div class="card">  <h4>CSS snippet</h4>  <p class="muted">Apply styles only if the browser supports a feature.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>/* Base styles for all browsers */\n.grid {\n  display: flex;\n  flex-wrap: wrap;\n}\n\n/* Enhanced grid for supporting browsers */\n@supports (display: grid) {\n  .grid {\n    display: grid;\n    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(200px, 1fr));\n  }\n}\n\n/* Check for a specific property value */\n@supports (backdrop-filter: blur(10px)) {\n  .overlay {\n    backdrop-filter: blur(10px);\n  }\n}</code></pre>  <div class="callout info">    <p><strong>Tip:</strong> Use <code>@supports not</code> to provide fallbacks for unsupported features.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="card">  <h4>CSS snippet</h4>  <p class="muted">Apply styles only if the browser supports a feature.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>/* Base styles for all browsers */\n.grid {\n  display: flex;\n  flex-wrap: wrap;\n}\n\n/* Enhanced grid for supporting browsers */\n@supports (display: grid) {\n  .grid {\n    display: grid;\n    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(200px, 1fr));\n  }\n}\n\n/* Check for a specific property value */\n@supports (backdrop-filter: blur(10px)) {\n  .overlay {\n    backdrop-filter: blur(10px);\n  }\n}</code></pre>  <div class="callout info">    <p><strong>Tip:</strong> Use <code>@supports not</code> to provide fallbacks for unsupported features.</p>  </div></div>	-
32.11.0	css_snippet/CSS scroll snap for carousels	<div class="card">  <h4>CSS snippet</h4>  <p class="muted">Create a smooth, scroll-snapping carousel with pure CSS.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>.carousel {\n  display: flex;\n  overflow-x: auto;\n  scroll-snap-type: x mandatory;\n  gap: var(--s-4);\n  padding: var(--s-4);\n  scroll-padding: var(--s-4);\n}\n\n.carousel > * {\n  flex: 0 0 80%;\n  scroll-snap-align: start;\n  scroll-snap-stop: always;\n  border-radius: var(--r-md);\n}</code></pre>  <div class="callout good">    <p><strong>Note:</strong> Works on touch and trackpad. Add <code>scroll-behavior: smooth</code> for buttery transitions.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="card">  <h4>CSS snippet</h4>  <p class="muted">Create a smooth, scroll-snapping carousel with pure CSS.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>.carousel {\n  display: flex;\n  overflow-x: auto;\n  scroll-snap-type: x mandatory;\n  gap: var(--s-4);\n  padding: var(--s-4);\n  scroll-padding: var(--s-4);\n}\n\n.carousel > * {\n  flex: 0 0 80%;\n  scroll-snap-align: start;\n  scroll-snap-stop: always;\n  border-radius: var(--r-md);\n}</code></pre>  <div class="callout good">    <p><strong>Note:</strong> Works on touch and trackpad. Add <code>scroll-behavior: smooth</code> for buttery transitions.</p>  </div></div>	-
32.12.0	css_snippet/CSS aspect-ratio for media boxes	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">CSS aspect-ratio for media boxes</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">Reserve the space before the image arrives, so nothing jumps.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>.thumb {  aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;  width: 100%;  border-radius: var(--r-sm);  background: var(--panel-2);  overflow: hidden;}.thumb > img {  width: 100%;  height: 100%;  object-fit: cover;  display: block;}/* square avatar tile */.avatar-tile {  aspect-ratio: 1;  width: var(--s-6);  border-radius: var(--r-pill);  background: var(--panel-2);}</code></pre>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Why it works</summary>    <div class="body">      <p class="t-small"><regex>user_name</regex>, the browser knows the box's height from the width, so it never has to guess. No more layout shift when the image lands.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">CSS aspect-ratio for media boxes</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">Reserve the space before the image arrives, so nothing jumps.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>.thumb {  aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;  width: 100%;  border-radius: var(--r-sm);  background: var(--panel-2);  overflow: hidden;}.thumb > img {  width: 100%;  height: 100%;  object-fit: cover;  display: block;}/* square avatar tile */.avatar-tile {  aspect-ratio: 1;  width: var(--s-6);  border-radius: var(--r-pill);  background: var(--panel-2);}</code></pre>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Why it works</summary>    <div class="body">      <p class="t-small">, the browser knows the box's height from the width, so it never has to guess. No more layout shift when the image lands.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
32.13.0	css_snippet/CSS min() max() and clamp() together	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">CSS min() max() and clamp() together</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">One line that scales between two honest bounds.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>h1 {  font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 4vw + 1rem, 3rem);  line-height: 1.15;}/* min() picks the smaller, max() the larger */.sidebar {  width: min(280px, 30%);}.reading {  max-width: min(65ch, 100% - var(--s-4));  margin-inline: auto;}</code></pre>  <div class="callout info">    <p class="t-small">The middle value grows with the viewport. The outer two are your floor and ceiling. Say goodbye to a stack of breakpoints for type.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">CSS min() max() and clamp() together</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">One line that scales between two honest bounds.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>h1 {  font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 4vw + 1rem, 3rem);  line-height: 1.15;}/* min() picks the smaller, max() the larger */.sidebar {  width: min(280px, 30%);}.reading {  max-width: min(65ch, 100% - var(--s-4));  margin-inline: auto;}</code></pre>  <div class="callout info">    <p class="t-small">The middle value grows with the viewport. The outer two are your floor and ceiling. Say goodbye to a stack of breakpoints for type.</p>  </div></div>	-
32.14.0	css_snippet/CSS scrollbar-color and scrollbar-width	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">CSS scrollbar-color and scrollbar-width</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">Friendly, standard properties instead of <code>::-webkit-scrollbar</code>.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>html {  scrollbar-color: var(--accent) var(--panel-2);  scrollbar-width: thin;}.scroller {  max-height: 12rem;  overflow: auto;  scrollbar-gutter: stable;}</code></pre>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What each part does</summary>    <div class="body">      <p class="t-small"><strong>scrollbar-color</strong> takes a thumb and a track colour. <strong>scrollbar-width</strong> takes <em>auto</em>, <em>thin</em>, or <em>none</em>. Widely supported now, so you can retire the vendor dance.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">CSS scrollbar-color and scrollbar-width</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">Friendly, standard properties instead of <code>::-webkit-scrollbar</code>.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>html {  scrollbar-color: var(--accent) var(--panel-2);  scrollbar-width: thin;}.scroller {  max-height: 12rem;  overflow: auto;  scrollbar-gutter: stable;}</code></pre>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What each part does</summary>    <div class="body">      <p class="t-small"><strong>scrollbar-color</strong> takes a thumb and a track colour. <strong>scrollbar-width</strong> takes <em>auto</em>, <em>thin</em>, or <em>none</em>. Widely supported now, so you can retire the vendor dance.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
32.15.0	css_snippet/CSS @layer for taming specificity	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">CSS @layer for taming specificity</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">Later layers win, no matter how specific the selector.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>@layer reset, base, utilities;@layer base {  .btn { background: var(--panel-2); }}@layer utilities {  .btn.primary { background: var(--accent); }}/* works too */@layer base {  h2 { font-size: var(--t-4); }}</code></pre>  <div class="callout good">    <p class="t-small">Order in the first line decides who wins. Keep that list short-ish and your cascade becomes a tidy little stack.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">CSS @layer for taming specificity</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">Later layers win, no matter how specific the selector.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>@layer reset, base, utilities;@layer base {  .btn { background: var(--panel-2); }}@layer utilities {  .btn.primary { background: var(--accent); }}/* works too */@layer base {  h2 { font-size: var(--t-4); }}</code></pre>  <div class="callout good">    <p class="t-small">Order in the first line decides who wins. Keep that list short-ish and your cascade becomes a tidy little stack.</p>  </div></div>	-
32.16.0	css_snippet/CSS text-wrap: balance and pretty	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">CSS text-wrap: balance and pretty</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">Let the browser nudge line breaks into place.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>h1, h2, h3 {  text-wrap: balance;  max-width: 30ch;}p {  text-wrap: pretty;}.badge {  text-wrap: nowrap;}</code></pre>  <ul class="list t-small">    <li><strong>balance</strong> &ndash; evens out short, multi-line blocks like headings.</li>    <li><strong>pretty</strong> &ndash; keeps the last line from being a lonely word.</li>    <li><strong>nowrap</strong> &ndash; handy for pills and labels.</li>  </ul></div>	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">CSS text-wrap: balance and pretty</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">Let the browser nudge line breaks into place.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>h1, h2, h3 {  text-wrap: balance;  max-width: 30ch;}p {  text-wrap: pretty;}.badge {  text-wrap: nowrap;}</code></pre>  <ul class="list t-small">    <li><strong>balance</strong> &ndash; evens out short, multi-line blocks like headings.</li>    <li><strong>pretty</strong> &ndash; keeps the last line from being a lonely word.</li>    <li><strong>nowrap</strong> &ndash; handy for pills and labels.</li>  </ul></div>	-
32.17.0	css_snippet/CSS :focus-visible for keyboard users	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">CSS :focus-visible for keyboard users</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">A ring for tabbers, quiet for clickers.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>.btn:focus-visible,.link:focus-visible {  outline: 2px solid var(--accent);  outline-offset: 2px;  border-radius: var(--r-sm);}.btn:focus:not(:focus-visible) {  outline: none;}</code></pre>  <details class="acc">    <summary>The short version</summary>    <div class="body">      <p class="t-small"><code>:focus-visible</code> fires when the user is likely keyboarding. The second rule silences the ring after a mouse click. Everyone stays happy.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">CSS :focus-visible for keyboard users</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">A ring for tabbers, quiet for clickers.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>.btn:focus-visible,.link:focus-visible {  outline: 2px solid var(--accent);  outline-offset: 2px;  border-radius: var(--r-sm);}.btn:focus:not(:focus-visible) {  outline: none;}</code></pre>  <details class="acc">    <summary>The short version</summary>    <div class="body">      <p class="t-small"><code>:focus-visible</code> fires when the user is likely keyboarding. The second rule silences the ring after a mouse click. Everyone stays happy.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
32.18.0	css_snippet/CSS :is() and :where() for compact selectors	<div class="card"><h4>CSS :is() and :where() for compact selectors</h4><pre class="code"><code>/* before */.card &gt; .title,.panel &gt; .title,.sheet &gt; .title { color: var(--ink); }/* after */:is(.card, .panel, .sheet) &gt; .title { color: var(--ink); }/* :where() = zero specificity, easy overrides */:where(.card, .panel, .sheet) &gt; .title { color: var(--ink); }</code></pre><p class="t-small dim">:is() keeps the highest specificity of its arguments. :where() throws it all away (0,0,0), so later rules win without a fight.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>CSS :is() and :where() for compact selectors</h4><pre class="code"><code>/* before */.card &gt; .title,.panel &gt; .title,.sheet &gt; .title { color: var(--ink); }/* after */:is(.card, .panel, .sheet) &gt; .title { color: var(--ink); }/* :where() = zero specificity, easy overrides */:where(.card, .panel, .sheet) &gt; .title { color: var(--ink); }</code></pre><p class="t-small dim">:is() keeps the highest specificity of its arguments. :where() throws it all away (0,0,0), so later rules win without a fight.</p></div>	-
32.19.0	css_snippet/CSS @property for typed custom properties	<div class="card"><h4>CSS @property for typed custom properties</h4><pre class="code"><code>@property --angle {  syntax: '&lt;angle&gt;';  inherits: false;  initial-value: 0deg;}.spinner {  --angle: 0deg;  rotate: var(--angle);  transition: --angle .6s ease;}.spinner:hover { --angle: 180deg; }</code></pre><p class="t-small dim">Typed via syntax, custom properties can animate. Untyped ones just snap.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>CSS @property for typed custom properties</h4><pre class="code"><code>@property --angle {  syntax: '&lt;angle&gt;';  inherits: false;  initial-value: 0deg;}.spinner {  --angle: 0deg;  rotate: var(--angle);  transition: --angle .6s ease;}.spinner:hover { --angle: 180deg; }</code></pre><p class="t-small dim">Typed via syntax, custom properties can animate. Untyped ones just snap.</p></div>	-
32.20.0	css_snippet/CSS background-clip: text for gradient headings	<div class="card"><h4>CSS background-clip: text for gradient headings</h4><pre class="code"><code>.gradient-text {  background: linear-gradient(90deg, var(--accent), var(--accent-2));  -webkit-background-clip: text;  background-clip: text;  color: transparent;  /* fallback for forced-colors */}@media (forced-colors: active) {  .gradient-text { color: CanvasText; background: none; }}</code></pre><p class="t-small dim">Paint the gradient, clip it to the glyphs, then hide the fill. The media query keeps it readable in high-contrast mode.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>CSS background-clip: text for gradient headings</h4><pre class="code"><code>.gradient-text {  background: linear-gradient(90deg, var(--accent), var(--accent-2));  -webkit-background-clip: text;  background-clip: text;  color: transparent;  /* fallback for forced-colors */}@media (forced-colors: active) {  .gradient-text { color: CanvasText; background: none; }}</code></pre><p class="t-small dim">Paint the gradient, clip it to the glyphs, then hide the fill. The media query keeps it readable in high-contrast mode.</p></div>	-
32.21.0	css_snippet/CSS writing-mode for vertical text	<div class="card"><h4>CSS writing-mode for vertical text</h4><pre class="code"><code>.vertical-label {  writing-mode: vertical-rl;  text-orientation: mixed;  /* or upright for CJK */  letter-spacing: .12em;  text-transform: uppercase;  font-size: var(--t-2);  color: var(--ink-2);}</code></pre><p class="t-small dim">Beats rotate() for accessibility: screen readers still read the string naturally, and logical properties keep padding sane.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>CSS writing-mode for vertical text</h4><pre class="code"><code>.vertical-label {  writing-mode: vertical-rl;  text-orientation: mixed;  /* or upright for CJK */  letter-spacing: .12em;  text-transform: uppercase;  font-size: var(--t-2);  color: var(--ink-2);}</code></pre><p class="t-small dim">Beats rotate() for accessibility: screen readers still read the string naturally, and logical properties keep padding sane.</p></div>	-
32.22.0	css_snippet/CSS mix-blend-mode for overlay effects	<div class="card"><h4>CSS mix-blend-mode for overlay effects</h4><pre class="code"><code>.hero {  position: relative;  background: var(--bg-alt);}.hero::before {  content: '';  position: absolute;  inset: 0;  background: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--accent), transparent);  mix-blend-mode: overlay;  pointer-events: none;}.hero &gt; * { position: relative; }</code></pre><p class="t-small dim">Blends the pseudo-element with whatever sits behind it. Keep the overlay pointer-events: none so links still click.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>CSS mix-blend-mode for overlay effects</h4><pre class="code"><code>.hero {  position: relative;  background: var(--bg-alt);}.hero::before {  content: '';  position: absolute;  inset: 0;  background: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--accent), transparent);  mix-blend-mode: overlay;  pointer-events: none;}.hero &gt; * { position: relative; }</code></pre><p class="t-small dim">Blends the pseudo-element with whatever sits behind it. Keep the overlay pointer-events: none so links still click.</p></div>	-
32.23.0	css_snippet/CSS will-change for smoother animations	<div class="card"><h4>CSS will-change for smoother animations</h4><pre class="code"><code>.slider {  transition: transform .3s ease;}.slider.is-active {  will-change: transform;}/* better: only while interacting */.slider:hover,.slider:focus-visible {  will-change: transform;}</code></pre><p class="t-small dim">Hints the browser to promote the layer. Add it just before motion starts, remove it after. Leaning on it forever eats memory.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>CSS will-change for smoother animations</h4><pre class="code"><code>.slider {  transition: transform .3s ease;}.slider.is-active {  will-change: transform;}/* better: only while interacting */.slider:hover,.slider:focus-visible {  will-change: transform;}</code></pre><p class="t-small dim">Hints the browser to promote the layer. Add it just before motion starts, remove it after. Leaning on it forever eats memory.</p></div>	-
32.24.0	css_snippet/CSS :disabled and :enabled for form controls	<div class="card"><h4>CSS :disabled and :enabled</h4><pre class="code"><code>input:disabled,button:disabled {  opacity: 0.5;  cursor: not-allowed;  background: var(--bg-alt);  color: var(--ink-3);  border-color: var(--line);}input:enabled,button:enabled {  background: var(--panel);  color: var(--ink);  border: 1px solid var(--line-2);}input:enabled:focus-visible {  outline: 2px solid var(--accent);  outline-offset: 2px;}</code></pre><p class="muted">Only works on native controls. Add <code>disabled</code> in HTML.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>CSS :disabled and :enabled</h4><pre class="code"><code>input:disabled,button:disabled {  opacity: 0.5;  cursor: not-allowed;  background: var(--bg-alt);  color: var(--ink-3);  border-color: var(--line);}input:enabled,button:enabled {  background: var(--panel);  color: var(--ink);  border: 1px solid var(--line-2);}input:enabled:focus-visible {  outline: 2px solid var(--accent);  outline-offset: 2px;}</code></pre><p class="muted">Only works on native controls. Add <code>disabled</code> in HTML.</p></div>	-
32.25.0	css_snippet/CSS :required and :optional for form fields	<div class="card"><h4>CSS :required and :optional</h4><pre class="code"><code>input:required {  border-left: 3px solid var(--warn);}input:required + label::after {  content: " *";  color: var(--bad);}input:optional {  border-left: 3px solid var(--line-2);}/* Reduce motion for any animation */@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {  input:required {    transition: none;  }}</code></pre><p class="muted">Mirror this to <code>select</code> and <code>textarea</code>.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>CSS :required and :optional</h4><pre class="code"><code>input:required {  border-left: 3px solid var(--warn);}input:required + label::after {  content: " *";  color: var(--bad);}input:optional {  border-left: 3px solid var(--line-2);}/* Reduce motion for any animation */@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {  input:required {    transition: none;  }}</code></pre><p class="muted">Mirror this to <code>select</code> and <code>textarea</code>.</p></div>	-
32.26.0	css_snippet/CSS :read-only and :read-write for form fields	<div class="card"><h4>CSS :read-only and :read-write</h4><pre class="code"><code>input:read-only,textarea:read-only {  background: var(--bg-alt);  color: var(--ink-3);  border-style: dashed;  cursor: default;}input:read-write,textarea:read-write {  background: var(--panel);  color: var(--ink);  border: 1px solid var(--line-2);}/* contenteditable also matches :read-write */[contenteditable]:read-write {  outline: 1px dashed var(--accent);}</code></pre><p class="muted">Use <code>readonly</code> for values you show but don't accept edits to.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>CSS :read-only and :read-write</h4><pre class="code"><code>input:read-only,textarea:read-only {  background: var(--bg-alt);  color: var(--ink-3);  border-style: dashed;  cursor: default;}input:read-write,textarea:read-write {  background: var(--panel);  color: var(--ink);  border: 1px solid var(--line-2);}/* contenteditable also matches :read-write */[contenteditable]:read-write {  outline: 1px dashed var(--accent);}</code></pre><p class="muted">Use <code>readonly</code> for values you show but don't accept edits to.</p></div>	-
32.27.0	css_snippet/CSS :placeholder-shown for input hints	<div class="card"><h4>CSS :placeholder-shown</h4><pre class="code"><code>input:placeholder-shown {  border-color: var(--line);  background: var(--bg-alt);}/* Hide the floating label while placeholder shows */.field {  position: relative;}.field label {  position: absolute;  top: 50%;  transform: translateY(-50%);  transition: 0.2s ease;  pointer-events: none;}.field:focus-within label,.field input:not(:placeholder-shown) + label {  top: 0;  font-size: 0.75rem;  color: var(--accent);}@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {  .field label { transition: none; }}</code></pre></div>	<div class="card"><h4>CSS :placeholder-shown</h4><pre class="code"><code>input:placeholder-shown {  border-color: var(--line);  background: var(--bg-alt);}/* Hide the floating label while placeholder shows */.field {  position: relative;}.field label {  position: absolute;  top: 50%;  transform: translateY(-50%);  transition: 0.2s ease;  pointer-events: none;}.field:focus-within label,.field input:not(:placeholder-shown) + label {  top: 0;  font-size: 0.75rem;  color: var(--accent);}@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {  .field label { transition: none; }}</code></pre></div>	-
32.28.0	css_snippet/CSS :valid and :invalid for form feedback	<div class="card"><h4>CSS :valid and :invalid</h4><pre class="code"><code>input:valid {  border-color: var(--good);}input:invalid {  border-color: var(--bad);}/* Only show after interaction */input:not(:focus):invalid {  background: var(--bad-soft);}input:invalid + .error-msg {  display: block;  color: var(--bad);}.error-msg { display: none; }</code></pre><p class="muted">Combine with <code>:user-invalid</code> when available for better timing.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>CSS :valid and :invalid</h4><pre class="code"><code>input:valid {  border-color: var(--good);}input:invalid {  border-color: var(--bad);}/* Only show after interaction */input:not(:focus):invalid {  background: var(--bad-soft);}input:invalid + .error-msg {  display: block;  color: var(--bad);}.error-msg { display: none; }</code></pre><p class="muted">Combine with <code>:user-invalid</code> when available for better timing.</p></div>	-
32.29.0	css_snippet/CSS :in-range and :out-of-range for number inputs	<div class="card"><h4>CSS :in-range and :out-of-range</h4><pre class="code"><code>input[type="number"]:in-range {  border-color: var(--good);  background: var(--good-soft);}input[type="number"]:out-of-range {  border-color: var(--bad);  background: var(--bad-soft);}input[type="number"]:out-of-range + .hint {  color: var(--bad);}.hint { color: var(--ink-3); }</code></pre><p class="muted">Set <code>min</code> and <code>max</code> on the input for this to work.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>CSS :in-range and :out-of-range</h4><pre class="code"><code>input[type="number"]:in-range {  border-color: var(--good);  background: var(--good-soft);}input[type="number"]:out-of-range {  border-color: var(--bad);  background: var(--bad-soft);}input[type="number"]:out-of-range + .hint {  color: var(--bad);}.hint { color: var(--ink-3); }</code></pre><p class="muted">Set <code>min</code> and <code>max</code> on the input for this to work.</p></div>	-
32.30.0	css_snippet/CSS image-rendering for pixel-art scaling	<div class="card"><h4>CSS snippet</h4><p class="muted t-small">Keep pixel art crunchy at any zoom <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><pre class="code"><code>/* Scale up small pixel art without smoothing */.sprite {  width: 8rem;  height: 8rem;  image-rendering: pixelated;  /* crisp-edges for browsers that dislike pixelated */  image-rendering: -moz-crisp-edges;}/* Downscaling photos? Use auto or high-quality */.photo {  image-rendering: auto;}</code></pre><p class="dim t-small"><code>pixelated</code> for upscaling, <code>auto</code> for real pictures.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>CSS snippet</h4><p class="muted t-small">Keep pixel art crunchy at any zoom .</p><pre class="code"><code>/* Scale up small pixel art without smoothing */.sprite {  width: 8rem;  height: 8rem;  image-rendering: pixelated;  /* crisp-edges for browsers that dislike pixelated */  image-rendering: -moz-crisp-edges;}/* Downscaling photos? Use auto or high-quality */.photo {  image-rendering: auto;}</code></pre><p class="dim t-small"><code>pixelated</code> for upscaling, <code>auto</code> for real pictures.</p></div>	-
32.31.0	css_snippet/CSS object-fit for media that fills its box	<div class="card"><h4>CSS snippet</h4><p class="muted t-small">Fit media into a box without squishing it, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><pre class="code"><code>.thumb {  width: 200px;  height: 200px;  object-fit: cover;      /* crop to fill */  object-position: center; /* focus point */  border-radius: var(--r-2);}/* contain keeps the whole image visible */.contain { object-fit: contain; }</code></pre><p class="dim t-small">Use <code>cover</code> for cards, <code>contain</code> for logos.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>CSS snippet</h4><p class="muted t-small">Fit media into a box without squishing it, .</p><pre class="code"><code>.thumb {  width: 200px;  height: 200px;  object-fit: cover;      /* crop to fill */  object-position: center; /* focus point */  border-radius: var(--r-2);}/* contain keeps the whole image visible */.contain { object-fit: contain; }</code></pre><p class="dim t-small">Use <code>cover</code> for cards, <code>contain</code> for logos.</p></div>	-
32.32.0	css_snippet/CSS scroll-margin-top for sticky headers	<div class="card"><h4>CSS snippet</h4><p class="muted t-small">Stop sticky headers from covering anchor targets, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><pre class="code"><code>/* Reserve room above each anchor target */section[id] {  scroll-margin-top: 5rem;}/* Or globally on any focusable jump target */:target {  scroll-margin-top: var(--s-7);}</code></pre><p class="dim t-small"><code>scroll-margin-top</code> matches your header height.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>CSS snippet</h4><p class="muted t-small">Stop sticky headers from covering anchor targets, .</p><pre class="code"><code>/* Reserve room above each anchor target */section[id] {  scroll-margin-top: 5rem;}/* Or globally on any focusable jump target */:target {  scroll-margin-top: var(--s-7);}</code></pre><p class="dim t-small"><code>scroll-margin-top</code> matches your header height.</p></div>	-
32.33.0	css_snippet/CSS touch-action for swipe without zoom	<div class="card"><h4>CSS snippet</h4><p class="muted t-small">Let swipes work without the page fighting back, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><pre class="code"><code>/* Allow vertical scroll, block horizontal pan */.carousel {  touch-action: pan-y;  overflow-x: auto;  scroll-snap-type: x mandatory;}/* Buttons: no double-tap zoom, still tappable */.btn {  touch-action: manipulation;}</code></pre><p class="dim t-small">Best used on interactive surfaces, not the whole page.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>CSS snippet</h4><p class="muted t-small">Let swipes work without the page fighting back, .</p><pre class="code"><code>/* Allow vertical scroll, block horizontal pan */.carousel {  touch-action: pan-y;  overflow-x: auto;  scroll-snap-type: x mandatory;}/* Buttons: no double-tap zoom, still tappable */.btn {  touch-action: manipulation;}</code></pre><p class="dim t-small">Best used on interactive surfaces, not the whole page.</p></div>	-
32.34.0	css_snippet/CSS content-visibility for faster rendering	<div class="card"><h4>CSS snippet</h4><p class="muted t-small">Skip work on offscreen content, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><pre class="code"><code>.comment {  content-visibility: auto;  /* Give the browser a size hint */  contain-intrinsic-size: 0 120px;}/* Use carefully: search-in-page may skip hidden text */@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {  .comment { content-visibility: visible; }}</code></pre><p class="dim t-small">Great for long feeds. Test with your own content.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>CSS snippet</h4><p class="muted t-small">Skip work on offscreen content, .</p><pre class="code"><code>.comment {  content-visibility: auto;  /* Give the browser a size hint */  contain-intrinsic-size: 0 120px;}/* Use carefully: search-in-page may skip hidden text */@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {  .comment { content-visibility: visible; }}</code></pre><p class="dim t-small">Great for long feeds. Test with your own content.</p></div>	-
32.35.0	css_snippet/CSS gap fallback for flexbox spacing	<div class="card"><h4>CSS snippet</h4><p class="muted t-small">Spacing that survives old Safari, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><pre class="code"><code>/* Base spacing via margins */.row > * + * {  margin-left: var(--s-3);}/* Modern browsers: switch to gap */@supports (gap: 1rem) {  .row {    gap: var(--s-3);  }  .row > * + * {    margin-left: 0;  }}</code></pre><p class="dim t-small"><code>@supports</code> keeps it honest for older engines.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>CSS snippet</h4><p class="muted t-small">Spacing that survives old Safari, .</p><pre class="code"><code>/* Base spacing via margins */.row > * + * {  margin-left: var(--s-3);}/* Modern browsers: switch to gap */@supports (gap: 1rem) {  .row {    gap: var(--s-3);  }  .row > * + * {    margin-left: 0;  }}</code></pre><p class="dim t-small"><code>@supports</code> keeps it honest for older engines.</p></div>	-
32.36.0	css_snippet/CSS :autofill for browser-filled fields	<div class="card">  <h4>CSS :autofill</h4>  <p class="muted">Browsers paint a yellow (or, in dark mode, a washed-out) background behind fields they fill for you. Outrun that with <code>:autofill</code>.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>input:autofill {  background-color: var(--panel-2);  color: var(--ink);  -webkit-text-fill-color: var(--ink);  -webkit-box-shadow: 0 0 0 1000px var(--panel-2) inset;}/* Safari needs the vendor prefix */input:-webkit-autofill {  -webkit-text-fill-color: var(--ink);  transition: background-color 9999s;}</code></pre>  <p class="t-small dim">The <code>-webkit-box-shadow</code> trick paints over the browser's yellow without fighting the animation.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <h4>CSS :autofill</h4>  <p class="muted">Browsers paint a yellow (or, in dark mode, a washed-out) background behind fields they fill for you. Outrun that with <code>:autofill</code>.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>input:autofill {  background-color: var(--panel-2);  color: var(--ink);  -webkit-text-fill-color: var(--ink);  -webkit-box-shadow: 0 0 0 1000px var(--panel-2) inset;}/* Safari needs the vendor prefix */input:-webkit-autofill {  -webkit-text-fill-color: var(--ink);  transition: background-color 9999s;}</code></pre>  <p class="t-small dim">The <code>-webkit-box-shadow</code> trick paints over the browser's yellow without fighting the animation.</p></div>	-
32.37.0	css_snippet/CSS ruby-align for elegant annotations	<div class="card">  <h4>CSS ruby-align</h4>  <p class="muted">For East Asian typography, <code>&lt;ruby&gt;</code> pairs base text with small annotations. <code>ruby-align</code> decides where those annotations sit.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>ruby {  ruby-align: center; /* default per spec varies */}/* Push annotations to the start of each base */.furigana-start ruby {  ruby-align: start;}/* Distribute across the full base width */.furigana-wide ruby {  ruby-align: space-between;}</code></pre>  <p class="t-small dim">Use <code>center</code> for balanced look, <code>start</code> for dictionary-style columns. Check the browser: support is still spotty outside Chromium.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <h4>CSS ruby-align</h4>  <p class="muted">For East Asian typography, <code>&lt;ruby&gt;</code> pairs base text with small annotations. <code>ruby-align</code> decides where those annotations sit.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>ruby {  ruby-align: center; /* default per spec varies */}/* Push annotations to the start of each base */.furigana-start ruby {  ruby-align: start;}/* Distribute across the full base width */.furigana-wide ruby {  ruby-align: space-between;}</code></pre>  <p class="t-small dim">Use <code>center</code> for balanced look, <code>start</code> for dictionary-style columns. Check the browser: support is still spotty outside Chromium.</p></div>	-
32.38.0	css_snippet/CSS font-variant-numeric for tabular numbers	<div class="card">  <h4>CSS font-variant-numeric</h4>  <p class="muted">Numbers in most fonts are proportional, so <strong>1</strong> and <strong>8</strong> take different widths. Tables wiggle. Flip them to tabular figures.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>table .num {  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;}/* Oldstyle figures for body text */.prose {  font-variant-numeric: oldstyle-nums;}/* Fractions and ordinals anyone? */.recipe .ratio {  font-variant-numeric: diagonal-fractions;}</code></pre>  <p class="t-small dim">Works in any font that offers the feature; silently ignored otherwise. Pair with <code>text-align: right</code> for clean columns.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <h4>CSS font-variant-numeric</h4>  <p class="muted">Numbers in most fonts are proportional, so <strong>1</strong> and <strong>8</strong> take different widths. Tables wiggle. Flip them to tabular figures.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>table .num {  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;}/* Oldstyle figures for body text */.prose {  font-variant-numeric: oldstyle-nums;}/* Fractions and ordinals anyone? */.recipe .ratio {  font-variant-numeric: diagonal-fractions;}</code></pre>  <p class="t-small dim">Works in any font that offers the feature; silently ignored otherwise. Pair with <code>text-align: right</code> for clean columns.</p></div>	-
32.39.0	css_snippet/CSS caret-color for custom cursors	<div class="card">  <h4>CSS caret-color</h4>  <p class="muted">The blinking text cursor inside inputs inherits <code>color</code> by default, which can vanish on dark panels. Nudge it with <code>caret-color</code>.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>input, textarea, [contenteditable] {  caret-color: var(--accent);}/* Or hide it entirely (accessibility risk!) */.no-caret {  caret-color: transparent;}</code></pre>  <p class="t-small dim">Prefer your accent over hiding. A transparent caret is a small cruelty to keyboard users.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <h4>CSS caret-color</h4>  <p class="muted">The blinking text cursor inside inputs inherits <code>color</code> by default, which can vanish on dark panels. Nudge it with <code>caret-color</code>.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>input, textarea, [contenteditable] {  caret-color: var(--accent);}/* Or hide it entirely (accessibility risk!) */.no-caret {  caret-color: transparent;}</code></pre>  <p class="t-small dim">Prefer your accent over hiding. A transparent caret is a small cruelty to keyboard users.</p></div>	-
32.40.0	css_snippet/CSS forced-color-adjust for high-contrast mode	<div class="card">  <h4>CSS forced-color-adjust</h4>  <p class="muted">Windows High Contrast Mode strips your palette and swaps in system colors. Usually that's good. Sometimes your logo or chart needs to keep its own colors.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>@media (forced-colors: active) {  .card {    border: 1px solid CanvasText;  }  .btn {    forced-color-adjust: auto;  }  .badge.accent {    forced-color-adjust: none;    color: var(--accent);  }}</code></pre>  <p class="t-small dim">Use <code>CanvasText</code>, <code>Canvas</code>, <code>LinkText</code> system colours so things stay legible. Apply <code>none</code> sparingly.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <h4>CSS forced-color-adjust</h4>  <p class="muted">Windows High Contrast Mode strips your palette and swaps in system colors. Usually that's good. Sometimes your logo or chart needs to keep its own colors.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>@media (forced-colors: active) {  .card {    border: 1px solid CanvasText;  }  .btn {    forced-color-adjust: auto;  }  .badge.accent {    forced-color-adjust: none;    color: var(--accent);  }}</code></pre>  <p class="t-small dim">Use <code>CanvasText</code>, <code>Canvas</code>, <code>LinkText</code> system colours so things stay legible. Apply <code>none</code> sparingly.</p></div>	-
32.41.0	css_snippet/CSS ::backdrop for dialog and fullscreen	<div class="card">  <h4>CSS ::backdrop</h4>  <p class="muted">The <code>::backdrop</code> pseudo-element paints behind a <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code> or a fullscreened element. Great for a blurred scrim.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>dialog::backdrop {  background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--ink) 60%, transparent);  backdrop-filter: blur(6px);}video::backdrop {  background: black;}</code></pre>  <p class="t-small dim">Keep the contrast high enough that the dialog's own text stays readable. If <code>backdrop-filter</code> hurts performance on phones, drop it and just darken.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <h4>CSS ::backdrop</h4>  <p class="muted">The <code>::backdrop</code> pseudo-element paints behind a <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code> or a fullscreened element. Great for a blurred scrim.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>dialog::backdrop {  background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--ink) 60%, transparent);  backdrop-filter: blur(6px);}video::backdrop {  background: black;}</code></pre>  <p class="t-small dim">Keep the contrast high enough that the dialog's own text stays readable. If <code>backdrop-filter</code> hurts performance on phones, drop it and just darken.</p></div>	-
32.42.0	css_snippet/CSS scroll-driven animations	<div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Scroll-driven progress</h4><p class="muted">CSS now animates on scroll—no JS. Use <code>animation-timeline: scroll()</code> on a progress bar.</p><pre class="code"><code>.progress{inline-size:100%;block-size:6px;background:var(--line);border-radius:var(--r-pill);overflow:hidden}.progress > span{display:block;block-size:100%;background:var(--accent);inline-size:0%  ;animation:grow linear;animation-timeline:scroll(root);}@keyframes grow{from{inline-size:0%}to{inline-size:100%}}</code></pre><div class="callout info"><span class="badge info"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</span> Supported in modern browsers. Fallback: set a default width, progress just won't animate.</div></div>	<div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Scroll-driven progress</h4><p class="muted">CSS now animates on scroll—no JS. Use <code>animation-timeline: scroll()</code> on a progress bar.</p><pre class="code"><code>.progress{inline-size:100%;block-size:6px;background:var(--line);border-radius:var(--r-pill);overflow:hidden}.progress > span{display:block;block-size:100%;background:var(--accent);inline-size:0%  ;animation:grow linear;animation-timeline:scroll(root);}@keyframes grow{from{inline-size:0%}to{inline-size:100%}}</code></pre><div class="callout info"><span class="badge info"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</span> Supported in modern browsers. Fallback: set a default width, progress just won't animate.</div></div>	-
32.43.0	css_snippet/CSS @starting-style for entry animations	<div class="card"><h4>Animate on entry with @starting-style</h4><p>Your element can fade in the moment it renders—no JS needed. <code>@starting-style</code> sets the "from" state.</p><pre class="code"><code>.popover{opacity:1;transform:scale(1);transition:opacity .3s,transform .3s}@starting-style{.popover{opacity:0;transform:scale(.9)}}</code></pre><p class="muted">Pair with <code>display:none</code> toggles via <code>details</code> or popover API for smooth show/hide.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Animate on entry with @starting-style</h4><p>Your element can fade in the moment it renders—no JS needed. <code>@starting-style</code> sets the "from" state.</p><pre class="code"><code>.popover{opacity:1;transform:scale(1);transition:opacity .3s,transform .3s}@starting-style{.popover{opacity:0;transform:scale(.9)}}</code></pre><p class="muted">Pair with <code>display:none</code> toggles via <code>details</code> or popover API for smooth show/hide.</p></div>	-
32.44.0	css_snippet/CSS subgrid for aligned cards	<div class="card"><h4>CSS subgrid</h4><p>Row alignment made easy. Subgrid lets a child grid use its parent's tracks.</p><pre class="code"><code>.grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);grid-template-rows:auto auto auto;gap:var(--s-3)}.card{grid-row:span 3;display:grid;grid-template-rows:subgrid}.card h3{grid-row:1}.card p{grid-row:2}.card footer{grid-row:3}</code></pre><p class="muted">Headers, bodies, and footers now line up perfectly across cards.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>CSS subgrid</h4><p>Row alignment made easy. Subgrid lets a child grid use its parent's tracks.</p><pre class="code"><code>.grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);grid-template-rows:auto auto auto;gap:var(--s-3)}.card{grid-row:span 3;display:grid;grid-template-rows:subgrid}.card h3{grid-row:1}.card p{grid-row:2}.card footer{grid-row:3}</code></pre><p class="muted">Headers, bodies, and footers now line up perfectly across cards.</p></div>	-
32.45.0	css_snippet/CSS view transitions	<div class="card"><h4>View Transitions</h4><p>Smoothly morph elements between states or pages—with just CSS.</p><pre class="code"><code>.card img{view-transition-name: hero}::view-transition-old(hero),::view-transition-new(hero){animation-duration:.4s}</code></pre><p class="muted">Works with <code>document.startViewTransition</code> or cross-document navigation in modern browsers.</p><div class="callout info"><span class="badge info"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</span> Chrome and Edge support; Safari and Firefox are catching up.</div></div>	<div class="card"><h4>View Transitions</h4><p>Smoothly morph elements between states or pages—with just CSS.</p><pre class="code"><code>.card img{view-transition-name: hero}::view-transition-old(hero),::view-transition-new(hero){animation-duration:.4s}</code></pre><p class="muted">Works with <code>document.startViewTransition</code> or cross-document navigation in modern browsers.</p><div class="callout info"><span class="badge info"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</span> Chrome and Edge support; Safari and Firefox are catching up.</div></div>	-
32.46.0	css_snippet/CSS scrollbar-gutter for stable layout	<div class="card"><h4>Stable layout with scrollbar-gutter</h4><p>No more jumps when the scrollbar pops in. Reserve its space upfront.</p><pre class="code"><code>html{scrollbar-gutter:stable}</code></pre><p>Or use <code>stable both-edges</code> to keep centering consistent when content grows.</p><div class="callout good"><span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</span> Supported in all modern browsers except Safari (as of 2025).</div></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Stable layout with scrollbar-gutter</h4><p>No more jumps when the scrollbar pops in. Reserve its space upfront.</p><pre class="code"><code>html{scrollbar-gutter:stable}</code></pre><p>Or use <code>stable both-edges</code> to keep centering consistent when content grows.</p><div class="callout good"><span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</span> Supported in all modern browsers except Safari (as of 2025).</div></div>	-
32.47.0	css_snippet/CSS @scope for scoped styling	<div class="card"><h4>CSS @scope</h4><p>Style elements within a boundary—without extra classes or shadow DOM.</p><pre class="code"><code>@scope (.card) to (.card-footer){p{color:var(--ink-2);margin-block:var(--s-2)}}</code></pre><p class="muted">Only paragraphs inside <code>.card</code> but before <code>.card-footer</code> get styled.</p><div class="callout info"><span class="badge info"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</span> Chrome and Edge support; others may need fallback class-based selectors.</div></div>	<div class="card"><h4>CSS @scope</h4><p>Style elements within a boundary—without extra classes or shadow DOM.</p><pre class="code"><code>@scope (.card) to (.card-footer){p{color:var(--ink-2);margin-block:var(--s-2)}}</code></pre><p class="muted">Only paragraphs inside <code>.card</code> but before <code>.card-footer</code> get styled.</p><div class="callout info"><span class="badge info"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</span> Chrome and Edge support; others may need fallback class-based selectors.</div></div>	-
32.48.0	css_snippet/CSS color-mix() for tinted UI	<div class="card"><h4>CSS color-mix()</h4><p class="muted t-small">Derive hover and tint colors from one token.</p><pre class="code"><code>:root {  --accent: #4f46e5;}.btn {  background: var(--accent);  transition: background 160ms ease;}.btn:hover {  /* 12% white, 88% accent */  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent), white 12%);}.btn-soft {  /* pale tint on any theme */  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 15%, var(--panel));  color: var(--accent);}</code></pre></div>	<div class="card"><h4>CSS color-mix()</h4><p class="muted t-small">Derive hover and tint colors from one token.</p><pre class="code"><code>:root {  --accent: #4f46e5;}.btn {  background: var(--accent);  transition: background 160ms ease;}.btn:hover {  /* 12% white, 88% accent */  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent), white 12%);}.btn-soft {  /* pale tint on any theme */  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 15%, var(--panel));  color: var(--accent);}</code></pre></div>	-
32.49.0	css_snippet/CSS spacing with ​clamp()​ in container	<div class="card"><h4>Fluid spacing with clamp()</h4><p class="muted t-small">One line replaces three breakpoints.</p><pre class="code"><code>:root {  /* min 16px, preferred 4vw, max 48px */  --section-pad: clamp(1rem, 4vw, 3rem);  --card-gap: clamp(0.75rem, 2vw, 1.5rem);}.section {  padding: var(--section-pad);}.card-grid {  display: grid;  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(220px, 1fr));  gap: var(--card-gap);}</code></pre></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Fluid spacing with clamp()</h4><p class="muted t-small">One line replaces three breakpoints.</p><pre class="code"><code>:root {  /* min 16px, preferred 4vw, max 48px */  --section-pad: clamp(1rem, 4vw, 3rem);  --card-gap: clamp(0.75rem, 2vw, 1.5rem);}.section {  padding: var(--section-pad);}.card-grid {  display: grid;  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(220px, 1fr));  gap: var(--card-gap);}</code></pre></div>	-
