32.59.0	css_snippet/CSS :has() for parent styling	<div class="card">  <h4>CSS :has() — style the parent</h4>  <p class="muted">Now you can target a parent based on what's inside it.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>/* Card gets a ring when it has a badge */.card:has(.badge) {  outline: 2px solid var(--accent);  outline-offset: 2px;}/* Form group turns red when its input is invalid */.field:has(input:invalid) {  border-color: var(--bad);}/* Hide label when placeholder is shown */.field:has(input:placeholder-shown) label {  opacity: 0.5;}</code></pre>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Check browser support</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Supported in all modern browsers (Chrome 105+, Safari 15.4+, Firefox 121+). For older browsers, use a JavaScript fallback or add a class manually.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="card">  <h4>CSS :has() — style the parent</h4>  <p class="muted">Now you can target a parent based on what's inside it.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>/* Card gets a ring when it has a badge */.card:has(.badge) {  outline: 2px solid var(--accent);  outline-offset: 2px;}/* Form group turns red when its input is invalid */.field:has(input:invalid) {  border-color: var(--bad);}/* Hide label when placeholder is shown */.field:has(input:placeholder-shown) label {  opacity: 0.5;}</code></pre>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Check browser support</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Supported in all modern browsers (Chrome 105+, Safari 15.4+, Firefox 121+). For older browsers, use a JavaScript fallback or add a class manually.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
