32.0.1	css_snippet/CSS conic-gradient for pie charts	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">Conic-gradient donut</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">Hey <regex>user_name</regex>, one declaration, four slices, zero JavaScript.</p>  <div class="row" style="gap:var(--s-5);align-items:center;flex-wrap:wrap">    <div style="--p1:38%;--p2:62%;--p3:84%;width:132px;aspect-ratio:1;border-radius:50%;background:conic-gradient(var(--accent) 0 var(--p1),var(--accent-2) var(--p1) var(--p2),var(--warn) var(--p2) var(--p3),var(--line-2) var(--p3) 100%);-webkit-mask:radial-gradient(circle,transparent 44%,#000 45%);mask:radial-gradient(circle,transparent 44%,#000 45%)"></div>    <ul class="list t-small">      <li><i class="dot" style="background:var(--accent)"></i> Cached 38%</li>      <li><i class="dot" style="background:var(--accent-2)"></i> Fresh 24%</li>      <li><i class="dot" style="background:var(--warn)"></i> Rebuilt 22%</li>      <li><i class="dot" style="background:var(--line-2)"></i> Missing 16%</li>    </ul>  </div>  <pre class="code"><code>.donut{  width:132px;  aspect-ratio:1;  border-radius:50%;  background:conic-gradient(    var(--accent) 0 38%,    var(--accent-2) 38% 62%,    var(--warn) 62% 84%,    var(--line-2) 84% 100%);  /* punch the hole */  mask:radial-gradient(circle,transparent 44%,#000 45%);}</code></pre>  <div class="callout info t-small">Slices are just colour stops. Reorder them and the chart reorders; no layout thrash, no repaint of the ring itself.</div></div>	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">Conic-gradient donut</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">Hey , one declaration, four slices, zero JavaScript.</p>  <div class="row" style="gap:var(--s-5);align-items:center;flex-wrap:wrap">    <div style="--p1:38%;--p2:62%;--p3:84%;width:132px;aspect-ratio:1;border-radius:50%;background:conic-gradient(var(--accent) 0 var(--p1),var(--accent-2) var(--p1) var(--p2),var(--warn) var(--p2) var(--p3),var(--line-2) var(--p3) 100%);-webkit-mask:radial-gradient(circle,transparent 44%,#000 45%);mask:radial-gradient(circle,transparent 44%,#000 45%)"></div>    <ul class="list t-small">      <li><i class="dot" style="background:var(--accent)"></i> Cached 38%</li>      <li><i class="dot" style="background:var(--accent-2)"></i> Fresh 24%</li>      <li><i class="dot" style="background:var(--warn)"></i> Rebuilt 22%</li>      <li><i class="dot" style="background:var(--line-2)"></i> Missing 16%</li>    </ul>  </div>  <pre class="code"><code>.donut{  width:132px;  aspect-ratio:1;  border-radius:50%;  background:conic-gradient(    var(--accent) 0 38%,    var(--accent-2) 38% 62%,    var(--warn) 62% 84%,    var(--line-2) 84% 100%);  /* punch the hole */  mask:radial-gradient(circle,transparent 44%,#000 45%);}</code></pre>  <div class="callout info t-small">Slices are just colour stops. Reorder them and the chart reorders; no layout thrash, no repaint of the ring itself.</div></div>	-
32.1.1	css_snippet/CSS env() for safe-area insets	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">env() safe-area insets</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">Right, <regex>user_name</regex> — that home indicator is eating your tab bar. Tell the viewport to stop being shy, then pad around the cutouts.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>/* in the head, once */&lt;meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1,viewport-fit=cover"&gt;/* then, in CSS */.tabbar{  position:fixed;  inset:auto 0 0 0;  padding:var(--s-3);  padding-bottom:calc(var(--s-3) + env(safe-area-inset-bottom, 0px));  background:var(--panel);  border-top:1px solid var(--line);}/* pair it with a fallback so short-landscape phones   don't get double padding */@media (orientation:landscape){  .tabbar{padding-left:env(safe-area-inset-left,0px);          padding-right:env(safe-area-inset-right,0px)}}</code></pre>  <ul class="list t-small">    <li><kbd>viewport-fit=cover</kbd> lets content reach the edges at all.</li>    <li>Always ship the second argument — <kbd>env(x, 0px)</kbd> — old browsers ignore the whole thing without it.</li>    <li>Insets are live: rotate the phone and the numbers move.</li>  </ul>  <div class="callout warn t-small">Don't put the padding on <kbd>body</kbd>. You'll fight overscroll and rubber-banding on iOS.</div></div>	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">env() safe-area insets</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">Right,  — that home indicator is eating your tab bar. Tell the viewport to stop being shy, then pad around the cutouts.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>/* in the head, once */&lt;meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1,viewport-fit=cover"&gt;/* then, in CSS */.tabbar{  position:fixed;  inset:auto 0 0 0;  padding:var(--s-3);  padding-bottom:calc(var(--s-3) + env(safe-area-inset-bottom, 0px));  background:var(--panel);  border-top:1px solid var(--line);}/* pair it with a fallback so short-landscape phones   don't get double padding */@media (orientation:landscape){  .tabbar{padding-left:env(safe-area-inset-left,0px);          padding-right:env(safe-area-inset-right,0px)}}</code></pre>  <ul class="list t-small">    <li><kbd>viewport-fit=cover</kbd> lets content reach the edges at all.</li>    <li>Always ship the second argument — <kbd>env(x, 0px)</kbd> — old browsers ignore the whole thing without it.</li>    <li>Insets are live: rotate the phone and the numbers move.</li>  </ul>  <div class="callout warn t-small">Don't put the padding on <kbd>body</kbd>. You'll fight overscroll and rubber-banding on iOS.</div></div>	-
32.2.1	css_snippet/CSS ladder for equal-height grid rows	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">Equal-height grid rows</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">Cards in a grid stretch by default, <regex>user_name</regex> — the trouble is usually a stray <kbd>align-items</kbd> or a flex parent stealing the height.</p>  <div class="grid two" style="gap:var(--s-3)">    <div class="card flat" style="margin:0"><strong>Short</strong><p class="muted t-small" style="margin:var(--s-1) 0 0">One line.</p></div>    <div class="card flat" style="margin:0"><strong>Taller</strong><p class="muted t-small" style="margin:var(--s-1) 0 0">Three whole lines of copy so this tile grows taller than its neighbour and shows the problem clearly.</p></div>  </div>  <pre class="code"><code>.grid{  display:grid;  grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(220px,1fr));  /* every row, same height as its tallest cell */  grid-auto-rows:1fr;  gap:var(--s-3);}/* let the tile push its own footer down */.tile{display:flex;flex-direction:column;height:100%}.tile .foot{margin-top:auto}</code></pre>  <ul class="list t-small">    <li><kbd>grid-auto-rows:1fr</kbd> makes the implicit rows match each other.</li>    <li><kbd>align-items:start</kbd> on the grid undoes the stretch — check that first.</li>    <li>A flex or grid parent with a fixed height will still clip children unless you set <kbd>min-height:0</kbd>.</li>  </ul></div>	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">Equal-height grid rows</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">Cards in a grid stretch by default,  — the trouble is usually a stray <kbd>align-items</kbd> or a flex parent stealing the height.</p>  <div class="grid two" style="gap:var(--s-3)">    <div class="card flat" style="margin:0"><strong>Short</strong><p class="muted t-small" style="margin:var(--s-1) 0 0">One line.</p></div>    <div class="card flat" style="margin:0"><strong>Taller</strong><p class="muted t-small" style="margin:var(--s-1) 0 0">Three whole lines of copy so this tile grows taller than its neighbour and shows the problem clearly.</p></div>  </div>  <pre class="code"><code>.grid{  display:grid;  grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(220px,1fr));  /* every row, same height as its tallest cell */  grid-auto-rows:1fr;  gap:var(--s-3);}/* let the tile push its own footer down */.tile{display:flex;flex-direction:column;height:100%}.tile .foot{margin-top:auto}</code></pre>  <ul class="list t-small">    <li><kbd>grid-auto-rows:1fr</kbd> makes the implicit rows match each other.</li>    <li><kbd>align-items:start</kbd> on the grid undoes the stretch — check that first.</li>    <li>A flex or grid parent with a fixed height will still clip children unless you set <kbd>min-height:0</kbd>.</li>  </ul></div>	-
32.3.1	css_snippet/CSS @media (hover: hover) for touch fallback	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">Hover, but only where hover exists</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">Yep, <regex>user_name</regex>, that's the sticky-hover classic: phones fake a hover on tap and forget to let go. Wrap it.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>/* touch first: no hover styles at all */.card{background:var(--panel);border:1px solid var(--line);      transition:transform .18s ease, box-shadow .18s ease}@media (hover:hover) and (pointer:fine){  .card:hover{    transform:translateY(-2px);    box-shadow:0 8px 24px color-mix(in srgb,var(--accent) 16%,transparent);  }}/* and stop the grey tap flash */.tapme{-webkit-tap-highlight-color:transparent}/* anything 'stuck' after a tap: reset in the same query */@media (hover:none){  .card:hover{transform:none;box-shadow:none;background:var(--panel)}}</code></pre>  <ul class="list t-small">    <li><kbd>hover:hover</kbd> — the device can hover. <kbd>pointer:fine</kbd> — it's a mouse or trackpad, not a finger.</li>    <li>Combine with <kbd>&#64;media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)</kbd> if the lift is decorative.</li>    <li>Always give a non-hover cue: a border, a chevron, a colour shift that persists.</li>  </ul>  <div class="callout good t-small">Rule of thumb: hover is a bonus, never the only way to know something is interactive.</div></div>	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">Hover, but only where hover exists</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">Yep, , that's the sticky-hover classic: phones fake a hover on tap and forget to let go. Wrap it.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>/* touch first: no hover styles at all */.card{background:var(--panel);border:1px solid var(--line);      transition:transform .18s ease, box-shadow .18s ease}@media (hover:hover) and (pointer:fine){  .card:hover{    transform:translateY(-2px);    box-shadow:0 8px 24px color-mix(in srgb,var(--accent) 16%,transparent);  }}/* and stop the grey tap flash */.tapme{-webkit-tap-highlight-color:transparent}/* anything 'stuck' after a tap: reset in the same query */@media (hover:none){  .card:hover{transform:none;box-shadow:none;background:var(--panel)}}</code></pre>  <ul class="list t-small">    <li><kbd>hover:hover</kbd> — the device can hover. <kbd>pointer:fine</kbd> — it's a mouse or trackpad, not a finger.</li>    <li>Combine with <kbd>&#64;media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)</kbd> if the lift is decorative.</li>    <li>Always give a non-hover cue: a border, a chevron, a colour shift that persists.</li>  </ul>  <div class="callout good t-small">Rule of thumb: hover is a bonus, never the only way to know something is interactive.</div></div>	-
32.4.1	css_snippet/CSS filter: drop-shadow for non-rectangular shapes	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">Shadows that hug the silhouette</h4>  <p class="muted t-small"><regex>user_name</regex>, <kbd>box-shadow</kbd> only knows rectangles. Clip anything and the shadow stays square and sad.</p>  <div class="row" style="gap:var(--s-5);flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:flex-end">    <div style="position:relative;width:120px;height:88px;background:var(--panel);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:var(--r-m,12px);box-shadow:0 6px 16px rgba(0,0,0,.18)"><span class="t-small muted" style="position:absolute;bottom:var(--s-1);left:var(--s-2)">box-shadow</span></div>    <div style="position:relative;width:120px;height:88px;background:var(--panel);border:1px solid var(--line);clip-path:polygon(0 0,100% 0,100% 74%,34% 74%,20% 100%,0 100%);filter:drop-shadow(0 6px 14px rgba(0,0,0,.22))"><span class="t-small muted" style="position:absolute;bottom:var(--s-4);left:var(--s-2)">drop-shadow</span></div>  </div>  <pre class="code"><code>/* rectangle: cheap, fast, stays inside the box */.plain{box-shadow:0 6px 16px rgb(0 0 0 / .18)}/* any silhouette: follows clip-path, svg, png alpha */.bubble{  clip-path:polygon(0 0,100% 0,100% 74%,34% 74%,20% 100%,0 100%);  filter:drop-shadow(0 6px 14px rgb(0 0 0 / .22));}/* dark mode: shadows read as a lift, not a smudge */@media (prefers-color-scheme:dark){  .bubble{filter:drop-shadow(0 6px 14px rgb(0 0 0 / .55))}}</code></pre>  <div class="callout warn t-small">One cost: <kbd>filter</kbd> makes the element its own compositing layer, and it clips descendants too. Fine for a badge; think twice over a whole scrolling list.</div></div>	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">Shadows that hug the silhouette</h4>  <p class="muted t-small">, <kbd>box-shadow</kbd> only knows rectangles. Clip anything and the shadow stays square and sad.</p>  <div class="row" style="gap:var(--s-5);flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:flex-end">    <div style="position:relative;width:120px;height:88px;background:var(--panel);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:var(--r-m,12px);box-shadow:0 6px 16px rgba(0,0,0,.18)"><span class="t-small muted" style="position:absolute;bottom:var(--s-1);left:var(--s-2)">box-shadow</span></div>    <div style="position:relative;width:120px;height:88px;background:var(--panel);border:1px solid var(--line);clip-path:polygon(0 0,100% 0,100% 74%,34% 74%,20% 100%,0 100%);filter:drop-shadow(0 6px 14px rgba(0,0,0,.22))"><span class="t-small muted" style="position:absolute;bottom:var(--s-4);left:var(--s-2)">drop-shadow</span></div>  </div>  <pre class="code"><code>/* rectangle: cheap, fast, stays inside the box */.plain{box-shadow:0 6px 16px rgb(0 0 0 / .18)}/* any silhouette: follows clip-path, svg, png alpha */.bubble{  clip-path:polygon(0 0,100% 0,100% 74%,34% 74%,20% 100%,0 100%);  filter:drop-shadow(0 6px 14px rgb(0 0 0 / .22));}/* dark mode: shadows read as a lift, not a smudge */@media (prefers-color-scheme:dark){  .bubble{filter:drop-shadow(0 6px 14px rgb(0 0 0 / .55))}}</code></pre>  <div class="callout warn t-small">One cost: <kbd>filter</kbd> makes the element its own compositing layer, and it clips descendants too. Fine for a badge; think twice over a whole scrolling list.</div></div>	-
32.5.1	css_snippet/CSS shape-outside for circular text wrap	<div class="card"><h4>Wrap text around a circle</h4><p>Float a circle and let the text hug its curve.</p><pre class="code"><code>.circle {  float: left;  width: 120px;  height: 120px;  border-radius: 50%;  shape-outside: circle(50%);  margin: 0 var(--s-4) var(--s-2) 0;}/* The circle itself can be invisible */.circle::after {  content: '';  display: block;  width: 100%;  height: 100%;  border-radius: inherit;  background: var(--accent-soft);}</code></pre></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Wrap text around a circle</h4><p>Float a circle and let the text hug its curve.</p><pre class="code"><code>.circle {  float: left;  width: 120px;  height: 120px;  border-radius: 50%;  shape-outside: circle(50%);  margin: 0 var(--s-4) var(--s-2) 0;}/* The circle itself can be invisible */.circle::after {  content: '';  display: block;  width: 100%;  height: 100%;  border-radius: inherit;  background: var(--accent-soft);}</code></pre></div>	-
32.6.1	css_snippet/CSS :is() for grouping selectors	<div class="card"><h4>Group selectors with :is()</h4><p>Write one rule for many cases. Specificity is the highest in the list.</p><pre class="code"><code>/* Before */article h1,article h2,article h3 { margin-top: 0; }/* After */article :is(h1, h2, h3) { margin-top: 0; }/* :is() is forgiving — unknown selectors are ignored */:is(.card, .panel, :--typo) { padding: var(--s-4); }</code></pre></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Group selectors with :is()</h4><p>Write one rule for many cases. Specificity is the highest in the list.</p><pre class="code"><code>/* Before */article h1,article h2,article h3 { margin-top: 0; }/* After */article :is(h1, h2, h3) { margin-top: 0; }/* :is() is forgiving — unknown selectors are ignored */:is(.card, .panel, :--typo) { padding: var(--s-4); }</code></pre></div>	-
32.7.1	css_snippet/CSS backdrop-filter for frosted glass	<div class="card"><h4>Frosted glass with backdrop-filter</h4><p>Blurs whatever sits behind the element. Needs a semi-transparent background.</p><pre class="code"><code>.glass {  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--panel) 70%, transparent);  backdrop-filter: blur(12px) saturate(140%);  -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(12px) saturate(140%); /* Safari */  border: 1px solid var(--line);  border-radius: var(--r-m);}</code></pre></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Frosted glass with backdrop-filter</h4><p>Blurs whatever sits behind the element. Needs a semi-transparent background.</p><pre class="code"><code>.glass {  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--panel) 70%, transparent);  backdrop-filter: blur(12px) saturate(140%);  -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(12px) saturate(140%); /* Safari */  border: 1px solid var(--line);  border-radius: var(--r-m);}</code></pre></div>	-
32.8.1	css_snippet/CSS logical properties for direction-aware layouts	<div class="card"><h4>Logical properties adapt to writing direction</h4><p>Use <code>margin-inline</code> instead of <code>margin-left</code> so your layout works in RTL too.</p><pre class="code"><code>/* Physical */.card { margin-left: var(--s-4); }/* Logical */.card { margin-inline-start: var(--s-4); }/* Also: *//* padding-block, border-inline-end, inset-inline-start */</code></pre></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Logical properties adapt to writing direction</h4><p>Use <code>margin-inline</code> instead of <code>margin-left</code> so your layout works in RTL too.</p><pre class="code"><code>/* Physical */.card { margin-left: var(--s-4); }/* Logical */.card { margin-inline-start: var(--s-4); }/* Also: *//* padding-block, border-inline-end, inset-inline-start */</code></pre></div>	-
32.9.1	css_snippet/CSS text-wrap: balance for headlines	<div class="card"><h4>Balance headline lines</h4><p>Stops one word from dangling on its own line. Best for short blocks like titles.</p><pre class="code"><code>h1, h2, h3 {  text-wrap: balance;}p {  text-wrap: pretty; /* avoids orphans */}</code></pre></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Balance headline lines</h4><p>Stops one word from dangling on its own line. Best for short blocks like titles.</p><pre class="code"><code>h1, h2, h3 {  text-wrap: balance;}p {  text-wrap: pretty; /* avoids orphans */}</code></pre></div>	-
32.10.1	css_snippet/CSS inset shorthand for positioning	<div class="card"><h4>Use <code>inset</code> for offsets</h4><p>Shorter than <code>top/right/bottom/left</code>, and it respects logical directions when needed.</p><pre class="code"><code>.overlay {  position: absolute;  inset: 0; /* top right bottom left all zero */}.badge {  position: absolute;  inset-block-start: var(--s-2);  inset-inline-end: var(--s-2);}</code></pre></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Use <code>inset</code> for offsets</h4><p>Shorter than <code>top/right/bottom/left</code>, and it respects logical directions when needed.</p><pre class="code"><code>.overlay {  position: absolute;  inset: 0; /* top right bottom left all zero */}.badge {  position: absolute;  inset-block-start: var(--s-2);  inset-inline-end: var(--s-2);}</code></pre></div>	-
32.11.1	css_snippet/CSS scrollbar-width for slimmer scrollbars	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">CSS scrollbar-width for slimmer scrollbars</h4>  <p class="muted">Hi <regex>user_name</regex> — here's a snippet to tame those chunky default scrollbars.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>.scroller {  scrollbar-width: thin;          /* Firefox */  scrollbar-color: var(--accent) var(--bg-alt); /* thumb track */}/* WebKit fallback */.scroller::-webkit-scrollbar {  width: 10px;  height: 10px;}.scroller::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb {  background: var(--accent);  border-radius: var(--r-pill);  border: 2px solid var(--bg-alt);}.scroller::-webkit-scrollbar-track {  background: var(--bg-alt);}</code></pre>  <p class="t-small muted">Result: a thin, theme-aware scrollbar that doesn't fight your layout.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">CSS scrollbar-width for slimmer scrollbars</h4>  <p class="muted">Hi  — here's a snippet to tame those chunky default scrollbars.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>.scroller {  scrollbar-width: thin;          /* Firefox */  scrollbar-color: var(--accent) var(--bg-alt); /* thumb track */}/* WebKit fallback */.scroller::-webkit-scrollbar {  width: 10px;  height: 10px;}.scroller::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb {  background: var(--accent);  border-radius: var(--r-pill);  border: 2px solid var(--bg-alt);}.scroller::-webkit-scrollbar-track {  background: var(--bg-alt);}</code></pre>  <p class="t-small muted">Result: a thin, theme-aware scrollbar that doesn't fight your layout.</p></div>	-
32.12.1	css_snippet/CSS color-scheme for native form controls	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">CSS color-scheme for native form controls</h4>  <p class="muted"><regex>user_name</regex>, tell the browser your theme so checkboxes and date pickers match.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>/* Follow the OS preference */:root {  color-scheme: light dark;}/* Force one mode */.force-dark {  color-scheme: dark;}/* Per-element override */input[type="date"] {  color-scheme: dark;}</code></pre>  <p class="t-small muted">That one line flips native scrollbars, form fields, and the default canvas background.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">CSS color-scheme for native form controls</h4>  <p class="muted">, tell the browser your theme so checkboxes and date pickers match.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>/* Follow the OS preference */:root {  color-scheme: light dark;}/* Force one mode */.force-dark {  color-scheme: dark;}/* Per-element override */input[type="date"] {  color-scheme: dark;}</code></pre>  <p class="t-small muted">That one line flips native scrollbars, form fields, and the default canvas background.</p></div>	-
32.13.1	css_snippet/CSS ::selection for custom text highlight	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">CSS ::selection for custom text highlight</h4>  <p class="muted">Hey <regex>user_name</regex>, make that selection highlight feel like yours.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>::selection {  background: var(--accent-soft);  color: var(--ink);  text-shadow: none; /* clean it up */}/* Scope it to a section */.article ::selection {  background: var(--accent);  color: var(--bg);}</code></pre>  <p class="t-small muted">Works in all modern browsers. Keep contrast high so the text stays readable.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">CSS ::selection for custom text highlight</h4>  <p class="muted">Hey , make that selection highlight feel like yours.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>::selection {  background: var(--accent-soft);  color: var(--ink);  text-shadow: none; /* clean it up */}/* Scope it to a section */.article ::selection {  background: var(--accent);  color: var(--bg);}</code></pre>  <p class="t-small muted">Works in all modern browsers. Keep contrast high so the text stays readable.</p></div>	-
32.14.1	css_snippet/CSS overscroll-behavior for no bounce	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">CSS overscroll-behavior for no bounce</h4>  <p class="muted"><regex>user_name</regex>, tame the rubber-band scroll and pull-to-refresh with one property.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>/* Stop the page from bouncing past the edges */body {  overscroll-behavior-y: none;}/* Inside a modal or drawer */.drawer {  overscroll-behavior: contain;  overflow-y: auto;}/* Only kill the horizontal chain */.carousel {  overscroll-behavior-x: none;}</code></pre>  <p class="t-small muted">Great for drawers, modals, and horizontal carousels that shouldn't yank the page.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">CSS overscroll-behavior for no bounce</h4>  <p class="muted">, tame the rubber-band scroll and pull-to-refresh with one property.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>/* Stop the page from bouncing past the edges */body {  overscroll-behavior-y: none;}/* Inside a modal or drawer */.drawer {  overscroll-behavior: contain;  overflow-y: auto;}/* Only kill the horizontal chain */.carousel {  overscroll-behavior-x: none;}</code></pre>  <p class="t-small muted">Great for drawers, modals, and horizontal carousels that shouldn't yank the page.</p></div>	-
32.15.1	css_snippet/CSS scroll-padding for anchored jumps	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">CSS scroll-padding for anchored jumps</h4>  <p class="muted">Hi <regex>user_name</regex>, keep your sticky header from eating anchor targets.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>html {  scroll-padding-top: 5rem; /* matches your sticky header */  scroll-behavior: smooth;}@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {  html {    scroll-behavior: auto;  }}</code></pre>  <p class="t-small muted">Different from <code>scroll-margin-top</code>: this sets the safe zone for the scroll container itself.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <h4 class="card-title">CSS scroll-padding for anchored jumps</h4>  <p class="muted">Hi , keep your sticky header from eating anchor targets.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>html {  scroll-padding-top: 5rem; /* matches your sticky header */  scroll-behavior: smooth;}@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {  html {    scroll-behavior: auto;  }}</code></pre>  <p class="t-small muted">Different from <code>scroll-margin-top</code>: this sets the safe zone for the scroll container itself.</p></div>	-
32.16.1	css_snippet/CSS :user-invalid for live validation	<div class="card">  <h4>CSS :user-invalid for live validation</h4>  <p class="muted">Style fields after the user has interacted and left them invalid—no JavaScript required.</p>  <div class="stack" style="--s:var(--s-4)">    <p><code>:user-invalid</code> matches an input only after the user has tried to submit or blurred it with invalid content. It's the polite sibling of <code>:invalid</code>, which fires too eagerly.</p>    <p>Use it to add a red border and a helper message. The browser won't show the message until the field is actually wrong.</p>  </div>  <pre class="code"><code>input:user-invalid {  border-color: var(--bad);}input:user-invalid + .hint {  display: block;  color: var(--bad);}.hint { display: none; }</code></pre>  <p class="t-small dim">Supported in modern browsers as of 2023. Fall back to <code>:invalid</code> for older ones if you must.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <h4>CSS :user-invalid for live validation</h4>  <p class="muted">Style fields after the user has interacted and left them invalid—no JavaScript required.</p>  <div class="stack" style="--s:var(--s-4)">    <p><code>:user-invalid</code> matches an input only after the user has tried to submit or blurred it with invalid content. It's the polite sibling of <code>:invalid</code>, which fires too eagerly.</p>    <p>Use it to add a red border and a helper message. The browser won't show the message until the field is actually wrong.</p>  </div>  <pre class="code"><code>input:user-invalid {  border-color: var(--bad);}input:user-invalid + .hint {  display: block;  color: var(--bad);}.hint { display: none; }</code></pre>  <p class="t-small dim">Supported in modern browsers as of 2023. Fall back to <code>:invalid</code> for older ones if you must.</p></div>	-
32.17.1	css_snippet/CSS text-emphasis for elegant emphasis	<div class="card">  <h4>CSS text-emphasis for elegant emphasis</h4>  <p class="muted">Draw small marks above or below text—great for East Asian typography or playful accents.</p>  <div class="stack" style="--s:var(--s-4)">    <p><code>text-emphasis</code> adds repeating symbols over each character. You control the mark with <code>text-emphasis-style</code> and its color with <code>text-emphasis-color</code>.</p>    <p>It's especially handy for Japanese emphasis dots, but works for any script. The marks won't affect line height.</p>  </div>  <pre class="code"><code>em {  text-emphasis: dot var(--accent);  text-emphasis-position: under right;  font-style: normal; /* optional */}</code></pre>  <p class="t-small dim">The <code>position</code> value controls where the mark appears—above or below—and its alignment.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <h4>CSS text-emphasis for elegant emphasis</h4>  <p class="muted">Draw small marks above or below text—great for East Asian typography or playful accents.</p>  <div class="stack" style="--s:var(--s-4)">    <p><code>text-emphasis</code> adds repeating symbols over each character. You control the mark with <code>text-emphasis-style</code> and its color with <code>text-emphasis-color</code>.</p>    <p>It's especially handy for Japanese emphasis dots, but works for any script. The marks won't affect line height.</p>  </div>  <pre class="code"><code>em {  text-emphasis: dot var(--accent);  text-emphasis-position: under right;  font-style: normal; /* optional */}</code></pre>  <p class="t-small dim">The <code>position</code> value controls where the mark appears—above or below—and its alignment.</p></div>	-
32.18.1	css_snippet/CSS font-synthesis for faux bold prevention	<div class="card">  <h4>CSS font-synthesis for faux bold prevention</h4>  <p class="muted">Stop browsers from faking bold or italic when a font lacks those faces.</p>  <div class="stack" style="--s:var(--s-4)">    <p>With <code>font-synthesis</code> you can tell the browser not to synthesize bold, italic, or small-caps. This keeps your typography true to the typeface, avoiding ugly faux styles.</p>    <p>Set it on the element or globally. It's a small win for design purists.</p>  </div>  <pre class="code"><code>body {  font-synthesis: none; /* no faux bold/italic */}/* Or prevent only weight synthesis */h1 {  font-synthesis-weight: none;}</code></pre>  <p class="t-small dim">If the font has a real bold, it will still be used. This only blocks fake ones.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <h4>CSS font-synthesis for faux bold prevention</h4>  <p class="muted">Stop browsers from faking bold or italic when a font lacks those faces.</p>  <div class="stack" style="--s:var(--s-4)">    <p>With <code>font-synthesis</code> you can tell the browser not to synthesize bold, italic, or small-caps. This keeps your typography true to the typeface, avoiding ugly faux styles.</p>    <p>Set it on the element or globally. It's a small win for design purists.</p>  </div>  <pre class="code"><code>body {  font-synthesis: none; /* no faux bold/italic */}/* Or prevent only weight synthesis */h1 {  font-synthesis-weight: none;}</code></pre>  <p class="t-small dim">If the font has a real bold, it will still be used. This only blocks fake ones.</p></div>	-
32.19.1	css_snippet/CSS all: unset for resetting styles	<div class="card">  <h4>CSS all: unset for resetting styles</h4>  <p class="muted">Wipe an element's styles clean—back to inherited or initial values.</p>  <div class="stack" style="--s:var(--s-4)">    <p><code>all</code> is a shorthand for every property. <code>all: unset</code> resets to inherited values if the property inherits, or initial if not. It's a sledgehammer for style isolation.</p>    <p>Commonly used on components that need to break free from parent styles, like a custom button inside a styled paragraph.</p>  </div>  <pre class="code"><code>.clean-slate {  all: unset;}/* Or reset to initial values */.reset-to-initial {  all: initial;}</code></pre>  <p class="t-small dim">Note: <code>all</code> doesn't affect custom properties or <code>direction</code>/<code>unicode-bidi</code>.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <h4>CSS all: unset for resetting styles</h4>  <p class="muted">Wipe an element's styles clean—back to inherited or initial values.</p>  <div class="stack" style="--s:var(--s-4)">    <p><code>all</code> is a shorthand for every property. <code>all: unset</code> resets to inherited values if the property inherits, or initial if not. It's a sledgehammer for style isolation.</p>    <p>Commonly used on components that need to break free from parent styles, like a custom button inside a styled paragraph.</p>  </div>  <pre class="code"><code>.clean-slate {  all: unset;}/* Or reset to initial values */.reset-to-initial {  all: initial;}</code></pre>  <p class="t-small dim">Note: <code>all</code> doesn't affect custom properties or <code>direction</code>/<code>unicode-bidi</code>.</p></div>	-
32.20.1	css_snippet/CSS text-size-adjust for mobile legibility	<div class="card">  <h4>CSS text-size-adjust for mobile legibility</h4>  <p class="muted">Control automatic text inflation on mobile devices—keep your layout predictable.</p>  <div class="stack" style="--s:var(--s-4)">    <p>Mobile browsers sometimes enlarge text in wide blocks to make it readable. <code>text-size-adjust</code> lets you disable or customize that behaviour.</p>    <p>Set it to <code>100%</code> to prevent inflation, or <code>none</code> to disable entirely. Use sparingly—it can harm accessibility.</p>  </div>  <pre class="code"><code>body {  text-size-adjust: 100%;}/* For a specific container */.card {  text-size-adjust: none;}</code></pre>  <p class="t-small dim">Best used on components where layout stability matters more than browser guessing.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <h4>CSS text-size-adjust for mobile legibility</h4>  <p class="muted">Control automatic text inflation on mobile devices—keep your layout predictable.</p>  <div class="stack" style="--s:var(--s-4)">    <p>Mobile browsers sometimes enlarge text in wide blocks to make it readable. <code>text-size-adjust</code> lets you disable or customize that behaviour.</p>    <p>Set it to <code>100%</code> to prevent inflation, or <code>none</code> to disable entirely. Use sparingly—it can harm accessibility.</p>  </div>  <pre class="code"><code>body {  text-size-adjust: 100%;}/* For a specific container */.card {  text-size-adjust: none;}</code></pre>  <p class="t-small dim">Best used on components where layout stability matters more than browser guessing.</p></div>	-
32.21.1	css_snippet/CSS variable fallback syntax	<div class="card"><h4>Fallback for custom properties</h4><pre class="code"><code>.btn{  color:var(--btn-ink, var(--ink));  background:var(--btn-bg, var(--accent));}</code></pre><p class="muted t-small">The second argument kicks in when <code>--btn-ink</code> is empty or missing. Nesting another <code>var()</code> keeps the fallback themeable.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Fallback for custom properties</h4><pre class="code"><code>.btn{  color:var(--btn-ink, var(--ink));  background:var(--btn-bg, var(--accent));}</code></pre><p class="muted t-small">The second argument kicks in when <code>--btn-ink</code> is empty or missing. Nesting another <code>var()</code> keeps the fallback themeable.</p></div>	-
32.22.1	css_snippet/CSS overscroll-behavior	<div class="card"><h4>Stop the rubber band</h4><pre class="code"><code>.sheet{  overscroll-behavior:contain;}html{  overscroll-behavior-y:none;}</code></pre><p class="muted t-small"><code>contain</code> keeps the scroll inside the sheet; <code>none</code> on <code>html</code> kills the whole-page bounce. Good for modals and drawers.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Stop the rubber band</h4><pre class="code"><code>.sheet{  overscroll-behavior:contain;}html{  overscroll-behavior-y:none;}</code></pre><p class="muted t-small"><code>contain</code> keeps the scroll inside the sheet; <code>none</code> on <code>html</code> kills the whole-page bounce. Good for modals and drawers.</p></div>	-
32.23.1	css_snippet/CSS font-synthesis	<div class="card"><h4>No faux bold</h4><pre class="code"><code>body{  font-synthesis:none;}</code></pre><p class="muted t-small">If a weight or style isn't loaded, the browser usually fakes it. This says: don't. Pick real faces instead.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>No faux bold</h4><pre class="code"><code>body{  font-synthesis:none;}</code></pre><p class="muted t-small">If a weight or style isn't loaded, the browser usually fakes it. This says: don't. Pick real faces instead.</p></div>	-
32.24.1	css_snippet/CSS accent-color	<div class="card"><h4>Tint the natives</h4><pre class="code"><code>input[type="checkbox"],input[type="radio"]{  accent-color:var(--accent);}</code></pre><p class="muted t-small">One line, and checkboxes, radios and range sliders pick up your accent. Works with the system dark mode too.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Tint the natives</h4><pre class="code"><code>input[type="checkbox"],input[type="radio"]{  accent-color:var(--accent);}</code></pre><p class="muted t-small">One line, and checkboxes, radios and range sliders pick up your accent. Works with the system dark mode too.</p></div>	-
32.25.1	css_snippet/CSS scrollbar-gutter	<div class="card"><h4>Reserve the scrollbar</h4><pre class="code"><code>html{  scrollbar-gutter:stable both-edges;}</code></pre><p class="muted t-small">No more content shimmy when the page grows a scrollbar. <code>both-edges</code> keeps centred layouts centred.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Reserve the scrollbar</h4><pre class="code"><code>html{  scrollbar-gutter:stable both-edges;}</code></pre><p class="muted t-small">No more content shimmy when the page grows a scrollbar. <code>both-edges</code> keeps centred layouts centred.</p></div>	-
32.26.1	css_snippet/CSS ::selection	<div class="card"><h4>Your highlight, your colour</h4><pre class="code"><code>::selection{  background:var(--accent);  color:var(--bg);}</code></pre><p class="muted t-small">Only background and colour are allowed here. Keep contrast high; some users select text to read it.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Your highlight, your colour</h4><pre class="code"><code>::selection{  background:var(--accent);  color:var(--bg);}</code></pre><p class="muted t-small">Only background and colour are allowed here. Keep contrast high; some users select text to read it.</p></div>	-
33.0.0	component_spec/Sourdough Starter Feed Card	<div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Sourdough Starter — Daily Feed</h4><p class="muted t-small">Keep it warm, keep it fed. That's the whole job, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Ratio</span><span class="value">1:5:5</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Temp</span><span class="value">24<span class="unit">°C</span></span></div></div><ul class="list"><li>Discard all but <strong>20 g</strong> starter.</li><li>Add 100 g flour + 100 g lukewarm water.</li><li>Stir, cover loosely, wait 4–8 h.</li><li>Ready when it doubles and domes.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Troubleshooting</summary><div class="body"><p><em>Acetone smell?</em> It's hungry — feed twice daily for two days.</p><p><em>Hooch (grey liquid)?</em> Pour it off or stir it in; either is fine.</p><p><em>No rise?</em> Check water isn't chlorinated and the room isn't cold.</p></div></details><p class="dim t-small">Fridge starter: feed once a week, let it sit out 2 h before returning.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Sourdough Starter — Daily Feed</h4><p class="muted t-small">Keep it warm, keep it fed. That's the whole job, .</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Ratio</span><span class="value">1:5:5</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Temp</span><span class="value">24<span class="unit">°C</span></span></div></div><ul class="list"><li>Discard all but <strong>20 g</strong> starter.</li><li>Add 100 g flour + 100 g lukewarm water.</li><li>Stir, cover loosely, wait 4–8 h.</li><li>Ready when it doubles and domes.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Troubleshooting</summary><div class="body"><p><em>Acetone smell?</em> It's hungry — feed twice daily for two days.</p><p><em>Hooch (grey liquid)?</em> Pour it off or stir it in; either is fine.</p><p><em>No rise?</em> Check water isn't chlorinated and the room isn't cold.</p></div></details><p class="dim t-small">Fridge starter: feed once a week, let it sit out 2 h before returning.</p></div>	-
33.1.0	component_spec/Home Wi-Fi Slow Fix Checklist	<div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Wi-Fi Acting Up?</h4><p class="muted t-small">Nine times out of ten it's placement, not the provider.</p><div class="callout info"><p><strong>Quick win:</strong> put the router high, central, and out in the open.</p></div><ol class="list"><li>Move it off the floor and out of a cupboard.</li><li>Keep it away from microwaves, baby monitors, and fish tanks.</li><li>Use the <strong>5 GHz</strong> band up close — faster, shorter range.</li><li>Use <strong>2.4 GHz</strong> for far rooms — slower, punches through walls.</li><li>Reboot monthly. Genuinely.</li><li>Check for firmware updates in the admin page.</li></ol><table class="table-wrap"><tr><th>Band</th><th>Speed</th><th>Range</th></tr><tr><td>2.4 GHz</td><td class="num">~150 Mbps</td><td>Far</td></tr><tr><td>5 GHz</td><td class="num">~800 Mbps</td><td>Near</td></tr></table><p class="dim t-small">Still bad? A mesh node or powerline kit beats a bigger antenna.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Wi-Fi Acting Up?</h4><p class="muted t-small">Nine times out of ten it's placement, not the provider.</p><div class="callout info"><p><strong>Quick win:</strong> put the router high, central, and out in the open.</p></div><ol class="list"><li>Move it off the floor and out of a cupboard.</li><li>Keep it away from microwaves, baby monitors, and fish tanks.</li><li>Use the <strong>5 GHz</strong> band up close — faster, shorter range.</li><li>Use <strong>2.4 GHz</strong> for far rooms — slower, punches through walls.</li><li>Reboot monthly. Genuinely.</li><li>Check for firmware updates in the admin page.</li></ol><table class="table-wrap"><tr><th>Band</th><th>Speed</th><th>Range</th></tr><tr><td>2.4 GHz</td><td class="num">~150 Mbps</td><td>Far</td></tr><tr><td>5 GHz</td><td class="num">~800 Mbps</td><td>Near</td></tr></table><p class="dim t-small">Still bad? A mesh node or powerline kit beats a bigger antenna.</p></div>	-
33.2.0	component_spec/Cast Iron Seasoning Guide	<div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Cast Iron — Season &amp; Rescue</h4><div class="row between"><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>Cached match</span><span class="badge good">No soap panic</span></div><p class="muted t-small">Modern dish soap is fine. The old rule is a myth, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><h5>Season it</h5><ol class="list"><li>Scrub bare metal, dry fully on the hob.</li><li>Rub a <em>thin</em> coat of flaxseed or canola oil all over.</li><li>Wipe it off like you made a mistake — thin is the secret.</li><li>Bake upside-down at 230 °C for 1 h. Cool in the oven.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Rust? Sticky? Flaking?</summary><div class="body"><p><strong>Rust:</strong> steel wool, then reseason.</p><p><strong>Sticky:</strong> too much oil — heat it, wipe, repeat.</p><p><strong>Flaking:</strong> strip with oven cleaner, then start over.</p></div></details><div class="meter"><span style="width:80%"></span></div><p class="dim t-small">A good pan is 80% patience, 20% bacon.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Cast Iron — Season &amp; Rescue</h4><div class="row between"><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>Cached match</span><span class="badge good">No soap panic</span></div><p class="muted t-small">Modern dish soap is fine. The old rule is a myth, .</p><h5>Season it</h5><ol class="list"><li>Scrub bare metal, dry fully on the hob.</li><li>Rub a <em>thin</em> coat of flaxseed or canola oil all over.</li><li>Wipe it off like you made a mistake — thin is the secret.</li><li>Bake upside-down at 230 °C for 1 h. Cool in the oven.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Rust? Sticky? Flaking?</summary><div class="body"><p><strong>Rust:</strong> steel wool, then reseason.</p><p><strong>Sticky:</strong> too much oil — heat it, wipe, repeat.</p><p><strong>Flaking:</strong> strip with oven cleaner, then start over.</p></div></details><div class="meter"><span style="width:80%"></span></div><p class="dim t-small">A good pan is 80% patience, 20% bacon.</p></div>	-
33.3.0	component_spec/Sleep Better Tonight Plan	<div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Sleep — The Boring Fixes That Work</h4><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Target</span><span class="value">7–9<span class="unit">h</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Caffeine cut-off</span><span class="value">2 PM</span><span class="delta down">half-life 6 h</span></div></div><ul class="list"><li>Same wake time daily — even Saturday. Brutal, effective.</li><li>Bright light within 30 min of waking.</li><li>No screens 45 min before bed, or dim + night mode.</li><li>Bedroom cool: 16–19 °C.</li><li>If awake 20 min, get up. Bed is not a waiting room.</li></ul><div class="callout warn"><p>Waking at 3 AM is normal. Fighting it is what keeps you up, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></div><blockquote class="quote"><p>Sleep is the best meditation.</p><cite>— the Dalai Lama</cite></blockquote></div>	<div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Sleep — The Boring Fixes That Work</h4><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Target</span><span class="value">7–9<span class="unit">h</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Caffeine cut-off</span><span class="value">2 PM</span><span class="delta down">half-life 6 h</span></div></div><ul class="list"><li>Same wake time daily — even Saturday. Brutal, effective.</li><li>Bright light within 30 min of waking.</li><li>No screens 45 min before bed, or dim + night mode.</li><li>Bedroom cool: 16–19 °C.</li><li>If awake 20 min, get up. Bed is not a waiting room.</li></ul><div class="callout warn"><p>Waking at 3 AM is normal. Fighting it is what keeps you up, .</p></div><blockquote class="quote"><p>Sleep is the best meditation.</p><cite>— the Dalai Lama</cite></blockquote></div>	-
33.4.0	component_spec/Bike Chain Cleaning Steps	<div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Bike Chain — 15-Minute Reset</h4><p class="muted t-small">Rain, grit, and four months of neglect — classic combo.</p><div class="timeline"><ul class="list"><li><span class="when">1</span> Degreaser on a brush. Scrub while pedalling backwards.</li><li><span class="when">2</span> Wipe with a rag until it stops coming off black.</li><li><span class="when">3</span> One drop of lube per roller. Not a drizzle.</li><li><span class="when">4</span> Wipe the excess. Dry lube = summer, wet lube = winter.</li></ul></div><details class="acc"><summary>How to check for stretch</summary><div class="body"><p>Use a chain checker or a ruler: 12 links should measure under 30.5 cm. Past that, replace it — or buy a whole new drivetrain later.</p></div></details><p class="dim t-small">Every 200 km, or any time it starts whispering.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Bike Chain — 15-Minute Reset</h4><p class="muted t-small">Rain, grit, and four months of neglect — classic combo.</p><div class="timeline"><ul class="list"><li><span class="when">1</span> Degreaser on a brush. Scrub while pedalling backwards.</li><li><span class="when">2</span> Wipe with a rag until it stops coming off black.</li><li><span class="when">3</span> One drop of lube per roller. Not a drizzle.</li><li><span class="when">4</span> Wipe the excess. Dry lube = summer, wet lube = winter.</li></ul></div><details class="acc"><summary>How to check for stretch</summary><div class="body"><p>Use a chain checker or a ruler: 12 links should measure under 30.5 cm. Past that, replace it — or buy a whole new drivetrain later.</p></div></details><p class="dim t-small">Every 200 km, or any time it starts whispering.</p></div>	-
33.5.0	component_spec/Rental Deposit Move-Out List	<div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Move-Out — Get That Deposit Back</h4><div class="callout good"><p><strong>Golden rule:</strong> photos on day one, photos on the last day. Dated.</p></div><ol class="list"><li>Reread the lease — it names the cleaning standard.</li><li>Patch nail holes, touch up paint if the wall's marked.</li><li>Oven, fridge, and blinds: the three everyone forgets.</li><li>Book the inspection. Attend it if you can.</li><li>Get anything agreed in writing, same day.</li></ol><table class="table-wrap"><tr><th>Item</th><th>Wear &amp; tear?</th></tr><tr><td>Faded carpet</td><td class="num">Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Burns in carpet</td><td class="num">No</td></tr><tr><td>Loose hinge</td><td class="num">Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Hole in door</td><td class="num">No</td></tr></table><p class="dim t-small">Deposit not back in 10 days? Follow up in writing. Paper wins, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Move-Out — Get That Deposit Back</h4><div class="callout good"><p><strong>Golden rule:</strong> photos on day one, photos on the last day. Dated.</p></div><ol class="list"><li>Reread the lease — it names the cleaning standard.</li><li>Patch nail holes, touch up paint if the wall's marked.</li><li>Oven, fridge, and blinds: the three everyone forgets.</li><li>Book the inspection. Attend it if you can.</li><li>Get anything agreed in writing, same day.</li></ol><table class="table-wrap"><tr><th>Item</th><th>Wear &amp; tear?</th></tr><tr><td>Faded carpet</td><td class="num">Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Burns in carpet</td><td class="num">No</td></tr><tr><td>Loose hinge</td><td class="num">Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Hole in door</td><td class="num">No</td></tr></table><p class="dim t-small">Deposit not back in 10 days? Follow up in writing. Paper wins, .</p></div>	-
33.6.0	component_spec/No-Knead Bread Bake Timeline	<div class="card"><h4>No-Knead Bread Timeline</h4><p class="muted">Makes one 1.5 lb loaf. Start 18 hours before you want bread.</p><ul class="list"><li><strong>5:00 PM</strong> — Mix 3 cups flour, 1.5 cups water, 1 tsp salt, 0.5 tsp yeast. Cover.</li><li><strong>5:10 PM</strong> — Walk away. Go live your life.</li><li><strong>9:00 AM</strong> — Dough looks bubbly and doubled. Scrape onto floured counter, fold 4 times, rest 15 min.</li><li><strong>9:15 AM</strong> — Shape into a ball, place on parchment, cover with a towel. Rise 1 hour.</li><li><strong>10:00 AM</strong> — Put Dutch oven in cold oven, set to 450°F. Wait 15 min to preheat.</li><li><strong>10:15 AM</strong> — Lower dough into hot pot, lid on. Bake 30 min.</li><li><strong>10:45 AM</strong> — Lid off. Bake 15 more min until deep brown.</li><li><strong>11:00 AM</strong> — Cool at least 30 min before slicing. Yes, it's hard. Do it anyway.</li></ul><div class="callout info"><p class="muted">If you sleep in, the dough waits. A couple extra hours just makes it sourer. No drama.</p></div></div>	<div class="card"><h4>No-Knead Bread Timeline</h4><p class="muted">Makes one 1.5 lb loaf. Start 18 hours before you want bread.</p><ul class="list"><li><strong>5:00 PM</strong> — Mix 3 cups flour, 1.5 cups water, 1 tsp salt, 0.5 tsp yeast. Cover.</li><li><strong>5:10 PM</strong> — Walk away. Go live your life.</li><li><strong>9:00 AM</strong> — Dough looks bubbly and doubled. Scrape onto floured counter, fold 4 times, rest 15 min.</li><li><strong>9:15 AM</strong> — Shape into a ball, place on parchment, cover with a towel. Rise 1 hour.</li><li><strong>10:00 AM</strong> — Put Dutch oven in cold oven, set to 450°F. Wait 15 min to preheat.</li><li><strong>10:15 AM</strong> — Lower dough into hot pot, lid on. Bake 30 min.</li><li><strong>10:45 AM</strong> — Lid off. Bake 15 more min until deep brown.</li><li><strong>11:00 AM</strong> — Cool at least 30 min before slicing. Yes, it's hard. Do it anyway.</li></ul><div class="callout info"><p class="muted">If you sleep in, the dough waits. A couple extra hours just makes it sourer. No drama.</p></div></div>	-
33.7.0	component_spec/Houseplant Watering Cheat Sheet	<div class="card"><h4>Houseplant Watering Cheat Sheet</h4><p class="muted">Stick a finger in the soil. Dry means water. Damp means wait.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Plant</th><th>Check every</th><th>Water when</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Pothos</td><td>7-10 days</td><td>Top 2" dry</td></tr><tr><td>Snake plant</td><td>2-3 weeks</td><td>Fully dry, then some</td></tr><tr><td>Fiddle leaf fig</td><td>7 days</td><td>Top inch dry</td></tr><tr><td>Succulents</td><td>2 weeks</td><td>Bone dry</td></tr><tr><td>Ferns</td><td>3-4 days</td><td>Always slightly moist</td></tr><tr><td>Peace lily</td><td>7 days</td><td>Slight droop is its way of asking</td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class="acc"><summary>Signs you're overwatering</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Yellow lower leaves</li><li>Mushy stems</li><li>Fungus gnats</li><li>Soil stays wet for 2+ weeks</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Signs you're underwatering</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Crispy brown edges</li><li>Drooping that doesn't recover overnight</li><li>Soil pulls away from pot edge</li></ul></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Houseplant Watering Cheat Sheet</h4><p class="muted">Stick a finger in the soil. Dry means water. Damp means wait.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Plant</th><th>Check every</th><th>Water when</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Pothos</td><td>7-10 days</td><td>Top 2" dry</td></tr><tr><td>Snake plant</td><td>2-3 weeks</td><td>Fully dry, then some</td></tr><tr><td>Fiddle leaf fig</td><td>7 days</td><td>Top inch dry</td></tr><tr><td>Succulents</td><td>2 weeks</td><td>Bone dry</td></tr><tr><td>Ferns</td><td>3-4 days</td><td>Always slightly moist</td></tr><tr><td>Peace lily</td><td>7 days</td><td>Slight droop is its way of asking</td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class="acc"><summary>Signs you're overwatering</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Yellow lower leaves</li><li>Mushy stems</li><li>Fungus gnats</li><li>Soil stays wet for 2+ weeks</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Signs you're underwatering</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Crispy brown edges</li><li>Drooping that doesn't recover overnight</li><li>Soil pulls away from pot edge</li></ul></div></details></div>	-
33.8.0	component_spec/Weekly Fridge Clean-Out Plan	<div class="card"><h4>Weekly Fridge Clean-Out Plan</h4><p class="muted">Every Sunday, 20 minutes. Saves money and prevents mystery containers.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Empty the front half.</strong> Everything you can see. Set it on the counter.</li><li><strong>Wipe shelves.</strong> Warm water, tiny bit of dish soap. Dry with a towel.</li><li><strong>Triage what's out.</strong> Still good? Back in the fridge. Sketchy? Compost or trash. Not sure? Smell it. Trust your nose.</li><li><strong>Rescue mission.</strong> Wilted greens, soft tomatoes, half an onion — make soup, stir-fry, or frittata tonight.</li><li><strong>Label leftovers.</strong> Date them with tape and a marker. Eat within 4 days.</li><li><strong>Restock mindfully.</strong> Grocery shop after, not before.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p>Bonus: keep a "eat me first" bin at eye level. It's weirdly motivating.</p></div></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Weekly Fridge Clean-Out Plan</h4><p class="muted">Every Sunday, 20 minutes. Saves money and prevents mystery containers.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Empty the front half.</strong> Everything you can see. Set it on the counter.</li><li><strong>Wipe shelves.</strong> Warm water, tiny bit of dish soap. Dry with a towel.</li><li><strong>Triage what's out.</strong> Still good? Back in the fridge. Sketchy? Compost or trash. Not sure? Smell it. Trust your nose.</li><li><strong>Rescue mission.</strong> Wilted greens, soft tomatoes, half an onion — make soup, stir-fry, or frittata tonight.</li><li><strong>Label leftovers.</strong> Date them with tape and a marker. Eat within 4 days.</li><li><strong>Restock mindfully.</strong> Grocery shop after, not before.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p>Bonus: keep a "eat me first" bin at eye level. It's weirdly motivating.</p></div></div>	-
33.9.0	component_spec/Small Space Home Workout	<div class="card"><h4>Small Space Home Workout</h4><p class="muted">20 minutes, no jumping, no equipment. Neighbors will never know.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Warm-up (3 min)</strong> — March in place, arm circles, hip circles.</li><li><strong>Squats (45 sec)</strong> — Slow and controlled. Rest 15 sec.</li><li><strong>Push-ups (45 sec)</strong> — Knees down if needed. Rest 15 sec.</li><li><strong>Reverse lunges (45 sec)</strong> — Alternate legs. Rest 15 sec.</li><li><strong>Glute bridges (45 sec)</strong> — Squeeze at the top. Rest 15 sec.</li><li><strong>Plank (30-45 sec)</strong> — Knees or toes. Rest 15 sec.</li><li><strong>Repeat circuit 2 more times.</strong></li><li><strong>Cool down (3 min)</strong> — Stretch hamstrings, chest, and shoulders.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p class="muted">Put a towel under your hands if the floor is slick. Hydrate between rounds.</p></div></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Small Space Home Workout</h4><p class="muted">20 minutes, no jumping, no equipment. Neighbors will never know.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Warm-up (3 min)</strong> — March in place, arm circles, hip circles.</li><li><strong>Squats (45 sec)</strong> — Slow and controlled. Rest 15 sec.</li><li><strong>Push-ups (45 sec)</strong> — Knees down if needed. Rest 15 sec.</li><li><strong>Reverse lunges (45 sec)</strong> — Alternate legs. Rest 15 sec.</li><li><strong>Glute bridges (45 sec)</strong> — Squeeze at the top. Rest 15 sec.</li><li><strong>Plank (30-45 sec)</strong> — Knees or toes. Rest 15 sec.</li><li><strong>Repeat circuit 2 more times.</strong></li><li><strong>Cool down (3 min)</strong> — Stretch hamstrings, chest, and shoulders.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p class="muted">Put a towel under your hands if the floor is slick. Hydrate between rounds.</p></div></div>	-
33.10.0	component_spec/Cast Iron Skillet Rescue	<div class="card"><h4>Cast Iron Skillet Rescue</h4><p class="muted">Rust and sticky spots are fixable. You don't need a new pan.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Step 1: Assess the damage</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li><strong>Rust</strong> — Orange, flaky patches.</li><li><strong>Sticky</strong> — Gummy residue from too much oil.</li><li><strong>Flaking</strong> — Seasoning peeling off.</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Step 2: Strip it down (if needed)</summary><div class="body"><p>For heavy rust or flaking, scrub with steel wool and hot water until bare metal shows. Dry immediately.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Step 3: Re-season</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Rub a thin layer of flaxseed or canola oil all over.</li><li>Wipe off excess — it should look almost dry.</li><li>Bake upside down at 450°F for 1 hour.</li><li>Let cool in oven. Repeat 2-3 times for a solid layer.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Step 4: Daily care</summary><div class="body"><p>Rinse with hot water, scrub with a brush, dry on the stove. A drop of oil after each use keeps it happy.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Cast Iron Skillet Rescue</h4><p class="muted">Rust and sticky spots are fixable. You don't need a new pan.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Step 1: Assess the damage</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li><strong>Rust</strong> — Orange, flaky patches.</li><li><strong>Sticky</strong> — Gummy residue from too much oil.</li><li><strong>Flaking</strong> — Seasoning peeling off.</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Step 2: Strip it down (if needed)</summary><div class="body"><p>For heavy rust or flaking, scrub with steel wool and hot water until bare metal shows. Dry immediately.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Step 3: Re-season</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Rub a thin layer of flaxseed or canola oil all over.</li><li>Wipe off excess — it should look almost dry.</li><li>Bake upside down at 450°F for 1 hour.</li><li>Let cool in oven. Repeat 2-3 times for a solid layer.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Step 4: Daily care</summary><div class="body"><p>Rinse with hot water, scrub with a brush, dry on the stove. A drop of oil after each use keeps it happy.</p></div></details></div>	-
33.11.0	component_spec/Kitchen Knife Sharpening Basics	<div class="card"><h4>Kitchen Knife Sharpening Basics</h4><p class="muted">A sharp knife is safer than a dull one. Here's how to keep it that way.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Honing rod</span><span class="value">Weekly</span><span class="delta">Realigns edge</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Whetstone</span><span class="value">Every 2-3 months</span><span class="delta">Removes metal</span></div></div><ol class="list"><li><strong>Hone first.</strong> Hold the rod vertically, knife at 20° angle, draw down each side 5-10 times.</li><li><strong>Test.</strong> Slice paper. If it drags, move to the stone.</li><li><strong>Soak your whetstone</strong> in water for 10 minutes.</li><li><strong>Sharpen at 20°.</strong> Push the blade across the stone like you're slicing a thin layer off the top. 10 passes per side.</li><li><strong>Flip and repeat.</strong> Then do 5 lighter passes per side.</li><li><strong>Wipe clean, dry, and hone again.</strong></li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p>Don't use a pull-through sharpener on good knives. It chews the edge.</p></div></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Kitchen Knife Sharpening Basics</h4><p class="muted">A sharp knife is safer than a dull one. Here's how to keep it that way.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Honing rod</span><span class="value">Weekly</span><span class="delta">Realigns edge</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Whetstone</span><span class="value">Every 2-3 months</span><span class="delta">Removes metal</span></div></div><ol class="list"><li><strong>Hone first.</strong> Hold the rod vertically, knife at 20° angle, draw down each side 5-10 times.</li><li><strong>Test.</strong> Slice paper. If it drags, move to the stone.</li><li><strong>Soak your whetstone</strong> in water for 10 minutes.</li><li><strong>Sharpen at 20°.</strong> Push the blade across the stone like you're slicing a thin layer off the top. 10 passes per side.</li><li><strong>Flip and repeat.</strong> Then do 5 lighter passes per side.</li><li><strong>Wipe clean, dry, and hone again.</strong></li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p>Don't use a pull-through sharpener on good knives. It chews the edge.</p></div></div>	-
33.12.0	component_spec/Eggshell Compost Boost	<article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>Crushed eggshells add calcium and keep the pile sweet. Here's the quick version—<regex>user_name</regex>.</p></article><section class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Eggshell Compost Boost</h3>  <p class="muted">Balance greens and browns, keep pests out, feed the soil.</p>  <div class="row between">    <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i> cached</span>    <span class="t-small dim">Updated for spring</span>  </div>  <div class="divider"></div>  <h4>Why it works</h4>  <p>Shells are ~95% calcium carbonate—the same stuff garden lime is made of. Worms and microbes break it down slowly. Plants use the calcium for strong cell walls, and it nudges pH up a touch if your soil runs sour.</p>  <h4>How to do it</h4>  <ol class="list">    <li>Rinse shells so they don't smell like yesterday's omelet.</li>    <li>Dry them on the counter, then crush to <strong>powder or small bits</strong>. Powder works faster.</li>    <li>Mix into the pile. Ratio: about <strong>1 cup crushed shells per 4 cups scraps</strong>.</li>    <li>Turn the pile monthly. Shells won't fully vanish for a year—that's fine.</li>  </ol>  <h4>Pest note</h4>  <p>Whole shells attract rodents and flies. Crushing is non-negotiable. If you see critters, bury shells deeper in the center.</p>  <details class="acc"><summary>Blender shortcut</summary><div class="body"><p>Pulse dry shells in a blender until sandy. Wear earplugs. It's loud.</p></div></details>  <details class="acc"><summary>Can I skip rinsing?</summary><div class="body"><p>Yes, but you'll smell it by Tuesday. A quick swish is worth it.</p></div></details>  <div class="callout good"><p><strong>Good sign:</strong> pile hits 130–150°F and smells earthy, not rotten.</p></div></section><article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>That's the whole shell game. Happy composting.</p></article>	<article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>Crushed eggshells add calcium and keep the pile sweet. Here's the quick version—.</p></article><section class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Eggshell Compost Boost</h3>  <p class="muted">Balance greens and browns, keep pests out, feed the soil.</p>  <div class="row between">    <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i> cached</span>    <span class="t-small dim">Updated for spring</span>  </div>  <div class="divider"></div>  <h4>Why it works</h4>  <p>Shells are ~95% calcium carbonate—the same stuff garden lime is made of. Worms and microbes break it down slowly. Plants use the calcium for strong cell walls, and it nudges pH up a touch if your soil runs sour.</p>  <h4>How to do it</h4>  <ol class="list">    <li>Rinse shells so they don't smell like yesterday's omelet.</li>    <li>Dry them on the counter, then crush to <strong>powder or small bits</strong>. Powder works faster.</li>    <li>Mix into the pile. Ratio: about <strong>1 cup crushed shells per 4 cups scraps</strong>.</li>    <li>Turn the pile monthly. Shells won't fully vanish for a year—that's fine.</li>  </ol>  <h4>Pest note</h4>  <p>Whole shells attract rodents and flies. Crushing is non-negotiable. If you see critters, bury shells deeper in the center.</p>  <details class="acc"><summary>Blender shortcut</summary><div class="body"><p>Pulse dry shells in a blender until sandy. Wear earplugs. It's loud.</p></div></details>  <details class="acc"><summary>Can I skip rinsing?</summary><div class="body"><p>Yes, but you'll smell it by Tuesday. A quick swish is worth it.</p></div></details>  <div class="callout good"><p><strong>Good sign:</strong> pile hits 130–150°F and smells earthy, not rotten.</p></div></section><article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>That's the whole shell game. Happy composting.</p></article>	-
33.13.0	component_spec/Coffee Grind Size Cheat Sheet	<article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>Grind is half the brew, <regex>user_name</regex>. Here's your cheat sheet.</p></article><section class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Coffee Grind Size Cheat Sheet</h3>  <p class="muted">Match the grind to the brewer, then dial in taste.</p>  <div class="row between">    <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i> cached</span>    <span class="t-small dim">For burr grinders</span>  </div>  <div class="divider"></div>  <h4>By brewer</h4>  <div class="table-wrap">    <table>      <thead><tr><th>Brewer</th><th>Grind</th><th>Texture</th></tr></thead>      <tbody>        <tr><td>French press</td><td>Coarse</td><td>Sea salt</td></tr>        <tr><td>Drip</td><td>Medium</td><td>Table salt</td></tr>        <tr><td>Pour over</td><td>Medium-fine</td><td>Fine sand</td></tr>        <tr><td>Espresso</td><td>Fine</td><td>Powder</td></tr>        <tr><td>Moka pot</td><td>Fine</td><td>Powder</td></tr>      </tbody>    </table>  </div>  <h4>Taste cues</h4>  <ul class="list">    <li><strong>Sour, weak?</strong> Grind finer.</li>    <li><strong>Bitter, harsh?</strong> Grind coarser.</li>    <li><strong>Perfect?</strong> Write down the setting. Repeat tomorrow.</li>  </ul>  <details class="acc"><summary>Blade grinder?</summary><div class="body"><p>Shake while pulsing. You'll get uneven bits, but aim for the texture listed above. Sift out dust if you can.</p></div></details>  <details class="acc"><summary>Scale tip</summary><div class="body"><p>Weigh beans, not scoops. 1:16 coffee-to-water for drip. 1:15 for pour over.</p></div></details>  <div class="callout info"><p><strong>One change at a time.</strong> Adjust grind, taste, repeat. Don't fiddle with dose and grind together.</p></div></section><article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>Dial it in, then leave it alone. Your taste buds will thank you.</p></article>	<article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>Grind is half the brew, . Here's your cheat sheet.</p></article><section class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Coffee Grind Size Cheat Sheet</h3>  <p class="muted">Match the grind to the brewer, then dial in taste.</p>  <div class="row between">    <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i> cached</span>    <span class="t-small dim">For burr grinders</span>  </div>  <div class="divider"></div>  <h4>By brewer</h4>  <div class="table-wrap">    <table>      <thead><tr><th>Brewer</th><th>Grind</th><th>Texture</th></tr></thead>      <tbody>        <tr><td>French press</td><td>Coarse</td><td>Sea salt</td></tr>        <tr><td>Drip</td><td>Medium</td><td>Table salt</td></tr>        <tr><td>Pour over</td><td>Medium-fine</td><td>Fine sand</td></tr>        <tr><td>Espresso</td><td>Fine</td><td>Powder</td></tr>        <tr><td>Moka pot</td><td>Fine</td><td>Powder</td></tr>      </tbody>    </table>  </div>  <h4>Taste cues</h4>  <ul class="list">    <li><strong>Sour, weak?</strong> Grind finer.</li>    <li><strong>Bitter, harsh?</strong> Grind coarser.</li>    <li><strong>Perfect?</strong> Write down the setting. Repeat tomorrow.</li>  </ul>  <details class="acc"><summary>Blade grinder?</summary><div class="body"><p>Shake while pulsing. You'll get uneven bits, but aim for the texture listed above. Sift out dust if you can.</p></div></details>  <details class="acc"><summary>Scale tip</summary><div class="body"><p>Weigh beans, not scoops. 1:16 coffee-to-water for drip. 1:15 for pour over.</p></div></details>  <div class="callout info"><p><strong>One change at a time.</strong> Adjust grind, taste, repeat. Don't fiddle with dose and grind together.</p></div></section><article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>Dial it in, then leave it alone. Your taste buds will thank you.</p></article>	-
33.14.0	component_spec/Freezer Meal Prep Basics	<article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>Cook once, eat for weeks. Here's the no-fuss version, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></article><section class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Freezer Meal Prep Basics</h3>  <p class="muted">What freezes well, what doesn't, and how to pack it.</p>  <div class="row between">    <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i> cached</span>    <span class="t-small dim">For small kitchens</span>  </div>  <div class="divider"></div>  <h4>Freezes great</h4>  <ul class="list">    <li>Soups, stews, chili</li>    <li>Cooked rice and grains</li>    <li>Meatballs, patties, shredded meat</li>    <li>Lasagna, casseroles</li>    <li>Muffins, pancakes, waffles</li>  </ul>  <h4>Skip the freezer</h4>  <ul class="list">    <li>Raw potatoes (turn mushy)</li>    <li>Boiled eggs (rubbery)</li>    <li>Lettuce, cucumbers, radishes</li>    <li>Fried foods (soggy)</li>  </ul>  <details class="acc"><summary>Packing rules</summary><div class="body"><p>Cool completely. Squeeze air out. Label with name and date. Freeze flat for soups—they stack like books.</p></div></details>  <details class="acc"><summary>Reheat magic</summary><div class="body"><p>Thaw overnight in fridge. Reheat on stove or microwave. Add fresh herbs after heating.</p></div></details>  <div class="callout good"><p><strong>Portion it.</strong> Freeze in single servings so you're not thawing a brick for one lunch.</p></div></section><article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>Future you will high-five present you. Trust.</p></article>	<article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>Cook once, eat for weeks. Here's the no-fuss version, .</p></article><section class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Freezer Meal Prep Basics</h3>  <p class="muted">What freezes well, what doesn't, and how to pack it.</p>  <div class="row between">    <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i> cached</span>    <span class="t-small dim">For small kitchens</span>  </div>  <div class="divider"></div>  <h4>Freezes great</h4>  <ul class="list">    <li>Soups, stews, chili</li>    <li>Cooked rice and grains</li>    <li>Meatballs, patties, shredded meat</li>    <li>Lasagna, casseroles</li>    <li>Muffins, pancakes, waffles</li>  </ul>  <h4>Skip the freezer</h4>  <ul class="list">    <li>Raw potatoes (turn mushy)</li>    <li>Boiled eggs (rubbery)</li>    <li>Lettuce, cucumbers, radishes</li>    <li>Fried foods (soggy)</li>  </ul>  <details class="acc"><summary>Packing rules</summary><div class="body"><p>Cool completely. Squeeze air out. Label with name and date. Freeze flat for soups—they stack like books.</p></div></details>  <details class="acc"><summary>Reheat magic</summary><div class="body"><p>Thaw overnight in fridge. Reheat on stove or microwave. Add fresh herbs after heating.</p></div></details>  <div class="callout good"><p><strong>Portion it.</strong> Freeze in single servings so you're not thawing a brick for one lunch.</p></div></section><article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>Future you will high-five present you. Trust.</p></article>	-
33.15.0	component_spec/Tire Pressure Check Steps	<article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>Five minutes now saves a blowout later, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></article><section class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Tire Pressure Check Steps</h3>  <p class="muted">Cold tires, correct PSI, no guesswork.</p>  <div class="row between">    <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i> cached</span>    <span class="t-small dim">Check monthly</span>  </div>  <div class="divider"></div>  <h4>What you need</h4>  <ul class="list">    <li>Pressure gauge (dial or digital)</li>    <li>Air compressor or gas station pump</li>    <li>Your car's PSI spec</li>  </ul>  <h4>Steps</h4>  <ol class="list">    <li>Check tires <strong>cold</strong>—before driving or 3+ hours after.</li>    <li>Find PSI on the driver's door jamb sticker. Not the tire sidewall.</li>    <li>Remove valve cap. Press gauge firmly.</li>    <li>Read PSI. If low, add air in short bursts.</li>    <li>Recheck. Replace cap.</li>    <li>Repeat for all four tires. Don't forget the spare.</li>  </ol>  <details class="acc"><summary>Why cold matters</summary><div class="body"><p>Driving heats tires and raises PSI by 2–4. Checking hot gives a false high reading.</p></div></details>  <details class="acc"><summary>TPMS light on?</summary><div class="body"><p>Check all tires. If they look fine, one sensor may be faulty. A $20 gauge beats a $200 guess.</p></div></details>  <div class="callout warn"><p><strong>Don't eyeball it.</strong> A tire can look fine and still be 10 PSI low.</p></div></section><article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>Your tires will thank you. So will your gas mileage.</p></article>	<article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>Five minutes now saves a blowout later, .</p></article><section class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Tire Pressure Check Steps</h3>  <p class="muted">Cold tires, correct PSI, no guesswork.</p>  <div class="row between">    <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i> cached</span>    <span class="t-small dim">Check monthly</span>  </div>  <div class="divider"></div>  <h4>What you need</h4>  <ul class="list">    <li>Pressure gauge (dial or digital)</li>    <li>Air compressor or gas station pump</li>    <li>Your car's PSI spec</li>  </ul>  <h4>Steps</h4>  <ol class="list">    <li>Check tires <strong>cold</strong>—before driving or 3+ hours after.</li>    <li>Find PSI on the driver's door jamb sticker. Not the tire sidewall.</li>    <li>Remove valve cap. Press gauge firmly.</li>    <li>Read PSI. If low, add air in short bursts.</li>    <li>Recheck. Replace cap.</li>    <li>Repeat for all four tires. Don't forget the spare.</li>  </ol>  <details class="acc"><summary>Why cold matters</summary><div class="body"><p>Driving heats tires and raises PSI by 2–4. Checking hot gives a false high reading.</p></div></details>  <details class="acc"><summary>TPMS light on?</summary><div class="body"><p>Check all tires. If they look fine, one sensor may be faulty. A $20 gauge beats a $200 guess.</p></div></details>  <div class="callout warn"><p><strong>Don't eyeball it.</strong> A tire can look fine and still be 10 PSI low.</p></div></section><article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>Your tires will thank you. So will your gas mileage.</p></article>	-
33.16.0	component_spec/Shower Curtain Clean Routine	<article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>That pink film isn't décor, <regex>user_name</regex>. Let's evict it.</p></article><section class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Shower Curtain Clean Routine</h3>  <p class="muted">Machine-wash friendly for most liners and fabric curtains.</p>  <div class="row between">    <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i> cached</span>    <span class="t-small dim">Monthly is plenty</span>  </div>  <div class="divider"></div>  <h4>What you need</h4>  <ul class="list">    <li>Washing machine</li>    <li>1/2 cup baking soda</li>    <li>1/2 cup white vinegar (optional)</li>    <li>Two old towels</li>  </ul>  <h4>Steps</h4>  <ol class="list">    <li>Remove curtain and liner from rod. Unhook rings.</li>    <li>Add towels to drum—they scrub the curtain.</li>    <li>Add baking soda. Use warm water, gentle cycle.</li>    <li>Add vinegar to rinse cycle if your machine allows.</li>    <li>Hang immediately to drip dry. Don't put plastic in the dryer.</li>  </ol>  <details class="acc"><summary>Fabric curtain?</summary><div class="body"><p>Check the tag. Most cotton and polyester blends handle this fine. Line dry only.</p></div></details>  <details class="acc"><summary>Heavy mildew</summary><div class="body"><p>Soak in a bucket with 1 part vinegar to 4 parts water for an hour before washing.</p></div></details>  <div class="callout good"><p><strong>Prevent it:</strong> Stretch the curtain fully after each shower. Airflow is the enemy of mildew.</p></div></section><article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>Clean curtain, clear conscience. Cheap therapy.</p></article>	<article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>That pink film isn't décor, . Let's evict it.</p></article><section class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Shower Curtain Clean Routine</h3>  <p class="muted">Machine-wash friendly for most liners and fabric curtains.</p>  <div class="row between">    <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i> cached</span>    <span class="t-small dim">Monthly is plenty</span>  </div>  <div class="divider"></div>  <h4>What you need</h4>  <ul class="list">    <li>Washing machine</li>    <li>1/2 cup baking soda</li>    <li>1/2 cup white vinegar (optional)</li>    <li>Two old towels</li>  </ul>  <h4>Steps</h4>  <ol class="list">    <li>Remove curtain and liner from rod. Unhook rings.</li>    <li>Add towels to drum—they scrub the curtain.</li>    <li>Add baking soda. Use warm water, gentle cycle.</li>    <li>Add vinegar to rinse cycle if your machine allows.</li>    <li>Hang immediately to drip dry. Don't put plastic in the dryer.</li>  </ol>  <details class="acc"><summary>Fabric curtain?</summary><div class="body"><p>Check the tag. Most cotton and polyester blends handle this fine. Line dry only.</p></div></details>  <details class="acc"><summary>Heavy mildew</summary><div class="body"><p>Soak in a bucket with 1 part vinegar to 4 parts water for an hour before washing.</p></div></details>  <div class="callout good"><p><strong>Prevent it:</strong> Stretch the curtain fully after each shower. Airflow is the enemy of mildew.</p></div></section><article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>Clean curtain, clear conscience. Cheap therapy.</p></article>	-
33.17.0	component_spec/Laptop Battery Life Tips	<article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>Your laptop isn't broken—it's thirsty. Let's fix that, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></article><section class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Laptop Battery Life Tips</h3>  <p class="muted">Small habits, longer runtime.</p>  <div class="row between">    <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i> cached</span>    <span class="t-small dim">Works on Mac and Windows</span>  </div>  <div class="divider"></div>  <h4>Quick wins</h4>  <ul class="list">    <li>Lower screen brightness to 50% or less.</li>    <li>Turn off Bluetooth and unused USB devices.</li>    <li>Close background apps—especially browsers with 30 tabs.</li>    <li>Use power-saving mode.</li>    <li>Keep it cool. Heat kills batteries.</li>  </ul>  <h4>Long-term care</h4>  <ul class="list">    <li>Don't leave it plugged in at 100% all day.</li>    <li>Ideal range: 20–80%. Some laptops let you cap charging.</li>    <li>Store at 50% if unused for months.</li>  </ul>  <details class="acc"><summary>Check battery health</summary><div class="body"><p><strong>Mac:</strong> Option-click the battery icon. <strong>Windows:</strong> Run <code>powercfg /batteryreport</code> in Command Prompt.</p></div></details>  <details class="acc"><summary>When to replace</summary><div class="body"><p>If health drops below 80% and runtime bugs you, a new battery is cheaper than a new laptop.</p></div></details>  <div class="callout info"><p><strong>Myth:</strong> Draining to 0% doesn't "reset" modern batteries. It just stresses them.</p></div></section><article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>Treat it like a phone and it'll keep up with you.</p></article>	<article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>Your laptop isn't broken—it's thirsty. Let's fix that, .</p></article><section class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Laptop Battery Life Tips</h3>  <p class="muted">Small habits, longer runtime.</p>  <div class="row between">    <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i> cached</span>    <span class="t-small dim">Works on Mac and Windows</span>  </div>  <div class="divider"></div>  <h4>Quick wins</h4>  <ul class="list">    <li>Lower screen brightness to 50% or less.</li>    <li>Turn off Bluetooth and unused USB devices.</li>    <li>Close background apps—especially browsers with 30 tabs.</li>    <li>Use power-saving mode.</li>    <li>Keep it cool. Heat kills batteries.</li>  </ul>  <h4>Long-term care</h4>  <ul class="list">    <li>Don't leave it plugged in at 100% all day.</li>    <li>Ideal range: 20–80%. Some laptops let you cap charging.</li>    <li>Store at 50% if unused for months.</li>  </ul>  <details class="acc"><summary>Check battery health</summary><div class="body"><p><strong>Mac:</strong> Option-click the battery icon. <strong>Windows:</strong> Run <code>powercfg /batteryreport</code> in Command Prompt.</p></div></details>  <details class="acc"><summary>When to replace</summary><div class="body"><p>If health drops below 80% and runtime bugs you, a new battery is cheaper than a new laptop.</p></div></details>  <div class="callout info"><p><strong>Myth:</strong> Draining to 0% doesn't "reset" modern batteries. It just stresses them.</p></div></section><article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>Treat it like a phone and it'll keep up with you.</p></article>	-
33.18.0	component_spec/Deglazing a Pan for Quick Sauce	<div class="card">  <h4>Deglazing a Pan for Quick Sauce</h4>  <p class="muted">Turn the browned bits stuck to the pan into a fast pan sauce. Works with stainless, cast iron, or carbon steel — not nonstick.</p>  <div class="callout info">    <p><strong>Why it works:</strong> The brown bits (fond) are concentrated flavor. A splash of liquid lifts them and becomes sauce in under a minute.</p>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Finish searing.</strong> Move meat to a plate. Leave the pan on medium heat — fond should be deep brown, not black.</li>    <li><strong>Pour in liquid.</strong> Use ¼–½ cup wine, broth, or even water. It will hiss and steam.</li>    <li><strong>Scrape.</strong> Use a wooden spoon or spatula to loosen every brown speck. Simmer 1–2 minutes until reduced by half.</li>    <li><strong>Swirl in cold butter.</strong> Off heat, add 1–2 tablespoons cold butter, swirling until glossy. Taste and season.</li>  </ol>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What liquid should I use?</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li><strong>Wine</strong> — dry white or red; let the alcohol cook off.</li>        <li><strong>Broth</strong> — chicken or beef; milder.</li>        <li><strong>Vinegar + water</strong> — 1 tablespoon vinegar to ½ cup water for a bright kick.</li>        <li><strong>Water</strong> — in a pinch, works fine.</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout warn">    <p><strong>If fond is black:</strong> It's bitter. Wipe the pan, add fresh fat, and start over — no sauce will fix burnt bits.</p>  </div>  <p class="muted">Add a minced shallot or garlic clove after scraping for extra depth. A pat of butter at the end makes it restaurant-silky.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <h4>Deglazing a Pan for Quick Sauce</h4>  <p class="muted">Turn the browned bits stuck to the pan into a fast pan sauce. Works with stainless, cast iron, or carbon steel — not nonstick.</p>  <div class="callout info">    <p><strong>Why it works:</strong> The brown bits (fond) are concentrated flavor. A splash of liquid lifts them and becomes sauce in under a minute.</p>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Finish searing.</strong> Move meat to a plate. Leave the pan on medium heat — fond should be deep brown, not black.</li>    <li><strong>Pour in liquid.</strong> Use ¼–½ cup wine, broth, or even water. It will hiss and steam.</li>    <li><strong>Scrape.</strong> Use a wooden spoon or spatula to loosen every brown speck. Simmer 1–2 minutes until reduced by half.</li>    <li><strong>Swirl in cold butter.</strong> Off heat, add 1–2 tablespoons cold butter, swirling until glossy. Taste and season.</li>  </ol>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What liquid should I use?</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li><strong>Wine</strong> — dry white or red; let the alcohol cook off.</li>        <li><strong>Broth</strong> — chicken or beef; milder.</li>        <li><strong>Vinegar + water</strong> — 1 tablespoon vinegar to ½ cup water for a bright kick.</li>        <li><strong>Water</strong> — in a pinch, works fine.</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout warn">    <p><strong>If fond is black:</strong> It's bitter. Wipe the pan, add fresh fat, and start over — no sauce will fix burnt bits.</p>  </div>  <p class="muted">Add a minced shallot or garlic clove after scraping for extra depth. A pat of butter at the end makes it restaurant-silky.</p></div>	-
33.19.0	component_spec/Snake Plant Watering & Care	<div class="card">  <h4>Snake Plant Watering &amp; Care</h4>  <p class="muted">Also called mother-in-law's tongue. Tough, but it has one fatal weakness: too much water.</p>  <div class="callout good">    <p><strong>Golden rule:</strong> Let the soil dry completely before watering. In summer that's every 2–3 weeks; in winter, every 4–6 weeks.</p>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>How to water properly</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Check soil with a finger or moisture meter. Bone dry? Water.</li>        <li>Water until it runs out the drainage hole. Never let it sit in a saucer of water.</li>        <li>Empty the saucer after 10 minutes.</li>      </ol>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Light &amp; placement</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Bright, indirect light is ideal. It tolerates low light but grows slower. Avoid direct afternoon sun — leaves can scorch.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Brown tips or mushy base?</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li><strong>Brown tips:</strong> Usually tap water minerals or inconsistent watering. Switch to filtered water.</li>        <li><strong>Mushy base:</strong> Root rot from overwatering. Remove from pot, trim mushy roots, repot in dry cactus mix, and wait a week before watering.</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <p class="muted">Feed once in spring and once in summer with diluted houseplant fertilizer. Otherwise, ignore it — it prefers neglect.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <h4>Snake Plant Watering &amp; Care</h4>  <p class="muted">Also called mother-in-law's tongue. Tough, but it has one fatal weakness: too much water.</p>  <div class="callout good">    <p><strong>Golden rule:</strong> Let the soil dry completely before watering. In summer that's every 2–3 weeks; in winter, every 4–6 weeks.</p>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>How to water properly</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Check soil with a finger or moisture meter. Bone dry? Water.</li>        <li>Water until it runs out the drainage hole. Never let it sit in a saucer of water.</li>        <li>Empty the saucer after 10 minutes.</li>      </ol>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Light &amp; placement</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Bright, indirect light is ideal. It tolerates low light but grows slower. Avoid direct afternoon sun — leaves can scorch.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Brown tips or mushy base?</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li><strong>Brown tips:</strong> Usually tap water minerals or inconsistent watering. Switch to filtered water.</li>        <li><strong>Mushy base:</strong> Root rot from overwatering. Remove from pot, trim mushy roots, repot in dry cactus mix, and wait a week before watering.</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <p class="muted">Feed once in spring and once in summer with diluted houseplant fertilizer. Otherwise, ignore it — it prefers neglect.</p></div>	-
33.20.0	component_spec/Cold Brew Coffee at Home	<div class="card">  <h4>Cold Brew Coffee at Home</h4>  <p class="muted">Smooth, low-acid, and forgiving. No fancy gear needed.</p>  <div class="callout info">    <p><strong>Basic ratio:</strong> 1 part coarse coffee to 4 parts cold water by weight. For a 1-quart jar: 100g coffee + 400g water.</p>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Grind coarse.</strong> Like sea salt. A burr grinder is best, but a blade grinder pulsed works in a pinch.</li>    <li><strong>Combine.</strong> Stir coffee and water in a jar. Cover.</li>    <li><strong>Steep 12–18 hours</strong> at room temperature (or 24 hours in the fridge for a cleaner taste).</li>    <li><strong>Strain.</strong> Pour through a fine-mesh sieve lined with a coffee filter or cheesecloth. Discard grounds.</li>    <li><strong>Dilute to taste.</strong> The concentrate is strong. Mix 1:1 with water or milk.</li>  </ol>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Cold brew vs. iced coffee</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Iced coffee is brewed hot and chilled — brighter, more acidic. Cold brew steeps cold — smoother, less acidic, higher caffeine per ounce.</p>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout warn">    <p><strong>Too weak?</strong> Next batch, use more coffee or steep longer. Don't over-dilute — taste before adding water.</p>  </div>  <p class="muted">Store concentrate in the fridge up to 2 weeks. Shake before serving.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <h4>Cold Brew Coffee at Home</h4>  <p class="muted">Smooth, low-acid, and forgiving. No fancy gear needed.</p>  <div class="callout info">    <p><strong>Basic ratio:</strong> 1 part coarse coffee to 4 parts cold water by weight. For a 1-quart jar: 100g coffee + 400g water.</p>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Grind coarse.</strong> Like sea salt. A burr grinder is best, but a blade grinder pulsed works in a pinch.</li>    <li><strong>Combine.</strong> Stir coffee and water in a jar. Cover.</li>    <li><strong>Steep 12–18 hours</strong> at room temperature (or 24 hours in the fridge for a cleaner taste).</li>    <li><strong>Strain.</strong> Pour through a fine-mesh sieve lined with a coffee filter or cheesecloth. Discard grounds.</li>    <li><strong>Dilute to taste.</strong> The concentrate is strong. Mix 1:1 with water or milk.</li>  </ol>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Cold brew vs. iced coffee</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Iced coffee is brewed hot and chilled — brighter, more acidic. Cold brew steeps cold — smoother, less acidic, higher caffeine per ounce.</p>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout warn">    <p><strong>Too weak?</strong> Next batch, use more coffee or steep longer. Don't over-dilute — taste before adding water.</p>  </div>  <p class="muted">Store concentrate in the fridge up to 2 weeks. Shake before serving.</p></div>	-
33.21.0	component_spec/Dishwasher Filter Cleaning	<div class="card">  <h4>Dishwasher Filter Cleaning</h4>  <p class="muted">A dirty filter makes dishes gritty and the whole kitchen smell like a wet dog. Most manufacturers say clean it monthly.</p>  <div class="callout good">    <p><strong>Time:</strong> 5–10 minutes. No tools needed for most models.</p>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Find the filter.</strong> Open the door and look at the bottom. It's usually a cylindrical mesh screen in the center or back corner.</li>    <li><strong>Remove it.</strong> Twist counterclockwise or lift straight up. Some have two parts — a coarse and a fine filter.</li>    <li><strong>Rinse under hot water.</strong> Use a soft brush (old toothbrush works) to scrub away food bits and grease.</li>    <li><strong>Soak if greasy.</strong> 10 minutes in hot water + a drop of dish soap.</li>    <li><strong>Reinstall.</strong> Twist until it locks. Run a rinse cycle to check.</li>  </ol>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Still smells? Try this.</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Place a cup of white vinegar on the top rack and run a hot water cycle. Then sprinkle baking soda on the bottom and run a short cycle. This kills odor-causing bacteria.</p>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout warn">    <p><strong>Never</strong> use bleach in a stainless steel dishwasher — it can corrode the tub.</p>  </div>  <p class="muted">Also check the spray arms for clogged holes. A toothpick clears them.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <h4>Dishwasher Filter Cleaning</h4>  <p class="muted">A dirty filter makes dishes gritty and the whole kitchen smell like a wet dog. Most manufacturers say clean it monthly.</p>  <div class="callout good">    <p><strong>Time:</strong> 5–10 minutes. No tools needed for most models.</p>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Find the filter.</strong> Open the door and look at the bottom. It's usually a cylindrical mesh screen in the center or back corner.</li>    <li><strong>Remove it.</strong> Twist counterclockwise or lift straight up. Some have two parts — a coarse and a fine filter.</li>    <li><strong>Rinse under hot water.</strong> Use a soft brush (old toothbrush works) to scrub away food bits and grease.</li>    <li><strong>Soak if greasy.</strong> 10 minutes in hot water + a drop of dish soap.</li>    <li><strong>Reinstall.</strong> Twist until it locks. Run a rinse cycle to check.</li>  </ol>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Still smells? Try this.</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Place a cup of white vinegar on the top rack and run a hot water cycle. Then sprinkle baking soda on the bottom and run a short cycle. This kills odor-causing bacteria.</p>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout warn">    <p><strong>Never</strong> use bleach in a stainless steel dishwasher — it can corrode the tub.</p>  </div>  <p class="muted">Also check the spray arms for clogged holes. A toothpick clears them.</p></div>	-
33.22.0	component_spec/Hanging Pictures Right	<div class="card">  <h4>Hanging Pictures Right</h4>  <p class="muted">No more crooked frames or Swiss-cheese walls.</p>  <div class="callout info">    <p><strong>Height rule:</strong> Center of the art at 57–60 inches from the floor — average eye level. Over furniture, leave 6–8 inches between the frame bottom and the tabletop or sofa back.</p>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Which hanger for my wall?</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li><strong>Drywall, light frame:</strong> Picture hanging hook (the tiny nail kind) or adhesive strips.</li>        <li><strong>Drywall, heavy frame:</strong> Use a drywall anchor and screw. Toggle bolts for really heavy pieces.</li>        <li><strong>Plaster or lath:</strong> Drill a pilot hole to avoid cracks. Use a screw with an anchor.</li>        <li><strong>Brick or concrete:</strong> Masonry bit + plastic anchor + screw.</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>How to hang straight every time</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Flip the frame over. Stretch a piece of painter's tape across the back, sticky side out.</li>        <li>Poke the tape where the hanger hooks sit. That marks the exact spots on the wall.</li>        <li>Stick the tape on the wall, level it, and nail through the marks.</li>      </ol>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout warn">    <p><strong>Adhesive strips</strong> can fail in humid rooms or on textured walls. For anything over 5 lbs, use a nail or anchor.</p>  </div>  <p class="muted">Use two hooks for wide or heavy frames to keep them from tilting.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <h4>Hanging Pictures Right</h4>  <p class="muted">No more crooked frames or Swiss-cheese walls.</p>  <div class="callout info">    <p><strong>Height rule:</strong> Center of the art at 57–60 inches from the floor — average eye level. Over furniture, leave 6–8 inches between the frame bottom and the tabletop or sofa back.</p>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Which hanger for my wall?</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li><strong>Drywall, light frame:</strong> Picture hanging hook (the tiny nail kind) or adhesive strips.</li>        <li><strong>Drywall, heavy frame:</strong> Use a drywall anchor and screw. Toggle bolts for really heavy pieces.</li>        <li><strong>Plaster or lath:</strong> Drill a pilot hole to avoid cracks. Use a screw with an anchor.</li>        <li><strong>Brick or concrete:</strong> Masonry bit + plastic anchor + screw.</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>How to hang straight every time</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Flip the frame over. Stretch a piece of painter's tape across the back, sticky side out.</li>        <li>Poke the tape where the hanger hooks sit. That marks the exact spots on the wall.</li>        <li>Stick the tape on the wall, level it, and nail through the marks.</li>      </ol>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout warn">    <p><strong>Adhesive strips</strong> can fail in humid rooms or on textured walls. For anything over 5 lbs, use a nail or anchor.</p>  </div>  <p class="muted">Use two hooks for wide or heavy frames to keep them from tilting.</p></div>	-
33.23.0	component_spec/Silence a Squeaky Door	<div class="card">  <h4>Silence a Squeaky Door</h4>  <p class="muted">That chirp is metal rubbing metal. Lubricant fixes it in 2 minutes.</p>  <div class="callout info">    <p><strong>Best lubes:</strong> White lithium grease, silicone spray, or petroleum jelly. Avoid cooking oil — it gets gummy.</p>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Wipe the hinge.</strong> Dirt and old grease cause friction. A rag and a little rubbing alcohol work.</li>    <li><strong>Lift the pin.</strong> Tap the hinge pin up from below with a screwdriver and hammer. Don't remove it completely — just lift it an inch.</li>    <li><strong>Lubricate.</strong> Apply a dab of grease to the pin and inside the hinge knuckles.</li>    <li><strong>Tap it back.</strong> Gently hammer the pin down until it's flush.</li>    <li><strong>Swing the door</strong> back and forth a few times to spread the lube.</li>  </ol>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Still squeaks?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>The hinge might be worn. If the door sags or the pin wiggles, replace the hinge — they're cheap and sold at any hardware store.</p>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout warn">    <p><strong>WD-40</strong> is a solvent, not a lubricant. It'll silence the squeak for a week, then it comes back worse. Use actual grease.</p>  </div>  <p class="muted">Do all three hinges while you're at it. Future you will thank you.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <h4>Silence a Squeaky Door</h4>  <p class="muted">That chirp is metal rubbing metal. Lubricant fixes it in 2 minutes.</p>  <div class="callout info">    <p><strong>Best lubes:</strong> White lithium grease, silicone spray, or petroleum jelly. Avoid cooking oil — it gets gummy.</p>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Wipe the hinge.</strong> Dirt and old grease cause friction. A rag and a little rubbing alcohol work.</li>    <li><strong>Lift the pin.</strong> Tap the hinge pin up from below with a screwdriver and hammer. Don't remove it completely — just lift it an inch.</li>    <li><strong>Lubricate.</strong> Apply a dab of grease to the pin and inside the hinge knuckles.</li>    <li><strong>Tap it back.</strong> Gently hammer the pin down until it's flush.</li>    <li><strong>Swing the door</strong> back and forth a few times to spread the lube.</li>  </ol>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Still squeaks?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>The hinge might be worn. If the door sags or the pin wiggles, replace the hinge — they're cheap and sold at any hardware store.</p>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout warn">    <p><strong>WD-40</strong> is a solvent, not a lubricant. It'll silence the squeak for a week, then it comes back worse. Use actual grease.</p>  </div>  <p class="muted">Do all three hinges while you're at it. Future you will thank you.</p></div>	-
33.24.0	component_spec/Pizza Reheating Rescue	<div class="card">  <h4>Pizza Reheating Rescue</h4>  <p class="muted">No microwave needed.</p>  <p>Here's how to bring leftover pizza back to life.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Skillet method</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Heat a non-stick skillet over medium. Add a slice, cover, cook 2–3 minutes until bottom is crisp and cheese is melty. No oil needed.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Oven method</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Preheat oven to 375°F (190°C). Put slices on a baking sheet, bake 6–8 minutes. For extra crisp, place directly on the rack.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Air fryer method</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Air fry at 350°F (175°C) for 3–4 minutes. Check often—cheese can fly off.</p>    </div>  </details>  <p class="muted t-small">Works best with thin crust. Thick crust may need a minute more.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <h4>Pizza Reheating Rescue</h4>  <p class="muted">No microwave needed.</p>  <p>Here's how to bring leftover pizza back to life.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Skillet method</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Heat a non-stick skillet over medium. Add a slice, cover, cook 2–3 minutes until bottom is crisp and cheese is melty. No oil needed.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Oven method</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Preheat oven to 375°F (190°C). Put slices on a baking sheet, bake 6–8 minutes. For extra crisp, place directly on the rack.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Air fryer method</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Air fry at 350°F (175°C) for 3–4 minutes. Check often—cheese can fly off.</p>    </div>  </details>  <p class="muted t-small">Works best with thin crust. Thick crust may need a minute more.</p></div>	-
33.25.0	component_spec/Fitted Sheet Folding Trick	<div class="card">  <h4>Fitted Sheet Folding Trick</h4>  <p class="muted">No more crumpled ball.</p>  <p>Here's the easy way to fold a fitted sheet.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li>Hold the sheet inside out, with the elastic edges facing you.</li>    <li>Put your hands into two adjacent corners (like mittens).</li>    <li>Fold one corner over the other, tucking it in. Repeat with the remaining two corners so all four are stacked.</li>    <li>Lay the sheet on a flat surface, smooth it out.</li>    <li>Fold in half lengthwise, then fold the elastic edges inward to make a rectangle.</li>    <li>Fold in half again or thirds to desired size.</li>  </ol>  <p class="muted t-small">The key is to tuck the corners into each other—no elastic snapping.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <h4>Fitted Sheet Folding Trick</h4>  <p class="muted">No more crumpled ball.</p>  <p>Here's the easy way to fold a fitted sheet.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li>Hold the sheet inside out, with the elastic edges facing you.</li>    <li>Put your hands into two adjacent corners (like mittens).</li>    <li>Fold one corner over the other, tucking it in. Repeat with the remaining two corners so all four are stacked.</li>    <li>Lay the sheet on a flat surface, smooth it out.</li>    <li>Fold in half lengthwise, then fold the elastic edges inward to make a rectangle.</li>    <li>Fold in half again or thirds to desired size.</li>  </ol>  <p class="muted t-small">The key is to tuck the corners into each other—no elastic snapping.</p></div>	-
33.26.0	component_spec/Fruit Fly Trap DIY	<div class="card">  <h4>Fruit Fly Trap DIY</h4>  <p class="muted">Three pantry traps that work.</p>  <p>Try these simple traps to catch fruit flies fast.</p>  <ul class="list">    <li><strong>Apple cider vinegar trap:</strong> Pour a little ACV into a bowl, add a drop of dish soap. Flies dive in and drown.</li>    <li><strong>Paper cone trap:</strong> Put a piece of overripe fruit in a jar. Roll paper into a cone, place in jar opening. Flies enter but can't find their way out.</li>    <li><strong>Wine trap:</strong> Leave a bit of red wine in a bottle. The narrow neck traps them.</li>  </ul>  <p class="muted t-small">Set traps near the source. Clean up any overripe produce to stop the cycle.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <h4>Fruit Fly Trap DIY</h4>  <p class="muted">Three pantry traps that work.</p>  <p>Try these simple traps to catch fruit flies fast.</p>  <ul class="list">    <li><strong>Apple cider vinegar trap:</strong> Pour a little ACV into a bowl, add a drop of dish soap. Flies dive in and drown.</li>    <li><strong>Paper cone trap:</strong> Put a piece of overripe fruit in a jar. Roll paper into a cone, place in jar opening. Flies enter but can't find their way out.</li>    <li><strong>Wine trap:</strong> Leave a bit of red wine in a bottle. The narrow neck traps them.</li>  </ul>  <p class="muted t-small">Set traps near the source. Clean up any overripe produce to stop the cycle.</p></div>	-
33.27.0	component_spec/Coffee Grinder Cleaning Steps	<div class="card">  <h4>Coffee Grinder Cleaning Steps</h4>  <p class="muted">Keep your grind fresh.</p>  <p>Clean your grinder every few weeks for better tasting coffee.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li>Unplug the grinder.</li>    <li>Empty any leftover beans.</li>    <li>For burr grinders: Remove the hopper and burr assembly if possible. Brush out grounds with a stiff brush.</li>    <li>For blade grinders: Wipe the blades and bowl with a damp cloth. Dry thoroughly.</li>    <li>Use a vacuum or pipe cleaner to get into crevices.</li>    <li>Reassemble and run a small handful of uncooked rice or grinder cleaning pellets through to absorb oils.</li>    <li>Discard the rice/pellets and wipe clean.</li>  </ol>  <p class="muted t-small">Never use water on burrs—moisture causes rust.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <h4>Coffee Grinder Cleaning Steps</h4>  <p class="muted">Keep your grind fresh.</p>  <p>Clean your grinder every few weeks for better tasting coffee.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li>Unplug the grinder.</li>    <li>Empty any leftover beans.</li>    <li>For burr grinders: Remove the hopper and burr assembly if possible. Brush out grounds with a stiff brush.</li>    <li>For blade grinders: Wipe the blades and bowl with a damp cloth. Dry thoroughly.</li>    <li>Use a vacuum or pipe cleaner to get into crevices.</li>    <li>Reassemble and run a small handful of uncooked rice or grinder cleaning pellets through to absorb oils.</li>    <li>Discard the rice/pellets and wipe clean.</li>  </ol>  <p class="muted t-small">Never use water on burrs—moisture causes rust.</p></div>	-
33.28.0	component_spec/Microwave Steam Clean	<div class="card">  <h4>Microwave Steam Clean</h4>  <p class="muted">No scrubbing needed.</p>  <p>Loosen stuck-on food with steam.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li>Fill a microwave-safe bowl with 1 cup water and a splash of vinegar or lemon juice.</li>    <li>Microwave on high for 3–5 minutes, until window steams up.</li>    <li>Let sit for 5 minutes (don't open yet).</li>    <li>Carefully remove bowl. Wipe interior with a damp cloth—grime slides right off.</li>    <li>For stubborn spots, repeat or use a paste of baking soda and water.</li>  </ol>  <p class="muted t-small">Leave the door open afterward to dry and prevent odors.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <h4>Microwave Steam Clean</h4>  <p class="muted">No scrubbing needed.</p>  <p>Loosen stuck-on food with steam.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li>Fill a microwave-safe bowl with 1 cup water and a splash of vinegar or lemon juice.</li>    <li>Microwave on high for 3–5 minutes, until window steams up.</li>    <li>Let sit for 5 minutes (don't open yet).</li>    <li>Carefully remove bowl. Wipe interior with a damp cloth—grime slides right off.</li>    <li>For stubborn spots, repeat or use a paste of baking soda and water.</li>  </ol>  <p class="muted t-small">Leave the door open afterward to dry and prevent odors.</p></div>	-
33.29.0	component_spec/Drain Unclogging Basics	<div class="card">  <h4>Drain Unclogging Basics</h4>  <p class="muted">Try these before calling a plumber.</p>  <p>Start with the gentlest method.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Boiling water:</strong> Pour a kettle of boiling water down the drain in stages. Works for grease and soap scum.</li>    <li><strong>Baking soda + vinegar:</strong> Pour 1/2 cup baking soda, then 1/2 cup vinegar. Cover with a plug. Wait 15 minutes, then flush with hot water.</li>    <li><strong>Plunger:</strong> Use a cup plunger for sinks, flange plunger for toilets. Seal the drain and plunge vigorously.</li>    <li><strong>Drain snake:</strong> Feed a manual snake into the drain, crank to break up clogs.</li>  </ol>  <p class="muted t-small">Avoid chemical drain cleaners—they can damage pipes.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <h4>Drain Unclogging Basics</h4>  <p class="muted">Try these before calling a plumber.</p>  <p>Start with the gentlest method.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Boiling water:</strong> Pour a kettle of boiling water down the drain in stages. Works for grease and soap scum.</li>    <li><strong>Baking soda + vinegar:</strong> Pour 1/2 cup baking soda, then 1/2 cup vinegar. Cover with a plug. Wait 15 minutes, then flush with hot water.</li>    <li><strong>Plunger:</strong> Use a cup plunger for sinks, flange plunger for toilets. Seal the drain and plunge vigorously.</li>    <li><strong>Drain snake:</strong> Feed a manual snake into the drain, crank to break up clogs.</li>  </ol>  <p class="muted t-small">Avoid chemical drain cleaners—they can damage pipes.</p></div>	-
33.30.0	component_spec/Fix a Dripping Kitchen Faucet	<div class="card">  <h4>Fix a Dripping Kitchen Faucet</h4>  <p class="muted">Most drips come from a worn washer, O‑ring, or cartridge. Turn off the water supply and relieve pressure before opening the handle.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Step‑by‑step</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Shut off the hot and cold valves under the sink (or main). Open the faucet to drain.</li>        <li>Plug the drain so small parts don’t vanish.</li>        <li>Pry off the handle cap, unscrew the set screw, lift off the handle.</li>        <li>Remove the retaining nut, pull out the cartridge or stem.</li>        <li>Inspect O‑rings and washers. Replace any that are cracked or flattened. Match parts exactly—take them to the store.</li>        <li>Reassemble in reverse order. Hand‑tighten the nut, then a quarter turn with a wrench.</li>        <li>Turn water back on slowly. Check for leaks. Run hot and cold to flush.</li>      </ol>      <p class="t-small muted">If the drip continues, the valve seat may be scratched. A seat‑grinding tool can smooth it, or replace the faucet.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="card">  <h4>Fix a Dripping Kitchen Faucet</h4>  <p class="muted">Most drips come from a worn washer, O‑ring, or cartridge. Turn off the water supply and relieve pressure before opening the handle.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Step‑by‑step</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Shut off the hot and cold valves under the sink (or main). Open the faucet to drain.</li>        <li>Plug the drain so small parts don’t vanish.</li>        <li>Pry off the handle cap, unscrew the set screw, lift off the handle.</li>        <li>Remove the retaining nut, pull out the cartridge or stem.</li>        <li>Inspect O‑rings and washers. Replace any that are cracked or flattened. Match parts exactly—take them to the store.</li>        <li>Reassemble in reverse order. Hand‑tighten the nut, then a quarter turn with a wrench.</li>        <li>Turn water back on slowly. Check for leaks. Run hot and cold to flush.</li>      </ol>      <p class="t-small muted">If the drip continues, the valve seat may be scratched. A seat‑grinding tool can smooth it, or replace the faucet.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
33.31.0	component_spec/Remove a Stripped Screw	<div class="card">  <h4>Remove a Stripped Screw</h4>  <p class="muted">Stripped screw? Don’t panic. A rubber band, a different bit, or a little gentle persuasion usually does it.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Methods that work</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li><strong>Rubber band trick:</strong> Place a wide rubber band over the screw head, press the driver in firmly, and turn slowly.</li>        <li><strong>Try a different driver:</strong> Sometimes a slightly larger flathead or a star bit grips better than the original.</li>        <li><strong>Tap and turn:</strong> Give the screw a few light taps with a hammer (if wood) to break the grip, then try again.</li>        <li><strong>Screw extractor:</strong> These bits bite into the stripped head. Drill a small pilot hole first, then use the extractor in reverse.</li>        <li><strong>Heat or lubricant:</strong> A drop of penetrating oil or a few seconds with a hair dryer can loosen rust or paint.</li>      </ul>      <p class="t-small muted">If all else fails, drill off the head and remove the shank with pliers.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="card">  <h4>Remove a Stripped Screw</h4>  <p class="muted">Stripped screw? Don’t panic. A rubber band, a different bit, or a little gentle persuasion usually does it.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Methods that work</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li><strong>Rubber band trick:</strong> Place a wide rubber band over the screw head, press the driver in firmly, and turn slowly.</li>        <li><strong>Try a different driver:</strong> Sometimes a slightly larger flathead or a star bit grips better than the original.</li>        <li><strong>Tap and turn:</strong> Give the screw a few light taps with a hammer (if wood) to break the grip, then try again.</li>        <li><strong>Screw extractor:</strong> These bits bite into the stripped head. Drill a small pilot hole first, then use the extractor in reverse.</li>        <li><strong>Heat or lubricant:</strong> A drop of penetrating oil or a few seconds with a hair dryer can loosen rust or paint.</li>      </ul>      <p class="t-small muted">If all else fails, drill off the head and remove the shank with pliers.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
33.32.0	component_spec/Eliminate Static Cling in Laundry	<div class="card">  <h4>Eliminate Static Cling in Laundry</h4>  <p class="muted">Static happens when fabrics rub together in a dry environment. A few tweaks to your laundry routine can zap it for good.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Prevention &amp; fixes</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li><strong>Don’t overdry.</strong> Remove clothes while slightly damp; static builds with heat and time.</li>        <li><strong>Add moisture.</strong> Toss in a damp towel or use wool dryer balls. A safety pin on a hem also helps.</li>        <li><strong>Separate fabrics.</strong> Synthetics generate more static than cotton; dry them separately or with a dryer sheet.</li>        <li><strong>Use vinegar.</strong> Add ½ cup white vinegar to the rinse cycle to soften and reduce static.</li>        <li><strong>Dryer sheet or aluminum foil ball.</strong> Both neutralize charge. Foil lasts for many loads.</li>      </ul>      <p class="t-small muted">Already clingy? Rub a dryer sheet or a bit of lotion on your hands and smooth the garment.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="card">  <h4>Eliminate Static Cling in Laundry</h4>  <p class="muted">Static happens when fabrics rub together in a dry environment. A few tweaks to your laundry routine can zap it for good.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Prevention &amp; fixes</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li><strong>Don’t overdry.</strong> Remove clothes while slightly damp; static builds with heat and time.</li>        <li><strong>Add moisture.</strong> Toss in a damp towel or use wool dryer balls. A safety pin on a hem also helps.</li>        <li><strong>Separate fabrics.</strong> Synthetics generate more static than cotton; dry them separately or with a dryer sheet.</li>        <li><strong>Use vinegar.</strong> Add ½ cup white vinegar to the rinse cycle to soften and reduce static.</li>        <li><strong>Dryer sheet or aluminum foil ball.</strong> Both neutralize charge. Foil lasts for many loads.</li>      </ul>      <p class="t-small muted">Already clingy? Rub a dryer sheet or a bit of lotion on your hands and smooth the garment.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
33.33.0	component_spec/Clean Your Coffee Maker	<div class="card">  <h4>Clean Your Coffee Maker</h4>  <p class="muted">Oils and mineral scale build up, making coffee bitter and slow. A monthly vinegar run keeps it tasting fresh.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Step‑by‑step</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Fill the reservoir with equal parts white vinegar and water.</li>        <li>Place a filter in the basket and start a brew cycle. Stop halfway and let it sit for 30 minutes.</li>        <li>Finish the cycle, then discard the vinegar solution.</li>        <li>Run two or three full cycles with plain water to rinse.</li>        <li>Wash the carafe, basket, and lid with warm soapy water. Rinse well.</li>        <li>Wipe the exterior with a damp cloth. Don’t immerse the base.</li>      </ol>      <p class="t-small muted">For pod machines, follow the manufacturer’s descaling instructions—often a similar vinegar or citric acid solution.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="card">  <h4>Clean Your Coffee Maker</h4>  <p class="muted">Oils and mineral scale build up, making coffee bitter and slow. A monthly vinegar run keeps it tasting fresh.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Step‑by‑step</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Fill the reservoir with equal parts white vinegar and water.</li>        <li>Place a filter in the basket and start a brew cycle. Stop halfway and let it sit for 30 minutes.</li>        <li>Finish the cycle, then discard the vinegar solution.</li>        <li>Run two or three full cycles with plain water to rinse.</li>        <li>Wash the carafe, basket, and lid with warm soapy water. Rinse well.</li>        <li>Wipe the exterior with a damp cloth. Don’t immerse the base.</li>      </ol>      <p class="t-small muted">For pod machines, follow the manufacturer’s descaling instructions—often a similar vinegar or citric acid solution.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
33.34.0	component_spec/Stop a Running Toilet	<div class="card">  <h4>Stop a Running Toilet</h4>  <p class="muted">A running toilet wastes water and money. The culprit is usually the flapper, fill valve, or float. Here’s how to diagnose and fix it.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Common fixes</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li><strong>Flapper:</strong> If water runs intermittently, the flapper may not seal. Turn off water, empty tank, and replace the flapper (cheap and easy).</li>        <li><strong>Float:</strong> If water runs constantly, the float may be set too high. Adjust the screw or clip so the water stops about an inch below the overflow tube.</li>        <li><strong>Fill valve:</strong> If the tank won’t fill or runs forever, the fill valve may be faulty. Replace it—it’s a bit more involved but doable.</li>        <li><strong>Chain:</strong> Make sure the chain isn’t caught under the flapper or too long.</li>      </ul>      <p class="t-small muted">Always turn off the water supply and flush before working. Keep a towel handy.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="card">  <h4>Stop a Running Toilet</h4>  <p class="muted">A running toilet wastes water and money. The culprit is usually the flapper, fill valve, or float. Here’s how to diagnose and fix it.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Common fixes</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li><strong>Flapper:</strong> If water runs intermittently, the flapper may not seal. Turn off water, empty tank, and replace the flapper (cheap and easy).</li>        <li><strong>Float:</strong> If water runs constantly, the float may be set too high. Adjust the screw or clip so the water stops about an inch below the overflow tube.</li>        <li><strong>Fill valve:</strong> If the tank won’t fill or runs forever, the fill valve may be faulty. Replace it—it’s a bit more involved but doable.</li>        <li><strong>Chain:</strong> Make sure the chain isn’t caught under the flapper or too long.</li>      </ul>      <p class="t-small muted">Always turn off the water supply and flush before working. Keep a towel handy.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
33.35.0	component_spec/Organize a Junk Drawer	<div class="card">  <h4>Organize a Junk Drawer</h4>  <p class="muted">The junk drawer is a black hole. With a few minutes and some dividers, it can become a useful spot for everyday tools.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Sort, purge, contain</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li><strong>Empty it completely.</strong> Wipe out crumbs and dust.</li>        <li><strong>Sort into keep, relocate, toss.</strong> Be ruthless: if you haven’t used it in a year, let it go.</li>        <li><strong>Group keepers:</strong> batteries, tape, scissors, pens, chargers, etc.</li>        <li><strong>Contain with dividers.</strong> Use small boxes, drawer organizers, or even cut cardboard. A cutlery tray works well.</li>        <li><strong>Label if needed.</strong> A label maker or masking tape and marker helps everyone put things back.</li>        <li><strong>Limit the space.</strong> If it overflows, it’s time for another purge.</li>      </ol>      <p class="t-small muted">Keep only what you reach for weekly. The rest can live elsewhere.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="card">  <h4>Organize a Junk Drawer</h4>  <p class="muted">The junk drawer is a black hole. With a few minutes and some dividers, it can become a useful spot for everyday tools.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Sort, purge, contain</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li><strong>Empty it completely.</strong> Wipe out crumbs and dust.</li>        <li><strong>Sort into keep, relocate, toss.</strong> Be ruthless: if you haven’t used it in a year, let it go.</li>        <li><strong>Group keepers:</strong> batteries, tape, scissors, pens, chargers, etc.</li>        <li><strong>Contain with dividers.</strong> Use small boxes, drawer organizers, or even cut cardboard. A cutlery tray works well.</li>        <li><strong>Label if needed.</strong> A label maker or masking tape and marker helps everyone put things back.</li>        <li><strong>Limit the space.</strong> If it overflows, it’s time for another purge.</li>      </ol>      <p class="t-small muted">Keep only what you reach for weekly. The rest can live elsewhere.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
33.36.0	component_spec/Tire Tread Depth Check	<div class="card"><h4>Tire Tread Depth Check</h4><p class="muted">Props: tread_depth</p><p>Check your tire tread depth with the penny test or a gauge to know when to replace.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Penny Test</summary><div class="body"><p>Insert a penny upside down into the tread. If you can see all of Lincoln's head, tread is 2/32" or less—replace soon.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Tread Depth Gauge</summary><div class="body"><p>Use a gauge for precise measurement. 2/32" is the legal minimum; 4/32" for wet, 6/32" for snow.</p></div></details><p class="callout info">Check monthly and before long trips.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Tire Tread Depth Check</h4><p class="muted">Props: tread_depth</p><p>Check your tire tread depth with the penny test or a gauge to know when to replace.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Penny Test</summary><div class="body"><p>Insert a penny upside down into the tread. If you can see all of Lincoln's head, tread is 2/32" or less—replace soon.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Tread Depth Gauge</summary><div class="body"><p>Use a gauge for precise measurement. 2/32" is the legal minimum; 4/32" for wet, 6/32" for snow.</p></div></details><p class="callout info">Check monthly and before long trips.</p></div>	-
33.37.0	component_spec/Cast Iron Cleaning & Care	<div class="card"><h4>Cast Iron Cleaning & Care</h4><p class="muted">Props: skillet_size</p><p>Keep your cast iron in top shape with these simple steps.</p><details class="acc"><summary>After Each Use</summary><div class="body"><p>Rinse with hot water and scrub with a stiff brush. Avoid soap unless heavily soiled. Dry immediately.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Deep Clean</summary><div class="body"><p>For stuck-on food, simmer water in the pan, then scrape with a wooden spatula. Dry and re-oil.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Rust Removal</summary><div class="body"><p>Scrub rust with steel wool, wash, dry, and re-season by coating with oil and baking at 450°F for an hour.</p></div></details><p class="callout good">A well-seasoned pan is naturally non-stick.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Cast Iron Cleaning & Care</h4><p class="muted">Props: skillet_size</p><p>Keep your cast iron in top shape with these simple steps.</p><details class="acc"><summary>After Each Use</summary><div class="body"><p>Rinse with hot water and scrub with a stiff brush. Avoid soap unless heavily soiled. Dry immediately.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Deep Clean</summary><div class="body"><p>For stuck-on food, simmer water in the pan, then scrape with a wooden spatula. Dry and re-oil.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Rust Removal</summary><div class="body"><p>Scrub rust with steel wool, wash, dry, and re-season by coating with oil and baking at 450°F for an hour.</p></div></details><p class="callout good">A well-seasoned pan is naturally non-stick.</p></div>	-
33.38.0	component_spec/Squeaky Floor Fix	<div class="card"><h4>Squeaky Floor Fix</h4><p class="muted">Props: floor_type</p><p>Silence that annoying squeak with a few simple techniques.</p><details class="acc"><summary>From Above</summary><div class="body"><p>If carpeted, locate squeak and drive a trim screw through the carpet into the joist. For hardwood, use a squeak-relief kit.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>From Below</summary><div class="body"><p>In basement, apply construction adhesive between subfloor and joist, then insert shims.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Lubricate</summary><div class="body"><p>For minor squeaks, sprinkle talcum powder into seams and work it in.</p></div></details><p class="callout warn">Avoid over-tightening screws to prevent damage.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Squeaky Floor Fix</h4><p class="muted">Props: floor_type</p><p>Silence that annoying squeak with a few simple techniques.</p><details class="acc"><summary>From Above</summary><div class="body"><p>If carpeted, locate squeak and drive a trim screw through the carpet into the joist. For hardwood, use a squeak-relief kit.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>From Below</summary><div class="body"><p>In basement, apply construction adhesive between subfloor and joist, then insert shims.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Lubricate</summary><div class="body"><p>For minor squeaks, sprinkle talcum powder into seams and work it in.</p></div></details><p class="callout warn">Avoid over-tightening screws to prevent damage.</p></div>	-
33.39.0	component_spec/Red Wine Stain Removal	<div class="card"><h4>Red Wine Stain Removal</h4><p class="muted">Props: fabric_type</p><p>Act fast to lift red wine stains from fabric.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Blot</summary><div class="body"><p>Blot with a clean cloth to absorb as much wine as possible. Don't rub—it spreads the stain.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Apply Salt</summary><div class="body"><p>Cover the stain with salt to draw out moisture. Let sit, then brush off.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Wash</summary><div class="body"><p>Pre-treat with stain remover or a mix of dish soap and hydrogen peroxide. Wash with cold water.</p></div></details><p class="callout info">For upholstery, use a steam cleaner after blotting.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Red Wine Stain Removal</h4><p class="muted">Props: fabric_type</p><p>Act fast to lift red wine stains from fabric.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Blot</summary><div class="body"><p>Blot with a clean cloth to absorb as much wine as possible. Don't rub—it spreads the stain.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Apply Salt</summary><div class="body"><p>Cover the stain with salt to draw out moisture. Let sit, then brush off.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Wash</summary><div class="body"><p>Pre-treat with stain remover or a mix of dish soap and hydrogen peroxide. Wash with cold water.</p></div></details><p class="callout info">For upholstery, use a steam cleaner after blotting.</p></div>	-
33.40.0	component_spec/Ant Removal Basics	<div class="card"><h4>Ant Removal Basics</h4><p class="muted">Props: ant_type</p><p>Say goodbye to ants with these steps.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Find the Trail</summary><div class="body"><p>Follow ants to locate entry points. Seal cracks with caulk.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Bait</summary><div class="body"><p>Use ant bait stations—ants carry poison back to the colony. Avoid sprays that kill on contact.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Clean</summary><div class="body"><p>Wipe surfaces with vinegar to erase pheromone trails. Store food in sealed containers.</p></div></details><p class="callout good">Be patient—baiting takes a few days.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Ant Removal Basics</h4><p class="muted">Props: ant_type</p><p>Say goodbye to ants with these steps.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Find the Trail</summary><div class="body"><p>Follow ants to locate entry points. Seal cracks with caulk.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Bait</summary><div class="body"><p>Use ant bait stations—ants carry poison back to the colony. Avoid sprays that kill on contact.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Clean</summary><div class="body"><p>Wipe surfaces with vinegar to erase pheromone trails. Store food in sealed containers.</p></div></details><p class="callout good">Be patient—baiting takes a few days.</p></div>	-
33.41.0	component_spec/Shower Drain Unclogging	<div class="card"><h4>Shower Drain Unclogging</h4><p class="muted">Props: drain_type</p><p>Clear that slow shower drain without harsh chemicals.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Remove Hair</summary><div class="body"><p>Unscrew drain cover and pull out hair with a wire hanger or drain snake.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Baking Soda & Vinegar</summary><div class="body"><p>Pour 1/2 cup baking soda, then 1/2 cup vinegar. Let fizz for 15 min, then flush with hot water.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Prevent</summary><div class="body"><p>Use a drain catcher to catch hair before it clogs.</p></div></details><p class="callout warn">Avoid chemical drain cleaners—they can damage pipes.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Shower Drain Unclogging</h4><p class="muted">Props: drain_type</p><p>Clear that slow shower drain without harsh chemicals.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Remove Hair</summary><div class="body"><p>Unscrew drain cover and pull out hair with a wire hanger or drain snake.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Baking Soda & Vinegar</summary><div class="body"><p>Pour 1/2 cup baking soda, then 1/2 cup vinegar. Let fizz for 15 min, then flush with hot water.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Prevent</summary><div class="body"><p>Use a drain catcher to catch hair before it clogs.</p></div></details><p class="callout warn">Avoid chemical drain cleaners—they can damage pipes.</p></div>	-
33.42.0	component_spec/Stop a Sliding Cutting Board	<div class="umi-page"><div class="stack"><h2 class="card-title">Stop a Sliding Cutting Board</h2><p class="muted">Hey <regex>user_name</regex>, a board that skates while you chop is a knife-into-finger situation waiting to happen. Two seconds of prep fixes it.</p><div class="card"><h4>Quick fixes</h4><ul class="list"><li><strong>Damp towel:</strong> Lay a thin, wrung-out towel under the board. The moisture creates grip without mess.</li><li><strong>Rubber bands:</strong> Stretch two bands around each end of the board—instant non-slip feet.</li><li><strong>Non-slip mat:</strong> A piece of shelf liner or a silicone mat cut to size works long-term.</li><li><strong>Sugar trick:</strong> Sprinkle a little water then sugar under the board; it sets like glue and rinses clean.</li></ul></div><details class="acc"><summary>Why this happens</summary><div class="body"><p>Most boards slide because the counter and board are both smooth and dry. A microscopic layer of water or a textured interface breaks the suction.</p></div></details><div class="callout warn"><p>Don't use tape—residue is a pain and it still shifts.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="stack"><h2 class="card-title">Stop a Sliding Cutting Board</h2><p class="muted">Hey , a board that skates while you chop is a knife-into-finger situation waiting to happen. Two seconds of prep fixes it.</p><div class="card"><h4>Quick fixes</h4><ul class="list"><li><strong>Damp towel:</strong> Lay a thin, wrung-out towel under the board. The moisture creates grip without mess.</li><li><strong>Rubber bands:</strong> Stretch two bands around each end of the board—instant non-slip feet.</li><li><strong>Non-slip mat:</strong> A piece of shelf liner or a silicone mat cut to size works long-term.</li><li><strong>Sugar trick:</strong> Sprinkle a little water then sugar under the board; it sets like glue and rinses clean.</li></ul></div><details class="acc"><summary>Why this happens</summary><div class="body"><p>Most boards slide because the counter and board are both smooth and dry. A microscopic layer of water or a textured interface breaks the suction.</p></div></details><div class="callout warn"><p>Don't use tape—residue is a pain and it still shifts.</p></div></div></div>	-
33.43.0	component_spec/De-Stink a Kitchen Sponge	<div class="umi-page"><div class="stack"><h2 class="card-title">De-Stink a Kitchen Sponge</h2><p class="muted"><regex>user_name</regex>, a smelly sponge is basically a bacteria condo. Here's how to kick them out.</p><div class="card"><h4>Methods that work</h4><ul class="list"><li><strong>Microwave:</strong> Soak sponge in water with a splash of lemon juice. Microwave on high for 1–2 minutes. Let cool before squeezing.</li><li><strong>Dishwasher:</strong> Run it through a hot cycle with your dishes. The heat sanitizes.</li><li><strong>Boiling:</strong> Drop it in boiling water for 5 minutes. Tongs help.</li><li><strong>Vinegar soak:</strong> Submerge in white vinegar for 5 minutes, then rinse and squeeze dry.</li></ul></div><details class="acc"><summary>How often?</summary><div class="body"><p>Once a day if you cook often. Replace the sponge every 1–2 weeks—no method makes an old sponge new again.</p></div></details><div class="callout info"><p>Never microwave a dry sponge or one with a metal scrubby layer. Fire hazard.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="stack"><h2 class="card-title">De-Stink a Kitchen Sponge</h2><p class="muted">, a smelly sponge is basically a bacteria condo. Here's how to kick them out.</p><div class="card"><h4>Methods that work</h4><ul class="list"><li><strong>Microwave:</strong> Soak sponge in water with a splash of lemon juice. Microwave on high for 1–2 minutes. Let cool before squeezing.</li><li><strong>Dishwasher:</strong> Run it through a hot cycle with your dishes. The heat sanitizes.</li><li><strong>Boiling:</strong> Drop it in boiling water for 5 minutes. Tongs help.</li><li><strong>Vinegar soak:</strong> Submerge in white vinegar for 5 minutes, then rinse and squeeze dry.</li></ul></div><details class="acc"><summary>How often?</summary><div class="body"><p>Once a day if you cook often. Replace the sponge every 1–2 weeks—no method makes an old sponge new again.</p></div></details><div class="callout info"><p>Never microwave a dry sponge or one with a metal scrubby layer. Fire hazard.</p></div></div></div>	-
33.44.0	component_spec/DIY Fruit Fly Trap That Works	<div class="umi-page"><div class="stack"><h2 class="card-title">DIY Fruit Fly Trap That Works</h2><p class="muted">Those tiny hoverers are annoying, <regex>user_name</regex>. This trap uses stuff you already have.</p><div class="card"><h4>You'll need</h4><ul class="list"><li>A small bowl or jar</li><li>Apple cider vinegar (or any fruit vinegar)</li><li>A few drops of dish soap</li><li>Plastic wrap and a rubber band (optional)</li></ul></div><ol class="list"><li>Pour about half an inch of vinegar into the bowl.</li><li>Add a drop of dish soap and swirl—this breaks the surface tension so flies sink.</li><li>If using plastic wrap, cover the bowl and poke a few small holes with a toothpick. Flies get in but can't find the way out.</li><li>Place near the fruit bowl or trash. Replace daily until they're gone.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Why it works</summary><div class="body"><p>Fruit flies are drawn to fermenting smells. The vinegar mimics that, and the soap makes them drown instead of float.</p></div></details><div class="callout good"><p>Also: take out the trash and rinse recycling. No food source, no flies.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="stack"><h2 class="card-title">DIY Fruit Fly Trap That Works</h2><p class="muted">Those tiny hoverers are annoying, . This trap uses stuff you already have.</p><div class="card"><h4>You'll need</h4><ul class="list"><li>A small bowl or jar</li><li>Apple cider vinegar (or any fruit vinegar)</li><li>A few drops of dish soap</li><li>Plastic wrap and a rubber band (optional)</li></ul></div><ol class="list"><li>Pour about half an inch of vinegar into the bowl.</li><li>Add a drop of dish soap and swirl—this breaks the surface tension so flies sink.</li><li>If using plastic wrap, cover the bowl and poke a few small holes with a toothpick. Flies get in but can't find the way out.</li><li>Place near the fruit bowl or trash. Replace daily until they're gone.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Why it works</summary><div class="body"><p>Fruit flies are drawn to fermenting smells. The vinegar mimics that, and the soap makes them drown instead of float.</p></div></details><div class="callout good"><p>Also: take out the trash and rinse recycling. No food source, no flies.</p></div></div></div>	-
33.45.0	component_spec/Remove Sticker Residue from Glass	<div class="umi-page"><div class="stack"><h2 class="card-title">Remove Sticker Residue from Glass</h2><p class="muted">That gummy patch on a jar is stubborn, <regex>user_name</regex>, but it doesn't stand a chance against a little oil and patience.</p><div class="card"><h4>What you need</h4><ul class="list"><li>Vegetable oil, mayo, or peanut butter</li><li>Baking soda (optional)</li><li>Dish soap</li><li>A cloth or sponge</li></ul></div><ol class="list"><li>Slather the sticky area with oil. Let it sit for 10–15 minutes.</li><li>Rub with a cloth. The adhesive should ball up and slide off.</li><li>If it's still tacky, sprinkle baking soda on a damp sponge and scrub gently.</li><li>Wash with dish soap and warm water to remove the oil.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>For tough residue</summary><div class="body"><p>Try rubbing alcohol or nail polish remover on a cotton ball. Test on a hidden spot first if the glass is painted.</p></div></details><div class="callout info"><p>Hot soapy water soak beforehand loosens the label itself, making the whole job easier.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="stack"><h2 class="card-title">Remove Sticker Residue from Glass</h2><p class="muted">That gummy patch on a jar is stubborn, , but it doesn't stand a chance against a little oil and patience.</p><div class="card"><h4>What you need</h4><ul class="list"><li>Vegetable oil, mayo, or peanut butter</li><li>Baking soda (optional)</li><li>Dish soap</li><li>A cloth or sponge</li></ul></div><ol class="list"><li>Slather the sticky area with oil. Let it sit for 10–15 minutes.</li><li>Rub with a cloth. The adhesive should ball up and slide off.</li><li>If it's still tacky, sprinkle baking soda on a damp sponge and scrub gently.</li><li>Wash with dish soap and warm water to remove the oil.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>For tough residue</summary><div class="body"><p>Try rubbing alcohol or nail polish remover on a cotton ball. Test on a hidden spot first if the glass is painted.</p></div></details><div class="callout info"><p>Hot soapy water soak beforehand loosens the label itself, making the whole job easier.</p></div></div></div>	-
33.46.0	component_spec/Make a Bed Like a Hotel	<div class="umi-page"><div class="stack"><h2 class="card-title">Make a Bed Like a Hotel</h2><p class="muted">You don't need new sheets, <regex>user_name</regex>—just a few folding tricks and a little patience.</p><div class="card"><h4>The layers</h4><ul class="list"><li><strong>Fitted sheet:</strong> Pull tight and smooth. No wrinkles.</li><li><strong>Flat sheet:</strong> Place right-side down, then fold the top edge back over the duvet or blanket for a crisp cuff.</li><li><strong>Duvet or blanket:</strong> Center it, then tuck the bottom edge under the mattress.</li><li><strong>Pillows:</strong> Fluff and stack. A quick karate chop to the top pillow adds that hotel look.</li></ul></div><ol class="list"><li>Start with a clean, smooth mattress pad.</li><li>Put on the fitted sheet, lifting corners to get a snug fit.</li><li>Lay the flat sheet, then fold back the top 12 inches.</li><li>Add duvet or blanket, tucking the foot end under the mattress.</li><li>Fold the flat sheet over the duvet to create a neat edge.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Extra polish</summary><div class="body"><p>Mist sheets with a little water and iron on low for a truly crisp finish. Or just enjoy the 90% version—it still looks great.</p></div></details></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="stack"><h2 class="card-title">Make a Bed Like a Hotel</h2><p class="muted">You don't need new sheets, —just a few folding tricks and a little patience.</p><div class="card"><h4>The layers</h4><ul class="list"><li><strong>Fitted sheet:</strong> Pull tight and smooth. No wrinkles.</li><li><strong>Flat sheet:</strong> Place right-side down, then fold the top edge back over the duvet or blanket for a crisp cuff.</li><li><strong>Duvet or blanket:</strong> Center it, then tuck the bottom edge under the mattress.</li><li><strong>Pillows:</strong> Fluff and stack. A quick karate chop to the top pillow adds that hotel look.</li></ul></div><ol class="list"><li>Start with a clean, smooth mattress pad.</li><li>Put on the fitted sheet, lifting corners to get a snug fit.</li><li>Lay the flat sheet, then fold back the top 12 inches.</li><li>Add duvet or blanket, tucking the foot end under the mattress.</li><li>Fold the flat sheet over the duvet to create a neat edge.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Extra polish</summary><div class="body"><p>Mist sheets with a little water and iron on low for a truly crisp finish. Or just enjoy the 90% version—it still looks great.</p></div></details></div></div>	-
33.47.0	component_spec/Deodorize a Smelly Microwave	<div class="umi-page"><div class="stack"><h2 class="card-title">Deodorize a Smelly Microwave</h2><p class="muted">That lingering burnt-popcorn ghost, <regex>user_name</regex>? Steam and lemon will evict it.</p><div class="card"><h4>What you need</h4><ul class="list"><li>1 cup water</li><li>1 lemon, sliced (or 2 tbsp vinegar)</li><li>A microwave-safe bowl</li><li>Clean cloth</li></ul></div><ol class="list"><li>Combine water and lemon slices in the bowl.</li><li>Microwave on high for 3–5 minutes, until the window steams up.</li><li>Let it sit for 5 minutes—don't open the door yet.</li><li>Carefully remove the bowl and wipe the interior with a damp cloth.</li><li>Leave the door open for an hour to air out.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>For stubborn odors</summary><div class="body"><p>Place a bowl of baking soda inside overnight. It absorbs lingering smells. Replace monthly.</p></div></details><div class="callout info"><p>Clean the turntable and door seal too—they hold onto odors.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="stack"><h2 class="card-title">Deodorize a Smelly Microwave</h2><p class="muted">That lingering burnt-popcorn ghost, ? Steam and lemon will evict it.</p><div class="card"><h4>What you need</h4><ul class="list"><li>1 cup water</li><li>1 lemon, sliced (or 2 tbsp vinegar)</li><li>A microwave-safe bowl</li><li>Clean cloth</li></ul></div><ol class="list"><li>Combine water and lemon slices in the bowl.</li><li>Microwave on high for 3–5 minutes, until the window steams up.</li><li>Let it sit for 5 minutes—don't open the door yet.</li><li>Carefully remove the bowl and wipe the interior with a damp cloth.</li><li>Leave the door open for an hour to air out.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>For stubborn odors</summary><div class="body"><p>Place a bowl of baking soda inside overnight. It absorbs lingering smells. Replace monthly.</p></div></details><div class="callout info"><p>Clean the turntable and door seal too—they hold onto odors.</p></div></div></div>	-
33.48.0	component_spec/Phone Charging Slow Fix	<div class="card"><h4>Phone Charging Slow Fix</h4><p class="muted">Speed up your phone's charge in a few minutes.</p><div class="stack"><p>Try these in order until it charges fast:</p><ol class="list"><li>Use the original charger and cable. Off-brand ones often deliver less power.</li><li>Check the charging port for lint or dust. A wooden toothpick can gently scoop it out.</li><li>Plug directly into a wall outlet, not a USB hub or car adapter.</li><li>Turn on airplane mode while charging. Fewer radios means more juice to the battery.</li><li>Close heavy apps and avoid using the phone while it charges.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>If it still crawls, try a different cable. Cables wear out more often than chargers.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Phone Charging Slow Fix</h4><p class="muted">Speed up your phone's charge in a few minutes.</p><div class="stack"><p>Try these in order until it charges fast:</p><ol class="list"><li>Use the original charger and cable. Off-brand ones often deliver less power.</li><li>Check the charging port for lint or dust. A wooden toothpick can gently scoop it out.</li><li>Plug directly into a wall outlet, not a USB hub or car adapter.</li><li>Turn on airplane mode while charging. Fewer radios means more juice to the battery.</li><li>Close heavy apps and avoid using the phone while it charges.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>If it still crawls, try a different cable. Cables wear out more often than chargers.</p></div></div></div>	-
33.49.0	component_spec/Red Wine Stain Rescue	<div class="card"><h4>Red Wine Stain Rescue</h4><p class="muted">Blot, don't rub. Then let chemistry do the rest.</p><div class="stack"><ol class="list"><li>Blot up as much wine as you can with a clean cloth. Press, don't scrub.</li><li>Pour a little cold water over the stain to dilute it.</li><li>Sprinkle salt or baking soda on the stain to absorb more.</li><li>Mix one part dish soap with two parts hydrogen peroxide. Dab it on.</li><li>Let it sit for 10 minutes, then blot with a damp cloth.</li><li>Launder as usual if it's clothing. For carpet, rinse and blot dry.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p>Test the peroxide mix on a hidden spot first. It can lighten some fabrics.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Red Wine Stain Rescue</h4><p class="muted">Blot, don't rub. Then let chemistry do the rest.</p><div class="stack"><ol class="list"><li>Blot up as much wine as you can with a clean cloth. Press, don't scrub.</li><li>Pour a little cold water over the stain to dilute it.</li><li>Sprinkle salt or baking soda on the stain to absorb more.</li><li>Mix one part dish soap with two parts hydrogen peroxide. Dab it on.</li><li>Let it sit for 10 minutes, then blot with a damp cloth.</li><li>Launder as usual if it's clothing. For carpet, rinse and blot dry.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p>Test the peroxide mix on a hidden spot first. It can lighten some fabrics.</p></div></div></div>	-
33.50.0	component_spec/Dishwasher Deep Clean	<div class="card"><h4>Dishwasher Deep Clean</h4><p class="muted">A clean dishwasher cleans dishes better.</p><div class="stack"><ol class="list"><li>Remove the bottom rack and take out the filter. Rinse it under warm water.</li><li>Scrub the filter with a soft brush and dish soap. Rinse well.</li><li>Check the spray arms for clogged holes. Poke them clear with a toothpick.</li><li>Place a cup of white vinegar on the top rack and run a hot cycle.</li><li>Sprinkle baking soda on the bottom and run a short hot cycle.</li><li>Wipe the door edges and gasket with a damp cloth.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p>Do this monthly to keep odors away and dishes sparkling.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Dishwasher Deep Clean</h4><p class="muted">A clean dishwasher cleans dishes better.</p><div class="stack"><ol class="list"><li>Remove the bottom rack and take out the filter. Rinse it under warm water.</li><li>Scrub the filter with a soft brush and dish soap. Rinse well.</li><li>Check the spray arms for clogged holes. Poke them clear with a toothpick.</li><li>Place a cup of white vinegar on the top rack and run a hot cycle.</li><li>Sprinkle baking soda on the bottom and run a short hot cycle.</li><li>Wipe the door edges and gasket with a damp cloth.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p>Do this monthly to keep odors away and dishes sparkling.</p></div></div></div>	-
33.51.0	component_spec/Ant Invasion Stop	<div class="card"><h4>Ant Invasion Stop</h4><p class="muted">Find the trail, block the entry, bait the rest.</p><div class="stack"><ol class="list"><li>Follow the ants to find where they're getting in. Seal cracks with caulk or petroleum jelly.</li><li>Wipe down counters and floors with vinegar water to erase scent trails.</li><li>Set bait stations near the trail, not on it. Ants take the bait back to the nest.</li><li>Keep food sealed and crumbs swept. Ants hate a clean kitchen.</li><li>If you prefer DIY, mix borax with sugar and water. Soak cotton balls and place them in jar lids.</li><li>Give it a week. Baits work slowly but wipe out the colony.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p>Keep borax baits away from pets and kids.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Ant Invasion Stop</h4><p class="muted">Find the trail, block the entry, bait the rest.</p><div class="stack"><ol class="list"><li>Follow the ants to find where they're getting in. Seal cracks with caulk or petroleum jelly.</li><li>Wipe down counters and floors with vinegar water to erase scent trails.</li><li>Set bait stations near the trail, not on it. Ants take the bait back to the nest.</li><li>Keep food sealed and crumbs swept. Ants hate a clean kitchen.</li><li>If you prefer DIY, mix borax with sugar and water. Soak cotton balls and place them in jar lids.</li><li>Give it a week. Baits work slowly but wipe out the colony.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p>Keep borax baits away from pets and kids.</p></div></div></div>	-
33.52.0	component_spec/Fitted Sheet Folding Hack	<div class="card"><h4>Fitted Sheet Folding Hack</h4><p class="muted">Tame the elastic monster in four steps.</p><div class="stack"><ol class="list"><li>Hold the sheet inside out. Put your hands into two adjacent corners.</li><li>Fold one corner over the other, like you're clapping. Tuck the elastic together.</li><li>Do the same with the other two corners, then fold the sheet in half so all corners meet.</li><li>Lay it flat and fold into a neat rectangle. Smooth as you go.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>Once you get it, it takes 20 seconds. Promise.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Fitted Sheet Folding Hack</h4><p class="muted">Tame the elastic monster in four steps.</p><div class="stack"><ol class="list"><li>Hold the sheet inside out. Put your hands into two adjacent corners.</li><li>Fold one corner over the other, like you're clapping. Tuck the elastic together.</li><li>Do the same with the other two corners, then fold the sheet in half so all corners meet.</li><li>Lay it flat and fold into a neat rectangle. Smooth as you go.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>Once you get it, it takes 20 seconds. Promise.</p></div></div></div>	-
33.53.0	component_spec/Cold Brew Coffee Basics	<div class="card"><h4>Cold Brew Coffee Basics</h4><p class="muted">Smooth, strong, and ridiculously easy.</p><div class="stack"><p><strong>Ratio:</strong> 1 cup coarse coffee to 4 cups cold water.</p><ol class="list"><li>Stir coffee and water together in a large jar.</li><li>Cover and let sit at room temperature for 12–18 hours.</li><li>Strain through a fine mesh sieve lined with a coffee filter or cheesecloth.</li><li>Store concentrate in the fridge for up to a week.</li><li>Dilute with water or milk to taste. Start with half concentrate, half water.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p>Coarse grind is key. Too fine and you'll get sludge.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Cold Brew Coffee Basics</h4><p class="muted">Smooth, strong, and ridiculously easy.</p><div class="stack"><p><strong>Ratio:</strong> 1 cup coarse coffee to 4 cups cold water.</p><ol class="list"><li>Stir coffee and water together in a large jar.</li><li>Cover and let sit at room temperature for 12–18 hours.</li><li>Strain through a fine mesh sieve lined with a coffee filter or cheesecloth.</li><li>Store concentrate in the fridge for up to a week.</li><li>Dilute with water or milk to taste. Start with half concentrate, half water.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p>Coarse grind is key. Too fine and you'll get sludge.</p></div></div></div>	-
33.54.0	component_spec/Dishwasher Filter Cleaning Steps	<div class="card"><h4>Dishwasher Filter Cleaning Steps</h4><p class="muted">If your dishes come out gritty or the machine smells, the filter is the usual suspect. Here's the quick routine.</p><ol class="list"><li>Pull out the bottom rack. The filter is under the spray arm—usually a cylinder plus a flat mesh screen.</li><li>Twist the cylinder counter-clockwise and lift it out. Remove the flat screen too if it's separate.</li><li>Rinse both under warm running water. Use a soft brush (an old toothbrush works) to scrub the mesh.</li><li>For greasy film, soak in hot water with a drop of dish soap for 5 minutes, then scrub again.</li><li>Check the sump area for glass shards or bones. Wipe with a damp cloth.</li><li>Reassemble and run a hot cycle with a cup of white vinegar in the top rack.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>Once a month is plenty. If you have hard water, check the filter every two weeks—minerals clog it faster.</p></div><p class="muted t-small">Remember, <regex>user_name</regex>—no need to scrub like you're angry at it.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Dishwasher Filter Cleaning Steps</h4><p class="muted">If your dishes come out gritty or the machine smells, the filter is the usual suspect. Here's the quick routine.</p><ol class="list"><li>Pull out the bottom rack. The filter is under the spray arm—usually a cylinder plus a flat mesh screen.</li><li>Twist the cylinder counter-clockwise and lift it out. Remove the flat screen too if it's separate.</li><li>Rinse both under warm running water. Use a soft brush (an old toothbrush works) to scrub the mesh.</li><li>For greasy film, soak in hot water with a drop of dish soap for 5 minutes, then scrub again.</li><li>Check the sump area for glass shards or bones. Wipe with a damp cloth.</li><li>Reassemble and run a hot cycle with a cup of white vinegar in the top rack.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>Once a month is plenty. If you have hard water, check the filter every two weeks—minerals clog it faster.</p></div><p class="muted t-small">Remember, —no need to scrub like you're angry at it.</p></div>	-
33.55.0	component_spec/Garbage Disposal Odor Reset	<div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Garbage Disposal Odor Reset</h4><p class="muted">Five minutes, three pantry items, one much happier sink. Hey <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="callout warn"><strong>Never</strong> pour bleach or drain cleaner into a disposal — it can wreck seals and the motor. Citrus and ice do the work.</div><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>What to use</th><th class="num">Time</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Clean the flaps</td><td>Old toothbrush + dish soap, both sides of the rubber baffle</td><td class="num">1 min</td></tr><tr><td>2. Ice + salt scrub</td><td>2 cups ice, 1/4 cup coarse salt, cold water running</td><td class="num">2 min</td></tr><tr><td>3. Citrus grind</td><td>Lemon or orange peels, cold water</td><td class="num">1 min</td></tr><tr><td>4. Baking soda rinse</td><td>1/2 cup soda, then hot tap water 30 sec</td><td class="num">1 min</td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class="acc"><summary>Why the rubber baffle is the real culprit</summary><div class="body"><p>Food film builds up under those flaps where water never reaches. That layer, not the chamber, is usually what smells. A weekly 20-second toothbrush pass keeps it gone.</p></div></details><div class="callout info">Repeat the ice scrub monthly. If the smell returns within days, check the dishwasher drain hose for a sagging loop holding standing water.</div><p class="t-small dim">Cold water only during grinding — hot water melts fats that then re-solidify downstream.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Garbage Disposal Odor Reset</h4><p class="muted">Five minutes, three pantry items, one much happier sink. Hey .</p><div class="callout warn"><strong>Never</strong> pour bleach or drain cleaner into a disposal — it can wreck seals and the motor. Citrus and ice do the work.</div><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>What to use</th><th class="num">Time</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Clean the flaps</td><td>Old toothbrush + dish soap, both sides of the rubber baffle</td><td class="num">1 min</td></tr><tr><td>2. Ice + salt scrub</td><td>2 cups ice, 1/4 cup coarse salt, cold water running</td><td class="num">2 min</td></tr><tr><td>3. Citrus grind</td><td>Lemon or orange peels, cold water</td><td class="num">1 min</td></tr><tr><td>4. Baking soda rinse</td><td>1/2 cup soda, then hot tap water 30 sec</td><td class="num">1 min</td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class="acc"><summary>Why the rubber baffle is the real culprit</summary><div class="body"><p>Food film builds up under those flaps where water never reaches. That layer, not the chamber, is usually what smells. A weekly 20-second toothbrush pass keeps it gone.</p></div></details><div class="callout info">Repeat the ice scrub monthly. If the smell returns within days, check the dishwasher drain hose for a sagging loop holding standing water.</div><p class="t-small dim">Cold water only during grinding — hot water melts fats that then re-solidify downstream.</p></div>	-
33.56.0	component_spec/Washing Machine Musty Smell Fix	<div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Washing Machine Musty Smell Fix</h4><p class="muted">That damp-sock note is biofilm, and it lives in three places. Hi <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Gasket fold</span><span class="value">1<small>st</small></span><span class="delta down">check first</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Detergent drawer</span><span class="value">2<small>nd</small></span><span class="delta down">pull it out</span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Run a hot, empty cycle with 2 cups white vinegar (or a washer cleaner tablet). No clothes, no detergent.</li><li>Wipe the rubber door gasket with a 1:1 vinegar-water cloth — lift the fold and go all the way around.</li><li>Scrub the detergent and fabric-softener compartments under running water; softener is basically food for mold.</li><li>Leave the door and drawer ajar between loads. This one habit prevents most of it.</li></ol><div class="callout good">Switch to half the detergent you use now. Modern machines need less than the cap suggests, and residue feeds the smell.</div><details class="acc"><summary>Sour clothes but a clean-smelling washer?</summary><div class="body"><p>Then it's dwell time. Move wet laundry to the dryer within an hour, and skip the extra-rinse habit — it adds moisture, not freshness.</p></div></details><p class="t-small dim">Monthly vinegar cycle, weekly gasket wipe, door open daily.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Washing Machine Musty Smell Fix</h4><p class="muted">That damp-sock note is biofilm, and it lives in three places. Hi .</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Gasket fold</span><span class="value">1<small>st</small></span><span class="delta down">check first</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Detergent drawer</span><span class="value">2<small>nd</small></span><span class="delta down">pull it out</span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Run a hot, empty cycle with 2 cups white vinegar (or a washer cleaner tablet). No clothes, no detergent.</li><li>Wipe the rubber door gasket with a 1:1 vinegar-water cloth — lift the fold and go all the way around.</li><li>Scrub the detergent and fabric-softener compartments under running water; softener is basically food for mold.</li><li>Leave the door and drawer ajar between loads. This one habit prevents most of it.</li></ol><div class="callout good">Switch to half the detergent you use now. Modern machines need less than the cap suggests, and residue feeds the smell.</div><details class="acc"><summary>Sour clothes but a clean-smelling washer?</summary><div class="body"><p>Then it's dwell time. Move wet laundry to the dryer within an hour, and skip the extra-rinse habit — it adds moisture, not freshness.</p></div></details><p class="t-small dim">Monthly vinegar cycle, weekly gasket wipe, door open daily.</p></div>	-
33.57.0	component_spec/Gutter Downspout Clear-Out	<div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Gutter Downspout Clear-Out</h4><p class="muted">Overflow stains the fascia and finds your basement. Twenty minutes on a dry day fixes it. Hello <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="callout warn">Work from a stable ladder on level ground, never in wind or rain, and never reach past your shoulder. Two hands on the ladder beats one quick grab.</div><ol class="list"><li>Flush from the top with a hose. Note where water backs up.</li><li>If it drains slowly, tap the downspout with your palm — a dull thud means a leaf plug.</li><li>Remove the bottom elbow first. Most blockages sit right there in the first 30 cm.</li><li>Push a plumber's snake or a stiff hose up from the bottom, then flush down again.</li><li>Reattach the elbow and confirm the spout discharges at least 1 m away from the foundation.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>The hose test that tells you it's done</summary><div class="body"><p>Full pressure at the top should produce full flow at the bottom within a few seconds. If it dribbles, there's still a plug above the elbow.</p></div></details><div class="callout info">Add a mesh strainer at the top of each downspout. Cheapest clog insurance there is.</div><p class="t-small dim">Twice a year: after autumn leaf drop and again in early spring.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Gutter Downspout Clear-Out</h4><p class="muted">Overflow stains the fascia and finds your basement. Twenty minutes on a dry day fixes it. Hello .</p><div class="callout warn">Work from a stable ladder on level ground, never in wind or rain, and never reach past your shoulder. Two hands on the ladder beats one quick grab.</div><ol class="list"><li>Flush from the top with a hose. Note where water backs up.</li><li>If it drains slowly, tap the downspout with your palm — a dull thud means a leaf plug.</li><li>Remove the bottom elbow first. Most blockages sit right there in the first 30 cm.</li><li>Push a plumber's snake or a stiff hose up from the bottom, then flush down again.</li><li>Reattach the elbow and confirm the spout discharges at least 1 m away from the foundation.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>The hose test that tells you it's done</summary><div class="body"><p>Full pressure at the top should produce full flow at the bottom within a few seconds. If it dribbles, there's still a plug above the elbow.</p></div></details><div class="callout info">Add a mesh strainer at the top of each downspout. Cheapest clog insurance there is.</div><p class="t-small dim">Twice a year: after autumn leaf drop and again in early spring.</p></div>	-
33.58.0	component_spec/Slow-Draining Bathroom Sink Fix	<div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Slow-Draining Bathroom Sink Fix</h4><p class="muted">It's hair and soap scum on the stopper, nine times out of ten. <regex>user_name</regex>, start there before anything dramatic.</p><div class="callout good">Order of operations: stopper first, then trap arm, then snake. Each step is easier than the next.</div><ol class="list"><li>Lift or unscrew the pop-up stopper. Most twist off; some have a set screw behind the faucet.</li><li>Wipe the hair wad off the stopper and scrub the drain opening with a bottle brush.</li><li>Still slow? Put a bucket under the P-trap, unscrew both slip nuts by hand, and rinse the trap out.</li><li>Reassemble hand-tight, run hot water two minutes, and check the joints for drips.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>If it's still sluggish after all that</summary><div class="body"><p>Run a hand snake 30–60 cm into the wall stub. If it hits resistance and won't turn, stop — that's a call-a-plumber moment, not a force-it moment.</p></div></details><div class="callout warn">Skip the chemical drain cleaner. It eats older metal pipes and makes the eventual manual job nastier.</div><p class="t-small dim">Once a month: pull the stopper, pull the hair, feel smug.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Slow-Draining Bathroom Sink Fix</h4><p class="muted">It's hair and soap scum on the stopper, nine times out of ten. , start there before anything dramatic.</p><div class="callout good">Order of operations: stopper first, then trap arm, then snake. Each step is easier than the next.</div><ol class="list"><li>Lift or unscrew the pop-up stopper. Most twist off; some have a set screw behind the faucet.</li><li>Wipe the hair wad off the stopper and scrub the drain opening with a bottle brush.</li><li>Still slow? Put a bucket under the P-trap, unscrew both slip nuts by hand, and rinse the trap out.</li><li>Reassemble hand-tight, run hot water two minutes, and check the joints for drips.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>If it's still sluggish after all that</summary><div class="body"><p>Run a hand snake 30–60 cm into the wall stub. If it hits resistance and won't turn, stop — that's a call-a-plumber moment, not a force-it moment.</p></div></details><div class="callout warn">Skip the chemical drain cleaner. It eats older metal pipes and makes the eventual manual job nastier.</div><p class="t-small dim">Once a month: pull the stopper, pull the hair, feel smug.</p></div>	-
33.59.0	component_spec/Refrigerator Coil Cleaning Basics	<div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Refrigerator Coil Cleaning Basics</h4><p class="muted">Dusty coils make the compressor work overtime — you pay for it twice. Good to see you, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="row between"><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>Do it twice a year</span><span class="badge info">Saves roughly 5–15% on fridge power</span></div><ol class="list"><li>Unplug the fridge. Non-negotiable.</li><li>Find the coils: behind the kick plate at the bottom, or on the back panel.</li><li>Vacuum with a brush attachment, running the nozzle along the fins, not across them.</li><li>For greasy kitchen dust, follow with a soft dry brush — water on coils invites rust.</li><li>Vacuum the floor around it too. That's where the next layer comes from.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Pets in the house?</summary><div class="body"><p>Double the frequency. Pet hair mats into a felt blanket on coils faster than household dust and insulates them almost completely.</p></div></details><div class="callout info">While you're back there, check the door gasket: close a sheet of paper in the door and tug. It should resist. If it slides out easily, the seal is failing.</div><p class="t-small dim">Coils clean, compressor relaxed, kitchen quieter.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Refrigerator Coil Cleaning Basics</h4><p class="muted">Dusty coils make the compressor work overtime — you pay for it twice. Good to see you, .</p><div class="row between"><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>Do it twice a year</span><span class="badge info">Saves roughly 5–15% on fridge power</span></div><ol class="list"><li>Unplug the fridge. Non-negotiable.</li><li>Find the coils: behind the kick plate at the bottom, or on the back panel.</li><li>Vacuum with a brush attachment, running the nozzle along the fins, not across them.</li><li>For greasy kitchen dust, follow with a soft dry brush — water on coils invites rust.</li><li>Vacuum the floor around it too. That's where the next layer comes from.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Pets in the house?</summary><div class="body"><p>Double the frequency. Pet hair mats into a felt blanket on coils faster than household dust and insulates them almost completely.</p></div></details><div class="callout info">While you're back there, check the door gasket: close a sheet of paper in the door and tug. It should resist. If it slides out easily, the seal is failing.</div><p class="t-small dim">Coils clean, compressor relaxed, kitchen quieter.</p></div>	-
33.60.0	component_spec/Ceiling Fan Direction & Wobble Fix	<div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Ceiling Fan Direction &amp; Wobble Fix</h4><p class="muted">Two settings and one screwdriver fix most fan complaints. Evening, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Summer</span><span class="value">CCW</span><span class="delta">counter-clockwise, air down</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Winter</span><span class="value">CW</span><span class="delta">clockwise, air up</span></div></div><p class="t-small dim">Most fans have a slide switch on the motor housing. If it's above a bed, let it fully stop before flipping it.</p><ol class="list"><li>Cut power at the breaker before any hands-on work.</li><li>Tighten every blade screw — loose ones cause most wobble and that rhythmic click.</li><li>Check the blade brackets; gently bend a misaligned bracket so all blades sit at the same height.</li><li>Still wobbling? Clip a balancing kit weight to a blade, test, and move it along the blade until the shake settles.</li><li>Confirm the mounting ball isn't rocking in its bracket.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Clicking only on high speed</summary><div class="body"><p>Usually a blade tip dipping into the pull-chain or light globe. Bend the bracket, don't trim the blade.</p></div></details><div class="callout warn">If the fan rocks at the ceiling mount itself, stop and check the electrical box. That's a support problem, not a fan problem.</div></div>	<div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Ceiling Fan Direction &amp; Wobble Fix</h4><p class="muted">Two settings and one screwdriver fix most fan complaints. Evening, .</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Summer</span><span class="value">CCW</span><span class="delta">counter-clockwise, air down</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Winter</span><span class="value">CW</span><span class="delta">clockwise, air up</span></div></div><p class="t-small dim">Most fans have a slide switch on the motor housing. If it's above a bed, let it fully stop before flipping it.</p><ol class="list"><li>Cut power at the breaker before any hands-on work.</li><li>Tighten every blade screw — loose ones cause most wobble and that rhythmic click.</li><li>Check the blade brackets; gently bend a misaligned bracket so all blades sit at the same height.</li><li>Still wobbling? Clip a balancing kit weight to a blade, test, and move it along the blade until the shake settles.</li><li>Confirm the mounting ball isn't rocking in its bracket.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Clicking only on high speed</summary><div class="body"><p>Usually a blade tip dipping into the pull-chain or light globe. Bend the bracket, don't trim the blade.</p></div></details><div class="callout warn">If the fan rocks at the ceiling mount itself, stop and check the electrical box. That's a support problem, not a fan problem.</div></div>	-
33.61.0	component_spec/Mattress Refresh Routine	<article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>Here's a mattress refresh that doesn't need a steam cleaner, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Mattress Refresh Routine</h3><ol class="list"><li>Strip the bed and wash all bedding on hot.</li><li>Vacuum the mattress with an upholstery attachment, going slow over seams and tufts.</li><li>Sprinkle a thin layer of baking soda. Let it sit 15–30 minutes.</li><li>Vacuum it all up. The soda pulls out moisture and odor.</li><li>Spot-clean stains with a cloth dampened with equal parts white vinegar and water. Blot, don't rub.</li><li>Stand the mattress on its side near a window or fan until fully dry.</li><li>Rotate head-to-foot before remaking.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>No baking soda? Plain vacuuming does more than you'd think.</p></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>How often?</summary><div class="body"><p>Every 3–6 months. More often if you sweat a lot or have pets.</p></div></details></article>	<article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>Here's a mattress refresh that doesn't need a steam cleaner, .</p><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Mattress Refresh Routine</h3><ol class="list"><li>Strip the bed and wash all bedding on hot.</li><li>Vacuum the mattress with an upholstery attachment, going slow over seams and tufts.</li><li>Sprinkle a thin layer of baking soda. Let it sit 15–30 minutes.</li><li>Vacuum it all up. The soda pulls out moisture and odor.</li><li>Spot-clean stains with a cloth dampened with equal parts white vinegar and water. Blot, don't rub.</li><li>Stand the mattress on its side near a window or fan until fully dry.</li><li>Rotate head-to-foot before remaking.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>No baking soda? Plain vacuuming does more than you'd think.</p></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>How often?</summary><div class="body"><p>Every 3–6 months. More often if you sweat a lot or have pets.</p></div></details></article>	-
33.62.0	component_spec/Shower Drain Hair Clog Fix	<article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>Hair clogs are stubborn but beatable, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Shower Drain Hair Clog Fix</h3><ol class="list"><li>Remove the drain cover. Most pop off or unscrew.</li><li>Look for a hair ball. A bent wire coat hanger with a small hook can fish it out.</li><li>If you can't see it, try a drain snake. Feed it in, twist, pull back.</li><li>Flush with hot (not boiling) water for a minute.</li><li>Pour in 1/2 cup baking soda, then 1/2 cup white vinegar. Let fizz 15 minutes.</li><li>Flush again with hot water.</li><li>Replace the cover and run the shower to check flow.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p>Skip chemical drain cleaners if you have a septic system or older pipes.</p></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Prevention</summary><div class="body"><p>A mesh hair catcher is the easiest win. Empty it weekly.</p></div></details></article>	<article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>Hair clogs are stubborn but beatable, .</p><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Shower Drain Hair Clog Fix</h3><ol class="list"><li>Remove the drain cover. Most pop off or unscrew.</li><li>Look for a hair ball. A bent wire coat hanger with a small hook can fish it out.</li><li>If you can't see it, try a drain snake. Feed it in, twist, pull back.</li><li>Flush with hot (not boiling) water for a minute.</li><li>Pour in 1/2 cup baking soda, then 1/2 cup white vinegar. Let fizz 15 minutes.</li><li>Flush again with hot water.</li><li>Replace the cover and run the shower to check flow.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p>Skip chemical drain cleaners if you have a septic system or older pipes.</p></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Prevention</summary><div class="body"><p>A mesh hair catcher is the easiest win. Empty it weekly.</p></div></details></article>	-
33.63.0	component_spec/Refrigerator Odor Removal	<article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>A smelly fridge is usually a hidden spill, <regex>user_name</regex>. Here's how to hunt it down.</p><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Refrigerator Odor Removal</h3><ol class="list"><li>Empty the fridge completely. Toss anything expired.</li><li>Remove shelves and drawers. Wash in warm soapy water, rinse, dry.</li><li>Wipe down the interior with a mix of 2 tablespoons baking soda per quart of warm water.</li><li>Check the door gasket and under crisper drawers for hidden spills.</li><li>Leave a box of baking soda or a bowl of activated charcoal on a shelf.</li><li>Let it air out with the door open for an hour before restocking.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p>Baking soda absorbs odors for about 3 months. Write the date on the box.</p></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>What about the freezer?</summary><div class="body"><p>Same drill. Defrost if there's ice buildup, then wipe down.</p></div></details></article>	<article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>A smelly fridge is usually a hidden spill, . Here's how to hunt it down.</p><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Refrigerator Odor Removal</h3><ol class="list"><li>Empty the fridge completely. Toss anything expired.</li><li>Remove shelves and drawers. Wash in warm soapy water, rinse, dry.</li><li>Wipe down the interior with a mix of 2 tablespoons baking soda per quart of warm water.</li><li>Check the door gasket and under crisper drawers for hidden spills.</li><li>Leave a box of baking soda or a bowl of activated charcoal on a shelf.</li><li>Let it air out with the door open for an hour before restocking.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p>Baking soda absorbs odors for about 3 months. Write the date on the box.</p></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>What about the freezer?</summary><div class="body"><p>Same drill. Defrost if there's ice buildup, then wipe down.</p></div></details></article>	-
33.0.1	component_spec/Washing Machine Deep Clean	<article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>Washers need a bath too, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Washing Machine Deep Clean</h3><ol class="list"><li>Run an empty hot water cycle with 2 cups white vinegar in the drum.</li><li>While it fills, wipe the door gasket (front-loaders) with a vinegar-dampened cloth.</li><li>Clean the detergent dispenser drawer. Soak it in warm soapy water.</li><li>After the cycle, sprinkle 1/2 cup baking soda directly into the drum. Run a short hot rinse cycle.</li><li>Leave the door and dispenser open to air dry.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>Once a month keeps musty smells away.</p></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Top-loader vs front-loader</summary><div class="body"><p>Top-loaders: also clean the fabric softener dispenser. Front-loaders: pay extra attention to the gasket folds.</p></div></details></article>	<article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>Washers need a bath too, .</p><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Washing Machine Deep Clean</h3><ol class="list"><li>Run an empty hot water cycle with 2 cups white vinegar in the drum.</li><li>While it fills, wipe the door gasket (front-loaders) with a vinegar-dampened cloth.</li><li>Clean the detergent dispenser drawer. Soak it in warm soapy water.</li><li>After the cycle, sprinkle 1/2 cup baking soda directly into the drum. Run a short hot rinse cycle.</li><li>Leave the door and dispenser open to air dry.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>Once a month keeps musty smells away.</p></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Top-loader vs front-loader</summary><div class="body"><p>Top-loaders: also clean the fabric softener dispenser. Front-loaders: pay extra attention to the gasket folds.</p></div></details></article>	-
33.1.1	component_spec/Refrigerator Coil Cleaning	<article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>Dusty coils make your fridge work harder, <regex>user_name</regex>. Here's the fix.</p><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Refrigerator Coil Cleaning</h3><ol class="list"><li>Unplug the fridge or turn it off at the breaker.</li><li>Pull the fridge away from the wall. Coils are either behind a kickplate at the bottom or on the back.</li><li>Remove the kickplate or access panel.</li><li>Vacuum the coils with a brush attachment. Go gently to avoid bending the fins.</li><li>For stubborn dust, use a coil brush (or a dry paintbrush) to loosen it, then vacuum again.</li><li>Check the drip pan underneath and wipe it clean if needed.</li><li>Replace the panel, push the fridge back, and plug it in.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p>Clean coils every 6–12 months. Your energy bill will thank you.</p></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>How do I know if they're dirty?</summary><div class="body"><p>If the back feels warm or the fridge runs constantly, it's time.</p></div></details></article>	<article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>Dusty coils make your fridge work harder, . Here's the fix.</p><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Refrigerator Coil Cleaning</h3><ol class="list"><li>Unplug the fridge or turn it off at the breaker.</li><li>Pull the fridge away from the wall. Coils are either behind a kickplate at the bottom or on the back.</li><li>Remove the kickplate or access panel.</li><li>Vacuum the coils with a brush attachment. Go gently to avoid bending the fins.</li><li>For stubborn dust, use a coil brush (or a dry paintbrush) to loosen it, then vacuum again.</li><li>Check the drip pan underneath and wipe it clean if needed.</li><li>Replace the panel, push the fridge back, and plug it in.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p>Clean coils every 6–12 months. Your energy bill will thank you.</p></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>How do I know if they're dirty?</summary><div class="body"><p>If the back feels warm or the fridge runs constantly, it's time.</p></div></details></article>	-
33.2.1	component_spec/Water Heater Temperature Reset	<div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Water Heater Temperature Reset</h4><p class="muted t-small">For <regex>user_name</regex> — 10 minutes, one screwdriver.</p><div class="callout warn"><p>Gas units: shut the gas off before opening the access panel. Unsure? Call a pro.</p></div><ol class="list"><li>Kill power at the breaker (electric) or gas valve (gas).</li><li>Lift the upper access panel; peel back insulation.</li><li>Turn the dial to <strong>120&nbsp;°F</strong> for household use, 130&nbsp;°F if you like long showers.</li><li>Press the reset button if the unit tripped.</li><li>Restore power and wait 2 hours before testing.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Why 120&nbsp;°F is the sweet spot</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted">Below 120&nbsp;°F invites bacteria growth. Above 140&nbsp;°F risks scalding, especially for kids. 120&nbsp;°F is the standard compromise.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Signs the element is the real problem</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted">If water is lukewarm at every tap no matter the setting, a heating element is likely dead. Test with a multimeter; replacements cost about $20.</p></div></details><p class="dim t-small">Tip: Flush the tank once a year to keep sediment from eating your capacity.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Water Heater Temperature Reset</h4><p class="muted t-small">For  — 10 minutes, one screwdriver.</p><div class="callout warn"><p>Gas units: shut the gas off before opening the access panel. Unsure? Call a pro.</p></div><ol class="list"><li>Kill power at the breaker (electric) or gas valve (gas).</li><li>Lift the upper access panel; peel back insulation.</li><li>Turn the dial to <strong>120&nbsp;°F</strong> for household use, 130&nbsp;°F if you like long showers.</li><li>Press the reset button if the unit tripped.</li><li>Restore power and wait 2 hours before testing.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Why 120&nbsp;°F is the sweet spot</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted">Below 120&nbsp;°F invites bacteria growth. Above 140&nbsp;°F risks scalding, especially for kids. 120&nbsp;°F is the standard compromise.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Signs the element is the real problem</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted">If water is lukewarm at every tap no matter the setting, a heating element is likely dead. Test with a multimeter; replacements cost about $20.</p></div></details><p class="dim t-small">Tip: Flush the tank once a year to keep sediment from eating your capacity.</p></div>	-
33.3.1	component_spec/Sticky Zipper Fix	<div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Sticky Zipper Fix</h4><p class="muted t-small">For <regex>user_name</regex> — rescue any zipper in under 5 minutes.</p><div class="row between"><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>No tools needed</span><span class="badge info"><i class="dot"></i>Works on bags, jackets, tents</span></div><ol class="list"><li><strong>Stop pulling.</strong> Force bends teeth and ruins the slider.</li><li>Rub a graphite pencil along both sides of the teeth — inside and out.</li><li>Zip up and down a few times to work it in.</li><li>Still rough? A dab of lip balm or beeswax on the teeth does the trick.</li><li>If the slider slides but the teeth stay open, pinch the slider sides gently with pliers.</li></ol><div class="divider"></div><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Common cause</span><span class="value">Bent teeth</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Best fix fast</span><span class="value">Graphite</span></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>When to replace the zipper</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted">If more than two teeth are missing or the slider body is cracked, replacement is cheaper than repair paste.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Sticky Zipper Fix</h4><p class="muted t-small">For  — rescue any zipper in under 5 minutes.</p><div class="row between"><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>No tools needed</span><span class="badge info"><i class="dot"></i>Works on bags, jackets, tents</span></div><ol class="list"><li><strong>Stop pulling.</strong> Force bends teeth and ruins the slider.</li><li>Rub a graphite pencil along both sides of the teeth — inside and out.</li><li>Zip up and down a few times to work it in.</li><li>Still rough? A dab of lip balm or beeswax on the teeth does the trick.</li><li>If the slider slides but the teeth stay open, pinch the slider sides gently with pliers.</li></ol><div class="divider"></div><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Common cause</span><span class="value">Bent teeth</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Best fix fast</span><span class="value">Graphite</span></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>When to replace the zipper</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted">If more than two teeth are missing or the slider body is cracked, replacement is cheaper than repair paste.</p></div></details></div>	-
33.4.1	component_spec/Battery Corrosion Clean-Up	<div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Battery Corrosion Clean-Up</h4><p class="muted t-small">For <regex>user_name</regex> — the crusty white stuff isn't a death sentence.</p><div class="callout warn"><p>Wear gloves and open a window. Battery residue is alkaline, and it's not friendly.</p></div><ol class="list"><li>Remove all batteries. Toss them — never try to recharge a leaker.</li><li>Mix 1 tbsp baking soda into 1 cup water.</li><li>Dip a cotton swab in the mix and dab the corroded contacts.</li><li>Scrub gently with an old toothbrush until the metal shines.</li><li>Dry completely with a paper towel, then let it air out 10 minutes.</li><li>Insert fresh batteries and test.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>What to do if the contacts are gone</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted">Small devices with chewed-up contacts can still work: fold a tiny square of aluminum foil and wedge it against each end of the battery. Temporary but effective.</p></div></details><p class="dim t-small">Prevent it: pull batteries out of anything you won't use for a month.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Battery Corrosion Clean-Up</h4><p class="muted t-small">For  — the crusty white stuff isn't a death sentence.</p><div class="callout warn"><p>Wear gloves and open a window. Battery residue is alkaline, and it's not friendly.</p></div><ol class="list"><li>Remove all batteries. Toss them — never try to recharge a leaker.</li><li>Mix 1 tbsp baking soda into 1 cup water.</li><li>Dip a cotton swab in the mix and dab the corroded contacts.</li><li>Scrub gently with an old toothbrush until the metal shines.</li><li>Dry completely with a paper towel, then let it air out 10 minutes.</li><li>Insert fresh batteries and test.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>What to do if the contacts are gone</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted">Small devices with chewed-up contacts can still work: fold a tiny square of aluminum foil and wedge it against each end of the battery. Temporary but effective.</p></div></details><p class="dim t-small">Prevent it: pull batteries out of anything you won't use for a month.</p></div>	-
33.5.1	component_spec/Fridge Water Line Tune-Up	<div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Fridge Water Line Tune-Up</h4><p class="muted t-small">For <regex>user_name</regex> — slow water or weak ice, start here.</p><div class="callout info"><p>Most fridge water complaints trace back to a kink, a clogged filter, or a saddle valve that was never fully open.</p></div><ol class="list"><li>Pull the fridge out a foot. Look for a pinched copper or plastic line behind it.</li><li>Straighten any sharp bends carefully — no yanking.</li><li>Change the water filter. Every 6 months, more often in hard water.</li><li>Find the shutoff valve. Turn it fully counter-clockwise.</li><li>Run 2–3 gallons through the dispenser to purge air and sediment.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Ice tastes like plastic?</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted">That's usually a stale filter. Swap it and dump the first two batches of ice.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>The saddle valve is the real villain</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted">Saddle valves clog and leak. If yours is more than five years old, replace it with a proper quarter-turn shutoff.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Fridge Water Line Tune-Up</h4><p class="muted t-small">For  — slow water or weak ice, start here.</p><div class="callout info"><p>Most fridge water complaints trace back to a kink, a clogged filter, or a saddle valve that was never fully open.</p></div><ol class="list"><li>Pull the fridge out a foot. Look for a pinched copper or plastic line behind it.</li><li>Straighten any sharp bends carefully — no yanking.</li><li>Change the water filter. Every 6 months, more often in hard water.</li><li>Find the shutoff valve. Turn it fully counter-clockwise.</li><li>Run 2–3 gallons through the dispenser to purge air and sediment.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Ice tastes like plastic?</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted">That's usually a stale filter. Swap it and dump the first two batches of ice.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>The saddle valve is the real villain</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted">Saddle valves clog and leak. If yours is more than five years old, replace it with a proper quarter-turn shutoff.</p></div></details></div>	-
33.6.1	component_spec/Desk Cable Management Basics	<div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Desk Cable Management Basics</h4><p class="muted t-small">For <regex>user_name</regex> — 20 minutes for a desk that stops snagging.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Cost</span><span class="value">$10</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Time</span><span class="value">20<span class="unit">min</span></span></div></div><ol class="list"><li><strong>Unplug everything.</strong> Start from a clean slate.</li><li>Group cables by destination: front-of-desk, back-of-desk, floor.</li><li>Run power bricks along the desk's far edge, hidden by a strip of hook-and-loop tape.</li><li>Bundle each group with velcro ties every 6 inches.</li><li>Leave one slack loop near each device so nothing pulls tight.</li><li>Label both ends of the confusing ones with a strip of painter's tape.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Velcro, not zip ties</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted">Zip ties are permanent and pinch cables. Velcro releases and won't crush the wire.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>One trick that changes everything</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted">Mount a single power strip under the desktop. Everything plugs in there, and only one cable hits the wall outlet.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Desk Cable Management Basics</h4><p class="muted t-small">For  — 20 minutes for a desk that stops snagging.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Cost</span><span class="value">$10</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Time</span><span class="value">20<span class="unit">min</span></span></div></div><ol class="list"><li><strong>Unplug everything.</strong> Start from a clean slate.</li><li>Group cables by destination: front-of-desk, back-of-desk, floor.</li><li>Run power bricks along the desk's far edge, hidden by a strip of hook-and-loop tape.</li><li>Bundle each group with velcro ties every 6 inches.</li><li>Leave one slack loop near each device so nothing pulls tight.</li><li>Label both ends of the confusing ones with a strip of painter's tape.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Velcro, not zip ties</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted">Zip ties are permanent and pinch cables. Velcro releases and won't crush the wire.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>One trick that changes everything</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted">Mount a single power strip under the desktop. Everything plugs in there, and only one cable hits the wall outlet.</p></div></details></div>	-
33.7.1	component_spec/Mattress Stain Removal	<div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Mattress Stain Removal</h4><p class="muted t-small">For <regex>user_name</regex> — act while it's still fresh.</p><div class="callout bad"><p>Never soak the mattress. Damp is fine. Wet means mold, and mold means a new mattress.</p></div><ol class="list"><li><strong>Blot, don't scrub.</strong> Press a clean cloth down until it stops picking up color.</li><li>Mix 1 tsp dish soap + 1 tbsp white vinegar in 1 cup cool water.</li><li>Dab the solution on with a sponge. Let it sit 5 minutes.</li><li>Blot dry with a towel, pressing hard to lift moisture.</li><li>Sprinkle baking soda over the area and leave it 30 minutes.</li><li>Vacuum it up. The powder pulls out residual smell and moisture.</li></ol><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Blood stains</span><span class="value">Cold water</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Urine</span><span class="value">Enzyme cleaner</span></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>The sun trick</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted">Prop the mattress up in direct sunlight for an hour after spot-treating. UV does double duty on stains and odor.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Mattress Stain Removal</h4><p class="muted t-small">For  — act while it's still fresh.</p><div class="callout bad"><p>Never soak the mattress. Damp is fine. Wet means mold, and mold means a new mattress.</p></div><ol class="list"><li><strong>Blot, don't scrub.</strong> Press a clean cloth down until it stops picking up color.</li><li>Mix 1 tsp dish soap + 1 tbsp white vinegar in 1 cup cool water.</li><li>Dab the solution on with a sponge. Let it sit 5 minutes.</li><li>Blot dry with a towel, pressing hard to lift moisture.</li><li>Sprinkle baking soda over the area and leave it 30 minutes.</li><li>Vacuum it up. The powder pulls out residual smell and moisture.</li></ol><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Blood stains</span><span class="value">Cold water</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Urine</span><span class="value">Enzyme cleaner</span></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>The sun trick</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted">Prop the mattress up in direct sunlight for an hour after spot-treating. UV does double duty on stains and odor.</p></div></details></div>	-
33.8.1	component_spec/Stop Dog Begging at Meals	<div class="card">  <h4>Stop Dog Begging at Meals</h4>  <p class="muted">A calm, consistent routine beats scolding every time.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Feed first.</strong> Give your dog their meal right before yours so they're not hungry when you sit down.</li>    <li><strong>Use a mat.</strong> Train a "go to mat" cue. Reward with a treat <em>before</em> they start whining.</li>    <li><strong>Ignore the stare.</strong> Any attention—even "no"—is a reward. Turn away, no eye contact.</li>    <li><strong>Reward quiet.</strong> Drop a treat on the mat when they're lying quietly. Timing is everything.</li>  </ol>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What if they won't stop?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>End the session. Put the dog in another room with a chew toy for 5 minutes. Try again tomorrow. Consistency over intensity.</p>    </div>  </details>  <p class="callout info">Progress takes about two weeks of daily practice. Be patient.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <h4>Stop Dog Begging at Meals</h4>  <p class="muted">A calm, consistent routine beats scolding every time.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Feed first.</strong> Give your dog their meal right before yours so they're not hungry when you sit down.</li>    <li><strong>Use a mat.</strong> Train a "go to mat" cue. Reward with a treat <em>before</em> they start whining.</li>    <li><strong>Ignore the stare.</strong> Any attention—even "no"—is a reward. Turn away, no eye contact.</li>    <li><strong>Reward quiet.</strong> Drop a treat on the mat when they're lying quietly. Timing is everything.</li>  </ol>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What if they won't stop?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>End the session. Put the dog in another room with a chew toy for 5 minutes. Try again tomorrow. Consistency over intensity.</p>    </div>  </details>  <p class="callout info">Progress takes about two weeks of daily practice. Be patient.</p></div>	-
33.9.1	component_spec/Keep Herbs Fresh for Weeks	<div class="card">  <h4>Keep Herbs Fresh for Weeks</h4>  <p class="muted">Two methods: one for soft herbs, one for hardy ones.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat">      <div class="label">Soft herbs</div>      <div class="value">Cilantro, parsley, dill</div>    </div>    <div class="stat">      <div class="label">Hardy herbs</div>      <div class="value">Rosemary, thyme, oregano</div>    </div>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Soft herbs: the jar method</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Trim stems and place in a jar with 1 inch of water.</li>        <li>Cover loosely with a plastic bag.</li>        <li>Refrigerate. Change water every 2 days.</li>      </ol>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Hardy herbs: the damp towel method</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Wrap herbs in a slightly damp paper towel.</li>        <li>Place in a zip-top bag, leaving it open a crack.</li>        <li>Store in the crisper drawer.</li>      </ol>    </div>  </details>  <p class="callout good">Basil is the odd one out—keep it at room temp in water, like flowers.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <h4>Keep Herbs Fresh for Weeks</h4>  <p class="muted">Two methods: one for soft herbs, one for hardy ones.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat">      <div class="label">Soft herbs</div>      <div class="value">Cilantro, parsley, dill</div>    </div>    <div class="stat">      <div class="label">Hardy herbs</div>      <div class="value">Rosemary, thyme, oregano</div>    </div>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Soft herbs: the jar method</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Trim stems and place in a jar with 1 inch of water.</li>        <li>Cover loosely with a plastic bag.</li>        <li>Refrigerate. Change water every 2 days.</li>      </ol>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Hardy herbs: the damp towel method</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Wrap herbs in a slightly damp paper towel.</li>        <li>Place in a zip-top bag, leaving it open a crack.</li>        <li>Store in the crisper drawer.</li>      </ol>    </div>  </details>  <p class="callout good">Basil is the odd one out—keep it at room temp in water, like flowers.</p></div>	-
33.10.1	component_spec/Tame Static Hair Fast	<div class="card">  <h4>Tame Static Hair Fast</h4>  <p class="muted">Dry air + friction = flyaways. Add moisture or neutralize the charge.</p>  <ul class="list">    <li><strong>Quick fix:</strong> Rub a dryer sheet lightly over your hair. Don't overdo it—it can leave residue.</li>    <li><strong>Hand lotion:</strong> A pea-sized amount on your hands, then smooth over hair. Avoid the roots.</li>    <li><strong>Metal hanger:</strong> Run a metal hanger or key down your hair to discharge static.</li>  </ul>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Prevent it long-term</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>Use a humidifier in dry months.</li>        <li>Switch to a wooden comb or natural-bristle brush.</li>        <li>Leave-in conditioner or a bit of hair oil helps.</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <p class="callout info">Static is worse in winter because indoor air is dry. A $20 humidifier solves most cases.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <h4>Tame Static Hair Fast</h4>  <p class="muted">Dry air + friction = flyaways. Add moisture or neutralize the charge.</p>  <ul class="list">    <li><strong>Quick fix:</strong> Rub a dryer sheet lightly over your hair. Don't overdo it—it can leave residue.</li>    <li><strong>Hand lotion:</strong> A pea-sized amount on your hands, then smooth over hair. Avoid the roots.</li>    <li><strong>Metal hanger:</strong> Run a metal hanger or key down your hair to discharge static.</li>  </ul>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Prevent it long-term</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>Use a humidifier in dry months.</li>        <li>Switch to a wooden comb or natural-bristle brush.</li>        <li>Leave-in conditioner or a bit of hair oil helps.</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <p class="callout info">Static is worse in winter because indoor air is dry. A $20 humidifier solves most cases.</p></div>	-
33.11.1	component_spec/Fix a Wobbly Table	<div class="card">  <h4>Fix a Wobbly Table</h4>  <p class="muted">Find the short leg, then shim or tighten.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat">      <div class="label">Likely cause</div>      <div class="value">Uneven floor or loose joint</div>    </div>    <div class="stat">      <div class="label">Tools</div>      <div class="value">Felt pad, shim, screwdriver</div>    </div>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>If the legs are uneven</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Rock the table to find the short leg.</li>        <li>Stick a felt pad or folded paper under it.</li>        <li>Trim to fit. Test again.</li>      </ol>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>If the joint is loose</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Tighten all visible screws and bolts.</li>        <li>If screws spin freely, fill the hole with wood glue and a toothpick, let dry, then re-screw.</li>        <li>For metal brackets, add a washer for grip.</li>      </ol>    </div>  </details>  <p class="callout warn">Don't overtighten—you can strip the wood.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <h4>Fix a Wobbly Table</h4>  <p class="muted">Find the short leg, then shim or tighten.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat">      <div class="label">Likely cause</div>      <div class="value">Uneven floor or loose joint</div>    </div>    <div class="stat">      <div class="label">Tools</div>      <div class="value">Felt pad, shim, screwdriver</div>    </div>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>If the legs are uneven</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Rock the table to find the short leg.</li>        <li>Stick a felt pad or folded paper under it.</li>        <li>Trim to fit. Test again.</li>      </ol>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>If the joint is loose</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Tighten all visible screws and bolts.</li>        <li>If screws spin freely, fill the hole with wood glue and a toothpick, let dry, then re-screw.</li>        <li>For metal brackets, add a washer for grip.</li>      </ol>    </div>  </details>  <p class="callout warn">Don't overtighten—you can strip the wood.</p></div>	-
33.12.1	component_spec/Remove Coffee Stains from Carpet	<div class="card">  <h4>Remove Coffee Stains from Carpet</h4>  <p class="muted">Blot, don't rub. Act fast for best results.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Blot</strong> with a clean cloth. Don't rub—it spreads the stain.</li>    <li><strong>Mix</strong> 1 tbsp dish soap + 1 tbsp white vinegar + 2 cups warm water.</li>    <li><strong>Apply</strong> the solution to the stain. Let sit 5 minutes.</li>    <li><strong>Blot again</strong> with a dry cloth. Repeat until lift.</li>    <li><strong>Rinse</strong> with plain water and blot dry.</li>  </ol>  <details class="acc">    <summary>For old, set-in stains</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Try a commercial carpet cleaner with oxygen bleach. Test on a hidden spot first. Or mix hydrogen peroxide 3% with a drop of dish soap—but only on light-colored carpets.</p>    </div>  </details>  <p class="callout good">Coffee + cream? Treat it like a protein stain: add a dab of enzyme detergent.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <h4>Remove Coffee Stains from Carpet</h4>  <p class="muted">Blot, don't rub. Act fast for best results.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Blot</strong> with a clean cloth. Don't rub—it spreads the stain.</li>    <li><strong>Mix</strong> 1 tbsp dish soap + 1 tbsp white vinegar + 2 cups warm water.</li>    <li><strong>Apply</strong> the solution to the stain. Let sit 5 minutes.</li>    <li><strong>Blot again</strong> with a dry cloth. Repeat until lift.</li>    <li><strong>Rinse</strong> with plain water and blot dry.</li>  </ol>  <details class="acc">    <summary>For old, set-in stains</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Try a commercial carpet cleaner with oxygen bleach. Test on a hidden spot first. Or mix hydrogen peroxide 3% with a drop of dish soap—but only on light-colored carpets.</p>    </div>  </details>  <p class="callout good">Coffee + cream? Treat it like a protein stain: add a dab of enzyme detergent.</p></div>	-
33.13.1	component_spec/Stop Phone Screen Auto-Dimming	<div class="card">  <h4>Stop Phone Screen Auto-Dimming</h4>  <p class="muted">Auto-brightness is usually the culprit. Here's how to take control.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat">      <div class="label">Android</div>      <div class="value">Settings &gt; Display &gt; Adaptive brightness</div>    </div>    <div class="stat">      <div class="label">iPhone</div>      <div class="value">Settings &gt; Accessibility &gt; Display &amp; Text Size &gt; Auto-Brightness</div>    </div>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Still dimming?</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>Check for a case or screen protector blocking the sensor.</li>        <li>Turn off "Battery Saver"—it often dims the screen.</li>        <li>Update your phone. Bugs happen.</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <p class="callout info">Some phones dim when they get hot. Let it cool down.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <h4>Stop Phone Screen Auto-Dimming</h4>  <p class="muted">Auto-brightness is usually the culprit. Here's how to take control.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat">      <div class="label">Android</div>      <div class="value">Settings &gt; Display &gt; Adaptive brightness</div>    </div>    <div class="stat">      <div class="label">iPhone</div>      <div class="value">Settings &gt; Accessibility &gt; Display &amp; Text Size &gt; Auto-Brightness</div>    </div>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Still dimming?</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>Check for a case or screen protector blocking the sensor.</li>        <li>Turn off "Battery Saver"—it often dims the screen.</li>        <li>Update your phone. Bugs happen.</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <p class="callout info">Some phones dim when they get hot. Let it cool down.</p></div>	-
33.14.1	component_spec/Ice Maker Slow / Not Making Ice	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Ice Maker Slow / Not Making Ice</h4><p class="muted t-small">Five-minute check, most fixes are free.</p><div class="callout warn"><strong>First:</strong> confirm the freezer is at 0&deg;F / &minus;18&deg;C or below. Warm freezer, slow ice.</div><div class="divider"></div><ol class="list"><li><strong>Water supply.</strong> Behind the fridge, check the saddle valve or shutoff is fully open. A half-open valve trickles.</li><li><strong>Water filter.</strong> Older than 6 months? Clogged carbon kills flow. Swap it or run filter bypass.</li><li><strong>Frozen fill tube.</strong> If the tube into the icemaker is iced solid, thaw 20 min with a hair dryer on low.</li><li><strong>Door seal.</strong> Dollar-bill test: close the door on a bill, pull. Should drag. If it slides free, the gasket is spent.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>No ice at all?</summary><div class="body"><p>Check the arm or bail wire is down (up = off). Then look for the icemaker's own power switch. Many fridges have both and only one gets noticed.</p></div></details><div class="divider"></div><ul class="list"><li><kbd>1&ndash;2 hrs</kbd> per batch is a healthy cycle</li><li><kbd>3&ndash;4 lbs</kbd> per day is typical output</li></ul><p class="dim t-small">Compressed air through the fill tube once a year keeps it honest.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Ice Maker Slow / Not Making Ice</h4><p class="muted t-small">Five-minute check, most fixes are free.</p><div class="callout warn"><strong>First:</strong> confirm the freezer is at 0&deg;F / &minus;18&deg;C or below. Warm freezer, slow ice.</div><div class="divider"></div><ol class="list"><li><strong>Water supply.</strong> Behind the fridge, check the saddle valve or shutoff is fully open. A half-open valve trickles.</li><li><strong>Water filter.</strong> Older than 6 months? Clogged carbon kills flow. Swap it or run filter bypass.</li><li><strong>Frozen fill tube.</strong> If the tube into the icemaker is iced solid, thaw 20 min with a hair dryer on low.</li><li><strong>Door seal.</strong> Dollar-bill test: close the door on a bill, pull. Should drag. If it slides free, the gasket is spent.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>No ice at all?</summary><div class="body"><p>Check the arm or bail wire is down (up = off). Then look for the icemaker's own power switch. Many fridges have both and only one gets noticed.</p></div></details><div class="divider"></div><ul class="list"><li><kbd>1&ndash;2 hrs</kbd> per batch is a healthy cycle</li><li><kbd>3&ndash;4 lbs</kbd> per day is typical output</li></ul><p class="dim t-small">Compressed air through the fill tube once a year keeps it honest.</p></div></div>	-
33.15.1	component_spec/Stop Squeaky Door Hinges	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Stop Squeaky Door Hinges</h4><p class="muted t-small">Squeak lives at the hinge pin. Grease the pin, not the plate.</p><div class="callout info">WD-40 quiets it for a week. Lubricant keeps it quiet for a year.</div><div class="divider"></div><ol class="list"><li><strong>Tap the pin up.</strong> Slip a nail under the pin head and hammer it upward until it clears the top knuckle.</li><li><strong>Wipe the pin.</strong> Old grease turns to gunk. A rag and a little degreaser.</li><li><strong>Grease it.</strong> White lithium grease, silicone spray, or a dab of petroleum jelly along the pin.</li><li><strong>Refit and swing.</strong> Tap the pin back down flush, then open and close a few times to spread it.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Pin won't budge?</summary><div class="body"><p>It may be a fixed pin. In that case, spray lubricant at the joint and work the door. If you must, replace the whole hinge with a matching size.</p></div></details><div class="divider"></div><ul class="list"><li>Never oil a painted pin &mdash; paint traps grit</li><li>Two drops is plenty, more just drips</li></ul><p class="dim t-small">A quiet house starts with the small hinges.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Stop Squeaky Door Hinges</h4><p class="muted t-small">Squeak lives at the hinge pin. Grease the pin, not the plate.</p><div class="callout info">WD-40 quiets it for a week. Lubricant keeps it quiet for a year.</div><div class="divider"></div><ol class="list"><li><strong>Tap the pin up.</strong> Slip a nail under the pin head and hammer it upward until it clears the top knuckle.</li><li><strong>Wipe the pin.</strong> Old grease turns to gunk. A rag and a little degreaser.</li><li><strong>Grease it.</strong> White lithium grease, silicone spray, or a dab of petroleum jelly along the pin.</li><li><strong>Refit and swing.</strong> Tap the pin back down flush, then open and close a few times to spread it.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Pin won't budge?</summary><div class="body"><p>It may be a fixed pin. In that case, spray lubricant at the joint and work the door. If you must, replace the whole hinge with a matching size.</p></div></details><div class="divider"></div><ul class="list"><li>Never oil a painted pin &mdash; paint traps grit</li><li>Two drops is plenty, more just drips</li></ul><p class="dim t-small">A quiet house starts with the small hinges.</p></div></div>	-
33.16.1	component_spec/Remove Rust from a Bicycle Chain	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Remove Rust from a Bicycle Chain</h4><p class="muted t-small">Untreated chain rust shortens life by half.</p><div class="callout bad"><strong>Stop riding it stiff.</strong> Swapping one link can be cheaper than a whole chain later.</div><div class="divider"></div><ol class="list"><li><strong>Soak in white vinegar.</strong> 1&ndash;3 hours in a shallow tray. Rust lifts as a dark film.</li><li><strong>Scrub.</strong> A brass-bristle brush, working along the links, not across.</li><li><strong>Rinse and dry.</strong> Water, then dryer heat or a rag, then a few minutes in the sun.</li><li><strong>Lube the pins.</strong> A drop of chain lube at each roller, then wipe the outside clean.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Won't flex after soaking?</summary><div class="body"><p>Stuck links are a sign it's beyond saving. Chains are consumables &mdash; measure with a chain checker before you risk the drivetrain.</p></div></details><div class="divider"></div><ul class="list"><li>Vinegar, not Coke &mdash; sugar just gums it up</li><li>Wipe excess lube or it collects grit</li></ul><p class="dim t-small">A rusted chain rarely means a rusted bike.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Remove Rust from a Bicycle Chain</h4><p class="muted t-small">Untreated chain rust shortens life by half.</p><div class="callout bad"><strong>Stop riding it stiff.</strong> Swapping one link can be cheaper than a whole chain later.</div><div class="divider"></div><ol class="list"><li><strong>Soak in white vinegar.</strong> 1&ndash;3 hours in a shallow tray. Rust lifts as a dark film.</li><li><strong>Scrub.</strong> A brass-bristle brush, working along the links, not across.</li><li><strong>Rinse and dry.</strong> Water, then dryer heat or a rag, then a few minutes in the sun.</li><li><strong>Lube the pins.</strong> A drop of chain lube at each roller, then wipe the outside clean.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Won't flex after soaking?</summary><div class="body"><p>Stuck links are a sign it's beyond saving. Chains are consumables &mdash; measure with a chain checker before you risk the drivetrain.</p></div></details><div class="divider"></div><ul class="list"><li>Vinegar, not Coke &mdash; sugar just gums it up</li><li>Wipe excess lube or it collects grit</li></ul><p class="dim t-small">A rusted chain rarely means a rusted bike.</p></div></div>	-
33.17.1	component_spec/Unstick a Stuck Drawer	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Unstick a Stuck Drawer</h4><p class="muted t-small">Wood swells with humidity. Most drawers just need alignment, not force.</p><div class="callout warn">Don't yank. A warped runner will crack the front face before it budges.</div><div class="divider"></div><ol class="list"><li><strong>Empty it, then test.</strong> A light drawer that still sticks tells you it's the slide or the frame, not the load.</li><li><strong>Check the runners.</strong> Wood slides: rub the bottom edge with a candle or paraffin. Metal slides: clean out debris with a dry brush.</li><li><strong>Square the face.</strong> If the drawer racked, take it out and press it flat on a table, then reinsert straight.</li><li><strong>Adjust the stop.</strong> A bent rear stop can hang the back. Bend it back gently with pliers.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Still swollen in summer?</summary><div class="body"><p>Run a bar of soap or a light sanding block over the sides. A 1 mm shave is often all it takes. Re-wax each fall.</p></div></details><div class="divider"></div><ul class="list"><li>Wax, never oil &mdash; oil picks up dust</li><li>Remove the drawer to work on it, not in place</li></ul><p class="dim t-small">Sticky drawer season ends when you stop fighting it.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Unstick a Stuck Drawer</h4><p class="muted t-small">Wood swells with humidity. Most drawers just need alignment, not force.</p><div class="callout warn">Don't yank. A warped runner will crack the front face before it budges.</div><div class="divider"></div><ol class="list"><li><strong>Empty it, then test.</strong> A light drawer that still sticks tells you it's the slide or the frame, not the load.</li><li><strong>Check the runners.</strong> Wood slides: rub the bottom edge with a candle or paraffin. Metal slides: clean out debris with a dry brush.</li><li><strong>Square the face.</strong> If the drawer racked, take it out and press it flat on a table, then reinsert straight.</li><li><strong>Adjust the stop.</strong> A bent rear stop can hang the back. Bend it back gently with pliers.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Still swollen in summer?</summary><div class="body"><p>Run a bar of soap or a light sanding block over the sides. A 1 mm shave is often all it takes. Re-wax each fall.</p></div></details><div class="divider"></div><ul class="list"><li>Wax, never oil &mdash; oil picks up dust</li><li>Remove the drawer to work on it, not in place</li></ul><p class="dim t-small">Sticky drawer season ends when you stop fighting it.</p></div></div>	-
33.18.1	component_spec/Fix a Leaky Washing Machine Hose	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Fix a Leaky Washing Machine Hose</h4><p class="muted t-small">Nine out of ten washer leaks are a $9 hose, not a $900 machine.</p><div class="callout bad"><strong>Before anything:</strong> close both supply valves behind the machine.</div><div class="divider"></div><ol class="list"><li><strong>Identify the source.</strong> Dry the area, run a cycle, and look. Hose joint, drain hose, or pump seal?</li><li><strong>Check the coupling.</strong> If the leak is at the wall, the washer inside the hose end is likely split. Replace it with a fresh rubber washer.</li><li><strong>Inspect both hoses.</strong> Bulges, cracks, or a date older than 5 years means replace both.</li><li><strong>Reconnect finger-tight plus a quarter turn.</strong> Too tight crushes the rubber and leaks worse.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Leak at the drain hose?</summary><div class="body"><p>Confirm the drain hose hooks into the standpipe at the right height (usually 30&ndash;36 in / 76&ndash;91 cm). Too low and it siphons; too high and it spills.</p></div></details><div class="divider"></div><ul class="list"><li>Stainless-braided hoses last longer</li><li>Tag the install date on the hose with a marker</li></ul><p class="dim t-small">Swap hoses every five years whether they leak or not.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Fix a Leaky Washing Machine Hose</h4><p class="muted t-small">Nine out of ten washer leaks are a $9 hose, not a $900 machine.</p><div class="callout bad"><strong>Before anything:</strong> close both supply valves behind the machine.</div><div class="divider"></div><ol class="list"><li><strong>Identify the source.</strong> Dry the area, run a cycle, and look. Hose joint, drain hose, or pump seal?</li><li><strong>Check the coupling.</strong> If the leak is at the wall, the washer inside the hose end is likely split. Replace it with a fresh rubber washer.</li><li><strong>Inspect both hoses.</strong> Bulges, cracks, or a date older than 5 years means replace both.</li><li><strong>Reconnect finger-tight plus a quarter turn.</strong> Too tight crushes the rubber and leaks worse.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Leak at the drain hose?</summary><div class="body"><p>Confirm the drain hose hooks into the standpipe at the right height (usually 30&ndash;36 in / 76&ndash;91 cm). Too low and it siphons; too high and it spills.</p></div></details><div class="divider"></div><ul class="list"><li>Stainless-braided hoses last longer</li><li>Tag the install date on the hose with a marker</li></ul><p class="dim t-small">Swap hoses every five years whether they leak or not.</p></div></div>	-
33.19.1	component_spec/Quiet a Squeaky Ceiling Fan	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Quiet a Squeaky Ceiling Fan</h4><p class="muted t-small">Squeak is metal-on-metal. The fix is usually a screwdriver and five minutes.</p><div class="callout info">Kill the breaker first. A fan that wobbles at 3 a.m. is not worth a ladder mistake.</div><div class="divider"></div><ol class="list"><li><strong>Check the blade screws.</strong> Loose blades chatter. Tighten all motor-to-blade screws evenly, in a star pattern.</li><li><strong>Balance with a kit.</strong> Clip the balancing weight to the top of one blade, spin, and move it if it gets worse.</li><li><strong>Lube the bearings.</strong> A drop of light machine oil at the motor shaft, if yours has an oil port.</li><li><strong>Tighten the downrod.</strong> A loose canopy or downrod set screw is a common chirp.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Real wobble, not just noise?</summary><div class="body"><p>Check the mount box. A fan box that's too small sags. If the fan hangs crooked from the ceiling, stop and call an electrician.</p></div></details><div class="divider"></div><ul class="list"><li>Fan blades must be level to 1/8 in</li><li>Wobble pairs with noise more often than not</li></ul><p class="dim t-small">Quiet fans run longer. Anxiety-free sleep is the real win.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Quiet a Squeaky Ceiling Fan</h4><p class="muted t-small">Squeak is metal-on-metal. The fix is usually a screwdriver and five minutes.</p><div class="callout info">Kill the breaker first. A fan that wobbles at 3 a.m. is not worth a ladder mistake.</div><div class="divider"></div><ol class="list"><li><strong>Check the blade screws.</strong> Loose blades chatter. Tighten all motor-to-blade screws evenly, in a star pattern.</li><li><strong>Balance with a kit.</strong> Clip the balancing weight to the top of one blade, spin, and move it if it gets worse.</li><li><strong>Lube the bearings.</strong> A drop of light machine oil at the motor shaft, if yours has an oil port.</li><li><strong>Tighten the downrod.</strong> A loose canopy or downrod set screw is a common chirp.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Real wobble, not just noise?</summary><div class="body"><p>Check the mount box. A fan box that's too small sags. If the fan hangs crooked from the ceiling, stop and call an electrician.</p></div></details><div class="divider"></div><ul class="list"><li>Fan blades must be level to 1/8 in</li><li>Wobble pairs with noise more often than not</li></ul><p class="dim t-small">Quiet fans run longer. Anxiety-free sleep is the real win.</p></div></div>	-
33.20.1	component_spec/How to Fix Scratched Eyeglasses	<div class="card"><h3>Scratched Eyeglass Lenses</h3><p class="muted">Light surface scratches only. Deep gouges or anti-reflective coating damage usually need a pro.</p><div class="callout warn"><p><strong>Safety first:</strong> Never rub dry—dust can scratch more. Use a clean microfiber cloth and mild soapy water.</p></div><ol class="list"><li><strong>Clean</strong> lenses with dish soap and warm water. Dry with a lint-free cloth.</li><li><strong>Apply a tiny dot</strong> of non-abrasive white toothpaste (not gel) or a dedicated lens polish to the scratch.</li><li><strong>Rub gently</strong> in a circular motion with your fingertip for 30–60 seconds. Don't press hard.</li><li><strong>Rinse and check.</strong> Repeat if needed. Finish with a clean microfiber buff.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>What about anti-reflective coatings?</summary><div class="body"><p>Those coatings are delicate. Polishing can remove them and make things worse. For coated lenses, try a scratch-filling product made for AR coatings, or see your optician.</p></div></details><div class="row between"><span class="t-small dim">Works for plastic (CR-39) and polycarbonate lenses</span><span class="badge info"><i class="dot"></i> Cached fix</span></div></div>	<div class="card"><h3>Scratched Eyeglass Lenses</h3><p class="muted">Light surface scratches only. Deep gouges or anti-reflective coating damage usually need a pro.</p><div class="callout warn"><p><strong>Safety first:</strong> Never rub dry—dust can scratch more. Use a clean microfiber cloth and mild soapy water.</p></div><ol class="list"><li><strong>Clean</strong> lenses with dish soap and warm water. Dry with a lint-free cloth.</li><li><strong>Apply a tiny dot</strong> of non-abrasive white toothpaste (not gel) or a dedicated lens polish to the scratch.</li><li><strong>Rub gently</strong> in a circular motion with your fingertip for 30–60 seconds. Don't press hard.</li><li><strong>Rinse and check.</strong> Repeat if needed. Finish with a clean microfiber buff.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>What about anti-reflective coatings?</summary><div class="body"><p>Those coatings are delicate. Polishing can remove them and make things worse. For coated lenses, try a scratch-filling product made for AR coatings, or see your optician.</p></div></details><div class="row between"><span class="t-small dim">Works for plastic (CR-39) and polycarbonate lenses</span><span class="badge info"><i class="dot"></i> Cached fix</span></div></div>	-
33.21.1	component_spec/Quiet a Noisy Hard Drive	<div class="card"><h3>Noisy Hard Drive? Don't Panic—Yet</h3><p class="muted">Some noise is normal. Grinding, clicking, or beeping means trouble.</p><div class="callout bad"><p><strong>If you hear clicking or grinding:</strong> Back up your data immediately. The drive may be failing. Stop using it for anything but recovery.</p></div><p>For <strong>normal</strong> spin and seek noise, these quiet it down:</p><ul class="list"><li><strong>Isolate vibration.</strong> Place the drive on a foam pad or rubber feet—don't bolt it to a metal case.</li><li><strong>Check mounting screws.</strong> Loose screws amplify hum. Tighten them.</li><li><strong>Move it away from you.</strong> A longer cable and a spot on the floor works wonders.</li><li><strong>Consider an SSD.</strong> No moving parts, no noise. If you're due for an upgrade, this is the fix.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Is my drive dying?</summary><div class="body"><p>Listen for rhythmic clicking (head crash) or a high-pitched whine that changes. Run a SMART check with CrystalDiskInfo or similar. If SMART shows reallocated sectors, replace the drive.</p></div></details><p class="t-small dim">External drives can also be noisy from the enclosure fan—clean it or set it on a hard surface.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h3>Noisy Hard Drive? Don't Panic—Yet</h3><p class="muted">Some noise is normal. Grinding, clicking, or beeping means trouble.</p><div class="callout bad"><p><strong>If you hear clicking or grinding:</strong> Back up your data immediately. The drive may be failing. Stop using it for anything but recovery.</p></div><p>For <strong>normal</strong> spin and seek noise, these quiet it down:</p><ul class="list"><li><strong>Isolate vibration.</strong> Place the drive on a foam pad or rubber feet—don't bolt it to a metal case.</li><li><strong>Check mounting screws.</strong> Loose screws amplify hum. Tighten them.</li><li><strong>Move it away from you.</strong> A longer cable and a spot on the floor works wonders.</li><li><strong>Consider an SSD.</strong> No moving parts, no noise. If you're due for an upgrade, this is the fix.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Is my drive dying?</summary><div class="body"><p>Listen for rhythmic clicking (head crash) or a high-pitched whine that changes. Run a SMART check with CrystalDiskInfo or similar. If SMART shows reallocated sectors, replace the drive.</p></div></details><p class="t-small dim">External drives can also be noisy from the enclosure fan—clean it or set it on a hard surface.</p></div>	-
33.22.1	component_spec/Reheat Pizza Without Soggy Crust	<div class="card"><h3>Crispy Leftover Pizza</h3><p class="muted">The microwave is the enemy of crust. A skillet or oven brings it back to life.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Skillet method</span><span class="value">5–7 min</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Oven method</span><span class="value">8–10 min</span></div></div><ol class="list"><li><strong>Skillet:</strong> Place slices in a dry nonstick skillet over medium-low. Cover with a lid or foil. The bottom crisps while the cheese melts.</li><li><strong>Oven:</strong> Preheat to 375°F (190°C). Put slices on a baking sheet or directly on the rack. Bake until crust is crisp and cheese bubbles.</li><li><strong>Air fryer:</strong> 350°F for 3–4 minutes. Shake once. Works great for one or two slices.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> A splash of water in the skillet (then cover) creates steam that melts the cheese without making the crust soggy—just keep the lid on only for the first minute.</p></div><p class="t-small dim">No skillet? A piece of foil on a dry pan works in a pinch.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h3>Crispy Leftover Pizza</h3><p class="muted">The microwave is the enemy of crust. A skillet or oven brings it back to life.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Skillet method</span><span class="value">5–7 min</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Oven method</span><span class="value">8–10 min</span></div></div><ol class="list"><li><strong>Skillet:</strong> Place slices in a dry nonstick skillet over medium-low. Cover with a lid or foil. The bottom crisps while the cheese melts.</li><li><strong>Oven:</strong> Preheat to 375°F (190°C). Put slices on a baking sheet or directly on the rack. Bake until crust is crisp and cheese bubbles.</li><li><strong>Air fryer:</strong> 350°F for 3–4 minutes. Shake once. Works great for one or two slices.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> A splash of water in the skillet (then cover) creates steam that melts the cheese without making the crust soggy—just keep the lid on only for the first minute.</p></div><p class="t-small dim">No skillet? A piece of foil on a dry pan works in a pinch.</p></div>	-
33.23.1	component_spec/Stop a Squeaky Belt on a Treadmill	<div class="card"><h3>Squeaky Treadmill Belt</h3><p class="muted">Most squeaks come from a dry belt or loose hardware. A little maintenance goes a long way.</p><div class="callout warn"><p><strong>Unplug first.</strong> Always disconnect power before working on your treadmill.</p></div><ol class="list"><li><strong>Check belt tension.</strong> If it's too loose, it slips and squeaks. Follow your manual's adjustment—usually a bolt at the rear roller.</li><li><strong>Lubricate.</strong> Use silicone-based treadmill lubricant (not WD-40). Apply under the belt along the deck. Run the treadmill at low speed for a few minutes to spread it.</li><li><strong>Tighten all bolts.</strong> Frame, motor cover, and console screws work loose over time.</li><li><strong>Inspect the belt for wear.</strong> Cracks or glazing mean it's time for a new belt.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>What if it's the motor?</summary><div class="body"><p>A high-pitched whine from the motor area could be a worn motor brush or bearing. That's usually a pro repair. If the noise only happens when you step on the belt, it's likely the belt or deck.</p></div></details><p class="t-small dim">Regular vacuuming under the belt prevents grit from causing wear and noise.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h3>Squeaky Treadmill Belt</h3><p class="muted">Most squeaks come from a dry belt or loose hardware. A little maintenance goes a long way.</p><div class="callout warn"><p><strong>Unplug first.</strong> Always disconnect power before working on your treadmill.</p></div><ol class="list"><li><strong>Check belt tension.</strong> If it's too loose, it slips and squeaks. Follow your manual's adjustment—usually a bolt at the rear roller.</li><li><strong>Lubricate.</strong> Use silicone-based treadmill lubricant (not WD-40). Apply under the belt along the deck. Run the treadmill at low speed for a few minutes to spread it.</li><li><strong>Tighten all bolts.</strong> Frame, motor cover, and console screws work loose over time.</li><li><strong>Inspect the belt for wear.</strong> Cracks or glazing mean it's time for a new belt.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>What if it's the motor?</summary><div class="body"><p>A high-pitched whine from the motor area could be a worn motor brush or bearing. That's usually a pro repair. If the noise only happens when you step on the belt, it's likely the belt or deck.</p></div></details><p class="t-small dim">Regular vacuuming under the belt prevents grit from causing wear and noise.</p></div>	-
33.24.1	component_spec/Get Rid of Static Cling on Clothes	<div class="card"><h3>Static Cling in Laundry</h3><p class="muted">Static builds up when clothes dry without enough moisture. A few small changes keep the zap away.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Wool dryer balls</span><span class="value">3–4</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Dryer sheet</span><span class="value">1</span></div></div><ul class="list"><li><strong>Add a ball of aluminum foil</strong> (about the size of a tennis ball) to the dryer. It discharges static and lasts for months.</li><li><strong>Use wool dryer balls</strong> with a few drops of essential oil if you like. They separate clothes and reduce cling.</li><li><strong>Don't overdry.</strong> Take clothes out while slightly damp—static is worst when everything is bone-dry.</li><li><strong>Separate synthetics.</strong> They generate more static than natural fibers. Dry them separately or with a dryer sheet.</li><li><strong>Try a vinegar rinse.</strong> Add 1/4 cup white vinegar to the rinse cycle. It softens and reduces static naturally.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Will a dryer sheet ruin my towels?</summary><div class="body"><p>Yes—the coating reduces absorbency. Skip sheets for towels and use wool balls or foil instead.</p></div></details><p class="t-small dim">Static is worse in dry climates. A humidifier in the laundry room helps too.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h3>Static Cling in Laundry</h3><p class="muted">Static builds up when clothes dry without enough moisture. A few small changes keep the zap away.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Wool dryer balls</span><span class="value">3–4</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Dryer sheet</span><span class="value">1</span></div></div><ul class="list"><li><strong>Add a ball of aluminum foil</strong> (about the size of a tennis ball) to the dryer. It discharges static and lasts for months.</li><li><strong>Use wool dryer balls</strong> with a few drops of essential oil if you like. They separate clothes and reduce cling.</li><li><strong>Don't overdry.</strong> Take clothes out while slightly damp—static is worst when everything is bone-dry.</li><li><strong>Separate synthetics.</strong> They generate more static than natural fibers. Dry them separately or with a dryer sheet.</li><li><strong>Try a vinegar rinse.</strong> Add 1/4 cup white vinegar to the rinse cycle. It softens and reduces static naturally.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Will a dryer sheet ruin my towels?</summary><div class="body"><p>Yes—the coating reduces absorbency. Skip sheets for towels and use wool balls or foil instead.</p></div></details><p class="t-small dim">Static is worse in dry climates. A humidifier in the laundry room helps too.</p></div>	-
33.25.1	component_spec/Remove Pet Hair from Furniture	<div class="card"><h3>Pet Hair on Furniture</h3><p class="muted">A vacuum alone won't grab embedded hair. These tools lift it out.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Rubber gloves</span><span class="value">Damp</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Squeegee</span><span class="value">Dry</span></div></div><ol class="list"><li><strong>Rubber glove method:</strong> Put on a clean rubber glove (or a damp sponge). Wipe the furniture in one direction. The hair clumps and lifts right off.</li><li><strong>Squeegee or window blade:</strong> Drag a squeegee across the upholstery. It gathers hair into a pile you can pick up.</li><li><strong>Dryer sheet:</strong> Rub a used dryer sheet over the surface. The anti-static coating releases hair and reduces future cling.</li><li><strong>Vacuum with a rubber pet attachment</strong> if you have one. Otherwise, use the crevice tool on low suction.</li><li><strong>Lint roller</strong> for touch-ups between deep cleans.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>What about bedding and curtains?</summary><div class="body"><p>Same methods work. For curtains, a damp rubber glove is easiest—no need to take them down. For bedding, toss in the dryer on air-fluff with a dryer sheet to loosen hair before washing.</p></div></details><p class="t-small dim">A slightly damp sponge works just as well as a glove in a pinch.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h3>Pet Hair on Furniture</h3><p class="muted">A vacuum alone won't grab embedded hair. These tools lift it out.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Rubber gloves</span><span class="value">Damp</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Squeegee</span><span class="value">Dry</span></div></div><ol class="list"><li><strong>Rubber glove method:</strong> Put on a clean rubber glove (or a damp sponge). Wipe the furniture in one direction. The hair clumps and lifts right off.</li><li><strong>Squeegee or window blade:</strong> Drag a squeegee across the upholstery. It gathers hair into a pile you can pick up.</li><li><strong>Dryer sheet:</strong> Rub a used dryer sheet over the surface. The anti-static coating releases hair and reduces future cling.</li><li><strong>Vacuum with a rubber pet attachment</strong> if you have one. Otherwise, use the crevice tool on low suction.</li><li><strong>Lint roller</strong> for touch-ups between deep cleans.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>What about bedding and curtains?</summary><div class="body"><p>Same methods work. For curtains, a damp rubber glove is easiest—no need to take them down. For bedding, toss in the dryer on air-fluff with a dryer sheet to loosen hair before washing.</p></div></details><p class="t-small dim">A slightly damp sponge works just as well as a glove in a pinch.</p></div>	-
33.26.1	component_spec/Unclog a Slow Kitchen Sink	<article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><div class="card"><h4>Unclog a Slow Kitchen Sink</h4><p class="muted"><regex>user_name</regex>, a slow drain usually means gunk, not doom.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Tools you already have</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Plunger</li><li>Bucket</li><li>Baking soda &amp; vinegar</li><li>Drain snake (optional)</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Step-by-step</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Remove the stopper and clear debris.</li><li>Place bucket under the P-trap.</li><li>Unscrew and clean the trap.</li><li>For stubborn clogs: pour 1/2 cup baking soda, then 1/2 cup vinegar.</li><li>Wait 15 mins, flush with hot water.</li><li>If still slow, use a drain snake.</li></ol></div></details><div class="callout warn"><span class="badge warn">careful</span> Skip chemical drain cleaners if you have older pipes.</div></div></article>	<article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><div class="card"><h4>Unclog a Slow Kitchen Sink</h4><p class="muted">, a slow drain usually means gunk, not doom.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Tools you already have</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Plunger</li><li>Bucket</li><li>Baking soda &amp; vinegar</li><li>Drain snake (optional)</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Step-by-step</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Remove the stopper and clear debris.</li><li>Place bucket under the P-trap.</li><li>Unscrew and clean the trap.</li><li>For stubborn clogs: pour 1/2 cup baking soda, then 1/2 cup vinegar.</li><li>Wait 15 mins, flush with hot water.</li><li>If still slow, use a drain snake.</li></ol></div></details><div class="callout warn"><span class="badge warn">careful</span> Skip chemical drain cleaners if you have older pipes.</div></div></article>	-
33.27.1	component_spec/Whiten Dingy White Sneakers	<article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><div class="card"><h4>Whiten Dingy White Sneakers</h4><p class="muted"><regex>user_name</regex>, those creases don't stand a chance.</p><details class="acc"><summary>What you need</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Old toothbrush</li><li>Baking soda</li><li>Laundry detergent</li><li>Warm water</li><li>White cloth</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How to do it</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Remove laces and insoles.</li><li>Mix 1 tbsp baking soda + 1 tsp detergent + warm water into a paste.</li><li>Scrub gently in circles.</li><li>Wipe clean with damp cloth.</li><li>Air dry away from direct heat.</li></ol></div></details><div class="callout info"><span class="badge info">tip</span> For yellowed soles, try a magic eraser.</div></div></article>	<article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><div class="card"><h4>Whiten Dingy White Sneakers</h4><p class="muted">, those creases don't stand a chance.</p><details class="acc"><summary>What you need</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Old toothbrush</li><li>Baking soda</li><li>Laundry detergent</li><li>Warm water</li><li>White cloth</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How to do it</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Remove laces and insoles.</li><li>Mix 1 tbsp baking soda + 1 tsp detergent + warm water into a paste.</li><li>Scrub gently in circles.</li><li>Wipe clean with damp cloth.</li><li>Air dry away from direct heat.</li></ol></div></details><div class="callout info"><span class="badge info">tip</span> For yellowed soles, try a magic eraser.</div></div></article>	-
33.28.1	component_spec/Revive Wilted Lettuce	<article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><div class="card"><h4>Revive Wilted Lettuce</h4><p class="muted"><regex>user_name</regex>, a cold bath works wonders.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Quick fix</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Fill a bowl with ice water.</li><li>Submerge the lettuce for 10–15 mins.</li><li>Dry in a salad spinner or with paper towels.</li><li>Store with a paper towel in a sealed bag.</li></ol></div></details><div class="callout good"><span class="badge good">works</span> Best for leaf and romaine; iceberg may stay crunchy already.</div></div></article>	<article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><div class="card"><h4>Revive Wilted Lettuce</h4><p class="muted">, a cold bath works wonders.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Quick fix</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Fill a bowl with ice water.</li><li>Submerge the lettuce for 10–15 mins.</li><li>Dry in a salad spinner or with paper towels.</li><li>Store with a paper towel in a sealed bag.</li></ol></div></details><div class="callout good"><span class="badge good">works</span> Best for leaf and romaine; iceberg may stay crunchy already.</div></div></article>	-
33.29.1	component_spec/De-Skunk a Dog	<article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><div class="card"><h4>De-Skunk a Dog</h4><p class="muted"><regex>user_name</regex>, hold your breath and grab the peroxide.</p><details class="acc"><summary>DIY skunk rinse</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>1 quart 3% hydrogen peroxide</li><li>1/4 cup baking soda</li><li>1 tsp liquid dish soap</li></ul><p>Mix and apply immediately (don't store it).</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Steps</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Wear gloves and old clothes.</li><li>Keep dog outside.</li><li>Wet dog, lather solution, avoid eyes.</li><li>Let sit 5 mins, rinse thoroughly.</li><li>Repeat if needed.</li><li>Follow with dog shampoo.</li></ol></div></details><div class="callout warn"><span class="badge warn">note</span> Don't use if dog has open wounds; call vet.</div></div></article>	<article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><div class="card"><h4>De-Skunk a Dog</h4><p class="muted">, hold your breath and grab the peroxide.</p><details class="acc"><summary>DIY skunk rinse</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>1 quart 3% hydrogen peroxide</li><li>1/4 cup baking soda</li><li>1 tsp liquid dish soap</li></ul><p>Mix and apply immediately (don't store it).</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Steps</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Wear gloves and old clothes.</li><li>Keep dog outside.</li><li>Wet dog, lather solution, avoid eyes.</li><li>Let sit 5 mins, rinse thoroughly.</li><li>Repeat if needed.</li><li>Follow with dog shampoo.</li></ol></div></details><div class="callout warn"><span class="badge warn">note</span> Don't use if dog has open wounds; call vet.</div></div></article>	-
33.30.1	component_spec/Remove Ballpoint Ink from Fabric	<article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><div class="card"><h4>Remove Ballpoint Ink from Fabric</h4><p class="muted"><regex>user_name</regex>, act fast and don't rub.</p><details class="acc"><summary>What to grab</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Rubbing alcohol</li><li>Cotton swabs</li><li>Clean cloth</li><li>Paper towels</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Method</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Place paper towel under stain.</li><li>Dab alcohol onto a cotton swab.</li><li>Blot the stain from the outside in.</li><li>Swap swabs as they pick up ink.</li><li>Launder as usual.</li></ol></div></details><div class="callout bad"><span class="badge bad">warning</span> Test on a hidden area first; alcohol can fade some dyes.</div></div></article>	<article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><div class="card"><h4>Remove Ballpoint Ink from Fabric</h4><p class="muted">, act fast and don't rub.</p><details class="acc"><summary>What to grab</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Rubbing alcohol</li><li>Cotton swabs</li><li>Clean cloth</li><li>Paper towels</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Method</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Place paper towel under stain.</li><li>Dab alcohol onto a cotton swab.</li><li>Blot the stain from the outside in.</li><li>Swap swabs as they pick up ink.</li><li>Launder as usual.</li></ol></div></details><div class="callout bad"><span class="badge bad">warning</span> Test on a hidden area first; alcohol can fade some dyes.</div></div></article>	-
33.31.1	component_spec/Quiet a Noisy Washing Machine	<article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><div class="card"><h4>Quiet a Noisy Washing Machine</h4><p class="muted"><regex>user_name</regex>, let's stop that walk.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Common fixes</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Check load balance—redistribute clothes.</li><li>Adjust the leveling feet.</li><li>Place anti-vibration pads under legs.</li><li>Ensure shipping bolts are removed (new machines).</li></ul></div></details><div class="callout info"><span class="badge info">tip</span> If it thumps during spin only, it's often an unbalanced load.</div></div></article>	<article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><div class="card"><h4>Quiet a Noisy Washing Machine</h4><p class="muted">, let's stop that walk.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Common fixes</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Check load balance—redistribute clothes.</li><li>Adjust the leveling feet.</li><li>Place anti-vibration pads under legs.</li><li>Ensure shipping bolts are removed (new machines).</li></ul></div></details><div class="callout info"><span class="badge info">tip</span> If it thumps during spin only, it's often an unbalanced load.</div></div></article>	-
33.32.1	component_spec/Yoga Mat Grip Restore	<div class="umi-page stack"><h2>Yoga Mat Grip Restore</h2><p class="muted">Slippery mats are usually oil, not age. Here's <regex>user_name</regex>'s five-minute reset.</p><div class="callout info"><strong>Why it happens:</strong> skin oils, lotion, and mat residue build a slick film. PVC and TPE mats hold it worst.</div><ol class="list"><li>Fill a tub or sink with <strong>warm</strong> water and a drop of mild dish soap.</li><li>Wipe both sides with a soft cloth. No scrubbing pads, no vinegar on PVC.</li><li>Rinse until the water runs clear and the surface stops feeling slick.</li><li>Hang over a shower rod or chair back. Air-dry fully, away from direct sun.</li></ol><div class="card"><h4>If it's still slick</h4><ul class="list"><li>New mats often need 3&ndash;5 washes to shed factory release film.</li><li>Use a <strong>mat towel</strong> over the top for hot yoga.</li><li>Try a rosin or chalk grip spray, not baby powder.</li><li>Store rolled, smooth-side out, so dust doesn't settle in the texture.</li></ul></div><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Dry time</span><span class="value">12&ndash;24<span class="unit">h</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Wash cadence</span><span class="value">2&ndash;4<span class="unit">wks</span></span></div></div><p class="dim t-small">Skip the dryer. Heat curls the mat and cooks the grip right out.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page stack"><h2>Yoga Mat Grip Restore</h2><p class="muted">Slippery mats are usually oil, not age. Here's 's five-minute reset.</p><div class="callout info"><strong>Why it happens:</strong> skin oils, lotion, and mat residue build a slick film. PVC and TPE mats hold it worst.</div><ol class="list"><li>Fill a tub or sink with <strong>warm</strong> water and a drop of mild dish soap.</li><li>Wipe both sides with a soft cloth. No scrubbing pads, no vinegar on PVC.</li><li>Rinse until the water runs clear and the surface stops feeling slick.</li><li>Hang over a shower rod or chair back. Air-dry fully, away from direct sun.</li></ol><div class="card"><h4>If it's still slick</h4><ul class="list"><li>New mats often need 3&ndash;5 washes to shed factory release film.</li><li>Use a <strong>mat towel</strong> over the top for hot yoga.</li><li>Try a rosin or chalk grip spray, not baby powder.</li><li>Store rolled, smooth-side out, so dust doesn't settle in the texture.</li></ul></div><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Dry time</span><span class="value">12&ndash;24<span class="unit">h</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Wash cadence</span><span class="value">2&ndash;4<span class="unit">wks</span></span></div></div><p class="dim t-small">Skip the dryer. Heat curls the mat and cooks the grip right out.</p></div>	-
33.33.1	component_spec/Squeaky Brakes Silence	<div class="umi-page stack"><h2>Squeaky Brakes Silence</h2><p class="muted">Morning squeal is usually harmless. A constant grind is not. Let's sort which one <regex>user_name</regex> has.</p><div class="callout good"><strong>Likely fine:</strong> a short squeal on the first few stops after a damp or cold night. Surface rust burns off in a block.</div><div class="callout bad"><strong>Stop driving:</strong> grinding metal, a pulsing pedal, or a squeal that never quits. That's pad-on-rotor contact.</div><details class="acc"><summary>Quick driveway checks</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Look through the wheel spokes at pad thickness. Under 3&nbsp;mm means new pads.</li><li>Check the rotor face for deep grooves or a blue tint.</li><li>Spin the wheel by hand. A scrape every rotation points to a warped rotor or bent dust shield.</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What usually fixes it</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Have the pads' backing plates greased with <strong>brake lube</strong>, not copper paste on the friction side.</li><li>Ask for a rotor resurface if the surface is glazed.</li><li>Replace shims and hardware clips on any pad swap.</li></ol></div></details><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Sound</th><th>When</th><th>Verdict</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Squeal</td><td>First stops, cold</td><td>Normal</td></tr><tr><td>Grind</td><td>Always</td><td>Inspect now</td></tr><tr><td>Clunk</td><td>Reverse</td><td>Loose hardware</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>	<div class="umi-page stack"><h2>Squeaky Brakes Silence</h2><p class="muted">Morning squeal is usually harmless. A constant grind is not. Let's sort which one  has.</p><div class="callout good"><strong>Likely fine:</strong> a short squeal on the first few stops after a damp or cold night. Surface rust burns off in a block.</div><div class="callout bad"><strong>Stop driving:</strong> grinding metal, a pulsing pedal, or a squeal that never quits. That's pad-on-rotor contact.</div><details class="acc"><summary>Quick driveway checks</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Look through the wheel spokes at pad thickness. Under 3&nbsp;mm means new pads.</li><li>Check the rotor face for deep grooves or a blue tint.</li><li>Spin the wheel by hand. A scrape every rotation points to a warped rotor or bent dust shield.</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What usually fixes it</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Have the pads' backing plates greased with <strong>brake lube</strong>, not copper paste on the friction side.</li><li>Ask for a rotor resurface if the surface is glazed.</li><li>Replace shims and hardware clips on any pad swap.</li></ol></div></details><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Sound</th><th>When</th><th>Verdict</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Squeal</td><td>First stops, cold</td><td>Normal</td></tr><tr><td>Grind</td><td>Always</td><td>Inspect now</td></tr><tr><td>Clunk</td><td>Reverse</td><td>Loose hardware</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>	-
33.34.1	component_spec/Fridge Door Seal Test	<div class="umi-page stack"><h2>Fridge Door Seal Test</h2><p class="muted">A leaky gasket makes the compressor run nonstop. The dollar-bill test tells <regex>user_name</regex> in ten seconds.</p><div class="card"><h4>The dollar-bill test</h4><ol class="list"><li>Open the door and lay a bill or a strip of paper across the gasket.</li><li>Close the door and try to pull it out.</li><li>Repeat every twelve inches around the frame.</li></ol><p class="dim t-small">Slight drag is good. Slides out freely means a gap at that spot.</p></div><div class="callout info"><strong>Common culprits:</strong> crumbs on the gasket, a worn hinge letting the door sag, or a bulging item pushing the shelf out.</div><details class="acc"><summary>Fixes in order of effort</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Wipe the gasket with warm soapy water and dry it. Corners hide the most gunk.</li><li>Straighten a pinched gasket by pressing it back into its channel.</li><li>Tighten the hinge screws or shim the hinge plate if the door sags.</li><li>Replace the gasket. They're model-specific and cheap, usually $25&ndash;$60.</li></ol></div></details><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Gasket life</span><span class="value">5&ndash;10<span class="unit">yrs</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Energy wasted if leaky</span><span class="value">up to 25<span class="unit">%</span></span></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page stack"><h2>Fridge Door Seal Test</h2><p class="muted">A leaky gasket makes the compressor run nonstop. The dollar-bill test tells  in ten seconds.</p><div class="card"><h4>The dollar-bill test</h4><ol class="list"><li>Open the door and lay a bill or a strip of paper across the gasket.</li><li>Close the door and try to pull it out.</li><li>Repeat every twelve inches around the frame.</li></ol><p class="dim t-small">Slight drag is good. Slides out freely means a gap at that spot.</p></div><div class="callout info"><strong>Common culprits:</strong> crumbs on the gasket, a worn hinge letting the door sag, or a bulging item pushing the shelf out.</div><details class="acc"><summary>Fixes in order of effort</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Wipe the gasket with warm soapy water and dry it. Corners hide the most gunk.</li><li>Straighten a pinched gasket by pressing it back into its channel.</li><li>Tighten the hinge screws or shim the hinge plate if the door sags.</li><li>Replace the gasket. They're model-specific and cheap, usually $25&ndash;$60.</li></ol></div></details><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Gasket life</span><span class="value">5&ndash;10<span class="unit">yrs</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Energy wasted if leaky</span><span class="value">up to 25<span class="unit">%</span></span></div></div></div>	-
33.35.1	component_spec/AirPods Charging Case Not Charging	<div class="umi-page stack"><h2>AirPods Charging Case Not Charging</h2><p class="muted">Most case issues are lint or a tired cable, not the case itself. Walk it down in order, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ol class="list"><li>Try a <strong>different cable and wall block.</strong> Skip the laptop port for this test.</li><li>Inspect the case's charge port with a flashlight. Lint is the villain nine times out of ten.</li><li>Dig it out with a <strong>wooden toothpick</strong> or a dry, soft brush. Never a metal pin.</li><li>If it's wireless-only, set it on the pad and check for case alignment. Cases can miss the coil by a few millimeters.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Reset the case and buds</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Put both buds in the case and close the lid.</li><li>Wait 30 seconds, then open and leave them in.</li><li>Hold the button on the back of the case for 15&ndash;20 seconds until the light blinks amber then white.</li><li>Re-pair from Bluetooth settings and test charging again.</li></ol></div></details><div class="callout warn"><strong>Watch for:</strong> a case that only charges when wiggle-held. That's a worn USB-C or Lightning port, and the case needs service.</div><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Normal case charge</span><span class="value">~1<span class="unit">hr</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Full cycles before wear</span><span class="value">~500</span></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page stack"><h2>AirPods Charging Case Not Charging</h2><p class="muted">Most case issues are lint or a tired cable, not the case itself. Walk it down in order, .</p><ol class="list"><li>Try a <strong>different cable and wall block.</strong> Skip the laptop port for this test.</li><li>Inspect the case's charge port with a flashlight. Lint is the villain nine times out of ten.</li><li>Dig it out with a <strong>wooden toothpick</strong> or a dry, soft brush. Never a metal pin.</li><li>If it's wireless-only, set it on the pad and check for case alignment. Cases can miss the coil by a few millimeters.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Reset the case and buds</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Put both buds in the case and close the lid.</li><li>Wait 30 seconds, then open and leave them in.</li><li>Hold the button on the back of the case for 15&ndash;20 seconds until the light blinks amber then white.</li><li>Re-pair from Bluetooth settings and test charging again.</li></ol></div></details><div class="callout warn"><strong>Watch for:</strong> a case that only charges when wiggle-held. That's a worn USB-C or Lightning port, and the case needs service.</div><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Normal case charge</span><span class="value">~1<span class="unit">hr</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Full cycles before wear</span><span class="value">~500</span></div></div></div>	-
33.36.1	component_spec/Wooden Cutting Board Rescue	<div class="umi-page stack"><h2>Wooden Cutting Board Rescue</h2><p class="muted">Wood self-heals if you feed it. Grey, cracked boards come back with oil and patience, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="callout bad"><strong>Never:</strong> soak it, dishwasher it, or leave it wet on a counter. Warping and splitting start there.</div><details class="acc"><summary>Deodorize &amp; sanitize</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Sprinkle coarse salt, then rub with half a lemon. Salt scrubs, acid lifts odor.</li><li>Rinse briefly and dry immediately with a towel.</li><li>Stand it on edge so both faces get airflow.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Oil &amp; wax routine</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Warm a food-safe mineral oil or a beeswax board butter.</li><li>Rub a thin coat along the grain with a lint-free cloth.</li><li>Let it sit 20 minutes, then buff off the excess.</li><li>Repeat until the wood stops drinking. Monthly for daily users.</li></ol></div></details><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Oil cadence</span><span class="value">4&ndash;6<span class="unit">wks</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Full dry</span><span class="value">12&ndash;24<span class="unit">h</span></span></div></div><p class="dim t-small">Deep cracks or a delaminated board? Retire it to a shop rag. No oil will glue it back structurally.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page stack"><h2>Wooden Cutting Board Rescue</h2><p class="muted">Wood self-heals if you feed it. Grey, cracked boards come back with oil and patience, .</p><div class="callout bad"><strong>Never:</strong> soak it, dishwasher it, or leave it wet on a counter. Warping and splitting start there.</div><details class="acc"><summary>Deodorize &amp; sanitize</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Sprinkle coarse salt, then rub with half a lemon. Salt scrubs, acid lifts odor.</li><li>Rinse briefly and dry immediately with a towel.</li><li>Stand it on edge so both faces get airflow.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Oil &amp; wax routine</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Warm a food-safe mineral oil or a beeswax board butter.</li><li>Rub a thin coat along the grain with a lint-free cloth.</li><li>Let it sit 20 minutes, then buff off the excess.</li><li>Repeat until the wood stops drinking. Monthly for daily users.</li></ol></div></details><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Oil cadence</span><span class="value">4&ndash;6<span class="unit">wks</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Full dry</span><span class="value">12&ndash;24<span class="unit">h</span></span></div></div><p class="dim t-small">Deep cracks or a delaminated board? Retire it to a shop rag. No oil will glue it back structurally.</p></div>	-
33.37.1	component_spec/Rescue Dry-erase Markers	<div class="umi-page stack"><h2>Rescue Dry-erase Markers</h2><p class="muted">Half the markers binned each year have a season of life left. Two minutes brings most back, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 320 40" role="img" aria-label="Bar showing ink recovered over time"><rect x="0" y="28" width="80" height="12" class="bar"></rect><rect x="90" y="18" width="80" height="22" class="bar alt"></rect><rect x="180" y="6" width="80" height="34" class="bar"></rect><text x="0" y="12" class="lbl">t=0</text><text x="90" y="12" class="lbl">2 min</text><text x="180" y="12" class="lbl">10 min</text></svg></div><details class="acc"><summary>Method 1 &mdash; Cap soak</summary><div class="body"><p>Stand the marker tip-down in an inch of rubbing alcohol for 30 seconds. Cap it, then rest it tip-down for a few minutes. The alcohol pulls ink back into the nib.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Method 2 &mdash; Jar trick</summary><div class="body"><p>Put a splash of water in a sealed jar, drop the markers in tip-up, and leave them overnight. The humidity loosens dried ink.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Method 3 &mdash; Nib flip</summary><div class="body"><p>Pull the nib with pliers, flip or replace it, and drop ink on the inner wick. Messy, but a last resort that works.</p></div></details><div class="callout info"><strong>Prevention:</strong> cap fully, store flat, and never lay a marker uncapped on a board all day.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page stack"><h2>Rescue Dry-erase Markers</h2><p class="muted">Half the markers binned each year have a season of life left. Two minutes brings most back, .</p><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 320 40" role="img" aria-label="Bar showing ink recovered over time"><rect x="0" y="28" width="80" height="12" class="bar"></rect><rect x="90" y="18" width="80" height="22" class="bar alt"></rect><rect x="180" y="6" width="80" height="34" class="bar"></rect><text x="0" y="12" class="lbl">t=0</text><text x="90" y="12" class="lbl">2 min</text><text x="180" y="12" class="lbl">10 min</text></svg></div><details class="acc"><summary>Method 1 &mdash; Cap soak</summary><div class="body"><p>Stand the marker tip-down in an inch of rubbing alcohol for 30 seconds. Cap it, then rest it tip-down for a few minutes. The alcohol pulls ink back into the nib.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Method 2 &mdash; Jar trick</summary><div class="body"><p>Put a splash of water in a sealed jar, drop the markers in tip-up, and leave them overnight. The humidity loosens dried ink.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Method 3 &mdash; Nib flip</summary><div class="body"><p>Pull the nib with pliers, flip or replace it, and drop ink on the inner wick. Messy, but a last resort that works.</p></div></details><div class="callout info"><strong>Prevention:</strong> cap fully, store flat, and never lay a marker uncapped on a board all day.</div></div>	-
33.38.1	component_spec/Unstick a Stuck Zipper	<div class="card"><h4>Unstick a Stuck Zipper</h4><p class="muted">Props: none</p><p>Zipper stuck? Don't yank it. Try these steps:</p><ol class="list"><li>Hold the fabric flat; gently wiggle the pull tab side to side.</li><li>If it moves a little, apply a graphite pencil to the teeth.</li><li>Still stuck? Rub with a dry bar of soap or a candle stub.</li><li>For metal zippers, a cotton swab with rubbing alcohol can dissolve gunk.</li></ol><p class="muted">Never force it—you'll break the teeth. Patience wins.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Unstick a Stuck Zipper</h4><p class="muted">Props: none</p><p>Zipper stuck? Don't yank it. Try these steps:</p><ol class="list"><li>Hold the fabric flat; gently wiggle the pull tab side to side.</li><li>If it moves a little, apply a graphite pencil to the teeth.</li><li>Still stuck? Rub with a dry bar of soap or a candle stub.</li><li>For metal zippers, a cotton swab with rubbing alcohol can dissolve gunk.</li></ol><p class="muted">Never force it—you'll break the teeth. Patience wins.</p></div>	-
33.39.1	component_spec/Quiet a Squeaky Door Hinge	<div class="card"><h4>Quiet a Squeaky Door Hinge</h4><p class="muted">Props: none</p><p>That squeak is metal grinding on metal. Silence it:</p><ul class="list"><li>Wipe the hinge with a damp cloth to remove grit.</li><li>Apply a drop of lubricant (WD-40, silicone spray, or even cooking oil) to the top of each hinge pin.</li><li>Open and close the door a few times to work it in.</li><li>Wipe excess to avoid drips.</li></ul><p class="muted">No lubricant? Rub a pencil lead on the pin.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h4>Quiet a Squeaky Door Hinge</h4><p class="muted">Props: none</p><p>That squeak is metal grinding on metal. Silence it:</p><ul class="list"><li>Wipe the hinge with a damp cloth to remove grit.</li><li>Apply a drop of lubricant (WD-40, silicone spray, or even cooking oil) to the top of each hinge pin.</li><li>Open and close the door a few times to work it in.</li><li>Wipe excess to avoid drips.</li></ul><p class="muted">No lubricant? Rub a pencil lead on the pin.</p></div>	-
34.0.0	http_status_table/HTTP Status Codes Cheat Sheet	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">HTTP status codes <span class="badge accent">quick ref</span></div><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>200</td><td>OK</td></tr><tr><td>201</td><td>Created</td></tr><tr><td>204</td><td>No Content</td></tr><tr><td>301</td><td>Moved Permanently</td></tr><tr><td>302</td><td>Found (temporary redirect)</td></tr><tr><td>304</td><td>Not Modified</td></tr><tr><td>400</td><td>Bad Request</td></tr><tr><td>401</td><td>Unauthorized</td></tr><tr><td>403</td><td>Forbidden</td></tr><tr><td>404</td><td>Not Found</td></tr><tr><td>405</td><td>Method Not Allowed</td></tr><tr><td>409</td><td>Conflict</td></tr><tr><td>418</td><td>I'm a teapot</td></tr><tr><td>429</td><td>Too Many Requests</td></tr><tr><td>500</td><td>Internal Server Error</td></tr><tr><td>502</td><td>Bad Gateway</td></tr><tr><td>503</td><td>Service Unavailable</td></tr><tr><td>504</td><td>Gateway Timeout</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="muted t-small">Handy when the network tab throws numbers at you. <regex>user_name</regex></p></div>	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">HTTP status codes <span class="badge accent">quick ref</span></div><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>200</td><td>OK</td></tr><tr><td>201</td><td>Created</td></tr><tr><td>204</td><td>No Content</td></tr><tr><td>301</td><td>Moved Permanently</td></tr><tr><td>302</td><td>Found (temporary redirect)</td></tr><tr><td>304</td><td>Not Modified</td></tr><tr><td>400</td><td>Bad Request</td></tr><tr><td>401</td><td>Unauthorized</td></tr><tr><td>403</td><td>Forbidden</td></tr><tr><td>404</td><td>Not Found</td></tr><tr><td>405</td><td>Method Not Allowed</td></tr><tr><td>409</td><td>Conflict</td></tr><tr><td>418</td><td>I'm a teapot</td></tr><tr><td>429</td><td>Too Many Requests</td></tr><tr><td>500</td><td>Internal Server Error</td><