34.1.0	http_status_table/HTTP Status Codes, Simply	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">HTTP status codes, simply</div><p class="muted">Servers talk in numbers. Here's the gist.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>200</td><td>All good, here's your stuff</td></tr><tr><td>301</td><td>Moved forever, update your links</td></tr><tr><td>304</td><td>Nothing changed, use your cache</td></tr><tr><td>400</td><td>Your request was malformed</td></tr><tr><td>401</td><td>Who are you? Log in</td></tr><tr><td>403</td><td>I know who you are, but no</td></tr><tr><td>404</td><td>Can't find that page</td></tr><tr><td>418</td><td>I'm a teapot (an Easter egg)</td></tr><tr><td>429</td><td>Slow down, too many requests</td></tr><tr><td>500</td><td>Something broke on the server</td></tr><tr><td>503</td><td>Server's taking a nap, try later</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="muted t-small"><regex>user_name</regex> 2xx good, 3xx redirect, 4xx you, 5xx them.</p></div>	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">HTTP status codes, simply</div><p class="muted">Servers talk in numbers. Here's the gist.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>200</td><td>All good, here's your stuff</td></tr><tr><td>301</td><td>Moved forever, update your links</td></tr><tr><td>304</td><td>Nothing changed, use your cache</td></tr><tr><td>400</td><td>Your request was malformed</td></tr><tr><td>401</td><td>Who are you? Log in</td></tr><tr><td>403</td><td>I know who you are, but no</td></tr><tr><td>404</td><td>Can't find that page</td></tr><tr><td>418</td><td>I'm a teapot (an Easter egg)</td></tr><tr><td>429</td><td>Slow down, too many requests</td></tr><tr><td>500</td><td>Something broke on the server</td></tr><tr><td>503</td><td>Server's taking a nap, try later</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="muted t-small"> 2xx good, 3xx redirect, 4xx you, 5xx them.</p></div>	-
34.2.0	http_status_table/401 vs 403: Auth vs Permission	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">401 vs 403</div><p class="muted">One is "who are you?", the other is "I know you, but no."</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>401</td><td>Unauthorized — you're not authenticated (log in)</td></tr><tr><td>403</td><td>Forbidden — you're authenticated but not allowed</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout info">401 often includes a <code>WWW-Authenticate</code> header. 403 means don't bother retrying with the same credentials.</div><p class="muted t-small"><regex>user_name</regex> easy to mix up. 401 = missing key. 403 = wrong key.</p></div>	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">401 vs 403</div><p class="muted">One is "who are you?", the other is "I know you, but no."</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>401</td><td>Unauthorized — you're not authenticated (log in)</td></tr><tr><td>403</td><td>Forbidden — you're authenticated but not allowed</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout info">401 often includes a <code>WWW-Authenticate</code> header. 403 means don't bother retrying with the same credentials.</div><p class="muted t-small"> easy to mix up. 401 = missing key. 403 = wrong key.</p></div>	-
34.3.0	http_status_table/2xx Success Codes	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">2xx — success</div><p class="muted">The server did the thing. Here's the nuance.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>200</td><td>OK — standard success</td></tr><tr><td>201</td><td>Created — new resource made (send Location header)</td></tr><tr><td>202</td><td>Accepted — queued for processing</td></tr><tr><td>203</td><td>Non-Authoritative Information — proxy modified response</td></tr><tr><td>204</td><td>No Content — success, but nothing to return</td></tr><tr><td>205</td><td>Reset Content — clear the form</td></tr><tr><td>206</td><td>Partial Content — range request fulfilled</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="muted t-small"><regex>user_name</regex> 204 is your friend for DELETE.</p></div>	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">2xx — success</div><p class="muted">The server did the thing. Here's the nuance.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>200</td><td>OK — standard success</td></tr><tr><td>201</td><td>Created — new resource made (send Location header)</td></tr><tr><td>202</td><td>Accepted — queued for processing</td></tr><tr><td>203</td><td>Non-Authoritative Information — proxy modified response</td></tr><tr><td>204</td><td>No Content — success, but nothing to return</td></tr><tr><td>205</td><td>Reset Content — clear the form</td></tr><tr><td>206</td><td>Partial Content — range request fulfilled</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="muted t-small"> 204 is your friend for DELETE.</p></div>	-
34.4.0	http_status_table/3xx Redirect Codes	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">3xx — redirects</div><p class="muted">Where to go next. Some are permanent, some are not.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>300</td><td>Multiple Choices — pick one</td></tr><tr><td>301</td><td>Moved Permanently — update your bookmarks</td></tr><tr><td>302</td><td>Found — temporary redirect (may change)</td></tr><tr><td>303</td><td>See Other — GET the other URL</td></tr><tr><td>304</td><td>Not Modified — use cached version</td></tr><tr><td>307</td><td>Temporary Redirect — keeps method and body</td></tr><tr><td>308</td><td>Permanent Redirect — keeps method and body</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout warn">307 and 308 preserve the HTTP method. 301/302 sometimes get rewritten to GET.</div><p class="muted t-small"><regex>user_name</regex> SEO rule of thumb: use 301 for permanent moves.</p></div>	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">3xx — redirects</div><p class="muted">Where to go next. Some are permanent, some are not.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>300</td><td>Multiple Choices — pick one</td></tr><tr><td>301</td><td>Moved Permanently — update your bookmarks</td></tr><tr><td>302</td><td>Found — temporary redirect (may change)</td></tr><tr><td>303</td><td>See Other — GET the other URL</td></tr><tr><td>304</td><td>Not Modified — use cached version</td></tr><tr><td>307</td><td>Temporary Redirect — keeps method and body</td></tr><tr><td>308</td><td>Permanent Redirect — keeps method and body</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout warn">307 and 308 preserve the HTTP method. 301/302 sometimes get rewritten to GET.</div><p class="muted t-small"> SEO rule of thumb: use 301 for permanent moves.</p></div>	-
34.5.0	http_status_table/4xx Client Error Codes	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">4xx — client errors</div><p class="muted">The request had a problem. Usually fixable on your end.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>400</td><td>Bad Request — malformed syntax</td></tr><tr><td>401</td><td>Unauthorized — authentication required</td></tr><tr><td>403</td><td>Forbidden — no permission</td></tr><tr><td>404</td><td>Not Found — no such resource</td></tr><tr><td>405</td><td>Method Not Allowed — wrong verb</td></tr><tr><td>406</td><td>Not Acceptable — can't satisfy Accept header</td></tr><tr><td>408</td><td>Request Timeout — took too long</td></tr><tr><td>409</td><td>Conflict — state clash (e.g., duplicate)</td></tr><tr><td>410</td><td>Gone — permanently removed</td></tr><tr><td>413</td><td>Payload Too Large</td></tr><tr><td>415</td><td>Unsupported Media Type</td></tr><tr><td>418</td><td>I'm a teapot — joke code from RFC 2324</td></tr><tr><td>422</td><td>Unprocessable Entity — semantic errors</td></tr><tr><td>429</td><td>Too Many Requests — rate limited</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="muted t-small"><regex>user_name</regex> 429 means back off, maybe check Retry-After.</p></div>	<div class="card"><div class="card-title">4xx — client errors</div><p class="muted">The request had a problem. Usually fixable on your end.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>400</td><td>Bad Request — malformed syntax</td></tr><tr><td>401</td><td>Unauthorized — authentication required</td></tr><tr><td>403</td><td>Forbidden — no permission</td></tr><tr><td>404</td><td>Not Found — no such resource</td></tr><tr><td>405</td><td>Method Not Allowed — wrong verb</td></tr><tr><td>406</td><td>Not Acceptable — can't satisfy Accept header</td></tr><tr><td>408</td><td>Request Timeout — took too long</td></tr><tr><td>409</td><td>Conflict — state clash (e.g., duplicate)</td></tr><tr><td>410</td><td>Gone — permanently removed</td></tr><tr><td>413</td><td>Payload Too Large</td></tr><tr><td>415</td><td>Unsupported Media Type</td></tr><tr><td>418</td><td>I'm a teapot — joke code from RFC 2324</td></tr><tr><td>422</td><td>Unprocessable Entity — semantic errors</td></tr><tr><td>429</td><td>Too Many Requests — rate limited</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="muted t-small"> 429 means back off, maybe check Retry-After.</p></div>	-
