35.25.0	html_cheat_sheet/Home Coffee Brewing Cheat Sheet	<div class="umi-page">  <div class="card">    <div class="card-title">Better Coffee, Fewer Guesses</div>    <p class="muted">Hi <regex>user_name</regex> — coffee is just ratios and temperatures you can actually control. Start here, then tweak one thing at a time.</p>    <div class="grid two">      <div class="stat"><div class="label">Golden ratio</div><div class="value">1:16 <span class="unit">coffee:water</span></div></div>      <div class="stat"><div class="label">Water temp</div><div class="value">92–96 <span class="unit">°C</span></div></div>    </div>    <div class="table-wrap">      <table>        <thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Grind</th><th>Time</th></tr></thead>        <tbody>          <tr><td>French press</td><td>Coarse</td><td>4 min</td></tr>          <tr><td>Pour over</td><td>Medium</td><td>3 min</td></tr>          <tr><td>Drip machine</td><td>Medium</td><td>Auto</td></tr>          <tr><td>Espresso</td><td>Fine</td><td>25–30 s</td></tr>        </tbody>      </table>    </div>    <details class="acc">      <summary>Taste fixer</summary>      <div class="body">        <ul class="list">          <li><strong>Sour, thin?</strong> Grind finer or brew longer.</li>          <li><strong>Bitter, harsh?</strong> Grind coarser or brew shorter.</li>          <li><strong>Weak?</strong> More coffee, not more time.</li>        </ul>      </div>    </details>    <blockquote class="quote">Change one variable at a time. Otherwise you're just stirring.<cite>— every barista, ever</cite></blockquote>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <div class="card">    <div class="card-title">Better Coffee, Fewer Guesses</div>    <p class="muted">Hi  — coffee is just ratios and temperatures you can actually control. Start here, then tweak one thing at a time.</p>    <div class="grid two">      <div class="stat"><div class="label">Golden ratio</div><div class="value">1:16 <span class="unit">coffee:water</span></div></div>      <div class="stat"><div class="label">Water temp</div><div class="value">92–96 <span class="unit">°C</span></div></div>    </div>    <div class="table-wrap">      <table>        <thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Grind</th><th>Time</th></tr></thead>        <tbody>          <tr><td>French press</td><td>Coarse</td><td>4 min</td></tr>          <tr><td>Pour over</td><td>Medium</td><td>3 min</td></tr>          <tr><td>Drip machine</td><td>Medium</td><td>Auto</td></tr>          <tr><td>Espresso</td><td>Fine</td><td>25–30 s</td></tr>        </tbody>      </table>    </div>    <details class="acc">      <summary>Taste fixer</summary>      <div class="body">        <ul class="list">          <li><strong>Sour, thin?</strong> Grind finer or brew longer.</li>          <li><strong>Bitter, harsh?</strong> Grind coarser or brew shorter.</li>          <li><strong>Weak?</strong> More coffee, not more time.</li>        </ul>      </div>    </details>    <blockquote class="quote">Change one variable at a time. Otherwise you're just stirring.<cite>— every barista, ever</cite></blockquote>  </div></div>	-
35.26.0	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Budgeting Cheat Sheet	<div class="umi-page">  <div class="card">    <div class="card-title">The Budget That Fits Your Brain</div>    <p class="muted">Well hello, <regex>user_name</regex>. Budgets fail when they're too clever. These three buckets are boring on purpose.</p>    <div class="grid two">      <div class="stat"><div class="label">Needs</div><div class="value">50<span class="unit">%</span></div></div>      <div class="stat"><div class="label">Wants</div><div class="value">30<span class="unit">%</span></div></div>      <div class="stat"><div class="label">Save / debt</div><div class="value">20<span class="unit">%</span></div></div>      <div class="stat"><div class="label">Emergency fund goal</div><div class="value">3–6 <span class="unit">months</span></div></div>    </div>    <details class="acc">      <summary>What goes where</summary>      <div class="body">        <ul class="list">          <li><strong>Needs:</strong> rent, groceries, transport, minimum debt payments.</li>          <li><strong>Wants:</strong> takeout, subscriptions, hobbies, the nice cheese.</li>          <li><strong>Save / debt:</strong> emergency fund, extra debt, sinking funds for big bills.</li>        </ul>      </div>    </details>    <details class="acc">      <summary>Sinking fund, plain words</summary>      <div class="body">        <p>Money you set aside monthly for a known future bill — car insurance, holidays, new tires. Divide the cost by the months until it's due, and save that.</p>      </div>    </details>    <div class="callout good">Start with one number: whatever you can save this month. Five dollars counts.</div>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <div class="card">    <div class="card-title">The Budget That Fits Your Brain</div>    <p class="muted">Well hello, . Budgets fail when they're too clever. These three buckets are boring on purpose.</p>    <div class="grid two">      <div class="stat"><div 