35.30.0	html_cheat_sheet/Great Outdoors Hiking Prep Cheat Sheet	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Hiking Prep Cheat Sheet</h2><p class="muted">Hey <regex>user_name</regex>, gear you never use still weighs you down. Pack light, then thank yourself at the summit.</p><div class="card"><details class="acc" open><summary>The 10 Essentials</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Water — 0.5 L per hour of walking</li><li>Extra food — one more snack than you think</li><li>Map + compass (offline maps on phone as backup)</li><li>Headlamp with fresh batteries</li><li>Sun protection: hat, sunscreen, sunglasses</li><li>First aid kit + any personal meds</li><li>Knife or multi-tool</li><li>Fire: lighter + waterproof matches</li><li>Emergency shelter — space blanket</li><li>Extra layers</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Weather Rules</summary><div class="body"><p>Check forecast twice: night before and morning of. Storms build after noon in mountains — plan to turn around by early afternoon. Add 10°F to your valley temp? No — subtract 3.5°F per 1,000 ft of elevation.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Trail Etiquette</summary><div class="body"><p>Uphill hikers get the right of way. Step aside, smile, done. Pack out every wrapper. Leave what you find.</p></div></details></div><div class="callout info"><strong>Before you go:</strong> share your route + return time with someone staying home. It takes 30 seconds and matters.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Hiking Prep Cheat Sheet</h2><p class="muted">Hey , gear you never use still weighs you down. Pack light, then thank yourself at the summit.</p><div class="card"><details class="acc" open><summary>The 10 Essentials</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Water — 0.5 L per hour of walking</li><li>Extra food — one more snack than you think</li><li>Map + compass (offline maps on phone as backup)</li><li>Headlamp with fresh batteries</li><li>Sun protection: hat, sunscreen, sunglasses</li><li>First aid kit + any personal meds</li><li>Knife or multi-tool</li><li>Fire: lighter + waterproof matches</li><li>Emergency shelter — space blanket</li><li>Extra layers</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Weather Rules</summary><div class="body"><p>Check forecast twice: night before and morning of. Storms build after noon in mountains — plan to turn around by early afternoon. Add 10°F to your valley temp? No — subtract 3.5°F per 1,000 ft of elevation.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Trail Etiquette</summary><div class="body"><p>Uphill hikers get the right of way. Step aside, smile, done. Pack out every wrapper. Leave what you find.</p></div></details></div><div class="callout info"><strong>Before you go:</strong> share your route + return time with someone staying home. It takes 30 seconds and matters.</div></div>	-
35.31.0	html_cheat_sheet/Car Tire Care Cheat Sheet	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Tire Care Cheat Sheet</h2><p class="muted">Tires are the only thing between <regex>user_name</regex> and asphalt. Give them five minutes a month.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><div class="label">Min tread depth</div><div class="value">2<span class="unit">/32 in</span></div></div><div class="stat"><div class="label">Replace at</div><div class="value">4<span class="unit">/32 in</span></div></div><div class="stat"><div class="label">Pressure check</div><div class="value">Monthly</div></div><div class="stat"><div class="label">Rotation</div><div class="value">6k<span class="unit"> mi</span></div></div></div><div class="card"><details class="acc" open><summary>Finding the right PSI</summary><div class="body"><p>Not on the tire sidewall — that's the max. Look on the sticker inside the driver's door jamb. Cold pressure only: check before driving or 3+ hours after.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>The penny test</summary><div class="body"><p>Stick Lincoln head-down into a tread groove. If you can see the top of his head, you're below 2/32 — replace now, especially before rain season.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Signs of trouble</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Bulges or bubbles — replace immediately</li><li>Uneven wear — alignment or pressure issue</li><li>Vibration at speed — balance needed</li><li>Cracks in sidewall — age, not mileage</li></ul></div></details></div><div class="callout warn">Tires age out too. Six to ten years from the DOT date code (last 4 digits on sidewall) is the general ceiling, even with tread left.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Tire Care Cheat Sheet</h2><p class="muted">Tires are the only thing between  and asphalt. Give them five minutes a month.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><div class="label">Min tread depth</div><div class="value">2<span class="unit">/32 in</span></div></div><div class="stat"><div class="label">Replace at</div><div class="value">4<span class="unit">/32 in</span></div></div><div class="stat"><div class="label">Pressure check</div><div class="value">Monthly</div></div><div class="stat"><div class="label">Rotation</div><div class="value">6k<span class="unit"> mi</span></div></div></div><div class="card"><details class="acc" open><summary>Finding the right PSI</summary><div class="body"><p>Not on the tire sidewall — that's the max. Look on the sticker inside the driver's door jamb. Cold pressure only: check before driving or 3+ hours after.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>The penny test</summary><div class="body"><p>Stick Lincoln head-down into a tread groove. If you can see the top of his head, you're below 2/32 — replace now, especially before rain season.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Signs of trouble</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Bulges or bubbles — replace immediately</li><li>Uneven wear — alignment or pressure issue</li><li>Vibration at speed — balance needed</li><li>Cracks in sidewall — age, not mileage</li></ul></div></details></div><div class="callout warn">Tires age out too. Six to ten years from the DOT date code (last 4 digits on sidewall) is the general ceiling, even with tread left.</div></div>	-
35.32.0	html_cheat_sheet/Basic Home Plumbing Fixes Cheat Sheet	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Home Plumbing Cheat Sheet</h2><p class="muted">Most leaks are one washer away from fixed, <regex>user_name</regex>. Here's where to start.</p><div class="card"><details class="acc" open><summary>Before anything: shut off the water</summary><div class="body"><p>Fixture shutoff valves sit under sinks and behind toilets. Whole-house valve is usually near the meter or where the main line enters. Turn clockwise to close. Open a faucet to drain pressure.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Clogged drain</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Boiling water first — clears grease</li><li>Plunger with a good seal — 15–20 firm pushes</li><li>Drain snake through the trap arm</li><li>Skip chemical drain cleaner: it eats pipes too</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Dripping faucet</summary><div class="body"><p>Usually a worn cartridge or O-ring. Shut off supply, plug the drain so tiny parts don't vanish, and photograph each layer as you disassemble. Take the old part to the hardware store to match it.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Running toilet</summary><div class="body"><p>Ninety percent of the time it's the flapper. Cheap, five-minute swap. Test: drop food coloring in the tank; if color shows in the bowl without flushing, the flapper is leaking.</p></div></details></div><div class="callout bad">Call a pro for: gas water heaters, anything behind a wall, sewage backups, and burst pipes. Not worth the DIY badge.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Home Plumbing Cheat Sheet</h2><p class="muted">Most leaks are one washer away from fixed, . Here's where to start.</p><div class="card"><details class="acc" open><summary>Before anything: shut off the water</summary><div class="body"><p>Fixture shutoff valves sit under sinks and behind toilets. Whole-house valve is usually near the meter or where the main line enters. Turn clockwise to close. Open a faucet to drain pressure.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Clogged drain</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Boiling water first — clears grease</li><li>Plunger with a good seal — 15–20 firm pushes</li><li>Drain snake through the trap arm</li><li>Skip chemical drain cleaner: it eats pipes too</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Dripping faucet</summary><div class="body"><p>Usually a worn cartridge or O-ring. Shut off supply, plug the drain so tiny parts don't vanish, and photograph each layer as you disassemble. Take the old part to the hardware store to match it.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Running toilet</summary><div class="body"><p>Ninety percent of the time it's the flapper. Cheap, five-minute swap. Test: drop food coloring in the tank; if color shows in the bowl without flushing, the flapper is leaking.</p></div></details></div><div class="callout bad">Call a pro for: gas water heaters, anything behind a wall, sewage backups, and burst pipes. Not worth the DIY badge.</div></div>	-
35.33.0	html_cheat_sheet/Board Game Night Cheat Sheet	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Board Game Night Cheat Sheet</h2><p class="muted">Good game night isn't about the games, <regex>user_name</regex>. It's about everyone leaving still friends.</p><div class="card"><details class="acc" open><summary>Picking games by group size</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li><strong>2–4 players:</strong> strategy depth — Catan, Ticket to Ride, Splendor</li><li><strong>4–6 players:</strong> social deduction — Coup, The Resistance</li><li><strong>6+ players:</strong> party games — Codenames, Wavelength, Just One</li><li><strong>Mixed skill:</strong> cooperative — Forbidden Island, Pandemic</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Teach a game in 3 minutes</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>State the goal in one sentence</li><li>Explain how a turn works</li><li>Name the win condition</li><li>Play one open practice round</li></ol><p class="muted">Skip edge cases. Nobody remembers rule seven anyway.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Etiquette that saves friendships</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>No phone scrolling during turns</li><li>Announce before touching someone's pieces</li><li>"I'm about to do something mean" buys goodwill</li><li>Losers pick the next game</li></ul></div></details></div><div class="callout good">Snacks: dry, one-handed, not greasy. Pretzels, grapes, popcorn in a bowl. Save the nachos for the halftime of a movie.</div><p class="t-small dim">Set a stopping time. Leaving at 10 with everyone happy beats leaving at 1 a.m. with grudges.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Board Game Night Cheat Sheet</h2><p class="muted">Good game night isn't about the games, . It's about everyone leaving still friends.</p><div class="card"><details class="acc" open><summary>Picking games by group size</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li><strong>2–4 players:</strong> strategy depth — Catan, Ticket to Ride, Splendor</li><li><strong>4–6 players:</strong> social deduction — Coup, The Resistance</li><li><strong>6+ players:</strong> party games — Codenames, Wavelength, Just One</li><li><strong>Mixed skill:</strong> cooperative — Forbidden Island, Pandemic</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Teach a game in 3 minutes</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>State the goal in one sentence</li><li>Explain how a turn works</li><li>Name the win condition</li><li>Play one open practice round</li></ol><p class="muted">Skip edge cases. Nobody remembers rule seven anyway.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Etiquette that saves friendships</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>No phone scrolling during turns</li><li>Announce before touching someone's pieces</li><li>"I'm about to do something mean" buys goodwill</li><li>Losers pick the next game</li></ul></div></details></div><div class="callout good">Snacks: dry, one-handed, not greasy. Pretzels, grapes, popcorn in a bowl. Save the nachos for the halftime of a movie.</div><p class="t-small dim">Set a stopping time. Leaving at 10 with everyone happy beats leaving at 1 a.m. with grudges.</p></div>	-
35.34.0	html_cheat_sheet/Wedding Guest Etiquette Cheat Sheet	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Wedding Guest Cheat Sheet</h2><p class="muted">Turns out the whole job is: show up, dress right, don't make it about you. <regex>user_name</regex>, here's the rest.</p><div class="card"><details class="acc" open><summary>Dress codes, decoded</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li><strong>Black tie:</strong> floor-length gown; tuxedo</li><li><strong>Cocktail:</strong> knee-length dress; suit, no tie needed</li><li><strong>Semiformal:</strong> dress or dressy separates; sport coat</li><li><strong>Casual:</strong> still no jeans with rips. Think smart-casual.</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Gift math (general US norm)</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Coworker / distant friend: $50–75</li><li>Close friend or relative: $100–150</li><li>Partner included: aim toward the higher end</li><li>Destination wedding: your presence is often the gift</li></ul><p class="muted">Off-registry? Cash in a card works everywhere.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Hard rules</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>No white, cream, or ivory unless asked</li><li>RSVP by the date, even if it's no</li><li>Don't bring a plus-one who wasn't invited</li><li>Phones away during the ceremony</li><li>Don't post photos before the couple does</li></ul></div></details></div><div class="callout info">Arrive 15 minutes early. Late entrance during vows is the one memory guests make sure to keep.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Wedding Guest Cheat Sheet</h2><p class="muted">Turns out the whole job is: show up, dress right, don't make it about you. , here's the rest.</p><div class="card"><details class="acc" open><summary>Dress codes, decoded</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li><strong>Black tie:</strong> floor-length gown; tuxedo</li><li><strong>Cocktail:</strong> knee-length dress; suit, no tie needed</li><li><strong>Semiformal:</strong> dress or dressy separates; sport coat</li><li><strong>Casual:</strong> still no jeans with rips. Think smart-casual.</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Gift math (general US norm)</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Coworker / distant friend: $50–75</li><li>Close friend or relative: $100–150</li><li>Partner included: aim toward the higher end</li><li>Destination wedding: your presence is often the gift</li></ul><p class="muted">Off-registry? Cash in a card works everywhere.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Hard rules</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>No white, cream, or ivory unless asked</li><li>RSVP by the date, even if it's no</li><li>Don't bring a plus-one who wasn't invited</li><li>Phones away during the ceremony</li><li>Don't post photos before the couple does</li></ul></div></details></div><div class="callout info">Arrive 15 minutes early. Late entrance during vows is the one memory guests make sure to keep.</div></div>	-
35.35.0	html_cheat_sheet/Home Fire Safety Cheat Sheet	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Home Fire Safety Cheat Sheet</h2><p class="muted">Two minutes of planning beats twenty of panic, <regex>user_name</regex>. Keep it simple.</p><div class="card"><details class="acc" open><summary>Smoke alarms</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>One in every bedroom, outside each sleeping area, and on every level</li><li>Test monthly — press and hold the button</li><li>Replace batteries yearly; replace the whole unit every 10 years</li><li>Interconnected alarms wake everyone faster</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Extinguishers: the PASS method</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li><strong>P</strong>ull the pin</li><li><strong>A</strong>im at the base of the fire</li><li><strong>S</strong>queeze the handle</li><li><strong>S</strong>weep side to side</li></ul><p class="muted">Only if the fire is small, contained, and you have a clear exit behind you.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Escape plan</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Two ways out of every room</li><li>Pick one outdoor meeting spot</li><li>Practice at night, twice a year</li><li>Sleep with bedroom doors closed — it slows smoke</li></ul></div></details></div><div class="callout bad">Get out, stay out, call for help. Never go back inside — not for pets, not for photos, not for anything.</div><svg viewBox="0 0 300 40" class="chart" aria-label="Fire extinguisher types"><text x="4" y="16" class="lbl">A — trash, wood, paper</text><text x="4" y="32" class="lbl">B — grease, gas</text><text x="150" y="16" class="lbl">C — electrical</text><text x="150" y="32" class="lbl">K — kitchen oils</text></svg></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Home Fire Safety Cheat Sheet</h2><p class="muted">Two minutes of planning beats twenty of panic, . Keep it simple.</p><div class="card"><details class="acc" open><summary>Smoke alarms</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>One in every bedroom, outside each sleeping area, and on every level</li><li>Test monthly — press and hold the button</li><li>Replace batteries yearly; replace the whole unit every 10 years</li><li>Interconnected alarms wake everyone faster</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Extinguishers: the PASS method</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li><strong>P</strong>ull the pin</li><li><strong>A</strong>im at the base of the fire</li><li><strong>S</strong>queeze the handle</li><li><strong>S</strong>weep side to side</li></ul><p class="muted">Only if the fire is small, contained, and you have a clear exit behind you.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Escape plan</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Two ways out of every room</li><li>Pick one outdoor meeting spot</li><li>Practice at night, twice a year</li><li>Sleep with bedroom doors closed — it slows smoke</li></ul></div></details></div><div class="callout bad">Get out, stay out, call for help. Never go back inside — not for pets, not for photos, not for anything.</div><svg viewBox="0 0 300 40" class="chart" aria-label="Fire extinguisher types"><text x="4" y="16" class="lbl">A — trash, wood, paper</text><text x="4" y="32" class="lbl">B — grease, gas</text><text x="150" y="16" class="lbl">C — electrical</text><text x="150" y="32" class="lbl">K — kitchen oils</text></svg></div>	-
35.36.0	html_cheat_sheet/Public Speaking Cheat Sheet	<p class="muted">Hi <regex>user_name</regex> — the room is friendlier than your nerves think.</p><div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Before you speak</h3>  <ul class="list">    <li><strong>Know your first 20 seconds cold.</strong> The rest rides along.</li>    <li><strong>Move once before you talk.</strong> Shake out your hands, roll your shoulders.</li>    <li><strong>Breathe 4 in, 6 out</strong> for four rounds. Slows the heart, steadies the voice.</li>    <li><strong>Arrive early and test the gear.</strong> Surprises belong in birthdays, not talks.</li>  </ul></div><details class="acc">  <summary>If your voice shakes mid-speech</summary>  <div class="body">    <p>Pause. Take a slow sip of water. Shaky voice is a symptom of adrenaline, not incompetence — and it reads far smaller to the audience than it feels to you. Land your next sentence slowly on a low pitch.</p>  </div></details><div class="callout good">  <p class="muted t-small">Pace check: about <strong>120–150 words per minute</strong> is comfortable. Slower than that, and people lean in.</p></div><svg viewBox="0 0 300 60" role="presentation" style="width:100%;height:auto;">  <rect x="10" y="34" width="70" height="14" rx="4" fill="var(--accent)"/>  <rect x="90" y="22" width="70" height="26" rx="4" fill="var(--accent-soft)"/>  <rect x="170" y="12" width="70" height="36" rx="4" fill="var(--accent)"/>  <text x="10" y="10" class="lbl" fill="var(--ink-3)" font-size="10">soft start → strong finish</text></svg>	<p class="muted">Hi  — the room is friendlier than your nerves think.</p><div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Before you speak</h3>  <ul class="list">    <li><strong>Know your first 20 seconds cold.</strong> The rest rides along.</li>    <li><strong>Move once before you talk.</strong> Shake out your hands, roll your shoulders.</li>    <li><strong>Breathe 4 in, 6 out</strong> for four rounds. Slows the heart, steadies the voice.</li>    <li><strong>Arrive early and test the gear.</strong> Surprises belong in birthdays, not talks.</li>  </ul></div><details class="acc">  <summary>If your voice shakes mid-speech</summary>  <div class="body">    <p>Pause. Take a slow sip of water. Shaky voice is a symptom of adrenaline, not incompetence — and it reads far smaller to the audience than it feels to you. Land your next sentence slowly on a low pitch.</p>  </div></details><div class="callout good">  <p class="muted t-small">Pace check: about <strong>120–150 words per minute</strong> is comfortable. Slower than that, and people lean in.</p></div><svg viewBox="0 0 300 60" role="presentation" style="width:100%;height:auto;">  <rect x="10" y="34" width="70" height="14" rx="4" fill="var(--accent)"/>  <rect x="90" y="22" width="70" height="26" rx="4" fill="var(--accent-soft)"/>  <rect x="170" y="12" width="70" height="36" rx="4" fill="var(--accent)"/>  <text x="10" y="10" class="lbl" fill="var(--ink-3)" font-size="10">soft start → strong finish</text></svg>	-
35.37.0	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Photography Cheat Sheet	<p class="muted">The exposure triangle, minus the jargon, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="table-wrap">  <table>    <thead><tr><th>Dial</th><th>Low number</th><th>High number</th></tr></thead>    <tbody>      <tr><td><strong>Aperture (f/)</strong></td><td>More light, blurrier background</td><td>Less light, deeper focus</td></tr>      <tr><td><strong>Shutter</strong></td><td>Freezes motion</td><td>Blurs motion</td></tr>      <tr><td><strong>ISO</strong></td><td>Cleaner image</td><td>Brighter but noisier</td></tr>    </tbody>  </table></div><details class="acc">  <summary>Blurry photos — the usual suspects</summary>  <div class="body">    <ol class="list">      <li>Shutter slower than <strong>1/focal length</strong>. Rule of thumb: at 50mm, keep it at 1/50s or faster.</li>      <li>You're tapping the screen, not the shutter button. Use two hands and tuck your elbows in.</li>      <li>Autofocus is missing your subject. Tap on the face to lock it.</li>    </ol>  </div></details><div class="callout info">  <p class="muted t-small">Portrait mode on a phone is just fake aperture — great results, but keep edges of hair and glasses in mind. Straight-on backgrounds read cleanest.</p></div><details class="tab">  <summary>Golden hour times, in brief</summary>  <div class="body">    <p>The hour after sunrise and hour before sunset, both roughly. Light turns warm and low, shadows go soft, skin looks kind.</p>  </div></details>	<p class="muted">The exposure triangle, minus the jargon, .</p><div class="table-wrap">  <table>    <thead><tr><th>Dial</th><th>Low number</th><th>High number</th></tr></thead>    <tbody>      <tr><td><strong>Aperture (f/)</strong></td><td>More light, blurrier background</td><td>Less light, deeper focus</td></tr>      <tr><td><strong>Shutter</strong></td><td>Freezes motion</td><td>Blurs motion</td></tr>      <tr><td><strong>ISO</strong></td><td>Cleaner image</td><td>Brighter but noisier</td></tr>    </tbody>  </table></div><details class="acc">  <summary>Blurry photos — the usual suspects</summary>  <div class="body">    <ol class="list">      <li>Shutter slower than <strong>1/focal length</strong>. Rule of thumb: at 50mm, keep it at 1/50s or faster.</li>      <li>You're tapping the screen, not the shutter button. Use two hands and tuck your elbows in.</li>      <li>Autofocus is missing your subject. Tap on the face to lock it.</li>    </ol>  </div></details><div class="callout info">  <p class="muted t-small">Portrait mode on a phone is just fake aperture — great results, but keep edges of hair and glasses in mind. Straight-on backgrounds read cleanest.</p></div><details class="tab">  <summary>Golden hour times, in brief</summary>  <div class="body">    <p>The hour after sunrise and hour before sunset, both roughly. Light turns warm and low, shadows go soft, skin looks kind.</p>  </div></details>	-
35.38.0	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Comfort: Sciatica Relief Cheat Sheet	<p class="muted">A short, gentle guide — see a clinician if anything feels sharp or numb, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Daily movers</h3>  <ul class="list">    <li><strong>Standing extension.</strong> Hands on hips, lean back <em>slightly</em>, 10 reps. Yes, 10. Not 100.</li>    <li><strong>Figure-4 glute stretch.</strong> 30 sec each side, twice a day.</li>    <li><strong>Walk, don't sit.</strong> Short, frequent walks beat long sitting almost every time.</li>  </ul></div><details class="acc">  <summary>Better sitting, for the days you must</summary>  <div class="body">    <p>Feet flat, knees slightly below hips, one small folded towel under the lower back. Every 30 minutes, stand for 2 minutes — a kitchen timer is a perfectly legitimate medical device.</p>  </div></details><div class="callout warn">  <p class="t-small">See a doctor if you have numbness in the saddle area, weakness in both legs, or loss of bladder/bowel control. Those warrant urgent attention, not a stretch routine.</p></div><div class="grid two">  <div class="stat"><span class="label">Cycles per day</span><span class="value">2–3</span></div>  <div class="stat"><span class="label">Hold</span><span class="value">30<span class="unit">s</span></span></div></div>	<p class="muted">A short, gentle guide — see a clinician if anything feels sharp or numb, .</p><div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Daily movers</h3>  <ul class="list">    <li><strong>Standing extension.</strong> Hands on hips, lean back <em>slightly</em>, 10 reps. Yes, 10. Not 100.</li>    <li><strong>Figure-4 glute stretch.</strong> 30 sec each side, twice a day.</li>    <li><strong>Walk, don't sit.</strong> Short, frequent walks beat long sitting almost every time.</li>  </ul></div><details class="acc">  <summary>Better sitting, for the days you must</summary>  <div class="body">    <p>Feet flat, knees slightly below hips, one small folded towel under the lower back. Every 30 minutes, stand for 2 minutes — a kitchen timer is a perfectly legitimate medical device.</p>  </div></details><div class="callout warn">  <p class="t-small">See a doctor if you have numbness in the saddle area, weakness in both legs, or loss of bladder/bowel control. Those warrant urgent attention, not a stretch routine.</p></div><div class="grid two">  <div class="stat"><span class="label">Cycles per day</span><span class="value">2–3</span></div>  <div class="stat"><span class="label">Hold</span><span class="value">30<span class="unit">s</span></span></div></div>	-
35.39.0	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Cardio Cheat Sheet	<p class="muted">Keep it simple, <regex>user_name</regex>. The best cardio is the one you'll actually repeat tomorrow.</p><div class="card flat">  <h3 class="card-title">Heart-rate zones in plain terms</h3>  <ul class="list">    <li><strong>Zone 2 (60–70% max HR).</strong> Chatty pace. Builds endurance and a healthier engine.</li>    <li><strong>Zone 3 (70–80%).</strong> Talk in short sentences. The everyday workhorse.</li>    <li><strong>Zone 4–5 (80%+).</strong> Hard to talk. Use it sparingly — 2 sessions a week max.</li>  </ul></div><div class="callout good">  <p class="t-small">Rough max HR: 220 − your age. Estimates are just that, but they're good enough for planning.</p></div><details class="acc">  <summary>A simple week</summary>  <div class="body">    <ol class="list">      <li>3 easy sessions — 30–45 min at Zone 2</li>      <li>1 harder session — intervals, 20–25 min total</li>      <li>1 easy session or long walk</li>      <li>Rest, genuinely rest</li>    </ol>  </div></details><svg viewBox="0 0 300 70" role="presentation" style="width:100%;height:auto;">  <rect x="10" y="28" width="60" height="30" rx="4" fill="var(--accent-soft)"/>  <rect x="80" y="18" width="60" height="40" rx="4" fill="var(--accent)"/>  <rect x="150" y="8" width="60" height="50" rx="4" fill="var(--warn)"/>  <text x="10" y="64" class="lbl" fill="var(--ink-3)" font-size="10">easy · moderate · hard</text></svg>	<p class="muted">Keep it simple, . The best cardio is the one you'll actually repeat tomorrow.</p><div class="card flat">  <h3 class="card-title">Heart-rate zones in plain terms</h3>  <ul class="list">    <li><strong>Zone 2 (60–70% max HR).</strong> Chatty pace. Builds endurance and a healthier engine.</li>    <li><strong>Zone 3 (70–80%).</strong> Talk in short sentences. The everyday workhorse.</li>    <li><strong>Zone 4–5 (80%+).</strong> Hard to talk. Use it sparingly — 2 sessions a week max.</li>  </ul></div><div class="callout good">  <p class="t-small">Rough max HR: 220 − your age. Estimates are just that, but they're good enough for planning.</p></div><details class="acc">  <summary>A simple week</summary>  <div class="body">    <ol class="list">      <li>3 easy sessions — 30–45 min at Zone 2</li>      <li>1 harder session — intervals, 20–25 min total</li>      <li>1 easy session or long walk</li>      <li>Rest, genuinely rest</li>    </ol>  </div></details><svg viewBox="0 0 300 70" role="presentation" style="width:100%;height:auto;">  <rect x="10" y="28" width="60" height="30" rx="4" fill="var(--accent-soft)"/>  <rect x="80" y="18" width="60" height="40" rx="4" fill="var(--accent)"/>  <rect x="150" y="8" width="60" height="50" rx="4" fill="var(--warn)"/>  <text x="10" y="64" class="lbl" fill="var(--ink-3)" font-size="10">easy · moderate · hard</text></svg>	-
35.40.0	html_cheat_sheet/Gut Health & Fiber Cheat Sheet	<p class="muted">Fiber, fermented things, and a little patience — <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="grid two">  <div class="stat"><span class="label">Daily fiber</span><span class="value">25–30<span class="unit">g</span></span></div>  <div class="stat"><span class="label">Plant variety</span><span class="value">30<span class="unit">/wk</span></span></div></div><div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">High-fiber, no drama</h3>  <ul class="list">    <li>Beans and lentils — a can of black beans is ~15g.</li>    <li>Oats, barley, whole wheat bread.</li>    <li>Fruit with skin: apple, pear, berries.</li>    <li>Vegetables you'll actually cook: broccoli, carrots, squash.</li>    <li>Nuts and seeds — chia, flax, almonds.</li>  </ul></div><details class="acc">  <summary>Pre vs pro</summary>  <div class="body">    <p><strong>Prebiotics</strong> are food for your gut bacteria — garlic, onions, oats, bananas. <strong>Probiotics</strong> are live cultures — yogurt, kefir, kimchi, sauerkraut. They work best together, like a good dinner party.</p>  </div></details><div class="callout info">  <p class="t-small">Increase fiber <em>slowly</em> and drink more water. Doubling it overnight is a fine way to feel worse.</p></div>	<p class="muted">Fiber, fermented things, and a little patience — .</p><div class="grid two">  <div class="stat"><span class="label">Daily fiber</span><span class="value">25–30<span class="unit">g</span></span></div>  <div class="stat"><span class="label">Plant variety</span><span class="value">30<span class="unit">/wk</span></span></div></div><div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">High-fiber, no drama</h3>  <ul class="list">    <li>Beans and lentils — a can of black beans is ~15g.</li>    <li>Oats, barley, whole wheat bread.</li>    <li>Fruit with skin: apple, pear, berries.</li>    <li>Vegetables you'll actually cook: broccoli, carrots, squash.</li>    <li>Nuts and seeds — chia, flax, almonds.</li>  </ul></div><details class="acc">  <summary>Pre vs pro</summary>  <div class="body">    <p><strong>Prebiotics</strong> are food for your gut bacteria — garlic, onions, oats, bananas. <strong>Probiotics</strong> are live cultures — yogurt, kefir, kimchi, sauerkraut. They work best together, like a good dinner party.</p>  </div></details><div class="callout info">  <p class="t-small">Increase fiber <em>slowly</em> and drink more water. Doubling it overnight is a fine way to feel worse.</p></div>	-
35.41.0	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Skincare Cheat Sheet	<p class="muted">Less is more, <regex>user_name</regex>. Four steps carry most of the weight.</p><ol class="list">  <li><strong>Cleanse.</strong> Gentle, lukewarm water. Twice a day is plenty.</li>  <li><strong>Treat</strong> (optional). One active — retinol, vitamin C, or a BHA. Pick one, not three.</li>  <li><strong>Moisturize.</strong> Ceramide creams for dry skin, gels for oily.</li>  <li><strong>Sunscreen.</strong> SPF 30+, every morning, cloudy included.</li></ol><details class="acc">  <summary>Retinol, honestly</summary>  <div class="body">    <p>Start at 0.3% or lower, twice a week, at night. Pea-sized for the whole face. Expect a few weeks of dryness — that's the price of the good stuff. Don't layer it with strong acids on the same night.</p>  </div></details><div class="callout warn">  <p class="t-small">Stinging, redness that doesn't fade, or sudden breakouts after a new product? Stop it for two weeks and reintroduce one thing at a time.</p></div><div class="table-wrap">  <table>    <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Morning</th><th>Night</th></tr></thead>    <tbody>      <tr><td>Cleanse</td><td>Yeah</td><td>Yeah</td></tr>      <tr><td>Treat</td><td>Optional</td><td>Optional</td></tr>      <tr><td>Moisturize</td><td>Yeah</td><td>Yeah</td></tr>      <tr><td>SPF</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>    </tbody>  </table></div>	<p class="muted">Less is more, . Four steps carry most of the weight.</p><ol class="list">  <li><strong>Cleanse.</strong> Gentle, lukewarm water. Twice a day is plenty.</li>  <li><strong>Treat</strong> (optional). One active — retinol, vitamin C, or a BHA. Pick one, not three.</li>  <li><strong>Moisturize.</strong> Ceramide creams for dry skin, gels for oily.</li>  <li><strong>Sunscreen.</strong> SPF 30+, every morning, cloudy included.</li></ol><details class="acc">  <summary>Retinol, honestly</summary>  <div class="body">    <p>Start at 0.3% or lower, twice a week, at night. Pea-sized for the whole face. Expect a few weeks of dryness — that's the price of the good stuff. Don't layer it with strong acids on the same night.</p>  </div></details><div class="callout warn">  <p class="t-small">Stinging, redness that doesn't fade, or sudden breakouts after a new product? Stop it for two weeks and reintroduce one thing at a time.</p></div><div class="table-wrap">  <table>    <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Morning</th><th>Night</th></tr></thead>    <tbody>      <tr><td>Cleanse</td><td>Yeah</td><td>Yeah</td></tr>      <tr><td>Treat</td><td>Optional</td><td>Optional</td></tr>      <tr><td>Moisturize</td><td>Yeah</td><td>Yeah</td></tr>      <tr><td>SPF</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>    </tbody>  </table></div>	-
35.42.0	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Knife Sharpening Cheat Sheet	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Knife Sharpening, <regex>user_name</regex></h3><p class="muted t-small">Honing straightens. Sharpening removes metal. Do the first weekly, the second twice a year.</p><div class="callout info"><strong>Angle by knife:</strong> Western chef's knife ~20° per side, Japanese gyuto ~15°, paring ~20°.</div><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>What it does</th><th class="num">How often</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Honing steel</td><td>Realigns the edge</td><td class="num">Weekly</td></tr><tr><td>Whetstone (1000 grit)</td><td>Sharpens</td><td class="num">2×/year</td></tr><tr><td>6000 grit stone</td><td>Polishes</td><td class="num">Optional</td></tr><tr><td>Pull-through</td><td>Fast fix, chews blades</td><td class="num">Emergency</td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class="acc"><summary>Whetstone, step by step</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Soak the 1000-grit stone 10 min until bubbles stop.</li><li>Hold the blade at ~20°, edge facing you.</li><li>Push in overlapping strokes, 10 per side.</li><li>Feel for a burr on the far side, then flip.</li><li>Rinse, dry, strop on newspaper.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Quick test</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Slice a ripe tomato without pressing. If the skin resists, back to the stone.</p></div></details><p class="dim t-small">Dishwasher = slow death for any good blade. Hand wash, dry right away.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Knife Sharpening, </h3><p class="muted t-small">Honing straightens. Sharpening removes metal. Do the first weekly, the second twice a year.</p><div class="callout info"><strong>Angle by knife:</strong> Western chef's knife ~20° per side, Japanese gyuto ~15°, paring ~20°.</div><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>What it does</th><th class="num">How often</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Honing steel</td><td>Realigns the edge</td><td class="num">Weekly</td></tr><tr><td>Whetstone (1000 grit)</td><td>Sharpens</td><td class="num">2×/year</td></tr><tr><td>6000 grit stone</td><td>Polishes</td><td class="num">Optional</td></tr><tr><td>Pull-through</td><td>Fast fix, chews blades</td><td class="num">Emergency</td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class="acc"><summary>Whetstone, step by step</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Soak the 1000-grit stone 10 min until bubbles stop.</li><li>Hold the blade at ~20°, edge facing you.</li><li>Push in overlapping strokes, 10 per side.</li><li>Feel for a burr on the far side, then flip.</li><li>Rinse, dry, strop on newspaper.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Quick test</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Slice a ripe tomato without pressing. If the skin resists, back to the stone.</p></div></details><p class="dim t-small">Dishwasher = slow death for any good blade. Hand wash, dry right away.</p></div>	-
35.43.0	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Personal Email Triage Cheat Sheet	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Email Triage, <regex>user_name</regex></h3><p class="muted t-small">Touch each message once. Decide, don't linger.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Delete</span><span class="value">70<span class="unit">%</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Reply</span><span class="value">15<span class="unit">%</span></span></div></div><details class="acc" open><summary>The 4 Ds</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li><strong>Delete</strong> — newsletters you haven't opened in a month.</li><li><strong>Do</strong> — anything under 2 minutes, right now.</li><li><strong>Delegate</strong> — forward with one clear line about what you need.</li><li><strong>Defer</strong> — star it, set a time, move on.</li></ul></div></details><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Batch</th><th>When</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Morning sweep</td><td>9:00–9:20</td></tr><tr><td>Afternoon sweep</td><td>2:00–2:15</td></tr><tr><td>Deep clean</td><td>Fri, 30 min</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout warn">Notifications off. An inbox is a to-do list other people write for you — you pick when to read it.</div><p class="dim t-small">Unsubscribe beats delete. One click, forever gone.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Email Triage, </h3><p class="muted t-small">Touch each message once. Decide, don't linger.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Delete</span><span class="value">70<span class="unit">%</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Reply</span><span class="value">15<span class="unit">%</span></span></div></div><details class="acc" open><summary>The 4 Ds</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li><strong>Delete</strong> — newsletters you haven't opened in a month.</li><li><strong>Do</strong> — anything under 2 minutes, right now.</li><li><strong>Delegate</strong> — forward with one clear line about what you need.</li><li><strong>Defer</strong> — star it, set a time, move on.</li></ul></div></details><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Batch</th><th>When</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Morning sweep</td><td>9:00–9:20</td></tr><tr><td>Afternoon sweep</td><td>2:00–2:15</td></tr><tr><td>Deep clean</td><td>Fri, 30 min</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout warn">Notifications off. An inbox is a to-do list other people write for you — you pick when to read it.</div><p class="dim t-small">Unsubscribe beats delete. One click, forever gone.</p></div>	-
35.44.0	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Grocery Store Navigation Cheat Sheet	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Shop the Perimeter, <regex>user_name</regex></h3><p class="muted t-small">Most stores hide the good stuff on the outside loop. The middle is where budgets go to die.</p><svg class="chart" viewBox="0 0 200 120" role="img" aria-label="Grocery store loop diagram"><rect x="5" y="5" width="190" height="110" fill="none" stroke="var(--line-2)" stroke-width="2" rx="8"/><rect x="30" y="30" width="140" height="60" fill="var(--accent-soft)" rx="4"/><text x="100" y="65" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl" fill="var(--ink-2)">Center aisles</text><text x="100" y="18" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl" fill="var(--good)">Produce → Dairy → Meat</text></svg><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Zone</th><th>Buy</th><th>Skip</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Produce</td><td>Fresh, seasonal</td><td>Pre-cut trays</td></tr><tr><td>Dairy</td><td>Milk, eggs, yogurt</td><td>Flavored tubes</td></tr><tr><td>Meat</td><td>Manager's specials</td><td>Marinated skewers</td></tr><tr><td>Center</td><td>Beans, rice, pasta</td><td>Chips, soda</td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class="acc"><summary>Impulse-buy shield</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Eat before you go.</li><li>Shop the list, not the shelf.</li><li>Wait 24h on any non-list item over $10.</li></ul></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Shop the Perimeter, </h3><p class="muted t-small">Most stores hide the good stuff on the outside loop. The middle is where budgets go to die.</p><svg class="chart" viewBox="0 0 200 120" role="img" aria-label="Grocery store loop diagram"><rect x="5" y="5" width="190" height="110" fill="none" stroke="var(--line-2)" stroke-width="2" rx="8"/><rect x="30" y="30" width="140" height="60" fill="var(--accent-soft)" rx="4"/><text x="100" y="65" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl" fill="var(--ink-2)">Center aisles</text><text x="100" y="18" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl" fill="var(--good)">Produce → Dairy → Meat</text></svg><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Zone</th><th>Buy</th><th>Skip</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Produce</td><td>Fresh, seasonal</td><td>Pre-cut trays</td></tr><tr><td>Dairy</td><td>Milk, eggs, yogurt</td><td>Flavored tubes</td></tr><tr><td>Meat</td><td>Manager's specials</td><td>Marinated skewers</td></tr><tr><td>Center</td><td>Beans, rice, pasta</td><td>Chips, soda</td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class="acc"><summary>Impulse-buy shield</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Eat before you go.</li><li>Shop the list, not the shelf.</li><li>Wait 24h on any non-list item over $10.</li></ul></div></details></div>	-
35.45.0	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Posture Reset Cheat Sheet	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Posture Reset, <regex>user_name</regex></h3><p class="muted t-small">Perfect posture isn't the goal. Changing position often is.</p><div class="callout good"><strong>The rule of 30:</strong> every half hour, stand up for 30 seconds. Motion beats any chair.</div><details class="acc" open><summary>Desk setup checklist</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Screen top at eye level, an arm's length away.</li><li>Elbows at 90°, wrists floating.</li><li>Feet flat, knees at hip height.</li><li>Lower back supported, not slumped.</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Three 30-second resets</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li><strong>Chin tuck:</strong> pull head straight back, hold 5s, ×5.</li><li><strong>Shoulder rolls:</strong> up, back, down, ×10.</li><li><strong>Doorway stretch:</strong> forearms on frame, lean forward 20s.</li></ol></div></details><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Habit</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Phone at eye level</td><td>Less neck strain</td></tr><tr><td>Walk on calls</td><td>Hips and back stay loose</td></tr><tr><td>Water bottle refill</td><td>Free excuse to stand</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="dim t-small">Pain that radiates or wakes you up? Talk to a physio, not a cheat sheet.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Posture Reset, </h3><p class="muted t-small">Perfect posture isn't the goal. Changing position often is.</p><div class="callout good"><strong>The rule of 30:</strong> every half hour, stand up for 30 seconds. Motion beats any chair.</div><details class="acc" open><summary>Desk setup checklist</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Screen top at eye level, an arm's length away.</li><li>Elbows at 90°, wrists floating.</li><li>Feet flat, knees at hip height.</li><li>Lower back supported, not slumped.</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Three 30-second resets</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li><strong>Chin tuck:</strong> pull head straight back, hold 5s, ×5.</li><li><strong>Shoulder rolls:</strong> up, back, down, ×10.</li><li><strong>Doorway stretch:</strong> forearms on frame, lean forward 20s.</li></ol></div></details><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Habit</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Phone at eye level</td><td>Less neck strain</td></tr><tr><td>Walk on calls</td><td>Hips and back stay loose</td></tr><tr><td>Water bottle refill</td><td>Free excuse to stand</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="dim t-small">Pain that radiates or wakes you up? Talk to a physio, not a cheat sheet.</p></div>	-
35.46.0	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Gift Giving Cheat Sheet	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Gift Giving, <regex>user_name</regex></h3><p class="muted t-small">People remember how a gift felt, not what it cost. Aim for specific.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Budget sweet spot</span><span class="value">$25</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Lead time</span><span class="value">3<span class="unit"> days</span></span></div></div><details class="acc" open><summary>The 3-question filter</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>What do they complain about weekly?</li><li>What do they never buy for themselves?</li><li>What's a small upgrade to something they already use daily?</li></ol></div></details><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Person</th><th>Safe pick</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Coffee person</td><td>Good beans + a mug</td></tr><tr><td>Reader</td><td>Bookmark + bookstore card</td></tr><tr><td>Plant parent</td><td>A pot they'd eye but not buy</td></tr><tr><td>Hard to read</td><td>Consumable: food, candle, tea</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout warn">Avoid "improvement" gifts — scales, organizers, self-help. They land wrong.</div><p class="dim t-small">Forgot entirely? A handwritten note and a plan for lunch beats a rushed gadget.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Gift Giving, </h3><p class="muted t-small">People remember how a gift felt, not what it cost. Aim for specific.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Budget sweet spot</span><span class="value">$25</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Lead time</span><span class="value">3<span class="unit"> days</span></span></div></div><details class="acc" open><summary>The 3-question filter</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>What do they complain about weekly?</li><li>What do they never buy for themselves?</li><li>What's a small upgrade to something they already use daily?</li></ol></div></details><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Person</th><th>Safe pick</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Coffee person</td><td>Good beans + a mug</td></tr><tr><td>Reader</td><td>Bookmark + bookstore card</td></tr><tr><td>Plant parent</td><td>A pot they'd eye but not buy</td></tr><tr><td>Hard to read</td><td>Consumable: food, candle, tea</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout warn">Avoid "improvement" gifts — scales, organizers, self-help. They land wrong.</div><p class="dim t-small">Forgot entirely? A handwritten note and a plan for lunch beats a rushed gadget.</p></div>	-
35.47.0	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Bill Negotiation Cheat Sheet	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Talk Your Bills Down, <regex>user_name</regex></h3><p class="muted t-small">Loyalty is a discount code nobody mentions. Ask for it. The worst answer is no.</p><details class="acc" open><summary>Before you call</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Pull last 3 bills, note the real total.</li><li>Find a competitor's price, screenshot it.</li><li>Have your account number and cancel date handy.</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Say it like this</summary><div class="body"><blockquote class="quote">"I've been with you 4 years. My bill just jumped to $X. I'm seeing $Y elsewhere. What can you do to keep me?"</blockquote><p class="t-small">Then stop talking. Silence does the work.</p></div></details><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Bill</th><th class="num">Typical save</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Internet</td><td class="num">$10–30/mo</td></tr><tr><td>Phone</td><td class="num">$5–20/mo</td></tr><tr><td>Insurance</td><td class="num">$50–200/yr</td></tr><tr><td>Streaming bundle</td><td class="num">$5–15/mo</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout info">Escalate once: "Can I speak to retention?" That's the team with the discounts.</div><p class="dim t-small">Get the new price in writing — email or chat transcript. Words on a call evaporate.</p></div>	<div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Talk Your Bills Down, </h3><p class="muted t-small">Loyalty is a discount code nobody mentions. Ask for it. The worst answer is no.</p><details class="acc" open><summary>Before you call</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Pull last 3 bills, note the real total.</li><li>Find a competitor's price, screenshot it.</li><li>Have your account number and cancel date handy.</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Say it like this</summary><div class="body"><blockquote class="quote">"I've been with you 4 years. My bill just jumped to $X. I'm seeing $Y elsewhere. What can you do to keep me?"</blockquote><p class="t-small">Then stop talking. Silence does the work.</p></div></details><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Bill</th><th class="num">Typical save</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Internet</td><td class="num">$10–30/mo</td></tr><tr><td>Phone</td><td class="num">$5–20/mo</td></tr><tr><td>Insurance</td><td class="num">$50–200/yr</td></tr><tr><td>Streaming bundle</td><td class="num">$5–15/mo</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout info">Escalate once: "Can I speak to retention?" That's the team with the discounts.</div><p class="dim t-small">Get the new price in writing — email or chat transcript. Words on a call evaporate.</p></div>	-
35.48.0	html_cheat_sheet/Home Air Filter & HVAC Cheat Sheet	<section class="umi-page stack">  <h2>Home Air Filter &amp; HVAC Cheat Sheet</h2>  <p class="muted">Filters are cheap. Compressors are not. Here's the quick version, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p>  <div class="callout info"><strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> check monthly, swap every 1–3 months (more often with pets, allergies, or a dusty remodel).</div>  <details class="acc" open>    <summary>MERV ratings, decoded</summary>    <div class="body">      <div class="table-wrap">        <table>          <thead><tr><th>MERV</th><th>Catches</th><th>Good for</th></tr></thead>          <tbody>            <tr><td class="num">1–4</td><td>Dust, lint, pollen</td><td>Cheap protection, basic systems</td></tr>            <tr><td class="num">5–8</td><td>Mold spores, pet dander</td><td>Most homes — the sweet spot</td></tr>            <tr><td class="num">9–12</td><td>Fine dust, smoke</td><td>Allergy households</td></tr>            <tr><td class="num">13+</td><td>Bacteria, virus carriers</td><td>Only if your blower can handle it</td></tr>          </tbody>        </table>      </div>      <p class="t-small dim">Too-high MERV starves airflow. A strained motor runs hot and dies loud.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Reading the size on the frame</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>It's three numbers, like <code>20x25x1</code>: width × height × thickness. Nominally. Actual size is usually a hair smaller — measure before you order online.</p>      <ul class="list">        <li>Bring the old filter to the store. Easiest cheat code.</li>        <li>Arrow on the frame points <em>toward</em> the blower/airflow direction.</li>        <li>No arrow printed? Air flows from return duct toward the unit.</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Signs it's overdue</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>Filter looks grey and fuzzy (hold it to light — can't see through? Swap).</li>        <li>Weak airflow from vents.</li>        <li>Dust settling fast on surfaces.</li>        <li>Musty smell when the fan kicks on.</li>      </ul>      <div class="callout warn"><strong>Smells like burning?</strong> Shut the system off and call a tech. That's not a filter problem.</div>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>One-time vs. washable</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Disposable pleated filters win on filtration. Washable/reusable ones save money but generally sit at MERV 1–4 — great if your main goal is protecting the equipment, not your sinuses.</p>      <blockquote class="quote">Change it on the first of the month and you'll never wonder again.<cite>— every HVAC tech, ever</cite></blockquote>    </div>  </details>  <div class="row between">    <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</span>    <span class="badge info">web-dev adjacent? no. useful? yes.</span>  </div></section>	<section class="umi-page stack">  <h2>Home Air Filter &amp; HVAC Cheat Sheet</h2>  <p class="muted">Filters are cheap. Compressors are not. Here's the quick version, .</p>  <div class="callout info"><strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> check monthly, swap every 1–3 months (more often with pets, allergies, or a dusty remodel).</div>  <details class="acc" open>    <summary>MERV ratings, decoded</summary>    <div class="body">      <div class="table-wrap">        <table>          <thead><tr><th>MERV</th><th>Catches</th><th>Good for</th></tr></thead>          <tbody>            <tr><td class="num">1–4</td><td>Dust, lint, pollen</td><td>Cheap protection, basic systems</td></tr>            <tr><td class="num">5–8</td><td>Mold spores, pet dander</td><td>Most homes — the sweet spot</td></tr>            <tr><td class="num">9–12</td><td>Fine dust, smoke</td><td>Allergy households</td></tr>            <tr><td class="num">13+</td><td>Bacteria, virus carriers</td><td>Only if your blower can handle it</td></tr>          </tbody>        </table>      </div>      <p class="t-small dim">Too-high MERV starves airflow. A strained motor runs hot and dies loud.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Reading the size on the frame</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>It's three numbers, like <code>20x25x1</code>: width × height × thickness. Nominally. Actual size is usually a hair smaller — measure before you order online.</p>      <ul class="list">        <li>Bring the old filter to the store. Easiest cheat code.</li>        <li>Arrow on the frame points <em>toward</em> the blower/airflow direction.</li>        <li>No arrow printed? Air flows from return duct toward the unit.</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Signs it's overdue</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>Filter looks grey and fuzzy (hold it to light — can't see through? Swap).</li>        <li>Weak airflow from vents.</li>        <li>Dust settling fast on surfaces.</li>        <li>Musty smell when the fan kicks on.</li>      </ul>      <div class="callout warn"><strong>Smells like burning?</strong> Shut the system off and call a tech. That's not a filter problem.</div>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>One-time vs. washable</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Disposable pleated filters win on filtration. Washable/reusable ones save money but generally sit at MERV 1–4 — great if your main goal is protecting the equipment, not your sinuses.</p>      <blockquote class="quote">Change it on the first of the month and you'll never wonder again.<cite>— every HVAC tech, ever</cite></blockquote>    </div>  </details>  <div class="row between">    <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</span>    <span class="badge info">web-dev adjacent? no. useful? yes.</span>  </div></section>	-
35.49.0	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Dog Care Cheat Sheet	<section class="umi-page stack">  <h2>Everyday Dog Care Cheat Sheet</h2>  <p class="muted">The unglamorous basics that keep tails wagging. Hi, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat"><span class="label">Walks</span><span class="value">2–3<span class="unit">/day</span></span></div>    <div class="stat"><span class="label">Fresh water</span><span class="value">always</span></div>  </div>  <details class="acc" open>    <summary>Feeding without guessing</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>Follow the bag's chart as a <em>starting</em> point — adjust by body condition, not by marketing.</li>        <li>You should feel ribs easily under a thin fat layer. No ribs visible, no ribs at all = adjust.</li>        <li>Treats ≤ 10% of daily calories. Yes, really.</li>        <li>Switch foods over 5–7 days, mixing old into new.</li>      </ul>      <div class="callout bad"><strong>Never feed:</strong> chocolate, grapes/raisins, onions, garlic, xylitol (sugar-free gum), alcohol, macadamia nuts.</div>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Body language, quick read</summary>    <div class="body">      <div class="table-wrap">        <table>          <thead><tr><th>Signal</th><th>Likely meaning</th></tr></thead>          <tbody>            <tr><td>Loose, wiggly body</td><td>Relaxed, playful</td></tr>            <tr><td>Stiff posture, hard stare</td><td>Uncomfortable — give space</td></tr>            <tr><td>Lip lick, yawn, whale eye</td><td>Stressed, not "guilty"</td></tr>            <tr><td>Tail high and fast</td><td>Aroused, could tip to overstimulated</td></tr>            <tr><td>Play bow</td><td>Let's go</td></tr>          </tbody>        </table>      </div>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Vet visit now (not next week)</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>Vomiting or diarrhea more than 24h, or with blood.</li>        <li>Not eating 2+ days.</li>        <li>Limping that doesn't improve in a day.</li>        <li>Straining to pee, especially male dogs — emergency.</li>        <li>Collapse, seizure, bloated hard belly.</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Grooming rhythm</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>Nails: every 3–4 weeks. If you hear clicking on tile, sooner.</li>        <li>Brush: short coats weekly, long coats 3–4×/week.</li>        <li>Bath: every 4–8 weeks unless they rolled in something philosophical.</li>        <li>Teeth: ideally daily. Aim for "more than never."</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details></section>	<section class="umi-page stack">  <h2>Everyday Dog Care Cheat Sheet</h2>  <p class="muted">The unglamorous basics that keep tails wagging. Hi, .</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat"><span class="label">Walks</span><span class="value">2–3<span class="unit">/day</span></span></div>    <div class="stat"><span class="label">Fresh water</span><span class="value">always</span></div>  </div>  <details class="acc" open>    <summary>Feeding without guessing</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>Follow the bag's chart as a <em>starting</em> point — adjust by body condition, not by marketing.</li>        <li>You should feel ribs easily under a thin fat layer. No ribs visible, no ribs at all = adjust.</li>        <li>Treats ≤ 10% of daily calories. Yes, really.</li>        <li>Switch foods over 5–7 days, mixing old into new.</li>      </ul>      <div class="callout bad"><strong>Never feed:</strong> chocolate, grapes/raisins, onions, garlic, xylitol (sugar-free gum), alcohol, macadamia nuts.</div>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Body language, quick read</summary>    <div class="body">      <div class="table-wrap">        <table> 