35.51.0	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Clothing Sizing & Fit Cheat Sheet	<section class="umi-page stack">  <h2>Everyday Clothing Sizing &amp; Fit Cheat Sheet</h2>  <p class="muted">Sizes are opinions. Measurements are facts. Trust the tape, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p>  <details class="acc" open>    <summary>Measure yourself once, shop forever</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li><strong>Chest/bust:</strong> around the fullest part, tape level, arms down.</li>        <li><strong>Natural waist:</strong> bend sideways — the crease is your waist, not your jeans' waist.</li>        <li><strong>Hips:</strong> around the fullest part, usually 8–9" below the waist.</li>        <li><strong>Inseam:</strong> from crotch seam to ankle bone on pants that fit.</li>        <li><strong>Sleeve:</strong> shoulder point to wrist, slightly bent arm.</li>      </ul>      <p class="t-small dim">Write these in your phone notes. You'll use them more than your insurance card.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Why sizes lie</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Vanity sizing creeps numbers down over time, and every brand drafts its own fit block. A medium is a suggestion. The size chart is the contract.</p>      <div class="table-wrap">        <table>          <thead><tr><th>Brand type</th><th>Tends to run</th></tr></thead>          <tbody>            <tr><td>Fast fashion</td><td>Small, inconsistent</td></tr>            <tr><td>Athletic</td><td>Fitted, snug shoulders</td></tr>            <tr><td>Heritage/workwear</td><td>Boxy, roomy</td></tr>            <tr><td>Luxury</td><td>Trim, closer to true measure</td></tr>          </tbody>        </table>      </div>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Fit checkpoints</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li><strong>Shirt:</strong> shoulder seam ends at your shoulder bone, not past it.</li>        <li><strong>Jacket sleeve:</strong> shows ½" of shirt cuff.</li>        <li><strong>Jacket length:</strong> covers your seat; for formal, to the thumb knuckle.</li>        <li><strong>Waistband:</strong> no gaping in back when you sit. If it gaps, size down or get it taken in.</li>        <li><strong>Break (pant hem):</strong> slight fold at the shoe. No puddle.</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Online ordering survival</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Measure a garment you already love, flat, and compare to the brand's chart.</li>        <li>Read reviews for "runs small/large" — those are the real spec sheet.</li>        <li>Buy two sizes when free returns exist. Return the loser.</li>        <li>Shoes: measure at the end of the day; feet swell. Size to the longer foot.</li>      </ol>    </div>  </details></section>	<section class="umi-page stack">  <h2>Everyday Clothing Sizing &amp; Fit Cheat Sheet</h2>  <p class="muted">Sizes are opinions. Measurements are facts. Trust the tape, .</p>  <details class="acc" open>    <summary>Measure yourself once, shop forever</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li><strong>Chest/bust:</strong> around the fullest part, tape level, arms down.</li>        <li><strong>Natural waist:</strong> bend sideways — the crease is your waist, not your jeans' waist.</li>        <li><strong>Hips:</strong> around the fullest part, usually 8–9" below the waist.</li>        <li><strong>Inseam:</strong> from crotch seam to ankle bone on pants that fit.</li>        <li><strong>Sleeve:</strong> shoulder point to wrist, slightly bent arm.</li>      </ul>      <p class="t-small dim">Write these in your phone notes. You'll use them more than your insurance card.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Why sizes lie</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Vanity sizing creeps numbers down over time, and every brand drafts its own fit block. A medium is a suggestion. The size chart is the contract.</p>      <div class="table-wrap">        <table>          <thead><tr><th>Brand type</th><th>Tends to run</th></tr></thead>          <tbody>            <tr><td>Fast fashion</td><td>Small, inconsistent</td></tr>            <tr><td>Athletic</td><td>Fitted, snug shoulders</td></tr>            <tr><td>Heritage/workwear</td><td>Boxy, roomy</td></tr>            <tr><td>Luxury</td><td>Trim, closer to true measure</td></tr>          </tbody>        </table>      </div>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Fit checkpoints</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li><strong>Shirt:</strong> shoulder seam ends at your shoulder bone, not past it.</li>        <li><strong>Jacket sleeve:</strong> shows ½" of shirt cuff.</li>        <li><strong>Jacket length:</strong> covers your seat; for formal, to the thumb knuckle.</li>        <li><strong>Waistband:</strong> no gaping in back when you sit. If it gaps, size down or get it taken in.</li>        <li><strong>Break (pant hem):</strong> slight fold at the shoe. No puddle.</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Online ordering survival</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Measure a garment you already love, flat, and compare to the brand's chart.</li>        <li>Read reviews for "runs small/large" — those are the real spec sheet.</li>        <li>Buy two sizes when free returns exist. Return the loser.</li>        <li>Shoes: measure at the end of the day; feet swell. Size to the longer foot.</li>      </ol>    </div>  </details></section>	-
35.52.0	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Plant Watering Cheat Sheet	<section class="umi-page stack">  <h2>Everyday Plant Watering Cheat Sheet</h2>  <p class="muted">Most houseplants die of kindness. Here's how to be kinder, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p>  <div class="callout good"><strong>Rule one:</strong> water on the calendar of the soil, not the calendar on your fridge.</div>  <details class="acc" open>    <summary>The finger test</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Push a finger in up to the second knuckle.</li>        <li>Dry all the way? Water. Damp? Wait.</li>        <li>For big pots, use a wooden chopstick — pull it out like a cake tester.</li>        <li>Water until it runs out the drainage hole, then stop.</li>      </ol>      <p class="t-small dim">No drainage hole? That's a bathtub, not a pot. Water less and empty the saucer.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Over- vs under-watering clues</summary>    <div class="body">      <div class="table-wrap">        <table>          <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Usually means</th></tr></thead>          <tbody>            <tr><td>Yellow, mushy lower leaves</td><td>Too much water</td></tr>            <tr><td>Crispy brown tips</td><td>Too little, or dry air</td></tr>            <tr><td>Wilting despite wet soil</td><td>Root rot — too much</td></tr>            <tr><td>Drooping with bone-dry soil</td><td>Too little</td></tr>            <tr><td>White crust on soil</td><td>Salt buildup — flush it</td></tr>          </tbody>        </table>      </div>      <p class="t-small dim">Yellow leaves can also mean light, food, or just autumn. One symptom isn't a diagnosis.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Rough watering rhythm</summary>    <div class="body">      <div class="table-wrap">        <table>          <thead><tr><th>Plant</th><th>Cadence (indoor)</th></tr></thead>          <tbody>            <tr><td>Succulents / cactus</td><td>Every 2–4 weeks, bone dry between</td></tr>            <tr><td>Snake plant / ZZ</td><td>Every 2–3 weeks</td></tr>            <tr><td>Pothos / philodendron</td><td>Weekly-ish, top inch dry</td></tr>            <tr><td>Ferns / calathea</td><td>Keep lightly moist, never soggy</td></tr>          </tbody>        </table>      </div>      <p class="t-small dim">Cooler months = slower growth = less water. Halve it.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Top or bottom watering?</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li><strong>Top:</strong> fast, flushes salts, mimics rain. Risk: uneven saturation.</li>        <li><strong>Bottom:</strong> set the pot in a saucer of water for 15–30 min; the soil drinks upward. Great for fuzzy leaves you shouldn't wet.</li>      </ul>      <p>Either works. Consistency beats technique.</p>    </div>  </details>  <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</span></section>	<section class="umi-page stack">  <h2>Everyday Plant Watering Cheat Sheet</h2>  <p class="muted">Most houseplants die of kindness. Here's how to be kinder, .</p>  <div class="callout good"><strong>Rule one:</strong> water on the calendar of the soil, not the calendar on your fridge.</div>  <details class="acc" open>    <summary>The finger test</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Push a finger in up to the second knuckle.</li>        <li>Dry all the way? Water. Damp? Wait.</li>        <li>For big pots, use a wooden chopstick — pull it out like a cake tester.</li>        <li>Water until it runs out the drainage hole, then stop.</li>      </ol>      <p class="t-small dim">No drainage hole? That's a bathtub, not a pot. Water less and empty the saucer.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Over- vs under-watering clues</summary>    <div class="body">      <div class="table-wrap">        <table>          <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Usually means</th></tr></thead>          <tbody>            <tr><td>Yellow, mushy lower leaves</td><td>Too much water</td></tr>            <tr><td>Crispy brown tips</td><td>Too little, or dry air</td></tr>            <tr><td>Wilting despite wet soil</td><td>Root rot — too much</td></tr>            <tr><td>Drooping with bone-dry soil</td><td>Too little</td></tr>            <tr><td>White crust on soil</td><td>Salt buildup — flush it</td></tr>          </tbody>        </table>      </div>      <p class="t-small dim">Yellow leaves can also mean light, food, or just autumn. One symptom isn't a diagnosis.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Rough watering rhythm</summary>    <div class="body">      <div class="table-wrap">        <table>          <thead><tr><th>Plant</th><th>Cadence (indoor)</th></tr></thead>          <tbody>            <tr><td>Succulents / cactus</td><td>Every 2–4 weeks, bone dry between</td></tr>            <tr><td>Snake plant / ZZ</td><td>Every 2–3 weeks</td></tr>            <tr><td>Pothos / philodendron</td><td>Weekly-ish, top inch dry</td></tr>            <tr><td>Ferns / calathea</td><td>Keep lightly moist, never soggy</td></tr>          </tbody>        </table>      </div>      <p class="t-small dim">Cooler months = slower growth = less water. Halve it.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Top or bottom watering?</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li><strong>Top:</strong> fast, flushes salts, mimics rain. Risk: uneven saturation.</li>        <li><strong>Bottom:</strong> set the pot in a saucer of water for 15–30 min; the soil drinks upward. Great for fuzzy leaves you shouldn't wet.</li>      </ul>      <p>Either works. Consistency beats technique.</p>    </div>  </details>  <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</span></section>	-
35.53.0	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Canned Food Pantry Cheat Sheet	<section class="umi-page stack">  <h2>Everyday Canned Food Pantry Cheat Sheet</h2>  <p class="muted">Cans don't expire the way you think. Here's the shelf exam, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p>  <div class="callout info"><strong>Best-by ≠ poison-by.</strong> Dates on cans are about peak quality, not safety.</div>  <details class="acc" open>    <summary>How long it actually lasts</summary>    <div class="body">      <div class="table-wrap">        <table>          <thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Quality window</th></tr></thead>          <tbody>            <tr><td>Low-acid (beans, tuna, soup, veg)</td><td>2–5 years</td></tr>            <tr><td>High-acid (tomatoes, fruit, pickles)</td><td>12–18 months</td></tr>            <tr><td>Home-canned (low-acid)</td><td>1 year, then quality drops</td></tr>          </tbody>        </table>      </div>      <p class="t-small dim">Past the window, flavor fades and texture goes soft. Still safe if the can passes the check below.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>The can exam: toss if any of these</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li><strong>Bulging</strong> ends or sides — pressure inside means bacteria. No exceptions.</li>        <li><strong>Leaking</strong> or sticky residue on the seam.</li>        <li><strong>Deep dents</strong> on the side seam or rim. Small dents in the middle of the body are usually fine.</li>        <li><strong>Spurting</strong> or hissing when you open it.</li>        <li><strong>Foul smell</strong> or cloudy liquid after opening.</li>      </ul>      <div class="callout bad"><strong>Never taste-test a suspect can.</strong> Botulism toxin has no taste. When in doubt, out it goes.</div>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Storage that keeps cans happy</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>Cool, dry, dark. Under 75°F ideally.</li>        <li>Off the floor, off concrete, away from the water heater.</li>        <li>Don't stack heavy cans on fragile ones.</li>        <li>Rotate: new cans to the back, old to the front. Date the lid with a marker.</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Cans vs. dried</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Dried beans and rice are cheaper per serving and last decades. Cans buy you time and no soaking. The honest answer: keep both, with cans as your "I'm tired tonight" layer.</p>      <ul class="list">        <li><strong>Cans win:</strong> speed, no prep, ready emergency food.</li>        <li><strong>Dried wins:</strong> cost, shelf life, flavor control.</li>        <li>Salt note: rinse canned beans to cut sodium ~40%.</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <div class="row between"><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</span><span class="badge info">pantry panic, solved</span></div></section>	<section class="umi-page stack">  <h2>Everyday Canned Food Pantry Cheat Sheet</h2>  <p class="muted">Cans don't expire the way you think. Here's the shelf exam, .</p>  <div class="callout info"><strong>Best-by ≠ poison-by.</strong> Dates on cans are about peak quality, not safety.</div>  <details class="acc" open>    <summary>How long it actually lasts</summary>    <div class="body">      <div class="table-wrap">        <table>          <thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Quality window</th></tr></thead>          <tbody>            <tr><td>Low-acid (beans, tuna, soup, veg)</td><td>2–5 years</td></tr>            <tr><td>High-acid (tomatoes, fruit, pickles)</td><td>12–18 months</td></tr>            <tr><td>Home-canned (low-acid)</td><td>1 year, then quality drops</td></tr>          </tbody>        </table>      </div>      <p class="t-small dim">Past the window, flavor fades and texture goes soft. Still safe if the can passes the check below.</p>    </div>  </details>