35.54.0	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Bread Baking Cheat Sheet	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Everyday Bread Baking <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached</span></h3>  <p class="muted t-small">Hey <regex>user_name</regex> — flour, water, salt, patience. That's the whole game.</p>  <div class="table-wrap">    <table>      <thead><tr><th>Dough</th><th class="num">Hydration</th><th>Feel</th></tr></thead>      <tbody>        <tr><td>Bagel / pizza</td><td class="num">58–62%</td><td>Stiff, barely tacky</td></tr>        <tr><td>Sandwich loaf</td><td class="num">65–70%</td><td>Soft, holds a dent</td></tr>        <tr><td>Focaccia / ciabatta</td><td class="num">75–85%</td><td>Wet, slumps off the hook</td></tr>      </tbody>    </table>  </div>  <p class="dim t-small">Hydration = water weight ÷ flour weight. 500g flour at 70% needs 350g water.</p>  <ul class="list">    <li><strong>Windowpane test:</strong> stretch a golf-ball piece thin enough to read through. Torn = keep kneading.</li>    <li><strong>Bulk rise:</strong> 50–75% bigger, not doubled. Poke it — the dent should spring back slowly.</li>    <li><strong>Oven spring:</strong> score at a shallow angle, steam the first 10 min, then dry heat to brown.</li>  </ul>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Dense loaf? Diagnose it</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>Under-proofed: tight crumb, tunnel at the edges.</li>        <li>Over-proofed: flat top, boozy smell, collapses on touch.</li>        <li>Dead yeast: check it foams in warm water before you commit a whole kilo of flour.</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout warn"><p>Water above 46°C kills yeast. Lukewarm means lukewarm.</p></div></div>	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Everyday Bread Baking <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached</span></h3>  <p class="muted t-small">Hey  — flour, water, salt, patience. That's the whole game.</p>  <div class="table-wrap">    <table>      <thead><tr><th>Dough</th><th class="num">Hydration</th><th>Feel</th></tr></thead>      <tbody>        <tr><td>Bagel / pizza</td><td class="num">58–62%</td><td>Stiff, barely tacky</td></tr>        <tr><td>Sandwich loaf</td><td class="num">65–70%</td><td>Soft, holds a dent</td></tr>        <tr><td>Focaccia / ciabatta</td><td class="num">75–85%</td><td>Wet, slumps off the hook</td></tr>      </tbody>    </table>  </div>  <p class="dim t-small">Hydration = water weight ÷ flour weight. 500g flour at 70% needs 350g water.</p>  <ul class="list">    <li><strong>Windowpane test:</strong> stretch a golf-ball piece thin enough to read through. Torn = keep kneading.</li>    <li><strong>Bulk rise:</strong> 50–75% bigger, not doubled. Poke it — the dent should spring back slowly.</li>    <li><strong>Oven spring:</strong> score at a shallow angle, steam the first 10 min, then dry heat to brown.</li>  </ul>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Dense loaf? Diagnose it</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>Under-proofed: tight crumb, tunnel at the edges.</li>        <li>Over-proofed: flat top, boozy smell, collapses on touch.</li>        <li>Dead yeast: check it foams in warm water before you commit a whole kilo of flour.</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout warn"><p>Water above 46°C kills yeast. Lukewarm means lukewarm.</p></div></div>	-
35.55.0	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Bike Locking Cheat Sheet	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Everyday Bike Locking <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached</span></h3>  <p class="muted t-small"><regex>user_name</regex>, a lock is only as good as what you loop it through.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat"><div class="label">U-lock</div><div class="value">Hard target</div><div class="delta">Frame + rear wheel</div></div>    <div class="stat"><div class="label">Cable</div><div class="value">Seconds</div><div class="delta down">Accessory only</div></div>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Lock through the <strong>frame triangle</strong>, not just a wheel.</li>    <li>Catch a fixed rack, post or bike rack — something bolted down.</li>    <li>Keep slack out of the U so a jack can't get a grip.</li>    <li>Loop a cable through both wheels if you have one.</li>    <li>Take the front wheel if it's quick-release.</li>  </ol>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What thieves actually target</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>Thin cable alone — cut in one snip.</li>        <li>Locked to a signpost that lifts out of the ground.</li>        <li>Wheel-only locking, leaving a free frame.</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Street smart spots</summary>    <div class="body"><p>Busy, lit, camera-visible beats hidden and quiet. Overnight, bring it inside if you can.</p></div>  </details>  <div class="callout good"><p>Snap a photo of your serial number and lock setup. If it goes, that's your report.</p></div></div>	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Everyday Bike Locking <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached</span></h3>  <p class="muted t-small">, a lock is only as good as what you loop it through.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat"><div class="label">U-lock</div><div class="value">Hard target</div><div class="delta">Frame + rear wheel</div></div>    <div class="stat"><div class="label">Cable</div><div class="value">Seconds</div><div class="delta down">Accessory only</div></div>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Lock through the <strong>frame triangle</strong>, not just a wheel.</li>    <li>Catch a fixed rack, post or bike rack — something bolted down.</li>    <li>Keep slack out of the U so a jack can't get a grip.</li>    <li>Loop a cable through both wheels if you have one.</li>    <li>Take the front wheel if it's quick-release.</li>  </ol>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What thieves actually target</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>Thin cable alone — cut in one snip.</li>        <li>Locked to a signpost that lifts out of the ground.</li>        <li>Wheel-only locking, leaving a free frame.</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Street smart spots</summary>    <div class="body"><p>Busy, lit, camera-visible beats hidden and quiet. Overnight, bring it inside if you can.</p></div>  </details>  <div class="callout good"><p>Snap a photo of your serial number and lock setup. If it goes, that's your report.</p></div></div>	-
35.56.0	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Nail Care Cheat Sheet	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Everyday Nail Care <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached</span></h3>  <p class="muted t-small">Boring truth, <regex>user_name</regex>: nails are files, not filing cabinets. Keep them dry and oiled.</p>  <div class="table-wrap">    <table>      <thead><tr><th>Habit</th><th>Why it works</th></tr></thead>      <tbody>        <tr><td>File one direction</td><td>Sawing splits the free edge</td></tr>        <tr><td>Trim after a shower</td><td>Softer nails cut clean</td></tr>        <tr><td>Cuticle oil nightly</td><td>Flexible nails bend instead of snapping</td></tr>        <tr><td>Gloves for dishes</td><td>Water and detergent strip oils</td></tr>      </tbody>    </table>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Shape with a fine file, tips down, corners gently rounded.</li>    <li>Push cuticles back — never cut them. That's your seal against infection.</li>    <li>Buff lightly, once a week at most.</li>    <li>Massage oil or hand cream into the nail and surrounding skin.</li>  </ol>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Peeling nails, five causes</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>Wet-dry cycles all day long.</li>        <li>Using nails as tools — open cans with a spoon.</li>        <li>Cheap acetone remover, no re-oiling after.</li>        <li>Aggressive buffing.</li>        <li>True deficiency is rare, but iron and biotin deserve a chat with a doctor.</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout info"><p>White spots? That's old trauma, not a calcium crisis. It grows out.</p></div></div>	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Everyday Nail Care <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached</span></h3>  <p class="muted t-small">Boring truth, : nails are files, not filing cabinets. Keep them dry and oiled.</p>  <div class="table-wrap">    <table>      <thead><tr><th>Habit</th><th>Why it works</th></tr></thead>      <tbody>        <tr><td>File one direction</td><td>Sawing splits the free edge</td></tr>        <tr><td>Trim after a shower</td><td>Softer nails cut clean</td></tr>        <tr><td>Cuticle oil nightly</td><td>Flexible nails bend instead of snapping</td></tr>        <tr><td>Gloves for dishes</td><td>Water and detergent strip oils</td></tr>      </tbody>    </table>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Shape with a fine file, tips down, corners gently rounded.</li>    <li>Push cuticles back — never cut them. That's your seal against infection.</li>    <li>Buff lightly, once a week at most.</li>    <li>Massage oil or hand cream into the nail and surrounding skin.</li>  </ol>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Peeling nails, five causes</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>Wet-dry cycles all day long.</li>        <li>Using nails as tools — open cans with a spoon.</li>        <li>Cheap acetone remover, no re-oiling after.</li>        <li>Aggressive buffing.</li>        <li>True deficiency is rare, but iron and biotin deserve a chat with a doctor.</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout info"><p>White spots? That's old trauma, not a calcium crisis. It grows out.</p></div></div>	-
35.57.0	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Rainy Day Commute Cheat Sheet	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Everyday Rainy Day Commute <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached</span></h3>  <p class="muted t-small"><regex>user_name</regex>, you can't beat rain. You can absolutely out-prepare it.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat"><div class="label">Layer 1</div><div class="value">Dry</div><div class="delta">Base shirt, stays home-safe</div></div>    <div class="stat"><div class="label">Layer 2</div><div class="value">Shell</div><div class="delta down">Packable, hooded</div></div>  </div>  <ul class="list">    <li><strong>Bag:</strong> roll-top or a rain cover. A tote soaks through by mile one.</li>    <li><strong>Feet:</strong> waterproof shoes, or spare socks in a zip bag. Dry socks are a small miracle.</li>    <li><strong>Phone:</strong> inside a sealed pocket, not the outside pouch.</li>    <li><strong>Umbrella:</strong> compact one lives in the bag permanently. Broken ones don't count.</li>  </ul>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Arriving less soggy</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>Shake the umbrella outside, not in the doorway.</li>        <li>Blot before you sit — wet backs make cold afternoons.</li>        <li>Hang the shell to dry; never ball it up wet in a bag overnight.</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout warn"><p>Wet floors plus slick soles = the day's most likely injury. Slow down on tile.</p></div></div>	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Everyday Rainy Day Commute <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached</span></h3>  <p class="muted t-small">, you can't beat rain. You can absolutely out-prepare it.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat"><div class="label">Layer 1</div><div class="value">Dry</div><div class="delta">Base shirt, stays home-safe</div></div>    <div class="stat"><div class="label">Layer 2</div><div class="value">Shell</div><div class="delta down">Packable, hooded</div></div>  </div>  <ul class="list">    <li><strong>Bag:</strong> roll-top or a rain cover. A tote soaks through by mile one.</li>    <li><strong>Feet:</strong> waterproof shoes, or spare socks in a zip bag. Dry socks are a small miracle.</li>    <li><strong>Phone:</strong> inside a sealed pocket, not the outside pouch.</li>    <li><strong>Umbrella:</strong> compact one lives in the bag permanently. Broken ones don't count.</li>  </ul>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Arriving less soggy</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>Shake the umbrella outside, not in the doorway.</li>        <li>Blot before you sit — wet backs make cold afternoons.</li>        <li>Hang the shell to dry; never ball it up wet in a bag overnight.</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout warn"><p>Wet floors plus slick soles = the day's most likely injury. Slow down on tile.</p></div></div>	-
35.58.0	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Spice Rack Cheat Sheet	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Everyday Spice Rack <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached</span></h3>  <p class="muted t-small">Your rack needs fewer jars than the shop wants, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p>  <div class="table-wrap">    <table>      <thead><tr><th>Blend</th><th>Mix</th></tr></thead>      <tbody>        <tr><td>Chili</td><td>Cumin, paprika, oregano, garlic, cayenne</td></tr>        <tr><td>Curry-ish</td><td>Cumin, coriander, turmeric, ginger, black pepper</td></tr>        <tr><td>Italian-ish</td><td>Basil, oregano, thyme, garlic</td></tr>        <tr><td>Warm sweet</td><td>Cinnamon, nutmeg, clove, allspice</td></tr>      </tbody>    </table>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Toast whole seeds in a dry pan until fragrant, then grind. Thirty seconds, big payoff.</li>    <li>Add ground spices to oil or fat early — flavour is fat-soluble.</li>    <li>Salt at the end to taste, not by habit.</li>  </ol>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Is it still good?</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>Whole spices: 2–3 years. Ground: 6–12 months.</li>        <li>Smell test wins over the date stamp.</li>        <li>Store away from the stove, dark and sealed.</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout good"><p>Buy small, refill often. A fresh pinch beats a dusty handful.</p></div></div>	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Everyday Spice Rack <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached</span></h3>  <p class="muted t-small">Your rack needs fewer jars than the shop wants, .</p>  <div class="table-wrap">    <table>      <thead><tr><th>Blend</th><th>Mix</th></tr></thead>      <tbody>        <tr><td>Chili</td><td>Cumin, paprika, oregano, garlic, cayenne</td></tr>        <tr><td>Curry-ish</td><td>Cumin, coriander, turmeric, ginger, black pepper</td></tr>        <tr><td>Italian-ish</td><td>Basil, oregano, thyme, garlic</td></tr>        <tr><td>Warm sweet</td><td>Cinnamon, nutmeg, clove, allspice</td></tr>      </tbody>    </table>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Toast whole seeds in a dry pan until fragrant, then grind. Thirty seconds, big payoff.</li>    <li>Add ground spices to oil or fat early — flavour is fat-soluble.</li>    <li>Salt at the end to taste, not by habit.</li>  </ol>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Is it still good?</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>Whole spices: 2–3 years. Ground: 6–12 months.</li>        <li>Smell test wins over the date stamp.</li>        <li>Store away from the stove, dark and sealed.</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout good"><p>Buy small, refill often. A fresh pinch beats a dusty handful.</p></div></div>	-
35.59.0	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Video Call Setup Cheat Sheet	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Everyday Video Call Setup <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached</span></h3>  <p class="muted t-small"><regex>user_name</regex>, you're not on camera. You're a well-lit rectangle, and that's easier.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat"><div class="label">Light</div><div class="value">In front</div><div class="delta">Never a window behind</div></div>    <div class="stat"><div class="label">Camera</div><div class="value">Eye level</div><div class="delta down">Book stack works fine</div></div>  </div>  <ul class="list">    <li><strong>Frame:</strong> top of head to mid-chest. Close enough to read your face, not your pores.</li>    <li><strong>Background:</strong> tidy, no bright clutter, door behind you closed.</li>    <li><strong>Mic:</strong> headset or earbuds beat laptop mics every time.</li>    <li><strong>Test:</strong> record thirty seconds before the real call.</li>  </ul>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Sound fixes, fastest first</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>Move the mic closer to your mouth, off the desk.</li>        <li>Hard floors and bare walls echo — a rug and curtains help.</li>        <li>Mute when you're not talking. Everyone forgives it.</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout info"><p>Glasses glare? Nudge the light one step to the side, not directly ahead.</p></div></div>	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Everyday Video Call Setup <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached</span></h3>  <p class="muted t-small">, you're not on camera. You're a well-lit rectangle, and that's easier.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat"><div class="label">Light</div><div class="value">In front</div><div class="delta">Never a window behind</div></div>    <div class="stat"><div class="label">Camera</div><div class="value">Eye level</div><div class="delta down">Book stack works fine</div></div>  </div>  <ul class="list">    <li><strong>Frame:</strong> top of head to mid-chest. Close enough to read your face, not your pores.</li>    <li><strong>Background:</strong> tidy, no bright clutter, door behind you closed.</li>    <li><strong>Mic:</strong> headset or earbuds beat laptop mics every time.</li>    <li><strong>Test:</strong> record thirty seconds before the real call.</li>  </ul>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Sound fixes, fastest first</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>Move the mic closer to your mouth, off the desk.</li>        <li>Hard floors and bare walls echo — a rug and curtains help.</li>        <li>Mute when you're not talking. Everyone forgives it.</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout info"><p>Glasses glare? Nudge the light one step to the side, not directly ahead.</p></div></div>	-
35.60.0	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Bathroom Cleaning Cheat Sheet	<div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Everyday Bathroom Cleaning Cheat Sheet</h2><p class="muted t-small">Top to bottom, dry to wet. The dirt falls, <regex>user_name</regex>. Work with gravity.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Order</th><th>Task</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Dust vents &amp; light fixtures</td><td>Dry first, so dust lands where you can wipe it</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Spray shower &amp; tub</td><td>Let cleaner sit 10 min while you work elsewhere</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Sink, counter, mirror</td><td>Mirror last on the dry side — no streaks</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Toilet: outside, then bowl</td><td>Bowl last, brush stays wet</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Floor, back to door</td><td>You walk out clean, not back in</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><div class="stack"><details class="acc"><summary>Mildew on caulk and grout</summary><div class="body"><p>Scrub with baking soda paste + a stiff brush. If stained deep, lay a paper towel soaked in vinegar on the line for an hour, then rinse. Bleach gel works too — ventilate, and never mix bleach with vinegar or ammonia.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Hard water spots on glass</summary><div class="body"><p>Equal parts vinegar and water in a spray bottle. Squeegee after every shower and the spots mostly quit showing up.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>10-minute weekly reset</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Spray shower, close the curtain.</li><li>Wipe sink and counter.</li><li>Swish bowl with brush, flush.</li><li>Shake out the bath mat.</li></ol><p class="dim t-small">Daily squeegee beats a heroic Saturday. Every single time.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Everyday Bathroom Cleaning Cheat Sheet</h2><p class="muted t-small">Top to bottom, dry to wet. The dirt falls, . Work with gravity.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Order</th><th>Task</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Dust vents &amp; light fixtures</td><td>Dry first, so dust lands where you can wipe it</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Spray shower &amp; tub</td><td>Let cleaner sit 10 min while you work elsewhere</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Sink, counter, mirror</td><td>Mirror last on the dry side — no streaks</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Toilet: outside, then bowl</td><td>Bowl last, brush stays wet</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Floor, back to door</td><td>You walk out clean, not back in</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><div class="stack"><details class="acc"><summary>Mildew on caulk and grout</summary><div class="body"><p>Scrub with baking soda paste + a stiff brush. If stained deep, lay a paper towel soaked in vinegar on the line for an hour, then rinse. Bleach gel works too — ventilate, and never mix bleach with vinegar or ammonia.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Hard water spots on glass</summary><div class="body"><p>Equal parts vinegar and water in a spray bottle. Squeegee after every shower and the spots mostly quit showing up.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>10-minute weekly reset</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Spray shower, close the curtain.</li><li>Wipe sink and counter.</li><li>Swish bowl with brush, flush.</li><li>Shake out the bath mat.</li></ol><p class="dim t-small">Daily squeegee beats a heroic Saturday. Every single time.</p></div></details></div>	-
35.61.0	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Grocery Shopping List Cheat Sheet	<div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Everyday Grocery Shopping List Cheat Sheet</h2><p class="muted t-small"><regex>user_name</regex>, a list is a route, not a wish. Build it in order and you shop in order.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Staples</span><span class="value">8<em class="unit"> items</em></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Meals planned</span><span class="value">5<em class="unit"> dinners</em></span></div></div><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Always check</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Produce</td><td>Onion, garlic, lemon, potatoes</td></tr><tr><td>Protein</td><td>Eggs, whatever's on sale, beans</td></tr><tr><td>Dairy</td><td>Milk, butter, yogurt, cheese</td></tr><tr><td>Pantry</td><td>Oil, rice, pasta, canned tomatoes</td></tr><tr><td>Freezer</td><td>Peas, spinach, one emergency meal</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Writing the list so you don't double-buy</summary><div class="body"><p>Write items in aisle order, not alphabetically. Snap a photo of the fridge before you leave — that's your real inventory.</p><ul class="list"><li>Circle what's for this week's dinners.</li><li>Star the one treat. Just one.</li><li>Cross off as you go so you never backtrack.</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Budget move</summary><div class="body"><p>Eat before you shop, shop the perimeter first, and buy the store brand on anything you'll cook with. The savings live in the middle aisles you skip.</p></div></details>	<div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Everyday Grocery Shopping List Cheat Sheet</h2><p class="muted t-small">, a list is a route, not a wish. Build it in order and you shop in order.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Staples</span><span class="value">8<em class="unit"> items</em></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Meals planned</span><span class="value">5<em class="unit"> dinners</em></span></div></div><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Always check</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Produce</td><td>Onion, garlic, lemon, potatoes</td></tr><tr><td>Protein</td><td>Eggs, whatever's on sale, beans</td></tr><tr><td>Dairy</td><td>Milk, butter, yogurt, cheese</td></tr><tr><td>Pantry</td><td>Oil, rice, pasta, canned tomatoes</td></tr><tr><td>Freezer</td><td>Peas, spinach, one emergency meal</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Writing the list so you don't double-buy</summary><div class="body"><p>Write items in aisle order, not alphabetically. Snap a photo of the fridge before you leave — that's your real inventory.</p><ul class="list"><li>Circle what's for this week's dinners.</li><li>Star the one treat. Just one.</li><li>Cross off as you go so you never backtrack.</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Budget move</summary><div class="body"><p>Eat before you shop, shop the perimeter first, and buy the store brand on anything you'll cook with. The savings live in the middle aisles you skip.</p></div></details>	-
35.62.0	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Shoe Care Cheat Sheet	<div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Everyday Shoe Care Cheat Sheet</h2><p class="muted t-small">Shoes die young from neglect, not miles, <regex>user_name>. Five minutes keeps them going for years.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Material</th><th>Clean with</th><th>Feed with</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Leather</td><td>Damp cloth, saddle soap</td><td>Cream polish monthly</td></tr><tr><td>Canvas</td><td>Soft brush + mild soap</td><td>Nothing — air dry</td></tr><tr><td>Suede</td><td>Suede brush, eraser</td><td>Protector spray</td></tr><tr><td>Mesh sneakers</td><td>Cold water, old toothbrush</td><td>Skip the dryer, always</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>White sneakers, back from the dead</summary><div class="body"><p>Mix baking soda and a little laundry detergent into a paste. Scrub with a toothbrush, wipe, air dry stuffed with newspaper. Never machine-dry — heat warps the sole and yellows the foam.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Got them soaked?</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Blot, don't rub.</li><li>Stuff with crumpled newspaper to hold shape.</li><li>Air dry away from radiators.</li><li>Condition leather once dry.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Rotate and rest</summary><div class="body"><p>Give each pair a full day off between wears. Cedar shoe trees soak up sweat overnight and keep the shape honest. Two pairs worn alternately outlast four worn daily.</p></div></details>	<div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Everyday Shoe Care Cheat Sheet</h2><p class="muted t-small">Shoes die young from neglect, not miles, <regex>user_name>. Five minutes keeps them going for years.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Material</th><th>Clean with</th><th>Feed with</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Leather</td><td>Damp cloth, saddle soap</td><td>Cream polish monthly</td></tr><tr><td>Canvas</td><td>Soft brush + mild soap</td><td>Nothing — air dry</td></tr><tr><td>Suede</td><td>Suede brush, eraser</td><td>Protector spray</td></tr><tr><td>Mesh sneakers</td><td>Cold water, old toothbrush</td><td>Skip the dryer, always</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>White sneakers, back from the dead</summary><div class="body"><p>Mix baking soda and a little laundry detergent into a paste. Scrub with a toothbrush, wipe, air dry stuffed with newspaper. Never machine-dry — heat warps the sole and yellows the foam.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Got them soaked?</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Blot, don't rub.</li><li>Stuff with crumpled newspaper to hold shape.</li><li>Air dry away from radiators.</li><li>Condition leather once dry.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Rotate and rest</summary><div class="body"><p>Give each pair a full day off between wears. Cedar shoe trees soak up sweat overnight and keep the shape honest. Two pairs worn alternately outlast four worn daily.</p></div></details>	-
35.63.0	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Pet Feeding &amp; Water Cheat Sheet	<div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Everyday Pet Feeding &amp; Water Cheat Sheet</h2><p class="muted t-small">Measure, schedule, watch the bowl, <regex>user_name>. That's most of it.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Adult cat</span><span class="value">2<em class="unit"> meals/day</em></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Adult dog</span><span class="value">2<em class="unit"> meals/day</em></span></div></div><div class="callout info"><p><strong>Bag labels lie a little.</strong> They list the max for an active dog. Start at the low end, adjust by body condition, and ask your vet for your pet's target weight.</p></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Body condition in one glance</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Ribs: easy to feel, not see.</li><li>Waist: visible from above.</li><li>Belly: tucks up before the hind legs.</li></ul><p class="dim t-small">Free-feed only if your pet self-regulates. Most don't.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Gulper or scarf-and-barf</summary><div class="body"><p>Use a slow-feeder bowl, or scatter kibble on a baking sheet. For cats, raise the bowl slightly and split meals into three or four smaller servings.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Water rules</summary><div class="body"><p>Rough guide: dogs drink about 1 oz per pound per day; cats about 4–6 oz. Wash bowls daily — slimy bowls turn pets off drinking, and that's how urinary trouble starts.</p></div></details>	<div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Everyday Pet Feeding &amp; Water Cheat Sheet</h2><p class="muted t-small">Measure, schedule, watch the bowl, <regex>user_name>. That's most of it.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Adult cat</span><span class="value">2<em class="unit"> meals/day</em></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Adult dog</span><span class="value">2<em class="unit"> meals/day</em></span></div></div><div class="callout info"><p><strong>Bag labels lie a little.</strong> They list the max for an active dog. Start at the low end, adjust by body condition, and ask your vet for your pet's target weight.</p></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Body condition in one glance</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Ribs: easy to feel, not see.</li><li>Waist: visible from above.</li><li>Belly: tucks up before the hind legs.</li></ul><p class="dim t-small">Free-feed only if your pet self-regulates. Most don't.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Gulper or scarf-and-barf</summary><div class="body"><p>Use a slow-feeder bowl, or scatter kibble on a baking sheet. For cats, raise the bowl slightly and split meals into three or four smaller servings.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Water rules</summary><div class="body"><p>Rough guide: dogs drink about 1 oz per pound per day; cats about 4–6 oz. Wash bowls daily — slimy bowls turn pets off drinking, and that's how urinary trouble starts.</p></div></details>	-
35.0.1	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Bath Towel &amp; Linen Care Cheat Sheet	<div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Everyday Bath Towel &amp; Linen Care Cheat Sheet</h2><p class="muted t-small"><regex>user_name</regex>, musty towels are a washing problem, not a towel problem. Here's the fix.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Wash every</th><th>Temp</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Bath towels</td><td>3–4 uses</td><td>Warm or hot</td></tr><tr><td>Hand towels</td><td>2–3 days</td><td>Warm</td></tr><tr><td>Washcloths</td><td>After every use</td><td>Hot</td></tr><tr><td>Sheets</td><td>Weekly</td><td>Warm</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>The musty smell cure</summary><div class="body"><p>Fabric softener and dryer sheets coat fibers and trap odors. Skip them. Instead: wash hot with a half cup of white vinegar in the rinse, then dry fully. If it persists, a hot wash with a scoop of oxygen bleach resets the whole load.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Fluffy again</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Wash towels separately from jeans and zippers.</li><li>Don't overload — towels need room to tumble.</li><li>Dry on medium; pull out slightly damp.</li><li>One wool dryer ball beats any sheet.</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Why they feel stiff</summary><div class="body"><p>Usually too much detergent left behind. Try a hot water rinse cycle with nothing added, and cut your detergent to two-thirds of the cap. Front loaders need less than you think.</p></div></details>	<div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Everyday Bath Towel &amp; Linen Care Cheat Sheet</h2><p class="muted t-small">, musty towels are a washing problem, not a towel problem. Here's the fix.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Wash every</th><th>Temp</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Bath towels</td><td>3–4 uses</td><td>Warm or hot</td></tr><tr><td>Hand towels</td><td>2–3 days</td><td>Warm</td></tr><tr><td>Washcloths</td><td>After every use</td><td>Hot</td></tr><tr><td>Sheets</td><td>Weekly</td><td>Warm</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>The musty smell cure</summary><div class="body"><p>Fabric softener and dryer sheets coat fibers and trap odors. Skip them. Instead: wash hot with a half cup of white vinegar in the rinse, then dry fully. If it persists, a hot wash with a scoop of oxygen bleach resets the whole load.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Fluffy again</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Wash towels separately from jeans and zippers.</li><li>Don't overload — towels need room to tumble.</li><li>Dry on medium; pull out slightly damp.</li><li>One wool dryer ball beats any sheet.</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Why they feel stiff</summary><div class="body"><p>Usually too much detergent left behind. Try a hot water rinse cycle with nothing added, and cut your detergent to two-thirds of the cap. Front loaders need less than you think.</p></div></details>	-
35.1.1	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Home Office Ergonomics Cheat Sheet	<div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Everyday Home Office Ergonomics Cheat Sheet</h2><p class="muted t-small">Set it once, <regex>user_name>, then stop rearranging your spine to fit the furniture.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Body part</th><th>Where it goes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Eyes</td><td>Top of screen at eye level, an arm's length away</td></tr><tr><td>Elbows</td><td>~90°, wrists flat, not bent up</td></tr><tr><td>Knees</td><td>~90°, feet flat or on a footrest</td></tr><tr><td>Hips</td><td>Back against the chair, slight recline</td></tr><tr><td>Screen</td><td>Straight ahead, no twist in the neck</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Laptop-only survival kit</summary><div class="body"><p>Raise the laptop on a stack of books, then add a keyboard and mouse below it. A separate keyboard is the single cheapest fix for neck pain.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>The 20-20-20 rule for eyes</summary><div class="body"><p>Every 20 minutes, look 20 feet away for 20 seconds. Your eyes have muscles too, and they've been holding a plank.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Movement beats posture</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Stand every 30–45 minutes.</li><li>Take calls on your feet.</li><li>Stretch hip flexors and chest daily.</li><li>No perfect posture exists. Only changing ones.</li></ol></div></details>	<div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Everyday Home Office Ergonomics Cheat Sheet</h2><p class="muted t-small">Set it once, <regex>user_name>, then stop rearranging your spine to fit the furniture.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Body part</th><th>Where it goes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Eyes</td><td>Top of screen at eye level, an arm's length away</td></tr><tr><td>Elbows</td><td>~90°, wrists flat, not bent up</td></tr><tr><td>Knees</td><td>~90°, feet flat or on a footrest</td></tr><tr><td>Hips</td><td>Back against the chair, slight recline</td></tr><tr><td>Screen</td><td>Straight ahead, no twist in the neck</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Laptop-only survival kit</summary><div class="body"><p>Raise the laptop on a stack of books, then add a keyboard and mouse below it. A separate keyboard is the single cheapest fix for neck pain.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>The 20-20-20 rule for eyes</summary><div class="body"><p>Every 20 minutes, look 20 feet away for 20 seconds. Your eyes have muscles too, and they've been holding a plank.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Movement beats posture</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Stand every 30–45 minutes.</li><li>Take calls on your feet.</li><li>Stretch hip flexors and chest daily.</li><li>No perfect posture exists. Only changing ones.</li></ol></div></details>	-
35.2.1	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Tea Brewing Cheat Sheet	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Everyday Tea Brewing</h2><p class="muted">Leaf, water, time. Get those right and you're basically a tea sommelier. <regex>user_name</regex>, here's the cheat sheet.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Tea</th><th class="num">Temp</th><th class="num">Time</th><th>Scoop per 8 oz</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>White</td><td class="num">175&deg;F</td><td class="num">2&ndash;3 min</td><td>1.5 tsp</td></tr><tr><td>Green</td><td class="num">175&deg;F</td><td class="num">2&ndash;3 min</td><td>1 tsp</td></tr><tr><td>Oolong</td><td class="num">195&deg;F</td><td class="num">3&ndash;4 min</td><td>1 tsp</td></tr><tr><td>Black</td><td class="num">205&deg;F</td><td class="num">3&ndash;5 min</td><td>1 tsp</td></tr><tr><td>Herbal</td><td class="num">205&deg;F</td><td class="num">5&ndash;7 min</td><td>1.5 tsp</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout info"><p><strong>No thermometer?</strong> Boil, then wait: 30 sec for green/white, 1 min for oolong, 2 min for black/herbal. Close enough.</p></div><details class="acc"><summary>Re-steeping rules</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Whole-leaf teas give 2&ndash;4 good cups.</li><li>Add 30 sec each round.</li><li>Broken fannings? One steep, done.</li></ul></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Everyday Tea Brewing</h2><p class="muted">Leaf, water, time. Get those right and you're basically a tea sommelier. , here's the cheat sheet.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Tea</th><th class="num">Temp</th><th class="num">Time</th><th>Scoop per 8 oz</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>White</td><td class="num">175&deg;F</td><td class="num">2&ndash;3 min</td><td>1.5 tsp</td></tr><tr><td>Green</td><td class="num">175&deg;F</td><td class="num">2&ndash;3 min</td><td>1 tsp</td></tr><tr><td>Oolong</td><td class="num">195&deg;F</td><td class="num">3&ndash;4 min</td><td>1 tsp</td></tr><tr><td>Black</td><td class="num">205&deg;F</td><td class="num">3&ndash;5 min</td><td>1 tsp</td></tr><tr><td>Herbal</td><td class="num">205&deg;F</td><td class="num">5&ndash;7 min</td><td>1.5 tsp</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout info"><p><strong>No thermometer?</strong> Boil, then wait: 30 sec for green/white, 1 min for oolong, 2 min for black/herbal. Close enough.</p></div><details class="acc"><summary>Re-steeping rules</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Whole-leaf teas give 2&ndash;4 good cups.</li><li>Add 30 sec each round.</li><li>Broken fannings? One steep, done.</li></ul></div></details></div>	-
35.3.1	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Hangover Recovery Cheat Sheet	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Everyday Hangover Recovery</h2><p class="muted">Not a cure, but a damage-control kit. <regex>user_name</regex>, hydrate like it's your job.</p><div class="callout warn"><p>Hair of the dog is a myth. More alcohol just delays the crash.</p></div><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Move</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Water + electrolytes</td><td>Alcohol is a diuretic; replace salt and potassium.</td></tr><tr><td>Bland carbs</td><td>Toast, crackers, rice settle the stomach.</td></tr><tr><td>Banana or coconut water</td><td>Potassium helps headaches and fatigue.</td></tr><tr><td>Ibuprofen with food</td><td>For pain &mdash; skip Tylenol/Acetaminophen, hard on the liver.</td></tr><tr><td>Nap, not a marathon</td><td>Sleep 20&ndash;90 min; longer can grog you up.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class="acc"><summary>Prevention, next time</summary><div class="body"><p>Alternate one drink with one water. Eat before and during. Clear spirits and light beer are gentler than dark, sugary mixes.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Everyday Hangover Recovery</h2><p class="muted">Not a cure, but a damage-control kit. , hydrate like it's your job.</p><div class="callout warn"><p>Hair of the dog is a myth. More alcohol just delays the crash.</p></div><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Move</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Water + electrolytes</td><td>Alcohol is a diuretic; replace salt and potassium.</td></tr><tr><td>Bland carbs</td><td>Toast, crackers, rice settle the stomach.</td></tr><tr><td>Banana or coconut water</td><td>Potassium helps headaches and fatigue.</td></tr><tr><td>Ibuprofen with food</td><td>For pain &mdash; skip Tylenol/Acetaminophen, hard on the liver.</td></tr><tr><td>Nap, not a marathon</td><td>Sleep 20&ndash;90 min; longer can grog you up.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class="acc"><summary>Prevention, next time</summary><div class="body"><p>Alternate one drink with one water. Eat before and during. Clear spirits and light beer are gentler than dark, sugary mixes.</p></div></details></div>	-
35.4.1	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Stretching Cheat Sheet	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Everyday Stretching</h2><p class="muted">Five minutes, head to toe. <regex>user_name</regex>, no need to touch your toes on day one.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Move</th><th class="num">Hold</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Neck</td><td>Slow ear-to-shoulder tilt</td><td class="num">20 sec/side</td></tr><tr><td>Shoulders</td><td>Cross-body arm hug</td><td class="num">30 sec/side</td></tr><tr><td>Back</td><td>Cat&ndash;cow on all fours</td><td class="num">8 breaths</td></tr><tr><td>Hips</td><td>Kneeling hip flexor lunge</td><td class="num">30 sec/side</td></tr><tr><td>Hamstrings</td><td>Seated forward fold, soft knee</td><td class="num">45 sec</td></tr><tr><td>Calves</td><td>Wall push, heel down</td><td class="num">30 sec/side</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout good"><p><strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> mild pull, never sharp pain. Breathe out as you sink in.</p></div><details class="acc"><summary>Static vs dynamic</summary><div class="body"><p>Before a workout: dynamic (leg swings, arm circles). After or any time for tension: static holds.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Everyday Stretching</h2><p class="muted">Five minutes, head to toe. , no need to touch your toes on day one.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Move</th><th class="num">Hold</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Neck</td><td>Slow ear-to-shoulder tilt</td><td class="num">20 sec/side</td></tr><tr><td>Shoulders</td><td>Cross-body arm hug</td><td class="num">30 sec/side</td></tr><tr><td>Back</td><td>Cat&ndash;cow on all fours</td><td class="num">8 breaths</td></tr><tr><td>Hips</td><td>Kneeling hip flexor lunge</td><td class="num">30 sec/side</td></tr><tr><td>Hamstrings</td><td>Seated forward fold, soft knee</td><td class="num">45 sec</td></tr><tr><td>Calves</td><td>Wall push, heel down</td><td class=