35.7.1	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Public Restroom Etiquette Cheat Sheet	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Everyday Public Restroom Etiquette</h2><p class="muted">Small courtesies, big difference. <regex>user_name</regex>, the unwritten rules, written down.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Move</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Empty row of stalls</td><td>Leave one stall between you and the next person.</td></tr><tr><td>Line forming</td><td>One queue for all, not one per stall.</td></tr><tr><td>Someone knocks</td><td>"Occupied" or a knock back. No essays.</td></tr><tr><td>No toilet paper</td><td>Ask a neighbor politely; return the favor.</td></tr><tr><td>Messy sink</td><td>Wipe the counter with a paper towel.</td></tr><tr><td>Phone call</td><td>Wrap it up. Nobody needs the play-by-play.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout good"><p><strong>Wash hands:</strong> 20 seconds, soap, backs of hands, thumbs, under nails. Sing "Happy Birthday" twice.</p></div><details class="acc"><summary>Flush first or sit first?</summary><div class="body"><p>Flush first to clear the bowl, then do your thing. And close the lid if there is one &mdash; aerosol clouds are real.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Everyday Public Restroom Etiquette</h2><p class="muted">Small courtesies, big difference. , the unwritten rules, written down.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Move</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Empty row of stalls</td><td>Leave one stall between you and the next person.</td></tr><tr><td>Line forming</td><td>One queue for all, not one per stall.</td></tr><tr><td>Someone knocks</td><td>"Occupied" or a knock back. No essays.</td></tr><tr><td>No toilet paper</td><td>Ask a neighbor politely; return the favor.</td></tr><tr><td>Messy sink</td><td>Wipe the counter with a paper towel.</td></tr><tr><td>Phone call</td><td>Wrap it up. Nobody needs the play-by-play.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout good"><p><strong>Wash hands:</strong> 20 seconds, soap, backs of hands, thumbs, under nails. Sing "Happy Birthday" twice.</p></div><details class="acc"><summary>Flush first or sit first?</summary><div class="body"><p>Flush first to clear the bowl, then do your thing. And close the lid if there is one &mdash; aerosol clouds are real.</p></div></details></div>	-
35.8.1	html_cheat_sheet/Fitted Sheet Folding Cheat Sheet	<div class="umi-page">  <h1 class="t-5">Fitted Sheet Folding Cheat Sheet</h1>  <p class="muted">Hey <regex>user_name</regex> — no shame in the fitted-sheet struggle. Here's the calm version.</p>  <div class="card">    <h2 class="card-title">The 4-Step Fold</h2>    <ol class="list">      <li><strong>Find the corners.</strong> Hold two adjacent corners of the sheet, right sides together. Let the rest dangle.</li>      <li><strong>Tuck one into the other.</strong> Pull the top corner over the bottom one so both elastic corners nest inside each other.</li>      <li><strong>Repeat on the other side.</strong> Now you have two corner-bundles. Fold one bundle over the other so all four corners are stacked.</li>      <li><strong>Lay flat and fold.</strong> Smooth the sheet into a rectangle, then fold in thirds like a letter. Done.</li>    </ol>  </div>  <div class="callout info">    <p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> If you're short on time, fold it into a loose rectangle and shove it in the linen closet. It still works.</p>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Why does this work?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Nesting the corners eliminates the awkward elastic pouches. Once they're all together, the sheet behaves like a flat rectangle.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1 class="t-5">Fitted Sheet Folding Cheat Sheet</h1>  <p class="muted">Hey  — no shame in the fitted-sheet struggle. Here's the calm version.</p>  <div class="card">    <h2 class="card-title">The 4-Step Fold</h2>    <ol class="list">      <li><strong>Find the corners.</strong> Hold two adjacent corners of the sheet, right sides together. Let the rest dangle.</li>      <li><strong>Tuck one into the other.</strong> Pull the top corner over the bottom one so both elastic corners nest inside each other.</li>      <li><strong>Repeat on the other side.</strong> Now you have two corner-bundles. Fold one bundle over the other so all four corners are stacked.</li>      <li><strong>Lay flat and fold.</strong> Smooth the sheet into a rectangle, then fold in thirds like a letter. Done.</li>    </ol>  </div>  <div class="callout info">    <p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> If you're short on time, fold it into a loose rectangle and shove it in the linen closet. It still works.</p>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Why does this work?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Nesting the corners eliminates the awkward elastic pouches. Once they're all together, the sheet behaves like a flat rectangle.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
35.9.1	html_cheat_sheet/Fresh Herb Storage Cheat Sheet	<div class="umi-page">  <h1 class="t-5">Fresh Herb Storage Cheat Sheet</h1>  <p class="muted">Hey <regex>user_name</regex> — stop letting your herbs turn to slime. Here's how to keep them perky.</p>  <div class="table-wrap">    <table>      <thead><tr><th>Herb Type</th><th>Storage Method</th><th>Expected Life</th></tr></thead>      <tbody>        <tr><td>Soft herbs (basil, cilantro, parsley)</td><td>Trim stems, stand in a glass of water, cover loosely with a plastic bag, refrigerate.</td><td>1–2 weeks</td></tr>        <tr><td>Hard herbs (rosemary, thyme, oregano)</td><td>Wrap in a barely damp paper towel, seal in a zip-top bag, refrigerate.</td><td>2–3 weeks</td></tr>        <tr><td>Basil (special case)</td><td>Keep at room temperature in water, like flowers. Do not refrigerate.</td><td>1 week</td></tr>        <tr><td>Dill, mint</td><td>Same as soft herbs — water glass + bag.</td><td>1–2 weeks</td></tr>      </tbody>    </table>  </div>  <div class="callout good">    <p><strong>Bonus:</strong> Freeze chopped herbs in olive oil in an ice cube tray. Pop one into a pan whenever you need a flavor boost.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1 class="t-5">Fresh Herb Storage Cheat Sheet</h1>  <p class="muted">Hey  — stop letting your herbs turn to slime. Here's how to keep them perky.</p>  <div class="table-wrap">    <table>      <thead><tr><th>Herb Type</th><th>Storage Method</th><th>Expected Life</th></tr></thead>      <tbody>        <tr><td>Soft herbs (basil, cilantro, parsley)</td><td>Trim stems, stand in a glass of water, cover loosely with a plastic bag, refrigerate.</td><td>1–2 weeks</td></tr>        <tr><td>Hard herbs (rosemary, thyme, oregano)</td><td>Wrap in a barely damp paper towel, seal in a zip-top bag, refrigerate.</td><td>2–3 weeks</td></tr>        <tr><td>Basil (special case)</td><td>Keep at room temperature in water, like flowers. Do not refrigerate.</td><td>1 week</td></tr>        <tr><td>Dill, mint</td><td>Same as soft herbs — water glass + bag.</td><td>1–2 weeks</td></tr>      </tbody>    </table>  </div>  <div class="callout good">    <p><strong>Bonus:</strong> Freeze chopped herbs in olive oil in an ice cube tray. Pop one into a pan whenever you need a flavor boost.</p>  </div></div>	-
35.10.1	html_cheat_sheet/Red Wine Stain Removal Cheat Sheet	<div class="umi-page">  <h1 class="t-5">Red Wine Stain Removal Cheat Sheet</h1>  <p class="muted">Hey <regex>user_name</regex> — wine happens. Here's how to make it un-happen.</p>  <div class="card">    <h2 class="card-title">Act Fast: The Blot &amp; Dilute Method</h2>    <ol class="list">      <li><strong>Blot, don't rub.</strong> Use a clean white cloth or paper towel to press down and lift as much wine as possible.</li>      <li><strong>Dilute with cold water.</strong> Pour a little cold water on the stain, then blot again. Repeat until the water runs clear.</li>      <li><strong>Apply a cleaning solution.</strong> Mix 1 tbsp dish soap, 1 tbsp white vinegar, and 2 cups cold water. Sponge onto the stain, blot, repeat.</li>      <li><strong>Rinse and dry.</strong> Sponge with clean water, blot dry, then place a stack of paper towels over the area and weigh it down. Let it air-dry.</li>    </ol>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What if the stain is already dry?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Soak the area with the cleaning solution for 10–15 minutes, then blot. You may need to repeat a few times. For old stains, a 50/50 mix of hydrogen peroxide and water can help, but test on a hidden spot first.</p>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout warn">    <p><strong>Avoid:</strong> Hot water, which sets the stain, and colored cloths, which can transfer dye.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1 class="t-5">Red Wine Stain Removal Cheat Sheet</h1>  <p class="muted">Hey  — wine happens. Here's how to make it un-happen.</p>  <div class="card">    <h2 class="card-title">Act Fast: The Blot &amp; Dilute Method</h2>    <ol class="list">      <li><strong>Blot, don't rub.</strong> Use a clean white cloth or paper towel to press down and lift as much wine as possible.</li>      <li><strong>Dilute with cold water.</strong> Pour a little cold water on the stain, then blot again. Repeat until the water runs clear.</li>      <li><strong>Apply a cleaning solution.</strong> Mix 1 tbsp dish soap, 1 tbsp white vinegar, and 2 cups cold water. Sponge onto the stain, blot, repeat.</li>      <li><strong>Rinse and dry.</strong> Sponge with clean water, blot dry, then place a stack of paper towels over the area and weigh it down. Let it air-dry.</li>    </ol>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What if the stain is already dry?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Soak the area with the cleaning solution for 10–15 minutes, then blot. You may need to repeat a few times. For old stains, a 50/50 mix of hydrogen peroxide and water can help, but test on a hidden spot first.</p>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout warn">    <p><strong>Avoid:</strong> Hot water, which sets the stain, and colored cloths, which can transfer dye.</p>  </div></div>	-
35.11.1	html_cheat_sheet/Thank You Note Cheat Sheet	<div class="umi-page">  <h1 class="t-5">Thank You Note Cheat Sheet</h1>  <p class="muted">Hey <regex>user_name</regex> — a good thank you note is short, specific, and sincere. Here's the formula.</p>  <div class="card">    <h2 class="card-title">The 3-Part Structure</h2>    <ol class="list">      <li><strong>Greeting:</strong> "Dear [Name],"</li>      <li><strong>Express gratitude + specific detail:</strong> "Thank you so much for the beautiful scarf. The colors are perfect for fall."</li>      <li><strong>Mention impact or future use:</strong> "I can't wait to wear it on chilly walks."</li>      <li><strong>Sign-off:</strong> "With love, [Your Name]" or "Warmly, [Your Name]"</li>    </ol>  </div>  <div class="callout info">    <p><strong>For gifts of money:</strong> Mention what you plan to use it for. "Thank you for your generous gift. I'm putting it toward a new laptop for school."</p>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>How long should it be?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Three to five sentences is plenty. The key is to be specific — mention the gift or gesture, and how it made you feel or how you'll use it.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1 class="t-5">Thank You Note Cheat Sheet</h1>  <p class="muted">Hey  — a good thank you note is short, specific, and sincere. Here's the formula.</p>  <div class="card">    <h2 class="card-title">The 3-Part Structure</h2>    <ol class="list">      <li><strong>Greeting:</strong> "Dear [Name],"</li>      <li><strong>Express gratitude + specific detail:</strong> "Thank you so much for the beautiful scarf. The colors are perfect for fall."</li>      <li><strong>Mention impact or future use:</strong> "I can't wait to wear it on chilly walks."</li>      <li><strong>Sign-off:</strong> "With love, [Your Name]" or "Warmly, [Your Name]"</li>    </ol>  </div>  <div class="callout info">    <p><strong>For gifts of money:</strong> Mention what you plan to use it for. "Thank you for your generous gift. I'm putting it toward a new laptop for school."</p>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>How long should it be?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Three to five sentences is plenty. The key is to be specific — mention the gift or gesture, and how it made you feel or how you'll use it.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
35.12.1	html_cheat_sheet/Suitcase Packing Cheat Sheet	<div class="umi-page">  <h1 class="t-5">Suitcase Packing Cheat Sheet</h1>  <p class="muted">Hey <regex>user_name</regex> — pack like a pro and skip the checked bag. Here's how.</p>  <div class="card">    <h2 class="card-title">The Roll &amp; Cube Method</h2>    <ul class="list">      <li><strong>Roll clothes:</strong> Rolling saves space and reduces wrinkles. Fold shirts in half, then roll tightly.</li>      <li><strong>Use packing cubes:</strong> One cube for tops, one for bottoms, one for underwear/socks. Keeps everything organized.</li>      <li><strong>Stuff shoes:</strong> Put socks and small items inside shoes to save space.</li>      <li><strong>Heaviest items at the bottom:</strong> Near the wheels, so the bag stands upright without tipping.</li>      <li><strong>Liquids in a clear bag:</strong> Keep at the top for easy access at security.</li>    </ul>  </div>  <div class="callout good">    <p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Lay out everything you think you need, then remove 25%. You won't miss it.</p>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What about wrinkle-prone clothes?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Fold them flat and place them at the top of the suitcase, or use tissue paper between layers. Hang them in the bathroom while you shower to steam out creases.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1 class="t-5">Suitcase Packing Cheat Sheet</h1>  <p class="muted">Hey  — pack like a pro and skip the checked bag. Here's how.</p>  <div class="card">    <h2 class="card-title">The Roll &amp; Cube Method</h2>    <ul class="list">      <li><strong>Roll clothes:</strong> Rolling saves space and reduces wrinkles. Fold shirts in half, then roll tightly.</li>      <li><strong>Use packing cubes:</strong> One cube for tops, one for bottoms, one for underwear/socks. Keeps everything organized.</li>      <li><strong>Stuff shoes:</strong> Put socks and small items inside shoes to save space.</li>      <li><strong>Heaviest items at the bottom:</strong> Near the wheels, so the bag stands upright without tipping.</li>      <li><strong>Liquids in a clear bag:</strong> Keep at the top for easy access at security.</li>    </ul>  </div>  <div class="callout good">    <p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Lay out everything you think you need, then remove 25%. You won't miss it.</p>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What about wrinkle-prone clothes?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Fold them flat and place them at the top of the suitcase, or use tissue paper between layers. Hang them in the bathroom while you shower to steam out creases.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
35.13.1	html_cheat_sheet/Cast Iron Care Cheat Sheet	<div class="umi-page">  <h1 class="t-5">Cast Iron Care Cheat Sheet</h1>  <p class="muted">Hey <regex>user_name</regex> — cast iron is nearly indestructible, but a little love keeps it nonstick and rust-free.</p>  <div class="card">    <h2 class="card-title">Daily Care</h2>    <ul class="list">      <li><strong>Wash:</strong> Use hot water and a stiff brush or chainmail scrubber. Avoid soap unless it's really gunky (modern dish soap won't strip seasoning).</li>      <li><strong>Dry immediately:</strong> Towel dry, then place on a low burner for a minute to evaporate all moisture.</li>      <li><strong>Oil:</strong> Rub a thin layer of flaxseed or canola oil over the entire surface while warm.</li>      <li><strong>Store:</strong> Keep in a dry place. If stacking, put a paper towel between pans.</li>    </ul>  </div>  <div class="callout warn">    <p><strong>Never:</strong> Soak in water, put in the dishwasher, or leave wet. Rust is the enemy.</p>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>How to fix rust?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Scrub with steel wool and a little oil until rust is gone. Wash, dry, and re-season: coat with oil and bake upside down at 450°F for an hour. Repeat a few times.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1 class="t-5">Cast Iron Care Cheat Sheet</h1>  <p class="muted">Hey  — cast iron is nearly indestructible, but a little love keeps it nonstick and rust-free.</p>  <div class="card">    <h2 class="card-title">Daily Care</h2>    <ul class="list">      <li><strong>Wash:</strong> Use hot water and a stiff brush or chainmail scrubber. Avoid soap unless it's really gunky (modern dish soap won't strip seasoning).</li>      <li><strong>Dry immediately:</strong> Towel dry, then place on a low burner for a minute to evaporate all moisture.</li>      <li><strong>Oil:</strong> Rub a thin layer of flaxseed or canola oil over the entire surface while warm.</li>      <li><strong>Store:</strong> Keep in a dry place. If stacking, put a paper towel between pans.</li>    </ul>  </div>  <div class="callout warn">    <p><strong>Never:</strong> Soak in water, put in the dishwasher, or leave wet. Rust is the enemy.</p>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>How to fix rust?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Scrub with steel wool and a little oil until rust is gone. Wash, dry, and re-season: coat with oil and bake upside down at 450°F for an hour. Repeat a few times.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
35.14.1	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Kitchen Food Safety Cheat Sheet	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Food Safety at a Glance</h3>  <p class="muted t-small">Hey <regex>user_name</regex> — cold things cold, hot things hot, and the clock is always running.</p>  <div class="table-wrap">    <table>      <thead><tr><th>Zone</th><th class="num">Temp</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead>      <tbody>        <tr><td>Danger zone</td><td class="num">40–140&nbsp;°F</td><td>Max 2 hours out</td></tr>        <tr><td>Fridge</td><td class="num">≤ 40&nbsp;°F</td><td>Check weekly</td></tr>        <tr><td>Freezer</td><td class="num">0&nbsp;°F</td><td>Label + date</td></tr>      </tbody>    </table>  </div>  <div class="callout info"><p>Two hours, not "two-ish." Halve it to one hour if the room is over 90&nbsp;°F.</p></div></div><details class="acc">  <summary>Safe internal cooking temps</summary>  <div class="body">    <ul class="list">      <li>Poultry (all of it): <strong>165&nbsp;°F</strong></li>      <li>Ground meats: <strong>160&nbsp;°F</strong></li>      <li>Beef, pork, lamb steaks: <strong>145&nbsp;°F</strong> + 3 min rest</li>      <li>Fish: <strong>145&nbsp;°F</strong> or opaque and flaking</li>      <li>Leftovers, reheated: <strong>165&nbsp;°F</strong></li>    </ul>  </div></details><details class="acc">  <summary>How long leftovers actually last</summary>  <div class="body">    <div class="table-wrap"><table>      <thead><tr><th>Item</th><th class="num">Fridge</th><th class="num">Freezer</th></tr></thead>      <tbody>        <tr><td>Cooked rice</td><td class="num">1 day</td><td class="num">1 mo</td></tr>        <tr><td>Soup / stew</td><td class="num">3–4 days</td><td class="num">2–3 mo</td></tr>        <tr><td>Cooked chicken</td><td class="num">3–4 days</td><td class="num">4 mo</td></tr>        <tr><td>Opened deli meat</td><td class="num">3–5 days</td><td class="num">1–2 mo</td></tr>      </tbody>    </table></div>  </div></details><details class="acc">  <summary>When in doubt, toss it</summary>  <div class="body">    <ul class="list">      <li>Smell or sliminess you have to argue yourself out of</li>      <li>Anything left out overnight</li>      <li>Fridge above 40&nbsp;°F for more than 4 hours</li>      <li>Mold on soft foods — the roots run deep, unlike hard cheese where you can cut an inch away</li>    </ul>    <p class="dim t-small">Your nose is a decent advisor, but never the only one.</p>  </div></details>	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Food Safety at a Glance</h3>  <p class="muted t-small">Hey  — cold things cold, hot things hot, and the clock is always running.</p>  <div class="table-wrap">    <table>      <thead><tr><th>Zone</th><th class="num">Temp</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead>      <tbody>        <tr><td>Danger zone</td><td class="num">40–140&nbsp;°F</td><td>Max 2 hours out</td></tr>        <tr><td>Fridge</td><td class="num">≤ 40&nbsp;°F</td><td>Check weekly</td></tr>        <tr><td>Freezer</td><td class="num">0&nbsp;°F</td><td>Label + date</td></tr>      </tbody>    </table>  </div>  <div class="callout info"><p>Two hours, not "two-ish." Halve it to one hour if the room is over 90&nbsp;°F.</p></div></div><details class="acc">  <summary>Safe internal cooking temps</summary>  <div class="body">    <ul class="list">      <li>Poultry (all of it): <strong>165&nbsp;°F</strong></li>      <li>Ground meats: <strong>160&nbsp;°F</strong></li>      <li>Beef, pork, lamb steaks: <strong>145&nbsp;°F</strong> + 3 min rest</li>      <li>Fish: <strong>145&nbsp;°F</strong> or opaque and flaking</li>      <li>Leftovers, reheated: <strong>165&nbsp;°F</strong></li>    </ul>  </div></details><details class="acc">  <summary>How long leftovers actually last</summary>  <div class="body">    <div class="table-wrap"><table>      <thead><tr><th>Item</th><th class="num">Fridge</th><th class="num">Freezer</th></tr></thead>      <tbody>        <tr><td>Cooked rice</td><td class="num">1 day</td><td class="num">1 mo</td></tr>        <tr><td>Soup / stew</td><td class="num">3–4 days</td><td class="num">2–3 mo</td></tr>        <tr><td>Cooked chicken</td><td class="num">3–4 days</td><td class="num">4 mo</td></tr>        <tr><td>Opened deli meat</td><td class="num">3–5 days</td><td class="num">1–2 mo</td></tr>      </tbody>    </table></div>  </div></details><details class="acc">  <summary>When in doubt, toss it</summary>  <div class="body">    <ul class="list">      <li>Smell or sliminess you have to argue yourself out of</li>      <li>Anything left out overnight</li>      <li>Fridge above 40&nbsp;°F for more than 4 hours</li>      <li>Mold on soft foods — the roots run deep, unlike hard cheese where you can cut an inch away</li>    </ul>    <p class="dim t-small">Your nose is a decent advisor, but never the only one.</p>  </div></details>	-
35.15.1	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Laundry Symbols Cheat Sheet	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Your Tag, Decoded</h3>  <p class="muted t-small">Those little hieroglyphs, <regex>user_name</regex>, in the order they appear: wash, bleach, dry, iron, dry clean.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat"><div class="label">Washtub</div><div class="value">Water</div></div>    <div class="stat"><div class="label">Triangle</div><div class="value">Bleach</div></div>    <div class="stat"><div class="label">Square + circle</div><div class="value">Tumble dry</div></div>    <div class="stat"><div class="label">Iron</div><div class="value">Heat</div></div>    <div class="stat"><div class="label">Circle</div><div class="value">Dry clean</div></div>    <div class="stat"><div class="label">Crossed out</div><div class="value">Never do it</div></div>  </div></div><details class="acc">  <summary>The numbers inside the tub</summary>  <div class="body">    <ul class="list">      <li><strong>No number:</strong> machine wash, normal, any temp</li>      <li><strong>One dot / 30:</strong> cold — 65–85&nbsp;°F</li>      <li><strong>Two dots / 40:</strong> warm — 105&nbsp;°F</li>      <li><strong>Three dots / 60:</strong> hot — 120&nbsp;°F</li>      <li><strong>Hand in tub:</strong> hand wash only, don't be brave</li>    </ul>  </div></details><details class="acc">  <summary>Tumble dry dots and dashes</summary>  <div class="body">    <ul class="list">      <li>One dot: low heat. Two: medium. Three: high.</li>      <li>Line underneath: permanent press — slightly cooler, shorter.</li>      <li>Two lines: gentle cycle, no heat.</li>      <li>Crossed out: line dry instead.</li>    </ul>  </div></details><details class="acc">  <summary>Iron heat, dot by dot</summary>  <div class="body">    <ul class="list">      <li>One dot (•): 230&nbsp;°F — acrylic, nylon, silk</li>      <li>Two dots (••): 300&nbsp;°F — wool, polyester</li>      <li>Three dots (•••): 390&nbsp;°F — cotton, linen</li>      <li>Crossed out: skip it, steam instead</li>    </ul>  </div></details><div class="callout warn"><p>If the label is worn smooth, treat it as gentle and cold. Fewer regrets.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Your Tag, Decoded</h3>  <p class="muted t-small">Those little hieroglyphs, , in the order they appear: wash, bleach, dry, iron, dry clean.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat"><div class="label">Washtub</div><div class="value">Water</div></div>    <div class="stat"><div class="label">Triangle</div><div class="value">Bleach</div></div>    <div class="stat"><div class="label">Square + circle</div><div class="value">Tumble dry</div></div>    <div class="stat"><div class="label">Iron</div><div class="value">Heat</div></div>    <div class="stat"><div class="label">Circle</div><div class="value">Dry clean</div></div>    <div class="stat"><div class="label">Crossed out</div><div class="value">Never do it</div></div>  </div></div><details class="acc">  <summary>The numbers inside the tub</summary>  <div class="body">    <ul class="list">      <li><strong>No number:</strong> machine wash, normal, any temp</li>      <li><strong>One dot / 30:</strong> cold — 65–85&nbsp;°F</li>      <li><strong>Two dots / 40:</strong> warm — 105&nbsp;°F</li>      <li><strong>Three dots / 60:</strong> hot — 120&nbsp;°F</li>      <li><strong>Hand in tub:</strong> hand wash only, don't be brave</li>    </ul>  </div></details><details class="acc">  <summary>Tumble dry dots and dashes</summary>  <div class="body">    <ul class="list">      <li>One dot: low heat. Two: medium. Three: high.</li>      <li>Line underneath: permanent press — slightly cooler, shorter.</li>      <li>Two lines: gentle cycle, no heat.</li>      <li>Crossed out: line dry instead.</li>    </ul>  </div></details><details class="acc">  <summary>Iron heat, dot by dot</summary>  <div class="body">    <ul class="list">      <li>One dot (•): 230&nbsp;°F — acrylic, nylon, silk</li>      <li>Two dots (••): 300&nbsp;°F — wool, polyester</li>      <li>Three dots (•••): 390&nbsp;°F — cotton, linen</li>      <li>Crossed out: skip it, steam instead</li>    </ul>  </div></details><div class="callout warn"><p>If the label is worn smooth, treat it as gentle and cold. Fewer regrets.</p></div>	-
35.16.1	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Medicine Cabinet Cheat Sheet	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Stock It Once, Thank Yourself Later</h3>  <p class="muted t-small">A boring cabinet, <regex>user_name</regex>, is a good cabinet. Restock twice a year.</p>  <div class="table-wrap">    <table>      <thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Reach for</th></tr></thead>      <tbody>        <tr><td>Pain, fever</td><td>Acetaminophen <em>or</em> ibuprofen</td></tr>        <tr><td>Small cut</td><td>Soap, water, gauze, bandage</td></tr>        <tr><td>Minor burn</td><td>Cool running water 10–20 min</td></tr>        <tr><td>Allergy, itch</td><td>Antihistamine, hydrocortisone</td></tr>        <tr><td>Stomach upset</td><td>Antacid, ORS packet</td></tr>      </tbody>    </table>  </div></div><details class="acc">  <summary>Expiry dates: what actually matters</summary>  <div class="body">    <ul class="list">      <li>Liquids and eye drops: take the date seriously.</li>      <li>Pills: usually still fine, just weaker. Replace annually anyway.</li>      <li>Anything cloudy, discoloured, or crumbling: bin it.</li>      <li>EpiPens and inhalers: check the dose counter <em>and</em> the date.</li>    </ul>  </div></details><details class="acc">  <summary>Four red flags — call for help</summary>  <div class="body">    <ul class="list">      <li>Burn larger than your palm, or any burn on face, hands, joints</li>      <li>Cut that won't stop bleeding after 10 min of firm pressure</li>      <li>Fever with stiff neck, confusion, or a rash that doesn't blanch</li>      <li>Trouble breathing, chest pain, one-sided weakness</li>    </ul>    <div class="callout bad"><p>Emergency numbers: know your local one before you need it. US/Canada 911, UK 999, EU 112.</p></div>  </div></details><div class="callout info"><p>Keep aspirin on hand for suspected heart attack — chew one unless allergic. A pharmacist can confirm anything else.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <h3 