35.18.1	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Hand Washing & Germs Cheat Sheet	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Twenty Seconds, Huge Payoff</h3>  <p class="muted t-small">It's the cheapest health upgrade there is, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p>  <div class="row between">    <div><div class="label muted t-small">Recommended scrub</div><div class="value">20 sec</div></div>    <div><div class="label muted t-small">Happy birthday, twice</div><div class="value">≈ tempo</div></div>  </div></div><details class="acc" open>  <summary>The six steps</summary>  <div class="body">    <ol class="list">      <li>Wet hands, apply soap.</li>      <li>Palm to palm, lather up.</li>      <li>Weave fingers, rub between them.</li>      <li>Backs of hands and thumbs — the usual skip.</li>      <li>Fingertips and nails against your palm.</li>      <li>Rinse, then dry on a clean towel. Damp hands spread more germs.</li>    </ol>  </div></details><details class="acc">  <summary>When soap beats sanitizer</summary>  <div class="body">    <div class="table-wrap"><table>      <thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Best tool</th></tr></thead>      <tbody>        <tr><td>Visible dirt, grease</td><td>Soap + water</td></tr>        <tr><td>After toilet, before food</td><td>Soap + water</td></tr>        <tr><td>Norovirus, C. diff risk</td><td>Soap + water, always</td></tr>        <tr><td>On a bus, no sink</td><td>60%+ sanitizer</td></tr>      </tbody>    </table></div>  </div></details><div class="callout warn"><p>Sanitizer is a great understudy, not the lead. Alcohol doesn't budge norovirus.</p></div><details class="acc">  <summary>High-touch spots people forget</summary>  <div class="body">    <ul class="list">      <li>Phone screen, laptop, keyboard</li>      <li>Car keys, wallet, door handles</li>      <li>Faucet and kettle handles — clean them weekly</li>    </ul>  </div></details>	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Twenty Seconds, Huge Payoff</h3>  <p class="muted t-small">It's the cheapest health upgrade there is, .</p>  <div class="row between">    <div><div class="label muted t-small">Recommended scrub</div><div class="value">20 sec</div></div>    <div><div class="label muted t-small">Happy birthday, twice</div><div class="value">≈ tempo</div></div>  </div></div><details class="acc" open>  <summary>The six steps</summary>  <div class="body">    <ol class="list">      <li>Wet hands, apply soap.</li>      <li>Palm to palm, lather up.</li>      <li>Weave fingers, rub between them.</li>      <li>Backs of hands and thumbs — the usual skip.</li>      <li>Fingertips and nails against your palm.</li>      <li>Rinse, then dry on a clean towel. Damp hands spread more germs.</li>    </ol>  </div></details><details class="acc">  <summary>When soap beats sanitizer</summary>  <div class="body">    <div class="table-wrap"><table>      <thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Best tool</th></tr></thead>      <tbody>        <tr><td>Visible dirt, grease</td><td>Soap + water</td></tr>        <tr><td>After toilet, before food</td><td>Soap + water</td></tr>        <tr><td>Norovirus, C. diff risk</td><td>Soap + water, always</td></tr>        <tr><td>On a bus, no sink</td><td>60%+ sanitizer</td></tr>      </tbody>    </table></div>  </div></details><div class="callout warn"><p>Sanitizer is a great understudy, not the lead. Alcohol doesn't budge norovirus.</p></div><details class="acc">  <summary>High-touch spots people forget</summary>  <div class="body">    <ul class="list">      <li>Phone screen, laptop, keyboard</li>      <li>Car keys, wallet, door handles</li>      <li>Faucet and kettle handles — clean them weekly</li>    </ul>  </div></details>	-
35.19.1	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Ladder & Step Stool Safety Cheat Sheet	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Three Points, Always</h3>  <p class="muted t-small">Most home falls start with a stretch, <regex>user_name</regex>. Don't be the stretch.</p>  <div class="callout warn"><p>The top step and top cap are off-limits. So is the second-to-top of any ladder with a full-height warning printed on it.</p></div></div><details class="acc">  <summary>Before you climb — a 30-second check</summary>  <div class="body">    <ul class="list">      <li>Inspect for cracked rails, loose rungs, worn feet.</li>      <li>Feet flat, on level ground. No carpet under the legs if you can help it.</li>      <li>Never lean a ladder against a window or a gutter.</li>      <li>Lock both spreaders on an A-frame.</li>      <li>Set it within arm's reach of the job — not the job within arm's reach of you.</li>    </ul>  </div></details><details class="acc">  <summary>The angle rule (extension ladders)</summary>  <div class="body">    <p>Rise 1, run 4. For every 4&nbsp;ft of height, the base sits 1&nbsp;ft out. The old test: stand at the feet, arms out — hands should just touch the rung at shoulder height.</p>    <div class="table-wrap"><table>      <thead><tr><th>Ladder height</th><th class="num">Base distance</th></tr></thead>      <tbody>        <tr><td>12 ft</td><td class="num">3 ft</td></tr>        <tr><td>16 ft</td><td class="num">4 ft</td></tr>        <tr><td>20 ft</td><td class="num">5 ft</td></tr>      </tbody>    </table></div>  </div></details><details class="acc">  <summary>Climbing rules that matter</summary>  <div class="body">    <ul class="list">      <li>Face the ladder. Always.</li>      <li>Three points of contact — two feet, one hand.</li>      <li>Keep your belt buckle between the rails.</li>      <li>Move the ladder instead of leaning. Every single time.</li>      <li>No ladder work in wind or rain, no drinking first, no bare feet.</li>    </ul>  </div></details><div class="callout info"><p>Around power lines, use a fiberglass ladder — aluminium conducts. And keep 10&nbsp;ft of clearance.</p></div>	<div class="card">  <h3 class="card-title">Three Points, Always</h3>  <p class="muted t-small">Most home falls start with a stretch, . Don't be the stretch.</p>  <div class="callout warn"><p>The top step and top cap are off-limits. So is the second-to-top of any ladder with a full-height warning printed on it.</p></div></div><details class="acc">  <summary>Before you climb — a 30-second check</summary>  <div class="body">    <ul class="list">      <li>Inspect for cracked rails, loose rungs, worn feet.</li>      <li>Feet flat, on level ground. No carpet under the legs if you can help it.</li>      <li>Never lean a ladder against a window or a gutter.</li>      <li>Lock both spreaders on an A-frame.</li>      <li>Set it within arm's reach of the job — not the job within arm's reach of you.</li>    </ul>  </div></details><details class="acc">  <summary>The angle rule (extension ladders)</summary>  <div class="body">    <p>Rise 1, run 4. For every 4&nbsp;ft of height, the base sits 1&nbsp;ft out. The old test: stand at the feet, arms out — hands should just touch the rung at shoulder height.</p>    <div class="table-wrap"><table>      <thead><tr><th>Ladder height</th><th class="num">Base distance</th></tr></thead>      <tbody>        <tr><td>12 ft</td><td class="num">3 ft</td></tr>        <tr><td>16 ft</td><td class="num">4 ft</td></tr>        <tr><td>20 ft</td><td class="num">5 ft</td></tr>      </tbody>    </table></div>  </div></details><details class="acc">  <summary>Climbing rules that matter</summary>  <div class="body">    <ul class="list">      <li>Face the ladder. Always.</li>      <li>Three points of contact — two feet, one hand.</li>      <li>Keep your belt buckle between the rails.</li>      <li>Move the ladder instead of leaning. Every single time.</li>      <li>No ladder work in wind or rain, no drinking first, no bare feet.</li>    </ul>  </div></details><div class="callout info"><p>Around power lines, use a fiberglass ladder — aluminium conducts. And keep 10&nbsp;ft of clearance.</p></div>	-
