35.22.1	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Phone Battery Saving Cheat Sheet	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Make the Battery Last the Day</p>  <p class="muted">For <regex>user_name</regex>: the big three settings do most of the work.</p>  <div class="table-wrap">    <table>      <thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Turn it down</th><th>Gain</th></tr></thead>      <tbody>        <tr><td>Screen brightness</td><td class="num">50%</td><td class="num">High</td></tr>        <tr><td>Always-on display</td><td class="num">Off</td><td class="num">Medium</td></tr>        <tr><td>Background app refresh</td><td class="num">Wi-Fi only</td><td class="num">Medium</td></tr>        <tr><td>Location</td><td class="num">While using</td><td class="num">High</td></tr>        <tr><td>Push email</td><td class="num">Fetch hourly</td><td class="num">Low</td></tr>      </tbody>    </table>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Heat and cold beat software, every time</summary>    <div class="body">      <p class="t-small">Leave the phone out of direct sun and off the dashboard. Below 0°C or above 35°C, drain speeds up no matter what you toggle.</p>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout info">Battery health under <strong>80%</strong>? A replacement is usually cheaper than a new phone.</div></div>	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Make the Battery Last the Day</p>  <p class="muted">For : the big three settings do most of the work.</p>  <div class="table-wrap">    <table>      <thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Turn it down</th><th>Gain</th></tr></thead>      <tbody>        <tr><td>Screen brightness</td><td class="num">50%</td><td class="num">High</td></tr>        <tr><td>Always-on display</td><td class="num">Off</td><td class="num">Medium</td></tr>        <tr><td>Background app refresh</td><td class="num">Wi-Fi only</td><td class="num">Medium</td></tr>        <tr><td>Location</td><td class="num">While using</td><td class="num">High</td></tr>        <tr><td>Push email</td><td class="num">Fetch hourly</td><td class="num">Low</td></tr>      </tbody>    </table>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Heat and cold beat software, every time</summary>    <div class="body">      <p class="t-small">Leave the phone out of direct sun and off the dashboard. Below 0°C or above 35°C, drain speeds up no matter what you toggle.</p>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout info">Battery health under <strong>80%</strong>? A replacement is usually cheaper than a new phone.</div></div>	-
35.23.1	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Home Internet Router Reset & Wi-Fi Troubleshooting	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Wi-Fi Troubleshooting, In Order</p>  <p class="muted">Try these top to bottom, <regex>user_name</regex>. Most problems are step one or two.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Reboot the router.</strong> Unplug 30 seconds, plug back in, wait 2 minutes.</li>    <li><strong>Reboot the modem too</strong> — then the router, in that order.</li>    <li><strong>Move the router</strong> off the floor, out of a cabinet, away from the microwave.</li>    <li><strong>Switch bands.</strong> 5 GHz is faster up close; 2.4 GHz reaches farther.</li>  </ol>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Signal still weak in one room?</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>Lower the router's antenna to vertical if it's flat</li>        <li>Add a mesh node near the dead zone, not in it</li>        <li>Check for a firmware update in the router app</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout warn">If the modem light blinks amber for 10 minutes, it's the line — call your provider before buying anything.</div></div>	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Wi-Fi Troubleshooting, In Order</p>  <p class="muted">Try these top to bottom, . Most problems are step one or two.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Reboot the router.</strong> Unplug 30 seconds, plug back in, wait 2 minutes.</li>    <li><strong>Reboot the modem too</strong> — then the router, in that order.</li>    <li><strong>Move the router</strong> off the floor, out of a cabinet, away from the microwave.</li>    <li><strong>Switch bands.</strong> 5 GHz is faster up close; 2.4 GHz reaches farther.</li>  </ol>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Signal still weak in one room?</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>Lower the router's antenna to vertical if it's flat</li>        <li>Add a mesh node near the dead zone, not in it</li>        <li>Check for a firmware update in the router app</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout warn">If the modem light blinks amber for 10 minutes, it's the line — call your provider before buying anything.</div></div>	-
35.24.1	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Home Password & Login Hygiene	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Passwords, Minus the Panic</p>  <p class="muted">Five moves, <regex>user_name</regex>. Do them once and you're mostly done.</p>  <div class="table-wrap">    <table>      <thead><tr><th>Move</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>      <tbody>        <tr><td>Use a password manager</td><td>One strong password unlocks the rest</td></tr>        <tr><td>Turn on 2FA</td><td>Stolen password alone won't work</td></tr>        <tr><td>Unique password per site</td><td>A leak stays a leak</td></tr>        <tr><td>Long beats weird</td><td>Four random words &gt; <code>P@ss1</code></td></tr>        <tr><td>Check breaches yearly</td><td>Find out before the email does</td></tr>      </tbody>    </table>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Prefer app-based 2FA over SMS</summary>    <div class="body">      <p class="t-small">SIM swaps are real but uncommon. If SMS is all a site offers, use it — it still beats nothing.</p>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout good">Start with your email account. It's the master key to every reset link.</div></div>	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Passwords, Minus the Panic</p>  <p class="muted">Five moves, . Do them once and you're mostly done.</p>  <div class="table-wrap">    <table>      <thead><tr><th>Move</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>      <tbody>        <tr><td>Use a password manager</td><td>One strong password unlocks the rest</td></tr>        <tr><td>Turn on 2FA</td><td>Stolen password alone won't work</td></tr>        <tr><td>Unique password per site</td><td>A leak stays a leak</td></tr>        <tr><td>Long beats weird</td><td>Four random words &gt; <code>P@ss1</code></td></tr>        <tr><td>Check breaches yearly</td><td>Find out before the email does</td></tr>      </tbody>    </table>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Prefer app-based 2FA over SMS</summary>    <div class="body">      <p class="t-small">SIM swaps are real but uncommon. If SMS is all a site offers, use it — it still beats nothing.</p>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout good">Start with your email account. It's the master key to every reset link.</div></div>	-
35.25.1	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Smoke & Carbon Monoxide Alarm Upkeep	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Alarms That Actually Work</p>  <p class="muted">A 10-second job, <regex>user_name</regex>. Here's the cadence.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat"><span class="label">Test</span><span class="value">Monthly</span></div>    <div class="stat"><span class="label">Replace unit</span><span class="value">10 <span class="unit">yrs</span></span></div>  </div>  <div class="table-wrap">    <table>      <thead><tr><th>Sound</th><th>Means</th><th>Do</th></tr></thead>      <tbody>        <tr><td>One chirp/min</td><td>Low battery</td><td>Swap battery</td></tr>        <tr><td>Rapid chirps</td><td>End of life</td><td>Replace unit</td></tr>        <tr><td>Continuous</td><td>Alarm</td><td>Get out, call 911</td></tr>      </tbody>    </table>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Where they belong</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>One in every bedroom</li>        <li>One outside each sleeping area</li>        <li>One on every floor, including the basement</li>        <li>CO alarm near bedrooms, not in the garage</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout bad">Never paint over an alarm or cover it "just for the night." That's how the news story starts.</div></div>	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Alarms That Actually Work</p>  <p class="muted">A 10-second job, . Here's the cadence.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat"><span class="label">Test</span><span class="value">Monthly</span></div>    <div class="stat"><span class="label">Replace unit</span><span class="value">10 <span class="unit">yrs</span></span></div>  </div>  <div class="table-wrap">    <table>      <thead><tr><th>Sound</th><th>Means</th><th>Do</th></tr></thead>      <tbody>        <tr><td>One chirp/min</td><td>Low battery</td><td>Swap battery</td></tr>        <tr><td>Rapid chirps</td><td>End of life</td><td>Replace unit</td></tr>        <tr><td>Continuous</td><td>Alarm</td><td>Get out, call 911</td></tr>      </tbody>    </table>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Where they belong</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>One in every bedroom</li>        <li>One outside each sleeping area</li>        <li>One on every floor, including the basement</li>        <li>CO alarm near bedrooms, not in the garage</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout bad">Never paint over an alarm or cover it "just for the night." That's how the news story starts.</div></div>	-
35.26.1	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Small Talk Cheat Sheet	<div class="stack">  <p class="t-small muted">For <regex>user_name</regex> — small talk is just curiosity with training wheels. Here's your pocket guide.</p>  <div class="card">    <h3 class="card-title">The 3-Second Rule</h3>    <p>If you hesitate, the moment gets awkward. Count to three and say anything—even “I like your jacket.” Momentum beats polish.</p>  </div>  <div class="card flat">    <h3 class="card-title">Openers That Work</h3>    <ul class="list">      <li><strong>Contextual:</strong> “This coffee line is wild today.”</li>      <li><strong>Compliment:</strong> “That pin is great—where's it from?”</li>      <li><strong>Helpful:</strong> “I think you dropped this.”</li>    </ul>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>If you blank: the ARE method</summary>    <div class="body">      <p><strong>A</strong>nchor (comment on something shared), <strong>R</strong>eveal (share a tiny thing about you), <strong>E</strong>ncourage (ask an open question). Example: “This venue is loud. I'm more of a quiet-bar person. How do you know the host?”</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Exit gracefully</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>“I'm going to grab another drink—great talking to you.” Smile, move. No apology needed.</p>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout info">    <p class="t-small">Remember: most people are relieved someone else started. You're doing them a favor.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="stack">  <p class="t-small muted">For  — small talk is just curiosity with training wheels. Here's your pocket guide.</p>  <div class="card">    <h3 class="card-title">The 3-Second Rule</h3>    <p>If you hesitate, the moment gets awkward. Count to three and say anything—even “I like your jacket.” Momentum beats polish.</p>  </div>  <div class="card flat">    <h3 class="card-title">Openers That Work</h3>    <ul class="list">      <li><strong>Contextual:</strong> “This coffee line is wild today.”</li>      <li><strong>Compliment:</strong> “That pin is great—where's it from?”</li>      <li><strong>Helpful:</strong> “I think you dropped this.”</li>    </ul>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>If you blank: the ARE method</summary>    <div class="body">      <p><strong>A</strong>nchor (comment on something shared), <strong>R</strong>eveal (share a tiny thing about you), <strong>E</strong>ncourage (ask an open question). Example: “This venue is loud. I'm more of a quiet-bar person. How do you know the host?”</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Exit gracefully</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>“I'm going to grab another drink—great talking to you.” Smile, move. No apology needed.</p>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout info">    <p class="t-small">Remember: most people are relieved someone else started. You're doing them a favor.</p>  </div></div>	-
35.27.1	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Drain Unclogging Cheat Sheet	<div class="stack">  <p class="t-small muted">For <regex>user_name</regex> — before you call a plumber, try these steps in order.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat">      <span class="label">First try</span>      <span class="value">Boiling water</span>    </div>    <div class="stat">      <span class="label">Then</span>      <span class="value">Plunger</span>    </div>  </div>  <div class="card">    <h3 class="card-title">Step 1: Boiling Water</h3>    <p>Pour a kettle of boiling water down the drain in stages. Wait 30 seconds between pours. Works for grease and soap scum.</p>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Step 2: Plunger (the right way)</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Block the overflow hole with a wet rag. Use a cup plunger (not flange) and plunge 15–20 times with force. Hold tight.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Step 3: Baking soda + vinegar</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Pour 1/2 cup baking soda, then 1/2 cup vinegar. Cover with a plug for 15 min, then flush with hot water.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Step 4: Drain snake</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Feed a hand snake into the drain, crank when you feel resistance, pull out gunk. Wear gloves.</p>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout warn">    <p class="t-small">Avoid chemical drain cleaners—they damage pipes and are dangerous if they splash back.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="stack">  <p class="t-small muted">For  — before you call a plumber, try these steps in order.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat">      <span class="label">First try</span>      <span class="value">Boiling water</span>    </div>    <div class="stat">      <span class="label">Then</span>      <span class="value">Plunger</span>    </div>  </div>  <div class="card">    <h3 class="card-title">Step 1: Boiling Water</h3>    <p>Pour a kettle of boiling water down the drain in stages. Wait 30 seconds between pours. Works for grease and soap scum.</p>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Step 2: Plunger (the right way)</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Block the overflow hole with a wet rag. Use a cup plunger (not flange) and plunge 15–20 times with force. Hold tight.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Step 3: Baking soda + vinegar</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Pour 1/2 cup baking soda, then 1/2 cup vinegar. Cover with a plug for 15 min, then flush with hot water.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Step 4: Drain snake</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Feed a hand snake into the drain, crank when you feel resistance, pull out gunk. Wear gloves.</p>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout warn">    <p class="t-small">Avoid chemical drain cleaners—they damage pipes and are dangerous if they splash back.</p>  </div></div>	-
35.28.1	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Smoothie Making Cheat Sheet	<div class="stack">  <p class="t-small muted">For <regex>user_name</regex> — blend like you mean it.</p>  <div class="card">    <h3 class="card-title">The Golden Ratio</h3>    <ul class="list">      <li><strong>1 cup</strong> liquid (milk, water, juice)</li>      <li><strong>1 cup</strong> leafy greens (optional)</li>      <li><strong>1 cup</strong> frozen fruit</li>      <li><strong>1/2</strong> banana or avocado for creaminess</li>      <li><strong>1 scoop</strong> protein powder or nut butter</li>    </ul>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Order matters</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Liquid first, then soft ingredients, then frozen on top. This prevents air pockets and blade jams.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Fix it fast</summary>    <div class="body">      <p><strong>Too thick?</strong> Add liquid 2 tbsp at a time. <strong>Too thin?</strong> Add frozen fruit or ice. <strong>Chunky?</strong> Blend longer—at least 60 seconds.</p>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout good">    <p class="t-small">Pro move: freeze leftover smoothie in ice cube trays. Reblend later for instant breakfast.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="stack">  <p class="t-small muted">For  — blend like you mean it.</p>  <div class="card">    <h3 class="card-title">The Golden Ratio</h3>    <ul class="list">      <li><strong>1 cup</strong> liquid (milk, water, juice)</li>      <li><strong>1 cup</strong> leafy greens (optional)</li>      <li><strong>1 cup</strong> frozen fruit</li>      <li><strong>1/2</strong> banana or avocado for creaminess</li>      <li><strong>1 scoop</strong> protein powder or nut butter</li>    </ul>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Order matters</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Liquid first, then soft ingredients, then frozen on top. This prevents air pockets and blade jams.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Fix it fast</summary>    <div class="body">      <p><strong>Too thick?</strong> Add liquid 2 tbsp at a time. <strong>Too thin?</strong> Add frozen fruit or ice. <strong>Chunky?</strong> Blend longer—at least 60 seconds.</p>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout good">    <p class="t-small">Pro move: freeze leftover smoothie in ice cube trays. Reblend later for instant breakfast.</p>  </div></div>	-
35.29.1	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Tire Changing Cheat Sheet	<div class="stack">  <p class="t-small muted">For <regex>user_name</regex> — flat tire? You've got this. Safety first.</p>  <div class="callout warn">    <p class="t-small"><strong>Pull over safely.</strong> Turn on hazards, find flat ground, engage parking brake.</p>  </div>  <div class="timeline">    <ul class="list">      <li><span class="when">Step 1</span> Gather tools: jack, lug wrench, spare, and (if you have it) wheel wedges.</li>      <li><span class="when">Step 2</span> Loosen lug nuts <em>before</em> jacking. Turn counterclockwise just a quarter turn.</li>      <li><span class="when">Step 3</span> Place jack under the frame notch near the flat tire. Raise until tire is off the ground.</li>      <li><span class="when">Step 4</span> Remove lug nuts, pull tire straight off. Put the spare on and hand-tighten nuts in a star pattern.</li>      <li><span class="when">Step 5</span> Lower the jack, then tighten nuts fully in a star pattern. Check spare pressure soon.</li>    </ul>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>If you're on a slope</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Put the car in Park (or 1st gear) and use wheel wedges on the opposite tire. If the slope is steep, call for help—it's not worth the risk.</p>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout info">    <p class="t-small">Spare tires are usually temporary (50 miles max at 50 mph). Get the flat repaired or replaced ASAP.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="stack">  <p class="t-small muted">For  — flat tire? You've got this. Safety first.</p>  <div class="callout warn">    <p class="t-small"><strong>Pull over safely.</strong> Turn on hazards, find flat ground, engage parking brake.</p>  </div>  <div class="timeline">    <ul class="list">      <li><span class="when">Step 1</span> Gather tools: jack, lug wrench, spare, and (if you have it) wheel wedges.</li>      <li><span class="when">Step 2</span> Loosen lug nuts <em>before</em> jacking. Turn counterclockwise just a quarter turn.</li>      <li><span class="when">Step 3</span> Place jack under the frame notch near the flat tire. Raise until tire is off the ground.</li>      <li><span class="when">Step 4</span> Remove lug nuts, pull tire straight off. Put the spare on and hand-tighten nuts in a star pattern.</li>      <li><span class="when">Step 5</span> Lower the jack, then tighten nuts fully in a star pattern. Check spare pressure soon.</li>    </ul>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>If you're on a slope</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Put the car in Park (or 1st gear) and use wheel wedges on the opposite tire. If the slope is steep, call for help—it's not worth the risk.</p>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout info">    <p class="t-small">Spare tires are usually temporary (50 miles max at 50 mph). Get the flat repaired or replaced ASAP.</p>  </div></div>	-
35.30.1	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Tie Tying Cheat Sheet	<div class="stack">  <p class="t-small muted">For <regex>user_name</regex> — the Four-in-Hand is your friend. Simple, slightly asymmetrical, always works.</p>  <div class="card">    <h3 class="card-title">Four-in-Hand Knot (Steps)</h3>    <ol class="list">      <li>Drape tie around neck, seam side down. Wide end on your dominant side, about 12 inches lower.</li>      <li>Cross wide end over narrow end.</li>      <li>Bring wide end under and up through the loop around your neck.</li>      <li>Cross wide end in front of the knot.</li>      <li>Pull wide end up through the loop again.</li>      <li>Slide wide end through the front knot loop.</li>      <li>Tighten by pulling wide end down while holding the knot.</li>    </ol>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Windsor vs. Four-in-Hand</summary>    <div class="body">      <p><strong>Windsor</strong> is wider and more formal—good for spread collars. <strong>Four-in-Hand</strong> is narrower and casual—works with most collars.</p>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout good">    <p class="t-small">Tie length should hit your belt buckle. If it's too short, the narrow end is too high. Start over with more length.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="stack">  <p class="t-small muted">For  — the Four-in-Hand is your friend. Simple, slightly asymmetrical, always works.</p>  <div class="card">    <h3 class="card-title">Four-in-Hand Knot (Steps)</h3>    <ol class="list">      <li>Drape tie around neck, seam side down. Wide end on your dominant side, about 12 inches lower.</li>      <li>Cross wide end over narrow end.</li>      <li>Bring wide end under and up through the loop around your neck.</li>      <li>Cross wide end in front of the knot.</li>      <li>Pull wide end up through the loop again.</li>      <li>Slide wide end through the front knot loop.</li>      <li>Tighten by pulling wide end down while holding the knot.</li>    </ol>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Windsor vs. Four-in-Hand</summary>    <div class="body">      <p><strong>Windsor</strong> is wider and more formal—good for spread collars. <strong>Four-in-Hand</strong> is narrower and casual—works with most collars.</p>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout good">    <p class="t-small">Tie length should hit your belt buckle. If it's too short, the narrow end is too high. Start over with more length.</p>  </div></div>	-
35.31.1	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday T-Shirt Folding Cheat Sheet	<div class="stack">  <p class="t-small muted">For <regex>user_name</regex> — two folds, no wrinkles, more drawer space.</p>  <div class="card">    <h3 class="card-title">The 3-Point Fold (fastest)</h3>    <ol class="list">      <li>Lay shirt flat, front down.</li>      <li>Pinch the left shoulder, then the right shoulder, then the bottom center.</li>      <li>Lift all three points and shake gently. The shirt folds itself.</li>      <li>Lay down, fold sleeves under, and you're done.</li>    </ol>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>KonMari vertical fold</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Fold shirt in half lengthwise, tuck sleeves in, then fold in thirds. Stand it upright in the drawer so you see every shirt at a glance.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>No-iron trick</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Fold straight from the dryer while still warm. The residual heat releases wrinkles.</p>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout info">    <p class="t-small">Keep a donation box nearby. If you haven't worn it in a year, it's not folding—it's just visiting.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="stack">  <p class="t-small muted">For  — two folds, no wrinkles, more drawer space.</p>  <div class="card">    <h3 class="card-title">The 3-Point Fold (fastest)</h3>    <ol class="list">      <li>Lay shirt flat, front down.</li>      <li>Pinch the left shoulder, then the right shoulder, then the bottom center.</li>      <li>Lift all three points and shake gently. The shirt folds itself.</li>      <li>Lay down, fold sleeves under, and you're done.</li>    </ol>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>KonMari vertical fold</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Fold shirt in half lengthwise, tuck sleeves in, then fold in thirds. Stand it upright in the drawer so you see every shirt at a glance.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>No-iron trick</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Fold straight from the dryer while still warm. The residual heat releases wrinkles.</p>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout info">    <p class="t-small">Keep a donation box nearby. If you haven't worn it in a year, it's not folding—it's just visiting.</p>  </div></div>	-
35.32.1	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Hand Sewing & Button Repair Cheat Sheet	<div class="umi-page"><p class="muted">Alright, <regex>user_name</regex> — five stitches cover almost every repair you'll meet.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Stitch</th><th>Use it for</th><th>Seam strength</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Running</td><td>Basting, quick tucks</td><td class="num">Low</td></tr><tr><td>Backstitch</td><td>Seams that must hold</td><td class="num">Highest</td></tr><tr><td>Whip</td><td>Closing a gap edge-to-edge</td><td class="num">Medium</td></tr><tr><td>Blind/ladder</td><td>Hems, invisible finishes</td><td class="num">Medium</td></tr><tr><td>Blanket</td><td>Fraying raw edges</td><td class="num">Low</td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class="acc"><summary>Button, step by step</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Mark the spot with a pin through the old holes.</li><li>Double thread, knot the end, take one anchor stitch.</li><li>Come up through hole A, down through B, 4–6 passes.</li><li>For thick fabric, sew over a toothpick to leave a thread shank.</li><li>Wrap the shank 3 times, then knot underneath and snip.</li></ol></div></details><div class="callout warn"><strong>Thread length:</strong> no longer than your forearm. Longer snarls every time.</div><p class="dim t-small">Match thread to the fabric, not your mood. Dark navy reads black on most garments.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><p class="muted">Alright,  — five stitches cover almost every repair you'll meet.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Stitch</th><th>Use it for</th><th>Seam strength</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Running</td><td>Basting, quick tucks</td><td class="num">Low</td></tr><tr><td>Backstitch</td><td>Seams that must hold</td><td class="num">Highest</td></tr><tr><td>Whip</td><td>Closing a gap edge-to-edge</td><td class="num">Medium</td></tr><tr><td>Blind/ladder</td><td>Hems, invisible finishes</td><td class="num">Medium</td></tr><tr><td>Blanket</td><td>Fraying raw edges</td><td class="num">Low</td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class="acc"><summary>Button, step by step</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Mark the spot with a pin through the old holes.</li><li>Double thread, knot the end, take one anchor stitch.</li><li>Come up through hole A, down through B, 4–6 passes.</li><li>For thick fabric, sew over a toothpick to leave a thread shank.</li><li>Wrap the shank 3 times, then knot underneath and snip.</li></ol></div></details><div class="callout warn"><strong>Thread length:</strong> no longer than your forearm. Longer snarls every time.</div><p class="dim t-small">Match thread to the fabric, not your mood. Dark navy reads black on most garments.</p></div>	-
35.33.1	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Coffee Grind & Brew Ratio Cheat Sheet	<div class="umi-page"><p class="muted">Here's the whole map, <regex>user_name</regex>. Grind and ratio, nothing else.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Grind</th><th>Ratio by weight</th><th>Water</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>French press</td><td>Coarse</td><td class="num">1:15</td><td>200°F</td></tr><tr><td>Drip</td><td>Medium</td><td class="num">1:16</td><td>195–205°F</td></tr><tr><td>Pour over</td><td>Medium-fine</td><td class="num">1:16</td><td>200°F</td></tr><tr><td>AeroPress</td><td>Fine</td><td class="num">1:13</td><td>185°F</td></tr><tr><td>Espresso</td><td>Very fine</td><td class="num">1:2</td><td>200°F</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Sour / thin</span><span class="value">Grind finer</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Bitter / harsh</span><span class="value">Grind coarser</span></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Weigh it once, forever</summary><div class="body"><p>30 g coffee, 480 g water, done. A $12 scale beats any upgrade you can buy.</p></div></details><p class="dim t-small">Water matters more than you'd like. If it tastes flat, try filtered.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><p class="muted">Here's the whole map, . Grind and ratio, nothing else.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Grind</th><th>Ratio by weight</th><th>Water</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>French press</td><td>Coarse</td><td class="num">1:15</td><td>200°F</td></tr><tr><td>Drip</td><td>Medium</td><td class="num">1:16</td><td>195–205°F</td></tr><tr><td>Pour over</td><td>Medium-fine</td><td class="num">1:16</td><td>200°F</td></tr><tr><td>AeroPress</td><td>Fine</td><td class="num">1:13</td><td>185°F</td></tr><tr><td>Espresso</td><td>Very fine</td><td class="num">1:2</td><td>200°F</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Sour / thin</span><span class="value">Grind finer</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Bitter / harsh</span><span class="value">Grind coarser</span></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Weigh it once, forever</summary><div class="body"><p>30 g coffee, 480 g water, done. A $12 scale beats any upgrade you can buy.</p></div></details><p class="dim t-small">Water matters more than you'd like. If it tastes flat, try filtered.</p></div>	-
35.34.1	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Ice & Snow Car Prep Cheat Sheet	<div class="umi-page"><p class="muted">Cold morning? Work in this order, <regex>user_name</regex>, and it's five minutes, not twenty.</p><ol class="list"><li>Start the car, full defrost, A/C on (yes, in winter — it dries the air).</li><li>Scrape flat surfaces first; never pour hot water on glass.</li><li>Lift wipers off the glass before you scrape. Don't yank them free.</li><li>Clear the roof and hood. That snow lands on someone at 40 mph.</li><li>De-ice locks with a lighter-warm key, not force.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Winter trunk kit</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Scraper + brush combo, spare gloves</li><li>Blanket, wool hat, hand warmers</li><li>Bag of sand or cat litter (traction)</li><li>Jumper cables, flashlight, phone cable</li><li>Granola bars and a bottle of water</li><li>Small folding shovel</li></ul></div></details><div class="callout bad"><strong>Not safe:</strong> idling in an unplowed spot with the tailpipe buried. Carbon monoxide backs up. Clear the pipe first.</div><p class="dim t-small">Stuck in snow? Straighten the wheels, rock gently between drive and reverse, and don't spin. Spinning digs you deeper.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><p class="muted">Cold morning? Work in this order, , and it's five minutes, not twenty.</p><ol class="list"><li>Start the car, full defrost, A/C on (yes, in winter — it dries the air).</li><li>Scrape flat surfaces first; never pour hot water on glass.</li><li>Lift wipers off the glass before you scrape. Don't yank them free.</li><li>Clear the roof and hood. That snow lands on someone at 40 mph.</li><li>De-ice locks with a lighter-warm key, not force.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Winter trunk kit</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Scraper + brush combo, spare gloves</li><li>Blanket, wool hat, hand warmers</li><li>Bag of sand or cat litter (traction)</li><li>Jumper cables, flashlight, phone cable</li><li>Granola bars and a bottle of water</li><li>Small folding shovel</li></ul></div></details><div class="callout bad"><strong>Not safe:</strong> idling in an unplowed spot with the tailpipe buried. Carbon monoxide backs up. Clear the pipe first.</div><p class="dim t-small">Stuck in snow? Straighten the wheels, rock gently between drive and reverse, and don't spin. Spinning digs you deeper.</p></div>	-
35.35.1	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Kitchen Knife & Cutting Board Care Cheat Sheet	<div class="umi-page"><p class="muted">Boards and knives live longer than most marriages if you do three things, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Wash</span><span class="value">By hand</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Oil</span><span class="value">Monthly</span></div></div><details class="acc" open><summary>Wood boards</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Warm water, mild soap, scrub with the grain.</li><li>Stand on edge to dry so both faces dry evenly.</li><li>Never soak, never dishwasher — heat warps and splits.</li><li>Mineral oil when it looks pale and thirsty. Food-safe oil only; olive oil goes rancid.</li><li>Deodorize: coarse salt + half a lemon, rub, rinse.</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Knives</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Dishwasher = hot water, detergent, and banging against other things. Skip it.</li><li>Wash, dry immediately. Carbon steel rusts while you blink.</li><li>Hone every few uses; sharpen a few times a year.</li><li>Store on a magnet strip or in a block, blade covered.</li></ul></div></details><div class="callout info"><strong>Sanity check:</strong> deep cuts and grooves in any board hide bacteria. Sand wood, replace plastic.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><p class="muted">Boards and knives live longer than most marriages if you do three things, .</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Wash</span><span class="value">By hand</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Oil</span><span class="value">Monthly</span></div></div><details class="acc" open><summary>Wood boards</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Warm water, mild soap, scrub with the grain.</li><li>Stand on edge to dry so both faces dry evenly.</li><li>Never soak, never dishwasher — heat warps and splits.</li><li>Mineral oil when it looks pale and thirsty. Food-safe oil only; olive oil goes rancid.</li><li>Deodorize: coarse salt + half a lemon, rub, rinse.</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Knives</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Dishwasher = hot water, detergent, and banging against other things. Skip it.</li><li>Wash, dry immediately. Carbon steel rusts while you blink.</li><li>Hone every few uses; sharpen a few times a year.</li><li>Store on a magnet strip or in a block, blade covered.</li></ul></div></details><div class="callout info"><strong>Sanity check:</strong> deep cuts and grooves in any board hide bacteria. Sand wood, replace plastic.</div></div>	-
35.36.1	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Bathroom Fan & Moisture Cheat Sheet	<div class="umi-page"><p class="muted">Steam is the whole story here, <regex>user_name</regex>. Move it out and the mold never gets a foothold.</p><div class="card"><p class="card-title">Run time</p><p>Shower → fan on before you step in, off <strong>15–20 minutes after</strong> you leave. A timer switch ends the argument.</p></div><details class="acc"><summary>Is the fan actually pulling air?</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Hold a tissue to the grille. It should stick.</li><li>Close the door, run the shower 2 minutes, check for lingering fog.</li><li>Clean the grille and blade yearly — dust kills airflow.</li><li>Check that the roof/flap vent outside swings freely.</li></ol></div></details><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Likely fix</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Mirror fogs anyway</td><td>Fan undersized or ducted badly</td></tr><tr><td>Rattling</td><td>Debris on blade</td></tr><tr><td>Weak at grille</td><td>Clogged duct or stuck flap</td></tr><tr><td>Damp walls long after</td><td>Leak or no exhaust path</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout good"><strong>Free win:</strong> squeegee the walls after every shower. Costs ten seconds, buys you years.</div><p class="dim t-small">No window, no fan? Crack the door and run a small fan pulling air outward.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><p class="muted">Steam is the whole story here, . Move it out and the mold never gets a foothold.</p><div class="card"><p class="card-title">Run time</p><p>Shower → fan on before you step in, off <strong>15–20 minutes after</strong> you leave. A timer switch ends the argument.</p></div><details class="acc"><summary>Is the fan actually pulling air?</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Hold a tissue to the grille. It should stick.</li><li>Close the door, run the shower 2 minutes, check for lingering fog.</li><li>Clean the grille and blade yearly — dust kills airflow.</li><li>Check that the roof/flap vent outside swings freely.</li></ol></div></details><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Likely fix</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Mirror fogs anyway</td><td>Fan undersized or ducted badly</td></tr><tr><td>Rattling</td><td>Debris on blade</td></tr><tr><td>Weak at grille</td><td>Clogged duct or stuck flap</td></tr><tr><td>Damp walls long after</td><td>Leak or no exhaust path</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout good"><strong>Free win:</strong> squeegee the walls after every shower. Costs ten seconds, buys you years.</div><p class="dim t-small">No window, no fan? Crack the door and run a small fan pulling air outward.</p></div>	-
35.37.1	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Spice Shelf Restocking Cheat Sheet	<div class="umi-page"><p class="muted">Two lists, <regex>user_name</regex>: the ten that earn their shelf, and the rule for when to let one go.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Whole spice</span><span class="value">3–4<unit>yr</unit></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Ground spice</span><span class="value">1–2<unit>yr</unit></span></div></div><details class="acc" open><summary>The core ten</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Kosher salt, black peppercorns</li><li>Garlic powder, onion powder</li><li>Paprika (sweet or smoked)</li><li>Cumin, chili powder</li><li>Oregano, thyme, bay leaves</li><li>Cinnamon, vanilla extract</li></ul></div></details><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Spice</th><th>Fades fastest</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ground cumin</td><td>Often</td></tr><tr><td>Ground cinnamon</td><td>Often</td></tr><tr><td>Dried herbs</td><td>Quickly</td></tr><tr><td>Whole peppercorns</td><td>Slowly</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout warn"><strong>Dead spice test:</strong> rub a pinch between your fingers. If you can't smell it clearly, neither can your dinner.</div><p class="dim t-small">Store away from the stove, the window, and the steam. Heat and light are the enemies, not time alone.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><p class="muted">Two lists, : the ten that earn their shelf, and the rule for when to let one go.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Whole spice</span><span class="value">3–4<unit>yr</unit></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Ground spice</span><span class="value">1–2<unit>yr</unit></span></div></div><details class="acc" open><summary>The core ten</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Kosher salt, black peppercorns</li><li>Garlic powder, onion powder</li><li>Paprika (sweet or smoked)</li><li>Cumin, chili powder</li><li>Oregano, thyme, bay leaves</li><li>Cinnamon, vanilla extract</li></ul></div></details><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Spice</th><th>Fades fastest</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ground cumin</td><td>Often</td></tr><tr><td>Ground cinnamon</td><td>Often</td></tr><tr><td>Dried herbs</td><td>Quickly</td></tr><tr><td>Whole peppercorns</td><td>Slowly</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout warn"><strong>Dead spice test:</strong> rub a pinch between your fingers. If you can't smell it clearly, neither can your dinner.</div><p class="dim t-small">Store away from the stove, the window, and the steam. Heat and light are the enemies, not time alone.</p></div>	-
35.38.1	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Kitchen Timer & Oven Temperature Cheat Sheet	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Oven Temperatures, <regex>user_name</regex></p>  <p class="muted">Gas marks are the UK shortcut. Fahrenheit is the US default. Celsius hides in between.</p>  <div class="table-wrap">    <table>      <thead><tr><th>°C</th><th class="num">°F</th><th class="num">Gas</th><th>Typical use</th></tr></thead>      <tbody>        <tr><td>140</td><td class="num">275</td><td class="num">1</td><td>Slow bake, meringue</td></tr>        <tr><td>160</td><td class="num">325</td><td class="num">3</td><td>Fruitcake, casseroles</td></tr>        <tr><td>180</td><td class="num">350</td><td class="num">4</td><td>Cookies, sheet cake</td></tr>        <tr><td>200</td><td class="num">400</td><td class="num">6</td><td>Roast veg, pizza</td></tr>        <tr><td>220</td><td class="num">425</td><td class="num">7</td><td>Bread crust, scones</td></tr>        <tr><td>240</td><td class="num">475</td><td class="num">9</td><td>High-heat sear</td></tr>      </tbody>    </table>  </div></div><details class="acc">  <summary>Quick mental math</summary>  <div class="body stack">    <p>C to F: double it, subtract 10%, add 32. So 180 &rarr; 360 &minus; 36 = 324 + 32 &asymp; 356. Close enough for a roast.</p>    <p>F to C: subtract 32, then halve and add a tenth. 350 &rarr; 318 &rarr; 159 + 16 &asymp; 175.</p>  </div></details><div class="callout warn">Fan ovens run hot. Drop 20°C / 25°F from any recipe written for a conventional oven.</div>	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Oven Temperatures, </p>  <p class="muted">Gas marks are the UK shortcut. Fahrenheit is the US default. Celsius hides in between.</p>  <div class="table-wrap">    <table>      <thead><tr><th>°C</th><th class="num">°F</th><th class="num">Gas</th><th>Typical use</th></tr></thead>      <tbody>        <tr><td>140</td><td class="num">275</td><td class="num">1</td><td>Slow bake, meringue</td></tr>        <tr><td>160</td><td class="num">325</td><td class="num">3</td><td>Fruitcake, casseroles</td></tr>        <tr><td>180</td><td class="num">350</td><td class="num">4</td><td>Cookies, sheet cake</td></tr>        <tr><td>200</td><td class="num">400</td><td class="num">6</td><td>Roast veg, pizza</td></tr>        <tr><td>220</td><td class="num">425</td><td class="num">7</td><td>Bread crust, scones</td></tr>        <tr><td>240</td><td class="num">475</td><td class="num">9</td><td>High-heat sear</td></tr>      </tbody>    </table>  </div></div><details class="acc">  <summary>Quick mental math</summary>  <div class="body stack">    <p>C to F: double it, subtract 10%, add 32. So 180 &rarr; 360 &minus; 36 = 324 + 32 &asymp; 356. Close enough for a roast.</p>    <p>F to C: subtract 32, then halve and add a tenth. 350 &rarr; 318 &rarr; 159 + 16 &asymp; 175.</p>  </div></details><div class="callout warn">Fan ovens run hot. Drop 20°C / 25°F from any recipe written for a conventional oven.</div>	-
35.39.1	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday First Aid Kit & Minor Injury Cheat Sheet	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Minor Cuts & Bumps, <regex>user_name</regex></p>  <p class="muted">Calm hands first. Most kitchen wounds need nothing more than water and a clean cloth.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li>Wash your hands before you touch anything else.</li>    <li>Rinse the wound under cool running water. No soap inside the cut.</li>    <li>Press with a clean cloth for five minutes. Don't peek.</li>    <li>Pat dry, then cover with a plaster or gauze.</li>    <li>Change the dressing daily, or when it gets wet.</li>  </ol>  <div class="table-wrap">    <table>      <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Do</th><th>Don't</th></tr></thead>      <tbody>        <tr><td>Scrape</td><td>Rinse and cover</td><td>Scrub at grit</td></tr>        <tr><td>Small burn</td><td>Cool water 10 min</td><td>Ice or butter</td></tr>        <tr><td>Nosebleed</td><td>Lean forward, pinch</td><td>Tilt head back</td></tr>        <tr><td>Bruise</td><td>Cold pack 15 min</td><td>Heat on day one</td></tr>      </tbody>    </table>  </div></div><details class="acc">  <summary>Restock list</summary>  <div class="body">    <p>Plasters in three sizes, gauze, medical tape, antiseptic wipes, tweezers, scissors, disposable gloves, burn gel, and a small torch.</p>  </div></details><div class="callout bad">Deep cuts, spurting blood, burns bigger than your palm, or anything on the eye: go get seen. That's not a plaster job.</div>	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Minor Cuts & Bumps, </p>  <p class="muted">Calm hands first. Most kitchen wounds need nothing more than water and a clean cloth.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li>Wash your hands before you touch anything else.</li>    <li>Rinse the wound under cool running water. No soap inside the cut.</li>    <li>Press with a clean cloth for five minutes. Don't peek.</li>    <li>Pat dry, then cover with a plaster or gauze.</li>    <li>Change the dressing daily, or when it gets wet.</li>  </ol>  <div class="table-wrap">    <table>      <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Do</th><th>Don't</th></tr></thead>      <tbody>        <tr><td>Scrape</td><td>Rinse and cover</td><td>Scrub at grit</td></tr>        <tr><td>Small burn</td><td>Cool water 10 min</td><td>Ice or butter</td></tr>        <tr><td>Nosebleed</td><td>Lean forward, pinch</td><td>Tilt head back</td></tr>        <tr><td>Bruise</td><td>Cold pack 15 min</td><td>Heat on day one</td></tr>      </tbody>    </table>  </div></div><details class="acc">  <summary>Restock list</summary>  <div class="body">    <p>Plasters in three sizes, gauze, medical tape, antiseptic wipes, tweezers, scissors, disposable gloves, burn gel, and a small torch.</p>  </div></details><div class="callout bad">Deep cuts, spurting blood, burns bigger than your palm, or anything on the eye: go get seen. That's not a plaster job.</div>	-
35.40.1	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Fridge & Freezer Storage Cheat Sheet	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Fridge Times, <regex>user_name</regex></p>  <p class="muted">Times assume your fridge sits at 4°C / 40°F or below. Warmer drawers shorten everything.</p>  <div class="table-wrap">    <table>      <thead><tr><th>Item</th><th class="num">Fridge</th><th class="num">Freezer</th></tr></thead>      <tbody>        <tr><td>Cooked chicken</td><td class="num">3&ndash;4 d</td><td class="num">4 mo</td></tr>        <tr><td>Cooked rice</td><td class="num">1 d</td><td class="num">1 mo</td></tr>        <tr><td>Opened milk</td><td class="num">5&ndash;7 d</td><td class="num">3 mo</td></tr>        <tr><td>Raw mince</td><td class="num">1&ndash;2 d</td><td class="num">3&ndash;4 mo</td></tr>        <tr><td>Soup / stew</td><td class="num">3&ndash;4 d</td><td class="num">3 mo</td></tr>        <tr><td>Hard cheese</td><td class="num">2&ndash;4 wk</td><td class="num">6 mo</td></tr>      </tbody>    </table>  </div></div><details class="acc">  <summary>Shelf order, top to bottom</summary>  <div class="body">    <p>Ready-to-eat, then dairy, then raw fish and meat, then veg drawers. Anything raw never sits above anything you'll eat cold.</p>  </div></details><div class="callout info">Cool leftovers within two hours, then refrigerate. Don't leave rice out overnight.</div>	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Fridge Times, </p>  <p class="muted">Times assume your fridge sits at 4°C / 40°F or below. Warmer drawers shorten everything.</p>  <div class="table-wrap">    <table>      <thead><tr><th>Item</th><th class="num">Fridge</th><th class="num">Freezer</th></tr></thead>      <tbody>        <tr><td>Cooked chicken</td><td class="num">3&ndash;4 d</td><td class="num">4 mo</td></tr>        <tr><td>Cooked rice</td><td class="num">1 d</td><td class="num">1 mo</td></tr>        <tr><td>Opened milk</td><td class="num">5&ndash;7 d</td><td class="num">3 mo</td></tr>        <tr><td>Raw mince</td><td class="num">1&ndash;2 d</td><td class="num">3&ndash;4 mo</td></tr>        <tr><td>Soup / stew</td><td class="num">3&ndash;4 d</td><td class="num">3 mo</td></tr>        <tr><td>Hard cheese</td><td class="num">2&ndash;4 wk</td><td class="num">6 mo</td></tr>      </tbody>    </table>  </div></div><details class="acc">  <summary>Shelf order, top to bottom</summary>  <div class="body">    <p>Ready-to-eat, then dairy, then raw fish and meat, then veg drawers. Anything raw never sits above anything you'll eat cold.</p>  </div></details><div class="callout info">Cool leftovers within two hours, then refrigerate. Don't leave rice out overnight.</div>	-
35.41.1	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Ironing & Wrinkle Removal Cheat Sheet	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Iron Settings, <regex>user_name</regex></p>  <p class="muted">Low heat first, then climb. Once you scorch a collar, there's no going back.</p>  <div class="table-wrap">    <table>      <thead><tr><th>Fabric</th><th class="num">Heat</th><th>Steam?</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead>      <tbody>        <tr><td>Acetate, nylon</td><td class="num">Low</td><td>No</td><td>Iron inside out</td></tr>        <tr><td>Silk, wool</td><td class="num">Low</td><td>Light</td><td>Press cloth on top</td></tr>        <tr><td>Polyester</td><td class="num">Med</td><td>Yes</td><td>Moves fast</td></tr>        <tr><td>Cotton</td><td class="num">High</td><td>Yes</td><td>Damp helps</td></tr>        <tr><td>Linen</td><td class="num">High</td><td>Yes</td><td>Iron while damp</td></tr>      </tbody>    </table>  </div></div><details class="acc">  <summary>No iron? Three tricks</summary>  <div class="body">    <p>Hang it in the bathroom while you shower. Or mist it and tumble on low for ten minutes. Or lay it flat under a damp towel and press with your palm.</p>  </div></details><details class="acc">  <summary>Shirt order</summary>  <div class="body">    <p>Collar, cuffs, sleeves, then body. Yoke last so the shoulders stay crisp.</p>  </div></details><div class="callout warn">Never iron over a print or a zip. The plate will pick it up and stamp it somewhere new.</div>	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Iron Settings, </p>  <p class="muted">Low heat first, then climb. Once you scorch a collar, there's no going back.</p>  <div class="table-wrap">    <table>      <thead><tr><th>Fabric</th><th class="num">Heat</th><th>Steam?</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead>      <tbody>        <tr><td>Acetate, nylon</td><td class="num">Low</td><td>No</td><td>Iron inside out</td></tr>        <tr><td>Silk, wool</td><td class="num">Low</td><td>Light</td><td>Press cloth on top</td></tr>        <tr><td>Polyester</td><td class="num">Med</td><td>Yes</td><td>Moves fast</td></tr>        <tr><td>Cotton</td><td class="num">High</td><td>Yes</td><td>Damp helps</td></tr>        <tr><td>Linen</td><td class="num">High</td><td>Yes</td><td>Iron while damp</td></tr>      </tbody>    </table>  </div></div><details class="acc">  <summary>No iron? Three tricks</summary>  <div class="body">    <p>Hang it in the bathroom while you shower. Or mist it and tumble on low for ten minutes. Or lay it flat under a damp towel and press with your palm.</p>  </div></details><details class="acc">  <summary>Shirt order</summary>  <div class="body">    <p>Collar, cuffs, sleeves, then body. Yoke last so the shoulders stay crisp.</p>  </div></details><div class="callout warn">Never iron over a print or a zip. The plate will pick it up and stamp it somewhere new.</div>	-
35.42.1	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Bike Chain & Tire Care Cheat Sheet	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Chain & Tires, <regex>user_name</regex></p>  <p class="muted">A quiet chain is a happy chain. Squeaks mean metal rubbing metal, and that wears things down fast.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat"><span class="label">Chain lube</span><span class="value">Every<span class="unit"> 160&nbsp;km</span></span></div>    <div class="stat"><span class="label">Tire check</span><span class="value">Weekly</span></div>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Wipe the chain with a dry rag before lubing. Dirt plus oil equals grinding paste.</li>    <li>One drop per link. Spin the pedals backwards as you go.</li>    <li>Wait five minutes, then wipe off the excess. Shiny, not wet.</li>  </ol></div><details class="acc">  <summary>Tire pressure starting points</summary>  <div class="body">    <p>Road bike: 80&ndash;100&nbsp;psi. Hybrid: 50&ndash;70&nbsp;psi. Mountain: 25&ndash;35&nbsp;psi. Check the sidewall for the real range.</p>  </div></details><details class="acc">  <summary>When the chain is done</summary>  <div class="body">    <p>Pull it away from the front chainring. If you can see a gap of half a tooth or more, replace it. Cheap chain, expensive lesson.</p>  </div></details><div class="callout good">Wet lube for rain, dry lube for dust. Pick one and stick with it in that season.</div>	<div class="card">  <p class="card-title">Chain & Tires, </p>  <p class="muted">A quiet chain is a happy chain. Squeaks mean metal rubbing metal, and that wears things down fast.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat"><span class="label">Chain lube</span><span class="value">Every<span class="unit"> 160&nbsp;km</span></span></div>    <div class="stat"><span class="label">Tire check</span><span class="value">Weekly</span></div>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Wipe the chain with a dry rag before lubing. Dirt plus oil equals grinding paste.</li>    <li>One drop per link. Spin the pedals backwards as you go.</li>    <li>Wait five minutes, then wipe off the excess. Shiny, not wet.</li>  </ol></div><details class="acc">  <summary>Tire pressure starting points</summary>  <div class="body">    <p>Road bike: 80&ndash;100&nbsp;psi. Hybrid: 50&ndash;70&nbsp;psi. Mountain: 25&ndash;35&nbsp;psi. Check the sidewall for the real range.</p>  </div></details><details class="acc">  <summary>When the chain is done</summary>  <div class="body">    <p>Pull it away from the front chainring. If you can see a gap of half a tooth or more, replace it. Cheap chain, expensive lesson.</p>  </div></details><div class="callout good">Wet lube for rain, dry lube for dust. Pick one and stick with it in that season.</div>	-
35.43.1	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Button & Zipper Repair Cheat Sheet	<div class="stack">  <p class="muted">Zipper and button fixes that don't need a tailor.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Zipper won't stay closed</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Run a graphite pencil along the teeth, or rub with candle wax. The coating adds friction so the slider grips.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Zipper stuck mid-track</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Don't yank. Rub a little lip balm or soap on the teeth, then gently work the slider back and forth.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Button fell off</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Use a toothpick as a spacer under the button while sewing. It creates a shank so the button sits right over thick fabric. Wrap thread 3–4 times around the shank, knot, and snip.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Prevent future snags</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Close zippers before washing. Zip pulls can catch on other clothes and bend teeth.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="stack">  <p class="muted">Zipper and button fixes that don't need a tailor.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Zipper won't stay closed</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Run a graphite pencil along the teeth, or rub with candle wax. The coating adds friction so the slider grips.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Zipper stuck mid-track</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Don't yank. Rub a little lip balm or soap on the teeth, then gently work the slider back and forth.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Button fell off</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Use a toothpick as a spacer under the button while sewing. It creates a shank so the button sits right over thick fabric. Wrap thread 3–4 times around the shank, knot, and snip.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Prevent future snags</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Close zippers before washing. Zip pulls can catch on other clothes and bend teeth.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
35.44.1	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Toilet Plunging Cheat Sheet	<div class="stack">  <p class="muted">Before you call anyone, try this. Most clogs give up in two minutes.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li>Turn off the water supply valve behind the toilet. If it's already overflowing, also shut off the main if you can't find the angle stop.</li>    <li>Wait for the bowl to drain. If it won't, use a bucket to remove some water so plunging doesn't splash.</li>    <li>Place the plunger so the rubber cup fully covers the drain hole. Push straight down slowly to seat it, then plunge with quick up-and-down strokes 15–20 times.</li>    <li>On the last stroke, pull up sharply to break the seal and create suction.</li>    <li>If water drains, flush once with the valve still off to test. If it backs up, repeat.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout info">    <p class="t-small">If it still won't drain after three rounds, stop. More plunging can overflow the bowl. Call someone.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="stack">  <p class="muted">Before you call anyone, try this. Most clogs give up in two minutes.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li>Turn off the water supply valve behind the toilet. If it's already overflowing, also shut off the main if you can't find the angle stop.</li>    <li>Wait for the bowl to drain. If it won't, use a bucket to remove some water so plunging doesn't splash.</li>    <li>Place the plunger so the rubber cup fully covers the drain hole. Push straight down slowly to seat it, then plunge with quick up-and-down strokes 15–20 times.</li>    <li>On the last stroke, pull up sharply to break the seal and create suction.</li>    <li>If water drains, flush once with the valve still off to test. If it backs up, repeat.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout info">    <p class="t-small">If it still won't drain after three rounds, stop. More plunging can overflow the bowl. Call someone.</p>  </div></div>	-
35.45.1	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Refrigerator Organization Cheat Sheet	<div class="stack">  <p class="muted">Temperature zones, so your food stops dying young.</p>  <div class="table-wrap">    <table>      <thead><tr><th>Zone</th><th>Store</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>      <tbody>        <tr><td>Top shelf</td><td>Leftovers, ready-to-eat</td><td>Warmest; no raw meat drips</td></tr>        <tr><td>Middle shelf</td><td>Dairy, eggs</td><td>Consistent temp</td></tr>        <tr><td>Bottom shelf</td><td>Raw meat, fish</td><td>Coldest; contains drips</td></tr>        <tr><td>Crisper drawers</td><td>Veggies (high humidity), fruit (low humidity)</td><td>Controls ethylene and moisture</td></tr>        <tr><td>Door</td><td>Condiments, butter</td><td>Warmest; stable items only</td></tr>      </tbody>    </table>  </div>  <div class="callout warn"><p>Don't store milk or eggs in the door. That's the first place to go warm.</p></div></div>	<div class="stack">  <p class="muted">Temperature zones, so your food stops dying young.</p>  <div class="table-wrap">    <table>      <thead><tr><th>Zone</th><th>Store</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>      <tbody>        <tr><td>Top shelf</td><td>Leftovers, ready-to-eat</td><td>Warmest; no raw meat drips</td></tr>        <tr><td>Middle shelf</td><td>Dairy, eggs</td><td>Consistent temp</td></tr>        <tr><td>Bottom shelf</td><td>Raw meat, fish</td><td>Coldest; contains drips</td></tr>        <tr><td>Crisper drawers</td><td>Veggies (high humidity), fruit (low humidity)</td><td>Controls ethylene and moisture</td></tr>        <tr><td>Door</td><td>Condiments, butter</td><td>Warmest; stable items only</td></tr>      </tbody>    </table>  </div>  <div class="callout warn"><p>Don't store milk or eggs in the door. That's the first place to go warm.</p></div></div>	-
35.46.1	html_cheat_sheet/Everyday Microwave Cleaning Cheat Sheet	<div class="stack">  <p class="muted">Steam does the scrubbing for you.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li>Fill a microwave-safe bowl with 1 cup water and 2 tablespoons white vinegar or lemon juice.</li>    <li>Microwave on high for 3–5 minutes until the window steams up.</li>    <li>Leave the door closed for 2 more minutes. Don't open early; the steam needs to loosen the gunk.</li>    <li>Carefully remove the bowl (it's hot). Wipe the inside with a damp cloth. Splatters slide right off.</li>    <li>Remove and wash the turntable and the wave guide cover if it's removable.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout good">    <p class="t-small">For stubborn smells, leave a bowl of baking soda inside overnight.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="stack">  <p class="muted">Steam does the scrubbing for you.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li>Fill a microwave-safe bowl with 1 cup water and 2 tablespoons white vinegar or lemon juice.</li>    <li>Microwave on high for 3–5 minutes until the window steams up.</li>    <li>Leave the door closed for 2 more minutes. Don't open early; the steam needs to loosen the gunk.</li>    <li>Carefully remove the bowl (it's hot). Wipe the inside with a damp cloth. Splatters slide right off.</li>    <li>Remove and wash the turntable and the wave guide cover if it's removable.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout good">    <p class="t-small">For stubborn smells, leave a bowl of baking soda inside overnight.</p>  </div></div>	-
