37.27.0	dashboard_split/Stargazing basics	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Stargazing basics</h1><p class="muted">No telescope needed to start.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">What you need</h4><ul class="list"><li>Dark spot: park, field, or balcony.</li><li>Red light (or red cellophane).</li><li>Star app, or a printed chart.</li><li>Warm layer and a blanket.</li></ul><div class="callout info"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> let your eyes adjust for 20 minutes. No phone.</p></div></div><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Start here</h4><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 100"><circle cx="50" cy="40" r="3" fill="currentColor"/><circle cx="100" cy="30" r="2" fill="currentColor"/><circle cx="150" cy="50" r="4" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="50" y1="40" x2="100" y2="30" class="line"/><line x1="100" y1="30" x2="150" y2="50" class="line"/></svg></div><ul class="list"><li>Find the Big Dipper.</li><li>Follow the two end stars to Polaris.</li><li>Look for Orion in winter.</li></ul></div></div><div class="card flat"><p class="t-small dim">Cloudy? The best nights are often after a cold front.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Stargazing basics</h1><p class="muted">No telescope needed to start.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">What you need</h4><ul class="list"><li>Dark spot: park, field, or balcony.</li><li>Red light (or red cellophane).</li><li>Star app, or a printed chart.</li><li>Warm layer and a blanket.</li></ul><div class="callout info"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> let your eyes adjust for 20 minutes. No phone.</p></div></div><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Start here</h4><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 100"><circle cx="50" cy="40" r="3" fill="currentColor"/><circle cx="100" cy="30" r="2" fill="currentColor"/><circle cx="150" cy="50" r="4" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="50" y1="40" x2="100" y2="30" class="line"/><line x1="100" y1="30" x2="150" y2="50" class="line"/></svg></div><ul class="list"><li>Find the Big Dipper.</li><li>Follow the two end stars to Polaris.</li><li>Look for Orion in winter.</li></ul></div></div><div class="card flat"><p class="t-small dim">Cloudy? The best nights are often after a cold front.</p></div></div>	-
37.28.0	dashboard_split/Playing harmonica	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Playing harmonica</h1><p class="muted">Pocket-sized, forgiving, and fun.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Get set</h4><ul class="list"><li>A 10-hole diatonic in C.</li><li>Hold it like a burger, not a baton.</li><li>Lips relaxed, breathe from the belly.</li><li>Practice single notes by covering holes with your tongue.</li></ul><div class="callout good"><p><strong>Your first riff:</strong> try “Oh Susanna” on holes 4 5 6.</p></div></div><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Daily practice</h4><ol class="list"><li>Five minutes long tones.</li><li>Scales up and down holes 1–10.</li><li>One simple song.</li><li>End on a note you like.</li></ol><div class="meter"><span style="width:60%"></span></div><p class="t-small">Progress after a week: audible.</p></div></div><div class="card flat"><h4 class="card-title">Care</h4><p class="t-small">Tap out moisture after playing. Don't share. Rinse your mouth first.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Playing harmonica</h1><p class="muted">Pocket-sized, forgiving, and fun.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Get set</h4><ul class="list"><li>A 10-hole diatonic in C.</li><li>Hold it like a burger, not a baton.</li><li>Lips relaxed, breathe from the belly.</li><li>Practice single notes by covering holes with your tongue.</li></ul><div class="callout good"><p><strong>Your first riff:</strong> try “Oh Susanna” on holes 4 5 6.</p></div></div><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Daily practice</h4><ol class="list"><li>Five minutes long tones.</li><li>Scales up and down holes 1–10.</li><li>One simple song.</li><li>End on a note you like.</li></ol><div class="meter"><span style="width:60%"></span></div><p class="t-small">Progress after a week: audible.</p></div></div><div class="card flat"><h4 class="card-title">Care</h4><p class="t-small">Tap out moisture after playing. Don't share. Rinse your mouth first.</p></div></div>	-
37.29.0	dashboard_split/Caring for a cat	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Caring for a cat</h1><p class="muted">They're low-maintenance until they're not. Here's the basics.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Daily needs</h4><ul class="list"><li>Fresh water (fountain helps).</li><li>Wet food, timed meals.</li><li>Clean litter box, once or twice.</li><li>Play: 10 minutes, wand toy.</li></ul><div class="callout info"><p><strong>Note:</strong> cats hide pain. A quiet cat is not always a fine cat.</p></div></div><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Vet & home</h4><div class="timeline"><ul class="list"><li><span class="when">Week 1</span> Vet check, microchip, vaccines.</li><li><span class="when">Month 1</span> Routine, scratching post, safe room.</li><li><span class="when">Yearly</span> Boosters, flea, worming.</li></ul></div></div></div><div class="card flat"><h4 class="card-title">Happy cat signs</h4><div class="row"><span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>slow blink</span><span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>kneading</span><span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>tail up</span></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Caring for a cat</h1><p class="muted">They're low-maintenance until they're not. Here's the basics.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Daily needs</h4><ul class="list"><li>Fresh water (fountain helps).</li><li>Wet food, timed meals.</li><li>Clean litter box, once or twice.</li><li>Play: 10 minutes, wand toy.</li></ul><div class="callout info"><p><strong>Note:</strong> cats hide pain. A quiet cat is not always a fine cat.</p></div></div><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Vet & home</h4><div class="timeline"><ul class="list"><li><span class="when">Week 1</span> Vet check, microchip, vaccines.</li><li><span class="when">Month 1</span> Routine, scratching post, safe room.</li><li><span class="when">Yearly</span> Boosters, flea, worming.</li></ul></div></div></div><div class="card flat"><h4 class="card-title">Happy cat signs</h4><div class="row"><span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>slow blink</span><span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>kneading</span><span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>tail up</span></div></div></div>	-
37.30.0	dashboard_split/Bread baking	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Bread baking</h1>  <p class="muted">Good bread is mostly patience, <regex>user_name</regex>. The dough does the work.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4>Core ratios</h4>      <ul class="list">        <li><strong>100%</strong> flour</li>        <li><strong>70%</strong> water</li>        <li><strong>2%</strong> salt</li>        <li><strong>0.5%</strong> instant yeast</li>      </ul>      <p class="t-small dim">Weigh everything. Cups lie.</p>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4>Timeline</h4>      <ul class="list timeline">        <li><span class="when">0h</span> Mix, rest 30 min</li>        <li><span class="when">1h</span> Fold every 30 min</li>        <li><span class="when">3h</span> Shape, proof</li>        <li><span class="when">4h</span> Bake at 240&deg;C</li>      </ul>    </div>  </div>  <div class="callout info">    <p>If it&#8217;s dense, you probably under-proofed. Give it another hour and try again.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Bread baking</h1>  <p class="muted">Good bread is mostly patience, . The dough does the work.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4>Core ratios</h4>      <ul class="list">        <li><strong>100%</strong> flour</li>        <li><strong>70%</strong> water</li>        <li><strong>2%</strong> salt</li>        <li><strong>0.5%</strong> instant yeast</li>      </ul>      <p class="t-small dim">Weigh everything. Cups lie.</p>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4>Timeline</h4>      <ul class="list timeline">        <li><span class="when">0h</span> Mix, rest 30 min</li>        <li><span class="when">1h</span> Fold every 30 min</li>        <li><span class="when">3h</span> Shape, proof</li>        <li><span class="when">4h</span> Bake at 240&deg;C</li>      </ul>    </div>  </div>  <div class="callout info">    <p>If it&#8217;s dense, you probably under-proofed. Give it another hour and try again.</p>  </div></div>	-
37.31.0	dashboard_split/Tying a tie	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Tying a tie</h1>  <p class="muted">Four-in-hand is the workhorse, <regex>user_name</regex>. Windsor for when you mean business.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4>Four-in-hand</h4>      <ol class="list">        <li>Drape tie, wide end ~30 cm lower</li>        <li>Cross wide over narrow</li>        <li>Wrap wide behind, then across front</li>        <li>Pull up through loop, tighten</li>      </ol>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4>Windsor</h4>      <ol class="list">        <li>Same start, wide end longer</li>        <li>Cross, then up through neck loop</li>        <li>Down to one side, around back</li>        <li>Across front, up through loop, down through knot</li>      </ol>    </div>  </div>  <div class="callout">    <p>The dimple: pinch just above the knot before you tighten. Small thing, big difference.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Tying a tie</h1>  <p class="muted">Four-in-hand is the workhorse, . Windsor for when you mean business.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4>Four-in-hand</h4>      <ol class="list">        <li>Drape tie, wide end ~30 cm lower</li>        <li>Cross wide over narrow</li>        <li>Wrap wide behind, then across front</li>        <li>Pull up through loop, tighten</li>      </ol>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4>Windsor</h4>      <ol class="list">        <li>Same start, wide end longer</li>        <li>Cross, then up through neck loop</li>        <li>Down to one side, around back</li>        <li>Across front, up through loop, down through knot</li>      </ol>    </div>  </div>  <div class="callout">    <p>The dimple: pinch just above the knot before you tighten. Small thing, big difference.</p>  </div></div>	-
37.32.0	dashboard_split/Watering houseplants	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Watering houseplants</h1>  <p class="muted">More plants die from kindness than neglect, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4>Check first</h4>      <ul class="list">        <li>Stick finger 3 cm into soil</li>        <li>Dry? Water. Damp? Wait.</li>        <li>Lift the pot&#8212;light means thirsty</li>      </ul>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4>Do it right</h4>      <ul class="list">        <li>Water until it runs out the bottom</li>        <li>Empty the saucer after 10 min</li>        <li>Morning is best</li>      </ul>    </div>  </div>  <div class="callout warn">    <p>Yellow lower leaves + soggy soil = too much water. Let it dry out.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Watering houseplants</h1>  <p class="muted">More plants die from kindness than neglect, .</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4>Check first</h4>      <ul class="list">        <li>Stick finger 3 cm into soil</li>        <li>Dry? Water. Damp? Wait.</li>        <li>Lift the pot&#8212;light means thirsty</li>      </ul>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4>Do it right</h4>      <ul class="list">        <li>Water until it runs out the bottom</li>        <li>Empty the saucer after 10 min</li>        <li>Morning is best</li>      </ul>    </div>  </div>  <div class="callout warn">    <p>Yellow lower leaves + soggy soil = too much water. Let it dry out.</p>  </div></div>	-
37.33.0	dashboard_split/Fixing a flat tyre	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Fixing a flat tyre</h1>  <p class="muted">Twenty minutes, two tyre levers, one <regex>user_name</regex> who&#8217;s done this before.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4>Tools</h4>      <ul class="list">        <li>Tyre levers (2&#8211;3)</li>        <li>Pump</li>        <li>Spare tube or patch kit</li>        <li>Wrench if nuts, not quick-release</li>      </ul>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4>Steps</h4>      <ol class="list">        <li>Remove wheel</li>        <li>Pry tyre off rim</li>        <li>Pull tube, find puncture</li>        <li>Patch or swap tube</li>        <li>Seat tyre, inflate slowly</li>      </ol>    </div>  </div>  <div class="callout info">    <p>Run a finger inside the tyre before you reinstall. The thorn might still be there.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Fixing a flat tyre</h1>  <p class="muted">Twenty minutes, two tyre levers, one  who&#8217;s done this before.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4>Tools</h4>      <ul class="list">        <li>Tyre levers (2&#8211;3)</li>        <li>Pump</li>        <li>Spare tube or patch kit</li>        <li>Wrench if nuts, not quick-release</li>      </ul>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4>Steps</h4>      <ol class="list">        <li>Remove wheel</li>        <li>Pry tyre off rim</li>        <li>Pull tube, find puncture</li>        <li>Patch or swap tube</li>        <li>Seat tyre, inflate slowly</li>      </ol>    </div>  </div>  <div class="callout info">    <p>Run a finger inside the tyre before you reinstall. The thorn might still be there.</p>  </div></div>	-
37.34.0	dashboard_split/Packing a suitcase	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Packing a suitcase</h1>  <p class="muted">Roll, don&#8217;t fold, <regex>user_name</regex>. And lay heavy things at the wheels.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4>Method</h4>      <ul class="list">        <li>Roll t-shirts and pants</li>        <li>Stuff socks into shoes</li>        <li>Use packing cubes or zip bags</li>        <li>Heaviest items near the bottom</li>      </ul>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4>Don&#8217;t forget</h4>      <ul class="list">        <li>Charger + adapter</li>        <li>Medications in carry-on</li>        <li>One outfit in your personal bag</li>      </ul>      <p class="t-small dim">Liquids: 100 ml max, clear bag.</p>    </div>  </div>  <div class="callout good">    <p>Leave 10% empty. You&#8217;ll buy something. You always do.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Packing a suitcase</h1>  <p class="muted">Roll, don&#8217;t fold, . And lay heavy things at the wheels.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4>Method</h4>      <ul class="list">        <li>Roll t-shirts and pants</li>        <li>Stuff socks into shoes</li>        <li>Use packing cubes or zip bags</li>        <li>Heaviest items near the bottom</li>      </ul>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4>Don&#8217;t forget</h4>      <ul class="list">        <li>Charger + adapter</li>        <li>Medications in carry-on</li>        <li>One outfit in your personal bag</li>      </ul>      <p class="t-small dim">Liquids: 100 ml max, clear bag.</p>    </div>  </div>  <div class="callout good">    <p>Leave 10% empty. You&#8217;ll buy something. You always do.</p>  </div></div>	-
37.35.0	dashboard_split/Basic sewing repairs	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Basic sewing repairs</h1>  <p class="muted">A needle, thread, and ten minutes can save a favourite shirt, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4>Button</h4>      <ol class="list">        <li>Thread needle, knot end</li>        <li>Anchor thread under button</li>        <li>Sew through holes 4&#8211;6 times</li>        <li>Wrap thread under button, knot</li>      </ol>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4>Hem</h4>      <ol class="list">        <li>Fold fabric up, pin</li>        <li>Knot thread, push up through fold</li>        <li>Tiny stitch, repeat along edge</li>        <li>Knot inside, trim</li>      </ol>    </div>  </div>  <div class="callout">    <p>Match thread to fabric, not the button. Buttons get replaced; fabric doesn&#8217;t.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Basic sewing repairs</h1>  <p class="muted">A needle, thread, and ten minutes can save a favourite shirt, .</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4>Button</h4>      <ol class="list">        <li>Thread needle, knot end</li>        <li>Anchor thread under button</li>        <li>Sew through holes 4&#8211;6 times</li>        <li>Wrap thread under button, knot</li>      </ol>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4>Hem</h4>      <ol class="list">        <li>Fold fabric up, pin</li>        <li>Knot thread, push up through fold</li>        <li>Tiny stitch, repeat along edge</li>        <li>Knot inside, trim</li>      </ol>    </div>  </div>  <div class="callout">    <p>Match thread to fabric, not the button. Buttons get replaced; fabric doesn&#8217;t.</p>  </div></div>	-
37.36.0	dashboard_split/Brewing pour-over coffee at home	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Pour-over, without the fuss</h2><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>What you'll need</h4><ul class="list"><li>Dripper (V60 or Chemex)</li><li>Filter paper that fits</li><li>Kettle, ideally gooseneck</li><li>Scale, if you have one</li><li>20g coffee, medium-fine</li><li>320g water, ~96°C</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Six steps</h4><ol class="list"><li>Rinse the filter, dump the water.</li><li>Add coffee, shake it level.</li><li>Pour 40g water, wait 30s. It'll puff.</li><li>Spiral pour to 160g by 1:00.</li><li>Pour to 320g by 2:00.</li><li>Finished by 3:00–3:30. Drink.</li></ol></div></div><div class="callout info"><strong>Taste the dial, not the recipe.</strong> Sour and thin? Grind finer. Bitter and slow? Grind coarser. <regex>user_name</regex>, that's the whole game.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Pour-over, without the fuss</h2><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>What you'll need</h4><ul class="list"><li>Dripper (V60 or Chemex)</li><li>Filter paper that fits</li><li>Kettle, ideally gooseneck</li><li>Scale, if you have one</li><li>20g coffee, medium-fine</li><li>320g water, ~96°C</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Six steps</h4><ol class="list"><li>Rinse the filter, dump the water.</li><li>Add coffee, shake it level.</li><li>Pour 40g water, wait 30s. It'll puff.</li><li>Spiral pour to 160g by 1:00.</li><li>Pour to 320g by 2:00.</li><li>Finished by 3:00–3:30. Drink.</li></ol></div></div><div class="callout info"><strong>Taste the dial, not the recipe.</strong> Sour and thin? Grind finer. Bitter and slow? Grind coarser. , that's the whole game.</div></div>	-
37.37.0	dashboard_split/Handling a kitchen fire	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Kitchen fire: the short version</h2><div class="callout bad"><strong>Never water a grease fire.</strong> It turns into a fireball. Water goes on oil like a match to a puddle of petrol.</div><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Do</h4><ul class="list"><li>Smother with a metal lid or baking sheet.</li><li>Turn off the heat.</li><li>Salt or baking soda, if you have plenty.</li><li>Fire extinguisher rated for grease, if it's growing.</li><li>Get out and call 911 if it's not shrinking fast.</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Don't</h4><ul class="list"><li>Don't move the pan to the sink.</li><li>Don't use flour — it can explode.</li><li>Don't wave a towel at it.</li><li>Don't stay if smoke fills the room.</li></ul></div></div><div class="callout warn"><regex>user_name</regex>, if it's already spreading, the pan isn't the point anymore — you are.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Kitchen fire: the short version</h2><div class="callout bad"><strong>Never water a grease fire.</strong> It turns into a fireball. Water goes on oil like a match to a puddle of petrol.</div><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Do</h4><ul class="list"><li>Smother with a metal lid or baking sheet.</li><li>Turn off the heat.</li><li>Salt or baking soda, if you have plenty.</li><li>Fire extinguisher rated for grease, if it's growing.</li><li>Get out and call 911 if it's not shrinking fast.</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Don't</h4><ul class="list"><li>Don't move the pan to the sink.</li><li>Don't use flour — it can explode.</li><li>Don't wave a towel at it.</li><li>Don't stay if smoke fills the room.</li></ul></div></div><div class="callout warn">, if it's already spreading, the pan isn't the point anymore — you are.</div></div>	-
37.38.0	dashboard_split/Writing a cover letter	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">A cover letter, minus the dread</h2><p class="muted">Three paragraphs. One page. That's the shape.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Paragraph 1 — Hook</h4><p>The role, and one concrete reason you're a fit. Skip "I am writing to apply."</p></div><div class="card"><h4>Paragraph 2 — Proof</h4><p>One story. A result with a number if you have one. Two sentences max.</p></div></div><div class="card flat"><h4>Paragraph 3 — Close</h4><p>What you'd bring, and a plain "thanks for reading." No dramatic sign-off.</p></div><div class="callout good"><strong>Trick:</strong> reread the job ad, pick the top three words, and make sure your letter echoes at least two. <regex>user_name</regex>, that's 80% of the battle.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">A cover letter, minus the dread</h2><p class="muted">Three paragraphs. One page. That's the shape.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Paragraph 1 — Hook</h4><p>The role, and one concrete reason you're a fit. Skip "I am writing to apply."</p></div><div class="card"><h4>Paragraph 2 — Proof</h4><p>One story. A result with a number if you have one. Two sentences max.</p></div></div><div class="card flat"><h4>Paragraph 3 — Close</h4><p>What you'd bring, and a plain "thanks for reading." No dramatic sign-off.</p></div><div class="callout good"><strong>Trick:</strong> reread the job ad, pick the top three words, and make sure your letter echoes at least two. , that's 80% of the battle.</div></div>	-
37.39.0	dashboard_split/Filing your taxes for the first time	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Taxes, first time</h2><div class="callout warn"><strong>Heads up:</strong> rules change by country and state. This is the rough shape, not legal advice. Check your tax authority's site.</div><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Gather these</h4><ul class="list"><li>W-2 (or local wage slip)</li><li>Bank interest statements</li><li>Student loan paperwork, if any</li><li>Last year's return, if you have one</li><li>ID and your tax ID number</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Then</h4><ol class="list"><li>Choose free filing software if income's low enough.</li><li>Enter each form where it asks.</li><li>Double-check the numbers.</li><li>E-file. Save the PDF.</li></ol></div></div><div class="callout info"><regex>user_name</regex>, if you owe and can't pay, file anyway — penalties for not filing are worse than for not paying. You can usually set up a plan.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Taxes, first time</h2><div class="callout warn"><strong>Heads up:</strong> rules change by country and state. This is the rough shape, not legal advice. Check your tax authority's site.</div><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Gather these</h4><ul class="list"><li>W-2 (or local wage slip)</li><li>Bank interest statements</li><li>Student loan paperwork, if any</li><li>Last year's return, if you have one</li><li>ID and your tax ID number</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Then</h4><ol class="list"><li>Choose free filing software if income's low enough.</li><li>Enter each form where it asks.</li><li>Double-check the numbers.</li><li>E-file. Save the PDF.</li></ol></div></div><div class="callout info">, if you owe and can't pay, file anyway — penalties for not filing are worse than for not paying. You can usually set up a plan.</div></div>	-
37.40.0	dashboard_split/Long-distance running training	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Running further, without the shin splints</h2><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>The one rule</h4><p>Increase weekly distance by no more than 10% each week. Boring, effective, keeps your shins intact.</p></div><div class="card"><h4>A simple week</h4><ul class="list"><li>Mon: easy run, chatty pace</li><li>Tue: rest or walk</li><li>Wed: intervals — 6×2 min hard, 1 min easy</li><li>Thu: rest</li><li>Fri: easy run</li><li>Sat: long slow run</li><li>Sun: full rest</li></ul></div></div><div class="callout good"><strong>Slow is fast.</strong> Most beginner runs are too fast, too soon. If you can't speak a sentence, ease off. <regex>user_name</regex>, your easy pace is the whole foundation.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Running further, without the shin splints</h2><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>The one rule</h4><p>Increase weekly distance by no more than 10% each week. Boring, effective, keeps your shins intact.</p></div><div class="card"><h4>A simple week</h4><ul class="list"><li>Mon: easy run, chatty pace</li><li>Tue: rest or walk</li><li>Wed: intervals — 6×2 min hard, 1 min easy</li><li>Thu: rest</li><li>Fri: easy run</li><li>Sat: long slow run</li><li>Sun: full rest</li></ul></div></div><div class="callout good"><strong>Slow is fast.</strong> Most beginner runs are too fast, too soon. If you can't speak a sentence, ease off. , your easy pace is the whole foundation.</div></div>	-
37.41.0	dashboard_split/Making a simple budget spreadsheet	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Budget spreadsheet, three columns</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th class="num">Plan</th><th class="num">Actual</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Rent</td><td class="num">1200</td><td class="num">1200</td></tr><tr><td>Food</td><td class="num">400</td><td class="num">460</td></tr><tr><td>Fun</td><td class="num">150</td><td class="num">90</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Left</strong></td><td class="num"><strong>0</strong></td><td class="num"><strong>+100</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Formulas</h4><p><code>Plan - Actual</code> gives the surprise number. Set colour rules if Actual is higher.</p></div><div class="card"><h4>Categories</h4><ul class="list"><li>Housing</li><li>Food</li><li>Transport</li><li>Fun</li><li>Savings</li><li>Everything else</li></ul></div></div><div class="callout info"><regex>user_name</regex>, six categories beat twenty. You'll actually keep updating it that way.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h2 class="card-title">Budget spreadsheet, three columns</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th class="num">Plan</th><th class="num">Actual</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Rent</td><td class="num">1200</td><td class="num">1200</td></tr><tr><td>Food</td><td class="num">400</td><td class="num">460</td></tr><tr><td>Fun</td><td class="num">150</td><td class="num">90</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Left</strong></td><td class="num"><strong>0</strong></td><td class="num"><strong>+100</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Formulas</h4><p><code>Plan - Actual</code> gives the surprise number. Set colour rules if Actual is higher.</p></div><div class="card"><h4>Categories</h4><ul class="list"><li>Housing</li><li>Food</li><li>Transport</li><li>Fun</li><li>Savings</li><li>Everything else</li></ul></div></div><div class="callout info">, six categories beat twenty. You'll actually keep updating it that way.</div></div>	-
37.42.0	dashboard_split/Campfire cooking	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Campfire cooking</h1><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Start with coals</h4><p>Let wood burn to coals, then rake them under a grate. Steady heat beats flames every time.</p><div class="callout info"><p>Add a <span class="badge">coal bed</span> about 3 inches deep for even cooking.</p></div></div><div class="card"><h4>Pack these tools</h4><ul class="list"><li>Heavy foil</li><li>Long tongs</li><li>Skewers</li><li>Cast-iron pan</li></ul><p class="muted t-small">A bandana doubles as a pot grabber.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Campfire cooking</h1><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Start with coals</h4><p>Let wood burn to coals, then rake them under a grate. Steady heat beats flames every time.</p><div class="callout info"><p>Add a <span class="badge">coal bed</span> about 3 inches deep for even cooking.</p></div></div><div class="card"><h4>Pack these tools</h4><ul class="list"><li>Heavy foil</li><li>Long tongs</li><li>Skewers</li><li>Cast-iron pan</li></ul><p class="muted t-small">A bandana doubles as a pot grabber.</p></div></div></div>	-
37.43.0	dashboard_split/Navigating with a map and compass	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Navigating with a map and compass</h1><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Map orientation</h4><p>Set the map by lining up its north arrow with your compass needle. Now what you see matches the paper.</p><div class="callout warn"><p>Remember magnetic declination—it can throw you off by degrees.</p></div></div><div class="card"><h4>Take a bearing</h4><ol class="list"><li>Point compass at landmark.</li><li>Rotate bezel to align needle with N.</li><li>Read the number at the direction-of-travel arrow.</li></ol><p class="muted t-small">Practice in your neighborhood first.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Navigating with a map and compass</h1><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Map orientation</h4><p>Set the map by lining up its north arrow with your compass needle. Now what you see matches the paper.</p><div class="callout warn"><p>Remember magnetic declination—it can throw you off by degrees.</p></div></div><div class="card"><h4>Take a bearing</h4><ol class="list"><li>Point compass at landmark.</li><li>Rotate bezel to align needle with N.</li><li>Read the number at the direction-of-travel arrow.</li></ol><p class="muted t-small">Practice in your neighborhood first.</p></div></div></div>	-
37.44.0	dashboard_split/Handling a bad day at work	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Handling a bad day at work</h1><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>In the moment</h4><p>Step away for five minutes. A short walk or a glass of water can reset your head.</p><div class="callout good"><p>Name the feeling silently: "This is frustration." It shrinks the edge.</p></div></div><div class="card"><h4>After hours</h4><ul class="list"><li>Write down one thing that went okay.</li><li>Do a tiny physical task—dishes, stretching.</li><li>Set a worry timer for tomorrow.</li></ul><p class="muted t-small">You don't have to solve it all tonight.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Handling a bad day at work</h1><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>In the moment</h4><p>Step away for five minutes. A short walk or a glass of water can reset your head.</p><div class="callout good"><p>Name the feeling silently: "This is frustration." It shrinks the edge.</p></div></div><div class="card"><h4>After hours</h4><ul class="list"><li>Write down one thing that went okay.</li><li>Do a tiny physical task—dishes, stretching.</li><li>Set a worry timer for tomorrow.</li></ul><p class="muted t-small">You don't have to solve it all tonight.</p></div></div></div>	-
37.45.0	dashboard_split/Making your own cleaning spray	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Making your own cleaning spray</h1><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Basic recipe</h4><p>Mix 1 cup water, 1 cup white vinegar, and 10 drops tea tree oil in a spray bottle.</p><div class="callout info"><p>Vinegar is acidic—avoid on marble and stone.</p></div></div><div class="card"><h4>Use it on</h4><ul class="list"><li>Kitchen counters</li><li>Bathroom sinks</li><li>Glass (dilute more)</li><li>Floor spots</li></ul><p class="muted t-small">Shake before each use.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Making your own cleaning spray</h1><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Basic recipe</h4><p>Mix 1 cup water, 1 cup white vinegar, and 10 drops tea tree oil in a spray bottle.</p><div class="callout info"><p>Vinegar is acidic—avoid on marble and stone.</p></div></div><div class="card"><h4>Use it on</h4><ul class="list"><li>Kitchen counters</li><li>Bathroom sinks</li><li>Glass (dilute more)</li><li>Floor spots</li></ul><p class="muted t-small">Shake before each use.</p></div></div></div>	-
37.46.0	dashboard_split/Fixing a running toilet	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Fixing a running toilet</h1><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>First checks</h4><p>Lift the tank lid. Is the flapper chain too tight or tangled? Adjust it so there's a little slack.</p><div class="callout warn"><p>Turn off the water supply before deeper work.</p></div></div><div class="card"><h4>Replace the flapper</h4><ol class="list"><li>Shut off water, flush to empty tank.</li><li>Unclip old flapper.</li><li>Take it to the store for a match.</li><li>Install new one and test.</li></ol><p class="muted t-small">Usually a 10-minute job.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Fixing a running toilet</h1><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>First checks</h4><p>Lift the tank lid. Is the flapper chain too tight or tangled? Adjust it so there's a little slack.</p><div class="callout warn"><p>Turn off the water supply before deeper work.</p></div></div><div class="card"><h4>Replace the flapper</h4><ol class="list"><li>Shut off water, flush to empty tank.</li><li>Unclip old flapper.</li><li>Take it to the store for a match.</li><li>Install new one and test.</li></ol><p class="muted t-small">Usually a 10-minute job.</p></div></div></div>	-
37.47.0	dashboard_split/Creating a morning routine	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Creating a morning routine</h1><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Start small</h4><p>Pick one thing you'll do every morning for a week. Just one. Maybe it's drinking a glass of water.</p><div class="callout good"><p>Tie it to something you already do—like after brushing your teeth.</p></div></div><div class="card"><h4>Build the stack</h4><ul class="list"><li>Wake at a consistent time.</li><li>Light stretch or walk.</li><li>Simple breakfast.</li><li>Review top 3 tasks.</li></ul><p class="muted t-small">Adjust as life changes. It's yours.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Creating a morning routine</h1><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Start small</h4><p>Pick one thing you'll do every morning for a week. Just one. Maybe it's drinking a glass of water.</p><div class="callout good"><p>Tie it to something you already do—like after brushing your teeth.</p></div></div><div class="card"><h4>Build the stack</h4><ul class="list"><li>Wake at a consistent time.</li><li>Light stretch or walk.</li><li>Simple breakfast.</li><li>Review top 3 tasks.</li></ul><p class="muted t-small">Adjust as life changes. It's yours.</p></div></div></div>	-
37.48.0	dashboard_split/Making good scrambled eggs	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Making good scrambled eggs</h1><p class="muted">Hey <regex>user_name</regex> — rubbery eggs usually mean high heat or overcooking. Low and slow wins.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Method</h4><ol class="list"><li>Crack 3 eggs into a bowl, pinch of salt, splash of milk.</li><li>Whisk until just combined — no streaks.</li><li>Melt butter in a pan over <strong>low</strong> heat.</li><li>Pour in eggs. Wait 20 sec, then stir slowly with a spatula.</li><li>Pull off heat while still glossy; carryover heat finishes them.</li></ol></div><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Troubleshooting</h4><ul class="list"><li><strong>Dry?</strong> Heat too high or cooked too long.</li><li><strong>Watery?</strong> Stirred too aggressively or added too much milk.</li><li><strong>Sticking?</strong> Not enough fat or pan not preheated.</li></ul><div class="meter"><span style="width:85%"></span></div><p class="t-small dim">Confidence: 85% — basic technique, reliable.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Making good scrambled eggs</h1><p class="muted">Hey  — rubbery eggs usually mean high heat or overcooking. Low and slow wins.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Method</h4><ol class="list"><li>Crack 3 eggs into a bowl, pinch of salt, splash of milk.</li><li>Whisk until just combined — no streaks.</li><li>Melt butter in a pan over <strong>low</strong> heat.</li><li>Pour in eggs. Wait 20 sec, then stir slowly with a spatula.</li><li>Pull off heat while still glossy; carryover heat finishes them.</li></ol></div><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Troubleshooting</h4><ul class="list"><li><strong>Dry?</strong> Heat too high or cooked too long.</li><li><strong>Watery?</strong> Stirred too aggressively or added too much milk.</li><li><strong>Sticking?</strong> Not enough fat or pan not preheated.</li></ul><div class="meter"><span style="width:85%"></span></div><p class="t-small dim">Confidence: 85% — basic technique, reliable.</p></div></div></div>	-
37.49.0	dashboard_split/Jump-starting a dead car battery	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Jump-starting a dead car battery</h1><p class="muted"><regex>user_name</regex>, the order of connections matters. Red first, black last — and never let the clamps touch.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Steps</h4><ol class="list"><li>Park the good car close but not touching. Both engines off.</li><li>Clamp <span class="badge bad">red</span> to dead battery's positive (+).</li><li>Clamp other red to good battery's positive.</li><li>Clamp <span class="badge">black</span> to good battery's negative (–).</li><li>Clamp other black to a bare metal bolt on the dead car's engine block. <em>Not</em> the battery.</li><li>Start good car, wait 2 min, then start dead car.</li></ol></div><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Safety</h4><div class="callout warn"><p>Batteries can vent hydrogen. No smoking, no sparks. If either battery is cracked or leaking, stop.</p></div><p class="t-small dim">If it doesn't start after a few tries, the battery may be beyond jumping.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Jump-starting a dead car battery</h1><p class="muted">, the order of connections matters. Red first, black last — and never let the clamps touch.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Steps</h4><ol class="list"><li>Park the good car close but not touching. Both engines off.</li><li>Clamp <span class="badge bad">red</span> to dead battery's positive (+).</li><li>Clamp other red to good battery's positive.</li><li>Clamp <span class="badge">black</span> to good battery's negative (–).</li><li>Clamp other black to a bare metal bolt on the dead car's engine block. <em>Not</em> the battery.</li><li>Start good car, wait 2 min, then start dead car.</li></ol></div><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Safety</h4><div class="callout warn"><p>Batteries can vent hydrogen. No smoking, no sparks. If either battery is cracked or leaking, stop.</p></div><p class="t-small dim">If it doesn't start after a few tries, the battery may be beyond jumping.</p></div></div></div>	-
37.50.0	dashboard_split/Hanging a picture straight	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Hanging a picture straight</h1><p class="muted">Eyeballing it rarely works, <regex>user_name</regex>. A pencil and a level save you from extra holes.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Method</h4><ol class="list"><li>Decide height: center of art at 57–60 in. from floor (gallery standard).</li><li>Mark the wall with a pencil where the hook will go.</li><li>Use a level to check the mark is horizontal.</li><li>Hammer in the hook or anchor.</li><li>Hang, then step back and check with a level on top of the frame.</li></ol></div><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Heavy pieces</h4><ul class="list"><li>Use two hooks 2–3 in. apart for stability.</li><li>For plaster walls, use a toggle anchor.</li><li>For brick, a masonry bit and sleeve anchor.</li></ul><div class="callout info"><p>Command strips work for light frames under 4 lbs on smooth paint.</p></div></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Hanging a picture straight</h1><p class="muted">Eyeballing it rarely works, . A pencil and a level save you from extra holes.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Method</h4><ol class="list"><li>Decide height: center of art at 57–60 in. from floor (gallery standard).</li><li>Mark the wall with a pencil where the hook will go.</li><li>Use a level to check the mark is horizontal.</li><li>Hammer in the hook or anchor.</li><li>Hang, then step back and check with a level on top of the frame.</li></ol></div><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Heavy pieces</h4><ul class="list"><li>Use two hooks 2–3 in. apart for stability.</li><li>For plaster walls, use a toggle anchor.</li><li>For brick, a masonry bit and sleeve anchor.</li></ul><div class="callout info"><p>Command strips work for light frames under 4 lbs on smooth paint.</p></div></div></div></div>	-
37.51.0	dashboard_split/Knife sharpening basics	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Knife sharpening basics</h1><p class="muted">A sharp knife is safer than a dull one, <regex>user_name</regex>. Less force, fewer slips.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Whetstone method</h4><ol class="list"><li>Soak a 1000/6000 grit stone in water 10 min.</li><li>Hold knife at ~15–20° angle to stone.</li><li>Push blade across stone like slicing a thin layer.</li><li>Do 10 passes per side, then flip.</li><li>Finish on fine grit, then strop on leather or newspaper.</li></ol></div><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Quick check</h4><p>Test on a tomato. If it slides, keep going.</p><div class="callout good"><p>Hone weekly with a rod; sharpen every 2–3 months for home cooks.</p></div><p class="t-small dim">Don't put good knives in the dishwasher.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Knife sharpening basics</h1><p class="muted">A sharp knife is safer than a dull one, . Less force, fewer slips.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Whetstone method</h4><ol class="list"><li>Soak a 1000/6000 grit stone in water 10 min.</li><li>Hold knife at ~15–20° angle to stone.</li><li>Push blade across stone like slicing a thin layer.</li><li>Do 10 passes per side, then flip.</li><li>Finish on fine grit, then strop on leather or newspaper.</li></ol></div><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Quick check</h4><p>Test on a tomato. If it slides, keep going.</p><div class="callout good"><p>Hone weekly with a rod; sharpen every 2–3 months for home cooks.</p></div><p class="t-small dim">Don't put good knives in the dishwasher.</p></div></div></div>	-
37.52.0	dashboard_split/Removing red wine stains	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Removing red wine stains</h1><p class="muted">Act fast, <regex>user_name</regex>. Blot, don't rub — rubbing pushes the pigment deeper.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Immediate steps</h4><ol class="list"><li>Blot with a clean cloth or paper towel.</li><li>Pour salt on the stain to absorb liquid (carpet/fabric).</li><li>Mix 1 tbsp dish soap + 1 cup hydrogen peroxide.</li><li>Dab solution on stain, let sit 5 min.</li><li>Rinse with cool water. Repeat if needed.</li></ol></div><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">For upholstery</h4><ul class="list"><li>Use a 50/50 white vinegar and water mix.</li><li>Test on hidden area first.</li><li>Blot with clean cloth until dry.</li></ul><div class="callout warn"><p>Never use hot water — it sets the stain.</p></div></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Removing red wine stains</h1><p class="muted">Act fast, . Blot, don't rub — rubbing pushes the pigment deeper.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Immediate steps</h4><ol class="list"><li>Blot with a clean cloth or paper towel.</li><li>Pour salt on the stain to absorb liquid (carpet/fabric).</li><li>Mix 1 tbsp dish soap + 1 cup hydrogen peroxide.</li><li>Dab solution on stain, let sit 5 min.</li><li>Rinse with cool water. Repeat if needed.</li></ol></div><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">For upholstery</h4><ul class="list"><li>Use a 50/50 white vinegar and water mix.</li><li>Test on hidden area first.</li><li>Blot with clean cloth until dry.</li></ul><div class="callout warn"><p>Never use hot water — it sets the stain.</p></div></div></div></div>	-
37.53.0	dashboard_split/Starting a small vegetable garden	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Starting a small vegetable garden</h1><p class="muted"><regex>user_name</regex>, start small — a 4x4 ft bed is plenty for a first season.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Easy winners</h4><ul class="list"><li>Lettuce and spinach (cut-and-come-again)</li><li>Cherry tomatoes</li><li>Zucchini</li><li>Radishes (fast, 30 days)</li><li>Herbs: basil, mint, parsley</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Soil & sun</h4><p>Most veggies need 6+ hours of sun. Mix compost into native soil or use raised beds with potting mix.</p><div class="callout info"><p>Water deeply 2–3 times a week, not daily. Roots grow down.</p></div><p class="t-small dim">Check your local frost dates before planting.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Starting a small vegetable garden</h1><p class="muted">, start small — a 4x4 ft bed is plenty for a first season.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Easy winners</h4><ul class="list"><li>Lettuce and spinach (cut-and-come-again)</li><li>Cherry tomatoes</li><li>Zucchini</li><li>Radishes (fast, 30 days)</li><li>Herbs: basil, mint, parsley</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Soil & sun</h4><p>Most veggies need 6+ hours of sun. Mix compost into native soil or use raised beds with potting mix.</p><div class="callout info"><p>Water deeply 2–3 times a week, not daily. Roots grow down.</p></div><p class="t-small dim">Check your local frost dates before planting.</p></div></div></div>	-
37.54.0	dashboard_split/Decluttering a room	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Decluttering a room</h1>  <p class="muted">One room, four piles, no decisions you have to keep forever.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4>How to do it, <regex>user_name</regex></h4>      <ol class="list">        <li>Pick one small zone — a shelf, a drawer, the floor by the door.</li>        <li>Make four piles: keep, donate, bin, belongs elsewhere.</li>        <li>Only touch each thing once. No “maybe” pile.</li>        <li>Put the “elsewhere” pile by the door and walk it out when you finish.</li>        <li>Wipe the cleared surface before you put anything back.</li>      </ol>      <p class="t-small dim">Set a timer for 20 minutes. Stop when it rings, even if you’re not done.</p>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4>Pile tracker</h4>      <div class="grid two">        <div class="stat"><span class="label">Keep</span><span class="value">14</span></div>        <div class="stat"><span class="label">Donate</span><span class="value">9</span></div>        <div class="stat"><span class="label">Bin</span><span class="value">6</span></div>        <div class="stat"><span class="label">Elsewhere</span><span class="value">3</span></div>      </div>      <details class="acc">        <summary>What counts as “elsewhere”?</summary>        <div class="body">Anything that lives in another room — mugs, chargers, library books. Don’t put it away now. Just pile it.</div>      </details>      <div class="callout info">        If you find a box you haven’t opened since you moved, that’s your answer. Donate it unopened.      </div>    </div>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Decluttering a room</h1>  <p class="muted">One room, four piles, no decisions you have to keep forever.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4>How to do it, </h4>      <ol class="list">        <li>Pick one small zone — a shelf, a drawer, the floor by the door.</li>        <li>Make four piles: keep, donate, bin, belongs elsewhere.</li>        <li>Only touch each thing once. No “maybe” pile.</li>        <li>Put the “elsewhere” pile by the door and walk it out when you finish.</li>        <li>Wipe the cleared surface before you put anything back.</li>      </ol>      <p class="t-small dim">Set a timer for 20 minutes. Stop when it rings, even if you’re not done.</p>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4>Pile tracker</h4>      <div class="grid two">        <div class="stat"><span class="label">Keep</span><span class="value">14</span></div>        <div class="stat"><span class="label">Donate</span><span class="value">9</span></div>        <div class="stat"><span class="label">Bin</span><span class="value">6</span></div>        <div class="stat"><span class="label">Elsewhere</span><span class="value">3</span></div>      </div>      <details class="acc">        <summary>What counts as “elsewhere”?</summary>        <div class="body">Anything that lives in another room — mugs, chargers, library books. Don’t put it away now. Just pile it.</div>      </details>      <div class="callout info">        If you find a box you haven’t opened since you moved, that’s your answer. Donate it unopened.      </div>    </div>  </div></div>	-
37.55.0	dashboard_split/Changing a bike inner tube	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Changing a bike inner tube</h1>  <p class="muted">Ten minutes, two tyre levers, one spare tube.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4>Steps</h4>      <ol class="list">        <li>Shift to the smallest cog, loosen the brake, remove the wheel.</li>        <li>Deflate fully. Hook one lever under the bead, slide the second lever around.</li>        <li>Pull the tube out. Leave the tyre on the rim.</li>        <li>Run a finger inside the tyre to find the thorn, glass, or wire. Remove it.</li>        <li>Fit one bead of the new tube. Add a puff of air so it holds shape.</li>        <li>Seat the second bead by hand. Never use levers for this.</li>        <li>Inflate to the pressure on the sidewall. Reinstall the wheel.</li>      </ol>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4>Kit check</h4>      <div class="grid two">        <div class="stat"><span class="label">Levers</span><span class="value">2</span></div>        <div class="stat"><span class="label">Spare tube</span><span class="value">1</span></div>        <div class="stat"><span class="label">Pump</span><span class="value">1</span></div>        <div class="stat"><span class="label">Patches</span><span class="value">4</span></div>      </div>      <details class="acc">        <summary>How do I find the hole?</summary>        <div class="body">Inflate the tube, listen, then hold it under water. Bubbles mark the spot.</div>      </details>      <div class="callout warn">        Line the tyre logo up with the valve stem. Next time you flat, you can see exactly where the puncture happened.      </div>    </div>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Changing a bike inner tube</h1>  <p class="muted">Ten minutes, two tyre levers, one spare tube.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4>Steps</h4>      <ol class="list">        <li>Shift to the smallest cog, loosen the brake, remove the wheel.</li>        <li>Deflate fully. Hook one lever under the bead, slide the second lever around.</li>        <li>Pull the tube out. Leave the tyre on the rim.</li>        <li>Run a finger inside the tyre to find the thorn, glass, or wire. Remove it.</li>        <li>Fit one bead of the new tube. Add a puff of air so it holds shape.</li>        <li>Seat the second bead by hand. Never use levers for this.</li>        <li>Inflate to the pressure on the sidewall. Reinstall the wheel.</li>      </ol>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4>Kit check</h4>      <div class="grid two">        <div class="stat"><span class="label">Levers</span><span class="value">2</span></div>        <div class="stat"><span class="label">Spare tube</span><span class="value">1</span></div>        <div class="stat"><span class="label">Pump</span><span class="value">1</span></div>        <div class="stat"><span class="label">Patches</span><span class="value">4</span></div>      </div>      <details class="acc">        <summary>How do I find the hole?</summary>        <div class="body">Inflate the tube, listen, then hold it under water. Bubbles mark the spot.</div>      </details>      <div class="callout warn">        Line the tyre logo up with the valve stem. Next time you flat, you can see exactly where the puncture happened.      </div>    </div>  </div></div>	-
37.56.0	dashboard_split/Boiling an egg	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Boiling an egg</h1>  <p class="muted">Start with cold water, add a pinch of salt, and time from the boil.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4>Times from boiling</h4>      <table>        <thead><tr><th>Result</th><th class="num">Minutes</th></tr></thead>        <tbody>          <tr><td>Runny yolk</td><td class="num">4</td></tr>          <tr><td>Jammy yolk</td><td class="num">6</td></tr>          <tr><td>Set, soft centre</td><td class="num">8</td></tr>          <tr><td>Hard boiled</td><td class="num">10</td></tr>        </tbody>      </table>      <p class="t-small dim">Large eggs, fridge-cold. Add a minute for extra-large.</p>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4>Peeling</h4>      <ol class="list">        <li>Plunge into cold water or an ice bath for two minutes.</li>        <li>Tap the wide end on the counter, where the air pocket sits.</li>        <li>Peel under running water. The stream gets under the membrane.</li>      </ol>      <details class="acc">        <summary>Why the grey ring?</summary>        <div class="body">That’s overcooked iron and sulfur meeting. It’s harmless but tastes dry. Cool eggs fast to avoid it.</div>      </details>      <div class="callout good">Older eggs peel easier than fresh ones — save the freshest for frying.</div>    </div>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Boiling an egg</h1>  <p class="muted">Start with cold water, add a pinch of salt, and time from the boil.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4>Times from boiling</h4>      <table>        <thead><tr><th>Result</th><th class="num">Minutes</th></tr></thead>        <tbody>          <tr><td>Runny yolk</td><td class="num">4</td></tr>          <tr><td>Jammy yolk</td><td class="num">6</td></tr>          <tr><td>Set, soft centre</td><td class="num">8</td></tr>          <tr><td>Hard boiled</td><td class="num">10</td></tr>        </tbody>      </table>      <p class="t-small dim">Large eggs, fridge-cold. Add a minute for extra-large.</p>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4>Peeling</h4>      <ol class="list">        <li>Plunge into cold water or an ice bath for two minutes.</li>        <li>Tap the wide end on the counter, where the air pocket sits.</li>        <li>Peel under running water. The stream gets under the membrane.</li>      </ol>      <details class="acc">        <summary>Why the grey ring?</summary>        <div class="body">That’s overcooked iron and sulfur meeting. It’s harmless but tastes dry. Cool eggs fast to avoid it.</div>      </details>      <div class="callout good">Older eggs peel easier than fresh ones — save the freshest for frying.</div>    </div>  </div></div>	-
37.57.0	dashboard_split/Jump-starting a car	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Jump-starting a car</h1>  <p class="muted">Red to red, black to ground. Last connection goes on the dead car’s bare metal.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4>Order of connections</h4>      <ol class="list">        <li>Red clamp to the dead battery’s positive post.</li>        <li>Red clamp to the good battery’s positive post.</li>        <li>Black clamp to the good battery’s negative post.</li>        <li>Black clamp to a bare metal bolt on the dead car, away from the battery.</li>        <li>Start the good car. Wait two minutes. Start the dead one.</li>        <li>Remove in reverse: ground, negative, positive, positive.</li>      </ol>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4>Safety check</h4>      <div class="grid two">        <div class="stat"><span class="label">Battery</span><span class="value">12.6<span class="unit">V</span></span></div>        <div class="stat"><span class="label">Running</span><span class="value">13.8<span class="unit">V</span></span></div>      </div>      <details class="acc">        <summary>Why not clamp black to the dead battery?</summary>        <div class="body">A spark there can ignite hydrogen gas from the battery. Grounding to bare metal moves the spark away.</div>      </details>      <div class="callout bad">        Never cross the clamps. Red to black makes a very expensive light show.      </div>    </div>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Jump-starting a car</h1>  <p class="muted">Red to red, black to ground. Last connection goes on the dead car’s bare metal.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4>Order of connections</h4>      <ol class="list">        <li>Red clamp to the dead battery’s positive post.</li>        <li>Red clamp to the good battery’s positive post.</li>        <li>Black clamp to the good battery’s negative post.</li>        <li>Black clamp to a bare metal bolt on the dead car, away from the battery.</li>        <li>Start the good car. Wait two minutes. Start the dead one.</li>        <li>Remove in reverse: ground, negative, positive, positive.</li>      </ol>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4>Safety check</h4>      <div class="grid two">        <div class="stat"><span class="label">Battery</span><span class="value">12.6<span class="unit">V</span></span></div>        <div class="stat"><span class="label">Running</span><span class="value">13.8<span class="unit">V</span></span></div>      </div>      <details class="acc">        <summary>Why not clamp black to the dead battery?</summary>        <div class="body">A spark there can ignite hydrogen gas from the battery. Grounding to bare metal moves the spark away.</div>      </details>      <div class="callout bad">        Never cross the clamps. Red to black makes a very expensive light show.      </div>    </div>  </div></div>	-
37.58.0	dashboard_split/Whistle-stop history of paper	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>A whistle-stop history of paper</h1>  <p class="muted">Pulp, press, and a very old secret the Chinese kept for centuries.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4>Timeline</h4>      <ul class="timeline">        <li><span class="when">105 CE</span> Cai Lun reports a method using mulberry bark and rags to the Han court.</li>        <li><span class="when">751 CE</span> Papermaking spreads west after the Battle of Talas.</li>        <li><span class="when">1050s</span> Baghdad’s paper mills supply books faster than parchment ever could.</li>        <li><span class="when">1282</span> Watermarks appear in Fabriano, Italy.</li>        <li><span class="when">1799</span> Louis-Nicolas Robert patents the continuous paper machine.</li>      </ul>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4>What it was made of</h4>      <div class="grid two">        <div class="stat"><span class="label">Rags</span><span class="value">1</span></div>        <div class="stat"><span class="label">Bark</span><span class="value">1</span></div>        <div class="stat"><span class="label">Water</span><span class="value">∞</span></div>        <div class="stat"><span class="label">Screens</span><span class="value">1</span></div>      </div>      <details class="acc">        <summary>Why did paper beat parchment?</summary>        <div class="body">One animal skin made a few pages. A vat of pulp made hundreds. Cheap wins.</div>      </details>      <div class="callout info">The word “paper” comes from papyrus, but the two are unrelated materials.</div>    </div>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>A whistle-stop history of paper</h1>  <p class="muted">Pulp, press, and a very old secret the Chinese kept for centuries.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4>Timeline</h4>      <ul class="timeline">        <li><span class="when">105 CE</span> Cai Lun reports a method using mulberry bark and rags to the Han court.</li>        <li><span class="when">751 CE</span> Papermaking spreads west after the Battle of Talas.</li>        <li><span class="when">1050s</span> Baghdad’s paper mills supply books faster than parchment ever could.</li>        <li><span class="when">1282</span> Watermarks appear in Fabriano, Italy.</li>        <li><span class="when">1799</span> Louis-Nicolas Robert patents the continuous paper machine.</li>      </ul>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4>What it was made of</h4>      <div class="grid two">        <div class="stat"><span class="label">Rags</span><span class="value">1</span></div>        <div class="stat"><span class="label">Bark</span><span class="value">1</span></div>        <div class="stat"><span class="label">Water</span><span class="value">∞</span></div>        <div class="stat"><span class="label">Screens</span><span class="value">1</span></div>      </div>      <details class="acc">        <summary>Why did paper beat parchment?</summary>        <div class="body">One animal skin made a few pages. A vat of pulp made hundreds. Cheap wins.</div>      </details>      <div class="callout info">The word “paper” comes from papyrus, but the two are unrelated materials.</div>    </div>  </div></div>	-
37.59.0	dashboard_split/Reading a tide table	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Reading a tide table</h1>  <p class="muted">Two highs, two lows, roughly every 12 hours and 25 minutes.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4>What the numbers mean</h4>      <ul class="list">        <li><strong>Time</strong> — when the tide turns, in local clock time.</li>        <li><strong>Height</strong> — metres above chart datum, the lowest recorded level.</li>        <li><strong>Spring / neap</strong> — big range after a full or new moon, small range after a quarter.</li>        <li><strong>Slack</strong> — the pause between push and pull. Fish it, but don’t swim it alone.</li>      </ul>      <p class="t-small dim">Add a safety margin for wind. Onshore gusts push water higher than predicted.</p>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4>Today’s shape</h4>      <div class="chart">        <svg viewBox="0 0 300 120">          <line class="guide" x1="0" y1="100" x2="300" y2="100" />          <line class="guide" x1="0" y1="20" x2="300" y2="20" />          <path class="line" d="M0 70 C50 20, 80 20, 130 70 S210 100, 260 70 S300 30, 300 30" fill="none" />          <text class="lbl" x="28" y="112">low</text>          <text class="lbl" x="112" y="112">high</text>          <text class="lbl" x="236" y="112">low</text>        </svg>      </div>      <details class="acc">        <summary>How do I estimate in between?</summary>        <div class="body">The rule of twelfths splits the range into six parts: 1, 2, 3, 3, 2, 1. Quick and good enough for a walk.</div>      </details>      <div class="callout warn">Never let a rising tide cut you off from the path you came in on.</div>    </div>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Reading a tide table</h1>  <p class="muted">Two highs, two lows, roughly every 12 hours and 25 minutes.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4>What the numbers mean</h4>      <ul class="list">        <li><strong>Time</strong> — when the tide turns, in local clock time.</li>        <li><strong>Height</strong> — metres above chart datum, the lowest recorded level.</li>        <li><strong>Spring / neap</strong> — big range after a full or new moon, small range after a quarter.</li>        <li><strong>Slack</strong> — the pause between push and pull. Fish it, but don’t swim it alone.</li>      </ul>      <p class="t-small dim">Add a safety margin for wind. Onshore gusts push water higher than predicted.</p>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4>Today’s shape</h4>      <div class="chart">        <svg viewBox="0 0 300 120">          <line class="guide" x1="0" y1="100" x2="300" y2="100" />          <line class="guide" x1="0" y1="20" x2="300" y2="20" />          <path class="line" d="M0 70 C50 20, 80 20, 130 70 S210 100, 260 70 S300 30, 300 30" fill="none" />          <text class="lbl" x="28" y="112">low</text>          <text class="lbl" x="112" y="112">high</text>          <text class="lbl" x="236" y="112">low</text>        </svg>      </div>      <details class="acc">        <summary>How do I estimate in between?</summary>        <div class="body">The rule of twelfths splits the range into six parts: 1, 2, 3, 3, 2, 1. Quick and good enough for a walk.</div>      </details>      <div class="callout warn">Never let a rising tide cut you off from the path you came in on.</div>    </div>  </div></div>	-
37.60.0	dashboard_split/Patching a bicycle puncture properly	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Patch a tube, keep the spare</h1><p class="muted">A good patch outlives the tube it's on. Here's the split: find it, then fix it.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>1. Find the hole</h4><ol class="list"><li>Inflate slightly. Listen, then feel for escaping air.</li><li>Or dunk sections in water — bubbles point the way.</li><li>Mark the spot with chalk. Dry the tube fully.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>Run a finger inside the tyre casing before refitting. The thorn is usually still in there, waiting.</p></div></div><div class="card"><h4>2. Patch it</h4><ol class="list"><li>Roughen an area bigger than the patch.</li><li>Thin cement layer. Wait 3–5 min until tacky, not wet.</li><li>Press patch hard 30 sec. Peel backing, dust with chalk.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Why patches fail</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Glue still wet — no grip</li><li>No roughening — smooth rubber shrugs</li><li>Patch too small for the split</li></ul></div></details></div></div><p class="small dim">Nice one, <regex>user_name</regex> — that tube's got a few more thousand kilometres in it.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Patch a tube, keep the spare</h1><p class="muted">A good patch outlives the tube it's on. Here's the split: find it, then fix it.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>1. Find the hole</h4><ol class="list"><li>Inflate slightly. Listen, then feel for escaping air.</li><li>Or dunk sections in water — bubbles point the way.</li><li>Mark the spot with chalk. Dry the tube fully.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>Run a finger inside the tyre casing before refitting. The thorn is usually still in there, waiting.</p></div></div><div class="card"><h4>2. Patch it</h4><ol class="list"><li>Roughen an area bigger than the patch.</li><li>Thin cement layer. Wait 3–5 min until tacky, not wet.</li><li>Press patch hard 30 sec. Peel backing, dust with chalk.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Why patches fail</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Glue still wet — no grip</li><li>No roughening — smooth rubber shrugs</li><li>Patch too small for the split</li></ul></div></details></div></div><p class="small dim">Nice one,  — that tube's got a few more thousand kilometres in it.</p></div>	-
37.61.0	dashboard_split/Writing a to-do list that actually works	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Lists that survive Tuesday</h1><p class="muted">Most lists fail from ambition, not laziness. Cap it.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>The rules</h4><ul class="list"><li><strong>Three big ones.</strong> Everything else is bonus.</li><li>Start each with a verb: <em>draft</em>, <em>call</em>, <em>book</em>.</li><li>If it takes under two minutes, do it now, don't list it.</li><li>Give vague tasks a first physical step.</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Split by energy</h4><div class="row between"><span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>Sharp</span><span class="t-small dim">writing, maths, decisions</span></div><div class="row between"><span class="badge info"><i class="dot"></i>Medium</span><span class="t-small dim">email, tidying, errands</span></div><div class="row between"><span class="badge"><i class="dot"></i>Flat</span><span class="t-small dim">folding laundry, deleting files</span></div><details class="acc"><summary>End-of-day ritual</summary><div class="body"><p>Cross out what you did. Move two things to tomorrow. Delete one entirely — that's the honest bit.</p></div></details></div></div><p class="small dim">Go gently, <regex>user_name</regex>. A finished short list beats a heroic one every time.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Lists that survive Tuesday</h1><p class="muted">Most lists fail from ambition, not laziness. Cap it.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>The rules</h4><ul class="list"><li><strong>Three big ones.</strong> Everything else is bonus.</li><li>Start each with a verb: <em>draft</em>, <em>call</em>, <em>book</em>.</li><li>If it takes under two minutes, do it now, don't list it.</li><li>Give vague tasks a first physical step.</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Split by energy</h4><div class="row between"><span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>Sharp</span><span class="t-small dim">writing, maths, decisions</span></div><div class="row between"><span class="badge info"><i class="dot"></i>Medium</span><span class="t-small dim">email, tidying, errands</span></div><div class="row between"><span class="badge"><i class="dot"></i>Flat</span><span class="t-small dim">folding laundry, deleting files</span></div><details class="acc"><summary>End-of-day ritual</summary><div class="body"><p>Cross out what you did. Move two things to tomorrow. Delete one entirely — that's the honest bit.</p></div></details></div></div><p class="small dim">Go gently, . A finished short list beats a heroic one every time.</p></div>	-
37.62.0	dashboard_split/Making a decent omelette	<div class="umi-page"><h1>The folded omelette</h1><p class="muted">Two eggs, low heat, and a little patience. That's the whole trick.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Timing</h4><div class="timeline"><ul class="list"><li><span class="when">0:00</span> Beat 2–3 eggs with a pinch of salt until uniform.</li><li><span class="when">0:30</span> Butter in a non-stick pan, medium-low. It should foam, not brown.</li><li><span class="when">1:00</span> Pour in. Stir gently with a spatula for 20 seconds — curds, not crust.</li><li><span class="when">1:30</span> Smooth the top, let it set 15 seconds.</li><li><span class="when">2:00</span> Fold, slide out, rest 30 seconds.</li></ul></div></div><div class="card"><h4>Fix-it table</h4><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Cause</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Rubbery</td><td>Heat too high, cooked too long</td></tr><tr><td>Sticking</td><td>Pan too cold or too little fat</td></tr><tr><td>Watery</td><td>Over-stirred, no rest</td></tr><tr><td>Pale and sad</td><td>No salt in the eggs</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout good"><p>Fillings go in at 1:20, warm, never cold from the fridge.</p></div></div></div><p class="small dim">Have fun, <regex>user_name</regex> — breakfast is a forgiving meal.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>The folded omelette</h1><p class="muted">Two eggs, low heat, and a little patience. That's the whole trick.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Timing</h4><div class="timeline"><ul class="list"><li><span class="when">0:00</span> Beat 2–3 eggs with a pinch of salt until uniform.</li><li><span class="when">0:30</span> Butter in a non-stick pan, medium-low. It should foam, not brown.</li><li><span class="when">1:00</span> Pour in. Stir gently with a spatula for 20 seconds — curds, not crust.</li><li><span class="when">1:30</span> Smooth the top, let it set 15 seconds.</li><li><span class="when">2:00</span> Fold, slide out, rest 30 seconds.</li></ul></div></div><div class="card"><h4>Fix-it table</h4><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Cause</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Rubbery</td><td>Heat too high, cooked too long</td></tr><tr><td>Sticking</td><td>Pan too cold or too little fat</td></tr><tr><td>Watery</td><td>Over-stirred, no rest</td></tr><tr><td>Pale and sad</td><td>No salt in the eggs</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout good"><p>Fillings go in at 1:20, warm, never cold from the fridge.</p></div></div></div><p class="small dim">Have fun,  — breakfast is a forgiving meal.</p></div>	-
37.63.0	dashboard_split/Reading your electricity bill	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Your bill, decoded</h1><p class="muted">Two numbers explain nearly all of it: how much you used, and when.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>The lines</h4><ul class="list"><li><strong>Usage (kWh)</strong> — one unit ≈ a 1000W heater for an hour.</li><li><strong>Standing charge</strong> — daily fee for being connected, even at zero use.</li><li><strong>Unit rate</strong> — price per kWh, often higher at peak hours.</li><li><strong>Adjustments</strong> — estimated reads, credits, previous balance.</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Where the money goes</h4><div class="meter"><span style="width:42%"></span></div><p class="t-small dim">Heating &amp; cooling ~42%</p><div class="meter"><span style="width:22%"></span></div><p class="t-small dim">Water heating ~22%</p><div class="meter"><span style="width:18%"></span></div><p class="t-small dim">Appliances ~18%</p><details class="acc"><summary>Quick savings check</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Wash at 30°C</li><li>Kill standby loads on one strip</li><li>Shift dishwasher to off-peak</li></ul></div></details></div></div><p class="small dim">If a number looks wrong, <regex>user_name</regex>, query it. Estimates get corrected on request more often than people think.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Your bill, decoded</h1><p class="muted">Two numbers explain nearly all of it: how much you used, and when.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>The lines</h4><ul class="list"><li><strong>Usage (kWh)</strong> — one unit ≈ a 1000W heater for an hour.</li><li><strong>Standing charge</strong> — daily fee for being connected, even at zero use.</li><li><strong>Unit rate</strong> — price per kWh, often higher at peak hours.</li><li><strong>Adjustments</strong> — estimated reads, credits, previous balance.</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Where the money goes</h4><div class="meter"><span style="width:42%"></span></div><p class="t-small dim">Heating &amp; cooling ~42%</p><div class="meter"><span style="width:22%"></span></div><p class="t-small dim">Water heating ~22%</p><div class="meter"><span style="width:18%"></span></div><p class="t-small dim">Appliances ~18%</p><details class="acc"><summary>Quick savings check</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Wash at 30°C</li><li>Kill standby loads on one strip</li><li>Shift dishwasher to off-peak</li></ul></div></details></div></div><p class="small dim">If a number looks wrong, , query it. Estimates get corrected on request more often than people think.</p></div>	-
37.0.1	dashboard_split/Getting better at push-ups	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Zero to twenty push-ups</h1><p class="muted">Start where you actually are. Nobody's watching.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Progression</h4><ol class="list"><li><strong>Wall</strong> — 3×10, easy angle.</li><li><strong>Incline</strong> — hands on a table or step.</li><li><strong>Negative</strong> — start tall, lower for 4 slow seconds, knees down.</li><li><strong>Full</strong> — 3–5 reps, add one weekly.</li></ol><div class="badge accent">Form over count</div></div><div class="card"><h4>Form cues</h4><ul class="list"><li>Hands a bit wider than shoulders, fingers spread.</li><li>Body one straight line — squeeze glutes, brace abs.</li><li>Elbows about 45°, not flared to 90°.</li><li>Full lockout, chest near the floor.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Wrists complaining?</summary><div class="body"><p>Try fists on a mat, or push-up handles. Slight elbow tuck helps too.</p></div></details></div></div><p class="small dim">Rest days count as training, <regex>user_name</regex>. Muscle is built in the gaps.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Zero to twenty push-ups</h1><p class="muted">Start where you actually are. Nobody's watching.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Progression</h4><ol class="list"><li><strong>Wall</strong> — 3×10, easy angle.</li><li><strong>Incline</strong> — hands on a table or step.</li><li><strong>Negative</strong> — start tall, lower for 4 slow seconds, knees down.</li><li><strong>Full</strong> — 3–5 reps, add one weekly.</li></ol><div class="badge accent">Form over count</div></div><div class="card"><h4>Form cues</h4><ul class="list"><li>Hands a bit wider than shoulders, fingers spread.</li><li>Body one straight line — squeeze glutes, brace abs.</li><li>Elbows about 45°, not flared to 90°.</li><li>Full lockout, chest near the floor.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Wrists complaining?</summary><div class="body"><p>Try fists on a mat, or push-up handles. Slight elbow tuck helps too.</p></div></details></div></div><p class="small dim">Rest days count as training, . Muscle is built in the gaps.</p></div>	-
37.1.1	dashboard_split/Deep cleaning a kitchen sink	<div class="umi-page"><h1>The sink reset</h1><p class="muted">Twenty minutes, once a month, and it stays pleasant in between.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Order matters</h4><ol class="list"><li>Clear and rinse. Pull the strainer, bin the gunk.</li><li>Scrub walls with hot soapy water and a non-scratch pad.</li><li>De-scale: vinegar-soaked cloth on limescale, 10 min, wipe.</li><li>Shine with a dry microfibre, following the grain.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p>No bleach and vinegar in the same basin. Pick one.</p></div></div><div class="card"><h4>The smell</h4><p>It's rarely the basin — it's the drain.</p><ul class="list"><li>Pour a kettle of hot water down slowly.</li><li>Then half a cup of baking soda, then vinegar. Fizz, wait 10 min, flush hot.</li><li>Weekly: run the disposal with ice and a lemon half.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Cleaning the disposal safely</summary><div class="body"><p>Power off at the breaker first. Never reach in with fingers, even off.</p></div></details></div></div><p class="small dim">Enjoy the shine, <regex>user_name</regex>. It lasts a satisfyingly long time.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>The sink reset</h1><p class="muted">Twenty minutes, once a month, and it stays pleasant in between.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Order matters</h4><ol class="list"><li>Clear and rinse. Pull the strainer, bin the gunk.</li><li>Scrub walls with hot soapy water and a non-scratch pad.</li><li>De-scale: vinegar-soaked cloth on limescale, 10 min, wipe.</li><li>Shine with a dry microfibre, following the grain.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p>No bleach and vinegar in the same basin. Pick one.</p></div></div><div class="card"><h4>The smell</h4><p>It's rarely the basin — it's the drain.</p><ul class="list"><li>Pour a kettle of hot water down slowly.</li><li>Then half a cup of baking soda, then vinegar. Fizz, wait 10 min, flush hot.</li><li>Weekly: run the disposal with ice and a lemon half.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Cleaning the disposal safely</summary><div class="body"><p>Power off at the breaker first. Never reach in with fingers, even off.</p></div></details></div></div><p class="small dim">Enjoy the shine, . It lasts a satisfyingly long time.</p></div>	-
37.2.1	dashboard_split/Campfire in the Rain	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Campfire in the Rain</h1><p class="muted">Wet wood and drizzle don't have to end the evening. Here's how to keep a fire alive when the sky is against you, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Build it</h4><ol class="list"><li><strong>Find dry ground:</strong> under a rock overhang, a tarp, or scrape back wet leaves.</li><li><strong>Dry tinder:</strong> birch bark, dead pine needles from the underside of branches, or a candle stub.</li><li><strong>Base layer:</strong> lay down a few dry sticks in a criss-cross so the fire isn't sitting on wet earth.</li><li><strong>Feather sticks:</strong> shave dry inner wood into curls, leaving them attached.</li><li><strong>Cover:</strong> prop a tarp or large leaves above the fire to deflect rain (high enough not to catch).</li></ol></div><div class="card"><h4>Keep it going</h4><ul class="list"><li>Feed it small dry twigs often rather than big wet logs.</li><li>Bank the coals with ash; they stay hot longer than flames.</li><li>Use a fire starter (cotton ball + petroleum jelly) if you have one.</li><li>Don't smother it: airflow matters more than fuel when it's damp.</li></ul><div class="callout info">Wet wood can still burn if you split it and expose the dry core. If you can, carry a small hatchet or baton logs with a sturdy stick.</div></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>What if everything is soaked?</summary><div class="body"><p>You can still get a flame with a candle and a bit of dry tinder from your pockets (lint, paper). If not, a small gas stove is the graceful pivot. No shame in a hot meal.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Campfire in the Rain</h1><p class="muted">Wet wood and drizzle don't have to end the evening. Here's how to keep a fire alive when the sky is against you, .</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Build it</h4><ol class="list"><li><strong>Find dry ground:</strong> under a rock overhang, a tarp, or scrape back wet leaves.</li><li><strong>Dry tinder:</strong> birch bark, dead pine needles from the underside of branches, or a candle stub.</li><li><strong>Base layer:</strong> lay down a few dry sticks in a criss-cross so the fire isn't sitting on wet earth.</li><li><strong>Feather sticks:</strong> shave dry inner wood into curls, leaving them attached.</li><li><strong>Cover:</strong> prop a tarp or large leaves above the fire to deflect rain (high enough not to catch).</li></ol></div><div class="card"><h4>Keep it going</h4><ul class="list"><li>Feed it small dry twigs often rather than big wet logs.</li><li>Bank the coals with ash; they stay hot longer than flames.</li><li>Use a fire starter (cotton ball + petroleum jelly) if you have one.</li><li>Don't smother it: airflow matters more than fuel when it's damp.</li></ul><div class="callout info">Wet wood can still burn if you split it and expose the dry core. If you can, carry a small hatchet or baton logs with a sturdy stick.</div></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>What if everything is soaked?</summary><div class="body"><p>You can still get a flame with a candle and a bit of dry tinder from your pockets (lint, paper). If not, a small gas stove is the graceful pivot. No shame in a hot meal.</p></div></details></div>	-
37.3.1	dashboard_split/Budgeting Basics	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Budgeting Basics</h1><p class="muted">A budget isn't a cage; it's a map. Here's a simple way to see where your money goes, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Track first</h4><p>For one month, write down every purchase. No judgment, just data. Use a notes app or a small notebook.</p><div class="stat"><span class="label">Typical spending</span><span class="value">$2,400<span class="unit">/mo</span></span><span class="delta down">+ $300 vs income</span></div></div><div class="card"><h4>Then plan</h4><ul class="list"><li><strong>Fixed costs:</strong> rent, utilities, loan payments.</li><li><strong>Variable costs:</strong> food, transport, fun.</li><li><strong>Savings:</strong> pay yourself first, even $20.</li></ul><p class="t-small dim">Adjust one category at a time. Small tweaks stick better than a total overhaul.</p></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>What if my income changes each month?</summary><div class="body"><p>Base your budget on your lowest recent month. Anything above that goes to savings or debt. This gives you a stable floor.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Budgeting Basics</h1><p class="muted">A budget isn't a cage; it's a map. Here's a simple way to see where your money goes, .</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Track first</h4><p>For one month, write down every purchase. No judgment, just data. Use a notes app or a small notebook.</p><div class="stat"><span class="label">Typical spending</span><span class="value">$2,400<span class="unit">/mo</span></span><span class="delta down">+ $300 vs income</span></div></div><div class="card"><h4>Then plan</h4><ul class="list"><li><strong>Fixed costs:</strong> rent, utilities, loan payments.</li><li><strong>Variable costs:</strong> food, transport, fun.</li><li><strong>Savings:</strong> pay yourself first, even $20.</li></ul><p class="t-small dim">Adjust one category at a time. Small tweaks stick better than a total overhaul.</p></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>What if my income changes each month?</summary><div class="body"><p>Base your budget on your lowest recent month. Anything above that goes to savings or debt. This gives you a stable floor.</p></div></details></div>	-
37.4.1	dashboard_split/Changing a Flat Tyre	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Changing a Flat Tyre</h1><p class="muted">It's a workout, but you can do it. Pull over somewhere safe and flat, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Before you start</h4><ul class="list"><li>Turn on hazard lights.</li><li>Apply the parking brake.</li><li>Get out the spare, jack, and wrench.</li><li>Loosen the lug nuts <em>before</em> jacking up the car.</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Steps</h4><ol class="list"><li>Place the jack under the frame near the flat tyre.</li><li>Raise until the tyre is just off the ground.</li><li>Remove lug nuts and pull off the wheel.</li><li>Put on the spare and hand-tighten nuts in a star pattern.</li><li>Lower, then tighten fully with the wrench.</li></ol></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>How tight should the nuts be?</summary><div class="body"><p>As tight as you can with the wrench. If you have a torque wrench, check your manual for the spec. Re-check after 50 km.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Changing a Flat Tyre</h1><p class="muted">It's a workout, but you can do it. Pull over somewhere safe and flat, .</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Before you start</h4><ul class="list"><li>Turn on hazard lights.</li><li>Apply the parking brake.</li><li>Get out the spare, jack, and wrench.</li><li>Loosen the lug nuts <em>before</em> jacking up the car.</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Steps</h4><ol class="list"><li>Place the jack under the frame near the flat tyre.</li><li>Raise until the tyre is just off the ground.</li><li>Remove lug nuts and pull off the wheel.</li><li>Put on the spare and hand-tighten nuts in a star pattern.</li><li>Lower, then tighten fully with the wrench.</li></ol></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>How tight should the nuts be?</summary><div class="body"><p>As tight as you can with the wrench. If you have a torque wrench, check your manual for the spec. Re-check after 50 km.</p></div></details></div>	-
37.5.1	dashboard_split/Knife Sharpening Basics	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Knife Sharpening Basics</h1><p class="muted">A sharp knife is safer than a dull one. It takes a few minutes and a little patience, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>What you need</h4><ul class="list"><li>A whetstone (1000/6000 grit is a good start).</li><li>Water or oil (depending on the stone).</li><li>A towel to stabilize the stone.</li><li>Optional: honing rod for quick touch-ups.</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Method</h4><ol class="list"><li>Soak the stone if it's water-based.</li><li>Hold the knife at a 15–20° angle.</li><li>Push the edge across the stone as if slicing a thin layer.</li><li>Do 10 passes per side, then check for a burr.</li><li>Flip and repeat. Finish on the finer grit.</li></ol></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>How do I know it's sharp?</summary><div class="body"><p>Slice a tomato or paper. If it glides without pressure, you're good. If not, a few more passes.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Knife Sharpening Basics</h1><p class="muted">A sharp knife is safer than a dull one. It takes a few minutes and a little patience, .</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>What you need</h4><ul class="list"><li>A whetstone (1000/6000 grit is a good start).</li><li>Water or oil (depending on the stone).</li><li>A towel to stabilize the stone.</li><li>Optional: honing rod for quick touch-ups.</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Method</h4><ol class="list"><li>Soak the stone if it's water-based.</li><li>Hold the knife at a 15–20° angle.</li><li>Push the edge across the stone as if slicing a thin layer.</li><li>Do 10 passes per side, then check for a burr.</li><li>Flip and repeat. Finish on the finer grit.</li></ol></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>How do I know it's sharp?</summary><div class="body"><p>Slice a tomato or paper. If it glides without pressure, you're good. If not, a few more passes.</p></div></details></div>	-
37.6.1	dashboard_split/Sourdough Starter	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Sourdough Starter</h1><p class="muted">Flour and water, a little time, and you've got a pet that makes bread. Here's the short version, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Day 1</h4><ul class="list"><li>Mix 50g whole wheat flour + 50g water in a jar.</li><li>Cover loosely. Leave at room temp.</li></ul><h4>Days 2–7</h4><ul class="list"><li>Once a day, discard half, add 50g flour + 50g water.</li><li>Stir. Watch for bubbles and rise.</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Ready when…</h4><p>It doubles in size 4–6 hours after feeding, smells pleasantly sour, and has a dome. That's about 5–10 days.</p><div class="callout good">If it smells like nail polish, it's hungry. Feed it more often.</div></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Can I use it right away?</summary><div class="body"><p>Wait until it's reliably rising. Then you're ready to bake. Your first loaf might be dense, but it'll still taste like victory.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Sourdough Starter</h1><p class="muted">Flour and water, a little time, and you've got a pet that makes bread. Here's the short version, .</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Day 1</h4><ul class="list"><li>Mix 50g whole wheat flour + 50g water in a jar.</li><li>Cover loosely. Leave at room temp.</li></ul><h4>Days 2–7</h4><ul class="list"><li>Once a day, discard half, add 50g flour + 50g water.</li><li>Stir. Watch for bubbles and rise.</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Ready when…</h4><p>It doubles in size 4–6 hours after feeding, smells pleasantly sour, and has a dome. That's about 5–10 days.</p><div class="callout good">If it smells like nail polish, it's hungry. Feed it more often.</div></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Can I use it right away?</summary><div class="body"><p>Wait until it's reliably rising. Then you're ready to bake. Your first loaf might be dense, but it'll still taste like victory.</p></div></details></div>	-
37.7.1	dashboard_split/Removing Red Wine Stains	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Removing Red Wine Stains</h1><p class="muted">Act fast, blot, don't rub. You've got this, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>On fabric</h4><ol class="list"><li>Blot with a clean cloth. Don't rub.</li><li>Cover with salt to absorb the wine.</li><li>Mix dish soap + hydrogen peroxide (1:1).</li><li>Dab the solution on the stain.</li><li>Rinse with cold water. Repeat if needed.</li></ol></div><div class="card"><h4>On carpet</h4><ul class="list"><li>Blot up as much as possible.</li><li>Mix 1 tbsp dish soap + 1 tbsp white vinegar + 2 cups warm water.</li><li>Sponge onto the stain, blot, repeat.</li><li>Rinse with water and blot dry.</li></ul><div class="callout warn">Test any solution on a hidden spot first.</div></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>What if it's dried?</summary><div class="body"><p>Soak the fabric in cold water with oxygen bleach (not chlorine) for 30 minutes, then wash as usual. For carpet, try a steam cleaner.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Removing Red Wine Stains</h1><p class="muted">Act fast, blot, don't rub. You've got this, .</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>On fabric</h4><ol class="list"><li>Blot with a clean cloth. Don't rub.</li><li>Cover with salt to absorb the wine.</li><li>Mix dish soap + hydrogen peroxide (1:1).</li><li>Dab the solution on the stain.</li><li>Rinse with cold water. Repeat if needed.</li></ol></div><div class="card"><h4>On carpet</h4><ul class="list"><li>Blot up as much as possible.</li><li>Mix 1 tbsp dish soap + 1 tbsp white vinegar + 2 cups warm water.</li><li>Sponge onto the stain, blot, repeat.</li><li>Rinse with water and blot dry.</li></ul><div class="callout warn">Test any solution on a hidden spot first.</div></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>What if it's dried?</summary><div class="body"><p>Soak the fabric in cold water with oxygen bleach (not chlorine) for 30 minutes, then wash as usual. For carpet, try a steam cleaner.</p></div></details></div>	-
37.8.1	dashboard_split/Freezer inventory	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Freezer inventory</h1><p class="muted">A split view: shelves on the left, what to do with the tally on the right.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>What's in there</h4><ul class="list"><li>Top shelf: berries (2 bags), peas, edamame</li><li>Middle: chicken thighs (4), stock (3 tubs), chili (1)</li><li>Bottom: bread ends, dumplings, ice packs</li><li>Door: butter, nuts, coffee beans</li></ul><p class="t-small dim">Tap any line to cross it off after you eat it.</p></div><div class="card"><h4>Use-by order</h4><ol class="list"><li>Chili from three weeks ago</li><li>Chicken thighs (freezer-burned edges)</li><li>Berries for smoothies</li><li>Bread ends for croutons</li></ol><p class="muted">Nothing here is a rule. Just a nudge so the oldest stuff leaves first.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Freezer inventory</h1><p class="muted">A split view: shelves on the left, what to do with the tally on the right.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>What's in there</h4><ul class="list"><li>Top shelf: berries (2 bags), peas, edamame</li><li>Middle: chicken thighs (4), stock (3 tubs), chili (1)</li><li>Bottom: bread ends, dumplings, ice packs</li><li>Door: butter, nuts, coffee beans</li></ul><p class="t-small dim">Tap any line to cross it off after you eat it.</p></div><div class="card"><h4>Use-by order</h4><ol class="list"><li>Chili from three weeks ago</li><li>Chicken thighs (freezer-burned edges)</li><li>Berries for smoothies</li><li>Bread ends for croutons</li></ol><p class="muted">Nothing here is a rule. Just a nudge so the oldest stuff leaves first.</p></div></div></div>	-
37.9.1	dashboard_split/Hydration log	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Hydration log</h1><p class="muted">Small sips, big picture. Left is today, right is the week.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Today</h4><p class="stat"><span class="label">Cups so far</span><span class="value">5</span></p><div class="meter"><span style="width: 62%"></span></div><p class="t-small dim">Target is roughly 8 cups. 62% done.</p><p><kbd>+1</kbd> next time you pass the tap.</p></div><div class="card"><h4>Last seven days</h4><svg class="chart" viewBox="0 0 140 60" role="img" aria-label="Bar chart of cups per day"><rect x="5" y="30" width="12" height="25" class="bar"></rect><rect x="25" y="20" width="12" height="35" class="bar"></rect><rect x="45" y="25" width="12" height="30" class="bar"></rect><rect x="65" y="10" width="12" height="45" class="bar alt"></rect><rect x="85" y="15" width="12" height="40" class="bar"></rect><rect x="105" y="28" width="12" height="27" class="bar"></rect><rect x="125" y="22" width="12" height="33" class="bar"></rect></svg><div class="legend"><i></i> Cups</div><p class="t-small dim">Best day: Thursday. Lowest: Monday. No judgement.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Hydration log</h1><p class="muted">Small sips, big picture. Left is today, right is the week.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Today</h4><p class="stat"><span class="label">Cups so far</span><span class="value">5</span></p><div class="meter"><span style="width: 62%"></span></div><p class="t-small dim">Target is roughly 8 cups. 62% done.</p><p><kbd>+1</kbd> next time you pass the tap.</p></div><div class="card"><h4>Last seven days</h4><svg class="chart" viewBox="0 0 140 60" role="img" aria-label="Bar chart of cups per day"><rect x="5" y="30" width="12" height="25" class="bar"></rect><rect x="25" y="20" width="12" height="35" class="bar"></rect><rect x="45" y="25" width="12" height="30" class="bar"></rect><rect x="65" y="10" width="12" height="45" class="bar alt"></rec