37.10.1	dashboard_split/Cast iron care	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Cast iron care</h1><p class="muted">Rust is just a seasoning reset. Here's the split.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Daily</h4><ul class="list"><li>Rinse while warm, scrub with coarse salt if needed.</li><li>Dry on the hob for a minute.</li><li>Wipe a drop of oil with a paper towel.</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>If something goes wrong</h4><details class="acc"><summary>Rust spots</summary><div class="body"><p>Scrub with steel wool, wash, dry, then reseason. It's fixable.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Sticky patch</summary><div class="body"><p>Too much oil last time. Bake it off at 220°C for an hour upside down.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Food tastes metallic</summary><div class="body"><p>Seasoning is thin. Cook bacon. That's the whole fix.</p></div></details></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Cast iron care</h1><p class="muted">Rust is just a seasoning reset. Here's the split.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Daily</h4><ul class="list"><li>Rinse while warm, scrub with coarse salt if needed.</li><li>Dry on the hob for a minute.</li><li>Wipe a drop of oil with a paper towel.</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>If something goes wrong</h4><details class="acc"><summary>Rust spots</summary><div class="body"><p>Scrub with steel wool, wash, dry, then reseason. It's fixable.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Sticky patch</summary><div class="body"><p>Too much oil last time. Bake it off at 220°C for an hour upside down.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Food tastes metallic</summary><div class="body"><p>Seasoning is thin. Cook bacon. That's the whole fix.</p></div></details></div></div></div>	-
37.11.1	dashboard_split/First aid kit	<div class="umi-page"><h1>First aid kit</h1><p class="muted">Two columns: what goes in, and what you'll actually reach for.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Pack list</h4><ul class="list"><li>Assorted plasters</li><li>Sterile gauze pads and tape</li><li>Antiseptic wipes</li><li>Crepe bandage</li><li>Small scissors and tweezers</li><li>Disposable gloves</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Most-used items</h4><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th class="num">Times used</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Plasters</td><td class="num">9</td></tr><tr><td>Wipes</td><td class="num">4</td></tr><tr><td>Tweezers</td><td class="num">3</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="t-small dim">Replace anything you use next time you shop.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>First aid kit</h1><p class="muted">Two columns: what goes in, and what you'll actually reach for.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Pack list</h4><ul class="list"><li>Assorted plasters</li><li>Sterile gauze pads and tape</li><li>Antiseptic wipes</li><li>Crepe bandage</li><li>Small scissors and tweezers</li><li>Disposable gloves</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Most-used items</h4><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th class="num">Times used</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Plasters</td><td class="num">9</td></tr><tr><td>Wipes</td><td class="num">4</td></tr><tr><td>Tweezers</td><td class="num">3</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="t-small dim">Replace anything you use next time you shop.</p></div></div></div>	-
37.12.1	dashboard_split/5-ingredient dinners	<div class="umi-page"><h1>5-ingredient dinners</h1><p class="muted">Fewer things, less thinking. Split by mood.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Warm and cosy</h4><ul class="list"><li>Tomato, garlic, pasta, olive oil, chilli</li><li>Potato, leek, stock, butter, thyme</li><li>Rice, egg, soy sauce, spring onion, sesame</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Cold and quick</h4><ul class="list"><li>Chickpeas, cucumber, feta, lemon, mint</li><li>Tortilla, cheese, beans, salsa, lime</li><li>Noodles, peanut butter, carrot, vinegar, coriander</li></ul></div></div><p class="muted">Cooking is just assembling. You've got this.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>5-ingredient dinners</h1><p class="muted">Fewer things, less thinking. Split by mood.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Warm and cosy</h4><ul class="list"><li>Tomato, garlic, pasta, olive oil, chilli</li><li>Potato, leek, stock, butter, thyme</li><li>Rice, egg, soy sauce, spring onion, sesame</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Cold and quick</h4><ul class="list"><li>Chickpeas, cucumber, feta, lemon, mint</li><li>Tortilla, cheese, beans, salsa, lime</li><li>Noodles, peanut butter, carrot, vinegar, coriander</li></ul></div></div><p class="muted">Cooking is just assembling. You've got this.</p></div>	-
37.13.1	dashboard_split/Squeaky hinge fix	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Squeaky hinge fix</h1><p class="muted">Two sides: what you need, and how to do it without taking the door off.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>You'll need</h4><ul class="list"><li>WD-40 or light machine oil</li><li>Cloth or paper towel</li><li>Small screwdriver (just in case)</li></ul><p class="t-small dim">That's it. No special tools.</p></div><div class="card"><h4>Steps</h4><ol class="list"><li>Wipe the hinge pin clean.</li><li>Drip oil onto the top of the pin.</li><li>Open and close the door a few times.</li><li>Wipe excess oil.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>If it still squeaks, the hinge might be loose. Tighten the screws.</p></div></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Squeaky hinge fix</h1><p class="muted">Two sides: what you need, and how to do it without taking the door off.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>You'll need</h4><ul class="list"><li>WD-40 or light machine oil</li><li>Cloth or paper towel</li><li>Small screwdriver (just in case)</li></ul><p class="t-small dim">That's it. No special tools.</p></div><div class="card"><h4>Steps</h4><ol class="list"><li>Wipe the hinge pin clean.</li><li>Drip oil onto the top of the pin.</li><li>Open and close the door a few times.</li><li>Wipe excess oil.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>If it still squeaks, the hinge might be loose. Tighten the screws.</p></div></div></div></div>	-
37.14.1	dashboard_split/Refrigerator Deep Clean	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Fridge deep clean</h1>  <p class="muted">A quick reset for the box that holds your food. <regex>user_name</regex>, do the shelves first, then the walls.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">Supplies</h4>      <ul class="list">        <li>Warm water + mild dish soap</li>        <li>Baking soda (for odors)</li>        <li>Microfiber cloths</li>        <li>A cooler or sink for cold items</li>      </ul>      <div class="callout info">        <p>Unplug if you can. If not, work fast and keep the door closed between steps.</p>      </div>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">Steps</h4>      <ol class="list">        <li>Empty everything into the cooler.</li>        <li>Remove shelves and drawers; wash in sink.</li>        <li>Wipe interior walls top to bottom.</li>        <li>Dry thoroughly, then replace parts.</li>        <li>Put a small dish of baking soda inside.</li>      </ol>      <p class="t-small dim">Toss anything expired while it's out.</p>    </div>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Fridge deep clean</h1>  <p class="muted">A quick reset for the box that holds your food. , do the shelves first, then the walls.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">Supplies</h4>      <ul class="list">        <li>Warm water + mild dish soap</li>        <li>Baking soda (for odors)</li>        <li>Microfiber cloths</li>        <li>A cooler or sink for cold items</li>      </ul>      <div class="callout info">        <p>Unplug if you can. If not, work fast and keep the door closed between steps.</p>      </div>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">Steps</h4>      <ol class="list">        <li>Empty everything into the cooler.</li>        <li>Remove shelves and drawers; wash in sink.</li>        <li>Wipe interior walls top to bottom.</li>        <li>Dry thoroughly, then replace parts.</li>        <li>Put a small dish of baking soda inside.</li>      </ol>      <p class="t-small dim">Toss anything expired while it's out.</p>    </div>  </div></div>	-
37.15.1	dashboard_split/Choosing a Password Manager	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Password manager</h1>  <p class="muted"><regex>user_name</regex>, the goal is simple: one strong password to rule them all, and unique keys everywhere else.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">What to look for</h4>      <ul class="list">        <li>Zero-knowledge encryption</li>        <li>Works on your phone + browser</li>        <li>Two-factor authentication support</li>        <li>Emergency access or recovery kit</li>      </ul>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">Quick wins</h4>      <ul class="list">        <li>Start with your email account.</li>        <li>Let it generate new passwords as you go.</li>        <li>Enable 2FA on the manager itself.</li>      </ul>      <div class="callout warn">        <p>Don't reuse your master password anywhere else. Ever.</p>      </div>    </div>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Password manager</h1>  <p class="muted">, the goal is simple: one strong password to rule them all, and unique keys everywhere else.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">What to look for</h4>      <ul class="list">        <li>Zero-knowledge encryption</li>        <li>Works on your phone + browser</li>        <li>Two-factor authentication support</li>        <li>Emergency access or recovery kit</li>      </ul>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">Quick wins</h4>      <ul class="list">        <li>Start with your email account.</li>        <li>Let it generate new passwords as you go.</li>        <li>Enable 2FA on the manager itself.</li>      </ul>      <div class="callout warn">        <p>Don't reuse your master password anywhere else. Ever.</p>      </div>    </div>  </div></div>	-
37.16.1	dashboard_split/Tire Pressure Basics	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Tire pressure</h1>  <p class="muted">Right pressure = better mileage, safer stops, longer tire life. <regex>user_name</regex>, check when tires are cold.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">How to check</h4>      <ol class="list">        <li>Find the sticker inside the driver's door jamb.</li>        <li>Remove the valve cap.</li>        <li>Press the gauge firmly onto the valve.</li>        <li>Read the number; add air if low.</li>        <li>Replace cap. Repeat for all four.</li>      </ol>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">Good to know</h4>      <ul class="list">        <li>Check monthly and before long trips.</li>        <li>Don't trust the eyeball test.</li>        <li>Temperature drops can lower psi.</li>      </ul>      <a class="badge info">Cached answer</a>    </div>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Tire pressure</h1>  <p class="muted">Right pressure = better mileage, safer stops, longer tire life. , check when tires are cold.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">How to check</h4>      <ol class="list">        <li>Find the sticker inside the driver's door jamb.</li>        <li>Remove the valve cap.</li>        <li>Press the gauge firmly onto the valve.</li>        <li>Read the number; add air if low.</li>        <li>Replace cap. Repeat for all four.</li>      </ol>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">Good to know</h4>      <ul class="list">        <li>Check monthly and before long trips.</li>        <li>Don't trust the eyeball test.</li>        <li>Temperature drops can lower psi.</li>      </ul>      <a class="badge info">Cached answer</a>    </div>  </div></div>	-
37.17.1	dashboard_split/Unclogging a Drain	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Clogged drain</h1>  <p class="muted"><regex>user_name</regex>, start gentle. Most clogs are hair and soap, not grease.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">Try in order</h4>      <ol class="list">        <li>Boiling water (for metal pipes only).</li>        <li>Plunger with a good seal.</li>        <li>Baking soda + vinegar, then hot water.</li>        <li>Zip-it tool or drain snake.</li>      </ol>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">Avoid</h4>      <ul class="list">        <li>Chemical drain cleaners if you can.</li>        <li>More water if it's already full.</li>      </ul>      <div class="callout good">        <p>If it's a double sink, seal one side while plunging the other.</p>      </div>    </div>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Clogged drain</h1>  <p class="muted">, start gentle. Most clogs are hair and soap, not grease.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">Try in order</h4>      <ol class="list">        <li>Boiling water (for metal pipes only).</li>        <li>Plunger with a good seal.</li>        <li>Baking soda + vinegar, then hot water.</li>        <li>Zip-it tool or drain snake.</li>      </ol>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">Avoid</h4>      <ul class="list">        <li>Chemical drain cleaners if you can.</li>        <li>More water if it's already full.</li>      </ul>      <div class="callout good">        <p>If it's a double sink, seal one side while plunging the other.</p>      </div>    </div>  </div></div>	-
37.18.1	dashboard_split/Making a Shopping List	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Shopping list</h1>  <p class="muted">A list is a tiny plan. <regex>user_name</regex>, group by aisle and you'll shop faster.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">Build it</h4>      <ol class="list">        <li>Check what you already have.</li>        <li>Plan 3–4 dinners.</li>        <li>List ingredients by store section.</li>        <li>Add staples that are running low.</li>      </ol>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">Sections</h4>      <ul class="list">        <li>Produce</li>        <li>Dairy & eggs</li>        <li>Pantry</li>        <li>Frozen</li>      </ul>      <p class="t-small dim">Keep it on your phone for easy edits.</p>    </div>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Shopping list</h1>  <p class="muted">A list is a tiny plan. , group by aisle and you'll shop faster.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">Build it</h4>      <ol class="list">        <li>Check what you already have.</li>        <li>Plan 3–4 dinners.</li>        <li>List ingredients by store section.</li>        <li>Add staples that are running low.</li>      </ol>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">Sections</h4>      <ul class="list">        <li>Produce</li>        <li>Dairy & eggs</li>        <li>Pantry</li>        <li>Frozen</li>      </ul>      <p class="t-small dim">Keep it on your phone for easy edits.</p>    </div>  </div></div>	-
37.19.1	dashboard_split/Stretching Before Bed	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Bedtime stretches</h1>  <p class="muted"><regex>user_name</regex>, keep it slow. You're not training, you're signaling your body to power down.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">5-minute flow</h4>      <ol class="list">        <li>Child's pose — 1 min</li>        <li>Seated forward fold — 1 min</li>        <li>Figure-four stretch — 1 min each side</li>        <li>Legs up the wall — 2 min</li>      </ol>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">Remember</h4>      <ul class="list">        <li>Breathe out longer than you breathe in.</li>        <li>No bouncing.</li>        <li>Dim the lights.</li>      </ul>      <div class="callout info">        <p>If a stretch hurts, back off. Gentle is the point.</p>      </div>    </div>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Bedtime stretches</h1>  <p class="muted">, keep it slow. You're not training, you're signaling your body to power down.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">5-minute flow</h4>      <ol class="list">        <li>Child's pose — 1 min</li>        <li>Seated forward fold — 1 min</li>        <li>Figure-four stretch — 1 min each side</li>        <li>Legs up the wall — 2 min</li>      </ol>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">Remember</h4>      <ul class="list">        <li>Breathe out longer than you breathe in.</li>        <li>No bouncing.</li>        <li>Dim the lights.</li>      </ul>      <div class="callout info">        <p>If a stretch hurts, back off. Gentle is the point.</p>      </div>    </div>  </div></div>	-
37.20.1	dashboard_split/Fixing a Squeaky Door	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Fixing a Squeaky Door</h1><p class="muted">A little quiet for you, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Why it squeaks</h4><p>Metal rubbing metal. The hinge pin has lost its film of oil, so it grinds instead of gliding.</p><div class="callout info">Quick test: hold the door still and swing the hinge. If the noise stops, it's the hinge, not the latch.</div></div><div class="card"><h4>Fix it in 5 minutes</h4><ol class="list"><li>Wipe dust off the hinge with a dry cloth.</li><li>Drip a little oil (WD-40, olive oil, or white lithium grease) onto the top of the hinge pin.</li><li>Open and close the door a few times to work it in.</li><li>Wipe away drips with a rag.</li></ol><p class="t-small dim">No oil? A pencil rubbed on the pin works in a pinch.</p></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Extra quiet tips</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>If the hinge still squeaks, tap the pin out with a hammer and nail, clean it, then grease.</li><li>For a squeaky latch, rub a little soap on the strike plate.</li></ul></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Fixing a Squeaky Door</h1><p class="muted">A little quiet for you, .</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Why it squeaks</h4><p>Metal rubbing metal. The hinge pin has lost its film of oil, so it grinds instead of gliding.</p><div class="callout info">Quick test: hold the door still and swing the hinge. If the noise stops, it's the hinge, not the latch.</div></div><div class="card"><h4>Fix it in 5 minutes</h4><ol class="list"><li>Wipe dust off the hinge with a dry cloth.</li><li>Drip a little oil (WD-40, olive oil, or white lithium grease) onto the top of the hinge pin.</li><li>Open and close the door a few times to work it in.</li><li>Wipe away drips with a rag.</li></ol><p class="t-small dim">No oil? A pencil rubbed on the pin works in a pinch.</p></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Extra quiet tips</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>If the hinge still squeaks, tap the pin out with a hammer and nail, clean it, then grease.</li><li>For a squeaky latch, rub a little soap on the strike plate.</li></ul></div></details></div>	-
37.21.1	dashboard_split/Reading a Nutrition Label	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Reading a Nutrition Label</h1><p class="muted">Food labels are just tiny puzzles, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Start here</h4><p><strong>Serving size</strong> is the whole game. Everything else on the label is based on it. If you eat two servings, double the numbers.</p><p><strong>Calories</strong> per serving: useful, but not the whole story.</p></div><div class="card"><h4>What to check</h4><ul class="list"><li><strong>Added sugars:</strong> keep it low.</li><li><strong>Sodium:</strong> under 5% DV is low, over 20% is high.</li><li><strong>Fiber:</strong> more is better.</li><li><strong>Protein:</strong> your friend for staying full.</li></ul><div class="callout good">The % Daily Value column is a quick cheat: 5% is low, 20% is high.</div></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Ingredient list trick</summary><div class="body"><p>Ingredients are listed by weight. If sugar is in the first three, it's a sugary food. If you can't pronounce it, doesn't mean it's bad—just maybe not whole food.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Reading a Nutrition Label</h1><p class="muted">Food labels are just tiny puzzles, .</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Start here</h4><p><strong>Serving size</strong> is the whole game. Everything else on the label is based on it. If you eat two servings, double the numbers.</p><p><strong>Calories</strong> per serving: useful, but not the whole story.</p></div><div class="card"><h4>What to check</h4><ul class="list"><li><strong>Added sugars:</strong> keep it low.</li><li><strong>Sodium:</strong> under 5% DV is low, over 20% is high.</li><li><strong>Fiber:</strong> more is better.</li><li><strong>Protein:</strong> your friend for staying full.</li></ul><div class="callout good">The % Daily Value column is a quick cheat: 5% is low, 20% is high.</div></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Ingredient list trick</summary><div class="body"><p>Ingredients are listed by weight. If sugar is in the first three, it's a sugary food. If you can't pronounce it, doesn't mean it's bad—just maybe not whole food.</p></div></details></div>	-
37.22.1	dashboard_split/Jump-Starting a Car	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Jump-Starting a Car</h1><p class="muted">Dead battery? You've got this, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>You'll need</h4><ul class="list"><li>Jumper cables (or a jump pack)</li><li>A working car (or jump pack)</li><li>Safety glasses (optional but smart)</li></ul><div class="callout warn">Never let the cable clamps touch each other while connected.</div></div><div class="card"><h4>Steps</h4><ol class="list"><li>Park both cars close but not touching. Turn both off.</li><li>Connect red clamp to dead battery's positive (+).</li><li>Connect other red clamp to good battery's positive (+).</li><li>Connect black clamp to good battery's negative (-).</li><li>Connect other black clamp to a metal bolt on the dead car's engine block (not the battery).</li><li>Start the good car, let it run a minute, then start the dead car.</li><li>Remove clamps in reverse order.</li></ol></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>If it still won't start</summary><div class="body"><p>Let the good car run longer, or your battery might be beyond a jump. Time for a new one.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Jump-Starting a Car</h1><p class="muted">Dead battery? You've got this, .</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>You'll need</h4><ul class="list"><li>Jumper cables (or a jump pack)</li><li>A working car (or jump pack)</li><li>Safety glasses (optional but smart)</li></ul><div class="callout warn">Never let the cable clamps touch each other while connected.</div></div><div class="card"><h4>Steps</h4><ol class="list"><li>Park both cars close but not touching. Turn both off.</li><li>Connect red clamp to dead battery's positive (+).</li><li>Connect other red clamp to good battery's positive (+).</li><li>Connect black clamp to good battery's negative (-).</li><li>Connect other black clamp to a metal bolt on the dead car's engine block (not the battery).</li><li>Start the good car, let it run a minute, then start the dead car.</li><li>Remove clamps in reverse order.</li></ol></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>If it still won't start</summary><div class="body"><p>Let the good car run longer, or your battery might be beyond a jump. Time for a new one.</p></div></details></div>	-
37.23.1	dashboard_split/Making a Phone Battery Last Longer	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Making a Phone Battery Last Longer</h1><p class="muted">Small tweaks, big difference, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Quick wins</h4><ul class="list"><li>Lower screen brightness or use auto-brightness.</li><li>Turn off Bluetooth and Wi-Fi when you're out and about.</li><li>Use dark mode if your phone has an OLED screen.</li><li>Close background apps that are constantly refreshing.</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Longer-term habits</h4><ul class="list"><li>Avoid extreme heat or cold—it stresses the battery.</li><li>Don't leave it plugged in at 100% all night, if you can help it.</li><li>Check which apps are using the most battery in settings.</li></ul><div class="callout info">Battery health degrades over time. If it's below 80%, a replacement might be the real fix.</div></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Low power mode</summary><div class="body"><p>It limits background activity and can add hours. Use it when you're far from a charger.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Making a Phone Battery Last Longer</h1><p class="muted">Small tweaks, big difference, .</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Quick wins</h4><ul class="list"><li>Lower screen brightness or use auto-brightness.</li><li>Turn off Bluetooth and Wi-Fi when you're out and about.</li><li>Use dark mode if your phone has an OLED screen.</li><li>Close background apps that are constantly refreshing.</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Longer-term habits</h4><ul class="list"><li>Avoid extreme heat or cold—it stresses the battery.</li><li>Don't leave it plugged in at 100% all night, if you can help it.</li><li>Check which apps are using the most battery in settings.</li></ul><div class="callout info">Battery health degrades over time. If it's below 80%, a replacement might be the real fix.</div></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Low power mode</summary><div class="body"><p>It limits background activity and can add hours. Use it when you're far from a charger.</p></div></details></div>	-
37.24.1	dashboard_split/Removing a Stripped Screw	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Removing a Stripped Screw</h1><p class="muted">Stubborn screw? Let's outsmart it, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>First attempts</h4><ul class="list"><li>Press down hard while turning slowly—sometimes it just needs pressure.</li><li>Try a different screwdriver that fits better.</li><li>If the head is painted, scrape the paint out of the slot.</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Next level</h4><ul class="list"><li>Place a wide rubber band over the screw head, then press the screwdriver into it.</li><li>Use a screw extractor kit (cheap and effective).</li><li>Carefully drill off the head with a metal-cutting bit, then remove the shaft with pliers.</li></ul><div class="callout warn">Wear safety glasses when drilling.</div></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Prevention for next time</summary><div class="body"><p>Always use the right size screwdriver. If it cams out, stop and reassess before you make it worse.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Removing a Stripped Screw</h1><p class="muted">Stubborn screw? Let's outsmart it, .</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>First attempts</h4><ul class="list"><li>Press down hard while turning slowly—sometimes it just needs pressure.</li><li>Try a different screwdriver that fits better.</li><li>If the head is painted, scrape the paint out of the slot.</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Next level</h4><ul class="list"><li>Place a wide rubber band over the screw head, then press the screwdriver into it.</li><li>Use a screw extractor kit (cheap and effective).</li><li>Carefully drill off the head with a metal-cutting bit, then remove the shaft with pliers.</li></ul><div class="callout warn">Wear safety glasses when drilling.</div></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Prevention for next time</summary><div class="body"><p>Always use the right size screwdriver. If it cams out, stop and reassess before you make it worse.</p></div></details></div>	-
37.25.1	dashboard_split/Basic Bike Maintenance	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Basic Bike Maintenance</h1><p class="muted">A little love keeps you rolling, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Weekly check</h4><ul class="list"><li><strong>Tyres:</strong> squeeze them. If they feel soft, pump to the pressure on the sidewall.</li><li><strong>Brakes:</strong> spin the wheel, pull the lever. Pads should hit the rim, not the tyre.</li><li><strong>Chain:</strong> if it sounds dry, add a drop of bike lube and wipe off excess.</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Monthly check</h4><ul class="list"><li>Check for loose bolts (stem, seat post, pedals).</li><li>Clean and lube the chain properly.</li><li>Look at brake pads for wear—replace if they're thin.</li><li>Check tyre tread for embedded glass or stones.</li></ul><div class="callout info">A clean bike is a happy bike. Wipe it down when it's dirty.</div></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>When to see a shop</summary><div class="body"><p>If gears skip, wheels wobble, or you hear grinding, it's probably time for a professional tune-up.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Basic Bike Maintenance</h1><p class="muted">A little love keeps you rolling, .</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Weekly check</h4><ul class="list"><li><strong>Tyres:</strong> squeeze them. If they feel soft, pump to the pressure on the sidewall.</li><li><strong>Brakes:</strong> spin the wheel, pull the lever. Pads should hit the rim, not the tyre.</li><li><strong>Chain:</strong> if it sounds dry, add a drop of bike lube and wipe off excess.</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Monthly check</h4><ul class="list"><li>Check for loose bolts (stem, seat post, pedals).</li><li>Clean and lube the chain properly.</li><li>Look at brake pads for wear—replace if they're thin.</li><li>Check tyre tread for embedded glass or stones.</li></ul><div class="callout info">A clean bike is a happy bike. Wipe it down when it's dirty.</div></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>When to see a shop</summary><div class="body"><p>If gears skip, wheels wobble, or you hear grinding, it's probably time for a professional tune-up.</p></div></details></div>	-
37.26.1	dashboard_split/Reading a train timetable	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Reading a train timetable</h1><p class="muted">Three columns do all the work, <regex>user_name</regex>. Learn them once and you'll never squint at a platform board again.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">The three columns</h4><ul class="list"><li><strong>Departs</strong> — wheels rolling. Not when you should arrive at the station.</li><li><strong>Arrives</strong> — scheduled stop. Delays push it, plans don't.</li><li><strong>Calling at</strong> — the stations between. Match your stop exactly.</li></ul><div class="callout info"><p>Some timetables mark departure as <kbd>d</kbd> and arrival as <kbd>a</kbd>. Same thing, shorter ink.</p></div></div><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Anatomy of one row</h4><svg class="chart" viewBox="0 0 320 90" role="img" aria-label="Row diagram"><rect x="8" y="30" width="70" height="28" rx="6" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.12"/><text x="43" y="48" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">08:14</text><line x1="86" y1="44" x2="150" y2="44" class="guide"/><rect x="158" y="30" width="70" height="28" rx="6" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.12"/><text x="193" y="48" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">09:02</text><line x1="236" y1="44" x2="300" y2="44" class="guide"/><text x="43" y="74" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl dim">depart</text><text x="193" y="74" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl dim">arrive</text></svg><div class="callout warn"><p>Beware the platform change. The board updates before the timetable does — trust the board at the station.</p></div></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>What the little footnotes mean</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li><strong>SX</strong> — runs on Saturdays only.</li><li><strong>M</strong> — Mondays only.</li><li><strong>1st</strong> — first class carriages only at that stop.</li></ul><p class="t-small muted">Footnotes apply to the specific column they sit above, not the whole row.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Peak vs off-peak</summary><div class="body"><p>Peak fares usually start around 06:30 and end around 09:30, then return for the evening. Off-peak tickets bought for peak trains get you a fine, <regex>user_name</regex>. Check the operator, not just the clock.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Reading a train timetable</h1><p class="muted">Three columns do all the work, . Learn them once and you'll never squint at a platform board again.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">The three columns</h4><ul class="list"><li><strong>Departs</strong> — wheels rolling. Not when you should arrive at the station.</li><li><strong>Arrives</strong> — scheduled stop. Delays push it, plans don't.</li><li><strong>Calling at</strong> — the stations between. Match your stop exactly.</li></ul><div class="callout info"><p>Some timetables mark departure as <kbd>d</kbd> and arrival as <kbd>a</kbd>. Same thing, shorter ink.</p></div></div><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Anatomy of one row</h4><svg class="chart" viewBox="0 0 320 90" role="img" aria-label="Row diagram"><rect x="8" y="30" width="70" height="28" rx="6" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.12"/><text x="43" y="48" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">08:14</text><line x1="86" y1="44" x2="150" y2="44" class="guide"/><rect x="158" y="30" width="70" height="28" rx="6" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.12"/><text x="193" y="48" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">09:02</text><line x1="236" y1="44" x2="300" y2="44" class="guide"/><text x="43" y="74" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl dim">depart</text><text x="193" y="74" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl dim">arrive</text></svg><div class="callout warn"><p>Beware the platform change. The board updates before the timetable does — trust the board at the station.</p></div></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>What the little footnotes mean</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li><strong>SX</strong> — runs on Saturdays only.</li><li><strong>M</strong> — Mondays only.</li><li><strong>1st</strong> — first class carriages only at that stop.</li></ul><p class="t-small muted">Footnotes apply to the specific column they sit above, not the whole row.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Peak vs off-peak</summary><div class="body"><p>Peak fares usually start around 06:30 and end around 09:30, then return for the evening. Off-peak tickets bought for peak trains get you a fine, . Check the operator, not just the clock.</p></div></details></div>	-
37.27.1	dashboard_split/Replacing a watch battery	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Replacing a watch battery</h1><p class="muted">Most quartz watches pop open easily, <regex>user_name>. The trick is patience and the right size cell — not brute force.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">You'll need</h4><ul class="list"><li>A case-back knife or a small flat-head screwdriver</li><li>Finger cots or tape (the movement hates fingerprints)</li><li>A fresh cell — check the old one for the number (usually CR2016 or CR2032)</li><li>A soft cloth and a toothpick</li></ul><div class="callout warn"><p>If it's a diving watch or has a screw-down back, stop and take it to someone with a press. Waterproofing depends on that gasket.</p></div></div><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Battery cells at a glance</h4><svg class="chart" viewBox="0 0 300 90" role="img" aria-label="Battery sizes"><rect x="20" y="30" width="80" height="28" rx="14" fill="var(--accent-soft)"/><text x="60" y="50" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">CR2016</text><rect x="120" y="26" width="80" height="36" rx="18" fill="var(--accent-soft)"/><text x="160" y="50" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">CR2025</text><rect x="220" y="22" width="70" height="44" rx="22" fill="var(--accent-soft)"/><text x="255" y="50" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">CR2032</text></svg><p class="t-small muted">The number is diameter / thickness in millimetres. Bigger last number, thicker cell.</p></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Step by step</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Set the watch face-down on a cloth so you can see the back hatch.</li><li>Find the tiny lip near the notch. Slide the blade under and twist gently.</li><li>Lift the back off. Snap a photo if springs fly out.</li><li>Use the toothpick to ease the old cell out from the edge.</li><li>Press the new cell in with the <strong>plus side facing up</strong>.</li><li>Check it ticks. Then reseat the gasket and press the back until it clicks.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>It still won't run</summary><div class="body"><p>If the hands don't move, the issue isn't the cell — the movement may need a clean, or the crown is pulled out to a setting position. Push the crown all the way in and try again.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Replacing a watch battery</h1><p class="muted">Most quartz watches pop open easily, <regex>user_name>. The trick is patience and the right size cell — not brute force.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">You'll need</h4><ul class="list"><li>A case-back knife or a small flat-head screwdriver</li><li>Finger cots or tape (the movement hates fingerprints)</li><li>A fresh cell — check the old one for the number (usually CR2016 or CR2032)</li><li>A soft cloth and a toothpick</li></ul><div class="callout warn"><p>If it's a diving watch or has a screw-down back, stop and take it to someone with a press. Waterproofing depends on that gasket.</p></div></div><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Battery cells at a glance</h4><svg class="chart" viewBox="0 0 300 90" role="img" aria-label="Battery sizes"><rect x="20" y="30" width="80" height="28" rx="14" fill="var(--accent-soft)"/><text x="60" y="50" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">CR2016</text><rect x="120" y="26" width="80" height="36" rx="18" fill="var(--accent-soft)"/><text x="160" y="50" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">CR2025</text><rect x="220" y="22" width="70" height="44" rx="22" fill="var(--accent-soft)"/><text x="255" y="50" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">CR2032</text></svg><p class="t-small muted">The number is diameter / thickness in millimetres. Bigger last number, thicker cell.</p></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Step by step</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Set the watch face-down on a cloth so you can see the back hatch.</li><li>Find the tiny lip near the notch. Slide the blade under and twist gently.</li><li>Lift the back off. Snap a photo if springs fly out.</li><li>Use the toothpick to ease the old cell out from the edge.</li><li>Press the new cell in with the <strong>plus side facing up</strong>.</li><li>Check it ticks. Then reseat the gasket and press the back until it clicks.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>It still won't run</summary><div class="body"><p>If the hands don't move, the issue isn't the cell — the movement may need a clean, or the crown is pulled out to a setting position. Push the crown all the way in and try again.</p></div></details></div>	-
37.28.1	dashboard_split/Getting a good night's sleep	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Getting a good night's sleep</h1><p class="muted">Sleep is mostly a timing problem, <regex>user_name>, not a willpower one. Get the rhythm right and the rest follows.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">The four levers</h4><ul class="list"><li><strong>Light</strong> — bright morning, dim evening. Your body reads light as the clock.</li><li><strong>Temperature</strong> — a cool room helps you drop off. Around 17–19°C works for most.</li><li><strong>Timing</strong> — same wake-up time every day, weekends included.</li><li><strong>Pressure</strong> — if you're awake 20 minutes, get up and read somewhere dim.</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Sleep pressure through the day</h4><svg class="chart" viewBox="0 0 300 110" role="img" aria-label="Sleep pressure rises through the day"><line x1="20" y1="90" x2="280" y2="90" class="axis"/><line x1="20" y1="10" x2="20" y2="90" class="axis"/><path d="M20 20 C 90 60, 180 80, 275 88" class="line" fill="none"/><text x="20" y="104" class="lbl dim">wake</text><text x="275" y="104" text-anchor="end" class="lbl dim">bed</text></svg><p class="t-small muted">The line is drowsiness. Naps take a bite out of it; caffeine hides it.</p></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Waking at 3am, wide awake</summary><div class="body"><p>Common and annoying. Usually a dip in the sleep cycle, a warm room, or a drink too close to bed. Don't check the clock — that teaches your brain to be alert at 3am. Stay still and dim.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>The bedtime wind-down</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>One hour before bed, dull the lights.</li><li>Put the phone across the room on charge.</li><li>Warm shower if you like — the drop in body temperature after helps.</li><li>Read something on paper until your eyelids argue.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p>Do this three nights in a row and your body starts expecting sleep at the same time. Boring, but it works.</p></div></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Getting a good night's sleep</h1><p class="muted">Sleep is mostly a timing problem, <regex>user_name>, not a willpower one. Get the rhythm right and the rest follows.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">The four levers</h4><ul class="list"><li><strong>Light</strong> — bright morning, dim evening. Your body reads light as the clock.</li><li><strong>Temperature</strong> — a cool room helps you drop off. Around 17–19°C works for most.</li><li><strong>Timing</strong> — same wake-up time every day, weekends included.</li><li><strong>Pressure</strong> — if you're awake 20 minutes, get up and read somewhere dim.</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Sleep pressure through the day</h4><svg class="chart" viewBox="0 0 300 110" role="img" aria-label="Sleep pressure rises through the day"><line x1="20" y1="90" x2="280" y2="90" class="axis"/><line x1="20" y1="10" x2="20" y2="90" class="axis"/><path d="M20 20 C 90 60, 180 80, 275 88" class="line" fill="none"/><text x="20" y="104" class="lbl dim">wake</text><text x="275" y="104" text-anchor="end" class="lbl dim">bed</text></svg><p class="t-small muted">The line is drowsiness. Naps take a bite out of it; caffeine hides it.</p></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Waking at 3am, wide awake</summary><div class="body"><p>Common and annoying. Usually a dip in the sleep cycle, a warm room, or a drink too close to bed. Don't check the clock — that teaches your brain to be alert at 3am. Stay still and dim.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>The bedtime wind-down</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>One hour before bed, dull the lights.</li><li>Put the phone across the room on charge.</li><li>Warm shower if you like — the drop in body temperature after helps.</li><li>Read something on paper until your eyelids argue.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p>Do this three nights in a row and your body starts expecting sleep at the same time. Boring, but it works.</p></div></div></details></div>	-
37.29.1	dashboard_split/Making a fire without matches	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Making a fire without matches</h1><p class="muted">Sparks need something to land on, <regex>user_name>. Build the tinder bundle first and the fire part is easy.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">The three-size rule</h4><ul class="list"><li><strong>Tinder</strong> — dry grass, birch bark, char cloth, cotton. Must catch from a spark.</li><li><strong>Kindling</strong> — pencil-thick dry twigs.</li><li><strong>Fuel</strong> — wrist-thick and up, dry deadwood.</li></ul><div class="callout warn"><p>Damp wood steals heat, no matter how good your spark. If it snaps easily, it's dry enough.</p></div></div><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Lay the tinder bundle</h4><svg class="chart" viewBox="0 0 260 110" role="img" aria-label="Layered fire lay"><path d="M60 80 L130 24 L200 80 Z" class="area" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.15"/><text x="130" y="54" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">tepee</text><line x1="60" y1="80" x2="200" y2="80" class="guide"/><rect x="60" y="86" width="140" height="14" rx="4" fill="var(--accent-soft)"/><text x="130" y="97" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">kindling bed</text></svg><p class="t-small muted">Tepee lay pulls air up through the middle. Best for a quick start.</p></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Using a ferro rod</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Anchor the rod close to the tinder.</li><li>Draw the striker <strong>down</strong> the rod with firm, long strokes — don't flick at it.</li><li>Catch the sparks on char cloth or dry pith.</li><li>Fold the tinder around the ember and blow gently, steadily, from below.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>If it goes out</summary><div class="body"><p>Feed small, then bigger. Smothering a young flame with big logs is the classic mistake. Give it air and patience, and don't rearrange everything at once.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Making a fire without matches</h1><p class="muted">Sparks need something to land on, <regex>user_name>. Build the tinder bundle first and the fire part is easy.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">The three-size rule</h4><ul class="list"><li><strong>Tinder</strong> — dry grass, birch bark, char cloth, cotton. Must catch from a spark.</li><li><strong>Kindling</strong> — pencil-thick dry twigs.</li><li><strong>Fuel</strong> — wrist-thick and up, dry deadwood.</li></ul><div class="callout warn"><p>Damp wood steals heat, no matter how good your spark. If it snaps easily, it's dry enough.</p></div></div><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Lay the tinder bundle</h4><svg class="chart" viewBox="0 0 260 110" role="img" aria-label="Layered fire lay"><path d="M60 80 L130 24 L200 80 Z" class="area" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.15"/><text x="130" y="54" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">tepee</text><line x1="60" y1="80" x2="200" y2="80" class="guide"/><rect x="60" y="86" width="140" height="14" rx="4" fill="var(--accent-soft)"/><text x="130" y="97" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">kindling bed</text></svg><p class="t-small muted">Tepee lay pulls air up through the middle. Best for a quick start.</p></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Using a ferro rod</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Anchor the rod close to the tinder.</li><li>Draw the striker <strong>down</strong> the rod with firm, long strokes — don't flick at it.</li><li>Catch the sparks on char cloth or dry pith.</li><li>Fold the tinder around the ember and blow gently, steadily, from below.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>If it goes out</summary><div class="body"><p>Feed small, then bigger. Smothering a young flame with big logs is the classic mistake. Give it air and patience, and don't rearrange everything at once.</p></div></details></div>	-
37.30.1	dashboard_split/Cutting onions without tears	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Cutting onions without tears</h1><p class="muted">It's the root, <regex>user_name>. Leave it attached and the fumes have less to work with.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">The physics of the sting</h4><p class="t-small">Sliced cells release a sulfur compound. It meets moisture in your eyes, turns into a mild acid, and your eyes flood to flush it out.</p><div class="callout info"><p>So: cut fewer cells at once, slow the reaction, or keep the gas out of your eyes.</p></div></div><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">What actually helps</h4><ul class="list"><li>Chill the onion 15 minutes first</li><li>Cut near a running tap or an open window</li><li>Keep the root end intact until the end</li><li>Breathe through your mouth, not your nose</li></ul><div class="callout bad"><p>Myth: rubbing your eyes after makes it better. It does not. Wash your hands first.</p></div></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>The dice in four moves</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Halve it from stem to root.</li><li>Peel, then make horizontal cuts towards the root.</li><li>Make vertical cuts parallel to the root.</li><li>Slice across and the dice fall out on their own.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>If you really hate it</summary><div class="body"><p>Buy frozen diced onion. Two minutes saved, zero tears. No shame, <regex>user_name>.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Cutting onions without tears</h1><p class="muted">It's the root, <regex>user_name>. Leave it attached and the fumes have less to work with.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">The physics of the sting</h4><p class="t-small">Sliced cells release a sulfur compound. It meets moisture in your eyes, turns into a mild acid, and your eyes flood to flush it out.</p><div class="callout info"><p>So: cut fewer cells at once, slow the reaction, or keep the gas out of your eyes.</p></div></div><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">What actually helps</h4><ul class="list"><li>Chill the onion 15 minutes first</li><li>Cut near a running tap or an open window</li><li>Keep the root end intact until the end</li><li>Breathe through your mouth, not your nose</li></ul><div class="callout bad"><p>Myth: rubbing your eyes after makes it better. It does not. Wash your hands first.</p></div></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>The dice in four moves</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Halve it from stem to root.</li><li>Peel, then make horizontal cuts towards the root.</li><li>Make vertical cuts parallel to the root.</li><li>Slice across and the dice fall out on their own.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>If you really hate it</summary><div class="body"><p>Buy frozen diced onion. Two minutes saved, zero tears. No shame, <regex>user_name>.</p></div></details></div>	-
37.31.1	dashboard_split/Changing a lightbulb safely	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Changing a lightbulb safely</h1><p class="muted">Boring job, real hazards, <regex>user_name>. Two minutes of care beats a burned hand or a broken fitting.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Before you touch it</h4><ul class="list"><li>Switch the light <strong>off</strong> at the wall, not just the pull cord.</li><li>Let a hot bulb cool for five minutes — halogen can burn you fast.</li><li>Use a ladder that's level, not a chair. Chairs wobble.</li></ul><div class="callout warn"><p>If the fitting is cracked or the bulb is stuck, stop. Forcing a stuck bayonet bulb can shatter glass in your hand.</p></div></div><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Two common bases</h4><svg class="chart" viewBox="0 0 280 100" role="img" aria-label="Bayonet and screw bases"><rect x="20" y="34" width="70" height="30" rx="6" fill="var(--accent-soft)"/><circle cx="30" cy="49" r="4" fill="currentColor"/><text x="55" y="54" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">bayonet</text><rect x="150" y="30" width="70" height="38" rx="6" fill="var(--accent-soft)"/><path d="M155 34 h60 M155 44 h60 M155 54 h60 M155 64 h60" class="guide"/><text x="185" y="54" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">screw</text></svg><p class="t-small muted">Push and twist for bayonet. Turn like a jar lid for screw.</p></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>What wattage to buy</summary><div class="body"><p>Never exceed the wattage printed inside the fitting. LEDs run cooler and use far less power, so a 9W LED can often replace a 60W old-style bulb and stay well inside the limit.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Bayonet still won't shift</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Press in firmly first, then push and twist.</li><li>Wear a glove or grip it with a dry cloth.</li><li>If it's really seized, cut the power and leave it for someone with a proper remover.</li></ol></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Changing a lightbulb safely</h1><p class="muted">Boring job, real hazards, <regex>user_name>. Two minutes of care beats a burned hand or a broken fitting.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Before you touch it</h4><ul class="list"><li>Switch the light <strong>off</strong> at the wall, not just the pull cord.</li><li>Let a hot bulb cool for five minutes — halogen can burn you fast.</li><li>Use a ladder that's level, not a chair. Chairs wobble.</li></ul><div class="callout warn"><p>If the fitting is cracked or the bulb is stuck, stop. Forcing a stuck bayonet bulb can shatter glass in your hand.</p></div></div><div class="card"><h4 class="card-title">Two common bases</h4><svg class="chart" viewBox="0 0 280 100" role="img" aria-label="Bayonet and screw bases"><rect x="20" y="34" width="70" height="30" rx="6" fill="var(--accent-soft)"/><circle cx="30" cy="49" r="4" fill="currentColor"/><text x="55" y="54" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">bayonet</text><rect x="150" y="30" width="70" height="38" rx="6" fill="var(--accent-soft)"/><path d="M155 34 h60 M155 44 h60 M155 54 h60 M155 64 h60" class="guide"/><text x="185" y="54" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">screw</text></svg><p class="t-small muted">Push and twist for bayonet. Turn like a jar lid for screw.</p></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>What wattage to buy</summary><div class="body"><p>Never exceed the wattage printed inside the fitting. LEDs run cooler and use far less power, so a 9W LED can often replace a 60W old-style bulb and stay well inside the limit.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Bayonet still won't shift</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Press in firmly first, then push and twist.</li><li>Wear a glove or grip it with a dry cloth.</li><li>If it's really seized, cut the power and leave it for someone with a proper remover.</li></ol></div></details></div>	-
37.32.1	dashboard_split/Cleaning a dishwasher	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Cleaning a dishwasher</h1>  <p class="muted">Because it's washing dishes, not itself. Five minutes a month keeps it sweet.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">The monthly reset</h4>      <ol class="list">        <li>Pull the bottom rack out.</li>        <li>Unscrew the filter — it's the cylindrical thing at the back. Rinse under hot water.</li>        <li>Scrub the door seal with a soapy sponge. Gunk loves that crevice.</li>        <li>Run a hot cycle with a cup of white vinegar in the top rack.</li>      </ol>      <div class="callout info">        <p>Skip the vinegar if your manual says so — some seals don't like acid. A dishwasher tablet works too.</p>      </div>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">Smell check</h4>      <p>If it still smells after a clean, run an empty cycle with a scoop of baking soda on the bottom. Then leave the door ajar between uses.</p>      <div class="stat">        <span class="label">Time</span>        <span class="value">5<span class="unit">min</span></span>      </div>      <div class="stat">        <span class="label">Frequency</span>        <span class="value">Monthly</span>      </div>    </div>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Cleaning a dishwasher</h1>  <p class="muted">Because it's washing dishes, not itself. Five minutes a month keeps it sweet.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">The monthly reset</h4>      <ol class="list">        <li>Pull the bottom rack out.</li>        <li>Unscrew the filter — it's the cylindrical thing at the back. Rinse under hot water.</li>        <li>Scrub the door seal with a soapy sponge. Gunk loves that crevice.</li>        <li>Run a hot cycle with a cup of white vinegar in the top rack.</li>      </ol>      <div class="callout info">        <p>Skip the vinegar if your manual says so — some seals don't like acid. A dishwasher tablet works too.</p>      </div>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">Smell check</h4>      <p>If it still smells after a clean, run an empty cycle with a scoop of baking soda on the bottom. Then leave the door ajar between uses.</p>      <div class="stat">        <span class="label">Time</span>        <span class="value">5<span class="unit">min</span></span>      </div>      <div class="stat">        <span class="label">Frequency</span>        <span class="value">Monthly</span>      </div>    </div>  </div></div>	-
37.33.1	dashboard_split/Getting rid of fruit flies	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Getting rid of fruit flies</h1>  <p class="muted">They're not evil, just enthusiastic. Break the cycle and they'll thin out in a few days.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">The trap</h4>      <ol class="list">        <li>Put a splash of apple cider vinegar in a small bowl.</li>        <li>Add a drop of dish soap — it breaks the surface tension.</li>        <li>Cover with cling film and poke a few holes.</li>        <li>Leave it near the fruit bowl. Empty daily.</li>      </ol>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">Prevention</h4>      <ul class="list">        <li>Rinse cans and bottles before recycling.</li>        <li>Keep fruit in the fridge if you can.</li>        <li>Take out the compost caddy every night.</li>        <li>Check the drain — they breed in gunky pipes too.</li>      </ul>      <div class="callout good">        <p>Most infestations clear in 3–5 days once the source is gone.</p>      </div>    </div>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Getting rid of fruit flies</h1>  <p class="muted">They're not evil, just enthusiastic. Break the cycle and they'll thin out in a few days.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">The trap</h4>      <ol class="list">        <li>Put a splash of apple cider vinegar in a small bowl.</li>        <li>Add a drop of dish soap — it breaks the surface tension.</li>        <li>Cover with cling film and poke a few holes.</li>        <li>Leave it near the fruit bowl. Empty daily.</li>      </ol>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">Prevention</h4>      <ul class="list">        <li>Rinse cans and bottles before recycling.</li>        <li>Keep fruit in the fridge if you can.</li>        <li>Take out the compost caddy every night.</li>        <li>Check the drain — they breed in gunky pipes too.</li>      </ul>      <div class="callout good">        <p>Most infestations clear in 3–5 days once the source is gone.</p>      </div>    </div>  </div></div>	-
37.34.1	dashboard_split/Ironing a shirt	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Ironing a shirt</h1>  <p class="muted">Low heat, steady strokes, and a little patience. Your collar will thank you.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">Order of operations</h4>      <ol class="list">        <li>Collar first — press from the points inward.</li>        <li>Cuffs, then sleeves. Iron towards the shoulder.</li>        <li>Body: start at the back, then front panels.</li>        <li>Hang immediately to let it cool flat.</li>      </ol>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">Heat cheat sheet</h4>      <ul class="list">        <li><strong>Cotton:</strong> high heat, steam on.</li>        <li><strong>Poly blend:</strong> medium, no steam.</li>        <li><strong>Linen:</strong> high, iron damp.</li>        <li><strong>Silk:</strong> low, use a cloth.</li>      </ul>      <div class="callout warn">        <p>Never iron over buttons or zips — they'll scratch the plate and crack.</p>      </div>    </div>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Ironing a shirt</h1>  <p class="muted">Low heat, steady strokes, and a little patience. Your collar will thank you.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">Order of operations</h4>      <ol class="list">        <li>Collar first — press from the points inward.</li>        <li>Cuffs, then sleeves. Iron towards the shoulder.</li>        <li>Body: start at the back, then front panels.</li>        <li>Hang immediately to let it cool flat.</li>      </ol>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">Heat cheat sheet</h4>      <ul class="list">        <li><strong>Cotton:</strong> high heat, steam on.</li>        <li><strong>Poly blend:</strong> medium, no steam.</li>        <li><strong>Linen:</strong> high, iron damp.</li>        <li><strong>Silk:</strong> low, use a cloth.</li>      </ul>      <div class="callout warn">        <p>Never iron over buttons or zips — they'll scratch the plate and crack.</p>      </div>    </div>  </div></div>	-
37.35.1	dashboard_split/Storing fresh herbs	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Storing fresh herbs</h1>  <p class="muted">Treat them like cut flowers or salad leaves — depends on the herb.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">Soft herbs (basil, cilantro, parsley)</h4>      <p>Trim the stems, stand them in a glass with an inch of water. Cover loosely with a plastic bag and leave on the counter. Basil hates the cold.</p>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">Hard herbs (rosemary, thyme, sage)</h4>      <p>Wrap in a slightly damp paper towel, then a bag, and refrigerate. They'll keep for a week or two.</p>      <div class="callout good">        <p>Freeze leftovers in olive oil in an ice cube tray. Instant flavour bombs.</p>      </div>    </div>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Storing fresh herbs</h1>  <p class="muted">Treat them like cut flowers or salad leaves — depends on the herb.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">Soft herbs (basil, cilantro, parsley)</h4>      <p>Trim the stems, stand them in a glass with an inch of water. Cover loosely with a plastic bag and leave on the counter. Basil hates the cold.</p>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">Hard herbs (rosemary, thyme, sage)</h4>      <p>Wrap in a slightly damp paper towel, then a bag, and refrigerate. They'll keep for a week or two.</p>      <div class="callout good">        <p>Freeze leftovers in olive oil in an ice cube tray. Instant flavour bombs.</p>      </div>    </div>  </div></div>	-
37.36.1	dashboard_split/Fixing a stuck zipper	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Fixing a stuck zipper</h1>  <p class="muted">Nine times out of ten it's a grain of grit or a bent tooth. You can usually save it.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">If it's stuck</h4>      <ol class="list">        <li>Don't yank. Hold the fabric flat.</li>        <li>Rub a graphite pencil along the teeth.</li>        <li>Or apply a little lip balm or soap.</li>        <li>Work the slider gently back and forth.</li>      </ol>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">If it splits open</h4>      <p>The slider is probably bent. Squeeze it gently with pliers — barely any pressure. If that fails, a new slider is a cheap fix at a tailor.</p>      <div class="callout info">        <p>For plastic zippers, a dab of silicone grease works wonders.</p>      </div>    </div>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Fixing a stuck zipper</h1>  <p class="muted">Nine times out of ten it's a grain of grit or a bent tooth. You can usually save it.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">If it's stuck</h4>      <ol class="list">        <li>Don't yank. Hold the fabric flat.</li>        <li>Rub a graphite pencil along the teeth.</li>        <li>Or apply a little lip balm or soap.</li>        <li>Work the slider gently back and forth.</li>      </ol>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">If it splits open</h4>      <p>The slider is probably bent. Squeeze it gently with pliers — barely any pressure. If that fails, a new slider is a cheap fix at a tailor.</p>      <div class="callout info">        <p>For plastic zippers, a dab of silicone grease works wonders.</p>      </div>    </div>  </div></div>	-
37.37.1	dashboard_split/Cleaning a coffee grinder	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Cleaning a coffee grinder</h1>  <p class="muted">Old oils go rancid and make every cup taste like yesterday. A quick clean fixes it.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">Quick clean (weekly)</h4>      <ol class="list">        <li>Unplug it.</li>        <li>Grind a handful of dry white rice until powdery.</li>        <li>Dump the rice and brush out the chamber.</li>        <li>Wipe the burrs with a dry cloth.</li>      </ol>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">Deep clean (monthly)</h4>      <p>Unscrew the top burr if your model allows. Brush with a stiff brush. Never rinse with water — moisture kills grinders.</p>      <div class="callout warn">        <p>Don't use rice if you have a high-end conical burr grinder; it can wear the burrs. Use grinder cleaning pellets instead.</p>      </div>    </div>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Cleaning a coffee grinder</h1>  <p class="muted">Old oils go rancid and make every cup taste like yesterday. A quick clean fixes it.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">Quick clean (weekly)</h4>      <ol class="list">        <li>Unplug it.</li>        <li>Grind a handful of dry white rice until powdery.</li>        <li>Dump the rice and brush out the chamber.</li>        <li>Wipe the burrs with a dry cloth.</li>      </ol>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h4 class="card-title">Deep clean (monthly)</h4>      <p>Unscrew the top burr if your model allows. Brush with a stiff brush. Never rinse with water — moisture kills grinders.</p>      <div class="callout warn">        <p>Don't use rice if you have a high-end conical burr grinder; it can wear the burrs. Use grinder cleaning pellets instead.</p>      </div>    </div>  </div></div>	-
37.38.1	dashboard_split/Smoke Detector Testing	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Smoke Detector Testing</h1><p class="muted">Quick check: press and hold the test button. If it doesn't chirp, it's not protecting you.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Test it</h4><ul class="list"><li>Press and hold the test button for a few seconds.</li><li>You should hear a loud, piercing alarm.</li><li>If it's weak or silent, replace the battery or the unit.</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Monthly routine</h4><ol class="list"><li>Test every month.</li><li>Vacuum the vents gently.</li><li>Replace batteries yearly (or when chirping).</li><li>Replace the whole unit every 10 years.</li></ol></div></div><div class="callout info"><p><strong>Nuisance alarms?</strong> Move it away from the kitchen or bathroom, or buy a photoelectric model.</p></div><p class="dim t-small">Not sure what kind you have? Look for the label on the back.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Smoke Detector Testing</h1><p class="muted">Quick check: press and hold the test button. If it doesn't chirp, it's not protecting you.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Test it</h4><ul class="list"><li>Press and hold the test button for a few seconds.</li><li>You should hear a loud, piercing alarm.</li><li>If it's weak or silent, replace the battery or the unit.</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Monthly routine</h4><ol class="list"><li>Test every month.</li><li>Vacuum the vents gently.</li><li>Replace batteries yearly (or when chirping).</li><li>Replace the whole unit every 10 years.</li></ol></div></div><div class="callout info"><p><strong>Nuisance alarms?</strong> Move it away from the kitchen or bathroom, or buy a photoelectric model.</p></div><p class="dim t-small">Not sure what kind you have? Look for the label on the back.</p></div>	-
37.39.1	dashboard_split/Cleaning a Washing Machine	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Cleaning a Washing Machine</h1><p class="muted">That musty smell? It's mildew. Here's how to evict it.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Run a hot cycle</h4><ol class="list"><li>Add 2 cups of white vinegar to the drum.</li><li>Run an empty hot water cycle.</li><li>Wipe the door seal and detergent drawer.</li></ol></div><div class="card"><h4>Monthly upkeep</h4><ul class="list"><li>Leave the door ajar between loads.</li><li>Use less detergent (excess feeds mildew).</li><li>Clean the filter if you have one.</li></ul></div></div><div class="callout warn"><p><strong>Don't mix vinegar and bleach.</strong> It creates toxic gas. Use one or the other.</p></div><p class="dim t-small">Front-loaders need this more often than top-loaders.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Cleaning a Washing Machine</h1><p class="muted">That musty smell? It's mildew. Here's how to evict it.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Run a hot cycle</h4><ol class="list"><li>Add 2 cups of white vinegar to the drum.</li><li>Run an empty hot water cycle.</li><li>Wipe the door seal and detergent drawer.</li></ol></div><div class="card"><h4>Monthly upkeep</h4><ul class="list"><li>Leave the door ajar between loads.</li><li>Use less detergent (excess feeds mildew).</li><li>Clean the filter if you have one.</li></ul></div></div><div class="callout warn"><p><strong>Don't mix vinegar and bleach.</strong> It creates toxic gas. Use one or the other.</p></div><p class="dim t-small">Front-loaders need this more often than top-loaders.</p></div>	-
37.40.1	dashboard_split/Tying a Tie	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Tying a Tie</h1><p class="muted">The four-in-hand is the easiest knot. Once you know it, you'll never forget.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Steps</h4><ol class="list"><li>Drape the tie around your neck, seam side down. Wide end on your right, about a foot lower.</li><li>Cross the wide end over the narrow end.</li><li>Bring the wide end up through the loop around your neck.</li><li>Wrap it across the front of the knot.</li><li>Pull it up through the loop again.</li><li>Slide the knot up and tighten.</li></ol></div><div class="card"><h4>Tips</h4><ul class="list"><li>The knot should sit at your collar, not below it.</li><li>The tie's tip should reach your belt buckle.</li><li>Dimple the knot with a pinch before tightening.</li></ul></div></div><div class="callout info"><p><strong>Windsor knot?</strong> Same idea, but wrap the wide end around twice before pulling through.</p></div><p class="dim t-small">Practice on your thigh first. Seriously.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Tying a Tie</h1><p class="muted">The four-in-hand is the easiest knot. Once you know it, you'll never forget.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Steps</h4><ol class="list"><li>Drape the tie around your neck, seam side down. Wide end on your right, about a foot lower.</li><li>Cross the wide end over the narrow end.</li><li>Bring the wide end up through the loop around your neck.</li><li>Wrap it across the front of the knot.</li><li>Pull it up through the loop again.</li><li>Slide the knot up and tighten.</li></ol></div><div class="card"><h4>Tips</h4><ul class="list"><li>The knot should sit at your collar, not below it.</li><li>The tie's tip should reach your belt buckle.</li><li>Dimple the knot with a pinch before tightening.</li></ul></div></div><div class="callout info"><p><strong>Windsor knot?</strong> Same idea, but wrap the wide end around twice before pulling through.</p></div><p class="dim t-small">Practice on your thigh first. Seriously.</p></div>	-
37.41.1	dashboard_split/Fixing a Squeaky Floor	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Fixing a Squeaky Floor</h1><p class="muted">Squeaks come from movement between wood and nails. Silence it from above or below.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>From above</h4><ul class="list"><li>Locate the squeak by walking slowly.</li><li>Drive a trim-head screw through the carpet or wood into the joist.</li><li>Countersink and fill the hole.</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>From below</h4><ul class="list"><li>If you have access, insert a shim coated in wood glue between the subfloor and joist.</li><li>Tap it in gently, then snap off the excess.</li></ul></div></div><div class="callout good"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> A little talcum powder in the gap can quiet minor squeaks temporarily.</p></div><p class="dim t-small">Find the exact spot by having someone walk while you listen below.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Fixing a Squeaky Floor</h1><p class="muted">Squeaks come from movement between wood and nails. Silence it from above or below.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>From above</h4><ul class="list"><li>Locate the squeak by walking slowly.</li><li>Drive a trim-head screw through the carpet or wood into the joist.</li><li>Countersink and fill the hole.</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>From below</h4><ul class="list"><li>If you have access, insert a shim coated in wood glue between the subfloor and joist.</li><li>Tap it in gently, then snap off the excess.</li></ul></div></div><div class="callout good"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> A little talcum powder in the gap can quiet minor squeaks temporarily.</p></div><p class="dim t-small">Find the exact spot by having someone walk while you listen below.</p></div>	-
37.42.1	dashboard_split/Folding a fitted sheet	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Folding a fitted sheet</h1><p class="muted">You don't need the corners to meet. You need the elastic to hide.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Four moves, no tears</h4><ol class="list"><li>Hold it inside-out, hands in two corners.</li><li>Fold corner over corner, tucking one into the other.</li><li>Lay flat, elastic bunched to one end.</li><li>Fold in thirds, then crosswise.</li></ol><p class="t-small dim">Shelf life: about a week before the pile collapses anyway.</p></div><div class="card"><h4>Where the pain lives</h4><svg class="chart" viewBox="0 0 200 90"><rect class="bar" x="10" y="30" width="26" height="50"></rect><rect class="bar alt" x="60" y="10" width="26" height="70"></rect><rect class="bar" x="110" y="55" width="26" height="25"></rect><rect class="bar alt" x="160" y="66" width="26" height="14"></rect><text class="lbl" x="23" y="88">corners</text><text class="lbl" x="73" y="88">elastic</text><text class="lbl" x="123" y="88">bulk</text><text class="lbl" x="173" y="88">fit</text></svg><p class="t-small muted">Corners and elastic cause most of the swearing, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>The absurdly fast version</summary><div class="body"><p>Fold the whole thing in half twice, then stuff it in the pillowcase with its sheet-mate. Nobody sees the cupboard.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Folding a fitted sheet</h1><p class="muted">You don't need the corners to meet. You need the elastic to hide.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Four moves, no tears</h4><ol class="list"><li>Hold it inside-out, hands in two corners.</li><li>Fold corner over corner, tucking one into the other.</li><li>Lay flat, elastic bunched to one end.</li><li>Fold in thirds, then crosswise.</li></ol><p class="t-small dim">Shelf life: about a week before the pile collapses anyway.</p></div><div class="card"><h4>Where the pain lives</h4><svg class="chart" viewBox="0 0 200 90"><rect class="bar" x="10" y="30" width="26" height="50"></rect><rect class="bar alt" x="60" y="10" width="26" height="70"></rect><rect class="bar" x="110" y="55" width="26" height="25"></rect><rect class="bar alt" x="160" y="66" width="26" height="14"></rect><text class="lbl" x="23" y="88">corners</text><text class="lbl" x="73" y="88">elastic</text><text class="lbl" x="123" y="88">bulk</text><text class="lbl" x="173" y="88">fit</text></svg><p class="t-small muted">Corners and elastic cause most of the swearing, .</p></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>The absurdly fast version</summary><div class="body"><p>Fold the whole thing in half twice, then stuff it in the pillowcase with its sheet-mate. Nobody sees the cupboard.</p></div></details></div>	-
37.43.1	dashboard_split/Reading a measuring tape	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Reading a measuring tape</h1><p class="muted">Tiny ticks, loud opinions. Here's the shorthand.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>The tick ladder</h4><ul class="list"><li><strong>Longest</strong> line = whole inch.</li><li>Half, then quarter, then eighth.</li><li><strong>Sixteenth</strong> = shortest marks.</li><li>Boxed numbers every 12 in = feet.</li><li>Red dot every 16 in = stud spacing.</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Half-inch feel</h4><div class="meter"><span style="width:50%"></span></div><p class="t-small dim">Halfway between two long marks. That's it. That's the secret.</p><div class="callout info"><p>Hook the metal tab over the edge and pull. It slides a little on purpose.</p></div></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>What's the slot in the end for?</summary><div class="body"><p>It hooks onto a nail head so you can measure solo. Also lets the hook shift for inside vs outside measuring, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Reading a measuring tape</h1><p class="muted">Tiny ticks, loud opinions. Here's the shorthand.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>The tick ladder</h4><ul class="list"><li><strong>Longest</strong> line = whole inch.</li><li>Half, then quarter, then eighth.</li><li><strong>Sixteenth</strong> = shortest marks.</li><li>Boxed numbers every 12 in = feet.</li><li>Red dot every 16 in = stud spacing.</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Half-inch feel</h4><div class="meter"><span style="width:50%"></span></div><p class="t-small dim">Halfway between two long marks. That's it. That's the secret.</p><div class="callout info"><p>Hook the metal tab over the edge and pull. It slides a little on purpose.</p></div></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>What's the slot in the end for?</summary><div class="body"><p>It hooks onto a nail head so you can measure solo. Also lets the hook shift for inside vs outside measuring, .</p></div></details></div>	-
37.44.1	dashboard_split/Brewing loose-leaf tea	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Brewing loose-leaf tea</h1><p class="muted">One teaspoon, one pot, and a timer you'll ignore once.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>By leaf type</h4><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Leaf</th><th class="num">Temp</th><th class="num">Time</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Green</td><td class="num">80&deg;C</td><td class="num">2 min</td></tr><tr><td>Black</td><td class="num">95&deg;C</td><td class="num">3 min</td></tr><tr><td>Oolong</td><td class="num">90&deg;C</td><td class="num">3 min</td></tr><tr><td>Herbal</td><td class="num">100&deg;C</td><td class="num">5 min</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><div class="card"><h4>Bitterness risk</h4><div class="meter"><span style="width:62%"></span></div><p class="t-small muted">Mostly time, not temperature. Pull the leaves early and breathe.</p><div class="callout warn"><p>Green tea scalded with boiling water tastes like lawn clippings.</p></div></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>No thermometer?</summary><div class="body"><p>Boil the kettle, then pour it into a cold mug and wait a minute for green tea. Close enough, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Brewing loose-leaf tea</h1><p class="muted">One teaspoon, one pot, and a timer you'll ignore once.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>By leaf type</h4><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Leaf</th><th class="num">Temp</th><th class="num">Time</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Green</td><td class="num">80&deg;C</td><td class="num">2 min</td></tr><tr><td>Black</td><td class="num">95&deg;C</td><td class="num">3 min</td></tr><tr><td>Oolong</td><td class="num">90&deg;C</td><td class="num">3 min</td></tr><tr><td>Herbal</td><td class="num">100&deg;C</td><td class="num">5 min</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><div class="card"><h4>Bitterness risk</h4><div class="meter"><span style="width:62%"></span></div><p class="t-small muted">Mostly time, not temperature. Pull the leaves early and breathe.</p><div class="callout warn"><p>Green tea scalded with boiling water tastes like lawn clippings.</p></div></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>No thermometer?</summary><div class="body"><p>Boil the kettle, then pour it into a cold mug and wait a minute for green tea. Close enough, .</p></div></details></div>	-
37.45.1	dashboard_split/Building a campfire in the rain	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Building a campfire in the rain</h1><p class="muted">Dry wood is the whole trick. Find it, hide it, then burn it.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Find the dry</h4><ul class="list"><li>Dead branches still on the tree.</li><li>Under dense evergreens.</li><li>Split a log: the middle stayed dry.</li><li>Pine resin, birch bark, dead grass.</li><li>Your own pocket lint. Genuinely.</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Wet-to-lit odds</h4><svg class="chart" viewBox="0 0 200 80"><line class="guide" x1="10" y1="60" x2="190" y2="60"></line><rect class="bar" x="20" y="46" width="22" height="14"></rect><rect class="bar alt" x="70" y="22" width="22" height="38"></rect><rect class="bar" x="120" y="34" width="22" height="26"></rect><rect class="bar alt" x="170" y="10" width="22" height="50"></rect><text class="lbl" x="31" y="76">raw</text><text class="lbl" x="81" y="76">tinder</text><text class="lbl" x="131" y="76">split</text><text class="lbl in" x="181" y="76">dry</text></svg><p class="t-small dim">Get the tinder lit and the rest forgives you.</p></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Keep it alive</summary><div class="body"><p>Shield with your body, stack small fuel over big, and don't smother it. Rain on a hot bed of coals is fine, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Building a campfire in the rain</h1><p class="muted">Dry wood is the whole trick. Find it, hide it, then burn it.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Find the dry</h4><ul class="list"><li>Dead branches still on the tree.</li><li>Under dense evergreens.</li><li>Split a log: the middle stayed dry.</li><li>Pine resin, birch bark, dead grass.</li><li>Your own pocket lint. Genuinely.</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Wet-to-lit odds</h4><svg class="chart" viewBox="0 0 200 80"><line class="guide" x1="10" y1="60" x2="190" y2="60"></line><rect class="bar" x="20" y="46" width="22" height="14"></rect><rect class="bar alt" x="70" y="22" width="22" height="38"></rect><rect class="bar" x="120" y="34" width="22" height="26"></rect><rect class="bar alt" x="170" y="10" width="22" height="50"></rect><text class="lbl" x="31" y="76">raw</text><text class="lbl" x="81" y="76">tinder</text><text class="lbl" x="131" y="76">split</text><text class="lbl in" x="181" y="76">dry</text></svg><p class="t-small dim">Get the tinder lit and the rest forgives you.</p></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Keep it alive</summary><div class="body"><p>Shield with your body, stack small fuel over big, and don't smother it. Rain on a hot bed of coals is fine, .</p></div></details></div>	-
37.46.1	dashboard_split/Sharpening a kitchen knife	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Sharpening a kitchen knife</h1><p class="muted">Ten minutes on a stone beats a year of wishful honing.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Two jobs, don't mix them</h4><ul class="list"><li><strong>Hone</strong> weekly: realigns the edge.</li><li><strong>Sharpen</strong> rarely: removes steel.</li><li>Angle: about 15&ndash;20&deg; per side.</li><li>Count strokes, keep them even.</li><li>Finish with light passes on each side.</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Edge after each step</h4><div class="meter"><span style="width:25%"></span></div><p class="t-small">Honing steel</p><div class="meter"><span style="width:88%"></span></div><p class="t-small">Whetstone</p><div class="callout good"><p>The paper test: a sharp knife glides through printer paper without tearing.</p></div></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>No stone at home?</summary><div class="body"><p>A coffee mug's unglazed base works in a pinch. Twenty light strokes per side, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Sharpening a kitchen knife</h1><p class="muted">Ten minutes on a stone beats a year of wishful honing.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Two jobs, don't mix them</h4><ul class="list"><li><strong>Hone</strong> weekly: realigns the edge.</li><li><strong>Sharpen</strong> rarely: removes steel.</li><li>Angle: about 15&ndash;20&deg; per side.</li><li>Count strokes, keep them even.</li><li>Finish with light passes on each side.</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h4>Edge after each step</h4><div class="meter"><span style="width:25%"></span></div><p class="t-small">Honing steel</p><div class="meter"><span style="width:88%"></span></div><p class="t-small">Whetstone</p><div class="callout good"><p>The paper test: a sharp knife glides through printer paper without tearing.</p></div></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>No stone at home?</summary><div class="body"><p>A coffee mug's unglazed base works in a pinch. Twenty light strokes per side, .</p></div></details></div>	-
37.47.1	dashboard_split/Making cheap coffee taste better	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Making cheap coffee taste better</h1><p class="muted">Cheap beans aren't the problem. Grind, ratio and water usually are.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Cheap fixes, ranked</h4><ol class="list"><li>Grind fresh, just before brewing.</li><li>Weigh it: about 1:16 coffee to water.</li><li>Use filtered or bottled water.</li><li>Brew hot but not boiling.</li><li>Clean the machine. Old oils ruin everything.</li></ol></div><div class="card"><h4>What each fix buys you</h4><svg class="chart" viewBox="0 0 200 90"><rect class="bar alt" x="14" y="24" width="24" height="56"></rect><rect class="bar" x="64" y="34" width="24" height="46"></rect><rect class="bar alt" x="114" y="44" width="24" height="36"></rect><rect class="bar" x="164" y="56" width="24" height="24"></rect><text class="lbl" x="26" y="88">fresh</text><text class="lbl" x="76" y="88">ratio</text><text class="lbl" x="126" y="88">water</text><text class="lbl" x="176" y="88">clean</text></svg><p class="t-small dim">Freshly ground does the heavy lifting, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Bean storage</summary><div class="body"><p>Airtight, cool, dark, and away from the kettle steam. Freezer's fine if sealed properly; fridge is not.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Making cheap coffee taste better</h1><p class="muted">Cheap beans aren't the problem. Grind, ratio and water usually are.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h4>Cheap fixes, ranked</h4><ol class="list"><li>Grind fresh, just before brewing.</li><li>Weigh it: about 1:16 coffee to water.</li><li>Use filtered or bottled water.</li><li>Brew hot but not boiling.</li><li>Clean the machine. Old oils ruin everything.</li></ol></div><div class="card"><h4>What each fix buys you</h4><svg class="chart" viewBox="0 0 200 90"><rect class="bar alt" x="14" y="24" width="24" height="56"></rect><rect class="bar" x="64" y="34" width="24" height="46"></rect><rect class="bar alt" x="114" y="44" width="24" height="36"></rect><rect class="bar" x="164" y="56" width="24" height="24"></rect><text class="lbl" x="26" y="88">fresh</text><text class="lbl" x="76" y="88">ratio</text><text class="lbl" x="126" y="88">water</text><text class="lbl" x="176" y="88">clean</text></svg><p class="t-small dim">Freshly ground does the heavy lifting, .</p></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Bean storage</summary><div class="body"><p>Airtight, cool, dark, and away from the kettle steam. Freezer's fine if sealed properly; fridge is not.</p></div></details></div>	-
38.0.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Morning Coffee Ritual	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Your Morning Coffee, Step by Step</h1>  <p class="muted">A pour-over that tastes like you meant it.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Boil</strong> water. Off the boil, wait 30 seconds. (205°F / 96°C is the sweet spot.)</li>    <li><strong>Rinse</strong> the paper filter with hot water, then dump that water out. Warms the vessel, kills the papery taste.</li>    <li><strong>Weigh</strong> 22 g beans, grind medium. Add to filter, shake flat.</li>    <li><strong>Bloom</strong> with 60 g water. Wait 45 seconds. You'll see happy little bubbles.</li>    <li><strong>Pour</strong> slowly in circles, up to 360 g total. Keep the water level steady.</li>    <li><strong>Draw down</strong> should finish around 3:30. If it's faster, grind finer. Slower? Coarser.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout info">    <p><strong>Taste check:</strong> Sour means under-extracted — grind finer. Bitter means too long — grind coarser. Small nudges.</p>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>No scale? Rough it.</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Two heaping tablespoons of beans ≈ 22 g. Fill your mug with water, pour most of it. You'll get close.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Your Morning Coffee, Step by Step</h1>  <p class="muted">A pour-over that tastes like you meant it.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Boil</strong> water. Off the boil, wait 30 seconds. (205°F / 96°C is the sweet spot.)</li>    <li><strong>Rinse</strong> the paper filter with hot water, then dump that water out. Warms the vessel, kills the papery taste.</li>    <li><strong>Weigh</strong> 22 g beans, grind medium. Add to filter, shake flat.</li>    <li><strong>Bloom</strong> with 60 g water. Wait 45 seconds. You'll see happy little bubbles.</li>    <li><strong>Pour</strong> slowly in circles, up to 360 g total. Keep the water level steady.</li>    <li><strong>Draw down</strong> should finish around 3:30. If it's faster, grind finer. Slower? Coarser.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout info">    <p><strong>Taste check:</strong> Sour means under-extracted — grind finer. Bitter means too long — grind coarser. Small nudges.</p>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>No scale? Rough it.</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Two heaping tablespoons of beans ≈ 22 g. Fill your mug with water, pour most of it. You'll get close.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
38.1.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Fix a Slow Laptop	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Tune Up a Sluggish Laptop</h1>  <p class="muted">Most slowness is just clutter wearing a trench coat.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Check storage.</strong> Open Settings → System → Storage. If you're above 90% full, that alone can cause lag. Clear Downloads, old installers, and that folder of memes from 2019.</li>    <li><strong>Trim startup.</strong> Task Manager → Startup tab. Disable anything you don't need launching at boot. (Spotify can wait.)</li>    <li><strong>Update everything.</strong> OS updates, graphics drivers, browser. Old drivers love to misbehave.</li>    <li><strong>Scan for junk.</strong> One quick malware scan with whatever came with your OS. Skip the sketchy "optimizer" apps.</li>    <li><strong>Uninstall unused programs.</strong> If you haven't opened it in six months, it's a squatter.</li>    <li><strong>Restart</strong> — not sleep, restart. Sleep keeps everything festering.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout warn">    <p>If it's still slow after all that, the bottleneck is probably the drive. A cheap SSD upgrade is the single biggest speed boost you can buy.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Tune Up a Sluggish Laptop</h1>  <p class="muted">Most slowness is just clutter wearing a trench coat.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Check storage.</strong> Open Settings → System → Storage. If you're above 90% full, that alone can cause lag. Clear Downloads, old installers, and that folder of memes from 2019.</li>    <li><strong>Trim startup.</strong> Task Manager → Startup tab. Disable anything you don't need launching at boot. (Spotify can wait.)</li>    <li><strong>Update everything.</strong> OS updates, graphics drivers, browser. Old drivers love to misbehave.</li>    <li><strong>Scan for junk.</strong> One quick malware scan with whatever came with your OS. Skip the sketchy "optimizer" apps.</li>    <li><strong>Uninstall unused programs.</strong> If you haven't opened it in six months, it's a squatter.</li>    <li><strong>Restart</strong> — not sleep, restart. Sleep keeps everything festering.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout warn">    <p>If it's still slow after all that, the bottleneck is probably the drive. A cheap SSD upgrade is the single biggest speed boost you can buy.</p>  </div></div>	-
38.2.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Plant a Tomato	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>From Seed to Sandwich: Growing Tomatoes</h1>  <p class="muted">Tomatoes are dramatic, thirsty, and worth it.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Start indoors</strong> 6–8 weeks before your last frost. Plant seeds ¼ inch deep in seed-starting mix. Keep soil warm (75°F / 24°C).</li>    <li><strong>Give light.</strong> Once sprouts appear, they need 14–16 hours of bright light daily. A south window plus a cheap shop light works.</li>    <li><strong>Harden off</strong> over a week: set them outside for an hour, then two, then more. Skipping this shocks them.</li>    <li><strong>Plant deep.</strong> Bury the stem up to the top few leaves — tomatoes root along the stem. In-ground or a 5-gallon pot.</li>    <li><strong>Water</strong> deeply once or twice a week. Not daily sips. Consistency prevents cracked fruit.</li>    <li><strong>Stake or cage</strong> early, before the plant flops over and sulks.</li>    <li><strong>Feed</strong> every two weeks once flowers show. Too much nitrogen = all leaves, no fruit.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout good">    <p>Pinch off the first yellow flowers if you want a bigger plant before the harvest rush. Feels wrong. Works.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>From Seed to Sandwich: Growing Tomatoes</h1>  <p class="muted">Tomatoes are dramatic, thirsty, and worth it.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Start indoors</strong> 6–8 weeks before your last frost. Plant seeds ¼ inch deep in seed-starting mix. Keep soil warm (75°F / 24°C).</li>    <li><strong>Give light.</strong> Once sprouts appear, they need 14–16 hours of bright light daily. A south window plus a cheap shop light works.</li>    <li><strong>Harden off</strong> over a week: set them outside for an hour, then two, then more. Skipping this shocks them.</li>    <li><strong>Plant deep.</strong> Bury the stem up to the top few leaves — tomatoes root along the stem. In-ground or a 5-gallon pot.</li>    <li><strong>Water</strong> deeply once or twice a week. Not daily sips. Consistency prevents cracked fruit.</li>    <li><strong>Stake or cage</strong> early, before the plant flops over and sulks.</li>    <li><strong>Feed</strong> every two weeks once flowers show. Too much nitrogen = all leaves, no fruit.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout good">    <p>Pinch off the first yellow flowers if you want a bigger plant before the harvest rush. Feels wrong. Works.</p>  </div></div>	-
38.3.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Wash a Car Properly	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Wash a Car Without Swirl Marks</h1>  <p class="muted">Two buckets. One secret. Zero regrets.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Park in shade.</strong> Hot paint bakes water spots into the clear coat.</li>    <li><strong>Rinse</strong> the whole car top-down to loosen grit.</li>    <li><strong>Two buckets:</strong> one with soapy water, one with clean rinse water. Dip mitt in soap, scrub a panel, rinse mitt in the clean bucket before reloading.</li>    <li><strong>Wash top-down</strong> in straight lines, not circles. Roof, windows, hood, sides, then the grimy rocker panels and wheels last.</li>    <li><strong>Rinse</strong> fully. Don't let soap dry on paint.</li>    <li><strong>Dry</strong> with a microfiber towel or leaf blower. Air-drying invites water spots.</li>    <li><strong>Optional wax</strong> every few months. Ten minutes, big payoff.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout warn">    <p>Never wash in direct sun, and never use dish soap. It strips wax faster than you can say "why is my paint dull."</p>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Wash a Car Without Swirl Marks</h1>  <p class="muted">Two buckets. One secret. Zero regrets.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Park in shade.</strong> Hot paint bakes water spots into the clear coat.</li>    <li><strong>Rinse</strong> the whole car top-down to loosen grit.</li>    <li><strong>Two buckets:</strong> one with soapy water, one with clean rinse water. Dip mitt in soap, scrub a panel, rinse mitt in the clean bucket before reloading.</li>    <li><strong>Wash top-down</strong> in straight lines, not circles. Roof, windows, hood, sides, then the grimy rocker panels and wheels last.</li>    <li><strong>Rinse</strong> fully. Don't let soap dry on paint.</li>    <li><strong>Dry</strong> with a microfiber towel or leaf blower. Air-drying invites water spots.</li>    <li><strong>Optional wax</strong> every few months. Ten minutes, big payoff.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout warn">    <p>Never wash in direct sun, and never use dish soap. It strips wax faster than you can say "why is my paint dull."</p>  </div></div>	-
38.4.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Learn a Song on Guitar	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Learn Any Song on Guitar (The Slow Way That Works)</h1>  <p class="muted">Fastest path is boring. Sorry. But it's short.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Pick a simple song.</strong> Four chords or fewer. "Horse With No Name," "Knockin' on Heaven's Door," anything by default.</li>    <li><strong>Learn the chords cold.</strong> One at a time. Fret each chord, strum once, lift your hand, re-form it. Ten reps per chord.</li>    <li><strong>Loop the transitions</strong> — the hard part isn't the chord, it's the getting there. Practice the change, not the shape.</li>    <li><strong>Slow way down.</strong> Metronome at half speed. If you can't play it slow, you can't play it fast.</li>    <li><strong>Add the strumming pattern.</strong> Down, down-up, up-down-up is the workhorse. Count out loud: 1, 2-and, 3-and, 4.</li>    <li><strong>Play along with the recording</strong> at full speed. You'll fumble. That's the point.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout info">    <p>Twenty focused minutes beats two distracted hours. Set a timer. Your fingers will thank you. Eventually.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Learn Any Song on Guitar (The Slow Way That Works)</h1>  <p class="muted">Fastest path is boring. Sorry. But it's short.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Pick a simple song.</strong> Four chords or fewer. "Horse With No Name," "Knockin' on Heaven's Door," anything by default.</li>    <li><strong>Learn the chords cold.</strong> One at a time. Fret each chord, strum once, lift your hand, re-form it. Ten reps per chord.</li>    <li><strong>Loop the transitions</strong> — the hard part isn't the chord, it's the getting there. Practice the change, not the shape.</li>    <li><strong>Slow way down.</strong> Metronome at half speed. If you can't play it slow, you can't play it fast.</li>    <li><strong>Add the strumming pattern.</strong> Down, down-up, up-down-up is the workhorse. Count out loud: 1, 2-and, 3-and, 4.</li>    <li><strong>Play along with the recording</strong> at full speed. You'll fumble. That's the point.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout info">    <p>Twenty focused minutes beats two distracted hours. Set a timer. Your fingers will thank you. Eventually.</p>  </div></div>	-
38.5.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: File Your Taxes Without Panic	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>File Your Taxes Without Spiraling</h1>  <p class="muted">It's paperwork, not a personality test.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Gather your documents.</strong> W-2s from every job, 1099s for freelance or gig work, student loan interest statements, mortgage interest, charitable donation receipts.</li>    <li><strong>Choose your method.</strong> Free file if your income is under the threshold; tax software if it's simple; a pro if you own a business, sold a house, or had a weird year.</li>    <li><strong>Enter everything.</strong> Slow is smooth. Miss a form, get a letter. Nothing scary, just annoying.</li>    <li><strong>Take the standard deduction</strong> unless your itemized list clearly beats it. For most people it does not.</li>    <li><strong>Double-check</strong> your name, Social Security number, and bank routing before submitting. Typos are the number one delay.</li>    <li><strong>Submit and save a copy.</strong> PDF or paper, keep it three years. Future you will be grateful.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout warn">    <p>If you owe money, you can set up a payment plan. The IRS is a bureaucracy, not a monster. Don't ignore the letter.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>File Your Taxes Without Spiraling</h1>  <p class="muted">It's paperwork, not a personality test.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Gather your documents.</strong> W-2s from every job, 1099s for freelance or gig work, student loan interest statements, mortgage interest, charitable donation receipts.</li>    <li><strong>Choose your method.</strong> Free file if your income is under the threshold; tax software if it's simple; a pro if you own a business, sold a house, or had a weird year.</li>    <li><strong>Enter everything.</strong> Slow is smooth. Miss a form, get a letter. Nothing scary, just annoying.</li>    <li><strong>Take the standard deduction</strong> unless your itemized list clearly beats it. For most people it does not.</li>    <li><strong>Double-check</strong> your name, Social Security number, and bank routing before submitting. Typos are the number one delay.</li>    <li><strong>Submit and save a copy.</strong> PDF or paper, keep it three years. Future you will be grateful.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout warn">    <p>If you owe money, you can set up a payment plan. The IRS is a bureaucracy, not a monster. Don't ignore the letter.</p>  </div></div>	-
38.6.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Change a Bike Tube	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Change a Bike Tube</h1><p class="muted">Flat tires happen. Here's the fix, step by step.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Gather tools</strong> — tire levers, new tube, pump, wrench (if needed).</li><li><strong>Remove wheel</strong> — flip bike, open brake, unscrew axle nuts or quick-release.</li><li><strong>Deflate &amp; pry</strong> — let out air, insert lever under bead, hook to spoke.</li><li><strong>Extract tube</strong> — pull old tube out, keep track of orientation.</li><li><strong>Check tire</strong> — run fingers inside for glass, thorns, etc. Remove any debris.</li><li><strong>Install new tube</strong> — inflate slightly, tuck into tire, seat bead by hand.</li><li><strong>Reinstall wheel</strong> — reverse removal, tighten axle, close brake.</li><li><strong>Inflate &amp; test</strong> — pump to recommended PSI, spin wheel, check for wobbles.</li></ol><p class="dim">That's it. You've got this, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Change a Bike Tube</h1><p class="muted">Flat tires happen. Here's the fix, step by step.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Gather tools</strong> — tire levers, new tube, pump, wrench (if needed).</li><li><strong>Remove wheel</strong> — flip bike, open brake, unscrew axle nuts or quick-release.</li><li><strong>Deflate &amp; pry</strong> — let out air, insert lever under bead, hook to spoke.</li><li><strong>Extract tube</strong> — pull old tube out, keep track of orientation.</li><li><strong>Check tire</strong> — run fingers inside for glass, thorns, etc. Remove any debris.</li><li><strong>Install new tube</strong> — inflate slightly, tuck into tire, seat bead by hand.</li><li><strong>Reinstall wheel</strong> — reverse removal, tighten axle, close brake.</li><li><strong>Inflate &amp; test</strong> — pump to recommended PSI, spin wheel, check for wobbles.</li></ol><p class="dim">That's it. You've got this, .</p></div>	-
38.7.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Bake a Loaf of Bread	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Bake a Loaf of Bread</h1><p class="muted">Flour, water, salt, yeast. Patience optional but recommended.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Mix dough</strong> — combine 3 cups flour, 1 tsp salt, 1 tsp yeast, 1.5 cups warm water.</li><li><strong>Knead</strong> — 8-10 minutes until smooth and elastic.</li><li><strong>First rise</strong> — cover, let sit 1-2 hours until doubled.</li><li><strong>Punch down</strong> — deflate gently, shape into loaf.</li><li><strong>Second rise</strong> — 30-45 minutes in greased pan.</li><li><strong>Bake</strong> — 375°F (190°C) for 30-35 minutes until golden and hollow-sounding.</li><li><strong>Cool</strong> — wait at least 15 minutes before slicing. I know it's hard.</li></ol><p class="dim">Fresh bread is worth it, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Bake a Loaf of Bread</h1><p class="muted">Flour, water, salt, yeast. Patience optional but recommended.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Mix dough</strong> — combine 3 cups flour, 1 tsp salt, 1 tsp yeast, 1.5 cups warm water.</li><li><strong>Knead</strong> — 8-10 minutes until smooth and elastic.</li><li><strong>First rise</strong> — cover, let sit 1-2 hours until doubled.</li><li><strong>Punch down</strong> — deflate gently, shape into loaf.</li><li><strong>Second rise</strong> — 30-45 minutes in greased pan.</li><li><strong>Bake</strong> — 375°F (190°C) for 30-35 minutes until golden and hollow-sounding.</li><li><strong>Cool</strong> — wait at least 15 minutes before slicing. I know it's hard.</li></ol><p class="dim">Fresh bread is worth it, .</p></div>	-
38.8.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Do Laundry	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Do Laundry</h1><p class="muted">Sort, wash, dry, fold. Repeat forever.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Sort</strong> — darks, lights, delicates, towels. Check pockets.</li><li><strong>Pre-treat</strong> — stains get a dab of detergent before washing.</li><li><strong>Load washer</strong> — don't overstuff; leave room to tumble.</li><li><strong>Add detergent</strong> — follow bottle, usually 1-2 tablespoons.</li><li><strong>Select cycle</strong> — cold for darks, warm for lights, gentle for delicates.</li><li><strong>Transfer to dryer</strong> — or hang dry. Clean lint filter first.</li><li><strong>Fold &amp; put away</strong> — the step everyone skips. Don't be everyone.</li></ol><p class="dim">You're on your way, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Do Laundry</h1><p class="muted">Sort, wash, dry, fold. Repeat forever.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Sort</strong> — darks, lights, delicates, towels. Check pockets.</li><li><strong>Pre-treat</strong> — stains get a dab of detergent before washing.</li><li><strong>Load washer</strong> — don't overstuff; leave room to tumble.</li><li><strong>Add detergent</strong> — follow bottle, usually 1-2 tablespoons.</li><li><strong>Select cycle</strong> — cold for darks, warm for lights, gentle for delicates.</li><li><strong>Transfer to dryer</strong> — or hang dry. Clean lint filter first.</li><li><strong>Fold &amp; put away</strong> — the step everyone skips. Don't be everyone.</li></ol><p class="dim">You're on your way, .</p></div>	-
38.9.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Set Up a New Phone	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Set Up a New Phone</h1><p class="muted">From box to fully loaded, in a few steps.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Charge it</strong> — plug in and let it get some juice.</li><li><strong>Power on</strong> — hold the button until you see the logo.</li><li><strong>Select language &amp; region</strong> — pick what you speak and where you are.</li><li><strong>Connect to Wi-Fi</strong> — choose your network, enter password.</li><li><strong>Sign in</strong> — use your Google or Apple account to restore data.</li><li><strong>Transfer data</strong> — from old phone or cloud backup.</li><li><strong>Set up security</strong> — fingerprint, face, or PIN. Don't skip this.</li><li><strong>Update apps</strong> — let the store do its thing, then explore.</li></ol><p class="dim">Enjoy the new toy, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Set Up a New Phone</h1><p class="muted">From box to fully loaded, in a few steps.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Charge it</strong> — plug in and let it get some juice.</li><li><strong>Power on</strong> — hold the button until you see the logo.</li><li><strong>Select language &amp; region</strong> — pick what you speak and where you are.</li><li><strong>Connect to Wi-Fi</strong> — choose your network, enter password.</li><li><strong>Sign in</strong> — use your Google or Apple account to restore data.</li><li><strong>Transfer data</strong> — from old phone or cloud backup.</li><li><strong>Set up security</strong> — fingerprint, face, or PIN. Don't skip this.</li><li><strong>Update apps</strong> — let the store do its thing, then explore.</li></ol><p class="dim">Enjoy the new toy, .</p></div>	-
38.10.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Paint a Room	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Paint a Room</h1><p class="muted">A fresh coat changes everything. Here's how.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Prep</strong> — clear furniture, cover floors, tape trim and outlets.</li><li><strong>Clean walls</strong> — wipe dust and grease so paint sticks.</li><li><strong>Patch holes</strong> — fill, sand, and prime any dings.</li><li><strong>Prime</strong> — especially if going light over dark.</li><li><strong>Cut in</strong> — brush edges and corners first.</li><li><strong>Roll</strong> — work in W patterns, maintain a wet edge.</li><li><strong>Second coat</strong> — wait for dry time, then repeat.</li><li><strong>Clean up</strong> — remove tape while paint is still slightly tacky.</li></ol><p class="dim">Your room will thank you, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Paint a Room</h1><p class="muted">A fresh coat changes everything. Here's how.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Prep</strong> — clear furniture, cover floors, tape trim and outlets.</li><li><strong>Clean walls</strong> — wipe dust and grease so paint sticks.</li><li><strong>Patch holes</strong> — fill, sand, and prime any dings.</li><li><strong>Prime</strong> — especially if going light over dark.</li><li><strong>Cut in</strong> — brush edges and corners first.</li><li><strong>Roll</strong> — work in W patterns, maintain a wet edge.</li><li><strong>Second coat</strong> — wait for dry time, then repeat.</li><li><strong>Clean up</strong> — remove tape while paint is still slightly tacky.</li></ol><p class="dim">Your room will thank you, .</p></div>	-
38.11.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Make a Smoothie	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Make a Smoothie</h1><p class="muted">Fruit, liquid, blender. Breakfast in a glass.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Choose base</strong> — milk, yogurt, juice, or water.</li><li><strong>Add fruit</strong> — fresh or frozen. Frozen makes it thick.</li><li><strong>Boost it</strong> — protein powder, nut butter, seeds, greens.</li><li><strong>Pour liquid first</strong> — helps blending.</li><li><strong>Blend</strong> — start low, then high until smooth.</li><li><strong>Taste &amp; adjust</strong> — more liquid for thinner, more fruit for thicker.</li><li><strong>Serve</strong> — pour and enjoy immediately.</li></ol><p class="dim">Cheers, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Make a Smoothie</h1><p class="muted">Fruit, liquid, blender. Breakfast in a glass.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Choose base</strong> — milk, yogurt, juice, or water.</li><li><strong>Add fruit</strong> — fresh or frozen. Frozen makes it thick.</li><li><strong>Boost it</strong> — protein powder, nut butter, seeds, greens.</li><li><strong>Pour liquid first</strong> — helps blending.</li><li><strong>Blend</strong> — start low, then high until smooth.</li><li><strong>Taste &amp; adjust</strong> — more liquid for thinner, more fruit for thicker.</li><li><strong>Serve</strong> — pour and enjoy immediately.</li></ol><p class="dim">Cheers, .</p></div>	-
38.12.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Jump-Start a Car	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Jump-Start a Car</h1><p class="muted">Dead battery, live friend. Let's get you rolling.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Park close.</strong> Nose to nose or side by side. Both engines off. Set parking brakes.</li><li><strong>Find the battery.</strong> Pop both hoods. Look for the plastic box with two posts. Red is positive (+), black is negative (−).</li><li><strong>Connect red first.</strong> Clamp one red end to the dead battery's + post. Clamp the other red end to the good battery's + post.</li><li><strong>Connect black to good battery.</strong> Clamp one black end to the good battery's − post. Clamp the other black end to a bare metal bolt on the dead car's engine block—not the dead battery itself.</li><li><strong>Start the good car.</strong> Let it run for two minutes. Then try the dead car. If it won't start, wait five more minutes and try again.</li><li><strong>Disconnect in reverse.</strong> Black off the engine block, black off the good battery, red off the good battery, red off the dead battery. Keep clamps from touching each other.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p><strong>Careful:</strong> Never let red and black clamps touch. If you see sparks when connecting the last black clamp, stop—you're on the wrong spot.</p></div><p class="dim t-small">Battery still dead after a drive? It might be time for a new one, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Jump-Start a Car</h1><p class="muted">Dead battery, live friend. Let's get you rolling.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Park close.</strong> Nose to nose or side by side. Both engines off. Set parking brakes.</li><li><strong>Find the battery.</strong> Pop both hoods. Look for the plastic box with two posts. Red is positive (+), black is negative (−).</li><li><strong>Connect red first.</strong> Clamp one red end to the dead battery's + post. Clamp the other red end to the good battery's + post.</li><li><strong>Connect black to good battery.</strong> Clamp one black end to the good battery's − post. Clamp the other black end to a bare metal bolt on the dead car's engine block—not the dead battery itself.</li><li><strong>Start the good car.</strong> Let it run for two minutes. Then try the dead car. If it won't start, wait five more minutes and try again.</li><li><strong>Disconnect in reverse.</strong> Black off the engine block, black off the good battery, red off the good battery, red off the dead battery. Keep clamps from touching each other.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p><strong>Careful:</strong> Never let red and black clamps touch. If you see sparks when connecting the last black clamp, stop—you're on the wrong spot.</p></div><p class="dim t-small">Battery still dead after a drive? It might be time for a new one, .</p></div>	-
38.13.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Unclog a Drain	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Unclog a Drain</h1><p class="muted">Before you call a plumber, try these.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Remove the stopper.</strong> Lift or unscrew it. Shine a flashlight down. Pull out any hair or gunk with a bent wire or tweezers.</li><li><strong>Boiling water.</strong> Pour a kettle of hot (not boiling for PVC pipes) water down the drain. Wait five minutes. Repeat.</li><li><strong>Baking soda + vinegar.</strong> Pour half a cup of baking soda, then half a cup of white vinegar. Cover with a plug. Wait 15 minutes. Flush with hot water.</li><li><strong>Plunger.</strong> Block the overflow hole with a wet rag. Place the plunger over the drain. Push and pull firmly 15–20 times. Hold the seal.</li><li><strong>Drain snake.</strong> Feed the auger into the drain. Crank when you feel resistance. Pull it out slowly and wipe the gunk off.</li><li><strong>Check the P-trap.</strong> Place a bucket under the sink. Unscrew the curved pipe. Clean it out. Reassemble and run water.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>If water backs up in other drains too, the clog is deeper. That's a job for a pro.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Unclog a Drain</h1><p class="muted">Before you call a plumber, try these.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Remove the stopper.</strong> Lift or unscrew it. Shine a flashlight down. Pull out any hair or gunk with a bent wire or tweezers.</li><li><strong>Boiling water.</strong> Pour a kettle of hot (not boiling for PVC pipes) water down the drain. Wait five minutes. Repeat.</li><li><strong>Baking soda + vinegar.</strong> Pour half a cup of baking soda, then half a cup of white vinegar. Cover with a plug. Wait 15 minutes. Flush with hot water.</li><li><strong>Plunger.</strong> Block the overflow hole with a wet rag. Place the plunger over the drain. Push and pull firmly 15–20 times. Hold the seal.</li><li><strong>Drain snake.</strong> Feed the auger into the drain. Crank when you feel resistance. Pull it out slowly and wipe the gunk off.</li><li><strong>Check the P-trap.</strong> Place a bucket under the sink. Unscrew the curved pipe. Clean it out. Reassemble and run water.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>If water backs up in other drains too, the clog is deeper. That's a job for a pro.</p></div></div>	-
38.14.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Tie a Necktie	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Tie a Necktie</h1><p class="muted">The classic four-in-hand. Four moves, no mirror required.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Drape it.</strong> Put the tie around your neck, seam side down. The wide end hangs about a foot lower than the narrow end.</li><li><strong>Cross.</strong> Bring the wide end over the narrow end, making an X near your collar.</li><li><strong>Loop around.</strong> Take the wide end and bring it behind the narrow end, then out to the side.</li><li><strong>Up and over.</strong> Bring the wide end up across the front, forming a loop around your neck. Then pull it down through the loop you just made around the knot.</li><li><strong>Tighten.</strong> Hold the narrow end and slide the knot up toward your collar. Pinch the dimple at the top for a crisp look.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p>The knot should sit snug but not choke. You've got this, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Tie a Necktie</h1><p class="muted">The classic four-in-hand. Four moves, no mirror required.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Drape it.</strong> Put the tie around your neck, seam side down. The wide end hangs about a foot lower than the narrow end.</li><li><strong>Cross.</strong> Bring the wide end over the narrow end, making an X near your collar.</li><li><strong>Loop around.</strong> Take the wide end and bring it behind the narrow end, then out to the side.</li><li><strong>Up and over.</strong> Bring the wide end up across the front, forming a loop around your neck. Then pull it down through the loop you just made around the knot.</li><li><strong>Tighten.</strong> Hold the narrow end and slide the knot up toward your collar. Pinch the dimple at the top for a crisp look.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p>The knot should sit snug but not choke. You've got this, .</p></div></div>	-
38.15.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Change a Flat Tire	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Change a Flat Tire</h1><p class="muted">Pull over somewhere safe. Hazards on. Let's do this.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Gather tools.</strong> Spare tire, jack, lug wrench. Check the spare's air pressure if you can.</li><li><strong>Loosen lugs first.</strong> Before jacking, turn each lug nut counterclockwise a quarter turn. The weight of the car helps hold the tire.</li><li><strong>Jack it up.</strong> Find the jack point (see manual). Raise until the flat tire is about an inch off the ground.</li><li><strong>Remove lugs.</strong> Unscrew all lug nuts. Pull the tire straight off.</li><li><strong>Mount the spare.</strong> Lift it on, aligning the holes. Screw the lug nuts on by hand until snug.</li><li><strong>Lower and tighten.</strong> Lower the jack. Tighten lugs in a star pattern—top, then bottom, then sides—until firm.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p>Spare tires are usually temporary. Drive under 50 mph and get the flat repaired or replaced soon.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Change a Flat Tire</h1><p class="muted">Pull over somewhere safe. Hazards on. Let's do this.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Gather tools.</strong> Spare tire, jack, lug wrench. Check the spare's air pressure if you can.</li><li><strong>Loosen lugs first.</strong> Before jacking, turn each lug nut counterclockwise a quarter turn. The weight of the car helps hold the tire.</li><li><strong>Jack it up.</strong> Find the jack point (see manual). Raise until the flat tire is about an inch off the ground.</li><li><strong>Remove lugs.</strong> Unscrew all lug nuts. Pull the tire straight off.</li><li><strong>Mount the spare.</strong> Lift it on, aligning the holes. Screw the lug nuts on by hand until snug.</li><li><strong>Lower and tighten.</strong> Lower the jack. Tighten lugs in a star pattern—top, then bottom, then sides—until firm.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p>Spare tires are usually temporary. Drive under 50 mph and get the flat repaired or replaced soon.</p></div></div>	-
38.16.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Iron a Shirt	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Iron a Shirt</h1><p class="muted">Crisp collar, sharp creases. No burnt fabric, promise.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Prep.</strong> Fill the iron with water. Set to the fabric setting (cotton = high, silk = low). Lay the shirt flat on the board.</li><li><strong>Collar.</strong> Iron from the points toward the center, underside first. Then flip and press the top.</li><li><strong>Cuffs.</strong> Open them flat. Iron the inside, then the outside.</li><li><strong>Sleeves.</strong> Lay a sleeve flat, seam down. Iron from shoulder to cuff. Repeat on the other side.</li><li><strong>Body.</strong> Iron the back, then the front panels. Work around the buttons carefully.</li><li><strong>Hang immediately.</strong> Put it on a hanger while warm. Button the top button to keep the collar shaped.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>Stuck-on wrinkles? Spritz with water and press again. Steam is your friend.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Iron a Shirt</h1><p class="muted">Crisp collar, sharp creases. No burnt fabric, promise.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Prep.</strong> Fill the iron with water. Set to the fabric setting (cotton = high, silk = low). Lay the shirt flat on the board.</li><li><strong>Collar.</strong> Iron from the points toward the center, underside first. Then flip and press the top.</li><li><strong>Cuffs.</strong> Open them flat. Iron the inside, then the outside.</li><li><strong>Sleeves.</strong> Lay a sleeve flat, seam down. Iron from shoulder to cuff. Repeat on the other side.</li><li><strong>Body.</strong> Iron the back, then the front panels. Work around the buttons carefully.</li><li><strong>Hang immediately.</strong> Put it on a hanger while warm. Button the top button to keep the collar shaped.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>Stuck-on wrinkles? Spritz with water and press again. Steam is your friend.</p></div></div>	-
38.17.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Wrap a Gift	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Wrap a Gift</h1><p class="muted">Paper, tape, scissors. Ten minutes, tops.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Measure.</strong> Roll out paper. The box should have enough to cover it with a two-inch overlap.</li><li><strong>Cut.</strong> Straight line. No one's judging, but sharp scissors help.</li><li><strong>Center the box.</strong> Place it upside down on the paper.</li><li><strong>Wrap the long sides.</strong> Bring one long edge to the center, then the other. Overlap by an inch. Tape along the seam.</li><li><strong>Fold the ends.</strong> At each open end, fold the corners in like an envelope. Crease sharply. Fold up and tape.</li><li><strong>Finish.</strong> Flip it over. Add a bow, ribbon, or a sprig of rosemary. Done.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p>Lumpy gift? Tissue paper hides a lot. You're a wrapping pro now, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Wrap a Gift</h1><p class="muted">Paper, tape, scissors. Ten minutes, tops.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Measure.</strong> Roll out paper. The box should have enough to cover it with a two-inch overlap.</li><li><strong>Cut.</strong> Straight line. No one's judging, but sharp scissors help.</li><li><strong>Center the box.</strong> Place it upside down on the paper.</li><li><strong>Wrap the long sides.</strong> Bring one long edge to the center, then the other. Overlap by an inch. Tape along the seam.</li><li><strong>Fold the ends.</strong> At each open end, fold the corners in like an envelope. Crease sharply. Fold up and tape.</li><li><strong>Finish.</strong> Flip it over. Add a bow, ribbon, or a sprig of rosemary. Done.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p>Lumpy gift? Tissue paper hides a lot. You're a wrapping pro now, .</p></div></div>	-
38.18.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Hem Your Own Pants	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Hem Your Own Pants</h1><p class="muted">No tailor, no drama. <regex>user_name</regex>, you got this.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Try them on</strong> with the shoes you'll wear. Fold the cuff under to the length you want. Pin it.</li><li><strong>Take them off.</strong> Measure from the fold to the original hem. That's your new inseam. Write it down.</li><li><strong>Cut</strong> the legs, leaving 1.5 inches (4 cm) extra for the new hem. Cut one leg, then match the other.</li><li><strong>Fold and press</strong> a half-inch (1 cm) up, then another inch (2.5 cm). Pin.</li><li><strong>Sew</strong> close to the inner fold with a straight stitch. Backstitch at the start and end.</li><li><strong>Press</strong> the hem flat. Try them on. Admire your work.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">If the fabric is thick, use a denim needle and go slow. You can also use hem tape for a no-sew fix.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Hem Your Own Pants</h1><p class="muted">No tailor, no drama. , you got this.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Try them on</strong> with the shoes you'll wear. Fold the cuff under to the length you want. Pin it.</li><li><strong>Take them off.</strong> Measure from the fold to the original hem. That's your new inseam. Write it down.</li><li><strong>Cut</strong> the legs, leaving 1.5 inches (4 cm) extra for the new hem. Cut one leg, then match the other.</li><li><strong>Fold and press</strong> a half-inch (1 cm) up, then another inch (2.5 cm). Pin.</li><li><strong>Sew</strong> close to the inner fold with a straight stitch. Backstitch at the start and end.</li><li><strong>Press</strong> the hem flat. Try them on. Admire your work.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">If the fabric is thick, use a denim needle and go slow. You can also use hem tape for a no-sew fix.</p></div></div>	-
38.19.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Clean a Coffee Grinder	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Clean a Coffee Grinder</h1><p class="muted">Old grounds make bitter coffee. <regex>user_name</regex>, let's fix that.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Unplug</strong> the grinder. Safety first.</li><li><strong>Empty</strong> the hopper and grounds bin. Dump old beans.</li><li><strong>Brush</strong> out loose grounds with a stiff brush. Get into the chute.</li><li><strong>Grind</strong> a handful of raw rice or grinder cleaning pellets. This scrubs the burrs.</li><li><strong>Vacuum</strong> or brush out the rice dust. Wipe with a dry cloth.</li><li><strong>Reassemble</strong> and grind a few beans to season. Toss those.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p class="t-small">Don't use water or soap. Moisture kills burrs.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Clean a Coffee Grinder</h1><p class="muted">Old grounds make bitter coffee. , let's fix that.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Unplug</strong> the grinder. Safety first.</li><li><strong>Empty</strong> the hopper and grounds bin. Dump old beans.</li><li><strong>Brush</strong> out loose grounds with a stiff brush. Get into the chute.</li><li><strong>Grind</strong> a handful of raw rice or grinder cleaning pellets. This scrubs the burrs.</li><li><strong>Vacuum</strong> or brush out the rice dust. Wipe with a dry cloth.</li><li><strong>Reassemble</strong> and grind a few beans to season. Toss those.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p class="t-small">Don't use water or soap. Moisture kills burrs.</p></div></div>	-
38.20.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Jump Rope Like a Boxer	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Jump Rope Like a Boxer</h1><p class="muted">It's cheaper than a gym. <regex>user_name</regex>, let's get skipping.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Size the rope.</strong> Stand on the middle. Handles should reach your armpits.</li><li><strong>Grip</strong> handles lightly, elbows close to your ribs. Turn the rope with your wrists, not arms.</li><li><strong>Jump</strong> just an inch off the ground. Land softly on the balls of your feet.</li><li><strong>Start</strong> with 30 seconds on, 30 off. Repeat 5 times.</li><li><strong>Increase</strong> by 15 seconds each session. You'll be a pro in no time.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p class="t-small">Wear supportive shoes and jump on a forgiving surface. Your knees will thank you.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Jump Rope Like a Boxer</h1><p class="muted">It's cheaper than a gym. , let's get skipping.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Size the rope.</strong> Stand on the middle. Handles should reach your armpits.</li><li><strong>Grip</strong> handles lightly, elbows close to your ribs. Turn the rope with your wrists, not arms.</li><li><strong>Jump</strong> just an inch off the ground. Land softly on the balls of your feet.</li><li><strong>Start</strong> with 30 seconds on, 30 off. Repeat 5 times.</li><li><strong>Increase</strong> by 15 seconds each session. You'll be a pro in no time.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p class="t-small">Wear supportive shoes and jump on a forgiving surface. Your knees will thank you.</p></div></div>	-
38.21.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Fold a Fitted Sheet	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Fold a Fitted Sheet</h1><p class="muted">The enemy of tidy linen closets. <regex>user_name</regex>, we meet at dawn.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Lay it flat</strong> with the elastic facing up. Smooth it out.</li><li><strong>Find the corners.</strong> Tuck one corner into the other, elastic to elastic. Do the same with the other two.</li><li><strong>Lay it down.</strong> The sheet should now look like a rectangle with rounded corners.</li><li><strong>Fold</strong> the sides in to the center, then fold in thirds.</li><li><strong>Fold</strong> in half or thirds again until it's a neat bundle.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">If it's still lumpy, fold it in half once more. Nobody's checking.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Fold a Fitted Sheet</h1><p class="muted">The enemy of tidy linen closets. , we meet at dawn.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Lay it flat</strong> with the elastic facing up. Smooth it out.</li><li><strong>Find the corners.</strong> Tuck one corner into the other, elastic to elastic. Do the same with the other two.</li><li><strong>Lay it down.</strong> The sheet should now look like a rectangle with rounded corners.</li><li><strong>Fold</strong> the sides in to the center, then fold in thirds.</li><li><strong>Fold</strong> in half or thirds again until it's a neat bundle.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">If it's still lumpy, fold it in half once more. Nobody's checking.</p></div></div>	-
38.22.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Make Cold Brew Coffee	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Make Cold Brew Coffee</h1><p class="muted">Smooth, strong, and zero barista attitude. <regex>user_name</regex>, here's the drill.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Grind</strong> 1 cup of coffee beans coarse, like sea salt.</li><li><strong>Combine</strong> grounds and 4 cups of cold water in a jar. Stir gently.</li><li><strong>Wait</strong> 12–18 hours at room temperature. Patience is key.</li><li><strong>Strain</strong> through a fine mesh sieve lined with a coffee filter or cheesecloth.</li><li><strong>Dilute</strong> with water or milk to taste. Serve over ice.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p class="t-small">Keeps in the fridge for up to a week. You just saved a fortune.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Make Cold Brew Coffee</h1><p class="muted">Smooth, strong, and zero barista attitude. , here's the drill.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Grind</strong> 1 cup of coffee beans coarse, like sea salt.</li><li><strong>Combine</strong> grounds and 4 cups of cold water in a jar. Stir gently.</li><li><strong>Wait</strong> 12–18 hours at room temperature. Patience is key.</li><li><strong>Strain</strong> through a fine mesh sieve lined with a coffee filter or cheesecloth.</li><li><strong>Dilute</strong> with water or milk to taste. Serve over ice.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p class="t-small">Keeps in the fridge for up to a week. You just saved a fortune.</p></div></div>	-
38.23.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Clean Your Dishwasher	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Clean Your Dishwasher</h1><p class="muted">Yes, it needs cleaning too. <regex>user_name</regex>, let's do it.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Remove</strong> the bottom rack and take out the filter. Rinse it under warm water.</li><li><strong>Scrub</strong> the filter with a soft brush and dish soap. Rinse well.</li><li><strong>Wipe</strong> the door gasket and edges with a damp cloth. Gunk loves it there.</li><li><strong>Place</strong> a cup of white vinegar on the top rack. Run a hot water cycle.</li><li><strong>Sprinkle</strong> a cup of baking soda on the bottom. Run a short hot cycle.</li><li><strong>Wipe</strong> the interior dry. Leave the door ajar to air out.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Do this monthly to keep dishes sparkling and odors away.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Clean Your Dishwasher</h1><p class="muted">Yes, it needs cleaning too. , let's do it.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Remove</strong> the bottom rack and take out the filter. Rinse it under warm water.</li><li><strong>Scrub</strong> the filter with a soft brush and dish soap. Rinse well.</li><li><strong>Wipe</strong> the door gasket and edges with a damp cloth. Gunk loves it there.</li><li><strong>Place</strong> a cup of white vinegar on the top rack. Run a hot water cycle.</li><li><strong>Sprinkle</strong> a cup of baking soda on the bottom. Run a short hot cycle.</li><li><strong>Wipe</strong> the interior dry. Leave the door ajar to air out.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Do this monthly to keep dishes sparkling and odors away.</p></div></div>	-
38.24.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Brew Pour-Over Coffee	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Brew Pour-Over Coffee</h1><p class="muted">A clean cup, about four minutes, no fancy gear beyond a cone and a kettle.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Heat water.</strong> Just off boil — around 200°F / 93°C. If you don't have a thermometer, boil and wait 30 seconds.</li><li><strong>Rinse the filter.</strong> Put the paper filter in the cone, pour hot water through it, then dump the rinse water. This kills the paper taste and warms the mug.</li><li><strong>Weigh and grind.</strong> 22g of coffee, ground like table salt — medium-fine. You want it to look like sand, not powder.</li><li><strong>Bloom.</strong> Add 60g of water, just enough to wet all the grounds. Wait 40 seconds. It'll puff up and smell like the good part of a bakery.</li><li><strong>Pour in circles.</strong> Slow spirals from the center outward, up to 360g total. Keep the water level steady and don't let the bed go dry.</li><li><strong>Let it drain.</strong> Total brew should finish around 3:30–4:00. If it gushes through in two minutes, grind finer next time.</li><li><strong>Taste and adjust.</strong> Sour and thin? Grind finer or use hotter water. Bitter and harsh? Grind coarser or cool the water a touch.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">One variable at a time, <regex>user_name</regex>. Change the grind, keep everything else the same, and you'll learn your setup fast.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Brew Pour-Over Coffee</h1><p class="muted">A clean cup, about four minutes, no fancy gear beyond a cone and a kettle.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Heat water.</strong> Just off boil — around 200°F / 93°C. If you don't have a thermometer, boil and wait 30 seconds.</li><li><strong>Rinse the filter.</strong> Put the paper filter in the cone, pour hot water through it, then dump the rinse water. This kills the paper taste and warms the mug.</li><li><strong>Weigh and grind.</strong> 22g of coffee, ground like table salt — medium-fine. You want it to look like sand, not powder.</li><li><strong>Bloom.</strong> Add 60g of water, just enough to wet all the grounds. Wait 40 seconds. It'll puff up and smell like the good part of a bakery.</li><li><strong>Pour in circles.</strong> Slow spirals from the center outward, up to 360g total. Keep the water level steady and don't let the bed go dry.</li><li><strong>Let it drain.</strong> Total brew should finish around 3:30–4:00. If it gushes through in two minutes, grind finer next time.</li><li><strong>Taste and adjust.</strong> Sour and thin? Grind finer or use hotter water. Bitter and harsh? Grind coarser or cool the water a touch.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">One variable at a time, . Change the grind, keep everything else the same, and you'll learn your setup fast.</p></div></div>	-
38.25.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Write a Thank-You Note	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Write a Thank-You Note</h1><p class="muted">Short beats long. Specific beats generic. Send it within a week.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Name the thing.</strong> Not "thanks for everything" — "thank you for the dinner on Saturday" or "for the reference call."</li><li><strong>Say what it meant.</strong> One sentence. "It was the first time I'd laughed all week" or "It made the interview feel less like a trap."</li><li><strong>Add one specific detail.</strong> The lemon tart. The story about the dog. The advice about the second interview. Specifics prove you were there.</li><li><strong>Close warmly.</strong> "With thanks," "Gratefully," or just "Thanks again." Then your name. That's it.</li><li><strong>Keep it under five sentences.</strong> If it's longer, it's a letter, not a note. Both are fine — but the note gets read.</li><li><strong>Handwrite if you can.</strong> If not, a short email beats a late card. Send it within 48 hours for interviews, a week for gifts.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p class="t-small"><regex>user_name</regex>, the bar is low and the payoff is high. A three-sentence note will put you in the top ten percent of humans.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Write a Thank-You Note</h1><p class="muted">Short beats long. Specific beats generic. Send it within a week.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Name the thing.</strong> Not "thanks for everything" — "thank you for the dinner on Saturday" or "for the reference call."</li><li><strong>Say what it meant.</strong> One sentence. "It was the first time I'd laughed all week" or "It made the interview feel less like a trap."</li><li><strong>Add one specific detail.</strong> The lemon tart. The story about the dog. The advice about the second interview. Specifics prove you were there.</li><li><strong>Close warmly.</strong> "With thanks," "Gratefully," or just "Thanks again." Then your name. That's it.</li><li><strong>Keep it under five sentences.</strong> If it's longer, it's a letter, not a note. Both are fine — but the note gets read.</li><li><strong>Handwrite if you can.</strong> If not, a short email beats a late card. Send it within 48 hours for interviews, a week for gifts.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p class="t-small">, the bar is low and the payoff is high. A three-sentence note will put you in the top ten percent of humans.</p></div></div>	-
38.26.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Start a Small Herb Garden	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Start a Small Herb Garden</h1><p class="muted">Three pots, one sunny windowsill, and a lot of future pesto.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Pick easy herbs.</strong> Basil, mint, and chives forgive beginners. Rosemary and thyme are fussier about water.</li><li><strong>Choose pots with drainage.</strong> A hole in the bottom is non-negotiable. Herbs hate soggy roots more than they hate anything else.</li><li><strong>Use potting mix, not garden soil.</strong> Garden soil compacts in pots. Any bag labeled "potting mix" will do.</li><li><strong>Give them light.</strong> Six hours of direct sun is ideal. A south-facing window works; a grow light works if your window faces a wall.</li><li><strong>Water when the top inch is dry.</strong> Stick a finger in. If it's damp, wait. Overwatering kills more potted herbs than neglect.</li><li><strong>Harvest often.</strong> Pinch basil above a leaf pair. Snip mint freely. The more you cut, the bushier they get.</li><li><strong>Feed lightly.</strong> A diluted liquid fertilizer every two weeks in summer. Skip it in winter, when growth slows.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Mint will try to take over, <regex>user_name</regex>. Give it its own pot unless you want a mint empire.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Start a Small Herb Garden</h1><p class="muted">Three pots, one sunny windowsill, and a lot of future pesto.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Pick easy herbs.</strong> Basil, mint, and chives forgive beginners. Rosemary and thyme are fussier about water.</li><li><strong>Choose pots with drainage.</strong> A hole in the bottom is non-negotiable. Herbs hate soggy roots more than they hate anything else.</li><li><strong>Use potting mix, not garden soil.</strong> Garden soil compacts in pots. Any bag labeled "potting mix" will do.</li><li><strong>Give them light.</strong> Six hours of direct sun is ideal. A south-facing window works; a grow light works if your window faces a wall.</li><li><strong>Water when the top inch is dry.</strong> Stick a finger in. If it's damp, wait. Overwatering kills more potted herbs than neglect.</li><li><strong>Harvest often.</strong> Pinch basil above a leaf pair. Snip mint freely. The more you cut, the bushier they get.</li><li><strong>Feed lightly.</strong> A diluted liquid fertilizer every two weeks in summer. Skip it in winter, when growth slows.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Mint will try to take over, . Give it its own pot unless you want a mint empire.</p></div></div>	-
38.27.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Back Up Your Computer	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Back Up Your Computer</h1><p class="muted">The goal: if your laptop vanishes tomorrow, you lose a day, not a decade.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Pick your important stuff.</strong> Documents, photos, that folder of tax returns. You don't need to back up the operating system if you have installers or cloud accounts.</li><li><strong>Get an external drive.</strong> A 1TB or 2TB USB drive is cheap. Plug it in and format it for your system (APFS for Mac, NTFS or exFAT for Windows).</li><li><strong>Turn on automatic backups.</strong> Mac: Time Machine. Windows: File History or Backup and Restore. Point it at the drive and let it run overnight.</li><li><strong>Add a cloud layer.</strong> For the irreplaceable stuff — photos, writing, scans — use a cloud service with version history. It's the off-site half of the rule.</li><li><strong>Follow 3-2-1.</strong> Three copies, on two different media, one off-site. External drive + cloud + the original on your laptop covers it.</li><li><strong>Test a restore.</strong> Once a year, pull one file back from the backup. A backup you've never restored is a hope, not a plan.</li><li><strong>Check it monthly.</strong> Open the backup app, confirm the last run succeeded, and glance at free space. Five minutes, big peace of mind.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p class="t-small">Don't rely on one drive that lives next to your laptop, <regex>user_name</regex>. Fire and theft don't care about your folder structure.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Back Up Your Computer</h1><p class="muted">The goal: if your laptop vanishes tomorrow, you lose a day, not a decade.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Pick your important stuff.</strong> Documents, photos, that folder of tax returns. You don't need to back up the operating system if you have installers or cloud accounts.</li><li><strong>Get an external drive.</strong> A 1TB or 2TB USB drive is cheap. Plug it in and format it for your system (APFS for Mac, NTFS or exFAT for Windows).</li><li><strong>Turn on automatic backups.</strong> Mac: Time Machine. Windows: File History or Backup and Restore. Point it at the drive and let it run overnight.</li><li><strong>Add a cloud layer.</strong> For the irreplaceable stuff — photos, writing, scans — use a cloud service with version history. It's the off-site half of the rule.</li><li><strong>Follow 3-2-1.</strong> Three copies, on two different media, one off-site. External drive + cloud + the original on your laptop covers it.</li><li><strong>Test a restore.</strong> Once a year, pull one file back from the backup. A backup you've never restored is a hope, not a plan.</li><li><strong>Check it monthly.</strong> Open the backup app, confirm the last run succeeded, and glance at free space. Five minutes, big peace of mind.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p class="t-small">Don't rely on one drive that lives next to your laptop, . Fire and theft don't care about your folder structure.</p></div></div>	-
38.28.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Make a Sourdough Starter	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Make a Sourdough Starter</h1><p class="muted">Flour, water, patience. About a week until it's ready to raise bread.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Day 1: Mix.</strong> In a clean jar, stir 50g whole wheat flour with 50g warm water. Cover loosely. Let it sit at room temperature.</li><li><strong>Day 2: Check.</strong> It might smell like wet cardboard or nothing at all. That's fine. Stir it down and leave it.</li><li><strong>Day 3: First feed.</strong> Discard half the jar, then add 50g flour and 50g water. Stir. You'll start to see tiny bubbles.</li><li><strong>Days 4–5: Feed daily.</strong> Same routine: discard half, feed equal parts flour and water by weight. It should smell sour and yeasty, not like nail polish.</li><li><strong>Days 6–7: Watch for rise.</strong> A healthy starter doubles within 4–8 hours of feeding and smells like yogurt and bread. That's your green light.</li><li><strong>Store it.</strong> Once active, keep it in the fridge and feed it once a week. Take it out a day before baking and feed it twice to wake it up.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">If it grows fuzzy mold or smells like acetone, toss it and start over, <regex>user_name</regex>. No shame — starters are hardy but not immortal.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Make a Sourdough Starter</h1><p class="muted">Flour, water, patience. About a week until it's ready to raise bread.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Day 1: Mix.</strong> In a clean jar, stir 50g whole wheat flour with 50g warm water. Cover loosely. Let it sit at room temperature.</li><li><strong>Day 2: Check.</strong> It might smell like wet cardboard or nothing at all. That's fine. Stir it down and leave it.</li><li><strong>Day 3: First feed.</strong> Discard half the jar, then add 50g flour and 50g water. Stir. You'll start to see tiny bubbles.</li><li><strong>Days 4–5: Feed daily.</strong> Same routine: discard half, feed equal parts flour and water by weight. It should smell sour and yeasty, not like nail polish.</li><li><strong>Days 6–7: Watch for rise.</strong> A healthy starter doubles within 4–8 hours of feeding and smells like yogurt and bread. That's your green light.</li><li><strong>Store it.</strong> Once active, keep it in the fridge and feed it once a week. Take it out a day before baking and feed it twice to wake it up.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">If it grows fuzzy mold or smells like acetone, toss it and start over, . No shame — starters are hardy but not immortal.</p></div></div>	-
38.29.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Write a Resume That Works	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Write a Resume That Works</h1><p class="muted">One page, plain formatting, numbers where you can. Recruiters skim in six seconds.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Dump everything first.</strong> In a scratch document, list every job, project, and skill. Don't edit yet. Volume comes before polish.</li><li><strong>Cut to the relevant.</strong> Keep what matches the job you want. Drop the summer job from high school if you're 30.</li><li><strong>Write bullets that start with verbs.</strong> "Managed," "built," "fixed," "taught." Not "Responsible for."</li><li><strong>Add a number to each bullet.</strong> "Managed a team of six," "cut wait times by 20%," "wrote 40 articles." Numbers are proof.</li><li><strong>Format for skimming.</strong> Name and contact at the top. Section headers. No columns, no graphics, no photo. A plain document parses better.</li><li><strong>Tailor for each application.</strong> Swap in the keywords from the posting. If they say "stakeholder management," use those words.</li><li><strong>Proofread out loud.</strong> Then have someone else read it. Typos read as carelessness, even when they're just typos.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p class="t-small">Save as PDF, name it Firstname_Lastname_Resume.pdf, and <regex>user_name</regex> — that's a resume that doesn't get thrown out.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Write a Resume That Works</h1><p class="muted">One page, plain formatting, numbers where you can. Recruiters skim in six seconds.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Dump everything first.</strong> In a scratch document, list every job, project, and skill. Don't edit yet. Volume comes before polish.</li><li><strong>Cut to the relevant.</strong> Keep what matches the job you want. Drop the summer job from high school if you're 30.</li><li><strong>Write bullets that start with verbs.</strong> "Managed," "built," "fixed," "taught." Not "Responsible for."</li><li><strong>Add a number to each bullet.</strong> "Managed a team of six," "cut wait times by 20%," "wrote 40 articles." Numbers are proof.</li><li><strong>Format for skimming.</strong> Name and contact at the top. Section headers. No columns, no graphics, no photo. A plain document parses better.</li><li><strong>Tailor for each application.</strong> Swap in the keywords from the posting. If they say "stakeholder management," use those words.</li><li><strong>Proofread out loud.</strong> Then have someone else read it. Typos read as carelessness, even when they're just typos.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p class="t-small">Save as PDF, name it Firstname_Lastname_Resume.pdf, and  — that's a resume that doesn't get thrown out.</p></div></div>	-
38.30.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Unclog a Sink Drain	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Unclog a Sink Drain</h1>  <p class="muted">No chemicals needed. <regex>user_name</regex>, you've got this.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li>Bail out standing water with a cup.</li>    <li>Pour 1/2 cup baking soda, then 1/2 cup vinegar. Wait 15 min.</li>    <li>Flush with a kettle of hot (not boiling) water.</li>    <li>If still slow, use a plunger: seal the overflow hole with a wet rag, plunge 10–15 times.</li>    <li>Still stuck? Remove the P-trap with a bucket underneath. Clean, reattach.</li>    <li>Run hot water for 2 minutes to test.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout warn">Skip chemical drain cleaners—they can damage pipes and don't always work.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Unclog a Sink Drain</h1>  <p class="muted">No chemicals needed. , you've got this.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li>Bail out standing water with a cup.</li>    <li>Pour 1/2 cup baking soda, then 1/2 cup vinegar. Wait 15 min.</li>    <li>Flush with a kettle of hot (not boiling) water.</li>    <li>If still slow, use a plunger: seal the overflow hole with a wet rag, plunge 10–15 times.</li>    <li>Still stuck? Remove the P-trap with a bucket underneath. Clean, reattach.</li>    <li>Run hot water for 2 minutes to test.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout warn">Skip chemical drain cleaners—they can damage pipes and don't always work.</div></div>	-
38.31.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Season a Cast Iron Skillet	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Season a Cast Iron Skillet</h1><p class="muted">For <regex>user_name</regex> — a little oil, a lot of heat, and patience.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Scrub it clean.</strong> Warm water, stiff brush, no soap. Dry it right away — rust hates a wet pan.</li><li><strong>Heat it up.</strong> Oven to 450°F (230°C). Put the pan in for 10 minutes to open the pores.</li><li><strong>Oil it thin.</strong> A teaspoon of flaxseed or canola oil. Rub it everywhere with a cloth. Then wipe it all off like you made a mistake — a microscopic layer is the goal.</li><li><strong>Bake it upside down.</strong> Put foil on the rack below to catch drips. 1 hour. Let it cool in the oven.</li><li><strong>Repeat.</strong> Three rounds gives a decent start. More rounds, darker and slicker.</li><li><strong>Cook with it often.</strong> Fry, sear, cornbread. The seasoning builds with use.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p><strong>Quick fix for rust:</strong> Scrub with steel wool, wash, dry, then season as above. It’ll come back.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Season a Cast Iron Skillet</h1><p class="muted">For  — a little oil, a lot of heat, and patience.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Scrub it clean.</strong> Warm water, stiff brush, no soap. Dry it right away — rust hates a wet pan.</li><li><strong>Heat it up.</strong> Oven to 450°F (230°C). Put the pan in for 10 minutes to open the pores.</li><li><strong>Oil it thin.</strong> A teaspoon of flaxseed or canola oil. Rub it everywhere with a cloth. Then wipe it all off like you made a mistake — a microscopic layer is the goal.</li><li><strong>Bake it upside down.</strong> Put foil on the rack below to catch drips. 1 hour. Let it cool in the oven.</li><li><strong>Repeat.</strong> Three rounds gives a decent start. More rounds, darker and slicker.</li><li><strong>Cook with it often.</strong> Fry, sear, cornbread. The seasoning builds with use.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p><strong>Quick fix for rust:</strong> Scrub with steel wool, wash, dry, then season as above. It’ll come back.</p></div></div>	-
38.32.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Fold a Paper Airplane That Flies Far	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Fold a Paper Airplane That Flies Far</h1><p class="muted">For <regex>user_name</regex> — the classic dart, tuned for distance.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Start with a rectangle.</strong> Letter or A4 paper, plain. No folds yet.</li><li><strong>Fold in half lengthwise.</strong> Crease well, then unfold. This is your center line.</li><li><strong>Fold the top corners to the center.</strong> Like a paper hat. The edges should meet the crease.</li><li><strong>Fold the new slanted edges to the center again.</strong> You’re making a narrower point.</li><li><strong>Fold the whole thing in half along the center line.</strong> The flaps go inside.</li><li><strong>Fold the wings down.</strong> Make each wing parallel to the body, about a finger’s width from the bottom.</li><li><strong>Throw it.</strong> Hold the body near the back, aim slightly up, and snap your wrist. Smooth, not hard.</li></ol><p class="t-small dim">Tweak: add a small paperclip to the nose for weight if it stalls.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Fold a Paper Airplane That Flies Far</h1><p class="muted">For  — the classic dart, tuned for distance.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Start with a rectangle.</strong> Letter or A4 paper, plain. No folds yet.</li><li><strong>Fold in half lengthwise.</strong> Crease well, then unfold. This is your center line.</li><li><strong>Fold the top corners to the center.</strong> Like a paper hat. The edges should meet the crease.</li><li><strong>Fold the new slanted edges to the center again.</strong> You’re making a narrower point.</li><li><strong>Fold the whole thing in half along the center line.</strong> The flaps go inside.</li><li><strong>Fold the wings down.</strong> Make each wing parallel to the body, about a finger’s width from the bottom.</li><li><strong>Throw it.</strong> Hold the body near the back, aim slightly up, and snap your wrist. Smooth, not hard.</li></ol><p class="t-small dim">Tweak: add a small paperclip to the nose for weight if it stalls.</p></div>	-
38.33.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Boil the Perfect Hard-Boiled Egg	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Boil the Perfect Hard-Boiled Egg</h1><p class="muted">For <regex>user_name</regex> — no green ring, easy peel, every time.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Start with older eggs.</strong> A week-old egg peels easier than a fresh one. Cold from the fridge.</li><li><strong>Boil, then rest.</strong> Put eggs in a pot, cover with an inch of cold water. Bring to a rolling boil, then turn off heat, cover, and wait 10–12 minutes.</li><li><strong>Ice bath.</strong> Move eggs to a bowl of ice water for 5 minutes. This stops cooking and loosens the shell.</li><li><strong>Peel under water.</strong> Tap the egg on the counter, roll gently, then peel under running water or in the bowl. The water helps separate the membrane.</li><li><strong>Eat or store.</strong> Fridge for up to a week. Peel just before serving.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p>Yolk just set, no gray ring. That’s the goal.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Boil the Perfect Hard-Boiled Egg</h1><p class="muted">For  — no green ring, easy peel, every time.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Start with older eggs.</strong> A week-old egg peels easier than a fresh one. Cold from the fridge.</li><li><strong>Boil, then rest.</strong> Put eggs in a pot, cover with an inch of cold water. Bring to a rolling boil, then turn off heat, cover, and wait 10–12 minutes.</li><li><strong>Ice bath.</strong> Move eggs to a bowl of ice water for 5 minutes. This stops cooking and loosens the shell.</li><li><strong>Peel under water.</strong> Tap the egg on the counter, roll gently, then peel under running water or in the bowl. The water helps separate the membrane.</li><li><strong>Eat or store.</strong> Fridge for up to a week. Peel just before serving.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p>Yolk just set, no gray ring. That’s the goal.</p></div></div>	-
38.34.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Fix a Running Toilet	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Fix a Running Toilet</h1><p class="muted">For <regex>user_name</regex> — quiet the tank in six steps.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Turn off the water.</strong> Valve behind the toilet, turn clockwise. Flush to empty the tank.</li><li><strong>Open the tank.</strong> Lift the lid, set it somewhere safe.</li><li><strong>Check the flapper.</strong> The rubber flapper at the bottom should seal. If it’s warped or worn, that’s often the culprit. Replace it (cheap, easy).</li><li><strong>Check the fill valve.</strong> If water keeps running after the tank is full, the fill valve may need adjusting or replacing.</li><li><strong>Adjust the chain.</strong> If the chain from the handle to the flapper is too tight, it holds the flapper open. Give it a little slack.</li><li><strong>Turn water back on and test.</strong> Flush a few times. Listen for silence after the tank fills.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p>If you see water on the floor, tighten the connections gently — don’t force.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Fix a Running Toilet</h1><p class="muted">For  — quiet the tank in six steps.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Turn off the water.</strong> Valve behind the toilet, turn clockwise. Flush to empty the tank.</li><li><strong>Open the tank.</strong> Lift the lid, set it somewhere safe.</li><li><strong>Check the flapper.</strong> The rubber flapper at the bottom should seal. If it’s warped or worn, that’s often the culprit. Replace it (cheap, easy).</li><li><strong>Check the fill valve.</strong> If water keeps running after the tank is full, the fill valve may need adjusting or replacing.</li><li><strong>Adjust the chain.</strong> If the chain from the handle to the flapper is too tight, it holds the flapper open. Give it a little slack.</li><li><strong>Turn water back on and test.</strong> Flush a few times. Listen for silence after the tank fills.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p>If you see water on the floor, tighten the connections gently — don’t force.</p></div></div>	-
38.35.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Make a Simple Vinaigrette	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Make a Simple Vinaigrette</h1><p class="muted">For <regex>user_name</regex> — three parts oil, one part acid, endless variations.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Gather your base.</strong> 3 tablespoons olive oil, 1 tablespoon vinegar (red wine, balsamic, or lemon juice).</li><li><strong>Add a pinch of salt and pepper.</strong> Start small; you can always add more.</li><li><strong>Whisk or shake.</strong> Combine in a jar and shake, or whisk in a bowl until emulsified (looks cloudy and slightly thickened).</li><li><strong>Taste and adjust.</strong> Too sharp? More oil. Too flat? More acid or salt. A tiny bit of Dijon mustard helps emulsify and adds tang.</li><li><strong>Use immediately or store.</strong> Shake again before using. Keeps in the fridge for a week.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>Variations: add minced garlic, herbs, honey, or shallot. Go wild.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Make a Simple Vinaigrette</h1><p class="muted">For  — three parts oil, one part acid, endless variations.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Gather your base.</strong> 3 tablespoons olive oil, 1 tablespoon vinegar (red wine, balsamic, or lemon juice).</li><li><strong>Add a pinch of salt and pepper.</strong> Start small; you can always add more.</li><li><strong>Whisk or shake.</strong> Combine in a jar and shake, or whisk in a bowl until emulsified (looks cloudy and slightly thickened).</li><li><strong>Taste and adjust.</strong> Too sharp? More oil. Too flat? More acid or salt. A tiny bit of Dijon mustard helps emulsify and adds tang.</li><li><strong>Use immediately or store.</strong> Shake again before using. Keeps in the fridge for a week.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>Variations: add minced garlic, herbs, honey, or shallot. Go wild.</p></div></div>	-
38.36.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Fix a Zipper That Won’t Stay Up	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Fix a Zipper That Won’t Stay Up</h1><p class="muted">For <regex>user_name</regex> — rescue that jacket, bag, or jeans.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Check the slider.</strong> The pull tab may be loose, but the real issue is often the slider’s grip on the teeth.</li><li><strong>Clean the teeth.</strong> Dirt or lint can block the slider. Scrub with an old toothbrush and a little soapy water.</li><li><strong>Tighten the slider.</strong> Use pliers to gently squeeze the top and bottom of the slider (the part that holds the teeth). Go slow — too much and it won’t move.</li><li><strong>Lubricate.</strong> Rub a pencil lead (graphite) or a little candle wax along the teeth. Zip up and down a few times.</li><li><strong>Test.</strong> Zip it up. If it still slides, squeeze the slider a tiny bit more.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p>If the teeth are missing or broken, you’ll need a zipper replacement — not a quick fix.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Fix a Zipper That Won’t Stay Up</h1><p class="muted">For  — rescue that jacket, bag, or jeans.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Check the slider.</strong> The pull tab may be loose, but the real issue is often the slider’s grip on the teeth.</li><li><strong>Clean the teeth.</strong> Dirt or lint can block the slider. Scrub with an old toothbrush and a little soapy water.</li><li><strong>Tighten the slider.</strong> Use pliers to gently squeeze the top and bottom of the slider (the part that holds the teeth). Go slow — too much and it won’t move.</li><li><strong>Lubricate.</strong> Rub a pencil lead (graphite) or a little candle wax along the teeth. Zip up and down a few times.</li><li><strong>Test.</strong> Zip it up. If it still slides, squeeze the slider a tiny bit more.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p>If the teeth are missing or broken, you’ll need a zipper replacement — not a quick fix.</p></div></div>	-
38.37.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Remove a Stripped Screw	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Remove a Stripped Screw</h1>  <p class="muted">Stuck screw, stripped head? Try these in order. Patience beats force.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Add grip.</strong> Stretch a wide rubber band over the screw head and press the driver in firmly. The rubber fills the stripped slots.</li>    <li><strong>Push and turn.</strong> Apply hard downward pressure while turning slowly counter-clockwise. Most stripped screws just need more push.</li>    <li><strong>Try a different driver.</strong> Switch to a slightly larger Phillips or a flathead that catches the remaining edges.</li>    <li><strong>Score a new slot.</strong> With a rotary tool or hacksaw blade, cut a shallow straight groove across the head. Use a flathead driver.</li>    <li><strong>Use a screw extractor.</strong> Drill a small pilot hole in the center, then twist in the extractor bit counter-clockwise.</li>    <li><strong>Last resort.</strong> Drill off the head with a bit slightly wider than the screw shaft, remove the part, and unscrew the stub with pliers.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout info">    <p><strong>Tip:</strong> A drop of valve-grinding compound or a pinch of abrasive cleanser on the driver tip adds grit and grip.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Remove a Stripped Screw</h1>  <p class="muted">Stuck screw, stripped head? Try these in order. Patience beats force.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Add grip.</strong> Stretch a wide rubber band over the screw head and press the driver in firmly. The rubber fills the stripped slots.</li>    <li><strong>Push and turn.</strong> Apply hard downward pressure while turning slowly counter-clockwise. Most stripped screws just need more push.</li>    <li><strong>Try a different driver.</strong> Switch to a slightly larger Phillips or a flathead that catches the remaining edges.</li>    <li><strong>Score a new slot.</strong> With a rotary tool or hacksaw blade, cut a shallow straight groove across the head. Use a flathead driver.</li>    <li><strong>Use a screw extractor.</strong> Drill a small pilot hole in the center, then twist in the extractor bit counter-clockwise.</li>    <li><strong>Last resort.</strong> Drill off the head with a bit slightly wider than the screw shaft, remove the part, and unscrew the stub with pliers.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout info">    <p><strong>Tip:</strong> A drop of valve-grinding compound or a pinch of abrasive cleanser on the driver tip adds grit and grip.</p>  </div></div>	-
38.38.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Whistle Loudly With Your Fingers	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Whistle Loudly With Your Fingers</h1>  <p class="muted">The kind of whistle that hails a cab or a dog. It's all about the air chamber.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Wash your hands.</strong> Clean fingers taste better and grip better.</li>    <li><strong>Make a V.</strong> Use your index and middle fingers (or thumb and index) to form a V shape, palms facing you.</li>    <li><strong>Place under tongue.</strong> Slide the V under your tongue, about halfway back. Your tongue rests on top.</li>    <li><strong>Close your lips.</strong> Seal your lips around your fingers. The only opening is a small slit between your fingers.</li>    <li><strong>Blow hard.</strong> Force air through the slit. Start with a gentle blow, then increase pressure until you get a tone.</li>    <li><strong>Adjust angle.</strong> Move fingers slightly forward or back, or change tongue position, until the sound is loud and clear.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout warn">    <p><strong>Near miss:</strong> If you get a weak hiss, your lips are too tight or the gap is too wide. Relax and try again.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Whistle Loudly With Your Fingers</h1>  <p class="muted">The kind of whistle that hails a cab or a dog. It's all about the air chamber.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Wash your hands.</strong> Clean fingers taste better and grip better.</li>    <li><strong>Make a V.</strong> Use your index and middle fingers (or thumb and index) to form a V shape, palms facing you.</li>    <li><strong>Place under tongue.</strong> Slide the V under your tongue, about halfway back. Your tongue rests on top.</li>    <li><strong>Close your lips.</strong> Seal your lips around your fingers. The only opening is a small slit between your fingers.</li>    <li><strong>Blow hard.</strong> Force air through the slit. Start with a gentle blow, then increase pressure until you get a tone.</li>    <li><strong>Adjust angle.</strong> Move fingers slightly forward or back, or change tongue position, until the sound is loud and clear.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout warn">    <p><strong>Near miss:</strong> If you get a weak hiss, your lips are too tight or the gap is too wide. Relax and try again.</p>  </div></div>	-
38.39.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Get Rid of Fruit Flies	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Get Rid of Fruit Flies</h1>  <p class="muted">Those tiny hovering flies? They're after fermenting fruit and damp drains. Here's how to evict them.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Find the source.</strong> Check fruit bowls, compost, recycling bins, and drains. Even a forgotten potato can breed them.</li>    <li><strong>Clean up.</strong> Remove overripe produce, take out the trash, and rinse bottles and cans before recycling.</li>    <li><strong>Make a trap.</strong> Pour a splash of apple cider vinegar into a jar. Add a drop of dish soap. Cover with plastic wrap and poke small holes.</li>    <li><strong>Place traps.</strong> Set near the source. Flies go in, can't get out, and drown.</li>    <li><strong>Treat drains.</strong> Pour boiling water down kitchen drains, or use a drain brush, to kill eggs and larvae.</li>    <li><strong>Repeat.</strong> Keep traps out for a week after you stop seeing flies, to catch newly hatched ones.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout good">    <p><strong>Good news:</strong> Fruit flies live only about a week. Break the cycle and they're gone for good.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Get Rid of Fruit Flies</h1>  <p class="muted">Those tiny hovering flies? They're after fermenting fruit and damp drains. Here's how to evict them.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Find the source.</strong> Check fruit bowls, compost, recycling bins, and drains. Even a forgotten potato can breed them.</li>    <li><strong>Clean up.</strong> Remove overripe produce, take out the trash, and rinse bottles and cans before recycling.</li>    <li><strong>Make a trap.</strong> Pour a splash of apple cider vinegar into a jar. Add a drop of dish soap. Cover with plastic wrap and poke small holes.</li>    <li><strong>Place traps.</strong> Set near the source. Flies go in, can't get out, and drown.</li>    <li><strong>Treat drains.</strong> Pour boiling water down kitchen drains, or use a drain brush, to kill eggs and larvae.</li>    <li><strong>Repeat.</strong> Keep traps out for a week after you stop seeing flies, to catch newly hatched ones.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout good">    <p><strong>Good news:</strong> Fruit flies live only about a week. Break the cycle and they're gone for good.</p>  </div></div>	-
38.40.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Clean a Whiteboard	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Clean a Whiteboard</h1>  <p class="muted">Ghost marks and smudges? You can bring it back to white. Here's how.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Dry erase first.</strong> Use a clean eraser to remove loose ink. Don't scrub hard; you'll just spread it.</li>    <li><strong>Wipe with a damp cloth.</strong> Microfiber cloth with warm water. Rinse often.</li>    <li><strong>For stubborn marks, use isopropyl alcohol.</strong> Apply to a cloth (not directly on the board) and wipe. Ventilate the room.</li>    <li><strong>Permanent marker?</strong> Scribble over it with a dry-erase marker, then wipe. The solvent in the fresh ink lifts the permanent stuff.</li>    <li><strong>Deep clean monthly.</strong> Use a whiteboard cleaner or a 50/50 mix of water and vinegar. Dry with a clean cloth.</li>    <li><strong>Prevent ghosting.</strong> Erase after every use, and don't leave ink on for weeks.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout info">    <p><strong>Tip:</strong> Never use abrasive sponges or glass cleaner with ammonia; they can damage the surface.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Clean a Whiteboard</h1>  <p class="muted">Ghost marks and smudges? You can bring it back to white. Here's how.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Dry erase first.</strong> Use a clean eraser to remove loose ink. Don't scrub hard; you'll just spread it.</li>    <li><strong>Wipe with a damp cloth.</strong> Microfiber cloth with warm water. Rinse often.</li>    <li><strong>For stubborn marks, use isopropyl alcohol.</strong> Apply to a cloth (not directly on the board) and wipe. Ventilate the room.</li>    <li><strong>Permanent marker?</strong> Scribble over it with a dry-erase marker, then wipe. The solvent in the fresh ink lifts the permanent stuff.</li>    <li><strong>Deep clean monthly.</strong> Use a whiteboard cleaner or a 50/50 mix of water and vinegar. Dry with a clean cloth.</li>    <li><strong>Prevent ghosting.</strong> Erase after every use, and don't leave ink on for weeks.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout info">    <p><strong>Tip:</strong> Never use abrasive sponges or glass cleaner with ammonia; they can damage the surface.</p>  </div></div>	-
38.41.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Get Gum Out of Hair	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Get Gum Out of Hair</h1>  <p class="muted">Don't reach for the scissors yet. These methods work, from gentlest to fastest.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Oil it up.</strong> Massage a generous amount of olive oil, baby oil, or peanut butter into the gum and hair. Let sit 5 minutes.</li>    <li><strong>Work it loose.</strong> Use your fingers or a wide-tooth comb to gently slide the gum down the hair strand. Don't pull.</li>    <li><strong>Ice it.</strong> If oil isn't cutting it, hold an ice cube on the gum for a few minutes until it hardens, then crack it off.</li>    <li><strong>Try alcohol or vinegar.</strong> Soak a cotton ball in rubbing alcohol or white vinegar and dab at the gum. It breaks down the stickiness.</li>    <li><strong>Wash and condition.</strong> Once the gum is out, shampoo and condition to remove oily residue.</li>    <li><strong>Last resort: cut.</strong> If the gum is at the very end of a strand, a small snip is fine. But try everything else first.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout warn">    <p><strong>Near miss:</strong> Don't use hot water; it melts the gum deeper into the hair.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Get Gum Out of Hair</h1>  <p class="muted">Don't reach for the scissors yet. These methods work, from gentlest to fastest.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Oil it up.</strong> Massage a generous amount of olive oil, baby oil, or peanut butter into the gum and hair. Let sit 5 minutes.</li>    <li><strong>Work it loose.</strong> Use your fingers or a wide-tooth comb to gently slide the gum down the hair strand. Don't pull.</li>    <li><strong>Ice it.</strong> If oil isn't cutting it, hold an ice cube on the gum for a few minutes until it hardens, then crack it off.</li>    <li><strong>Try alcohol or vinegar.</strong> Soak a cotton ball in rubbing alcohol or white vinegar and dab at the gum. It breaks down the stickiness.</li>    <li><strong>Wash and condition.</strong> Once the gum is out, shampoo and condition to remove oily residue.</li>    <li><strong>Last resort: cut.</strong> If the gum is at the very end of a strand, a small snip is fine. But try everything else first.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout warn">    <p><strong>Near miss:</strong> Don't use hot water; it melts the gum deeper into the hair.</p>  </div></div>	-
38.42.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Sharpen a Kitchen Knife	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Sharpen a Kitchen Knife</h1>  <p class="muted">A dull knife is more dangerous than a sharp one. Here's how to get a good edge with a whetstone.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Soak the stone.</strong> If using a water stone, submerge it in water for 10–15 minutes until bubbles stop.</li>    <li><strong>Set the angle.</strong> Hold the knife at about a 15–20° angle to the stone (two stacked coins under the spine is a good guide).</li>    <li><strong>Draw across.</strong> Starting at the heel, push the blade across the stone in a sweeping motion, as if slicing a thin layer off the stone. Do 10 passes on one side.</li>    <li><strong>Flip and repeat.</strong> Do 10 passes on the other side.</li>    <li><strong>Check the burr.</strong> Feel the edge lightly (careful!). A burr—a slight rough lip—means you're done with the coarse side.</li>    <li><strong>Hone and strop.</strong> Switch to a finer grit stone for 5–10 passes per side, then strop on leather or newspaper to polish the edge.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout good">    <p><strong>Test:</strong> A sharp knife slices a ripe tomato under its own weight.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Sharpen a Kitchen Knife</h1>  <p class="muted">A dull knife is more dangerous than a sharp one. Here's how to get a good edge with a whetstone.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Soak the stone.</strong> If using a water stone, submerge it in water for 10–15 minutes until bubbles stop.</li>    <li><strong>Set the angle.</strong> Hold the knife at about a 15–20° angle to the stone (two stacked coins under the spine is a good guide).</li>    <li><strong>Draw across.</strong> Starting at the heel, push the blade across the stone in a sweeping motion, as if slicing a thin layer off the stone. Do 10 passes on one side.</li>    <li><strong>Flip and repeat.</strong> Do 10 passes on the other side.</li>    <li><strong>Check the burr.</strong> Feel the edge lightly (careful!). A burr—a slight rough lip—means you're done with the coarse side.</li>    <li><strong>Hone and strop.</strong> Switch to a finer grit stone for 5–10 passes per side, then strop on leather or newspaper to polish the edge.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout good">    <p><strong>Test:</strong> A sharp knife slices a ripe tomato under its own weight.</p>  </div></div>	-
38.43.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Descale a Kettle	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Descale a Kettle</h1><p class="muted">White crust is just limescale. A little acid and patience, and your kettle will taste like water again.</p><div class="card"><ol class="list"><li>Fill the kettle halfway with equal parts water and white vinegar. If the scale is thick, use straight vinegar.</li><li>Bring it to a boil, then let it sit for 20 minutes. The acid does the work.</li><li>Pour it out and rinse well. If any crust remains, scrub gently with a non-scratch sponge.</li><li>Boil fresh water twice and dump it. This clears the vinegar taste.</li><li>Dry the outside with a cloth. Leave the lid open to air out.</li></ol></div><div class="callout info"><p>Skip the vinegar if you have a stainless steel kettle and you dislike the smell. Citric acid powder works just as well and leaves no odor.</p></div><div class="row between"><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>5 steps</span><span class="badge">~30 min</span></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Descale a Kettle</h1><p class="muted">White crust is just limescale. A little acid and patience, and your kettle will taste like water again.</p><div class="card"><ol class="list"><li>Fill the kettle halfway with equal parts water and white vinegar. If the scale is thick, use straight vinegar.</li><li>Bring it to a boil, then let it sit for 20 minutes. The acid does the work.</li><li>Pour it out and rinse well. If any crust remains, scrub gently with a non-scratch sponge.</li><li>Boil fresh water twice and dump it. This clears the vinegar taste.</li><li>Dry the outside with a cloth. Leave the lid open to air out.</li></ol></div><div class="callout info"><p>Skip the vinegar if you have a stainless steel kettle and you dislike the smell. Citric acid powder works just as well and leaves no odor.</p></div><div class="row between"><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>5 steps</span><span class="badge">~30 min</span></div></div>	-
38.44.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Clean a Cast Iron Skillet	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Clean a Cast Iron Skillet</h1><p class="muted">Cast iron is tough. A little soap won't kill it, but a good drying will save it.</p><div class="card"><ol class="list"><li>Let the skillet cool until it's warm, not hot. Sudden cold water can crack it.</li><li>Scrub with a stiff brush and hot water. For stuck bits, add coarse salt and scrub.</li><li>Use a drop of mild soap if needed. Modern seasoning handles it fine.</li><li>Dry it immediately with a towel, then set it on low heat for a minute to evaporate any moisture.</li><li>Rub a thin layer of oil over the cooking surface while it's still warm. Wipe off the excess.</li></ol></div><div class="callout warn"><p>Never soak it. A wet skillet left in the sink is a rusty skillet by morning.</p></div><div class="row between"><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>5 steps</span><span class="badge">~10 min</span></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Clean a Cast Iron Skillet</h1><p class="muted">Cast iron is tough. A little soap won't kill it, but a good drying will save it.</p><div class="card"><ol class="list"><li>Let the skillet cool until it's warm, not hot. Sudden cold water can crack it.</li><li>Scrub with a stiff brush and hot water. For stuck bits, add coarse salt and scrub.</li><li>Use a drop of mild soap if needed. Modern seasoning handles it fine.</li><li>Dry it immediately with a towel, then set it on low heat for a minute to evaporate any moisture.</li><li>Rub a thin layer of oil over the cooking surface while it's still warm. Wipe off the excess.</li></ol></div><div class="callout warn"><p>Never soak it. A wet skillet left in the sink is a rusty skillet by morning.</p></div><div class="row between"><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>5 steps</span><span class="badge">~10 min</span></div></div>	-
38.45.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Remove a Tick Safely	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Remove a Tick Safely</h1><p class="muted">Don't panic and don't burn it. Slow and steady with fine tweezers does the job.</p><div class="card"><ol class="list"><li>Grab fine-tipped tweezers. Get as close to the skin as you can.</li><li>Pull straight up with steady, even pressure. Don't twist or jerk.</li><li>If the mouthparts stay behind, try to remove them with clean tweezers. If you can't, leave them; the skin will push them out.</li><li>Clean the bite and your hands with rubbing alcohol or soap and water.</li><li>Save the tick in a sealed bag or jar. Note the date. If you feel unwell in the next month, show it to a doctor.</li></ol></div><div class="callout bad"><p>Don't use petroleum jelly, nail polish, or a match. Those old tricks make the tick regurgitate into you.</p></div><div class="row between"><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>5 steps</span><span class="badge">~5 min</span></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Remove a Tick Safely</h1><p class="muted">Don't panic and don't burn it. Slow and steady with fine tweezers does the job.</p><div class="card"><ol class="list"><li>Grab fine-tipped tweezers. Get as close to the skin as you can.</li><li>Pull straight up with steady, even pressure. Don't twist or jerk.</li><li>If the mouthparts stay behind, try to remove them with clean tweezers. If you can't, leave them; the skin will push them out.</li><li>Clean the bite and your hands with rubbing alcohol or soap and water.</li><li>Save the tick in a sealed bag or jar. Note the date. If you feel unwell in the next month, show it to a doctor.</li></ol></div><div class="callout bad"><p>Don't use petroleum jelly, nail polish, or a match. Those old tricks make the tick regurgitate into you.</p></div><div class="row between"><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>5 steps</span><span class="badge">~5 min</span></div></div>	-
38.46.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Clean a Microwave	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Clean a Microwave</h1><p class="muted">You don't need to scrub. Steam loosens everything and the smell goes with it.</p><div class="card"><ol class="list"><li>Fill a microwave-safe bowl with one cup of water and a splash of vinegar or lemon juice.</li><li>Microwave on high for 3 minutes. Let it sit for 5 more with the door closed.</li><li>Remove the bowl and wipe the inside with a damp cloth. Splatters should slide right off.</li><li>Wash the turntable and any racks by hand with soapy water.</li><li>Leave the door open for a bit to air out and dry.</li></ol></div><div class="callout good"><p>For stubborn spots, a paste of baking soda and water works as a gentle scrub. Rinse well.</p></div><div class="row between"><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>5 steps</span><span class="badge">~15 min</span></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Clean a Microwave</h1><p class="muted">You don't need to scrub. Steam loosens everything and the smell goes with it.</p><div class="card"><ol class="list"><li>Fill a microwave-safe bowl with one cup of water and a splash of vinegar or lemon juice.</li><li>Microwave on high for 3 minutes. Let it sit for 5 more with the door closed.</li><li>Remove the bowl and wipe the inside with a damp cloth. Splatters should slide right off.</li><li>Wash the turntable and any racks by hand with soapy water.</li><li>Leave the door open for a bit to air out and dry.</li></ol></div><div class="callout good"><p>For stubborn spots, a paste of baking soda and water works as a gentle scrub. Rinse well.</p></div><div class="row between"><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>5 steps</span><span class="badge">~15 min</span></div></div>	-
38.47.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Store Fresh Herbs	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Store Fresh Herbs</h1><p class="muted">Treat them like flowers or salad, depending on the stem. Two rules, and they'll last a week or more.</p><div class="card"><ol class="list"><li>Wash and dry them well. A salad spinner or a towel works. Wet herbs rot.</li><li>For soft herbs like basil, parsley, and cilantro: trim the stems and stand them in a glass of water. Cover loosely with a plastic bag and refrigerate. Basil prefers the counter.</li><li>For woody herbs like rosemary, thyme, and sage: wrap them in a barely damp paper towel, then in a plastic bag. Store in the crisper.</li><li>Check every few days. Pick out any slimy leaves so they don't spread.</li><li>When they start to fade, chop and freeze them in olive oil in an ice cube tray.</li></ol></div><div class="callout info"><p>Don't store herbs near the back of the fridge. They freeze and turn to mush.</p></div><div class="row between"><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>5 steps</span><span class="badge">~10 min</span></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Store Fresh Herbs</h1><p class="muted">Treat them like flowers or salad, depending on the stem. Two rules, and they'll last a week or more.</p><div class="card"><ol class="list"><li>Wash and dry them well. A salad spinner or a towel works. Wet herbs rot.</li><li>For soft herbs like basil, parsley, and cilantro: trim the stems and stand them in a glass of water. Cover loosely with a plastic bag and refrigerate. Basil prefers the counter.</li><li>For woody herbs like rosemary, thyme, and sage: wrap them in a barely damp paper towel, then in a plastic bag. Store in the crisper.</li><li>Check every few days. Pick out any slimy leaves so they don't spread.</li><li>When they start to fade, chop and freeze them in olive oil in an ice cube tray.</li></ol></div><div class="callout info"><p>Don't store herbs near the back of the fridge. They freeze and turn to mush.</p></div><div class="row between"><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>5 steps</span><span class="badge">~10 min</span></div></div>	-
38.48.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Clean a Shower Curtain	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Clean a Shower Curtain</h1><p class="muted">That pink film is mildew. It comes off with a wash and a little prevention.</p><div class="card"><ol class="list"><li>Take the curtain down and remove any rings or hooks.</li><li>For plastic liners: machine wash on gentle with a couple of towels and a half cup of baking soda. Or hand wash in the tub with warm water and vinegar.</li><li>For fabric curtains: follow the care label. Usually a gentle cycle with mild detergent works.</li><li>Hang it back up to dry. The shower rod makes a perfect drying line.</li><li>To prevent mildew, spread the curtain out after every shower and run the fan.</li></ol></div><div class="callout warn"><p>Skip the bleach unless the label says it's safe. It can yellow plastic and weaken fabric.</p></div><div class="row between"><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>5 steps</span><span class="badge">~30 min</span></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Clean a Shower Curtain</h1><p class="muted">That pink film is mildew. It comes off with a wash and a little prevention.</p><div class="card"><ol class="list"><li>Take the curtain down and remove any rings or hooks.</li><li>For plastic liners: machine wash on gentle with a couple of towels and a half cup of baking soda. Or hand wash in the tub with warm water and vinegar.</li><li>For fabric curtains: follow the care label. Usually a gentle cycle with mild detergent works.</li><li>Hang it back up to dry. The shower rod makes a perfect drying line.</li><li>To prevent mildew, spread the curtain out after every shower and run the fan.</li></ol></div><div class="callout warn"><p>Skip the bleach unless the label says it's safe. It can yellow plastic and weaken fabric.</p></div><div class="row between"><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>5 steps</span><span class="badge">~30 min</span></div></div>	-
38.49.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Make a Bed Like a Hotel	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Make a Bed Like a Hotel</h1><p class="muted">Takes about 3 minutes once you get the rhythm. No starch required, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Strip and shake.</strong> Pull everything off. Give the fitted sheet a good snap to uncurl the elastic.</li><li><strong>Fitted sheet.</strong> Start at one corner, then the diagonal one. Lift the mattress edge with your knee to tuck.</li><li><strong>Flat sheet.</strong> Right side down. Align the top edge with the head of the bed, then tuck the foot under the mattress.</li><li><strong>Hospital corners.</strong> At the foot, lift the side hem to form a 45° triangle. Tuck the hanging flap under, then let the triangle fall and tuck it too.</li><li><strong>Duvet or blanket.</strong> Lay it over, leaving the top sheet folded back about 12 inches. Smooth from center outward.</li><li><strong>Pillows.</strong> Fluff, karate-chop the middle, and prop them against the headboard.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Why hospital corners?</summary><div class="body"><p>They keep the sheet from pulling loose overnight. Once you learn the fold, you can do it half-asleep. I promise.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Make a Bed Like a Hotel</h1><p class="muted">Takes about 3 minutes once you get the rhythm. No starch required, .</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Strip and shake.</strong> Pull everything off. Give the fitted sheet a good snap to uncurl the elastic.</li><li><strong>Fitted sheet.</strong> Start at one corner, then the diagonal one. Lift the mattress edge with your knee to tuck.</li><li><strong>Flat sheet.</strong> Right side down. Align the top edge with the head of the bed, then tuck the foot under the mattress.</li><li><strong>Hospital corners.</strong> At the foot, lift the side hem to form a 45° triangle. Tuck the hanging flap under, then let the triangle fall and tuck it too.</li><li><strong>Duvet or blanket.</strong> Lay it over, leaving the top sheet folded back about 12 inches. Smooth from center outward.</li><li><strong>Pillows.</strong> Fluff, karate-chop the middle, and prop them against the headboard.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Why hospital corners?</summary><div class="body"><p>They keep the sheet from pulling loose overnight. Once you learn the fold, you can do it half-asleep. I promise.</p></div></details></div>	-
38.50.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Make a Paper Airplane That Flies Far	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Make a Paper Airplane That Flies Far</h1><p class="muted">All you need is a sheet of A4 or letter paper. Fold on a hard, flat surface.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Crease the center.</strong> Fold the paper in half lengthwise, then unfold. This is your guide line.</li><li><strong>Fold the top corners.</strong> Bring each top corner down to meet the center crease. You now have a triangle at the top.</li><li><strong>Fold the triangle down.</strong> Take the point of that triangle and fold it down so the tip touches the bottom edge of the triangle.</li><li><strong>Fold the top edges again.</strong> Bring the new top corners to the center line, leaving a small triangle peeking at the bottom.</li><li><strong>Fold in half.</strong> Along the original center crease, fold the plane in half so the folds are on the outside.</li><li><strong>Make the wings.</strong> Fold each wing down so its edge aligns with the bottom of the fuselage. Crease sharply.</li><li><strong>Adjust and throw.</strong> Open the wings so they're flat. Add a tiny upward curve to the back edges. Hold the bottom, aim slightly up, and throw with a smooth flick.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Tip for distance</summary><div class="body"><p>Throw at about 45 degrees, not straight ahead. The curve on the wings creates lift.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Make a Paper Airplane That Flies Far</h1><p class="muted">All you need is a sheet of A4 or letter paper. Fold on a hard, flat surface.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Crease the center.</strong> Fold the paper in half lengthwise, then unfold. This is your guide line.</li><li><strong>Fold the top corners.</strong> Bring each top corner down to meet the center crease. You now have a triangle at the top.</li><li><strong>Fold the triangle down.</strong> Take the point of that triangle and fold it down so the tip touches the bottom edge of the triangle.</li><li><strong>Fold the top edges again.</strong> Bring the new top corners to the center line, leaving a small triangle peeking at the bottom.</li><li><strong>Fold in half.</strong> Along the original center crease, fold the plane in half so the folds are on the outside.</li><li><strong>Make the wings.</strong> Fold each wing down so its edge aligns with the bottom of the fuselage. Crease sharply.</li><li><strong>Adjust and throw.</strong> Open the wings so they're flat. Add a tiny upward curve to the back edges. Hold the bottom, aim slightly up, and throw with a smooth flick.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Tip for distance</summary><div class="body"><p>Throw at about 45 degrees, not straight ahead. The curve on the wings creates lift.</p></div></details></div>	-
38.51.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Fix a Zipper That Won't Stay Up	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Fix a Zipper That Won't Stay Up</h1><p class="muted">Before you toss the jacket, try this. It's usually a five-minute fix.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Inspect the slider.</strong> Look at the metal or plastic piece that moves. If it's loose, that's the problem.</li><li><strong>Clean the zipper.</strong> Use a damp cloth to wipe the teeth. Grit can make it slip.</li><li><strong>Lubricate.</strong> Rub a graphite pencil along both sides of the zipper teeth. Or use a little lip balm or candle wax. Work the zipper up and down.</li><li><strong>Tighten the slider.</strong> Use pliers to gently squeeze the top and bottom of the slider (not the sides). Go slow—a little pressure goes a long way.</li><li><strong>Test.</strong> Zip it up and see if it holds. If it still slips, squeeze a tiny bit more.</li><li><strong>Last resort.</strong> If the slider is cracked or the teeth are missing, replace the slider or take it to a tailor.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Why graphite?</summary><div class="body"><p>It's a dry lubricant. No stains, no grease, and it's probably already in your pencil cup.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Fix a Zipper That Won't Stay Up</h1><p class="muted">Before you toss the jacket, try this. It's usually a five-minute fix.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Inspect the slider.</strong> Look at the metal or plastic piece that moves. If it's loose, that's the problem.</li><li><strong>Clean the zipper.</strong> Use a damp cloth to wipe the teeth. Grit can make it slip.</li><li><strong>Lubricate.</strong> Rub a graphite pencil along both sides of the zipper teeth. Or use a little lip balm or candle wax. Work the zipper up and down.</li><li><strong>Tighten the slider.</strong> Use pliers to gently squeeze the top and bottom of the slider (not the sides). Go slow—a little pressure goes a long way.</li><li><strong>Test.</strong> Zip it up and see if it holds. If it still slips, squeeze a tiny bit more.</li><li><strong>Last resort.</strong> If the slider is cracked or the teeth are missing, replace the slider or take it to a tailor.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Why graphite?</summary><div class="body"><p>It's a dry lubricant. No stains, no grease, and it's probably already in your pencil cup.</p></div></details></div>	-
38.52.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Change a Car Battery	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Change a Car Battery</h1><p class="muted">Ten minutes, one wrench, zero drama. <regex>user_name</regex>, here's the order.</p><div class="card"><div class="card-title">Steps</div><ol class="list"><li>Park flat, ignition off, keys in your pocket.</li><li>Pop the hood. Find the battery — usually a black brick with two posts, red cover on one.</li><li>Loosen the negative terminal (black, marked –) with a 10&nbsp;mm wrench. Negative first, always.</li><li>Loosen the positive terminal (red, +). Tuck both cables aside so they don't spring back.</li><li>Unbolt the hold-down clamp at the base of the battery.</li><li>Lift the battery straight up. Old ones are heavier than they look.</li><li>Set the new battery in, clamp it down, then reconnect positive first, negative last.</li><li>Tighten, wiggle-test both posts, and start the car.</li></ol></div><details class="acc"><summary>Why negative first?</summary><div class="body"><p>If your wrench touches metal while you're on the positive post, you get a spark. Negative-first means the wrench has nothing to arc to. Boring is safe.</p></div></details><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Battery terminals crusty? Scrub with a wire brush and a splash of baking soda water before reconnecting.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Change a Car Battery</h1><p class="muted">Ten minutes, one wrench, zero drama. , here's the order.</p><div class="card"><div class="card-title">Steps</div><ol class="list"><li>Park flat, ignition off, keys in your pocket.</li><li>Pop the hood. Find the battery — usually a black brick with two posts, red cover on one.</li><li>Loosen the negative terminal (black, marked –) with a 10&nbsp;mm wrench. Negative first, always.</li><li>Loosen the positive terminal (red, +). Tuck both cables aside so they don't spring back.</li><li>Unbolt the hold-down clamp at the base of the battery.</li><li>Lift the battery straight up. Old ones are heavier than they look.</li><li>Set the new battery in, clamp it down, then reconnect positive first, negative last.</li><li>Tighten, wiggle-test both posts, and start the car.</li></ol></div><details class="acc"><summary>Why negative first?</summary><div class="body"><p>If your wrench touches metal while you're on the positive post, you get a spark. Negative-first means the wrench has nothing to arc to. Boring is safe.</p></div></details><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Battery terminals crusty? Scrub with a wire brush and a splash of baking soda water before reconnecting.</p></div></div>	-
38.53.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Hang a Picture Perfectly Level	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Hang a Picture Perfectly Level</h1><p class="muted"><regex>user_name</regex>, the secret is doing the work on the back of the frame, not the wall.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><div class="card-title">Steps</div><ol class="list"><li>Decide the height. Rule of thumb: center of the art at 57&nbsp;inches from the floor.</li><li>Flip the frame over. Measure the distance from the top edge down to the hanging wire or D-ring.</li><li>Add the drop to your wall height. Mark that spot with a pencil.</li><li>Hold a level against the wall mark to confirm it's flat.</li><li>Hammer the hook or nail at the mark — into a stud if the frame is heavy.</li><li>Hang the frame, then nudge side to side until the top edge reads level.</li><li>Stick a tiny felt pad on the bottom corners so it doesn't drift.</li></ol></div><div class="card flat"><div class="card-title">Tools</div><ul class="list"><li>tape measure</li><li>level (phone app is fine)</li><li>pencil</li><li>hammer</li><li>hooks rated for the weight</li></ul></div></div><div class="callout good"><p class="t-small">Stud finder saves you a patch job later. Hold a magnet along the wall — it'll grab the drywall screws.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Hang a Picture Perfectly Level</h1><p class="muted">, the secret is doing the work on the back of the frame, not the wall.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><div class="card-title">Steps</div><ol class="list"><li>Decide the height. Rule of thumb: center of the art at 57&nbsp;inches from the floor.</li><li>Flip the frame over. Measure the distance from the top edge down to the hanging wire or D-ring.</li><li>Add the drop to your wall height. Mark that spot with a pencil.</li><li>Hold a level against the wall mark to confirm it's flat.</li><li>Hammer the hook or nail at the mark — into a stud if the frame is heavy.</li><li>Hang the frame, then nudge side to side until the top edge reads level.</li><li>Stick a tiny felt pad on the bottom corners so it doesn't drift.</li></ol></div><div class="card flat"><div class="card-title">Tools</div><ul class="list"><li>tape measure</li><li>level (phone app is fine)</li><li>pencil</li><li>hammer</li><li>hooks rated for the weight</li></ul></div></div><div class="callout good"><p class="t-small">Stud finder saves you a patch job later. Hold a magnet along the wall — it'll grab the drywall screws.</p></div></div>	-
38.54.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Deep Clean Your Fridge	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Deep Clean Your Fridge</h1><p class="muted"><regex>user_name</regex>, this is a 30-minute job once you commit. Start by unplugging if you can reach the cord.</p><div class="card"><div class="card-title">Steps</div><ol class="list"><li>Pull everything out. Toss old leftovers, wilted greens, mystery jars.</li><li>Take out shelves and drawers. Let them warm to room temp — cold glass cracks under hot water.</li><li>Wash shelves in the sink with warm soapy water. Rinse, dry, set aside.</li><li>Wipe the interior top to bottom with a cloth and a mix of equal parts white vinegar and water.</li><li>Scrub the door gaskets with an old toothbrush — that's where the smell lives.</li><li>Dry everything, then slide shelves back in.</li><li>Put a small open box of baking soda on a shelf to absorb future odors.</li></ol></div><details class="acc"><summary>Sticky spill that won't budge</summary><div class="body"><p>Lay a warm wet cloth on it for five minutes, then wipe. Heat does the work, not elbow grease.</p></div></details><div class="callout warn"><p class="t-small">Never use bleach inside a fridge. The fumes stick to food.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Deep Clean Your Fridge</h1><p class="muted">, this is a 30-minute job once you commit. Start by unplugging if you can reach the cord.</p><div class="card"><div class="card-title">Steps</div><ol class="list"><li>Pull everything out. Toss old leftovers, wilted greens, mystery jars.</li><li>Take out shelves and drawers. Let them warm to room temp — cold glass cracks under hot water.</li><li>Wash shelves in the sink with warm soapy water. Rinse, dry, set aside.</li><li>Wipe the interior top to bottom with a cloth and a mix of equal parts white vinegar and water.</li><li>Scrub the door gaskets with an old toothbrush — that's where the smell lives.</li><li>Dry everything, then slide shelves back in.</li><li>Put a small open box of baking soda on a shelf to absorb future odors.</li></ol></div><details class="acc"><summary>Sticky spill that won't budge</summary><div class="body"><p>Lay a warm wet cloth on it for five minutes, then wipe. Heat does the work, not elbow grease.</p></div></details><div class="callout warn"><p class="t-small">Never use bleach inside a fridge. The fumes stick to food.</p></div></div>	-
38.55.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Write a Cover Letter	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Write a Cover Letter</h1><p class="muted">Short and specific beats long and flowery. <regex>user_name</regex>, here's a four-paragraph shape.</p><div class="card"><div class="card-title">Steps</div><ol class="list"><li>Address a real person if you can find one. "Dear hiring team" is the backup.</li><li>Open with one line on the role, and one line on why you're a fit. No throat-clearing.</li><li>Paragraph two: one concrete story that proves a skill. Numbers help.</li><li>Paragraph three: why this company, not just any company. Be specific.</li><li>Close with a short thank-you and your contact info.</li></ol></div><div class="card flat"><div class="card-title">Length</div><p class="t-small">One page. Three or four paragraphs. Recruiters skim.</p></div><details class="tab"><summary>Sample opener</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">"I'm applying for the data analyst role. Last year I cut a reporting cycle from five days to two at my current job, and I'd like to do similar work with your team."</p></div></details><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Mirror the job posting's words. If they say "stakeholder management," use that exact phrase somewhere.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Write a Cover Letter</h1><p class="muted">Short and specific beats long and flowery. , here's a four-paragraph shape.</p><div class="card"><div class="card-title">Steps</div><ol class="list"><li>Address a real person if you can find one. "Dear hiring team" is the backup.</li><li>Open with one line on the role, and one line on why you're a fit. No throat-clearing.</li><li>Paragraph two: one concrete story that proves a skill. Numbers help.</li><li>Paragraph three: why this company, not just any company. Be specific.</li><li>Close with a short thank-you and your contact info.</li></ol></div><div class="card flat"><div class="card-title">Length</div><p class="t-small">One page. Three or four paragraphs. Recruiters skim.</p></div><details class="tab"><summary>Sample opener</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">"I'm applying for the data analyst role. Last year I cut a reporting cycle from five days to two at my current job, and I'd like to do similar work with your team."</p></div></details><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Mirror the job posting's words. If they say "stakeholder management," use that exact phrase somewhere.</p></div></div>	-
38.56.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Fix a Squeaky Door Hinge	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Fix a Squeaky Door Hinge</h1><p class="muted"><regex>user_name</regex>, ninety seconds and one dab of grease. You don't have to take the door off.</p><div class="card"><div class="card-title">Steps</div><ol class="list"><li>Open the door so you can see the hinge pins — the vertical rod in the middle of each hinge.</li><li>Wipe dust off the hinge with a dry cloth.</li><li>Dab a little white lithium grease or petroleum jelly on the tip of the pin.</li><li>Open and close the door several times so the grease works its way in.</li><li>If it still squeaks, tap the pin up from below with a hammer and a nail set, then grease again.</li><li>Wipe off any drips so the door doesn't stain.</li></ol></div><div class="callout warn"><p class="t-small">Skip WD-40 for this one — it's a solvent, not a lubricant. It'll quiet the hinge for a week, then it comes back louder.</p></div><details class="acc"><summary>What if the whole hinge is loose?</summary><div class="body"><p>Tighten the screws with a screwdriver. If a screw spins freely, the hole is stripped — push a wooden matchstick or golf tee into the hole, snap it flush, then drive the screw back in.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Fix a Squeaky Door Hinge</h1><p class="muted">, ninety seconds and one dab of grease. You don't have to take the door off.</p><div class="card"><div class="card-title">Steps</div><ol class="list"><li>Open the door so you can see the hinge pins — the vertical rod in the middle of each hinge.</li><li>Wipe dust off the hinge with a dry cloth.</li><li>Dab a little white lithium grease or petroleum jelly on the tip of the pin.</li><li>Open and close the door several times so the grease works its way in.</li><li>If it still squeaks, tap the pin up from below with a hammer and a nail set, then grease again.</li><li>Wipe off any drips so the door doesn't stain.</li></ol></div><div class="callout warn"><p class="t-small">Skip WD-40 for this one — it's a solvent, not a lubricant. It'll quiet the hinge for a week, then it comes back louder.</p></div><details class="acc"><summary>What if the whole hinge is loose?</summary><div class="body"><p>Tighten the screws with a screwdriver. If a screw spins freely, the hole is stripped — push a wooden matchstick or golf tee into the hole, snap it flush, then drive the screw back in.</p></div></details></div>	-
38.57.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Make Whipped Cream From Scratch	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Make Whipped Cream From Scratch</h1><p class="muted"><regex>user_name</regex>, cold cream, cold bowl, and a little patience. Three ingredients.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><div class="card-title">Steps</div><ol class="list"><li>Chill a metal bowl and your beaters in the freezer for ten minutes.</li><li>Pour in one cup of cold heavy cream.</li><li>Add one tablespoon of sugar and a splash of vanilla.</li><li>Beat on medium until it thickens — about three to four minutes with a mixer.</li><li>Watch closely: soft peaks flop over, stiff peaks stand up. Stop at stiff.</li><li>Use right away, or cover and refrigerate for up to a day.</li></ol></div><div class="card flat"><div class="card-title">Ratios</div><ul class="list"><li>1 cup cream</li><li>1 tbsp sugar</li><li>1/2 tsp vanilla</li></ul></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>I went too far and now it's butter</summary><div class="body"><p>Keep going. Add a pinch of salt, squeeze out the buttermilk, and you've accidentally made butter. Put it on toast and call it a win.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Make Whipped Cream From Scratch</h1><p class="muted">, cold cream, cold bowl, and a little patience. Three ingredients.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><div class="card-title">Steps</div><ol class="list"><li>Chill a metal bowl and your beaters in the freezer for ten minutes.</li><li>Pour in one cup of cold heavy cream.</li><li>Add one tablespoon of sugar and a splash of vanilla.</li><li>Beat on medium until it thickens — about three to four minutes with a mixer.</li><li>Watch closely: soft peaks flop over, stiff peaks stand up. Stop at stiff.</li><li>Use right away, or cover and refrigerate for up to a day.</li></ol></div><div class="card flat"><div class="card-title">Ratios</div><ul class="list"><li>1 cup cream</li><li>1 tbsp sugar</li><li>1/2 tsp vanilla</li></ul></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>I went too far and now it's butter</summary><div class="body"><p>Keep going. Add a pinch of salt, squeeze out the buttermilk, and you've accidentally made butter. Put it on toast and call it a win.</p></div></details></div>	-
38.58.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Store Avocados So They Stay Fresh	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Store Avocados So They Stay Fresh</h1><p class="muted">A ripe avocado waits for no one. Here's how to bend the clock, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="card"><div class="card-title">The plan</div><ol class="list"><li><strong>Pick the right stage.</strong> Firm avocados go in the fridge; ripe ones get eaten or prepped. Cold slows ripening, it doesn't stop it.</li><li><strong>Whole and ripe?</strong> Fridge, in the crisper drawer. They'll hold for 2–3 days.</li><li><strong>Half an avocado?</strong> Leave the pit in. Rub the exposed flesh with lemon or lime juice.</li><li><strong>Wrap it tight.</strong> Press plastic wrap directly onto the surface, then pop it in an airtight container. Air is the enemy.</li><li><strong>Add a buddy.</strong> Store the half with a chunk of onion in the container. Sounds odd, works well.</li><li><strong>Frozen for the long haul.</strong> Mash with lime juice, freeze in a bag, flatten, and label. Good for smoothies and guac later.</li></ol></div><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Brown spots aren't spoiled, just oxidized. Scrape them off and carry on. The rest is still good.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Store Avocados So They Stay Fresh</h1><p class="muted">A ripe avocado waits for no one. Here's how to bend the clock, .</p><div class="card"><div class="card-title">The plan</div><ol class="list"><li><strong>Pick the right stage.</strong> Firm avocados go in the fridge; ripe ones get eaten or prepped. Cold slows ripening, it doesn't stop it.</li><li><strong>Whole and ripe?</strong> Fridge, in the crisper drawer. They'll hold for 2–3 days.</li><li><strong>Half an avocado?</strong> Leave the pit in. Rub the exposed flesh with lemon or lime juice.</li><li><strong>Wrap it tight.</strong> Press plastic wrap directly onto the surface, then pop it in an airtight container. Air is the enemy.</li><li><strong>Add a buddy.</strong> Store the half with a chunk of onion in the container. Sounds odd, works well.</li><li><strong>Frozen for the long haul.</strong> Mash with lime juice, freeze in a bag, flatten, and label. Good for smoothies and guac later.</li></ol></div><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Brown spots aren't spoiled, just oxidized. Scrape them off and carry on. The rest is still good.</p></div></div>	-
38.59.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Remove Hard Water Stains from Glass	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Remove Hard Water Stains from Glass</h1><p class="muted">That cloudy film is just minerals throwing a tantrum. Time to show them the door, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="card"><div class="card-title">The method</div><ol class="list"><li><strong>Start dry.</strong> Wipe the glass with a dry cloth to remove loose dust. Skip this and you'll just smear grit around.</li><li><strong>Mix the paste.</strong> In a bowl, combine equal parts white vinegar and baking soda. It'll fizz like a science fair volcano—that's fine.</li><li><strong>Apply generously.</strong> Spread the paste over the stained areas with a sponge or your hands. Let it sit for 15–20 minutes.</li><li><strong>Scrub gently.</strong> Use a non-scratch sponge or a soft brush. No steel wool, ever. Glass scratches and then it's over.</li><li><strong>Rinse and check.</strong> Rinse with warm water. If stains remain, repeat once more.</li><li><strong>Dry and protect.</strong> Squeegee or dry with a microfiber cloth. For prevention, apply a light coat of car wax or a commercial water repellent.</li></ol></div><div class="callout warn"><p class="t-small">Never mix vinegar and bleach. Ever. That's a one-way ticket to a very bad afternoon.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Remove Hard Water Stains from Glass</h1><p class="muted">That cloudy film is just minerals throwing a tantrum. Time to show them the door, .</p><div class="card"><div class="card-title">The method</div><ol class="list"><li><strong>Start dry.</strong> Wipe the glass with a dry cloth to remove loose dust. Skip this and you'll just smear grit around.</li><li><strong>Mix the paste.</strong> In a bowl, combine equal parts white vinegar and baking soda. It'll fizz like a science fair volcano—that's fine.</li><li><strong>Apply generously.</strong> Spread the paste over the stained areas with a sponge or your hands. Let it sit for 15–20 minutes.</li><li><strong>Scrub gently.</strong> Use a non-scratch sponge or a soft brush. No steel wool, ever. Glass scratches and then it's over.</li><li><strong>Rinse and check.</strong> Rinse with warm water. If stains remain, repeat once more.</li><li><strong>Dry and protect.</strong> Squeegee or dry with a microfiber cloth. For prevention, apply a light coat of car wax or a commercial water repellent.</li></ol></div><div class="callout warn"><p class="t-small">Never mix vinegar and bleach. Ever. That's a one-way ticket to a very bad afternoon.</p></div></div>	-
38.60.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Make Pasta by Hand (No Machine)	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Make Pasta by Hand (No Machine)</h1><p class="muted">No machine, no problem. Your hands and a rolling pin are enough, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="card"><div class="card-title">The steps</div><ol class="list"><li><strong>Mound the flour.</strong> On a clean counter, pile 2 cups of all-purpose flour into a mound. Make a well in the center.</li><li><strong>Add the eggs.</strong> Crack 3 large eggs into the well. Add a pinch of salt and a drizzle of olive oil.</li><li><strong>Whisk gently.</strong> Use a fork to beat the eggs, gradually pulling in flour from the inner walls. Keep the well intact until the eggs are mixed.</li><li><strong>Bring it together.</strong> Once the mixture thickens, use your hands to knead everything into a shaggy ball.</li><li><strong>Knead the dough.</strong> Knead for 8–10 minutes until smooth and elastic. If sticky, add flour a tablespoon at a time.</li><li><strong>Rest.</strong> Wrap in plastic and rest at room temperature for 30 minutes. This relaxes the gluten and makes rolling easier.</li><li><strong>Roll and cut.</strong> Divide into four pieces. Roll each as thin as possible (almost see-through). Dust with flour, roll up loosely, and cut into strips. Unfurl and toss with a little flour.</li></ol></div><div class="callout good"><p class="t-small">Cook fresh pasta for just 2–3 minutes in salted boiling water. It's fast.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Make Pasta by Hand (No Machine)</h1><p class="muted">No machine, no problem. Your hands and a rolling pin are enough, .</p><div class="card"><div class="card-title">The steps</div><ol class="list"><li><strong>Mound the flour.</strong> On a clean counter, pile 2 cups of all-purpose flour into a mound. Make a well in the center.</li><li><strong>Add the eggs.</strong> Crack 3 large eggs into the well. Add a pinch of salt and a drizzle of olive oil.</li><li><strong>Whisk gently.</strong> Use a fork to beat the eggs, gradually pulling in flour from the inner walls. Keep the well intact until the eggs are mixed.</li><li><strong>Bring it together.</strong> Once the mixture thickens, use your hands to knead everything into a shaggy ball.</li><li><strong>Knead the dough.</strong> Knead for 8–10 minutes until smooth and elastic. If sticky, add flour a tablespoon at a time.</li><li><strong>Rest.</strong> Wrap in plastic and rest at room temperature for 30 minutes. This relaxes the gluten and makes rolling easier.</li><li><strong>Roll and cut.</strong> Divide into four pieces. Roll each as thin as possible (almost see-through). Dust with flour, roll up loosely, and cut into strips. Unfurl and toss with a little flour.</li></ol></div><div class="callout good"><p class="t-small">Cook fresh pasta for just 2–3 minutes in salted boiling water. It's fast.</p></div></div>	-
38.61.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Get Rid of Ants in the Kitchen	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Get Rid of Ants in the Kitchen</h1><p class="muted">Ants are just tiny uninvited guests. Here's how to show them the exit, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="card"><div class="card-title">The strategy</div><ol class="list"><li><strong>Find the trail.</strong> Follow the line of ants to locate their entry point. They leave a pheromone trail, so they'll keep coming back to the same spot.</li><li><strong>Clean thoroughly.</strong> Wipe down counters, floors, and cabinets with soapy water or a vinegar solution. This erases the scent trail.</li><li><strong>Seal entry points.</strong> Use caulk, petroleum jelly, or even a line of cinnamon to block cracks and crevices. Ants hate the smell.</li><li><strong>Set bait.</strong> Place commercial ant baits or a homemade mix of borax and sugar water near the trail. They'll take it back to the nest.</li><li><strong>Be patient.</strong> Don't kill the ants you see feeding on the bait. They need to carry it back. Give it a few days.</li><li><strong>Remove food sources.</strong> Keep counters crumb-free, store food in sealed containers, and take out the trash regularly.</li></ol></div><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">If you have pets or kids, use borax baits in closed containers or opt for a professional.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Get Rid of Ants in the Kitchen</h1><p class="muted">Ants are just tiny uninvited guests. Here's how to show them the exit, .</p><div class="card"><div class="card-title">The strategy</div><ol class="list"><li><strong>Find the trail.</strong> Follow the line of ants to locate their entry point. They leave a pheromone trail, so they'll keep coming back to the same spot.</li><li><strong>Clean thoroughly.</strong> Wipe down counters, floors, and cabinets with soapy water or a vinegar solution. This erases the scent trail.</li><li><strong>Seal entry points.</strong> Use caulk, petroleum jelly, or even a line of cinnamon to block cracks and crevices. Ants hate the smell.</li><li><strong>Set bait.</strong> Place commercial ant baits or a homemade mix of borax and sugar water near the trail. They'll take it back to the nest.</li><li><strong>Be patient.</strong> Don't kill the ants you see feeding on the bait. They need to carry it back. Give it a few days.</li><li><strong>Remove food sources.</strong> Keep counters crumb-free, store food in sealed containers, and take out the trash regularly.</li></ol></div><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">If you have pets or kids, use borax baits in closed containers or opt for a professional.</p></div></div>	-
38.62.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Clean a Fish Tank Without Stressing Fish	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Clean a Fish Tank Without Stressing Fish</h1><p class="muted">A clean tank is a happy tank. Here's how to do it gently, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="card"><div class="card-title">The routine</div><ol class="list"><li><strong>Unplug equipment.</strong> Turn off the filter, heater, and lights. Give the heater a few minutes to cool.</li><li><strong>Prep your water.</strong> Fill a bucket with tap water and add a water conditioner. Let it sit to reach room temperature.</li><li><strong>Scrub the glass.</strong> Use an algae pad or sponge to wipe the inside walls. Avoid soap—it's toxic to fish.</li><li><strong>Vacuum the gravel.</strong> Use a siphon to remove about 20–25% of the water while vacuuming debris from the substrate.</li><li><strong>Clean decorations.</strong> Rinse rocks and plants in the old tank water. Never use tap water directly; it kills beneficial bacteria.</li><li><strong>Refill.</strong> Slowly pour the conditioned water back in. Don't blast the fish.</li><li><strong>Restart.</strong> Plug everything back in and check the temperature. Wait 15 minutes before feeding.</li></ol></div><div class="callout warn"><p class="t-small">Never do a 100% water change. That crashes the ecosystem and stresses fish.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Clean a Fish Tank Without Stressing Fish</h1><p class="muted">A clean tank is a happy tank. Here's how to do it gently, .</p><div class="card"><div class="card-title">The routine</div><ol class="list"><li><strong>Unplug equipment.</strong> Turn off the filter, heater, and lights. Give the heater a few minutes to cool.</li><li><strong>Prep your water.</strong> Fill a bucket with tap water and add a water conditioner. Let it sit to reach room temperature.</li><li><strong>Scrub the glass.</strong> Use an algae pad or sponge to wipe the inside walls. Avoid soap—it's toxic to fish.</li><li><strong>Vacuum the gravel.</strong> Use a siphon to remove about 20–25% of the water while vacuuming debris from the substrate.</li><li><strong>Clean decorations.</strong> Rinse rocks and plants in the old tank water. Never use tap water directly; it kills beneficial bacteria.</li><li><strong>Refill.</strong> Slowly pour the conditioned water back in. Don't blast the fish.</li><li><strong>Restart.</strong> Plug everything back in and check the temperature. Wait 15 minutes before feeding.</li></ol></div><div class="callout warn"><p class="t-small">Never do a 100% water change. That crashes the ecosystem and stresses fish.</p></div></div>	-
38.63.0	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Remove Permanent Marker from a Whiteboard	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Remove Permanent Marker from a Whiteboard</h1><p class="muted">Permanent marker on a whiteboard: a classic oops. Here's the fix, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="card"><div class="card-title">The rescue</div><ol class="list"><li><strong>Try the dry-erase trick.</strong> Scribble over the permanent marks with a fresh dry-erase marker. Let it sit for a few seconds, then wipe. The solvents in the dry-erase ink break down the permanent ink.</li><li><strong>Rubbing alcohol.</strong> If that fails, dab some 70% isopropyl alcohol on a cloth and rub. It dissolves the ink.</li><li><strong>Hand sanitizer.</strong> In a pinch, hand sanitizer works. Apply, wait a moment, then wipe.</li><li><strong>Hairspray.</strong> Spray on, let it sit for 10 seconds, and wipe off. The alcohol in hairspray does the job.</li><li><strong>WD-40.</strong> Spray a small amount on a rag, rub gently, then clean off the residue with soap and water.</li><li><strong>Magic eraser.</strong> A melamine sponge can work, but test a small area first—it can dull the surface.</li></ol></div><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Always test on a corner first. Some whiteboards have delicate coatings.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Remove Permanent Marker from a Whiteboard</h1><p class="muted">Permanent marker on a whiteboard: a classic oops. Here's the fix, .</p><div class="card"><div class="card-title">The rescue</div><ol class="list"><li><strong>Try the dry-erase trick.</strong> Scribble over the permanent marks with a fresh dry-erase marker. Let it sit for a few seconds, then wipe. The solvents in the dry-erase ink break down the permanent ink.</li><li><strong>Rubbing alcohol.</strong> If that fails, dab some 70% isopropyl alcohol on a cloth and rub. It dissolves the ink.</li><li><strong>Hand sanitizer.</strong> In a pinch, hand sanitizer works. Apply, wait a moment, then wipe.</li><li><strong>Hairspray.</strong> Spray on, let it sit for 10 seconds, and wipe off. The alcohol in hairspray does the job.</li><li><strong>WD-40.</strong> Spray a small amount on a rag, rub gently, then clean off the residue with soap and water.</li><li><strong>Magic eraser.</strong> A melamine sponge can work, but test a small area first—it can dull the surface.</li></ol></div><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Always test on a corner first. Some whiteboards have delicate coatings.</p></div></div>	-
38.0.1	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Defrost a Freezer Fast	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Defrost a Freezer Fast</h1>  <p class="muted">Hey <regex>user_name</regex> — ice steals space and power. Here's the least-miserable path.</p>  <div class="callout info"><strong>Prep:</strong> pull everything into coolers with ice packs. A full freezer holds cold for hours if you don't open it.</div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Unplug it. Empty every shelf and drawer, including the ice bin.</li>    <li>Lay towels and shallow pans along the bottom and front edge.</li>    <li>Set a pot of hot (not boiling) water on a shelf, close the door 10 min.</li>    <li>Swap the water, repeat. Aim a fan at the open door to speed melt.</li>    <li>Never chip ice with a knife — one nick in a coolant line kills the unit.</li>    <li>Wipe walls with a baking-soda solution (1 tbsp per quart), dry fully.</li>    <li>Plug in, let it hit temp for an hour, then reload oldest food first.</li>  </ol>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Timing &amp; troubleshooting</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li><strong>Thin frost:</strong> 30–45 min. <strong>Heavy:</strong> 2–3 hrs.</li>        <li>Door gasket leaking? Close on a dollar bill — if it slides free, replace it.</li>        <li>Drain line clogged on a frost-free model? Clear the tube behind the kick plate.</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Defrost a Freezer Fast</h1>  <p class="muted">Hey  — ice steals space and power. Here's the least-miserable path.</p>  <div class="callout info"><strong>Prep:</strong> pull everything into coolers with ice packs. A full freezer holds cold for hours if you don't open it.</div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Unplug it. Empty every shelf and drawer, including the ice bin.</li>    <li>Lay towels and shallow pans along the bottom and front edge.</li>    <li>Set a pot of hot (not boiling) water on a shelf, close the door 10 min.</li>    <li>Swap the water, repeat. Aim a fan at the open door to speed melt.</li>    <li>Never chip ice with a knife — one nick in a coolant line kills the unit.</li>    <li>Wipe walls with a baking-soda solution (1 tbsp per quart), dry fully.</li>    <li>Plug in, let it hit temp for an hour, then reload oldest food first.</li>  </ol>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Timing &amp; troubleshooting</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li><strong>Thin frost:</strong> 30–45 min. <strong>Heavy:</strong> 2–3 hrs.</li>        <li>Door gasket leaking? Close on a dollar bill — if it slides free, replace it.</li>        <li>Drain line clogged on a frost-free model? Clear the tube behind the kick plate.</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details></div>	-
38.1.1	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Remove a Red Wine Stain	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Remove a Red Wine Stain</h1>  <p class="muted"><regex>user_name</regex>, speed beats scrubbing. Blot, don't rub — rubbing pushes pigment deeper.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat"><span class="label">Act within</span><span class="value">5<span class="unit"> min</span></span><span class="delta down">for best odds</span></div>    <div class="stat"><span class="label">You need</span><span class="value">Salt<span class="unit"> + cold water</span></span></div>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Blot up liquid with a clean cloth, pressing straight down.</li>    <li>Pour salt over the wet patch to pull moisture out. Let it sit 5 min, vacuum.</li>    <li>Mix 1 tbsp dish soap + 1 tbsp white vinegar + 2 cups cold water.</li>    <li>Dab the solution on with a sponge, working from the edge inward.</li>    <li>Rinse with cold water, blot dry, repeat until the cloth lifts clean.</li>    <li>On fabric still damp: cover with baking soda paste overnight, then wash cold.</li>  </ol>  <div class="legend"><i></i> cold water only <i class="alt"></i> never hot — it sets the dye</div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>If it's already dried</summary>    <div class="body"><p>Sponge with 3% hydrogen peroxide, wait 5 min, flush with cold water. Test on a hidden spot first — peroxide can lighten some colors.</p></div>  </details></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Remove a Red Wine Stain</h1>  <p class="muted">, speed beats scrubbing. Blot, don't rub — rubbing pushes pigment deeper.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat"><span class="label">Act within</span><span class="value">5<span class="unit"> min</span></span><span class="delta down">for best odds</span></div>    <div class="stat"><span class="label">You need</span><span class="value">Salt<span class="unit"> + cold water</span></span></div>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Blot up liquid with a clean cloth, pressing straight down.</li>    <li>Pour salt over the wet patch to pull moisture out. Let it sit 5 min, vacuum.</li>    <li>Mix 1 tbsp dish soap + 1 tbsp white vinegar + 2 cups cold water.</li>    <li>Dab the solution on with a sponge, working from the edge inward.</li>    <li>Rinse with cold water, blot dry, repeat until the cloth lifts clean.</li>    <li>On fabric still damp: cover with baking soda paste overnight, then wash cold.</li>  </ol>  <div class="legend"><i></i> cold water only <i class="alt"></i> never hot — it sets the dye</div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>If it's already dried</summary>    <div class="body"><p>Sponge with 3% hydrogen peroxide, wait 5 min, flush with cold water. Test on a hidden spot first — peroxide can lighten some colors.</p></div>  </details></div>	-
38.2.1	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Make Great Guacamole	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Make Great Guacamole</h1>  <p class="muted">Hi <regex>user_name</regex> — good guac is texture, not paste. Salt early, mash lightly.</p>  <div class="recipe">    <div class="meta">~10 min · serves 4 · no blender</div>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li><strong>3 ripe avocados.</strong> Halve, pit, scoop into a bowl. Ripe = gives slightly to a gentle press.</li>        <li>Add <strong>1/2 tsp kosher salt</strong> and mash with a fork, leaving lumps.</li>        <li>Fold in <strong>1/4 cup diced white onion</strong>, <strong>1 small diced jalapeño</strong> (seeds out), <strong>2 tbsp cilantro</strong>.</li>        <li>Squeeze in <strong>juice of 1/2 lime</strong>. Taste, adjust salt and lime together.</li>        <li>Add <strong>1 small diced tomato</strong> last, so it doesn't water things down.</li>      </ol>    </div>  </div>  <div class="callout good"><strong>Keep it green:</strong> press plastic wrap directly onto the surface. The enemy is air, not time.</div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Too spicy? Too bland?</summary>    <div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Hot: stir in more avocado or a spoon of sour cream.</li><li>Flat: a pinch more salt, then a squeeze of lime. Salt first, always.</li></ul></div>  </details></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Make Great Guacamole</h1>  <p class="muted">Hi  — good guac is texture, not paste. Salt early, mash lightly.</p>  <div class="recipe">    <div class="meta">~10 min · serves 4 · no blender</div>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li><strong>3 ripe avocados.</strong> Halve, pit, scoop into a bowl. Ripe = gives slightly to a gentle press.</li>        <li>Add <strong>1/2 tsp kosher salt</strong> and mash with a fork, leaving lumps.</li>        <li>Fold in <strong>1/4 cup diced white onion</strong>, <strong>1 small diced jalapeño</strong> (seeds out), <strong>2 tbsp cilantro</strong>.</li>        <li>Squeeze in <strong>juice of 1/2 lime</strong>. Taste, adjust salt and lime together.</li>        <li>Add <strong>1 small diced tomato</strong> last, so it doesn't water things down.</li>      </ol>    </div>  </div>  <div class="callout good"><strong>Keep it green:</strong> press plastic wrap directly onto the surface. The enemy is air, not time.</div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Too spicy? Too bland?</summary>    <div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Hot: stir in more avocado or a spoon of sour cream.</li><li>Flat: a pinch more salt, then a squeeze of lime. Salt first, always.</li></ul></div>  </details></div>	-
38.3.1	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Build a Fire in a Fireplace	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Build a Fire in a Fireplace</h1>  <p class="muted"><regex>user_name</regex>, dry wood, dry air, cold air in. That's the whole trick.</p>  <div class="callout bad"><strong>First:</strong> open the damper. A closed flue turns a cozy evening into a house full of smoke.</div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Clear ash to about an inch — a little insulates, too much smothers.</li>    <li>Put two split logs parallel, a hand-width apart, flat side down.</li>    <li>Lay a loose bed of crumpled paper or newspaper between them.</li>    <li>Build a teepee of kindling over the paper, leaning inward.</li>    <li>Lay one more split log on top, bridging the two — the log cabin.</li>    <li>Light the paper at several spots. Leave the screen cracked until flames catch.</li>    <li>Add larger logs only once you have steady flame, one at a time, keeping air gaps.</li>  </ol>  <div class="legend"><i></i> tinder <i class="alt"></i> kindling <i class="alt"></i> fuel logs</div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Smoke coming into the room?</summary>    <div class="body"><p>Check the damper, open a nearby window an inch for make-up air, and warm the flue with a rolled newspaper torch for 20 seconds before lighting.</p></div>  </details></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Build a Fire in a Fireplace</h1>  <p class="muted">, dry wood, dry air, cold air in. That's the whole trick.</p>  <div class="callout bad"><strong>First:</strong> open the damper. A closed flue turns a cozy evening into a house full of smoke.</div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Clear ash to about an inch — a little insulates, too much smothers.</li>    <li>Put two split logs parallel, a hand-width apart, flat side down.</li>    <li>Lay a loose bed of crumpled paper or newspaper between them.</li>    <li>Build a teepee of kindling over the paper, leaning inward.</li>    <li>Lay one more split log on top, bridging the two — the log cabin.</li>    <li>Light the paper at several spots. Leave the screen cracked until flames catch.</li>    <li>Add larger logs only once you have steady flame, one at a time, keeping air gaps.</li>  </ol>  <div class="legend"><i></i> tinder <i class="alt"></i> kindling <i class="alt"></i> fuel logs</div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Smoke coming into the room?</summary>    <div class="body"><p>Check the damper, open a nearby window an inch for make-up air, and warm the flue with a rolled newspaper torch for 20 seconds before lighting.</p></div>  </details></div>	-
38.4.1	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Apply for a Passport	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Apply for a Passport</h1>  <p class="muted"><regex>user_name</regex>, the hard part is the photo and the waiting. Everything else is paper.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li>Check your eligibility type: first-time, renewal, or child — each has its own form.</li>    <li>Gather proof of citizenship (birth certificate or old passport) and a photo ID copy.</li>    <li>Get a <strong>2x2 inch</strong> photo: plain background, no glasses, neutral face.</li>    <li>Fill the form in black ink or online. Sign it — an unsigned form is the #1 rejection.</li>    <li>First-time or child? Apply in person at an acceptance facility. Renewals can go by mail.</li>    <li>Pay by check or money order; fees and expedite options change, so verify before mailing.</li>    <li>Mail certified, keep tracking, and note the ~6–8 week routine turnaround.</li>  </ol>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat"><span class="label">Photo size</span><span class="value">2<span class="unit"> x 2 in</span></span></div>    <div class="stat"><span class="label">Routine wait</span><span class="value">6–8<span class="unit"> weeks</span></span></div>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Common photo flunks</summary>    <div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Shadows behind your head.</li><li>Slight smile or squint — keep it neutral.</li><li>Glare on glasses. Take them off.</li><li>Photo older than 6 months.</li></ul></div>  </details></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Apply for a Passport</h1>  <p class="muted">, the hard part is the photo and the waiting. Everything else is paper.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li>Check your eligibility type: first-time, renewal, or child — each has its own form.</li>    <li>Gather proof of citizenship (birth certificate or old passport) and a photo ID copy.</li>    <li>Get a <strong>2x2 inch</strong> photo: plain background, no glasses, neutral face.</li>    <li>Fill the form in black ink or online. Sign it — an unsigned form is the #1 rejection.</li>    <li>First-time or child? Apply in person at an acceptance facility. Renewals can go by mail.</li>    <li>Pay by check or money order; fees and expedite options change, so verify before mailing.</li>    <li>Mail certified, keep tracking, and note the ~6–8 week routine turnaround.</li>  </ol>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat"><span class="label">Photo size</span><span class="value">2<span class="unit"> x 2 in</span></span></div>    <div class="stat"><span class="label">Routine wait</span><span class="value">6–8<span class="unit"> weeks</span></span></div>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Common photo flunks</summary>    <div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Shadows behind your head.</li><li>Slight smile or squint — keep it neutral.</li><li>Glare on glasses. Take them off.</li><li>Photo older than 6 months.</li></ul></div>  </details></div>	-
38.5.1	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Make a Great Cup of Tea	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Make a Great Cup of Tea</h1><p class="muted">A good cup is mostly about water temperature and not walking away. Here's the flow.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Boil fresh water.</strong> Fill the kettle with cold tap water, not reboiled. Reboiled water tastes flat.</li><li><strong>Warm the cup.</strong> Pour a splash of hot water in, swirl, dump. Keeps your tea hot longer.</li><li><strong>Measure the leaves.</strong> One teaspoon loose leaf or one bag per 8 oz cup. For strong tea, add a little more leaf, not more time.</li><li><strong>Pour at the right temp.</strong> Black and herbal: just off boil. Green: 170–180°F. White: 160–170°F. No thermometer? Let boiled water sit 2–3 minutes for green.</li><li><strong>Steep.</strong> Black 3–5 min, green 2–3 min, white 4–5 min, herbal 5–7 min. Set a timer—oversteeped tea turns bitter fast.</li><li><strong>Remove the leaves.</strong> Pull the bag or strain. Don't let it sit in the cup.</li><li><strong>Add milk or lemon.</strong> Milk goes in after pouring for black tea; lemon with green or black, but not both.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>If it tastes weak, next time use more leaf, not longer steeping. Bitterness comes from time, strength from quantity.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Make a Great Cup of Tea</h1><p class="muted">A good cup is mostly about water temperature and not walking away. Here's the flow.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Boil fresh water.</strong> Fill the kettle with cold tap water, not reboiled. Reboiled water tastes flat.</li><li><strong>Warm the cup.</strong> Pour a splash of hot water in, swirl, dump. Keeps your tea hot longer.</li><li><strong>Measure the leaves.</strong> One teaspoon loose leaf or one bag per 8 oz cup. For strong tea, add a little more leaf, not more time.</li><li><strong>Pour at the right temp.</strong> Black and herbal: just off boil. Green: 170–180°F. White: 160–170°F. No thermometer? Let boiled water sit 2–3 minutes for green.</li><li><strong>Steep.</strong> Black 3–5 min, green 2–3 min, white 4–5 min, herbal 5–7 min. Set a timer—oversteeped tea turns bitter fast.</li><li><strong>Remove the leaves.</strong> Pull the bag or strain. Don't let it sit in the cup.</li><li><strong>Add milk or lemon.</strong> Milk goes in after pouring for black tea; lemon with green or black, but not both.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>If it tastes weak, next time use more leaf, not longer steeping. Bitterness comes from time, strength from quantity.</p></div></div>	-
38.6.1	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Sew on a Button	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Sew on a Button</h1><p class="muted">A five-minute fix that saves a shirt. No machine needed.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Thread the needle.</strong> Cut 18 inches of thread. Knot one end. Double it if you want extra strength.</li><li><strong>Mark the spot.</strong> If the button fell off, the old holes are still visible. Push the needle up through one hole on the fabric.</li><li><strong>Add a spacer.</strong> Place a toothpick or matchstick across the button's top. Sew over it—this creates a shank so the button can button easily.</li><li><strong>Sew through the holes.</strong> Push needle down through the opposite hole, then up through a third, and so on. For a four-hole button, sew an X or two parallel lines.</li><li><strong>Repeat six to eight times.</strong> Keep tension firm but not tight enough to wrinkle the fabric.</li><li><strong>Remove the spacer.</strong> Pull it out, then lift the button and wrap thread around the stitches underneath a few times to form a shank.</li><li><strong>Knot and trim.</strong> Push needle to the back, make a knot, and trim close.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>If the button is decorative and never buttons, skip the spacer and sew flat.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Sew on a Button</h1><p class="muted">A five-minute fix that saves a shirt. No machine needed.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Thread the needle.</strong> Cut 18 inches of thread. Knot one end. Double it if you want extra strength.</li><li><strong>Mark the spot.</strong> If the button fell off, the old holes are still visible. Push the needle up through one hole on the fabric.</li><li><strong>Add a spacer.</strong> Place a toothpick or matchstick across the button's top. Sew over it—this creates a shank so the button can button easily.</li><li><strong>Sew through the holes.</strong> Push needle down through the opposite hole, then up through a third, and so on. For a four-hole button, sew an X or two parallel lines.</li><li><strong>Repeat six to eight times.</strong> Keep tension firm but not tight enough to wrinkle the fabric.</li><li><strong>Remove the spacer.</strong> Pull it out, then lift the button and wrap thread around the stitches underneath a few times to form a shank.</li><li><strong>Knot and trim.</strong> Push needle to the back, make a knot, and trim close.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>If the button is decorative and never buttons, skip the spacer and sew flat.</p></div></div>	-
38.7.1	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Parallel Park	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Parallel Park</h1><p class="muted">It's geometry, not magic. Here's the sequence.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Find a spot.</strong> You need about 1.5 times your car's length. Signal and pull alongside the front car, about two feet away, rear bumpers aligned.</li><li><strong>Check the mirror.</strong> Look for traffic. When clear, shift to reverse.</li><li><strong>Crank the wheel right.</strong> Turn the wheel all the way right while backing up slowly. Your car should angle toward the curb at about 45 degrees.</li><li><strong>Straighten when you see the curb.</strong> Once your car is at 45 degrees, straighten the wheel and keep backing until your front bumper clears the car ahead.</li><li><strong>Crank left.</strong> Turn the wheel all the way left and back in. Your car should swing into the spot.</li><li><strong>Fine-tune.</strong> Straighten out, center yourself, and adjust forward or back if needed.</li><li><strong>Park.</strong> Set the brake, turn off the engine, and check your spacing before opening the door.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>If you're too far from the curb, pull forward and try again. Small adjustments are normal.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Parallel Park</h1><p class="muted">It's geometry, not magic. Here's the sequence.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Find a spot.</strong> You need about 1.5 times your car's length. Signal and pull alongside the front car, about two feet away, rear bumpers aligned.</li><li><strong>Check the mirror.</strong> Look for traffic. When clear, shift to reverse.</li><li><strong>Crank the wheel right.</strong> Turn the wheel all the way right while backing up slowly. Your car should angle toward the curb at about 45 degrees.</li><li><strong>Straighten when you see the curb.</strong> Once your car is at 45 degrees, straighten the wheel and keep backing until your front bumper clears the car ahead.</li><li><strong>Crank left.</strong> Turn the wheel all the way left and back in. Your car should swing into the spot.</li><li><strong>Fine-tune.</strong> Straighten out, center yourself, and adjust forward or back if needed.</li><li><strong>Park.</strong> Set the brake, turn off the engine, and check your spacing before opening the door.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>If you're too far from the curb, pull forward and try again. Small adjustments are normal.</p></div></div>	-
38.8.1	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Poach an Egg	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Poach an Egg</h1><p class="muted">The trick is fresh eggs and a gentle swirl. No special pan required.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Use the freshest eggs you have.</strong> Older whites spread. If you only have old eggs, add a splash of vinegar to the water.</li><li><strong>Heat water to a bare simmer.</strong> About 180°F. Small bubbles on the bottom, no rolling boil.</li><li><strong>Add a splash of vinegar.</strong> One tablespoon white vinegar helps the white coagulate. It won't affect taste.</li><li><strong>Crack the egg into a small bowl.</strong> This lets you slide it in gently.</li><li><strong>Create a gentle whirlpool.</strong> Stir the water with a spoon, then slide the egg into the center. The swirl wraps the white around the yolk.</li><li><strong>Poach 3–4 minutes.</strong> The white should be set, the yolk still runny. Lift with a slotted spoon.</li><li><strong>Drain.</strong> Rest the spoon on a paper towel for a few seconds before serving.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p>If the egg sticks to the bottom, your water was too hot. Lower the heat and try again.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Poach an Egg</h1><p class="muted">The trick is fresh eggs and a gentle swirl. No special pan required.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Use the freshest eggs you have.</strong> Older whites spread. If you only have old eggs, add a splash of vinegar to the water.</li><li><strong>Heat water to a bare simmer.</strong> About 180°F. Small bubbles on the bottom, no rolling boil.</li><li><strong>Add a splash of vinegar.</strong> One tablespoon white vinegar helps the white coagulate. It won't affect taste.</li><li><strong>Crack the egg into a small bowl.</strong> This lets you slide it in gently.</li><li><strong>Create a gentle whirlpool.</strong> Stir the water with a spoon, then slide the egg into the center. The swirl wraps the white around the yolk.</li><li><strong>Poach 3–4 minutes.</strong> The white should be set, the yolk still runny. Lift with a slotted spoon.</li><li><strong>Drain.</strong> Rest the spoon on a paper towel for a few seconds before serving.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p>If the egg sticks to the bottom, your water was too hot. Lower the heat and try again.</p></div></div>	-
38.9.1	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Remove Sticker Residue	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Remove Sticker Residue</h1><p class="muted">That gummy patch comes off with things you already own.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Start with heat.</strong> A hair dryer on low, held a few inches away, softens the adhesive. Peel slowly.</li><li><strong>Try oil.</strong> Dab a little cooking oil, baby oil, or peanut butter on the residue. Let it sit 5–10 minutes. Oil breaks down most sticky stuff.</li><li><strong>Rub with a cloth.</strong> Use a soft cloth or your thumb. Don't scrape with a knife—you'll scratch glass or plastic.</li><li><strong>Try rubbing alcohol.</strong> For hard surfaces, 70% isopropyl alcohol on a cotton ball works. Test on plastic first.</li><li><strong>Use vinegar for glass.</strong> White vinegar on a rag cuts through residue without streaks.</li><li><strong>Wipe clean.</strong> Wash with soap and water to remove any oily film.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p>Don't use acetone or nail polish remover on plastic—it can melt the surface.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Remove Sticker Residue</h1><p class="muted">That gummy patch comes off with things you already own.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Start with heat.</strong> A hair dryer on low, held a few inches away, softens the adhesive. Peel slowly.</li><li><strong>Try oil.</strong> Dab a little cooking oil, baby oil, or peanut butter on the residue. Let it sit 5–10 minutes. Oil breaks down most sticky stuff.</li><li><strong>Rub with a cloth.</strong> Use a soft cloth or your thumb. Don't scrape with a knife—you'll scratch glass or plastic.</li><li><strong>Try rubbing alcohol.</strong> For hard surfaces, 70% isopropyl alcohol on a cotton ball works. Test on plastic first.</li><li><strong>Use vinegar for glass.</strong> White vinegar on a rag cuts through residue without streaks.</li><li><strong>Wipe clean.</strong> Wash with soap and water to remove any oily film.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p>Don't use acetone or nail polish remover on plastic—it can melt the surface.</p></div></div>	-
38.10.1	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Pack a Carry-On for a Week	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Pack a Carry-On for a Week</h1><p class="muted">One bag, seven days, zero checked-luggage fees. Here's the trick.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Pick your bag.</strong> A 40L carry-on or a 35L backpack—whatever fits your airline's overhead bin. Weigh it empty so you know your budget.</li><li><strong>Plan outfits around a color.</strong> Choose two neutrals (black, navy, grey) and one accent. Everything mixes with everything else, so you need fewer pieces.</li><li><strong>Use the bundle or rolling method.</strong> Roll soft knits; bundle wrinkle-prone shirts and dresses around a core. Packing cubes help, but aren't required.</li><li><strong>Limit shoes to three pairs.</strong> One worn on the plane (bulkiest), one walking, one dressier. Stuff socks inside them to save space.</li><li><strong>Pack toiletries in a clear quart bag.</strong> Decant into 100ml bottles. Solid shampoo bars and deodorant don't count against liquid limits.</li><li><strong>Layer electronics and chargers in one pouch.</strong> Keep it at the top of your bag so you can pull it out at security without unpacking everything.</li><li><strong>Do a dry run.</strong> Carry your packed bag around the block. If it's heavy or awkward, remove one outfit—you won't miss it.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Wear your heaviest jacket and bulkiest shoes on travel day. They're free baggage.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Pack a Carry-On for a Week</h1><p class="muted">One bag, seven days, zero checked-luggage fees. Here's the trick.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Pick your bag.</strong> A 40L carry-on or a 35L backpack—whatever fits your airline's overhead bin. Weigh it empty so you know your budget.</li><li><strong>Plan outfits around a color.</strong> Choose two neutrals (black, navy, grey) and one accent. Everything mixes with everything else, so you need fewer pieces.</li><li><strong>Use the bundle or rolling method.</strong> Roll soft knits; bundle wrinkle-prone shirts and dresses around a core. Packing cubes help, but aren't required.</li><li><strong>Limit shoes to three pairs.</strong> One worn on the plane (bulkiest), one walking, one dressier. Stuff socks inside them to save space.</li><li><strong>Pack toiletries in a clear quart bag.</strong> Decant into 100ml bottles. Solid shampoo bars and deodorant don't count against liquid limits.</li><li><strong>Layer electronics and chargers in one pouch.</strong> Keep it at the top of your bag so you can pull it out at security without unpacking everything.</li><li><strong>Do a dry run.</strong> Carry your packed bag around the block. If it's heavy or awkward, remove one outfit—you won't miss it.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Wear your heaviest jacket and bulkiest shoes on travel day. They're free baggage.</div></div>	-
38.11.1	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Kill a Squeaky Door Hinge	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Kill a Squeaky Door Hinge</h1><p class="muted">That chirp isn't haunted—it's just metal rubbing on metal. A five-minute fix.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Find the source.</strong> Open and close the door slowly. Listen for the squeak—usually the top hinge or the hinge the door swings on.</li><li><strong>Clean the hinge.</strong> Wipe away dust and old gunk with a rag and a little rubbing alcohol or a dab of dish soap. Let it dry.</li><li><strong>Lubricate.</strong> Add a drop of white lithium grease (best), silicone spray, or plain old petroleum jelly to the hinge pin. Avoid WD-40 for long-term—it evaporates.</li><li><strong>Work it in.</strong> Open and close the door a half-dozen times to spread the lube. Wipe off any drips.</li><li><strong>Still squeaky?</strong> Pull the hinge pin up with a screwdriver or nail. Wipe it clean, grease it, and tap it back in gently with a hammer (protect the door with a cloth).</li><li><strong>Check the other hinges.</strong> If one is dry, the others probably are too. Give them all a drop.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><strong>Silence.</strong> If the squeak persists, the hinge might be worn. A replacement hinge costs a couple of dollars and is easy to swap.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Kill a Squeaky Door Hinge</h1><p class="muted">That chirp isn't haunted—it's just metal rubbing on metal. A five-minute fix.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Find the source.</strong> Open and close the door slowly. Listen for the squeak—usually the top hinge or the hinge the door swings on.</li><li><strong>Clean the hinge.</strong> Wipe away dust and old gunk with a rag and a little rubbing alcohol or a dab of dish soap. Let it dry.</li><li><strong>Lubricate.</strong> Add a drop of white lithium grease (best), silicone spray, or plain old petroleum jelly to the hinge pin. Avoid WD-40 for long-term—it evaporates.</li><li><strong>Work it in.</strong> Open and close the door a half-dozen times to spread the lube. Wipe off any drips.</li><li><strong>Still squeaky?</strong> Pull the hinge pin up with a screwdriver or nail. Wipe it clean, grease it, and tap it back in gently with a hammer (protect the door with a cloth).</li><li><strong>Check the other hinges.</strong> If one is dry, the others probably are too. Give them all a drop.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><strong>Silence.</strong> If the squeak persists, the hinge might be worn. A replacement hinge costs a couple of dollars and is easy to swap.</div></div>	-
38.12.1	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Get Wax Out of a Candle Jar	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Get Wax Out of a Candle Jar</h1><p class="muted">Turn a spent candle into a clean container—or rescue a candle that's burned unevenly.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Freeze it.</strong> Pop the jar in the freezer for an hour. Wax shrinks and often pops right out.</li><li><strong>If it doesn't budge,</strong> use a butter knife to gently pry around the edge. Never use a sharp knife—one slip and you'll scratch the glass.</li><li><strong>For stubborn wax,</strong> place the jar in a bowl of hot (not boiling) water. Let it sit until the wax softens, then scrape with a plastic spatula.</li><li><strong>Remove residue</strong> with a paper towel dipped in a little rubbing alcohol or white vinegar. Wipe until clean.</li><li><strong>Wash with warm soapy water</strong> and a soft sponge. If you want to reuse it for food, make sure all wax and fragrance oil is gone.</li><li><strong>Dry upside down</strong> on a dish rack to avoid water spots.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><strong>Reuse idea:</strong> A clean candle jar makes a great holder for cotton swabs, makeup brushes, or pens.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Get Wax Out of a Candle Jar</h1><p class="muted">Turn a spent candle into a clean container—or rescue a candle that's burned unevenly.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Freeze it.</strong> Pop the jar in the freezer for an hour. Wax shrinks and often pops right out.</li><li><strong>If it doesn't budge,</strong> use a butter knife to gently pry around the edge. Never use a sharp knife—one slip and you'll scratch the glass.</li><li><strong>For stubborn wax,</strong> place the jar in a bowl of hot (not boiling) water. Let it sit until the wax softens, then scrape with a plastic spatula.</li><li><strong>Remove residue</strong> with a paper towel dipped in a little rubbing alcohol or white vinegar. Wipe until clean.</li><li><strong>Wash with warm soapy water</strong> and a soft sponge. If you want to reuse it for food, make sure all wax and fragrance oil is gone.</li><li><strong>Dry upside down</strong> on a dish rack to avoid water spots.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><strong>Reuse idea:</strong> A clean candle jar makes a great holder for cotton swabs, makeup brushes, or pens.</div></div>	-
38.13.1	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Make Crispy Sweet Potato Fries	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Make Crispy Sweet Potato Fries</h1><p class="muted">The secret is a cornstarch dust and a hot oven. No deep fryer required.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Preheat oven to 425°F (220°C).</strong> Line a baking sheet with parchment paper—no oil needed on the sheet.</li><li><strong>Cut sweet potatoes into ¼-inch sticks.</strong> Try to keep them uniform so they cook evenly.</li><li><strong>Soak in cold water for 30 minutes.</strong> This removes excess starch, which is the enemy of crispiness. Drain and pat completely dry.</li><li><strong>Toss with cornstarch.</strong> In a bowl, sprinkle 1 tablespoon of cornstarch over the dry fries and toss until lightly coated. Add 1 tablespoon of oil, salt, and any spices (paprika, garlic powder).</li><li><strong>Arrange in a single layer.</strong> Leave space between fries—crowding steams them. Use two sheets if needed.</li><li><strong>Bake 15 minutes, flip, then 10–15 more.</strong> Watch for edges to brown. They'll crisp up more as they cool.</li><li><strong>Salt immediately</strong> when they come out, then serve hot. They lose crunch as they sit.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><strong>Don't skip the soak.</strong> It's the difference between soggy and shatteringly crisp.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Make Crispy Sweet Potato Fries</h1><p class="muted">The secret is a cornstarch dust and a hot oven. No deep fryer required.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Preheat oven to 425°F (220°C).</strong> Line a baking sheet with parchment paper—no oil needed on the sheet.</li><li><strong>Cut sweet potatoes into ¼-inch sticks.</strong> Try to keep them uniform so they cook evenly.</li><li><strong>Soak in cold water for 30 minutes.</strong> This removes excess starch, which is the enemy of crispiness. Drain and pat completely dry.</li><li><strong>Toss with cornstarch.</strong> In a bowl, sprinkle 1 tablespoon of cornstarch over the dry fries and toss until lightly coated. Add 1 tablespoon of oil, salt, and any spices (paprika, garlic powder).</li><li><strong>Arrange in a single layer.</strong> Leave space between fries—crowding steams them. Use two sheets if needed.</li><li><strong>Bake 15 minutes, flip, then 10–15 more.</strong> Watch for edges to brown. They'll crisp up more as they cool.</li><li><strong>Salt immediately</strong> when they come out, then serve hot. They lose crunch as they sit.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><strong>Don't skip the soak.</strong> It's the difference between soggy and shatteringly crisp.</div></div>	-
38.14.1	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Calculate a Tip Without a Phone	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Calculate a Tip Without a Phone</h1><p class="muted">Mental math that won't leave you staring at the bill like it's a riddle.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Find 10%.</strong> Move the decimal point one place left. On a $48 bill, 10% is $4.80.</li><li><strong>Double it for 20%.</strong> $4.80 × 2 = $9.60. That's a solid tip for good service.</li><li><strong>For 15%,</strong> take the 10% ($4.80) and add half of it ($2.40) to get $7.20.</li><li><strong>For 25%,</strong> add half of the 10% to the doubled amount: $9.60 + $2.40 = $12.00.</li><li><strong>Round to a friendly number.</strong> $9.60 becomes $10. Easy to add in your head.</li><li><strong>Check for auto-gratuity.</strong> Parties of 6+ often include 18–20% already. Don't tip twice.</li><li><strong>Split the bill? Do the math on the total,</strong> not per person, then divide. That way the tip percentage stays accurate.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> 15% for okay, 20% for good, 25% for excellent. Adjust for local custom—in the US, 20% is the new baseline.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Calculate a Tip Without a Phone</h1><p class="muted">Mental math that won't leave you staring at the bill like it's a riddle.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Find 10%.</strong> Move the decimal point one place left. On a $48 bill, 10% is $4.80.</li><li><strong>Double it for 20%.</strong> $4.80 × 2 = $9.60. That's a solid tip for good service.</li><li><strong>For 15%,</strong> take the 10% ($4.80) and add half of it ($2.40) to get $7.20.</li><li><strong>For 25%,</strong> add half of the 10% to the doubled amount: $9.60 + $2.40 = $12.00.</li><li><strong>Round to a friendly number.</strong> $9.60 becomes $10. Easy to add in your head.</li><li><strong>Check for auto-gratuity.</strong> Parties of 6+ often include 18–20% already. Don't tip twice.</li><li><strong>Split the bill? Do the math on the total,</strong> not per person, then divide. That way the tip percentage stays accurate.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> 15% for okay, 20% for good, 25% for excellent. Adjust for local custom—in the US, 20% is the new baseline.</div></div>	-
38.15.1	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Clean Your Phone Screen Properly	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Clean Your Phone Screen Properly</h1><p class="muted">Your phone is gross. Here's how to fix that without wrecking the coating.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Power down and unplug.</strong> A dark screen shows smudges better, and it's safer for the device.</li><li><strong>Remove the case.</strong> Grit hides in the crevices. You'll want to clean that too.</li><li><strong>Wipe with a microfiber cloth.</strong> Dry first—this removes dust and fingerprints without scratching. Don't use paper towels or tissues; wood fibers can scratch.</li><li><strong>For stubborn smudges,</strong> dampen (not soak) a corner of the cloth with distilled water or a 70% isopropyl alcohol wipe. Never spray directly on the screen.</li><li><strong>Gently wipe the screen.</strong> Don't scrub. Alcohol can wear down oleophobic coatings over time, so use it sparingly.</li><li><strong>Clean the case</strong> with soap and water if it's plastic or silicone; use a disinfecting wipe for leather. Let it dry completely before putting it back on.</li><li><strong>Use a dry cloth to buff away streaks.</strong> Hold it up to the light to check for missed spots.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><strong>Avoid:</strong> window cleaner, vinegar, bleach, and compressed air. They can damage the screen or push debris into openings.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Clean Your Phone Screen Properly</h1><p class="muted">Your phone is gross. Here's how to fix that without wrecking the coating.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Power down and unplug.</strong> A dark screen shows smudges better, and it's safer for the device.</li><li><strong>Remove the case.</strong> Grit hides in the crevices. You'll want to clean that too.</li><li><strong>Wipe with a microfiber cloth.</strong> Dry first—this removes dust and fingerprints without scratching. Don't use paper towels or tissues; wood fibers can scratch.</li><li><strong>For stubborn smudges,</strong> dampen (not soak) a corner of the cloth with distilled water or a 70% isopropyl alcohol wipe. Never spray directly on the screen.</li><li><strong>Gently wipe the screen.</strong> Don't scrub. Alcohol can wear down oleophobic coatings over time, so use it sparingly.</li><li><strong>Clean the case</strong> with soap and water if it's plastic or silicone; use a disinfecting wipe for leather. Let it dry completely before putting it back on.</li><li><strong>Use a dry cloth to buff away streaks.</strong> Hold it up to the light to check for missed spots.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><strong>Avoid:</strong> window cleaner, vinegar, bleach, and compressed air. They can damage the screen or push debris into openings.</div></div>	-
38.16.1	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Unclog a Drain With Baking Soda and Vinegar	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Unclog a Drain, Gently</h1><p class="muted">For slow sinks and light clogs. A full-standing drain needs a snake.</p><div class="row between"><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</span><span class="badge info">10 min</span></div><div class="meter"><span style="width:88%"></span></div><ol class="step-list"><li class="step"><div><strong>Clear the strainer</strong><p class="dim">Pull hair and gunk out first. The potion can't reach past a mat of debris.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Pour in half a cup of baking soda</strong><p class="dim">Dry, straight down the drain. No water yet.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Follow with a cup of white vinegar</strong><p class="dim">Stand back. It foams and fizzes like a tiny science fair.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Wait 10 minutes</strong><p class="dim">Cover the drain if the smell bugs you. The fizz is doing the scrubbing.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Flush with hot tap water</strong><p class="dim">A full kettle of near-boiling water works better. Not boiling, if you have PVC pipes.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Repeat once if needed</strong><p class="dim">Still slow? The clog is deeper. Time for a drain snake or a plumber.</p></div></li></ol><div class="callout warn"><span class="who">Careful:</span> Never combine with bleach or commercial drain cleaner. That mix makes toxic gas.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Unclog a Drain, Gently</h1><p class="muted">For slow sinks and light clogs. A full-standing drain needs a snake.</p><div class="row between"><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</span><span class="badge info">10 min</span></div><div class="meter"><span style="width:88%"></span></div><ol class="step-list"><li class="step"><div><strong>Clear the strainer</strong><p class="dim">Pull hair and gunk out first. The potion can't reach past a mat of debris.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Pour in half a cup of baking soda</strong><p class="dim">Dry, straight down the drain. No water yet.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Follow with a cup of white vinegar</strong><p class="dim">Stand back. It foams and fizzes like a tiny science fair.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Wait 10 minutes</strong><p class="dim">Cover the drain if the smell bugs you. The fizz is doing the scrubbing.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Flush with hot tap water</strong><p class="dim">A full kettle of near-boiling water works better. Not boiling, if you have PVC pipes.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Repeat once if needed</strong><p class="dim">Still slow? The clog is deeper. Time for a drain snake or a plumber.</p></div></li></ol><div class="callout warn"><span class="who">Careful:</span> Never combine with bleach or commercial drain cleaner. That mix makes toxic gas.</div></div>	-
38.17.1	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Make Overnight Oats You'll Actually Eat	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Overnight Oats, Done Right</h1><p class="muted">Five minutes tonight, breakfast handled all week.</p><div class="row between"><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</span><span class="badge info">5 min + overnight</span></div><div class="meter"><span style="width:92%"></span></div><ol class="step-list"><li class="step"><div><strong>Pick a jar with a lid</strong><p class="dim">A pint jar or any lidded container. Shake-friendly beats pretty.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Add 1/2 cup rolled oats</strong><p class="dim">Rolled, not instant, not steel-cut. Instant turns to paste, steel-cut stays crunchy.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Pour in 1/2 cup milk of choice</strong><p class="dim">Equal parts by volume. That's the whole secret.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Stir in 1/4 cup yogurt</strong><p class="dim">Greek or plain. This is what makes it creamy instead of sad.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Sweeten and flavor</strong><p class="dim">A spoon of honey, a pinch of salt, cinnamon, or a glob of peanut butter. Salt matters more than you think.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Lid on, fridge overnight</strong><p class="dim">At least 4 hours. They keep 4 days, so make three jars at once.</p></div></li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Topping ideas</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Berries and a squeeze of lemon</li><li>Banana, cocoa, and a pinch of espresso powder</li><li>Apple, cinnamon, walnuts</li><li>Mango and coconut flakes</li></ul></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Overnight Oats, Done Right</h1><p class="muted">Five minutes tonight, breakfast handled all week.</p><div class="row between"><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</span><span class="badge info">5 min + overnight</span></div><div class="meter"><span style="width:92%"></span></div><ol class="step-list"><li class="step"><div><strong>Pick a jar with a lid</strong><p class="dim">A pint jar or any lidded container. Shake-friendly beats pretty.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Add 1/2 cup rolled oats</strong><p class="dim">Rolled, not instant, not steel-cut. Instant turns to paste, steel-cut stays crunchy.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Pour in 1/2 cup milk of choice</strong><p class="dim">Equal parts by volume. That's the whole secret.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Stir in 1/4 cup yogurt</strong><p class="dim">Greek or plain. This is what makes it creamy instead of sad.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Sweeten and flavor</strong><p class="dim">A spoon of honey, a pinch of salt, cinnamon, or a glob of peanut butter. Salt matters more than you think.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Lid on, fridge overnight</strong><p class="dim">At least 4 hours. They keep 4 days, so make three jars at once.</p></div></li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Topping ideas</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Berries and a squeeze of lemon</li><li>Banana, cocoa, and a pinch of espresso powder</li><li>Apple, cinnamon, walnuts</li><li>Mango and coconut flakes</li></ul></div></details></div>	-
38.18.1	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Test a Smoke Detector and Swap the Battery	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Smoke Detector Tune-Up</h1><p class="muted">Ten minutes, twice a year. Chirping at 3 a.m. is optional.</p><div class="row between"><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</span><span class="badge good">Safety</span></div><div class="meter"><span style="width:95%"></span></div><ol class="step-list"><li class="step"><div><strong>Tell someone, or cover your ears</strong><p class="dim">Testing is loud. Warn the house first.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Press and hold the test button</strong><p class="dim">Hold 5 seconds until the alarm sounds. No sound? Replace the whole unit, not just the battery.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Twist the unit off its base</strong><p class="dim">A quarter turn usually. Hardwired units have a plug to unclip first.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Swap the battery</strong><p class="dim">Match the type exactly, usually 9V or AA. Never mix old and new.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Vacuum the sensor slots</strong><p class="dim">Dust and cobwebs cause false alarms. A soft brush attachment works great.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Twist it back and retest</strong><p class="dim">If it stays silent, the unit is done. Replace the detector entirely.</p></div></li></ol><div class="callout info"><span class="who">Heads up:</span> Any detector older than 10 years should be replaced, battery or not. Write the install date on the back.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Smoke Detector Tune-Up</h1><p class="muted">Ten minutes, twice a year. Chirping at 3 a.m. is optional.</p><div class="row between"><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</span><span class="badge good">Safety</span></div><div class="meter"><span style="width:95%"></span></div><ol class="step-list"><li class="step"><div><strong>Tell someone, or cover your ears</strong><p class="dim">Testing is loud. Warn the house first.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Press and hold the test button</strong><p class="dim">Hold 5 seconds until the alarm sounds. No sound? Replace the whole unit, not just the battery.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Twist the unit off its base</strong><p class="dim">A quarter turn usually. Hardwired units have a plug to unclip first.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Swap the battery</strong><p class="dim">Match the type exactly, usually 9V or AA. Never mix old and new.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Vacuum the sensor slots</strong><p class="dim">Dust and cobwebs cause false alarms. A soft brush attachment works great.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Twist it back and retest</strong><p class="dim">If it stays silent, the unit is done. Replace the detector entirely.</p></div></li></ol><div class="callout info"><span class="who">Heads up:</span> Any detector older than 10 years should be replaced, battery or not. Write the install date on the back.</div></div>	-
38.19.1	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Revive a Dried-Out Houseplant	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Revive a Thirsty Houseplant</h1><p class="muted">Crispy and drooping is fixable. Mushy and black usually isn't.</p><div class="row between"><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</span><span class="badge warn">Triage</span></div><div class="meter"><span style="width:80%"></span></div><ol class="step-list"><li class="step"><div><strong>Check the soil first</strong><p class="dim">Stick a finger two inches in. Bone dry means thirsty. Soggy and smelly means overwatered, a different problem.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Trim the truly dead bits</strong><p class="dim">Brown, brittle leaves won't come back. Snip with clean scissors so the plant stops spending on them.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Water from the bottom</strong><p class="dim">Set the pot in a bowl of water for 30 minutes. Roots drink upward and you avoid a soggy top layer.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Let it drain fully</strong><p class="dim">Lift it out, let it drip. Standing water in a saucer rots roots.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Move it to gentle light</strong><p class="dim">Bright shade, no direct sun. A recovering plant sunburns easily.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Wait a week, then look</strong><p class="dim">New growth at the tips means you won. If the stem is still firm, there's hope.</p></div></li></ol><div class="callout bad"><span class="who">Almost gone:</span> Mushy stems at the base? Cut above the rot and try propagating a healthy cutting in water.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Revive a Thirsty Houseplant</h1><p class="muted">Crispy and drooping is fixable. Mushy and black usually isn't.</p><div class="row between"><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</span><span class="badge warn">Triage</span></div><div class="meter"><span style="width:80%"></span></div><ol class="step-list"><li class="step"><div><strong>Check the soil first</strong><p class="dim">Stick a finger two inches in. Bone dry means thirsty. Soggy and smelly means overwatered, a different problem.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Trim the truly dead bits</strong><p class="dim">Brown, brittle leaves won't come back. Snip with clean scissors so the plant stops spending on them.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Water from the bottom</strong><p class="dim">Set the pot in a bowl of water for 30 minutes. Roots drink upward and you avoid a soggy top layer.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Let it drain fully</strong><p class="dim">Lift it out, let it drip. Standing water in a saucer rots roots.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Move it to gentle light</strong><p class="dim">Bright shade, no direct sun. A recovering plant sunburns easily.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Wait a week, then look</strong><p class="dim">New growth at the tips means you won. If the stem is still firm, there's hope.</p></div></li></ol><div class="callout bad"><span class="who">Almost gone:</span> Mushy stems at the base? Cut above the rot and try propagating a healthy cutting in water.</div></div>	-
38.20.1	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Write a Thank-You Note People Keep	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Thank-You Notes That Land</h1><p class="muted">Three sentences. Specific beats fancy every time.</p><div class="row between"><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</span><span class="badge info">Short &amp; warm</span></div><div class="meter"><span style="width:90%"></span></div><ol class="step-list"><li class="step"><div><strong>Open with the actual thing</strong><p class="dim">"Thank you for the cast iron skillet." Naming it proves you remembered and cared.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Say what it meant or how you'll use it</strong><p class="dim">"I've already made cornbread twice. It's a keeper." One concrete detail does the heavy lifting.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Add a line about them</strong><p class="dim">"It was good to see you at the party." Or reference the time, the talk, the laugh.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Close simply</strong><p class="dim">"Thanks again, and warm wishes." Then your name. No need for a paragraph.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Sign it by hand</strong><p class="dim">Even a typed note gets a handwritten sign-off. It reads as real.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Send it within a week</strong><p class="dim">Late is fine, just not never. The gesture still counts.</p></div></li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>A skeleton you can steal</summary><div class="body"><p class="dim">"Thank you so much for ___. I've been ___ and it means a lot. It was great to ___. Thanks again, — [Your name]"</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Thank-You Notes That Land</h1><p class="muted">Three sentences. Specific beats fancy every time.</p><div class="row between"><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</span><span class="badge info">Short &amp; warm</span></div><div class="meter"><span style="width:90%"></span></div><ol class="step-list"><li class="step"><div><strong>Open with the actual thing</strong><p class="dim">"Thank you for the cast iron skillet." Naming it proves you remembered and cared.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Say what it meant or how you'll use it</strong><p class="dim">"I've already made cornbread twice. It's a keeper." One concrete detail does the heavy lifting.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Add a line about them</strong><p class="dim">"It was good to see you at the party." Or reference the time, the talk, the laugh.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Close simply</strong><p class="dim">"Thanks again, and warm wishes." Then your name. No need for a paragraph.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Sign it by hand</strong><p class="dim">Even a typed note gets a handwritten sign-off. It reads as real.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Send it within a week</strong><p class="dim">Late is fine, just not never. The gesture still counts.</p></div></li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>A skeleton you can steal</summary><div class="body"><p class="dim">"Thank you so much for ___. I've been ___ and it means a lot. It was great to ___. Thanks again, — [Your name]"</p></div></details></div>	-
38.21.1	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Clean a Coffee Maker So It Tastes Better	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Wake Up Your Coffee Maker</h1><p class="muted">If the coffee tastes off, the machine is usually the culprit, not the beans.</p><div class="row between"><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</span><span class="badge info">30 min</span></div><div class="meter"><span style="width:86%"></span></div><ol class="step-list"><li class="step"><div><strong>Empty the reservoir and set the carafe</strong><p class="dim">Make sure the whole brew path is ready, and the carafe is where it should be.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Mix 1 part white vinegar, 1 part water</strong><p class="dim">Half and half, enough to fill the reservoir. Skip scented or cleaning vinegar.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Run a full brew cycle</strong><p class="dim">Then let it sit 15 minutes with the hot vinegar solution in the carafe. That's where the descaling happens.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Pour it back in and run again</strong><p class="dim">One more cycle loosens anything stubborn in the tubes.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Flush with clean water, twice</strong><p class="dim">Two full cycles of fresh water. Don't skip this, or breakfast tastes like salad.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Take apart and wash the rest</strong><p class="dim">Carafe, basket, and lid in warm soapy water. Wipe the outside and dry it all.</p></div></li></ol><div class="callout good"><span class="who">Cadence:</span> Quick rinse weekly, full vinegar descale every 3 months. Hard water? Every month.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Wake Up Your Coffee Maker</h1><p class="muted">If the coffee tastes off, the machine is usually the culprit, not the beans.</p><div class="row between"><span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</span><span class="badge info">30 min</span></div><div class="meter"><span style="width:86%"></span></div><ol class="step-list"><li class="step"><div><strong>Empty the reservoir and set the carafe</strong><p class="dim">Make sure the whole brew path is ready, and the carafe is where it should be.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Mix 1 part white vinegar, 1 part water</strong><p class="dim">Half and half, enough to fill the reservoir. Skip scented or cleaning vinegar.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Run a full brew cycle</strong><p class="dim">Then let it sit 15 minutes with the hot vinegar solution in the carafe. That's where the descaling happens.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Pour it back in and run again</strong><p class="dim">One more cycle loosens anything stubborn in the tubes.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Flush with clean water, twice</strong><p class="dim">Two full cycles of fresh water. Don't skip this, or breakfast tastes like salad.</p></div></li><li class="step"><div><strong>Take apart and wash the rest</strong><p class="dim">Carafe, basket, and lid in warm soapy water. Wipe the outside and dry it all.</p></div></li></ol><div class="callout good"><span class="who">Cadence:</span> Quick rinse weekly, full vinegar descale every 3 months. Hard water? Every month.</div></div>	-
38.22.1	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Replace a Toilet Paper Roll in a Charming O	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Replace a Toilet Paper Roll</h1><p class="muted">Small task, real satisfaction. Let's do it cleanly, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Notice the holder type.</strong> Spring-loaded bar? Just compress it and lift one end. Fixed spindle? Pull the spindle out sideways.</li><li><strong>Slide the empty tube off</strong> and drop it in the recycling, not the floor.</li><li><strong>Mount the new roll</strong> so paper feeds over the top. Looks neat and is genuinely easier to grab.</li><li><strong>Seat the spring bar</strong> back in both cups until you feel it click.</li><li><strong>Give it a test pull.</strong> One sheet. If the roll spins free or the paper tears sideways, the bar isn't seated.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>The over vs. under debate</summary><div class="body"><p>Over is the standard, and yes, older patents back that up. If you're in a home that insists on under, you have my sympathy and respect.</p></div></details><div class="callout info"><p>If the roll keeps falling off mid-pull, the spring has lost tension. A small rubber band inside the bar buys you a few more weeks before replacement.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Replace a Toilet Paper Roll</h1><p class="muted">Small task, real satisfaction. Let's do it cleanly, .</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Notice the holder type.</strong> Spring-loaded bar? Just compress it and lift one end. Fixed spindle? Pull the spindle out sideways.</li><li><strong>Slide the empty tube off</strong> and drop it in the recycling, not the floor.</li><li><strong>Mount the new roll</strong> so paper feeds over the top. Looks neat and is genuinely easier to grab.</li><li><strong>Seat the spring bar</strong> back in both cups until you feel it click.</li><li><strong>Give it a test pull.</strong> One sheet. If the roll spins free or the paper tears sideways, the bar isn't seated.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>The over vs. under debate</summary><div class="body"><p>Over is the standard, and yes, older patents back that up. If you're in a home that insists on under, you have my sympathy and respect.</p></div></details><div class="callout info"><p>If the roll keeps falling off mid-pull, the spring has lost tension. A small rubber band inside the bar buys you a few more weeks before replacement.</p></div></div>	-
38.23.1	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Load a Dishwasher So Everything Actually Ge	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Load a Dishwasher Properly</h1><p class="muted">Most dishwasher disappointment is a loading problem, not a machine problem.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Scrape, don't rinse.</strong> Modern detergents need food residue to work. A quick scrape is all you owe it.</li><li><strong>Bottom rack, back to front:</strong> plates and serving dishes, facing the center so spray reaches the dirty surface.</li><li><strong>Top rack, angled down:</strong> glasses and cups upside down, tilted slightly so water can't pool in the bottoms.</li><li><strong>Utensil basket:</strong> sharp knives point down, spoons and forks mixed pointing up and down so they don't nest.</li><li><strong>Never block the spray arm</strong> and don't stack anything in front of the detergent door.</li><li><strong>Run hot water at the sink</strong> until it's warm before starting, so the first rinse isn't cold.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>What should never go in</summary><div class="body"><p>Cast iron, most good knives, wooden spoons, and antique china with gold trim. Hand-wash those and stay friends with them.</p></div></details><div class="callout warn"><p>Plastic containers flip and fill with water. Anchor them on the top rack under a heavy cup or accept the swimming pool.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Load a Dishwasher Properly</h1><p class="muted">Most dishwasher disappointment is a loading problem, not a machine problem.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Scrape, don't rinse.</strong> Modern detergents need food residue to work. A quick scrape is all you owe it.</li><li><strong>Bottom rack, back to front:</strong> plates and serving dishes, facing the center so spray reaches the dirty surface.</li><li><strong>Top rack, angled down:</strong> glasses and cups upside down, tilted slightly so water can't pool in the bottoms.</li><li><strong>Utensil basket:</strong> sharp knives point down, spoons and forks mixed pointing up and down so they don't nest.</li><li><strong>Never block the spray arm</strong> and don't stack anything in front of the detergent door.</li><li><strong>Run hot water at the sink</strong> until it's warm before starting, so the first rinse isn't cold.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>What should never go in</summary><div class="body"><p>Cast iron, most good knives, wooden spoons, and antique china with gold trim. Hand-wash those and stay friends with them.</p></div></details><div class="callout warn"><p>Plastic containers flip and fill with water. Anchor them on the top rack under a heavy cup or accept the swimming pool.</p></div></div>	-
38.24.1	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Do Laundry Without Shrinking Anything You L	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Do Laundry Without Shrinking Anything</h1><p class="muted">Two rules solve 90% of laundry disasters, <regex>user_name</regex>: read the tag, and respect heat.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Read the care tag</strong> on the item you're least sure about. It usually knows better than you do.</li><li><strong>Sort into three piles:</strong> lights, darks, and things you'd cry over.</li><li><strong>Check pockets and zip zippers.</strong> Zipped zippers don't snag, empty pockets don't ruin the drum.</li><li><strong>Cold water for everything</strong> except towels and sheets. Cold prevents both shrinking and colour bleed.</li><li><strong>Turn knits and prints inside out.</strong> This protects the surface where pilling and fading show first.</li><li><strong>Dry on low or air-dry.</strong> Heat is the shrinking culprit, full stop.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>If it's already shrunk</summary><div class="body"><p>Soak the item in cool water with a cap of hair conditioner for 20 minutes, then gently stretch it back to shape and lay flat to dry. It sometimes works, which is better than never.</p></div></details><div class="callout info"><p>Wool and cashmere: hand-wash cool, press water out with a towel, dry flat. Never hang a wet sweater. Gravity does not negotiate.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Do Laundry Without Shrinking Anything</h1><p class="muted">Two rules solve 90% of laundry disasters, : read the tag, and respect heat.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Read the care tag</strong> on the item you're least sure about. It usually knows better than you do.</li><li><strong>Sort into three piles:</strong> lights, darks, and things you'd cry over.</li><li><strong>Check pockets and zip zippers.</strong> Zipped zippers don't snag, empty pockets don't ruin the drum.</li><li><strong>Cold water for everything</strong> except towels and sheets. Cold prevents both shrinking and colour bleed.</li><li><strong>Turn knits and prints inside out.</strong> This protects the surface where pilling and fading show first.</li><li><strong>Dry on low or air-dry.</strong> Heat is the shrinking culprit, full stop.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>If it's already shrunk</summary><div class="body"><p>Soak the item in cool water with a cap of hair conditioner for 20 minutes, then gently stretch it back to shape and lay flat to dry. It sometimes works, which is better than never.</p></div></details><div class="callout info"><p>Wool and cashmere: hand-wash cool, press water out with a towel, dry flat. Never hang a wet sweater. Gravity does not negotiate.</p></div></div>	-
38.25.1	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Charge a Dead Phone Battery Faster	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Charge a Phone Battery Faster</h1><p class="muted">Charging speed is mostly about heat and the right brick. Here's the short version.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Use the charger that came in the box</strong> or one rated for your phone's fast-charge standard. A random gas-station brick wins nothing.</li><li><strong>Skip the car and the laptop ports.</strong> They trickle. Fine overnight, slow when you need it.</li><li><strong>Take the case off</strong> if it's thick. Trapped heat throttles charging speed.</li><li><strong>Don't use it while it charges</strong> unless you must. Screen on plus juice flowing equals slower fill and a warm battery.</li><li><strong>Turn on airplane mode</strong> if you're in a hurry. Cutting the radios gets you a noticeable bump.</li><li><strong>Recharge between 20% and 80%</strong> on most days rather than draining to zero. Lithium cells age kinder that way.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Is the cable the problem?</summary><div class="body"><p>Often, yes. Cables fray internally before they look ruined. Swap in a known-good cable before blaming the phone.</p></div></details><div class="callout warn"><p>If the phone gets hot enough to notice while charging, stop and let it cool. Heat is what actually kills batteries.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Charge a Phone Battery Faster</h1><p class="muted">Charging speed is mostly about heat and the right brick. Here's the short version.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Use the charger that came in the box</strong> or one rated for your phone's fast-charge standard. A random gas-station brick wins nothing.</li><li><strong>Skip the car and the laptop ports.</strong> They trickle. Fine overnight, slow when you need it.</li><li><strong>Take the case off</strong> if it's thick. Trapped heat throttles charging speed.</li><li><strong>Don't use it while it charges</strong> unless you must. Screen on plus juice flowing equals slower fill and a warm battery.</li><li><strong>Turn on airplane mode</strong> if you're in a hurry. Cutting the radios gets you a noticeable bump.</li><li><strong>Recharge between 20% and 80%</strong> on most days rather than draining to zero. Lithium cells age kinder that way.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Is the cable the problem?</summary><div class="body"><p>Often, yes. Cables fray internally before they look ruined. Swap in a known-good cable before blaming the phone.</p></div></details><div class="callout warn"><p>If the phone gets hot enough to notice while charging, stop and let it cool. Heat is what actually kills batteries.</p></div></div>	-
38.26.1	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Return an Online Order Without Losing Your 	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Return an Online Order</h1><p class="muted">Returns are a paperwork sport. Move through the steps in order and it's painless.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Find the return window</strong> in your order confirmation email or account page. It's usually 30 days from delivery.</li><li><strong>Start the return in your account</strong>, not by email, so you get a tracking number in writing.</li><li><strong>Pick the reason honestly.</strong> \"Changed my mind\" is fine. Lying about damage gets returns rejected.</li><li><strong>Print the label or save the QR code.</strong> No printer? Most drop-off points scan a code from your phone.</li><li><strong>Repack in the original box</strong> with tags and accessories. Anything missing can trim your refund.</li><li><strong>Keep the drop-off receipt</strong> until the refund lands. If it vanishes, that slip is your evidence.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Who pays shipping?</summary><div class="body"><p>Defective or wrong item: they do. Changed your mind: usually you do, often deducted from the refund. Check before you ship.</p></div></details><div class="callout info"><p>Refunds take 3-10 business days after the warehouse scans the package. If it's day 14, that's when you email, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Return an Online Order</h1><p class="muted">Returns are a paperwork sport. Move through the steps in order and it's painless.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Find the return window</strong> in your order confirmation email or account page. It's usually 30 days from delivery.</li><li><strong>Start the return in your account</strong>, not by email, so you get a tracking number in writing.</li><li><strong>Pick the reason honestly.</strong> \"Changed my mind\" is fine. Lying about damage gets returns rejected.</li><li><strong>Print the label or save the QR code.</strong> No printer? Most drop-off points scan a code from your phone.</li><li><strong>Repack in the original box</strong> with tags and accessories. Anything missing can trim your refund.</li><li><strong>Keep the drop-off receipt</strong> until the refund lands. If it vanishes, that slip is your evidence.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Who pays shipping?</summary><div class="body"><p>Defective or wrong item: they do. Changed your mind: usually you do, often deducted from the refund. Check before you ship.</p></div></details><div class="callout info"><p>Refunds take 3-10 business days after the warehouse scans the package. If it's day 14, that's when you email, .</p></div></div>	-
38.27.1	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Cook Rice on the Stove Without a Rice Cooke	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Cook Rice on the Stove</h1><p class="muted">No cooker required. Ratio, lid, and patience, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Rinse the rice</strong> in cool water until the water runs mostly clear. This is the anti-sticky step.</li><li><strong>Use 1 part rice to 1.75 parts water</strong> for long-grain white. Add a pinch of salt.</li><li><strong>Bring to a boil uncovered</strong>, then drop to the lowest simmer your stove can hold.</li><li><strong>Clamp the lid on</strong> and don't peek or stir. Steam is doing the work now.</li><li><strong>Simmer 15 minutes</strong>, then take it off the heat and let it sit covered for 10 more.</li><li><strong>Fluff with a fork</strong>, not a spoon. Fork tines separate grains; spoons mash them.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Brown rice and other grains</summary><div class="body"><p>Brown rice needs about 2.25 parts water and 40-45 minutes. Basmati and jasmine follow the white rice timing, just with a rinse and a shorter soak.</p></div></details><div class="callout warn"><p>Rice stuck to the bottom of the pot? You stirred it. Now you know. Serve from the top and soak the pan before scrubbing.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Cook Rice on the Stove</h1><p class="muted">No cooker required. Ratio, lid, and patience, .</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Rinse the rice</strong> in cool water until the water runs mostly clear. This is the anti-sticky step.</li><li><strong>Use 1 part rice to 1.75 parts water</strong> for long-grain white. Add a pinch of salt.</li><li><strong>Bring to a boil uncovered</strong>, then drop to the lowest simmer your stove can hold.</li><li><strong>Clamp the lid on</strong> and don't peek or stir. Steam is doing the work now.</li><li><strong>Simmer 15 minutes</strong>, then take it off the heat and let it sit covered for 10 more.</li><li><strong>Fluff with a fork</strong>, not a spoon. Fork tines separate grains; spoons mash them.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Brown rice and other grains</summary><div class="body"><p>Brown rice needs about 2.25 parts water and 40-45 minutes. Basmati and jasmine follow the white rice timing, just with a rinse and a shorter soak.</p></div></details><div class="callout warn"><p>Rice stuck to the bottom of the pot? You stirred it. Now you know. Serve from the top and soak the pan before scrubbing.</p></div></div>	-
38.28.1	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Chill a Drink in 5 Minutes	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Chill a Drink in 5 Minutes</h1><p class="muted">No ice? No problem. Physics is on your side, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Wrap it wet.</strong> Soak a paper towel, wring it slightly, and wrap the can or bottle.</li><li><strong>Freezer, not fridge.</strong> Fridge adds hours. Freezer does the work in minutes.</li><li><strong>Add salt to the ice bath.</strong> Saltwater melts colder than plain water, so it pulls heat out faster.</li><li><strong>Spin, don't stare.</strong> Roll the can between your palms every minute. Moving liquid meets more cold surface.</li><li><strong>Five minutes, tops.</strong> Longer and you risk a burst can or a slushy mess.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Song reference</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small muted">Put on one good song and rotate your drink once per chorus.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Chill a Drink in 5 Minutes</h1><p class="muted">No ice? No problem. Physics is on your side, .</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Wrap it wet.</strong> Soak a paper towel, wring it slightly, and wrap the can or bottle.</li><li><strong>Freezer, not fridge.</strong> Fridge adds hours. Freezer does the work in minutes.</li><li><strong>Add salt to the ice bath.</strong> Saltwater melts colder than plain water, so it pulls heat out faster.</li><li><strong>Spin, don't stare.</strong> Roll the can between your palms every minute. Moving liquid meets more cold surface.</li><li><strong>Five minutes, tops.</strong> Longer and you risk a burst can or a slushy mess.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Song reference</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small muted">Put on one good song and rotate your drink once per chorus.</p></div></details></div>	-
38.29.1	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Tie a Tie (Four-in-Hand)	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Tie a Tie: Four-in-Hand</h1><p class="muted">The knot that forgives, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ol class="list"><li>Drape the tie around your neck, wide end on your right, hanging about a foot lower than the narrow end.</li><li>Cross the wide end over the narrow one.</li><li>Loop the wide end back underneath, toward your neck.</li><li>Bring the wide end across the front, left to right.</li><li>Pull it up through the loop at your neck.</li><li>Thread it down through the knot you just made.</li><li>Hold the narrow end, slide the knot up, and tighten gently. Dimple optional, charm mandatory.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Sizing check</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small muted">Knot should sit at your collar, tip at your belt buckle.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Tie a Tie: Four-in-Hand</h1><p class="muted">The knot that forgives, .</p><ol class="list"><li>Drape the tie around your neck, wide end on your right, hanging about a foot lower than the narrow end.</li><li>Cross the wide end over the narrow one.</li><li>Loop the wide end back underneath, toward your neck.</li><li>Bring the wide end across the front, left to right.</li><li>Pull it up through the loop at your neck.</li><li>Thread it down through the knot you just made.</li><li>Hold the narrow end, slide the knot up, and tighten gently. Dimple optional, charm mandatory.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Sizing check</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small muted">Knot should sit at your collar, tip at your belt buckle.</p></div></details></div>	-
38.30.1	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Write a To-Do List You Actually Finish	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Write a To-Do List You Actually Finish</h1><p class="muted">Fewer things, more done, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Brain dump first.</strong> Every task onto paper, no order, no judgment.</li><li><strong>Pick three.</strong> Circle the ones that matter today. Three is a list; twelve is a wish.</li><li><strong>Verbs first.</strong> "Email Sam the draft," not "Sam stuff."</li><li><strong>Add a size.</strong> Mark each as 5 min, 30 min, or "whole afternoon."</li><li><strong>Start with the smallest.</strong> Momentum is cheaper than motivation.</li><li><strong>Leave one blank slot.</strong> A gap is where real life lands.</li><li><strong>Tonight, cross off or carry.</strong> Anything carried three days gets cut or scheduled.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>One rule</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small muted">If it takes under two minutes, do it now instead of writing it down.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Write a To-Do List You Actually Finish</h1><p class="muted">Fewer things, more done, .</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Brain dump first.</strong> Every task onto paper, no order, no judgment.</li><li><strong>Pick three.</strong> Circle the ones that matter today. Three is a list; twelve is a wish.</li><li><strong>Verbs first.</strong> "Email Sam the draft," not "Sam stuff."</li><li><strong>Add a size.</strong> Mark each as 5 min, 30 min, or "whole afternoon."</li><li><strong>Start with the smallest.</strong> Momentum is cheaper than motivation.</li><li><strong>Leave one blank slot.</strong> A gap is where real life lands.</li><li><strong>Tonight, cross off or carry.</strong> Anything carried three days gets cut or scheduled.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>One rule</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small muted">If it takes under two minutes, do it now instead of writing it down.</p></div></details></div>	-
38.31.1	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Brew Better Cold Brew at Home	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Brew Better Cold Brew</h1><p class="muted">Low effort, big payoff, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Coarse grind.</strong> Like raw sugar. Fine grounds turn muddy and bitter.</li><li><strong>Ratio:</strong> 1 cup grounds to 4 cups cold water in a jar or pitcher.</li><li><strong>Stir once</strong> so every ground gets wet. No dry islands.</li><li><strong>Cover and wait 12&ndash;18 hours</strong> at room temp. Longer means stronger, not better.</li><li><strong>Strain twice.</strong> Once through a sieve, once through a paper filter or cloth.</li><li><strong>Store</strong> the concentrate in the fridge up to a week.</li><li><strong>Serve</strong> 1 part concentrate to 2 parts water or milk over ice. Adjust until it tastes like yours.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Too bitter?</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small muted">Shorten the steep or dilute more. Bitterness is time and heat, not fate.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Brew Better Cold Brew</h1><p class="muted">Low effort, big payoff, .</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Coarse grind.</strong> Like raw sugar. Fine grounds turn muddy and bitter.</li><li><strong>Ratio:</strong> 1 cup grounds to 4 cups cold water in a jar or pitcher.</li><li><strong>Stir once</strong> so every ground gets wet. No dry islands.</li><li><strong>Cover and wait 12&ndash;18 hours</strong> at room temp. Longer means stronger, not better.</li><li><strong>Strain twice.</strong> Once through a sieve, once through a paper filter or cloth.</li><li><strong>Store</strong> the concentrate in the fridge up to a week.</li><li><strong>Serve</strong> 1 part concentrate to 2 parts water or milk over ice. Adjust until it tastes like yours.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Too bitter?</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small muted">Shorten the steep or dilute more. Bitterness is time and heat, not fate.</p></div></details></div>	-
38.32.1	dashboard_steps/dashboard_steps: Wrap a Gift Without Losing the Tape	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Wrap a Gift Without Losing the Tape</h1><p class="muted">Paper, tape, patience. In that order, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Tape the tape.</strong> Stick a strip to the table edge so it&#39;s findable, not lost in the paper.</li><li><strong>Measure first.</strong> Paper should wrap around with about 2 inches of overlap.</li><li><strong>Cut straight.</strong> One clean line beats three ragged ones.</li><li><strong>Center, flip, fold.</strong> Box face-down on the paper, pull one side over, tape it.</li><li><strong>Fold the ends.</strong> Crease the corners into triangles, then fold them in like a hospital corner.</li><li><strong>Tape the seams</strong> flat, pressing as you go.</li><li><strong>Skip the bow if you want.</strong> A plain ribbon or a doodle on the tag is plenty.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Odd shapes</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small muted">Soft or round items: wrap in tissue first, then treat it like a box.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Wrap a Gift Without Losing the Tape</h1><p class="muted">Paper, tape, patience. In that order, .</p><ol class="list"><li><strong>Tape the tape.</strong> Stick a strip to the table edge so it&#39;s findable, not lost in the paper.</li><li><strong>Measure first.</strong> Paper should wrap around with about 2 inches of overlap.</li><li><strong>Cut straight.</strong> One clean line beats three ragged ones.</li><li><strong>Center, flip, fold.</strong> Box face-down on the paper, pull one side over, tape it.</li><li><strong>Fold the ends.</strong> Crease the corners into triangles, then fold them in like a hospital corner.</li><li><strong>Tape the seams</strong> flat, pressing as you go.</li><li><strong>Skip the bow if you want.</strong> A plain ribbon or a doodle on the tag is plenty.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Odd shapes</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small muted">Soft or round items: wrap in tissue first, then treat it like a box.</p></div></details></div>	-
39.0.0	dashboard_faq/Coffee Grinder Care	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Coffee grinder care</h1><p class="muted">A clean grinder makes better coffee. Here's the short version.</p><details class="acc"><summary>How often should I clean it?</summary><div class="body">Every week if you grind daily. Every few days if you use oily dark roasts.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What do I need?</summary><div class="body">Dry rice or grinder tablets, a stiff brush, and a damp cloth. No water inside the burr chamber.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Step by step</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Unplug it.</li><li>Empty the hopper and grounds bin.</li><li>Run a small handful of dry rice or a tablet through.</li><li>Brush out the chute and burrs.</li><li>Wipe the exterior with a damp cloth.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Can I wash the parts?</summary><div class="body">Only the hopper and bin if the manual says they're removable and washable. Burrs stay dry.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I know it's clean?</summary><div class="body">No stale smell, no visible grounds, and the next dose tastes like the beans, not yesterday's.</div></details><p class="dim t-small">If it's been years, consider replacing the burrs. They dull.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Coffee grinder care</h1><p class="muted">A clean grinder makes better coffee. Here's the short version.</p><details class="acc"><summary>How often should I clean it?</summary><div class="body">Every week if you grind daily. Every few days if you use oily dark roasts.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What do I need?</summary><div class="body">Dry rice or grinder tablets, a stiff brush, and a damp cloth. No water inside the burr chamber.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Step by step</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Unplug it.</li><li>Empty the hopper and grounds bin.</li><li>Run a small handful of dry rice or a tablet through.</li><li>Brush out the chute and burrs.</li><li>Wipe the exterior with a damp cloth.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Can I wash the parts?</summary><div class="body">Only the hopper and bin if the manual says they're removable and washable. Burrs stay dry.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I know it's clean?</summary><div class="body">No stale smell, no visible grounds, and the next dose tastes like the beans, not yesterday's.</div></details><p class="dim t-small">If it's been years, consider replacing the burrs. They dull.</p></div>	-
39.1.0	dashboard_faq/Slow Sink Fix	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Slow sink? Start here.</h1><p class="muted">Most clogs live in the P-trap. You can usually fix it without calling anyone.</p><details class="acc"><summary>First, try hot water</summary><div class="body">Boil a kettle and pour it slowly down the drain. Wait five minutes. Repeat. This melts grease.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Then try baking soda and vinegar</summary><div class="body">Pour half a cup of baking soda, then half a cup of white vinegar. Let it fizz for 15 minutes. Flush with hot water.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Still slow? Check the P-trap</summary><div class="body">Put a bucket under the trap. Unscrew the slip nuts by hand. Clean out the gunk. Reassemble hand-tight.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>When to call a pro</summary><div class="body">If multiple drains are slow, or you see water backing up elsewhere, the clog is deeper in the line.</div></details><p class="dim t-small">Skip chemical drain cleaners if you have older pipes. They're harsh.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Slow sink? Start here.</h1><p class="muted">Most clogs live in the P-trap. You can usually fix it without calling anyone.</p><details class="acc"><summary>First, try hot water</summary><div class="body">Boil a kettle and pour it slowly down the drain. Wait five minutes. Repeat. This melts grease.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Then try baking soda and vinegar</summary><div class="body">Pour half a cup of baking soda, then half a cup of white vinegar. Let it fizz for 15 minutes. Flush with hot water.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Still slow? Check the P-trap</summary><div class="body">Put a bucket under the trap. Unscrew the slip nuts by hand. Clean out the gunk. Reassemble hand-tight.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>When to call a pro</summary><div class="body">If multiple drains are slow, or you see water backing up elsewhere, the clog is deeper in the line.</div></details><p class="dim t-small">Skip chemical drain cleaners if you have older pipes. They're harsh.</p></div>	-
39.2.0	dashboard_faq/Fold a Fitted Sheet	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Fold a fitted sheet</h1><p class="muted">It's not you. Fitted sheets are just rude. Here's the trick.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Lay it flat, inside out</summary><div class="body">Elastic edges up. Smooth it with your hands.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Find the corners</summary><div class="body">Tuck one corner into the next, nesting them. Do the same on the other side. You now have a rectangle-ish shape.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Fold in thirds</summary><div class="body">Fold the long sides toward the middle. Then fold the whole thing in half or thirds.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Stack and store</summary><div class="body">Put the folded sheet inside its matching pillowcase. Instant tidy set.</div></details><p class="dim t-small">If it's still lumpy, nobody will know. Sheets live in closets, not museums.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Fold a fitted sheet</h1><p class="muted">It's not you. Fitted sheets are just rude. Here's the trick.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Lay it flat, inside out</summary><div class="body">Elastic edges up. Smooth it with your hands.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Find the corners</summary><div class="body">Tuck one corner into the next, nesting them. Do the same on the other side. You now have a rectangle-ish shape.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Fold in thirds</summary><div class="body">Fold the long sides toward the middle. Then fold the whole thing in half or thirds.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Stack and store</summary><div class="body">Put the folded sheet inside its matching pillowcase. Instant tidy set.</div></details><p class="dim t-small">If it's still lumpy, nobody will know. Sheets live in closets, not museums.</p></div>	-
39.3.0	dashboard_faq/Red Wine Stain Rescue	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Red wine stain rescue</h1><p class="muted">Act fast. Blot, don't rub. Here's the sequence.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Blot immediately</summary><div class="body">Use a clean cloth or paper towel. Press down. Don't scrub. Scrubbing spreads the stain.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Salt or baking soda</summary><div class="body">Cover the stain with salt or baking soda. It pulls some of the wine out. Let it sit 10 minutes, then vacuum or brush off.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Cold water rinse</summary><div class="body">Run cold water through the back of the fabric. Hot water sets the stain.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Detergent or vinegar</summary><div class="body">Dab with dish soap or white vinegar. Rinse. Repeat until the stain lifts.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Launder as usual</summary><div class="body">Check the stain before drying. Heat sets whatever's left.</div></details><p class="dim t-small">For old stains, try an oxygen bleach soak. Patience helps.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Red wine stain rescue</h1><p class="muted">Act fast. Blot, don't rub. Here's the sequence.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Blot immediately</summary><div class="body">Use a clean cloth or paper towel. Press down. Don't scrub. Scrubbing spreads the stain.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Salt or baking soda</summary><div class="body">Cover the stain with salt or baking soda. It pulls some of the wine out. Let it sit 10 minutes, then vacuum or brush off.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Cold water rinse</summary><div class="body">Run cold water through the back of the fabric. Hot water sets the stain.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Detergent or vinegar</summary><div class="body">Dab with dish soap or white vinegar. Rinse. Repeat until the stain lifts.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Launder as usual</summary><div class="body">Check the stain before drying. Heat sets whatever's left.</div></details><p class="dim t-small">For old stains, try an oxygen bleach soak. Patience helps.</p></div>	-
39.4.0	dashboard_faq/Better Sleep Basics	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Better sleep basics</h1><p class="muted">Sleep is a skill. These are the fundamentals.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Same time, every day</summary><div class="body">Your body loves rhythm. Wake up at the same time, even on weekends.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Light matters</summary><div class="body">Get bright light in the morning. Dim the lights an hour before bed.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Cool, dark, quiet</summary><div class="body">A cool room (around 65°F / 18°C) helps. So do blackout curtains and earplugs if needed.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Watch the caffeine</summary><div class="body">Caffeine lingers for 6–8 hours. Cut it off by early afternoon.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Wind down</summary><div class="body">No screens for 30 minutes before bed. Read, stretch, or just breathe.</div></details><p class="dim t-small">If you still can't sleep, get up and do something boring in dim light. Don't toss and turn.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Better sleep basics</h1><p class="muted">Sleep is a skill. These are the fundamentals.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Same time, every day</summary><div class="body">Your body loves rhythm. Wake up at the same time, even on weekends.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Light matters</summary><div class="body">Get bright light in the morning. Dim the lights an hour before bed.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Cool, dark, quiet</summary><div class="body">A cool room (around 65°F / 18°C) helps. So do blackout curtains and earplugs if needed.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Watch the caffeine</summary><div class="body">Caffeine lingers for 6–8 hours. Cut it off by early afternoon.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Wind down</summary><div class="body">No screens for 30 minutes before bed. Read, stretch, or just breathe.</div></details><p class="dim t-small">If you still can't sleep, get up and do something boring in dim light. Don't toss and turn.</p></div>	-
39.5.0	dashboard_faq/Keep Herbs Fresh	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Keep herbs fresh</h1><p class="muted">Two methods: soft herbs and hard herbs.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Soft herbs (basil, cilantro, parsley)</summary><div class="body">Treat them like flowers. Trim the stems, put them in a glass of water, cover loosely with a plastic bag, and refrigerate. Change the water every couple of days.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Hard herbs (rosemary, thyme, oregano)</summary><div class="body">Wrap them in a slightly damp paper towel, then in a plastic bag. Store in the crisper drawer.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Basil is special</summary><div class="body">Basil hates cold. Leave it on the counter in water, like a bouquet.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Freeze extra</summary><div class="body">Chop and freeze in olive oil in an ice cube tray. Drop into soups later.</div></details><p class="dim t-small">If they're already wilted, they're still good for cooking. Just not for garnish.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Keep herbs fresh</h1><p class="muted">Two methods: soft herbs and hard herbs.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Soft herbs (basil, cilantro, parsley)</summary><div class="body">Treat them like flowers. Trim the stems, put them in a glass of water, cover loosely with a plastic bag, and refrigerate. Change the water every couple of days.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Hard herbs (rosemary, thyme, oregano)</summary><div class="body">Wrap them in a slightly damp paper towel, then in a plastic bag. Store in the crisper drawer.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Basil is special</summary><div class="body">Basil hates cold. Leave it on the counter in water, like a bouquet.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Freeze extra</summary><div class="body">Chop and freeze in olive oil in an ice cube tray. Drop into soups later.</div></details><p class="dim t-small">If they're already wilted, they're still good for cooking. Just not for garnish.</p></div>	-
39.6.0	dashboard_faq/Unclog a Vacuum Filter	<div class="umi-page stack"><h1>Unclog a Vacuum Filter</h1><p class="muted">Loss of suction is usually a clogged filter, not a dying motor. Here's the fix, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="callout info"><strong>Quick check:</strong> if suction returns when the filter is out (don't run it long that way), the filter is your culprit.</div><details class="acc"><summary>How do I know the filter is the problem?</summary><div class="body">Weak pick-up, a hot exhaust, a musty smell, or visible dust on the pleats. Machines that used to grab a rug now just skim it.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Can I wash a HEPA filter?</summary><div class="body">Only if it says washable. Rinse from the clean side with cool water until it runs clear. Never use soap or a brush, and never put it in the dryer or a hot oven. Air-dry 24 hours, bone dry, before it goes back in. Damp pleats breed mildew.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Dry-only filters?</summary><div class="body">Tap them gently over a bin and brush the pleats with a soft dry brush. Replace every 3-6 months, sooner if you have pets or fine dust.</div></details><div class="card"><p class="card-title">Drying timeline</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">0 h</span>Rinse, shake off excess.</li><li><span class="when">4 h</span>Turn it over once.</li><li><span class="when">24 h</span>Fully dry, tap and reinstall.</li></ul></div><div class="callout warn">A wet filter in a running vacuum can fry the motor. When in doubt, wait another night.</div><p class="dim t-small">After every third wash, check the replacement date on the housing.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page stack"><h1>Unclog a Vacuum Filter</h1><p class="muted">Loss of suction is usually a clogged filter, not a dying motor. Here's the fix, .</p><div class="callout info"><strong>Quick check:</strong> if suction returns when the filter is out (don't run it long that way), the filter is your culprit.</div><details class="acc"><summary>How do I know the filter is the problem?</summary><div class="body">Weak pick-up, a hot exhaust, a musty smell, or visible dust on the pleats. Machines that used to grab a rug now just skim it.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Can I wash a HEPA filter?</summary><div class="body">Only if it says washable. Rinse from the clean side with cool water until it runs clear. Never use soap or a brush, and never put it in the dryer or a hot oven. Air-dry 24 hours, bone dry, before it goes back in. Damp pleats breed mildew.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Dry-only filters?</summary><div class="body">Tap them gently over a bin and brush the pleats with a soft dry brush. Replace every 3-6 months, sooner if you have pets or fine dust.</div></details><div class="card"><p class="card-title">Drying timeline</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">0 h</span>Rinse, shake off excess.</li><li><span class="when">4 h</span>Turn it over once.</li><li><span class="when">24 h</span>Fully dry, tap and reinstall.</li></ul></div><div class="callout warn">A wet filter in a running vacuum can fry the motor. When in doubt, wait another night.</div><p class="dim t-small">After every third wash, check the replacement date on the housing.</p></div>	-
39.7.0	dashboard_faq/Ripen an Avocado Fast	<div class="umi-page stack"><h1>Ripen an Avocado Fast</h1><p class="muted">A rock-hard avocado is not a lost cause, <regex>user_name</regex>. It's just on its own clock.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">On counter</span><span class="value">3-5 <span class="unit">days</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">In paper bag</span><span class="value">1-3 <span class="unit">days</span></span><span class="delta down">faster</span></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>The paper bag trick</summary><div class="body">Drop the avocado in a paper bag with a ripe banana or apple. Those fruits release ethylene gas, which tells the avocado to hurry up. Fold the top closed and leave it on the counter.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Can I speed it up more?</summary><div class="body">Warmth helps, heat does not. A sunny counter is fine, an oven is not. Adding a second ripe banana nudges it along. Never microwave to ripen, that just cooks it.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I tell it's ready?</summary><div class="body">Cradle it in your palm and press gently near the stem. It should give slightly, like a ripe peach. Rock solid means wait. Mushy means guacamole, right now.</div></details><div class="callout good">Already soft? Move it to the fridge. Cold pauses ripening for several days.</div><svg viewBox="0 0 200 60" class="chart" role="img" aria-label="Ripening progress bar"><rect x="4" y="22" width="192" height="16" rx="8" fill="var(--line)"/><rect x="4" y="22" width="120" height="16" rx="8" fill="var(--accent)"/></svg><p class="dim t-small">Fridge once ripe, freezer only as purée.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page stack"><h1>Ripen an Avocado Fast</h1><p class="muted">A rock-hard avocado is not a lost cause, . It's just on its own clock.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">On counter</span><span class="value">3-5 <span class="unit">days</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">In paper bag</span><span class="value">1-3 <span class="unit">days</span></span><span class="delta down">faster</span></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>The paper bag trick</summary><div class="body">Drop the avocado in a paper bag with a ripe banana or apple. Those fruits release ethylene gas, which tells the avocado to hurry up. Fold the top closed and leave it on the counter.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Can I speed it up more?</summary><div class="body">Warmth helps, heat does not. A sunny counter is fine, an oven is not. Adding a second ripe banana nudges it along. Never microwave to ripen, that just cooks it.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I tell it's ready?</summary><div class="body">Cradle it in your palm and press gently near the stem. It should give slightly, like a ripe peach. Rock solid means wait. Mushy means guacamole, right now.</div></details><div class="callout good">Already soft? Move it to the fridge. Cold pauses ripening for several days.</div><svg viewBox="0 0 200 60" class="chart" role="img" aria-label="Ripening progress bar"><rect x="4" y="22" width="192" height="16" rx="8" fill="var(--line)"/><rect x="4" y="22" width="120" height="16" rx="8" fill="var(--accent)"/></svg><p class="dim t-small">Fridge once ripe, freezer only as purée.</p></div>	-
39.8.0	dashboard_faq/Fix a Squeaky Door Hinge	<div class="umi-page stack"><h1>Fix a Squeaky Door Hinge</h1><p class="muted">That squeak is metal on metal, dry and complaining. Lube, and it quiets down, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><details class="acc"><summary>What should I use?</summary><div class="body">A drop of light machine oil, silicone spray, or lithium grease. Cooking oil works in a pinch but goes gummy and attracts dust. WD-40 helps briefly; it cleans more than it lubricates.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I apply it?</summary><div class="body">Wipe the hinge clean first. Open the door to take pressure off, then place one drop at the top of each hinge pin and one at the bottom. Swing the door back and forth a dozen times so it works in. Wipe the drip.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Still squeaking?</summary><div class="body">Pull the pin with a punch or a nail and a hammer, wipe it, grease it, and tap it back. If the hinge is loose, tighten the screws, or swap in longer ones. A stripped screw hole gets a wooden matchstick and glue as a plug.</div></details><div class="callout warn">Only touch hinges on a door you can support. A heavy door off its pin is a bad afternoon.</div><ol class="list"><li>Clean the hinge.</li><li>One drop per pin, top and bottom.</li><li>Swing the door to spread it.</li><li>Wipe the excess.</li></ol><p class="dim t-small">Repeat once a year, more in humid rooms.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page stack"><h1>Fix a Squeaky Door Hinge</h1><p class="muted">That squeak is metal on metal, dry and complaining. Lube, and it quiets down, .</p><details class="acc"><summary>What should I use?</summary><div class="body">A drop of light machine oil, silicone spray, or lithium grease. Cooking oil works in a pinch but goes gummy and attracts dust. WD-40 helps briefly; it cleans more than it lubricates.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I apply it?</summary><div class="body">Wipe the hinge clean first. Open the door to take pressure off, then place one drop at the top of each hinge pin and one at the bottom. Swing the door back and forth a dozen times so it works in. Wipe the drip.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Still squeaking?</summary><div class="body">Pull the pin with a punch or a nail and a hammer, wipe it, grease it, and tap it back. If the hinge is loose, tighten the screws, or swap in longer ones. A stripped screw hole gets a wooden matchstick and glue as a plug.</div></details><div class="callout warn">Only touch hinges on a door you can support. A heavy door off its pin is a bad afternoon.</div><ol class="list"><li>Clean the hinge.</li><li>One drop per pin, top and bottom.</li><li>Swing the door to spread it.</li><li>Wipe the excess.</li></ol><p class="dim t-small">Repeat once a year, more in humid rooms.</p></div>	-
39.9.0	dashboard_faq/Revive Stale Bread	<div class="umi-page stack"><h1>Revive Stale Bread</h1><p class="muted">Stale is not spoiled. Starch just re-crystallised, and heat can undo it, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="callout info">Rule of thumb: dry heat for crusty loaves, moisture for soft ones.</div><details class="acc"><summary>Crusty loaf or baguette</summary><div class="body">Run it under the tap for two seconds, or flick water over the crust. Bake at 180&deg;C / 350&deg;F for 8-10 minutes. The crust crackles back, the inside softens. Eat it the same day; it stales again faster the second time.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Soft sandwich bread</summary><div class="body">Skip the oven. Wrap a slice in a damp paper towel and microwave 10-15 seconds. Or steam it over a pot for a minute. It'll be tender, not toasted.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What if it's beyond saving?</summary><div class="body">Hard as a brick and dry through? Make croutons, breadcrumbs, or a panzanella. Toss cubes with oil, salt, and herbs, bake at 180&deg;C for 12 minutes. Nothing wasted.</div></details><div class="card"><p class="card-title">Temperature guide</p><div class="row between"><span>Oven</span><span class="badge accent">180&deg;C / 350&deg;F</span></div><div class="row between"><span>Time</span><span class="badge">8-10 min</span></div></div><p class="dim t-small">Store bread cut-side down, or freeze it sliced.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page stack"><h1>Revive Stale Bread</h1><p class="muted">Stale is not spoiled. Starch just re-crystallised, and heat can undo it, .</p><div class="callout info">Rule of thumb: dry heat for crusty loaves, moisture for soft ones.</div><details class="acc"><summary>Crusty loaf or baguette</summary><div class="body">Run it under the tap for two seconds, or flick water over the crust. Bake at 180&deg;C / 350&deg;F for 8-10 minutes. The crust crackles back, the inside softens. Eat it the same day; it stales again faster the second time.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Soft sandwich bread</summary><div class="body">Skip the oven. Wrap a slice in a damp paper towel and microwave 10-15 seconds. Or steam it over a pot for a minute. It'll be tender, not toasted.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What if it's beyond saving?</summary><div class="body">Hard as a brick and dry through? Make croutons, breadcrumbs, or a panzanella. Toss cubes with oil, salt, and herbs, bake at 180&deg;C for 12 minutes. Nothing wasted.</div></details><div class="card"><p class="card-title">Temperature guide</p><div class="row between"><span>Oven</span><span class="badge accent">180&deg;C / 350&deg;F</span></div><div class="row between"><span>Time</span><span class="badge">8-10 min</span></div></div><p class="dim t-small">Store bread cut-side down, or freeze it sliced.</p></div>	-
39.10.0	dashboard_faq/Remove Chewing Gum	<div class="umi-page stack"><h1>Remove Chewing Gum</h1><p class="muted">Gum hates cold and loves oil. Pick the method that fits the surface, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><details class="acc"><summary>In hair</summary><div class="body">Work a spoonful of olive oil, peanut butter, or conditioner into the gum and the strands around it. Wait two minutes, then slide the gum out in one piece. Wash after. Ice also works: freeze it stiff, then crumble it out.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>On fabric</summary><div class="body">Freeze the garment for an hour, then chip the gum off with a butter knife. Any residue gets a dab of rubbing alcohol or vinegar, then a normal wash. Check before it goes in the dryer.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>In carpet</summary><div class="body">Hold an ice cube on it until it hardens, then lift with a spoon. Trim the last fibres with scissors if a tuft clings. Don't rub, that spreads it deeper.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>On hard surfaces</summary><div class="body">A plastic scraper plus warm soapy water. A little oil loosens the grip. Avoid metal blades on wood or plastic.</div></details><div class="callout warn">Never iron or heat gum on fabric. It melts into the weave and stays.</div><p class="dim t-small">Test any solvent on a hidden seam first.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page stack"><h1>Remove Chewing Gum</h1><p class="muted">Gum hates cold and loves oil. Pick the method that fits the surface, .</p><details class="acc"><summary>In hair</summary><div class="body">Work a spoonful of olive oil, peanut butter, or conditioner into the gum and the strands around it. Wait two minutes, then slide the gum out in one piece. Wash after. Ice also works: freeze it stiff, then crumble it out.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>On fabric</summary><div class="body">Freeze the garment for an hour, then chip the gum off with a butter knife. Any residue gets a dab of rubbing alcohol or vinegar, then a normal wash. Check before it goes in the dryer.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>In carpet</summary><div class="body">Hold an ice cube on it until it hardens, then lift with a spoon. Trim the last fibres with scissors if a tuft clings. Don't rub, that spreads it deeper.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>On hard surfaces</summary><div class="body">A plastic scraper plus warm soapy water. A little oil loosens the grip. Avoid metal blades on wood or plastic.</div></details><div class="callout warn">Never iron or heat gum on fabric. It melts into the weave and stays.</div><p class="dim t-small">Test any solvent on a hidden seam first.</p></div>	-
39.11.0	dashboard_faq/Stop Glasses From Fogging	<div class="umi-page stack"><h1>Stop Glasses From Fogging</h1><p class="muted">Fog is warm breath hitting cool glass. Break that meeting and the lenses stay clear, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><details class="acc"><summary>With a face mask</summary><div class="body">Pinch the nose wire snug against your cheeks, and pull the top edge high, under your glasses. Tuck the bottom under your chin. Tape the top edge if it's a loose cloth mask. The goal is no breath escaping upward.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Everyday anti-fog</summary><div class="body">A drop of dish soap rubbed on both sides, then buffed dry, leaves an invisible film that stops droplets forming. Shaving foam works the same way. Commercial anti-fog sprays last longer but cost more.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Cooking and hot drinks</summary><div class="body">Warm the lenses first. A few seconds over steam or in a pocket raises their temperature so breath can't condense. Tilt your head back over the pot instead of leaning in.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Why do coatings matter?</summary><div class="body">Anti-reflective coatings fog more easily than bare lenses because the surface is rougher at a microscopic level. Clean with a microfibre cloth, not a shirt tail, which adds oils that trap moisture.</div></details><div class="callout good">Best combo: snug mask plus a soap film. Two defences beat one.</div><p class="dim t-small">Reapply the soap film every couple of days.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page stack"><h1>Stop Glasses From Fogging</h1><p class="muted">Fog is warm breath hitting cool glass. Break that meeting and the lenses stay clear, .</p><details class="acc"><summary>With a face mask</summary><div class="body">Pinch the nose wire snug against your cheeks, and pull the top edge high, under your glasses. Tuck the bottom under your chin. Tape the top edge if it's a loose cloth mask. The goal is no breath escaping upward.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Everyday anti-fog</summary><div class="body">A drop of dish soap rubbed on both sides, then buffed dry, leaves an invisible film that stops droplets forming. Shaving foam works the same way. Commercial anti-fog sprays last longer but cost more.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Cooking and hot drinks</summary><div class="body">Warm the lenses first. A few seconds over steam or in a pocket raises their temperature so breath can't condense. Tilt your head back over the pot instead of leaning in.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Why do coatings matter?</summary><div class="body">Anti-reflective coatings fog more easily than bare lenses because the surface is rougher at a microscopic level. Clean with a microfibre cloth, not a shirt tail, which adds oils that trap moisture.</div></details><div class="callout good">Best combo: snug mask plus a soap film. Two defences beat one.</div><p class="dim t-small">Reapply the soap film every couple of days.</p></div>	-
39.12.0	dashboard_faq/Quiet Your Keys	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Quiet Your Keys</h1><p class="muted">Jingle all the way is a great song, less great in a meeting. <regex>user_name</regex>, here's how to hush the bunch.</p><div class="stack"><details class="acc"><summary>Why do keys jingle?</summary><div class="body"><p>Metal on metal, with air between. Reduce movement or add padding and the noise mostly stops.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Fast fixes</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Add a fabric key sleeve or a thick rubber band around the bunch.</li><li>Switch to a flat key organizer that clamps keys side by side.</li><li>Use a leather key wrap that folds over the teeth.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>DIY: the rubber band trick</summary><div class="body"><p>Wrap a wide rubber band snugly around all keys, twice. Tuck the loose end under. Takes ten seconds, works surprisingly well.</p></div></details><div class="callout info"><p class="muted">Bonus: a quiet keychain is also a smaller, less pickpocket-friendly target.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Quiet Your Keys</h1><p class="muted">Jingle all the way is a great song, less great in a meeting. , here's how to hush the bunch.</p><div class="stack"><details class="acc"><summary>Why do keys jingle?</summary><div class="body"><p>Metal on metal, with air between. Reduce movement or add padding and the noise mostly stops.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Fast fixes</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Add a fabric key sleeve or a thick rubber band around the bunch.</li><li>Switch to a flat key organizer that clamps keys side by side.</li><li>Use a leather key wrap that folds over the teeth.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>DIY: the rubber band trick</summary><div class="body"><p>Wrap a wide rubber band snugly around all keys, twice. Tuck the loose end under. Takes ten seconds, works surprisingly well.</p></div></details><div class="callout info"><p class="muted">Bonus: a quiet keychain is also a smaller, less pickpocket-friendly target.</p></div></div></div>	-
39.13.0	dashboard_faq/Pick a Perfect Watermelon	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Pick a Perfect Watermelon</h1><p class="muted"><regex>user_name</regex>, thumping is fun but not the whole story. Use three quick checks.</p><div class="stack"><details class="acc"><summary>1. Field spot</summary><div class="body"><p>Look for a creamy yellow patch where it rested on the ground. White means it was picked early. Orange-ish is overripe.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>2. Webbing</summary><div class="body"><p>Brown web-like lines on the surface are from bees pollinating. More webbing usually means sweeter fruit.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>3. Thump test</summary><div class="body"><p>Give it a firm tap. A hollow, deep sound means ripe. A dull thud means underripe or mealy.</p></div></details><div class="callout good"><p>Symmetry matters too. A lopsided melon had uneven water and sun. Aim for round and heavy for its size.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Pick a Perfect Watermelon</h1><p class="muted">, thumping is fun but not the whole story. Use three quick checks.</p><div class="stack"><details class="acc"><summary>1. Field spot</summary><div class="body"><p>Look for a creamy yellow patch where it rested on the ground. White means it was picked early. Orange-ish is overripe.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>2. Webbing</summary><div class="body"><p>Brown web-like lines on the surface are from bees pollinating. More webbing usually means sweeter fruit.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>3. Thump test</summary><div class="body"><p>Give it a firm tap. A hollow, deep sound means ripe. A dull thud means underripe or mealy.</p></div></details><div class="callout good"><p>Symmetry matters too. A lopsided melon had uneven water and sun. Aim for round and heavy for its size.</p></div></div></div>	-
39.14.0	dashboard_faq/Clean Cast Iron Without Fear	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Clean Cast Iron Without Fear</h1><p class="muted"><regex>user_name</regex>, soap won't ruin it. Modern dish soap is gentle enough. Here's the routine.</p><div class="stack"><details class="acc"><summary>Daily clean</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>While still warm, wipe out food with a paper towel.</li><li>Scrub with hot water and a stiff brush or chain mail. A little soap is fine.</li><li>Dry on the stove over low heat for a minute.</li><li>Rub a thin layer of oil over the cooking surface.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>If something stuck</summary><div class="body"><p>Simmer water in the pan for a few minutes, then scrape with a wooden spatula. No need to chisel.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Rust rescue</summary><div class="body"><p>Scrub rust off with steel wool, wash, dry, and re-season: oil and bake upside down at 450°F for an hour.</p></div></details><div class="callout warn"><p>Never soak it overnight or put it in the dishwasher. That's the one rule worth keeping.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Clean Cast Iron Without Fear</h1><p class="muted">, soap won't ruin it. Modern dish soap is gentle enough. Here's the routine.</p><div class="stack"><details class="acc"><summary>Daily clean</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>While still warm, wipe out food with a paper towel.</li><li>Scrub with hot water and a stiff brush or chain mail. A little soap is fine.</li><li>Dry on the stove over low heat for a minute.</li><li>Rub a thin layer of oil over the cooking surface.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>If something stuck</summary><div class="body"><p>Simmer water in the pan for a few minutes, then scrape with a wooden spatula. No need to chisel.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Rust rescue</summary><div class="body"><p>Scrub rust off with steel wool, wash, dry, and re-season: oil and bake upside down at 450°F for an hour.</p></div></details><div class="callout warn"><p>Never soak it overnight or put it in the dishwasher. That's the one rule worth keeping.</p></div></div></div>	-
39.15.0	dashboard_faq/Phone Battery Longevity	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Phone Battery Longevity</h1><p class="muted"><regex>user_name</regex>, your battery is a tiny chemical plant. Treat it kindly and it'll last years.</p><div class="stack"><details class="acc"><summary>Keep it between 20% and 80%</summary><div class="body"><p>Full charges and full drains stress lithium cells. Topping up mid-range is gentler. Many phones have an optimized charging setting, turn it on.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Avoid heat</summary><div class="body"><p>Heat is the real battery killer. Don't leave your phone on a car dash or charge it under a pillow. Remove thick cases while fast charging.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Slow charge overnight</summary><div class="body"><p>Fast charging is convenient but generates heat. Overnight, use a slower charger if you can.</p></div></details><div class="callout info"><p>Don't worry about occasional full charges. One hundred percent isn't instant doom, it's just less ideal.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Phone Battery Longevity</h1><p class="muted">, your battery is a tiny chemical plant. Treat it kindly and it'll last years.</p><div class="stack"><details class="acc"><summary>Keep it between 20% and 80%</summary><div class="body"><p>Full charges and full drains stress lithium cells. Topping up mid-range is gentler. Many phones have an optimized charging setting, turn it on.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Avoid heat</summary><div class="body"><p>Heat is the real battery killer. Don't leave your phone on a car dash or charge it under a pillow. Remove thick cases while fast charging.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Slow charge overnight</summary><div class="body"><p>Fast charging is convenient but generates heat. Overnight, use a slower charger if you can.</p></div></details><div class="callout info"><p>Don't worry about occasional full charges. One hundred percent isn't instant doom, it's just less ideal.</p></div></div></div>	-
39.16.0	dashboard_faq/Rescue a Stripped Screw	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Rescue a Stripped Screw</h1><p class="muted"><regex>user_name</regex>, a stripped screw is a stubborn guest. Here's how to evict it, gentlest first.</p><div class="stack"><details class="acc"><summary>Rubber band trick</summary><div class="body"><p>Place a wide rubber band over the screw head, press your screwdriver in, and turn slowly. The rubber fills the gaps and adds grip.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Try a different driver</summary><div class="body"><p>Sometimes a slightly larger flathead or a star bit grabs where the original failed. Push down hard while turning.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Extractor bit</summary><div class="body"><p>If the head is really chewed up, use a screw extractor. Drill a small pilot hole, then turn the extractor counterclockwise.</p></div></details><div class="callout warn"><p>Last resort: drill off the head, remove the item, and unscrew the shaft with pliers. Wear eye protection.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Rescue a Stripped Screw</h1><p class="muted">, a stripped screw is a stubborn guest. Here's how to evict it, gentlest first.</p><div class="stack"><details class="acc"><summary>Rubber band trick</summary><div class="body"><p>Place a wide rubber band over the screw head, press your screwdriver in, and turn slowly. The rubber fills the gaps and adds grip.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Try a different driver</summary><div class="body"><p>Sometimes a slightly larger flathead or a star bit grabs where the original failed. Push down hard while turning.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Extractor bit</summary><div class="body"><p>If the head is really chewed up, use a screw extractor. Drill a small pilot hole, then turn the extractor counterclockwise.</p></div></details><div class="callout warn"><p>Last resort: drill off the head, remove the item, and unscrew the shaft with pliers. Wear eye protection.</p></div></div></div>	-
39.17.0	dashboard_faq/Fold a T-Shirt in Two Seconds	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Fold a T-Shirt in Two Seconds</h1><p class="muted"><regex>user_name</regex>, this is the classic pinch-and-flip. It looks like a magic trick and feels like one.</p><div class="stack"><details class="acc"><summary>Step by step</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Lay the shirt flat, front side up.</li><li>Pinch a point on the shoulder, about a third of the way in from the collar.</li><li>Pinch the matching point on the bottom hem directly below it.</li><li>Cross the bottom pinch over the top pinch, then lift both.</li><li>The shirt folds itself. Lay it down and smooth.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Why it works</summary><div class="body"><p>You're creating a fold line along the shirt's natural drape. Gravity does the rest.</p></div></details><div class="callout good"><p>Great for travel. You can fold a whole drawer in a minute once you get the rhythm.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Fold a T-Shirt in Two Seconds</h1><p class="muted">, this is the classic pinch-and-flip. It looks like a magic trick and feels like one.</p><div class="stack"><details class="acc"><summary>Step by step</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Lay the shirt flat, front side up.</li><li>Pinch a point on the shoulder, about a third of the way in from the collar.</li><li>Pinch the matching point on the bottom hem directly below it.</li><li>Cross the bottom pinch over the top pinch, then lift both.</li><li>The shirt folds itself. Lay it down and smooth.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Why it works</summary><div class="body"><p>You're creating a fold line along the shirt's natural drape. Gravity does the rest.</p></div></details><div class="callout good"><p>Great for travel. You can fold a whole drawer in a minute once you get the rhythm.</p></div></div></div>	-
39.18.0	dashboard_faq/Stop Junk Mail	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Stop Junk Mail</h1><p class="muted">Five minutes now, a lighter mailbox forever.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Where do I start?</summary><div class="body"><p>Two free opt-outs cover most of it:</p><ol class="list"><li><b>Prescreened credit offers</b> — optoutprescreen.com or 1-888-5-OPTOUT. Kills insurance and card offers for five years.</li><li><b>DMAchoice</b> — dmachoice.org, small fee, handles catalogs and coupon packs.</li></ol><p class="dim t-small">Both take effect in about 4–6 weeks. Be patient.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Catalogs keep coming anyway</summary><div class="body"><p>Call the 800 number printed on the catalog's back cover and ask for "permanent removal." It's faster than any registry, and they honor it. One call per brand.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What about mail for old tenants?</summary><div class="body"><p>Write "Not at this address — return to sender" on the envelope and drop it in a blue box. Don't open it; that's a federal hobby you don't want.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Do I need to worry about anything important?</summary><div class="body"><div class="callout warn">Never opt out of anything from a bank, lender, or government agency you actually use. When in doubt, open it first.</div></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Any ongoing tricks?</summary><div class="body"><p>Keep a "junk" tray by the door, sort weekly, and cancel as you go. <regex>user_name</regex>, the mailbox gets quiet faster than you'd think.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Stop Junk Mail</h1><p class="muted">Five minutes now, a lighter mailbox forever.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Where do I start?</summary><div class="body"><p>Two free opt-outs cover most of it:</p><ol class="list"><li><b>Prescreened credit offers</b> — optoutprescreen.com or 1-888-5-OPTOUT. Kills insurance and card offers for five years.</li><li><b>DMAchoice</b> — dmachoice.org, small fee, handles catalogs and coupon packs.</li></ol><p class="dim t-small">Both take effect in about 4–6 weeks. Be patient.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Catalogs keep coming anyway</summary><div class="body"><p>Call the 800 number printed on the catalog's back cover and ask for "permanent removal." It's faster than any registry, and they honor it. One call per brand.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What about mail for old tenants?</summary><div class="body"><p>Write "Not at this address — return to sender" on the envelope and drop it in a blue box. Don't open it; that's a federal hobby you don't want.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Do I need to worry about anything important?</summary><div class="body"><div class="callout warn">Never opt out of anything from a bank, lender, or government agency you actually use. When in doubt, open it first.</div></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Any ongoing tricks?</summary><div class="body"><p>Keep a "junk" tray by the door, sort weekly, and cancel as you go. , the mailbox gets quiet faster than you'd think.</p></div></details></div>	-
39.19.0	dashboard_faq/Unclog a Vacuum	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Unclog a Vacuum</h1><p class="muted">Loss of suction is almost always one of four things.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Check the filter first</summary><div class="body"><p>Pull it out and look through it against a light. If light won't pass, neither will air. Rinse foam filters in cool water and dry <b>24 hours</b> — a damp filter smells like a wet dog and can damage the motor.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Then the hose and wand</summary><div class="body"><p>Drop a coin or a small screw down the hose and listen for it landing. No sound? Something's stuck. A broom handle or a straightened coat hanger clears most blockages. Check the elbow bend, where clogs love to hide.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>The brush roll</summary><div class="body"><p>Flip the vacuum and cut hair off the roller with scissors. Long hair wrapped tight is the #1 cause of a burning smell. Never pull it with your hands.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Burning smell — is it dead?</summary><div class="body"><div class="callout bad">Stop using it. A burnt smell usually means a stretched belt or a hot motor. Replace the belt (cheap), and if the smell returns, retire the vacuum.</div></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How often should I do all this?</summary><div class="body"><p>Filters monthly, brush roll every few weeks, hose only when suction drops. <regex>user_name</regex>, a two-minute habit beats a new vacuum every year.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Unclog a Vacuum</h1><p class="muted">Loss of suction is almost always one of four things.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Check the filter first</summary><div class="body"><p>Pull it out and look through it against a light. If light won't pass, neither will air. Rinse foam filters in cool water and dry <b>24 hours</b> — a damp filter smells like a wet dog and can damage the motor.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Then the hose and wand</summary><div class="body"><p>Drop a coin or a small screw down the hose and listen for it landing. No sound? Something's stuck. A broom handle or a straightened coat hanger clears most blockages. Check the elbow bend, where clogs love to hide.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>The brush roll</summary><div class="body"><p>Flip the vacuum and cut hair off the roller with scissors. Long hair wrapped tight is the #1 cause of a burning smell. Never pull it with your hands.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Burning smell — is it dead?</summary><div class="body"><div class="callout bad">Stop using it. A burnt smell usually means a stretched belt or a hot motor. Replace the belt (cheap), and if the smell returns, retire the vacuum.</div></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How often should I do all this?</summary><div class="body"><p>Filters monthly, brush roll every few weeks, hose only when suction drops. , a two-minute habit beats a new vacuum every year.</p></div></details></div>	-
39.20.0	dashboard_faq/Clean a Cast Iron Pan	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Clean a Cast Iron Pan</h1><p class="muted">The pan your grandkids will fight over, if you treat it right.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Can I use soap?</summary><div class="body"><p>Yes, a little. Modern dish soap won't strip seasoning. What wrecks it is soaking, scraping with metal, or leaving it wet.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Everyday cleaning</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Wash while still warm with hot water and a stiff brush.</li><li>Dry on the stove over low heat for 2 minutes.</li><li>Wipe a <b>thin</b> film of oil across the whole pan, then buff it off.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>It's rusty. Now what?</summary><div class="body"><p>Scour the rust off with steel wool or salt and oil, wash, dry, then re-season. Rust is a surface problem, not a funeral.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I season it?</summary><div class="body"><p>Rub a very thin layer of flaxseed or canola oil all over, inside and out, then bake upside down at 450°F for an hour. Repeat 3–4 times. Thin coats, hot oven, patience.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Sticky spots?</summary><div class="body"><p>That's too much oil baked on. Scrub it back to bare metal in that spot and season again. <regex>user_name</regex>, less oil than you think.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Clean a Cast Iron Pan</h1><p class="muted">The pan your grandkids will fight over, if you treat it right.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Can I use soap?</summary><div class="body"><p>Yes, a little. Modern dish soap won't strip seasoning. What wrecks it is soaking, scraping with metal, or leaving it wet.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Everyday cleaning</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Wash while still warm with hot water and a stiff brush.</li><li>Dry on the stove over low heat for 2 minutes.</li><li>Wipe a <b>thin</b> film of oil across the whole pan, then buff it off.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>It's rusty. Now what?</summary><div class="body"><p>Scour the rust off with steel wool or salt and oil, wash, dry, then re-season. Rust is a surface problem, not a funeral.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I season it?</summary><div class="body"><p>Rub a very thin layer of flaxseed or canola oil all over, inside and out, then bake upside down at 450°F for an hour. Repeat 3–4 times. Thin coats, hot oven, patience.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Sticky spots?</summary><div class="body"><p>That's too much oil baked on. Scrub it back to bare metal in that spot and season again. , less oil than you think.</p></div></details></div>	-
39.21.0	dashboard_faq/Fix a Noisy Door	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Fix a Noisy Door</h1><p class="muted">A squeak is just metal asking for a drink.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Quick squeak fix</summary><div class="body"><p>Pull the hinge pin out with a screwdriver, wipe it clean, rub it with a little petroleum jelly or white lithium grease, and slide it back. Do all three hinges — the squeak often comes from the one you didn't suspect.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Do NOT use cooking spray</summary><div class="body"><div class="callout warn">Oil from the kitchen turns gummy and attracts dust. You'll trade a squeak for a grinding sound in a month.</div></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Door sticks near the top or bottom</summary><div class="body"><p>Tighten every hinge screw first. If one spins loose, fill the hole with a wooden matchstick or toothpick dipped in glue, snap it off, and re-drive the screw.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>It rubs along the whole frame</summary><div class="body"><p>The house has shifted or the door swelled. Lightly sand the sticking edge, seal it with primer, and check again in a week. Seasonal swelling often fixes itself.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Still groaning?</summary><div class="body"><p>Check the strike plate too — a rubbing latch sounds like a hinge from across the room. <regex>user_name</regex>, give it all a listen with the door half open.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Fix a Noisy Door</h1><p class="muted">A squeak is just metal asking for a drink.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Quick squeak fix</summary><div class="body"><p>Pull the hinge pin out with a screwdriver, wipe it clean, rub it with a little petroleum jelly or white lithium grease, and slide it back. Do all three hinges — the squeak often comes from the one you didn't suspect.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Do NOT use cooking spray</summary><div class="body"><div class="callout warn">Oil from the kitchen turns gummy and attracts dust. You'll trade a squeak for a grinding sound in a month.</div></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Door sticks near the top or bottom</summary><div class="body"><p>Tighten every hinge screw first. If one spins loose, fill the hole with a wooden matchstick or toothpick dipped in glue, snap it off, and re-drive the screw.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>It rubs along the whole frame</summary><div class="body"><p>The house has shifted or the door swelled. Lightly sand the sticking edge, seal it with primer, and check again in a week. Seasonal swelling often fixes itself.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Still groaning?</summary><div class="body"><p>Check the strike plate too — a rubbing latch sounds like a hinge from across the room. , give it all a listen with the door half open.</p></div></details></div>	-
39.22.0	dashboard_faq/Revive Dead Batteries	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Revive Dead Batteries</h1><p class="muted">You can't bring a battery back to life. You can make the next one last.</p><details class="acc"><summary>What's actually killing it?</summary><div class="body"><p>Heat, mostly. A phone left on a hot dashboard ages faster in one afternoon than a month of normal use. Screen brightness and background location are the other two big draws.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Charging habits that help</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Stay between 20% and 80% when you can.</li><li>Skip the overnight trickle if your phone charges fast.</li><li>Take the case off while fast-charging — heat has nowhere to go.</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Does a new charger matter?</summary><div class="body"><p>Use the one that came with your device or a certified equivalent. Cheap unbranded bricks can overheat the battery quietly.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Is my battery already worn out?</summary><div class="body"><p>Check the battery health in settings. Under 80% of original capacity means it's near the end. Replacement is usually cheap and often worth it over a new device.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>One weird trick?</summary><div class="body"><p>Lower the screen brightness to about 50% and turn off always-on display. <regex>user_name</regex>, that alone buys most people an extra hour.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Revive Dead Batteries</h1><p class="muted">You can't bring a battery back to life. You can make the next one last.</p><details class="acc"><summary>What's actually killing it?</summary><div class="body"><p>Heat, mostly. A phone left on a hot dashboard ages faster in one afternoon than a month of normal use. Screen brightness and background location are the other two big draws.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Charging habits that help</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Stay between 20% and 80% when you can.</li><li>Skip the overnight trickle if your phone charges fast.</li><li>Take the case off while fast-charging — heat has nowhere to go.</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Does a new charger matter?</summary><div class="body"><p>Use the one that came with your device or a certified equivalent. Cheap unbranded bricks can overheat the battery quietly.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Is my battery already worn out?</summary><div class="body"><p>Check the battery health in settings. Under 80% of original capacity means it's near the end. Replacement is usually cheap and often worth it over a new device.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>One weird trick?</summary><div class="body"><p>Lower the screen brightness to about 50% and turn off always-on display. , that alone buys most people an extra hour.</p></div></details></div>	-
39.23.0	dashboard_faq/Get Out a Grease Stain	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Get Out a Grease Stain</h1><p class="muted">Grease needs to be dissolved, not scrubbed. Heat sets it, so act cool.</p><details class="acc"><summary>First move, right now</summary><div class="body"><p>Blot, don't rub. Sprinkle cornstarch or baking soda on the spot, let it sit 15 minutes, then brush it off. That pulls out the surface oil before it spreads.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>The dish soap method</summary><div class="body"><p>Rub a drop of grease-cutting dish soap into the stain with your fingertips — no water yet. Let it sit 10 minutes, then rinse with <b>cold</b> water. Repeat if needed. This is the workhorse.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Old or set-in stains</summary><div class="body"><p>Work in a paste of dish soap and baking soda, wait 30 minutes, then wash as usual. For very old stains, a degreaser made for laundry works better than anything in the kitchen.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>On a couch or carpet?</summary><div class="body"><p>Blot with dish soap and warm water, then rinse with a clean damp cloth. Don't soak the cushion. Test on a hidden corner first.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Before you dry it</summary><div class="body"><div class="callout info">If you can still see the spot, don't put it in the dryer. Heat locks the stain in like a decision you can't take back. Air dry and try again.</div></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>If it's already been dried</summary><div class="body"><p>Rinse with hot water and repeat the soap treatment. <regex>user_name</regex>, dried-in grease is stubborn, but it's not gone yet.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Get Out a Grease Stain</h1><p class="muted">Grease needs to be dissolved, not scrubbed. Heat sets it, so act cool.</p><details class="acc"><summary>First move, right now</summary><div class="body"><p>Blot, don't rub. Sprinkle cornstarch or baking soda on the spot, let it sit 15 minutes, then brush it off. That pulls out the surface oil before it spreads.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>The dish soap method</summary><div class="body"><p>Rub a drop of grease-cutting dish soap into the stain with your fingertips — no water yet. Let it sit 10 minutes, then rinse with <b>cold</b> water. Repeat if needed. This is the workhorse.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Old or set-in stains</summary><div class="body"><p>Work in a paste of dish soap and baking soda, wait 30 minutes, then wash as usual. For very old stains, a degreaser made for laundry works better than anything in the kitchen.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>On a couch or carpet?</summary><div class="body"><p>Blot with dish soap and warm water, then rinse with a clean damp cloth. Don't soak the cushion. Test on a hidden corner first.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Before you dry it</summary><div class="body"><div class="callout info">If you can still see the spot, don't put it in the dryer. Heat locks the stain in like a decision you can't take back. Air dry and try again.</div></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>If it's already been dried</summary><div class="body"><p>Rinse with hot water and repeat the soap treatment. , dried-in grease is stubborn, but it's not gone yet.</p></div></details></div>	-
39.24.0	dashboard_faq/Keep Bread From Molding	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Keep Bread From Molding</h1><article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>Bread molds fast because it's warm, moist, and sealed in plastic. Here's how to slow that down, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></article><details class="acc"><summary>Why does bread mold so fast?</summary><div class="body"><p>Mold spores are already on the crust. Trapped moisture from a warm loaf creates a perfect breeding ground. Plastic bags lock that moisture in.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Where should I store it?</summary><div class="body"><p>A bread box or paper bag keeps the crust dry and lets air circulate. Avoid the fridge — it makes bread stale faster. If you must freeze, slice first and wrap tight.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How long does it last?</summary><div class="body"><p>At room temp in a paper bag: 2–3 days. In a bread box: up to 4. Freezer: 3 months. Fridge: don't.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Can I save a slightly moldy loaf?</summary><div class="body"><p>No. If you see mold, the roots have spread. Toss it. Better safe than sorry.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Keep Bread From Molding</h1><article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>Bread molds fast because it's warm, moist, and sealed in plastic. Here's how to slow that down, .</p></article><details class="acc"><summary>Why does bread mold so fast?</summary><div class="body"><p>Mold spores are already on the crust. Trapped moisture from a warm loaf creates a perfect breeding ground. Plastic bags lock that moisture in.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Where should I store it?</summary><div class="body"><p>A bread box or paper bag keeps the crust dry and lets air circulate. Avoid the fridge — it makes bread stale faster. If you must freeze, slice first and wrap tight.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How long does it last?</summary><div class="body"><p>At room temp in a paper bag: 2–3 days. In a bread box: up to 4. Freezer: 3 months. Fridge: don't.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Can I save a slightly moldy loaf?</summary><div class="body"><p>No. If you see mold, the roots have spread. Toss it. Better safe than sorry.</p></div></details></div>	-
39.25.0	dashboard_faq/Steam-Clean Your Microwave	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Steam-Clean Your Microwave</h1><article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>No scrubbing, no harsh fumes. Just water, vinegar, and five minutes, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></article><details class="acc"><summary>What you need</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>1 cup water</li><li>1 cup white vinegar</li><li>Microwave-safe bowl</li><li>Sponge or cloth</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Step by step</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Mix water and vinegar in the bowl.</li><li>Microwave on high for 5 minutes. The liquid should boil and steam up the window.</li><li>Let it sit for 2–3 minutes. Don't open the door yet — the steam does the work.</li><li>Remove the bowl carefully. Wipe the inside with a damp sponge. Everything slides off.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Why vinegar?</summary><div class="body"><p>Vinegar cuts grease and kills odors. The steam loosens dried-on food so you don't have to scrape.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What about the turntable?</summary><div class="body"><p>Take it out and wash it in the sink with warm soapy water. Let it dry before putting it back.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Steam-Clean Your Microwave</h1><article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>No scrubbing, no harsh fumes. Just water, vinegar, and five minutes, .</p></article><details class="acc"><summary>What you need</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>1 cup water</li><li>1 cup white vinegar</li><li>Microwave-safe bowl</li><li>Sponge or cloth</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Step by step</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Mix water and vinegar in the bowl.</li><li>Microwave on high for 5 minutes. The liquid should boil and steam up the window.</li><li>Let it sit for 2–3 minutes. Don't open the door yet — the steam does the work.</li><li>Remove the bowl carefully. Wipe the inside with a damp sponge. Everything slides off.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Why vinegar?</summary><div class="body"><p>Vinegar cuts grease and kills odors. The steam loosens dried-on food so you don't have to scrape.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What about the turntable?</summary><div class="body"><p>Take it out and wash it in the sink with warm soapy water. Let it dry before putting it back.</p></div></details></div>	-
39.26.0	dashboard_faq/Banish Fruit Flies	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Banish Fruit Flies</h1><article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>Those tiny drifters are after ripe fruit and damp drains. Here's how to evict them, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></article><details class="acc"><summary>Why are they here?</summary><div class="body"><p>They lay eggs on overripe fruit, in recycling bins, and in the gunk inside drains. One forgotten banana can hatch a swarm.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>DIY trap that works</summary><div class="body"><p>Put a splash of apple cider vinegar in a small bowl. Add a drop of dish soap. Cover with plastic wrap and poke a few holes. They crawl in and can't get out.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Stop them at the source</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Refrigerate ripe fruit.</li><li>Take out recycling daily.</li><li>Pour boiling water down drains once a week.</li><li>Wipe up spills immediately.</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How long until they're gone?</summary><div class="body"><p>With traps and no new food sources, about 3–5 days. Be patient — they multiply fast but die fast too.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Banish Fruit Flies</h1><article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>Those tiny drifters are after ripe fruit and damp drains. Here's how to evict them, .</p></article><details class="acc"><summary>Why are they here?</summary><div class="body"><p>They lay eggs on overripe fruit, in recycling bins, and in the gunk inside drains. One forgotten banana can hatch a swarm.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>DIY trap that works</summary><div class="body"><p>Put a splash of apple cider vinegar in a small bowl. Add a drop of dish soap. Cover with plastic wrap and poke a few holes. They crawl in and can't get out.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Stop them at the source</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Refrigerate ripe fruit.</li><li>Take out recycling daily.</li><li>Pour boiling water down drains once a week.</li><li>Wipe up spills immediately.</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How long until they're gone?</summary><div class="body"><p>With traps and no new food sources, about 3–5 days. Be patient — they multiply fast but die fast too.</p></div></details></div>	-
39.27.0	dashboard_faq/Fix a Sliding Zipper	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Fix a Sliding Zipper</h1><article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>A zipper that won't stay up is usually just loose. You can tighten it in seconds, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></article><details class="acc"><summary>Why does it slide down?</summary><div class="body"><p>The slider (the part you pull) has widened over time. It no longer grips the teeth. A quick squeeze fixes that.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>The pliers trick</summary><div class="body"><p>Move the slider to the bottom of the zipper. Use needle-nose pliers to gently squeeze the top and bottom of the slider. Don't crush it — just a firm press. Test the zipper. Repeat if needed.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>If that doesn't work</summary><div class="body"><p>Rub a graphite pencil along the teeth. The graphite adds friction. Or replace the slider — they're cheap and sold at craft stores.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Prevention</summary><div class="body"><p>Don't yank the zipper. Pull straight, not at an angle. And keep it clean — grit wears down the slider.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Fix a Sliding Zipper</h1><article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>A zipper that won't stay up is usually just loose. You can tighten it in seconds, .</p></article><details class="acc"><summary>Why does it slide down?</summary><div class="body"><p>The slider (the part you pull) has widened over time. It no longer grips the teeth. A quick squeeze fixes that.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>The pliers trick</summary><div class="body"><p>Move the slider to the bottom of the zipper. Use needle-nose pliers to gently squeeze the top and bottom of the slider. Don't crush it — just a firm press. Test the zipper. Repeat if needed.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>If that doesn't work</summary><div class="body"><p>Rub a graphite pencil along the teeth. The graphite adds friction. Or replace the slider — they're cheap and sold at craft stores.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Prevention</summary><div class="body"><p>Don't yank the zipper. Pull straight, not at an angle. And keep it clean — grit wears down the slider.</p></div></details></div>	-
39.28.0	dashboard_faq/Remove a Stripped Screw	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Remove a Stripped Screw</h1><article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>Stripped screw? Don't panic. A rubber band or a little patience will get it out, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></article><details class="acc"><summary>Try a rubber band first</summary><div class="body"><p>Place a wide rubber band flat over the screw head. Press your screwdriver into the rubber band and turn slowly. The rubber fills the stripped gaps and gives grip.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>If that fails: the rubber band + pressure method</summary><div class="body"><p>Use a screwdriver one size larger. Push down hard while turning counterclockwise. Sometimes the extra pressure is all it takes.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Last resort: drill it out</summary><div class="body"><p>Use a drill bit slightly smaller than the screw head. Drill into the center until the head pops off. Then remove the shaft with pliers. Only do this if you don't need the screw again.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Prevent future strips</summary><div class="body"><p>Always use the right size screwdriver. Push down before turning. And don't overtighten.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Remove a Stripped Screw</h1><article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>Stripped screw? Don't panic. A rubber band or a little patience will get it out, .</p></article><details class="acc"><summary>Try a rubber band first</summary><div class="body"><p>Place a wide rubber band flat over the screw head. Press your screwdriver into the rubber band and turn slowly. The rubber fills the stripped gaps and gives grip.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>If that fails: the rubber band + pressure method</summary><div class="body"><p>Use a screwdriver one size larger. Push down hard while turning counterclockwise. Sometimes the extra pressure is all it takes.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Last resort: drill it out</summary><div class="body"><p>Use a drill bit slightly smaller than the screw head. Drill into the center until the head pops off. Then remove the shaft with pliers. Only do this if you don't need the screw again.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Prevent future strips</summary><div class="body"><p>Always use the right size screwdriver. Push down before turning. And don't overtighten.</p></div></details></div>	-
39.29.0	dashboard_faq/Safe Tick Removal	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Safe Tick Removal</h1><p class="muted">Ticks are tiny, but the way you remove one matters. Do it right and you lower the risk of infection.</p><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">What you'll need</h2><ul class="list"><li>Fine-tipped tweezers</li><li>Rubbing alcohol or soap and water</li><li>A small jar or ziplock bag (to keep the tick for testing)</li><li>Antiseptic ointment</li></ul></div><div class="callout warn"><p><strong>Don't</strong> use petroleum jelly, nail polish, or a lit match. Those old tricks can make the tick regurgitate into your skin.</p></div><details class="acc"><summary>Step-by-step removal</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Grab the tick with tweezers as close to your skin as possible.</li><li>Pull straight up with steady, even pressure. Don't twist or jerk.</li><li>If mouthparts stay behind, remove them with clean tweezers. If you can't, leave them—your skin will push them out.</li><li>Clean the bite and your hands with alcohol or soap and water.</li><li>Save the tick in a jar or bag with a damp cotton ball. Note the date.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>When to see a doctor</summary><div class="body"><p>Watch for a rash (especially a bull's-eye), fever, headache, or muscle aches in the weeks after. If any appear, call your doctor and mention the tick bite. Early treatment is easy and effective.</p></div></details><div class="badge info"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Safe Tick Removal</h1><p class="muted">Ticks are tiny, but the way you remove one matters. Do it right and you lower the risk of infection.</p><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">What you'll need</h2><ul class="list"><li>Fine-tipped tweezers</li><li>Rubbing alcohol or soap and water</li><li>A small jar or ziplock bag (to keep the tick for testing)</li><li>Antiseptic ointment</li></ul></div><div class="callout warn"><p><strong>Don't</strong> use petroleum jelly, nail polish, or a lit match. Those old tricks can make the tick regurgitate into your skin.</p></div><details class="acc"><summary>Step-by-step removal</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Grab the tick with tweezers as close to your skin as possible.</li><li>Pull straight up with steady, even pressure. Don't twist or jerk.</li><li>If mouthparts stay behind, remove them with clean tweezers. If you can't, leave them—your skin will push them out.</li><li>Clean the bite and your hands with alcohol or soap and water.</li><li>Save the tick in a jar or bag with a damp cotton ball. Note the date.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>When to see a doctor</summary><div class="body"><p>Watch for a rash (especially a bull's-eye), fever, headache, or muscle aches in the weeks after. If any appear, call your doctor and mention the tick bite. Early treatment is easy and effective.</p></div></details><div class="badge info"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</div></div>	-
39.30.0	dashboard_faq/Erase Permanent Marker from a Whiteboard	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Erase Permanent Marker from a Whiteboard</h1><p class="muted">Someone grabbed the wrong pen. It happens. Here's how to undo it without ruining the board.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Method 1: Dry-erase marker over permanent</summary><div class="body"><p>Trace directly over the permanent ink with a fresh dry-erase marker. Let it sit for a few seconds, then wipe with a soft cloth. The solvent in the dry-erase ink lifts the permanent stuff. Works best on fresh stains.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Method 2: Rubbing alcohol</summary><div class="body"><p>Put a little rubbing alcohol (isopropyl) on a cloth or paper towel. Wipe gently. Don't pour it directly on the board—you don't want it running behind the frame.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Method 3: Hand sanitizer</summary><div class="body"><p>Gel hand sanitizer clings to the board and gives the alcohol time to work. Squeeze a dab on the mark, wait 10 seconds, then wipe. Great for vertical boards.</p></div></details><div class="callout good"><p><strong>Prevention tip:</strong> Keep a dedicated dry-erase marker near the board and hide the permanent ones. Or label them clearly.</p></div><div class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>quick fix</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Erase Permanent Marker from a Whiteboard</h1><p class="muted">Someone grabbed the wrong pen. It happens. Here's how to undo it without ruining the board.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Method 1: Dry-erase marker over permanent</summary><div class="body"><p>Trace directly over the permanent ink with a fresh dry-erase marker. Let it sit for a few seconds, then wipe with a soft cloth. The solvent in the dry-erase ink lifts the permanent stuff. Works best on fresh stains.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Method 2: Rubbing alcohol</summary><div class="body"><p>Put a little rubbing alcohol (isopropyl) on a cloth or paper towel. Wipe gently. Don't pour it directly on the board—you don't want it running behind the frame.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Method 3: Hand sanitizer</summary><div class="body"><p>Gel hand sanitizer clings to the board and gives the alcohol time to work. Squeeze a dab on the mark, wait 10 seconds, then wipe. Great for vertical boards.</p></div></details><div class="callout good"><p><strong>Prevention tip:</strong> Keep a dedicated dry-erase marker near the board and hide the permanent ones. Or label them clearly.</p></div><div class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>quick fix</div></div>	-
39.31.0	dashboard_faq/Fold a Burrito Without Blowouts	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Fold a Burrito Without Blowouts</h1><p class="muted">A good fold keeps everything in and nothing on your shirt.</p><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">The setup</h2><ul class="list"><li>Warm your tortilla so it's pliable—15 seconds per side in a dry pan.</li><li>Keep fillings in a line down the center, not piled to the edges.</li><li>Leave a 2-inch border on all sides.</li></ul></div><details class="acc"><summary>The fold, step by step</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Fold the left and right sides inward, over the filling.</li><li>Fold the bottom edge up and over, tucking it tight against the filling.</li><li>Roll forward, keeping tension, until the seam is on the bottom.</li><li>Let it rest seam-side down for a minute so the tortilla holds its shape.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>If it tears</summary><div class="body"><p>Don't panic. Lay a small piece of tortilla or a lettuce leaf over the tear, then continue rolling. Or call it a "burrito bowl" and grab a fork.</p></div></details><div class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Fold a Burrito Without Blowouts</h1><p class="muted">A good fold keeps everything in and nothing on your shirt.</p><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">The setup</h2><ul class="list"><li>Warm your tortilla so it's pliable—15 seconds per side in a dry pan.</li><li>Keep fillings in a line down the center, not piled to the edges.</li><li>Leave a 2-inch border on all sides.</li></ul></div><details class="acc"><summary>The fold, step by step</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Fold the left and right sides inward, over the filling.</li><li>Fold the bottom edge up and over, tucking it tight against the filling.</li><li>Roll forward, keeping tension, until the seam is on the bottom.</li><li>Let it rest seam-side down for a minute so the tortilla holds its shape.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>If it tears</summary><div class="body"><p>Don't panic. Lay a small piece of tortilla or a lettuce leaf over the tear, then continue rolling. Or call it a "burrito bowl" and grab a fork.</p></div></details><div class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</div></div>	-
39.32.0	dashboard_faq/Clean a Coffee Grinder	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Clean a Coffee Grinder</h1><p class="muted">Old grounds go stale and make fresh coffee taste off. A quick clean keeps every cup tasting like the first.</p><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">What you'll need</h2><ul class="list"><li>Dry uncooked rice (for blade grinders) or grinder cleaning pellets (for burr grinders)</li><li>A small brush or toothbrush</li><li>A damp cloth</li><li>Optional: a vacuum with a brush attachment</li></ul></div><details class="acc"><summary>Blade grinder</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Unplug the grinder.</li><li>Wipe the bowl and blades with a damp cloth. Don't submerge the base.</li><li>Add 1/4 cup uncooked rice and pulse until powdery. The rice absorbs oils and smells.</li><li>Dump the rice, wipe again, and let it air dry completely.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Burr grinder</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Unplug and empty the hopper and grounds bin.</li><li>Remove the top burr if your model allows.</li><li>Brush away grounds from the burrs and chute. Never wash burrs with water—moisture rusts them.</li><li>Run a handful of cleaning pellets or a few tablespoons of rice through on a coarse setting. Discard.</li><li>Reassemble and run a small amount of coffee through to season before brewing.</li></ol></div></details><div class="callout info"><p><strong>How often?</strong> Every few weeks for daily use, or whenever you switch beans and notice a flavor carryover.</p></div><div class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Clean a Coffee Grinder</h1><p class="muted">Old grounds go stale and make fresh coffee taste off. A quick clean keeps every cup tasting like the first.</p><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">What you'll need</h2><ul class="list"><li>Dry uncooked rice (for blade grinders) or grinder cleaning pellets (for burr grinders)</li><li>A small brush or toothbrush</li><li>A damp cloth</li><li>Optional: a vacuum with a brush attachment</li></ul></div><details class="acc"><summary>Blade grinder</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Unplug the grinder.</li><li>Wipe the bowl and blades with a damp cloth. Don't submerge the base.</li><li>Add 1/4 cup uncooked rice and pulse until powdery. The rice absorbs oils and smells.</li><li>Dump the rice, wipe again, and let it air dry completely.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Burr grinder</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Unplug and empty the hopper and grounds bin.</li><li>Remove the top burr if your model allows.</li><li>Brush away grounds from the burrs and chute. Never wash burrs with water—moisture rusts them.</li><li>Run a handful of cleaning pellets or a few tablespoons of rice through on a coarse setting. Discard.</li><li>Reassemble and run a small amount of coffee through to season before brewing.</li></ol></div></details><div class="callout info"><p><strong>How often?</strong> Every few weeks for daily use, or whenever you switch beans and notice a flavor carryover.</p></div><div class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</div></div>	-
39.33.0	dashboard_faq/Unclog a Toilet Without a Plunger	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Unclog a Toilet Without a Plunger</h1><p class="muted">No plunger? No problem. These tricks use stuff you probably already have.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Hot water and dish soap</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Squirt a generous amount of dish soap into the bowl.</li><li>Let it sit for 10–15 minutes to lubricate the clog.</li><li>Heat a large pot of water—hot but not boiling (boiling can crack porcelain).</li><li>Pour it slowly from waist height into the bowl. The pressure and soap often do the trick.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>DIY snake with a wire hanger</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Straighten a wire coat hanger, leaving a small hook at one end.</li><li>Push the hooked end into the drain, fishing around gently.</li><li>Once you feel resistance, pull up with a twisting motion to dislodge the clog.</li><li>Flush with hot water to clear the rest.</li></ol></div></details><div class="callout warn"><p><strong>Don't</strong> use chemical drain cleaners as a first resort. They can damage pipes and are dangerous if they splash back. Try the gentle methods first.</p></div><div class="badge info"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Unclog a Toilet Without a Plunger</h1><p class="muted">No plunger? No problem. These tricks use stuff you probably already have.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Hot water and dish soap</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Squirt a generous amount of dish soap into the bowl.</li><li>Let it sit for 10–15 minutes to lubricate the clog.</li><li>Heat a large pot of water—hot but not boiling (boiling can crack porcelain).</li><li>Pour it slowly from waist height into the bowl. The pressure and soap often do the trick.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>DIY snake with a wire hanger</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Straighten a wire coat hanger, leaving a small hook at one end.</li><li>Push the hooked end into the drain, fishing around gently.</li><li>Once you feel resistance, pull up with a twisting motion to dislodge the clog.</li><li>Flush with hot water to clear the rest.</li></ol></div></details><div class="callout warn"><p><strong>Don't</strong> use chemical drain cleaners as a first resort. They can damage pipes and are dangerous if they splash back. Try the gentle methods first.</p></div><div class="badge info"><i class="dot"></i>cached match</div></div>	-
39.34.0	dashboard_faq/Remove Sticker Residue from Glass	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Remove Sticker Residue from Glass</h1><p class="muted">That sticky ghost of a label doesn't have to stay. Here's how to get glass clean again.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Oil and baking soda paste</summary><div class="body"><p>Mix a few drops of cooking oil with a spoonful of baking soda to make a paste. Rub it onto the residue with your fingers or a cloth. Let it sit for 5 minutes, then wipe clean. The oil breaks down the adhesive, and the baking soda adds gentle grit.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Rubbing alcohol or vinegar</summary><div class="body"><p>Soak a cotton ball or cloth in rubbing alcohol or white vinegar. Press it against the residue for a minute, then rub. Alcohol works faster on stubborn glue; vinegar is great for light residue.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Heat and peel</summary><div class="body"><p>For big stickers, warm the glass with a hair dryer on low for 30 seconds. The adhesive softens, and the sticker often peels off in one piece. Wipe away any leftover goo with oil or alcohol.</p></div></details><div class="callout good"><p><strong>Prevention:</strong> When you buy something with a label, peel it off while the glass is warm from washing. It comes off much easier.</p></div><div class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>quick fix</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Remove Sticker Residue from Glass</h1><p class="muted">That sticky ghost of a label doesn't have to stay. Here's how to get glass clean again.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Oil and baking soda paste</summary><div class="body"><p>Mix a few drops of cooking oil with a spoonful of baking soda to make a paste. Rub it onto the residue with your fingers or a cloth. Let it sit for 5 minutes, then wipe clean. The oil breaks down the adhesive, and the baking soda adds gentle grit.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Rubbing alcohol or vinegar</summary><div class="body"><p>Soak a cotton ball or cloth in rubbing alcohol or white vinegar. Press it against the residue for a minute, then rub. Alcohol works faster on stubborn glue; vinegar is great for light residue.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Heat and peel</summary><div class="body"><p>For big stickers, warm the glass with a hair dryer on low for 30 seconds. The adhesive softens, and the sticker often peels off in one piece. Wipe away any leftover goo with oil or alcohol.</p></div></details><div class="callout good"><p><strong>Prevention:</strong> When you buy something with a label, peel it off while the glass is warm from washing. It comes off much easier.</p></div><div class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>quick fix</div></div>	-
39.35.0	dashboard_faq/Silence a Running Toilet	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Silence a Running Toilet</h1><p class="muted">That hiss after every flush? It's just one part. <regex>user_name</regex>, you can fix it.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Quick fix first: jiggle the handle</summary><div class="body">Lift the tank lid. Push the flapper down with your hand. If the hissing stops, the flapper isn't sealing. A 5-minute fix: clean the rim of the flush valve and the flapper with a cloth. If it's warped, replace the flapper ($5 part).</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Adjust the fill valve</summary><div class="body">If the water level is above the overflow tube, turn the adjustment screw or clip on the fill valve to lower the float. Target: water sits about 1 inch below the top of the overflow tube.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Replace the flapper</summary><div class="body">Turn off the water at the wall. Flush to empty the tank. Unclip the old flapper and take it to the hardware store for an exact match. Clip on the new one. Turn water back on and test.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>When to call a plumber</summary><div class="body">If the tank itself is cracked, or the leak is at the base of the toilet (water on the floor), stop and call someone. That's not a DIY fix.</div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Silence a Running Toilet</h1><p class="muted">That hiss after every flush? It's just one part. , you can fix it.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Quick fix first: jiggle the handle</summary><div class="body">Lift the tank lid. Push the flapper down with your hand. If the hissing stops, the flapper isn't sealing. A 5-minute fix: clean the rim of the flush valve and the flapper with a cloth. If it's warped, replace the flapper ($5 part).</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Adjust the fill valve</summary><div class="body">If the water level is above the overflow tube, turn the adjustment screw or clip on the fill valve to lower the float. Target: water sits about 1 inch below the top of the overflow tube.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Replace the flapper</summary><div class="body">Turn off the water at the wall. Flush to empty the tank. Unclip the old flapper and take it to the hardware store for an exact match. Clip on the new one. Turn water back on and test.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>When to call a plumber</summary><div class="body">If the tank itself is cracked, or the leak is at the base of the toilet (water on the floor), stop and call someone. That's not a DIY fix.</div></details></div>	-
39.36.0	dashboard_faq/Clean White Sneakers	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Clean White Sneakers</h1><p class="muted">Before you throw them in the wash, try this. <regex>user_name</regex>, your shoes will thank you.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Remove laces and insoles</summary><div class="body">Wash laces by hand with dish soap and warm water. Sprinkle baking soda on insoles and let sit overnight to absorb odors.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Make a paste</summary><div class="body">Mix 1 tablespoon baking soda, 1 tablespoon white vinegar, and a few drops of dish soap. It will fizz. That's normal.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Scrub gently</summary><div class="body">Use an old toothbrush. Work the paste into the fabric in circles. Don't soak the shoes. Wipe with a damp cloth.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Air dry (no sun!)</summary><div class="body">Stuff with paper towels to hold shape. Air dry indoors. Direct sunlight can yellow the soles.</div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Clean White Sneakers</h1><p class="muted">Before you throw them in the wash, try this. , your shoes will thank you.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Remove laces and insoles</summary><div class="body">Wash laces by hand with dish soap and warm water. Sprinkle baking soda on insoles and let sit overnight to absorb odors.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Make a paste</summary><div class="body">Mix 1 tablespoon baking soda, 1 tablespoon white vinegar, and a few drops of dish soap. It will fizz. That's normal.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Scrub gently</summary><div class="body">Use an old toothbrush. Work the paste into the fabric in circles. Don't soak the shoes. Wipe with a damp cloth.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Air dry (no sun!)</summary><div class="body">Stuff with paper towels to hold shape. Air dry indoors. Direct sunlight can yellow the soles.</div></details></div>	-
39.37.0	dashboard_faq/Remove Blood Stains	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Remove Blood Stains</h1><p class="muted">Cold water only. Hot water sets the stain. <regex>user_name</regex>, here's the drill.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Act fast (but don't panic)</summary><div class="body">Blot with a clean cloth and cold water. Never rub. Rubbing pushes blood deeper into fibers.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>For fresh stains</summary><div class="body">Soak in cold water with a tablespoon of salt for 30 minutes. Then wash as usual with cold water.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>For dried stains</summary><div class="body">Apply a paste of cornstarch and cold water. Let it dry, then brush off. Follow with a cold-water soak and a dab of dish soap.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What not to do</summary><div class="body">No hot water. No iron. No dryer until the stain is gone. Heat makes it permanent.</div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Remove Blood Stains</h1><p class="muted">Cold water only. Hot water sets the stain. , here's the drill.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Act fast (but don't panic)</summary><div class="body">Blot with a clean cloth and cold water. Never rub. Rubbing pushes blood deeper into fibers.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>For fresh stains</summary><div class="body">Soak in cold water with a tablespoon of salt for 30 minutes. Then wash as usual with cold water.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>For dried stains</summary><div class="body">Apply a paste of cornstarch and cold water. Let it dry, then brush off. Follow with a cold-water soak and a dab of dish soap.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What not to do</summary><div class="body">No hot water. No iron. No dryer until the stain is gone. Heat makes it permanent.</div></details></div>	-
39.38.0	dashboard_faq/Free a Stuck Zipper	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Free a Stuck Zipper</h1><p class="muted">Don't yank. <regex>user_name</regex>, try these first.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Check for fabric</summary><div class="body">Shine a light on the zipper. Often a thread or lining is caught. Use a pin to gently work it out. Never pull the slider against the jam.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Lubricate with graphite</summary><div class="body">Rub a pencil lead along the teeth. The graphite is a dry lubricant that won't stain fabric. Work the slider back and forth.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Use lip balm or soap</summary><div class="body">In a pinch, run a waxed lip balm or a dry bar of soap along the teeth. Avoid oil-based products that can stain.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>If the slider is stuck</summary><div class="body">Grip the slider with pliers (gently) and rock it side to side while pulling down. If the zipper is broken, a tailor can replace it for a few dollars.</div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Free a Stuck Zipper</h1><p class="muted">Don't yank. , try these first.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Check for fabric</summary><div class="body">Shine a light on the zipper. Often a thread or lining is caught. Use a pin to gently work it out. Never pull the slider against the jam.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Lubricate with graphite</summary><div class="body">Rub a pencil lead along the teeth. The graphite is a dry lubricant that won't stain fabric. Work the slider back and forth.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Use lip balm or soap</summary><div class="body">In a pinch, run a waxed lip balm or a dry bar of soap along the teeth. Avoid oil-based products that can stain.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>If the slider is stuck</summary><div class="body">Grip the slider with pliers (gently) and rock it side to side while pulling down. If the zipper is broken, a tailor can replace it for a few dollars.</div></details></div>	-
39.39.0	dashboard_faq/Banish Kitchen Ants	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Banish Kitchen Ants</h1><p class="muted">You don't need harsh sprays. <regex>user_name</regex>, here's a trail-stopping trick.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Find the trail</summary><div class="body">Watch where they go. Ants leave a pheromone trail. Wipe it with vinegar and water (1:1) to erase the scent.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Make a bait</summary><div class="body">Mix 1 part borax with 3 parts sugar. Add a little water to make a paste. Put it on a piece of cardboard near the trail. They'll take it back to the nest.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Caulk entry points</summary><div class="body">Seal cracks along baseboards, pipes, and windows with caulk or petroleum jelly. Ants won't cross a petroleum jelly barrier.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Keep it clean</summary><div class="body">Wipe counters, sweep floors, and store food in sealed containers. Ants come for crumbs. No crumbs, no ants.</div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Banish Kitchen Ants</h1><p class="muted">You don't need harsh sprays. , here's a trail-stopping trick.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Find the trail</summary><div class="body">Watch where they go. Ants leave a pheromone trail. Wipe it with vinegar and water (1:1) to erase the scent.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Make a bait</summary><div class="body">Mix 1 part borax with 3 parts sugar. Add a little water to make a paste. Put it on a piece of cardboard near the trail. They'll take it back to the nest.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Caulk entry points</summary><div class="body">Seal cracks along baseboards, pipes, and windows with caulk or petroleum jelly. Ants won't cross a petroleum jelly barrier.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Keep it clean</summary><div class="body">Wipe counters, sweep floors, and store food in sealed containers. Ants come for crumbs. No crumbs, no ants.</div></details></div>	-
39.40.0	dashboard_faq/Erase Deodorant Marks	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Erase Deodorant Marks</h1><p class="muted">Those white streaks? Not permanent. <regex>user_name</regex>, here's how to lift them.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Rub with a dryer sheet</summary><div class="body">A used dryer sheet works like an eraser. Rub the white mark in circles. It lifts the waxy residue.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Use a nylon or tights</summary><div class="body">Stretch a nylon over your hand and rub the stain. The friction lifts the deodorant without wetting the fabric.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>For set-in stains</summary><div class="body">Make a paste of baking soda and water. Apply, let sit 15 minutes, then wash as usual.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Prevent future marks</summary><div class="body">Let deodorant dry completely before dressing. Or switch to a clear gel. For dark clothes, a quick swipe with a damp cloth before wearing helps.</div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Erase Deodorant Marks</h1><p class="muted">Those white streaks? Not permanent. , here's how to lift them.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Rub with a dryer sheet</summary><div class="body">A used dryer sheet works like an eraser. Rub the white mark in circles. It lifts the waxy residue.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Use a nylon or tights</summary><div class="body">Stretch a nylon over your hand and rub the stain. The friction lifts the deodorant without wetting the fabric.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>For set-in stains</summary><div class="body">Make a paste of baking soda and water. Apply, let sit 15 minutes, then wash as usual.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Prevent future marks</summary><div class="body">Let deodorant dry completely before dressing. Or switch to a clear gel. For dark clothes, a quick swipe with a damp cloth before wearing helps.</div></details></div>	-
39.41.0	dashboard_faq/Keep Denim Dark	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Keep Denim Dark</h1>  <p class="muted">Fading is mostly friction plus detergent. Cool water, inside-out, and less washing keep the indigo where it belongs.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>How often should I actually wash jeans?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Every 5&ndash;10 wears, unless they smell or have visible stains. Spot-clean between washes with a damp cloth and a drop of mild soap.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What settings and soap?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Cold water, gentle cycle, inside-out, and a detergent made for dark colors (no bleach, no optical brighteners). Skip fabric softener &mdash; it dulls the dye.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>How do I dry them?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Air-dry in the shade, inside-out. Direct sun and a hot dryer are the two fastest ways to grey your blacks. If you must machine-dry, low heat and take them out slightly damp.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Can I re-darken faded jeans?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Yes &mdash; a fabric dye made for denim (indigo or black) can bring them back. It won't be perfect, but it buys years. Follow the packet; the stovetop method gives the most even result.</p>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout info">    <p><strong>Quick tip:</strong> Freeze jeans overnight to kill odor-causing bacteria between washes. It won't clean stains, but it extends wear time.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Keep Denim Dark</h1>  <p class="muted">Fading is mostly friction plus detergent. Cool water, inside-out, and less washing keep the indigo where it belongs.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>How often should I actually wash jeans?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Every 5&ndash;10 wears, unless they smell or have visible stains. Spot-clean between washes with a damp cloth and a drop of mild soap.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What settings and soap?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Cold water, gentle cycle, inside-out, and a detergent made for dark colors (no bleach, no optical brighteners). Skip fabric softener &mdash; it dulls the dye.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>How do I dry them?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Air-dry in the shade, inside-out. Direct sun and a hot dryer are the two fastest ways to grey your blacks. If you must machine-dry, low heat and take them out slightly damp.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Can I re-darken faded jeans?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Yes &mdash; a fabric dye made for denim (indigo or black) can bring them back. It won't be perfect, but it buys years. Follow the packet; the stovetop method gives the most even result.</p>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout info">    <p><strong>Quick tip:</strong> Freeze jeans overnight to kill odor-causing bacteria between washes. It won't clean stains, but it extends wear time.</p>  </div></div>	-
39.42.0	dashboard_faq/Banish Fungus Gnats	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Banish Fungus Gnats</h1>  <p class="muted">Those tiny flies hovering over your monstera are fungus gnats. They breed in wet topsoil, so the fix is drying things out and trapping the adults.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Why do they keep coming back?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Because the top inch of soil stays moist, which is exactly where they lay eggs. Let the top 1&ndash;2 inches dry out between waterings. Bottom-watering helps too.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>How do I trap the adults?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Yellow sticky traps stuck in the soil catch the flyers. Replace them every couple of weeks until you stop seeing new catches.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What about the larvae?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Water with a 1:4 mix of 3% hydrogen peroxide and water, or use mosquito dunks soaked in your watering can. Both kill larvae without harming the plant.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Should I repot?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Only if it's really bad. A fresh, well-draining mix and a clean pot helps, but drying out and trapping is usually enough.</p>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout good">    <p><strong>Prevention:</strong> Add a half-inch of sand or fine gravel on top of the soil. Adults can't lay eggs through it, and water still gets through.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Banish Fungus Gnats</h1>  <p class="muted">Those tiny flies hovering over your monstera are fungus gnats. They breed in wet topsoil, so the fix is drying things out and trapping the adults.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Why do they keep coming back?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Because the top inch of soil stays moist, which is exactly where they lay eggs. Let the top 1&ndash;2 inches dry out between waterings. Bottom-watering helps too.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>How do I trap the adults?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Yellow sticky traps stuck in the soil catch the flyers. Replace them every couple of weeks until you stop seeing new catches.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What about the larvae?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Water with a 1:4 mix of 3% hydrogen peroxide and water, or use mosquito dunks soaked in your watering can. Both kill larvae without harming the plant.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Should I repot?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Only if it's really bad. A fresh, well-draining mix and a clean pot helps, but drying out and trapping is usually enough.</p>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout good">    <p><strong>Prevention:</strong> Add a half-inch of sand or fine gravel on top of the soil. Adults can't lay eggs through it, and water still gets through.</p>  </div></div>	-
39.43.0	dashboard_faq/Clean a Candle Jar	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Clean a Candle Jar</h1>  <p class="muted">That pretty glass jar deserves a second life. Here's how to get the wax out without scratching or shattering it.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Freezer method</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Pop the jar in the freezer for an hour. The wax shrinks and usually pops right out. If it doesn't, gently pry with a butter knife.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Hot water method</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Fill the jar with very hot (not boiling) water and let it sit until the wax floats to the top. Fish it out with a spoon and wipe the jar clean.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Getting rid of the wick sticker</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Soak in warm soapy water, then rub with a little oil or Goo Gone. A plastic scraper (like an old credit card) works without scratching.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Removing the smoky film</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Wash with dish soap and a bottle brush, then rinse with a splash of white vinegar. Dry upside down on a towel.</p>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout warn">    <p><strong>Don't:</strong> Pour boiling water into a cold jar &mdash; thermal shock can crack it. Let the jar warm up first, or use hot tap water.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Clean a Candle Jar</h1>  <p class="muted">That pretty glass jar deserves a second life. Here's how to get the wax out without scratching or shattering it.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Freezer method</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Pop the jar in the freezer for an hour. The wax shrinks and usually pops right out. If it doesn't, gently pry with a butter knife.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Hot water method</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Fill the jar with very hot (not boiling) water and let it sit until the wax floats to the top. Fish it out with a spoon and wipe the jar clean.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Getting rid of the wick sticker</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Soak in warm soapy water, then rub with a little oil or Goo Gone. A plastic scraper (like an old credit card) works without scratching.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Removing the smoky film</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Wash with dish soap and a bottle brush, then rinse with a splash of white vinegar. Dry upside down on a towel.</p>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout warn">    <p><strong>Don't:</strong> Pour boiling water into a cold jar &mdash; thermal shock can crack it. Let the jar warm up first, or use hot tap water.</p>  </div></div>	-
39.44.0	dashboard_faq/Deodorize Your Blender	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Deodorize Your Blender</h1>  <p class="muted">Blenders trap smells in the gasket and around the blade. A quick blend of things you already have fixes it in five minutes.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>The 30-second rinse</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Fill halfway with warm water, add a drop of dish soap, and blend on low for 10 seconds. Dump, rinse, done.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>For stubborn smells</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Blend a mix of warm water and a tablespoon of baking soda, then let it sit for 15 minutes. Rinse well. For really bad cases, add a splash of white vinegar too.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Don't forget the gasket</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>That rubber ring under the blade holds onto odors. Remove it if you can, wash with hot soapy water, and let it air-dry completely before reassembling.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Prevent future smells</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Rinse immediately after use. If you can't, fill with warm water and a drop of soap and blend for a few seconds before leaving it.</p>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout info">    <p><strong>Quick tip:</strong> A handful of ice cubes and a tablespoon of salt blended for 30 seconds also scrubs and deodorizes the blades.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Deodorize Your Blender</h1>  <p class="muted">Blenders trap smells in the gasket and around the blade. A quick blend of things you already have fixes it in five minutes.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>The 30-second rinse</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Fill halfway with warm water, add a drop of dish soap, and blend on low for 10 seconds. Dump, rinse, done.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>For stubborn smells</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Blend a mix of warm water and a tablespoon of baking soda, then let it sit for 15 minutes. Rinse well. For really bad cases, add a splash of white vinegar too.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Don't forget the gasket</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>That rubber ring under the blade holds onto odors. Remove it if you can, wash with hot soapy water, and let it air-dry completely before reassembling.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Prevent future smells</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Rinse immediately after use. If you can't, fill with warm water and a drop of soap and blend for a few seconds before leaving it.</p>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout info">    <p><strong>Quick tip:</strong> A handful of ice cubes and a tablespoon of salt blended for 30 seconds also scrubs and deodorizes the blades.</p>  </div></div>	-
39.45.0	dashboard_faq/Restore Cloudy Headlights	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Restore Cloudy Headlights</h1>  <p class="muted">Yellow, hazy headlights aren't just ugly &mdash; they cut your night visibility. You can fix them in an afternoon with a kit or a few household items.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Why do they cloud up?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>UV light and road grit slowly oxidize the plastic lens. The damage is on the surface, so polishing it away restores clarity.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>DIY method</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Wash the lens, then wet-sand with 800, 1500, and 2000 grit sandpaper, keeping it wet. Finish with a plastic polish or a coat of clear car wax. It's a workout, but it works.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Kit method</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Buy a headlight restoration kit (they're cheap). Follow the included steps &mdash; usually sanding discs plus a sealant wipe. The sealant is the key: it slows re-yellowing.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>How to make it last</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Apply a UV-blocking clear coat or wax every few months. Park facing away from the sun when you can. Without protection, they'll haze again in a year or two.</p>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout warn">    <p><strong>Don't:</strong> Use household abrasive cleaners or steel wool &mdash; they scratch too deep and make it worse.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Restore Cloudy Headlights</h1>  <p class="muted">Yellow, hazy headlights aren't just ugly &mdash; they cut your night visibility. You can fix them in an afternoon with a kit or a few household items.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Why do they cloud up?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>UV light and road grit slowly oxidize the plastic lens. The damage is on the surface, so polishing it away restores clarity.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>DIY method</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Wash the lens, then wet-sand with 800, 1500, and 2000 grit sandpaper, keeping it wet. Finish with a plastic polish or a coat of clear car wax. It's a workout, but it works.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Kit method</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Buy a headlight restoration kit (they're cheap). Follow the included steps &mdash; usually sanding discs plus a sealant wipe. The sealant is the key: it slows re-yellowing.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>How to make it last</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Apply a UV-blocking clear coat or wax every few months. Park facing away from the sun when you can. Without protection, they'll haze again in a year or two.</p>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout warn">    <p><strong>Don't:</strong> Use household abrasive cleaners or steel wool &mdash; they scratch too deep and make it worse.</p>  </div></div>	-
39.46.0	dashboard_faq/Fix a Separating Zipper	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Fix a Separating Zipper</h1>  <p class="muted">When a zipper splits open behind the slider, it's usually because the slider has loosened. You can often fix it without replacing the zipper.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>First, check the slider</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Look at the gap between the two sides of the slider. If it's wider than the zipper teeth, that's your problem. Squeeze it gently with pliers to close the gap.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>How to squeeze without breaking it</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Use needlenose pliers and apply light pressure on the top and bottom of the slider, not the sides. Test the zipper after each squeeze. Go slow &mdash; too much and it won't move at all.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>If that doesn't work</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>The teeth may be worn or bent. Check for a missing tooth or a bent one and gently straighten it with pliers. If teeth are missing, the zipper needs replacing.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Make it slide smoother</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Rub a pencil lead or a little beeswax along the teeth. It lubricates without staining fabric.</p>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout good">    <p><strong>Prevention:</strong> Always zip before washing and close the slider fully. It keeps the teeth aligned and the slider tight.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Fix a Separating Zipper</h1>  <p class="muted">When a zipper splits open behind the slider, it's usually because the slider has loosened. You can often fix it without replacing the zipper.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>First, check the slider</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Look at the gap between the two sides of the slider. If it's wider than the zipper teeth, that's your problem. Squeeze it gently with pliers to close the gap.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>How to squeeze without breaking it</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Use needlenose pliers and apply light pressure on the top and bottom of the slider, not the sides. Test the zipper after each squeeze. Go slow &mdash; too much and it won't move at all.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>If that doesn't work</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>The teeth may be worn or bent. Check for a missing tooth or a bent one and gently straighten it with pliers. If teeth are missing, the zipper needs replacing.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Make it slide smoother</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Rub a pencil lead or a little beeswax along the teeth. It lubricates without staining fabric.</p>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout good">    <p><strong>Prevention:</strong> Always zip before washing and close the slider fully. It keeps the teeth aligned and the slider tight.</p>  </div></div>	-
39.47.0	dashboard_faq/Descale a Clogged Showerhead	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Descale a Clogged Showerhead</h1><p class="muted">Mineral crust chokes the spray. A soak fixes it.</p><details class="acc"><summary>What causes the white buildup?</summary><div class="body"><p>Hard water leaves calcium and magnesium behind when it evaporates. Those minerals stack up inside the nozzles and slow the flow.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I clean it without harsh chemicals?</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Unscrew the showerhead if it's removable. If not, fill a plastic bag with vinegar and rubber-band it around the head.</li><li>Submerge the nozzles in white vinegar. A 1:1 mix with water works for light buildup.</li><li>Let it sit for 1 hour, or overnight for heavy crust.</li><li>Scrub the holes with an old toothbrush.</li><li>Run hot water for a minute to flush.</li></ol><p class="t-small dim">Skip vinegar on brass or nickel finishes—it can dull them. Use a commercial descaler instead.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How often should I do this?</summary><div class="body"><p>Every 3–6 months, or when you notice the spray pattern going crooked. <regex>user_name</regex>, a quick monthly wipe of the face with a vinegar-damp cloth keeps it clear longer.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Descale a Clogged Showerhead</h1><p class="muted">Mineral crust chokes the spray. A soak fixes it.</p><details class="acc"><summary>What causes the white buildup?</summary><div class="body"><p>Hard water leaves calcium and magnesium behind when it evaporates. Those minerals stack up inside the nozzles and slow the flow.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I clean it without harsh chemicals?</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Unscrew the showerhead if it's removable. If not, fill a plastic bag with vinegar and rubber-band it around the head.</li><li>Submerge the nozzles in white vinegar. A 1:1 mix with water works for light buildup.</li><li>Let it sit for 1 hour, or overnight for heavy crust.</li><li>Scrub the holes with an old toothbrush.</li><li>Run hot water for a minute to flush.</li></ol><p class="t-small dim">Skip vinegar on brass or nickel finishes—it can dull them. Use a commercial descaler instead.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How often should I do this?</summary><div class="body"><p>Every 3–6 months, or when you notice the spray pattern going crooked. , a quick monthly wipe of the face with a vinegar-damp cloth keeps it clear longer.</p></div></details></div>	-
39.48.0	dashboard_faq/Remove Red Wine from Carpet	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Remove Red Wine from Carpet</h1><p class="muted">Act fast. Blot, don't rub.</p><details class="acc"><summary>First aid for a fresh spill</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Blot with a clean white cloth or paper towel. Press down, lift, repeat. Don't scrub—it spreads the dye.</li><li>Pour a little club soda or cold water on the spot. Blot again.</li><li>Mix 1 tbsp dish soap + 1 tbsp white vinegar + 2 cups cold water. Apply to the stain, blot.</li><li>Rinse with cold water, blot dry.</li><li>If any pink remains, dab with hydrogen peroxide (3%) on a cotton ball. Let sit 5 minutes, then blot.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What if the stain is dry?</summary><div class="body"><p>Re-wet it with cold water first. Then follow the steps above. Older stains may need a second round of peroxide.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Will this work on wool or silk?</summary><div class="body"><p>Skip vinegar and peroxide on wool, silk, or vintage rugs. Use a carpet-specific enzyme cleaner and test in a hidden spot. <regex>user_name</regex>, when in doubt, call a pro.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Remove Red Wine from Carpet</h1><p class="muted">Act fast. Blot, don't rub.</p><details class="acc"><summary>First aid for a fresh spill</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Blot with a clean white cloth or paper towel. Press down, lift, repeat. Don't scrub—it spreads the dye.</li><li>Pour a little club soda or cold water on the spot. Blot again.</li><li>Mix 1 tbsp dish soap + 1 tbsp white vinegar + 2 cups cold water. Apply to the stain, blot.</li><li>Rinse with cold water, blot dry.</li><li>If any pink remains, dab with hydrogen peroxide (3%) on a cotton ball. Let sit 5 minutes, then blot.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What if the stain is dry?</summary><div class="body"><p>Re-wet it with cold water first. Then follow the steps above. Older stains may need a second round of peroxide.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Will this work on wool or silk?</summary><div class="body"><p>Skip vinegar and peroxide on wool, silk, or vintage rugs. Use a carpet-specific enzyme cleaner and test in a hidden spot. , when in doubt, call a pro.</p></div></details></div>	-
39.49.0	dashboard_faq/Speed Up a Slow Computer	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Speed Up a Slow Computer</h1><p class="muted">No new hardware required, <regex>user_name</regex>. Just a few clicks and some patience.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why is my computer slow?</summary><div class="body"><p>Usually it's too many startup programs, a nearly full drive, or background updates. Rarely is it the hardware itself.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What should I do first?</summary><div class="body"><p>Open Task Manager (Ctrl+Shift+Esc) and check the Startup tab. Disable anything you don't need at login. That alone can cut boot time in half.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I free up space?</summary><div class="body"><p>Run Disk Cleanup (or Storage Sense on Windows 10/11) and clear temporary files. Aim to keep at least 15% of your drive empty.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Are browser tabs the problem?</summary><div class="body"><p>Yes, often. Each tab eats memory. Try an extension like OneTab or just close what you're not reading.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Will defragmenting help?</summary><div class="body"><p>Only if you have a spinning hard drive. If you have an SSD, don't defrag—it's unnecessary and adds wear. Windows optimizes SSDs automatically.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What about malware?</summary><div class="body"><p>Run a full scan with Windows Defender or Malwarebytes. Adware and crypto miners are common causes of sudden slowness.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Should I reinstall Windows?</summary><div class="body"><p>It's a last resort. Try the steps above first. A clean install takes an afternoon and you'll lose apps, so back up first.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>When is it time for new hardware?</summary><div class="body"><p>If you have an old spinning drive, swapping to an SSD is the single biggest upgrade. If you're already on SSD and still slow, more RAM might help.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Speed Up a Slow Computer</h1><p class="muted">No new hardware required, . Just a few clicks and some patience.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why is my computer slow?</summary><div class="body"><p>Usually it's too many startup programs, a nearly full drive, or background updates. Rarely is it the hardware itself.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What should I do first?</summary><div class="body"><p>Open Task Manager (Ctrl+Shift+Esc) and check the Startup tab. Disable anything you don't need at login. That alone can cut boot time in half.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I free up space?</summary><div class="body"><p>Run Disk Cleanup (or Storage Sense on Windows 10/11) and clear temporary files. Aim to keep at least 15% of your drive empty.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Are browser tabs the problem?</summary><div class="body"><p>Yes, often. Each tab eats memory. Try an extension like OneTab or just close what you're not reading.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Will defragmenting help?</summary><div class="body"><p>Only if you have a spinning hard drive. If you have an SSD, don't defrag—it's unnecessary and adds wear. Windows optimizes SSDs automatically.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What about malware?</summary><div class="body"><p>Run a full scan with Windows Defender or Malwarebytes. Adware and crypto miners are common causes of sudden slowness.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Should I reinstall Windows?</summary><div class="body"><p>It's a last resort. Try the steps above first. A clean install takes an afternoon and you'll lose apps, so back up first.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>When is it time for new hardware?</summary><div class="body"><p>If you have an old spinning drive, swapping to an SSD is the single biggest upgrade. If you're already on SSD and still slow, more RAM might help.</p></div></details></div>	-
39.50.0	dashboard_faq/Keep Bananas From Ripening Too Fast	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Keep Bananas From Ripening Too Fast</h1><p class="muted">A little science and a bit of separation, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why do bananas ripen so fast?</summary><div class="body"><p>They release ethylene gas, which speeds up ripening. The more bananas together, the faster they all go.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Should I separate them?</summary><div class="body"><p>Yes. Break the bunch apart and keep them apart. Each banana alone produces less concentrated gas around the others.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Where should I store them?</summary><div class="body"><p>On the counter, away from direct sun and other fruit. Not in the fridge unless they're already ripe.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Can I refrigerate them?</summary><div class="body"><p>Once they're ripe, yes. The skin will turn brown but the fruit inside stays good for days longer. Let them come to room temp before eating.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What about wrapping the stems?</summary><div class="body"><p>Wrap the crown (where they were joined) in plastic wrap. That's where most ethylene escapes. It slows ripening by a day or two.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Do green bags work?</summary><div class="body"><p>They can help, but they're not magic. The key is airflow and separating from other ethylene producers like apples.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I speed ripening instead?</summary><div class="body"><p>Put them in a paper bag with an apple. The apple's ethylene will ripen them overnight.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What if they're already too ripe?</summary><div class="body"><p>Peel and freeze them for smoothies, or mash into banana bread. Overripe is perfect for baking.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Keep Bananas From Ripening Too Fast</h1><p class="muted">A little science and a bit of separation, .</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why do bananas ripen so fast?</summary><div class="body"><p>They release ethylene gas, which speeds up ripening. The more bananas together, the faster they all go.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Should I separate them?</summary><div class="body"><p>Yes. Break the bunch apart and keep them apart. Each banana alone produces less concentrated gas around the others.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Where should I store them?</summary><div class="body"><p>On the counter, away from direct sun and other fruit. Not in the fridge unless they're already ripe.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Can I refrigerate them?</summary><div class="body"><p>Once they're ripe, yes. The skin will turn brown but the fruit inside stays good for days longer. Let them come to room temp before eating.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What about wrapping the stems?</summary><div class="body"><p>Wrap the crown (where they were joined) in plastic wrap. That's where most ethylene escapes. It slows ripening by a day or two.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Do green bags work?</summary><div class="body"><p>They can help, but they're not magic. The key is airflow and separating from other ethylene producers like apples.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I speed ripening instead?</summary><div class="body"><p>Put them in a paper bag with an apple. The apple's ethylene will ripen them overnight.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What if they're already too ripe?</summary><div class="body"><p>Peel and freeze them for smoothies, or mash into banana bread. Overripe is perfect for baking.</p></div></details></div>	-
39.51.0	dashboard_faq/Remove Hard Water Stains From Glass	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Remove Hard Water Stains From Glass</h1><p class="muted">For shower doors and windows, <regex>user_name</regex>. A little acid goes a long way.</p><details class="acc"><summary>What are hard water stains?</summary><div class="body"><p>Mineral deposits, mostly calcium and magnesium, left behind when water evaporates. They etch into glass over time.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What's the best homemade cleaner?</summary><div class="body"><p>Equal parts white vinegar and water. Spray, let sit 10–15 minutes, then scrub with a non-scratch pad.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Will vinegar damage the glass?</summary><div class="body"><p>No, glass is fine. Avoid vinegar on natural stone or aluminum frames—it can etch those.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What if vinegar doesn't work?</summary><div class="body"><p>Try a commercial lime remover or a paste of baking soda and dish soap. For tough spots, use a razor blade at a shallow angle.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I prevent them?</summary><div class="body"><p>Squeegee after every shower. That's the single best habit. A water softener also helps if your water is very hard.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Can I use steel wool?</summary><div class="body"><p>No, it will scratch the glass. Use a plastic scraper or a dedicated glass-safe pad.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Is there a no-scrub method?</summary><div class="body"><p>Spray with vinegar, cover with plastic wrap, and leave overnight. The acid does the work. Rinse in the morning.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What about commercial products?</summary><div class="body"><p>Look for ones with sulfamic or citric acid. They're effective but follow the dwell time on the label.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Remove Hard Water Stains From Glass</h1><p class="muted">For shower doors and windows, . A little acid goes a long way.</p><details class="acc"><summary>What are hard water stains?</summary><div class="body"><p>Mineral deposits, mostly calcium and magnesium, left behind when water evaporates. They etch into glass over time.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What's the best homemade cleaner?</summary><div class="body"><p>Equal parts white vinegar and water. Spray, let sit 10–15 minutes, then scrub with a non-scratch pad.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Will vinegar damage the glass?</summary><div class="body"><p>No, glass is fine. Avoid vinegar on natural stone or aluminum frames—it can etch those.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What if vinegar doesn't work?</summary><div class="body"><p>Try a commercial lime remover or a paste of baking soda and dish soap. For tough spots, use a razor blade at a shallow angle.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I prevent them?</summary><div class="body"><p>Squeegee after every shower. That's the single best habit. A water softener also helps if your water is very hard.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Can I use steel wool?</summary><div class="body"><p>No, it will scratch the glass. Use a plastic scraper or a dedicated glass-safe pad.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Is there a no-scrub method?</summary><div class="body"><p>Spray with vinegar, cover with plastic wrap, and leave overnight. The acid does the work. Rinse in the morning.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What about commercial products?</summary><div class="body"><p>Look for ones with sulfamic or citric acid. They're effective but follow the dwell time on the label.</p></div></details></div>	-
39.52.0	dashboard_faq/Remove Musty Smell From Towels	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Remove Musty Smell From Towels</h1><p class="muted">It's not you, <regex>user_name</regex>. It's the bacteria.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why do towels get musty?</summary><div class="body"><p>They stay damp too long, and bacteria or mildew grow in the fibers. Fabric softener makes it worse by trapping residue.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I strip the smell?</summary><div class="body"><p>Wash with hot water, a cup of white vinegar, and half a cup of baking soda. Skip detergent this round. Run an extra rinse.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Will vinegar damage my towels?</summary><div class="body"><p>No, it's gentle on cotton and helps remove buildup. Don't use it with bleach, though.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Should I use bleach?</summary><div class="body"><p>Only for white towels. Bleach kills mildew but can weaken fibers over time. Use it sparingly.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I prevent it?</summary><div class="body"><p>Hang towels to dry after each use. Wash weekly. Avoid fabric softener and dryer sheets—they coat fibers and trap odor.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Can I dry them outside?</summary><div class="body"><p>Yes, sunlight is a natural disinfectant. A few hours in direct sun helps a lot.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What if the smell persists?</summary><div class="body"><p>Try an oxygen bleach soak (like OxiClean) overnight. If that fails, the towels may be too far gone.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How often should I replace towels?</summary><div class="body"><p>Every 1–2 years for daily-use towels. If they're thin and scratchy, they're done.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Remove Musty Smell From Towels</h1><p class="muted">It's not you, . It's the bacteria.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why do towels get musty?</summary><div class="body"><p>They stay damp too long, and bacteria or mildew grow in the fibers. Fabric softener makes it worse by trapping residue.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I strip the smell?</summary><div class="body"><p>Wash with hot water, a cup of white vinegar, and half a cup of baking soda. Skip detergent this round. Run an extra rinse.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Will vinegar damage my towels?</summary><div class="body"><p>No, it's gentle on cotton and helps remove buildup. Don't use it with bleach, though.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Should I use bleach?</summary><div class="body"><p>Only for white towels. Bleach kills mildew but can weaken fibers over time. Use it sparingly.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I prevent it?</summary><div class="body"><p>Hang towels to dry after each use. Wash weekly. Avoid fabric softener and dryer sheets—they coat fibers and trap odor.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Can I dry them outside?</summary><div class="body"><p>Yes, sunlight is a natural disinfectant. A few hours in direct sun helps a lot.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What if the smell persists?</summary><div class="body"><p>Try an oxygen bleach soak (like OxiClean) overnight. If that fails, the towels may be too far gone.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How often should I replace towels?</summary><div class="body"><p>Every 1–2 years for daily-use towels. If they're thin and scratchy, they're done.</p></div></details></div>	-
39.53.0	dashboard_faq/Remove Super Glue From Skin	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Remove Super Glue From Skin</h1><p class="muted">Don't panic, <regex>user_name</regex>. It'll come off.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Is it dangerous?</summary><div class="body"><p>Usually not. It bonds fast but skin flakes off naturally in a few days. Only worry if it's near eyes or mouth.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What's the fastest way?</summary><div class="body"><p>Soak in warm soapy water, then gently peel. Acetone (nail polish remover) works faster but dries skin.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Can I use acetone?</summary><div class="body"><p>Yes, but only on hands and arms. Avoid broken skin. Apply with a cotton ball and rub gently.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What if I don't have acetone?</summary><div class="body"><p>Try vegetable oil or petroleum jelly. Massage in, wait a few minutes, then peel. It's slower but gentler.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Should I pull hard?</summary><div class="body"><p>No. That can tear skin. Be patient—use a pumice stone or emery board after soaking.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What if my fingers are stuck together?</summary><div class="body"><p>Don't yank them apart. Soak in warm water with soap, then roll a pencil between them to gently separate.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>When should I see a doctor?</summary><div class="body"><p>If it's in your eye, on a large area, or won't come off after a few days. Also if skin is torn or bleeding.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I avoid this next time?</summary><div class="body"><p>Wear nitrile gloves. They're cheap and super glue doesn't stick to them.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Remove Super Glue From Skin</h1><p class="muted">Don't panic, . It'll come off.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Is it dangerous?</summary><div class="body"><p>Usually not. It bonds fast but skin flakes off naturally in a few days. Only worry if it's near eyes or mouth.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What's the fastest way?</summary><div class="body"><p>Soak in warm soapy water, then gently peel. Acetone (nail polish remover) works faster but dries skin.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Can I use acetone?</summary><div class="body"><p>Yes, but only on hands and arms. Avoid broken skin. Apply with a cotton ball and rub gently.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What if I don't have acetone?</summary><div class="body"><p>Try vegetable oil or petroleum jelly. Massage in, wait a few minutes, then peel. It's slower but gentler.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Should I pull hard?</summary><div class="body"><p>No. That can tear skin. Be patient—use a pumice stone or emery board after soaking.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What if my fingers are stuck together?</summary><div class="body"><p>Don't yank them apart. Soak in warm water with soap, then roll a pencil between them to gently separate.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>When should I see a doctor?</summary><div class="body"><p>If it's in your eye, on a large area, or won't come off after a few days. Also if skin is torn or bleeding.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I avoid this next time?</summary><div class="body"><p>Wear nitrile gloves. They're cheap and super glue doesn't stick to them.</p></div></details></div>	-
39.54.0	dashboard_faq/Stop Static Cling in Clothes	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Stop Static Cling in Clothes</h1><p class="muted">Zap the zap, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why does static happen?</summary><div class="body"><p>Friction in the dryer builds up electric charge, especially with synthetic fabrics and dry air.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What's the quickest fix?</summary><div class="body"><p>Rub a dryer sheet on the clingy garment. Or spritz with a little water and smooth it out.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Can I prevent it in the wash?</summary><div class="body"><p>Add half a cup of white vinegar to the rinse cycle. It softens fabrics and reduces static.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Are dryer sheets safe for all fabrics?</summary><div class="body"><p>No, they can leave residue on towels and reduce absorbency. Use wool dryer balls instead.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Do dryer balls help?</summary><div class="body"><p>Yes, wool balls separate clothes and reduce drying time. Add a few drops of essential oil if you like scent.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What about humidifiers?</summary><div class="body"><p>If your home is dry, a humidifier can reduce static on clothes and hair. Aim for 40–50% humidity.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Should I avoid synthetic fabrics?</summary><div class="body"><p>Not necessarily, but they're more prone to static. Natural fibers like cotton and linen are less clingy.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Is there a no-dryer method?</summary><div class="body"><p>Line dry. Static builds in the dryer, so air-drying avoids it entirely.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Stop Static Cling in Clothes</h1><p class="muted">Zap the zap, .</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why does static happen?</summary><div class="body"><p>Friction in the dryer builds up electric charge, especially with synthetic fabrics and dry air.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What's the quickest fix?</summary><div class="body"><p>Rub a dryer sheet on the clingy garment. Or spritz with a little water and smooth it out.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Can I prevent it in the wash?</summary><div class="body"><p>Add half a cup of white vinegar to the rinse cycle. It softens fabrics and reduces static.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Are dryer sheets safe for all fabrics?</summary><div class="body"><p>No, they can leave residue on towels and reduce absorbency. Use wool dryer balls instead.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Do dryer balls help?</summary><div class="body"><p>Yes, wool balls separate clothes and reduce drying time. Add a few drops of essential oil if you like scent.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What about humidifiers?</summary><div class="body"><p>If your home is dry, a humidifier can reduce static on clothes and hair. Aim for 40–50% humidity.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Should I avoid synthetic fabrics?</summary><div class="body"><p>Not necessarily, but they're more prone to static. Natural fibers like cotton and linen are less clingy.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Is there a no-dryer method?</summary><div class="body"><p>Line dry. Static builds in the dryer, so air-drying avoids it entirely.</p></div></details></div>	-
39.55.0	dashboard_faq/Fresh Washer, No Funk	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Fresh Washer, No Funk</h1>  <p class="muted">Your washing machine cleans clothes—who cleans the cleaner? A monthly ritual keeps it from smelling like a swamp.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Why does my washer smell musty?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Soap scum, fabric softener, and lint build up in the drum and detergent drawer. Damp, dark, and full of organic residue—mildew moves in. Front-loaders are worse because water pools in the door gasket.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What do I need for the monthly clean?</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>2 cups white vinegar <em>or</em> 1 cup baking soda</li>        <li>Old toothbrush or small brush</li>        <li>Clean cloth</li>        <li>Hot water cycle</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Step-by-step: the empty hot cycle</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Pour 2 cups vinegar into the detergent dispenser.</li>        <li>Run a hot water cycle with no clothes.</li>        <li>While it fills, scrub the door gasket with the brush and vinegar.</li>        <li>Wipe the detergent drawer and its housing.</li>        <li>When the cycle ends, leave the door and drawer ajar to dry.</li>      </ol>      <p class="t-small dim">For extra oomph, sprinkle 1 cup baking soda directly into the drum before the cycle.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>How often should I do this?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Once a month if you wash often. Every 2–3 months is fine for light use. Also: use less detergent than you think. Excess soap is the root of all evil smells.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What about the drain pump filter?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>On front-loaders, there's a small access panel near the bottom. Place a towel and shallow pan underneath, unscrew the cap, and pull out lint, coins, and hair. That gunk smells worse than the drum. Do this every few months.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Fresh Washer, No Funk</h1>  <p class="muted">Your washing machine cleans clothes—who cleans the cleaner? A monthly ritual keeps it from smelling like a swamp.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Why does my washer smell musty?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Soap scum, fabric softener, and lint build up in the drum and detergent drawer. Damp, dark, and full of organic residue—mildew moves in. Front-loaders are worse because water pools in the door gasket.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What do I need for the monthly clean?</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>2 cups white vinegar <em>or</em> 1 cup baking soda</li>        <li>Old toothbrush or small brush</li>        <li>Clean cloth</li>        <li>Hot water cycle</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Step-by-step: the empty hot cycle</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Pour 2 cups vinegar into the detergent dispenser.</li>        <li>Run a hot water cycle with no clothes.</li>        <li>While it fills, scrub the door gasket with the brush and vinegar.</li>        <li>Wipe the detergent drawer and its housing.</li>        <li>When the cycle ends, leave the door and drawer ajar to dry.</li>      </ol>      <p class="t-small dim">For extra oomph, sprinkle 1 cup baking soda directly into the drum before the cycle.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>How often should I do this?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Once a month if you wash often. Every 2–3 months is fine for light use. Also: use less detergent than you think. Excess soap is the root of all evil smells.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What about the drain pump filter?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>On front-loaders, there's a small access panel near the bottom. Place a towel and shallow pan underneath, unscrew the cap, and pull out lint, coins, and hair. That gunk smells worse than the drum. Do this every few months.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
39.56.0	dashboard_faq/Static Cling Be Gone	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Static Cling Be Gone</h1>  <p class="muted">That crackle when you pull apart a sock? It's dry air plus synthetic fabrics. Here's how to tame it.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Why does static happen?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Friction in the dryer strips electrons. Dry air (winter, heated rooms) lets the charge build. Synthetics hold it; natural fibers let it go. So the fix is moisture, or fewer synthetics, or both.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Quick fixes you already have</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>A damp washcloth tossed in the dryer for the last 10 minutes.</li>        <li>A safety pin hidden in a hem—it bleeds off charge.</li>        <li>Rub a dryer sheet on the inside of your pants.</li>        <li>Lightly mist clothes with water before folding.</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Long-term laundry tweaks</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>Don't overdry. Take clothes out when slightly damp.</li>        <li>Use wool dryer balls—they separate fabrics and cut static.</li>        <li>Add 1/4 cup white vinegar to the rinse cycle (softens and reduces static).</li>        <li>Line dry synthetics when you can.</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Does dryer sheet residue matter?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Yes—those sheets coat fabric with a waxy film that can reduce absorbency (bad for towels) and build up in the dryer. Use them sparingly, or switch to wool balls.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Static Cling Be Gone</h1>  <p class="muted">That crackle when you pull apart a sock? It's dry air plus synthetic fabrics. Here's how to tame it.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Why does static happen?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Friction in the dryer strips electrons. Dry air (winter, heated rooms) lets the charge build. Synthetics hold it; natural fibers let it go. So the fix is moisture, or fewer synthetics, or both.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Quick fixes you already have</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>A damp washcloth tossed in the dryer for the last 10 minutes.</li>        <li>A safety pin hidden in a hem—it bleeds off charge.</li>        <li>Rub a dryer sheet on the inside of your pants.</li>        <li>Lightly mist clothes with water before folding.</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Long-term laundry tweaks</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>Don't overdry. Take clothes out when slightly damp.</li>        <li>Use wool dryer balls—they separate fabrics and cut static.</li>        <li>Add 1/4 cup white vinegar to the rinse cycle (softens and reduces static).</li>        <li>Line dry synthetics when you can.</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Does dryer sheet residue matter?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Yes—those sheets coat fabric with a waxy film that can reduce absorbency (bad for towels) and build up in the dryer. Use them sparingly, or switch to wool balls.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
39.57.0	dashboard_faq/Dishwasher Deep Clean	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Dishwasher Deep Clean</h1>  <p class="muted">If your glasses come out cloudy and the whole kitchen smells like a wet dog, it's time for a dishwasher spa day.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Why does my dishwasher smell?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Food particles, grease, and detergent residue collect in the filter, door gasket, and spray arms. Standing water in the bottom (from a clogged filter) makes it worse. Clean those three spots and the smell usually vanishes.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What you'll need</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>White vinegar (1 cup)</li>        <li>Baking soda (1 cup)</li>        <li>Old toothbrush</li>        <li>Dish soap and a sponge</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Step 1: Clean the filter</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Pull out the bottom rack. The filter is usually a cylindrical mesh under the spray arm. Twist to unlock, remove, and rinse under hot water. Scrub with the toothbrush and dish soap. Replace.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Step 2: The vinegar cycle</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Place a bowl of white vinegar on the top rack. Run a hot water cycle (no detergent). The vinegar dissolves grease and mineral buildup.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Step 3: The baking soda cycle</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Sprinkle 1 cup baking soda across the bottom. Run a short hot cycle. It deodorizes and gently scours. Wipe the door edges and gasket with a damp cloth.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>How often?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Filter: monthly. Full clean: every 3–6 months, or whenever you notice a smell or cloudy glasses.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Dishwasher Deep Clean</h1>  <p class="muted">If your glasses come out cloudy and the whole kitchen smells like a wet dog, it's time for a dishwasher spa day.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Why does my dishwasher smell?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Food particles, grease, and detergent residue collect in the filter, door gasket, and spray arms. Standing water in the bottom (from a clogged filter) makes it worse. Clean those three spots and the smell usually vanishes.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What you'll need</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>White vinegar (1 cup)</li>        <li>Baking soda (1 cup)</li>        <li>Old toothbrush</li>        <li>Dish soap and a sponge</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Step 1: Clean the filter</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Pull out the bottom rack. The filter is usually a cylindrical mesh under the spray arm. Twist to unlock, remove, and rinse under hot water. Scrub with the toothbrush and dish soap. Replace.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Step 2: The vinegar cycle</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Place a bowl of white vinegar on the top rack. Run a hot water cycle (no detergent). The vinegar dissolves grease and mineral buildup.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Step 3: The baking soda cycle</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Sprinkle 1 cup baking soda across the bottom. Run a short hot cycle. It deodorizes and gently scours. Wipe the door edges and gasket with a damp cloth.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>How often?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Filter: monthly. Full clean: every 3–6 months, or whenever you notice a smell or cloudy glasses.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
39.58.0	dashboard_faq/Garbage Disposal Odor Fix	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Garbage Disposal Odor Fix</h1>  <p class="muted">That whiff from under the sink? It's not the trash—it's the disposal. Here's a 5-minute rescue, plus a weekly habit that keeps it sweet.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Why does it smell?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Food bits and grease coat the inside of the disposal and the rubber splash guard. Bacteria feast, and you smell the aftermath. The splash guard is the usual culprit.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>DIY deodorizing cubes</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Freeze vinegar or lemon juice in an ice cube tray. Toss a few cubes into the disposal and run cold water while you grind them. The ice scours, the acid deodorizes. Do this weekly.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>The deep-clean method</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Unplug or switch off the disposal at the breaker.</li>        <li>Remove the rubber splash guard (if removable) and scrub it with dish soap.</li>        <li>Use a brush to scrub the visible interior.</li>        <li>Pour 1/2 cup baking soda down the drain, then 1 cup white vinegar. Let fizz 5 minutes.</li>        <li>Flush with hot water for 1 minute.</li>      </ol>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What not to put down there</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>Coffee grounds (fine, but they cling)</li>        <li>Eggshells (membrane sticks)</li>        <li>Grease, oil, fat</li>        <li>Fibrous veggies (celery, corn husks)</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Garbage Disposal Odor Fix</h1>  <p class="muted">That whiff from under the sink? It's not the trash—it's the disposal. Here's a 5-minute rescue, plus a weekly habit that keeps it sweet.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Why does it smell?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Food bits and grease coat the inside of the disposal and the rubber splash guard. Bacteria feast, and you smell the aftermath. The splash guard is the usual culprit.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>DIY deodorizing cubes</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Freeze vinegar or lemon juice in an ice cube tray. Toss a few cubes into the disposal and run cold water while you grind them. The ice scours, the acid deodorizes. Do this weekly.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>The deep-clean method</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Unplug or switch off the disposal at the breaker.</li>        <li>Remove the rubber splash guard (if removable) and scrub it with dish soap.</li>        <li>Use a brush to scrub the visible interior.</li>        <li>Pour 1/2 cup baking soda down the drain, then 1 cup white vinegar. Let fizz 5 minutes.</li>        <li>Flush with hot water for 1 minute.</li>      </ol>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What not to put down there</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>Coffee grounds (fine, but they cling)</li>        <li>Eggshells (membrane sticks)</li>        <li>Grease, oil, fat</li>        <li>Fibrous veggies (celery, corn husks)</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details></div>	-
39.59.0	dashboard_faq/Keyboard Cleanup	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Keyboard Cleanup</h1>  <p class="muted">Crumbs, dust, and a faint sheen of lunch. Your keyboard deserves better—and it's easier than you think.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What you'll need</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>Isopropyl alcohol (70%)</li>        <li>Microfiber cloth or cotton swabs</li>        <li>Compressed air (or a soft brush)</li>        <li>A toothpick for stubborn gunk</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Step 1: Dry debris first</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Turn the keyboard upside down and gently shake. Use compressed air in short bursts at an angle—don't spray directly into the keys. A soft brush works too.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Step 2: Wipe and swab</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Dampen (don't soak) a microfiber cloth with alcohol. Wipe the keycaps and the spaces between. Use cotton swabs for crevices. Never spray liquid directly on the keyboard.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Step 3: Sticky keys rescue</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>If a key sticks, it's often a sugary spill. With the keyboard off, use a toothpick to gently scrape around the key edges. A tiny amount of alcohol on a swab can help dissolve residue. Let it dry completely before typing.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>How often?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Quick shake-and-wipe weekly; deep clean monthly. If someone in the house is sick, wipe it down more often—keyboards are germ magnets.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Keyboard Cleanup</h1>  <p class="muted">Crumbs, dust, and a faint sheen of lunch. Your keyboard deserves better—and it's easier than you think.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What you'll need</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>Isopropyl alcohol (70%)</li>        <li>Microfiber cloth or cotton swabs</li>        <li>Compressed air (or a soft brush)</li>        <li>A toothpick for stubborn gunk</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Step 1: Dry debris first</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Turn the keyboard upside down and gently shake. Use compressed air in short bursts at an angle—don't spray directly into the keys. A soft brush works too.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Step 2: Wipe and swab</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Dampen (don't soak) a microfiber cloth with alcohol. Wipe the keycaps and the spaces between. Use cotton swabs for crevices. Never spray liquid directly on the keyboard.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Step 3: Sticky keys rescue</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>If a key sticks, it's often a sugary spill. With the keyboard off, use a toothpick to gently scrape around the key edges. A tiny amount of alcohol on a swab can help dissolve residue. Let it dry completely before typing.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>How often?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Quick shake-and-wipe weekly; deep clean monthly. If someone in the house is sick, wipe it down more often—keyboards are germ magnets.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
39.60.0	dashboard_faq/Mattress Refresh	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Mattress Refresh</h1>  <p class="muted">You spend a third of your life on it. A little baking soda and a vacuum can make it feel (and smell) brand new.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Why does my mattress smell?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Sweat, skin cells, dust mites, and spills. The smell is usually trapped moisture and bacteria. Regular deodorizing and airing out prevent buildup.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>The baking soda method</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Strip the bed and wash all bedding.</li>        <li>Sprinkle a generous layer of baking soda over the mattress.</li>        <li>Let it sit for at least 30 minutes (or several hours).</li>        <li>Vacuum it up with an upholstery attachment.</li>      </ol>      <p class="t-small dim">For extra freshness, add a few drops of lavender oil to the baking soda before sprinkling.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Spot-cleaning stains</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Blot (don't rub) with a clean cloth and a mix of 1 part dish soap to 2 parts cold water. For urine, use an enzyme cleaner. For blood, cold water only. Finish with a light sprinkle of baking soda to absorb lingering moisture.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>How often?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Deodorize every 1–2 months. Spot-clean as needed. Rotate the mattress every 3 months to even out wear.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Mattress Refresh</h1>  <p class="muted">You spend a third of your life on it. A little baking soda and a vacuum can make it feel (and smell) brand new.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Why does my mattress smell?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Sweat, skin cells, dust mites, and spills. The smell is usually trapped moisture and bacteria. Regular deodorizing and airing out prevent buildup.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>The baking soda method</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Strip the bed and wash all bedding.</li>        <li>Sprinkle a generous layer of baking soda over the mattress.</li>        <li>Let it sit for at least 30 minutes (or several hours).</li>        <li>Vacuum it up with an upholstery attachment.</li>      </ol>      <p class="t-small dim">For extra freshness, add a few drops of lavender oil to the baking soda before sprinkling.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Spot-cleaning stains</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Blot (don't rub) with a clean cloth and a mix of 1 part dish soap to 2 parts cold water. For urine, use an enzyme cleaner. For blood, cold water only. Finish with a light sprinkle of baking soda to absorb lingering moisture.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>How often?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Deodorize every 1–2 months. Spot-clean as needed. Rotate the mattress every 3 months to even out wear.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
39.61.0	dashboard_faq/Deodorize a Smelly Microwave	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Deodorize a Smelly Microwave</h1><article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>Microwave odors are just stale food particles trapped in grease. Steam loosens them; acid neutralizes. Here's the fix.</p></article><details class="acc"><summary>What you need</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>1 cup water</li><li>1 lemon (or 2 tbsp white vinegar)</li><li>Microwave-safe bowl</li><li>Clean cloth</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Steps</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Slice lemon, add to water in bowl.</li><li>Microwave on high 3 minutes. Let sit 5 minutes with door closed.</li><li>Remove bowl carefully (steam is hot).</li><li>Wipe interior, door, and seals with damp cloth.</li><li>Leave door ajar to dry.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Why it works</summary><div class="body"><p>Steam loosens baked-on grease and food spatter. Lemon's citric acid (or vinegar's acetic acid) neutralizes odor molecules. The wipe removes the source; drying prevents mildew.</p></div></details><div class="callout info"><p>For stubborn smells, repeat with fresh lemon. If odor persists, check the vent filter (if removable) and clean it.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Deodorize a Smelly Microwave</h1><article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>Microwave odors are just stale food particles trapped in grease. Steam loosens them; acid neutralizes. Here's the fix.</p></article><details class="acc"><summary>What you need</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>1 cup water</li><li>1 lemon (or 2 tbsp white vinegar)</li><li>Microwave-safe bowl</li><li>Clean cloth</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Steps</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Slice lemon, add to water in bowl.</li><li>Microwave on high 3 minutes. Let sit 5 minutes with door closed.</li><li>Remove bowl carefully (steam is hot).</li><li>Wipe interior, door, and seals with damp cloth.</li><li>Leave door ajar to dry.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Why it works</summary><div class="body"><p>Steam loosens baked-on grease and food spatter. Lemon's citric acid (or vinegar's acetic acid) neutralizes odor molecules. The wipe removes the source; drying prevents mildew.</p></div></details><div class="callout info"><p>For stubborn smells, repeat with fresh lemon. If odor persists, check the vent filter (if removable) and clean it.</p></div></div>	-
39.62.0	dashboard_faq/Remove Hard Water Stains From Shower Glass	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Remove Hard Water Stains From Shower Glass</h1><article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>Those cloudy spots are calcium and magnesium deposits. Acid dissolves them. Here's how to clear the view.</p></article><details class="acc"><summary>What you need</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>White vinegar or commercial hard water remover</li><li>Spray bottle</li><li>Plastic scraper (for tough spots)</li><li>Microfiber cloth</li><li>Rubber gloves</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Steps</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Spray glass generously with vinegar. Let sit 10–15 minutes.</li><li>For heavy buildup, cover with plastic wrap to keep it wet longer.</li><li>Wipe with microfiber. For stubborn spots, gently scrape with plastic scraper.</li><li>Rinse and dry with a clean cloth.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Why it works</summary><div class="body"><p>Acid reacts with mineral deposits, breaking them down so they wipe away. Vinegar is mild but effective for regular maintenance. Commercial removers may contain stronger acids for severe cases.</p></div></details><div class="callout warn"><p>Never mix vinegar with bleach—it creates toxic chlorine gas. Ventilate the bathroom while working.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Remove Hard Water Stains From Shower Glass</h1><article class="msg umi"><span class="who">Umi</span><p>Those cloudy spots are calcium and magnesium deposits. Acid dissolves them. Here's how to clear the view.</p></article><details class="acc"><summary>What you need</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>White vinegar or commercial hard water remover</li><li>Spray bottle</li><li>Plastic scraper (for tough spots)</li><li>Microfiber cloth</li><li>Rubber gloves</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Steps</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Spray glass generously with vinegar. Let sit 10–15 minutes.</li><li>For heavy buildup, cover with plastic wrap to keep it wet longer.</li><li>Wipe with microfiber. For stubborn spots, gently scrape with plastic scraper.</li><li>Rinse and dry with a clean cloth.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Why it works</summary><div class="body"><p>Acid reacts with mineral deposits, breaking them down so they wipe away. Vinegar is mild but effective for regular maintenance. Commercial removers may contain stronger acids for severe cases.</p></div></details><div class="callout warn"><p>Never mix vinegar with bleach—it creates toxic chlorine gas. Ventilate the bathroom while working.</p></div></div>	-
39.63.0	dashboard_faq/Stop a Musty Car Smell	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Stop a Musty Car Smell</h1><p class="muted">That wet-sock smell usually means moisture is trapped in the AC system or carpet. Here’s how to dry it out and keep it that way, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why does my car smell musty after rain?</summary><div class="body">Water sneaks in through clogged sunroof drains, worn door seals, or a damp AC evaporator. Fix the leak first or the smell returns.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I clean the AC system?</summary><div class="body">Run the fan on high with recirculation off for 10 minutes after each AC use. Once a year, spray an evaporator cleaner into the intake (usually near the cabin filter) and let it drain. Replace the cabin filter every 15,000 miles.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What about the carpets?</summary><div class="body">If they’re damp, pull them out and dry in direct sun. Sprinkle baking soda, let sit overnight, then vacuum. For deep funk, use a wet-dry vac with a 50/50 vinegar-water rinse.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Can I just use an air freshener?</summary><div class="body">It masks, never fixes. The smell will punch through in a day. Kill the moisture source first, then use a charcoal bag or a few drops of tea tree oil on a cotton ball under the seat.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I prevent it from coming back?</summary><div class="body">Keep a small silica gel pack in the car, crack windows on sunny days, and run the AC on fresh-air mode for the last few minutes of every drive. That dries the evaporator so mold can’t grow.</div></details><div class="callout info">If the smell is sweet or like maple syrup, that’s coolant—see a mechanic. Musty is almost always water.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Stop a Musty Car Smell</h1><p class="muted">That wet-sock smell usually means moisture is trapped in the AC system or carpet. Here’s how to dry it out and keep it that way, .</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why does my car smell musty after rain?</summary><div class="body">Water sneaks in through clogged sunroof drains, worn door seals, or a damp AC evaporator. Fix the leak first or the smell returns.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I clean the AC system?</summary><div class="body">Run the fan on high with recirculation off for 10 minutes after each AC use. Once a year, spray an evaporator cleaner into the intake (usually near the cabin filter) and let it drain. Replace the cabin filter every 15,000 miles.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What about the carpets?</summary><div class="body">If they’re damp, pull them out and dry in direct sun. Sprinkle baking soda, let sit overnight, then vacuum. For deep funk, use a wet-dry vac with a 50/50 vinegar-water rinse.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Can I just use an air freshener?</summary><div class="body">It masks, never fixes. The smell will punch through in a day. Kill the moisture source first, then use a charcoal bag or a few drops of tea tree oil on a cotton ball under the seat.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I prevent it from coming back?</summary><div class="body">Keep a small silica gel pack in the car, crack windows on sunny days, and run the AC on fresh-air mode for the last few minutes of every drive. That dries the evaporator so mold can’t grow.</div></details><div class="callout info">If the smell is sweet or like maple syrup, that’s coolant—see a mechanic. Musty is almost always water.</div></div>	-
39.0.1	dashboard_faq/Clean a Humidifier Properly	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Clean a Humidifier Properly</h1><p class="muted">A dirty humidifier just sprays germs into the air. Here’s the quick weekly routine that keeps it safe and silent, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><details class="acc"><summary>How often should I clean it?</summary><div class="body">Every day: empty and rinse the tank. Every week: deep clean with vinegar. Every month: check the filter or replace it. If you see pink slime or white crust, clean it now.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What’s the best cleaner?</summary><div class="body">White vinegar. Fill the tank with 1 part vinegar to 3 parts water, let sit 20 minutes, then scrub with a soft brush. For stubborn scale, use a paste of baking soda and a little water.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Can I use bleach?</summary><div class="body">Only if the manual says so. A dilute bleach solution (1 teaspoon per gallon) can disinfect, but rinse thoroughly and let it air dry completely—bleach fumes are harsh. Vinegar is safer for most homes.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What about the filter?</summary><div class="body">Most wick filters should be rinsed weekly and replaced every 1–2 months. If it’s hard, discolored, or smelly, replace it. Never run the humidifier without a filter if it’s designed for one.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I stop it from getting gross?</summary><div class="body">Use distilled water—tap water leaves minerals that feed mold. Empty the tank daily and let it dry upside down. Don’t leave standing water in it when not in use.</div></details><div class="callout good">A clean humidifier runs quieter and lasts years longer.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Clean a Humidifier Properly</h1><p class="muted">A dirty humidifier just sprays germs into the air. Here’s the quick weekly routine that keeps it safe and silent, .</p><details class="acc"><summary>How often should I clean it?</summary><div class="body">Every day: empty and rinse the tank. Every week: deep clean with vinegar. Every month: check the filter or replace it. If you see pink slime or white crust, clean it now.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What’s the best cleaner?</summary><div class="body">White vinegar. Fill the tank with 1 part vinegar to 3 parts water, let sit 20 minutes, then scrub with a soft brush. For stubborn scale, use a paste of baking soda and a little water.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Can I use bleach?</summary><div class="body">Only if the manual says so. A dilute bleach solution (1 teaspoon per gallon) can disinfect, but rinse thoroughly and let it air dry completely—bleach fumes are harsh. Vinegar is safer for most homes.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What about the filter?</summary><div class="body">Most wick filters should be rinsed weekly and replaced every 1–2 months. If it’s hard, discolored, or smelly, replace it. Never run the humidifier without a filter if it’s designed for one.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I stop it from getting gross?</summary><div class="body">Use distilled water—tap water leaves minerals that feed mold. Empty the tank daily and let it dry upside down. Don’t leave standing water in it when not in use.</div></details><div class="callout good">A clean humidifier runs quieter and lasts years longer.</div></div>	-
39.1.1	dashboard_faq/Banish Closet Moths	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Banish Closet Moths</h1><p class="muted">Those tiny holes are moth larvae having a feast. Here’s how to evict them and keep your wool safe, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><details class="acc"><summary>How do I know it’s moths?</summary><div class="body">Look for small irregular holes, silky webbing, or tiny cream-colored larvae in folds. Adults are small tan moths that avoid light. Check wool, silk, and fur first.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What kills them naturally?</summary><div class="body">Cedar, lavender, and cloves repel but don’t kill. To kill eggs and larvae, freeze infested items at 0°F for 72 hours, or wash in hot water and dry on high heat. Vacuum the closet thoroughly.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Are mothballs safe?</summary><div class="body">They work but are toxic and stink. Avoid if you have pets or kids. Safer: sealed cedar blocks, lavender sachets, or a few drops of cedarwood oil on cotton balls.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I prevent them coming back?</summary><div class="body">Store clean clothes—body oils attract moths. Seal woolens in airtight bags or bins. Keep the closet dry and ventilated. Refresh cedar every few months by sanding it lightly.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Do I need to toss everything?</summary><div class="body">No. If the damage is minor, freeze or wash the item. If it’s a precious piece, a tailor can reweave small holes. Just clean the whole closet and store the rest safely.</div></details><div class="callout warn">Moths love dark, undisturbed corners. Shake out and rotate stored clothes each season.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Banish Closet Moths</h1><p class="muted">Those tiny holes are moth larvae having a feast. Here’s how to evict them and keep your wool safe, .</p><details class="acc"><summary>How do I know it’s moths?</summary><div class="body">Look for small irregular holes, silky webbing, or tiny cream-colored larvae in folds. Adults are small tan moths that avoid light. Check wool, silk, and fur first.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What kills them naturally?</summary><div class="body">Cedar, lavender, and cloves repel but don’t kill. To kill eggs and larvae, freeze infested items at 0°F for 72 hours, or wash in hot water and dry on high heat. Vacuum the closet thoroughly.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Are mothballs safe?</summary><div class="body">They work but are toxic and stink. Avoid if you have pets or kids. Safer: sealed cedar blocks, lavender sachets, or a few drops of cedarwood oil on cotton balls.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I prevent them coming back?</summary><div class="body">Store clean clothes—body oils attract moths. Seal woolens in airtight bags or bins. Keep the closet dry and ventilated. Refresh cedar every few months by sanding it lightly.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Do I need to toss everything?</summary><div class="body">No. If the damage is minor, freeze or wash the item. If it’s a precious piece, a tailor can reweave small holes. Just clean the whole closet and store the rest safely.</div></details><div class="callout warn">Moths love dark, undisturbed corners. Shake out and rotate stored clothes each season.</div></div>	-
39.2.1	dashboard_faq/Speed Up a Slow Laptop	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Speed Up a Slow Laptop</h1><p class="muted">Before you buy a new one, try these five fixes. Most slow laptops just need a little breathing room, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><details class="acc"><summary>What’s the first thing to check?</summary><div class="body">Open Task Manager (Ctrl+Shift+Esc) and sort by CPU or Memory. If one app is hogging everything, close it. If your disk is at 100% for no reason, that’s often a Windows update or a failing hard drive.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I free up space?</summary><div class="body">Run Disk Cleanup and remove temporary files. Uninstall programs you don’t use. Move large files (videos, photos) to an external drive or cloud. Keep at least 15% of your drive empty.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Can I upgrade the hardware?</summary><div class="body">If you have an old spinning hard drive, swapping to an SSD is the single biggest speed boost. Adding more RAM helps if you constantly max out memory. Check your laptop’s manual for compatibility.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What about startup programs?</summary><div class="body">In Task Manager, go to the Startup tab and disable anything you don’t need at boot (like Spotify, Skype, or game launchers). They’ll still work when you open them manually.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Any software tricks?</summary><div class="body">Keep your OS and drivers updated. Run a malware scan. Adjust visual effects for best performance (search “Adjust the appearance and performance of Windows”). And restart regularly—really.</div></details><div class="callout good">An SSD upgrade can make a 5-year-old laptop feel new again for under $50.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Speed Up a Slow Laptop</h1><p class="muted">Before you buy a new one, try these five fixes. Most slow laptops just need a little breathing room, .</p><details class="acc"><summary>What’s the first thing to check?</summary><div class="body">Open Task Manager (Ctrl+Shift+Esc) and sort by CPU or Memory. If one app is hogging everything, close it. If your disk is at 100% for no reason, that’s often a Windows update or a failing hard drive.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I free up space?</summary><div class="body">Run Disk Cleanup and remove temporary files. Uninstall programs you don’t use. Move large files (videos, photos) to an external drive or cloud. Keep at least 15% of your drive empty.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Can I upgrade the hardware?</summary><div class="body">If you have an old spinning hard drive, swapping to an SSD is the single biggest speed boost. Adding more RAM helps if you constantly max out memory. Check your laptop’s manual for compatibility.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What about startup programs?</summary><div class="body">In Task Manager, go to the Startup tab and disable anything you don’t need at boot (like Spotify, Skype, or game launchers). They’ll still work when you open them manually.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Any software tricks?</summary><div class="body">Keep your OS and drivers updated. Run a malware scan. Adjust visual effects for best performance (search “Adjust the appearance and performance of Windows”). And restart regularly—really.</div></details><div class="callout good">An SSD upgrade can make a 5-year-old laptop feel new again for under $50.</div></div>	-
39.3.1	dashboard_faq/Tame Pet Hair Everywhere	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Tame Pet Hair Everywhere</h1><p class="muted">Fur finds a way. These tricks lift it off fabric fast so you’re not wearing your pet, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><details class="acc"><summary>What’s the quickest fix for clothes?</summary><div class="body">Dampen a rubber glove or a clean sponge and wipe in one direction. The hair clumps and lifts right off. For a lint roller, use short strokes—don’t press hard or you’ll just push hair deeper.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I clean the couch?</summary><div class="body">First, vacuum with a rubber pet-hair attachment. Then mist the fabric lightly with water and run a squeegee or rubber brush over it. For deep-set hair, use a pumice stone (gently) on upholstery.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What about carpets?</summary><div class="body">Vacuum slowly and overlap passes. Then sprinkle baking soda, let sit 15 minutes, and vacuum again. A window squeegee works wonders on carpeted stairs and car seats.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Any laundry tips?</summary><div class="body">Before washing, toss pet-hair-covered clothes in the dryer on air-fluff for 10 minutes with a dryer sheet. The hair collects in the lint trap. Then wash as usual with half a cup of white vinegar.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I prevent the hair storm?</summary><div class="body">Brush your pet outdoors daily. Keep a throw blanket on the couch that you can wash often. And change your HVAC filter every 1–3 months—it traps airborne fur.</div></details><div class="callout info">A squeegee is the secret weapon for pet hair on any fabric. Keep one just for this.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Tame Pet Hair Everywhere</h1><p class="muted">Fur finds a way. These tricks lift it off fabric fast so you’re not wearing your pet, .</p><details class="acc"><summary>What’s the quickest fix for clothes?</summary><div class="body">Dampen a rubber glove or a clean sponge and wipe in one direction. The hair clumps and lifts right off. For a lint roller, use short strokes—don’t press hard or you’ll just push hair deeper.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I clean the couch?</summary><div class="body">First, vacuum with a rubber pet-hair attachment. Then mist the fabric lightly with water and run a squeegee or rubber brush over it. For deep-set hair, use a pumice stone (gently) on upholstery.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What about carpets?</summary><div class="body">Vacuum slowly and overlap passes. Then sprinkle baking soda, let sit 15 minutes, and vacuum again. A window squeegee works wonders on carpeted stairs and car seats.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Any laundry tips?</summary><div class="body">Before washing, toss pet-hair-covered clothes in the dryer on air-fluff for 10 minutes with a dryer sheet. The hair collects in the lint trap. Then wash as usual with half a cup of white vinegar.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I prevent the hair storm?</summary><div class="body">Brush your pet outdoors daily. Keep a throw blanket on the couch that you can wash often. And change your HVAC filter every 1–3 months—it traps airborne fur.</div></details><div class="callout info">A squeegee is the secret weapon for pet hair on any fabric. Keep one just for this.</div></div>	-
39.4.1	dashboard_faq/Remove Toilet Bowl Rings	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Remove Toilet Bowl Rings</h1><p class="muted">That stubborn ring is mineral buildup, not just dirt. Here’s how to dissolve it without scrubbing your arm off, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><details class="acc"><summary>What causes the ring?</summary><div class="body">Hard water leaves calcium and magnesium deposits at the water line. Over time they harden into a crust that regular cleaners can’t touch. Acid is the answer.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What’s the easiest method?</summary><div class="body">Turn off the water supply and flush to empty the bowl. Pour in a cup of white vinegar and let it sit overnight. In the morning, scrub with a pumice stone (wet it first) and the ring wipes away.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Can I use a commercial cleaner?</summary><div class="body">Look for a cleaner that says “removes hard water stains” and contains hydrochloric acid or citric acid. Follow the label—never mix with bleach. Ventilate the bathroom.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Is a pumice stone safe?</summary><div class="body">Yes, if you wet it and use light pressure. It’s softer than porcelain but harder than the mineral ring. Don’t use it dry or on colored toilets.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I prevent it from returning?</summary><div class="body">Once a week, pour a cup of vinegar into the bowl and let it sit for 15 minutes before flushing. Or install a water softener. A clean bowl stays cleaner longer.</div></details><div class="callout warn">Never mix vinegar and bleach—it creates toxic chlorine gas.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Remove Toilet Bowl Rings</h1><p class="muted">That stubborn ring is mineral buildup, not just dirt. Here’s how to dissolve it without scrubbing your arm off, .</p><details class="acc"><summary>What causes the ring?</summary><div class="body">Hard water leaves calcium and magnesium deposits at the water line. Over time they harden into a crust that regular cleaners can’t touch. Acid is the answer.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What’s the easiest method?</summary><div class="body">Turn off the water supply and flush to empty the bowl. Pour in a cup of white vinegar and let it sit overnight. In the morning, scrub with a pumice stone (wet it first) and the ring wipes away.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Can I use a commercial cleaner?</summary><div class="body">Look for a cleaner that says “removes hard water stains” and contains hydrochloric acid or citric acid. Follow the label—never mix with bleach. Ventilate the bathroom.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Is a pumice stone safe?</summary><div class="body">Yes, if you wet it and use light pressure. It’s softer than porcelain but harder than the mineral ring. Don’t use it dry or on colored toilets.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I prevent it from returning?</summary><div class="body">Once a week, pour a cup of vinegar into the bowl and let it sit for 15 minutes before flushing. Or install a water softener. A clean bowl stays cleaner longer.</div></details><div class="callout warn">Never mix vinegar and bleach—it creates toxic chlorine gas.</div></div>	-
39.5.1	dashboard_faq/Banish Bathroom Mildew	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Banish Bathroom Mildew</h1><p>Mildew loves damp, dark corners. Starve it of moisture and it packs up.</p><details class="acc"><summary>What kills mildew best?</summary><div class="body"><p>Distilled white vinegar. Spray undiluted, let sit 10 minutes, scrub with an old toothbrush. For stubborn spots, make a paste of baking soda and water.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I stop it coming back?</summary><div class="body"><p>Run the exhaust fan during and 20 minutes after showers. Squeegee walls. Fix drips. Keep a small bowl of baking soda in the corner to absorb damp.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Is bleach safe on grout?</summary><div class="body"><p>Diluted bleach works but can erode grout over time. Vinegar is gentler. Never mix bleach and vinegar—toxic gas.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What about the shower curtain?</summary><div class="body"><p>Machine wash on gentle with a couple of towels and half a cup of vinegar. Air dry. Or replace a plastic liner—cheap and quick.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How can I prevent mildew on walls?</summary><div class="body"><p>Wipe down after the last shower. Leave the door open. A squeegee takes 10 seconds.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Does paint stop mildew?</summary><div class="body"><p>Mildew-resistant paint helps, but only after you kill the spores. Otherwise it grows through.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>When should I call a pro?</summary><div class="body"><p>If mildew covers more than a square metre or smells earthy all the time, there may be a leak. A pro can find it.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Banish Bathroom Mildew</h1><p>Mildew loves damp, dark corners. Starve it of moisture and it packs up.</p><details class="acc"><summary>What kills mildew best?</summary><div class="body"><p>Distilled white vinegar. Spray undiluted, let sit 10 minutes, scrub with an old toothbrush. For stubborn spots, make a paste of baking soda and water.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I stop it coming back?</summary><div class="body"><p>Run the exhaust fan during and 20 minutes after showers. Squeegee walls. Fix drips. Keep a small bowl of baking soda in the corner to absorb damp.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Is bleach safe on grout?</summary><div class="body"><p>Diluted bleach works but can erode grout over time. Vinegar is gentler. Never mix bleach and vinegar—toxic gas.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What about the shower curtain?</summary><div class="body"><p>Machine wash on gentle with a couple of towels and half a cup of vinegar. Air dry. Or replace a plastic liner—cheap and quick.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How can I prevent mildew on walls?</summary><div class="body"><p>Wipe down after the last shower. Leave the door open. A squeegee takes 10 seconds.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Does paint stop mildew?</summary><div class="body"><p>Mildew-resistant paint helps, but only after you kill the spores. Otherwise it grows through.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>When should I call a pro?</summary><div class="body"><p>If mildew covers more than a square metre or smells earthy all the time, there may be a leak. A pro can find it.</p></div></details></div>	-
39.6.1	dashboard_faq/Silence a Squeaky Fan	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Silence a Squeaky Fan</h1><p>Squeaks usually mean dry bearings or a loose screw. Both are easy fixes.</p><details class="acc"><summary>First, safety</summary><div class="body"><p>Turn off the power at the breaker. Let blades stop completely. Don't skip this.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Check for loose screws</summary><div class="body"><p>Tighten every screw on the blade brackets and motor housing. A quarter-turn can quiet a whole fan.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Lubricate the bearings</summary><div class="body"><p>If there's an oil port, add a few drops of sewing machine oil. No port? A shot of silicone spray where the shaft meets the motor. Spin by hand to work it in.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Balance the blades</summary><div class="body"><p>A wobble can cause squeaks. Use a balancing kit or tape a coin to the top of a blade to find the sweet spot.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What if it still squeaks?</summary><div class="body"><p>The bearing may be worn. For a ceiling fan, replacement is often cheaper than repair. For a box fan, just retire it.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Can I use WD-40?</summary><div class="body"><p>It works short-term but attracts dust. Use a proper lubricant for a lasting fix.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I prevent future squeaks?</summary><div class="body"><p>Dust the blades monthly. A clean fan runs quieter and lasts longer.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Silence a Squeaky Fan</h1><p>Squeaks usually mean dry bearings or a loose screw. Both are easy fixes.</p><details class="acc"><summary>First, safety</summary><div class="body"><p>Turn off the power at the breaker. Let blades stop completely. Don't skip this.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Check for loose screws</summary><div class="body"><p>Tighten every screw on the blade brackets and motor housing. A quarter-turn can quiet a whole fan.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Lubricate the bearings</summary><div class="body"><p>If there's an oil port, add a few drops of sewing machine oil. No port? A shot of silicone spray where the shaft meets the motor. Spin by hand to work it in.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Balance the blades</summary><div class="body"><p>A wobble can cause squeaks. Use a balancing kit or tape a coin to the top of a blade to find the sweet spot.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What if it still squeaks?</summary><div class="body"><p>The bearing may be worn. For a ceiling fan, replacement is often cheaper than repair. For a box fan, just retire it.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Can I use WD-40?</summary><div class="body"><p>It works short-term but attracts dust. Use a proper lubricant for a lasting fix.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I prevent future squeaks?</summary><div class="body"><p>Dust the blades monthly. A clean fan runs quieter and lasts longer.</p></div></details></div>	-
39.7.1	dashboard_faq/Clean a Grimy Stovetop	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Clean a Grimy Stovetop</h1><p>Grease and burnt-on bits don't stand a chance against hot water and patience.</p><details class="acc"><summary>For electric coil burners</summary><div class="body"><p>Remove coils and drip pans. Soak pans in hot soapy water. Scrub with baking soda paste. Don't get the coil connections wet.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>For smooth glass tops</summary><div class="body"><p>While slightly warm, sprinkle baking soda, add a splash of vinegar, cover with a hot wet towel for 15 minutes. Wipe clean. For scorch marks, use a razor blade at a low angle.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>For gas grates</summary><div class="body"><p>Soak in hot water and dish soap. Scrub with a stiff brush. For stubborn spots, make a paste of baking soda and water, let sit, then scrub.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What about the knobs?</summary><div class="body"><p>Pull them off and wash in soapy water. Don't soak—water can ruin the mechanism.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How often should I deep clean?</summary><div class="body"><p>Once a week for the surface, once a month for the parts. A daily wipe-down keeps it manageable.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Can I use oven cleaner?</summary><div class="body"><p>Only on enamel surfaces, never on glass tops or aluminum. Ventilate well and wear gloves.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>My stovetop is still stained—help?</summary><div class="body"><p>For glass, a ceramic cooktop cleaner and a little elbow grease. For coils, sometimes drip pans just need replacing.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Clean a Grimy Stovetop</h1><p>Grease and burnt-on bits don't stand a chance against hot water and patience.</p><details class="acc"><summary>For electric coil burners</summary><div class="body"><p>Remove coils and drip pans. Soak pans in hot soapy water. Scrub with baking soda paste. Don't get the coil connections wet.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>For smooth glass tops</summary><div class="body"><p>While slightly warm, sprinkle baking soda, add a splash of vinegar, cover with a hot wet towel for 15 minutes. Wipe clean. For scorch marks, use a razor blade at a low angle.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>For gas grates</summary><div class="body"><p>Soak in hot water and dish soap. Scrub with a stiff brush. For stubborn spots, make a paste of baking soda and water, let sit, then scrub.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What about the knobs?</summary><div class="body"><p>Pull them off and wash in soapy water. Don't soak—water can ruin the mechanism.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How often should I deep clean?</summary><div class="body"><p>Once a week for the surface, once a month for the parts. A daily wipe-down keeps it manageable.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Can I use oven cleaner?</summary><div class="body"><p>Only on enamel surfaces, never on glass tops or aluminum. Ventilate well and wear gloves.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>My stovetop is still stained—help?</summary><div class="body"><p>For glass, a ceramic cooktop cleaner and a little elbow grease. For coils, sometimes drip pans just need replacing.</p></div></details></div>	-
39.8.1	dashboard_faq/Get Wax Out of Carpet	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Get Wax Out of Carpet</h1><p>Wax hardens as it cools. Use that to your advantage—freeze it, then pick it off.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Step 1: Harden the wax</summary><div class="body"><p>Rub an ice cube in a plastic bag over the wax until it's brittle. Pick off as much as you can with a dull knife.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Step 2: Iron it out</summary><div class="body"><p>Place a brown paper bag or clean towel over the spot. Iron on low. The wax melts and transfers to the paper. Keep moving the iron and replace the paper as needed.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Step 3: Clean residue</summary><div class="body"><p>Dab with a cloth dampened with rubbing alcohol or vinegar. Blot, don't rub.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What if the wax is colored?</summary><div class="body"><p>Dye may remain. Mix a little dish soap with warm water, blot, then rinse. Repeat until gone.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Can I use a hair dryer?</summary><div class="body"><p>Yes, to soften, but you risk spreading it. The iron-and-paper method is cleaner.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I prevent future spills?</summary><div class="body"><p>Use a candle holder with a wide base, and keep candles away from edges.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Will this work on synthetic carpets?</summary><div class="body"><p>Yes, but test the alcohol on a hidden spot first. Some synthetics are sensitive.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Get Wax Out of Carpet</h1><p>Wax hardens as it cools. Use that to your advantage—freeze it, then pick it off.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Step 1: Harden the wax</summary><div class="body"><p>Rub an ice cube in a plastic bag over the wax until it's brittle. Pick off as much as you can with a dull knife.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Step 2: Iron it out</summary><div class="body"><p>Place a brown paper bag or clean towel over the spot. Iron on low. The wax melts and transfers to the paper. Keep moving the iron and replace the paper as needed.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Step 3: Clean residue</summary><div class="body"><p>Dab with a cloth dampened with rubbing alcohol or vinegar. Blot, don't rub.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What if the wax is colored?</summary><div class="body"><p>Dye may remain. Mix a little dish soap with warm water, blot, then rinse. Repeat until gone.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Can I use a hair dryer?</summary><div class="body"><p>Yes, to soften, but you risk spreading it. The iron-and-paper method is cleaner.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I prevent future spills?</summary><div class="body"><p>Use a candle holder with a wide base, and keep candles away from edges.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Will this work on synthetic carpets?</summary><div class="body"><p>Yes, but test the alcohol on a hidden spot first. Some synthetics are sensitive.</p></div></details></div>	-
39.9.1	dashboard_faq/Fix a Slow-Draining Sink	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Fix a Slow-Draining Sink</h1><p>Most slow drains are just hair and soap scum. You can clear them without harsh chemicals.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Try boiling water first</summary><div class="body"><p>Pour a kettle of boiling water down the drain in stages. Works for grease clogs in the kitchen.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Baking soda and vinegar</summary><div class="body"><p>Pour half a cup of baking soda, then half a cup of vinegar. Cover with a plug for 15 minutes. Flush with hot water. Repeat if needed.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Use a plunger</summary><div class="body"><p>Block the overflow hole with a wet rag. Plunge with quick, firm strokes. Works for both sinks.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Snake the drain</summary><div class="body"><p>Remove the stopper. Feed a drain snake or a bent wire hanger down the drain. Twist and pull to hook the clog.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Clean the P-trap</summary><div class="body"><p>Place a bucket under the trap. Unscrew the slip nuts, remove the trap, and clean it out. Reassemble and run water.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What about chemical drain cleaners?</summary><div class="body"><p>They can damage pipes and are dangerous. Use them only as a last resort, and never after a plunger.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Prevention tips</summary><div class="body"><p>Use a mesh strainer. Once a week, pour a kettle of boiling water down the drain. That's it.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Fix a Slow-Draining Sink</h1><p>Most slow drains are just hair and soap scum. You can clear them without harsh chemicals.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Try boiling water first</summary><div class="body"><p>Pour a kettle of boiling water down the drain in stages. Works for grease clogs in the kitchen.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Baking soda and vinegar</summary><div class="body"><p>Pour half a cup of baking soda, then half a cup of vinegar. Cover with a plug for 15 minutes. Flush with hot water. Repeat if needed.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Use a plunger</summary><div class="body"><p>Block the overflow hole with a wet rag. Plunge with quick, firm strokes. Works for both sinks.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Snake the drain</summary><div class="body"><p>Remove the stopper. Feed a drain snake or a bent wire hanger down the drain. Twist and pull to hook the clog.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Clean the P-trap</summary><div class="body"><p>Place a bucket under the trap. Unscrew the slip nuts, remove the trap, and clean it out. Reassemble and run water.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What about chemical drain cleaners?</summary><div class="body"><p>They can damage pipes and are dangerous. Use them only as a last resort, and never after a plunger.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Prevention tips</summary><div class="body"><p>Use a mesh strainer. Once a week, pour a kettle of boiling water down the drain. That's it.</p></div></details></div>	-
39.10.1	dashboard_faq/Remove Ink Stains from Fabric	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Remove Ink Stains from Fabric</h1><p>Act fast. The longer ink sits, the harder it sets.</p><details class="acc"><summary>First, blot—don't rub</summary><div class="body"><p>Use a clean cloth to blot up excess ink. Rubbing spreads it deeper into the fibers.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Try alcohol</summary><div class="body"><p>Isopropyl alcohol (or hairspray with alcohol) on a cotton ball. Dab from the outside in. Replace the cotton ball as it picks up ink.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>For stubborn stains</summary><div class="body"><p>Make a paste of baking soda and water. Apply, let sit 15 minutes, then rinse. For white fabrics, a little hydrogen peroxide can help—test first.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What about delicate fabrics?</summary><div class="body"><p>Use a mild dish soap solution and cold water. Dab gently. If it's silk or wool, consider a pro.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Can I use nail polish remover?</summary><div class="body"><p>It can work on some inks, but it may damage synthetic fabrics. Test on a hidden seam first.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>After stain removal</summary><div class="body"><p>Launder as usual, but check the stain before drying—heat sets any residue.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Prevention tip</summary><div class="body"><p>Store pens with caps on, and keep a stain pen in your bag. <regex>user_name</regex>, you'll thank yourself later.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Remove Ink Stains from Fabric</h1><p>Act fast. The longer ink sits, the harder it sets.</p><details class="acc"><summary>First, blot—don't rub</summary><div class="body"><p>Use a clean cloth to blot up excess ink. Rubbing spreads it deeper into the fibers.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Try alcohol</summary><div class="body"><p>Isopropyl alcohol (or hairspray with alcohol) on a cotton ball. Dab from the outside in. Replace the cotton ball as it picks up ink.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>For stubborn stains</summary><div class="body"><p>Make a paste of baking soda and water. Apply, let sit 15 minutes, then rinse. For white fabrics, a little hydrogen peroxide can help—test first.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What about delicate fabrics?</summary><div class="body"><p>Use a mild dish soap solution and cold water. Dab gently. If it's silk or wool, consider a pro.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Can I use nail polish remover?</summary><div class="body"><p>It can work on some inks, but it may damage synthetic fabrics. Test on a hidden seam first.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>After stain removal</summary><div class="body"><p>Launder as usual, but check the stain before drying—heat sets any residue.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Prevention tip</summary><div class="body"><p>Store pens with caps on, and keep a stain pen in your bag. , you'll thank yourself later.</p></div></details></div>	-
39.11.1	dashboard_faq/Banish Salt Stains from Your Boots	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Banish Salt Stains from Your Boots</h1><p class="muted">Hi <regex>user_name</regex> — winter leaves chalky rings on leather, and they come off faster than you'd think.</p><div class="callout info"><strong>Do this first.</strong> Blot, don't scrub. Salt drives deeper when it's wet and agitated.</div><details class="acc"><summary>What exactly are those white rings?</summary><div class="body">Dried road salt plus the minerals it pulls from the leather. They're water-soluble, which is why water also spreads them if you're careless.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Quick fix with stuff you already own</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Mix 1 part white vinegar with 1 part cool water.</li><li>Dab a cloth — don't drown the boot — and work only the stain.</li><li>Wipe with a clean damp cloth to lift the vinegar.</li><li>Air dry away from radiators, then condition the leather.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>I only have dish soap. Is that okay?</summary><div class="body">Yes. A drop of mild soap in cool water works on fresh spots. Straight vinegar handles the stubborn, older rings.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I keep it from happening again?</summary><div class="body">Rinse boots with plain water after slushy days, dry overnight, and rub in a wax or leather conditioner before the season starts.</div></details><div class="callout warn">Skip the washing machine and the dryer. Heat cracks leather and bakes salt in deeper.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Banish Salt Stains from Your Boots</h1><p class="muted">Hi  — winter leaves chalky rings on leather, and they come off faster than you'd think.</p><div class="callout info"><strong>Do this first.</strong> Blot, don't scrub. Salt drives deeper when it's wet and agitated.</div><details class="acc"><summary>What exactly are those white rings?</summary><div class="body">Dried road salt plus the minerals it pulls from the leather. They're water-soluble, which is why water also spreads them if you're careless.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Quick fix with stuff you already own</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Mix 1 part white vinegar with 1 part cool water.</li><li>Dab a cloth — don't drown the boot — and work only the stain.</li><li>Wipe with a clean damp cloth to lift the vinegar.</li><li>Air dry away from radiators, then condition the leather.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>I only have dish soap. Is that okay?</summary><div class="body">Yes. A drop of mild soap in cool water works on fresh spots. Straight vinegar handles the stubborn, older rings.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I keep it from happening again?</summary><div class="body">Rinse boots with plain water after slushy days, dry overnight, and rub in a wax or leather conditioner before the season starts.</div></details><div class="callout warn">Skip the washing machine and the dryer. Heat cracks leather and bakes salt in deeper.</div></div>	-
39.12.1	dashboard_faq/Stop Glasses From Fogging Up	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Stop Glasses From Fogging Up</h1><p class="muted">Hello <regex>user_name</regex> — fog is just warm, wet air cooling on cold lenses. Block the air and it stops.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Mask wearer's number one fix</summary><div class="body">Pinch the nose wire down tight and tuck the top edge of the mask <em>under</em> your glasses, not over. That stops the breath stream before it reaches the lens.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>The soap trick that actually works</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Rub a tiny dab of plain hand soap on both sides of the lens.</li><li>Buff it off gently with a soft cloth — you're leaving an invisible film, not suds.</li><li>Redo it every morning or two.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Does spit work? Be honest.</summary><div class="body">Swimmers do it, and it half-works, but it's messy and you'll see streaks. Soap is the civilised version.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Coming in from the cold</summary><div class="body">Give the lenses ten seconds to warm before you walk into a steamy room. Or look down and let your brow shield them.</div></details><div class="callout good">Microfiber cloths stay lint-free and won't scratch coatings. Keep one in your bag.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Stop Glasses From Fogging Up</h1><p class="muted">Hello  — fog is just warm, wet air cooling on cold lenses. Block the air and it stops.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Mask wearer's number one fix</summary><div class="body">Pinch the nose wire down tight and tuck the top edge of the mask <em>under</em> your glasses, not over. That stops the breath stream before it reaches the lens.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>The soap trick that actually works</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Rub a tiny dab of plain hand soap on both sides of the lens.</li><li>Buff it off gently with a soft cloth — you're leaving an invisible film, not suds.</li><li>Redo it every morning or two.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Does spit work? Be honest.</summary><div class="body">Swimmers do it, and it half-works, but it's messy and you'll see streaks. Soap is the civilised version.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Coming in from the cold</summary><div class="body">Give the lenses ten seconds to warm before you walk into a steamy room. Or look down and let your brow shield them.</div></details><div class="callout good">Microfiber cloths stay lint-free and won't scratch coatings. Keep one in your bag.</div></div>	-
39.13.1	dashboard_faq/Make Your Razor Last Longer	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Make Your Razor Last Longer</h1><p class="muted">Hi <regex>user_name</regex> — most blades die of rust and gunk, not shaving. Dry them and they'll surprise you.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Typical life</span><span class="value">5<span class="unit">shaves</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Dried after use</span><span class="value">20<span class="unit">shaves</span></span></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>The two-second habit</summary><div class="body">After shaving, shake the water off and stand the razor upright, blade up and away from the shower spray. Standing water is what eats the edge.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Skip the strop myths</summary><div class="body">Rubbing a blade up your forearm or on denim helps a little, but only if you're already drying it. Don't bother on a rusty cartridge.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Shaving cream matters</summary><div class="body">Soap scum and hair clog the gaps between blades and dull the cut. Rinse the head thoroughly under running water after every pass.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>When to actually replace it</summary><div class="body">If it tugs instead of glides, it's done. No trick restores a rolled edge.</div></details><div class="callout warn">Storing a razor in a closed shower caddy traps moisture. Out in the open, blade up, is best.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Make Your Razor Last Longer</h1><p class="muted">Hi  — most blades die of rust and gunk, not shaving. Dry them and they'll surprise you.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Typical life</span><span class="value">5<span class="unit">shaves</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Dried after use</span><span class="value">20<span class="unit">shaves</span></span></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>The two-second habit</summary><div class="body">After shaving, shake the water off and stand the razor upright, blade up and away from the shower spray. Standing water is what eats the edge.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Skip the strop myths</summary><div class="body">Rubbing a blade up your forearm or on denim helps a little, but only if you're already drying it. Don't bother on a rusty cartridge.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Shaving cream matters</summary><div class="body">Soap scum and hair clog the gaps between blades and dull the cut. Rinse the head thoroughly under running water after every pass.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>When to actually replace it</summary><div class="body">If it tugs instead of glides, it's done. No trick restores a rolled edge.</div></details><div class="callout warn">Storing a razor in a closed shower caddy traps moisture. Out in the open, blade up, is best.</div></div>	-
39.14.1	dashboard_faq/Quiet a Clicking Ceiling Fan	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Quiet a Clicking Ceiling Fan</h1><p class="muted">Hi <regex>user_name</regex> — a rhythmic click is almost always something loose, not the motor giving up.</p><div class="callout info">Turn the fan off at the wall before you touch anything. Safety first.</div><details class="acc"><summary>Step 1 — The obvious one</summary><div class="body">Tighten every visible screw on the blade brackets and the housing. Most clicks are a single loose blade screw, once per rotation.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Step 2 — Check the pull chain</summary><div class="body">If the chain or its little beaded tassel hangs close to the shade or a bulb, air movement makes it tick. Trim or move it.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Step 3 — Look at the light kit</summary><div class="body">Glass shades and bulb cups rattle against the fan housing. Give them a gentle twist to seat them, or add a small rubber washer.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Step 4 — Blade balance</summary><div class="body">A wobbly fan can clack each pass. After the fan cools, tighten the mounting screws and check the blades aren't bent or dusty on one side.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Still clicking?</summary><div class="body">Could be a dry bearing or a worn pull-chain switch. That's an electrician's five-minute job, so call one before you climb a ladder twice.</div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Quiet a Clicking Ceiling Fan</h1><p class="muted">Hi  — a rhythmic click is almost always something loose, not the motor giving up.</p><div class="callout info">Turn the fan off at the wall before you touch anything. Safety first.</div><details class="acc"><summary>Step 1 — The obvious one</summary><div class="body">Tighten every visible screw on the blade brackets and the housing. Most clicks are a single loose blade screw, once per rotation.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Step 2 — Check the pull chain</summary><div class="body">If the chain or its little beaded tassel hangs close to the shade or a bulb, air movement makes it tick. Trim or move it.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Step 3 — Look at the light kit</summary><div class="body">Glass shades and bulb cups rattle against the fan housing. Give them a gentle twist to seat them, or add a small rubber washer.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Step 4 — Blade balance</summary><div class="body">A wobbly fan can clack each pass. After the fan cools, tighten the mounting screws and check the blades aren't bent or dusty on one side.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Still clicking?</summary><div class="body">Could be a dry bearing or a worn pull-chain switch. That's an electrician's five-minute job, so call one before you climb a ladder twice.</div></details></div>	-
39.15.1	dashboard_faq/Revive a Dried-Out Pen	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Revive a Dried-Out Pen</h1><p class="muted">Hi <regex>user_name</regex> — ink dries at the tip, not in the tube. Loosen the tip and you're back in business.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Ballpoint, method one</summary><div class="body">Scribble hard on scrap paper using fast zig-zags for five seconds. The friction warms the ball and often breaks the dried film.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Ballpoint, method two</summary><div class="body">Hold the tip briefly against a warm (not hot) mug of water, or breathe on it. Roll the ball with your thumb. A tiny dab of water or saliva on the tip also works.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Gel pens</summary><div class="body">Shake it tip-down with the cap off, then press lightly. If the tip is clogged, wipe it on your palm with a rolling motion. Rubbing alcohol on a cotton swab clears stubborn gel.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Fountain pens</summary><div class="body">Don't fight a dry nib. Pull the converter and flush the whole thing with cool water until it runs clear. Dry it, refill, and write on.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Store pens tip-down</summary><div class="body">Gravity keeps ink at the ball and cuts drying in half. A cup with the writing end down is your friend.</div></details><div class="callout warn">Never heat a pen in a microwave or over a flame — ink cartridges burst.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Revive a Dried-Out Pen</h1><p class="muted">Hi  — ink dries at the tip, not in the tube. Loosen the tip and you're back in business.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Ballpoint, method one</summary><div class="body">Scribble hard on scrap paper using fast zig-zags for five seconds. The friction warms the ball and often breaks the dried film.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Ballpoint, method two</summary><div class="body">Hold the tip briefly against a warm (not hot) mug of water, or breathe on it. Roll the ball with your thumb. A tiny dab of water or saliva on the tip also works.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Gel pens</summary><div class="body">Shake it tip-down with the cap off, then press lightly. If the tip is clogged, wipe it on your palm with a rolling motion. Rubbing alcohol on a cotton swab clears stubborn gel.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Fountain pens</summary><div class="body">Don't fight a dry nib. Pull the converter and flush the whole thing with cool water until it runs clear. Dry it, refill, and write on.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Store pens tip-down</summary><div class="body">Gravity keeps ink at the ball and cuts drying in half. A cup with the writing end down is your friend.</div></details><div class="callout warn">Never heat a pen in a microwave or over a flame — ink cartridges burst.</div></div>	-
39.16.1	dashboard_faq/Get the Burnt Smell Out of Your House	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Get the Burnt Smell Out of Your House</h1><p class="muted">Hi <regex>user_name</regex> — masks won't help. You have to remove the smoke, then the smell.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Step 1 — Clear the air properly</summary><div class="body">Open two windows on opposite sides, and run the exhaust fan or a box fan pointing outward. Fresh air in one side, smoky air out the other.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Step 2 — Find the source</summary><div class="body">Check the toaster, oven coils, and the drip tray under the stovetop. A lump of charred food will keep re-scenting the room every time you heat the oven.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Step 3 — Absorb what's left</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Set out bowls of baking soda or ground coffee overnight.</li><li>Simmer a pot of water with lemon slices or a splash of vanilla on low for 20 minutes.</li><li>Wipe hard surfaces, cabinets included, with a mild vinegar solution.</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What about fabrics?</summary><div class="body">Curtains, cushions, and jackets hold smoke. Air them outside for a few hours, or run a wash cycle with a cup of white vinegar in the rinse.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Should I light a candle? No.</summary><div class="body">Candles and plugins layer a second smell over the first. Fix the air, then scent if you like.</div></details><div class="callout good">If a burnt smell returns every time you bake, run the oven's self-clean cycle or clean the racks with baking soda paste.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Get the Burnt Smell Out of Your House</h1><p class="muted">Hi  — masks won't help. You have to remove the smoke, then the smell.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Step 1 — Clear the air properly</summary><div class="body">Open two windows on opposite sides, and run the exhaust fan or a box fan pointing outward. Fresh air in one side, smoky air out the other.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Step 2 — Find the source</summary><div class="body">Check the toaster, oven coils, and the drip tray under the stovetop. A lump of charred food will keep re-scenting the room every time you heat the oven.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Step 3 — Absorb what's left</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Set out bowls of baking soda or ground coffee overnight.</li><li>Simmer a pot of water with lemon slices or a splash of vanilla on low for 20 minutes.</li><li>Wipe hard surfaces, cabinets included, with a mild vinegar solution.</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What about fabrics?</summary><div class="body">Curtains, cushions, and jackets hold smoke. Air them outside for a few hours, or run a wash cycle with a cup of white vinegar in the rinse.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Should I light a candle? No.</summary><div class="body">Candles and plugins layer a second smell over the first. Fix the air, then scent if you like.</div></details><div class="callout good">If a burnt smell returns every time you bake, run the oven's self-clean cycle or clean the racks with baking soda paste.</div></div>	-
39.17.1	dashboard_faq/Banish Burnt Smell from Your House	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Banish Burnt Smell from Your House</h1>  <p class="muted">That scorched-toast vibe is clingy. Here's how to send it packing, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p>  <div class="card">    <h2 class="card-title">Quick steps</h2>    <ol class="list">      <li><strong>Open everything.</strong> Windows, doors, even the oven door. Cross-ventilate for 15 minutes.</li>      <li><strong>Find the source.</strong> Check the oven, toaster, microwave, and any pans. Remove burnt bits.</li>      <li><strong>Simmer a fix.</strong> In a pot, combine 1 cup water, 1 cup white vinegar, and a sliced lemon. Simmer on low for 10–15 minutes. <em>Don't let it boil dry.</em></li>      <li><strong>Absorb leftovers.</strong> Set out bowls of baking soda or activated charcoal overnight.</li>      <li><strong>Wipe surfaces.</strong> Wash counters, stovetop, and walls near the source with warm soapy water.</li>    </ol>  </div>  <div class="card flat">    <h2 class="card-title">If it's stubborn</h2>    <ul class="list">      <li>Run the microwave with a bowl of water and lemon for 3 minutes, then wipe inside.</li>      <li>Change your HVAC filter if the smell spread.</li>      <li>For fabrics (curtains, couch), spritz with a 50/50 vinegar-water mix and air dry.</li>    </ul>    <div class="callout info">      <p><strong>Good to know:</strong> Vinegar smell fades as it dries, taking the burnt smell with it.</p>    </div>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What if the smell is in the oven itself?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Make a paste of baking soda and water, coat the inside (avoid heating elements), let sit 20 minutes, then wipe. Run a self-clean cycle if you have one, but ventilate well.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Banish Burnt Smell from Your House</h1>  <p class="muted">That scorched-toast vibe is clingy. Here's how to send it packing, .</p>  <div class="card">    <h2 class="card-title">Quick steps</h2>    <ol class="list">      <li><strong>Open everything.</strong> Windows, doors, even the oven door. Cross-ventilate for 15 minutes.</li>      <li><strong>Find the source.</strong> Check the oven, toaster, microwave, and any pans. Remove burnt bits.</li>      <li><strong>Simmer a fix.</strong> In a pot, combine 1 cup water, 1 cup white vinegar, and a sliced lemon. Simmer on low for 10–15 minutes. <em>Don't let it boil dry.</em></li>      <li><strong>Absorb leftovers.</strong> Set out bowls of baking soda or activated charcoal overnight.</li>      <li><strong>Wipe surfaces.</strong> Wash counters, stovetop, and walls near the source with warm soapy water.</li>    </ol>  </div>  <div class="card flat">    <h2 class="card-title">If it's stubborn</h2>    <ul class="list">      <li>Run the microwave with a bowl of water and lemon for 3 minutes, then wipe inside.</li>      <li>Change your HVAC filter if the smell spread.</li>      <li>For fabrics (curtains, couch), spritz with a 50/50 vinegar-water mix and air dry.</li>    </ul>    <div class="callout info">      <p><strong>Good to know:</strong> Vinegar smell fades as it dries, taking the burnt smell with it.</p>    </div>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What if the smell is in the oven itself?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Make a paste of baking soda and water, coat the inside (avoid heating elements), let sit 20 minutes, then wipe. Run a self-clean cycle if you have one, but ventilate well.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
39.18.1	dashboard_faq/De-Funk Your Gym Bag	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h1 class="card-title">De-Funk Your Gym Bag</h1><p class="muted">That bag has seen things. Let's fix the smell.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why does my gym bag smell like a locker room?</summary><div class="body"><p>Sweat, damp towels, and dark fabric are a petri dish. The odor is bacteria feasting on your leftovers. The fix is drying it out and killing the bacteria, not covering it up.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I actually clean it?</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Empty it completely. Shake out crumbs and hair ties.</li><li>Mix 1 part white vinegar with 2 parts water in a spray bottle.</li><li>Spray the inside and wipe with a microfiber cloth.</li><li>If the bag is washable, machine wash cold on gentle and air dry. If not, let it sit in the sun for a few hours.</li></ol><p class="callout info">Vinegar kills odor-causing bacteria. Baking soda absorbs leftover smells. Use both for a one-two punch.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What about the stink that won't quit?</summary><div class="body"><p>Sprinkle baking soda inside, close the bag, and leave it overnight. Vacuum or shake it out the next morning. For stubborn cases, add a few drops of tea tree oil to a cotton ball and tuck it in a pocket.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I keep it fresh between washes?</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Never put damp gear in the bag. Hang it to dry first.</li><li>Keep a dryer sheet or cedar block inside when not in use.</li><li>Wipe the inside with a disinfecting wipe once a week.</li></ul><p>Do that and your bag will smell like a bag, not a biohazard.</p></div></details></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h1 class="card-title">De-Funk Your Gym Bag</h1><p class="muted">That bag has seen things. Let's fix the smell.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why does my gym bag smell like a locker room?</summary><div class="body"><p>Sweat, damp towels, and dark fabric are a petri dish. The odor is bacteria feasting on your leftovers. The fix is drying it out and killing the bacteria, not covering it up.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I actually clean it?</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Empty it completely. Shake out crumbs and hair ties.</li><li>Mix 1 part white vinegar with 2 parts water in a spray bottle.</li><li>Spray the inside and wipe with a microfiber cloth.</li><li>If the bag is washable, machine wash cold on gentle and air dry. If not, let it sit in the sun for a few hours.</li></ol><p class="callout info">Vinegar kills odor-causing bacteria. Baking soda absorbs leftover smells. Use both for a one-two punch.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What about the stink that won't quit?</summary><div class="body"><p>Sprinkle baking soda inside, close the bag, and leave it overnight. Vacuum or shake it out the next morning. For stubborn cases, add a few drops of tea tree oil to a cotton ball and tuck it in a pocket.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I keep it fresh between washes?</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Never put damp gear in the bag. Hang it to dry first.</li><li>Keep a dryer sheet or cedar block inside when not in use.</li><li>Wipe the inside with a disinfecting wipe once a week.</li></ul><p>Do that and your bag will smell like a bag, not a biohazard.</p></div></details></div></div>	-
39.19.1	dashboard_faq/Remove Chewing Gum from Fabric	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h1 class="card-title">Remove Chewing Gum from Fabric</h1><p class="muted">Gum on fabric is a nuisance, but not a lost cause. Here's how to get it off without losing your cool or your clothes.</p><details class="acc"><summary>First things first: don't pull it.</summary><div class="body"><p>Pulling stretches the gum and pushes it deeper into the fibers. Instead, reach for ice. Hardening the gum makes it brittle and easier to lift off.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>The ice method</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Put ice cubes in a plastic bag and hold it against the gum for a few minutes.</li><li>Once the gum is frozen solid, use a dull knife or spoon to lift it off.</li><li>Peel away as much as you can. Don't rush this part.</li></ol><p class="callout good">Works on most fabrics, including denim, cotton, and upholstery. For delicate fabrics, test a small area first.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What if there's still residue?</summary><div class="body"><p>Apply a small amount of oil (olive, baby, or coconut) to the remaining gum. Let it sit for a few minutes to break down the stickiness. Then wash the fabric as usual. If the fabric is dry-clean only, take it to a pro and point out the spot.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Can I use peanut butter?</summary><div class="body"><p>Yes, the oils in peanut butter can loosen gum. It's messy but effective. Just make sure you wash out the peanut butter afterward, or you'll trade one stain for another.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What about gum on carpet?</summary><div class="body"><p>Same ice method. Once frozen, scrape it up with a spoon. Vacuum thoroughly. If a stain remains, dab with a mix of dish soap and warm water, then blot dry.</p></div></details></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h1 class="card-title">Remove Chewing Gum from Fabric</h1><p class="muted">Gum on fabric is a nuisance, but not a lost cause. Here's how to get it off without losing your cool or your clothes.</p><details class="acc"><summary>First things first: don't pull it.</summary><div class="body"><p>Pulling stretches the gum and pushes it deeper into the fibers. Instead, reach for ice. Hardening the gum makes it brittle and easier to lift off.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>The ice method</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Put ice cubes in a plastic bag and hold it against the gum for a few minutes.</li><li>Once the gum is frozen solid, use a dull knife or spoon to lift it off.</li><li>Peel away as much as you can. Don't rush this part.</li></ol><p class="callout good">Works on most fabrics, including denim, cotton, and upholstery. For delicate fabrics, test a small area first.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What if there's still residue?</summary><div class="body"><p>Apply a small amount of oil (olive, baby, or coconut) to the remaining gum. Let it sit for a few minutes to break down the stickiness. Then wash the fabric as usual. If the fabric is dry-clean only, take it to a pro and point out the spot.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Can I use peanut butter?</summary><div class="body"><p>Yes, the oils in peanut butter can loosen gum. It's messy but effective. Just make sure you wash out the peanut butter afterward, or you'll trade one stain for another.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What about gum on carpet?</summary><div class="body"><p>Same ice method. Once frozen, scrape it up with a spoon. Vacuum thoroughly. If a stain remains, dab with a mix of dish soap and warm water, then blot dry.</p></div></details></div></div>	-
39.20.1	dashboard_faq/Eliminate Pet Odors from Carpet	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h1 class="card-title">Eliminate Pet Odors from Carpet</h1><p class="muted">Your carpet is holding onto smells you can't see. Here's how to evict them.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why does my carpet still smell after vacuuming?</summary><div class="body"><p>Vacuuming removes surface dirt, not odor molecules. Pet dander, urine, and saliva soak into the pad and fibers. You need to break down the proteins, not just clean the surface.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>The enzyme cleaner approach</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Blot up any fresh mess with a clean cloth. Don't rub.</li><li>Apply an enzyme cleaner generously to the spot. These cleaners use beneficial bacteria to eat the odor-causing proteins.</li><li>Let it sit for 10–15 minutes, then blot dry.</li><li>Rinse with a little water and blot again. Let it air dry completely.</li></ol><p class="callout info">Enzyme cleaners work best when they can soak into the pad. For old stains, you may need to apply more than once.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What about the whole room?</summary><div class="body"><p>Sprinkle baking soda over the carpet, let it sit for a few hours, then vacuum. For a deeper clean, rent a carpet cleaner and use a pet-specific shampoo. Follow with a rinse cycle.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I prevent future odors?</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Blot accidents immediately.</li><li>Keep a designated pet towel for muddy paws.</li><li>Vacuum regularly with a HEPA filter.</li><li>Use a carpet deodorizer made for pets.</li></ul><p>Consistency wins this battle.</p></div></details></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h1 class="card-title">Eliminate Pet Odors from Carpet</h1><p class="muted">Your carpet is holding onto smells you can't see. Here's how to evict them.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why does my carpet still smell after vacuuming?</summary><div class="body"><p>Vacuuming removes surface dirt, not odor molecules. Pet dander, urine, and saliva soak into the pad and fibers. You need to break down the proteins, not just clean the surface.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>The enzyme cleaner approach</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Blot up any fresh mess with a clean cloth. Don't rub.</li><li>Apply an enzyme cleaner generously to the spot. These cleaners use beneficial bacteria to eat the odor-causing proteins.</li><li>Let it sit for 10–15 minutes, then blot dry.</li><li>Rinse with a little water and blot again. Let it air dry completely.</li></ol><p class="callout info">Enzyme cleaners work best when they can soak into the pad. For old stains, you may need to apply more than once.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What about the whole room?</summary><div class="body"><p>Sprinkle baking soda over the carpet, let it sit for a few hours, then vacuum. For a deeper clean, rent a carpet cleaner and use a pet-specific shampoo. Follow with a rinse cycle.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How do I prevent future odors?</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Blot accidents immediately.</li><li>Keep a designated pet towel for muddy paws.</li><li>Vacuum regularly with a HEPA filter.</li><li>Use a carpet deodorizer made for pets.</li></ul><p>Consistency wins this battle.</p></div></details></div></div>	-
39.21.1	dashboard_faq/Clean and Descale a Kettle	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h1 class="card-title">Clean and Descale a Kettle</h1><p class="muted">That white crust is limescale, and it's making your tea taste off. Here's how to get rid of it.</p><details class="acc"><summary>What is limescale?</summary><div class="body"><p>It's calcium carbonate from hard water. It builds up every time you boil. Left alone, it flakes into your drinks and makes the kettle less efficient.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>The vinegar method</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Fill the kettle halfway with equal parts water and white vinegar.</li><li>Boil, then let it sit for 20–30 minutes.</li><li>Pour out the solution. If scale remains, repeat.</li><li>Boil fresh water twice and discard to remove vinegar taste.</li></ol><p class="callout warn">Don't use this on aluminum kettles. The vinegar can pit the metal. Stainless steel and glass are fine.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Citric acid alternative</summary><div class="body"><p>Add 1–2 tablespoons of citric acid powder to a full kettle of water. Boil, then let sit for 15 minutes. Rinse well. Citric acid is odorless and works just as well.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How often should I descale?</summary><div class="body"><p>Every 2–4 weeks if you have hard water. Once every few months if your water is soft. If you see white flakes in your tea, it's time.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What about the outside?</summary><div class="body"><p>Wipe with a damp cloth and a little dish soap. For stainless steel, use a microfiber cloth to avoid scratches. Don't submerge the base or cord.</p></div></details></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h1 class="card-title">Clean and Descale a Kettle</h1><p class="muted">That white crust is limescale, and it's making your tea taste off. Here's how to get rid of it.</p><details class="acc"><summary>What is limescale?</summary><div class="body"><p>It's calcium carbonate from hard water. It builds up every time you boil. Left alone, it flakes into your drinks and makes the kettle less efficient.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>The vinegar method</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Fill the kettle halfway with equal parts water and white vinegar.</li><li>Boil, then let it sit for 20–30 minutes.</li><li>Pour out the solution. If scale remains, repeat.</li><li>Boil fresh water twice and discard to remove vinegar taste.</li></ol><p class="callout warn">Don't use this on aluminum kettles. The vinegar can pit the metal. Stainless steel and glass are fine.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Citric acid alternative</summary><div class="body"><p>Add 1–2 tablespoons of citric acid powder to a full kettle of water. Boil, then let sit for 15 minutes. Rinse well. Citric acid is odorless and works just as well.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How often should I descale?</summary><div class="body"><p>Every 2–4 weeks if you have hard water. Once every few months if your water is soft. If you see white flakes in your tea, it's time.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What about the outside?</summary><div class="body"><p>Wipe with a damp cloth and a little dish soap. For stainless steel, use a microfiber cloth to avoid scratches. Don't submerge the base or cord.</p></div></details></div></div>	-
39.22.1	dashboard_faq/Remove Sweat Stains from Shirts	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h1 class="card-title">Remove Sweat Stains from Shirts</h1><p class="muted">Yellow pits are a classic problem. The fix is simpler than you think.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why do sweat stains turn yellow?</summary><div class="body"><p>It's a reaction between sweat and the aluminum in antiperspirants. The residue bonds to fabric fibers and oxidizes over time. That's why old stains are darker.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>The pre-treatment that works</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Mix 1 tablespoon of dish soap, 2 tablespoons of hydrogen peroxide, and 1 tablespoon of baking soda.</li><li>Apply the paste to the stain with an old toothbrush.</li><li>Let it sit for 30–60 minutes.</li><li>Wash as usual with cold water. Check before drying—heat sets stains.</li></ol><p class="callout good">This combo breaks down the protein and lifts the discoloration. For white shirts, hydrogen peroxide also brightens.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What if the stain is old?</summary><div class="body"><p>Soak the shirt in a mix of 1 quart warm water and 1 cup white vinegar for an hour. Then apply the paste above. Repeat if needed. Old stains may need two rounds.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Can I prevent them?</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Switch to an aluminum-free deodorant.</li><li>Let antiperspirant dry completely before dressing.</li><li>Wash shirts after each wear, especially in summer.</li></ul><p>Prevention is easier than removal.</p></div></details></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h1 class="card-title">Remove Sweat Stains from Shirts</h1><p class="muted">Yellow pits are a classic problem. The fix is simpler than you think.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why do sweat stains turn yellow?</summary><div class="body"><p>It's a reaction between sweat and the aluminum in antiperspirants. The residue bonds to fabric fibers and oxidizes over time. That's why old stains are darker.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>The pre-treatment that works</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Mix 1 tablespoon of dish soap, 2 tablespoons of hydrogen peroxide, and 1 tablespoon of baking soda.</li><li>Apply the paste to the stain with an old toothbrush.</li><li>Let it sit for 30–60 minutes.</li><li>Wash as usual with cold water. Check before drying—heat sets stains.</li></ol><p class="callout good">This combo breaks down the protein and lifts the discoloration. For white shirts, hydrogen peroxide also brightens.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What if the stain is old?</summary><div class="body"><p>Soak the shirt in a mix of 1 quart warm water and 1 cup white vinegar for an hour. Then apply the paste above. Repeat if needed. Old stains may need two rounds.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Can I prevent them?</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Switch to an aluminum-free deodorant.</li><li>Let antiperspirant dry completely before dressing.</li><li>Wash shirts after each wear, especially in summer.</li></ul><p>Prevention is easier than removal.</p></div></details></div></div>	-
39.23.1	dashboard_faq/Get Rid of Cigarette Smoke Smell	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h1 class="card-title">Get Rid of Cigarette Smoke Smell</h1><p class="muted">Smoke smell lingers in everything. Here's how to clear the air.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why does smoke smell stick around?</summary><div class="body"><p>Smoke particles are tiny and oily. They settle into fabrics, walls, and vents. Air fresheners just mask the smell; you need to remove the particles.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Start with ventilation</summary><div class="body"><p>Open windows and doors. Use fans to push air out. If you have an HVAC system, replace the filter and run the fan to cycle air. This won't solve it alone, but it's step one.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Clean surfaces and fabrics</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Wash curtains, bedding, and slipcovers in hot water with your usual detergent.</li><li>Wipe down walls, ceilings, and hard surfaces with a mix of warm water and white vinegar.</li><li>Vacuum carpets and upholstery thoroughly. Sprinkle baking soda first, let sit, then vacuum.</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Odor absorbers that actually work</summary><div class="body"><p>Place bowls of white vinegar or activated charcoal around the room. Coffee grounds also absorb odors. Leave them out for a few days, stirring occasionally. For cars, put a bowl of vinegar on the floor overnight.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>When to call a pro</summary><div class="body"><p>If the smell persists after all this, you may need professional ozone treatment or duct cleaning. That's a job for a specialist, not a DIY spray.</p></div></details></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><h1 class="card-title">Get Rid of Cigarette Smoke Smell</h1><p class="muted">Smoke smell lingers in everything. Here's how to clear the air.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why does smoke smell stick around?</summary><div class="body"><p>Smoke particles are tiny and oily. They settle into fabrics, walls, and vents. Air fresheners just mask the smell; you need to remove the particles.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Start with ventilation</summary><div class="body"><p>Open windows and doors. Use fans to push air out. If you have an HVAC system, replace the filter and run the fan to cycle air. This won't solve it alone, but it's step one.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Clean surfaces and fabrics</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Wash curtains, bedding, and slipcovers in hot water with your usual detergent.</li><li>Wipe down walls, ceilings, and hard surfaces with a mix of warm water and white vinegar.</li><li>Vacuum carpets and upholstery thoroughly. Sprinkle baking soda first, let sit, then vacuum.</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Odor absorbers that actually work</summary><div class="body"><p>Place bowls of white vinegar or activated charcoal around the room. Coffee grounds also absorb odors. Leave them out for a few days, stirring occasionally. For cars, put a bowl of vinegar on the floor overnight.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>When to call a pro</summary><div class="body"><p>If the smell persists after all this, you may need professional ozone treatment or duct cleaning. That's a job for a specialist, not a DIY spray.</p></div></details></div></div>	-
39.24.1	dashboard_faq/Unshrink a Wool Sweater	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Unshrink a Wool Sweater</h1><p class="muted">Wool shrinks because heat and agitation make the fibres lock together. Warm water and gentle stretching can nudge them back.</p><div class="callout info"><p>Only works on wool, and only if it hasn't been felted solid. If the knit now looks like cardboard, <regex>user_name</regex>, it's a cushion cover.</p></div><details class="acc"><summary>What you need</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>A basin of lukewarm water (not hot)</li><li>A capful of hair conditioner or wool wash</li><li>Two towels</li><li>Patience — 30 minutes of soaking</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Step by step</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Soak the sweater for 30 minutes. The conditioner relaxes the fibres.</li><li>Lift it out, don't wring. Press water out against the basin edge.</li><li>Roll it in a towel and press to blot — no twisting.</li><li>Lay flat on the second towel and gently stretch width and length with both palms.</li><li>Pin the edges to shape and let it dry away from sun and radiators.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How much will it come back?</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted">Usually 1–2 cm per stretch session. It may take two or three rounds. Stop if the yarn starts to look fuzzy and thin.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Preventing round two</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Wash cold, by hand or on a wool cycle. Never tumble dry. Dry flat, always.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Unshrink a Wool Sweater</h1><p class="muted">Wool shrinks because heat and agitation make the fibres lock together. Warm water and gentle stretching can nudge them back.</p><div class="callout info"><p>Only works on wool, and only if it hasn't been felted solid. If the knit now looks like cardboard, , it's a cushion cover.</p></div><details class="acc"><summary>What you need</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>A basin of lukewarm water (not hot)</li><li>A capful of hair conditioner or wool wash</li><li>Two towels</li><li>Patience — 30 minutes of soaking</li></ul></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Step by step</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Soak the sweater for 30 minutes. The conditioner relaxes the fibres.</li><li>Lift it out, don't wring. Press water out against the basin edge.</li><li>Roll it in a towel and press to blot — no twisting.</li><li>Lay flat on the second towel and gently stretch width and length with both palms.</li><li>Pin the edges to shape and let it dry away from sun and radiators.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>How much will it come back?</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted">Usually 1–2 cm per stretch session. It may take two or three rounds. Stop if the yarn starts to look fuzzy and thin.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Preventing round two</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Wash cold, by hand or on a wool cycle. Never tumble dry. Dry flat, always.</p></div></details></div>	-
39.25.1	dashboard_faq/Stop a Toilet From Running	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Stop a Toilet From Running</h1><p class="muted">A running toilet is almost always the flapper or the fill valve. Both are cheap fixes and you can do this before dinner.</p><div class="callout warn"><p>Turn the shut-off valve behind the toilet clockwise before you open the tank, <regex>user_name</regex>. Water is quiet until it isn't.</p></div><details class="acc"><summary>First: is it the flapper?</summary><div class="body"><p>Drop a few drops of food dye in the tank. Wait 10 minutes. Colour in the bowl means the flapper is leaking.</p><svg viewBox="0 0 200 70" role="img" aria-label="Tank with dye test"><rect x="10" y="10" width="80" height="50" rx="4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"/><circle cx="30" cy="35" r="6" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.5"/><path d="M95 35 h20" class="line" stroke="currentColor"/><rect x="120" y="22" width="60" height="26" rx="6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"/><text x="122" y="62" class="lbl" fill="currentColor">bowl</text></svg></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Replace the flapper</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Shut off water. Flush to empty the tank.</li><li>Unhook the old flapper from the overflow tube.</li><li>Take it to the shop and match the size exactly.</li><li>Clip the new one on, hook the chain with about 1 cm of slack.</li><li>Turn water on and watch one full flush.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Still running?</summary><div class="body"><p>Then it's the fill valve. The float cup may be set too high, so water spills into the overflow tube. Bend or adjust the float down a touch.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Rough costs</summary><div class="body"><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Flapper</span><span class="value">£3–8</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Fill valve</span><span class="value">£10–20</span></div></div></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Stop a Toilet From Running</h1><p class="muted">A running toilet is almost always the flapper or the fill valve. Both are cheap fixes and you can do this before dinner.</p><div class="callout warn"><p>Turn the shut-off valve behind the toilet clockwise before you open the tank, . Water is quiet until it isn't.</p></div><details class="acc"><summary>First: is it the flapper?</summary><div class="body"><p>Drop a few drops of food dye in the tank. Wait 10 minutes. Colour in the bowl means the flapper is leaking.</p><svg viewBox="0 0 200 70" role="img" aria-label="Tank with dye test"><rect x="10" y="10" width="80" height="50" rx="4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"/><circle cx="30" cy="35" r="6" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.5"/><path d="M95 35 h20" class="line" stroke="currentColor"/><rect x="120" y="22" width="60" height="26" rx="6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"/><text x="122" y="62" class="lbl" fill="currentColor">bowl</text></svg></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Replace the flapper</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Shut off water. Flush to empty the tank.</li><li>Unhook the old flapper from the overflow tube.</li><li>Take it to the shop and match the size exactly.</li><li>Clip the new one on, hook the chain with about 1 cm of slack.</li><li>Turn water on and watch one full flush.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Still running?</summary><div class="body"><p>Then it's the fill valve. The float cup may be set too high, so water spills into the overflow tube. Bend or adjust the float down a touch.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Rough costs</summary><div class="body"><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Flapper</span><span class="value">£3–8</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Fill valve</span><span class="value">£10–20</span></div></div></div></details></div>	-
39.26.1	dashboard_faq/Season a New Nonstick Pan	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Season a New Nonstick Pan</h1><p class="muted">Nonstick pans don't need polymerised seasoning like cast iron, but a quick warm-up wipe removes factory residue and helps the coating last.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why it matters</summary><div class="body"><p>New pans carry a thin manufacturing film. That's the smell and the grey rag you get on the first wash. It isn't dangerous, just unappetising.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>The five-minute routine</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Wash with warm water and a soft sponge. No steel wool, ever.</li><li>Dry it, then set it on medium heat for two minutes.</li><li>Add a teaspoon of neutral oil and swirl to coat.</li><li>Wipe with a paper towel and let it cool.</li><li>Wipe once more to remove excess oil.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Everyday rules</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Medium heat is the ceiling. High heat kills coatings.</li><li>No metal utensils, no dishwasher.</li><li>Let it cool before washing, and never plunge a hot pan in cold water.</li></ul></div></details><div class="callout good"><p><regex>user_name</regex>, a well-kept nonstick pan lasts years. A mistreated one lasts a month and change.</p></div><details class="acc"><summary>When to replace it</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted">If food sticks in the middle no matter what, or the coating is flaking, retire it. Flakes are not a great condiment.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Season a New Nonstick Pan</h1><p class="muted">Nonstick pans don't need polymerised seasoning like cast iron, but a quick warm-up wipe removes factory residue and helps the coating last.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why it matters</summary><div class="body"><p>New pans carry a thin manufacturing film. That's the smell and the grey rag you get on the first wash. It isn't dangerous, just unappetising.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>The five-minute routine</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Wash with warm water and a soft sponge. No steel wool, ever.</li><li>Dry it, then set it on medium heat for two minutes.</li><li>Add a teaspoon of neutral oil and swirl to coat.</li><li>Wipe with a paper towel and let it cool.</li><li>Wipe once more to remove excess oil.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Everyday rules</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Medium heat is the ceiling. High heat kills coatings.</li><li>No metal utensils, no dishwasher.</li><li>Let it cool before washing, and never plunge a hot pan in cold water.</li></ul></div></details><div class="callout good"><p>, a well-kept nonstick pan lasts years. A mistreated one lasts a month and change.</p></div><details class="acc"><summary>When to replace it</summary><div class="body"><p class="muted">If food sticks in the middle no matter what, or the coating is flaking, retire it. Flakes are not a great condiment.</p></div></details></div>	-
39.27.1	dashboard_faq/Get Rid of Ants in the Kitchen	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Get Rid of Ants in the Kitchen</h1><p class="muted">Ants follow a scent trail. Kill the trail and the scouts stop calling the rest of the family over for lunch.</p><div class="callout warn"><p>Don't spray where you see them, unless you want the survivors to scatter into new rooms. Bait, don't blast.</p></div><details class="acc"><summary>Find the entry point</summary><div class="body"><p>Watch a single ant for 30 seconds. It will walk back to the gap it came through — usually a skirting board joint, window frame, or along a pipe.</p><svg viewBox="0 0 220 60" role="img" aria-label="Ant trail to gap"><circle cx="20" cy="30" r="5" fill="currentColor"/><circle cx="55" cy="22" r="5" fill="currentColor"/><circle cx="90" cy="36" r="5" fill="currentColor"/><circle cx="125" cy="24" r="5" fill="currentColor"/><path d="M20 30 L55 22 L90 36 L125 24 L175 30" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/><rect x="175" y="14" width="14" height="32" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"/><text x="150" y="58" class="lbl" fill="currentColor">gap</text></svg></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Clean the trail</summary><div class="body"><p>Wipe surfaces and skirting with warm soapy water, then a vinegar-and-water rinse. The scent line is what keeps them coming back.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Bait properly</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Put gel bait near — not on — the trail.</li><li>Leave it alone for a week. Yes, a week. Let them take it home.</li><li>Resist squashing foragers. Dead ants release alarm scent and more arrive.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Seal the way in</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Silicone along skirting gaps and pipe entries. Ants can squeeze through one millimetre.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Keep them gone</summary><div class="body"><p>Wipe counters, tip the bin often, and store sugar in sealed jars. Boring, but it's the whole trick.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Get Rid of Ants in the Kitchen</h1><p class="muted">Ants follow a scent trail. Kill the trail and the scouts stop calling the rest of the family over for lunch.</p><div class="callout warn"><p>Don't spray where you see them, unless you want the survivors to scatter into new rooms. Bait, don't blast.</p></div><details class="acc"><summary>Find the entry point</summary><div class="body"><p>Watch a single ant for 30 seconds. It will walk back to the gap it came through — usually a skirting board joint, window frame, or along a pipe.</p><svg viewBox="0 0 220 60" role="img" aria-label="Ant trail to gap"><circle cx="20" cy="30" r="5" fill="currentColor"/><circle cx="55" cy="22" r="5" fill="currentColor"/><circle cx="90" cy="36" r="5" fill="currentColor"/><circle cx="125" cy="24" r="5" fill="currentColor"/><path d="M20 30 L55 22 L90 36 L125 24 L175 30" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/><rect x="175" y="14" width="14" height="32" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"/><text x="150" y="58" class="lbl" fill="currentColor">gap</text></svg></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Clean the trail</summary><div class="body"><p>Wipe surfaces and skirting with warm soapy water, then a vinegar-and-water rinse. The scent line is what keeps them coming back.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Bait properly</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Put gel bait near — not on — the trail.</li><li>Leave it alone for a week. Yes, a week. Let them take it home.</li><li>Resist squashing foragers. Dead ants release alarm scent and more arrive.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Seal the way in</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Silicone along skirting gaps and pipe entries. Ants can squeeze through one millimetre.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Keep them gone</summary><div class="body"><p>Wipe counters, tip the bin often, and store sugar in sealed jars. Boring, but it's the whole trick.</p></div></details></div>	-
39.28.1	dashboard_faq/Remove Lipstick From Fabric	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Remove Lipstick From Fabric</h1><p class="muted">Lipstick is wax plus pigment. You have to lift the wax first, then the colour. Skip that order and you'll just smear it.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Step 1 — lift the wax</summary><div class="body"><p>Scrape off any excess with a blunt knife or a credit card. Then press a paper towel onto the mark and hold a warm iron over it. The wax transfers to the towel. Repeat with a clean patch of towel each time.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Step 2 — break the pigment</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Dab with liquid dish soap and a soft brush.</li><li>Work from the outside of the stain inward, so you don't spread the edge.</li><li>Rinse with cool water and check before drying.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>For stubborn marks</summary><div class="body"><p>Go over it with a little isopropyl alcohol on a cotton pad. Test on an inside seam first, <regex>user_name</regex> — some dyes dislike the stuff.</p></div></details><div class="callout bad"><p>No dryer and no hot air until the mark is fully gone. Heat sets wax stains permanently.</p></div><details class="acc"><summary>For upholstery</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Same order of work, but blot instead of soak. Keep fabric barely damp and dry it with a towel rather than a hairdryer.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Remove Lipstick From Fabric</h1><p class="muted">Lipstick is wax plus pigment. You have to lift the wax first, then the colour. Skip that order and you'll just smear it.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Step 1 — lift the wax</summary><div class="body"><p>Scrape off any excess with a blunt knife or a credit card. Then press a paper towel onto the mark and hold a warm iron over it. The wax transfers to the towel. Repeat with a clean patch of towel each time.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Step 2 — break the pigment</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Dab with liquid dish soap and a soft brush.</li><li>Work from the outside of the stain inward, so you don't spread the edge.</li><li>Rinse with cool water and check before drying.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>For stubborn marks</summary><div class="body"><p>Go over it with a little isopropyl alcohol on a cotton pad. Test on an inside seam first,  — some dyes dislike the stuff.</p></div></details><div class="callout bad"><p>No dryer and no hot air until the mark is fully gone. Heat sets wax stains permanently.</p></div><details class="acc"><summary>For upholstery</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Same order of work, but blot instead of soak. Keep fabric barely damp and dry it with a towel rather than a hairdryer.</p></div></details></div>	-
39.29.1	dashboard_faq/Clean Burned-On Rice From a Pot	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Clean Burned-On Rice From a Pot</h1><p class="muted">The trick is softening, not scrubbing. Give the starch water and heat, and it releases on its own.</p><div class="callout info"><p>Works for stainless and enamel. For nonstick, use baking soda and water only — no abrasive powder.</p></div><details class="acc"><summary>The boil-and-wait method</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Fill the pot with water, a few centimetres above the burnt line.</li><li>Add two tablespoons of baking soda and a drop of dish soap.</li><li>Bring to a gentle simmer for 10 minutes.</li><li>Take it off the heat and let it sit 30 minutes.</li><li>Scrape with a wooden spoon or silicone spatula. Most of it lifts clean.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Still clinging?</summary><div class="body"><p>Make a paste of baking soda and a little water, spread it over the patch, and leave it an hour. Then scrub gently with a nylon pad.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What not to do</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Don't pour cold water into a hot pot — it can warp or crack.</li><li>No metal scrapers on nonstick.</li><li>Don't soak overnight if the pot has a riveted handle in bare wood.</li></ul></div></details><div class="callout good"><p>Prevention, <regex>user_name</regex>: lower the heat to a bare simmer once the rice comes to a boil. Burnt rice is a heating problem, not a rice problem.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Clean Burned-On Rice From a Pot</h1><p class="muted">The trick is softening, not scrubbing. Give the starch water and heat, and it releases on its own.</p><div class="callout info"><p>Works for stainless and enamel. For nonstick, use baking soda and water only — no abrasive powder.</p></div><details class="acc"><summary>The boil-and-wait method</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Fill the pot with water, a few centimetres above the burnt line.</li><li>Add two tablespoons of baking soda and a drop of dish soap.</li><li>Bring to a gentle simmer for 10 minutes.</li><li>Take it off the heat and let it sit 30 minutes.</li><li>Scrape with a wooden spoon or silicone spatula. Most of it lifts clean.</li></ol></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Still clinging?</summary><div class="body"><p>Make a paste of baking soda and a little water, spread it over the patch, and leave it an hour. Then scrub gently with a nylon pad.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What not to do</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Don't pour cold water into a hot pot — it can warp or crack.</li><li>No metal scrapers on nonstick.</li><li>Don't soak overnight if the pot has a riveted handle in bare wood.</li></ul></div></details><div class="callout good"><p>Prevention, : lower the heat to a bare simmer once the rice comes to a boil. Burnt rice is a heating problem, not a rice problem.</p></div></div>	-
39.30.1	dashboard_faq/Remove Coffee Stains from a Mug	<div class="umi-page">  <h2>Remove Coffee Stains from a Mug</h2>  <p class="muted">That brown ring isn't permanent. Here's how to lift it without scrubbing your arm off.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What you'll need</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>Baking soda</li>        <li>Dish soap</li>        <li>Hot water</li>        <li>A non-scratch sponge or cloth</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Steps</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Sprinkle a generous pinch of baking soda into the mug.</li>        <li>Add a drop of dish soap and a little hot water.</li>        <li>Scrub with a non-scratch sponge. The stain should lift easily.</li>        <li>Rinse well and wash as usual.</li>      </ol>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout info">    <p>For stubborn stains, let the baking soda paste sit for 10 minutes before scrubbing.</p>  </div>  <p class="t-small dim">Thanks for asking, <regex>user_name</regex>. Now go enjoy that clean cuppa.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h2>Remove Coffee Stains from a Mug</h2>  <p class="muted">That brown ring isn't permanent. Here's how to lift it without scrubbing your arm off.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What you'll need</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>Baking soda</li>        <li>Dish soap</li>        <li>Hot water</li>        <li>A non-scratch sponge or cloth</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Steps</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Sprinkle a generous pinch of baking soda into the mug.</li>        <li>Add a drop of dish soap and a little hot water.</li>        <li>Scrub with a non-scratch sponge. The stain should lift easily.</li>        <li>Rinse well and wash as usual.</li>      </ol>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout info">    <p>For stubborn stains, let the baking soda paste sit for 10 minutes before scrubbing.</p>  </div>  <p class="t-small dim">Thanks for asking, . Now go enjoy that clean cuppa.</p></div>	-
39.31.1	dashboard_faq/Get Rid of Fruit Flies	<div class="umi-page">  <h2>Get Rid of Fruit Flies</h2>  <p class="muted">Those tiny buzzing guests are annoying. Let's show them the door.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Why they show up</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Fruit flies are attracted to overripe fruit, fermenting liquids, and damp spots. Remove their food source and they'll move on.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Make a simple trap</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Pour a little apple cider vinegar into a small bowl.</li>        <li>Add a drop of dish soap (breaks the surface tension).</li>        <li>Cover with plastic wrap and poke a few holes.</li>        <li>Place near the fruit bowl. Flies go in, but can't get out.</li>      </ol>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout good">    <p>Also: take out the trash, clean drains, and store fruit in the fridge for a few days.</p>  </div>  <p class="t-small dim">You've got this, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h2>Get Rid of Fruit Flies</h2>  <p class="muted">Those tiny buzzing guests are annoying. Let's show them the door.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Why they show up</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Fruit flies are attracted to overripe fruit, fermenting liquids, and damp spots. Remove their food source and they'll move on.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Make a simple trap</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Pour a little apple cider vinegar into a small bowl.</li>        <li>Add a drop of dish soap (breaks the surface tension).</li>        <li>Cover with plastic wrap and poke a few holes.</li>        <li>Place near the fruit bowl. Flies go in, but can't get out.</li>      </ol>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout good">    <p>Also: take out the trash, clean drains, and store fruit in the fridge for a few days.</p>  </div>  <p class="t-small dim">You've got this, .</p></div>	-
39.32.1	dashboard_faq/Clean a Burnt Pot	<div class="umi-page">  <h2>Clean a Burnt Pot</h2>  <p class="muted">Burnt-on food doesn't have to mean a new pot. Here's a gentle rescue.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What you'll need</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>Baking soda</li>        <li>Water</li>        <li>Dish soap</li>        <li>Wooden spoon or spatula</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Steps</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Cover the burnt area with water and add a few tablespoons of baking soda.</li>        <li>Bring to a boil, then simmer for 10-15 minutes.</li>        <li>Remove from heat and let cool slightly.</li>        <li>Scrape gently with a wooden spoon. Most of the burnt bits should lift.</li>        <li>Wash as usual.</li>      </ol>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout info">    <p>For really stubborn spots, add a drop of dish soap to the baking soda solution.</p>  </div>  <p class="t-small dim">Good luck, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h2>Clean a Burnt Pot</h2>  <p class="muted">Burnt-on food doesn't have to mean a new pot. Here's a gentle rescue.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What you'll need</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>Baking soda</li>        <li>Water</li>        <li>Dish soap</li>        <li>Wooden spoon or spatula</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Steps</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Cover the burnt area with water and add a few tablespoons of baking soda.</li>        <li>Bring to a boil, then simmer for 10-15 minutes.</li>        <li>Remove from heat and let cool slightly.</li>        <li>Scrape gently with a wooden spoon. Most of the burnt bits should lift.</li>        <li>Wash as usual.</li>      </ol>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout info">    <p>For really stubborn spots, add a drop of dish soap to the baking soda solution.</p>  </div>  <p class="t-small dim">Good luck, .</p></div>	-
39.33.1	dashboard_faq/Remove Sticker Residue	<div class="umi-page">  <h2>Remove Sticker Residue</h2>  <p class="muted">That gummy mess after peeling a label? Easy to erase.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Try these methods</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li><strong>Rubbing alcohol:</strong> Dab on a cotton ball and rub. Works on glass, plastic, and metal.</li>        <li><strong>Oil:</strong> Vegetable or baby oil. Let it sit for a few minutes, then wipe.</li>        <li><strong>Vinegar:</strong> Soak a cloth and press onto the residue. Wait, then scrub.</li>        <li><strong>Heat:</strong> A hair dryer on low can soften the adhesive for easy peeling.</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout good">    <p>Test any method on a hidden spot first, especially on painted or delicate surfaces.</p>  </div>  <p class="t-small dim">Happy de-stickering, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h2>Remove Sticker Residue</h2>  <p class="muted">That gummy mess after peeling a label? Easy to erase.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Try these methods</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li><strong>Rubbing alcohol:</strong> Dab on a cotton ball and rub. Works on glass, plastic, and metal.</li>        <li><strong>Oil:</strong> Vegetable or baby oil. Let it sit for a few minutes, then wipe.</li>        <li><strong>Vinegar:</strong> Soak a cloth and press onto the residue. Wait, then scrub.</li>        <li><strong>Heat:</strong> A hair dryer on low can soften the adhesive for easy peeling.</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout good">    <p>Test any method on a hidden spot first, especially on painted or delicate surfaces.</p>  </div>  <p class="t-small dim">Happy de-stickering, .</p></div>	-
39.34.1	dashboard_faq/Unclog a Shower Drain	<div class="umi-page">  <h2>Unclog a Shower Drain</h2>  <p class="muted">Standing water is no fun. Let's get things flowing again.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>First, try a plunger</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Block the overflow hole with a wet rag, then plunge the drain a few times. Sometimes that's all it takes.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>If that doesn't work</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Remove the drain cover (usually a screw or two).</li>        <li>Use a wire hanger or drain snake to pull out hair and gunk.</li>        <li>Flush with hot water.</li>      </ol>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout warn">    <p>Avoid chemical drain cleaners if you can. They can damage pipes and are harsh on the environment.</p>  </div>  <p class="t-small dim">You got this, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h2>Unclog a Shower Drain</h2>  <p class="muted">Standing water is no fun. Let's get things flowing again.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>First, try a plunger</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Block the overflow hole with a wet rag, then plunge the drain a few times. Sometimes that's all it takes.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>If that doesn't work</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Remove the drain cover (usually a screw or two).</li>        <li>Use a wire hanger or drain snake to pull out hair and gunk.</li>        <li>Flush with hot water.</li>      </ol>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout warn">    <p>Avoid chemical drain cleaners if you can. They can damage pipes and are harsh on the environment.</p>  </div>  <p class="t-small dim">You got this, .</p></div>	-
39.35.1	dashboard_faq/Silence a Squeaky Door	<div class="umi-page">  <h2>Silence a Squeaky Door</h2>  <p class="muted">That high-pitched squeak is usually an easy fix.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What you'll need</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>Lubricant (WD-40, silicone spray, or even cooking oil in a pinch)</li>        <li>A cloth or paper towel</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Steps</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Wipe the hinge pins to remove dust and grime.</li>        <li>Apply lubricant to the top of each hinge pin.</li>        <li>Open and close the door a few times to work it in.</li>        <li>Wipe away any excess.</li>      </ol>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout info">    <p>If it still squeaks, the hinge might be loose. Tighten the screws.</p>  </div>  <p class="t-small dim">Sweet silence, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h2>Silence a Squeaky Door</h2>  <p class="muted">That high-pitched squeak is usually an easy fix.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What you'll need</summary>    <div class="body">      <ul class="list">        <li>Lubricant (WD-40, silicone spray, or even cooking oil in a pinch)</li>        <li>A cloth or paper towel</li>      </ul>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Steps</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Wipe the hinge pins to remove dust and grime.</li>        <li>Apply lubricant to the top of each hinge pin.</li>        <li>Open and close the door a few times to work it in.</li>        <li>Wipe away any excess.</li>      </ol>    </div>  </details>  <div class="callout info">    <p>If it still squeaks, the hinge might be loose. Tighten the screws.</p>  </div>  <p class="t-small dim">Sweet silence, .</p></div>	-
