43.38.1	dashboard_tabs/Home Weather Station Dashboard	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Home Weather Station</h1><p class="muted">Nice setup, <regex>user_name</regex>. The backyard tells its own story.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Temperature</span><span class="value">64<span class="unit">°F</span></span><span class="delta down">−6° since 6am</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Humidity</span><span class="value">71<span class="unit">%</span></span><span class="delta">dew point 55°F</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Pressure</span><span class="value">1012<span class="unit">hPa</span></span><span class="delta down">falling, storm likely</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Wind</span><span class="value">9<span class="unit">mph SW</span></span><span class="delta">gusting 17</span></div></div><details class="tab"><summary>Pressure chart</summary><div class="body"><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 300 90"><polyline class="line" points="12,30 60,34 108,42 156,52 204,64 252,72 288,76"/><line class="axis" x1="12" y1="82" x2="288" y2="82"/><text class="lbl" x="12" y="18">1020</text><text class="lbl" x="12" y="78">1005</text></svg></div><p class="t-small dim">Steady fall of 3+ hPa in 3 hours = incoming front. Batten the patio umbrella.</p></div></details><details class="tab"><summary>Rain this month</summary><div class="body"><div class="meter"><span></span></div><p class="t-small dim">2.4 in of a 4.0 in monthly average.</p><ul class="list"><li>Wettest day: 0.9 in on the 11th</li><li>7 dry days in a row</li></ul></div></details><details class="tab"><summary>Sensor health</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Anemometer: spinning freely, cleaned last month.</li><li>Rain gauge: clear of leaves, tipping bucket counts even.</li><li>Indoor module: battery at 78%.</li></ul><div class="callout info"><p>Mount at 5 ft over grass for true readings. Roofs run hot.</p></div></div></details><details class="tab"><summary>Reading glossary</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li><strong>Dew point</strong> — how muggy it actually feels.</li><li><strong>Gust</strong> — a 3-second spike, not average wind.</li><li><strong>Sea-level pressure</strong> — pressure normalized so stations at any altitude compare fairly.</li></ul></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Home Weather Station</h1><p class="muted">Nice setup, . The backyard tells its own story.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Temperature</span><span class="value">64<span class="unit">°F</span></span><span class="delta down">−6° since 6am</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Humidity</span><span class="value">71<span class="unit">%</span></span><span class="delta">dew point 55°F</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Pressure</span><span class="value">1012<span class="unit">hPa</span></span><span class="delta down">falling, storm likely</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Wind</span><span class="value">9<span class="unit">mph SW</span></span><span class="delta">gusting 17</span></div></div><details class="tab"><summary>Pressure chart</summary><div class="body"><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 300 90"><polyline class="line" points="12,30 60,34 108,42 156,52 204,64 252,72 288,76"/><line class="axis" x1="12" y1="82" x2="288" y2="82"/><text class="lbl" x="12" y="18">1020</text><text class="lbl" x="12" y="78">1005</text></svg></div><p class="t-small dim">Steady fall of 3+ hPa in 3 hours = incoming front. Batten the patio umbrella.</p></div></details><details class="tab"><summary>Rain this month</summary><div class="body"><div class="meter"><span></span></div><p class="t-small dim">2.4 in of a 4.0 in monthly average.</p><ul class="list"><li>Wettest day: 0.9 in on the 11th</li><li>7 dry days in a row</li></ul></div></details><details class="tab"><summary>Sensor health</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Anemometer: spinning freely, cleaned last month.</li><li>Rain gauge: clear of leaves, tipping bucket counts even.</li><li>Indoor module: battery at 78%.</li></ul><div class="callout info"><p>Mount at 5 ft over grass for true readings. Roofs run hot.</p></div></div></details><details class="tab"><summary>Reading glossary</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li><strong>Dew point</strong> — how muggy it actually feels.</li><li><strong>Gust</strong> — a 3-second spike, not average wind.</li><li><strong>Sea-level pressure</strong> — pressure normalized so stations at any altitude compare fairly.</li></ul></div></details></div>	-
43.39.1	dashboard_tabs/Backyard Chick Care Dashboard	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Backyard Chick Care</h1><p class="muted">Morning, <regex>user_name</regex> — the ladies are watching. Onward to eggs.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Eggs today</span><span class="value">5<span class="unit">/ 7 hens</span></span><span class="delta">3 brown, 2 blue</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Feed left</span><span class="value">18<span class="unit">lb</span></span><span class="delta">~2 weeks at 1.2 lb/day</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Coop temp</span><span class="value">48<span class="unit">°F</span></span><span class="delta">no heat lamp needed</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Waterer</span><span class="value">3.5<span class="unit">gal</span></span><span class="delta down">check for ice tonight</span></div></div><details class="tab"><summary>Egg stats</summary><div class="body"><div class="meter"><span></span></div><p class="t-small dim">Five of seven hens are laying — the two pullets should start by five months old.</p><ul class="list"><li>Hens stop laying during molt (they'll look like a pillow fight).</li><li>Shorter daylight under 14 hours slows production.</li></ul></div></details><details class="tab"><summary>Routine</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li><strong>Morning</strong> — Open coop, count birds, top up feed and water</li><li><strong>Midday</strong> — Collect eggs, check for soft-shells</li><li><strong>Evening</strong> — Secure the latch (raccoons are tiny geniuses)</li><li><strong>Weekly</strong> — Fresh bedding, sweep the dropping tray</li></ul></div></details><details class="tab"><summary>Health watch</summary><div class="body"><div class="callout warn"><p>Any hen that sits puffed up and refuses treats needs a check. Sick birds go quiet before anything else shows.</p></div><ul class="list"><li>Ruffled feathers, drooped tail</li><li>Pale comb, no morning egg song</li><li>Limping or curled toes</li></ul></div></details><details class="tab"><summary>Keeping hens happy</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Lice and mites love a dirty coop — diatomaceous earth around the roost helps.</li><li>Hard-boiled eggshells are fine calcium back, but a feed supplement is safer.</li><li>Never feed avocado, onions, or chocolate.</li><li>Nesting box: one per four hens, lined with pine shavings.</li></ul></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Backyard Chick Care</h1><p class="muted">Morning,  — the ladies are watching. Onward to eggs.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Eggs today</span><span class="value">5<span class="unit">/ 7 hens</span></span><span class="delta">3 brown, 2 blue</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Feed left</span><span class="value">18<span class="unit">lb</span></span><span class="delta">~2 weeks at 1.2 lb/day</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Coop temp</span><span class="value">48<span class="unit">°F</span></span><span class="delta">no heat lamp needed</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Waterer</span><span class="value">3.5<span class="unit">gal</span></span><span class="delta down">check for ice tonight</span></div></div><details class="tab"><summary>Egg stats</summary><div class="body"><div class="meter"><span></span></div><p class="t-small dim">Five of seven hens are laying — the two pullets should start by five months old.</p><ul class="list"><li>Hens stop laying during molt (they'll look like a pillow fight).</li><li>Shorter daylight under 14 hours slows production.</li></ul></div></details><details class="tab"><summary>Routine</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li><strong>Morning</strong> — Open coop, count birds, top up feed and water</li><li><strong>Midday</strong> — Collect eggs, check for soft-shells</li><li><strong>Evening</strong> — Secure the latch (raccoons are tiny geniuses)</li><li><strong>Weekly</strong> — Fresh 