44.56.0	dashboard_timeline/Garage Sale Timeline	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Garage Sale Timeline</h1><p class="muted">Sort, price, sell, release, <regex>user_name</regex>. The last one feels best.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Prep window</span><span class="value">7<span class="unit">days</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Run time</span><span class="value">4<span class="unit">hours</span></span></div></div><div class="card"><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day -7</span><p>Pick a date, check your area's permit rules, tell the neighbours.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day -5</span><p>Gather everything in one room. Sort: keep, sell, donate, bin.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day -3</span><p>Price in round numbers. Sticker each item. Bundle the small stuff.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day -1</span><p>Signs made, change from the bank, tables borrowed, cash box ready.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 0, 06:30</span><p>Set up. The early crowd is real and they are fast.</p></li><li><span class="when">09:00</span><p>Half-price hour. Say it on a sign and mean it.</p></li><li><span class="when">13:00</span><p>Takedown, leftovers to a charity shop, count the cash.</p></li></ul></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Garage Sale Timeline</h1><p class="muted">Sort, price, sell, release, . The last one feels best.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Prep window</span><span class="value">7<span class="unit">days</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Run time</span><span class="value">4<span class="unit">hours</span></span></div></div><div class="card"><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day -7</span><p>Pick a date, check your area's permit rules, tell the neighbours.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day -5</span><p>Gather everything in one room. Sort: keep, sell, donate, bin.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day -3</span><p>Price in round numbers. Sticker each item. Bundle the small stuff.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day -1</span><p>Signs made, change from the bank, tables borrowed, cash box ready.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 0, 06:30</span><p>Set up. The early crowd is real and they are fast.</p></li><li><span class="when">09:00</span><p>Half-price hour. Say it on a sign and mean it.</p></li><li><span class="when">13:00</span><p>Takedown, leftovers to a charity shop, count the cash.</p></li></ul></div></div>	-
44.57.0	dashboard_timeline/Air Fryer Chicken Timeline	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Air Fryer Chicken</h1><p class="muted">Crispy outside, juicy inside. Times for a 1.5 lb batch.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">0 min</span><p>Pat chicken dry, rub with oil and seasoning. Preheat air fryer to 375°F.</p></li><li><span class="when">3 min</span><p>Arrange chicken in a single layer, skin side up. Don't crowd the basket.</p></li><li><span class="when">12 min</span><p>Flip pieces. Rotate the basket if your model runs hot in back.</p></li><li><span class="when">22 min</span><p>Check internal temp: 165°F for breast, 175°F for thighs.</p></li><li><span class="when">25 min</span><p>Rest 5 minutes. The carryover heat finishes the job.</p></li><li><span class="when">30 min</span><p>Serve with something green. You earned it, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info"><p>Thin cutlets? Start checking at 10 minutes. They cook fast.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Air Fryer Chicken</h1><p class="muted">Crispy outside, juicy inside. Times for a 1.5 lb batch.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">0 min</span><p>Pat chicken dry, rub with oil and seasoning. Preheat air fryer to 375°F.</p></li><li><span class="when">3 min</span><p>Arrange chicken in a single layer, skin side up. Don't crowd the basket.</p></li><li><span class="when">12 min</span><p>Flip pieces. Rotate the basket if your model runs hot in back.</p></li><li><span class="when">22 min</span><p>Check internal temp: 165°F for breast, 175°F for thighs.</p></li><li><span class="when">25 min</span><p>Rest 5 minutes. The carryover heat finishes the job.</p></li><li><span class="when">30 min</span><p>Serve with something green. You earned it, .</p></li></ul><div class="callout info"><p>Thin cutlets? Start checking at 10 minutes. They cook fast.</p></div></div>	-
44.58.0	dashboard_timeline/Phone Charging Timeline	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Phone Charging</h1><p class="muted">A gentle routine to keep your battery happy for years.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">7:00 AM</span><p>Unplug. Batteries dislike sitting at 100% for hours.</p></li><li><span class="when">1:00 PM</span><p>Top-up if below 40%. Short charges are fine.</p></li><li><span class="when">6:00 PM</span><p>Plug in for the evening if you're below 50%.</p></li><li><span class="when">10:30 PM</span><p>Optional overnight charge to 80% if your phone supports it.</p></li><li><span class="when">11:00 PM</span><p>Enable optimized charging. It learns your alarm, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></li></ul><div class="callout good"><p>Keep it between 20% and 80% most days. That's the sweet spot.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Phone Charging</h1><p class="muted">A gentle routine to keep your battery happy for years.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">7:00 AM</span><p>Unplug. Batteries dislike sitting at 100% for hours.</p></li><li><span class="when">1:00 PM</span><p>Top-up if below 40%. Short charges are fine.</p></li><li><span class="when">6:00 PM</span><p>Plug in for the evening if you're below 50%.</p></li><li><span class="when">10:30 PM</span><p>Optional overnight charge to 80% if your phone supports it.</p></li><li><span class="when">11:00 PM</span><p>Enable optimized charging. It learns your alarm, .</p></li></ul><div class="callout good"><p>Keep it between 20% and 80% most days. That's the sweet spot.</p></div></div>	-
44.59.0	dashboard_timeline/Wine Decanting Timeline	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Decanting Wine</h1><p class="muted">Let it breathe. Times vary by age and grape.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">2 hours before</span><p>Young, tannic reds (Cabernet, Syrah) — decant now.</p></li><li><span class="when">45 min before</span><p>Medium-bodied reds (Merlot, Zinfandel) — a short rest is plenty.</p></li><li><span class="when">20 min before</span><p>Old, delicate reds (Pinot Noir, aged Burgundy) — decant gently, serve soon.</p></li><li><span class="when">10 min before</span><p>White wines — usually just a splash in the glass, not a decanter.</p></li><li><span class="when">Serve</span><p>Pour slowly. Leave the last ounce with the sediment, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></li></ul><div class="callout warn"><p>Too long in the decanter and old wine fades. Set a timer.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Decanting Wine</h1><p class="muted">Let it breathe. Times vary by age and grape.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">2 hours before</span><p>Young, tannic reds (Cabernet, Syrah) — decant now.</p></li><li><span class="when">45 min before</span><p>Medium-bodied reds (Merlot, Zinfandel) — a short rest is plenty.</p></li><li><span class="when">20 min before</span><p>Old, delicate reds (Pinot Noir, aged Burgundy) — decant gently, serve soon.</p></li><li><span class="when">10 min before</span><p>White wines — usually just a splash in the glass, not a decanter.</p></li><li><span class="when">Serve</span><p>Pour slowly. Leave the last ounce with the sediment, .</p></li></ul><div class="callout warn"><p>Too long in the decanter and old wine fades. Set a timer.</p></div></div>	-
44.60.0	dashboard_timeline/Bread Machine Timeline	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Bread Machine</h1><p class="muted">Set it and forget it — mostly. Here's the rhythm.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">0 min</span><p>Add liquids first, then flour, then yeast on top. Keep yeast away from salt.</p></li><li><span class="when">5 min</span><p>Kneading begins. Check the dough ball — it should be smooth and slightly tacky.</p></li><li><span class="when">30 min</span><p>First rise. Don't peek. The machine handles the warmth.</p></li><li><span class="when">1 hr 30 min</span><p>Punch down and second rise. Some cycles skip this — check your manual.</p></li><li><span class="when">2 hr 30 min</span><p>Baking cycle. Smell it yet?</p></li><li><span class="when">3 hr</span><p>Remove loaf immediately. Cool on a rack before slicing.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info"><p>Times vary by machine and recipe. Your model's manual is the real boss.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Bread Machine</h1><p class="muted">Set it and forget it — mostly. Here's the rhythm.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">0 min</span><p>Add liquids first, then flour, then yeast on top. Keep yeast away from salt.</p></li><li><span class="when">5 min</span><p>Kneading begins. Check the dough ball — it should be smooth and slightly tacky.</p></li><li><span class="when">30 min</span><p>First rise. Don't peek. The machine handles the warmth.</p></li><li><span class="when">1 hr 30 min</span><p>Punch down and second rise. Some cycles skip this — check your manual.</p></li><li><span class="when">2 hr 30 min</span><p>Baking cycle. Smell it yet?</p></li><li><span class="when">3 hr</span><p>Remove loaf immediately. Cool on a rack before slicing.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info"><p>Times vary by machine and recipe. Your model's manual is the real boss.</p></div></div>	-
44.61.0	dashboard_timeline/Paint Drying Timeline	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Paint Drying</h1><p class="muted">From wet to walkable to fully cured.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">0–1 hr</span><p>Touch dry. Don't lean anything against the wall yet.</p></li><li><span class="when">4–6 hrs</span><p>Recoat window for most latex paints. Check the can.</p></li><li><span class="when">24 hrs</span><p>Light use. You can carefully move furniture back.</p></li><li><span class="when">3–7 days</span><p>Full cure. Avoid scrubbing or hanging heavy art.</p></li><li><span class="when">30 days</span><p>Maximum hardness. Now it's really done, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></li></ul><div class="callout warn"><p>Humidity and low temps slow everything down. Be patient.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Paint Drying</h1><p class="muted">From wet to walkable to fully cured.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">0–1 hr</span><p>Touch dry. Don't lean anything against the wall yet.</p></li><li><span class="when">4–6 hrs</span><p>Recoat window for most latex paints. Check the can.</p></li><li><span class="when">24 hrs</span><p>Light use. You can carefully move furniture back.</p></li><li><span class="when">3–7 days</span><p>Full cure. Avoid scrubbing or hanging heavy art.</p></li><li><span class="when">30 days</span><p>Maximum hardness. Now it's really done, .</p></li></ul><div class="callout warn"><p>Humidity and low temps slow everything down. Be patient.</p></div></div>	-
44.62.0	dashboard_timeline/New Puppy Timeline	<div class="umi-page"><h1>New Puppy</h1><p class="muted">The first 24 hours. Keep it calm and boring — that's the goal.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Arrival</span><p>Let the puppy sniff one room. No big introductions yet.</p></li><li><span class="when">+30 min</span><p>Offer water. Take outside to potty. Praise quietly.</p></li><li><span class="when">+2 hrs</span><p>Small meal. Same food the breeder or shelter used.</p></li><li><span class="when">+4 hrs</span><p>Nap time in the crate. Leave the door open at first.</p></li><li><span class="when">+6 hrs</span><p>Another potty break. Every hour is normal for a young pup.</p></li><li><span class="when">Bedtime</span><p>Crate near your bed. Whining is okay — comfort, don't cave.</p></li></ul><div class="callout good"><p>Slow is fast with puppies, <regex>user_name</regex>. You've got this.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>New Puppy</h1><p class="muted">The first 24 hours. Keep it calm and boring — that's the goal.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Arrival</span><p>Let the puppy sniff one room. No big introductions yet.</p></li><li><span class="when">+30 min</span><p>Offer water. Take outside to potty. Praise quietly.</p></li><li><span class="when">+2 hrs</span><p>Small meal. Same food the breeder or shelter used.</p></li><li><span class="when">+4 hrs</span><p>Nap time in the crate. Leave the door open at first.</p></li><li><span class="when">+6 hrs</span><p>Another potty break. Every hour is normal for a young pup.</p></li><li><span class="when">Bedtime</span><p>Crate near your bed. Whining is okay — comfort, don't cave.</p></li></ul><div class="callout good"><p>Slow is fast with puppies, . You've got this.</p></div></div>	-
44.63.0	dashboard_timeline/Beekeeping Inspection Timeline	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Beekeeping Inspection Timeline</h1><p class="muted">A calm walk through a hive check, from smoker to notes. Times are a guide, not gospel.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">8:00</span><p>Gather gear: smoker, hive tool, brush, veil. Light the smoker with dry pine needles. Let it settle to cool white smoke.</p></li><li><span class="when">8:15</span><p>Approach from the side. Give two gentle puffs at the entrance. Wait 30 seconds for the guard bees to calm.</p></li><li><span class="when">8:20</span><p>Lift the outer cover. Set it upside down on the ground. Crack the inner cover with the hive tool and puff smoke.</p></li><li><span class="when">8:25</span><p>Check frames one by one. Look for eggs, larvae, capped brood. Spot the queen? Note her colour mark.</p></li><li><span class="when">8:45</span><p>Look for queen cells. If you see more than two, consider a split or swarm control. Check for pests: varroa, wax moths, small hive beetles.</p></li><li><span class="when">9:00</span><p>Close up: inner cover, outer cover. Reduce entrance if robbing is a risk. Top up the feeder if needed.</p></li><li><span class="when">9:10</span><p>Log it: date, weather, temperament, brood pattern, stores. A five-minute note now saves a headache later.</p></li></ul></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Beekeeping Inspection Timeline</h1><p class="muted">A calm walk through a hive check, from smoker to notes. Times are a guide, not gospel.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">8:00</span><p>Gather gear: smoker, hive tool, brush, veil. Light the smoker with dry pine needles. Let it settle to cool white smoke.</p></li><li><span class="when">8:15</span><p>Approach from the side. Give two gentle puffs at the entrance. Wait 30 seconds for the guard bees to calm.</p></li><li><span class="when">8:20</span><p>Lift the outer cover. Set it upside down on the ground. Crack the inner cover with the hive tool and puff smoke.</p></li><li><span class="when">8:25</span><p>Check frames one by one. Look for eggs, larvae, capped brood. Spot the queen? Note her colour mark.</p></li><li><span class="when">8:45</span><p>Look for queen cells. If you see more than two, consider a split or swarm control. Check for pests: varroa, wax moths, small hive beetles.</p></li><li><span class="when">9:00</span><p>Close up: inner cover, outer cover. Reduce entrance if robbing is a risk. Top up the feeder if needed.</p></li><li><span class="when">9:10</span><p>Log it: date, weather, temperament, brood pattern, stores. A five-minute note now saves a headache later.</p></li></ul></div>	-
44.0.1	dashboard_timeline/Museum Visit Timeline	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Museum Visit Timeline</h1><p class="muted">From ticket to gift shop, without the footache.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">9:30</span><p>Arrive at opening. Beat the school groups and get a quiet first look at the big galleries.</p></li><li><span class="when">9:45</span><p>Grab a map at the desk. Pick three must-see exhibits. Mark them. Everything else is gravy.</p></li><li><span class="when">10:00</span><p>Start with the special exhibition. It's usually timed-entry and busiest later. Go while your eyes are fresh.</p></li><li><span class="when">11:00</span><p>Wander the permanent collection. Sit on the benches. Look longer at fewer pieces.</p></li><li><span class="when">12:30</span><p>Lunch at the cafe or a nearby bench. Rest your feet. Hydrate. You'll thank yourself at 3pm.</p></li><li><span class="when">13:30</span><p>Hit the second floor or the sculpture garden. Take one photo you'll actually keep.</p></li><li><span class="when">15:00</span><p>Gift shop. Buy the postcard. Exit through the quiet hall and let it all settle.</p></li></ul></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Museum Visit Timeline</h1><p class="muted">From ticket to gift shop, without the footache.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">9:30</span><p>Arrive at opening. Beat the school groups and get a quiet first look at the big galleries.</p></li><li><span class="when">9:45</span><p>Grab a map at the desk. Pick three must-see exhibits. Mark them. Everything else is gravy.</p></li><li><span class="when">10:00</span><p>Start with the special exhibition. It's usually timed-entry and busiest later. Go while your eyes are fresh.</p></li><li><span class="when">11:00</span><p>Wander the permanent collection. Sit on the benches. Look longer at fewer pieces.</p></li><li><span class="when">12:30</span><p>Lunch at the cafe or a nearby bench. Rest your feet. Hydrate. You'll thank yourself at 3pm.</p></li><li><span class="when">13:30</span><p>Hit the second floor or the sculpture garden. Take one photo you'll actually keep.</p></li><li><span class="when">15:00</span><p>Gift shop. Buy the postcard. Exit through the quiet hall and let it all settle.</p></li></ul></div>	-
44.1.1	dashboard_timeline/Tea Ceremony Timeline	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Tea Ceremony Timeline</h1><p class="muted">A slow, deliberate sequence. Breathe between each step.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">0:00</span><p>Prepare the room. Sweep the tatami. Arrange flowers simply. Set the kettle to heat.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:10</span><p>Guests arrive. Bow. Admire the scroll and the flower arrangement. Sit in seiza if comfortable.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:20</span><p>Clean the utensils in view of guests. Fold the cloth. Rinse the bowl. This is the ritual of attention.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:30</span><p>Measure matcha. Pour hot water. Whisk in a zigzag motion until a fine foam forms.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:35</span><p>Serve the bowl to the first guest. They bow, rotate the bowl, drink. Pass it on.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:45</span><p>Offer a sweet. The bitterness of tea meets the sweetness of wagashi. A quiet conversation.</p></li><li><span class="when">1:00</span><p>Clean up. Thank your guests. The ceremony ends, but the calm stays.</p></li></ul></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Tea Ceremony Timeline</h1><p class="muted">A slow, deliberate sequence. Breathe between each step.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">0:00</span><p>Prepare the room. Sweep the tatami. Arrange flowers simply. Set the kettle to heat.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:10</span><p>Guests arrive. Bow. Admire the scroll and the flower arrangement. Sit in seiza if comfortable.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:20</span><p>Clean the utensils in view of guests. Fold the cloth. Rinse the bowl. This is the ritual of attention.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:30</span><p>Measure matcha. Pour hot water. Whisk in a zigzag motion until a fine foam forms.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:35</span><p>Serve the bowl to the first guest. They bow, rotate the bowl, drink. Pass it on.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:45</span><p>Offer a sweet. The bitterness of tea meets the sweetness of wagashi. A quiet conversation.</p></li><li><span class="when">1:00</span><p>Clean up. Thank your guests. The ceremony ends, but the calm stays.</p></li></ul></div>	-
44.2.1	dashboard_timeline/Birdwatching Outing Timeline	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Birdwatching Outing Timeline</h1><p class="muted">Early starts, quiet steps, and the reward of a flash of colour.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">5:30</span><p>Wake before dawn. Dress in muted colours. Pack binoculars, field guide, water, and a snack.</p></li><li><span class="when">6:00</span><p>Arrive at the reserve or park. Listen first. The dawn chorus is a map of who's around.</p></li><li><span class="when">6:15</span><p>Walk slowly. Stop often. Scan the treeline and the water's edge. Let birds come to you.</p></li><li><span class="when">7:00</span><p>Find a quiet spot. Sit still for ten minutes. You'll see more when you're not moving.</p></li><li><span class="when">7:30</span><p>Log your sightings in an app or notebook. Note behaviour, not just species.</p></li><li><span class="when">8:30</span><p>Head back. The light is changing, the birds are quieter. A good morning's work.</p></li><li><span class="when">9:00</span><p>Review your list over coffee. Plan the next outing. Maybe a new habitat next time.</p></li></ul></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Birdwatching Outing Timeline</h1><p class="muted">Early starts, quiet steps, and the reward of a flash of colour.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">5:30</span><p>Wake before dawn. Dress in muted colours. Pack binoculars, field guide, water, and a snack.</p></li><li><span class="when">6:00</span><p>Arrive at the reserve or park. Listen first. The dawn chorus is a map of who's around.</p></li><li><span class="when">6:15</span><p>Walk slowly. Stop often. Scan the treeline and the water's edge. Let birds come to you.</p></li><li><span class="when">7:00</span><p>Find a quiet spot. Sit still for ten minutes. You'll see more when you're not moving.</p></li><li><span class="when">7:30</span><p>Log your sightings in an app or notebook. Note behaviour, not just species.</p></li><li><span class="when">8:30</span><p>Head back. The light is changing, the birds are quieter. A good morning's work.</p></li><li><span class="when">9:00</span><p>Review your list over coffee. Plan the next outing. Maybe a new habitat next time.</p></li></ul></div>	-
44.3.1	dashboard_timeline/Rock Climbing Session Timeline	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Rock Climbing Session Timeline</h1><p class="muted">Warm up, project, cool down. Your fingers will thank you.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">0:00</span><p>Arrive at the gym. Change shoes. Do a dynamic warm-up: arm circles, leg swings, easy traversing.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:15</span><p>Start on easy routes. Two or three grades below your max. Focus on footwork and breathing.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:30</span><p>Move to your project. Read the route from the ground. Plan rests. Then climb.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:50</span><p>Rest between attempts. Five to ten minutes. Shake out your arms. Drink water.</p></li><li><span class="when">1:30</span><p>Switch to a different style: slab, overhang, or cracks. Keep it fun, not just hard.</p></li><li><span class="when">2:00</span><p>Cool down with easy climbs. Stretch your forearms and shoulders. Roll out your back if you have a roller.</p></li><li><span class="when">2:30</span><p>Log your attempts. Note what worked. Leave before you're completely wrecked.</p></li></ul></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Rock Climbing Session Timeline</h1><p class="muted">Warm up, project, cool down. Your fingers will thank you.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">0:00</span><p>Arrive at the gym. Change shoes. Do a dynamic warm-up: arm circles, leg swings, easy traversing.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:15</span><p>Start on easy routes. Two or three grades below your max. Focus on footwork and breathing.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:30</span><p>Move to your project. Read the route from the ground. Plan rests. Then climb.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:50</span><p>Rest between attempts. Five to ten minutes. Shake out your arms. Drink water.</p></li><li><span class="when">1:30</span><p>Switch to a different style: slab, overhang, or cracks. Keep it fun, not just hard.</p></li><li><span class="when">2:00</span><p>Cool down with easy climbs. Stretch your forearms and shoulders. Roll out your back if you have a roller.</p></li><li><span class="when">2:30</span><p>Log your attempts. Note what worked. Leave before you're completely wrecked.</p></li></ul></div>	-
44.4.1	dashboard_timeline/Pottery Class Timeline	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Pottery Class Timeline</h1><p class="muted">From wedging clay to the kiln, a beginner's evening.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">18:00</span><p>Arrive and tie back hair. Put on an apron. Collect your clay and tools.</p></li><li><span class="when">18:10</span><p>Wedge the clay. Knead it to remove air bubbles. This is the meditation part.</p></li><li><span class="when">18:20</span><p>Centre the clay on the wheel. Elbows on your knees. Press down and in. Feel the hum.</p></li><li><span class="when">18:40</span><p>Open the clay. Pull up the walls. Keep your hands wet. Don't rush; let the wheel do the work.</p></li><li><span class="when">19:00</span><p>Shape the rim. Use a sponge to smooth. Sign the bottom with your initials.</p></li><li><span class="when">19:15</span><p>Clean up your wheel. Put scraps in the reclaim bucket. Wash tools.</p></li><li><span class="when">19:30</span><p>Choose a glaze. Leave your piece on the shelf to dry. It'll be fired by next week.</p></li></ul></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Pottery Class Timeline</h1><p class="muted">From wedging clay to the kiln, a beginner's evening.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">18:00</span><p>Arrive and tie back hair. Put on an apron. Collect your clay and tools.</p></li><li><span class="when">18:10</span><p>Wedge the clay. Knead it to remove air bubbles. This is the meditation part.</p></li><li><span class="when">18:20</span><p>Centre the clay on the wheel. Elbows on your knees. Press down and in. Feel the hum.</p></li><li><span class="when">18:40</span><p>Open the clay. Pull up the walls. Keep your hands wet. Don't rush; let the wheel do the work.</p></li><li><span class="when">19:00</span><p>Shape the rim. Use a sponge to smooth. Sign the bottom with your initials.</p></li><li><span class="when">19:15</span><p>Clean up your wheel. Put scraps in the reclaim bucket. Wash tools.</p></li><li><span class="when">19:30</span><p>Choose a glaze. Leave your piece on the shelf to dry. It'll be fired by next week.</p></li></ul></div>	-
44.5.1	dashboard_timeline/Coffee Brew Timeline	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Coffee Brew: Pour Over</h1><p class="muted">A calm four-minute ritual for one cup. Times are from the moment you start pouring.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">0:00</span><p>Rinse filter with hot water, discard. Add 22g medium-fine coffee. Shake flat.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:30</span><p>Bloom: pour 60g water just off boil. Swirl gently. Wait 30s.</p></li><li><span class="when">1:00</span><p>First pour: spiral out to 200g total. Keep the bed even.</p></li><li><span class="when">1:45</span><p>Second pour: top up to 360g. Let it drain naturally.</p></li><li><span class="when">3:30</span><p>Drawdown should finish. If it stalls past 4:00, grind coarser next time.</p></li><li><span class="when">4:00</span><p>Serve. Swirl the carafe, then pour.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info"><p>No scale? A level tablespoon is about 5g. Adjust to taste.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Coffee Brew: Pour Over</h1><p class="muted">A calm four-minute ritual for one cup. Times are from the moment you start pouring.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">0:00</span><p>Rinse filter with hot water, discard. Add 22g medium-fine coffee. Shake flat.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:30</span><p>Bloom: pour 60g water just off boil. Swirl gently. Wait 30s.</p></li><li><span class="when">1:00</span><p>First pour: spiral out to 200g total. Keep the bed even.</p></li><li><span class="when">1:45</span><p>Second pour: top up to 360g. Let it drain naturally.</p></li><li><span class="when">3:30</span><p>Drawdown should finish. If it stalls past 4:00, grind coarser next time.</p></li><li><span class="when">4:00</span><p>Serve. Swirl the carafe, then pour.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info"><p>No scale? A level tablespoon is about 5g. Adjust to taste.</p></div></div>	-
44.6.1	dashboard_timeline/First Apartment Move-In Timeline	<div class="umi-page"><h1>First Apartment Move-In</h1><p class="muted">The first 48 hours, in the order that keeps chaos low.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Before</span><p>Set up utilities (electric, water, internet) at least a week out. Confirm keys pickup time.</p></li><li><span class="when">Hour 0</span><p>Do a walkthrough with the landlord. Photograph every wall, appliance, and floor. Note pre-existing damage.</p></li><li><span class="when">Hour 1</span><p>Clean the bathroom and kitchen first. You'll want them ready when you're tired.</p></li><li><span class="when">Hour 3</span><p>Unload the truck. Put boxes in their rooms, not the hallway.</p></li><li><span class="when">Hour 5</span><p>Make the bed. You'll thank yourself tonight.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>Test smoke detectors, find the circuit breaker, locate the water shut-off. Know your escape routes.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Change the locks or ask the landlord to. Update your address with the post office and bank.</p></li></ul><div class="callout good"><p>Bonus: a plunger and a fire extinguisher before you need them.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>First Apartment Move-In</h1><p class="muted">The first 48 hours, in the order that keeps chaos low.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Before</span><p>Set up utilities (electric, water, internet) at least a week out. Confirm keys pickup time.</p></li><li><span class="when">Hour 0</span><p>Do a walkthrough with the landlord. Photograph every wall, appliance, and floor. Note pre-existing damage.</p></li><li><span class="when">Hour 1</span><p>Clean the bathroom and kitchen first. You'll want them ready when you're tired.</p></li><li><span class="when">Hour 3</span><p>Unload the truck. Put boxes in their rooms, not the hallway.</p></li><li><span class="when">Hour 5</span><p>Make the bed. You'll thank yourself tonight.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>Test smoke detectors, find the circuit breaker, locate the water shut-off. Know your escape routes.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Change the locks or ask the landlord to. Update your address with the post office and bank.</p></li></ul><div class="callout good"><p>Bonus: a plunger and a fire extinguisher before you need them.</p></div></div>	-
44.7.1	dashboard_timeline/Vegetable Garden Planting Timeline	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Vegetable Garden Timeline</h1><p class="muted">A temperate-climate guide. Shift by your last frost date.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">6 weeks before last frost</span><p>Start tomatoes, peppers, and eggplant indoors under lights.</p></li><li><span class="when">4 weeks before</span><p>Start broccoli, cabbage, and lettuce indoors. Harden off on nice days.</p></li><li><span class="when">2 weeks before</span><p>Direct sow peas, spinach, radishes, and carrots. Prepare beds with compost.</p></li><li><span class="when">Last frost</span><p>Transplant hardened seedlings. Water deeply. Mulch to keep moisture in.</p></li><li><span class="when">2 weeks after</span><p>Plant beans, corn, and squash. Thin seedlings to proper spacing.</p></li><li><span class="when">Midsummer</span><p>Side-dress with compost. Water in the morning. Watch for pests.</p></li><li><span class="when">First frost</span><p>Harvest everything tender. Plant garlic for next year. Cover hardy greens.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info"><p>Your local extension office has a free planting calendar by zip code.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Vegetable Garden Timeline</h1><p class="muted">A temperate-climate guide. Shift by your last frost date.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">6 weeks before last frost</span><p>Start tomatoes, peppers, and eggplant indoors under lights.</p></li><li><span class="when">4 weeks before</span><p>Start broccoli, cabbage, and lettuce indoors. Harden off on nice days.</p></li><li><span class="when">2 weeks before</span><p>Direct sow peas, spinach, radishes, and carrots. Prepare beds with compost.</p></li><li><span class="when">Last frost</span><p>Transplant hardened seedlings. Water deeply. Mulch to keep moisture in.</p></li><li><span class="when">2 weeks after</span><p>Plant beans, corn, and squash. Thin seedlings to proper spacing.</p></li><li><span class="when">Midsummer</span><p>Side-dress with compost. Water in the morning. Watch for pests.</p></li><li><span class="when">First frost</span><p>Harvest everything tender. Plant garlic for next year. Cover hardy greens.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info"><p>Your local extension office has a free planting calendar by zip code.</p></div></div>	-
44.8.1	dashboard_timeline/Sourdough Starter Feeding Timeline	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Sourdough Starter: Feeding</h1><p class="muted">A predictable rhythm for a happy starter. Adjust to your kitchen temperature.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Mix 50g whole wheat flour + 50g water in a jar. Cover loosely. Wait 24h.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>Discard half. Add 50g flour + 50g water. Stir. Wait 24h.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3-6</span><p>Repeat the discard-and-feed daily. Look for bubbles and a sour smell.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 7</span><p>If it doubles within 6 hours, you're ready to bake. If not, keep feeding.</p></li><li><span class="when">Ongoing</span><p>Feed once a day at room temp, or once a week in the fridge.</p></li><li><span class="when">Before baking</span><p>Feed 4-8 hours ahead. Use it when it's domed and just past peak.</p></li></ul><div class="callout warn"><p>If it smells like nail polish, it's hungry. Feed more often.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Sourdough Starter: Feeding</h1><p class="muted">A predictable rhythm for a happy starter. Adjust to your kitchen temperature.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Mix 50g whole wheat flour + 50g water in a jar. Cover loosely. Wait 24h.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>Discard half. Add 50g flour + 50g water. Stir. Wait 24h.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3-6</span><p>Repeat the discard-and-feed daily. Look for bubbles and a sour smell.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 7</span><p>If it doubles within 6 hours, you're ready to bake. If not, keep feeding.</p></li><li><span class="when">Ongoing</span><p>Feed once a day at room temp, or once a week in the fridge.</p></li><li><span class="when">Before baking</span><p>Feed 4-8 hours ahead. Use it when it's domed and just past peak.</p></li></ul><div class="callout warn"><p>If it smells like nail polish, it's hungry. Feed more often.</p></div></div>	-
44.9.1	dashboard_timeline/Home Office Setup Timeline	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Home Office Setup</h1><p class="muted">A phased approach: start with the body, end with the cables.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Phase 1</span><p>Pick a spot with a door if possible. Near a window, away from the bedroom.</p></li><li><span class="when">Phase 2</span><p>Get the chair right. Your back will thank you. Then set the desk height even with elbows.</p></li><li><span class="when">Phase 3</span><p>Monitor at eye level, an arm's length away. Keyboard and mouse close.</p></li><li><span class="when">Phase 4</span><p>Lighting: a warm lamp for evenings, daylight for the morning. Avoid facing a window directly.</p></li><li><span class="when">Phase 5</span><p>Sound: headphones or a small speaker. If calls are frequent, a mic on a boom arm.</p></li><li><span class="when">Phase 6</span><p>Cable management: raceway or clips. Label the power bricks.</p></li><li><span class="when">Phase 7</span><p>Add a plant, a coaster, and a drawer for pens. The rest is decoration.</p></li></ul><div class="callout good"><p>Stand up every hour. A timer works wonders.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Home Office Setup</h1><p class="muted">A phased approach: start with the body, end with the cables.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Phase 1</span><p>Pick a spot with a door if possible. Near a window, away from the bedroom.</p></li><li><span class="when">Phase 2</span><p>Get the chair right. Your back will thank you. Then set the desk height even with elbows.</p></li><li><span class="when">Phase 3</span><p>Monitor at eye level, an arm's length away. Keyboard and mouse close.</p></li><li><span class="when">Phase 4</span><p>Lighting: a warm lamp for evenings, daylight for the morning. Avoid facing a window directly.</p></li><li><span class="when">Phase 5</span><p>Sound: headphones or a small speaker. If calls are frequent, a mic on a boom arm.</p></li><li><span class="when">Phase 6</span><p>Cable management: raceway or clips. Label the power bricks.</p></li><li><span class="when">Phase 7</span><p>Add a plant, a coaster, and a drawer for pens. The rest is decoration.</p></li></ul><div class="callout good"><p>Stand up every hour. A timer works wonders.</p></div></div>	-
44.10.1	dashboard_timeline/Emergency Preparedness Timeline	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Emergency Preparedness</h1><p class="muted">Small steps now, much easier later. Do them in order.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Water: one gallon per person per day for three days. Store in a cool, dark place.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>Food: three days of non-perishables. Don't forget a manual can opener.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>Light and power: flashlights, batteries, a power bank. A hand-crank radio.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 4</span><p>First aid kit and any medications. Copies of IDs and insurance in a waterproof bag.</p></li><li><span class="when">Month 2</span><p>Make a family plan: meeting spots, out-of-town contact, texts over calls.</p></li><li><span class="when">Month 3</span><p>Learn your building's shut-offs: gas, water, electric. Keep a wrench by the gas meter.</p></li><li><span class="when">Ongoing</span><p>Check batteries twice a year. Rotate food and water once a year.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info"><p>Ready.gov has printable checklists in many languages.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Emergency Preparedness</h1><p class="muted">Small steps now, much easier later. Do them in order.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Water: one gallon per person per day for three days. Store in a cool, dark place.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>Food: three days of non-perishables. Don't forget a manual can opener.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 3</span><p>Light and power: flashlights, batteries, a power bank. A hand-crank radio.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 4</span><p>First aid kit and any medications. Copies of IDs and insurance in a waterproof bag.</p></li><li><span class="when">Month 2</span><p>Make a family plan: meeting spots, out-of-town contact, texts over calls.</p></li><li><span class="when">Month 3</span><p>Learn your building's shut-offs: gas, water, electric. Keep a wrench by the gas meter.</p></li><li><span class="when">Ongoing</span><p>Check batteries twice a year. Rotate food and water once a year.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info"><p>Ready.gov has printable checklists in many languages.</p></div></div>	-
44.11.1	dashboard_timeline/Sourdough Starter Feeding Schedule	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Sourdough Starter Feeding Schedule</h1><p class="muted">A gentle rhythm for keeping your starter bubbly and ready.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">7:30 AM</span><p>Discard all but 50g. Add 50g flour + 50g water. Stir well.</p></li><li><span class="when">12:30 PM</span><p>Check rise. If doubled, it's ready for baking. If not, wait.</p></li><li><span class="when">7:30 PM</span><p>Second feeding: same ratio. This keeps it active overnight.</p></li><li><span class="when">10:00 PM</span><p>If baking tomorrow, feed once more before bed. Cover loosely.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info"><p>If your starter smells like nail polish, it's hungry. Feed it more often.</p></div><p class="t-small dim">Times are flexible. Adjust to your schedule.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Sourdough Starter Feeding Schedule</h1><p class="muted">A gentle rhythm for keeping your starter bubbly and ready.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">7:30 AM</span><p>Discard all but 50g. Add 50g flour + 50g water. Stir well.</p></li><li><span class="when">12:30 PM</span><p>Check rise. If doubled, it's ready for baking. If not, wait.</p></li><li><span class="when">7:30 PM</span><p>Second feeding: same ratio. This keeps it active overnight.</p></li><li><span class="when">10:00 PM</span><p>If baking tomorrow, feed once more before bed. Cover loosely.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info"><p>If your starter smells like nail polish, it's hungry. Feed it more often.</p></div><p class="t-small dim">Times are flexible. Adjust to your schedule.</p></div>	-
44.12.1	dashboard_timeline/Car Wash Timeline	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Car Wash Timeline</h1><p class="muted">A clean car in about 90 minutes, if you don't stop to admire it too long.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">0:00</span><p>Pull into shade. Gather buckets, soap, mitt, towels. Rinse loose grit with hose.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:15</span><p>Wash wheels first with dedicated brush. Rinse. Two-bucket method on paint, top down.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:40</span><p>Rinse entire car. Dry with microfiber, patting not dragging. Open doors to wipe jambs.</p></li><li><span class="when">1:00</span><p>Apply spray wax or quick detailer. Buff with clean towel. Glass inside and out.</p></li><li><span class="when">1:20</span><p>Dress tires if you like. Stand back. Nod. Done.</p></li></ul><p class="dim t-small">Tip: wash from the top down, and never let soap dry on paint in direct sun.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Car Wash Timeline</h1><p class="muted">A clean car in about 90 minutes, if you don't stop to admire it too long.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">0:00</span><p>Pull into shade. Gather buckets, soap, mitt, towels. Rinse loose grit with hose.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:15</span><p>Wash wheels first with dedicated brush. Rinse. Two-bucket method on paint, top down.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:40</span><p>Rinse entire car. Dry with microfiber, patting not dragging. Open doors to wipe jambs.</p></li><li><span class="when">1:00</span><p>Apply spray wax or quick detailer. Buff with clean towel. Glass inside and out.</p></li><li><span class="when">1:20</span><p>Dress tires if you like. Stand back. Nod. Done.</p></li></ul><p class="dim t-small">Tip: wash from the top down, and never let soap dry on paint in direct sun.</p></div>	-
44.13.1	dashboard_timeline/Cat Litter Change Timeline	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Cat Litter Change Timeline</h1><p class="muted">Your cat has opinions. This keeps them quiet.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Daily</span><p>Scoop solids into a bag. Stir litter to even it out. Top up if low.</p></li><li><span class="when">Weekly</span><p>Wipe the box with a pet-safe cleaner. Check for scratches or cracks. Replace any worn liners.</p></li><li><span class="when">Every 2-4 weeks</span><p>Dump all litter. Wash the box with mild soap and hot water. Dry fully before refilling.</p></li><li><span class="when">Monthly</span><p>Inspect the scoop and mat. Deep clean the surrounding floor. Consider a second box if you have one cat.</p></li></ul><p class="dim t-small">Tip: most cats prefer unscented clumping litter and a box they can turn around in.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Cat Litter Change Timeline</h1><p class="muted">Your cat has opinions. This keeps them quiet.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Daily</span><p>Scoop solids into a bag. Stir litter to even it out. Top up if low.</p></li><li><span class="when">Weekly</span><p>Wipe the box with a pet-safe cleaner. Check for scratches or cracks. Replace any worn liners.</p></li><li><span class="when">Every 2-4 weeks</span><p>Dump all litter. Wash the box with mild soap and hot water. Dry fully before refilling.</p></li><li><span class="when">Monthly</span><p>Inspect the scoop and mat. Deep clean the surrounding floor. Consider a second box if you have one cat.</p></li></ul><p class="dim t-small">Tip: most cats prefer unscented clumping litter and a box they can turn around in.</p></div>	-
44.14.1	dashboard_timeline/Guitar String Change Timeline	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Guitar String Change Timeline</h1><p class="muted">New strings, new you. Roughly 30 minutes, plus stretching time.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">0:00</span><p>Loosen all strings. Remove them. Wipe fretboard and bridge with a dry cloth.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:05</span><p>Start with the low E. Thread, wind downward, leave 2-3 wraps. Tune to pitch loosely.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:15</span><p>Repeat for A, D, G, B, high E. Snip excess. Tune all strings to pitch.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:20</span><p>Stretch each string gently, retune. Repeat 2-3 times until it holds.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:30</span><p>Play a chord. Smile. Recheck tuning after a few minutes.</p></li></ul><p class="dim t-small">Tip: change one string at a time to keep tension on the neck, unless you're doing a deep clean.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Guitar String Change Timeline</h1><p class="muted">New strings, new you. Roughly 30 minutes, plus stretching time.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">0:00</span><p>Loosen all strings. Remove them. Wipe fretboard and bridge with a dry cloth.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:05</span><p>Start with the low E. Thread, wind downward, leave 2-3 wraps. Tune to pitch loosely.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:15</span><p>Repeat for A, D, G, B, high E. Snip excess. Tune all strings to pitch.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:20</span><p>Stretch each string gently, retune. Repeat 2-3 times until it holds.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:30</span><p>Play a chord. Smile. Recheck tuning after a few minutes.</p></li></ul><p class="dim t-small">Tip: change one string at a time to keep tension on the neck, unless you're doing a deep clean.</p></div>	-
44.15.1	dashboard_timeline/Aquarium Water Change Timeline	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Aquarium Water Change Timeline</h1><p class="muted">Happy fish, clear water. About 45 minutes for a 20-gallon tank.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">0:00</span><p>Unplug heater and filter. Gather buckets, siphon, conditioner, towels.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:05</span><p>Siphon 20-25% of water into a bucket. Vacuum gravel gently as you go.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:25</span><p>Refill with dechlorinated water at matching temperature. Pour slowly over a plate to avoid disturbing substrate.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:40</span><p>Plug equipment back in. Check filter flow. Wipe glass inside if needed.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:45</span><p>Observe fish for stress. Feed lightly later, not immediately.</p></li></ul><p class="dim t-small">Tip: never change more than 30% at once unless it's an emergency.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Aquarium Water Change Timeline</h1><p class="muted">Happy fish, clear water. About 45 minutes for a 20-gallon tank.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">0:00</span><p>Unplug heater and filter. Gather buckets, siphon, conditioner, towels.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:05</span><p>Siphon 20-25% of water into a bucket. Vacuum gravel gently as you go.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:25</span><p>Refill with dechlorinated water at matching temperature. Pour slowly over a plate to avoid disturbing substrate.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:40</span><p>Plug equipment back in. Check filter flow. Wipe glass inside if needed.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:45</span><p>Observe fish for stress. Feed lightly later, not immediately.</p></li></ul><p class="dim t-small">Tip: never change more than 30% at once unless it's an emergency.</p></div>	-
44.16.1	dashboard_timeline/Meal Prep Sunday Timeline	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Meal Prep Sunday Timeline</h1><p class="muted">Two hours now, five easy lunches later.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">0:00</span><p>Preheat oven to 425°F. Wash and chop vegetables. Start rice or quinoa on the stove.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:20</span><p>Toss vegetables with oil and salt. Roast on one sheet pan. Season chicken or tofu on another.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:40</span><p>Flip veggies. Check grains. Make a sauce or dressing in a jar.</p></li><li><span class="when">1:00</span><p>Pull everything out. Cool slightly. Portion into containers with grains, protein, veggies.</p></li><li><span class="when">1:30</span><p>Label with dates. Refrigerate. Wipe counters. Done.</p></li></ul><p class="dim t-small">Tip: keep wet sauces separate until eating day so nothing gets soggy.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Meal Prep Sunday Timeline</h1><p class="muted">Two hours now, five easy lunches later.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">0:00</span><p>Preheat oven to 425°F. Wash and chop vegetables. Start rice or quinoa on the stove.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:20</span><p>Toss vegetables with oil and salt. Roast on one sheet pan. Season chicken or tofu on another.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:40</span><p>Flip veggies. Check grains. Make a sauce or dressing in a jar.</p></li><li><span class="when">1:00</span><p>Pull everything out. Cool slightly. Portion into containers with grains, protein, veggies.</p></li><li><span class="when">1:30</span><p>Label with dates. Refrigerate. Wipe counters. Done.</p></li></ul><p class="dim t-small">Tip: keep wet sauces separate until eating day so nothing gets soggy.</p></div>	-
44.17.1	dashboard_timeline/Cast Iron Seasoning Timeline	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Cast Iron Seasoning Timeline</h1>  <p class="muted">A good seasoning is layers of patience. Here's how it goes.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Scrub &amp; dry:</strong> Wash with hot water and a stiff brush. Dry thoroughly.</li>    <li><strong>Oil lightly:</strong> Rub a thin layer of flaxseed or canola oil all over.</li>    <li><strong>Bake:</strong> Place upside down in a 450°F oven for 1 hour.</li>    <li><strong>Cool:</strong> Let it cool in the oven before handling.</li>    <li><strong>Repeat:</strong> Do this 3–4 times for a solid layer.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout info"><strong>Tip:</strong> The more layers, the better the non-stick. Don't rush it.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Cast Iron Seasoning Timeline</h1>  <p class="muted">A good seasoning is layers of patience. Here's how it goes.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Scrub &amp; dry:</strong> Wash with hot water and a stiff brush. Dry thoroughly.</li>    <li><strong>Oil lightly:</strong> Rub a thin layer of flaxseed or canola oil all over.</li>    <li><strong>Bake:</strong> Place upside down in a 450°F oven for 1 hour.</li>    <li><strong>Cool:</strong> Let it cool in the oven before handling.</li>    <li><strong>Repeat:</strong> Do this 3–4 times for a solid layer.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout info"><strong>Tip:</strong> The more layers, the better the non-stick. Don't rush it.</div></div>	-
44.18.1	dashboard_timeline/Compost Pile Timeline	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Compost Pile Timeline</h1>  <ul class="timeline">    <li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Add browns (leaves, cardboard) and greens (kitchen scraps). Aim for 2:1 brown to green.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>Turn the pile to aerate. It should heat up within a day.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>Turn again. Check moisture—like a wrung-out sponge.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Week 4</span><p>Turn and monitor. The pile should be warm and shrinking.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Month 2-3</span><p>Compost is ready when dark, crumbly, and earthy-smelling.</p></li>  </ul>  <div class="callout good">Your compost is ready when you can't recognize any original ingredients.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Compost Pile Timeline</h1>  <ul class="timeline">    <li><span class="when">Day 1</span><p>Add browns (leaves, cardboard) and greens (kitchen scraps). Aim for 2:1 brown to green.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Day 3</span><p>Turn the pile to aerate. It should heat up within a day.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>Turn again. Check moisture—like a wrung-out sponge.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Week 4</span><p>Turn and monitor. The pile should be warm and shrinking.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Month 2-3</span><p>Compost is ready when dark, crumbly, and earthy-smelling.</p></li>  </ul>  <div class="callout good">Your compost is ready when you can't recognize any original ingredients.</div></div>	-
44.19.1	dashboard_timeline/Knife Sharpening Timeline	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Knife Sharpening Timeline</h1>  <p class="muted">A sharp knife is a safe knife. Here's the process.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Gather:</strong> Whetstone (1000/6000 grit), honing rod, towel.</li>    <li><strong>Soak:</strong> Soak the stone in water for 10–15 minutes.</li>    <li><strong>Angle:</strong> Hold knife at 15–20 degrees. Maintain that angle.</li>    <li><strong>Sweep:</strong> Push the blade across the stone in sweeping motions. 10 passes per side.</li>    <li><strong>Flip:</strong> Repeat on the other side.</li>    <li><strong>Hone:</strong> Use the honing rod to realign the edge.</li>    <li><strong>Test:</strong> Slice a tomato or paper. It should glide.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout warn">Don't skip the honing—it keeps the edge true between sharpenings.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Knife Sharpening Timeline</h1>  <p class="muted">A sharp knife is a safe knife. Here's the process.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Gather:</strong> Whetstone (1000/6000 grit), honing rod, towel.</li>    <li><strong>Soak:</strong> Soak the stone in water for 10–15 minutes.</li>    <li><strong>Angle:</strong> Hold knife at 15–20 degrees. Maintain that angle.</li>    <li><strong>Sweep:</strong> Push the blade across the stone in sweeping motions. 10 passes per side.</li>    <li><strong>Flip:</strong> Repeat on the other side.</li>    <li><strong>Hone:</strong> Use the honing rod to realign the edge.</li>    <li><strong>Test:</strong> Slice a tomato or paper. It should glide.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout warn">Don't skip the honing—it keeps the edge true between sharpenings.</div></div>	-
44.20.1	dashboard_timeline/Leather Boot Care Timeline	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Leather Boot Care Timeline</h1>  <ul class="timeline">    <li><span class="when">After each wear</span><p>Brush off dirt and let them air dry. Use cedar shoe trees.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Monthly</span><p>Wipe with a damp cloth and apply conditioner.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Seasonally</span><p>Deep clean with saddle soap, then condition and polish.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">As needed</span><p>Waterproof with wax or spray before wet weather.</p></li>  </ul>  <div class="callout info">Rotate your boots to let them dry completely between wears.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Leather Boot Care Timeline</h1>  <ul class="timeline">    <li><span class="when">After each wear</span><p>Brush off dirt and let them air dry. Use cedar shoe trees.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Monthly</span><p>Wipe with a damp cloth and apply conditioner.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Seasonally</span><p>Deep clean with saddle soap, then condition and polish.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">As needed</span><p>Waterproof with wax or spray before wet weather.</p></li>  </ul>  <div class="callout info">Rotate your boots to let them dry completely between wears.</div></div>	-
44.21.1	dashboard_timeline/Sewing a Button Timeline	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Sewing a Button Timeline</h1>  <p class="muted">Lost a button? Here's how to put it back.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Thread:</strong> Cut 18" of thread, knot the end, thread the needle.</li>    <li><strong>Anchor:</strong> Push needle from back to front where the button goes.</li>    <li><strong>Position:</strong> Place button on needle, then push needle back down through another hole.</li>    <li><strong>Repeat:</strong> Go through each pair of holes 3–4 times.</li>    <li><strong>Shank:</strong> Before final pass, wrap thread around the stitches under the button to create a shank.</li>    <li><strong>Secure:</strong> Knot on the back and trim.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout good">A shank gives room for thick fabric and keeps the button from pulling tight.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Sewing a Button Timeline</h1>  <p class="muted">Lost a button? Here's how to put it back.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li><strong>Thread:</strong> Cut 18" of thread, knot the end, thread the needle.</li>    <li><strong>Anchor:</strong> Push needle from back to front where the button goes.</li>    <li><strong>Position:</strong> Place button on needle, then push needle back down through another hole.</li>    <li><strong>Repeat:</strong> Go through each pair of holes 3–4 times.</li>    <li><strong>Shank:</strong> Before final pass, wrap thread around the stitches under the button to create a shank.</li>    <li><strong>Secure:</strong> Knot on the back and trim.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout good">A shank gives room for thick fabric and keeps the button from pulling tight.</div></div>	-
44.22.1	dashboard_timeline/Firewood Seasoning Timeline	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Firewood Seasoning Timeline</h1>  <ul class="timeline">    <li><span class="when">Spring</span><p>Split and stack wood in a sunny, breezy spot. Off the ground.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Summer</span><p>Let it bake. Turn the stack if needed.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Fall</span><p>Check moisture with a meter. Aim for under 20%.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Winter</span><p>Ready to burn. Enjoy the fire.</p></li>  </ul>  <div class="callout info">Seasoned wood is lighter, has cracks in the ends, and sounds hollow when knocked.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Firewood Seasoning Timeline</h1>  <ul class="timeline">    <li><span class="when">Spring</span><p>Split and stack wood in a sunny, breezy spot. Off the ground.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Summer</span><p>Let it bake. Turn the stack if needed.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Fall</span><p>Check moisture with a meter. Aim for under 20%.</p></li>    <li><span class="when">Winter</span><p>Ready to burn. Enjoy the fire.</p></li>  </ul>  <div class="callout info">Seasoned wood is lighter, has cracks in the ends, and sounds hollow when knocked.</div></div>	-
44.23.1	dashboard_timeline/Sourdough Starter Schedule Timeline	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><div class="card-title">Sourdough Starter Schedule <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached</span></div><p class="muted t-small">A steady 12-hour rhythm keeps the yeasts happy. Here's the plan for <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 0 · 8:00</span><p>Mix 50g wholemeal flour + 50g water. Scrape down, cover loosely.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 1 · 8:00</span><p>First feed: discard half, add 50g flour + 50g water. Look for tiny bubbles.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 2 · 8:00</span><p>Feed again. It may smell like nail polish — that's normal, keep going.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3 · 20:00</span><p>Second daily feed. Rise should double within 6 hours now.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 5 · 8:00</span><p>Peak test: a spoonful floats in water. Starter is ready to bake.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 7 · 8:00</span><p>Shift to once-a-day feed, store in the fridge between uses.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Troubleshooting</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Liquid on top (hooch)? Pour it off, feed more often.</li><li>No rise after 5 days? Move somewhere warmer, 24–26°C.</li><li>Pink or fuzzy? Bin it and start fresh.</li></ul></div></details></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><div class="card-title">Sourdough Starter Schedule <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached</span></div><p class="muted t-small">A steady 12-hour rhythm keeps the yeasts happy. Here's the plan for .</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 0 · 8:00</span><p>Mix 50g wholemeal flour + 50g water. Scrape down, cover loosely.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 1 · 8:00</span><p>First feed: discard half, add 50g flour + 50g water. Look for tiny bubbles.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 2 · 8:00</span><p>Feed again. It may smell like nail polish — that's normal, keep going.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 3 · 20:00</span><p>Second daily feed. Rise should double within 6 hours now.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 5 · 8:00</span><p>Peak test: a spoonful floats in water. Starter is ready to bake.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 7 · 8:00</span><p>Shift to once-a-day feed, store in the fridge between uses.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Troubleshooting</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Liquid on top (hooch)? Pour it off, feed more often.</li><li>No rise after 5 days? Move somewhere warmer, 24–26°C.</li><li>Pink or fuzzy? Bin it and start fresh.</li></ul></div></details></div></div>	-
44.24.1	dashboard_timeline/First Day of School Timeline	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><div class="card-title">First Day of School <span class="badge info"><i class="dot"></i>cached</span></div><p class="muted t-small">Calm beats chaos, <regex>user_name</regex>. Here's the shape of the morning.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Night before · 19:30</span><p>Pack bag together. Lay out clothes. Charge nothing — screens off.</p></li><li><span class="when">19:45</span><p>Early bedtime. Nerves burn energy; sleep is the fix.</p></li><li><span class="when">07:00</span><p>Wake up, get dressed before breakfast, not after.</p></li><li><span class="when">07:20</span><p>Eat something with protein. Toast and egg beats cereal.</p></li><li><span class="when">07:45</span><p>Shoes, bag, photo by the door. Leave 10 minutes early.</p></li><li><span class="when">08:15</span><p>Drop-off. Short goodbye, big wave, walk away smiling.</p></li><li><span class="when">15:30</span><p>Pick-up. Ask one open question, then let them decompress.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Tip: rehearse the route once on the weekend so the walk feels familiar.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><div class="card-title">First Day of School <span class="badge info"><i class="dot"></i>cached</span></div><p class="muted t-small">Calm beats chaos, . Here's the shape of the morning.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Night before · 19:30</span><p>Pack bag together. Lay out clothes. Charge nothing — screens off.</p></li><li><span class="when">19:45</span><p>Early bedtime. Nerves burn energy; sleep is the fix.</p></li><li><span class="when">07:00</span><p>Wake up, get dressed before breakfast, not after.</p></li><li><span class="when">07:20</span><p>Eat something with protein. Toast and egg beats cereal.</p></li><li><span class="when">07:45</span><p>Shoes, bag, photo by the door. Leave 10 minutes early.</p></li><li><span class="when">08:15</span><p>Drop-off. Short goodbye, big wave, walk away smiling.</p></li><li><span class="when">15:30</span><p>Pick-up. Ask one open question, then let them decompress.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Tip: rehearse the route once on the weekend so the walk feels familiar.</p></div></div></div>	-
44.25.1	dashboard_timeline/Tomato Harvest Timeline	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><div class="card-title">Tomato Harvest <span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>cached</span></div><p class="muted t-small">From flower to sauce pot, <regex>user_name</regex>. Roughly 20 weeks end to end.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Week 0</span><p>Sow seeds indoors, 6mm deep, keep at 20°C. Germination in 7–14 days.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 6</span><p>Pot on when two true leaves appear. Harden off over a week.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 8</span><p>Plant out after last frost. Bury stems deep, water well.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 12</span><p>First flowers. Tap canes daily to help pollination.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 16</span><p>Fruit sets and swells. Feed weekly with tomato fertiliser.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 18</span><p>Colour break: green to orange to red. Pick when fully coloured.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 20</span><p>Glut time. Blanch, freeze, or slow-roast a big tray.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Green tomatoes at season's end?</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Lay them in a single layer in a box with a ripe banana. They'll ripen off the vine in 1–2 weeks.</p></div></details></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><div class="card-title">Tomato Harvest <span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>cached</span></div><p class="muted t-small">From flower to sauce pot, . Roughly 20 weeks end to end.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Week 0</span><p>Sow seeds indoors, 6mm deep, keep at 20°C. Germination in 7–14 days.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 6</span><p>Pot on when two true leaves appear. Harden off over a week.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 8</span><p>Plant out after last frost. Bury stems deep, water well.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 12</span><p>First flowers. Tap canes daily to help pollination.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 16</span><p>Fruit sets and swells. Feed weekly with tomato fertiliser.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 18</span><p>Colour break: green to orange to red. Pick when fully coloured.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 20</span><p>Glut time. Blanch, freeze, or slow-roast a big tray.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Green tomatoes at season's end?</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Lay them in a single layer in a box with a ripe banana. They'll ripen off the vine in 1–2 weeks.</p></div></details></div></div>	-
44.26.1	dashboard_timeline/Job Application Timeline	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><div class="card-title">Job Application Timeline <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached</span></div><p class="muted t-small">Roughly six weeks, with a lot of waiting, <regex>user_name</regex>. Here's what's normal.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 0</span><p>Tailor the CV, write a short cover note. Save the job advert as a PDF.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>Submit. Note the date in a spreadsheet — you'll thank yourself later.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 7–14</span><p>Recruiter screen. Usually a 20-minute call about basics.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 14–21</span><p>First interview. Research the team, prep three STAR stories.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 21–28</span><p>Second round or task. Ask about timeline and next steps.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 30–40</span><p>References and offer. Negotiate, then sign.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 42+</span><p>Silence after two weeks? One polite follow-up, then move on.</p></li></ul><div class="callout warn"><p class="t-small">Ghosting is common. Keep three applications in play at once.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><div class="card-title">Job Application Timeline <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached</span></div><p class="muted t-small">Roughly six weeks, with a lot of waiting, . Here's what's normal.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Day 0</span><p>Tailor the CV, write a short cover note. Save the job advert as a PDF.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 2</span><p>Submit. Note the date in a spreadsheet — you'll thank yourself later.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 7–14</span><p>Recruiter screen. Usually a 20-minute call about basics.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 14–21</span><p>First interview. Research the team, prep three STAR stories.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 21–28</span><p>Second round or task. Ask about timeline and next steps.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 30–40</span><p>References and offer. Negotiate, then sign.</p></li><li><span class="when">Day 42+</span><p>Silence after two weeks? One polite follow-up, then move on.</p></li></ul><div class="callout warn"><p class="t-small">Ghosting is common. Keep three applications in play at once.</p></div></div></div>	-
44.27.1	dashboard_timeline/Coffee Grind Timeline	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><div class="card-title">Coffee Grind Freshness <span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>cached</span></div><p class="muted t-small">Ground coffee fades fast, <regex>user_name</regex>. Here's the clock.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">T-0s</span><p>Grind. Aromatics start escaping the moment the burr stops.</p></li><li><span class="when">T+15 min</span><p>Best window. Brew now for the brightest cup.</p></li><li><span class="when">T+2 hours</span><p>Noticeably flatter. Still drinkable, just less sparkle.</p></li><li><span class="when">T+24 hours</span><p>Stored in an airtight jar in the dark: acceptable, not great.</p></li><li><span class="when">T+1 week</span><p>Cardboard territory. Use it in the moka or a milk drink.</p></li><li><span class="when">T+2 weeks</span><p>Compost it. Seriously, life's short.</p></li></ul><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Whole bean shelf life</span><span class="value">4<span class="unit"> weeks</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Ground shelf life</span><span class="value">2<span class="unit"> weeks</span></span></div></div><div class="callout good"><p class="t-small">Buy a hand grinder. Two minutes of cranking is worth it.</p></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><div class="card-title">Coffee Grind Freshness <span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>cached</span></div><p class="muted t-small">Ground coffee fades fast, . Here's the clock.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">T-0s</span><p>Grind. Aromatics start escaping the moment the burr stops.</p></li><li><span class="when">T+15 min</span><p>Best window. Brew now for the brightest cup.</p></li><li><span class="when">T+2 hours</span><p>Noticeably flatter. Still drinkable, just less sparkle.</p></li><li><span class="when">T+24 hours</span><p>Stored in an airtight jar in the dark: acceptable, not great.</p></li><li><span class="when">T+1 week</span><p>Cardboard territory. Use it in the moka or a milk drink.</p></li><li><span class="when">T+2 weeks</span><p>Compost it. Seriously, life's short.</p></li></ul><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Whole bean shelf life</span><span class="value">4<span class="unit"> weeks</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Ground shelf life</span><span class="value">2<span class="unit"> weeks</span></span></div></div><div class="callout good"><p class="t-small">Buy a hand grinder. Two minutes of cranking is worth it.</p></div></div></div>	-
44.28.1	dashboard_timeline/House Move Timeline	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><div class="card-title">House Move Countdown <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached</span></div><p class="muted t-small">Eight weeks out is the sweet spot, <regex>user_name</regex>. Tick them off.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">8 weeks</span><p>Confirm dates, book a removals firm, start the declutter pile.</p></li><li><span class="when">6 weeks</span><p>Get quotes, buy boxes, notify schools and GP if you're moving far.</p></li><li><span class="when">4 weeks</span><p>Start packing rarely used rooms. Label every box by room.</p></li><li><span class="when">2 weeks</span><p>Redirect post, sort utilities, back up important documents.</p></li><li><span class="when">3 days</span><p>Pack an essentials box: kettle, mugs, bedding, chargers, snacks.</p></li><li><span class="when">Move day</span><p>Do a final meter reading. Take photos of the empty rooms.</p></li><li><span class="when">+1 week</span><p>Register with the council, find the fuse box, meet a neighbour.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Packing hacks that actually work</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Fill gaps in boxes with towels and socks.</li><li>Screw a plate of hardware to each flat-pack — no mystery screws.</li><li>Photograph the back of the telly before unplugging anything.</li></ul></div></details></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="card"><div class="card-title">House Move Countdown <span class="badge accent"><i class="dot"></i>cached</span></div><p class="muted t-small">Eight weeks out is the sweet spot, . Tick them off.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">8 weeks</span><p>Confirm dates, book a removals firm, start the declutter pile.</p></li><li><span class="when">6 weeks</span><p>Get quotes, buy boxes, notify schools and GP if you're moving far.</p></li><li><span class="when">4 weeks</span><p>Start packing rarely used rooms. Label every box by room.</p></li><li><span class="when">2 weeks</span><p>Redirect post, sort utilities, back up important documents.</p></li><li><span class="when">3 days</span><p>Pack an essentials box: kettle, mugs, bedding, chargers, snacks.</p></li><li><span class="when">Move day</span><p>Do a final meter reading. Take photos of the empty rooms.</p></li><li><span class="when">+1 week</span><p>Register with the council, find the fuse box, meet a neighbour.</p></li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Packing hacks that actually work</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Fill gaps in boxes with towels and socks.</li><li>Screw a plate of hardware to each flat-pack — no mystery screws.</li><li>Photograph the back of the telly before unplugging anything.</li></ul></div></details></div></div>	-
44.29.1	dashboard_timeline/Pizza Dough Timeline	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Pizza Dough Timeline</h1><p class="muted">Hi <regex>user_name</regex>. Cold ferment does the heavy lifting. You just wait, then bake.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">T-24h</span><p>Mix flour, water, salt, yeast. Rest 20 min for autolyse.</p></li><li><span class="when">T-23h</span><p>Knead 8 min until smooth. Cover and leave 1h at room temp.</p></li><li><span class="when">T-22h</span><p>Ball it up. Fridge, 24 to 72 hours. Flavor grows with time.</p></li><li><span class="when">T-2h</span><p>Pull dough out. Let it warm up, covered.</p></li><li><span class="when">T-30m</span><p>Preheat oven 260°C with steel or stone inside.</p></li><li><span class="when">T-0</span><p>Stretch, top light, bake 6-8 min until spotted.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info">Wet dough sticks. Flour your hands, not the bench.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Pizza Dough Timeline</h1><p class="muted">Hi . Cold ferment does the heavy lifting. You just wait, then bake.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">T-24h</span><p>Mix flour, water, salt, yeast. Rest 20 min for autolyse.</p></li><li><span class="when">T-23h</span><p>Knead 8 min until smooth. Cover and leave 1h at room temp.</p></li><li><span class="when">T-22h</span><p>Ball it up. Fridge, 24 to 72 hours. Flavor grows with time.</p></li><li><span class="when">T-2h</span><p>Pull dough out. Let it warm up, covered.</p></li><li><span class="when">T-30m</span><p>Preheat oven 260°C with steel or stone inside.</p></li><li><span class="when">T-0</span><p>Stretch, top light, bake 6-8 min until spotted.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info">Wet dough sticks. Flour your hands, not the bench.</div></div>	-
44.30.1	dashboard_timeline/Oil Change Timeline	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Oil Change Timeline</h1><p class="muted">Hey <regex>user_name</regex>. Warm engine, safe setup, fresh oil. Twenty minutes if nothing fights you.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">0:00</span><p>Run engine 2 min to warm oil. Park level, engine off.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:05</span><p>Jack up, set stands, chock wheels. Never work under a jack alone.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:08</span><p>Drain plug out, oil pan under. Let it run dry.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:12</span><p>Swap filter. Smear new gasket with fresh oil.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:15</span><p>Plug back, torque snug. Not gorilla tight.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:18</span><p>Fill oil, check dipstick, run, check for drips.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:20</span><p>Recycle old oil at a parts store. Done.</p></li></ul><div class="callout warn">Hot oil burns. Gloves and eye cover.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Oil Change Timeline</h1><p class="muted">Hey . Warm engine, safe setup, fresh oil. Twenty minutes if nothing fights you.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">0:00</span><p>Run engine 2 min to warm oil. Park level, engine off.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:05</span><p>Jack up, set stands, chock wheels. Never work under a jack alone.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:08</span><p>Drain plug out, oil pan under. Let it run dry.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:12</span><p>Swap filter. Smear new gasket with fresh oil.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:15</span><p>Plug back, torque snug. Not gorilla tight.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:18</span><p>Fill oil, check dipstick, run, check for drips.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:20</span><p>Recycle old oil at a parts store. Done.</p></li></ul><div class="callout warn">Hot oil burns. Gloves and eye cover.</div></div>	-
44.31.1	dashboard_timeline/Chicken Stock Timeline	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Chicken Stock Timeline</h1><p class="muted">Hi <regex>user_name</regex>. Bones, water, patience. Skim early, season late.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">0:00</span><p>Bones in pot, cold water to cover. Bring to bare simmer.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:15</span><p>Skim the grey foam. This keeps it clear.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:30</span><p>Add onion, carrot, celery, bay. No salt yet.</p></li><li><span class="when">2:00</span><p>Keep it barely bubbling. Bubbles break clarity.</p></li><li><span class="when">4:00</span><p>Strain through a fine sieve. Discard solids.</p></li><li><span class="when">4:15</span><p>Chill fast, skim fat, salt to taste.</p></li></ul><div class="callout good">Keeps 5 days fridge, 3 months frozen.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Chicken Stock Timeline</h1><p class="muted">Hi . Bones, water, patience. Skim early, season late.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">0:00</span><p>Bones in pot, cold water to cover. Bring to bare simmer.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:15</span><p>Skim the grey foam. This keeps it clear.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:30</span><p>Add onion, carrot, celery, bay. No salt yet.</p></li><li><span class="when">2:00</span><p>Keep it barely bubbling. Bubbles break clarity.</p></li><li><span class="when">4:00</span><p>Strain through a fine sieve. Discard solids.</p></li><li><span class="when">4:15</span><p>Chill fast, skim fat, salt to taste.</p></li></ul><div class="callout good">Keeps 5 days fridge, 3 months frozen.</div></div>	-
44.32.1	dashboard_timeline/Passport Renewal Timeline	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Passport Renewal Timeline</h1><p class="muted">Hey <regex>user_name</regex>. Photo first, form second, mail last. Start early, breathe later.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Week 0</span><p>Check expiry. Many countries want 6 months left.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 0</span><p>Get a photo. Plain background, no glasses, neutral face.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Fill the form. Name exactly as on old passport.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Pay fee, keep the receipt copy.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>Mail tracked, or book an appointment.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 6-10</span><p>Wait. Track it. Don't book flights yet.</p></li></ul><div class="callout warn">Sign the form only when told. Some offices want it blank.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Passport Renewal Timeline</h1><p class="muted">Hey . Photo first, form second, mail last. Start early, breathe later.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">Week 0</span><p>Check expiry. Many countries want 6 months left.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 0</span><p>Get a photo. Plain background, no glasses, neutral face.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Fill the form. Name exactly as on old passport.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 1</span><p>Pay fee, keep the receipt copy.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 2</span><p>Mail tracked, or book an appointment.</p></li><li><span class="when">Week 6-10</span><p>Wait. Track it. Don't book flights yet.</p></li></ul><div class="callout warn">Sign the form only when told. Some offices want it blank.</div></div>	-
44.33.1	dashboard_timeline/Bike Commute Timeline	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Bike Commute Timeline</h1><p class="muted">Morning, <regex>user_name</regex>. Five minutes of prep beats ten of panic.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">0:00</span><p>Tires squeezed. Brakes tested. Chain looks oily.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:02</span><p>Lights on, front and back. Bag strapped tight.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:04</span><p>Helmet, then gloves. Last look in the mirror.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:05</span><p>Roll out. Take the quiet street first.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:20</span><p>Arrive. Lock frame and wheel to the rack.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:22</span><p>Lights off, sweat dabbed, jacket smoothed.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info">Chain squeak? A drop of lube tonight.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Bike Commute Timeline</h1><p class="muted">Morning, . Five minutes of prep beats ten of panic.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">0:00</span><p>Tires squeezed. Brakes tested. Chain looks oily.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:02</span><p>Lights on, front and back. Bag strapped tight.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:04</span><p>Helmet, then gloves. Last look in the mirror.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:05</span><p>Roll out. Take the quiet street first.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:20</span><p>Arrive. Lock frame and wheel to the rack.</p></li><li><span class="when">0:22</span><p>Lights off, sweat dabbed, jacket smoothed.</p></li></ul><div class="callout info">Chain squeak? A drop of lube tonight.</div></div>	-
44.34.1	dashboard_timeline/Turkey Roast Timeline	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Turkey Roast Timeline</h1><p class="muted">Hi <regex>user_name</regex>. Thaw, dry, roast, rest. The rest is not optional.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">T-3 days</span><p>Move turkey to fridge to thaw. On a tray, bottom shelf.</p></li><li><span class="when">T-12h</span><p>Dry brine: salt all over, uncovered, back in fridge.</p></li><li><span class="when">T-1h</span><p>Out of fridge. Pat dry. Oven to 220°C.</p></li><li><span class="when">T-45m</span><p>Into the oven, breast up. Drop to 180°C.</p></li><li><span class="when">T-0</span><p>Breast hits 74°C. Out. Tent with foil.</p></li><li><span class="when">+30m</span><p>Rest. Then carve. Juices stay put.</p></li></ul><div class="callout good">Thermometer beats guessing, every time.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Turkey Roast Timeline</h1><p class="muted">Hi . Thaw, dry, roast, rest. The rest is not optional.</p><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">T-3 days</span><p>Move turkey to fridge to thaw. On a tray, bottom shelf.</p></li><li><span class="when">T-12h</span><p>Dry brine: salt all over, uncovered, back in fridge.</p></li><li><span class="when">T-1h</span><p>Out of fridge. Pat dry. Oven to 220°C.</p></li><li><span class="when">T-45m</span><p>Into the oven, breast up. Drop to 180°C.</p></li><li><span class="when">T-0</span><p>Breast hits 74°C. Out. Tent with foil.</p></li><li><span class="when">+30m</span><p>Rest. Then carve. Juices stay put.</p></li></ul><div class="callout good">Thermometer beats guessing, every time.</div></div>	-
44.35.1	dashboard_timeline/Jazz Gig Load-In Timeline	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Jazz Gig Load-In Timeline</h1><p class="muted">Hey <regex>user_name</regex> — soundcheck is the whole gig in miniature. Tap a row for the why.</p><div class="card"><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">4:30 PM</span><p>Arrive, park the van close, say hi to the door guy</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Doors and loading bays get contested. Being early buys goodwill and a level spot.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">4:45</span><p>Load in: drums first, then amps, then smalls</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Heaviest and bulkiest items set the layout. Small gear gets stacked in corners last.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">5:15</span><p>Stage plot: kit upstage center, amps angled, monitors where you can hear them</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Angled amps bleed less into vocal mics, so the front-of-house mix starts cleaner.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">5:45</span><p>Line check with the sound engineer, one mic at a time</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Isolating each channel catches a bad cable now, not mid-solo.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">6:15</span><p>Soundcheck a full tune, then a quiet one</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>The quiet tune reveals feedback and monitor balance the loud one hides.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">6:45</span><p>Break: water, snack, wash hands, retune</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Strings drift while they warm up. A quick retune beats a mid-set scramble.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">7:00</span><p>Doors. Set lists taped down, spare strings within reach</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Anything you might need mid-set should be grabbable without leaving the stage.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">7:30</span><p>Downbeat — first tune short and swinging</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>An easy opener settles nerves and lets the room settle in with you.</p></div></details></li></ul></div><div class="callout info"><p>Standing rule: if the room is loud, play quieter than you want. It reads better from the back.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Jazz Gig Load-In Timeline</h1><p class="muted">Hey  — soundcheck is the whole gig in miniature. Tap a row for the why.</p><div class="card"><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">4:30 PM</span><p>Arrive, park the van close, say hi to the door guy</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Doors and loading bays get contested. Being early buys goodwill and a level spot.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">4:45</span><p>Load in: drums first, then amps, then smalls</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Heaviest and bulkiest items set the layout. Small gear gets stacked in corners last.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">5:15</span><p>Stage plot: kit upstage center, amps angled, monitors where you can hear them</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Angled amps bleed less into vocal mics, so the front-of-house mix starts cleaner.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">5:45</span><p>Line check with the sound engineer, one mic at a time</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Isolating each channel catches a bad cable now, not mid-solo.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">6:15</span><p>Soundcheck a full tune, then a quiet one</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>The quiet tune reveals feedback and monitor balance the loud one hides.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">6:45</span><p>Break: water, snack, wash hands, retune</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Strings drift while they warm up. A quick retune beats a mid-set scramble.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">7:00</span><p>Doors. Set lists taped down, spare strings within reach</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Anything you might need mid-set should be grabbable without leaving the stage.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">7:30</span><p>Downbeat — first tune short and swinging</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>An easy opener settles nerves and lets the room settle in with you.</p></div></details></li></ul></div><div class="callout info"><p>Standing rule: if the room is loud, play quieter than you want. It reads better from the back.</p></div></div>	-
44.36.1	dashboard_timeline/Weekly Grocery Run Timeline	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Weekly Grocery Run Timeline</h1><p class="muted">Hi <regex>user_name</regex> — Saturday morning, one store, about 90 minutes door to door.</p><div class="card"><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">8:00 AM</span><p>Check the fridge, freezer, and pantry for what's actually gone</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Buying a second jar of something you already have is the most common waste. Two minutes of looking fixes it.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">8:15</span><p>Build the list by aisle, not by recipe</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Aisle order matches the store layout, so you walk each section once instead of three times.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">8:30</span><p>Plan two dinners that use overlapping ingredients</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Half a bunch of cilantro for one meal and half for another means none gets thrown out.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">8:45</span><p>Eat something small before leaving</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Shopping hungry adds about a third to the bill, mostly in snack form.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">9:00</span><p>Produce first, then dry goods, dairy, meat, frozen last</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Cold items spend the least time out of the case this way.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">9:40</span><p>At checkout: bag cold items together</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>One cold bag insulates itself. It also makes unpacking at home faster.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">10:00</span><p>Home: fridge items away first, then pantry</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Cold chain matters more than shelf order. Milk before cereal.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">10:15</span><p>Wash and cut what you'll cook this week</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Prepped vegetables get eaten. Whole ones get forgotten in the drawer.</p></div></details></li></ul></div><div class="callout info"><p>Keep a running list on the fridge. By Friday it writes itself.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Weekly Grocery Run Timeline</h1><p class="muted">Hi  — Saturday morning, one store, about 90 minutes door to door.</p><div class="card"><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">8:00 AM</span><p>Check the fridge, freezer, and pantry for what's actually gone</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Buying a second jar of something you already have is the most common waste. Two minutes of looking fixes it.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">8:15</span><p>Build the list by aisle, not by recipe</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Aisle order matches the store layout, so you walk each section once instead of three times.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">8:30</span><p>Plan two dinners that use overlapping ingredients</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Half a bunch of cilantro for one meal and half for another means none gets thrown out.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">8:45</span><p>Eat something small before leaving</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Shopping hungry adds about a third to the bill, mostly in snack form.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">9:00</span><p>Produce first, then dry goods, dairy, meat, frozen last</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Cold items spend the least time out of the case this way.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">9:40</span><p>At checkout: bag cold items together</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>One cold bag insulates itself. It also makes unpacking at home faster.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">10:00</span><p>Home: fridge items away first, then pantry</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Cold chain matters more than shelf order. Milk before cereal.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">10:15</span><p>Wash and cut what you'll cook this week</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Prepped vegetables get eaten. Whole ones get forgotten in the drawer.</p></div></details></li></ul></div><div class="callout info"><p>Keep a running list on the fridge. By Friday it writes itself.</p></div></div>	-
44.37.1	dashboard_timeline/Haircut Appointment Timeline	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Haircut Appointment Timeline</h1><p class="muted">Hey <regex>user_name</regex> — one hour, start to finish, including the awkward small talk.</p><div class="card"><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">2 weeks before</span><p>Book the slot — Tuesday or Wednesday afternoon if you can</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Weekend slots book out first and run late. Midweek shops are calmer and often quieter.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">Day before</span><p>Wash your hair, skip the heavy product</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Stylists can read clean, product-free hair. A waxy buildup hides your actual texture.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">Morning of</span><p>Bring one photo and one sentence</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Photos carry more than words. One sentence — length, shape, or "same but shorter" — fills in the rest.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">Arrive</span><p>Five minutes early, coat off, phone away</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Being ready when the chair opens keeps the whole shop on schedule.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">First 10 min</span><p>Consultation: say what you hated about the last cut</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Stylists learn more from what went wrong than from what you want. Be specific and kind.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">Next 30 min</span><p>The cut. If it's going wrong, say so early</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>A trim is easy to correct. Waiting until the end means losing length you wanted to keep.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">Last 10 min</span><p>Style and dry. Watch how they do it</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>The product and motion they use at home is the difference between the mirror and tomorrow morning.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">Before you pay</span><p>Ask about rebooking, tip, book it</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>You're already here and they know your hair now. Rebooking takes ten seconds and saves a phone call later.</p></div></details></li></ul></div><div class="callout info"><p>If you liked the cut, ask for the same stylist by name next time. Consistency beats luck.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Haircut Appointment Timeline</h1><p class="muted">Hey  — one hour, start to finish, including the awkward small talk.</p><div class="card"><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">2 weeks before</span><p>Book the slot — Tuesday or Wednesday afternoon if you can</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Weekend slots book out first and run late. Midweek shops are calmer and often quieter.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">Day before</span><p>Wash your hair, skip the heavy product</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Stylists can read clean, product-free hair. A waxy buildup hides your actual texture.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">Morning of</span><p>Bring one photo and one sentence</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Photos carry more than words. One sentence — length, shape, or "same but shorter" — fills in the rest.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">Arrive</span><p>Five minutes early, coat off, phone away</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Being ready when the chair opens keeps the whole shop on schedule.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">First 10 min</span><p>Consultation: say what you hated about the last cut</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Stylists learn more from what went wrong than from what you want. Be specific and kind.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">Next 30 min</span><p>The cut. If it's going wrong, say so early</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>A trim is easy to correct. Waiting until the end means losing length you wanted to keep.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">Last 10 min</span><p>Style and dry. Watch how they do it</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>The product and motion they use at home is the difference between the mirror and tomorrow morning.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">Before you pay</span><p>Ask about rebooking, tip, book it</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>You're already here and they know your hair now. Rebooking takes ten seconds and saves a phone call later.</p></div></details></li></ul></div><div class="callout info"><p>If you liked the cut, ask for the same stylist by name next time. Consistency beats luck.</p></div></div>	-
44.38.1	dashboard_timeline/Board Game Night Timeline	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Board Game Night Timeline</h1><p class="muted">Hey <regex>user_name</regex> — four players, one medium-weight game, about three hours.</p><div class="card"><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">2 days out</span><p>Pick the game and send a how-to-play video link</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Watching a ten-minute video beforehand turns a 30-minute teach into a five-minute refresher.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">Day of, 6:00 PM</span><p>Clear the table, good lighting, chairs that don't wobble</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Nobody can reach the far side of a cluttered table, and dim light means misread cards.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">6:30</span><p>Snacks that don't grease fingers; drinks on coasters off the table</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Chips and cards do not mix. Napkins and a side table solve most of it.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">7:00</span><p>People arrive. Ten minutes of catching up, then sit</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Letting the chat happen before the rules means fewer interruptions once you start.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">7:10</span><p>Teach in this order: goal, turn structure, then the one tricky rule</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>People remember the goal and the flow. Edge cases only stick once they've played a turn.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">7:30</span><p>Play a practice round open-handed, no hidden info</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>One open round answers more questions than ten minutes of explaining.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">7:45</span><p>Real game. Deal, then let it run</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Rules questions during play get answered in one sentence. Resist the urge to re-teach.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">9:30</span><p>Last round called. Winner announced, table cleared</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>A stated ending keeps the night fun instead of grinding to a halt.</p></div></details></li></ul></div><div class="callout good"><p>House rule: whoever teaches the game goes last on the first turn. It's the small tax for talking so much.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Board Game Night Timeline</h1><p class="muted">Hey  — four players, one medium-weight game, about three hours.</p><div class="card"><ul class="timeline"><li><span class="when">2 days out</span><p>Pick the game and send a how-to-play video link</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Watching a ten-minute video beforehand turns a 30-minute teach into a five-minute refresher.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">Day of, 6:00 PM</span><p>Clear the table, good lighting, chairs that don't wobble</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Nobody can reach the far side of a cluttered table, and dim light means misread cards.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">6:30</span><p>Snacks that don't grease fingers; drinks on coasters off the table</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Chips and cards do not mix. Napkins and a side table solve most of it.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">7:00</span><p>People arrive. Ten minutes of catching up, then sit</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Letting the chat happen before the rules means fewer interruptions once you start.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">7:10</span><p>Teach in this order: goal, turn structure, then the one tricky rule</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>People remember the goal and the flow. Edge cases only stick once they've played a turn.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">7:30</span><p>Play a practice round open-handed, no hidden info</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>One open round answers more questions than ten minutes of explaining.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">7:45</span><p>Real game. Deal, then let it run</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>Rules questions during play get answered in one sentence. Resist the urge to re-teach.</p></div></details></li><li><span class="when">9:30</span><p>Last round called. Winner announced, table cleared</p><details class="acc"><summary>Why</summary><div class="body"><p>A stated ending keeps the night fun instead of grinding to a halt.</p></div></details></li></ul></div><div class="callout good"><p>House rule: whoever teaches the game goes last on the first turn. It's the small tax for talking so much.</p></div></div>	-
45.0.0	dashboard_formula/Pancake Perfection Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Pancake Perfection</h1><p>Here's the formula for a stack that flips without drama, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><pre class="code"><code>fluff = (1 cup flour) : (1 cup milk) : (1 egg) : (2 tsp baking powder)</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Whisk the dry ingredients.</li><li>Whisk the wet ingredients separately.</li><li>Combine with a few gentle strokes—lumps are fine.</li><li>Cook on medium heat until bubbles pop and stay open.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>Rest the batter for 5 minutes while the pan heats.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Pancake Perfection</h1><p>Here's the formula for a stack that flips without drama, .</p><pre class="code"><code>fluff = (1 cup flour) : (1 cup milk) : (1 egg) : (2 tsp baking powder)</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Whisk the dry ingredients.</li><li>Whisk the wet ingredients separately.</li><li>Combine with a few gentle strokes—lumps are fine.</li><li>Cook on medium heat until bubbles pop and stay open.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>Rest the batter for 5 minutes while the pan heats.</p></div></div>	-
45.1.0	dashboard_formula/Savings Goal Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Savings Goal</h1><p>To hit your target, <regex>user_name</regex>, use this formula.</p><div class="card"><div class="card-title">Formula</div><pre class="code"><code>monthly = (goal - current) / months</code></pre></div><ol class="list"><li>Pick your goal amount.</li><li>Subtract what you've already saved.</li><li>Divide by the number of months left.</li><li>Round up to the nearest $10 for a buffer.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p>Automate the transfer on payday and forget it.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Savings Goal</h1><p>To hit your target, , use this formula.</p><div class="card"><div class="card-title">Formula</div><pre class="code"><code>monthly = (goal - current) / months</code></pre></div><ol class="list"><li>Pick your goal amount.</li><li>Subtract what you've already saved.</li><li>Divide by the number of months left.</li><li>Round up to the nearest $10 for a buffer.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p>Automate the transfer on payday and forget it.</p></div></div>	-
45.2.0	dashboard_formula/Coffee Ratio Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Coffee Ratio</h1><p>For a balanced cup, <regex>user_name</regex>, start here.</p><pre class="code"><code>ratio = 1 g coffee : 16 g water</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Weigh your coffee beans.</li><li>Multiply by 16 for the water weight.</li><li>Heat water to 200°F.</li><li>Brew and adjust to taste.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p>Too bitter? Grind coarser or shorten the brew time.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Coffee Ratio</h1><p>For a balanced cup, , start here.</p><pre class="code"><code>ratio = 1 g coffee : 16 g water</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Weigh your coffee beans.</li><li>Multiply by 16 for the water weight.</li><li>Heat water to 200°F.</li><li>Brew and adjust to taste.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p>Too bitter? Grind coarser or shorten the brew time.</p></div></div>	-
45.3.0	dashboard_formula/BMI Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>BMI Formula</h1><p>Here's the math, <regex>user_name</regex>. Just a number, not a verdict.</p><pre class="code"><code>BMI = (weight in kg) / (height in m)²</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Convert pounds to kilograms (divide by 2.205).</li><li>Convert inches to meters (multiply by 0.0254).</li><li>Square your height in meters.</li><li>Divide weight by that squared height.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>BMI doesn't measure muscle, bone, or how great you are.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>BMI Formula</h1><p>Here's the math, . Just a number, not a verdict.</p><pre class="code"><code>BMI = (weight in kg) / (height in m)²</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Convert pounds to kilograms (divide by 2.205).</li><li>Convert inches to meters (multiply by 0.0254).</li><li>Square your height in meters.</li><li>Divide weight by that squared height.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>BMI doesn't measure muscle, bone, or how great you are.</p></div></div>	-
45.4.0	dashboard_formula/Reading Time Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Reading Time</h1><p>To estimate how long a book will take, <regex>user_name</regex>:</p><pre class="code"><code>hours = total_words / 300 / 60</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Find the word count (or estimate pages × 250).</li><li>Divide by 300 (average words per minute).</li><li>Divide by 60 to get hours.</li><li>Add breaks for tea and staring out the window.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p>That novel is about 6 hours of pure escape.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Reading Time</h1><p>To estimate how long a book will take, :</p><pre class="code"><code>hours = total_words / 300 / 60</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Find the word count (or estimate pages × 250).</li><li>Divide by 300 (average words per minute).</li><li>Divide by 60 to get hours.</li><li>Add breaks for tea and staring out the window.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p>That novel is about 6 hours of pure escape.</p></div></div>	-
45.5.0	dashboard_formula/Pizza Dough Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Pizza Dough</h1><p>For a crust with a good chew, <regex>user_name</regex>, use baker's percentages.</p><pre class="code"><code>flour 100% | water 65% | salt 2% | yeast 0.5%</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Weigh your flour (say 500g).</li><li>Add water at 65% (325g).</li><li>Add salt (10g) and yeast (2.5g).</li><li>Knead, then let it rise slowly in the fridge.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>A cold ferment for 24 hours deepens the flavor.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Pizza Dough</h1><p>For a crust with a good chew, , use baker's percentages.</p><pre class="code"><code>flour 100% | water 65% | salt 2% | yeast 0.5%</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Weigh your flour (say 500g).</li><li>Add water at 65% (325g).</li><li>Add salt (10g) and yeast (2.5g).</li><li>Knead, then let it rise slowly in the fridge.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>A cold ferment for 24 hours deepens the flavor.</p></div></div>	-
45.6.0	dashboard_formula/Tip Split Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Tip Split Formula</h1><pre class="code"><code>per_person = (bill * (1 + tip_rate)) / people</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Choose tip rate (e.g. 0.20 for 20%).</li><li>Multiply bill by 1 + tip_rate to get total.</li><li>Divide total by number of people.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>Double-check for auto-gratuity on parties of 6+.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Tip Split Formula</h1><pre class="code"><code>per_person = (bill * (1 + tip_rate)) / people</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Choose tip rate (e.g. 0.20 for 20%).</li><li>Multiply bill by 1 + tip_rate to get total.</li><li>Divide total by number of people.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>Double-check for auto-gratuity on parties of 6+.</p></div></div>	-
45.7.0	dashboard_formula/Paint Coverage Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Paint Coverage Formula</h1><pre class="code"><code>gallons = (wall_area * coats) / coverage_per_gallon</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Measure wall area: (height * width) minus windows/doors.</li><li>Decide number of coats (usually 2).</li><li>Divide by coverage per gallon (check the can, often 350 sq ft).</li></ol><p class="muted">Round up to the nearest gallon.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Paint Coverage Formula</h1><pre class="code"><code>gallons = (wall_area * coats) / coverage_per_gallon</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Measure wall area: (height * width) minus windows/doors.</li><li>Decide number of coats (usually 2).</li><li>Divide by coverage per gallon (check the can, often 350 sq ft).</li></ol><p class="muted">Round up to the nearest gallon.</p></div>	-
45.8.0	dashboard_formula/Sleep Cycle Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Sleep Cycle Formula</h1><pre class="code"><code>bedtime = wake_time - (cycles * 90 minutes) - 15 minutes</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Pick number of cycles: 5 or 6 (7.5 or 9 hours).</li><li>Subtract cycles * 90 minutes from your wake time.</li><li>Subtract 15 minutes to fall asleep.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p>Works best with consistent wake times.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Sleep Cycle Formula</h1><pre class="code"><code>bedtime = wake_time - (cycles * 90 minutes) - 15 minutes</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Pick number of cycles: 5 or 6 (7.5 or 9 hours).</li><li>Subtract cycles * 90 minutes from your wake time.</li><li>Subtract 15 minutes to fall asleep.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p>Works best with consistent wake times.</p></div></div>	-
45.9.0	dashboard_formula/Marinade Ratio Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Marinade Ratio Formula</h1><pre class="code"><code>marinade = meat_weight * 0.5 cups per pound</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Weigh your meat in pounds.</li><li>Multiply by 0.5 cups for total marinade.</li><li>Mix: 3 parts oil, 1 part acid, seasonings to taste.</li></ol><p class="muted">Marinate 30 min for fish, 2–24 hours for chicken/beef.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Marinade Ratio Formula</h1><pre class="code"><code>marinade = meat_weight * 0.5 cups per pound</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Weigh your meat in pounds.</li><li>Multiply by 0.5 cups for total marinade.</li><li>Mix: 3 parts oil, 1 part acid, seasonings to taste.</li></ol><p class="muted">Marinate 30 min for fish, 2–24 hours for chicken/beef.</p></div>	-
45.10.0	dashboard_formula/Screen Time Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Screen Time Formula</h1><pre class="code"><code>ratio = screen_hours / waking_hours</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Log total screen hours (phone, TV, computer).</li><li>Calculate waking hours (24 - sleep hours).</li><li>Divide screen by waking for your ratio.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p>Aim for under 0.25 (about 4 hours if you're awake 16).</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Screen Time Formula</h1><pre class="code"><code>ratio = screen_hours / waking_hours</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Log total screen hours (phone, TV, computer).</li><li>Calculate waking hours (24 - sleep hours).</li><li>Divide screen by waking for your ratio.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p>Aim for under 0.25 (about 4 hours if you're awake 16).</p></div></div>	-
45.11.0	dashboard_formula/Hydration Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Hydration Formula</h1><pre class="code"><code>ounces = body_weight_lbs * 0.5</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Weigh yourself in pounds.</li><li>Multiply by 0.5 for baseline ounces.</li><li>Add 8 oz per 15 min of exercise.</li></ol><p class="muted">Adjust for heat and caffeine.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Hydration Formula</h1><pre class="code"><code>ounces = body_weight_lbs * 0.5</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Weigh yourself in pounds.</li><li>Multiply by 0.5 for baseline ounces.</li><li>Add 8 oz per 15 min of exercise.</li></ol><p class="muted">Adjust for heat and caffeine.</p></div>	-
45.12.0	dashboard_formula/Sourdough Starter Ratio Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Sourdough Starter Ratio Formula</h1><p class="muted">Keep your starter bubbly and predictable. <regex>user_name</regex>, here’s the math.</p><pre class="code"><code>flour = starter_weight × ratiowater = flour</code></pre><div class="callout info"><p>Typical ratio: 1:1:1 (starter:flour:water) for daily feeds; 1:5:5 for less frequent.</p></div><ol class="list"><li>Weigh your starter. For 100g starter, 1:1:1 means 100g flour + 100g water.</li><li>Mix until no dry flour remains. It’ll be thick like muffin batter.</li><li>Cover and let rise until doubled, 4–8 hours at room temp.</li></ol><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Example</span><span class="value">100g <span class="unit">starter</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Flour</span><span class="value">100g</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Water</span><span class="value">100g</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Total</span><span class="value">300g</span></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Why ratios matter</summary><div class="body"><p>Higher ratios (more flour/water) slow fermentation, letting you go longer between feeds. Lower ratios speed it up.</p></div></details><p class="dim t-small">Starter looking sluggish? Try a 1:5:5 feed and a warm spot.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Sourdough Starter Ratio Formula</h1><p class="muted">Keep your starter bubbly and predictable. , here’s the math.</p><pre class="code"><code>flour = starter_weight × ratiowater = flour</code></pre><div class="callout info"><p>Typical ratio: 1:1:1 (starter:flour:water) for daily feeds; 1:5:5 for less frequent.</p></div><ol class="list"><li>Weigh your starter. For 100g starter, 1:1:1 means 100g flour + 100g water.</li><li>Mix until no dry flour remains. It’ll be thick like muffin batter.</li><li>Cover and let rise until doubled, 4–8 hours at room temp.</li></ol><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Example</span><span class="value">100g <span class="unit">starter</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Flour</span><span class="value">100g</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Water</span><span class="value">100g</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Total</span><span class="value">300g</span></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Why ratios matter</summary><div class="body"><p>Higher ratios (more flour/water) slow fermentation, letting you go longer between feeds. Lower ratios speed it up.</p></div></details><p class="dim t-small">Starter looking sluggish? Try a 1:5:5 feed and a warm spot.</p></div>	-
45.13.0	dashboard_formula/Gear Inch Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Gear Inch Formula</h1><p class="muted">For my cycling pals: gear inches tell you how far you go per pedal rev. <regex>user_name</regex>, check your setup.</p><pre class="code"><code>gear_inches = (chainring_teeth / cog_teeth) × wheel_diameter_inches</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Count teeth on your front chainring and rear cog.</li><li>Measure wheel diameter (including tire) in inches. Typical 700c is ~27".</li><li>Divide chainring by cog, multiply by wheel diameter.</li></ol><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Example</span><span class="value">50/16 <span class="unit">× 27</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Gear inches</span><span class="value">84.4"</span></div></div><p class="dim t-small">Higher gear inches = harder to pedal, more distance per stroke.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Common setups</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Road climbing: 30–40 gear inches</li><li>Flat cruising: 60–80</li><li>Sprinting: 90+</li></ul></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Gear Inch Formula</h1><p class="muted">For my cycling pals: gear inches tell you how far you go per pedal rev. , check your setup.</p><pre class="code"><code>gear_inches = (chainring_teeth / cog_teeth) × wheel_diameter_inches</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Count teeth on your front chainring and rear cog.</li><li>Measure wheel diameter (including tire) in inches. Typical 700c is ~27".</li><li>Divide chainring by cog, multiply by wheel diameter.</li></ol><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Example</span><span class="value">50/16 <span class="unit">× 27</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Gear inches</span><span class="value">84.4"</span></div></div><p class="dim t-small">Higher gear inches = harder to pedal, more distance per stroke.</p><details class="acc"><summary>Common setups</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Road climbing: 30–40 gear inches</li><li>Flat cruising: 60–80</li><li>Sprinting: 90+</li></ul></div></details></div>	-
45.14.0	dashboard_formula/Coffee Bloom Ratio Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Coffee Bloom Ratio Formula</h1><p class="muted">Your pour-over’s first kiss with water. <regex>user_name</regex>, bloom like a pro.</p><pre class="code"><code>bloom_water = coffee_dose × 2</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Weigh your coffee. For 20g coffee, you’ll use 40g water.</li><li>Heat water to 200°F (93°C).</li><li>Pour just enough to saturate grounds, wait 30–45 seconds. It’ll bubble and sigh.</li><li>Then continue with your normal brew ratio (usually 1:16).</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p>Fresh beans bloom more—that’s CO₂ escaping. Stale beans barely bubble.</p></div><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Dose</span><span class="value">20g</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Bloom water</span><span class="value">40g</span></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Coffee Bloom Ratio Formula</h1><p class="muted">Your pour-over’s first kiss with water. , bloom like a pro.</p><pre class="code"><code>bloom_water = coffee_dose × 2</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Weigh your coffee. For 20g coffee, you’ll use 40g water.</li><li>Heat water to 200°F (93°C).</li><li>Pour just enough to saturate grounds, wait 30–45 seconds. It’ll bubble and sigh.</li><li>Then continue with your normal brew ratio (usually 1:16).</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p>Fresh beans bloom more—that’s CO₂ escaping. Stale beans barely bubble.</p></div><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Dose</span><span class="value">20g</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Bloom water</span><span class="value">40g</span></div></div></div>	-
45.15.0	dashboard_formula/Lawn Watering Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Lawn Watering Formula</h1><p class="muted">Give your grass a drink, not a flood. <regex>user_name</regex>, here’s the number.</p><pre class="code"><code>water_inches_per_week = 1 + (temperature_°F - 70) / 10</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Check your local temp average. At 70°F, baseline is 1 inch per week.</li><li>For every 10°F above 70, add 0.1 inch. At 90°F, that’s 1.2 inches.</li><li>Water deeply 2–3 times a week, not daily. Early morning is best.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p>If it’s below 70°F, 1 inch per week is still fine—don’t underwater.</p></div><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Temp</span><span class="value">85°F</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Water</span><span class="value">1.5 <span class="unit">in</span></span></div></div><p class="dim t-small">Stick a tuna can in the sprinkler zone—when it’s full, you’re done.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Lawn Watering Formula</h1><p class="muted">Give your grass a drink, not a flood. , here’s the number.</p><pre class="code"><code>water_inches_per_week = 1 + (temperature_°F - 70) / 10</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Check your local temp average. At 70°F, baseline is 1 inch per week.</li><li>For every 10°F above 70, add 0.1 inch. At 90°F, that’s 1.2 inches.</li><li>Water deeply 2–3 times a week, not daily. Early morning is best.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p>If it’s below 70°F, 1 inch per week is still fine—don’t underwater.</p></div><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Temp</span><span class="value">85°F</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Water</span><span class="value">1.5 <span class="unit">in</span></span></div></div><p class="dim t-small">Stick a tuna can in the sprinkler zone—when it’s full, you’re done.</p></div>	-
45.16.0	dashboard_formula/Meat Cooking Time Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Meat Cooking Time Formula</h1><p class="muted">No more dry roast. <regex>user_name</regex>, use this and relax.</p><pre class="code"><code>minutes = weight_lbs × minutes_per_lb</code></pre><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Meat</th><th>Minutes per lb</th><th>Oven Temp</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Beef (ribeye roast)</td><td class="num">20</td><td>325°F</td></tr><tr><td>Pork loin</td><td class="num">25</td><td>350°F</td></tr><tr><td>Chicken (whole)</td><td class="num">20</td><td>350°F</td></tr><tr><td>Lamb shoulder</td><td class="num">30</td><td>325°F</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ol class="list"><li>Weigh your meat. A 3 lb chicken: 3 × 20 = 60 minutes.</li><li>Use a meat thermometer to confirm: poultry 165°F, beef medium-rare 135°F.</li><li>Rest 10–20 minutes before carving.</li></ol><p class="dim t-small">Ovens vary. The thermometer is your friend.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Meat Cooking Time Formula</h1><p class="muted">No more dry roast. , use this and relax.</p><pre class="code"><code>minutes = weight_lbs × minutes_per_lb</code></pre><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Meat</th><th>Minutes per lb</th><th>Oven Temp</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Beef (ribeye roast)</td><td class="num">20</td><td>325°F</td></tr><tr><td>Pork loin</td><td class="num">25</td><td>350°F</td></tr><tr><td>Chicken (whole)</td><td class="num">20</td><td>350°F</td></tr><tr><td>Lamb shoulder</td><td class="num">30</td><td>325°F</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ol class="list"><li>Weigh your meat. A 3 lb chicken: 3 × 20 = 60 minutes.</li><li>Use a meat thermometer to confirm: poultry 165°F, beef medium-rare 135°F.</li><li>Rest 10–20 minutes before carving.</li></ol><p class="dim t-small">Ovens vary. The thermometer is your friend.</p></div>	-
45.17.0	dashboard_formula/Garden Seed Spacing Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Garden Seed Spacing Formula</h1><p class="muted">Crowded carrots are sad carrots. <regex>user_name</regex>, space them right.</p><pre class="code"><code>plants_per_sqft = (12 / spacing_inches)²</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Check seed packet for spacing. For 6-inch spacing: 12/6 = 2, so 2×2 = 4 plants per square foot.</li><li>For 3-inch spacing: 12/3 = 4, so 4×4 = 16 plants per square foot.</li><li>Mark your grid and sow accordingly.</li></ol><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Spacing</span><span class="value">6 <span class="unit">in</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Plants/sq ft</span><span class="value">4</span></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Common veggie spacings</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Lettuce: 6 in → 4 per sq ft</li><li>Carrots: 3 in → 16 per sq ft</li><li>Tomatoes: 24 in → 1 per 4 sq ft</li></ul></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Garden Seed Spacing Formula</h1><p class="muted">Crowded carrots are sad carrots. , space them right.</p><pre class="code"><code>plants_per_sqft = (12 / spacing_inches)²</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Check seed packet for spacing. For 6-inch spacing: 12/6 = 2, so 2×2 = 4 plants per square foot.</li><li>For 3-inch spacing: 12/3 = 4, so 4×4 = 16 plants per square foot.</li><li>Mark your grid and sow accordingly.</li></ol><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Spacing</span><span class="value">6 <span class="unit">in</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Plants/sq ft</span><span class="value">4</span></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Common veggie spacings</summary><div class="body"><ul class="list"><li>Lettuce: 6 in → 4 per sq ft</li><li>Carrots: 3 in → 16 per sq ft</li><li>Tomatoes: 24 in → 1 per 4 sq ft</li></ul></div></details></div>	-
45.18.0	dashboard_formula/Caffeine Cutoff Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Caffeine Cutoff Formula</h1><p class="muted">Hey <regex>user_name</regex> — half of your caffeine is still in you after about 5 hours. Do the math before the espresso does it for you.</p><div class="callout info"><strong>Formula</strong><pre class="code"><code>last_sip = bedtime − (5 h × cups_of_buffer)</code></pre></div><div class="grid"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Half-life</span><span class="value">5<span class="unit">h</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Quarter left after</span><span class="value">10<span class="unit">h</span></span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Pick your bedtime — say 11:00 pm.</li><li>Subtract 5 h for one cup, 10 h for a heavy day.</li><li>That's your last legal sip. Weep quietly, drink water.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>What about tea and cola?</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Tea runs ~40 mg a cup, cola ~35 mg. Scale the buffer: lighter drinks buy you about an hour back each.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Caffeine Cutoff Formula</h1><p class="muted">Hey  — half of your caffeine is still in you after about 5 hours. Do the math before the espresso does it for you.</p><div class="callout info"><strong>Formula</strong><pre class="code"><code>last_sip = bedtime − (5 h × cups_of_buffer)</code></pre></div><div class="grid"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Half-life</span><span class="value">5<span class="unit">h</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Quarter left after</span><span class="value">10<span class="unit">h</span></span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Pick your bedtime — say 11:00 pm.</li><li>Subtract 5 h for one cup, 10 h for a heavy day.</li><li>That's your last legal sip. Weep quietly, drink water.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>What about tea and cola?</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Tea runs ~40 mg a cup, cola ~35 mg. Scale the buffer: lighter drinks buy you about an hour back each.</p></div></details></div>	-
45.19.0	dashboard_formula/Flight Arrival Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Flight Arrival Formula</h1><p class="muted">Airport clocks lie, <regex>user_name</regex>. This one doesn't.</p><pre class="code"><code>arrival_local = departure_local + duration − timezone_gain</code></pre><div class="callout good"><strong>Worked example</strong><p class="t-small">Leave 9:00 am, fly 7 h 30 m, gain 3 h east. Land at 1:30 pm local.</p></div><table class="table-wrap"><thead><tr><th>Leg</th><th class="num">Minutes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Departure</td><td class="num">540</td></tr><tr><td>Duration</td><td class="num">+450</td></tr><tr><td>Timezone</td><td class="num">−180</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Arrival</strong></td><td class="num"><strong>810</strong></td></tr></tbody></table><ol class="list"><li>Convert everything to minutes past midnight.</li><li>Add duration, subtract the timezone shift.</li><li>Fold back into 24 h. Flying west? You add instead.</li></ol></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Flight Arrival Formula</h1><p class="muted">Airport clocks lie, . This one doesn't.</p><pre class="code"><code>arrival_local = departure_local + duration − timezone_gain</code></pre><div class="callout good"><strong>Worked example</strong><p class="t-small">Leave 9:00 am, fly 7 h 30 m, gain 3 h east. Land at 1:30 pm local.</p></div><table class="table-wrap"><thead><tr><th>Leg</th><th class="num">Minutes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Departure</td><td class="num">540</td></tr><tr><td>Duration</td><td class="num">+450</td></tr><tr><td>Timezone</td><td class="num">−180</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Arrival</strong></td><td class="num"><strong>810</strong></td></tr></tbody></table><ol class="list"><li>Convert everything to minutes past midnight.</li><li>Add duration, subtract the timezone shift.</li><li>Fold back into 24 h. Flying west? You add instead.</li></ol></div>	-
45.20.0	dashboard_formula/Aquarium Stocking Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Aquarium Stocking Formula</h1><p class="muted">The old inch-per-gallon rule is a starting line, not a finish, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><pre class="code"><code>adult_inches ≤ gallons ÷ 2</code></pre><div class="callout warn"><strong>Honesty label</strong><p class="t-small">This only holds for slim, cold-water fish in a filtered tank. Goldfish and plecos blow straight past it.</p></div><ol class="list"><li>Total your fish at <em>adult</em> size, not pet-store size.</li><li>Halve the tank volume for the inch budget.</li><li>Subtract surface area if you skim on filtration.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Why half, not one?</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Because oxygen, waste, and swimming room all scale with surface, and most tanks are tall and narrow rather than wide. Big footprint beats big litres.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Aquarium Stocking Formula</h1><p class="muted">The old inch-per-gallon rule is a starting line, not a finish, .</p><pre class="code"><code>adult_inches ≤ gallons ÷ 2</code></pre><div class="callout warn"><strong>Honesty label</strong><p class="t-small">This only holds for slim, cold-water fish in a filtered tank. Goldfish and plecos blow straight past it.</p></div><ol class="list"><li>Total your fish at <em>adult</em> size, not pet-store size.</li><li>Halve the tank volume for the inch budget.</li><li>Subtract surface area if you skim on filtration.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Why half, not one?</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Because oxygen, waste, and swimming room all scale with surface, and most tanks are tall and narrow rather than wide. Big footprint beats big litres.</p></div></details></div>	-
45.21.0	dashboard_formula/Study Session Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Study Session Formula</h1><p class="muted">Break the pile into blocks, <regex>user_name</regex>. The pile always looks smaller after that.</p><pre class="code"><code>sessions = workload ÷ (focus_block × 0.8)</code></pre><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Focus block</span><span class="value">25<span class="unit">min</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Real output</span><span class="value">20<span class="unit">min</span></span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Estimate pages or problems — say 300 pages.</li><li>Divide by 20 real minutes of reading. That's your session count.</li><li>Cap at four blocks, then take a 20-minute walk. Non-negotiable.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>When does revision win?</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Spaced recall beats rereading. An hour spread across four days does more than three hours tonight.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Study Session Formula</h1><p class="muted">Break the pile into blocks, . The pile always looks smaller after that.</p><pre class="code"><code>sessions = workload ÷ (focus_block × 0.8)</code></pre><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Focus block</span><span class="value">25<span class="unit">min</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Real output</span><span class="value">20<span class="unit">min</span></span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Estimate pages or problems — say 300 pages.</li><li>Divide by 20 real minutes of reading. That's your session count.</li><li>Cap at four blocks, then take a 20-minute walk. Non-negotiable.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>When does revision win?</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Spaced recall beats rereading. An hour spread across four days does more than three hours tonight.</p></div></details></div>	-
45.22.0	dashboard_formula/Paint Colour Mix Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Paint Colour Mix Formula</h1><p class="muted">Tint slowly, <regex>user_name</regex>. You can always add another drop. You can never take one out.</p><pre class="code"><code>drops = litres × shade_strength × 0.5</code></pre><div class="callout info"><strong>Rule of thumb</strong><p class="t-small">Half a millilitre of universal tint per litre shifts a base by roughly one visible step.</p></div><ol class="list"><li>Start with 80% of the drops you think you need.</li><li>Stir for two full minutes — patches settle if you rush.</li><li>Paint a sample card and let it dry. Wet paint lies about colour.</li></ol><table class="table-wrap"><thead><tr><th>Shift wanted</th><th class="num">Tint per L</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Barely there</td><td class="num">0.3 mL</td></tr><tr><td>Light</td><td class="num">0.5 mL</td></tr><tr><td>Deep</td><td class="num">1.5 mL</td></tr></tbody></table></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Paint Colour Mix Formula</h1><p class="muted">Tint slowly, . You can always add another drop. You can never take one out.</p><pre class="code"><code>drops = litres × shade_strength × 0.5</code></pre><div class="callout info"><strong>Rule of thumb</strong><p class="t-small">Half a millilitre of universal tint per litre shifts a base by roughly one visible step.</p></div><ol class="list"><li>Start with 80% of the drops you think you need.</li><li>Stir for two full minutes — patches settle if you rush.</li><li>Paint a sample card and let it dry. Wet paint lies about colour.</li></ol><table class="table-wrap"><thead><tr><th>Shift wanted</th><th class="num">Tint per L</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Barely there</td><td class="num">0.3 mL</td></tr><tr><td>Light</td><td class="num">0.5 mL</td></tr><tr><td>Deep</td><td class="num">1.5 mL</td></tr></tbody></table></div>	-
45.23.0	dashboard_formula/Laundry Load Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Laundry Load Formula</h1><p class="muted">Stuff it or starve it, <regex>user_name</regex> — either way the rinse suffers.</p><pre class="code"><code>fill = (hand_widths − 1) ÷ hand_widths</code></pre><div class="callout good"><strong>The hand test</strong><p class="t-small">Lay a flat palm on top of the drum. One hand's width of clear space above the clothes is the sweet spot.</p></div><div class="grid"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Target fill</span><span class="value">75<span class="unit">%</span></span><span class="delta down">never 100</span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Load loosely, no pressing down.</li><li>Check the palm gap — if clothes touch your hand, remove some.</li><li>Heavy towels? Go to 60% so they can tumble.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Detergent per load</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Most cups overdo it by double. Front loaders: half dose is plenty unless clothes are visibly soiled.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Laundry Load Formula</h1><p class="muted">Stuff it or starve it,  — either way the rinse suffers.</p><pre class="code"><code>fill = (hand_widths − 1) ÷ hand_widths</code></pre><div class="callout good"><strong>The hand test</strong><p class="t-small">Lay a flat palm on top of the drum. One hand's width of clear space above the clothes is the sweet spot.</p></div><div class="grid"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Target fill</span><span class="value">75<span class="unit">%</span></span><span class="delta down">never 100</span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Load loosely, no pressing down.</li><li>Check the palm gap — if clothes touch your hand, remove some.</li><li>Heavy towels? Go to 60% so they can tumble.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Detergent per load</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Most cups overdo it by double. Front loaders: half dose is plenty unless clothes are visibly soiled.</p></div></details></div>	-
45.24.0	dashboard_formula/Pace Split Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Pace Split Formula</h1><p class="muted">Turn a finish time into a per-kilometre pace you can actually hold.</p><pre class="code"><code>pace_min_per_km = total_minutes / distance_km</code></pre><p>If you're chasing 1:45 for 21.1&nbsp;km, that's 105&nbsp;min / 21.1 = <strong>4.98&nbsp;min/km</strong>, about 4:59. Round to 4:55 and you've got a tiny cushion.</p><ol class="list"><li>Convert your goal time to minutes.</li><li>Divide by the race distance in kilometres.</li><li>Round down by 5 seconds for a small safety margin.</li><li>Check the first 5&nbsp;km split against it, then relax.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Miles instead?</summary><div class="body"><p>Same idea, different denominator: 13.1 miles gives you minutes per mile. No conversion gymnastics needed.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Pace Split Formula</h1><p class="muted">Turn a finish time into a per-kilometre pace you can actually hold.</p><pre class="code"><code>pace_min_per_km = total_minutes / distance_km</code></pre><p>If you're chasing 1:45 for 21.1&nbsp;km, that's 105&nbsp;min / 21.1 = <strong>4.98&nbsp;min/km</strong>, about 4:59. Round to 4:55 and you've got a tiny cushion.</p><ol class="list"><li>Convert your goal time to minutes.</li><li>Divide by the race distance in kilometres.</li><li>Round down by 5 seconds for a small safety margin.</li><li>Check the first 5&nbsp;km split against it, then relax.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Miles instead?</summary><div class="body"><p>Same idea, different denominator: 13.1 miles gives you minutes per mile. No conversion gymnastics needed.</p></div></details></div>	-
45.25.0	dashboard_formula/Bread Hydration Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Bread Hydration Formula</h1><p class="muted">Hydration is just water as a share of flour. Once you see it, you can't unsee it.</p><pre class="code"><code>hydration_% = (water_g / flour_g) × 100</code></pre><p>500&nbsp;g flour and 375&nbsp;g water is <strong>75% hydration</strong> — a friendly everyday sourdough. Bump water and the crumb opens up, but so does the mess.</p><ol class="list"><li>Weigh your flour, including any in the starter.</li><li>Weigh the water. Yes, weigh it.</li><li>Divide water by flour, then multiply by 100.</li><li>Adjust by 5% at a time until the dough feels right.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>Starter counts too. 100&nbsp;g of 100% starter adds 50&nbsp;g flour and 50&nbsp;g water. Add both to your totals.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Bread Hydration Formula</h1><p class="muted">Hydration is just water as a share of flour. Once you see it, you can't unsee it.</p><pre class="code"><code>hydration_% = (water_g / flour_g) × 100</code></pre><p>500&nbsp;g flour and 375&nbsp;g water is <strong>75% hydration</strong> — a friendly everyday sourdough. Bump water and the crumb opens up, but so does the mess.</p><ol class="list"><li>Weigh your flour, including any in the starter.</li><li>Weigh the water. Yes, weigh it.</li><li>Divide water by flour, then multiply by 100.</li><li>Adjust by 5% at a time until the dough feels right.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>Starter counts too. 100&nbsp;g of 100% starter adds 50&nbsp;g flour and 50&nbsp;g water. Add both to your totals.</p></div></div>	-
45.26.0	dashboard_formula/Compound Interest Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Compound Interest Formula</h1><p class="muted">The quiet engine behind every "start early" lecture you've ever received.</p><pre class="code"><code>A = P (1 + r / n)<sup>n t</sup></code></pre><p>P is your starting pot, r the annual rate as a decimal, n how often it compounds per year, t the years. £1,000 at 5% monthly for 10 years lands near <strong>£1,647</strong>.</p><ol class="list"><li>Write the rate as a decimal: 5% → 0.05.</li><li>Divide by compounds per year: 0.05 / 12 ≈ 0.004167.</li><li>Add 1, raise to (n × t) = 120.</li><li>Multiply by your starting amount.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Why does frequency matter?</summary><div class="body"><p>More compounding periods means interest earns interest sooner. The difference is small monthly vs yearly, but it adds up over decades.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Compound Interest Formula</h1><p class="muted">The quiet engine behind every "start early" lecture you've ever received.</p><pre class="code"><code>A = P (1 + r / n)<sup>n t</sup></code></pre><p>P is your starting pot, r the annual rate as a decimal, n how often it compounds per year, t the years. £1,000 at 5% monthly for 10 years lands near <strong>£1,647</strong>.</p><ol class="list"><li>Write the rate as a decimal: 5% → 0.05.</li><li>Divide by compounds per year: 0.05 / 12 ≈ 0.004167.</li><li>Add 1, raise to (n × t) = 120.</li><li>Multiply by your starting amount.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Why does frequency matter?</summary><div class="body"><p>More compounding periods means interest earns interest sooner. The difference is small monthly vs yearly, but it adds up over decades.</p></div></details></div>	-
45.27.0	dashboard_formula/Paint Wall Area Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Paint Wall Area Formula</h1><p class="muted">Measure twice, buy once — and maybe keep the receipt anyway.</p><pre class="code"><code>wall_area = perimeter × height − openings</code></pre><p>A 4&nbsp;m × 5&nbsp;m room has a 18&nbsp;m perimeter. At 2.4&nbsp;m high that's 43.2&nbsp;m², minus a door (1.8&nbsp;m²) and two windows (3&nbsp;m²) leaves <strong>38.4&nbsp;m²</strong>.</p><ol class="list"><li>Add up all wall lengths for the perimeter.</li><li>Multiply by ceiling height.</li><li>Subtract doors and windows.</li><li>Divide by the tin's coverage (often 10–12&nbsp;m²/L) and round up.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p>Two coats, always. Double the litres unless the tin says otherwise.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Paint Wall Area Formula</h1><p class="muted">Measure twice, buy once — and maybe keep the receipt anyway.</p><pre class="code"><code>wall_area = perimeter × height − openings</code></pre><p>A 4&nbsp;m × 5&nbsp;m room has a 18&nbsp;m perimeter. At 2.4&nbsp;m high that's 43.2&nbsp;m², minus a door (1.8&nbsp;m²) and two windows (3&nbsp;m²) leaves <strong>38.4&nbsp;m²</strong>.</p><ol class="list"><li>Add up all wall lengths for the perimeter.</li><li>Multiply by ceiling height.</li><li>Subtract doors and windows.</li><li>Divide by the tin's coverage (often 10–12&nbsp;m²/L) and round up.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p>Two coats, always. Double the litres unless the tin says otherwise.</p></div></div>	-
45.28.0	dashboard_formula/Calorie Deficit Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Calorie Deficit Formula</h1><p class="muted">Slow and steady wins — and keeps the muscle.</p><pre class="code"><code>deficit = TDEE − intake</code></pre><p>If your total daily energy expenditure is 2,400&nbsp;kcal and you eat 1,900, that's a 500&nbsp;kcal deficit — roughly 0.45&nbsp;kg a week.</p><ol class="list"><li>Estimate your TDEE (activity included).</li><li>Subtract your daily intake.</li><li>Aim for 300–500&nbsp;kcal below, not below your basal needs.</li><li>Track weekly averages, not daily swings.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>The scale is noisy</summary><div class="body"><p>Water, salt, and hormones all move the number. Judge by a two-week trend, not a Tuesday morning surprise.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Calorie Deficit Formula</h1><p class="muted">Slow and steady wins — and keeps the muscle.</p><pre class="code"><code>deficit = TDEE − intake</code></pre><p>If your total daily energy expenditure is 2,400&nbsp;kcal and you eat 1,900, that's a 500&nbsp;kcal deficit — roughly 0.45&nbsp;kg a week.</p><ol class="list"><li>Estimate your TDEE (activity included).</li><li>Subtract your daily intake.</li><li>Aim for 300–500&nbsp;kcal below, not below your basal needs.</li><li>Track weekly averages, not daily swings.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>The scale is noisy</summary><div class="body"><p>Water, salt, and hormones all move the number. Judge by a two-week trend, not a Tuesday morning surprise.</p></div></details></div>	-
45.29.0	dashboard_formula/Gear Ratio Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Gear Ratio Formula</h1><p class="muted">Chainring teeth over cog teeth. The rest is legs.</p><pre class="code"><code>ratio = chainring_teeth / cog_teeth</code></pre><p>A 50-tooth chainring and an 11-tooth cog gives <strong>4.55</strong> — a big gear for flat roads and strong mornings.</p><ol class="list"><li>Count (or look up) your chainring teeth.</li><li>Count the cassette cog teeth.</li><li>Divide chainring by cog.</li><li>Multiply by wheel diameter for gear inches.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>Spin to win. A cadence of 85–95&nbsp;rpm usually beats mashing a big gear.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Gear Ratio Formula</h1><p class="muted">Chainring teeth over cog teeth. The rest is legs.</p><pre class="code"><code>ratio = chainring_teeth / cog_teeth</code></pre><p>A 50-tooth chainring and an 11-tooth cog gives <strong>4.55</strong> — a big gear for flat roads and strong mornings.</p><ol class="list"><li>Count (or look up) your chainring teeth.</li><li>Count the cassette cog teeth.</li><li>Divide chainring by cog.</li><li>Multiply by wheel diameter for gear inches.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>Spin to win. A cadence of 85–95&nbsp;rpm usually beats mashing a big gear.</p></div></div>	-
45.30.0	dashboard_formula/Cake Pan Swap Formula	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Cake Pan Swap Formula</h1>  <p class="muted">Different pan, same batter—just scale it by area. <regex>user_name</regex>, here's the quick math.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>multiplier = (new_width × new_length) ÷ (old_width × old_length)</code></pre>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat">      <span class="label">Original pan</span>      <span class="value">8 × 8</span>      <span class="unit">in</span>    </div>    <div class="stat">      <span class="label">New pan</span>      <span class="value">9 × 13</span>      <span class="unit">in</span>    </div>  </div>  <p class="muted">Area old = 64, new = 117 → multiplier ≈ 1.83. So use about 1.8 times the batter.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li>Measure your pan's inside dimensions.</li>    <li>Multiply width by length for both pans.</li>    <li>Divide new area by old area to get the multiplier.</li>    <li>Scale all ingredients, including leaveners and salt.</li>    <li>Keep the same oven temp, but start checking early—different depths bake differently.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout info">    <strong>Heads up:</strong> round pans use πr². An 8-inch round is about 50 sq in, not 64.  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Round pan example</summary>    <div class="body">      <p class="muted">8-inch round: radius 4, area = π × 4² ≈ 50.3 sq in. 9-inch round: radius 4.5, area = π × 4.5² ≈ 63.6 sq in. Multiplier ≈ 1.26.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Cake Pan Swap Formula</h1>  <p class="muted">Different pan, same batter—just scale it by area. , here's the quick math.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>multiplier = (new_width × new_length) ÷ (old_width × old_length)</code></pre>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat">      <span class="label">Original pan</span>      <span class="value">8 × 8</span>      <span class="unit">in</span>    </div>    <div class="stat">      <span class="label">New pan</span>      <span class="value">9 × 13</span>      <span class="unit">in</span>    </div>  </div>  <p class="muted">Area old = 64, new = 117 → multiplier ≈ 1.83. So use about 1.8 times the batter.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li>Measure your pan's inside dimensions.</li>    <li>Multiply width by length for both pans.</li>    <li>Divide new area by old area to get the multiplier.</li>    <li>Scale all ingredients, including leaveners and salt.</li>    <li>Keep the same oven temp, but start checking early—different depths bake differently.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout info">    <strong>Heads up:</strong> round pans use πr². An 8-inch round is about 50 sq in, not 64.  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Round pan example</summary>    <div class="body">      <p class="muted">8-inch round: radius 4, area = π × 4² ≈ 50.3 sq in. 9-inch round: radius 4.5, area = π × 4.5² ≈ 63.6 sq in. Multiplier ≈ 1.26.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
45.31.0	dashboard_formula/Mulch Coverage Formula	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Mulch Coverage Formula</h1>  <p class="muted">Spread the love, not the mulch. <regex>user_name</regex>, find your magic number.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>cubic_yards = (area_sqft × depth_in) ÷ 324</code></pre>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat">      <span class="label">Bed size</span>      <span class="value">4 × 8</span>      <span class="unit">ft</span>    </div>    <div class="stat">      <span class="label">Depth</span>      <span class="value">3</span>      <span class="unit">in</span>    </div>  </div>  <p class="muted">Area = 32 sq ft. 32 × 3 = 96. 96 ÷ 324 ≈ 0.3 cubic yards.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li>Measure length and width of your bed in feet.</li>    <li>Multiply to get square footage.</li>    <li>Decide on depth: 2–4 inches is typical for beds.</li>    <li>Plug into formula: (sq ft × inches) ÷ 324.</li>    <li>Bags: one 2-cubic-foot bag covers about 12 sq ft at 2 inches deep.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout good">    <strong>Tip:</strong> Bulk is cheaper for big jobs—one cubic yard covers about 100 sq ft at 3 inches deep.  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Bag math cheat sheet</summary>    <div class="body">      <p class="muted">A 2 cu ft bag = 0.074 cu yd. At 3 inches deep, one bag covers about 10.8 sq ft. So a 32 sq ft bed needs about 3 bags.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Mulch Coverage Formula</h1>  <p class="muted">Spread the love, not the mulch. , find your magic number.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>cubic_yards = (area_sqft × depth_in) ÷ 324</code></pre>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat">      <span class="label">Bed size</span>      <span class="value">4 × 8</span>      <span class="unit">ft</span>    </div>    <div class="stat">      <span class="label">Depth</span>      <span class="value">3</span>      <span class="unit">in</span>    </div>  </div>  <p class="muted">Area = 32 sq ft. 32 × 3 = 96. 96 ÷ 324 ≈ 0.3 cubic yards.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li>Measure length and width of your bed in feet.</li>    <li>Multiply to get square footage.</li>    <li>Decide on depth: 2–4 inches is typical for beds.</li>    <li>Plug into formula: (sq ft × inches) ÷ 324.</li>    <li>Bags: one 2-cubic-foot bag covers about 12 sq ft at 2 inches deep.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout good">    <strong>Tip:</strong> Bulk is cheaper for big jobs—one cubic yard covers about 100 sq ft at 3 inches deep.  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Bag math cheat sheet</summary>    <div class="body">      <p class="muted">A 2 cu ft bag = 0.074 cu yd. At 3 inches deep, one bag covers about 10.8 sq ft. So a 32 sq ft bed needs about 3 bags.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
45.32.0	dashboard_formula/Wood Stain Coverage Formula	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Wood Stain Coverage Formula</h1>  <p class="muted">One coat or two? Stain soaks in, so coverage varies. <regex>user_name</regex>, here's how to estimate.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>quarts_needed = (area_sqft × coats) ÷ coverage_per_quart</code></pre>  <p class="muted">Most stains cover 150–250 sq ft per quart. Check the can—porous wood drinks more.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat">      <span class="label">Deck area</span>      <span class="value">192</span>      <span class="unit">sq ft</span>    </div>    <div class="stat">      <span class="label">Coats</span>      <span class="value">2</span>    </div>  </div>  <p class="muted">192 × 2 = 384. At 200 sq ft/quart → 1.92 quarts, round up to 2.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li>Measure area: length × width for flat surfaces; add railings separately.</li>    <li>Multiply by number of coats you plan.</li>    <li>Divide by the coverage listed on your stain can.</li>    <li>Round up to the nearest quart or gallon.</li>    <li>Buy a little extra for touch-ups.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout warn">    <strong>Near miss:</strong> Rough or weathered wood can cut coverage by 20–30%. Soak a test patch first.  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Railings and spindles</summary>    <div class="body">      <p class="muted">A standard 8-foot railing section has about 10 sq ft of surface. Count them separately—don't just use floor area.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Wood Stain Coverage Formula</h1>  <p class="muted">One coat or two? Stain soaks in, so coverage varies. , here's how to estimate.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>quarts_needed = (area_sqft × coats) ÷ coverage_per_quart</code></pre>  <p class="muted">Most stains cover 150–250 sq ft per quart. Check the can—porous wood drinks more.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat">      <span class="label">Deck area</span>      <span class="value">192</span>      <span class="unit">sq ft</span>    </div>    <div class="stat">      <span class="label">Coats</span>      <span class="value">2</span>    </div>  </div>  <p class="muted">192 × 2 = 384. At 200 sq ft/quart → 1.92 quarts, round up to 2.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li>Measure area: length × width for flat surfaces; add railings separately.</li>    <li>Multiply by number of coats you plan.</li>    <li>Divide by the coverage listed on your stain can.</li>    <li>Round up to the nearest quart or gallon.</li>    <li>Buy a little extra for touch-ups.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout warn">    <strong>Near miss:</strong> Rough or weathered wood can cut coverage by 20–30%. Soak a test patch first.  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Railings and spindles</summary>    <div class="body">      <p class="muted">A standard 8-foot railing section has about 10 sq ft of surface. Count them separately—don't just use floor area.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
45.33.0	dashboard_formula/Tile Adhesive Formula	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Tile Adhesive Formula</h1>  <p class="muted">Tile size and trowel notch change everything. <regex>user_name</regex>, let's get your thinset numbers right.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>bags = (area_sqft × coverage_factor) ÷ bag_yield</code></pre>  <p class="muted">A 50 lb bag yields roughly 80–100 sq ft with a 1/4" trowel. Larger tiles need more.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat">      <span class="label">Area</span>      <span class="value">100</span>      <span class="unit">sq ft</span>    </div>    <div class="stat">      <span class="label">Trowel</span>      <span class="value">1/4"</span>      <span class="unit">square notch</span>    </div>  </div>  <p class="muted">100 sq ft ÷ 90 sq ft/bag ≈ 1.1 bags. Buy 2 to be safe.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li>Measure your tiling area.</li>    <li>Check the bag for coverage at your trowel size.</li>    <li>Divide area by coverage per bag.</li>    <li>Round up—always.</li>    <li>Keep a spare bag for mistakes and future repairs.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout info">    <strong>Reminder:</strong> Back-buttering large tiles doubles adhesive use. Factor that in.  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Trowel size guide</summary>    <div class="body">      <p class="muted">1/4" × 1/4" for small tiles, 1/4" × 3/8" for 12×12, 1/2" × 1/2" for large format. Bigger notch = more adhesive.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Tile Adhesive Formula</h1>  <p class="muted">Tile size and trowel notch change everything. , let's get your thinset numbers right.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>bags = (area_sqft × coverage_factor) ÷ bag_yield</code></pre>  <p class="muted">A 50 lb bag yields roughly 80–100 sq ft with a 1/4" trowel. Larger tiles need more.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat">      <span class="label">Area</span>      <span class="value">100</span>      <span class="unit">sq ft</span>    </div>    <div class="stat">      <span class="label">Trowel</span>      <span class="value">1/4"</span>      <span class="unit">square notch</span>    </div>  </div>  <p class="muted">100 sq ft ÷ 90 sq ft/bag ≈ 1.1 bags. Buy 2 to be safe.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li>Measure your tiling area.</li>    <li>Check the bag for coverage at your trowel size.</li>    <li>Divide area by coverage per bag.</li>    <li>Round up—always.</li>    <li>Keep a spare bag for mistakes and future repairs.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout info">    <strong>Reminder:</strong> Back-buttering large tiles doubles adhesive use. Factor that in.  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Trowel size guide</summary>    <div class="body">      <p class="muted">1/4" × 1/4" for small tiles, 1/4" × 3/8" for 12×12, 1/2" × 1/2" for large format. Bigger notch = more adhesive.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
45.34.0	dashboard_formula/Fertilizer Rate Formula	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Fertilizer Rate Formula</h1>  <p class="muted">Don't guess—overfeeding burns grass. <regex>user_name</regex>, here's the calm way to calculate.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>lbs_product = (target_N_lbs_per_1000 × area_sqft × 1000) ÷ (100 × N_percent)</code></pre>  <p class="muted">Most lawns want 0.5–1 lb of actual nitrogen per 1000 sq ft per application.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat">      <span class="label">Lawn size</span>      <span class="value">5000</span>      <span class="unit">sq ft</span>    </div>    <div class="stat">      <span class="label">Fertilizer N</span>      <span class="value">10</span>      <span class="unit">%</span>    </div>  </div>  <p class="muted">Target 1 lb N per 1000 sq ft: (1 × 5000 × 1000) ÷ (100 × 10) = 5 lbs of product.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li>Find your lawn's square footage.</li>    <li>Check the first number on the fertilizer bag—that's N%.</li>    <li>Decide your target nitrogen rate (start low).</li>    <li>Use the formula to get pounds of product.</li>    <li>Apply evenly with a spreader, water in lightly.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout good">    <strong>Safety first:</strong> Never exceed 1 lb N per 1000 sq ft in one go. Split applications are kinder.  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What about P and K?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p class="muted">The other numbers are phosphorus and potassium. They matter too, but nitrogen drives growth. Soil test for best results.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Fertilizer Rate Formula</h1>  <p class="muted">Don't guess—overfeeding burns grass. , here's the calm way to calculate.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>lbs_product = (target_N_lbs_per_1000 × area_sqft × 1000) ÷ (100 × N_percent)</code></pre>  <p class="muted">Most lawns want 0.5–1 lb of actual nitrogen per 1000 sq ft per application.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat">      <span class="label">Lawn size</span>      <span class="value">5000</span>      <span class="unit">sq ft</span>    </div>    <div class="stat">      <span class="label">Fertilizer N</span>      <span class="value">10</span>      <span class="unit">%</span>    </div>  </div>  <p class="muted">Target 1 lb N per 1000 sq ft: (1 × 5000 × 1000) ÷ (100 × 10) = 5 lbs of product.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li>Find your lawn's square footage.</li>    <li>Check the first number on the fertilizer bag—that's N%.</li>    <li>Decide your target nitrogen rate (start low).</li>    <li>Use the formula to get pounds of product.</li>    <li>Apply evenly with a spreader, water in lightly.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout good">    <strong>Safety first:</strong> Never exceed 1 lb N per 1000 sq ft in one go. Split applications are kinder.  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What about P and K?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p class="muted">The other numbers are phosphorus and potassium. They matter too, but nitrogen drives growth. Soil test for best results.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
45.35.0	dashboard_formula/Seam Allowance Formula	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Seam Allowance Formula</h1>  <p class="muted">Tiny seam, big difference. <regex>user_name</regex>, here's how to adjust without redrawing everything.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>new_cut_size = finished_size + (2 × new_seam_allowance)</code></pre>  <p class="muted">For a 6-inch finished square with 1/4" seam: cut 6.5 inches. With 5/8" seam: cut 7.25 inches.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat">      <span class="label">Finished size</span>      <span class="value">6</span>      <span class="unit">in</span>    </div>    <div class="stat">      <span class="label">Seam allowance</span>      <span class="value">1/4</span>      <span class="unit">in</span>    </div>  </div>  <p class="muted">Cut size = 6 + (2 × 0.25) = 6.5 inches.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li>Decide your finished size.</li>    <li>Pick your seam allowance (1/4" for quilting, 5/8" for garments).</li>    <li>Multiply seam allowance by 2—one for each side.</li>    <li>Add to finished size to get cut size.</li>    <li>Measure twice, cut once.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout info">    <strong>Quilting tip:</strong> Consistent 1/4" seams matter more than perfect cutting. Test your seam on scraps.  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Converting a pattern</summary>    <div class="body">      <p class="muted">If a pattern uses 5/8" and you want 1/4", add 0.75" to each cut dimension (difference × 2). So a 10" cut becomes 10.75".</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Seam Allowance Formula</h1>  <p class="muted">Tiny seam, big difference. , here's how to adjust without redrawing everything.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>new_cut_size = finished_size + (2 × new_seam_allowance)</code></pre>  <p class="muted">For a 6-inch finished square with 1/4" seam: cut 6.5 inches. With 5/8" seam: cut 7.25 inches.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat">      <span class="label">Finished size</span>      <span class="value">6</span>      <span class="unit">in</span>    </div>    <div class="stat">      <span class="label">Seam allowance</span>      <span class="value">1/4</span>      <span class="unit">in</span>    </div>  </div>  <p class="muted">Cut size = 6 + (2 × 0.25) = 6.5 inches.</p>  <ol class="list">    <li>Decide your finished size.</li>    <li>Pick your seam allowance (1/4" for quilting, 5/8" for garments).</li>    <li>Multiply seam allowance by 2—one for each side.</li>    <li>Add to finished size to get cut size.</li>    <li>Measure twice, cut once.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout info">    <strong>Quilting tip:</strong> Consistent 1/4" seams matter more than perfect cutting. Test your seam on scraps.  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Converting a pattern</summary>    <div class="body">      <p class="muted">If a pattern uses 5/8" and you want 1/4", add 0.75" to each cut dimension (difference × 2). So a 10" cut becomes 10.75".</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
45.36.0	dashboard_formula/Terrarium Drainage Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Terrarium Drainage Formula</h1><p class="muted">Keep roots dry, keep moss happy. Here's the math.</p><pre class="code"><code>drainage_cm = total_cm × 0.2</code></pre><div class="callout info"><strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> 20% drainage, 80% substrate. For a 15 cm tall terrarium, that's 3 cm of gravel.</div><h2>Steps</h2><ol class="list"><li><p>Measure the inside height of your container in cm.</p></li><li><p>Multiply by 0.2 to get the drainage layer depth.</p></li><li><p>Invert as needed: total_cm = drainage_cm ÷ 0.2.</p></li><li><p>Add a mesh screen or sphagnum layer to stop soil sifting down.</p></li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Example: 20 cm tall jar</summary><div class="body"><p><code>drainage = 20 × 0.2 = 4 cm</code>. Use 4 cm of pebbles, then 16 cm of soil mix. For a closed terrarium, skip drainage if you're confident about your watering—otherwise 20% is a safe bet.</p></div></details><p class="muted t-small">Adjust for very tall containers: use 15% if the glass is deep and narrow.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Terrarium Drainage Formula</h1><p class="muted">Keep roots dry, keep moss happy. Here's the math.</p><pre class="code"><code>drainage_cm = total_cm × 0.2</code></pre><div class="callout info"><strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> 20% drainage, 80% substrate. For a 15 cm tall terrarium, that's 3 cm of gravel.</div><h2>Steps</h2><ol class="list"><li><p>Measure the inside height of your container in cm.</p></li><li><p>Multiply by 0.2 to get the drainage layer depth.</p></li><li><p>Invert as needed: total_cm = drainage_cm ÷ 0.2.</p></li><li><p>Add a mesh screen or sphagnum layer to stop soil sifting down.</p></li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Example: 20 cm tall jar</summary><div class="body"><p><code>drainage = 20 × 0.2 = 4 cm</code>. Use 4 cm of pebbles, then 16 cm of soil mix. For a closed terrarium, skip drainage if you're confident about your watering—otherwise 20% is a safe bet.</p></div></details><p class="muted t-small">Adjust for very tall containers: use 15% if the glass is deep and narrow.</p></div>	-
45.37.0	dashboard_formula/Resin Mix Ratio Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Resin Mix Ratio Formula</h1><p class="muted">Epoxy isn't guesswork. Get it right and it cures glass-clear.</p><pre class="code"><code>hardener_ml = resin_ml ÷ ratio</code></pre><p class="muted">Most kit resins are 1:1, 2:1, or 4:1 resin:hardener by volume. Check your bottle.</p><div class="callout warn"><strong>Never eyeball it.</strong> Off-ratio resin stays tacky forever. Measure by volume, not weight (unless the kit says otherwise).</div><h2>Steps</h2><ol class="list"><li><p>Find the ratio printed on the bottle (e.g., 2:1).</p></li><li><p>Decide how much mixed resin you need (total volume).</p></li><li><p>Calculate resin part: <code>resin_ml = total_ml × (ratio / (ratio + 1))</code>.</p></li><li><p>Calculate hardener: <code>hardener_ml = total_ml - resin_ml</code>.</p></li><li><p>Mix for 3 full minutes, scraping the sides.</p></li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Example: 90 ml total, 2:1 ratio</summary><div class="body"><p>Resin = 90 × (2/3) = 60 ml. Hardener = 30 ml. That's 60 ml resin + 30 ml hardener = 90 ml total.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Resin Mix Ratio Formula</h1><p class="muted">Epoxy isn't guesswork. Get it right and it cures glass-clear.</p><pre class="code"><code>hardener_ml = resin_ml ÷ ratio</code></pre><p class="muted">Most kit resins are 1:1, 2:1, or 4:1 resin:hardener by volume. Check your bottle.</p><div class="callout warn"><strong>Never eyeball it.</strong> Off-ratio resin stays tacky forever. Measure by volume, not weight (unless the kit says otherwise).</div><h2>Steps</h2><ol class="list"><li><p>Find the ratio printed on the bottle (e.g., 2:1).</p></li><li><p>Decide how much mixed resin you need (total volume).</p></li><li><p>Calculate resin part: <code>resin_ml = total_ml × (ratio / (ratio + 1))</code>.</p></li><li><p>Calculate hardener: <code>hardener_ml = total_ml - resin_ml</code>.</p></li><li><p>Mix for 3 full minutes, scraping the sides.</p></li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Example: 90 ml total, 2:1 ratio</summary><div class="body"><p>Resin = 90 × (2/3) = 60 ml. Hardener = 30 ml. That's 60 ml resin + 30 ml hardener = 90 ml total.</p></div></details></div>	-
45.38.0	dashboard_formula/Compost Carbon Nitrogen Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Compost Carbon: Nitrogen Formula</h1><p class="muted">The secret to fast, hot, smell-free compost? Balance your browns and greens.</p><pre class="code"><code>target_C:N = 30:1</code></pre><p class="muted">Browns (carbon) = dry leaves, cardboard, straw. Greens (nitrogen) = grass, veggie scraps, coffee grounds.</p><div class="callout good"><strong>Quick rule:</strong> 2 to 3 parts browns by volume for every 1 part greens. If it smells sour, add more browns.</div><h2>Steps</h2><ol class="list"><li><p>Estimate your green volume (the smelly stuff).</p></li><li><p>Multiply by 2.5 to get browns volume.</p></li><li><p>Layer them like a lasagna, then turn.</p></li><li><p>If it heats up within 2 days, you nailed it.</p></li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Example: 4 litres of kitchen scraps</summary><div class="body"><p>Browns needed = 4 × 2.5 = 10 litres. Grab a bin's worth of shredded paper and dry leaves.</p></div></details><p class="muted t-small">For slow-cold composting, 1:1 is fine but takes longer.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Compost Carbon: Nitrogen Formula</h1><p class="muted">The secret to fast, hot, smell-free compost? Balance your browns and greens.</p><pre class="code"><code>target_C:N = 30:1</code></pre><p class="muted">Browns (carbon) = dry leaves, cardboard, straw. Greens (nitrogen) = grass, veggie scraps, coffee grounds.</p><div class="callout good"><strong>Quick rule:</strong> 2 to 3 parts browns by volume for every 1 part greens. If it smells sour, add more browns.</div><h2>Steps</h2><ol class="list"><li><p>Estimate your green volume (the smelly stuff).</p></li><li><p>Multiply by 2.5 to get browns volume.</p></li><li><p>Layer them like a lasagna, then turn.</p></li><li><p>If it heats up within 2 days, you nailed it.</p></li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Example: 4 litres of kitchen scraps</summary><div class="body"><p>Browns needed = 4 × 2.5 = 10 litres. Grab a bin's worth of shredded paper and dry leaves.</p></div></details><p class="muted t-small">For slow-cold composting, 1:1 is fine but takes longer.</p></div>	-
45.39.0	dashboard_formula/Yeast Hydration Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Yeast Hydration Formula</h1><p class="muted">Give your yeast a warm bath before the dough party. It's the difference between rise and sigh.</p><pre class="code"><code>water_ml = yeast_g × 4</code></pre><p class="muted">Use lukewarm water, 38–43°C. Too hot kills it, too cold slows it down.</p><div class="callout info"><strong>Standard sachet:</strong> 7g yeast needs 28ml water. That's 4 parts water to 1 part yeast by weight.</div><h2>Steps</h2><ol class="list"><li><p>Weigh your yeast in grams.</p></li><li><p>Multiply by 4 to get millilitres of water.</p></li><li><p>Stir gently and let sit 5–10 minutes.</p></li><li><p>Look for foam—that's your proof it's alive.</p></li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Example: 10g yeast</summary><div class="body"><p>Water = 10 × 4 = 40 ml. If no foam after 15 min, your yeast is dead. Time for a new packet.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Yeast Hydration Formula</h1><p class="muted">Give your yeast a warm bath before the dough party. It's the difference between rise and sigh.</p><pre class="code"><code>water_ml = yeast_g × 4</code></pre><p class="muted">Use lukewarm water, 38–43°C. Too hot kills it, too cold slows it down.</p><div class="callout info"><strong>Standard sachet:</strong> 7g yeast needs 28ml water. That's 4 parts water to 1 part yeast by weight.</div><h2>Steps</h2><ol class="list"><li><p>Weigh your yeast in grams.</p></li><li><p>Multiply by 4 to get millilitres of water.</p></li><li><p>Stir gently and let sit 5–10 minutes.</p></li><li><p>Look for foam—that's your proof it's alive.</p></li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Example: 10g yeast</summary><div class="body"><p>Water = 10 × 4 = 40 ml. If no foam after 15 min, your yeast is dead. Time for a new packet.</p></div></details></div>	-
45.40.0	dashboard_formula/Screen Protector Fit Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Screen Protector Fit Formula</h1><p class="muted">Oversized protector? Don't guess. Measure twice, cut once.</p><pre class="code"><code>cut_width = screen_width - 2 mm</code><br><code>cut_height = screen_height - 2 mm</code></pre><p class="muted">A 1 mm margin all around prevents edge lifting.</p><div class="callout warn"><strong>Be brave.</strong> Cut too small and dust sneaks in. Cut too big and you get bubbles. 1 mm margin is the sweet spot.</div><h2>Steps</h2><ol class="list"><li><p>Measure your actual screen width and height in mm.</p></li><li><p>Subtract 2 mm from each dimension.</p></li><li><p>Mark the protector with a fine-tip marker.</p></li><li><p>Cut with sharp scissors or a craft knife.</p></li><li><p>Peel, align, and squeegee.</p></li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Example: 150 mm × 70 mm phone</summary><div class="body"><p>Cut to 148 mm × 68 mm. That leaves a 1 mm gap all around for cases and curved edges.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Screen Protector Fit Formula</h1><p class="muted">Oversized protector? Don't guess. Measure twice, cut once.</p><pre class="code"><code>cut_width = screen_width - 2 mm</code><br><code>cut_height = screen_height - 2 mm</code></pre><p class="muted">A 1 mm margin all around prevents edge lifting.</p><div class="callout warn"><strong>Be brave.</strong> Cut too small and dust sneaks in. Cut too big and you get bubbles. 1 mm margin is the sweet spot.</div><h2>Steps</h2><ol class="list"><li><p>Measure your actual screen width and height in mm.</p></li><li><p>Subtract 2 mm from each dimension.</p></li><li><p>Mark the protector with a fine-tip marker.</p></li><li><p>Cut with sharp scissors or a craft knife.</p></li><li><p>Peel, align, and squeegee.</p></li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Example: 150 mm × 70 mm phone</summary><div class="body"><p>Cut to 148 mm × 68 mm. That leaves a 1 mm gap all around for cases and curved edges.</p></div></details></div>	-
45.41.0	dashboard_formula/Soil Amendment Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Soil Amendment Formula</h1><p class="muted">Your soil is probably fine. It just needs a little help. Here's how much compost to add.</p><pre class="code"><code>compost_kg = area_m² × 2</code></pre><p class="muted">For a 5 cm layer of compost over 1 m², you need about 2 kg (roughly 10 litres).</p><div class="callout good"><strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> 5 cm of compost on top, then mix into the top 15 cm. For clay, double it.</div><h2>Steps</h2><ol class="list"><li><p>Measure the area you're amending in square metres.</p></li><li><p>Multiply by 2 to get kilograms of compost (or 10 for litres).</p></li><li><p>Spread evenly and work into the soil.</p></li><li><p>Water and let it rest a week before planting.</p></li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Example: 3 m² raised bed</summary><div class="body"><p>Compost needed = 3 × 2 = 6 kg, or about 30 litres. That's one big bag.</p></div></details><p class="muted t-small">Sandy soil? Use 1.5 kg per m². Clay? Use 3 kg per m².</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Soil Amendment Formula</h1><p class="muted">Your soil is probably fine. It just needs a little help. Here's how much compost to add.</p><pre class="code"><code>compost_kg = area_m² × 2</code></pre><p class="muted">For a 5 cm layer of compost over 1 m², you need about 2 kg (roughly 10 litres).</p><div class="callout good"><strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> 5 cm of compost on top, then mix into the top 15 cm. For clay, double it.</div><h2>Steps</h2><ol class="list"><li><p>Measure the area you're amending in square metres.</p></li><li><p>Multiply by 2 to get kilograms of compost (or 10 for litres).</p></li><li><p>Spread evenly and work into the soil.</p></li><li><p>Water and let it rest a week before planting.</p></li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Example: 3 m² raised bed</summary><div class="body"><p>Compost needed = 3 × 2 = 6 kg, or about 30 litres. That's one big bag.</p></div></details><p class="muted t-small">Sandy soil? Use 1.5 kg per m². Clay? Use 3 kg per m².</p></div>	-
45.42.0	dashboard_formula/Ladder Angle Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Ladder Angle Formula</h1><p class="muted"><regex>user_name</regex>, here's a quick way to set your ladder at the safest angle.</p><pre class="code"><code>angle = atan(1/4) ≈ 75.5°</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Measure the working length of the ladder (the part that touches the wall and ground).</li><li>Divide that length by 4.</li><li>Place the base of the ladder that distance from the wall.</li><li>Check the angle: it should be about 75 degrees.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>For a 16-foot ladder, the base should be 4 feet from the wall.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Ladder Angle Formula</h1><p class="muted">, here's a quick way to set your ladder at the safest angle.</p><pre class="code"><code>angle = atan(1/4) ≈ 75.5°</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Measure the working length of the ladder (the part that touches the wall and ground).</li><li>Divide that length by 4.</li><li>Place the base of the ladder that distance from the wall.</li><li>Check the angle: it should be about 75 degrees.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>For a 16-foot ladder, the base should be 4 feet from the wall.</p></div></div>	-
45.43.0	dashboard_formula/Packing Cube Volume Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Packing Cube Volume Formula</h1><p class="muted"><regex>user_name</regex>, figure out the total volume of your packing cubes to match your suitcase.</p><pre class="code"><code>total volume = length × width × height × number of cubes</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Measure one cube: length, width, height in inches.</li><li>Multiply them to get cubic inches.</li><li>Multiply by the number of cubes you plan to use.</li><li>Compare to your suitcase's volume (often listed in liters; 1 liter ≈ 61 cubic inches).</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>A typical medium cube is 14×10×4 inches = 560 cubic inches ≈ 9.2 liters.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Packing Cube Volume Formula</h1><p class="muted">, figure out the total volume of your packing cubes to match your suitcase.</p><pre class="code"><code>total volume = length × width × height × number of cubes</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Measure one cube: length, width, height in inches.</li><li>Multiply them to get cubic inches.</li><li>Multiply by the number of cubes you plan to use.</li><li>Compare to your suitcase's volume (often listed in liters; 1 liter ≈ 61 cubic inches).</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>A typical medium cube is 14×10×4 inches = 560 cubic inches ≈ 9.2 liters.</p></div></div>	-
45.44.0	dashboard_formula/Rain Barrel Capacity Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Rain Barrel Capacity Formula</h1><p class="muted"><regex>user_name</regex>, estimate how much rain you can capture.</p><pre class="code"><code>gallons = roof area (sq ft) × rainfall (inches) × 0.623</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Measure the footprint of your roof (length × width).</li><li>Find your average rainfall in inches.</li><li>Multiply roof area by rainfall by 0.623.</li><li>The result is gallons collected (assuming 100% efficiency; typical is 80-90%).</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>A 1,000 sq ft roof with 1 inch of rain yields about 623 gallons.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Rain Barrel Capacity Formula</h1><p class="muted">, estimate how much rain you can capture.</p><pre class="code"><code>gallons = roof area (sq ft) × rainfall (inches) × 0.623</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Measure the footprint of your roof (length × width).</li><li>Find your average rainfall in inches.</li><li>Multiply roof area by rainfall by 0.623.</li><li>The result is gallons collected (assuming 100% efficiency; typical is 80-90%).</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>A 1,000 sq ft roof with 1 inch of rain yields about 623 gallons.</p></div></div>	-
45.45.0	dashboard_formula/Deck Board Spacing Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Deck Board Spacing Formula</h1><p class="muted"><regex>user_name</regex>, plan your deck boards with the right gap.</p><pre class="code"><code>number of boards = (deck width + gap) / (board width + gap)</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Measure the width of your deck in inches.</li><li>Decide on gap size (typically 1/8 to 1/4 inch).</li><li>Measure the width of one board.</li><li>Plug into formula: (deck width + gap) / (board width + gap).</li><li>Round down to the nearest whole number.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>For a 12-foot deck (144 inches) with 5.5-inch boards and 1/8-inch gap: (144 + 0.125) / (5.5 + 0.125) ≈ 25 boards.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Deck Board Spacing Formula</h1><p class="muted">, plan your deck boards with the right gap.</p><pre class="code"><code>number of boards = (deck width + gap) / (board width + gap)</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Measure the width of your deck in inches.</li><li>Decide on gap size (typically 1/8 to 1/4 inch).</li><li>Measure the width of one board.</li><li>Plug into formula: (deck width + gap) / (board width + gap).</li><li>Round down to the nearest whole number.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>For a 12-foot deck (144 inches) with 5.5-inch boards and 1/8-inch gap: (144 + 0.125) / (5.5 + 0.125) ≈ 25 boards.</p></div></div>	-
45.46.0	dashboard_formula/Solar Panel Tilt Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Solar Panel Tilt Formula</h1><p class="muted"><regex>user_name</regex>, find the ideal tilt for your solar panels based on latitude.</p><pre class="code"><code>tilt angle = latitude × 0.76 + 3.1</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Find your latitude in degrees.</li><li>Multiply by 0.76.</li><li>Add 3.1.</li><li>The result is the optimal year-round tilt angle.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>For latitude 40°, tilt ≈ 40 × 0.76 + 3.1 = 33.5°.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Solar Panel Tilt Formula</h1><p class="muted">, find the ideal tilt for your solar panels based on latitude.</p><pre class="code"><code>tilt angle = latitude × 0.76 + 3.1</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Find your latitude in degrees.</li><li>Multiply by 0.76.</li><li>Add 3.1.</li><li>The result is the optimal year-round tilt angle.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>For latitude 40°, tilt ≈ 40 × 0.76 + 3.1 = 33.5°.</p></div></div>	-
45.47.0	dashboard_formula/Hiking Time Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Hiking Time Formula</h1><p class="muted"><regex>user_name</regex>, use Naismith's Rule to estimate your hike time.</p><pre class="code"><code>time = (distance / 3 mph) + (elevation gain / 2000 ft) hours</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Measure your route distance in miles.</li><li>Divide by 3 mph for base time.</li><li>Measure elevation gain in feet.</li><li>Divide by 2000 to get hours added for climbing.</li><li>Add both for total time.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>A 6-mile hike with 1,500 ft gain: (6/3) + (1500/2000) = 2.75 hours.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Hiking Time Formula</h1><p class="muted">, use Naismith's Rule to estimate your hike time.</p><pre class="code"><code>time = (distance / 3 mph) + (elevation gain / 2000 ft) hours</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Measure your route distance in miles.</li><li>Divide by 3 mph for base time.</li><li>Measure elevation gain in feet.</li><li>Divide by 2000 to get hours added for climbing.</li><li>Add both for total time.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>A 6-mile hike with 1,500 ft gain: (6/3) + (1500/2000) = 2.75 hours.</p></div></div>	-
45.48.0	dashboard_formula/Spice Blend Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Spice Blend Formula</h1><p class="muted">Four parts base, two parts heat, one part aromatics. Scale to taste.</p><pre class="code"><code>base : heat : aromatics = 4 : 2 : 1</code></pre><ol class="list"><li><span class="badge accent">Step 1</span> Pick a base: paprika, cumin, or garlic powder.</li><li><span class="badge accent">Step 2</span> Choose heat: chili powder, cayenne, or black pepper.</li><li><span class="badge accent">Step 3</span> Add aromatics: oregano, thyme, or ginger.</li><li><span class="badge accent">Step 4</span> Mix, taste, adjust. A pinch of salt never hurts.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Tip: bloom it</summary><div class="body"><p>Toast whole spices first, then grind. The flavour jumps.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Spice Blend Formula</h1><p class="muted">Four parts base, two parts heat, one part aromatics. Scale to taste.</p><pre class="code"><code>base : heat : aromatics = 4 : 2 : 1</code></pre><ol class="list"><li><span class="badge accent">Step 1</span> Pick a base: paprika, cumin, or garlic powder.</li><li><span class="badge accent">Step 2</span> Choose heat: chili powder, cayenne, or black pepper.</li><li><span class="badge accent">Step 3</span> Add aromatics: oregano, thyme, or ginger.</li><li><span class="badge accent">Step 4</span> Mix, taste, adjust. A pinch of salt never hurts.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Tip: bloom it</summary><div class="body"><p>Toast whole spices first, then grind. The flavour jumps.</p></div></details></div>	-
45.49.0	dashboard_formula/Shelf Sag Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Shelf Sag Formula</h1><p class="muted">A quick rule of thumb for wood shelves. Keep the load under the limit.</p><pre class="code"><code>max_load = (width_cm * thickness_cm^2) / span_cm * 8</code></pre><ol class="list"><li><span class="badge accent">Step 1</span> Measure width, thickness, and span in cm.</li><li><span class="badge accent">Step 2</span> Plug into the formula. Use inches? Convert first.</li><li><span class="badge accent">Step 3</span> If the load is close, add a centre support.</li></ol><aside class="callout warn">Hardwood is stiffer than pine. Treat this as a starting point.</aside></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Shelf Sag Formula</h1><p class="muted">A quick rule of thumb for wood shelves. Keep the load under the limit.</p><pre class="code"><code>max_load = (width_cm * thickness_cm^2) / span_cm * 8</code></pre><ol class="list"><li><span class="badge accent">Step 1</span> Measure width, thickness, and span in cm.</li><li><span class="badge accent">Step 2</span> Plug into the formula. Use inches? Convert first.</li><li><span class="badge accent">Step 3</span> If the load is close, add a centre support.</li></ol><aside class="callout warn">Hardwood is stiffer than pine. Treat this as a starting point.</aside></div>	-
45.50.0	dashboard_formula/Pizza Stone Preheat Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Pizza Stone Preheat Formula</h1><p class="muted">Give the stone time to soak up heat, not just the air.</p><pre class="code"><code>preheat_min = thickness_in * 15</code></pre><ol class="list"><li><span class="badge accent">Step 1</span> Measure your stone's thickness in inches.</li><li><span class="badge accent">Step 2</span> Multiply by 15 for a rough minute count.</li><li><span class="badge accent">Step 3</span> Add 5 minutes if the oven is cold.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Check with an IR thermometer</summary><div class="body"><p>Surface should read 450–500°F before the first pie.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Pizza Stone Preheat Formula</h1><p class="muted">Give the stone time to soak up heat, not just the air.</p><pre class="code"><code>preheat_min = thickness_in * 15</code></pre><ol class="list"><li><span class="badge accent">Step 1</span> Measure your stone's thickness in inches.</li><li><span class="badge accent">Step 2</span> Multiply by 15 for a rough minute count.</li><li><span class="badge accent">Step 3</span> Add 5 minutes if the oven is cold.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Check with an IR thermometer</summary><div class="body"><p>Surface should read 450–500°F before the first pie.</p></div></details></div>	-
45.51.0	dashboard_formula/Bike Tire Pressure Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Bike Tire Pressure Formula</h1><p class="muted">A simple starting point for road tires. Adjust for comfort and grip.</p><pre class="code"><code>psi = rider_weight_lb / 7 + 30</code></pre><ol class="list"><li><span class="badge accent">Step 1</span> Weigh yourself with gear.</li><li><span class="badge accent">Step 2</span> Divide by 7, then add 30.</li><li><span class="badge accent">Step 3</span> Drop 5 psi for wet roads, add 5 for speed.</li></ol><aside class="callout info">Tubeless? Run 5–10 psi lower.</aside></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Bike Tire Pressure Formula</h1><p class="muted">A simple starting point for road tires. Adjust for comfort and grip.</p><pre class="code"><code>psi = rider_weight_lb / 7 + 30</code></pre><ol class="list"><li><span class="badge accent">Step 1</span> Weigh yourself with gear.</li><li><span class="badge accent">Step 2</span> Divide by 7, then add 30.</li><li><span class="badge accent">Step 3</span> Drop 5 psi for wet roads, add 5 for speed.</li></ol><aside class="callout info">Tubeless? Run 5–10 psi lower.</aside></div>	-
45.52.0	dashboard_formula/Nail Polish Dry Time Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Nail Polish Dry Time Formula</h1><p class="muted">Thin coats dry faster. Thick ones look great then smudge.</p><pre class="code"><code>dry_min = coats * 2 + humidity_pct / 20</code></pre><ol class="list"><li><span class="badge accent">Step 1</span> Count your coats. Usually two.</li><li><span class="badge accent">Step 2</span> Estimate humidity, divide by 20.</li><li><span class="badge accent">Step 3</span> Multiply coats by 2 and add humidity factor.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Speed it up</summary><div class="body"><p>Dip fingers in ice water for 3 minutes. It sets the polish.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Nail Polish Dry Time Formula</h1><p class="muted">Thin coats dry faster. Thick ones look great then smudge.</p><pre class="code"><code>dry_min = coats * 2 + humidity_pct / 20</code></pre><ol class="list"><li><span class="badge accent">Step 1</span> Count your coats. Usually two.</li><li><span class="badge accent">Step 2</span> Estimate humidity, divide by 20.</li><li><span class="badge accent">Step 3</span> Multiply coats by 2 and add humidity factor.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Speed it up</summary><div class="body"><p>Dip fingers in ice water for 3 minutes. It sets the polish.</p></div></details></div>	-
45.53.0	dashboard_formula/Baby Formula Scoop Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Baby Formula Scoop Formula</h1><p class="muted">Always follow the tin. This is just a memory aid.</p><pre class="code"><code>scoops = water_fl_oz / 2</code></pre><ol class="list"><li><span class="badge accent">Step 1</span> Measure water first, not powder.</li><li><span class="badge accent">Step 2</span> One unpacked scoop per 2 fl oz.</li><li><span class="badge accent">Step 3</span> Shake well, test temperature.</li></ol><aside class="callout bad">Never dilute or concentrate. Check the label every time.</aside></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Baby Formula Scoop Formula</h1><p class="muted">Always follow the tin. This is just a memory aid.</p><pre class="code"><code>scoops = water_fl_oz / 2</code></pre><ol class="list"><li><span class="badge accent">Step 1</span> Measure water first, not powder.</li><li><span class="badge accent">Step 2</span> One unpacked scoop per 2 fl oz.</li><li><span class="badge accent">Step 3</span> Shake well, test temperature.</li></ol><aside class="callout bad">Never dilute or concentrate. Check the label every time.</aside></div>	-
45.54.0	dashboard_formula/Paper Airplane Glide Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Paper Airplane Glide Formula</h1><p class="muted">Throw angle, not brute force, buys distance.</p><pre class="code"><code>glide_distance ≈ (height × glide_ratio) + (throw_speed² / 2g) × launch_ratio</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Fold a clean dart with sharp creases.</li><li>Launch at 10–15° above horizontal.</li><li>Aim for level, not upward.</li><li>Step outside to kill indoor air currents.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>Great folds still fail in a crosswind. Check the weather before judging the design.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Paper Airplane Glide Formula</h1><p class="muted">Throw angle, not brute force, buys distance.</p><pre class="code"><code>glide_distance ≈ (height × glide_ratio) + (throw_speed² / 2g) × launch_ratio</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Fold a clean dart with sharp creases.</li><li>Launch at 10–15° above horizontal.</li><li>Aim for level, not upward.</li><li>Step outside to kill indoor air currents.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>Great folds still fail in a crosswind. Check the weather before judging the design.</p></div></div>	-
45.55.0	dashboard_formula/Sock Matching Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Sock Matching Formula</h1><p class="muted">The math is simple; the dryer is not.</p><pre class="code"><code>pairs = floor(total_socks / 2)odds = total_socks − (pairs × 2)</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Empty the basket and count every sock.</li><li>Sort into piles by colour and pattern.</li><li>Pair what you can.</li><li>Lay odds aside for the next cycle.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p>Those odd socks aren't gone. They'll resurface the moment you replace them.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Sock Matching Formula</h1><p class="muted">The math is simple; the dryer is not.</p><pre class="code"><code>pairs = floor(total_socks / 2)odds = total_socks − (pairs × 2)</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Empty the basket and count every sock.</li><li>Sort into piles by colour and pattern.</li><li>Pair what you can.</li><li>Lay odds aside for the next cycle.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p>Those odd socks aren't gone. They'll resurface the moment you replace them.</p></div></div>	-
45.56.0	dashboard_formula/Watering Can Coverage Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Watering Can Coverage Formula</h1><p class="muted">Most gardens want about a gallon per square foot per week.</p><pre class="code"><code>coverage_ft² = can_volume_gal × 1.5</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Check your can's capacity (a 2-gal can is typical).</li><li>Measure the bed's square footage.</li><li>Divide bed area by coverage per can.</li><li>Refill and repeat until the soil is damp 6" down.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p>Water early, water deep. Surface sprinkles don't reach roots.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Watering Can Coverage Formula</h1><p class="muted">Most gardens want about a gallon per square foot per week.</p><pre class="code"><code>coverage_ft² = can_volume_gal × 1.5</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Check your can's capacity (a 2-gal can is typical).</li><li>Measure the bed's square footage.</li><li>Divide bed area by coverage per can.</li><li>Refill and repeat until the soil is damp 6" down.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p>Water early, water deep. Surface sprinkles don't reach roots.</p></div></div>	-
45.57.0	dashboard_formula/Shower Time Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Shower Time Formula</h1><p class="muted">A long shower wastes more than time.</p><pre class="code"><code>gallons_used = flow_rate_gpm × minutes</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Confirm your showerhead's flow rate (usually 2.5 gpm).</li><li>Time a typical shower.</li><li>Multiply rate by minutes.</li><li>Swap to a low-flow head to cut usage.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>Ten minutes feels short until you see it in gallons.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Shower Time Formula</h1><p class="muted">A long shower wastes more than time.</p><pre class="code"><code>gallons_used = flow_rate_gpm × minutes</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Confirm your showerhead's flow rate (usually 2.5 gpm).</li><li>Time a typical shower.</li><li>Multiply rate by minutes.</li><li>Swap to a low-flow head to cut usage.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p>Ten minutes feels short until you see it in gallons.</p></div></div>	-
45.58.0	dashboard_formula/Microwave Popcorn Time Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Microwave Popcorn Time Formula</h1><p class="muted">The bag tells you nothing. Your ears do.</p><pre class="code"><code>stop_time = time_when_pops_drop_below_2_sec</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Set the microwave to 3–4 minutes.</li><li>Stay close and listen.</li><li>When pops slow to 2+ seconds apart, stop.</li><li>Pour immediately to avoid steam sogginess.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p>Burnt popcorn smells like regret. Stop early if unsure.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Microwave Popcorn Time Formula</h1><p class="muted">The bag tells you nothing. Your ears do.</p><pre class="code"><code>stop_time = time_when_pops_drop_below_2_sec</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Set the microwave to 3–4 minutes.</li><li>Stay close and listen.</li><li>When pops slow to 2+ seconds apart, stop.</li><li>Pour immediately to avoid steam sogginess.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p>Burnt popcorn smells like regret. Stop early if unsure.</p></div></div>	-
45.59.0	dashboard_formula/Grocery Budget Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Grocery Budget Formula</h1><p class="muted">A budget is just a plan with a receipt.</p><pre class="code"><code>grocery_budget = monthly_income × 0.10 to 0.15</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Find your monthly take-home pay.</li><li>Multiply by 0.10 for a lean budget, 0.15 for comfortable.</li><li>Divide by the number of shopping trips.</li><li>Track every receipt for one month.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p>Cash envelopes still work. They're just harder to overspend.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Grocery Budget Formula</h1><p class="muted">A budget is just a plan with a receipt.</p><pre class="code"><code>grocery_budget = monthly_income × 0.10 to 0.15</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Find your monthly take-home pay.</li><li>Multiply by 0.10 for a lean budget, 0.15 for comfortable.</li><li>Divide by the number of shopping trips.</li><li>Track every receipt for one month.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p>Cash envelopes still work. They're just harder to overspend.</p></div></div>	-
45.60.0	dashboard_formula/Ice Cream Churn Ratio Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Ice Cream Churn Ratio</h1><p class="muted">Here you go, <regex>user_name</regex> — cold math, warm outcome.</p><pre class="code"><code>base = canister_qt * 0.5   # cups of mixcream: milk = 2 : 1sugar = base * 0.18  cup</code></pre><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">2 qt canister</span><span class="value">4<span class="unit"> cups mix</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Sugar</span><span class="value">0.7<span class="unit"> cup</span></span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Chill the base 4+ hours — cold churns faster and creamier.</li><li>Fill the canister only half full; mix expands as it freezes.</li><li>Salt the ice 1 part rock salt to 6 parts ice for a colder bath.</li><li>Churn until it looks like soft-serve, then freeze 2 hours to firm.</li></ol><div class="callout info">More fat, less ice. Swap half the milk for cream if it turns out gritty.</div><details class="acc"><summary>Why half full?</summary><div class="body">Air gets folded in as it churns. Overfilled canisters stall the dasher and make a buttery mess.</div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Ice Cream Churn Ratio</h1><p class="muted">Here you go,  — cold math, warm outcome.</p><pre class="code"><code>base = canister_qt * 0.5   # cups of mixcream: milk = 2 : 1sugar = base * 0.18  cup</code></pre><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">2 qt canister</span><span class="value">4<span class="unit"> cups mix</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Sugar</span><span class="value">0.7<span class="unit"> cup</span></span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Chill the base 4+ hours — cold churns faster and creamier.</li><li>Fill the canister only half full; mix expands as it freezes.</li><li>Salt the ice 1 part rock salt to 6 parts ice for a colder bath.</li><li>Churn until it looks like soft-serve, then freeze 2 hours to firm.</li></ol><div class="callout info">More fat, less ice. Swap half the milk for cream if it turns out gritty.</div><details class="acc"><summary>Why half full?</summary><div class="body">Air gets folded in as it churns. Overfilled canisters stall the dasher and make a buttery mess.</div></details></div>	-
45.61.0	dashboard_formula/Fence Post Spacing Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Fence Post Spacing</h1><p class="muted">Good fences start with good arithmetic, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><pre class="code"><code>panels = round(run_ft / 8)posts = panels + 1spacing = run_ft / panels   # ft</code></pre><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">100 ft run</span><span class="value">13<span class="unit"> posts</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Spacing</span><span class="value">8.3<span class="unit"> ft</span></span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Measure the run in feet, corner to corner.</li><li>Divide by your panel width (8 ft is standard).</li><li>Round to a whole number of panels, then add one post.</li><li>Add a post for every corner, gate, or slope break.</li></ol><div class="callout warn">Frost depth sets hole depth — 1/3 of post length in the ground is the floor, not the goal.</div><details class="acc"><summary>Corner posts</summary><div class="body">Brace corners with a diagonal and set them in concrete. Line posts can go in gravel for drainage.</div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Fence Post Spacing</h1><p class="muted">Good fences start with good arithmetic, .</p><pre class="code"><code>panels = round(run_ft / 8)posts = panels + 1spacing = run_ft / panels   # ft</code></pre><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">100 ft run</span><span class="value">13<span class="unit"> posts</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Spacing</span><span class="value">8.3<span class="unit"> ft</span></span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Measure the run in feet, corner to corner.</li><li>Divide by your panel width (8 ft is standard).</li><li>Round to a whole number of panels, then add one post.</li><li>Add a post for every corner, gate, or slope break.</li></ol><div class="callout warn">Frost depth sets hole depth — 1/3 of post length in the ground is the floor, not the goal.</div><details class="acc"><summary>Corner posts</summary><div class="body">Brace corners with a diagonal and set them in concrete. Line posts can go in gravel for drainage.</div></details></div>	-
45.62.0	dashboard_formula/Kettlebell Swing Volume Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Kettlebell Swing Volume</h1><p class="muted">Swing smart, <regex>user_name</regex>. Hinges beat heroics.</p><pre class="code"><code>volume = sets * reps * weight_kgweekly = volume * sessions</code></pre><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Per session</span><span class="value">1,200<span class="unit"> kg</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Per week (3x)</span><span class="value">3,600<span class="unit"> kg</span></span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Start at 10 sets of 10 with a weight you can snap, not grind.</li><li>Rest 45–60 seconds between sets; form first, clock second.</li><li>Add a set per week until 15, then bump the bell.</li><li>Deload every fourth week by cutting volume in half.</li></ol><div class="callout bad">Sharp lower-back fatigue means you're pulling with arms, not hinging hips.</div><details class="acc"><summary>Talk test</summary><div class="body">If you can't say a short sentence between sets, you're going too heavy for volume work.</div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Kettlebell Swing Volume</h1><p class="muted">Swing smart, . Hinges beat heroics.</p><pre class="code"><code>volume = sets * reps * weight_kgweekly = volume * sessions</code></pre><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Per session</span><span class="value">1,200<span class="unit"> kg</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Per week (3x)</span><span class="value">3,600<span class="unit"> kg</span></span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Start at 10 sets of 10 with a weight you can snap, not grind.</li><li>Rest 45–60 seconds between sets; form first, clock second.</li><li>Add a set per week until 15, then bump the bell.</li><li>Deload every fourth week by cutting volume in half.</li></ol><div class="callout bad">Sharp lower-back fatigue means you're pulling with arms, not hinging hips.</div><details class=