45.63.0	dashboard_formula/Chicken Wing Sauce Ratio Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Wing Sauce Ratio</h1><p class="muted">Sauce is chemistry you can eat, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><pre class="code"><code>sauce_cup = wings_lb * 0.33butter = sauce_cup * 0.5hot_sauce = sauce_cup * 0.5</code></pre><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">2 lb wings</span><span class="value">0.66<span class="unit"> cup sauce</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Split</span><span class="value">1:1<span class="unit"> butter : sauce</span></span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Melt butter low and slow — never boil it or it splits.</li><li>Whisk in hot sauce off the heat.</li><li>Toss wings while they're still screaming hot.</li><li>Add a pinch of sugar if the vinegar bites too hard.</li></ol><div class="callout good">Toss, don't dunk. A thin coat clings better than a puddle.</div><details class="acc"><summary>Thicker sauce?</summary><div class="body">Simmer a teaspoon of cornstarch slurry into the butter first, then add the sauce.</div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Wing Sauce Ratio</h1><p class="muted">Sauce is chemistry you can eat, .</p><pre class="code"><code>sauce_cup = wings_lb * 0.33butter = sauce_cup * 0.5hot_sauce = sauce_cup * 0.5</code></pre><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">2 lb wings</span><span class="value">0.66<span class="unit"> cup sauce</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Split</span><span class="value">1:1<span class="unit"> butter : sauce</span></span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Melt butter low and slow — never boil it or it splits.</li><li>Whisk in hot sauce off the heat.</li><li>Toss wings while they're still screaming hot.</li><li>Add a pinch of sugar if the vinegar bites too hard.</li></ol><div class="callout good">Toss, don't dunk. A thin coat clings better than a puddle.</div><details class="acc"><summary>Thicker sauce?</summary><div class="body">Simmer a teaspoon of cornstarch slurry into the butter first, then add the sauce.</div></details></div>	-
45.0.1	dashboard_formula/Gutter Downspout Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Gutter Downspout Count</h1><p class="muted">Water goes where it wants. Give it a plan, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><pre class="code"><code>roof_sqft = footprint * pitch_factordownspouts = ceil(roof_sqft / 800)max_run = 40 ft</code></pre><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">1,600 sq ft roof</span><span class="value">2<span class="unit"> downspouts</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Max gutter run</span><span class="value">40<span class="unit"> ft</span></span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Estimate roof area: footprint times 1.1–1.4 for pitch.</li><li>One downspout per 800 sq ft, minimum two per roof.</li><li>Keep any single run under 40 feet with a slight slope to the outlet.</li><li>Extend discharge 4+ feet from the foundation.</li></ol><div class="callout info">Big roof in a wet climate? Size up the gutter profile before adding more outlets.</div><details class="acc"><summary>Why 800 sq ft?</summary><div class="body">A 2x3 downspout handles roughly that much roof in a typical 1-inch-per-hour storm.</div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Gutter Downspout Count</h1><p class="muted">Water goes where it wants. Give it a plan, .</p><pre class="code"><code>roof_sqft = footprint * pitch_factordownspouts = ceil(roof_sqft / 800)max_run = 40 ft</code></pre><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">1,600 sq ft roof</span><span class="value">2<span class="unit"> downspouts</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Max gutter run</span><span class="value">40<span class="unit"> ft</span></span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Estimate roof area: footprint times 1.1–1.4 for pitch.</li><li>One downspout per 800 sq ft, minimum two per roof.</li><li>Keep any single run under 40 feet with a slight slope to the outlet.</li><li>Extend discharge 4+ feet from the foundation.</li></ol><div class="callout info">Big roof in a wet climate? Size up the gutter profile before adding more outlets.</div><details class="acc"><summary>Why 800 sq ft?</summary><div class="body">A 2x3 downspout handles roughly that much roof in a typical 1-inch-per-hour storm.</div></details></div>	-
45.1.1	dashboard_formula/Charcoal Starter Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Charcoal Starter Amount</h1><p class="muted">Fuel first, fire later, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><pre class="code"><code>lit = grill_diameter_in / 6   # chimney-fullsbriquettes = lit * 90</code></pre><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">22 in kettle</span><span class="value">3.7<span class="unit"> chimney-fulls</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Briquettes</span><span class="value">330<span class="unit"> total</span></span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Fill the chimney, light it, wait for ash-grey tops.</li><li>Bank coals to one side for two-zone cooking.</li><li>Add a half-chimney for anything over 40 minutes.</li><li>Close vents fully when done to snuff and save leftovers.</li></ol><div class="callout good">Leftover briquettes burn again next time — don't dump them.</div><details class="acc"><summary>Lump vs briquettes</summary><div class="body">Lump lights faster and runs hotter; use about 20% less by volume and expect a shorter burn.</div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Charcoal Starter Amount</h1><p class="muted">Fuel first, fire later, .</p><pre class="code"><code>lit = grill_diameter_in / 6   # chimney-fullsbriquettes = lit * 90</code></pre><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">22 in kettle</span><span class="value">3.7<span class="unit"> chimney-fulls</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Briquettes</span><span class="value">330<span class="unit"> total</span></span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Fill the chimney, light it, wait for ash-grey tops.</li><li>Bank coals to one side for two-zone cooking.</li><li>Add a half-chimney for anything over 40 minutes.</li><li>Close vents fully when done to snuff and save leftovers.</li></ol><div class="callout good">Leftover briquettes burn again next time — don't dump them.</div><details class="acc"><summary>Lump vs briquettes</summary><div class="body">Lump lights faster and runs hotter; use about 20% less by volume and expect a shorter burn.</div></details></div>	-
45.2.1	dashboard_formula/Detergent Dose Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Detergent Dose Formula</h1><p class="muted">For <regex>user_name</regex> — less soap than you think. Suds don't clean; water does.</p><pre class="code"><code>dose_ml = load_kg × base × hardness × soilbase:      8 ml/kg  (front loader)           14 ml/kg (top loader)hardness:  ×1 for soft, ×1.4 for hard watersoil:      ×1 light, ×1.5 heavy/greasy</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Weigh the dry load, roughly. A full drum is about 6 kg.</li><li>Multiply by your machine's base rate.</li><li>Bump for hard water if your kettle furs up.</li><li>Bump once more for grease, sweat, or garden dirt.</li><li>Cap it: never fill the cup past the top line.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Quick reference</summary><div class="body"><div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Load</th><th class="num">Front</th><th class="num">Top</th></tr><tr><td>3 kg light</td><td class="num">24 ml</td><td class="num">42 ml</td></tr><tr><td>6 kg normal</td><td class="num">48 ml</td><td class="num">84 ml</td></tr><tr><td>6 kg heavy, hard water</td><td class="num">67 ml</td><td class="num">118 ml</td></tr></table></div></div></details><div class="callout warn"><p>Too much detergent is the usual cause of musty smells and stiff towels. It never rinses out.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Detergent Dose Formula</h1><p class="muted">For  — less soap than you think. Suds don't clean; water does.</p><pre class="code"><code>dose_ml = load_kg × base × hardness × soilbase:      8 ml/kg  (front loader)           14 ml/kg (top loader)hardness:  ×1 for soft, ×1.4 for hard watersoil:      ×1 light, ×1.5 heavy/greasy</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Weigh the dry load, roughly. A full drum is about 6 kg.</li><li>Multiply by your machine's base rate.</li><li>Bump for hard water if your kettle furs up.</li><li>Bump once more for grease, sweat, or garden dirt.</li><li>Cap it: never fill the cup past the top line.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Quick reference</summary><div class="body"><div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Load</th><th class="num">Front</th><th class="num">Top</th></tr><tr><td>3 kg light</td><td class="num">24 ml</td><td class="num">42 ml</td></tr><tr><td>6 kg normal</td><td class="num">48 ml</td><td class="num">84 ml</td></tr><tr><td>6 kg heavy, hard water</td><td class="num">67 ml</td><td class="num">118 ml</td></tr></table></div></div></details><div class="callout warn"><p>Too much detergent is the usual cause of musty smells and stiff towels. It never rinses out.</p></div></div>	-
45.3.1	dashboard_formula/Microwave Defrost Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Microwave Defrost Formula</h1><p class="muted">For <regex>user_name</regex>. Defrost, don't cook — go low and slow, then stand.</p><pre class="code"><code>minutes = weight_g ÷ 100 × factorfactor by power (use 30% defrost setting):  thin cuts / mince      1.4  bone-in pieces         2.2  dense block (stew meat) 2.4plus standing time: 5–10 min, covered</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Weigh the frozen piece. Guessing is fine, be generous.</li><li>Divide grams by 100, multiply by the factor.</li><li>Set power to 30%, not full. Full power cooks the edges grey.</li><li>Stop halfway, separate anything stuck together, flip it.</li><li>Stand 5–10 minutes under a plate so heat finishes the middle.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Two examples</summary><div class="body"><div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Item</th><th class="num">Weight</th><th class="num">Time</th></tr><tr><td>Chicken breasts (2)</td><td class="num">400 g</td><td class="num">5.6 min</td></tr><tr><td>Beef mince block</td><td class="num">500 g</td><td class="num">7 min</td></tr></table></div></div></details><div class="callout bad"><p>Never refreeze meat thawed at room temperature. If in doubt, cook it now.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Microwave Defrost Formula</h1><p class="muted">For . Defrost, don't cook — go low and slow, then stand.</p><pre class="code"><code>minutes = weight_g ÷ 100 × factorfactor by power (use 30% defrost setting):  thin cuts / mince      1.4  bone-in pieces         2.2  dense block (stew meat) 2.4plus standing time: 5–10 min, covered</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Weigh the frozen piece. Guessing is fine, be generous.</li><li>Divide grams by 100, multiply by the factor.</li><li>Set power to 30%, not full. Full power cooks the edges grey.</li><li>Stop halfway, separate anything stuck together, flip it.</li><li>Stand 5–10 minutes under a plate so heat finishes the middle.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Two examples</summary><div class="body"><div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Item</th><th class="num">Weight</th><th class="num">Time</th></tr><tr><td>Chicken breasts (2)</td><td class="num">400 g</td><td class="num">5.6 min</td></tr><tr><td>Beef mince block</td><td class="num">500 g</td><td class="num">7 min</td></tr></table></div></div></details><div class="callout bad"><p>Never refreeze meat thawed at room temperature. If in doubt, cook it now.</p></div></div>	-
45.4.1	dashboard_formula/Curtain Width Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Curtain Width Formula</h1><p class="muted">For <regex>user_name</regex>. Flat curtains look mean. Gather is everything.</p><pre class="code"><code>fabric_width = pole_width × fullness + 10 cm (returns)fullness:  1.5  eyelet, relaxed           2.0  pencil pleat (standard)           2.5  gathered, plushfabric_length = drop + 15 cm (hems &amp; heading)</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Measure the pole or track, tip to tip, not the window.</li><li>Multiply by the fullness factor for your heading type.</li><li>Add 10 cm so the edges turn back to the wall.</li><li>Measure drop: from eyelet line or hook line to where you want them to sit.</li><li>Add 15 cm for hems. Repeat for each panel, dividing the width first.</li></ol><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Pole</span><span class="value">180 <span class="unit">cm</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Fabric per panel</span><span class="value">190 <span class="unit">cm</span></span></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Patterned fabric?</summary><div class="body"><p>Add one full pattern repeat per length so the motifs line up across the pair. It hurts to buy it. It hurts more not to.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Curtain Width Formula</h1><p class="muted">For . Flat curtains look mean. Gather is everything.</p><pre class="code"><code>fabric_width = pole_width × fullness + 10 cm (returns)fullness:  1.5  eyelet, relaxed           2.0  pencil pleat (standard)           2.5  gathered, plushfabric_length = drop + 15 cm (hems &amp; heading)</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Measure the pole or track, tip to tip, not the window.</li><li>Multiply by the fullness factor for your heading type.</li><li>Add 10 cm so the edges turn back to the wall.</li><li>Measure drop: from eyelet line or hook line to where you want them to sit.</li><li>Add 15 cm for hems. Repeat for each panel, dividing the width first.</li></ol><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Pole</span><span class="value">180 <span class="unit">cm</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Fabric per panel</span><span class="value">190 <span class="unit">cm</span></span></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Patterned fabric?</summary><div class="body"><p>Add one full pattern repeat per length so the motifs line up across the pair. It hurts to buy it. It hurts more not to.</p></div></details></div>	-
45.5.1	dashboard_formula/Bath Fill Time Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Bath Fill Time Formula</h1><p class="muted">For <regex>user_name</regex>. Know before you pull the plug on a cold surprise.</p><pre class="code"><code>fill_minutes = tub_litres ÷ flow_rate_lpmflow_rate: test it — fill a 10 L bucket,note the seconds, then 600 ÷ seconds = lpm</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Fill a bucket for exactly 10 litres and time it.</li><li>Divide 600 by that time in seconds. That's litres per minute.</li><li>Estimate tub volume: length × width × depth × 0.6.</li><li>Divide tub litres by flow rate.</li><li>Check your hot cylinder can spare it — a bath takes 80–120 L.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Worked example</summary><div class="body"><p>Tub is 140 × 70 × 45 cm ≈ 265 L gross, so about 160 L filled. Bucket test: 20 seconds → 30 lpm. 160 ÷ 30 ≈ 5.3 minutes. Pleasant.</p></div></details><div class="callout info"><p>Run hot first, then cold. Mixing at the tap loses more heat than you'd like.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Bath Fill Time Formula</h1><p class="muted">For . Know before you pull the plug on a cold surprise.</p><pre class="code"><code>fill_minutes = tub_litres ÷ flow_rate_lpmflow_rate: test it — fill a 10 L bucket,note the seconds, then 600 ÷ seconds = lpm</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Fill a bucket for exactly 10 litres and time it.</li><li>Divide 600 by that time in seconds. That's litres per minute.</li><li>Estimate tub volume: length × width × depth × 0.6.</li><li>Divide tub litres by flow rate.</li><li>Check your hot cylinder can spare it — a bath takes 80–120 L.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Worked example</summary><div class="body"><p>Tub is 140 × 70 × 45 cm ≈ 265 L gross, so about 160 L filled. Bucket test: 20 seconds → 30 lpm. 160 ÷ 30 ≈ 5.3 minutes. Pleasant.</p></div></details><div class="callout info"><p>Run hot first, then cold. Mixing at the tap loses more heat than you'd like.</p></div></div>	-
45.6.1	dashboard_formula/Tea Brew Ratio Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Tea Brew Ratio Formula</h1><p class="muted">For <regex>user_name</regex>. Leaf, water, time, heat. Get the heat wrong and the rest is wasted.</p><pre class="code"><code>leaf_g = water_ml × ratioratio:  1.0 g / 100 ml  delicate green &amp; white        1.5 g / 100 ml  oolong        2.0 g / 100 ml  black &amp; pu'er        2.5 g / 100 ml  herbal (no leaf, real weight)</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Boil the kettle, then let it fall to the right heat.</li><li>Weigh the leaf into the pot or infuser.</li><li>Pour, and start a timer.</li><li>Strain fully. Leaf left in keeps steeping bitter.</li><li>Re-steep good leaf — most oolongs give four or more.</li></ol><div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Type</th><th class="num">Temp</th><th class="num">Time</th></tr><tr><td>White</td><td class="num">80 °C</td><td class="num">3 min</td></tr><tr><td>Green</td><td class="num">75 °C</td><td class="num">2 min</td></tr><tr><td>Oolong</td><td class="num">90 °C</td><td class="num">3 min</td></tr><tr><td>Black</td><td class="num">95 °C</td><td class="num">4 min</td></tr></table></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Tea Brew Ratio Formula</h1><p class="muted">For . Leaf, water, time, heat. Get the heat wrong and the rest is wasted.</p><pre class="code"><code>leaf_g = water_ml × ratioratio:  1.0 g / 100 ml  delicate green &amp; white        1.5 g / 100 ml  oolong        2.0 g / 100 ml  black &amp; pu'er        2.5 g / 100 ml  herbal (no leaf, real weight)</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Boil the kettle, then let it fall to the right heat.</li><li>Weigh the leaf into the pot or infuser.</li><li>Pour, and start a timer.</li><li>Strain fully. Leaf left in keeps steeping bitter.</li><li>Re-steep good leaf — most oolongs give four or more.</li></ol><div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Type</th><th class="num">Temp</th><th class="num">Time</th></tr><tr><td>White</td><td class="num">80 °C</td><td class="num">3 min</td></tr><tr><td>Green</td><td class="num">75 °C</td><td class="num">2 min</td></tr><tr><td>Oolong</td><td class="num">90 °C</td><td class="num">3 min</td></tr><tr><td>Black</td><td class="num">95 °C</td><td class="num">4 min</td></tr></table></div></div>	-
45.7.1	dashboard_formula/Sleep Window Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Sleep Window Formula</h1><p class="muted">For <regex>user_name</regex>. Wake at the end of a cycle, not the middle of one.</p><pre class="code"><code>bedtime = wake_time − (cycles × 90 min) − 15 mincycles: 5 = 7.5 h  (most adults)        6 = 9 h    (athletes, teenagers)        4 = 6 h    (short-term only)</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Pick your alarm time.</li><li>Count back in 90-minute blocks from it.</li><li>Subtract 15 minutes for falling asleep.</li><li>Choose 5 cycles for steady weeks, 6 if you're training hard.</li><li>Keep the swap within an hour, even at weekends.</li></ol><div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Alarm</th><th class="num">5 cycles</th><th class="num">6 cycles</th></tr><tr><td>06:00</td><td class="num">22:15</td><td class="num">20:45</td></tr><tr><td>07:00</td><td class="num">23:15</td><td class="num">21:45</td></tr><tr><td>08:00</td><td class="num">00:15</td><td class="num">22:45</td></tr></table></div><div class="callout good"><p>Waking groggy usually means a 4- or 5-cycle alarm landed mid-cycle. Shift by 20 minutes before you shift by an hour.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Sleep Window Formula</h1><p class="muted">For . Wake at the end of a cycle, not the middle of one.</p><pre class="code"><code>bedtime = wake_time − (cycles × 90 min) − 15 mincycles: 5 = 7.5 h  (most adults)        6 = 9 h    (athletes, teenagers)        4 = 6 h    (short-term only)</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Pick your alarm time.</li><li>Count back in 90-minute blocks from it.</li><li>Subtract 15 minutes for falling asleep.</li><li>Choose 5 cycles for steady weeks, 6 if you're training hard.</li><li>Keep the swap within an hour, even at weekends.</li></ol><div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Alarm</th><th class="num">5 cycles</th><th class="num">6 cycles</th></tr><tr><td>06:00</td><td class="num">22:15</td><td class="num">20:45</td></tr><tr><td>07:00</td><td class="num">23:15</td><td class="num">21:45</td></tr><tr><td>08:00</td><td class="num">00:15</td><td class="num">22:45</td></tr></table></div><div class="callout good"><p>Waking groggy usually means a 4- or 5-cycle alarm landed mid-cycle. Shift by 20 minutes before you shift by an hour.</p></div></div>	-
45.8.1	dashboard_formula/Wheelchair Ramp Slope Formula	<div class="umi-page">  <div class="card">    <h1 class="card-title">Wheelchair Ramp Slope Formula</h1>    <pre class="code"><code>slope_ratio = rise / runrun_needed = rise × slope_target</code></pre>    <p class="muted t-small">Rise is the step height. Run is the horizontal length. Aim for 1:12 when you can, 1:8 only for very short rises.</p>    <div class="grid two">      <div class="stat">        <div class="label">Typical rise</div>        <div class="value">6<span class="unit">in</span></div>      </div>      <div class="stat">        <div class="label">Run for 1:12</div>        <div class="value">72<span class="unit">in</span></div>      </div>    </div>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Measure the total rise in inches.</li>    <li>Pick a safe ratio. 1:12 is the standard for long ramps.</li>    <li>Multiply rise by the ratio to get the run.</li>    <li>Add a level landing at the top if the ramp is longer than 30 feet.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout info">    <p><span class="badge info"><i class="dot"></i>check</span> For a 6-inch step at 1:12, you need 72 inches of run—about 6 feet.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <div class="card">    <h1 class="card-title">Wheelchair Ramp Slope Formula</h1>    <pre class="code"><code>slope_ratio = rise / runrun_needed = rise × slope_target</code></pre>    <p class="muted t-small">Rise is the step height. Run is the horizontal length. Aim for 1:12 when you can, 1:8 only for very short rises.</p>    <div class="grid two">      <div class="stat">        <div class="label">Typical rise</div>        <div class="value">6<span class="unit">in</span></div>      </div>      <div class="stat">        <div class="label">Run for 1:12</div>        <div class="value">72<span class="unit">in</span></div>      </div>    </div>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Measure the total rise in inches.</li>    <li>Pick a safe ratio. 1:12 is the standard for long ramps.</li>    <li>Multiply rise by the ratio to get the run.</li>    <li>Add a level landing at the top if the ramp is longer than 30 feet.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout info">    <p><span class="badge info"><i class="dot"></i>check</span> For a 6-inch step at 1:12, you need 72 inches of run—about 6 feet.</p>  </div></div>	-
45.9.1	dashboard_formula/Egg Boiling Time Formula	<div class="umi-page">  <div class="card">    <h1 class="card-title">Egg Boiling Time Formula</h1>    <pre class="code"><code>time = base_time + (altitude_ft / 1000) × 1.2base_time: soft=6, jammy=8, hard=10 minutes</code></pre>    <p class="muted t-small">Start with boiling water. The altitude bump adds about a minute per 1000 feet if you're up high.</p>  </div>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat">      <div class="label">Soft boil</div>      <div class="value">6<span class="unit">min</span></div>    </div>    <div class="stat">      <div class="label">Hard boil</div>      <div class="value">10<span class="unit">min</span></div>    </div>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Bring water to a rolling boil.</li>    <li>Lower eggs in gently with a spoon.</li>    <li>Set your timer for the doneness you want.</li>    <li>Move eggs to an ice bath to stop cooking.</li>  </ol></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <div class="card">    <h1 class="card-title">Egg Boiling Time Formula</h1>    <pre class="code"><code>time = base_time + (altitude_ft / 1000) × 1.2base_time: soft=6, jammy=8, hard=10 minutes</code></pre>    <p class="muted t-small">Start with boiling water. The altitude bump adds about a minute per 1000 feet if you're up high.</p>  </div>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat">      <div class="label">Soft boil</div>      <div class="value">6<span class="unit">min</span></div>    </div>    <div class="stat">      <div class="label">Hard boil</div>      <div class="value">10<span class="unit">min</span></div>    </div>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Bring water to a rolling boil.</li>    <li>Lower eggs in gently with a spoon.</li>    <li>Set your timer for the doneness you want.</li>    <li>Move eggs to an ice bath to stop cooking.</li>  </ol></div>	-
45.10.1	dashboard_formula/Sourdough Starter Feed Ratio	<div class="umi-page">  <div class="card">    <h1 class="card-title">Sourdough Starter Feed Ratio</h1>    <pre class="code"><code>new_flour = starter_weight × ratio_flournew_water = new_flour × hydration_percent</code></pre>    <p class="muted t-small">A 1:1:1 ratio means equal parts starter, flour, and water. A 1:5:5 is a bigger meal for a hungry starter.</p>  </div>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat">      <div class="label">Starter</div>      <div class="value">50<span class="unit">g</span></div>    </div>    <div class="stat">      <div class="label">1:2:2 feed</div>      <div class="value">100<span class="unit">g</span></div>    </div>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Weigh your starter in a clean jar.</li>    <li>Decide your ratio: 1:2:2 for daily, 1:5:5 if it's been a while.</li>    <li>Add the matching weight of flour and water.</li>    <li>Stir, cover, and let it rise until doubled.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout good">    <p><span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>tip</span> If your starter smells like acetone, it's hungry. Give it a bigger feed.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <div class="card">    <h1 class="card-title">Sourdough Starter Feed Ratio</h1>    <pre class="code"><code>new_flour = starter_weight × ratio_flournew_water = new_flour × hydration_percent</code></pre>    <p class="muted t-small">A 1:1:1 ratio means equal parts starter, flour, and water. A 1:5:5 is a bigger meal for a hungry starter.</p>  </div>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat">      <div class="label">Starter</div>      <div class="value">50<span class="unit">g</span></div>    </div>    <div class="stat">      <div class="label">1:2:2 feed</div>      <div class="value">100<span class="unit">g</span></div>    </div>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Weigh your starter in a clean jar.</li>    <li>Decide your ratio: 1:2:2 for daily, 1:5:5 if it's been a while.</li>    <li>Add the matching weight of flour and water.</li>    <li>Stir, cover, and let it rise until doubled.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout good">    <p><span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>tip</span> If your starter smells like acetone, it's hungry. Give it a bigger feed.</p>  </div></div>	-
45.11.1	dashboard_formula/Yarn Length Formula	<div class="umi-page">  <div class="card">    <h1 class="card-title">Yarn Length Formula</h1>    <pre class="code"><code>total_yards = stitches × rows × stitch_yardagestitch_yardage ≈ 0.02 for worsted, 0.04 for bulky</code></pre>    <p class="muted t-small">This is a rough estimate. Always buy one extra skein for safety—dye lots are fickle.</p>  </div>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat">      <div class="label">Scarf</div>      <div class="value">400<span class="unit">yd</span></div>    </div>    <div class="stat">      <div class="label">Hat</div>      <div class="value">150<span class="unit">yd</span></div>    </div>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Swatch a 4-inch square and count stitches and rows.</li>    <li>Multiply by the total project dimensions.</li>    <li>Multiply by your stitch yardage factor.</li>    <li>Round up to the nearest skein.</li>  </ol></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <div class="card">    <h1 class="card-title">Yarn Length Formula</h1>    <pre class="code"><code>total_yards = stitches × rows × stitch_yardagestitch_yardage ≈ 0.02 for worsted, 0.04 for bulky</code></pre>    <p class="muted t-small">This is a rough estimate. Always buy one extra skein for safety—dye lots are fickle.</p>  </div>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat">      <div class="label">Scarf</div>      <div class="value">400<span class="unit">yd</span></div>    </div>    <div class="stat">      <div class="label">Hat</div>      <div class="value">150<span class="unit">yd</span></div>    </div>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Swatch a 4-inch square and count stitches and rows.</li>    <li>Multiply by the total project dimensions.</li>    <li>Multiply by your stitch yardage factor.</li>    <li>Round up to the nearest skein.</li>  </ol></div>	-
45.12.1	dashboard_formula/Dive Mask Fog Formula	<div class="umi-page">  <div class="card">    <h1 class="card-title">Dive Mask Fog Formula</h1>    <pre class="code"><code>defog_strength = temperature_drop × humidityapply: one drop per lens, rinse lightly</code></pre>    <p class="muted t-small">Fog forms when warm, moist air hits cool glass. The trick is a thin surfactant layer that stops droplets from clinging.</p>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Clean the lens with a gentle soap to remove factory residue.</li>    <li>Apply a drop of defog or a tiny bit of baby shampoo.</li>    <li>Rinse once—don't scrub it all off.</li>    <li>Put the mask on and press it to your face to seal.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout warn">    <p><span class="badge warn"><i class="dot"></i>avoid</span> Don't spit in your mask unless you're in a pinch. It works, but it's not elegant.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <div class="card">    <h1 class="card-title">Dive Mask Fog Formula</h1>    <pre class="code"><code>defog_strength = temperature_drop × humidityapply: one drop per lens, rinse lightly</code></pre>    <p class="muted t-small">Fog forms when warm, moist air hits cool glass. The trick is a thin surfactant layer that stops droplets from clinging.</p>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Clean the lens with a gentle soap to remove factory residue.</li>    <li>Apply a drop of defog or a tiny bit of baby shampoo.</li>    <li>Rinse once—don't scrub it all off.</li>    <li>Put the mask on and press it to your face to seal.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout warn">    <p><span class="badge warn"><i class="dot"></i>avoid</span> Don't spit in your mask unless you're in a pinch. It works, but it's not elegant.</p>  </div></div>	-
45.13.1	dashboard_formula/Succulent Watering Formula	<div class="umi-page">  <div class="card">    <h1 class="card-title">Succulent Watering Formula</h1>    <pre class="code"><code>days_between = soil_dry_days + (light_hours / 4)water_amount = pot_volume × 0.1</code></pre>    <p class="muted t-small">Soak the soil until water runs out the bottom, then wait until it's bone dry before watering again. More light means faster drying.</p>  </div>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat">      <div class="label">Summer</div>      <div class="value">10<span class="unit">days</span></div>    </div>    <div class="stat">      <div class="label">Winter</div>      <div class="value">21<span class="unit">days</span></div>    </div>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Check the soil with a chopstick. If it's dry all the way down, water.</li>    <li>Water thoroughly until it drains.</li>    <li>Empty the saucer so roots don't sit in water.</li>    <li>Adjust frequency with the seasons.</li>  </ol></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <div class="card">    <h1 class="card-title">Succulent Watering Formula</h1>    <pre class="code"><code>days_between = soil_dry_days + (light_hours / 4)water_amount = pot_volume × 0.1</code></pre>    <p class="muted t-small">Soak the soil until water runs out the bottom, then wait until it's bone dry before watering again. More light means faster drying.</p>  </div>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="stat">      <div class="label">Summer</div>      <div class="value">10<span class="unit">days</span></div>    </div>    <div class="stat">      <div class="label">Winter</div>      <div class="value">21<span class="unit">days</span></div>    </div>  </div>  <ol class="list">    <li>Check the soil with a chopstick. If it's dry all the way down, water.</li>    <li>Water thoroughly until it drains.</li>    <li>Empty the saucer so roots don't sit in water.</li>    <li>Adjust frequency with the seasons.</li>  </ol></div>	-
45.14.1	dashboard_formula/Slow Cooker Conversion Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Slow Cooker Conversion Formula</h1><p class="muted">Hi <regex>user_name</regex> — oven recipes shrink to a gentler clock in the crock. Here's the swap.</p><pre class="code"><code>slow_hours = oven_minutes / 60 * 2temp = 200°F low / 300°F high</code></pre><div class="grid two">  <div class="stat"><span class="label">Oven</span><span class="value">350°F</span><span class="delta down">1 hour</span></div>  <div class="stat"><span class="label">Crock</span><span class="value">Low</span><span class="delta">2 hours</span></div></div><ol class="list">  <li>Double the oven time in hours.</li>  <li>Use low for lean cuts, high for quick weeknight meals.</li>  <li>Cut liquid by about half — nothing boils off.</li>  <li>Add dairy in the last 30 minutes.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Why low beats high for tough meat</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Low heat gives collagen time to melt. High gets you there faster, but the texture is a little less forgiving.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Slow Cooker Conversion Formula</h1><p class="muted">Hi  — oven recipes shrink to a gentler clock in the crock. Here's the swap.</p><pre class="code"><code>slow_hours = oven_minutes / 60 * 2temp = 200°F low / 300°F high</code></pre><div class="grid two">  <div class="stat"><span class="label">Oven</span><span class="value">350°F</span><span class="delta down">1 hour</span></div>  <div class="stat"><span class="label">Crock</span><span class="value">Low</span><span class="delta">2 hours</span></div></div><ol class="list">  <li>Double the oven time in hours.</li>  <li>Use low for lean cuts, high for quick weeknight meals.</li>  <li>Cut liquid by about half — nothing boils off.</li>  <li>Add dairy in the last 30 minutes.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Why low beats high for tough meat</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Low heat gives collagen time to melt. High gets you there faster, but the texture is a little less forgiving.</p></div></details></div>	-
45.15.1	dashboard_formula/Concrete Bag Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Concrete Bag Formula</h1><p class="muted"><regex>user_name</regex>, post holes eat more mix than you'd guess. Measure, don't guess.</p><pre class="code"><code>volume_ft3 = pi * r² * depth * postsbags = volume_ft3 / 0.6</code></pre><div class="grid two">  <div class="stat"><span class="label">6" hole, 3' deep</span><span class="value">0.59<span class="unit"> ft³</span></span><span class="delta">≈ 1 bag</span></div>  <div class="stat"><span class="label">10 posts</span><span class="value">6<span class="unit"> ft³</span></span><span class="delta">≈ 10 bags</span></div></div><ol class="list">  <li>Radius = half the hole diameter, in feet.</li>  <li>Multiply by depth, then by number of posts.</li>  <li>Divide by 0.6 ft³ per 80-lb bag.</li>  <li>Round up — always one spare.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">A 50-lb bag is 0.375 ft³. Same formula, smaller scoop.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Concrete Bag Formula</h1><p class="muted">, post holes eat more mix than you'd guess. Measure, don't guess.</p><pre class="code"><code>volume_ft3 = pi * r² * depth * postsbags = volume_ft3 / 0.6</code></pre><div class="grid two">  <div class="stat"><span class="label">6" hole, 3' deep</span><span class="value">0.59<span class="unit"> ft³</span></span><span class="delta">≈ 1 bag</span></div>  <div class="stat"><span class="label">10 posts</span><span class="value">6<span class="unit"> ft³</span></span><span class="delta">≈ 10 bags</span></div></div><ol class="list">  <li>Radius = half the hole diameter, in feet.</li>  <li>Multiply by depth, then by number of posts.</li>  <li>Divide by 0.6 ft³ per 80-lb bag.</li>  <li>Round up — always one spare.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">A 50-lb bag is 0.375 ft³. Same formula, smaller scoop.</p></div></div>	-
45.16.1	dashboard_formula/Running Shoe Mileage Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Running Shoe Mileage Formula</h1><p class="muted"><regex>user_name</regex>, your shoes quietly log miles. Here's the rule of thumb.</p><pre class="code"><code>replace_at = 400 * (weight_lbs / 150)remaining = replace_at - miles_run</code></pre><div class="grid two">  <div class="stat"><span class="label">150 lb runner</span><span class="value">400<span class="unit"> mi</span></span></div>  <div class="stat"><span class="label">200 lb runner</span><span class="value">533<span class="unit"> mi</span></span></div></div><ol class="list">  <li>Start with a 400-mile base.</li>  <li>Scale by body weight against 150 lb.</li>  <li>Subtract miles for a remaining count.</li>  <li>Track on a note — your knees will thank you.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p class="t-small">Signs you're past due: flattened tread, wrinkles in the midsole, mystery aches.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Running Shoe Mileage Formula</h1><p class="muted">, your shoes quietly log miles. Here's the rule of thumb.</p><pre class="code"><code>replace_at = 400 * (weight_lbs / 150)remaining = replace_at - miles_run</code></pre><div class="grid two">  <div class="stat"><span class="label">150 lb runner</span><span class="value">400<span class="unit"> mi</span></span></div>  <div class="stat"><span class="label">200 lb runner</span><span class="value">533<span class="unit"> mi</span></span></div></div><ol class="list">  <li>Start with a 400-mile base.</li>  <li>Scale by body weight against 150 lb.</li>  <li>Subtract miles for a remaining count.</li>  <li>Track on a note — your knees will thank you.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p class="t-small">Signs you're past due: flattened tread, wrinkles in the midsole, mystery aches.</p></div></div>	-
45.17.1	dashboard_formula/Candle Burn Time Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Candle Burn Time Formula</h1><p class="muted">Hi <regex>user_name</regex> — wax burns at roughly 7 hours per ounce, give or take a draft.</p><pre class="code"><code>hours = wax_oz * 7burn_rate = wax_oz / hours</code></pre><div class="grid two">  <div class="stat"><span class="label">8 oz jar</span><span class="value">56<span class="unit"> hrs</span></span></div>  <div class="stat"><span class="label">16 oz jar</span><span class="value">112<span class="unit"> hrs</span></span></div></div><ol class="list">  <li>Weigh or read the wax weight, not jar size.</li>  <li>Multiply by 7 hours per ounce.</li>  <li>Trim the wick to ¼" before every burn.</li>  <li>Keep the first burn two hours — full melt pool.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>The 7 is a starting point</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Soy runs slower than paraffin, drafts run faster, and tall jars burn hot in the middle.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Candle Burn Time Formula</h1><p class="muted">Hi  — wax burns at roughly 7 hours per ounce, give or take a draft.</p><pre class="code"><code>hours = wax_oz * 7burn_rate = wax_oz / hours</code></pre><div class="grid two">  <div class="stat"><span class="label">8 oz jar</span><span class="value">56<span class="unit"> hrs</span></span></div>  <div class="stat"><span class="label">16 oz jar</span><span class="value">112<span class="unit"> hrs</span></span></div></div><ol class="list">  <li>Weigh or read the wax weight, not jar size.</li>  <li>Multiply by 7 hours per ounce.</li>  <li>Trim the wick to ¼" before every burn.</li>  <li>Keep the first burn two hours — full melt pool.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>The 7 is a starting point</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Soy runs slower than paraffin, drafts run faster, and tall jars burn hot in the middle.</p></div></details></div>	-
45.18.1	dashboard_formula/Garage Door Spring Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Garage Door Spring Formula</h1><p class="muted"><regex>user_name</regex>, springs die by cycles, not years. Though years help.</p><pre class="code"><code>cycles_per_year = trips_per_day * 2 * 365life_years = rated_cycles / cycles_per_year</code></pre><div class="grid two">  <div class="stat"><span class="label">10,000-cycle spring</span><span class="value">10.9<span class="unit"> yr</span></span><span class="delta down">4 trips/day</span></div>  <div class="stat"><span class="label">25,000-cycle spring</span><span class="value">27.4<span class="unit"> yr</span></span><span class="delta down">4 trips/day</span></div></div><ol class="list">  <li>Count open-close pairs per day, double it.</li>  <li>Multiply by 365 for cycles a year.</li>  <li>Divide the spring's rated cycles by that.</li>  <li>Replace in pairs — matched springs, matched life.</li></ol><div class="callout bad"><p class="t-small">Never wind a torsion spring yourself. That's a pro with a winding bar, full stop.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Garage Door Spring Formula</h1><p class="muted">, springs die by cycles, not years. Though years help.</p><pre class="code"><code>cycles_per_year = trips_per_day * 2 * 365life_years = rated_cycles / cycles_per_year</code></pre><div class="grid two">  <div class="stat"><span class="label">10,000-cycle spring</span><span class="value">10.9<span class="unit"> yr</span></span><span class="delta down">4 trips/day</span></div>  <div class="stat"><span class="label">25,000-cycle spring</span><span class="value">27.4<span class="unit"> yr</span></span><span class="delta down">4 trips/day</span></div></div><ol class="list">  <li>Count open-close pairs per day, double it.</li>  <li>Multiply by 365 for cycles a year.</li>  <li>Divide the spring's rated cycles by that.</li>  <li>Replace in pairs — matched springs, matched life.</li></ol><div class="callout bad"><p class="t-small">Never wind a torsion spring yourself. That's a pro with a winding bar, full stop.</p></div></div>	-
45.19.1	dashboard_formula/Grout Amount Formula	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Grout Amount</h1>  <p class="muted">Tile size, joint width, and depth decide how much grout disappears into the gaps.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>grout_kg = (area_m2 × joint_factor) / coverage_per_kg</code></pre>  <ol class="list">    <li>Measure the tiled area in square metres.</li>    <li>Find the joint factor: small tiles and wide joints raise it.</li>    <li>Divide by the grout bag's coverage rate (on the label).</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout info">    <p><strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> a 3 mm joint with 300×300 mm tiles uses roughly 0.7 kg/m². Double it for 150×150 mm tiles.</p>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What if I'm using epoxy grout?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Epoxy is denser, so it covers less area per kilogram. Check the bucket—usually 15–20% more product for the same job.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Grout Amount</h1>  <p class="muted">Tile size, joint width, and depth decide how much grout disappears into the gaps.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>grout_kg = (area_m2 × joint_factor) / coverage_per_kg</code></pre>  <ol class="list">    <li>Measure the tiled area in square metres.</li>    <li>Find the joint factor: small tiles and wide joints raise it.</li>    <li>Divide by the grout bag's coverage rate (on the label).</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout info">    <p><strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> a 3 mm joint with 300×300 mm tiles uses roughly 0.7 kg/m². Double it for 150×150 mm tiles.</p>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What if I'm using epoxy grout?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Epoxy is denser, so it covers less area per kilogram. Check the bucket—usually 15–20% more product for the same job.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
45.20.1	dashboard_formula/Recessed Light Spacing Formula	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Recessed Light Spacing</h1>  <p class="muted">Even light beats bright spots. The ceiling height sets the rhythm.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>spacing_ft = ceiling_height_ft ÷ 2</code></pre>  <ol class="list">    <li>Measure floor to ceiling in feet.</li>    <li>Divide by two. That's the distance between lights.</li>    <li>Keep the first row half that distance from the wall.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout good">    <p>8 ft ceiling → 4 ft spacing. 10 ft ceiling → 5 ft spacing. Simple.</p>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Do I need more lights over a kitchen island?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Yes—treat the island as its own zone. Use two or three smaller cans centred over the counter, spaced evenly.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Recessed Light Spacing</h1>  <p class="muted">Even light beats bright spots. The ceiling height sets the rhythm.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>spacing_ft = ceiling_height_ft ÷ 2</code></pre>  <ol class="list">    <li>Measure floor to ceiling in feet.</li>    <li>Divide by two. That's the distance between lights.</li>    <li>Keep the first row half that distance from the wall.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout good">    <p>8 ft ceiling → 4 ft spacing. 10 ft ceiling → 5 ft spacing. Simple.</p>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Do I need more lights over a kitchen island?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Yes—treat the island as its own zone. Use two or three smaller cans centred over the counter, spaced evenly.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
45.21.1	dashboard_formula/Paint Quantity Formula	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Paint Quantity</h1>  <p class="muted">One gallon covers about 350 square feet. Walls, ceiling, and a second coat change the math.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>gallons = (wall_area × coats) ÷ 350</code></pre>  <ol class="list">    <li>Add up the wall area: perimeter × height.</li>    <li>Subtract doors and windows (about 20 sq ft each).</li>    <li>Multiply by number of coats, then divide by 350.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout warn">    <p>Dark colours and fresh drywall drink more paint. Buy an extra quart just in case.</p>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What about the ceiling?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Measure length × width and add it to your total. Ceilings usually need one coat if you're just refreshing.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Paint Quantity</h1>  <p class="muted">One gallon covers about 350 square feet. Walls, ceiling, and a second coat change the math.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>gallons = (wall_area × coats) ÷ 350</code></pre>  <ol class="list">    <li>Add up the wall area: perimeter × height.</li>    <li>Subtract doors and windows (about 20 sq ft each).</li>    <li>Multiply by number of coats, then divide by 350.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout warn">    <p>Dark colours and fresh drywall drink more paint. Buy an extra quart just in case.</p>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>What about the ceiling?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Measure length × width and add it to your total. Ceilings usually need one coat if you're just refreshing.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
45.22.1	dashboard_formula/Rice Water Ratio Formula	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Rice Water Ratio</h1>  <p class="muted">Rice is forgiving if you get the water right. The rest is patience.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>water_cups = rice_cups × 2</code></pre>  <ol class="list">    <li>Measure rice with any cup.</li>    <li>Use twice as much water.</li>    <li>Bring to a boil, cover, simmer 15 minutes, rest 5.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout info">    <p>Brown rice needs a little more: 2.5 cups water per cup of rice.</p>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Does the type of pot matter?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>A tight-fitting lid matters more than the pot. Heavy bottoms help, but a good lid traps the steam.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Rice Water Ratio</h1>  <p class="muted">Rice is forgiving if you get the water right. The rest is patience.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>water_cups = rice_cups × 2</code></pre>  <ol class="list">    <li>Measure rice with any cup.</li>    <li>Use twice as much water.</li>    <li>Bring to a boil, cover, simmer 15 minutes, rest 5.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout info">    <p>Brown rice needs a little more: 2.5 cups water per cup of rice.</p>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Does the type of pot matter?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>A tight-fitting lid matters more than the pot. Heavy bottoms help, but a good lid traps the steam.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
45.23.1	dashboard_formula/Coffee Brew Ratio Formula	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Coffee Brew Ratio</h1>  <p class="muted">A good cup starts with a scale. Eyeballing leads to sad mornings.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>coffee_g = water_g ÷ 16</code></pre>  <ol class="list">    <li>Weigh your water in grams.</li>    <li>Divide by 16 for a balanced cup.</li>    <li>Adjust: 15 for stronger, 17 for lighter.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout good">    <p>500 g water → 31 g coffee. That's about two heaped tablespoons.</p>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Does this work for cold brew?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Cold brew likes a concentrate: 1:8. Dilute with water or milk after steeping.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Coffee Brew Ratio</h1>  <p class="muted">A good cup starts with a scale. Eyeballing leads to sad mornings.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>coffee_g = water_g ÷ 16</code></pre>  <ol class="list">    <li>Weigh your water in grams.</li>    <li>Divide by 16 for a balanced cup.</li>    <li>Adjust: 15 for stronger, 17 for lighter.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout good">    <p>500 g water → 31 g coffee. That's about two heaped tablespoons.</p>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Does this work for cold brew?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Cold brew likes a concentrate: 1:8. Dilute with water or milk after steeping.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
45.24.1	dashboard_formula/Phone Charge Time Formula	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Phone Charge Time</h1>  <p class="muted">Battery size and charger wattage set the clock. Cable quality sneaks in too.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>hours = (battery_mAh × 3.7) ÷ (charger_W × 1000)</code></pre>  <ol class="list">    <li>Find your battery capacity in mAh.</li>    <li>Multiply by 3.7 (nominal voltage) to get mWh.</li>    <li>Divide by charger watts. That's your rough time.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout info">    <p>4000 mAh battery on a 20 W charger: about 0.75 hours, or 45 minutes.</p>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Why does my phone slow down after 80%?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Batteries charge fast to 80%, then trickle to protect cells. The last 20% takes as long as the first 80%.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Phone Charge Time</h1>  <p class="muted">Battery size and charger wattage set the clock. Cable quality sneaks in too.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>hours = (battery_mAh × 3.7) ÷ (charger_W × 1000)</code></pre>  <ol class="list">    <li>Find your battery capacity in mAh.</li>    <li>Multiply by 3.7 (nominal voltage) to get mWh.</li>    <li>Divide by charger watts. That's your rough time.</li>  </ol>  <div class="callout info">    <p>4000 mAh battery on a 20 W charger: about 0.75 hours, or 45 minutes.</p>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Why does my phone slow down after 80%?</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Batteries charge fast to 80%, then trickle to protect cells. The last 20% takes as long as the first 80%.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
45.25.1	dashboard_formula/Laundry Bleach Dilution Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Laundry Bleach Dilution Formula</h1><pre class="code"><code>bleach_oz = (load_gallons × 1.5) / 16</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Estimate load water volume in gallons.</li><li>Multiply by 1.5 oz per gallon.</li><li>Divide by 16 to get cups if needed.</li></ol><p class="callout info"><strong>Heads up:</strong> Use only unscented chlorine bleach for disinfecting. Add after the washer fills.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Laundry Bleach Dilution Formula</h1><pre class="code"><code>bleach_oz = (load_gallons × 1.5) / 16</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Estimate load water volume in gallons.</li><li>Multiply by 1.5 oz per gallon.</li><li>Divide by 16 to get cups if needed.</li></ol><p class="callout info"><strong>Heads up:</strong> Use only unscented chlorine bleach for disinfecting. Add after the washer fills.</p></div>	-
45.26.1	dashboard_formula/Ceiling Fan Size Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Ceiling Fan Size Formula</h1><pre class="code"><code>cfm = length_ft × width_ft × 2.5</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Measure room length and width in feet.</li><li>Multiply them for square footage.</li><li>Multiply by 2.5 for recommended CFM.</li></ol><p class="callout good"><strong>Tip:</strong> For rooms over 400 sq ft, use two fans or a large 60-inch model.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Ceiling Fan Size Formula</h1><pre class="code"><code>cfm = length_ft × width_ft × 2.5</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Measure room length and width in feet.</li><li>Multiply them for square footage.</li><li>Multiply by 2.5 for recommended CFM.</li></ol><p class="callout good"><strong>Tip:</strong> For rooms over 400 sq ft, use two fans or a large 60-inch model.</p></div>	-
45.27.1	dashboard_formula/Paver Patio Base Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Paver Patio Base Formula</h1><pre class="code"><code>base_tons = (area_sqft × depth_in × 1.4) / 2000</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Calculate patio area in square feet.</li><li>Choose base depth (4–6 inches).</li><li>Multiply and divide for tons of gravel.</li></ol><p class="callout warn"><strong>Note:</strong> Add 10% for compaction and waste.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Paver Patio Base Formula</h1><pre class="code"><code>base_tons = (area_sqft × depth_in × 1.4) / 2000</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Calculate patio area in square feet.</li><li>Choose base depth (4–6 inches).</li><li>Multiply and divide for tons of gravel.</li></ol><p class="callout warn"><strong>Note:</strong> Add 10% for compaction and waste.</p></div>	-
45.28.1	dashboard_formula/Projector Throw Distance Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Projector Throw Distance Formula</h1><pre class="code"><code>distance_ft = screen_width_ft × throw_ratio</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Find your projector's throw ratio (e.g., 1.5:1).</li><li>Measure desired screen width in feet.</li><li>Multiply width by throw ratio.</li></ol><p class="callout good"><strong>Example:</strong> 1.5:1 ratio and 6 ft wide screen needs 9 ft distance.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Projector Throw Distance Formula</h1><pre class="code"><code>distance_ft = screen_width_ft × throw_ratio</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Find your projector's throw ratio (e.g., 1.5:1).</li><li>Measure desired screen width in feet.</li><li>Multiply width by throw ratio.</li></ol><p class="callout good"><strong>Example:</strong> 1.5:1 ratio and 6 ft wide screen needs 9 ft distance.</p></div>	-
45.29.1	dashboard_formula/Rain Barrel Overflow Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Rain Barrel Overflow Formula</h1><pre class="code"><code>flow_gpm = (roof_sqft × rainfall_in_per_hr × 0.0104) / 60</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Calculate roof catchment area.</li><li>Multiply by rainfall intensity in inches per hour.</li><li>Convert to gallons per minute.</li></ol><p class="callout warn"><strong>Tip:</strong> Size overflow pipe to handle at least this flow to prevent flooding.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Rain Barrel Overflow Formula</h1><pre class="code"><code>flow_gpm = (roof_sqft × rainfall_in_per_hr × 0.0104) / 60</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Calculate roof catchment area.</li><li>Multiply by rainfall intensity in inches per hour.</li><li>Convert to gallons per minute.</li></ol><p class="callout warn"><strong>Tip:</strong> Size overflow pipe to handle at least this flow to prevent flooding.</p></div>	-
45.30.1	dashboard_formula/Aquarium Heater Wattage Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Aquarium Heater Wattage Formula</h1><p class="muted">Hi <regex>user_name</regex> — small tanks lose heat fast. A little headroom beats a shivering fish.</p><div class="card"><div class="card-title">The formula</div><pre class="code"><code>watts = gallons x 5          (mild room)watts = gallons x 7.5        (cold room)watts = gallons x 10         (unheated room)</code></pre></div><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">29 gal, mild room</span><span class="value">145<span class="unit">W</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">29 gal, cold room</span><span class="value">290<span class="unit">W</span></span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Measure tank water volume, not the box size.</li><li>Note the coldest the room ever gets at night.</li><li>Pick the row that matches that worst case.</li><li>Two smaller heaters beat one big one if it fails.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Below 20 gallons, a preset 50 W heater is often plenty. Above 55 gallons, split the load.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Aquarium Heater Wattage Formula</h1><p class="muted">Hi  — small tanks lose heat fast. A little headroom beats a shivering fish.</p><div class="card"><div class="card-title">The formula</div><pre class="code"><code>watts = gallons x 5          (mild room)watts = gallons x 7.5        (cold room)watts = gallons x 10         (unheated room)</code></pre></div><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">29 gal, mild room</span><span class="value">145<span class="unit">W</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">29 gal, cold room</span><span class="value">290<span class="unit">W</span></span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Measure tank water volume, not the box size.</li><li>Note the coldest the room ever gets at night.</li><li>Pick the row that matches that worst case.</li><li>Two smaller heaters beat one big one if it fails.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Below 20 gallons, a preset 50 W heater is often plenty. Above 55 gallons, split the load.</p></div></div>	-
45.31.1	dashboard_formula/Tiling Adhesive Coverage Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Tiling Adhesive Coverage Formula</h1><p class="muted">Hi <regex>user_name</regex> — buying one extra bag costs less than a mid-job supply run.</p><div class="card"><div class="card-title">The formula</div><pre class="code"><code>bags = (area_sqft x trowel_factor) / bag_coveragesmall tile (thin bed)        -> 1.0medium tile (3/8 inch)       -> 1.3large tile (1/2 inch)        -> 1.6</code></pre><p class="t-small dim">A 50 lb bag usually covers ~60 sq ft with a 1/4 inch trowel.</p></div><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Area</span><span class="value">60<span class="unit">sq ft</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Large-tile bag</span><span class="value">1.6<span class="unit">bags</span></span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Measure the wall or floor in square feet.</li><li>Match trowel size to tile size.</li><li>Multiply area by the trowel factor.</li><li>Divide by bag coverage, then round up.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Backsplash shortcuts</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">A backsplash is one bag, almost always. Grid or handmade tile hides a thicker bed — add 20%.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Tiling Adhesive Coverage Formula</h1><p class="muted">Hi  — buying one extra bag costs less than a mid-job supply run.</p><div class="card"><div class="card-title">The formula</div><pre class="code"><code>bags = (area_sqft x trowel_factor) / bag_coveragesmall tile (thin bed)        -> 1.0medium tile (3/8 inch)       -> 1.3large tile (1/2 inch)        -> 1.6</code></pre><p class="t-small dim">A 50 lb bag usually covers ~60 sq ft with a 1/4 inch trowel.</p></div><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Area</span><span class="value">60<span class="unit">sq ft</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Large-tile bag</span><span class="value">1.6<span class="unit">bags</span></span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Measure the wall or floor in square feet.</li><li>Match trowel size to tile size.</li><li>Multiply area by the trowel factor.</li><li>Divide by bag coverage, then round up.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Backsplash shortcuts</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">A backsplash is one bag, almost always. Grid or handmade tile hides a thicker bed — add 20%.</p></div></details></div>	-
45.32.1	dashboard_formula/Moving Box Count Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Moving Box Count Formula</h1><p class="muted">Hi <regex>user_name</regex> — count rooms, not cupboards. Nobody ever complained about two spare boxes.</p><div class="card"><div class="card-title">The formula</div><pre class="code"><code>boxes  = small + med + large + wardrobesmall  = rooms x 4med    = rooms x 6large  = rooms x 2wardrobe = closets x 2</code></pre></div><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">4 rooms + 1 closet</span><span class="value">50<span class="unit">boxes</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Wardrobe</span><span class="value">2<span class="unit">boxes</span></span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Count main rooms, not hallways.</li><li>Add 4 small boxes per room for oddities.</li><li>Heavy things go small, light things go large.</li><li>Add 10% and tape one extra flat.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p class="t-small">Pillows, blankets, and towels pack first — they cushion everything else.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Moving Box Count Formula</h1><p class="muted">Hi  — count rooms, not cupboards. Nobody ever complained about two spare boxes.</p><div class="card"><div class="card-title">The formula</div><pre class="code"><code>boxes  = small + med + large + wardrobesmall  = rooms x 4med    = rooms x 6large  = rooms x 2wardrobe = closets x 2</code></pre></div><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">4 rooms + 1 closet</span><span class="value">50<span class="unit">boxes</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Wardrobe</span><span class="value">2<span class="unit">boxes</span></span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Count main rooms, not hallways.</li><li>Add 4 small boxes per room for oddities.</li><li>Heavy things go small, light things go large.</li><li>Add 10% and tape one extra flat.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p class="t-small">Pillows, blankets, and towels pack first — they cushion everything else.</p></div></div>	-
45.33.1	dashboard_formula/Bread Proof Time Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Bread Proof Time Formula</h1><p class="muted">Hi <regex>user_name</regex> — time is a guess, dough is the truth. Watch the rise, not the clock.</p><div class="card"><div class="card-title">The formula</div><pre class="code"><code>time = 90 min x 2^((24 - temp_C)/8)22 C -> 90 min    26 C -> 74 min18 C -> 143 min   14 C -> 227 min</code></pre><p class="t-small dim">Every 8°C cooler roughly doubles the wait.</p></div><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Room</span><span class="value">20<span class="unit">°C</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Est. proof</span><span class="value">122<span class="unit">min</span></span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Read the room temperature near the bowl.</li><li>Pick your nearest row above.</li><li>Stop when a fingerprint springs back slowly.</li><li>Cold prove in the fridge for flavour if you have time.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Signs it's over-proofed</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Saggy dome, sour smell, collapses on the peel. Punch down and reshape into a flatbread, no shame.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Bread Proof Time Formula</h1><p class="muted">Hi  — time is a guess, dough is the truth. Watch the rise, not the clock.</p><div class="card"><div class="card-title">The formula</div><pre class="code"><code>time = 90 min x 2^((24 - temp_C)/8)22 C -> 90 min    26 C -> 74 min18 C -> 143 min   14 C -> 227 min</code></pre><p class="t-small dim">Every 8°C cooler roughly doubles the wait.</p></div><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Room</span><span class="value">20<span class="unit">°C</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Est. proof</span><span class="value">122<span class="unit">min</span></span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Read the room temperature near the bowl.</li><li>Pick your nearest row above.</li><li>Stop when a fingerprint springs back slowly.</li><li>Cold prove in the fridge for flavour if you have time.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Signs it's over-proofed</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Saggy dome, sour smell, collapses on the peel. Punch down and reshape into a flatbread, no shame.</p></div></details></div>	-
45.34.1	dashboard_formula/Bird Feeder Refill Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Bird Feeder Refill Formula</h1><p class="muted">Hi <regex>user_name</regex> — birds don't keep receipts. A steady supply beats a feast-and-famine routine.</p><div class="card"><div class="card-title">The formula</div><pre class="code"><code>days = seed_lbs / (feeders x rate)rate = 0.25 lb/day  (quiet feeder)rate = 0.50 lb/day  (busy feeder)rate = 1.00 lb/day  (peak winter flock)</code></pre></div><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">2 lb seed, 3 quiet feeders</span><span class="value">2.7<span class="unit">days</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Busy winter</span><span class="value">1.3<span class="unit">days</span></span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Count active feeders and note traffic.</li><li>Pick the rate that matches the season.</li><li>Multiply feeders by rate.</li><li>Divide seed on hand by that number.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Cheaper fill</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Black oil sunflower seed has the highest value per pound. Millet gets kicked to the ground — feed less of it.</p></div></details><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Clean feeders every two weeks with warm soapy water to stop mould.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Bird Feeder Refill Formula</h1><p class="muted">Hi  — birds don't keep receipts. A steady supply beats a feast-and-famine routine.</p><div class="card"><div class="card-title">The formula</div><pre class="code"><code>days = seed_lbs / (feeders x rate)rate = 0.25 lb/day  (quiet feeder)rate = 0.50 lb/day  (busy feeder)rate = 1.00 lb/day  (peak winter flock)</code></pre></div><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">2 lb seed, 3 quiet feeders</span><span class="value">2.7<span class="unit">days</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Busy winter</span><span class="value">1.3<span class="unit">days</span></span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Count active feeders and note traffic.</li><li>Pick the rate that matches the season.</li><li>Multiply feeders by rate.</li><li>Divide seed on hand by that number.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Cheaper fill</summary><div class="body"><p class="t-small">Black oil sunflower seed has the highest value per pound. Millet gets kicked to the ground — feed less of it.</p></div></details><div class="callout info"><p class="t-small">Clean feeders every two weeks with warm soapy water to stop mould.</p></div></div>	-
45.35.1	dashboard_formula/Formula for Cast Iron Pan Seasoning Oil	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Cast Iron Seasoning Oil</h1><p class="muted">Thin layers, hot oven, repeat. Here's the math for a perfect coat.</p><pre class="code"><code>oil_grams = pan_diameter_inches * 0.1</code></pre><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">10-inch pan</span><span class="value">1.0 <span class="unit">g</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">12-inch pan</span><span class="value">1.2 <span class="unit">g</span></span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Preheat oven to 450°F (230°C).</li><li>Wipe pan with a tiny amount of flaxseed or canola oil.</li><li>Bake upside down for 1 hour.</li><li>Cool, repeat 2–3 times for a strong layer.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> If it feels sticky after cooling, you used too much oil. Scrub it off and start again with less.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Cast Iron Seasoning Oil</h1><p class="muted">Thin layers, hot oven, repeat. Here's the math for a perfect coat.</p><pre class="code"><code>oil_grams = pan_diameter_inches * 0.1</code></pre><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">10-inch pan</span><span class="value">1.0 <span class="unit">g</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">12-inch pan</span><span class="value">1.2 <span class="unit">g</span></span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Preheat oven to 450°F (230°C).</li><li>Wipe pan with a tiny amount of flaxseed or canola oil.</li><li>Bake upside down for 1 hour.</li><li>Cool, repeat 2–3 times for a strong layer.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> If it feels sticky after cooling, you used too much oil. Scrub it off and start again with less.</p></div></div>	-
45.36.1	dashboard_formula/Formula for Sock Drawer Organization	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Sock Drawer Organization</h1><p class="muted">Stop digging. A simple grid keeps every pair in sight.</p><pre class="code"><code>pairs_per_row = floor(drawer_width / 4)<br>rows = floor(drawer_depth / 3)</code></pre><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Drawer 24" x 18"</span><span class="value">36 <span class="unit">pairs</span></span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Measure drawer width and depth.</li><li>Fold each pair into a small bundle about 4" x 3".</li><li>Arrange in rows, file-folder style.</li><li>Group by color or thickness.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p><strong>Works best:</strong> with a shallow drawer (under 6" deep). Deep drawers need stackable dividers.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Sock Drawer Organization</h1><p class="muted">Stop digging. A simple grid keeps every pair in sight.</p><pre class="code"><code>pairs_per_row = floor(drawer_width / 4)<br>rows = floor(drawer_depth / 3)</code></pre><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Drawer 24" x 18"</span><span class="value">36 <span class="unit">pairs</span></span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Measure drawer width and depth.</li><li>Fold each pair into a small bundle about 4" x 3".</li><li>Arrange in rows, file-folder style.</li><li>Group by color or thickness.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p><strong>Works best:</strong> with a shallow drawer (under 6" deep). Deep drawers need stackable dividers.</p></div></div>	-
45.37.1	dashboard_formula/Formula for Perfect Hard-Boiled Egg Yolk	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Perfect Hard-Boiled Egg Yolk</h1><p class="muted">Green ring? That's iron sulfide. Avoid it with precise timing and an ice bath.</p><pre class="code"><code>cook_time_min = 9 + (egg_start_temp == 'fridge' ? 2 : 0)</code></pre><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Fridge cold</span><span class="value">11 <span class="unit">min</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Room temp</span><span class="value">9 <span class="unit">min</span></span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Bring water to a rolling boil.</li><li>Gently lower eggs into water.</li><li>Start timer immediately.</li><li>Transfer to ice bath for 5 minutes.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p><strong>Why ice?</strong> Stops cooking and prevents the green ring from forming.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Perfect Hard-Boiled Egg Yolk</h1><p class="muted">Green ring? That's iron sulfide. Avoid it with precise timing and an ice bath.</p><pre class="code"><code>cook_time_min = 9 + (egg_start_temp == 'fridge' ? 2 : 0)</code></pre><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Fridge cold</span><span class="value">11 <span class="unit">min</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Room temp</span><span class="value">9 <span class="unit">min</span></span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Bring water to a rolling boil.</li><li>Gently lower eggs into water.</li><li>Start timer immediately.</li><li>Transfer to ice bath for 5 minutes.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p><strong>Why ice?</strong> Stops cooking and prevents the green ring from forming.</p></div></div>	-
45.38.1	dashboard_formula/Formula for T-Shirt Size to Yardage	<div class="umi-page"><h1>T-Shirt Fabric Yardage</h1><p class="muted">Never guess again. One formula covers most casual tees.</p><pre class="code"><code>yards = (chest_inches / 2 + 4) / 36 * 2</code></pre><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Chest 40"</span><span class="value">1.33 <span class="unit">yd</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Chest 48"</span><span class="value">1.56 <span class="unit">yd</span></span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Measure your chest circumference.</li><li>Add 4" for ease and seam allowance.</li><li>Divide by 2 for front/back pieces.</li><li>Multiply by 2 for sleeves and neckband.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p><strong>Note:</strong> For long sleeves or a loose fit, add 0.5–0.75 yards. Always buy a bit extra.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>T-Shirt Fabric Yardage</h1><p class="muted">Never guess again. One formula covers most casual tees.</p><pre class="code"><code>yards = (chest_inches / 2 + 4) / 36 * 2</code></pre><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Chest 40"</span><span class="value">1.33 <span class="unit">yd</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Chest 48"</span><span class="value">1.56 <span class="unit">yd</span></span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Measure your chest circumference.</li><li>Add 4" for ease and seam allowance.</li><li>Divide by 2 for front/back pieces.</li><li>Multiply by 2 for sleeves and neckband.</li></ol><div class="callout warn"><p><strong>Note:</strong> For long sleeves or a loose fit, add 0.5–0.75 yards. Always buy a bit extra.</p></div></div>	-
45.39.1	dashboard_formula/Formula for Bird Bath Refill Frequency	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Bird Bath Refill Frequency</h1><p class="muted">Evaporation is real. Keep your feathered friends hydrated with this simple rule.</p><pre class="code"><code>days_between_refills = max(1, 7 - (temp_high_F / 10))</code></pre><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">75°F high</span><span class="value">0 <span class="unit">days</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">90°F high</span><span class="value">2 <span class="unit">days</span></span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Check water level daily in summer.</li><li>Refill with fresh water, not just top-off.</li><li>Scrub algae weekly.</li><li>Add a solar fountain or dripper for movement.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> A clean bath invites more birds. Dirty water spreads disease.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Bird Bath Refill Frequency</h1><p class="muted">Evaporation is real. Keep your feathered friends hydrated with this simple rule.</p><pre class="code"><code>days_between_refills = max(1, 7 - (temp_high_F / 10))</code></pre><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">75°F high</span><span class="value">0 <span class="unit">days</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">90°F high</span><span class="value">2 <span class="unit">days</span></span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Check water level daily in summer.</li><li>Refill with fresh water, not just top-off.</li><li>Scrub algae weekly.</li><li>Add a solar fountain or dripper for movement.</li></ol><div class="callout info"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> A clean bath invites more birds. Dirty water spreads disease.</p></div></div>	-
45.40.1	dashboard_formula/Formula for Cutting Board Oil Reapplication	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Cutting Board Oil Reapplication</h1><p class="muted">A thirsty board is a cracked board. Here's the rhythm.</p><pre class="code"><code>days_until_oil = 30 / (washes_per_week + 1)</code></pre><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Daily wash</span><span class="value">4 <span class="unit">days</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Twice weekly</span><span class="value">10 <span class="unit">days</span></span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Wash with mild soap and warm water.</li><li>Dry thoroughly.</li><li>Apply a thin coat of food-grade mineral oil.</li><li>Let sit overnight, wipe excess.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p><strong>Bonus:</strong> Use a beeswax-oil blend for extra protection and shine.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Cutting Board Oil Reapplication</h1><p class="muted">A thirsty board is a cracked board. Here's the rhythm.</p><pre class="code"><code>days_until_oil = 30 / (washes_per_week + 1)</code></pre><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Daily wash</span><span class="value">4 <span class="unit">days</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Twice weekly</span><span class="value">10 <span class="unit">days</span></span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Wash with mild soap and warm water.</li><li>Dry thoroughly.</li><li>Apply a thin coat of food-grade mineral oil.</li><li>Let sit overnight, wipe excess.</li></ol><div class="callout good"><p><strong>Bonus:</strong> Use a beeswax-oil blend for extra protection and shine.</p></div></div>	-
45.41.1	dashboard_formula/Spaghetti Portion Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Spaghetti Portion Formula</h1><p class="muted">So you don't end up with a pot of leftovers again, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><pre class="code"><code>dry_grams = people × appetite_factor × 75g</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Count your eaters. That's <code>people</code>.</li><li>Pick an <code>appetite_factor</code>: 0.7 for light eaters, 1.0 for average, 1.3 for hungry ones.</li><li>Multiply: <code>people × factor × 75</code>. For two average eaters: 2 × 1.0 × 75 = 150g.</li><li>Boil in well-salted water, and save a mug of pasta water before draining.</li></ol><div class="callout info">The 75g is a starting point. Your first batch will tell you if you're a 60g or a 90g household.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Spaghetti Portion Formula</h1><p class="muted">So you don't end up with a pot of leftovers again, .</p><pre class="code"><code>dry_grams = people × appetite_factor × 75g</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Count your eaters. That's <code>people</code>.</li><li>Pick an <code>appetite_factor</code>: 0.7 for light eaters, 1.0 for average, 1.3 for hungry ones.</li><li>Multiply: <code>people × factor × 75</code>. For two average eaters: 2 × 1.0 × 75 = 150g.</li><li>Boil in well-salted water, and save a mug of pasta water before draining.</li></ol><div class="callout info">The 75g is a starting point. Your first batch will tell you if you're a 60g or a 90g household.</div></div>	-
45.42.1	dashboard_formula/Room Lighting Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Room Lighting Formula</h1><p class="muted">A quick way to ballpark lumens, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><pre class="code"><code>lumens = area_ft² × footcandles</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Measure length × width in feet. A 12×15 room is 180 ft².</li><li>Choose footcandles by room: 10–20 for cozy living spaces, 30–40 for kitchens, 50+ for task areas.</li><li>Multiply: 180 ft² × 15 = 2,700 lumens total.</li><li>Divide by your bulb's lumens to get the count. 2,700 ÷ 800 ≈ 3–4 bulbs.</li></ol><div class="callout warn">Lumens aren't watts. A 9W LED can easily hit 800 lumens. Check the box.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Room Lighting Formula</h1><p class="muted">A quick way to ballpark lumens, .</p><pre class="code"><code>lumens = area_ft² × footcandles</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Measure length × width in feet. A 12×15 room is 180 ft².</li><li>Choose footcandles by room: 10–20 for cozy living spaces, 30–40 for kitchens, 50+ for task areas.</li><li>Multiply: 180 ft² × 15 = 2,700 lumens total.</li><li>Divide by your bulb's lumens to get the count. 2,700 ÷ 800 ≈ 3–4 bulbs.</li></ol><div class="callout warn">Lumens aren't watts. A 9W LED can easily hit 800 lumens. Check the box.</div></div>	-
45.43.1	dashboard_formula/Accent Wall Paint Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Accent Wall Paint Formula</h1><p class="muted">One wall, one colour, zero guesswork, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><pre class="code"><code>gallons = (wall_area × coats) / 350</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Measure wall height × width. Subtract doors and windows.</li><li>Decide coats: 2 for a colour change, 1 if touching up.</li><li>Divide by 350 (average sq ft per gallon).</li><li>A 10×8 wall (80 ft²) with 2 coats: 160 ÷ 350 ≈ 0.46 gallons. Buy a quart.</li></ol><div class="callout good">Quarts are your friend for accent walls. They're cheaper and you'll have less waste.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Accent Wall Paint Formula</h1><p class="muted">One wall, one colour, zero guesswork, .</p><pre class="code"><code>gallons = (wall_area × coats) / 350</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Measure wall height × width. Subtract doors and windows.</li><li>Decide coats: 2 for a colour change, 1 if touching up.</li><li>Divide by 350 (average sq ft per gallon).</li><li>A 10×8 wall (80 ft²) with 2 coats: 160 ÷ 350 ≈ 0.46 gallons. Buy a quart.</li></ol><div class="callout good">Quarts are your friend for accent walls. They're cheaper and you'll have less waste.</div></div>	-
45.44.1	dashboard_formula/Raised Bed Soil Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Raised Bed Soil Formula</h1><p class="muted">Fill it right the first time, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><pre class="code"><code>cubic_feet = length_ft × width_ft × depth_ft</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Measure your bed in feet: length, width, and depth (usually 0.5–1 ft).</li><li>Multiply them. A 4×8 bed at 0.5 ft deep: 4 × 8 × 0.5 = 16 ft³.</li><li>Convert to bags: 16 ÷ 1.5 ≈ 11 bags of 1.5 ft³ each.</li><li>Mix in compost: aim for 20–30% of total volume.</li></ol><div class="callout info">Bags settle. Buy one extra and return it if unopened.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Raised Bed Soil Formula</h1><p class="muted">Fill it right the first time, .</p><pre class="code"><code>cubic_feet = length_ft × width_ft × depth_ft</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Measure your bed in feet: length, width, and depth (usually 0.5–1 ft).</li><li>Multiply them. A 4×8 bed at 0.5 ft deep: 4 × 8 × 0.5 = 16 ft³.</li><li>Convert to bags: 16 ÷ 1.5 ≈ 11 bags of 1.5 ft³ each.</li><li>Mix in compost: aim for 20–30% of total volume.</li></ol><div class="callout info">Bags settle. Buy one extra and return it if unopened.</div></div>	-
45.45.1	dashboard_formula/Tile Count Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Tile Count Formula</h1><p class="muted">A little waste is a good thing, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><pre class="code"><code>tiles = (area_ft² / tile_ft²) × 1.10</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Measure the room area in square feet.</li><li>Find your tile's area: a 12×12 tile is 1 ft²; a 6×6 is 0.25 ft².</li><li>Divide, then multiply by 1.10 for cuts and breaks.</li><li>For a 50 ft² room with 1 ft² tiles: (50 / 1) × 1.10 = 55 tiles.</li></ol><div class="callout warn">Buy all tiles at once. Dye lots vary, and that one extra box saves you a return trip.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Tile Count Formula</h1><p class="muted">A little waste is a good thing, .</p><pre class="code"><code>tiles = (area_ft² / tile_ft²) × 1.10</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Measure the room area in square feet.</li><li>Find your tile's area: a 12×12 tile is 1 ft²; a 6×6 is 0.25 ft².</li><li>Divide, then multiply by 1.10 for cuts and breaks.</li><li>For a 50 ft² room with 1 ft² tiles: (50 / 1) × 1.10 = 55 tiles.</li></ol><div class="callout warn">Buy all tiles at once. Dye lots vary, and that one extra box saves you a return trip.</div></div>	-
45.46.1	dashboard_formula/Rice Water Ratio	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Rice Water Ratio</h1><p class="muted">The classic stovetop method for fluffy grains.</p><pre class="code"><code>water = rice × ratio</code></pre><p>Where <code>ratio</code> is 1.5 for long-grain white, 1.25 for short-grain, 2 for brown.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">White long-grain</span><span class="value">1 : 1.5</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Brown</span><span class="value">1 : 2</span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Rinse rice until water runs clear.</li><li>Combine rice and water in pot.</li><li>Bring to boil, then reduce to simmer.</li><li>Cover and cook 18 min (white) or 45 min (brown).</li><li>Rest 10 min, then fluff.</li></ol><div class="callout good">Add a pinch of salt per cup for flavor.</div><p class="dim t-small">Adapted by <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Rice Water Ratio</h1><p class="muted">The classic stovetop method for fluffy grains.</p><pre class="code"><code>water = rice × ratio</code></pre><p>Where <code>ratio</code> is 1.5 for long-grain white, 1.25 for short-grain, 2 for brown.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">White long-grain</span><span class="value">1 : 1.5</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Brown</span><span class="value">1 : 2</span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Rinse rice until water runs clear.</li><li>Combine rice and water in pot.</li><li>Bring to boil, then reduce to simmer.</li><li>Cover and cook 18 min (white) or 45 min (brown).</li><li>Rest 10 min, then fluff.</li></ol><div class="callout good">Add a pinch of salt per cup for flavor.</div><p class="dim t-small">Adapted by .</p></div>	-
45.47.1	dashboard_formula/Garden Seed Spacing	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Garden Seed Spacing</h1><p class="muted">Maximize yield with proper spacing.</p><pre class="code"><code>plants = (bed_length / spacing) × (bed_width / spacing)</code></pre><p><code>spacing</code> is the recommended distance between plants.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Bed length</span><span class="value">8 ft</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Bed width</span><span class="value">4 ft</span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Measure bed dimensions.</li><li>Check seed packet for spacing.</li><li>Divide length and width by spacing.</li><li>Multiply results for total plants.</li><li>Thin seedlings to final spacing.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Example</summary><div class="body"><p>8×4 ft bed, 6 in spacing: (8/0.5)×(4/0.5)=16×8=128 plants.</p></div></details><div class="callout good">Companion planting can increase yield.</div><p class="dim t-small">For <regex>user_name</regex>'s spring garden.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Garden Seed Spacing</h1><p class="muted">Maximize yield with proper spacing.</p><pre class="code"><code>plants = (bed_length / spacing) × (bed_width / spacing)</code></pre><p><code>spacing</code> is the recommended distance between plants.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Bed length</span><span class="value">8 ft</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Bed width</span><span class="value">4 ft</span></div></div><ol class="list"><li>Measure bed dimensions.</li><li>Check seed packet for spacing.</li><li>Divide length and width by spacing.</li><li>Multiply results for total plants.</li><li>Thin seedlings to final spacing.</li></ol><details class="acc"><summary>Example</summary><div class="body"><p>8×4 ft bed, 6 in spacing: (8/0.5)×(4/0.5)=16×8=128 plants.</p></div></details><div class="callout good">Companion planting can increase yield.</div><p class="dim t-small">For 's spring garden.</p></div>	-
45.48.1	dashboard_formula/Hot Water Bottle Fill Formula	<div class="umi-page"><div class="stack"><h1>Hot Water Bottle Fill Formula</h1><p class="muted">So it stays warm and doesn't split at the seams, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="card"><p class="card-title">The formula</p><pre class="code"><code>fill_volume = bottle_capacity × fill_fractionair_gap = bottle_capacity − fill_volume</code></pre><p class="t-small dim">Use fill_fraction ≈ 0.75 for expansion and comfort. For a 2 L bottle, fill ≈ 1.5 L, leaving a 0.5 L air gap.</p></div><div class="callout info"><p><strong>Quick sanity check:</strong> the air gap should be about a quarter of the bottle. If it sloshes heavily, you've overfilled.</p></div><div class="card flat"><p class="card-title">Steps</p><ol class="list"><li>Measure your bottle's nominal capacity in litres (usually printed on the bottom or side).</li><li>Multiply by 0.75 to get the target fill volume.</li><li>Pour hot (not boiling) water to that mark, leaving the calculated air gap.</li><li>Squeeze gently to check for give — a healthy gap feels springy, not stiff.</li></ol></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="stack"><h1>Hot Water Bottle Fill Formula</h1><p class="muted">So it stays warm and doesn't split at the seams, .</p><div class="card"><p class="card-title">The formula</p><pre class="code"><code>fill_volume = bottle_capacity × fill_fractionair_gap = bottle_capacity − fill_volume</code></pre><p class="t-small dim">Use fill_fraction ≈ 0.75 for expansion and comfort. For a 2 L bottle, fill ≈ 1.5 L, leaving a 0.5 L air gap.</p></div><div class="callout info"><p><strong>Quick sanity check:</strong> the air gap should be about a quarter of the bottle. If it sloshes heavily, you've overfilled.</p></div><div class="card flat"><p class="card-title">Steps</p><ol class="list"><li>Measure your bottle's nominal capacity in litres (usually printed on the bottom or side).</li><li>Multiply by 0.75 to get the target fill volume.</li><li>Pour hot (not boiling) water to that mark, leaving the calculated air gap.</li><li>Squeeze gently to check for give — a healthy gap feels springy, not stiff.</li></ol></div></div></div>	-
45.49.1	dashboard_formula/Wheelbarrow Load Formula	<div class="umi-page"><div class="stack"><h1>Wheelbarrow Load Formula</h1><p class="muted">Fewer trips, fewer blisters — here's the count, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="card"><p class="card-title">The formula</p><pre class="code"><code>loads = total_volume ÷ wheelbarrow_volumeloads = total_volume × 1.25</code></pre></div><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Standard wheelbarrow</span><span class="value">6 <span class="unit">cu ft</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">1 cubic yard</span><span class="value">27 <span class="unit">cu ft</span></span></div></div><div class="card flat"><p class="card-title">Steps</p><ol class="list"><li>Measure the volume of material to move (area × depth for beds).</li><li>Note your wheelbarrow's capacity — typically 6 cu ft, or about 0.17 m³.</li><li>Divide total volume by wheelbarrow volume, then round up.</li><li>Add one spare load for settling and spillage.</li></ol></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="stack"><h1>Wheelbarrow Load Formula</h1><p class="muted">Fewer trips, fewer blisters — here's the count, .</p><div class="card"><p class="card-title">The formula</p><pre class="code"><code>loads = total_volume ÷ wheelbarrow_volumeloads = total_volume × 1.25</code></pre></div><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Standard wheelbarrow</span><span class="value">6 <span class="unit">cu ft</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">1 cubic yard</span><span class="value">27 <span class="unit">cu ft</span></span></div></div><div class="card flat"><p class="card-title">Steps</p><ol class="list"><li>Measure the volume of material to move (area × depth for beds).</li><li>Note your wheelbarrow's capacity — typically 6 cu ft, or about 0.17 m³.</li><li>Divide total volume by wheelbarrow volume, then round up.</li><li>Add one spare load for settling and spillage.</li></ol></div></div></div>	-
45.50.1	dashboard_formula/Dumbbell Weight Selection Formula	<div class="umi-page"><div class="stack"><h1>Dumbbell Weight Selection Formula</h1><p class="muted">Start where you can finish with good form, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="card"><p class="card-title">The formula</p><pre class="code"><code>start_weight = body_weight × 0.05next_weight = current_weight × 1.1</code></pre></div><div class="callout good"><p><strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> for most beginners, 5% of body weight per dumbbell for compound moves (rows, presses). Isolation moves (curls, raises) often start lighter — around 2–3%.</p></div><div class="card flat"><p class="card-title">Steps</p><ol class="list"><li>Weigh yourself (or estimate) to get body weight in kilograms or pounds.</li><li>Multiply by 0.05 to find your starting dumbbell weight.</li><li>Round to the nearest available increment (usually 5 lb or 2.5 kg).</li><li>After 2–3 weeks of comfortable sets, increase by about 10%.</li></ol></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="stack"><h1>Dumbbell Weight Selection Formula</h1><p class="muted">Start where you can finish with good form, .</p><div class="card"><p class="card-title">The formula</p><pre class="code"><code>start_weight = body_weight × 0.05next_weight = current_weight × 1.1</code></pre></div><div class="callout good"><p><strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> for most beginners, 5% of body weight per dumbbell for compound moves (rows, presses). Isolation moves (curls, raises) often start lighter — around 2–3%.</p></div><div class="card flat"><p class="card-title">Steps</p><ol class="list"><li>Weigh yourself (or estimate) to get body weight in kilograms or pounds.</li><li>Multiply by 0.05 to find your starting dumbbell weight.</li><li>Round to the nearest available increment (usually 5 lb or 2.5 kg).</li><li>After 2–3 weeks of comfortable sets, increase by about 10%.</li></ol></div></div></div>	-
45.51.1	dashboard_formula/Aquarium Water Change Formula	<div class="umi-page"><div class="stack"><h1>Aquarium Water Change Formula</h1><p class="muted">Keep the water fresh without stressing the fish, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="card"><p class="card-title">The formula</p><pre class="code"><code>change_volume = tank_volume × change_fractionchange_fraction = nitrate_level ÷ target_nitrate × 0.1</code></pre></div><div class="callout info"><p>Typical weekly change: 10–20% for lightly stocked tanks. If nitrates climb above 40 ppm, do a larger change or more frequent ones.</p></div><div class="card flat"><p class="card-title">Steps</p><ol class="list"><li>Test nitrate (or just aim for routine maintenance).</li><li>Decide change fraction — start at 15% for a community tank.</li><li>Multiply tank volume by that fraction to get litres or gallons to remove.</li><li>Siphon out that amount, then replace with dechlorinated water of similar temperature.</li></ol></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="stack"><h1>Aquarium Water Change Formula</h1><p class="muted">Keep the water fresh without stressing the fish, .</p><div class="card"><p class="card-title">The formula</p><pre class="code"><code>change_volume = tank_volume × change_fractionchange_fraction = nitrate_level ÷ target_nitrate × 0.1</code></pre></div><div class="callout info"><p>Typical weekly change: 10–20% for lightly stocked tanks. If nitrates climb above 40 ppm, do a larger change or more frequent ones.</p></div><div class="card flat"><p class="card-title">Steps</p><ol class="list"><li>Test nitrate (or just aim for routine maintenance).</li><li>Decide change fraction — start at 15% for a community tank.</li><li>Multiply tank volume by that fraction to get litres or gallons to remove.</li><li>Siphon out that amount, then replace with dechlorinated water of similar temperature.</li></ol></div></div></div>	-
45.52.1	dashboard_formula/Canning Batch Formula	<div class="umi-page"><div class="stack"><h1>Canning Batch Formula</h1><p class="muted">So you don't run out of clean jars halfway through, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="card"><p class="card-title">The formula</p><pre class="code"><code>jars = total_produce_weight ÷ weight_per_jarjars = total_quarts × 1.15</code></pre></div><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Tomatoes</span><span class="value">2.5 <span class="unit">lb / quart</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Peaches</span><span class="value">2 <span class="unit">lb / quart</span></span></div></div><div class="card flat"><p class="card-title">Steps</p><ol class="list"><li>Weigh your produce after trimming and peeling.</li><li>Divide by the typical weight per jar for that fruit or veg.</li><li>Add 15% extra jars for imperfect packing and headspace.</li><li>Wash and heat that many jars plus lids before you start.</li></ol></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="stack"><h1>Canning Batch Formula</h1><p class="muted">So you don't run out of clean jars halfway through, .</p><div class="card"><p class="card-title">The formula</p><pre class="code"><code>jars = total_produce_weight ÷ weight_per_jarjars = total_quarts × 1.15</code></pre></div><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Tomatoes</span><span class="value">2.5 <span class="unit">lb / quart</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Peaches</span><span class="value">2 <span class="unit">lb / quart</span></span></div></div><div class="card flat"><p class="card-title">Steps</p><ol class="list"><li>Weigh your produce after trimming and peeling.</li><li>Divide by the typical weight per jar for that fruit or veg.</li><li>Add 15% extra jars for imperfect packing and headspace.</li><li>Wash and heat that many jars plus lids before you start.</li></ol></div></div></div>	-
45.53.1	dashboard_formula/Snow Shovel Load Formula	<div class="umi-page"><div class="stack"><h1>Snow Shovel Load Formula</h1><p class="muted">Your back will thank you for knowing the weight, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="card"><p class="card-title">The formula</p><pre class="code"><code>load_weight = shovel_volume × snow_densityshovel_volume = width × depth × length</code></pre></div><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Fluffy snow</span><span class="value">10 <span class="unit">lb / cu ft</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Wet snow</span><span class="value">20 <span class="unit">lb / cu ft</span></span></div></div><div class="card flat"><p class="card-title">Steps</p><ol class="list"><li>Measure your shovel blade width and the average snow depth.</li><li>Estimate the length of your push stroke.</li><li>Multiply to get volume in cubic feet.</li><li>Multiply by snow density (10–20 lb/cu ft) to get load weight — keep it under 20–25 lb per scoop to avoid strain.</li></ol></div></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><div class="stack"><h1>Snow Shovel Load Formula</h1><p class="muted">Your back will thank you for knowing the weight, .</p><div class="card"><p class="card-title">The formula</p><pre class="code"><code>load_weight = shovel_volume × snow_densityshovel_volume = width × depth × length</code></pre></div><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Fluffy snow</span><span class="value">10 <span class="unit">lb / cu ft</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Wet snow</span><span class="value">20 <span class="unit">lb / cu ft</span></span></div></div><div class="card flat"><p class="card-title">Steps</p><ol class="list"><li>Measure your shovel blade width and the average snow depth.</li><li>Estimate the length of your push stroke.</li><li>Multiply to get volume in cubic feet.</li><li>Multiply by snow density (10–20 lb/cu ft) to get load weight — keep it under 20–25 lb per scoop to avoid strain.</li></ol></div></div></div>	-
45.54.1	dashboard_formula/Pizza Dough Hydration Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Pizza Dough Hydration Formula</h1><div class="card"><p class="muted">Hydration % = (Water weight ÷ Flour weight) × 100. Use grams for accuracy.</p><pre class="code"><code>water_g = flour_g × hydration / 100</code></pre><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Flour</span><span class="value">500<span class="unit">g</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Hydration</span><span class="value">65<span class="unit">%</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Water</span><span class="value">325<span class="unit">g</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Salt</span><span class="value">10<span class="unit">g</span></span></div></div></div><h2>Steps</h2><ol class="list"><li>Weigh flour.</li><li>Multiply flour by hydration (0.65 for 65%).</li><li>Add salt at 2% of flour weight.</li><li>Mix, rest 20 min, then knead.</li><li>Cold ferment 24–72 h for flavor.</li></ol><p class="muted t-small">Hi <regex>user_name</regex>, 65% is a friendly all-rounder. Bump to 70% for a puffier rim if your flour can take it.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Pizza Dough Hydration Formula</h1><div class="card"><p class="muted">Hydration % = (Water weight ÷ Flour weight) × 100. Use grams for accuracy.</p><pre class="code"><code>water_g = flour_g × hydration / 100</code></pre><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Flour</span><span class="value">500<span class="unit">g</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Hydration</span><span class="value">65<span class="unit">%</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Water</span><span class="value">325<span class="unit">g</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Salt</span><span class="value">10<span class="unit">g</span></span></div></div></div><h2>Steps</h2><ol class="list"><li>Weigh flour.</li><li>Multiply flour by hydration (0.65 for 65%).</li><li>Add salt at 2% of flour weight.</li><li>Mix, rest 20 min, then knead.</li><li>Cold ferment 24–72 h for flavor.</li></ol><p class="muted t-small">Hi , 65% is a friendly all-rounder. Bump to 70% for a puffier rim if your flour can take it.</p></div>	-
45.55.1	dashboard_formula/Wall Paint Coverage Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Wall Paint Coverage Formula</h1><div class="card"><p class="muted">Wall area = perimeter × ceiling height − openings. One gallon covers ~350 sq ft per coat.</p><pre class="code"><code>wall_area = perimeter × height − openingsgallons = (wall_area × coats) / 350</code></pre><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Perimeter</span><span class="value">44<span class="unit">ft</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Height</span><span class="value">8<span class="unit">ft</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Wall area</span><span class="value">312<span class="unit">sq ft</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Paint (2 coats)</span><span class="value">1.8<span class="unit">gal</span></span></div></div></div><h2>Steps</h2><ol class="list"><li>Add wall lengths for perimeter.</li><li>Multiply by ceiling height.</li><li>Subtract doors and windows (20 sq ft each).</li><li>Multiply by number of coats.</li><li>Divide by 350 sq ft per gallon.</li><li>Round up—buy an extra quart for touch-ups.</li></ol><p class="muted t-small">Hi <regex>user_name</regex>, two gallons gives you wiggle room. Leftover paint is a gift to future you.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Wall Paint Coverage Formula</h1><div class="card"><p class="muted">Wall area = perimeter × ceiling height − openings. One gallon covers ~350 sq ft per coat.</p><pre class="code"><code>wall_area = perimeter × height − openingsgallons = (wall_area × coats) / 350</code></pre><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Perimeter</span><span class="value">44<span class="unit">ft</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Height</span><span class="value">8<span class="unit">ft</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Wall area</span><span class="value">312<span class="unit">sq ft</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Paint (2 coats)</span><span class="value">1.8<span class="unit">gal</span></span></div></div></div><h2>Steps</h2><ol class="list"><li>Add wall lengths for perimeter.</li><li>Multiply by ceiling height.</li><li>Subtract doors and windows (20 sq ft each).</li><li>Multiply by number of coats.</li><li>Divide by 350 sq ft per gallon.</li><li>Round up—buy an extra quart for touch-ups.</li></ol><p class="muted t-small">Hi , two gallons gives you wiggle room. Leftover paint is a gift to future you.</p></div>	-
45.56.1	dashboard_formula/Cold Brew Ratio Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Cold Brew Ratio Formula</h1><div class="card"><p class="muted">For concentrate, use 1 part coffee to 4 parts water by weight. Dilute 1:1 with water or milk before drinking.</p><pre class="code"><code>water_g = coffee_g × 4concentrate_yield ≈ water_g × 0.8</code></pre><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Coffee</span><span class="value">100<span class="unit">g</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Water</span><span class="value">400<span class="unit">g</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Yield</span><span class="value">320<span class="unit">g</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Dilution</span><span class="value">1:1</span></div></div></div><h2>Steps</h2><ol class="list"><li>Weigh coffee beans, grind coarse.</li><li>Add 4× water by weight.</li><li>Stir, cover, steep 12–18 h at room temp.</li><li>Strain through a fine mesh or filter.</li><li>Dilute concentrate 1:1 with water or milk.</li></ol><p class="muted t-small">Hi <regex>user_name</regex>, cold brew is forgiving. If it's too strong, just add more water—no rules here.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Cold Brew Ratio Formula</h1><div class="card"><p class="muted">For concentrate, use 1 part coffee to 4 parts water by weight. Dilute 1:1 with water or milk before drinking.</p><pre class="code"><code>water_g = coffee_g × 4concentrate_yield ≈ water_g × 0.8</code></pre><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Coffee</span><span class="value">100<span class="unit">g</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Water</span><span class="value">400<span class="unit">g</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Yield</span><span class="value">320<span class="unit">g</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Dilution</span><span class="value">1:1</span></div></div></div><h2>Steps</h2><ol class="list"><li>Weigh coffee beans, grind coarse.</li><li>Add 4× water by weight.</li><li>Stir, cover, steep 12–18 h at room temp.</li><li>Strain through a fine mesh or filter.</li><li>Dilute concentrate 1:1 with water or milk.</li></ol><p class="muted t-small">Hi , cold brew is forgiving. If it's too strong, just add more water—no rules here.</p></div>	-
45.57.1	dashboard_formula/Candle Wax Amount Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Candle Wax Amount Formula</h1><div class="card"><p class="muted">Wax weight = container volume (oz) × 0.8 (wax density) + 0.2 oz for fragrance oil at 6%.</p><pre class="code"><code>wax_oz = container_oz × 0.8fo_oz = wax_oz × 0.06</code></pre><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Container</span><span class="value">8<span class="unit">oz</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Wax</span><span class="value">6.4<span class="unit">oz</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Fragrance</span><span class="value">0.38<span class="unit">oz</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Total</span><span class="value">6.8<span class="unit">oz</span></span></div></div></div><h2>Steps</h2><ol class="list"><li>Fill container with water, measure ounces.</li><li>Multiply by 0.8 for wax weight.</li><li>Add fragrance oil at 6% of wax weight.</li><li>Melt wax, cool to 185°F, add oil.</li><li>Pour at 135°F, cure 48 h.</li></ol><p class="muted t-small">Hi <regex>user_name</regex>, a kitchen scale makes this easy. No scale? 6.4 oz is about 1 cup of soy flakes.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Candle Wax Amount Formula</h1><div class="card"><p class="muted">Wax weight = container volume (oz) × 0.8 (wax density) + 0.2 oz for fragrance oil at 6%.</p><pre class="code"><code>wax_oz = container_oz × 0.8fo_oz = wax_oz × 0.06</code></pre><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Container</span><span class="value">8<span class="unit">oz</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Wax</span><span class="value">6.4<span class="unit">oz</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Fragrance</span><span class="value">0.38<span class="unit">oz</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Total</span><span class="value">6.8<span class="unit">oz</span></span></div></div></div><h2>Steps</h2><ol class="list"><li>Fill container with water, measure ounces.</li><li>Multiply by 0.8 for wax weight.</li><li>Add fragrance oil at 6% of wax weight.</li><li>Melt wax, cool to 185°F, add oil.</li><li>Pour at 135°F, cure 48 h.</li></ol><p class="muted t-small">Hi , a kitchen scale makes this easy. No scale? 6.4 oz is about 1 cup of soy flakes.</p></div>	-
45.58.1	dashboard_formula/Rice to Water Ratio Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Rice to Water Ratio Formula</h1><div class="card"><p class="muted">Use 1 part rice to 1.5 parts water for long-grain white rice. Add a pinch of salt.</p><pre class="code"><code>water_cups = rice_cups × 1.5</code></pre><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Rice</span><span class="value">2<span class="unit">cups</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Water</span><span class="value">3<span class="unit">cups</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Salt</span><span class="value">0.5<span class="unit">tsp</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Yield</span><span class="value">6<span class="unit">cups</span></span></div></div></div><h2>Steps</h2><ol class="list"><li>Rinse rice until water runs clear.</li><li>Combine rice, water, and salt in pot.</li><li>Bring to boil, then reduce to low.</li><li>Cover and simmer 18 min.</li><li>Rest 10 min off heat, then fluff.</li></ol><p class="muted t-small">Hi <regex>user_name</regex>, brown rice needs 2 parts water and 40 min. White rice is the easy one.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Rice to Water Ratio Formula</h1><div class="card"><p class="muted">Use 1 part rice to 1.5 parts water for long-grain white rice. Add a pinch of salt.</p><pre class="code"><code>water_cups = rice_cups × 1.5</code></pre><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Rice</span><span class="value">2<span class="unit">cups</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Water</span><span class="value">3<span class="unit">cups</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Salt</span><span class="value">0.5<span class="unit">tsp</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Yield</span><span class="value">6<span class="unit">cups</span></span></div></div></div><h2>Steps</h2><ol class="list"><li>Rinse rice until water runs clear.</li><li>Combine rice, water, and salt in pot.</li><li>Bring to boil, then reduce to low.</li><li>Cover and simmer 18 min.</li><li>Rest 10 min off heat, then fluff.</li></ol><p class="muted t-small">Hi , brown rice needs 2 parts water and 40 min. White rice is the easy one.</p></div>	-
45.59.1	dashboard_formula/Cake Pan Conversion Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Cake Pan Conversion Formula</h1><p>So you want to bake a bigger cake. Here's the math.</p><pre class="code"><code>factor = (new_radius / old_radius)<sup>2</sup></code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Measure the radius (half the diameter) of both pans.</li><li>Square the new radius, then divide by the squared old radius.</li><li>Multiply every ingredient by that factor.</li><li>Keep the oven temperature the same, but check for doneness a little earlier or later.</li></ol><p class="muted">Example: 8-inch to 9-inch pan: (4.5/4)<sup>2</sup> ≈ 1.27, so use about 27% more of everything.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Cake Pan Conversion Formula</h1><p>So you want to bake a bigger cake. Here's the math.</p><pre class="code"><code>factor = (new_radius / old_radius)<sup>2</sup></code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Measure the radius (half the diameter) of both pans.</li><li>Square the new radius, then divide by the squared old radius.</li><li>Multiply every ingredient by that factor.</li><li>Keep the oven temperature the same, but check for doneness a little earlier or later.</li></ol><p class="muted">Example: 8-inch to 9-inch pan: (4.5/4)<sup>2</sup> ≈ 1.27, so use about 27% more of everything.</p></div>	-
45.60.1	dashboard_formula/Gravel Driveway Coverage Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Gravel Driveway Coverage Formula</h1><p>No more guessing at the quarry. Measure twice, order once.</p><pre class="code"><code>tons = (length_ft × width_ft × depth_in) / 12 × 1.4</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Measure length and width in feet.</li><li>Decide on depth in inches (2–4 inches for a driveway).</li><li>Plug into the formula. The 1.4 is the density factor for typical gravel.</li><li>Round up to the nearest half-ton.</li></ol><p class="muted">Example: 20 ft × 10 ft × 3 in gives about 7 tons.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Gravel Driveway Coverage Formula</h1><p>No more guessing at the quarry. Measure twice, order once.</p><pre class="code"><code>tons = (length_ft × width_ft × depth_in) / 12 × 1.4</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Measure length and width in feet.</li><li>Decide on depth in inches (2–4 inches for a driveway).</li><li>Plug into the formula. The 1.4 is the density factor for typical gravel.</li><li>Round up to the nearest half-ton.</li></ol><p class="muted">Example: 20 ft × 10 ft × 3 in gives about 7 tons.</p></div>	-
45.61.1	dashboard_formula/Curtain Fabric Yardage Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Curtain Fabric Yardage Formula</h1><p>Before you buy that bolt, do a little math. Your windows will thank you.</p><pre class="code"><code>yards = (window_width × fullness × panels) / 36 + hem_allowance</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Measure window width in inches.</li><li>Choose fullness: 2 for standard, 2.5 for extra lush.</li><li>Multiply by number of panels (usually 2).</li><li>Divide by 36 to convert to yards, then add hem allowance (about 0.5 yards per panel).</li></ol><p class="muted">Example: 60-inch window, 2× fullness, 2 panels: (60 × 2 × 2)/36 = 6.67 yards, plus hems ≈ 7.67 yards.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Curtain Fabric Yardage Formula</h1><p>Before you buy that bolt, do a little math. Your windows will thank you.</p><pre class="code"><code>yards = (window_width × fullness × panels) / 36 + hem_allowance</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Measure window width in inches.</li><li>Choose fullness: 2 for standard, 2.5 for extra lush.</li><li>Multiply by number of panels (usually 2).</li><li>Divide by 36 to convert to yards, then add hem allowance (about 0.5 yards per panel).</li></ol><p class="muted">Example: 60-inch window, 2× fullness, 2 panels: (60 × 2 × 2)/36 = 6.67 yards, plus hems ≈ 7.67 yards.</p></div>	-
45.62.1	dashboard_formula/Drywall Sheet Count Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Drywall Sheet Count Formula</h1><p>Don't get stuck with a half-finished wall. Plan your drywall order like a pro.</p><pre class="code"><code>sheets = (wall_area_sqft / 32) × 1.1</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Measure total wall area: length × height for each wall, then sum.</li><li>Divide by 32 (square feet per 4×8 sheet).</li><li>Multiply by 1.1 to add 10% for cuts and waste.</li><li>Round up to the next whole sheet.</li></ol><p class="muted">Example: 500 sq ft of wall: (500/32) × 1.1 ≈ 17.2, so buy 18 sheets.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Drywall Sheet Count Formula</h1><p>Don't get stuck with a half-finished wall. Plan your drywall order like a pro.</p><pre class="code"><code>sheets = (wall_area_sqft / 32) × 1.1</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Measure total wall area: length × height for each wall, then sum.</li><li>Divide by 32 (square feet per 4×8 sheet).</li><li>Multiply by 1.1 to add 10% for cuts and waste.</li><li>Round up to the next whole sheet.</li></ol><p class="muted">Example: 500 sq ft of wall: (500/32) × 1.1 ≈ 17.2, so buy 18 sheets.</p></div>	-
45.63.1	dashboard_formula/Pizza Dough Amount Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Pizza Dough Amount Formula</h1><p>Ever ended up with a doughy frisbee? Let's get the ratio right.</p><pre class="code"><code>dough_grams = 3.14 × (diameter_in / 2)<sup>2</sup> × 0.4</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Measure your pizza pan or stone diameter in inches.</li><li>Square the radius (half the diameter), multiply by π.</li><li>Multiply by 0.4 grams per square inch for a thin crust (use 0.5 for thicker).</li><li>Round to the nearest 10 grams.</li></ol><p class="muted">Example: 12-inch pizza: 3.14 × 6<sup>2</sup> × 0.4 ≈ 45 grams.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Pizza Dough Amount Formula</h1><p>Ever ended up with a doughy frisbee? Let's get the ratio right.</p><pre class="code"><code>dough_grams = 3.14 × (diameter_in / 2)<sup>2</sup> × 0.4</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Measure your pizza pan or stone diameter in inches.</li><li>Square the radius (half the diameter), multiply by π.</li><li>Multiply by 0.4 grams per square inch for a thin crust (use 0.5 for thicker).</li><li>Round to the nearest 10 grams.</li></ol><p class="muted">Example: 12-inch pizza: 3.14 × 6<sup>2</sup> × 0.4 ≈ 45 grams.</p></div>	-
45.0.2	dashboard_formula/Wallpaper Roll Count Formula	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Wallpaper Roll Count Formula</h1><p>Wallpaper math: not glamorous, but essential. Here's how to avoid the dreaded mismatch.</p><pre class="code"><code>rolls = (wall_area_sqft / 30) × 1.15</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Measure total wall area: perimeter × height, minus doors and windows.</li><li>Divide by 30 (usable square feet per single roll).</li><li>Multiply by 1.15 for pattern matching and waste.</li><li>Round up to the nearest whole roll.</li></ol><p class="muted">Example: 400 sq ft: (400/30) × 1.15 ≈ 15.3, so buy 16 rolls.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Wallpaper Roll Count Formula</h1><p>Wallpaper math: not glamorous, but essential. Here's how to avoid the dreaded mismatch.</p><pre class="code"><code>rolls = (wall_area_sqft / 30) × 1.15</code></pre><ol class="list"><li>Measure total wall area: perimeter × height, minus doors and windows.</li><li>Divide by 30 (usable square feet per single roll).</li><li>Multiply by 1.15 for pattern matching and waste.</li><li>Round up to the nearest whole roll.</li></ol><p class="muted">Example: 400 sq ft: (400/30) × 1.15 ≈ 15.3, so buy 16 rolls.</p></div>	-
45.1.2	dashboard_formula/Pizza Party Formula	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Pizza Party Count</h1>  <p>No one wants to be the person who ordered too little pizza. Or too much. Well, too much is fine.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>pizzas = (guests × slices_per_person) / slices_per_pizza</code></pre>  <p><strong>Rules of thumb:</strong></p>  <ul class="list">    <li>Light eaters: 2 slices</li>    <li>Regular: 3 slices</li>    <li>Hungry crowd: 4 slices</li>  </ul>  <p><strong>Example:</strong> 10 guests × 3 = 30 slices ÷ 8 = 3.75 → order 4 pizzas.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Steps</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Count guests.</li>        <li>Pick slices per person.</li>        <li>Divide by slices per pizza.</li>        <li>Round up.</li>      </ol>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Pizza Party Count</h1>  <p>No one wants to be the person who ordered too little pizza. Or too much. Well, too much is fine.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>pizzas = (guests × slices_per_person) / slices_per_pizza</code></pre>  <p><strong>Rules of thumb:</strong></p>  <ul class="list">    <li>Light eaters: 2 slices</li>    <li>Regular: 3 slices</li>    <li>Hungry crowd: 4 slices</li>  </ul>  <p><strong>Example:</strong> 10 guests × 3 = 30 slices ÷ 8 = 3.75 → order 4 pizzas.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Steps</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Count guests.</li>        <li>Pick slices per person.</li>        <li>Divide by slices per pizza.</li>        <li>Round up.</li>      </ol>    </div>  </details></div>	-
45.2.2	dashboard_formula/Pasta Water Formula	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Pasta Water Ratio</h1>  <p>Pasta needs room to swim. Too little water and it sticks; too much and it takes forever to boil.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>water_quarts = pasta_pounds × 4</code></pre>  <p><strong>Example:</strong> 1 lb pasta → 4 quarts water.</p>  <p><strong>Salt:</strong> 1 tablespoon per 4 quarts.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Steps</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Fill pot with water.</li>        <li>Add salt, bring to rolling boil.</li>        <li>Add pasta, stir.</li>        <li>Cook to package time, minus 1 minute.</li>      </ol>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Pasta Water Ratio</h1>  <p>Pasta needs room to swim. Too little water and it sticks; too much and it takes forever to boil.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>water_quarts = pasta_pounds × 4</code></pre>  <p><strong>Example:</strong> 1 lb pasta → 4 quarts water.</p>  <p><strong>Salt:</strong> 1 tablespoon per 4 quarts.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Steps</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Fill pot with water.</li>        <li>Add salt, bring to rolling boil.</li>        <li>Add pasta, stir.</li>        <li>Cook to package time, minus 1 minute.</li>      </ol>    </div>  </details></div>	-
45.3.2	dashboard_formula/Candle Wax Formula	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Candle Wax Amount</h1>  <p>Wax is measured by weight, not volume. A scale is your friend here.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>wax_oz = container_oz × 0.8</code></pre>  <p><strong>Example:</strong> 8 oz jar → 6.4 oz wax.</p>  <p><strong>Fragrance:</strong> 1 oz per pound of wax (6%).</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Steps</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Weigh empty container.</li>        <li>Fill with water to desired level, weigh again.</li>        <li>Subtract for water weight, multiply by 0.8.</li>        <li>Melt wax, add fragrance at 185°F.</li>      </ol>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Candle Wax Amount</h1>  <p>Wax is measured by weight, not volume. A scale is your friend here.</p>  <pre class="code"><code>wax_oz = container_oz × 0.8</code></pre>  <p><strong>Example:</strong> 8 oz jar → 6.4 oz wax.</p>  <p><strong>Fragrance:</strong> 1 oz per pound of wax (6%).</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Steps</summary>    <div class="body">      <ol class="list">        <li>Weigh empty container.</li>        <li>Fill with water to desired level, weigh again.</li>        <li>Subtract for water weight, multiply by 0.8.</li>        <li>Melt wax, add fragrance at 185°F.</li>      </ol>    </div>  </details></div>	-
46.0.0	dashboard_glossary/Coffee Brewing Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Coffee, decoded</h1><p class="muted">A few words that make baristas sound mysterious, <regex>user_name</regex>. Now you're in on it.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Water temp</span><span class="value">92<span class="unit">°C</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Ratio start</span><span class="value">1:16</span></div></div><ul class="list"><li><b>Bloom</b> — the first pour, when grounds puff and release gas. If it doesn't bubble, your beans are tired.</li><li><b>Extraction</b> — how much flavour you pulled out. Too little tastes sour; too much tastes bitter.</li><li><b>Ratio</b> — coffee to water by weight. 1:16 is a friendly default.</li><li><b>Crema</b> — the tan foam on espresso. Pretty, but not a flavour guarantee.</li><li><b>Tamp</b> — pressing the grounds level. Firm and even beats hard.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Quick pourover steps</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Rinse the filter, warm the cup.</li><li>30g coffee, medium grind.</li><li>Pour 60g water, wait 40s.</li><li>Pour to 500g in slow circles.</li></ol></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Coffee, decoded</h1><p class="muted">A few words that make baristas sound mysterious, . Now you're in on it.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Water temp</span><span class="value">92<span class="unit">°C</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Ratio start</span><span class="value">1:16</span></div></div><ul class="list"><li><b>Bloom</b> — the first pour, when grounds puff and release gas. If it doesn't bubble, your beans are tired.</li><li><b>Extraction</b> — how much flavour you pulled out. Too little tastes sour; too much tastes bitter.</li><li><b>Ratio</b> — coffee to water by weight. 1:16 is a friendly default.</li><li><b>Crema</b> — the tan foam on espresso. Pretty, but not a flavour guarantee.</li><li><b>Tamp</b> — pressing the grounds level. Firm and even beats hard.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Quick pourover steps</summary><div class="body"><ol class="list"><li>Rinse the filter, warm the cup.</li><li>30g coffee, medium grind.</li><li>Pour 60g water, wait 40s.</li><li>Pour to 500g in slow circles.</li></ol></div></details></div>	-
46.1.0	dashboard_glossary/Knitting Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Knitting, translated</h1><p class="muted">The hobby has its own dialect. Here's the useful half, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Garter stitch</b> — knit every row. Flat, squishy, lies still.</li><li><b>Stockinette</b> — knit one row, purl one. Smooth face, curly edges.</li><li><b>Frogging</b> — ripping out rows. Named for the rip-it sound. We've all been there.</li><li><b>WIP</b> — work in progress. Plural: a drawer.</li><li><b>Gauge</b> — stitches per inch. Match it or the sweater fits someone else.</li><li><b>YO</b> — yarn over, a deliberate little hole.</li></ul><div class="callout info"><p>Swatch before you commit. Ten minutes now, a wearable later.</p></div><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 300 60" role="img" aria-label="Knit and purl stitch pattern"><path class="line" d="M10 40 q15 -22 30 0 q15 22 30 0 q15 -22 30 0 q15 22 30 0 q15 -22 30 0 q15 22 30 0 q15 -22 30 0" fill="none"/></svg></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Knitting, translated</h1><p class="muted">The hobby has its own dialect. Here's the useful half, .</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Garter stitch</b> — knit every row. Flat, squishy, lies still.</li><li><b>Stockinette</b> — knit one row, purl one. Smooth face, curly edges.</li><li><b>Frogging</b> — ripping out rows. Named for the rip-it sound. We've all been there.</li><li><b>WIP</b> — work in progress. Plural: a drawer.</li><li><b>Gauge</b> — stitches per inch. Match it or the sweater fits someone else.</li><li><b>YO</b> — yarn over, a deliberate little hole.</li></ul><div class="callout info"><p>Swatch before you commit. Ten minutes now, a wearable later.</p></div><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 300 60" role="img" aria-label="Knit and purl stitch pattern"><path class="line" d="M10 40 q15 -22 30 0 q15 22 30 0 q15 -22 30 0 q15 22 30 0 q15 -22 30 0 q15 22 30 0 q15 -22 30 0" fill="none"/></svg></div></div>	-
46.2.0	dashboard_glossary/Home Networking Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Home network, plain English</h1><p class="muted">Enough vocabulary to hold your own when the internet misbehaves, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Modem</b> — the box that talks to your provider. Your front door.</li><li><b>Router</b> — sorts traffic inside the house. Your hallway.</li><li><b>SSID</b> — the Wi-Fi name you pick from a list. Not the password. Different thing.</li><li><b>DHCP</b> — hands each device an address automatically, so you never assign one by hand.</li><li><b>Bandwidth</b> — how much fits at once. <b>Latency</b> — how long one round trip takes.</li><li><b>DNS</b> — the phone book turning names into numbers.</li></ul><div class="callout warn"><p>Most "slow internet" complaints are actually weak Wi-Fi. Test plugged in first.</p></div><details class="acc"><summary>Is it the router or the ISP?</summary><div class="body"><p>Unplug the router, plug a laptop straight into the modem. Fast? It's the router. Still slow? Call your provider.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Home network, plain English</h1><p class="muted">Enough vocabulary to hold your own when the internet misbehaves, .</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Modem</b> — the box that talks to your provider. Your front door.</li><li><b>Router</b> — sorts traffic inside the house. Your hallway.</li><li><b>SSID</b> — the Wi-Fi name you pick from a list. Not the password. Different thing.</li><li><b>DHCP</b> — hands each device an address automatically, so you never assign one by hand.</li><li><b>Bandwidth</b> — how much fits at once. <b>Latency</b> — how long one round trip takes.</li><li><b>DNS</b> — the phone book turning names into numbers.</li></ul><div class="callout warn"><p>Most "slow internet" complaints are actually weak Wi-Fi. Test plugged in first.</p></div><details class="acc"><summary>Is it the router or the ISP?</summary><div class="body"><p>Unplug the router, plug a laptop straight into the modem. Fast? It's the router. Still slow? Call your provider.</p></div></details></div>	-
46.3.0	dashboard_glossary/Bread Baking Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Bread words worth knowing</h1><p class="muted">Sourdough people use odd words with total confidence. Here's the key, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Hydration</span><span class="value">75<span class="unit">%</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Bulk</span><span class="value">4<span class="unit">h</span></span></div></div><ul class="list"><li><b>Starter</b> — flour and water with wild yeast living in it. Feed it, and it feeds you.</li><li><b>Autolyse</b> — resting flour and water before salt. Less kneading, better crumb.</li><li><b>Bulk ferment</b> — the first long rise, before shaping.</li><li><b>Proof</b> — the final rise. Poke it: slow spring back means ready.</li><li><b>Crumb</b> — the inside texture. Open crumb means big airy holes.</li><li><b>Scoring</b> — the slash on top. It tells the loaf where to expand.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Why is my loaf dense?</summary><div class="body"><p>Usually under-proofed or the starter was sluggish. Give it more time, warmer spot, no rush.</p></div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Bread words worth knowing</h1><p class="muted">Sourdough people use odd words with total confidence. Here's the key, .</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Hydration</span><span class="value">75<span class="unit">%</span></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Bulk</span><span class="value">4<span class="unit">h</span></span></div></div><ul class="list"><li><b>Starter</b> — flour and water with wild yeast living in it. Feed it, and it feeds you.</li><li><b>Autolyse</b> — resting flour and water before salt. Less kneading, better crumb.</li><li><b>Bulk ferment</b> — the first long rise, before shaping.</li><li><b>Proof</b> — the final rise. Poke it: slow spring back means ready.</li><li><b>Crumb</b> — the inside texture. Open crumb means big airy holes.</li><li><b>Scoring</b> — the slash on top. It tells the loaf where to expand.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Why is my loaf dense?</summary><div class="body"><p>Usually under-proofed or the starter was sluggish. Give it more time, warmer spot, no rush.</p></div></details></div>	-
46.4.0	dashboard_glossary/Personal Finance Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Money words, no jargon</h1><p class="muted">Six terms that do most of the work in any finance conversation, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>APR</b> — yearly cost of borrowing, including fees. Loans quote this.</li><li><b>APY</b> — yearly earnings on savings, with compounding. Higher is nicer here.</li><li><b>Compound interest</b> — returns earning returns. Boring until it's exciting.</li><li><b>Premium</b> — what you pay monthly for insurance. <b>Deductible</b> — what you pay before it kicks in.</li><li><b>Liquidity</b> — how fast an asset becomes cash without losing value.</li><li><b>Net worth</b> — everything you own minus everything you owe.</li></ul><div class="callout good"><p>Rule of 72: divide 72 by the rate to estimate years to double. At 6%, about 12 years.</p></div><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 300 80" role="img" aria-label="Compounding growth curve"><path class="area" d="M10 70 C90 68 170 55 290 12 L290 74 L10 74 Z"/><path class="line" d="M10 70 C90 68 170 55 290 12" fill="none"/></svg></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Money words, no jargon</h1><p class="muted">Six terms that do most of the work in any finance conversation, .</p><ul class="list"><li><b>APR</b> — yearly cost of borrowing, including fees. Loans quote this.</li><li><b>APY</b> — yearly earnings on savings, with compounding. Higher is nicer here.</li><li><b>Compound interest</b> — returns earning returns. Boring until it's exciting.</li><li><b>Premium</b> — what you pay monthly for insurance. <b>Deductible</b> — what you pay before it kicks in.</li><li><b>Liquidity</b> — how fast an asset becomes cash without losing value.</li><li><b>Net worth</b> — everything you own minus everything you owe.</li></ul><div class="callout good"><p>Rule of 72: divide 72 by the rate to estimate years to double. At 6%, about 12 years.</p></div><div class="chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 300 80" role="img" aria-label="Compounding growth curve"><path class="area" d="M10 70 C90 68 170 55 290 12 L290 74 L10 74 Z"/><path class="line" d="M10 70 C90 68 170 55 290 12" fill="none"/></svg></div></div>	-
46.5.0	dashboard_glossary/Photography Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Camera talk, sorted</h1><p class="muted">Three settings do almost everything. The rest is vocabulary, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Aperture</span><span class="value">f/2.8</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Shutter</span><span class="value">1/250<span class="unit">s</span></span></div></div><ul class="list"><li><b>Aperture</b> — the hole size. Lower f-number, more light and blurrier background.</li><li><b>Shutter speed</b> — how long the sensor sees. Fast freezes motion; slow smears it.</li><li><b>ISO</b> — sensitivity. Raise it in the dark, pay in grain.</li><li><b>Exposure triangle</b> — those three, trading off against each other.</li><li><b>Depth of field</b> — how much is in focus front to back.</li><li><b>White balance</b> — fixes colour cast so whites look white.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Blurry photos, why?</summary><div class="body"><p>Shutter too slow for how steady your hands are. Speed up, brace your elbows, or raise ISO.</p></div></details><div class="legend"><i></i> f/1.8 — creamy <i class="alt"></i> f/16 — everything sharp</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Camera talk, sorted</h1><p class="muted">Three settings do almost everything. The rest is vocabulary, .</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Aperture</span><span class="value">f/2.8</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Shutter</span><span class="value">1/250<span class="unit">s</span></span></div></div><ul class="list"><li><b>Aperture</b> — the hole size. Lower f-number, more light and blurrier background.</li><li><b>Shutter speed</b> — how long the sensor sees. Fast freezes motion; slow smears it.</li><li><b>ISO</b> — sensitivity. Raise it in the dark, pay in grain.</li><li><b>Exposure triangle</b> — those three, trading off against each other.</li><li><b>Depth of field</b> — how much is in focus front to back.</li><li><b>White balance</b> — fixes colour cast so whites look white.</li></ul><details class="acc"><summary>Blurry photos, why?</summary><div class="body"><p>Shutter too slow for how steady your hands are. Speed up, brace your elbows, or raise ISO.</p></div></details><div class="legend"><i></i> f/1.8 — creamy <i class="alt"></i> f/16 — everything sharp</div></div>	-
46.6.0	dashboard_glossary/Sourdough Glossary	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Sourdough Glossary</h1>  <p class="t-small dim">A quick reference for <regex>user_name</regex>.</p>  <div class="card">    <ul class="list">      <li><b>Autolyse</b> — Resting flour and water before adding starter or salt; hydrates flour and builds extensibility.</li>      <li><b>Bulk fermentation</b> — The first rise, where the whole dough mass ferments and develops flavor.</li>      <li><b>Crumb</b> — The internal texture of the baked loaf; can be open (large holes) or tight.</li>      <li><b>Hydration</b> — Ratio of water to flour by weight, expressed as a percentage (e.g., 75%).</li>      <li><b>Levain</b> — A preferment made from starter, flour, and water, used to leaven a specific dough.</li>      <li><b>Proof</b> — The final rise after shaping, before baking.</li>      <li><b>Starter</b> — A stable culture of wild yeast and bacteria kept alive with regular feedings.</li>    </ul>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Quick tip</summary>    <div class="body"><p>If your dough feels slack, give it a fold. Time does most of the work.</p></div>  </details></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Sourdough Glossary</h1>  <p class="t-small dim">A quick reference for .</p>  <div class="card">    <ul class="list">      <li><b>Autolyse</b> — Resting flour and water before adding starter or salt; hydrates flour and builds extensibility.</li>      <li><b>Bulk fermentation</b> — The first rise, where the whole dough mass ferments and develops flavor.</li>      <li><b>Crumb</b> — The internal texture of the baked loaf; can be open (large holes) or tight.</li>      <li><b>Hydration</b> — Ratio of water to flour by weight, expressed as a percentage (e.g., 75%).</li>      <li><b>Levain</b> — A preferment made from starter, flour, and water, used to leaven a specific dough.</li>      <li><b>Proof</b> — The final rise after shaping, before baking.</li>      <li><b>Starter</b> — A stable culture of wild yeast and bacteria kept alive with regular feedings.</li>    </ul>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Quick tip</summary>    <div class="body"><p>If your dough feels slack, give it a fold. Time does most of the work.</p></div>  </details></div>	-
46.7.0	dashboard_glossary/Rock Climbing Glossary	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Rock Climbing Glossary</h1>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <ul class="list">        <li><b>Beta</b> — Advice or sequence for a climb.</li>        <li><b>Belay</b> — Securing a climber with a rope.</li>        <li><b>Crux</b> — The hardest move or section.</li>        <li><b>Dyno</b> — A dynamic leap to a hold.</li>      </ul>    </div>    <div class="card">      <ul class="list">        <li><b>Flash</b> — Finishing a climb first try with beta.</li>        <li><b>Onsight</b> — First try with no beta.</li>        <li><b>Pump</b> — Forearm fatigue from over-gripping.</li>        <li><b>Send</b> — To climb a route cleanly.</li>      </ul>    </div>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Safety first</summary>    <div class="body"><p>Always double-check your knot and belay device. It takes ten seconds.</p></div>  </details></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Rock Climbing Glossary</h1>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <ul class="list">        <li><b>Beta</b> — Advice or sequence for a climb.</li>        <li><b>Belay</b> — Securing a climber with a rope.</li>        <li><b>Crux</b> — The hardest move or section.</li>        <li><b>Dyno</b> — A dynamic leap to a hold.</li>      </ul>    </div>    <div class="card">      <ul class="list">        <li><b>Flash</b> — Finishing a climb first try with beta.</li>        <li><b>Onsight</b> — First try with no beta.</li>        <li><b>Pump</b> — Forearm fatigue from over-gripping.</li>        <li><b>Send</b> — To climb a route cleanly.</li>      </ul>    </div>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Safety first</summary>    <div class="body"><p>Always double-check your knot and belay device. It takes ten seconds.</p></div>  </details></div>	-
46.8.0	dashboard_glossary/Yoga Glossary	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Yoga Glossary</h1>  <div class="card">    <ul class="list">      <li><b>Asana</b> — Physical posture or seat.</li>      <li><b>Chakra</b> — Energy center in the subtle body.</li>      <li><b>Mantra</b> — Sacred sound or phrase repeated.</li>      <li><b>Namaste</b> — A respectful greeting; "I bow to you."</li>      <li><b>Pranayama</b> — Breath control practices.</li>      <li><b>Savasana</b> — Corpse pose; final relaxation.</li>      <li><b>Vinyasa</b> — Linking breath and movement.</li>    </ul>  </div>  <div class="callout info">    <p><span class="badge info"><i class="dot"></i>Note</span> Sanskrit terms can have multiple meanings depending on context.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Yoga Glossary</h1>  <div class="card">    <ul class="list">      <li><b>Asana</b> — Physical posture or seat.</li>      <li><b>Chakra</b> — Energy center in the subtle body.</li>      <li><b>Mantra</b> — Sacred sound or phrase repeated.</li>      <li><b>Namaste</b> — A respectful greeting; "I bow to you."</li>      <li><b>Pranayama</b> — Breath control practices.</li>      <li><b>Savasana</b> — Corpse pose; final relaxation.</li>      <li><b>Vinyasa</b> — Linking breath and movement.</li>    </ul>  </div>  <div class="callout info">    <p><span class="badge info"><i class="dot"></i>Note</span> Sanskrit terms can have multiple meanings depending on context.</p>  </div></div>	-
46.9.0	dashboard_glossary/Gardening Glossary	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Gardening Glossary</h1>  <div class="card">    <ul class="list">      <li><b>Annual</b> — Completes its life cycle in one season.</li>      <li><b>Perennial</b> — Lives for more than two years.</li>      <li><b>Bolting</b> — Premature flowering, often triggered by heat.</li>      <li><b>Companion planting</b> — Growing certain plants together for mutual benefit.</li>      <li><b>Deadheading</b> — Removing spent flowers to encourage more blooms.</li>      <li><b>Mulch</b> — Material spread on soil to retain moisture and suppress weeds.</li>    </ul>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>For <regex>user_name</regex></summary>    <div class="body"><p>Start with easy herbs like basil and mint. They forgive a lot.</p></div>  </details></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Gardening Glossary</h1>  <div class="card">    <ul class="list">      <li><b>Annual</b> — Completes its life cycle in one season.</li>      <li><b>Perennial</b> — Lives for more than two years.</li>      <li><b>Bolting</b> — Premature flowering, often triggered by heat.</li>      <li><b>Companion planting</b> — Growing certain plants together for mutual benefit.</li>      <li><b>Deadheading</b> — Removing spent flowers to encourage more blooms.</li>      <li><b>Mulch</b> — Material spread on soil to retain moisture and suppress weeds.</li>    </ul>  </div>  <details class="acc">    <summary>For </summary>    <div class="body"><p>Start with easy herbs like basil and mint. They forgive a lot.</p></div>  </details></div>	-
46.10.0	dashboard_glossary/Wine Tasting Glossary	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Wine Tasting Glossary</h1>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <ul class="list">        <li><b>Tannin</b> — Astringent compounds from skins, seeds, and oak.</li>        <li><b>Terroir</b> — The complete natural environment where grapes grow.</li>        <li><b>Legs</b> — The streaks left on the glass; indicate alcohol or sugar.</li>        <li><b>Body</b> — The weight and texture of the wine on your palate.</li>      </ul>    </div>    <div class="card">      <ul class="list">        <li><b>Dry</b> — Not sweet; fermented to near-zero sugar.</li>        <li><b>Oaky</b> — Flavors from barrel aging, like vanilla or toast.</li>        <li><b>Nose</b> — The aroma of the wine.</li>        <li><b>Finish</b> — The lingering taste after swallowing.</li>      </ul>    </div>  </div>  <p class="t-small dim">Sip, don't gulp. For <regex>user_name</regex>, that's the only rule.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Wine Tasting Glossary</h1>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <ul class="list">        <li><b>Tannin</b> — Astringent compounds from skins, seeds, and oak.</li>        <li><b>Terroir</b> — The complete natural environment where grapes grow.</li>        <li><b>Legs</b> — The streaks left on the glass; indicate alcohol or sugar.</li>        <li><b>Body</b> — The weight and texture of the wine on your palate.</li>      </ul>    </div>    <div class="card">      <ul class="list">        <li><b>Dry</b> — Not sweet; fermented to near-zero sugar.</li>        <li><b>Oaky</b> — Flavors from barrel aging, like vanilla or toast.</li>        <li><b>Nose</b> — The aroma of the wine.</li>        <li><b>Finish</b> — The lingering taste after swallowing.</li>      </ul>    </div>  </div>  <p class="t-small dim">Sip, don't gulp. For , that's the only rule.</p></div>	-
46.11.0	dashboard_glossary/Ham Radio Glossary	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Ham Radio Glossary</h1>  <div class="card">    <ul class="list">      <li><b>73</b> — Best regards (sign-off).</li>      <li><b>CQ</b> — Calling any station.</li>      <li><b>QRP</b> — Low-power operation (usually 5 watts or less).</li>      <li><b>QSO</b> — A two-way radio contact.</li>      <li><b>RST</b> — Readability, strength, tone report.</li>      <li><b>Elmer</b> — A mentor who helps new hams.</li>      <li><b>DX</b> — Distant station or country.</li>    </ul>  </div>  <div class="callout good">    <p><span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>Tip</span> Listen first. You'll learn the rhythm before you key up, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Ham Radio Glossary</h1>  <div class="card">    <ul class="list">      <li><b>73</b> — Best regards (sign-off).</li>      <li><b>CQ</b> — Calling any station.</li>      <li><b>QRP</b> — Low-power operation (usually 5 watts or less).</li>      <li><b>QSO</b> — A two-way radio contact.</li>      <li><b>RST</b> — Readability, strength, tone report.</li>      <li><b>Elmer</b> — A mentor who helps new hams.</li>      <li><b>DX</b> — Distant station or country.</li>    </ul>  </div>  <div class="callout good">    <p><span class="badge good"><i class="dot"></i>Tip</span> Listen first. You'll learn the rhythm before you key up, .</p>  </div></div>	-
46.12.0	dashboard_glossary/Chess Openings Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Chess Openings Glossary</h1><p class="muted">Terms you'll hear at the board, minus the intimidation.</p><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Opening Ideas</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Gambit</b> — Sacrifice material (usually a pawn) for faster development or an attack. Bold, sometimes reckless.</li><li><b>Tempo</b> — A unit of time measured in moves. Gaining tempo means developing while threatening something.</li><li><b>Development</b> — Getting your pieces off the back rank and into useful squares.</li><li><b>Castling</b> — The one move where king and rook swap. Tucks the king safe and activates the rook.</li><li><b>En passant</b> — A pawn can capture an adjacent pawn that just moved two squares, as if it moved one.</li><li><b>Fianchetto</b> — Developing a bishop to the long diagonal, often behind a knight pawn.</li></ul></div><div class="card flat"><h2 class="card-title">Common Openings</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Sicilian Defense</b> — 1.e4 c5. Black fights for the center asymmetrically. Sharp and popular.</li><li><b>Ruy Lopez</b> — 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bb5. Spanish opening. Rich in strategy.</li><li><b>Queen's Gambit</b> — 1.d4 d5 2.c4. Not a true gambit; the pawn is usually regained.</li></ul></div><div class="callout info"><b>Tip:</b> Don't memorize lines before you understand the ideas. Openings are conversations, not scripts.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Chess Openings Glossary</h1><p class="muted">Terms you'll hear at the board, minus the intimidation.</p><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Opening Ideas</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Gambit</b> — Sacrifice material (usually a pawn) for faster development or an attack. Bold, sometimes reckless.</li><li><b>Tempo</b> — A unit of time measured in moves. Gaining tempo means developing while threatening something.</li><li><b>Development</b> — Getting your pieces off the back rank and into useful squares.</li><li><b>Castling</b> — The one move where king and rook swap. Tucks the king safe and activates the rook.</li><li><b>En passant</b> — A pawn can capture an adjacent pawn that just moved two squares, as if it moved one.</li><li><b>Fianchetto</b> — Developing a bishop to the long diagonal, often behind a knight pawn.</li></ul></div><div class="card flat"><h2 class="card-title">Common Openings</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Sicilian Defense</b> — 1.e4 c5. Black fights for the center asymmetrically. Sharp and popular.</li><li><b>Ruy Lopez</b> — 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bb5. Spanish opening. Rich in strategy.</li><li><b>Queen's Gambit</b> — 1.d4 d5 2.c4. Not a true gambit; the pawn is usually regained.</li></ul></div><div class="callout info"><b>Tip:</b> Don't memorize lines before you understand the ideas. Openings are conversations, not scripts.</div></div>	-
46.13.0	dashboard_glossary/Screenwriting Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Screenwriting Glossary</h1><p class="muted">The vocabulary behind every script you've ever cried at.</p><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Script Mechanics</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Slugline</b> — The all-caps scene heading: INT. COFFEE SHOP - DAY. Tells us where and when.</li><li><b>Action line</b> — What we see happening. Present tense, visual, no camera directions unless you're the director.</li><li><b>Parenthetical</b> — A brief note under a character name, like (whispering). Use sparingly.</li><li><b>V.O.</b> — Voice over. Narration not heard by other characters.</li><li><b>O.S.</b> — Off screen. A character is in the scene but not visible.</li><li><b>Montage</b> — A series of quick shots compressing time or information.</li></ul></div><div class="card flat"><h2 class="card-title">Story Tools</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Logline</b> — One sentence that sells your story. Protagonist, goal, obstacle, stakes.</li><li><b>Beat</b> — A small event that changes the emotional direction of a scene.</li><li><b>Arc</b> — The journey a character takes over the story.</li></ul></div><div class="callout good"><b>Rule of thumb:</b> If it can't be seen or heard, it doesn't belong in the script.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Screenwriting Glossary</h1><p class="muted">The vocabulary behind every script you've ever cried at.</p><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Script Mechanics</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Slugline</b> — The all-caps scene heading: INT. COFFEE SHOP - DAY. Tells us where and when.</li><li><b>Action line</b> — What we see happening. Present tense, visual, no camera directions unless you're the director.</li><li><b>Parenthetical</b> — A brief note under a character name, like (whispering). Use sparingly.</li><li><b>V.O.</b> — Voice over. Narration not heard by other characters.</li><li><b>O.S.</b> — Off screen. A character is in the scene but not visible.</li><li><b>Montage</b> — A series of quick shots compressing time or information.</li></ul></div><div class="card flat"><h2 class="card-title">Story Tools</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Logline</b> — One sentence that sells your story. Protagonist, goal, obstacle, stakes.</li><li><b>Beat</b> — A small event that changes the emotional direction of a scene.</li><li><b>Arc</b> — The journey a character takes over the story.</li></ul></div><div class="callout good"><b>Rule of thumb:</b> If it can't be seen or heard, it doesn't belong in the script.</div></div>	-
46.14.0	dashboard_glossary/Beekeeping Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Beekeeping Glossary</h1><p class="muted">Because bees don't come with a manual.</p><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Hive &amp; Hardware</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Super</b> — A box added on top for honey storage. The bees' attic.</li><li><b>Brood box</b> — The lower box where the queen lays eggs and the colony raises young.</li><li><b>Queen excluder</b> — A grate that keeps the queen out of honey supers. Workers slip through; she doesn't.</li><li><b>Frames</b> — Wooden rectangles holding wax foundation. Bees build comb inside them.</li><li><b>Smoker</b> — Puffs cool smoke to calm bees during inspection.</li></ul></div><div class="card flat"><h2 class="card-title">Bee Life</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Brood</b> — Eggs, larvae, and pupae. The next generation in progress.</li><li><b>Nectar flow</b> — When flowers are producing heavily and bees bring in surplus nectar.</li><li><b>Robbing</b> — Bees stealing honey from a weaker hive. Lock your doors.</li><li><b>Swarm</b> — When half the colony leaves with the old queen to start a new home.</li></ul></div><div class="callout warn"><b>Heads up:</b> Always inspect on a warm, calm day. Wind makes bees grumpy and you clumsy.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Beekeeping Glossary</h1><p class="muted">Because bees don't come with a manual.</p><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Hive &amp; Hardware</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Super</b> — A box added on top for honey storage. The bees' attic.</li><li><b>Brood box</b> — The lower box where the queen lays eggs and the colony raises young.</li><li><b>Queen excluder</b> — A grate that keeps the queen out of honey supers. Workers slip through; she doesn't.</li><li><b>Frames</b> — Wooden rectangles holding wax foundation. Bees build comb inside them.</li><li><b>Smoker</b> — Puffs cool smoke to calm bees during inspection.</li></ul></div><div class="card flat"><h2 class="card-title">Bee Life</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Brood</b> — Eggs, larvae, and pupae. The next generation in progress.</li><li><b>Nectar flow</b> — When flowers are producing heavily and bees bring in surplus nectar.</li><li><b>Robbing</b> — Bees stealing honey from a weaker hive. Lock your doors.</li><li><b>Swarm</b> — When half the colony leaves with the old queen to start a new home.</li></ul></div><div class="callout warn"><b>Heads up:</b> Always inspect on a warm, calm day. Wind makes bees grumpy and you clumsy.</div></div>	-
46.15.0	dashboard_glossary/Pottery Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Pottery Glossary</h1><p class="muted">Clay, fire, and the words in between.</p><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Clay &amp; Forming</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Wedging</b> — Kneading clay to remove air pockets and even out moisture. Think of it as clay yoga.</li><li><b>Wheel throwing</b> — Shaping clay on a spinning wheel using your hands.</li><li><b>Slip</b> — Clay mixed with water into a creamy glue. Used for joining.</li><li><b>Score and slip</b> — Rough up the surface, add slip, then press together. The potter's handshake.</li></ul></div><div class="card flat"><h2 class="card-title">Firing &amp; Finishing</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Bisque</b> — The first firing, which turns raw clay into hard, porous ceramic. No glaze yet.</li><li><b>Glaze</b> — A glassy coating applied before the final firing. Can be matte, glossy, or surprise.</li><li><b>Kiln</b> — The oven that fires clay to over 2000°F. Don't open it mid-fire unless you enjoy heartbreak.</li><li><b>Greenware</b> — Unfired clay. Fragile and thirsty.</li></ul></div><div class="callout info"><b>Patience:</b> Rushing the drying or firing stages cracks more pots than bad technique.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Pottery Glossary</h1><p class="muted">Clay, fire, and the words in between.</p><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Clay &amp; Forming</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Wedging</b> — Kneading clay to remove air pockets and even out moisture. Think of it as clay yoga.</li><li><b>Wheel throwing</b> — Shaping clay on a spinning wheel using your hands.</li><li><b>Slip</b> — Clay mixed with water into a creamy glue. Used for joining.</li><li><b>Score and slip</b> — Rough up the surface, add slip, then press together. The potter's handshake.</li></ul></div><div class="card flat"><h2 class="card-title">Firing &amp; Finishing</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Bisque</b> — The first firing, which turns raw clay into hard, porous ceramic. No glaze yet.</li><li><b>Glaze</b> — A glassy coating applied before the final firing. Can be matte, glossy, or surprise.</li><li><b>Kiln</b> — The oven that fires clay to over 2000°F. Don't open it mid-fire unless you enjoy heartbreak.</li><li><b>Greenware</b> — Unfired clay. Fragile and thirsty.</li></ul></div><div class="callout info"><b>Patience:</b> Rushing the drying or firing stages cracks more pots than bad technique.</div></div>	-
46.16.0	dashboard_glossary/Astronomy Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Astronomy Glossary</h1><p class="muted">The night sky has a lot of vocabulary. Here's the friendly version.</p><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Cosmic Distances</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Light-year</b> — The distance light travels in one year. About 6 trillion miles. Not a measure of time.</li><li><b>Astronomical Unit (AU)</b> — The average distance from Earth to the Sun. Handy for local addresses.</li><li><b>Parsec</b> — About 3.26 light-years. Astronomers use it for serious distances.</li></ul></div><div class="card flat"><h2 class="card-title">Objects</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Nebula</b> — A cloud of gas and dust. Some are star nurseries; others are stellar graveyards.</li><li><b>Galaxy</b> — A gravitationally bound system of stars, gas, dust, and dark matter. We live in the Milky Way.</li><li><b>Meteoroid</b> — A small rock in space. If it enters the atmosphere and burns, it's a <b>meteor</b>. If it lands, it's a <b>meteorite</b>.</li><li><b>Exoplanet</b> — A planet orbiting a star other than our Sun.</li></ul></div><div class="callout good"><b>Fun fact:</b> When you look at the Andromeda Galaxy, you're seeing light that left before humans existed.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Astronomy Glossary</h1><p class="muted">The night sky has a lot of vocabulary. Here's the friendly version.</p><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Cosmic Distances</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Light-year</b> — The distance light travels in one year. About 6 trillion miles. Not a measure of time.</li><li><b>Astronomical Unit (AU)</b> — The average distance from Earth to the Sun. Handy for local addresses.</li><li><b>Parsec</b> — About 3.26 light-years. Astronomers use it for serious distances.</li></ul></div><div class="card flat"><h2 class="card-title">Objects</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Nebula</b> — A cloud of gas and dust. Some are star nurseries; others are stellar graveyards.</li><li><b>Galaxy</b> — A gravitationally bound system of stars, gas, dust, and dark matter. We live in the Milky Way.</li><li><b>Meteoroid</b> — A small rock in space. If it enters the atmosphere and burns, it's a <b>meteor</b>. If it lands, it's a <b>meteorite</b>.</li><li><b>Exoplanet</b> — A planet orbiting a star other than our Sun.</li></ul></div><div class="callout good"><b>Fun fact:</b> When you look at the Andromeda Galaxy, you're seeing light that left before humans existed.</div></div>	-
46.17.0	dashboard_glossary/Weather Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Weather Glossary</h1><p class="muted">So you can sound smart while getting rained on.</p><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Air &amp; Moisture</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Dew point</b> — The temperature at which air must cool to become saturated. High dew point = muggy. Low = crisp.</li><li><b>Humidity</b> — The amount of water vapor in the air. Relative humidity is a percentage of the maximum possible at that temperature.</li><li><b>Barometric pressure</b> — The weight of the atmosphere. Falling pressure often means a storm is coming.</li></ul></div><div class="card flat"><h2 class="card-title">Storms &amp; Safety</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Watch</b> — Conditions are favorable. Be ready.</li><li><b>Warning</b> — It's happening or imminent. Take action now.</li><li><b>Front</b> — The boundary between two air masses. Cold fronts bring drama; warm fronts bring drizzle.</li><li><b>Wind chill</b> — How cold it feels when wind strips heat from your skin.</li></ul></div><div class="callout bad"><b>Remember:</b> If thunder roars, go indoors. Lightning doesn't care about your plans.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Weather Glossary</h1><p class="muted">So you can sound smart while getting rained on.</p><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Air &amp; Moisture</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Dew point</b> — The temperature at which air must cool to become saturated. High dew point = muggy. Low = crisp.</li><li><b>Humidity</b> — The amount of water vapor in the air. Relative humidity is a percentage of the maximum possible at that temperature.</li><li><b>Barometric pressure</b> — The weight of the atmosphere. Falling pressure often means a storm is coming.</li></ul></div><div class="card flat"><h2 class="card-title">Storms &amp; Safety</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Watch</b> — Conditions are favorable. Be ready.</li><li><b>Warning</b> — It's happening or imminent. Take action now.</li><li><b>Front</b> — The boundary between two air masses. Cold fronts bring drama; warm fronts bring drizzle.</li><li><b>Wind chill</b> — How cold it feels when wind strips heat from your skin.</li></ul></div><div class="callout bad"><b>Remember:</b> If thunder roars, go indoors. Lightning doesn't care about your plans.</div></div>	-
46.18.0	dashboard_glossary/Bicycle Maintenance Glossary	<div class="umi-page">  <h1 class="card-title">Bicycle Maintenance Glossary</h1>  <p class="muted">Hey <regex>user_name</regex>, here’s a quick rundown of bike terms. Bookmark it for your next tune-up.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Cadence</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>The number of pedal revolutions per minute (RPM). A comfortable cadence is usually 60–90 RPM for casual riding.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Derailleur</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>The mechanism that moves the chain between gears. Front derailleur shifts chainrings; rear derailleur shifts cassette cogs.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Chainring</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>The toothed wheel attached to the crankset. It drives the chain and comes in various sizes for different gear ratios.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Cassette</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>The cluster of gears on the rear wheel. It slides onto the freehub body and is secured by a lockring.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Bottom Bracket</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>The bearing assembly that connects the crankset to the frame. It allows the cranks to spin smoothly.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1 class="card-title">Bicycle Maintenance Glossary</h1>  <p class="muted">Hey , here’s a quick rundown of bike terms. Bookmark it for your next tune-up.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Cadence</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>The number of pedal revolutions per minute (RPM). A comfortable cadence is usually 60–90 RPM for casual riding.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Derailleur</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>The mechanism that moves the chain between gears. Front derailleur shifts chainrings; rear derailleur shifts cassette cogs.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Chainring</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>The toothed wheel attached to the crankset. It drives the chain and comes in various sizes for different gear ratios.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Cassette</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>The cluster of gears on the rear wheel. It slides onto the freehub body and is secured by a lockring.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Bottom Bracket</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>The bearing assembly that connects the crankset to the frame. It allows the cranks to spin smoothly.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
46.19.0	dashboard_glossary/Coffee Roasting Glossary	<div class="umi-page">  <h1 class="card-title">Coffee Roasting Glossary</h1>  <p class="muted">Here you go, <regex>user_name</regex>. The words you’ll hear around a roaster.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>First Crack</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>The audible popping sound when beans expand and moisture escapes. It signals the start of drinkable roast levels.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Development Time</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>The period after first crack until the roast ends. It builds sweetness and body while reducing acidity.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Charge Temperature</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>The temperature of the roaster drum before adding green beans. It influences the initial roast curve.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Rate of Rise (RoR)</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>The change in bean temperature over time. A steadily declining RoR helps avoid baked or scorched flavors.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Drop</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>The moment you dump the roasted beans into the cooling tray. Also the act of ending the roast.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1 class="card-title">Coffee Roasting Glossary</h1>  <p class="muted">Here you go, . The words you’ll hear around a roaster.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>First Crack</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>The audible popping sound when beans expand and moisture escapes. It signals the start of drinkable roast levels.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Development Time</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>The period after first crack until the roast ends. It builds sweetness and body while reducing acidity.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Charge Temperature</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>The temperature of the roaster drum before adding green beans. It influences the initial roast curve.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Rate of Rise (RoR)</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>The change in bean temperature over time. A steadily declining RoR helps avoid baked or scorched flavors.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Drop</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>The moment you dump the roasted beans into the cooling tray. Also the act of ending the roast.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
46.20.0	dashboard_glossary/Urban Gardening Glossary	<div class="umi-page">  <h1 class="card-title">Urban Gardening Glossary</h1>  <p class="muted">For your city patch, <regex>user_name</regex>. These terms keep your balcony lush.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Grow Bag</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>A fabric or plastic bag used as a lightweight, portable plant container. Great for tomatoes and peppers on patios.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Self-Watering Planter</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>A container with a built-in reservoir that wicks moisture to the soil. Reduces watering frequency.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Vertical Gardening</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Growing plants upward using trellises, walls, or stacked containers. Maximizes limited floor space.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Bolting</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>When a plant suddenly produces a flower stalk and stops leaf growth. Common in lettuce during heat.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Companion Planting</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Growing certain plants together for mutual benefit, like basil with tomatoes to deter pests.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1 class="card-title">Urban Gardening Glossary</h1>  <p class="muted">For your city patch, . These terms keep your balcony lush.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Grow Bag</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>A fabric or plastic bag used as a lightweight, portable plant container. Great for tomatoes and peppers on patios.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Self-Watering Planter</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>A container with a built-in reservoir that wicks moisture to the soil. Reduces watering frequency.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Vertical Gardening</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Growing plants upward using trellises, walls, or stacked containers. Maximizes limited floor space.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Bolting</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>When a plant suddenly produces a flower stalk and stops leaf growth. Common in lettuce during heat.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Companion Planting</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Growing certain plants together for mutual benefit, like basil with tomatoes to deter pests.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
46.21.0	dashboard_glossary/Film Photography Glossary	<div class="umi-page">  <h1 class="card-title">Film Photography Glossary</h1>  <p class="muted">Shooting on film, <regex>user_name</regex>? Here’s the lingo.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Exposure Latitude</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>The range of exposure errors a film can tolerate while still yielding a usable image. Negative film has wide latitude.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Push Processing</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Underexposing film then extending development time to compensate. Increases contrast and grain.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Stop Bath</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>An acidic solution that halts developer action after development. Usually a diluted acetic acid mix.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Contact Sheet</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>A print of all frames from a roll of film, used to preview and select images for enlargement.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Dodging and Burning</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Darkroom techniques to lighten (dodge) or darken (burn) specific areas of a print during exposure.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1 class="card-title">Film Photography Glossary</h1>  <p class="muted">Shooting on film, ? Here’s the lingo.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Exposure Latitude</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>The range of exposure errors a film can tolerate while still yielding a usable image. Negative film has wide latitude.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Push Processing</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Underexposing film then extending development time to compensate. Increases contrast and grain.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Stop Bath</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>An acidic solution that halts developer action after development. Usually a diluted acetic acid mix.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Contact Sheet</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>A print of all frames from a roll of film, used to preview and select images for enlargement.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Dodging and Burning</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Darkroom techniques to lighten (dodge) or darken (burn) specific areas of a print during exposure.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
46.22.0	dashboard_glossary/Sewing Glossary	<div class="umi-page">  <h1 class="card-title">Sewing Glossary</h1>  <p class="muted">Here’s the stitch, <regex>user_name</regex>. Terms to help you sew with confidence.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Seam Allowance</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>The space between the fabric edge and the stitching line. Standard is 5/8 inch in patterns.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Basting</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Temporary long stitches used to hold fabric in place before final sewing. Often removed later.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Grainline</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>The direction of threads in woven fabric. Patterns indicate grainline to align pieces correctly.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Selvage</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>The finished edge of a fabric roll that prevents fraying. Often marked with manufacturer info.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Backstitch</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>A few reverse stitches at the beginning and end of a seam to secure the thread and prevent unraveling.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1 class="card-title">Sewing Glossary</h1>  <p class="muted">Here’s the stitch, . Terms to help you sew with confidence.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Seam Allowance</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>The space between the fabric edge and the stitching line. Standard is 5/8 inch in patterns.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Basting</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Temporary long stitches used to hold fabric in place before final sewing. Often removed later.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Grainline</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>The direction of threads in woven fabric. Patterns indicate grainline to align pieces correctly.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Selvage</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>The finished edge of a fabric roll that prevents fraying. Often marked with manufacturer info.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Backstitch</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>A few reverse stitches at the beginning and end of a seam to secure the thread and prevent unraveling.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
46.23.0	dashboard_glossary/Digital Music Production Glossary	<div class="umi-page">  <h1 class="card-title">Digital Music Production Glossary</h1>  <p class="muted">Making beats, <regex>user_name</regex>? Here are the terms you’ll need.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>DAW</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Digital Audio Workstation: software for recording, editing, and mixing music. Examples: Ableton Live, Logic Pro.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>MIDI</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Musical Instrument Digital Interface. A protocol for sending note and control data between devices and software.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Sidechain Compression</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>A technique where one sound triggers compression on another, often used to duck bass when the kick hits.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Reverb</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>An effect that simulates the natural reflections of a space, adding depth and atmosphere to a track.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Quantization</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Aligning notes to a rhythmic grid to correct timing. Can make performances sound tighter or more mechanical.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1 class="card-title">Digital Music Production Glossary</h1>  <p class="muted">Making beats, ? Here are the terms you’ll need.</p>  <details class="acc">    <summary>DAW</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Digital Audio Workstation: software for recording, editing, and mixing music. Examples: Ableton Live, Logic Pro.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>MIDI</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Musical Instrument Digital Interface. A protocol for sending note and control data between devices and software.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Sidechain Compression</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>A technique where one sound triggers compression on another, often used to duck bass when the kick hits.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Reverb</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>An effect that simulates the natural reflections of a space, adding depth and atmosphere to a track.</p>    </div>  </details>  <details class="acc">    <summary>Quantization</summary>    <div class="body">      <p>Aligning notes to a rhythmic grid to correct timing. Can make performances sound tighter or more mechanical.</p>    </div>  </details></div>	-
46.24.0	dashboard_glossary/Camping Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Camping Glossary</h1><p class="muted">Talk like you've slept under the stars—without the pine needles in your socks.</p><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Trail Talk</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Leave No Trace</b> — Pack it in, pack it out. Leave only footprints.</li><li><b>Base weight</b> — Your pack's weight without consumables (food, water, fuel).</li><li><b>Cat hole</b> — A 6–8 inch hole for human waste, dug 200 feet from water.</li><li><b>Trail magic</b> — Unexpected kindness, like a cooler of sodas on a hot day.</li><li><b>Thru-hike</b> — Hiking a long trail end-to-end in one season.</li><li><b>Hiker trash</b> — Affectionate term for long-distance hikers.</li><li><b>Cowboy camping</b> — Sleeping under the stars without a tent.</li></ul></div><div class="callout good"><b>Good to know:</b> The best campsite is the one you leave cleaner than you found it.</div><p class="t-small dim">For <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Camping Glossary</h1><p class="muted">Talk like you've slept under the stars—without the pine needles in your socks.</p><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Trail Talk</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Leave No Trace</b> — Pack it in, pack it out. Leave only footprints.</li><li><b>Base weight</b> — Your pack's weight without consumables (food, water, fuel).</li><li><b>Cat hole</b> — A 6–8 inch hole for human waste, dug 200 feet from water.</li><li><b>Trail magic</b> — Unexpected kindness, like a cooler of sodas on a hot day.</li><li><b>Thru-hike</b> — Hiking a long trail end-to-end in one season.</li><li><b>Hiker trash</b> — Affectionate term for long-distance hikers.</li><li><b>Cowboy camping</b> — Sleeping under the stars without a tent.</li></ul></div><div class="callout good"><b>Good to know:</b> The best campsite is the one you leave cleaner than you found it.</div><p class="t-small dim">For .</p></div>	-
46.25.0	dashboard_glossary/Cocktail Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Cocktail Glossary</h1><p class="muted">From jigger to julep—your home bar cheat sheet.</p><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Bar Basics</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Jigger</b> — The double-coned measuring tool.</li><li><b>Shake</b> — For drinks with citrus, dairy, or egg; chills and aerates.</li><li><b>Stir</b> — For spirit-only drinks; keeps them clear and silky.</li><li><b>Dry</b> — Less vermouth in a martini (ironically, not about sweetness).</li><li><b>Dirty</b> — With olive brine.</li><li><b>Muddle</b> — Gently mash herbs or fruit to release flavor.</li><li><b>Rinse</b> — Coat the glass with a strong spirit, then dump it out.</li><li><b>Expression</b> — A twist of citrus peel squeezed over the drink.</li></ul></div><div class="callout info"><b>Rule of thumb:</b> If it has juice, shake it. If it's all booze, stir it.</div><p class="t-small dim">For <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Cocktail Glossary</h1><p class="muted">From jigger to julep—your home bar cheat sheet.</p><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Bar Basics</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Jigger</b> — The double-coned measuring tool.</li><li><b>Shake</b> — For drinks with citrus, dairy, or egg; chills and aerates.</li><li><b>Stir</b> — For spirit-only drinks; keeps them clear and silky.</li><li><b>Dry</b> — Less vermouth in a martini (ironically, not about sweetness).</li><li><b>Dirty</b> — With olive brine.</li><li><b>Muddle</b> — Gently mash herbs or fruit to release flavor.</li><li><b>Rinse</b> — Coat the glass with a strong spirit, then dump it out.</li><li><b>Expression</b> — A twist of citrus peel squeezed over the drink.</li></ul></div><div class="callout info"><b>Rule of thumb:</b> If it has juice, shake it. If it's all booze, stir it.</div><p class="t-small dim">For .</p></div>	-
46.26.0	dashboard_glossary/Fishing Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Fishing Glossary</h1><p class="muted">A few terms to keep you from feeling like a fish out of water.</p><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Angler's Lexicon</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Leader</b> — The clear line between your main line and the hook.</li><li><b>Trolling</b> — Dragging lures behind a moving boat.</li><li><b>Casting</b> — Flinging your line out with a rod.</li><li><b>Bobber</b> — A float that signals when a fish nibbles.</li><li><b>Lure</b> — Artificial bait designed to attract fish.</li><li><b>Catch and release</b> — Landing a fish and letting it go.</li><li><b>Creel</b> — A basket for carrying your catch (or a limit).</li><li><b>Spool</b> — The part of the reel that holds line.</li></ul></div><div class="callout warn"><b>Watch out:</b> A "limit" is the max number of fish you can keep. Check local rules.</div><p class="t-small dim">For <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Fishing Glossary</h1><p class="muted">A few terms to keep you from feeling like a fish out of water.</p><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Angler's Lexicon</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Leader</b> — The clear line between your main line and the hook.</li><li><b>Trolling</b> — Dragging lures behind a moving boat.</li><li><b>Casting</b> — Flinging your line out with a rod.</li><li><b>Bobber</b> — A float that signals when a fish nibbles.</li><li><b>Lure</b> — Artificial bait designed to attract fish.</li><li><b>Catch and release</b> — Landing a fish and letting it go.</li><li><b>Creel</b> — A basket for carrying your catch (or a limit).</li><li><b>Spool</b> — The part of the reel that holds line.</li></ul></div><div class="callout warn"><b>Watch out:</b> A "limit" is the max number of fish you can keep. Check local rules.</div><p class="t-small dim">For .</p></div>	-
46.27.0	dashboard_glossary/Motorcycle Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Motorcycle Glossary</h1><p class="muted">Ride smarter with these two-wheeled terms.</p><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Rider's Dictionary</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Countersteering</b> — Push the bar left to go right. Physics is weird.</li><li><b>Apex</b> — The innermost point of a corner.</li><li><b>Power band</b> — The RPM range where the engine makes peak power.</li><li><b>Throttle blip</b> — A quick twist to match revs when downshifting.</li><li><b>Trail braking</b> — Braking while turning into a corner.</li><li><b>ATGATT</b> — All The Gear, All The Time.</li><li><b>Saddle</b> — The seat (also called a seat).</li><li><b>Chicken strips</b> — Unused tread on the edges of a tire.</li></ul></div><div class="callout info"><b>Remember:</b> Look where you want to go. Your bike follows your eyes.</div><p class="t-small dim">For <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Motorcycle Glossary</h1><p class="muted">Ride smarter with these two-wheeled terms.</p><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Rider's Dictionary</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Countersteering</b> — Push the bar left to go right. Physics is weird.</li><li><b>Apex</b> — The innermost point of a corner.</li><li><b>Power band</b> — The RPM range where the engine makes peak power.</li><li><b>Throttle blip</b> — A quick twist to match revs when downshifting.</li><li><b>Trail braking</b> — Braking while turning into a corner.</li><li><b>ATGATT</b> — All The Gear, All The Time.</li><li><b>Saddle</b> — The seat (also called a seat).</li><li><b>Chicken strips</b> — Unused tread on the edges of a tire.</li></ul></div><div class="callout info"><b>Remember:</b> Look where you want to go. Your bike follows your eyes.</div><p class="t-small dim">For .</p></div>	-
46.28.0	dashboard_glossary/Hiking Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Hiking Glossary</h1><p class="muted">Trail talk for those who prefer their walks vertical.</p><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">On the Trail</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Switchback</b> — A zigzag path to climb steep terrain.</li><li><b>Elevation gain</b> — Total uphill, often measured in feet or meters.</li><li><b>Scramble</b> — Using hands and feet to climb easy rock.</li><li><b>Cairn</b> — A stack of rocks marking the trail.</li><li><b>Blaze</b> — A painted mark on a tree or rock to guide you.</li><li><b>Bushwhack</b> — Off-trail travel through vegetation.</li><li><b>Trailhead</b> — Where the trail begins.</li><li><b>Thru-hike</b> — Hiking an entire long-distance trail in one go.</li></ul></div><div class="callout good"><b>Pro tip:</b> A cairn is not a trail decoration—it's a sign. Don't knock it over.</div><p class="t-small dim">For <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Hiking Glossary</h1><p class="muted">Trail talk for those who prefer their walks vertical.</p><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">On the Trail</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Switchback</b> — A zigzag path to climb steep terrain.</li><li><b>Elevation gain</b> — Total uphill, often measured in feet or meters.</li><li><b>Scramble</b> — Using hands and feet to climb easy rock.</li><li><b>Cairn</b> — A stack of rocks marking the trail.</li><li><b>Blaze</b> — A painted mark on a tree or rock to guide you.</li><li><b>Bushwhack</b> — Off-trail travel through vegetation.</li><li><b>Trailhead</b> — Where the trail begins.</li><li><b>Thru-hike</b> — Hiking an entire long-distance trail in one go.</li></ul></div><div class="callout good"><b>Pro tip:</b> A cairn is not a trail decoration—it's a sign. Don't knock it over.</div><p class="t-small dim">For .</p></div>	-
46.29.0	dashboard_glossary/Chess Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Chess Glossary</h1><p class="muted">A few terms to help you follow the game, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Key Terms</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Fianchetto</b> — Developing a bishop to the long diagonal (b2 or g2), often behind a pawn chain.</li><li><b>Zugzwang</b> — A situation where any move you make worsens your position; you'd rather pass, but you can't.</li><li><b>En passant</b> — A special pawn capture: if a pawn moves two squares and lands beside an enemy pawn, the enemy can capture it as if it had moved one square.</li><li><b>Fork</b> — A single piece attacks two or more enemy pieces at once, often winning material.</li><li><b>Pin</b> — A piece is attacked and cannot move without exposing a more valuable piece behind it.</li><li><b>Skewer</b> — Like a pin, but the more valuable piece is in front and must move, exposing the one behind.</li><li><b>Gambit</b> — An opening that sacrifices material (usually a pawn) for a lead in development or attack.</li></ul></div><div class="callout info">New to chess? Start by learning these, then play a few games—patterns will click.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Chess Glossary</h1><p class="muted">A few terms to help you follow the game, .</p><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Key Terms</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Fianchetto</b> — Developing a bishop to the long diagonal (b2 or g2), often behind a pawn chain.</li><li><b>Zugzwang</b> — A situation where any move you make worsens your position; you'd rather pass, but you can't.</li><li><b>En passant</b> — A special pawn capture: if a pawn moves two squares and lands beside an enemy pawn, the enemy can capture it as if it had moved one square.</li><li><b>Fork</b> — A single piece attacks two or more enemy pieces at once, often winning material.</li><li><b>Pin</b> — A piece is attacked and cannot move without exposing a more valuable piece behind it.</li><li><b>Skewer</b> — Like a pin, but the more valuable piece is in front and must move, exposing the one behind.</li><li><b>Gambit</b> — An opening that sacrifices material (usually a pawn) for a lead in development or attack.</li></ul></div><div class="callout info">New to chess? Start by learning these, then play a few games—patterns will click.</div></div>	-
46.30.0	dashboard_glossary/Birdwatching Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Birdwatching Glossary</h1><p class="muted">For your next outing, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Key Terms</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Lifer</b> — A species you see for the first time in your life. Big deal in birding circles.</li><li><b>Twitch</b> — Chasing a rare bird that's been reported nearby, often with great urgency.</li><li><b>Patch</b> — Your local, regularly visited birding spot. Every birder has one.</li><li><b>Jizz</b> — The overall impression of a bird's size, shape, and behavior—the vibe that helps ID it at a glance.</li><li><b>Pish</b> — A soft, repetitive sound made to attract curious birds.</li><li><b>Scope</b> — Short for spotting scope; a telescope for long-distance viewing.</li><li><b>Dip</b> — To miss seeing a bird you were hoping to find. It happens.</li></ul></div><div class="callout info">Keep a list, but don't stress the numbers. The birds don't care.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Birdwatching Glossary</h1><p class="muted">For your next outing, .</p><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Key Terms</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Lifer</b> — A species you see for the first time in your life. Big deal in birding circles.</li><li><b>Twitch</b> — Chasing a rare bird that's been reported nearby, often with great urgency.</li><li><b>Patch</b> — Your local, regularly visited birding spot. Every birder has one.</li><li><b>Jizz</b> — The overall impression of a bird's size, shape, and behavior—the vibe that helps ID it at a glance.</li><li><b>Pish</b> — A soft, repetitive sound made to attract curious birds.</li><li><b>Scope</b> — Short for spotting scope; a telescope for long-distance viewing.</li><li><b>Dip</b> — To miss seeing a bird you were hoping to find. It happens.</li></ul></div><div class="callout info">Keep a list, but don't stress the numbers. The birds don't care.</div></div>	-
46.31.0	dashboard_glossary/Bread Making Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Bread Making Glossary</h1><p class="muted">A few terms for your next loaf, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Key Terms</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Autolyse</b> — Resting flour and water before adding yeast or salt, letting gluten develop on its own.</li><li><b>Bulk fermentation</b> — The first rise, where the whole dough mass ferments together before shaping.</li><li><b>Proof</b> — The final rise after shaping, just before baking.</li><li><b>Score</b> — Slashing the dough's surface to control where it expands in the oven.</li><li><b>Crumb</b> — The interior texture of the bread: open, tight, custardy, etc.</li><li><b>Starter</b> — A living culture of flour and water with wild yeast and bacteria, used for sourdough.</li><li><b>Hydration</b> — The ratio of water to flour in a dough, usually expressed as a percentage.</li></ul></div><div class="callout info">Start with a simple recipe and keep notes. Bread rewards patience.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Bread Making Glossary</h1><p class="muted">A few terms for your next loaf, .</p><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Key Terms</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Autolyse</b> — Resting flour and water before adding yeast or salt, letting gluten develop on its own.</li><li><b>Bulk fermentation</b> — The first rise, where the whole dough mass ferments together before shaping.</li><li><b>Proof</b> — The final rise after shaping, just before baking.</li><li><b>Score</b> — Slashing the dough's surface to control where it expands in the oven.</li><li><b>Crumb</b> — The interior texture of the bread: open, tight, custardy, etc.</li><li><b>Starter</b> — A living culture of flour and water with wild yeast and bacteria, used for sourdough.</li><li><b>Hydration</b> — The ratio of water to flour in a dough, usually expressed as a percentage.</li></ul></div><div class="callout info">Start with a simple recipe and keep notes. Bread rewards patience.</div></div>	-
46.32.0	dashboard_glossary/Fermentation Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Fermentation Glossary</h1><p class="muted">For your kitchen experiments, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Key Terms</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Lacto-fermentation</b> — Using lactic acid bacteria (often with salt) to preserve vegetables, like sauerkraut.</li><li><b>Starter</b> — A culture of microbes that kicks off fermentation, from sourdough to ginger bug.</li><li><b>Brine</b> — A saltwater solution that creates an environment where good bacteria thrive and bad ones don't.</li><li><b>Kahm yeast</b> — A harmless white film on the surface of ferments; skim it off if you like.</li><li><b>Air lock</b> — A one-way valve that lets gas escape without letting oxygen in.</li><li><b>Pellicle</b> — The cellulose layer formed by kombucha and vinegar cultures; also called SCOBY.</li><li><b>Secondary fermentation</b> — A second round, often for flavoring or carbonation.</li></ul></div><div class="callout info">Sanitize your gear, keep things submerged, and trust your nose.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Fermentation Glossary</h1><p class="muted">For your kitchen experiments, .</p><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Key Terms</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Lacto-fermentation</b> — Using lactic acid bacteria (often with salt) to preserve vegetables, like sauerkraut.</li><li><b>Starter</b> — A culture of microbes that kicks off fermentation, from sourdough to ginger bug.</li><li><b>Brine</b> — A saltwater solution that creates an environment where good bacteria thrive and bad ones don't.</li><li><b>Kahm yeast</b> — A harmless white film on the surface of ferments; skim it off if you like.</li><li><b>Air lock</b> — A one-way valve that lets gas escape without letting oxygen in.</li><li><b>Pellicle</b> — The cellulose layer formed by kombucha and vinegar cultures; also called SCOBY.</li><li><b>Secondary fermentation</b> — A second round, often for flavoring or carbonation.</li></ul></div><div class="callout info">Sanitize your gear, keep things submerged, and trust your nose.</div></div>	-
46.33.0	dashboard_glossary/Cheese Making Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Cheese Making Glossary</h1><p class="muted">For your next batch, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Key Terms</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Rennet</b> — An enzyme that coagulates milk, separating curds from whey.</li><li><b>Curd</b> — The solid part of coagulated milk; the building block of cheese.</li><li><b>Whey</b> — The liquid left after curds form; useful in baking and soups.</li><li><b>Starter culture</b> — Bacteria added to milk to acidify it and develop flavor.</li><li><b>Pressing</b> — Compressing curds to expel whey and shape the cheese.</li><li><b>Rind</b> — The outer surface, which can be natural, washed, bloomy, or waxed.</li><li><b>Affinage</b> — The aging process, where cheese develops texture and flavor.</li></ul></div><div class="callout info">Start with a simple fresh cheese, then work up to aged ones.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Cheese Making Glossary</h1><p class="muted">For your next batch, .</p><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Key Terms</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Rennet</b> — An enzyme that coagulates milk, separating curds from whey.</li><li><b>Curd</b> — The solid part of coagulated milk; the building block of cheese.</li><li><b>Whey</b> — The liquid left after curds form; useful in baking and soups.</li><li><b>Starter culture</b> — Bacteria added to milk to acidify it and develop flavor.</li><li><b>Pressing</b> — Compressing curds to expel whey and shape the cheese.</li><li><b>Rind</b> — The outer surface, which can be natural, washed, bloomy, or waxed.</li><li><b>Affinage</b> — The aging process, where cheese develops texture and flavor.</li></ul></div><div class="callout info">Start with a simple fresh cheese, then work up to aged ones.</div></div>	-
46.34.0	dashboard_glossary/Bookbinding Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Bookbinding Glossary</h1><p class="muted">For your next project, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Key Terms</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Signature</b> — A group of folded sheets ready to be sewn together.</li><li><b>Grain</b> — The direction of paper fibers; folding with the grain gives a cleaner edge.</li><li><b>Coptic binding</b> — A stitch that links signatures and boards without glue, allowing the book to lie flat.</li><li><b>Endpaper</b> — The paper glued to the inside cover and first page, hiding the binding.</li><li><b>Spine</b> — The back edge of the book where pages are bound.</li><li><b>Headband</b> — A decorative band at the top and bottom of the spine.</li><li><b>Case binding</b> — A hardcover where the cover is made separately and then glued to the text block.</li></ul></div><div class="callout info">Start with a simple pamphlet stitch, then try Coptic.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Bookbinding Glossary</h1><p class="muted">For your next project, .</p><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Key Terms</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Signature</b> — A group of folded sheets ready to be sewn together.</li><li><b>Grain</b> — The direction of paper fibers; folding with the grain gives a cleaner edge.</li><li><b>Coptic binding</b> — A stitch that links signatures and boards without glue, allowing the book to lie flat.</li><li><b>Endpaper</b> — The paper glued to the inside cover and first page, hiding the binding.</li><li><b>Spine</b> — The back edge of the book where pages are bound.</li><li><b>Headband</b> — A decorative band at the top and bottom of the spine.</li><li><b>Case binding</b> — A hardcover where the cover is made separately and then glued to the text block.</li></ul></div><div class="callout info">Start with a simple pamphlet stitch, then try Coptic.</div></div>	-
46.35.0	dashboard_glossary/Falconry Glossary	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Falconry Glossary</h1>  <p class="muted">The words flown around the mews. <regex>user_name</regex></p>  <ul class="list">    <li><b>Jess</b> — A leather strap fastened to the bird's leg for control.</li>    <li><b>Seel</b> — To sew a hawk's eyelids shut for taming.</li>    <li><b>Weathering</b> — Letting a bird sit outside to bathe and sun.</li>    <li><b>Mews</b> — The housing where falcons are kept.</li>    <li><b>Creance</b> — A long line used in early training flights.</li>    <li><b>Cast</b> — Two hawks flown together, or to hold a bird.</li>    <li><b>Hood</b> — A leather cap covering the eyes to calm the bird.</li>  </ul>  <p class="dim t-small">Tip: Never seel a bird you want to fly free.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Falconry Glossary</h1>  <p class="muted">The words flown around the mews. </p>  <ul class="list">    <li><b>Jess</b> — A leather strap fastened to the bird's leg for control.</li>    <li><b>Seel</b> — To sew a hawk's eyelids shut for taming.</li>    <li><b>Weathering</b> — Letting a bird sit outside to bathe and sun.</li>    <li><b>Mews</b> — The housing where falcons are kept.</li>    <li><b>Creance</b> — A long line used in early training flights.</li>    <li><b>Cast</b> — Two hawks flown together, or to hold a bird.</li>    <li><b>Hood</b> — A leather cap covering the eyes to calm the bird.</li>  </ul>  <p class="dim t-small">Tip: Never seel a bird you want to fly free.</p></div>	-
46.36.0	dashboard_glossary/Cheese Glossary	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Cheese Glossary</h1>  <p class="muted">From curd to cave. <regex>user_name</regex></p>  <dl>    <dt>Rennet</dt><dd>Enzyme that coagulates milk into curds.</dd>    <dt>Culture</dt><dd>Bacteria added to acidify and flavor milk.</dd>    <dt>Curd</dt><dd>The solid part of coagulated milk.</dd>    <dt>Whey</dt><dd>The liquid left after curds form.</dd>    <dt>Cheddaring</dt><dd>Stacking and turning curd slabs to expel whey.</dd>    <dt>Affinage</dt><dd>The art of aging cheese in a controlled cave.</dd>    <dt>Rind</dt><dd>The outer layer, natural or washed.</dd>  </dl>  <p class="dim t-small">Remember: whey too many terms? Start with rennet and culture.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Cheese Glossary</h1>  <p class="muted">From curd to cave. </p>  <dl>    <dt>Rennet</dt><dd>Enzyme that coagulates milk into curds.</dd>    <dt>Culture</dt><dd>Bacteria added to acidify and flavor milk.</dd>    <dt>Curd</dt><dd>The solid part of coagulated milk.</dd>    <dt>Whey</dt><dd>The liquid left after curds form.</dd>    <dt>Cheddaring</dt><dd>Stacking and turning curd slabs to expel whey.</dd>    <dt>Affinage</dt><dd>The art of aging cheese in a controlled cave.</dd>    <dt>Rind</dt><dd>The outer layer, natural or washed.</dd>  </dl>  <p class="dim t-small">Remember: whey too many terms? Start with rennet and culture.</p></div>	-
46.37.0	dashboard_glossary/Guitar Setup Glossary	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Guitar Setup Glossary</h1>  <p class="muted">Make it play like butter. <regex>user_name</regex></p>  <ul class="list">    <li><b>Action</b> — The distance between strings and frets.</li>    <li><b>Intonation</b> — How in-tune the guitar stays up the neck.</li>    <li><b>Truss Rod</b> — Metal rod that adjusts neck relief.</li>    <li><b>Relief</b> — Slight forward bow of the neck.</li>    <li><b>Fret Buzz</b> — String rattling against a fret.</li>    <li><b>Nut</b> — Slotted piece at the top guiding strings.</li>    <li><b>Saddle</b> — Bridge piece that sets string height.</li>  </ul>  <p class="dim t-small">Set action low but not so low you buzz like a bee.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Guitar Setup Glossary</h1>  <p class="muted">Make it play like butter. </p>  <ul class="list">    <li><b>Action</b> — The distance between strings and frets.</li>    <li><b>Intonation</b> — How in-tune the guitar stays up the neck.</li>    <li><b>Truss Rod</b> — Metal rod that adjusts neck relief.</li>    <li><b>Relief</b> — Slight forward bow of the neck.</li>    <li><b>Fret Buzz</b> — String rattling against a fret.</li>    <li><b>Nut</b> — Slotted piece at the top guiding strings.</li>    <li><b>Saddle</b> — Bridge piece that sets string height.</li>  </ul>  <p class="dim t-small">Set action low but not so low you buzz like a bee.</p></div>	-
46.38.0	dashboard_glossary/Chess Clock Glossary	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Chess Clock Glossary</h1>  <p class="muted">Tick tock, know the terms. <regex>user_name</regex></p>  <ul class="list">    <li><b>Increment</b> — Seconds added after each move.</li>    <li><b>Delay</b> — Free time before the clock counts down.</li>    <li><b>Flag Fall</b> — Running out of time and losing.</li>    <li><b>Sudden Death</b> — A final period with no increment.</li>    <li><b>Time Control</b> — The format: moves plus minutes.</li>    <li><b>Bronstein Delay</b> — Delay that returns unused time.</li>    <li><b>Fischer Increment</b> — Increment named after Bobby Fischer.</li>  </ul>  <p class="dim t-small">Increment is your friend; delay is your cushion.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Chess Clock Glossary</h1>  <p class="muted">Tick tock, know the terms. </p>  <ul class="list">    <li><b>Increment</b> — Seconds added after each move.</li>    <li><b>Delay</b> — Free time before the clock counts down.</li>    <li><b>Flag Fall</b> — Running out of time and losing.</li>    <li><b>Sudden Death</b> — A final period with no increment.</li>    <li><b>Time Control</b> — The format: moves plus minutes.</li>    <li><b>Bronstein Delay</b> — Delay that returns unused time.</li>    <li><b>Fischer Increment</b> — Increment named after Bobby Fischer.</li>  </ul>  <p class="dim t-small">Increment is your friend; delay is your cushion.</p></div>	-
46.39.0	dashboard_glossary/Aquarium Glossary	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Aquarium Glossary</h1>  <p class="muted">Keep your fish happy. <regex>user_name</regex></p>  <ul class="list">    <li><b>Cycling</b> — Building beneficial bacteria to process waste.</li>    <li><b>Ammonia</b> — Toxic waste from fish and food.</li>    <li><b>Nitrite</b> — Toxic intermediate in the nitrogen cycle.</li>    <li><b>Nitrate</b> — Less toxic end product, removed by water changes.</li>    <li><b>Sump</b> — Additional water volume and filtration below the tank.</li>    <li><b>Refugium</b> — A safe zone for plants or pods to grow.</li>    <li><b>Water Change</b> — Replacing part of the water to dilute waste.</li>  </ul>  <p class="dim t-small">Cycle first, add fish later. Patience is a fishkeeper's virtue.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Aquarium Glossary</h1>  <p class="muted">Keep your fish happy. </p>  <ul class="list">    <li><b>Cycling</b> — Building beneficial bacteria to process waste.</li>    <li><b>Ammonia</b> — Toxic waste from fish and food.</li>    <li><b>Nitrite</b> — Toxic intermediate in the nitrogen cycle.</li>    <li><b>Nitrate</b> — Less toxic end product, removed by water changes.</li>    <li><b>Sump</b> — Additional water volume and filtration below the tank.</li>    <li><b>Refugium</b> — A safe zone for plants or pods to grow.</li>    <li><b>Water Change</b> — Replacing part of the water to dilute waste.</li>  </ul>  <p class="dim t-small">Cycle first, add fish later. Patience is a fishkeeper's virtue.</p></div>	-
46.40.0	dashboard_glossary/Caving Glossary	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Caving Glossary</h1>  <p class="muted">Down under, safely. <regex>user_name</regex></p>  <ul class="list">    <li><b>Rappel</b> — Descending a rope with a friction device.</li>    <li><b>Belay</b> — Backing up a climber or rappeller.</li>    <li><b>Squeeze</b> — A tight passage you must wiggle through.</li>    <li><b>SRT</b> — Single Rope Technique for vertical caving.</li>    <li><b>Pitch</b> — A vertical drop in a cave.</li>    <li><b>Traverse</b> — Moving sideways along a cave wall.</li>    <li><b>Carbide</b> — Old-school lamp fuel, now mostly LED.</li>  </ul>  <p class="dim t-small">Never cave alone. Take three sources of light.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Caving Glossary</h1>  <p class="muted">Down under, safely. </p>  <ul class="list">    <li><b>Rappel</b> — Descending a rope with a friction device.</li>    <li><b>Belay</b> — Backing up a climber or rappeller.</li>    <li><b>Squeeze</b> — A tight passage you must wiggle through.</li>    <li><b>SRT</b> — Single Rope Technique for vertical caving.</li>    <li><b>Pitch</b> — A vertical drop in a cave.</li>    <li><b>Traverse</b> — Moving sideways along a cave wall.</li>    <li><b>Carbide</b> — Old-school lamp fuel, now mostly LED.</li>  </ul>  <p class="dim t-small">Never cave alone. Take three sources of light.</p></div>	-
46.41.0	dashboard_glossary/Baking Glossary	<div class="umi-page">  <h1 class="card-title">Baking Glossary</h1>  <p class="t-small muted">A handful of words that make recipes less mysterious, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h2 class="card-title">Fold</h2>      <p class="muted">Gently combine a light mixture into a heavier one. Use a spatula, go slow, keep the air.</p>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h2 class="card-title">Cream</h2>      <p class="muted">Beat butter and sugar until pale and fluffy. This traps air for lift.</p>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h2 class="card-title">Proof</h2>      <p class="muted">Letting dough rise before baking. Also, testing yeast in warm water to see if it's alive.</p>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h2 class="card-title">Score</h2>      <p class="muted">Slashing the top of bread before it bakes. Helps it expand and look fancy.</p>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h2 class="card-title">Blind bake</h2>      <p class="muted">Baking a crust without filling, usually with weights or beans to keep it flat.</p>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h2 class="card-title">Crumb</h2>      <p class="muted">The inside texture of bread. Open crumb means big airy holes; tight crumb is dense.</p>    </div>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1 class="card-title">Baking Glossary</h1>  <p class="t-small muted">A handful of words that make recipes less mysterious, .</p>  <div class="grid two">    <div class="card">      <h2 class="card-title">Fold</h2>      <p class="muted">Gently combine a light mixture into a heavier one. Use a spatula, go slow, keep the air.</p>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h2 class="card-title">Cream</h2>      <p class="muted">Beat butter and sugar until pale and fluffy. This traps air for lift.</p>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h2 class="card-title">Proof</h2>      <p class="muted">Letting dough rise before baking. Also, testing yeast in warm water to see if it's alive.</p>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h2 class="card-title">Score</h2>      <p class="muted">Slashing the top of bread before it bakes. Helps it expand and look fancy.</p>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h2 class="card-title">Blind bake</h2>      <p class="muted">Baking a crust without filling, usually with weights or beans to keep it flat.</p>    </div>    <div class="card">      <h2 class="card-title">Crumb</h2>      <p class="muted">The inside texture of bread. Open crumb means big airy holes; tight crumb is dense.</p>    </div>  </div></div>	-
46.42.0	dashboard_glossary/Knot Tying Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Knot Tying Glossary</h1><p class="muted">The vocabulary of rope, hitches, and the occasional swearing.</p><div class="grid two">  <div class="card">    <div class="card-title">Rope Anatomy</div>    <ul class="list">      <li><b>Standing end</b> — the long, fixed part of the rope. It does the heavy lifting.</li>      <li><b>Working end</b> — the bit you tie with. Also called the live end.</li>      <li><b>Bight</b> — a U-shaped fold. Not a loop; a loop crosses itself.</li>      <li><b>Bitter end</b> — the very tip of the rope, historically the last bit to let go.</li>    </ul>  </div>  <div class="card">    <div class="card-title">Knot Types</div>    <ul class="list">      <li><b>Hitch</b> — ties rope to an object. Two half hitches is a classic.</li>      <li><b>Bend</b> — joins two ropes together. Sheet bend, carrick bend.</li>      <li><b>Loop</b> — a knot that makes a fixed circle in the rope.</li>      <li><b>Stopper</b> — keeps the rope from slipping through a hole. Figure-eight is the go-to.</li>    </ul>  </div></div><details class="acc">  <summary>What's the difference between a knot and a hitch?</summary>  <div class="body">A knot is self-contained; a hitch relies on the object it's tied around to hold its shape.  </div></details><div class="callout warn">  <b>Watch out:</b> A knot weakens rope—sometimes by half. Use the right one for the load.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Knot Tying Glossary</h1><p class="muted">The vocabulary of rope, hitches, and the occasional swearing.</p><div class="grid two">  <div class="card">    <div class="card-title">Rope Anatomy</div>    <ul class="list">      <li><b>Standing end</b> — the long, fixed part of the rope. It does the heavy lifting.</li>      <li><b>Working end</b> — the bit you tie with. Also called the live end.</li>      <li><b>Bight</b> — a U-shaped fold. Not a loop; a loop crosses itself.</li>      <li><b>Bitter end</b> — the very tip of the rope, historically the last bit to let go.</li>    </ul>  </div>  <div class="card">    <div class="card-title">Knot Types</div>    <ul class="list">      <li><b>Hitch</b> — ties rope to an object. Two half hitches is a classic.</li>      <li><b>Bend</b> — joins two ropes together. Sheet bend, carrick bend.</li>      <li><b>Loop</b> — a knot that makes a fixed circle in the rope.</li>      <li><b>Stopper</b> — keeps the rope from slipping through a hole. Figure-eight is the go-to.</li>    </ul>  </div></div><details class="acc">  <summary>What's the difference between a knot and a hitch?</summary>  <div class="body">A knot is self-contained; a hitch relies on the object it's tied around to hold its shape.  </div></details><div class="callout warn">  <b>Watch out:</b> A knot weakens rope—sometimes by half. Use the right one for the load.</div></div>	-
46.43.0	dashboard_glossary/Coffee Cupping Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Coffee Cupping Glossary</h1><p class="muted">How to sound like you know what you're tasting.</p><div class="grid two">  <div class="card">    <div class="card-title">Taste Descriptors</div>    <ul class="list">      <li><b>Acidity</b> — brightness, not sourness. Think green apple, not lemon juice.</li>      <li><b>Body</b> — the weight on your tongue. Tea-like to syrupy.</li>      <li><b>Sweetness</b> — perceived sugar. Caramel, honey, fruit.</li>      <li><b>Clean cup</b> — no off-flavors. Every note is intentional.</li>      <li><b>Balance</b> — no single attribute shouting over the others.</li>    </ul>  </div>  <div class="card">    <div class="card-title">Process & Defects</div>    <ul class="list">      <li><b>Cupping</b> — the standardized tasting ritual: slurp, spit, score.</li>      <li><b>Fermentation</b> — the coffee cherry's sugars breaking down. Done right, it's fruity; wrong, it's vinegar.</li>      <li><b>Quaker</b> — an unripe bean that tastes like paper. Not a compliment.</li>      <li><b>Rust</b> — a defect, often from poor storage. Tastes like metal.</li>    </ul>  </div></div><details class="acc">  <summary>Why do cuppers slurp so loud?</summary>  <div class="body">It sprays coffee across the palate to hit all taste buds at once. Not rude—efficient.  </div></details><div class="callout good">  <b>Good sign:</b> A clean cup with balanced acidity and a long finish usually scores above 85.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Coffee Cupping Glossary</h1><p class="muted">How to sound like you know what you're tasting.</p><div class="grid two">  <div class="card">    <div class="card-title">Taste Descriptors</div>    <ul class="list">      <li><b>Acidity</b> — brightness, not sourness. Think green apple, not lemon juice.</li>      <li><b>Body</b> — the weight on your tongue. Tea-like to syrupy.</li>      <li><b>Sweetness</b> — perceived sugar. Caramel, honey, fruit.</li>      <li><b>Clean cup</b> — no off-flavors. Every note is intentional.</li>      <li><b>Balance</b> — no single attribute shouting over the others.</li>    </ul>  </div>  <div class="card">    <div class="card-title">Process & Defects</div>    <ul class="list">      <li><b>Cupping</b> — the standardized tasting ritual: slurp, spit, score.</li>      <li><b>Fermentation</b> — the coffee cherry's sugars breaking down. Done right, it's fruity; wrong, it's vinegar.</li>      <li><b>Quaker</b> — an unripe bean that tastes like paper. Not a compliment.</li>      <li><b>Rust</b> — a defect, often from poor storage. Tastes like metal.</li>    </ul>  </div></div><details class="acc">  <summary>Why do cuppers slurp so loud?</summary>  <div class="body">It sprays coffee across the palate to hit all taste buds at once. Not rude—efficient.  </div></details><div class="callout good">  <b>Good sign:</b> A clean cup with balanced acidity and a long finish usually scores above 85.</div></div>	-
46.44.0	dashboard_glossary/Birding Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Birding Glossary</h1><p class="muted">A field guide to the words birders actually use.</p><div class="grid two">  <div class="card">    <div class="card-title">Birder Lingo</div>    <ul class="list">      <li><b>Lifer</b> — a species you've seen for the first time. Big deal.</li>      <li><b>Twitching</b> — chasing a rare bird reported nearby. Slightly obsessive.</li>      <li><b>Dipping</b> — missing the bird you went to see. Painful.</li>      <li><b>Jizz</b> — the overall impression of a bird's shape and movement. You know it when you see it.</li>      <li><b>Mega</b> — an ultra-rare bird that draws a crowd.</li>    </ul>  </div>  <div class="card">    <div class="card-title">Field Marks</div>    <ul class="list">      <li><b>Eyering</b> — the ring of feathers around the eye. Often a key ID feature.</li>      <li><b>Supercilium</b> — the eyebrow stripe. Say it with confidence.</li>      <li><b>Malar</b> — the stripe on the cheek, below the eye.</li>      <li><b>Primary projection</b> — how far the wingtips extend past the tertials. Subtle but useful.</li>    </ul>  </div></div><details class="acc">  <summary>What's the difference between a 'birder' and a 'twitcher'?</summary>  <div class="body">A birder watches birds regularly. A twitcher will drive hours for a rare one. Both are fine.  </div></details><div class="callout info">  <b>Etiquette:</b> Don't flush the bird for a photo. The view is worth more than the shot.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Birding Glossary</h1><p class="muted">A field guide to the words birders actually use.</p><div class="grid two">  <div class="card">    <div class="card-title">Birder Lingo</div>    <ul class="list">      <li><b>Lifer</b> — a species you've seen for the first time. Big deal.</li>      <li><b>Twitching</b> — chasing a rare bird reported nearby. Slightly obsessive.</li>      <li><b>Dipping</b> — missing the bird you went to see. Painful.</li>      <li><b>Jizz</b> — the overall impression of a bird's shape and movement. You know it when you see it.</li>      <li><b>Mega</b> — an ultra-rare bird that draws a crowd.</li>    </ul>  </div>  <div class="card">    <div class="card-title">Field Marks</div>    <ul class="list">      <li><b>Eyering</b> — the ring of feathers around the eye. Often a key ID feature.</li>      <li><b>Supercilium</b> — the eyebrow stripe. Say it with confidence.</li>      <li><b>Malar</b> — the stripe on the cheek, below the eye.</li>      <li><b>Primary projection</b> — how far the wingtips extend past the tertials. Subtle but useful.</li>    </ul>  </div></div><details class="acc">  <summary>What's the difference between a 'birder' and a 'twitcher'?</summary>  <div class="body">A birder watches birds regularly. A twitcher will drive hours for a rare one. Both are fine.  </div></details><div class="callout info">  <b>Etiquette:</b> Don't flush the bird for a photo. The view is worth more than the shot.</div></div>	-
46.45.0	dashboard_glossary/Woodworking Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Woodworking Glossary</h1><p class="muted">The words on the shop wall, minus the sawdust.</p><div class="grid two">  <div class="card">    <div class="card-title">Wood Anatomy</div>    <ul class="list">      <li><b>Grain</b> — the direction of the wood fibers. Cut with it, not against.</li>      <li><b>Figure</b> — the visual pattern, like curl or burl. Pretty, sometimes tricky.</li>      <li><b>Racking</b> — a frame going out of square when pushed sideways. The enemy of shelves.</li>      <li><b>Cupping</b> — a board warping across its width. Blame moisture.</li>    </ul>  </div>  <div class="card">    <div class="card-title">Joinery & Cuts</div>    <ul class="list">      <li><b>Rabbet</b> — an L-shaped cut along the edge. Good for cabinet backs.</li>      <li><b>Dado</b> — a groove across the grain. Shelves sit in these.</li>      <li><b>Rip cut</b> — cutting along the grain. Long and straight.</li>      <li><b>Crosscut</b> — cutting across the grain. Shorter, more tearout.</li>      <li><b>Mortise & tenon</b> — the classic strong joint. A hole and a peg, basically.</li>    </ul>  </div></div><details class="acc">  <summary>What's the difference between a rabbet and a dado?</summary>  <div class="body">A rabbet runs along the edge; a dado runs across the face. Both are grooves, just different addresses.  </div></details><div class="callout warn">  <b>Safety note:</b> Never cut a board that's cupped or twisted without jointing it first. It can bind and kick back.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Woodworking Glossary</h1><p class="muted">The words on the shop wall, minus the sawdust.</p><div class="grid two">  <div class="card">    <div class="card-title">Wood Anatomy</div>    <ul class="list">      <li><b>Grain</b> — the direction of the wood fibers. Cut with it, not against.</li>      <li><b>Figure</b> — the visual pattern, like curl or burl. Pretty, sometimes tricky.</li>      <li><b>Racking</b> — a frame going out of square when pushed sideways. The enemy of shelves.</li>      <li><b>Cupping</b> — a board warping across its width. Blame moisture.</li>    </ul>  </div>  <div class="card">    <div class="card-title">Joinery & Cuts</div>    <ul class="list">      <li><b>Rabbet</b> — an L-shaped cut along the edge. Good for cabinet backs.</li>      <li><b>Dado</b> — a groove across the grain. Shelves sit in these.</li>      <li><b>Rip cut</b> — cutting along the grain. Long and straight.</li>      <li><b>Crosscut</b> — cutting across the grain. Shorter, more tearout.</li>      <li><b>Mortise & tenon</b> — the classic strong joint. A hole and a peg, basically.</li>    </ul>  </div></div><details class="acc">  <summary>What's the difference between a rabbet and a dado?</summary>  <div class="body">A rabbet runs along the edge; a dado runs across the face. Both are grooves, just different addresses.  </div></details><div class="callout warn">  <b>Safety note:</b> Never cut a board that's cupped or twisted without jointing it first. It can bind and kick back.</div></div>	-
46.46.0	dashboard_glossary/Wild Mushroom Foraging Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Wild Mushroom Foraging Glossary</h1><p class="muted">A quick reference for the woods, <regex>user_name</regex>. Learn the language before you learn the land.</p><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Terms</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Flush</b> — A sudden burst of fruiting bodies, often after rain. "The chanterelles are flushing."</li><li><b>Mycelium</b> — The underground network of fungal threads; the mushroom is just the fruit.</li><li><b>Spore print</b> — A deposit of spores left on paper or glass, used to identify color and species.</li><li><b>Bolete</b> — A fleshy mushroom with pores under the cap instead of gills.</li><li><b>Mycorrhizal</b> — A fungus that lives in symbiosis with tree roots, like chanterelles and oaks.</li><li><b>Saprobe</b> — A fungus that feeds on dead organic matter, like oyster mushrooms on logs.</li><li><b>Fairy ring</b> — A circle of mushrooms caused by mycelium growing outward from a center.</li><li><b>Puffball</b> — A round mushroom that releases spores in a cloud when mature.</li></ul></div><div class="callout info"><p>Always verify with a local expert, <regex>user_name</regex>. Some mushrooms are lookalikes.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Wild Mushroom Foraging Glossary</h1><p class="muted">A quick reference for the woods, . Learn the language before you learn the land.</p><div class="card"><h2 class="card-title">Terms</h2><ul class="list"><li><b>Flush</b> — A sudden burst of fruiting bodies, often after rain. "The chanterelles are flushing."</li><li><b>Mycelium</b> — The underground network of fungal threads; the mushroom is just the fruit.</li><li><b>Spore print</b> — A deposit of spores left on paper or glass, used to identify color and species.</li><li><b>Bolete</b> — A fleshy mushroom with pores under the cap instead of gills.</li><li><b>Mycorrhizal</b> — A fungus that lives in symbiosis with tree roots, like chanterelles and oaks.</li><li><b>Saprobe</b> — A fungus that feeds on dead organic matter, like oyster mushrooms on logs.</li><li><b>Fairy ring</b> — A circle of mushrooms caused by mycelium growing outward from a center.</li><li><b>Puffball</b> — A round mushroom that releases spores in a cloud when mature.</li></ul></div><div class="callout info"><p>Always verify with a local expert, . Some mushrooms are lookalikes.</p></div></div>	-
46.47.0	dashboard_glossary/Sleep Hygiene Glossary	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Sleep Hygiene Glossary</h1>  <p class="muted">Hey <regex>user_name</regex>, here's a quick rundown of sleep terms you might see. No quizzes, promise.</p>  <ul class="list">    <li><b>Circadian rhythm</b> — your body's 24-hour internal clock that regulates sleep-wake cycles.</li>    <li><b>Sleep debt</b> — the cumulative effect of not getting enough sleep over time.</li>    <li><b>REM sleep</b> — rapid eye movement sleep, when dreaming is most vivid.</li>    <li><b>Sleep onset latency</b> — how long it takes to fall asleep after going to bed.</li>    <li><b>Sleep efficiency</b> — the ratio of time asleep to time in bed.</li>    <li><b>Hygiene</b> — in this context, habits and environment that promote good sleep.</li>  </ul>  <div class="callout info">    <p>Small changes add up. Pick one term to improve tonight.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Sleep Hygiene Glossary</h1>  <p class="muted">Hey , here's a quick rundown of sleep terms you might see. No quizzes, promise.</p>  <ul class="list">    <li><b>Circadian rhythm</b> — your body's 24-hour internal clock that regulates sleep-wake cycles.</li>    <li><b>Sleep debt</b> — the cumulative effect of not getting enough sleep over time.</li>    <li><b>REM sleep</b> — rapid eye movement sleep, when dreaming is most vivid.</li>    <li><b>Sleep onset latency</b> — how long it takes to fall asleep after going to bed.</li>    <li><b>Sleep efficiency</b> — the ratio of time asleep to time in bed.</li>    <li><b>Hygiene</b> — in this context, habits and environment that promote good sleep.</li>  </ul>  <div class="callout info">    <p>Small changes add up. Pick one term to improve tonight.</p>  </div></div>	-
46.48.0	dashboard_glossary/Tea Glossary	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Tea Glossary</h1>  <p class="muted"><regex>user_name</regex>, here's the tea on tea terms. Steep yourself in these.</p>  <ul class="list">    <li><b>Camellia sinensis</b> — the plant all true teas come from.</li>    <li><b>Oxidation</b> — the process that turns green tea leaves into black tea.</li>    <li><b>Liquor</b> — the brewed tea liquid, not the alcohol kind.</li>    <li><b>Steeping</b> — soaking tea leaves in hot water to extract flavor.</li>    <li><b>Terroir</b> — how the growing region affects tea's taste.</li>    <li><b>Gaiwan</b> — a traditional Chinese lidded bowl for brewing tea.</li>  </ul>  <div class="callout good">    <p>Now you can sound fancy at your next tea party.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Tea Glossary</h1>  <p class="muted">, here's the tea on tea terms. Steep yourself in these.</p>  <ul class="list">    <li><b>Camellia sinensis</b> — the plant all true teas come from.</li>    <li><b>Oxidation</b> — the process that turns green tea leaves into black tea.</li>    <li><b>Liquor</b> — the brewed tea liquid, not the alcohol kind.</li>    <li><b>Steeping</b> — soaking tea leaves in hot water to extract flavor.</li>    <li><b>Terroir</b> — how the growing region affects tea's taste.</li>    <li><b>Gaiwan</b> — a traditional Chinese lidded bowl for brewing tea.</li>  </ul>  <div class="callout good">    <p>Now you can sound fancy at your next tea party.</p>  </div></div>	-
46.49.0	dashboard_glossary/Digital Privacy Glossary	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Digital Privacy Glossary</h1>  <p class="muted"><regex>user_name</regex>, here's a plain-language guide to digital privacy terms. No shadows here.</p>  <ul class="list">    <li><b>Encryption</b> — scrambling data so only authorized parties can read it.</li>    <li><b>Cookie</b> — a small file websites store on your device to remember you.</li>    <li><b>Incognito mode</b> — a browser setting that doesn't save your history locally.</li>    <li><b>VPN</b> — virtual private network, masks your IP address and encrypts traffic.</li>    <li><b>Two-factor authentication</b> — a second proof of identity beyond a password.</li>    <li><b>Data broker</b> — a company that collects and sells personal data.</li>  </ul>  <div class="callout warn">    <p>Knowledge is power. Use it wisely.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Digital Privacy Glossary</h1>  <p class="muted">, here's a plain-language guide to digital privacy terms. No shadows here.</p>  <ul class="list">    <li><b>Encryption</b> — scrambling data so only authorized parties can read it.</li>    <li><b>Cookie</b> — a small file websites store on your device to remember you.</li>    <li><b>Incognito mode</b> — a browser setting that doesn't save your history locally.</li>    <li><b>VPN</b> — virtual private network, masks your IP address and encrypts traffic.</li>    <li><b>Two-factor authentication</b> — a second proof of identity beyond a password.</li>    <li><b>Data broker</b> — a company that collects and sells personal data.</li>  </ul>  <div class="callout warn">    <p>Knowledge is power. Use it wisely.</p>  </div></div>	-
46.50.0	dashboard_glossary/Cooking Techniques Glossary	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Cooking Techniques Glossary</h1>  <p class="muted"><regex>user_name</regex>, here's a quick reference for common cooking techniques. No need to flip through cookbooks.</p>  <ul class="list">    <li><b>Sauté</b> — cooking food quickly in a little oil over high heat.</li>    <li><b>Braise</b> — browning then simmering in liquid, usually covered.</li>    <li><b>Blanch</b> — briefly boiling then plunging into ice water.</li>    <li><b>Julienne</b> — cutting food into thin, matchstick-like strips.</li>    <li><b>Deglaze</b> — adding liquid to a hot pan to loosen browned bits.</li>    <li><b>Emulsify</b> — mixing two liquids that don't normally combine, like oil and vinegar.</li>  </ul>  <div class="callout info">    <p>Try one new technique this week. Your taste buds will thank you.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Cooking Techniques Glossary</h1>  <p class="muted">, here's a quick reference for common cooking techniques. No need to flip through cookbooks.</p>  <ul class="list">    <li><b>Sauté</b> — cooking food quickly in a little oil over high heat.</li>    <li><b>Braise</b> — browning then simmering in liquid, usually covered.</li>    <li><b>Blanch</b> — briefly boiling then plunging into ice water.</li>    <li><b>Julienne</b> — cutting food into thin, matchstick-like strips.</li>    <li><b>Deglaze</b> — adding liquid to a hot pan to loosen browned bits.</li>    <li><b>Emulsify</b> — mixing two liquids that don't normally combine, like oil and vinegar.</li>  </ul>  <div class="callout info">    <p>Try one new technique this week. Your taste buds will thank you.</p>  </div></div>	-
46.51.0	dashboard_glossary/Weather Watching Glossary	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Weather Watching Glossary</h1>  <p class="muted"><regex>user_name</regex>, here's a handy list of weather terms for your sky-watching adventures.</p>  <ul class="list">    <li><b>Cumulus</b> — puffy, white clouds with flat bottoms.</li>    <li><b>Cirrus</b> — wispy, high-altitude clouds made of ice crystals.</li>    <li><b>Front</b> — the boundary between two air masses.</li>    <li><b>Dew point</b> — the temperature at which air becomes saturated with moisture.</li>    <li><b>Barometric pressure</b> — the weight of the atmosphere, often hints at coming weather.</li>    <li><b>Virga</b> — precipitation that evaporates before reaching the ground.</li>  </ul>  <div class="callout good">    <p>Keep looking up. The sky has stories to tell.</p>  </div></div>	<div class="umi-page">  <h1>Weather Watching Glossary</h1>  <p class="muted">, here's a handy list of weather terms for your sky-watching adventures.</p>  <ul class="list">    <li><b>Cumulus</b> — puffy, white clouds with flat bottoms.</li>    <li><b>Cirrus</b> — wispy, high-altitude clouds made of ice crystals.</li>    <li><b>Front</b> — the boundary between two air masses.</li>    <li><b>Dew point</b> — the temperature at which air becomes saturated with moisture.</li>    <li><b>Barometric pressure</b> — the weight of the atmosphere, often hints at coming weather.</li>    <li><b>Virga</b> — precipitation that evaporates before reaching the ground.</li>  </ul>  <div class="callout good">    <p>Keep looking up. The sky has stories to tell.</p>  </div></div>	-
46.52.0	dashboard_glossary/Sailing Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Sailing Glossary</h1><p class="muted">Enough words to hold your own at the dock, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Bow</b> — the front of the boat. <b>Stern</b> — the back. <b>Port</b> — left when facing the bow. <b>Starboard</b> — right.</li><li><b>Hull</b> — the body of the boat, buoyant and watertight.</li><li><b>Mast</b> — the vertical pole that holds the sails. <b>Boom</b> — the horizontal spar along the foot of the mainsail.</li><li><b>Tack</b> — turning the bow through the wind so the sails swap sides. <b>Jibe</b> — the same swap, but turning the stern through the wind.</li><li><b>Heel</b> — the lean of the boat under wind pressure.</li><li><b>Knot</b> — one nautical mile per hour. A nautical mile is 1.15 statute miles.</li><li><b>Reef</b> — to shorten a sail when the wind picks up. Also a shallow ridge you'd rather not hit.</li></ul><div class="callout good"><p>Sailors say "port" and "starboard" because "left" and "right" depend on which way you're facing. Boats are bossy that way.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Sailing Glossary</h1><p class="muted">Enough words to hold your own at the dock, .</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Bow</b> — the front of the boat. <b>Stern</b> — the back. <b>Port</b> — left when facing the bow. <b>Starboard</b> — right.</li><li><b>Hull</b> — the body of the boat, buoyant and watertight.</li><li><b>Mast</b> — the vertical pole that holds the sails. <b>Boom</b> — the horizontal spar along the foot of the mainsail.</li><li><b>Tack</b> — turning the bow through the wind so the sails swap sides. <b>Jibe</b> — the same swap, but turning the stern through the wind.</li><li><b>Heel</b> — the lean of the boat under wind pressure.</li><li><b>Knot</b> — one nautical mile per hour. A nautical mile is 1.15 statute miles.</li><li><b>Reef</b> — to shorten a sail when the wind picks up. Also a shallow ridge you'd rather not hit.</li></ul><div class="callout good"><p>Sailors say "port" and "starboard" because "left" and "right" depend on which way you're facing. Boats are bossy that way.</p></div></div>	-
46.53.0	dashboard_glossary/3D Printing Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>3D Printing Glossary</h1><p class="muted">Terms for <regex>user_name</regex> — no spaghetti required.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>FDM</b> — Fused Deposition Modelling: melted filament laid down layer by layer. The common desktop method.</li><li><b>Slicer</b> — software that turns your 3D model into machine instructions (G-code).</li><li><b>Layer height</b> — thickness of each printed layer. Smaller = smoother but slower.</li><li><b>Infill</b> — the internal lattice. 15–20% is fine for most decorative prints; more for strength.</li><li><b>Supports</b> — extra printed scaffolding under overhangs, snapped off after.</li><li><b>Bed adhesion</b> — getting that first layer to stick. The eternal struggle.</li><li><b>Stringing</b> — wispy plastic threads between parts, usually a retraction or temperature issue.</li></ul><div class="callout warn"><p>First layer too high? Nothing sticks. Too low? The nozzle drags. The sweet spot is narrow and worth the patience.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>3D Printing Glossary</h1><p class="muted">Terms for  — no spaghetti required.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>FDM</b> — Fused Deposition Modelling: melted filament laid down layer by layer. The common desktop method.</li><li><b>Slicer</b> — software that turns your 3D model into machine instructions (G-code).</li><li><b>Layer height</b> — thickness of each printed layer. Smaller = smoother but slower.</li><li><b>Infill</b> — the internal lattice. 15–20% is fine for most decorative prints; more for strength.</li><li><b>Supports</b> — extra printed scaffolding under overhangs, snapped off after.</li><li><b>Bed adhesion</b> — getting that first layer to stick. The eternal struggle.</li><li><b>Stringing</b> — wispy plastic threads between parts, usually a retraction or temperature issue.</li></ul><div class="callout warn"><p>First layer too high? Nothing sticks. Too low? The nozzle drags. The sweet spot is narrow and worth the patience.</p></div></div>	-
46.54.0	dashboard_glossary/Bread Scoring Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Bread Scoring Glossary</h1><p class="muted">Small cuts, big vocabulary. For <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Lame</b> (lahm) — the razor blade on a stick used to slash dough. Also a French word for blade.</li><li><b>Score</b> — a deliberate cut that guides where the loaf opens.</li><li><b>Grigne</b> (green-yuh) — the raised lip or "ear" that forms along a well-scored cut.</li><li><b>Bloom</b> — the final expansion in the oven, often following the score line.</li><li><b>Oven spring</b> — the rapid rise during the first minutes of baking.</li><li><b>Proof</b> — the final rise before baking. Under-proofed dough tears; over-proofed dough slumps.</li><li><b>Dough strength</b> — how well gluten holds gas. Shows up in a clean, confident score.</li></ul><div class="callout info"><p>A shallow, angled cut gives a dramatic ear. A deep vertical cut gives a more even split.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Bread Scoring Glossary</h1><p class="muted">Small cuts, big vocabulary. For .</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Lame</b> (lahm) — the razor blade on a stick used to slash dough. Also a French word for blade.</li><li><b>Score</b> — a deliberate cut that guides where the loaf opens.</li><li><b>Grigne</b> (green-yuh) — the raised lip or "ear" that forms along a well-scored cut.</li><li><b>Bloom</b> — the final expansion in the oven, often following the score line.</li><li><b>Oven spring</b> — the rapid rise during the first minutes of baking.</li><li><b>Proof</b> — the final rise before baking. Under-proofed dough tears; over-proofed dough slumps.</li><li><b>Dough strength</b> — how well gluten holds gas. Shows up in a clean, confident score.</li></ul><div class="callout info"><p>A shallow, angled cut gives a dramatic ear. A deep vertical cut gives a more even split.</p></div></div>	-
46.55.0	dashboard_glossary/Pottery Glazing Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Pottery Glazing Glossary</h1><p class="muted">Words for the kiln shelf. For <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Glaze</b> — a glassy coating that melts onto pottery during firing.</li><li><b>Bisque</b> — pottery fired once without glaze, ready for glazing.</li><li><b>Celadon</b> — a family of green glazes, traditionally jade-like, often with fine crackle.</li><li><b>Shino</b> — a Japanese-style glaze, usually white or orange, often with pinholed texture.</li><li><b>Crawl</b> — when glaze pulls apart during firing, leaving bare patches like islands.</li><li><b>Fit</b> — how well glaze and clay expand and contract together. Poor fit means crazing or shivering.</li><li><b>Kiln wash</b> — a protective coating on kiln shelves to stop drips from fusing.</li></ul><div class="callout good"><p>A good glaze fit is quiet chemistry. You mostly notice it by its absence — no cracks, no flakes.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Pottery Glazing Glossary</h1><p class="muted">Words for the kiln shelf. For .</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Glaze</b> — a glassy coating that melts onto pottery during firing.</li><li><b>Bisque</b> — pottery fired once without glaze, ready for glazing.</li><li><b>Celadon</b> — a family of green glazes, traditionally jade-like, often with fine crackle.</li><li><b>Shino</b> — a Japanese-style glaze, usually white or orange, often with pinholed texture.</li><li><b>Crawl</b> — when glaze pulls apart during firing, leaving bare patches like islands.</li><li><b>Fit</b> — how well glaze and clay expand and contract together. Poor fit means crazing or shivering.</li><li><b>Kiln wash</b> — a protective coating on kiln shelves to stop drips from fusing.</li></ul><div class="callout good"><p>A good glaze fit is quiet chemistry. You mostly notice it by its absence — no cracks, no flakes.</p></div></div>	-
46.56.0	dashboard_glossary/Rock Tumbling Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Rock Tumbling Glossary</h1><p class="muted">From rough to shine, for <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Grit</b> — abrasive powder graded by particle size. Coarse shapes, fine smooths, polish gleams.</li><li><b>Slurry</b> — the muddy mix of grit, water, and rock dust inside the barrel.</li><li><b>Burnishing</b> — the final step with soap or borax to clean and brighten stones.</li><li><b>Mohs scale</b> — hardness from 1 (talc) to 10 (diamond). Tumbling works best on stones of similar hardness.</li><li><b>Stage</b> — one grit run, usually 4–7 days per stage.</li><li><b>Cerium oxide</b> — a very fine polish, great for quartz and agate.</li><li><b>Barrel</b> — the rotating drum that does the work. Don't overfill; leave room to tumble.</li></ul><div class="callout warn"><p>Mixing soft and hard stones in one barrel means the soft ones vanish while the hard ones are still rough.</p></div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Rock Tumbling Glossary</h1><p class="muted">From rough to shine, for .</p><ul class="list"><li><b>Grit</b> — abrasive powder graded by particle size. Coarse shapes, fine smooths, polish gleams.</li><li><b>Slurry</b> — the muddy mix of grit, water, and rock dust inside the barrel.</li><li><b>Burnishing</b> — the final step with soap or borax to clean and brighten stones.</li><li><b>Mohs scale</b> — hardness from 1 (talc) to 10 (diamond). Tumbling works best on stones of similar hardness.</li><li><b>Stage</b> — one grit run, usually 4–7 days per stage.</li><li><b>Cerium oxide</b> — a very fine polish, great for quartz and agate.</li><li><b>Barrel</b> — the rotating drum that does the work. Don't overfill; leave room to tumble.</li></ul><div class="callout warn"><p>Mixing soft and hard stones in one barrel means the soft ones vanish while the hard ones are still rough.</p></div></div>	-
46.57.0	dashboard_glossary/Sourdough Starter Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Sourdough Starter Glossary</h1><p class="muted">Hey <regex>user_name</regex> — the words that make a jar of flour and water feel like a pet.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><div class="label">Terms</div><div class="value">8</div></div><div class="stat"><div class="label">Read time</div><div class="value">2<span class="unit"> min</span></div></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Starter</summary><div class="body">Flour plus water, colonised by wild yeast and bacteria. Feed it, and it bubbles back at you.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Hooch</summary><div class="body">The grey liquid that pools on top when the starter is hungry. Stir it in or pour it off — both work.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Autolyse</summary><div class="body">Resting flour and water before salt or starter. The gluten hydrates itself and the dough gets friendlier.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Bulk ferment</summary><div class="body">The first long rise, before shaping. Judged by dough feel, not by the clock.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Levain</summary><div class="body">A fresh offshoot of your starter, built for one bake at a set ratio.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Crumb</summary><div class="body">The inside of the loaf. Open crumb means big airy holes; tight crumb means dense and even.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Dough temperature</summary><div class="body">Your real control knob. Warmer dough ferments faster, cooler dough slower — every other variable is downstream.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Discard</summary><div class="body">Starter you remove before feeding, so the jar doesn't overflow the kitchen. Bake it into pancakes.</div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Sourdough Starter Glossary</h1><p class="muted">Hey  — the words that make a jar of flour and water feel like a pet.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><div class="label">Terms</div><div class="value">8</div></div><div class="stat"><div class="label">Read time</div><div class="value">2<span class="unit"> min</span></div></div></div><details class="acc"><summary>Starter</summary><div class="body">Flour plus water, colonised by wild yeast and bacteria. Feed it, and it bubbles back at you.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Hooch</summary><div class="body">The grey liquid that pools on top when the starter is hungry. Stir it in or pour it off — both work.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Autolyse</summary><div class="body">Resting flour and water before salt or starter. The gluten hydrates itself and the dough gets friendlier.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Bulk ferment</summary><div class="body">The first long rise, before shaping. Judged by dough feel, not by the clock.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Levain</summary><div class="body">A fresh offshoot of your starter, built for one bake at a set ratio.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Crumb</summary><div class="body">The inside of the loaf. Open crumb means big airy holes; tight crumb means dense and even.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Dough temperature</summary><div class="body">Your real control knob. Warmer dough ferments faster, cooler dough slower — every other variable is downstream.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Discard</summary><div class="body">Starter you remove before feeding, so the jar doesn't overflow the kitchen. Bake it into pancakes.</div></details></div>	-
46.58.0	dashboard_glossary/Home Plumbing Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Home Plumbing Glossary</h1><p class="muted">Loosely translated from Plumber, <regex>user_name</regex>. Useful before you call one.</p><div class="card"><div class="card-title">The basics</div><ul class="list"><li><b>Shutoff valve</b> — the small knob under a sink or behind a toilet. Know where yours lives before you need it.</li><li><b>P-trap</b> — the curved pipe that holds water to block sewer gas. A P-trap is fine; an S-trap is a code headache.</li><li><b>Cleanout</b> — a capped access point for a snake or auger. Usually on the main line.</li><li><b>Supply line</b> — the thin tube bringing fresh water <em>in</em>. Drain pipes take it away.</li><li><b>Flapper</b> — the rubber puck in a toilet tank. Cheap, easy, the usual culprit for a running toilet.</li><li><b>Auger / snake</b> — a flexible cable that clears clogs. Power snakes are the rented kind.</li><li><b>Hose bib</b> — the outdoor spigot. Turn it off inside before winter.</li><li><b>Vent stack</b> — the pipe going up through the roof. It keeps drains flowing smoothly.</li></ul></div><div class="callout info"><b>Tip:</b> before any job, close the shutoff, open the lowest tap to drain pressure, then work.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Home Plumbing Glossary</h1><p class="muted">Loosely translated from Plumber, . Useful before you call one.</p><div class="card"><div class="card-title">The basics</div><ul class="list"><li><b>Shutoff valve</b> — the small knob under a sink or behind a toilet. Know where yours lives before you need it.</li><li><b>P-trap</b> — the curved pipe that holds water to block sewer gas. A P-trap is fine; an S-trap is a code headache.</li><li><b>Cleanout</b> — a capped access point for a snake or auger. Usually on the main line.</li><li><b>Supply line</b> — the thin tube bringing fresh water <em>in</em>. Drain pipes take it away.</li><li><b>Flapper</b> — the rubber puck in a toilet tank. Cheap, easy, the usual culprit for a running toilet.</li><li><b>Auger / snake</b> — a flexible cable that clears clogs. Power snakes are the rented kind.</li><li><b>Hose bib</b> — the outdoor spigot. Turn it off inside before winter.</li><li><b>Vent stack</b> — the pipe going up through the roof. It keeps drains flowing smoothly.</li></ul></div><div class="callout info"><b>Tip:</b> before any job, close the shutoff, open the lowest tap to drain pressure, then work.</div></div>	-
46.59.0	dashboard_glossary/PC Building Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>PC Building Glossary</h1><p class="muted">A starter map for your first build, <regex>user_name</regex>. Nothing here bites.</p><svg class="chart" viewBox="0 0 300 60" role="img" aria-label="Bars showing relative budget share: CPU, GPU, rest"><rect class="bar" x="20" y="20" width="40" height="30"></rect><rect class="bar alt" x="110" y="8" width="40" height="42"></rect><rect class="bar" x="200" y="30" width="40" height="20"></rect><text x="40" y="15" class="lbl" text-anchor="middle">CPU</text><text x="130" y="6" class="lbl" text-anchor="middle">GPU</text><text x="220" y="26" class="lbl" text-anchor="middle">Rest</text></svg><details class="acc"><summary>CPU</summary><div class="body">The processor. Sits in the socket, cooled by a heatsink or AIO.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>GPU</summary><div class="body">The graphics card. Slots into the top PCIe lane, usually the priciest part.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>TDP</summary><div class="body">Thermal design power — roughly the heat a part makes. Add them up to size your PSU.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Thermal paste</summary><div class="body">The thin goo between CPU and cooler. A pea-sized dot is plenty.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Chipset</summary><div class="body">The board's feature set — extra USB, overclocking, PCIe lanes. It must match the CPU socket.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>VRM</summary><div class="body">Voltage regulator modules. They feed the CPU clean power; better VRMs mean steadier performance.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>XMP / EXPO</summary><div class="body">A one-click profile that runs RAM at its advertised speed. Turn it on in BIOS.</div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>PC Building Glossary</h1><p class="muted">A starter map for your first build, . Nothing here bites.</p><svg class="chart" viewBox="0 0 300 60" role="img" aria-label="Bars showing relative budget share: CPU, GPU, rest"><rect class="bar" x="20" y="20" width="40" height="30"></rect><rect class="bar alt" x="110" y="8" width="40" height="42"></rect><rect class="bar" x="200" y="30" width="40" height="20"></rect><text x="40" y="15" class="lbl" text-anchor="middle">CPU</text><text x="130" y="6" class="lbl" text-anchor="middle">GPU</text><text x="220" y="26" class="lbl" text-anchor="middle">Rest</text></svg><details class="acc"><summary>CPU</summary><div class="body">The processor. Sits in the socket, cooled by a heatsink or AIO.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>GPU</summary><div class="body">The graphics card. Slots into the top PCIe lane, usually the priciest part.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>TDP</summary><div class="body">Thermal design power — roughly the heat a part makes. Add them up to size your PSU.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Thermal paste</summary><div class="body">The thin goo between CPU and cooler. A pea-sized dot is plenty.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Chipset</summary><div class="body">The board's feature set — extra USB, overclocking, PCIe lanes. It must match the CPU socket.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>VRM</summary><div class="body">Voltage regulator modules. They feed the CPU clean power; better VRMs mean steadier performance.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary>XMP / EXPO</summary><div class="body">A one-click profile that runs RAM at its advertised speed. Turn it on in BIOS.</div></details></div>	-
46.60.0	dashboard_glossary/Home Brewing Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Home Brewing Glossary</h1><p class="muted">Brew-day vocabulary, <regex>user_name</regex>. Cheers in advance.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><div class="label">Terms</div><div class="value">8</div></div><div class="stat"><div class="label">Brew length</div><div class="value">4<span class="unit"> h</span></div></div></div><ul class="list"><li><b>Wort</b> — the sweet, unfermented liquid before yeast goes in.</li><li><b>Mash</b> — steeping crushed grain in warm water to convert starch to sugar.</li><li><b>Sparge</b> — rinsing the grain bed with hot water to capture leftover sugars.</li><li><b>Krausen</b> — the foamy head of fermentation. A healthy beer is a burping beer.</li><li><b>Original gravity (OG)</b> — sugar density before fermentation; it predicts strength.</li><li><b>Final gravity (FG)</b> — density after. OG minus FG roughly gives alcohol.</li><li><b>IBU</b> — International Bitterness Units. Bigger number, more bite.</li><li><b>Dry hopping</b> — adding hops after fermentation for aroma instead of bitterness.</li></ul><div class="callout good">Sanitise everything the wort touches. Wild yeast is a charming idea and a sad pint.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Home Brewing Glossary</h1><p class="muted">Brew-day vocabulary, . Cheers in advance.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><div class="label">Terms</div><div class="value">8</div></div><div class="stat"><div class="label">Brew length</div><div class="value">4<span class="unit"> h</span></div></div></div><ul class="list"><li><b>Wort</b> — the sweet, unfermented liquid before yeast goes in.</li><li><b>Mash</b> — steeping crushed grain in warm water to convert starch to sugar.</li><li><b>Sparge</b> — rinsing the grain bed with hot water to capture leftover sugars.</li><li><b>Krausen</b> — the foamy head of fermentation. A healthy beer is a burping beer.</li><li><b>Original gravity (OG)</b> — sugar density before fermentation; it predicts strength.</li><li><b>Final gravity (FG)</b> — density after. OG minus FG roughly gives alcohol.</li><li><b>IBU</b> — International Bitterness Units. Bigger number, more bite.</li><li><b>Dry hopping</b> — adding hops after fermentation for aroma instead of bitterness.</li></ul><div class="callout good">Sanitise everything the wort touches. Wild yeast is a charming idea and a sad pint.</div></div>	-
46.61.0	dashboard_glossary/Guitar Repair Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Guitar Repair Glossary</h1><p class="muted">A quick reference for the words your repair person uses, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Action</div>  <p>The height of the strings above the frets. Lower action feels faster but can buzz; higher is cleaner but stiffer.</p></div><div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Relief</div>  <p>A slight forward bow in the neck. A little relief keeps strings from rattling against the frets.</p></div><div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Intonation</div>  <p>Whether the guitar plays in tune up and down the neck. Adjusted by moving the saddles.</p></div><div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Fret Buzz</div>  <p>A metallic rattle when a string hits a fret. Usually a sign of low action, uneven frets, or too little relief.</p></div><div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Nut</div>  <p>The slotted piece at the top of the neck that guides strings to the tuners. A worn nut causes tuning trouble.</p></div><div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Saddle</div>  <p>The part on the bridge that each string rests on. Moving it changes intonation.</p></div><details class="acc">  <summary>More terms</summary>  <div class="body">    <p><b>Truss rod</b> — a metal rod inside the neck that adjusts relief.</p>    <p><b>Fret level</b> — grinding frets flat so they're all the same height.</p>    <p><b>Shim</b> — a thin piece of material used to change neck angle.</p>  </div></details></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Guitar Repair Glossary</h1><p class="muted">A quick reference for the words your repair person uses, .</p><div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Action</div>  <p>The height of the strings above the frets. Lower action feels faster but can buzz; higher is cleaner but stiffer.</p></div><div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Relief</div>  <p>A slight forward bow in the neck. A little relief keeps strings from rattling against the frets.</p></div><div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Intonation</div>  <p>Whether the guitar plays in tune up and down the neck. Adjusted by moving the saddles.</p></div><div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Fret Buzz</div>  <p>A metallic rattle when a string hits a fret. Usually a sign of low action, uneven frets, or too little relief.</p></div><div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Nut</div>  <p>The slotted piece at the top of the neck that guides strings to the tuners. A worn nut causes tuning trouble.</p></div><div class="card">  <div class="card-title">Saddle</div>  <p>The part on the bridge that each string rests on. Moving it changes intonation.</p></div><details class="acc">  <summary>More terms</summary>  <div class="body">    <p><b>Truss rod</b> — a metal rod inside the neck that adjusts relief.</p>    <p><b>Fret level</b> — grinding frets flat so they're all the same height.</p>    <p><b>Shim</b> — a thin piece of material used to change neck angle.</p>  </div></details></div>	-
46.62.0	dashboard_glossary/Photography Exposure Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Photography Exposure Glossary</h1><p class="muted">The three dials, plus friends. For <regex>user_name</regex>, on any camera.</p><details class="acc"><summary><b>Aperture</b></summary><div class="body">The hole in the lens, written f/1.8, f/8, f/16. Small number, big hole, more light and blurrier background. Each full stop doubles or halves the light.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary><b>Shutter Speed</b></summary><div class="body">How long the sensor sees light, in fractions of a second. 1/500 freezes a runner; 1/30 blurs them. Handheld, stay near 1/60 or faster.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary><b>ISO</b></summary><div class="body">Sensor sensitivity. Raise it in dim light, accept the grain. ISO 100 is clean; ISO 6400 is noisy but sometimes the only way home.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary><b>Stop</b></summary><div class="body">A doubling or halving of light. One stop of aperture, shutter or ISO all swap equally — that trade is the whole game.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary><b>Depth of Field</b></summary><div class="body">How much is acceptably sharp, front to back. Wide aperture plus close subject equals thin slice of focus.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary><b>Exposure Compensation</b></summary><div class="body">The +/− dial on auto modes. Snow looks grey? Add +1. Backlit portrait dark? Add +1.</div></details><div class="callout good">Start in aperture priority, pick f/2.8 or f/8, and let the camera do the maths while you watch the light.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Photography Exposure Glossary</h1><p class="muted">The three dials, plus friends. For , on any camera.</p><details class="acc"><summary><b>Aperture</b></summary><div class="body">The hole in the lens, written f/1.8, f/8, f/16. Small number, big hole, more light and blurrier background. Each full stop doubles or halves the light.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary><b>Shutter Speed</b></summary><div class="body">How long the sensor sees light, in fractions of a second. 1/500 freezes a runner; 1/30 blurs them. Handheld, stay near 1/60 or faster.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary><b>ISO</b></summary><div class="body">Sensor sensitivity. Raise it in dim light, accept the grain. ISO 100 is clean; ISO 6400 is noisy but sometimes the only way home.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary><b>Stop</b></summary><div class="body">A doubling or halving of light. One stop of aperture, shutter or ISO all swap equally — that trade is the whole game.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary><b>Depth of Field</b></summary><div class="body">How much is acceptably sharp, front to back. Wide aperture plus close subject equals thin slice of focus.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary><b>Exposure Compensation</b></summary><div class="body">The +/− dial on auto modes. Snow looks grey? Add +1. Backlit portrait dark? Add +1.</div></details><div class="callout good">Start in aperture priority, pick f/2.8 or f/8, and let the camera do the maths while you watch the light.</div></div>	-
46.63.0	dashboard_glossary/Sewing & Alterations Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Sewing &amp; Alterations Glossary</h1><p class="muted">The words on the pattern envelope, in plain speech for <regex>user_name</regex>.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Stitches</span><span class="value">3</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Fabric terms</span><span class="value">3</span></div></div><details class="acc"><summary><b>Selvedge</b></summary><div class="body">The firm, woven edge of the fabric that doesn't fray. Pattern pieces normally sit parallel to it.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary><b>Grainline</b></summary><div class="body">The arrow on a pattern piece. Line it parallel to the selvedge or the finished garment will twist on the body.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary><b>Nap</b></summary><div class="body">Directional pile — velvet, corduroy, fleece. Cut every piece the same way or panels catch the light differently.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary><b>Basting</b></summary><div class="body">Long, loose temporary stitches that hold pieces while you check fit. Pull them out later; no shame in them.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary><b>Seam Allowance</b></summary><div class="body">The margin between your stitch line and the raw edge. Most commercial patterns use 5/8 inch; many indie ones use 3/8.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary><b>Ease</b></summary><div class="body">Two meanings: extra room built into a garment, and the gentle gathering of a sleeve cap into an armhole. Context tells you which.</div></details><div class="callout good">Press as you sew. Half of a professional-looking hem is a hot iron, not a clever machine setting.</div></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Sewing &amp; Alterations Glossary</h1><p class="muted">The words on the pattern envelope, in plain speech for .</p><div class="grid two"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Stitches</span><span class="value">3</span></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Fabric terms</span><span class="value">3</span></div></div><details class="acc"><summary><b>Selvedge</b></summary><div class="body">The firm, woven edge of the fabric that doesn't fray. Pattern pieces normally sit parallel to it.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary><b>Grainline</b></summary><div class="body">The arrow on a pattern piece. Line it parallel to the selvedge or the finished garment will twist on the body.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary><b>Nap</b></summary><div class="body">Directional pile — velvet, corduroy, fleece. Cut every piece the same way or panels catch the light differently.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary><b>Basting</b></summary><div class="body">Long, loose temporary stitches that hold pieces while you check fit. Pull them out later; no shame in them.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary><b>Seam Allowance</b></summary><div class="body">The margin between your stitch line and the raw edge. Most commercial patterns use 5/8 inch; many indie ones use 3/8.</div></details><details class="acc"><summary><b>Ease</b></summary><div class="body">Two meanings: extra room built into a garment, and the gentle gathering of a sleeve cap into an armhole. Context tells you which.</div></details><div class="callout good">Press as you sew. Half of a professional-looking hem is a hot iron, not a clever machine setting.</div></div>	-
46.0.1	dashboard_glossary/Film Noir Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Film Noir Glossary</h1><p class="muted">Shadows, slang, and a lit cigarette. Here's the lingo.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Femme Fatale</h3><p>A dangerously attractive woman who leads the hero astray. She usually knows more than she lets on.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Hardboiled</h3><p>Tough, unsentimental style. Think trench coats and one-liners.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Voice-over</h3><p>The narrator's weary inner monologue, often past tense and full of regret.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Chiaroscuro</h3><p>High-contrast lighting. The dark isn't just dark; it's a character.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">MacGuffin</h3><p>The thing everyone chases. Often a briefcase, a statue, or a secret.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Fall Guy</h3><p>The patsy who takes the blame. Usually the protagonist, at least for a while.</p></div></div><p class="t-small dim">That's the rundown, <regex>user_name</regex>. Now go watch <em>Double Indemnity</em>.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Film Noir Glossary</h1><p class="muted">Shadows, slang, and a lit cigarette. Here's the lingo.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Femme Fatale</h3><p>A dangerously attractive woman who leads the hero astray. She usually knows more than she lets on.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Hardboiled</h3><p>Tough, unsentimental style. Think trench coats and one-liners.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Voice-over</h3><p>The narrator's weary inner monologue, often past tense and full of regret.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Chiaroscuro</h3><p>High-contrast lighting. The dark isn't just dark; it's a character.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">MacGuffin</h3><p>The thing everyone chases. Often a briefcase, a statue, or a secret.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Fall Guy</h3><p>The patsy who takes the blame. Usually the protagonist, at least for a while.</p></div></div><p class="t-small dim">That's the rundown, . Now go watch <em>Double Indemnity</em>.</p></div>	-
46.1.1	dashboard_glossary/Violin Anatomy Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Violin Anatomy Glossary</h1><p class="muted">Know your instrument, <regex>user_name</regex>. Here's the tour.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">F-holes</h3><p>The two curved openings on the belly. They let sound out and look like an elegant <em>f</em>.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Bridge</h3><p>The carved wooden piece that lifts the strings. It transfers vibration to the body.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Soundpost</h3><p>A small spruce dowel inside, under the bridge. It supports the top and shapes the tone.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Purfling</h3><p>The thin inlay near the edge. Decorative and structural.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Frog</h3><p>The part of the bow you hold. Not a amphibian, despite the name.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Chinrest</h3><p>The contoured piece where your jaw sits. Comfort is key.</p></div></div><p class="t-small dim">That's the nickel tour. Now go rosin up.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Violin Anatomy Glossary</h1><p class="muted">Know your instrument, . Here's the tour.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">F-holes</h3><p>The two curved openings on the belly. They let sound out and look like an elegant <em>f</em>.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Bridge</h3><p>The carved wooden piece that lifts the strings. It transfers vibration to the body.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Soundpost</h3><p>A small spruce dowel inside, under the bridge. It supports the top and shapes the tone.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Purfling</h3><p>The thin inlay near the edge. Decorative and structural.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Frog</h3><p>The part of the bow you hold. Not a amphibian, despite the name.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Chinrest</h3><p>The contoured piece where your jaw sits. Comfort is key.</p></div></div><p class="t-small dim">That's the nickel tour. Now go rosin up.</p></div>	-
46.2.1	dashboard_glossary/Cloud Spotting Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Cloud Spotting Glossary</h1><p class="muted">Look up. Here's what you're seeing.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Cumulus</h3><p>Puffy, white, flat-bottomed. The classic cloud. Fair weather, usually.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Cumulonimbus</h3><p>The big one. Towering, dark, with an anvil top. Thunderstorms live here.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Stratus</h3><p>Low, grey, uniform. Like a blanket over the sky. Can bring drizzle.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Cirrus</h3><p>Wispy, high, made of ice. 'Mare's tails' when they curl.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Alto</h3><p>Mid-level prefix. Altostratus, altocumulus. Higher than stratus, lower than cirrus.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Nimbostratus</h3><p>Thick, dark, rain-bearing. The one that ruins picnics.</p></div></div><p class="t-small dim">Keep looking up, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Cloud Spotting Glossary</h1><p class="muted">Look up. Here's what you're seeing.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Cumulus</h3><p>Puffy, white, flat-bottomed. The classic cloud. Fair weather, usually.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Cumulonimbus</h3><p>The big one. Towering, dark, with an anvil top. Thunderstorms live here.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Stratus</h3><p>Low, grey, uniform. Like a blanket over the sky. Can bring drizzle.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Cirrus</h3><p>Wispy, high, made of ice. 'Mare's tails' when they curl.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Alto</h3><p>Mid-level prefix. Altostratus, altocumulus. Higher than stratus, lower than cirrus.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Nimbostratus</h3><p>Thick, dark, rain-bearing. The one that ruins picnics.</p></div></div><p class="t-small dim">Keep looking up, .</p></div>	-
46.3.1	dashboard_glossary/Beer Style Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Beer Style Glossary</h1><p class="muted">From pilsner to porter. The vocabulary of the taproom.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">IPA</h3><p>India Pale Ale. Hopped up for preservation, now hopped up for flavor. Citrus, pine, bitterness.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Stout</h3><p>Dark, roasty, often with coffee or chocolate notes. Guinness is the famous one.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Pilsner</h3><p>Pale lager from Czechia. Crisp, clean, golden. The original pale lager.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Witbier</h3><p>Belgian wheat beer with orange peel and coriander. Hazy and refreshing.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Saison</h3><p>Farmhouse ale from Wallonia. Dry, spicy, yeast-driven. Brewed in winter for summer drinking.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">ABV</h3><p>Alcohol by Volume. The percentage that tells you how slow to sip.</p></div></div><p class="t-small dim">Cheers, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Beer Style Glossary</h1><p class="muted">From pilsner to porter. The vocabulary of the taproom.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">IPA</h3><p>India Pale Ale. Hopped up for preservation, now hopped up for flavor. Citrus, pine, bitterness.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Stout</h3><p>Dark, roasty, often with coffee or chocolate notes. Guinness is the famous one.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Pilsner</h3><p>Pale lager from Czechia. Crisp, clean, golden. The original pale lager.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Witbier</h3><p>Belgian wheat beer with orange peel and coriander. Hazy and refreshing.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Saison</h3><p>Farmhouse ale from Wallonia. Dry, spicy, yeast-driven. Brewed in winter for summer drinking.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">ABV</h3><p>Alcohol by Volume. The percentage that tells you how slow to sip.</p></div></div><p class="t-small dim">Cheers, .</p></div>	-
46.4.1	dashboard_glossary/Telescope & Stargazing Glossary	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Telescope & Stargazing Glossary</h1><p class="muted">Point your scope, learn the words.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Aperture</h3><p>The diameter of the main lens or mirror. Bigger aperture means more light and more detail.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Right Ascension</h3><p>Celestial longitude. Measured in hours, minutes, seconds. Like time zones for the sky.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Declination</h3><p>Celestial latitude. 0° at the celestial equator, 90° at the poles.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Magnitude</h3><p>Brightness. Lower numbers are brighter. Vega is 0, the faintest stars are 6.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Barlow Lens</h3><p>A lens that doubles or triples your magnification. Cheap way to boost power.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Collimation</h3><p>Aligning the mirrors in a reflector. Necessary for sharp views, slightly tedious.</p></div></div><p class="t-small dim">Clear skies, <regex>user_name</regex>.</p></div>	<div class="umi-page"><h1>Telescope & Stargazing Glossary</h1><p class="muted">Point your scope, learn the words.</p><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Aperture</h3><p>The diameter of the main lens or mirror. Bigger aperture means more light and more detail.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Right Ascension</h3><p>Celestial longitude. Measured in hours, minutes, seconds. Like time zones for the sky.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Declination</h3><p>Celestial latitude. 0° at the celestial equator, 90° at the poles.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Magnitude</h3><p>Brightness. Lower numbers are brighter. Vega is 0, the faintest stars are 6.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Barlow Lens</h3><p>A lens that doubles or triples your magnification. Cheap way to boost power.</p></div><div class="card"><h3 class="card-title">Collimation</h3><p>Aligning the mirrors in a reflector. Necessary for sharp views, slightly tedious.</p></div></div><p class="t-small dim">Clear skies, .</p></div>	-
